// Generated by tools/build-daily.js from content/daily/*.js: do not edit by hand.
const DAILY={};
// Agnes, bank teller at Nordbank, Frostholm (administrators, no faction). Threads: counting every coin twice, who on the island has money and where it came from, her discretion, Ole next door at the harbour, her quiet flat in Skyline Apartments.
DAILY['Agnes']={
7:["(Agnes counts a stack of notes, then counts it again.) Welcome to Nordbank. The north-west road's just opened, and half of Frostholm's been in to see whether their money survived the snow. It did. Mostly.",[
  ["Are you the teller?","The only one. I count, I stamp, and I forget. Particularly the last."],
  ["Do you know everyone's balance?","Everyone's. It's not a gift. It's a hazard of the trade."],
  ["Can I open an account?","If you've a passport and a respectable reference. Do you have either?"]],
 "Please step back from the counter. The line is behind you."],
8:["(Agnes straightens a pile of coins.) Sunday, and I'm here to balance the books. The bank's closed, but the ledger doesn't care. Every coin twice, as always.",[
  ["Why twice?","The first time's for the bank. The second's for me."],
  ["Is that a habit?","It's an obsession. But it's served me well. I've never been a penny out."],
  ["What's the strangest deposit you've had?","A bag of gold teeth. I asked no questions. I counted them twice."]],
 "Don't breathe on the notes. They're easily offended."],
9:["(Agnes checks a passbook.) Everyone's talking about the funeral tomorrow. The bank's closed for the day. I've put up a notice, with a black border. I drew it myself.",[
  ["Is that your handwriting?","Of course. A bank should look dignified, in life and in death."],
  ["Did you know the king?","He had an account here. A small one. He deposited a krona on his birthday, every year. I'll miss it."],
  ["What happens to his money?","I couldn't say. Not that I wouldn't. It's just not mine to tell."]],
 "Please observe the black border. And the silence."],
10:["(Agnes sits upright at the counter.) The funeral's under way, and the town is empty. I've counted the till three times. Nothing's missing. Not even a button.",[
  ["Is it strange to be alone?","A bank's rarely quiet. It's like a library with money."],
  ["Did you listen to the service?","On the radio, with one ear. The other was on the vault."],
  ["What would the king say?","'Don't forget to lock up.' He was very practical."]],
 "A respectful day. Lock up well."],
11:["(Agnes taps a ledger.) Business is brisk. A great many people paid off their debts after the funeral, as if the king's death had reminded them of their own. I've never seen so much honesty.",[
  ["Is honesty good for business?","It's good for the books. Less good for the gossip."],
  ["Who paid off the most?","I couldn't possibly say. But a man in a fur collar was surprisingly prompt."],
  ["Do you ever refuse a deposit?","When the notes smell of fish. Or the smell of something worse."]],
 "Sign here. And please don't smudge."],
12:["(Agnes holds a note up to the light.) A strange bill, this one. Very new, very crisp, from a bundle that's come through Frostholm three times this week. Someone's moving money in circles.",[
  ["Is that illegal?","Not by itself. It's merely odd. I note it, and I keep it in my head."],
  ["Who's doing it?","A company with a lot of letters in its name. I'd say no more."],
  ["Would you tell the police?","If they asked. Nobody ever does. They think a teller only counts."]],
 "Don't repeat that. A bank thrives on discretion."],
13:["(Agnes arranges coins by size.) Tomorrow's the big day, and the bank will be closed. Extra cash has been ordered, in case the island needs to buy something. It never does, but it always asks.",[
  ["Who's asking for cash?","Everyone. Kelp Cola, the Party, the Church. They all want notes in small denominations."],
  ["Is that suspicious?","Small notes are for bribes and tips. I don't ask which."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About the safe, a little. The rest, no. Money's the one thing that behaves."]],
 "Lock up your purses. Tomorrow's a day for it."],
14:["(Agnes stands behind a bolted door.) The big day, and I've closed early. I sat at the window and counted the passers-by. One hundred and eleven, and not one with a smile.",[
  ["Is it a tense day?","Very. Money is quiet when people are afraid. It stays in pockets."],
  ["Did you hear anything?","A rumour that someone's moving funds to the north. I'll keep it to myself."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A teller's seen worse. She's seen a run on the bank."]],
 "Come back when it's over. I'll have the lights on."],
15:["(Agnes smooths a ledger.) The morning after, and a lot of people are paying in cash they'd rather forget they'd got. I take it, count it, and ask nothing.",[
  ["Is that the job?","It's the whole job. A bank is a very patient priest."],
  ["Any large deposits?","A few. One from a man who said he'd won it at cards. I wished him luck."],
  ["Do you ever speculate?","Never. I save. A teller with a gambling habit is a very short story."]],
 "Next, please. Mind the step."],
16:["(Agnes hands over a receipt.) There's a debate tonight, and I'm told the cola men are betting on the Party. Nobody on the island's ever been right about a debate, but the wagers are large.",[
  ["Who's placing the bets?","I couldn't say. But I've seen several coins change hands that were not in the till."],
  ["Will you bet?","Never. But I'll count the winnings."],
  ["Which side do you favour?","I've no politics. I've a pension plan."]],
 "A debate's a bad investment. Unless you're selling tickets."],
17:["(Agnes grips the counter.) The floor shook, and a drawer of coins slid open. I caught half of them, and counted the rest on the floor. All present.",[
  ["Was that the volcano?","It was something. The vault door hummed. I've never heard it do that."],
  ["Are you worried?","About the vault, no. About the staff, only me."],
  ["What do you do in a tremor?","Count. It's the only thing that keeps me calm."]],
 "If it shakes again, stay behind the counter. It's thick."],
18:["(Agnes lines up the coins.) The air smells of sulphur, and the customers are fewer. Those who came wanted to withdraw their savings, and put them under mattresses. I let them.",[
  ["Is that wise?","It's their money. A mattress is a bank with no interest."],
  ["Are people panicking?","A little. They're counting, too. It's catching."],
  ["Do you think the bank's safe?","It's stone, with a good lock. It's the people who are fragile."]],
 "Breathe through a handkerchief. The smell's worse by the door."],
19:["(Agnes examines a coin.) A man came in wearing a false moustache, and tried to cash a cheque. I asked him to remove it. He said it was glued on. I said, so is the cheque.",[
  ["Was he a prince?","I couldn't possibly say. But his signature looked remarkably royal."],
  ["Did you cash it?","I did. The money was real, and so was the cheque. The moustache wasn't, and I ignored it."],
  ["Is a bank always so discreet?","A bank that talks is a bank that closes."]],
 "Thank you for banking with Nordbank. Please keep your moustache on."],
20:["(Agnes checks a lock.) A rumour of a robbery tonight. Not here, I'm told. At the palace. It's the talk of the town, and the Syndicate's behind it, they say. Everyone says it's a secret.",[
  ["Do you know the Syndicate?","They have accounts with us. All perfectly legal, with lawyers. I've counted their notes for years."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Not for a teller. We're the least interesting people on the island."],
  ["Would they rob the bank?","Never. They'd be robbing themselves."]],
 "I never said a word. And neither did you."],
21:["(Agnes has a scarf over her mouth.) The volcano's erupted. The sky's black. The bank's full of people withdrawing their savings, in tears. I've counted till my arm hurts.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I know. The lava's in the north, far from the towns. We were lucky."],
  ["How's the bank?","Full, and grey. The ash comes in under the door. I've laid sacks along the sill."],
  ["Will you close?","Never. People want their money when the world's ending. It's very human."]],
 "Wait your turn. And keep your hands off the counter. It's grey."],
22:["(Agnes brushes ash from the ledger.) The ash settles on every note. I've had to wash a hundred banknotes in a basin, and dry them on a line. It's the strangest day of my career.",[
  ["Is money washable?","If you're careful. It comes up a bit pale, but it's legal tender."],
  ["Is business quieter?","People are too busy sweeping to spend. It's the only blessing."],
  ["How's Ole?","Next door at the harbour, grey as a pigeon. He phoned to ask if the bank's dusty. I said, very."]],
 "Please don't sneeze near the notes."],
23:["(Agnes taps the vault door.) Odd withdrawals today, from the oil company. A very large sum, in cash, for 'repairs'. I counted it twice, and then again. It was all there.",[
  ["Repairs to what?","A pipeline, I think. Something's gone wrong with one. Nothing to do with me."],
  ["Does it worry you?","A large cash withdrawal always worries me. It's like a long silence in a conversation."],
  ["Would you tell the police?","I would, if I were asked. For now, I've counted it, and that's the end."]],
 "If the vault's open, don't lean on it."],
24:["(Agnes straightens a pen.) A very polite monk came to deposit a donation, for a retreat to the mountains. A chest of coins, and a reliquary on top. I said we'd keep the coins. The relic stayed with him.",[
  ["A relic? In a bank?","I find religion's very difficult to insure."],
  ["How much was in the chest?","Enough to feed forty monks for a winter. I counted it twice, and I said a prayer."],
  ["Are the Church's accounts healthy?","Modest. Honest. The best sort."]],
 "Go in peace. And please keep the receipt."],
25:["(Agnes examines a signature.) A visit from the palace this morning, a small, shy young woman with a blue scarf. She wanted to open an account in her own name. I took her details, and said nothing.",[
  ["Was it the princess?","I couldn't possibly confirm. But she signed with a flourish, and the flourish was royal."],
  ["Why does she want an account?","Independence, I'd say. A woman's first bank book is a very private thing."],
  ["Will you keep the secret?","I've kept a hundred. This will be the hundred and first."]],
 "Walk in peace. And please look after your savings."],
26:["(Agnes straightens a stack of forms.) A delegation from Diversified Petroleum, buying up half the spa, they say. A very large sum, in a small briefcase. I asked for ID, and a smile. I got the ID.",[
  ["Is it unusual?","Everything's unusual in September. But money's always money."],
  ["Who's the buyer?","The boss himself. He insisted on counting it with me. We counted it together. It's the closest I've come to a date."],
  ["Did he tip you?","He tried. I said it was against the rules. He looked genuinely surprised."]],
 "Next customer, please. Mind the briefcase."],
27:["(Agnes checks the vault clock.) Tomorrow's the big push, and every faction's emptied its pockets. I've counted more cash this week than in my whole career. My fingers ache.",[
  ["Do you know who's winning?","The one with the most notes. Not always the right one."],
  ["Is the island buying itself?","In a sense. Everyone's bidding. Nobody's asking the island."],
  ["Do you mind?","I mind the paper cuts."]],
 "Rest your hands. Tomorrow's heavy."],
28:["(Agnes sits very straight.) Today every faction's pushing, and the bank's been open since six. Notes in, notes out. Cola, oil, Party, Church, Palace. I've counted the lot, and said nothing.",[
  ["Is it exhausting?","It's invigorating, in a dreadful way. I feel like a clock, in a house full of fire."],
  ["Is there a pattern?","The money goes to whoever shouts. I've been noting it down."],
  ["Will it end well?","It always does. Eventually. Someone pays."]],
 "If you're withdrawing, hurry. The queue is out the door."],
29:["(Agnes counts for the last time.) Tomorrow's the end of it, and the vault's never been so full or so empty. I've balanced the books, and balanced my nerves. Both are close.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a teller. I'm always hopeful. It's balanced against caution."],
  ["What do you want?","A quiet island, a clean ledger, and a hot bath."],
  ["Any regrets?","That I never asked the king what he did with his krona."]],
 "Take care of your pennies. They're all you've got."],
30:["(Agnes closes the till gently.) The last day. Whoever's in charge, the coins will still be round. I've counted them twice, and they're all there.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Steady. A bank's a steadying place. A tower of paper, standing on a hill."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The king's krona, the ash, and your polite way of asking. I'll remember that."],
  ["Any last words?","Save a little. Spend a little. And never trust a man who won't count his change."]],
 "Thank you for your custom. Mind the step."],
};
// Aino, bottling line worker at the Kelp Cola factory (Kelp Cola, and the Union). Threads: the noise and the line, Kurt the foreman, wages and the Union, her flat in Kingsgrad Apartments and neighbour Saga, Phil's new formula, the ash.
DAILY['Aino']={
2:["(Aino is shouting, then realises she's outside the factory and lowers her voice.) Sorry. Eight hours on the line and I forget how to talk. You're the British reporter, aren't you? Everyone's heard.",[
  ["That's me. Is it always that loud in there?","Louder. Imagine a thousand bottles all arguing at once. You get used to it, then you can't stand quiet."],
  ["What do you do on the line?","I watch the bottles go by and take out the ones that look wrong. About nine thousand a shift. You'd think I'd learn to like them."],
  ["Do you like working for Kelp Cola?","It's a job, and a good one for Kingsgrad. I just wish they'd share a bit more. Don't print that. Or do."]],
 "I'm off to find some quiet. Good luck finding any."],
3:["(Aino is rubbing her ears.) Kelp Cola HQ is open to visitors today, so the whole place has had its floors mopped. Kurt's been shouting about the mopping since six. He wants a shine you can see your face in.",[
  ["Who's Kurt?","The foreman. A man who's never smiled on a Tuesday, and every day's a Tuesday to Kurt."],
  ["Is Phil nice?","The CEO? He's cheerful, mostly. He waves at the line from the gallery, and we wave back. It's very friendly. Nobody's raised his pay."],
  ["Are you in the Union?","Of course. Helen runs it. I pay my dues and turn up to meetings and try not to fall asleep."]],
 "Mind the floor. It's still wet. Kurt will make you mop it twice."],
4:["(Aino is sitting on a crate eating a sandwich with great concentration.) Pirkko's van does the best sandwiches on the island. Don't tell her I said so. She puts too much butter, but she's earned it.",[
  ["Is Pirkko a friend?","Everyone on the line's her friend. She knows all our orders. Mine's cheese and pickle, and a look that says carry on."],
  ["What's your lunchbreak like?","Fifteen minutes, with the whole factory arguing about football. Not my sport. I'd rather read."],
  ["What do you read?","Detective stories. Murder in a train, murder in a library. Anywhere but a bottling plant."]],
 "Finish your sandwich, or you'll have to wear it."],
5:["(Aino has a plaster on one finger.) The cap machine took a bit of me this morning. Don't worry, it was the plaster that won. The line doesn't stop for fingers.",[
  ["Is it dangerous work?","Not dangerous, just relentless. Like a very rude conveyor belt."],
  ["Does the Union do anything about it?","Helen's pushing for a safer guard on the capper. And a longer break. And a pay rise. And better soap."],
  ["What would you change?","Everything, a bit. The noise first, then the wages, then Kurt's habit of whistling off-key."]],
 "Take care of your hands. They're your living."],
6:["(Aino is waiting for the tram, stamping her feet.) Eastbrook's open, and Frostholm's opening, and everyone's going somewhere except me. I'm going home to a flat with a damp ceiling.",[
  ["Where do you live?","Kingsgrad Apartments, third floor. Saga's next door. We take turns to cook and to be miserable."],
  ["Is Saga on the line too?","No, a different job. She's lovely. She plays the accordion badly and with great conviction."],
  ["Do you ever go out?","When there's money. And when the tram's running. And when Kurt's let us go on time."]],
 "If the tram comes, tell it I'm here. It never believes me."],
7:["(Aino is stretching her back.) The north-west's open, they say, and the oil people will be on the tram. They've a smell of money about them. I can't help it. I look at their shoes.",[
  ["What do you think of Diversified Petroleum?","They're loud, rich, and a bit too interested in us. The Union says watch for their offers."],
  ["Has the oil company made offers?","A man in a suit offered Petra a job, and she said she'd think about it. She's still thinking, on the sly."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola better to work for?","It's ours. That's different. Even when it's cross with us."]],
 "Watch out for anyone offering twice your wage. It's got a catch."],
8:["(Aino is humming a hymn, off key.) Sorry. The sermon on the wireless got stuck in my head. Father Thyme was lost for words, they say, but the radio ran on anyway.",[
  ["Are you religious?","Not much. But I like the singing. It's the only thing louder than the line."],
  ["What did you make of the sermon?","I wasn't listening. I was thinking about how to tell Kurt his whistle goes right through me."],
  ["Do you ever get quiet time?","On Sundays. I lie in the flat and listen to the pipes, and think: nobody's bottling anything. It's heaven."]],
 "Sing something. It makes the day go by faster."],
9:["(Aino is holding up a bottle against the light, squinting.) New formula today, they say. Phil's got a cluster of men in white coats testing it. The line's running a different syrup. It smells like a bakery on fire.",[
  ["What does the new formula taste like?","Sweeter, with something sharp underneath. Like a kind compliment from your boss. Suspicious."],
  ["Is it any better?","Ask me after the tenth bottle. I tend to get used to anything."],
  ["Is the Party making their own cola today?","The People's Cola. I heard Helen laughing about it in the canteen. She wishes them well. And every barrel."]],
 "Don't drink too many. They go straight to your head."],
10:["(Aino is in her Sunday coat, though it's not Sunday.) The funeral's today, and the line's stopped for an hour at noon in respect. Even Kurt took his cap off. It was the quietest I've ever heard the factory.",[
  ["What was the quiet like?","Strange. I could hear the fans, and the rain on the roof, and someone sniffing in the back. It felt very big."],
  ["Did you like the king?","He opened the factory, nineteen sixty-one. I'd just started. He pressed a button and the first bottle came out. I still think of it."],
  ["Will the funeral change anything?","It'll change who's in charge. Which is what everyone's wondering about. Which side do you back?"]],
 "Take a minute for him. Just a minute. It's what he'd get."],
11:["(Aino is flexing her fingers and glaring at the floor.) The Union's fighting for the mill hands today, a fair day's pay. I want it for all of us. I'm marching in spirit, though the line won't let me out.",[
  ["Do you support the mill workers?","Of course. They're us, just with sawdust. Helen says a fair wage for one is a fair wage for all."],
  ["Is Kurt on the same side?","Kurt's on Kurt's side. But he's been quiet. I think he's thinking about it, and that's alarming."],
  ["Will you go on strike?","Not yet. We'd need a very good reason and a very good biscuit supply."]],
 "Save your strength. It'll be a long fight."],
12:["(Aino is sitting on a bench with her shoes off.) My feet have had enough. Today's a nothing sort of day. Everyone's talking about money, and about a record for Ruby. I don't know why records matter.",[
  ["What do you listen to?","Pop. Anything with a good beat and a better chorus. Saga has a radio and plays it loud, and I'm too tired to complain."],
  ["Do you know Ruby?","From the record shop? A bit. She sold me a single once and told me my taste was a cry for help."],
  ["Is the line quiet today?","Quieter. Something's off with the machine. Kurt's tearing his hair out. It's the best day all week."]],
 "Don't tell Kurt I'm enjoying it."],
13:["(Aino is in a hat with a paper flag on it, scowling.) Free Samples day. They've sent us out with trays of tiny bottles. Rather a daft job. I'm a bottler, not a salesman. But Kurt says it counts.",[
  ["How do people react?","Some take two. Some say no thanks. One man tried to pay me, which was lovely."],
  ["Are you enjoying it?","More than the line. It's quiet, and people say thank you. It's like being a person again."],
  ["Is the new formula selling?","They like it, I think. Or they like free things. It's hard to tell."]],
 "Take a sample. They don't bite. I checked."],
14:["(Aino is brushing her hair in the window of a tram, very formally.) The big day's tomorrow, the whole island gathered. I'll be there with the Union, near the front, if I can find a spot.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Noise. A lot of it. It's like the line, except people talk back."],
  ["What would you like to hear?","That workers matter. That the factory's more than a few bosses and a bottle. That Helen's right."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm always nervous. It keeps me sharp. Kurt calls it fidgeting."]],
 "See you in the crowd. I'll be the one in the green cap."],
15:["(Aino is slumped against a pile of crates.) Everyone's arguing about crowns and crosses today, and I'm sat here with a sore back and a clean conscience. The line doesn't care who's king.",[
  ["Who would you pick?","Someone who understands a shift. Prince Leopold's got style, but has he ever stood for eight hours?"],
  ["Does the cola factory take sides?","The cola takes the side that sells. I think Phil likes the royals, because they pour on the sparkle."],
  ["What about the Church?","Pirkko's on their side. She says Augustus is a saint. I say a saint would stop the line for a tea break."]],
 "A tea break's the only real faith I've got."],
16:["(Aino is listening to a radio on the factory floor, which isn't allowed.) Debate day. The Party versus the Democrats. I'm for whoever can raise the wages. Helen's been giving me facts all week, and I've forgotten most of them.",[
  ["Who do the Union favour?","Whoever listens. At the moment, it's a mix. Helen likes Erik, though she'd never tell the Party."],
  ["Is Kurt listening too?","He's hiding behind a pallet. He pretends he doesn't care. He's got his ear against the radio."],
  ["What would make you vote?","A raise, a break, and better soap."]],
 "Turn it down if you see Kurt. And turn it up if you don't."],
17:["(Aino is bracing herself against a conveyor.) Did you feel it just now? The whole line gave a little shiver. The bottles all chimed. It was lovely, if you didn't think about it.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say steam's coming off the north. I think the mountain's just clearing its throat."],
  ["Are you scared?","A bit. But a bottling plant is the safest place to stand in an earthquake. We've loud noises already."],
  ["What did Kurt say?","He said stand still and stop panicking. Then he went white and held onto a crate."]],
 "If it shakes again, duck under the packing bench."],
18:["(Aino is wearing a paper badge that says Inside Kelpy.) They want us to take a tour of the new mascot, a thing called Kelpy. It's a big green kelp costume. Someone's going to wear it. I hope it's not me.",[
  ["Who's inside the costume?","They won't say. Kurt wants it to be a mystery. Everyone suspects Kurt."],
  ["Is Kelpy a good idea?","It's a very big sock with eyes. Children love it. I'll admit, I laughed."],
  ["Do you like the new marketing?","It's marketing. It pays for the cola, which pays for me. So, yes, with an eyebrow raised."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I laughed at Kelpy. It's bad for morale."],
19:["(Aino is chewing a thumbnail.) There's something going on with the Party. They want comrades, volunteers, people. A young woman came to the factory gate with a stack of leaflets. I took one and used it as a coaster.",[
  ["Are Kelp workers joining the Party?","Some. Mostly for the free tea. The Party's good at tea."],
  ["Have you thought of joining?","I'm Union. That's enough shouting for one woman."],
  ["Did you hear about the Midnight Note?","Royal people in disguise, I heard. It'll be a laugh. I'd go if I had a wig."]],
 "Keep an eye on the gate. Something's brewing."],
20:["(Aino is stacking crates, humming along with the hum of the machines.) There's talk of a man at the Hotel Aurora. People whisper his name. I don't know it, but they all go quiet when they say it.",[
  ["Who is he?","No one's saying. They say he's from abroad. Or from the past."],
  ["Is it bad?","I don't know. But Petra's brought her sandwich in a tin and won't put it down. That's always a warning."],
  ["Are you worried?","I've a shift to finish. Worry's for after."]],
 "Keep your head down and your sandwich covered."],
21:["(Aino has ash in her hair, on her shoulders, and in her tea.) The mountain's gone up. The sky's black. The line's stopped, and for the first time in my life the factory's silent. It's awful. I miss the noise.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far as we know. Kurt's counting heads on the factory floor. He's counted twice. He's very nervous."],
  ["Where were you when it blew?","On the tram. It stopped. Everyone stood at the windows. Nobody said a word."],
  ["What happens now?","Cleaning. A lot of it. The bottles are all grey, and the syrup's got a skin."]],
 "Cover your mouth. And go home as soon as you can."],
22:["(Aino is sweeping ash into a heap, glumly.) There's trouble at a farm, someone from the cola side said. Something with the farm and the company. I don't ask. I sweep.",[
  ["Is Kelp Cola doing something with a farm?","They want sugar beet, or water, or just land. I don't know. Phil's got a map on his wall with pins in it."],
  ["Does the Union know?","Helen does. She's furious. She says the company shouldn't go round buying up people's fields."],
  ["How's the cleaning going?","Ash up to the ankles. We've swept the floor four times and it's still grey."]],
 "Don't carry anything dusty into the packing hall."],
23:["(Aino is sitting on an upturned crate, arms around her knees.) Aftershocks, all day. The line's still off, so we sit about. For once I'm tired of resting. I'd give a week's pay for the roar.",[
  ["Is the factory damaged?","A few cracks. A pipe or two. Kurt's got a ladder and a spanner and an expression."],
  ["Is the Union meeting?","Tonight, in Saltwick. Helen will tell us what to ask for. It'll be a good long list."],
  ["How are you holding up?","I'm making tea and saying nothing. Saga's lent me her accordion. It's the best medicine."]],
 "Tea, rest, and no hurry. It'll all be there tomorrow."],
24:["(Aino is wrapped in a blanket, eating from a tin.) The Church is climbing the peaks today, some retreat from the ash. I'd go, if I could. A day on a mountain with no bottles sounds like heaven.",[
  ["Do you want to go?","I'd like to be somewhere clean. Somewhere high up. Somewhere with no Kurt."],
  ["Is the line running yet?","Half of it. Enough to hear it hum. It's good, in a worrying sort of way."],
  ["What's Kurt up to?","Complaining that someone broke the machine on purpose. I'd like to meet the man who thinks that's funny."]],
 "Save me a sandwich, if you see Pirkko."],
25:["(Aino is wearing a paper crown made from a cola box.) Someone handed them out. Crowns for everyone, they said. The royals' idea. It's quite a good crown, and it keeps my hair out of my eyes.",[
  ["Do you feel royal?","Not particularly. But I have a lovely headache."],
  ["What about the Democrats and the princess?","Erik wants her at the vote, I hear. I'd go if there's a seat."],
  ["Do you care who's in charge?","I care who signs the pay slips."]],
 "Keep the crown. It's practical. Sort of."],
26:["(Aino is flipping a bottle top between her fingers.) A Prince in Every Bottle, they're calling it. Pictures of the princes under the caps. Phil thinks it'll sell like mad. I think it'll make people cry.",[
  ["Which prince is on your cap?","Leopold, five times in a row. I've a collection. He looks like a man who's lost his keys."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola winning?","Phil thinks so. I think the cola's winning and the staff are bottling."],
  ["Do you mind working on it?","If it keeps the line running and the wages coming, I'd put Kurt on a cap."]],
 "If you get Augustus, I'll trade you for Leopold."],
27:["(Aino is seated by the packing bench, leafing through a detective novel.) A revolution at the pictures tomorrow, they say, a card game at the Midnight Note. It's all happening. I'm in the middle of chapter nine.",[
  ["Are you going to the pictures?","If I'm off shift. Even a revolution needs an audience."],
  ["Who's the murderer in your book?","The butler. It's always the butler. I'd have liked a bottler for a change."],
  ["Is the Union calm?","Helen's drawing up a plan. We'll be told by dawn, and the plan will be on a napkin."]],
 "Don't spoil the ending. I'm nearly there."],
28:["(Aino has a Kelp Cola cap pinned to her lapel.) Big day, all the factions at once. Kelp Cola's going abroad, they say. Imagine that, bottles on a boat. Maybe I'll go too, in a crate.",[
  ["Is the company going abroad?","Phil's off with a case of bottles and a suit. Whether it works, I can't say."],
  ["Will you miss the noise?","Terribly. I might hum it."],
  ["What do you hope for?",["A raise, a rest, a quiet week with no ash. And that Helen gets what she asks for.",[
    ["What would Helen ask?","A fair share for the people who make the thing. That's all. It's hardly a revolution."],
    ["Would the company agree?","If the right people were watching. They always behave better when someone's looking."]]]]],
 "Keep the line moving. In every sense."],
29:["(Aino is in her best blouse, pressed flat.) There's some kind of primary today, and I don't understand it. Phil's in a state, and Kurt's in a suit. It's like a wedding where no one's sure who's marrying.",[
  ["Who do you want to win?","Someone who'll remember who made the cola."],
  ["Will you vote?","If they let me. If there's a box and a pencil."],
  ["Are you ready for tomorrow?","I've pressed my blouse and fed the cat. I'm as ready as I'll be."]],
 "Get an early night. Tomorrow will be long."],
30:["(Aino is standing in the factory yard in the cold, a bottle in her hand, not drinking it.) Last day, then. The line's running, quiet and steady. I think I'm going to cry, but it might be the ash.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Strange. Like a bottle just before the cap goes on."],
  ["What will you remember?","The noise. The ash. The friends I made at lunch. And Kurt, on his best day, smiling."],
  ["What's next?","Whatever's next. A shift, a sandwich, a tram. That's enough for me."]],
 "Thanks for asking. Not many do. Cheers."],
};
// Anna, leader of the Communist Party, at the Party HQ in Saltwick (Communist Party). Threads: the Great Debate with Erik (16
// September) and her index cards, Yuri at the Hotel Aurora and his hurry, her mother the herring gutter (map, knife, ribbon),
// the Party's month (members, the film, the flag) and Helen and Bob.
DAILY['Anna']={
1:["(Anna is pinning a map of the island to the wall, with Saltwick shaded red.) Only Saltwick's dug out so far, comrade, so only Saltwick matters today. The rest of the island is still under the snow.",[
  ["Why is Saltwick red?","Because it's ours: fishermen, gutters, dockers. By the end of the month I'd like a great many more red pins."],
  ["What will the Party do, now the king's dead?",["Talk to people. Organise. Ask them a question nobody's asked them in three hundred years: who should own this island?",[
    ["And the answer?","The people who work it. And Moscow to help us keep it, because nobody else ever offered."],
    ["Three hundred years is a long time.","So is a winter here. We survive both."]]]],
  ["Nice map. Did you draw it?","My mother did, for the herring co-operative, in 1951. She marked the best fishing grounds in pencil. I keep meaning to rub them out, and never do."]],
 "Shut the door behind you, comrade. The heating's a luxury."],
2:["The road to Kingsgrad's clear. Half of Saltwick has gone to stare at the palace gates, as if the old king might still wave.",[
  ["Will you go to Kingsgrad?","When I have business there. The palace isn't business. It's a museum with people still living in it."],
  ["What do you make of Prince Leopold?","A nice young man who's never gutted a fish. That's the whole problem, in one sentence."],
  ["Maybe they just want to see the shops.","(She laughs.) Fair. Kingsgrad has shoes. Saltwick has boots. There's a difference, and you can hear it on the stairs."]],
 "Mind the slush on the main street. It's worse than the snow was."],
3:["Phil's cola lorries are back on the road. The first thing through when the snow clears: not bread, not medicine. Cola.",[
  ["People like cola.","People like what they're sold. Show me the islander who asked for kelp in a bottle."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola an enemy of the Party?","Phil pays the girls on his bottling line less than the bottle's worth. Helen has the figures. Ask her, and she'll talk till supper."],
  ["Have you ever tried it?","Once, in 1974. I'm still thinking about it, and not kindly."]],
 "Tell Phil the workers send their regards. He'll know what it means."],
4:["Eastbrook's dug out, and Erik telephoned within the hour. The first thing that man does with a clear road is propose a public debate.",[
  ["Will you accept?","Of course. Never refuse a man the chance to lose in front of his friends. We've said the sixteenth."],
  ["What's Erik like?",["Clever. Earnest. Thinks a vote every four years is freedom.",[
    ["Isn't it?","A vote to choose which landlord you pay? Freedom is owning the house."],
    ["Will you win?","I've argued with fishwives since I was nine. Erik has argued with his coffee."]]]],
  ["A debate, on an island this small?","Small places have the longest memories. Whoever wins, they'll still be talking about it at funerals in forty years."]],
 "The sixteenth of September. Bring your notebook."],
5:["The monks have dug out the road down from the mountain. Father Thyme will be preaching about the king by Sunday, mark my words.",[
  ["You're not religious, then.","My grandmother was. She lit candles for the herring. The herring came, or didn't, regardless."],
  ["Does the Church back anyone?","Augustus, the pious brother. A king who prays. As if what this island's short of is prayer."],
  ["Monks have to eat too.","They do, and they grow their own, which is more than Phil does. I'll give them that much."]],
 "Bread first, comrade. Prayers after, if there's time."],
6:["(Anna has the window open in spite of the cold, and is airing a jacket.) Someone sat beside me at Ray's last night and smoked his way through a box of cigars. Syndicate. I smell like a casino.",[
  ["The Syndicate drinks in Saltwick?","They collect in Saltwick. A woman called Lucia does the rounds of the boats with a little book. The men pay her, or their nets go missing."],
  ["Can't the police stop them?","The police are in Kingsgrad, guarding shoe shops. In Saltwick we stop things ourselves, or they don't get stopped."],
  ["Cigars aren't a crime.","Not in Kingsgrad. In Saltwick they're a declaration."]],
 "Leave the window open on your way out. I want the smoke gone before the meeting."],
7:["Frostholm's open, and our friend at the Hotel Aurora has telephoned already. Three times.",[
  ["Which friend?",["Yuri. A timber man from Leningrad. He buys a great deal of vodka and takes a great interest in our Party.",[
    ["Is he a member?","He's a friend of the Party. That's a different thing, and he'd like you to remember it."],
    ["Timber from Leningrad?","That's what he says. I've never once seen him look at a tree."]]]],
  ["What did he want?","To know how many members we have. I told him more than last year. He wanted a number. Businessmen always want a number."],
  ["Frostholm's all oil money, isn't it?","Glass, neon and Diversified Petroleum. The only honest thing in Frostholm is the cold."]],
 "If you go to Frostholm, don't let anyone buy you a drink. It always costs more than a drink."],
8:["There's talk off the boats: something's gone wrong out at the oil rig. The men say the water's dark past the point.",[
  ["An oil spill?","Nobody's saying the word. Dario's men are saying 'routine'. Routine is what the rich say when they've broken something."],
  ["Are you going up to the monastery for the sermon?","I don't climb mountains to be told to be patient. I'll read about it in the Gazette tomorrow, and argue with it then."],
  ["Do you rest on Sundays?","The Party rests when the fish do. (She nods at a pile of leaflets.) Sunday is for folding."]],
 "If you hear any more about the rig, tell Sam. He's worried, and Sam never worries."],
9:["Helen has an idea in her head, and when Helen has an idea, the rest of us carry it. A cola. Our own.",[
  ["A Communist cola?","A people's cola. Made by workers, sold at a fair price, with nobody in a villa getting fat off it."],
  ["Will it work?","It'll be drunk. Whether it's any good is between Helen and God, and Helen doesn't believe in Him."],
  ["What should it taste like?","Not kelp. That's my only instruction."]],
 "Go and see Helen if you want to know more. I only sign things."],
10:["(Anna is brushing down a black coat that has plainly not been worn in years.) The funeral. The Party will be represented. Somebody has to stand there and not bow.",[
  ["You're going to the king's funeral?","Respect for the dead costs nothing. Respect for the crown is another bill entirely, and I'm not paying it."],
  ["Is that coat older than me?","It was my mother's funeral coat. It's done three funerals and a wedding. It didn't care for the wedding."],
  ["Will Erik be there?","In his best tie, bowing to the coffin. And he'll talk about his vote over the sandwiches."]],
 "Kingsgrad, in black. What a month this is."],
11:["Helen's gone all Eastbrook on me. The mill hands, Dagny's saw, the pay rise. She's had the figures on my desk since six.",[
  ["The mill hands vote Democrat, don't they?","They vote for whoever keeps their fingers on their hands. A union card doesn't ask your party."],
  ["How was the funeral?","Long and cold. A great many people in very good coats, all watching each other. I watched them back."],
  ["Dagny sounds tough.","Dagny owns a mill, a house and half of Eastbrook. It's easy to be tough when you own the floor you're standing on."]],
 "If you're going to Eastbrook, take Helen's side. It's the right one."],
12:["(Anna is laying index cards out on her desk, like a game of patience.) Debate cards. One argument to a card. Erik will have a speech. I'll have a hand.",[
  ["Show me one.","'Who owns the boat you fish from?' Every man in Saltwick knows the answer, and it isn't him."],
  ["Do you rehearse?","In front of the mirror, and then in front of Sam. The mirror argues back more."],
  ["You play patience?","It's the only game where nobody cheats but you."]],
 "Four days. Then Erik and I see who's been reading what."],
13:["Phil's people are handing out free Kelp Cola on every corner in Kingsgrad. Free. Nothing on earth costs more.",[
  ["Free is free.","Free today. Habit tomorrow. A price rise the day after. It's the oldest trick there is; the Church thought of it first."],
  ["Did you take one?","Sam did. He poured it into the harbour and said the fish would want a word."],
  ["How's the People's Cola?","Ask Helen. I've learnt not to ask her about it before lunch."]],
 "Don't drink anything you didn't pay for. That's Party policy."],
14:["The big day. Everyone on the island in one place, and all of them pretending they're not counting heads.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches. Handshakes. Promises nobody will keep. I'll be keeping a list."],
  ["Nervous?","I was gutting herring on the quay in February at ten years old. Nothing has made me nervous since."],
  ["Who are you counting?","Friends. Enemies. And the ones who haven't decided yet. That last pile is the only one that matters."]],
 "Stand where you can see the whole room. That's where I'll be."],
15:["Now Leopold and Augustus are squabbling over which comes first, the crown or the cross. Brothers, fighting over an empty chair.",[
  ["Whose side are you on?","Neither. When two landlords fight, the tenant ought to be looking for the door."],
  ["The debate's tomorrow.",["Tomorrow. I've slept four hours since Friday, and I feel wonderful.",[
    ["What's your opening line?","I'm not telling a reporter my opening line. You'll hear it with everyone else."],
    ["Is Erik worried?","Erik's bought a new tie. That's how you can tell."]]]],
  ["You must be enjoying this.","I enjoy watching a monarchy argue with itself. It saves me a great deal of work."]],
 "Get a good seat tomorrow. Front row, where the spit lands."],
16:["(Anna is pacing with a cup of tea she isn't drinking.) Today. Erik and me, in front of everyone. I've done it a hundred times in my head, and I win every time.",[
  ["Good luck.","Luck is for card players. I've got arguments. (She smiles.) But thank you, comrade."],
  ["What's the hardest thing he could ask?","'Why Moscow?' Every Democrat asks it. Because nobody else ever lifted a finger for a fisherman. Short, you see."],
  ["Your tea's going cold.","It went cold an hour ago. I'm holding it to warm my hands. It's an old habit from the quay."]],
 "Afterwards, comrade. We'll talk afterwards."],
17:["Did you feel that? The floor did a little shuffle. The filing cabinet's walked an inch towards the door.",[
  ["An earthquake?","A tremor. The old men say the mountain in the north is stretching. It does it every hundred years or so, apparently."],
  ["How do you feel about yesterday?","Tired. I said what I came to say. People make up their own minds; that's the one part I can't do for them."],
  ["Maybe the cabinet wants to leave the Party.","(She shoves it back.) Nobody leaves this Party. They just get very tired and sit down."]],
 "If the floor goes again, stand in a doorway. That's the whole of my advice."],
18:["Our friend from the Hotel Aurora came over to Saltwick. He brought vodka, a box of chocolates and a great many opinions.",[
  ["Opinions on what?","On how fast the Party ought to go. Yuri likes speed. I like a Party that's still standing next year."],
  ["Did you drink his vodka?","One glass. You don't refuse a guest. You also don't drink the second; that's when the opinions get louder."],
  ["Chocolates? He's courting you.","(She snorts.) He's courting the Party. I'm only the one who holds the door."]],
 "The chocolates are on Helen's desk if you want one. They're Swiss. Of course they are."],
19:["New members, comrade. That's today's work. Saltwick, the docks, anyone with two hands and a grievance.",[
  ["Who do you want?","Gunnar, to start with. Tom, if he'd stop being nice to everyone. Bob, if Helen can't manage it, and she can't."],
  ["What do members get?","A card, a vote at meetings, and soup on Thursdays. The soup is better than it sounds."],
  ["What if nobody joins?","Then I knock on the same doors next week. Doors get tired before I do."]],
 "If you meet anyone sensible, send them to me."],
20:["Erik's people have been asking questions about our friend at the Hotel Aurora. Very nosy, for democrats.",[
  ["Who is Yuri, really?","A businessman who likes the Party. If Erik wants to know more, he can buy his own vodka."],
  ["Why are they curious?","Because it's easier to look for a Russian under the bed than to mend the guard on Dagny's saw."],
  ["Doesn't it worry you?","What worries me is the ground shaking and chimneys cracking. Erik's people are a lesser grade of worry."]],
 "Tell Erik to look under his own bed first."],
21:["(Anna's window is grey with ash. She has a damp cloth tied over her mouth.) The mountain's gone up, comrade. Ash everywhere. Saltwick's under a grey snow.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Everyone's counted. Helen did it street by street. Nobody hurt in Saltwick, thank... thank Helen."],
  ["What do people need?","Water that isn't grey, cloths for their faces, and someone to tell them what's happening. The palace hasn't said a word."],
  ["Is this the end of the world?","It's the end of the washing that was on the line. The world's tougher than washing."]],
 "Keep your collar up. The ash gets in everywhere."],
22:["We've shovel brigades on every street. Ash doesn't care whose roof it's sitting on, so neither do we.",[
  ["The Party's doing the clean-up?","Somebody has to. The palace sent a letter of sympathy. You can't sweep a street with a letter."],
  ["How's the harbour?","Grey water, dead fish on the tide line. Sam went out to look and came back silent. More silent."],
  ["Hand me a shovel.","(She does.) There. Now you're in the brigade. Not the Party: I don't sign people up in a crisis. Usually."]],
 "Leave the shovel by the door when you're done. They're counted."],
23:["Dario's rig is pumping away while we're still shovelling ash off our roofs. That's this island's priorities, in a sentence.",[
  ["Should the rig stop?","It should never have started. It's drilling under our fishing grounds. Ask any man on the harbour."],
  ["What will the Party do about it?","(She looks at you steadily.) What the Party always does. Make a great deal of noise, and put it in writing."],
  ["The aftershocks keep coming.","Every hour or so. I've stopped putting the teapot on the shelf."]],
 "Good day, comrade. Mind how you go on the ash."],
24:["(Anna is sharpening an old gutting knife on a whetstone.) My mother's. When things are bad, I sharpen it. It helps me think.",[
  ["Things are bad?","Ash, tremors, and every faction on the island smelling blood. Things are interesting, which is worse."],
  ["Tell me about your mother.",["She gutted herring for forty years and led the first strike on the quay, in 1938. Over a ha'penny a barrel.",[
    ["Did she win?","She got the ha'penny. And the sack. She always said it was a bargain."],
    ["So it runs in the family.","The stubbornness does. The politics I had to read for."]]]],
  ["Is it sharp enough yet?","(She slices a sheet of paper clean in two.) It'll do. It always does."]],
 "Off you go, comrade. Some of us have knives to finish."],
25:["Now the Democrats are courting Princess Daphne. A princess for democracy. I've heard everything now.",[
  ["Is that so strange?","A royal who wants a vote? It's like a fox standing for the henhouse committee."],
  ["Would you ever court a royal?","I'd court the palace kitchen. They do the work; the princess does the waving."],
  ["Three days till the twenty-eighth.","Everyone's saving their best for it. So are we. I won't tell you what. Read the papers."]],
 "Give Daphne my regards. She won't know who I am."],
26:["Our friend from Frostholm wants a date. A public vote, he says, to join the Soviet Union. 'Before the snow comes back, Anna.'",[
  ["Will you call one?","When Saltwick's ready, not when Frostholm's in a hurry. I don't take orders from hotel rooms."],
  ["Why is he in such a hurry?","Businessmen always are. I suspect the people he works for telephone him a great deal."],
  ["Do you trust him?","I trust the fishermen. Everyone else I watch. You too, comrade. (She smiles.) Nothing personal."]],
 "Saltwick sets its own pace. Remember that."],
27:["Otto shows cowboys and spacemen in Kingsgrad, and the island thinks that's what life looks like. We've something better for him.",[
  ["What's better?","A film about real people. Tractors. Rivers. Workers singing. A little long, I'll grant you, but honest."],
  ["Otto won't show it.","Otto likes a full house. Give a man a full house and he'll show anything."],
  ["You mean STORMWING?","Spaceships, lasers, a hero with teeth. The young ones queue round the block. (She sighs.) I'd go myself, if anyone asked me."]],
 "One reel of truth against a hundred of nonsense. Fair odds."],
28:["(Anna hasn't slept. There's a red ribbon pinned to her coat.) Today everyone on this island plays their best card. Ours is a good one.",[
  ["What's your card?","You'll see it when you look up. That's all I'll say to a reporter."],
  ["You haven't slept.","I'll sleep in October. October's quiet. Nobody ever had a revolution in October. (She pauses.) Well. Once."],
  ["What does the ribbon mean?","My mother wore it on the quay in 1938. I wear it on the days that matter. This is the third time."]],
 "Look up when you're in Kingsgrad, comrade."],
29:["Erik came round. He brought biscuits, I made tea, and we were civil for nearly an hour. That's a record.",[
  ["What did you talk about?","Tomorrow. Whatever happens, he and I still have to live on the same island. Neither of us said it out loud, but we both meant it."],
  ["So you're friends now?","Rivals who'd pull each other out of the harbour. On Sarivalto, that's the next best thing."],
  ["Biscuits? You've gone soft.","They were Eastbrook biscuits. Mostly sawdust. I ate four, for solidarity."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. Wear something warm."],
30:["(Anna is sitting very still at her desk, the map with its red pins behind her.) The last day. Whatever's decided, I've done what I could.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like the morning of a long voyage, when the bags are packed and there's nothing left but to wait for the boat."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Sit in this chair, like every other day. Whoever runs the island, the fishermen will still need someone to shout for them."],
  ["There's a lot of red on that map now.","(She looks at it.) My mother's pencil marks are still under there, you know. I never did rub them out."]],
 "Thank you for listening, comrade. Whatever you end up writing."],
};
// Arto, out of work in Frostholm since the cannery closed, hanging about the phone box on the boulevard by day and The Midnight Note by
// evening (The Unemployed). Threads: his bitterness about the oil company, waiting by the phone for work, Raili his friend, his habit of
// listening to anyone who promises a job, the library.
DAILY['Arto']={
7:["(A thin man in a purple cap leans on the phone box, one eye on the receiver.) Don't use it. I'm expecting a call. Been expecting it since June. You're the reporter, aren't you? Everyone's got an opinion about you.",[
  ["What opinion's that?","That you've come to write about our troubles. I say, good. We've plenty. Pull up a lamp-post."],
  ["Who's going to ring you?","Anyone. A foreman, a boat-owner, a man with a barrow. I'm not fussy. I used to be, at the cannery. Then it shut."],
  ["Is Frostholm any better since the road opened?","It's busier. It's the same people with the same troubles, only more of them. And the oil company's got new lorries."]],
 "If the phone rings, shout. Don't answer. It's mine."],
8:["(Arto's reading a library book with a cracked spine.) It's about whales. I come in every day, to the library, and read about whales. I've now read about whales for longer than anyone's ever had to.",[
  ["Why whales?","They don't need jobs. They just go. I find it very restful."],
  ["Is the library good?","It's warm and it's free. A man needs a place that's both. Mary's the librarian. She leaves the heater on, and pretends she's forgotten."],
  ["What did you do at the cannery?","Gutted herring. Eleven years. I was the fastest on the line. I've a certificate, but it's for fish."]],
 "I'll be back at the phone. The whales can wait."],
9:["(Arto looks at a leaflet, and then at you, hopefully.) The Reds are giving out leaflets about a People's Cola. They say it'd need workers. A lot of them. I asked, 'Where?' The girl said, 'Everywhere.'",[
  ["Would you work for the Party?","I'd work for a lump of coal if it paid. I don't mind who signs the cheque, as long as the cheque's real."],
  ["Do you believe them?","I believe the girl. I'm not sure I believe the leaflet. But I've kept it, in my pocket, next to the bus timetable."],
  ["Who else has promised work?","Everyone. Kelp Cola says it's hiring. The oil men say it's coming. I say I'll believe it when I've a name tag."]],
 "Don't laugh. A man's allowed to hope, even on an empty stomach."],
10:["(Arto's wearing a jacket that's a size too big, with a black band on the arm.) The funeral's today. I've listened to it on the radio, outside the café. It was the only place with the radio on.",[
  ["Did you like the king?","He did what he could. He used to ride through Frostholm in a car with the windows down and wave. It's more than the oil company does."],
  ["Is Raili with you?","She's at the radio shop, listening to the coverage. She says it's the only way to hear anything. I say it's a lot of men talking in black."],
  ["How do you feel about it?","A bit lost. The old king was a kind of weather. Now there's no weather. Just opinions."]],
 "Pay your respects. And a coin, if you've got one. I'll take it for the bus."],
11:["(Arto reads a headline pasted to a wall.) 'Eastbrook Mill Hands Demand Fair Pay.' I'd happily demand it, if I'd a job to demand it from. I'd take a demand over nothing.",[
  ["Do you support the strikers?","With my whole heart. And with my empty pocket. It's not much, but it's all I've got."],
  ["Would you work at the mill?","I'd work at anything. But Dagny's got thirty-one men and no vacancy. And I'm no sawyer. I'm a gutter. A fish man."],
  ["What do you think of the union?","They've got a good voice. They're what the island needs. But they can't give a man a wage that doesn't exist."]],
 "Say a good word for the ones without a mill. We exist too."],
12:["(Arto's studying a small card.) A man at the café gave me this. 'Diversified Petroleum: Work for the Future.' There's no address, only a number. I've rung it twelve times. It rings out.",[
  ["Are you going to keep trying?","I'll try till I'm sixty. Or until the phone box is torn down. It's a kind of fishing."],
  ["Who gave it to you?","A man in a fur collar. He said, 'Good things come to those who wait.' I said, 'I've been waiting.' He said, 'Wait more.'"],
  ["Do you trust the oil company?","I've no reason to. They bought the cannery's land. They shut the cannery. They're the only ones hiring, and they're the reason I need one."]],
 "A job's a job. Even if it's from the devil in a fur collar."],
13:["(Arto's holding a bottle of cola and sipping it as if it might vanish.) Free samples. Kelp Cola's giving it away on the boulevard. Raili got two. I got one. It's very sweet. It's the first thing I've had all day.",[
  ["Is it good?","It's wonderful. It's also bribery. But I like being bribed with fizz. It's cheap."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola hiring?","They've a stand. I asked a boy in a cap. He said, 'We'll let you know.' It's what every employer says. It's the sound of a door closing, politely."],
  ["Is it a good sign?","It's a sign. I'll take any sign. I'll take a sign saying 'Beware of the dog', as long as there's a dog and a job attached."]],
 "Don't spill it. It's the best meal I'll have until Thursday."],
14:["(Arto puts on a tie, which has a small stain.) The big day. They say the whole island's going. I've got a clean shirt, a free afternoon and nothing to lose. It's the best I've felt in months.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Hope. A lot of it, thrown about like confetti. I'd like to catch a handful."],
  ["Is Raili going?","She's gone ahead, to bag a good spot. She says she'll hold a space for me. She's the kindest person I know."],
  ["Will you speak to anyone?","Anyone who'll listen. Anyone who's got a job. A man in a hat. A woman in a coat. It's very optimistic of me."]],
 "Wish me a handshake. It's all it takes."],
15:["(Arto leans on the wall with a faint, glowing look.) I spoke to six people yesterday. Four promised me something. One, a woman from the Party, gave me a badge. I've put it on. It makes me feel official.",[
  ["Which promised what?","A man from the cola people said 'soon'. A man from the monks said 'prayer'. And a lady said 'we'll see'. I'm collecting them."],
  ["Was it a good day?","It was the best in months. People looked at me. They saw me. A man with a cap and a nothing, and they listened."],
  ["Will anything come of it?","Probably not. But I've a pocketful of 'soon', and that's something."]],
 "I'll keep the badge. It's the only decoration I've got."],
16:["(Arto's reading a poster, mouth moving.) 'The Great Debate.' I don't know who's winning, but it's the one thing in Frostholm that doesn't cost anything. I'm going to stand at the back and hear both sides.",[
  ["Which side do you lean to?","The side that's got a job to offer. I'm a very easy convert."],
  ["Do you believe either?","I believe whoever's speaking at the time. It's a flaw, but it's honest. I've never had anything to lose."],
  ["Is Raili going?","She's gone to listen on the radio. She says you hear more with your eyes shut."]],
 "I'll take notes. In my head. I've no pencil."],
17:["(Arto grips the phone box as it shudders.) What was that? The whole booth trembled. I thought, 'It's ringing at last.' It wasn't. It was the earth.",[
  ["It was a tremor.","I'd say so. The old men by the library say it's the mountain. I say it's the island shifting in its sleep. It's been cold, after all."],
  ["Does it worry you?","I've nothing to lose, which is an odd sort of safety. But I'd like to see a job before I see a volcano."],
  ["What would you do?","Run to the beach and watch. It'd be the best show Frostholm's had in years."]],
 "If it rings, take the call. If it shakes, take cover. If both, take the call under a table."],
18:["(Arto has a sheet of newspaper folded under his arm.) There's a notice in the Gazette. Kelp Cola's looking for people for a new campaign. 'Inside Kelpy.' Something to do with a giant cup. I've applied.",[
  ["Applied for what?","I don't know. 'Assistant'. It might be carrying the cup. It might be being the cup. I'd be a very good cup."],
  ["Do you want to be a mascot?","I'd be a lamp-post if it paid. A cup's a step up."],
  ["Is Jaana the cup?","I'm told so, by someone who shouldn't say. I won't repeat it. I'm a gentleman, in a threadbare way."]],
 "If they ask for a reference, I'll give yours. You've an honest face."],
19:["(Arto wipes his forehead.) The floor's been jumpy again. I was at the library and the books fell off the shelf. Mary was furious. 'They're alphabetical,' she said, 'and now they're all over the floor.'",[
  ["Did you help pick them up?","Of course. It was my first job in two years. I did it properly. I alphabetised the whales."],
  ["Did Mary pay you?","With a cup of tea and a stale biscuit. It was a good wage."],
  ["Is the volcano close?","The steam's up above the north road. Everyone's looking at it. It's like a very polite warning."]],
 "I'll see you when the world's still. If it ever is."],
20:["(Arto's got a handkerchief over his nose.) The whole of Frostholm smells of sulphur. The oil company's said it's nothing to worry about. When an oil company says 'nothing', you should worry.",[
  ["What do you think's going to happen?","The mountain's going to go. And the oil company will own the clean-up. And they'll hire me to hold a shovel for eleven marks a day. It's the cycle."],
  ["Is that a good thing?","It's a thing. I'm nearly at the point where I'll take it."],
  ["Is Raili afraid?","She says she's too tired. But I've seen her sleeping with her boots on."]],
 "If it goes, I'll be on the boulevard. Looking for work in the ruins."],
21:["(Arto's face is smudged grey, and his purple cap's turned the colour of dust.) It went. The whole sky. I was in the doorway of the Midnight Note and Leo was playing something slow, and the world went dark.",[
  ["Was it frightening?","Beautiful. And terrifying. Like a very large shadow falling over everyone. Then Rita sang, and we all stood still."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody. It's a miracle, Sid says, or at least a lucky accident. He gave everyone a free drink, which is a miracle in itself."],
  ["How are you?","Alive. Grey. Slightly drunk. Very glad. It's the most excited I've been since the cannery shut."]],
 "Don't breathe too deep. It's like swallowing a pencil."],
22:["(Arto has a shovel over his shoulder, a first.) I got a job. A real one. Four hours, clearing ash from the boulevard. The council's hiring anyone who can lift. I've never been prouder of a shovel.",[
  ["Congratulations. Who's paying?","The town, with money from somewhere. I don't ask. I've decided to be grateful, and I'll ask questions later."],
  ["How's the work?","Heavy. Hot. Grey. But it's honest. I've had eight people say 'thank you' to me today, and I've not said a word."],
  ["Is Raili working?","She's handing out tea, at the corner. She says it's a very good arrangement: she gets to talk, I get to dig."]],
 "Come and see me. I'm the one with the shovel. And the smile."],
23:["(Arto leans on the shovel.) The tremors haven't stopped. Every time, I jump and the shovel clangs. A man at the next kerb thought I was doing a dance.",[
  ["Is the job lasting?","Four more days, they say. Then who knows. But it's four more than I'd have had."],
  ["Are you saving up?","Every mark. I've a little tin. Ilse the log-loader told me about hers. She's saving for a truck. I'm saving for a pair of boots."],
  ["Is the oil company helping?","They sent a lorry of water. It's a very good lorry. It's got a flag on it. They've never been so charitable."]],
 "If you find me at the phone box, ring me. It's a long shot, but I'm in the mood."],
24:["(Arto sits on a bench, eating a sandwich very slowly.) The clean-up's going well. It's the island's first sign of togetherness. I've seen a priest and a Red carry the same bucket.",[
  ["Is it changing the mood?","Slightly. It's like a long tired breath. But I wouldn't trust it. In a week, they'll be back to knocking each other's hats off."],
  ["What would you like to happen?","For people to remember who dug. And to give us a proper job, with a chair."],
  ["Is that too much to ask?","It's too much to expect. But it's not too much to ask. There's a difference. I learned it at the library."]],
 "I'll take the bench. It's the nicest thing I own."],
25:["(Arto's looking at a poster.) 'A Princess for Democracy.' They've put them on the phone box. I'm disappointed. It was my box. I've been guarding it for months, and now it's got a princess.",[
  ["Do you like the princess?","I don't know her. But she looks kind. She's got a nice face for a poster. I'd work for her, I think."],
  ["Is it true she'd bring work?","The Democrats say there'd be a vote, and then work. I'm not sure what order it comes in. But I'd like both."],
  ["Is the phone box still yours?","I've papered over the princess with the whale poster. It's a good compromise."]],
 "Let me know if you hear about a job. Any job. Even the whale one."],
26:["(Arto's got a cola bottle, half full.) They've put up posters saying 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' I've checked six. I've found none. I'm beginning to think it's metaphorical.",[
  ["Maybe it is.","I'd like it to be a real prince. He could hire me. I'd be a very good footman. I've the legs for it."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","As much as ever. Which is to say, a little. A thimble of hope's enough to keep a man warm."],
  ["What does Raili think?","She says the whole thing's a circus. But she'd go. She says every circus needs a clown, and we're free."]],
 "If the prince arrives, tell him I'm available. And cheap."],
27:["(Arto's examining his shoes.) Three days. Everyone says it'll be settled. I've heard that in June and July and August. I'm a believer in the long game, mostly because I can't afford the short one.",[
  ["What do you think will happen?","Something loud. Followed by something quiet. Then someone will ask for my vote, and I'll give it for a sandwich."],
  ["Is that cynical?","It's realistic. A man who's been unemployed for two years doesn't have a very high price. But I've a conscience. It's just on layaway."],
  ["Is there anyone you'd vote for?","The one who looks me in the eye. There's one or two. They're rare."]],
 "I'll be at the box. Same as ever. The only fixed point on the island."],
28:["(Arto stands very straight, in a freshly brushed jacket.) Today's the biggest day, and I've nowhere to go. But everyone's out, and I'm out. We're all out. It's like a very large picnic with a grudge.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A job. A roof that doesn't leak. A drink that doesn't have an advertisement on it. It's not much."],
  ["Is Raili with you?","She's at the radio shop, listening. She'll tell me what happened. I prefer it that way. She tells it better."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Of the future? A bit. Of the present? It's the only thing I'm used to."]],
 "I'll cheer for whoever's loudest. It's a very good system."],
29:["(Arto folds his cap in his hands.) One more day. I've been over it in my head. If any of them gave me work, I'd take it. Red, white, blue, or oil. I'm not proud. I'm hungry.",[
  ["That's honest.","It's all I've got. Honesty's cheap, but it's the only currency I've a surplus of."],
  ["What do you want from tomorrow?","A sign. A wage. A bit of dignity. In any order."],
  ["Do you think it'll come?","It'll come to someone. It may as well be me."]],
 "Wish me luck. I'll wish you the same. You've come a long way for a story."],
30:["(Arto's standing by the phone box, hands in his pockets.) It's the last day. And the phone hasn't rung. It's funny, I almost don't mind. I've had the shovel, the whales and the sky turning grey. It's been a month.",[
  ["Any last words, Arto?","Don't forget the ones who wait by the phone. We're patient. We're also, if you ask nicely, very good at what we do."],
  ["What will you do now?","Wait. And if it rings, answer. And if it doesn't, go to the library and read about whales."],
  ["Was it worth waiting?","Today, yes. I met you. And a very nice man with a spade."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. If you see a job, put in a word. Any word. I'll take 'yes'."],
};
// Arvid, chef at the Cedar Grill, Eastbrook (Royal Family). Threads: his training in the palace kitchens and Marta, the king's portrait above the stove, cooking for a Democrat town, his sourdough, his nerves in a crisis.
DAILY['Arvid']={
4:["(Arvid stirs a pot without looking at it.) The reporter. Sit down, you look hungry. Eastbrook's just come out of the snow, and I've nothing but soup, so it's soup.",[
  ["What's in it?","Barley, root vegetables, and a bone. The secret is time, and a good bone."],
  ["Is that the old king above the stove?","It is. I trained in his kitchens. He ate my pastry once and said it was almost as good as Marta's. I've never been prouder."],
  ["Eastbrook's a Democrat town. Does it bother you?","They eat my soup. That's all the politics I need."]],
 "Don't touch the pot. It's a hot one."],
5:["(Arvid kneads dough with large hands.) Sourdough, five days old. It's a living thing, like a pet that demands flour. I talk to it. Don't tell Linnea.",[
  ["Does it answer?","It bubbles. I take that as agreement."],
  ["Did you learn this at the palace?","From Marta. She's the finest cook on the island, and the sharpest tongue. I'm still trying to impress her."],
  ["Is she that frightening?","Only if you leave the oven on. She once threw a ladle at a duke."]],
 "Take a roll. It's still warm."],
6:["(Arvid chops onions at remarkable speed.) Linnea sends over a crate of lemons every Friday. I send back a pie. It's an arrangement, and we've never discussed it.",[
  ["You two are close.","Neighbours in the lofts, and in the cause. We're the last two royalists in Eastbrook, and we're outnumbered by about four hundred."],
  ["Does that make you nervous?","Not at all. The Democrats like my food. I'm a royalist, not a fool."],
  ["Do you ever talk politics?","With Linnea, in whispers. With the Democrats, never. With the soup, constantly."]],
 "Eat first, argue later. That's my rule."],
7:["(Arvid has flour up to his elbows.) The road's open to Frostholm now, and a man came asking about a banquet. Diversified Petroleum, wanting forty plates. I said I'd think about it.",[
  ["Will you take the job?","Their money's good, and their manners aren't. I'd cook for the devil if he paid in advance."],
  ["What did they want?","Steak. Imported wine. A very big fuss. I told them I do soup and stews."],
  ["Did that put them off?","They asked if I did it with caviar. I showed them the door."]],
 "I cook for people, not for oil. There's a difference."],
8:["(Arvid dips a spoon into a pot, tastes, nods.) Sunday. A quiet lunch, and a few old men playing cards. I do a roast, and it's the same every week, and nobody's complained.",[
  ["Is that why you like Sundays?","Because nothing happens. A cook needs a day when nothing happens."],
  ["Do you miss the palace kitchens?","The size, yes. The shouting, no. Though I miss Marta, in her way."],
  ["What's the best thing you ever cooked?","A wedding cake for the old king's sister. Five tiers, and a marzipan swan. I was very young."]],
 "Sit near the stove. It's the warmest seat."],
9:["(Arvid frowns at a bottle of cola.) The cola men left a crate with me. Free, they said. I've tasted it, and it'd make a good gravy, with a bit of pepper. I won't tell them.",[
  ["Is the cola good?","It's sweet. Too sweet. But a splash in a stew works wonders."],
  ["Will you stock it?","Only if they pay me to. I cook for taste, not for logos."],
  ["Is there a new formula?","They say so. I'd say, stick to the old, and spend the money on better chefs."]],
 "Don't tell Linnea about the gravy. She'd never let me forget it."],
10:["(Arvid has a black ribbon pinned to the portrait.) Today's the funeral. The grill's closed for the morning, and I've baked a loaf for the family. It won't reach them, but I baked it anyway.",[
  ["Will you go to the palace?","I'd love to. But I'm a chef, not a guest. I'll listen on the radio, with a pot of tea."],
  ["Did you know the king well?","He used to wander into the kitchens, late at night, for a slice of cold pie. I'd turn a blind eye."],
  ["What would he say about all this?","He'd ask for the pie. And thank you for it."]],
 "A good man. A good appetite."],
11:["(Arvid scrapes the grill.) The funeral's over, and the whole street is hungry. The mourners come in grey, and leave a good deal happier after a bowl of soup.",[
  ["Does food help?","It's what we've got. A warm bowl, and a seat, and someone to say it'll be all right."],
  ["What do people say to you?","Everything. A chef's like a barber. They tell you their troubles, and you stir."],
  ["Who was in today?","Erik, Ruby, Dex. Half the town. They all ordered the same thing and said nothing."]],
 "It's a thick one today. Hold the spoon with both hands."],
12:["(Arvid arranges small cakes on a tray.) A little something for Ruby's record party. I've baked forty. She says they'll vanish in ten minutes. I say five.",[
  ["Is Ruby a good customer?","The best. She eats, she talks, she pays. And she once sent a record over, to cheer me up."],
  ["What's the record about?","I couldn't tell you. I'm a cook, not a critic. But she says it's rare, and I believe her."],
  ["Will you go?","If I've time. A good cook's never off duty."]],
 "Take a cake. They're better warm."],
13:["(Arvid peels potatoes with great speed.) Tomorrow's the great day, and the grill will be full by eight. I've ordered a ton of potatoes, three sacks of onions, and an entire pig.",[
  ["That's a lot of food.","A crowd eats, whatever else they do. And I won't be the cook who ran out."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A little. I'm always nervous. It's what keeps the soup honest."],
  ["Do you know what's happening?","Only what I hear. Which is nothing, and everything."]],
 "Eat well tonight. Tomorrow won't wait."],
14:["(Arvid ladles stew into bowls.) The big day. Everyone's out, and the grill's half empty. I've made enough stew for an army and got a family of four.",[
  ["Are you disappointed?","Not at all. A small crowd's a good crowd. They tip better."],
  ["What does the king's portrait say?","He's looking at the door, waiting for a customer. As always."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a cook. I'm hopeful by nature. We expect the best, and plan for the worst."]],
 "Save some for later. It's a long day."],
15:["(Arvid's eyes are red.) I was up till two, scraping pots. The whole town's still talking about yesterday. I've been serving soup to people who can't stop gossiping.",[
  ["What were they saying?","Everything. A bit of it true. Most of it spiced."],
  ["Did you hear anything useful?","That the prince is thinking. That the Church is praying. That the Party's shouting. All of it normal."],
  ["Are you worried?","Not today. The sourdough rose beautifully. That's a good sign."]],
 "A good loaf's a good omen. Take two."],
16:["(Arvid leans on the counter.) A debate tonight between the Party and the Democrats. The whole town will want a seat, and a stew. I've made double.",[
  ["Will you listen?","On the radio, over the pot. I'll cheer for whoever's funniest."],
  ["Are you a Democrat?","I'm a royalist who likes a good argument. It's a rare mix."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one with the better sandwiches. It's usually the way."]],
 "A debate's like a pie. Best when it's hot."],
17:["(Arvid steadies a stack of plates.) Did you feel that? The pot rippled. The ladle jumped. I thought the stove had gone mad.",[
  ["It was a tremor.","So I gathered. I've been cooking for thirty years, and I've never seen the soup move by itself."],
  ["Are you worried?","About the soup, mostly. It'd be a tragedy to lose a good batch."],
  ["Do you think it's the volcano?","The old men say so. I say, keep stirring. Panic never improved a sauce."]],
 "Hold on to something. Preferably the stove."],
18:["(Arvid wrinkles his nose.) There's a smell. Rotten eggs. It's in the kitchen, in the bread, in the stew. I've opened every window, and it makes no difference.",[
  ["It's sulphur from the volcano.","I thought as much. It's not good for a restaurant. The tourists won't come."],
  ["Did you have tourists?","One. He ate here twice and said it was the best meal he'd had. Then he left. I've not seen him since."],
  ["Will you close?","Never. A cook doesn't close. He just adds more pepper."]],
 "Eat quickly. The smell spoils the flavour."],
19:["(Arvid peels an orange with great care.) A masquerade tonight, at the Midnight Note. Half my regulars are going. I've lent my best moustache to Veikko. It's very fine.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm baking, mostly. But I'd like to see it. A mask's good for the soul."],
  ["I heard there's a prince.","That's what they say. A disguised prince, dancing with the common folk. It'd be lovely, if it's true."],
  ["Do you approve?","I approve of anything that stops people arguing. Even a moustache."]],
 "Be careful out there. The dark hides a lot."],
20:["(Arvid sprinkles herbs.) A rumour about a robbery at the palace. I used to cook there. I know every corridor. I hope nobody gets hurt.",[
  ["Would you stop them?","With a rolling pin, if I had to. Marta would help. She's a terror."],
  ["Who'd steal from the king?","The Syndicate, I'd guess. They think they're above everyone. They're not above Marta."],
  ["Is the palace safe?","As safe as a house with a thousand rooms. Not very."]],
 "Bolt your door tonight. Just in case."],
21:["(Arvid is covered in a fine grey dust.) It's gone up. The sky's black, and the ash is raining on the stove. I've shut the shutters, and the kitchen's full of neighbours.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far as I know. The lava's up north. The town's just dusty."],
  ["What are you cooking?","Everything. A big pot of stew, for whoever comes. It's what I do in an emergency."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Terribly. But I'm also busy. It's the best cure."]],
 "Eat up. It might be a long night."],
22:["(Arvid sweeps ash into a corner.) It's in the flour, in the salt, in the water. I've been boiling the same pot for two days, and it still tastes of volcano.",[
  ["Is it edible?","With enough pepper, yes. The customers don't complain. They're too grateful."],
  ["How's the town?","Tired. Kind. Everyone's sharing what they've got. I've given away eighty bowls."],
  ["Do you mind?","Not at all. A chef who won't feed the hungry isn't a chef."]],
 "Take a bowl. It's on me."],
23:["(Arvid stirs gently.) Little tremors, all day. I've begun to cook to them. A pinch of salt on the shake, and a stir on the settle. It's quite soothing.",[
  ["Is that how you cope?","I cook. It's what I do. The rest of the world can do what it likes."],
  ["Any news from Linnea?","She's counting the aftershocks. One every hour. She says it's better than a clock."],
  ["Will it end?","Everything ends. Even soup. But you can always make more."]],
 "Hold your bowl steady, and enjoy the music."],
24:["(Arvid dishes out a hot meal.) The monks are going up to the mountains, I hear. A retreat. I sent them a basket of bread. It's the least I could do.",[
  ["Are you religious?","In a quiet way. I say a grace before I eat, and another when I'm done."],
  ["Is the Church doing well?","They seem calm. Calmer than the rest of us. That's the point of prayer."],
  ["Would you go with them?","And leave the stove? Never. The stove needs me."]],
 "Eat, and give thanks. Both are good for you."],
25:["(Arvid ties on a fresh apron.) The princess is touring the island, they say. She might come by the grill. I've been polishing the plates for an hour.",[
  ["Do you hope she does?","I'd love it. I'd cook her my finest, and hide the garlic."],
  ["What would you say?","'Welcome, Your Highness.' And then I'd faint. It's what I do."],
  ["Is she popular?","Everyone likes Daphne. Even the Democrats. It's not easy to dislike someone who says thank you."]],
 "Polish the spoons. They're the first thing she'd notice."],
26:["(Arvid is counting plates.) Cola men, oil men, Party men. They've been in and out all day, ordering, paying, leaving. I've never been so busy, or so confused.",[
  ["Who tips best?","The oil men. Big notes, small smiles. The Party men leave a leaflet."],
  ["Do you mind?","As long as the plates come back clean."],
  ["Are you tired?","Dreadfully. But the soup's still hot, and so am I."]],
 "If you're hungry, now's the time. Tomorrow I'll be closed."],
27:["(Arvid sharpens a knife.) A film at the cinema tomorrow, and the grill will be empty. The whole town will be at the pictures. I'll bake some pies and sell them at the door.",[
  ["Is that allowed?","I'll ask forgiveness, not permission. It's the chef's way."],
  ["Do you like films?","I like Westerns, and anything with a feast in it. I watch for the cooking."],
  ["Will you go?","If I can find a seat. Otherwise, I'll stay and make my own show."]],
 "Bring a hat. The pies are hot."],
28:["(Arvid wipes his brow.) Today every faction's pushing. The whole town's at my door, begging for lunch. I've never cooked so fast.",[
  ["How's it going?","Like a bad dream, with garnish. But I'm enjoying it."],
  ["Do you take sides?","I take orders. It's easier."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About the soup, yes. About the island, I'll have to wait and see."]],
 "Order now. In an hour, I'll be out of bread."],
29:["(Arvid sits for a moment.) Tomorrow's the end of it. I've made a pot of my best, and a loaf of my finest, and I'm ready for whatever comes.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I always am. It's the baker's disease."],
  ["What would you like?","A calm island, a full oven, and a good night's sleep."],
  ["Any regrets?","Only that I never learned to say no to a second helping."]],
 "Eat, rest, and see what the morning brings."],
30:["(Arvid lifts a spoon to the king's portrait.) The last day. The island's future is about to be settled. I've peeled my last potato, and I feel curiously calm.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like a cook before a dinner party. It's all been done. All that's left is to taste."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The king, the ash, and the soup. And the odd reporter who stopped to eat."],
  ["Any last words?","Eat well. Love your neighbours. And never trust a thin cook."]],
 "Thank you for coming. The pot's still warm."],
};
// Augustus, the late king's younger son, in the Royal Library (Royal Family, close to the Orthodox Church). Threads: his
// saints and his fasting; the pull between the monastery and the crown; his promise to his father to keep the family together;
// his grandmother Queen Astrid's diaries, which he found behind the Lives of the Saints and reads a few pages at a time.
DAILY['Augustus']={
2:["(Augustus marks his place with a dried flower.) Saint Seraphim lived for a thousand days on a rock, praying. I've managed an afternoon in this chair. It's a start.",[
  ["A thousand days on a rock?","With bread brought up by a peasant. I've asked Marta to bring me bread up here. She brought me a pie and a lecture."],
  ["Why read about suffering?","Because it ends. Every one of these books ends with the saint at peace. I like to know how the story finishes."],
  ["Get off the rock, Your Highness.","(He laughs, quietly, as if laughing in a library were a sin.) You sound like my sister."]],
 "Shut the door softly. The books don't like a draught."],
3:["(Augustus has a small leather book open, not a saint's life.) I've found something. Behind the Lives of the Saints on the third shelf: my grandmother's diaries. Queen Astrid's.",[
  ["What do they say?","The first page is 1921. She's nineteen, and she thinks my grandfather has ridiculous ears. I'm reading a few pages a day, to make them last."],
  ["Should you be reading them?","She hid them in the holiest books in the library. I think she wanted them found by someone who'd read the holy books."],
  ["Did your grandfather have ridiculous ears?","There's a portrait in the chapel. (He considers.) She was right. It's the first unkind thing I've ever agreed with."]],
 "Don't mention the diaries to Daphne. She'd read them all in an evening and tell everyone at dinner."],
4:["Brother Ilja wrote from the monastery. He's copying the Psalms by hand and wanted my opinion on an illuminated letter. It's the nicest letter I've had since Father died.",[
  ["What was your opinion?","That the B was too gold. He'll be crushed. He'll also gild it again, because Ilja believes in more gold, always."],
  ["Do you write to the monks often?","Every week. They're the only people who write back about God and not about the crown."],
  ["Isn't that a bit lonely?","It's quiet. There's a difference, though I'll admit it's narrower in winter."]],
 "If you go up the mountain, tell Ilja the B is lovely really."],
5:["I went up the mountain this morning. Brother Paavo had his hands in the frozen garden, talking to the cabbages. Father Thyme gave me tea and didn't mention the crown once.",[
  ["Didn't he?","He mentioned it by not mentioning it. Father Thyme's silences have chapters."],
  ["Do you want to be a monk?",["I want to be useful. Some days that looks like a cassock and some days like a crown.",[
    ["And today?","Today it looks like a library chair. I'm putting off the question, and God is being patient about it."],
    ["Can't you be both?","A king who's a monk would fast through every state banquet. Marta would resign."]]]],
  ["Talking to cabbages?","Paavo says they grow better for it. He talks to the bees too, in summer. I'd rather a cabbage than some of Leopold's ministers."]],
 "Go up, if you can. The view from the mountain makes the island look very small, and very worth it."],
6:["(Augustus reads aloud from the diary.) '1924. Today I watched him crowned, and his ears did not seem to matter in the least.' My grandmother, falling in love.",[
  ["That's rather sweet.","It is. She spends three pages on the crown and one line on him, and the line's the one that matters."],
  ["Was it a happy marriage?","She doesn't say yet. I've rationed myself to four pages a day. It's harder than fasting."],
  ["What's the crown like?","Heavier than it looks, she says. Gold over iron. She tried it on once when nobody was looking, and it gave her a headache."]],
 "Four pages a day. Don't let me cheat."],
7:["It's the feast of a minor saint today, so I'm fasting. Marta sent up soup anyway, with a note that said 'Saints eat'.",[
  ["Did you eat it?","I looked at it for a long time. Then Oskar ate it. He said he was doing it for the soup's sake."],
  ["Which saint?","Saint Mamas of Caesarea, who tamed lions. I've no lions. I thought I'd try taming my appetite instead."],
  ["Isn't fasting a bit much?","Leopold says so. Daphne says I'm doing it for attention. Father used to say a fast was a way of being hungry on purpose. He didn't approve."]],
 "If you see Oskar, ask him how the soup was. He'll blush."],
8:["It's Sunday, and Father Thyme hasn't found the words for a sermon on my father. He wrote that he'd ask someone who writes. (He looks at you for a moment.)",[
  ["Will you be there?","Of course. In the third pew, on the left, where my father used to sit when he came up the mountain."],
  ["What would you say about him?","That he was hard, and generous, and afraid of dying, and that he made his peace with God. I sat with him when he did."],
  ["Why can't Father Thyme write it?","Because he loved him, I think. It's harder to write about someone you've heard confess."]],
 "God go with whoever's at the lectern. They'll need Him."],
9:["Father asked me to keep the family together. At breakfast today Leopold read the paper and Daphne read Leopold. Nobody read me.",[
  ["What did you do?","I said grace, rather loudly. Leopold said 'Amen' without looking up. It's a beginning."],
  ["Is the family falling apart?",["It's bending. Families are like trees here: they bend in the snow and you only know which ones snap in spring.",[
    ["And your father?","He was the trunk. Without him we're three branches arguing about the sun."],
    ["Can you hold them?","I can pray, and I can turn up at breakfast. I don't know what else holding is."]]]],
  ["Read the paper at breakfast yourself.","I'd have to read the Gazette. Nora writes about us the way a cat writes about mice."]],
 "Pray for us, if you pray. If you don't, just be kind at breakfast."],
10:["(Augustus is in black, with the order of service in his hands.) I chose the readings for today. Psalm 90, and the Wisdom of Solomon. Leopold wanted something shorter.",[
  ["Why those?","'Our days are like grass.' Father would have hated it. But it's true, and today's a day for true things."],
  ["How are you?","Quiet inside, which is how I know I'm not all right. I'm going to sit with him in the chapel before anyone comes."],
  ["Will Leopold manage?","He'll manage. He'll stand straight and not cry until someone else does. I may have to go first, for his sake."]],
 "Sit near the back. You see more from there, and God sees you just the same."],
11:["(Augustus is sitting with a book closed on his knee.) The chapel's empty again. I went down to the crypt this morning and told him the readings went well.",[
  ["Did he answer?","No. He never did when I talked about the Bible. He'd change the subject to fishing."],
  ["Who did you see at the funeral?","Everyone. Gustavo kissed my sister's hand on the way in, and she let him. I didn't like it, and I'm ashamed of how much I didn't like it."],
  ["Back to the saints?","Saint Monica today, who prayed for seventeen years for her son. I thought it might teach me patience with mine. I mean, with my brother."]],
 "Light a candle in the chapel if you pass it. Any reason will do."],
12:["(Augustus has the diary open.) It turns out my grandmother nearly became a nun. The year before she married. She'd packed her bag for the convent.",[
  ["What stopped her?","A letter from my grandfather, with a pressed flower in it. The flower's still between the pages. (He shows you: brown, flat, sixty years of patience.)"],
  ["Does she regret it?","She doesn't say. She writes 'I chose the crown, God help me', and then two pages about a hat. I think that's an answer."],
  ["Sounds familiar.","(He closes the diary.) Yes. That's why I'm rationing it. I'm afraid of skipping to the end and finding out what I'll do."]],
 "Don't tell Leopold. He'd think I'd found a prophecy."],
13:["A letter came up from the monastery, from Brother Anselm. Six pages. He signs it 'your brother in Christ', underlined.",[
  ["What does he want?","Me, on my knees, in a cell, with the crown left on a cushion for someone else. He puts it more beautifully than that, and much longer."],
  ["Is he right?","Brother Anselm is always sure, and that frightens me. God gives me questions. He seems to give Anselm answers."],
  ["Underlined?","Twice. In red. I think the second line was for Leopold, in case he reads my post. He does."]],
 "If you see Brother Anselm, say I'm thinking. Not more than that."],
14:["The whole island in one place today. I'll stand where I'm told, and pray for it under my breath. Everyone wants something from today.",[
  ["What do you want?","Nobody to shout. And for Leopold to see that I'm standing beside him, not behind him."],
  ["Are you nervous?","No. Crowds are only many people who are each alone. God sees each of them. I find that comforting, and Leopold finds it terrifying."],
  ["Will anyone listen?","Somebody always listens. That's why you whisper prayers."]],
 "Peace go with you. You'll be in the thick of it, I expect."],
15:["(Augustus has two letters on the reading table, side by side, and his hands flat between them.) The monastery wants an answer, and the family wants an answer, and they both want it today.",[
  ["What does your heart say?","My heart quotes both letters. It's a well-read heart and no use at all."],
  ["What would your grandmother do?",["She packed a bag for the convent and then married a king. (He smiles faintly.) So she'd do both, badly, and write about hats.",[
    ["Have you read to the end?","No. I won't. I'm not going to let a diary decide for me."],
    ["Maybe you're more like her than you think.","Everyone says I'm like my father. Nobody's ever said I'm like her. I think I'd prefer it."]]]],
  ["Can't God decide?","He usually decides through people. Today, I suspect, through whoever comes into the library next."]],
 "Close the door on your way. I need the quiet, whatever it says."],
16:["I've spent a great deal of the last day on my knees. They're sore. My conscience is a little less sore. Neither's healed.",[
  ["Do you feel better?","Clearer. Not better. Clear is a kind of cold: you see further and it hurts more."],
  ["The Communists and Democrats are debating.","I know. Anna will say God's an opiate, and Erik will say he's a private matter. I'd like to hear one of them say He's a person."],
  ["What did Leopold say?","He knocked on the door and asked if I wanted to play chess. We haven't played since we were twelve. He lost on purpose. I let him."]],
 "Go and hear them debate. Tell me if anyone mentions the soul. I'll be astonished."],
17:["(Several books lie on the floor of the library.) The shelves shook. I've been picking up the saints. Saint Simeon came down off his pillar at last.",[
  ["The ground moved?","For a few seconds. Psalm 46: 'Though the earth be removed.' I read it aloud, and Ingrid came in and read it with me."],
  ["Are you all right?","A book of homilies hit my foot. I offered it up. It's the most saintly thing I've done all week."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Viktor says there's steam over the mountain in the north. The monks will be praying. I'll join them from here."]],
 "Mind the books on the floor. Some of them are very holy and very heavy."],
18:["Brother Ilja wrote in a hurry. A relic has gone missing from the monastery. A holy thing, very old. Father Thyme is beside himself.",[
  ["What sort of relic?","Ilja didn't say, and he always says. That's how I know it's bad."],
  ["Who'd steal from monks?","Somebody who doesn't believe in it, or somebody who believes in it far too much. I'm not sure which frightens me more."],
  ["Could it just be mislaid?","In a monastery? Brother Mikael counts the spoons. Nothing's mislaid on that mountain."]],
 "Pray it comes home. Or, if you don't pray, keep your eyes open."],
19:["(Augustus reads from the diary.) '1950. Leopold born today. Loud. Has his grandfather's ears.' I've just discovered my brother's ears are hereditary.",[
  ["Will you tell him?","Never. I'll keep it for when he's crowned, or when he's unbearable. Whichever comes first."],
  ["Is there anything about you?","Not yet. I'm 1950. I come along in '53. I'm almost afraid to turn the page and find out what she thought."],
  ["Your grandmother was funny.","She was. Nobody told me. In the portraits she looks like a frozen pond."]],
 "Four pages a day. I'm nearly at myself."],
20:["I saw Daphne in the garden early, smoking one of Father's old cigarillos behind the hedge. She thought nobody saw. Viktor did, and so did I.",[
  ["Will you tell anyone?","Who would I tell? Father's dead, and Leopold would only ask for one."],
  ["She's grieving in her own way.","Yes. Daphne grieves like a cat: sideways, at night, and pretending she isn't."],
  ["Should you speak to her?","I tried. She asked me if I'd ever done anything wrong on purpose. I said not on purpose. She said that was the problem."]],
 "Keep it to yourself. You're very good at that, for a journalist."],
21:["(Augustus is at the library window, white-faced, with ash on the glass.) The mountain's burning in the north. I can see it from here, red under the smoke.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm praying. I'm also shaking. I've learned today they're not the same thing."],
  ["Is the monastery safe?","It's on the other mountain, thank God. I telephoned. Brother Teodor answered and said, 'Supper's late.' That was the whole message."],
  ["What can we do?","Get people indoors, and keep them calm. Leopold's on the steps. I'm going to sit in the chapel and leave the door open, for anyone who wants it."]],
 "Cover your mouth when you go out. And God keep you."],
22:["(Augustus is brushing ash off the pages of a book with a goose feather.) It got into the library. Into the saints. Saint Francis is grey from cover to cover.",[
  ["Will the books recover?","Most of them. A feather and patience. It's a monk's job, really. I'm practising, perhaps."],
  ["Is anyone hurt in the north?","No one. The lava's taken fences and an old shepherd's hut on the far slopes. I said a prayer of thanks so long that Ingrid brought me a cushion."],
  ["Practising for what?","(He doesn't look up from the feather.) For whatever comes. It's always good to be good with your hands."]],
 "Shake your coat before you come into the library. Saint Francis has suffered enough."],
23:["Leopold's sent soup to the north. Marta made it. I said grace over the pots before they went, which made Marta very cross, because it slowed the van down.",[
  ["Kind of him.","It was. People think my brother's proud. He is. He's also kind, quietly, when nobody's looking. Father was the same."],
  ["Did you go too?","I wanted to. Edmund said a prince on the road in the ash would only be another thing to rescue."],
  ["Marta crossed with a prince?","Marta's crossed with God most days. A prince is no trouble to her."]],
 "If you're going north, take a scarf. And say a prayer at the edge of the lava. People need one."],
24:["The monks are going up into the peaks today, on retreat. Father Thyme asked me once, years ago, if I'd come one autumn. This would have been the autumn.",[
  ["Why aren't you going?","There's a king coming on the twenty-eighth, and a family that needs me at the table. Retreats are for people with nothing to stay for."],
  ["Do you mind?","A little. More than a little. I've been looking at the mountain out of the window all day, like a dog at a closed door."],
  ["Go next year.","Next year. (He says it as if trying the word on.) Yes. I'd like there to be a next year where I choose."]],
 "If you see the monks on the road, wave. They'll be singing. Badly."],
25:["Leopold asked me what I'd think of a king who answered to a parliament. He asked me, not Father Thyme. I've been thinking ever since.",[
  ["What do you think?","That God anoints kings, and people elect parliaments, and the trouble's always been deciding which of them gets the last word."],
  ["Did your grandmother have views?","She did. 'A king who listens is worth two who talk.' 1938. Then two pages about a hat."],
  ["And the Church?","The Church would say a king answers to God first. I'm not sure God would mind a few more people asking questions."]],
 "Don't tell Brother Anselm I said that. He'd underline it."],
26:["(Augustus has the diary open at the last pages.) I've reached 1953. 'A second son. Quiet. Watches everything. He will be the kind one, I think.'",[
  ["That's lovely.","I read it four times and then went into the chapel for a while. Nobody's ever written anything about me before."],
  ["Was she right?","I'm trying to be. It's harder than being holy. Holiness you can do alone."],
  ["Is it the end of the diary?","Almost. There are three pages left. I'm saving them for the end of the month. I think I'll need them."]],
 "Three pages left. Don't let me cheat now."],
27:["Edmund has told me I'm to wear a sash for the state visit. Blue silk, with a star on it. I'd much rather wear a cassock.",[
  ["You'd look good in either.","You're kind, or blind. I look like a man wrapped up as a present by someone who doesn't like him."],
  ["What's the visiting king like?","Pious, I'm told. He'll go to the chapel and look at our icons and say his are bigger. Kings and churches are much the same everywhere."],
  ["Will you behave?","I'll fast at the banquet and smile at the soup. Marta will take it personally. Everything is personal in a kitchen."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. Pray for good weather and no ash."],
28:["(Augustus straightens his sash with a sigh.) A king at the door, and every faction on the island making its biggest push. I've never felt so much like a chess piece.",[
  ["Which piece?","A bishop, obviously. Moves diagonally, never straight, and everyone forgets he's on the board until the end."],
  ["Are you all right?","I've prayed for Leopold, for Daphne, for the island, for the visiting king and for Marta's soufflé. In that order, roughly."],
  ["Who'll win today?","Nobody wins on a day like this. Somebody just loses less. I'll be in the chapel if anyone needs an answer to that."]],
 "Go and see it all. Come back and tell me who was kind."],
29:["The question of who comes after Father is being settled. The Church has said what it thinks of me. Leopold has said what he thinks. Nobody's asked what I think, which is a mercy.",[
  ["What do you think?","I think I promised my father to keep the family together, and I mean to, whatever's on my head."],
  ["Have you read the last pages?","Not yet. Tomorrow. I want to have chosen my life before I read how she finished hers."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Yes. But I've read a great many lives of the saints, and all of them were frightened, and every one of them got up anyway."]],
 "Pray for us, please. Even if you don't. Especially if you don't."],
30:["(Augustus has the diary open at its final page, and is smiling.) I read the last three pages. She ends with: 'Whatever they make of us, we were a family.' That's all. That's everything.",[
  ["What happens to you now?","Whatever happens, I'll be in a library somewhere with a saint and a feather, and that will be enough."],
  ["Will you show Leopold?","Today. And Daphne. We'll read it together, the three of us, at the long table. Lotta can serve the soup slowly."],
  ["She'd be proud of you.","(He looks down.) She'd say my ears were ridiculous. And then she'd be proud. I've learned that's how it works."]],
 "God bless you, journalist. The library door's always open."],
};
// Bertil, civil servant at the Civil Service Bureau, Kingsgrad (administrators, no faction). Threads: forms and rules whoever is in charge, his grey suit and tidy habits, his dealings with Hanna and Signe, a rubber stamp he is proud of, his quiet fear that the island might change.
DAILY['Bertil']={
2:["(Bertil adjusts a stack of forms so the edges align.) A visitor. Form 7-B, please, for foreign press. No? Never mind, I'll fill it in myself. You'll be the British reporter. Welcome to the Bureau.",[
  ["What does the Bureau do?","Everything that isn't done by someone else. Registers, permits, stamps, and the occasional lost cat."],
  ["Who's in charge on the island now?","Nobody, officially. But the forms still need filing. That's where I come in."],
  ["Can I have a stamp?","Certainly. It's blue, and says RECEIVED. It's my favourite. I had it made in Helsinki."]],
 "Please don't sit on the in-tray. It's already weary."],
3:["(Bertil squares a ruler against a ledger.) The king died, and the Bureau's first thought was whether his pension was paid up. It was. The second was where to file the death. We've a drawer for that.",[
  ["That sounds cold.","It's not cold. It's orderly. Order is how we show respect."],
  ["Do you miss him?","He signed every document in green ink, with a flourish. I've kept a sample."],
  ["What happens to the forms now?","They wait. Forms are patient. It's their one virtue."]],
 "Take a blotter. They're free, and nobody counts them."],
4:["(Bertil sips tea from a thin cup.) Whoever wins, the Bureau will still be here. We've outlasted three kings, two governors, and a very persistent mouse.",[
  ["How do you stay neutral?","By having no opinions. Strictly speaking, I haven't any. I've rules, which are better."],
  ["Is it a quiet job?","Very. Except when Hanna calls about her pencil requisition. She's persistent."],
  ["Do you like Hanna?","Enormously. She fills in every form, and her handwriting is superb."]],
 "Don't breathe on the ink. It's still wet."],
5:["(Bertil holds a form to the light.) A request from the lighthouse for a new set of lamp oil receipts. Signe's receipts are kept in a biscuit tin. I've been trying to get her a proper file for years.",[
  ["Is she difficult?","Not difficult. Eccentric. She sends me a postcard at Christmas, with a drawing of a seal."],
  ["Do you ever visit?","Once, in 1971. I got seasick, and the stair defeated me. I send forms instead."],
  ["Are the receipts in order?","They're in a tin. It's not the same, but it's close."]],
 "I'll file that under 'sentimental'. We've a drawer for it."],
6:["(Bertil unrolls a chart.) A plan of the island's districts, with the roads marked in pencil as they reopen. Each one I colour in with a different crayon. It's the only fun I have.",[
  ["Which colour is Kingsgrad?","Grey. It suits the town."],
  ["Is the map official?","Only because I drew it. But I'll file it, so it'll be official by Thursday."],
  ["What's your hobby?","Ledgers. I like a long column that adds up. It's a very quiet thrill."]],
 "Careful with the crayon. It's the last green."],
7:["(Bertil stamps a document with precision.) The north-west's open, and the first applications from Diversified Petroleum arrived at nine. Eleven permits, a land licence and a letter of friendship. I've filed all three.",[
  ["A letter of friendship?","From Dario, the boss. It said we'd be great friends. It was typed on very thick paper."],
  ["Do you trust him?","I trust his paper. The man himself is a matter for the police."],
  ["Will you grant the permits?","If they fill in the forms correctly. I've never refused a correct form in my life."]],
 "Correct forms, polite requests. That's the Bureau's whole philosophy."],
8:["(Bertil refolds a sheet.) Sunday, and I'm here anyway. The Bureau's always open to me. There's no better place to be on a quiet day than between two filing cabinets.",[
  ["Don't you have a life outside?","I've a flat, a plant, and a wireless. They're all very well behaved."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Occasionally. I find the liturgy soothing. It has a clear structure."],
  ["What does your plant do?","It sits. I water it on alternate days. We're very alike."]],
 "A quiet Sunday's the best sort. Don't spoil it."],
9:["(Bertil stares at a file.) The funeral's tomorrow, and I've drawn up a seating plan for the Royal Chapel. Edmund wouldn't let me touch the front pews. I've made do with the back.",[
  ["Is that a big job?","Two hundred names, in three colours, with notes. I've never been so happy."],
  ["Who's at the front?","The family, naturally. And the Church, and the heads of the factions. I'd rather not say how they're placed."],
  ["Will you be there?","In the fourth row from the back, with a pencil. I'll watch the arrangements work."]],
 "Be seated by ten. Punctuality's the soul of ceremony."],
10:["(Bertil wears a black armband over his grey suit.) A fine funeral. Every seat filled, every wreath placed, every speech within the timetable. Edmund and I exchanged a nod. I've never been so moved.",[
  ["Was the king well sent off?","Beautifully. No slips. The only delay was a cough in the third row."],
  ["Did you know him?","I met him once, when he signed a land transfer. He thanked me, and said my handwriting was admirable. I've never forgotten."],
  ["What happens now?","Now the paperwork begins. A new reign needs a great many forms."]],
 "A good day for the island. And for the files."],
11:["(Bertil shuffles papers into folders.) The morning after, and the Bureau's drowned in letters of condolence. Seven hundred, from all over the Baltic. I'm sorting them by region.",[
  ["Do they all get a reply?","Every one. A printed card, with a signature. I've a stamp for it."],
  ["Is that quite personal?","It's dignified. And it's what the old king would've wanted."],
  ["Any odd ones?","One from a lighthouse keeper in Norway, who'd never met the king. It was very moving."]],
 "Please don't read the letters. They're confidential, and rather sad."],
12:["(Bertil frowns at an application.) A complaint from the Party, and another from Diversified Petroleum, both on the same land. I've to decide who gets the permit. It isn't easy.",[
  ["Who is in the right?","The one who filled in the form first. That's the rule, and I follow the rule."],
  ["What if both did?","Then I toss a coin. It's in the regulations, subsection nine."],
  ["Does anyone try to bribe you?","Constantly. The Syndicate sent a ham. I returned it, with a form."]],
 "Rules are the island's skeleton. Don't break a bone."],
13:["(Bertil straightens his tie.) Tomorrow's the great day, and the Bureau's been told to stay open. I've issued fifty extra passes, and I've written an essay on crowd management.",[
  ["Do you know what's going to happen?","Not at all. A stamp can't predict the future. It can only record it."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a civil servant. I worry on schedule, and I've scheduled it for later."],
  ["Will you attend?","From the Bureau, with the window open. I'll file whatever I see."]],
 "Bring a pen. History's easier to record than to remember."],
14:["(Bertil sits very still.) The great day, and the town's quiet. Everyone's out, and the Bureau's empty. I've never heard the clock tick so loudly.",[
  ["Is it strange?","It's like a church on a weekday. You feel something's watching."],
  ["Have you done anything?","I've filed a report on the weather. And a second on the mood. The mood was hard to classify."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Of change, a little. But the Bureau's survived worse."]],
 "Stay calm. Or at least look it."],
15:["(Bertil sorts a pile of memoranda.) Yesterday's over, and my desk's flooded with petitions. I've three hundred to process, and not a single one's in the right format.",[
  ["What's wrong with them?","Handwriting. Margins. Ink. One's in crayon. I'll treat it with dignity."],
  ["Did anything big happen?","Everything and nothing. I'll know more once I've read the minutes."],
  ["Are you coping?","With tea, and a very sharp pencil."]],
 "Return the forms by Friday. Preferably with ink."],
16:["(Bertil adjusts a clock.) A debate tonight, and I've been asked to provide a timekeeper. I've been given a stopwatch, and a bell. I take it as a great honour.",[
  ["Who's debating?","The Party and the Democrats. Both on the stroke of eight. I'll ring at ten minutes."],
  ["Will you take sides?","Never. I'll cut off whoever runs over."],
  ["Is that fair?","It's the only fair thing about it."]],
 "Remember: ten minutes each. No exceptions."],
17:["(Bertil clutches a ledger as it slides.) A tremor. The shelves swayed. A file of 1958 fell off the top, and a mouse ran out. I'm shaken, but unhurt.",[
  ["Was the mouse a problem?","The same persistent mouse. He's been here since before me. I've given up."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","The Bureau has no opinion. Though I've drafted a memo."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm a civil servant. We don't panic. We form a committee."]],
 "If it happens again, stand under a filing cabinet."],
18:["(Bertil points to a window crack.) A new crack, running diagonally. It wasn't there yesterday. I've logged it, measured it, and photographed it for the file.",[
  ["Is the building safe?","The Bureau has stood for ninety years. It'll outlast the mountain."],
  ["Do you smell that?","Sulphur. It's drifting from the north. I've opened a window, and written a note."],
  ["Will you evacuate?","If instructed. In an orderly line, by rank."]],
 "Don't touch the crack. It's evidence."],
19:["(Bertil blinks at a note.) A licence request for a masked ball at the Midnight Note. Sid applied quite properly. I've granted it, with one clause: no false moustaches.",[
  ["Why no moustaches?","They make identification difficult. I have a rule about identification."],
  ["Is it enforceable?","No. But it's on the paper, and that's half the battle."],
  ["Are you going?","I'm not invited. I wasn't even invited to the wedding of my own niece. I filed a complaint."]],
 "Stamped, signed, and slightly sad. That's the Bureau."],
20:["(Bertil holds a telegram at arm's length.) A report of a burglary planned at the palace. The sender is anonymous, which is irregular. I've forwarded it to Hanna with a covering note.",[
  ["Do you believe it?","I believe in procedure. I've sent it where it needs to go."],
  ["What would a thief want from a palace?","Whatever isn't listed in the inventory. Which, I regret to say, is rather a lot."],
  ["Who keeps the inventory?","I do. Twice a year. It's thirty-one pages, and not a spoon unaccounted for."]],
 "If anything's missing, I'll have it in triplicate."],
21:["(Bertil wears a handkerchief over his nose.) The volcano's erupted. The sky is grey. The files are grey. I've closed the windows, taped the cracks, and begun a memorandum.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, thank heaven. The Bureau has no casualties, except a fern."],
  ["How are you holding up?","I've a list. Lists help. Item one: breathe."],
  ["What does the Bureau do in an emergency?","Convenes. We convened at nine. We've been convening since."]],
 "Stay indoors, and please don't touch the ash. It stains."],
22:["(Bertil brushes ash off a form.) Every file's grey. Every desk. Every pen. I've issued a notice to all staff: wipe before you write.",[
  ["Is work continuing?","Of course. The island doesn't stop for a volcano. It merely sneezes."],
  ["Any good news?","The mouse survived. He was found in the stamp drawer, very grey, very calm."],
  ["How's Kingsgrad?","Sweeping. Everyone's sweeping. It's quite a communal feeling."]],
 "Please wipe your feet. Twice."],
23:["(Bertil frowns at a report.) Two incidents tonight, so I'm told. A disturbance at a pipeline, and a quarrel at a station. I've filed both under 'miscellaneous', as is the custom.",[
  ["Sounds serious.","It's all serious. But it's all a matter of procedure."],
  ["Do you know the cause?","Not yet. A civil servant never guesses. He waits for the forms."],
  ["Are you tired?","Dreadfully. But the stamps are still crisp."]],
 "A little patience. And a lot of paper."],
24:["(Bertil scans a long list.) The Church has requested transit permits for forty monks, a quantity of candles and a donkey. I've granted all three. The donkey was a surprise.",[
  ["Is it a pilgrimage?","A retreat. To the peaks. I've written it down as a 'seasonal relocation'."],
  ["Isn't it dangerous?","Not as dangerous as being without a permit."],
  ["Do you trust the monks?","I trust their handwriting. It's excellent."]],
 "Safe travels. Please return the form on arrival."],
25:["(Bertil straightens his tie.) A tea at the palace this afternoon, with a very mixed guest list. The princess, some Democrats, a bishop. I've drawn up a seating plan, with a map.",[
  ["Is it a political tea?","Everything is. Even the sugar."],
  ["Who sits where?","The princess near the window. The bishop by the door. And the Democrat in the middle, where he can't escape."],
  ["Will you attend?","As an observer. I'll sit in the corner, with a pencil."]],
 "Pour the tea carefully. Cups are tricky in a crisis."],
26:["(Bertil sorts a heap of permits.) Cola, oil, Party, church. I've never signed so many in a day. I've worn the stamp pad down, and had to borrow Hanna's.",[
  ["Are they all legitimate?","They're all complete. Legitimacy's a different department."],
  ["Is the island busy?","It's frantic. Everyone's in a hurry to arrange something. I'm in a hurry to arrange a nap."],
  ["Do you ever relax?","On Sundays, with the crossword. Seven letters, 'procedure'."]],
 "If you need a stamp, join the queue. It's long."],
27:["(Bertil removes his glasses.) Tomorrow's a very big day, and I've drawn up the arrangements for every office. Passes, parking, permits. It's all in a binder, and the binder is very thick.",[
  ["Are you ready?","As ready as a man can be who's read all the regulations."],
  ["What if it goes wrong?","Then there's a form for that. Form 99. I wrote it myself."],
  ["Do you ever doubt?","Occasionally. But a good file calms the nerves."]],
 "Sleep well. Tomorrow's paper will be heavy."],
28:["(Bertil stands at the window.) Every faction on the island is moving today. I can hear horns, and shouting, and a very bad brass band. It's the most disorder I've ever seen, and I've prepared for it.",[
  ["How's the Bureau?","Full. Everyone wants a stamp, a pass, a favour. I've issued four hundred permits since breakfast."],
  ["What's the mood?","Taut, like a drawn string. I'd say it's an administrative emergency."],
  ["Will you survive?","With tea, and a very good stamp."]],
 "Take a number. And a seat. It might be a while."],
29:["(Bertil closes a file with a snap.) Tomorrow's the end of it, and I've done everything I can. Every form filed, every pass issued. The rest is up to the island.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","As hopeful as a man can be who knows the law."],
  ["What do you want?","Order. Whoever wins, let it be orderly. A minute's silence, a signature, and a handshake."],
  ["Any worries?","That the next government might not like forms. It would be a terrible loss."]],
 "Go home and rest. The Bureau will carry on."],
30:["(Bertil lays his stamp down gently.) The last day. I've signed my last pass, and stamped my last permit. Now we wait for the island to speak.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Calm. A civil servant's always ready. Whoever's in charge, the paperwork goes on."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The king's funeral, the ash, and your very polite questions. I've filed them all."],
  ["Any last words?","Keep a copy of everything. It's the best advice I know."]],
 "Thank you for your visit. Please sign the book on your way out."],
};
// Birgit, manager of the Nordmart supermarket in Frostholm (no faction). Threads: clipboards and strict schedules, aisle-blockers, the oil workers' late shopping, her neighbour Sanna at Polar Burger, the lift in Skyline Apartments, stockpiling before and after the eruption.
DAILY['Birgit']={
7:["(Birgit is holding a clipboard and does not look up.) Aisle three, you're blocking it. Thank you. Reporter, are you? The roads have been open for an hour and I've already had four deliveries wrong.",[
  ["Sorry. Nice shop.","It's a supermarket, not a shop. A shop is where you chat. A supermarket is where you move. You'll learn."],
  ["How's business now the north's open?","Brisk. The oil lot buy by the trolley. I've rationed the tinned peaches."],
  ["Do you ever relax?","On the second Tuesday of the month, between four and four-fifteen. It's on the schedule."]],
 "Move along, please. There's a queue."],
8:["(Birgit consults a chart on the wall.) It's Sunday and the monks' delivery is late. I've a van with no driver and a driver with no excuse. Do you know how many spreadsheets I've written this week?",[
  ["How many?","Eleven. Four on the beans alone. I don't trust a man who hasn't counted his beans."],
  ["Do you go to church?","I'm busy on Sundays. The Almighty can take it up with the manager."],
  ["What would help?","A world where deliveries arrive when they say. I'd even settle for a day."]],
 "If you see my driver, tell him to ring. He knows what for."],
9:["(Birgit sets down a crate of cola with a thump.) Two companies, three labels, one shelf. They've each paid me for the end of the aisle. I've put the beans at the end of the aisle. Everyone's cross.",[
  ["Who paid you?","Kelp Cola's boys, and a young man for the Party who offered me a flag. I said I'd take the money."],
  ["Is there a cola war in Frostholm?","There's a cola war in every shop. I just manage the casualties."],
  ["Which sells best?","The one at eye level. Nobody ever buys what's at the bottom, and that's a fact I've proved."]],
 "Don't touch the display. I've measured it."],
10:["(Birgit has the radio on a low, solemn tune, and a black ribbon pinned to the clipboard.) We stay open. The old king would have understood. He was a man of schedules himself. He opened every Parliament at precisely ten.",[
  ["Did you admire him?","I admired his punctuality. It's rarer than wisdom."],
  ["Is the shop quiet?","The oil men are at the funeral, on the radio. For once nobody's blocking the aisle."],
  ["What happens next?","A great deal of upheaval. Which is why I'm doubling the bread."]],
 "I'll observe a minute's silence at noon. It's on the schedule."],
11:["(Birgit looks at the till roll.) Everyone's buying bread. Something's coming, or someone's saying so. It's alms at the church, it's pay at the mill. I'd say people are nervous, but they're buying the right things.",[
  ["Why bread?","Bread's the sign. When people buy bread, they're afraid. When they buy chocolate, they're hopeful. Right now it's mostly bread."],
  ["Do you give to the church?","A crate of tins every Sunday. I've a form for it. It saves arguments."],
  ["Is there enough for everyone?","I plan for it. It's called stock control. It's the only politics I understand."]],
 "Take a trolley. Not that one. It pulls to the left."],
12:["(Birgit holds a crisp banknote to the light.) A man from the oil office paid for forty trolleys of tinned food, in cash. For a 'department'. I asked what department needs forty trolleys. He said, 'Accounts.'",[
  ["Who was he?","A Mr Hale, I believe, from Diversified Petroleum. I gave him a receipt and an unconvincing smile."],
  ["Is it odd?","Odd as a screw in a loaf. But the money's real, and so is the soup."],
  ["Do you ask questions?","Only about quantities. I never ask why."]],
 "If you see forty trolleys, they're mine."],
13:["Free samples today. (Birgit stands by a table of tiny cups.) I've been standing here since eight, telling people to take one. Some have taken three. There's always a Sanna among them.",[
  ["Does Sanna come by?","Between shifts, always. She's a good friend and a terrible shopper. She reads every label and buys the same pie."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola doing well?","Their samples vanish faster than my patience. The new formula is a hit."],
  ["Do you like the free days?","They're chaos with a banner. But the till doesn't lie."]],
 "Take a cup and keep moving."],
14:["(Birgit looks at her watch twice.) The whole island's off to the big day, and the supermarket's like a morgue. I've sent half the staff home and told the other half to dust.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm staying. Someone has to keep the lights on. A crowd's a crowd, and I've seen enough of them at the checkout."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","I think everyone will shout, then queue for a hot dog. It's the way of crowds."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm prepared. I've doubled the bread and tripled the batteries."]],
 "If something happens, I'm here. If nothing happens, also here."],
15:["(Birgit waves a paper at you.) The Royals and the Church, shouting about crowns and crosses. I've told them both: there's a queue at the checkout, and nobody gets served out of turn.",[
  ["Are they in your shop?","Both, on the same afternoon. A priest with a cart and a prince's man with a list. It was very quiet. Very cold."],
  ["Who did you serve first?","The one with the exact change. It's the only fair rule I know."],
  ["Which side are you on?","The side that returns the trolley."]],
 "Keep to the left. It's not just manners, it's safety."],
16:["(Birgit's radio is on full, in the stockroom.) A debate. Of course they'd hold it on a Thursday, when I've three deliveries. Whoever wins, I want the schedule changed.",[
  ["Are you following it?","I'm listening between pallets. They say a great deal and mean very little. It's the opposite of a shopping list."],
  ["Who's got the better case?","The Democrats say everyone's equal. The Party says nobody's more equal than anybody. I say: pay at the till."],
  ["Will it change the shop?","Not unless they want to change the bread. And I'd fight for the bread."]],
 "If they ask for my vote, I'll ask for their delivery times."],
17:["(A row of tins trembles on a shelf and Birgit slaps a hand on it.) The ground. Again. It's the fourth time today. I've written it on the schedule as 'minor disturbance'.",[
  ["Is it frightening?","It's disruptive. A tin falls, and I've a form to fill."],
  ["Where's Dario? I heard he's gone missing.","So I'm told. The oil office has been very quiet. Nobody's bought a paperclip in a day."],
  ["Is the shop safe?","It's a steel frame with a good lease. I've seen worse come through."]],
 "If it gets worse, go to the loading bay. It's the sturdiest part."],
18:["(Birgit is adding a line to the order sheet: 'water, batteries, candles'.) I've never sold so many torches. A man bought thirty. Thirty! What does he plan to light?",[
  ["Are people stockpiling?","Like squirrels. The monks said something about the mountain, and now everyone's got a plan."],
  ["Do you believe the monks?","I believe in being prepared. And in the supply chain. A volcano's just another delay."],
  ["Is there enough?","I've asked for twice what I need. I'll be right or ridiculous. I'm fine with either."]],
 "One tin of peaches per customer. No exceptions. Not even you."],
19:["(Birgit's counting tins with a pencil.) I had a woman this morning ask for a hundred loaves for 'a rally'. A rally! In a snowstorm. She had a badge and a very good argument.",[
  ["Which party was she from?","The red one. 'Comrades Wanted.' She wanted bread, and a discount, and a flag in my window. I gave her the bread."],
  ["Did you give the discount?","A modest one, for a loyal shopper. I'm not above being practical."],
  ["Do you vote?","On the day. I fill in the form and file it. It's the same as any other."]],
 "Next in line, please. And no politics at the checkout."],
20:["(Birgit snaps the clipboard against her leg.) Something's going on at the Hotel Aurora, and every man with a hat is buying cigarettes. I've had three suits and one raincoat. They all pay in coins.",[
  ["Is that strange?","It's very strange. A spy pays in notes. A man hiding pays in coins. I've worked it out."],
  ["Are you curious?","A little. But curiosity doesn't stack shelves."],
  ["Who do you think is behind it?","Nobody I'd serve before noon."]],
 "I've said nothing. I saw nothing. I scanned your items."],
21:["(Birgit stands in a supermarket full of people holding baskets, her clipboard raised like a signal.) Quiet! Orderly! There's enough for everyone! The mountain's erupted, and I will not have a stampede at the bread.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Grey, and furious. But the shelves are standing, and I've enough for a fortnight."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody. It's a miracle. I've said so to the till and to the Lord, in that order."],
  ["How are people behaving?","Better than expected. A few arguments over the water. One man tried to take twelve loaves. I've put him at the back."]],
 "One basket each. Wipe your feet. And don't panic."],
22:["(Birgit waves a broom.) It's on everything. The doors, the trolleys, the tins. I've closed the door at eleven for a deep clean. The ash comes in on every shoe.",[
  ["Are you open?","Yes, with a mat. And a notice. And a very stern face."],
  ["What's selling?","Bread, masks, cloths, tinned soup. Not a single bag of crisps. That tells you the mood."],
  ["Is Sanna all right?","She's been grilling with a scarf over her face. The burgers taste of ash, and nobody's complained."]],
 "Take a cloth. They're by the door. They're free."],
23:["(Birgit is dragging a pallet.) The oil men have been in, buying everything. They say 'Deep Repairs', whatever that means. I say: pay at the till.",[
  ["What are they repairing?","Something at the rigs. I don't ask. I only count the tins."],
  ["Is it a big order?","Enough to feed a battalion. I've put up the price of the beans, and they haven't noticed."],
  ["Have you seen the Party men?","Spanners, I'm told. A great many spanners. In a supermarket. I've no idea where they got them."]],
 "Check the receipt. It's very long."],
24:["The ash is slowing. (Birgit rubs her neck.) We've had a clean-up crew in, hired by someone. Men in good gloves. They were very thorough. Too thorough, if you ask me.",[
  ["Who hired them?","They didn't say. A very quiet team. They cleaned the stockroom twice, and I'd swear they were looking for something."],
  ["Did they find it?","If they did, they didn't say. They left with a nod and a very clean floor."],
  ["Is the shop back to normal?","As normal as it'll be. The tins are straight. The aisles are clear."]],
 "Don't ask what they were looking for. I didn't."],
25:["(Birgit points at a poster in the window.) A crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. I told the man, I'll hang it if you pay for the glass cleaner.",[
  ["Did he pay?","A fair deal. I'm not political. I'm just fond of clean glass."],
  ["What do you think of the idea?","It's a very good slogan. Whether it's a good plan, I'll know when I see the invoice."],
  ["Do you want a vote?","I want a refund policy. I'd settle for a vote."]],
 "Next! You, with the flag. Aisle five."],
26:["(Birgit eyes a pyramid of cola.) Kelp's got a prince on the bottle now. A prince! In a supermarket! It's brought a very different class of shopper. They hold the bottle and sigh.",[
  ["Is it selling?","Faster than the bread. A romantic nation. Who'd have guessed?"],
  ["Which prince is it?","Leopold, I think. The one with the jaw. The label's rather flattering."],
  ["Is Dario still missing?","I heard he's bought the spa. Or he's buying it. I don't know. The oil lot don't shop here any more."]],
 "If you want the prince, he's in aisle two. Keep it moving."],
27:["(Birgit tucks her pencil behind her ear.) A film and a card game, I'm told. High stakes, whatever that means. I'm not a gambler. I'm a manager. I only bet on a well-ordered shelf.",[
  ["Will you go to the pictures?","I'd like to. But I've a rota. And a very long stock count."],
  ["Is the Syndicate around?","I've no idea. I count the till. If there's a shortfall, I look for the Syndicate."],
  ["Will it be a big night?","Everyone's hoping for something. I'm hoping for a quiet closing."]],
 "Tell Sanna to save me a burger. I'll be late."],
28:["(Birgit's put a notice on the door: 'Open As Usual. Please Queue.') The big push. Everyone's buying flags, and I've sold out of masking tape. Do you know how much masking tape a rally takes?",[
  ["How much?","More than I've ever had. They use it for posters, for banners, for arguments. It's the glue of democracy."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About the stock, yes. Not about the politics."],
  ["Are the oil men still shopping?","Less than before. They seem to be watching something. It makes me uneasy."]],
 "Don't stand in the doorway. It's a thoroughfare."],
29:["(Birgit surveys the shop.) Tomorrow's the end. Whatever the outcome, the shelves must be full on Monday. I've doubled the order. I've tripled the bread. I'll sleep afterwards.",[
  ["Are you ready?","The stock's ready. I'm only mostly ready."],
  ["What do you hope for?","Order. Calm. A reliable delivery. I'd like the same for the island, if it isn't too much to ask."],
  ["Any last words?","Check your receipt. And thank you for not blocking the aisle."]],
 "Go home. Rest. It's all on the schedule."],
30:["(Birgit has her clipboard under her arm and her coat on, for once.) The last day. I've done the count. It's all there. The island may change, but the stock is correct.",[
  ["How has the month been?","Chaotic. Dusty. I've written a hundred and four forms, and I regret none of them."],
  ["What will you remember?","The day the ash came in on the trolleys. It's on every tin."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come again. Bring a bag. They're five pennies."]],
 "Mind the step. It's wet, and I've mopped it twice."],
};
// Bjarne, foreman of the Eastbrook Lumber Company's mill (Democratic Party). Threads: Dagny the owner and the ledger on his desk,
// his crew (he's never let a man go hungry, and slips them money from his own pocket), the timber orders (palace benches for the
// funeral, the rally stage on the 28th), the saw guard, and letters from his daughter Annika in Turku, who wants him to retire.
DAILY['Bjarne']={
4:["(Bjarne is brushing sawdust out of his beard with the back of a big hand.) The road's open, the yard's full of logs, and the saw's running again. A mill in winter is a lonely thing. Now it's noisy. I like noisy.",[
  ["How long have you run the mill?","Thirty-one years on the floor, nineteen as foreman. I started sweeping up when I was fourteen. I'm still sweeping up. Just with a bigger broom."],
  ["Who owns it?","Dagny. Her father owned it before her. He'd come down and eat his lunch with the men. Dagny eats hers in Frostholm."],
  ["You look like a lumberjack.","(He laughs, a deep rumble.) I am a lumberjack. Or I was, before my knees. Now I'm a man with a pencil and a ledger and very good knees for sitting."]],
 "Mind the saw on your way out. It doesn't care who you are."],
5:["(Bjarne is at his desk with a big ledger open, running a thick finger down a column.) Orders. The palace wants pine benches for the funeral. Two hundred of them, by the tenth. For the mourners to sit on.",[
  ["Can you make two hundred in five days?","If the saw holds and the lads don't sleep. They'll grumble, and then they'll do it. They always do. Then they'll grumble that they did."],
  ["Is the palace a good customer?","The palace pays late and complains early. But it's an honour, a funeral for a king. My father built the platform at his coronation. Now I'm building his benches. That's a life, that is."],
  ["Is that the famous ledger?","(He closes it, not too quickly.) It's a ledger. Every mill has one. Mine's got a coffee ring on every page and sawdust in the spine."]],
 "Two hundred benches. If you see a man with a hammer, send him up."],
6:["(Bjarne is reading a letter, and smiling into his beard.) From my daughter, Annika. In Turku. She wants me to retire and come and live with her and the grandchildren, and eat herring and do nothing. Doing nothing would kill me in a week.",[
  ["How old are your grandchildren?","Four and seven. The seven-year-old sent a drawing of me. I've got a beard down to my knees and a saw for a hand. She's very accurate."],
  ["Would you ever go?","One day. Not yet. Not with things the way they are. Somebody's got to look after these lads, and Dagny won't."],
  ["Does she worry about you?","She worries about the saw. Every letter. 'Dad, mind the saw.' I've been minding that saw since before she was born."]],
 "Annika wants a photograph. I've never liked them. I always look like a bear."],
7:["(Bjarne is sitting on a log with a sandwich, watching Ilse load timber onto a cart.) Look at her. Ilse. Strongest on the crew and won't let anybody forget it. She lifted a log this morning that Knut and I couldn't shift together.",[
  ["Is she always this strong?","She's been loading since she was sixteen. Her father worked here. He was strong too. She's stronger. And angrier, which helps."],
  ["Does she get paid more?","(He stops chewing.) She gets paid the same as everyone else. Which is the same as six years ago. Don't put that in your paper. Or do."],
  ["Want to arm-wrestle me?","(He looks at you, very kindly.) I wouldn't want to hurt your writing arm. Ask Ilse. She'll do it for free, and you'll write with your left for a week."]],
 "Benches for the palace. Back to it."],
8:["(Bjarne is sweeping sawdust in an empty mill.) Sunday. The saw's off and the lads are home, and I come in and sweep. It's a habit. I like the mill quiet. You can hear it think.",[
  ["What does a mill think about?","Wood. Weather. Who's going to pay the bill. Same as me, really."],
  ["How are the benches coming?","A hundred and sixty. The rest by Wednesday. The lads have earned a drink. I've stood them one at the Timberline Friday, out of my own pocket. Don't tell Dagny."],
  ["Don't you ever rest?","Sweeping's resting. Ask any foreman. It's the only time nobody asks you anything."]],
 "Close the door gently. The mill's sleeping."],
9:["(Bjarne is shaking his head over the ledger.) The price of bread's gone up again. Third time this year. And Dagny's got me writing the same wages in the same column as six years ago.",[
  ["What does Dagny say?","That the mill can't afford it. And then she drives off in a new car. I keep her books. I know what the mill can afford."],
  ["Will you tell the men?","They know. They're not stupid. They've been talking to Helen from the union, in Saltwick. I'm not in the union. I'm a foreman. But I've got ears."],
  ["What will you do?",["(He looks at the ledger for a long time.) I'll keep the books straight. That's my job. Whoever wants to read them, they're on my desk.",[
    ["Isn't that disloyal?","Loyal to Dagny or loyal to the lads? That's the question I go to sleep with. I've never been good at sleeping."],
    ["Your desk's very easy to get to.","(He smiles.) Is it? I never noticed. I'll leave it where it is, then."]]]]],
 "Mind the books. They've got more sawdust in them than numbers."],
10:["(Bjarne has a black band on his sleeve and his hat in his hands.) The funeral's today, on our benches. I drove the last forty over myself, at first light. I wanted to see them in the chapel. They looked right.",[
  ["Did you stay for the funeral?","No. That's for the important folk. I stood at the back for a minute, and saw the coffin, and came home. It was pine. Not ours. I'd have done it better."],
  ["Did the palace pay?","(He laughs.) The palace never pays on the day. In three months, with a letter on thick paper, and a crest. My father framed one. I frame mine in the drawer."],
  ["How do you feel?","Sad, a bit. He was the king all my life. I didn't agree with him. But I'll miss knowing he was there, like a big old tree you never climbed."]],
 "Go gently. It's a day for it."],
11:["(Bjarne is standing at his desk with his arms folded, watching the mill floor. The saw's quiet, and the men are talking.) The lads want a rise, and a guard on the saw. Helen's sent someone to speak to Dagny for them. I'm keeping out of it.",[
  ["Whose side are you on?","I'm the foreman. I'm on the side of the timber getting cut. (He pauses.) And of the lads going home with ten fingers. Write down both. I'll deny the second."],
  ["Has Dagny listened before?","Every week I tell her. Every week she tells me the mill can't afford it. I keep her books. Every week, I go back to my desk and look at the books."],
  ["Is that ledger on your desk?","(He doesn't look round.) It's always on my desk. I never move it. I'm going to the far end of the mill now, to check the stacks. Takes a good ten minutes."]],
 "I'll be at the stacks. Mind the saw."],
12:["(Bjarne is shaving a long curl of wood off a plank with a hand plane, slowly, as if it calms him.) Yesterday. All that talking. The lads are still arguing about it over their sandwiches. I'm planing a plank. Wood doesn't argue.",[
  ["How are the men?","Talking. Some of them think the world's changed. Some of them think it never will. That's how men are after a fight. Like dogs after a scrap."],
  ["And Dagny?","Dagny's in Frostholm. Dagny's always in Frostholm after anything happens. She'll be back on Monday with her pencil."],
  ["What are you making?","A shelf for Ruby's shop. She's run out of room for her jazz. She says she'll pay me in records. I don't own a record player. I'll take them anyway."]],
 "Smell that? Fresh pine. That's the best smell on the island."],
13:["(Bjarne is standing by the saw with a long bar of steel in his hand, frowning at the blade.) The blade's going dull. Third time this year. It's the knots. The logs from the north are all knots, this season. Hard winter.",[
  ["Can you sharpen it?","I can. Knut can, better. A good sawyer sharpens his own blade. He'll be on it with a file this afternoon, swearing at it like a brother."],
  ["Why's the wood so knotted?","Trees grow knots where they've been hurt. Broken branches, frost, bad years. Every knot's a story. These ones are all bad winters."],
  ["Is the saw safe?","(He looks at you a long moment.) The saw's as safe as it's allowed to be. I've said so, and I'll keep saying so, to anyone who asks."]],
 "Stand back from the blade. It bites."],
14:["(Bjarne is in a clean shirt, which makes him look like a stranger.) The fourteenth. The whole island together. I've given the lads the day off. Dagny doesn't know yet. She'll find out on Monday, and I'll be ready.",
 [
  ["What are you hoping for?","That people talk to each other. Mill men and fishermen. Monks and oil men. And that somebody listens to somebody. I'm not greedy."],
  ["Won't Dagny be angry?","Very. And I'll tell her the mill shut out of respect for the island. And she'll look at me, and I'll look at her, and we'll both know."],
  ["You look smart.","(He tugs at his collar.) My daughter sent this shirt for Christmas, three years ago. First time I've worn it. It itches like a guilty conscience."]],
 "Go on, then. See you in the crowd. I'm the big one."],
15:["(Bjarne is reading another letter, and frowning this time.) Annika's heard about the shaking ground in the papers in Turku. Already! Before we've had any. She wants me on the next boat.",
 [
  ["What shaking ground?","Nothing yet. A rattle in the cups, maybe. But the papers in Finland have got hold of the steam from the old mountain, and now they're writing about it as if we're Pompeii."],
  ["Will you go?","No. My place is here. (He folds the letter.) But I'll write back very kindly, and tell her I'm minding the saw."],
  ["The royals and the church are at odds today.","Let them be. Crowns and crosses. I've got two hundred benches' worth of splinters in my hands and a dull blade. Those are my troubles."]],
 "Mind the stacks. They've been creaking."],
16:["(Bjarne is wiping his hands on a rag, getting ready to leave.) Debate day. The lads are off to hear Erik and the Communists go at each other. I'm going too, at the back, where I can leave early.",
 [
  ["Who do you think will do better?","Erik's a good talker. He talks like a teacher: kind and long. Anna talks like a knife. Helen talks like the lads talk, and that's what worries me."],
  ["Why does Helen worry you?","Because she's been in this mill, and she's right about the saw and the pay. And I've been here thirty years, and I haven't fixed either. That's not a nice thought."],
  ["Will Ilse heckle?","Ilse will heckle until they carry her out. She's been rehearsing on the logs all week."]],
 "Lock the gate behind you. The mill doesn't want to hear any speeches."],
17:["(The stacks of timber creak, and Bjarne puts out a big hand to steady one.) That's twice today. The ground. Stacks don't like it. I've had the lads re-stack the tall ones. A falling log's a bad way to go.",
 [
  ["Is it the volcano?","Steam off the old mountain, they say. My grandfather saw it go, as a boy. He said the trees in the north went down like matches."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not yet, and not on my floor. I've told the lads: when the ground shakes, step back from the saw and the stacks. Knut says he's never stepped back from anything. Knut's a fool."],
  ["Have you heard from Annika?","Another letter. (He pats his pocket.) She's right, as usual. I'm writing back. I'm telling her about the ground. Gently."]],
 "Stay clear of the tall stacks."],
18:["(Bjarne is counting a stack of planks, very slowly, while Dagny's voice carries from the other end of the mill.) Dagny's on the floor today. Counting the timber, same as me. She doesn't trust my counting. I don't trust hers.",
 [
  ["What's she counting for?","Money. She's selling a big load to Kelp Cola for their new warehouse. And the Democrats want a stage for their rally. She wants to charge Erik for every plank. Every plank!"],
  ["Will you charge Erik?","I'll charge Erik for every plank I count. Some planks I won't count. (He winks.) My eyes are getting bad, at my age."],
  ["Do you like Dagny?","I liked her when she was six, and sat on my shoulders, and her father laughed. I'd like to like her again. She doesn't make it easy."]],
 "Don't count the planks on your way out. It confuses me."],
19:["(Bjarne is up on the loading bay, shouting orders over the saw.) Rally stage, for Erik. Pine, the best we've got. I'm building it here and taking it to Kingsgrad in pieces on the twenty-seventh.",
 [
  ["How big will the stage be?","Big enough for Erik and a gramophone and Ruby and three other people who want to talk. Strong enough for Ilse to stand on if there's trouble."],
  ["Does Dagny know you're building it?","She knows about the planks she's counted. (He grins.) She doesn't know about the ones she hasn't."],
  ["Isn't the foreman meant to stay neutral?","The foreman's meant to cut timber. This is cutting timber. Very carefully. Very democratically."]],
 "Mind the stage parts. They're not for sitting on yet."],
20:["(Bjarne is sitting on a log with a cup of tea, staring at the trees in the forest beyond the yard.) The forest's quiet. No birds. Have you noticed? Birds always know before we do.",
 [
  ["Know what?","That something's coming. My grandfather said the birds left the island for a week before the mountain went, last time. He was a boy. He never forgot the silence."],
  ["Are you scared?","I'm watching. That's what you do with something big you can't stop. You watch it, and you keep your men out of its way."],
  ["What does Annika say?","She sends a letter every day now. They all say the same thing. 'Come home.' (He sighs.) This is home. That's the trouble with it."]],
 "Listen. No birds. Remember that."],
21:["(Bjarne is herding his crew out of the mill and into a lorry, with a voice like a foghorn.) The mountain's gone up! Everyone out! Off the floor and into the lorry, to the Community Centre! Leave the saw!",
 [
  ["Is everyone safe?","I've counted them twice. Knut, Ilse, every lad on the crew. Nobody hurt. Ash coming down in the north like snow. We're going to Erik's hall."],
  ["What about the mill?","The mill's been here eighty years. It'll be here tomorrow. Timber can wait. People can't."],
  ["Can I help?","Get in the lorry. And hold on. Ilse's driving. She drives the way she loads logs."]],
 "Lorry! Now! I mean it."],
22:["(Bjarne is standing in the mill yard, grey with ash, looking at the logs.) Every log grey. Every roof grey. The saw's full of it. But everyone's alive. I've written to Annika to tell her. First thing.",
 [
  ["How's the mill?","Ash in the gears, ash on the belts, ash on the timber. We'll be cleaning a week before the saw runs. But the walls are up and the roof's on. My father built that roof. It held."],
  ["What about the forest?","The north of the forest's grey and burnt. The south is just grey. Trees come back. That's the thing about trees. They're patient."],
  ["Will Dagny pay the men while it's shut?",["(He's quiet for a moment.) She hasn't said. I'll see they eat. I've never let a man go hungry. I'm not starting now.",[
    ["Out of your own pocket?","Out of my own pocket, out of the soup pot by the stove, out of Annika's Christmas money if I have to. A man who works for me eats."],
    ["Does Dagny know you do that?","No. And she never will, unless a reporter tells her. (He looks at you.) You won't."]]]]],
 "Mind the ash. It's thick in the yard."],
23:["(Bjarne is up a ladder, brushing ash off the mill's roof with a broom.) My father built this roof in 1949. It's held the snow and the wind and a volcano. Now it's got to hold me and a broom.",
 [
  ["Should you be up a ladder?","Probably not. My knees are telling me so. But Knut's up the other one, and I'm not having a sawyer outdo a foreman."],
  ["How are the lads?","Tired and grey and cheerful. Ilse's carrying sacks of ash like they're pillows. Knut's telling everyone he caught a lump of lava with his bare hand. He didn't."],
  ["Want me to hold the ladder?","(He looks down.) If you would. And if I fall, step aside. I'm a big man. I'd flatten you."]],
 "Thanks. Mind the ash coming down."],
24:["(Bjarne is at his desk, writing a letter in big careful handwriting.) Annika. I'm telling her I'm staying, at least till the vote. And that I've built a stage. She'll think I've gone mad.",
 [
  ["Have you?","A bit. (He chuckles.) Building stages for speeches at my age. My father would have laughed himself silly. Then he'd have built a bigger one."],
  ["Will you ever retire?","When the lads are paid fair and the saw's safe and the island's got a vote. Then I'll go to Turku and eat herring and be a grandfather. Not before."],
  ["Dex says you're building his cart.","Dex has a napkin with a drawing on it. I've said I'll look at the napkin. Dex heard 'I'll build you a cart'. Dex hears what he wants. It's a gift."]],
 "Post box is on the way. Drop this in for me?"],
25:["(Bjarne is stacking finished stage parts under a tarpaulin.) Stage is done, near enough. Erik came up to look at it today, in his good jacket. Said he was off to see a princess. I didn't ask.",
 [
  ["Does a princess matter to the vote?","Everybody matters to a vote. That's the point of it. Even princesses get one, I suppose. Even me."],
  ["How's the stage?","Pine, pegged and bolted. You could hold a dance on it. You could hold a war on it. I hope you do the first one."],
  ["What does Dagny think?","Dagny thinks I'm building a shed. She hasn't come close enough to look. I've put a door on one end, just in case."]],
 "Don't touch the tarpaulin. It's holding history."],
26:["(Bjarne is sharpening an axe on a whetstone, with long, slow strokes.) Dario's buying the hot springs. Kelp Cola's buying our timber. Everybody's buying something. The vote's about the only thing on the island that isn't for sale.",
 [
  ["Is the mill for sale?","(He stops sharpening.) Dagny's had a man from Diversified Petroleum in. Twice. She says it's about timber for the rig. I've kept the books for thirty years. I know a sale when I smell one."],
  ["What would happen to the men?","Oil men don't need sawyers. They need timber for a while, and then they don't. I'd be finding the lads new jobs, or none."],
  ["What are you sharpening that for?","For splitting firewood. Nothing else. (He smiles.) An axe is a peaceful thing, if you let it be."]],
 "Keep that one under your hat. I'm not sure of it yet."],
27:["(Bjarne is loading stage parts onto a lorry with Ilse, while Knut supervises from a log.) The stage is going to Kingsgrad. Ilse's driving. Knut's sitting on the back telling everyone where to put things. I'm doing all the work.",
 [
  ["How long to set it up?","Four hours, with the lads. Two hours with Ilse. Ilse doesn't stop for tea."],
  ["Will the rally go well?","I don't know. Nobody knows. There'll be a crowd, and people who want the crowd to go home. I've built the stage strong. That's all I can do."],
  ["You're proud of it.","(He pats the timber.) Best pine on the island. If it holds Erik, and Ilse, and Ruby's gramophone, it'll hold anything."]],
 "Stand back. Ilse's reversing."],
28:["(Bjarne is in his clean shirt again, with a hammer in his belt.) Rally day. The stage is up in the king's square. I'm standing under it with a hammer in case anything wobbles. Including Erik.",
 [
  ["How does the stage look?","Like it was built for a king. (He laughs.) That's a joke. It's built for everyone. It's built for a vote."],
  ["Are the lads coming?","Every one. Dagny's given them the day. I didn't even ask. She looked at me, and I looked at her, and we didn't say anything. Maybe she's getting her father's sense back."],
  ["Will you give a speech?","Me? (He looks horrified.) I'll hold the hammer. That's my speech. It says: this'll hold."]],
 "Go on, find a spot. I'll be under the stage."],
29:["(Bjarne is sitting on a log in the quiet mill, eating his lunch.) The saw's back on tomorrow, whatever's decided. Timber doesn't care who wins. I've always liked that about it.",
 [
  ["What's happening today?","Communists and Democrats being friendly. Helen came up to the mill and shook my hand. I shook hers. Neither of us let go first. We're both stubborn."],
  ["What do the lads think?","They think a vote would be grand. They think a rise would be grander. They think lunch should be longer. They're mill hands. They're always right about lunch."],
  ["What will you do after?","Write to Annika. Tell her who won, and what it means for her old dad. And tell her about the stage. She'll want to see a photograph. I'll look like a bear."]],
 "Sit, if you like. The log's comfortable."],
30:["(Bjarne is sweeping the empty mill, slowly, the way he does on Sundays.) The last day. I came in to sweep. I don't know what else to do with my hands.",
 [
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote, a fair wage, a guard on every saw. And the lads going home with ten fingers. That's all a foreman wants. Same as any father."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm sixty-two. I've seen a king crowned and a king buried and a mountain go up. I'm nervous. But I'm sweeping. Sweeping helps."],
  ["Thank you, Bjarne.",["(He leans on the broom.) What for? I've just been a big man with a pencil. (He smiles into his beard.) But thank you. It's nice to be asked.",[
    ["Will you go to Turku?","Maybe. For Christmas. To see the grandchildren and the drawing of the bear. Then back here. The saw won't mind itself."],
    ["What will you tell Annika?","That her old dad's still here, and he minded the saw, and he did his best. That's all any of us can say."]]]]],
 "Close the door gently. The mill's waiting too."],
};
// Bob, fishmonger at the fish processing plant in Saltwick (the Union, no faction). Threads: the suppers he cooks for Helen (and
// six years together), why he won't join the Party, the Union cookbook for the strike fund, Kate's gossip at the next bench,
// and the fish getting thinner and oilier.
DAILY['Bob']={
1:["(Bob is filleting a cod with quick, neat strokes.) Snow's off Saltwick and the boats are in. First proper fish in a fortnight. Smell that. No, really: that's what fresh smells like.",[
  ["What will you do with it?","Half goes to Olga's shop, half goes in the smoker, and the cheeks go home to Helen. Cod cheek's the best bit, and nobody knows it."],
  ["You're quick with that knife.","Twenty years of it. Kate's quicker. Don't tell her I said so; she'll want it in writing."],
  ["Is this the Communist end of town?","This is the fish end of town. I'm Union, not Party. Helen does the politics. I do the cod."]],
 "Mind the floor, it's slippery. Everything here is."],
2:["Kate says somebody from Kingsgrad's been asking after my old house. The road's been open a day, and Sally's already sending people.",[
  ["You're selling your house?","Since I moved in with Helen. It's sat empty a good while. Sally at the estate agent's in Kingsgrad has it on her books."],
  ["Who's asking?","Kate didn't know, which means it's somebody very mysterious. Kate knows everything."],
  ["Will you miss it?","The stove. It's a good stove. Helen's flat is better in every other way, mostly because Helen's in it."]],
 "If you want a small house with a good stove, see Sally."],
3:["Helen's meeting ran till midnight, so I made fish soup at eleven and kept it on a low flame. It was perfect at five past twelve.",[
  ["What goes in your fish soup?","Cod, potato, leek, a bay leaf, and cream if Olga's got any. And patience. Mostly patience."],
  ["Does she appreciate it?","She ate two bowls without a word. That's appreciation, from Helen."],
  ["Why not go to her meetings?","Somebody has to keep the soup on. That's my contribution to the struggle."]],
 "Off you go. These fish won't fillet themselves."],
4:["Kate's been telling me the Eastbrook gossip since the road opened at dawn. I don't know how. Nobody from here's been to Eastbrook yet.",[
  ["How does Kate know?","The milk boat. The radio. And something else she won't tell me about. I suspect witchcraft."],
  ["What's the gossip?","That Erik's been snowed in with forty pounds of coffee and has drunk most of it. Don't print that; she'll want paying."],
  ["Do you gossip too?","I listen. Kate talks, I listen, and the fish get filleted. We've got a system."]],
 "Ask Kate yourself. She's at the next bench, pretending not to hear."],
5:["(Bob wipes his hands.) You'll have heard I'm the man who won't join the Party. Everybody tells everybody everything in Saltwick.",[
  ["Why won't you?",["I joined the Union at sixteen because the plant was killing people. That was a reason. I've not found another one yet.",[
    ["Not even Helen?","Helen's a reason to cook. Not a reason to join. She knows the difference; it's the others who don't."],
    ["What would change your mind?","If Anna ever asked me to fillet something, I'd think about it."]]]],
  ["Do the comrades mind?","Some of them look at me at meetings like I'm a fish that's walked in. I bring the soup, and they forgive me for an hour."],
  ["Do politics bore you?","Politics are fine. Meetings are the trouble. A meeting is a soup that never comes to the boil."]],
 "Right. Back to the bench."],
6:["Lucia from the Syndicate came round the plant with her little book. Kate told her where to put it. In those words. It was quite something.",[
  ["What happened?","Lucia smiled, wrote something down and left. That smile's worse than the book. Kate's pretending she isn't worried."],
  ["Does the plant pay the Syndicate?","The plant doesn't. Some of the boats do. That's between them and their nets."],
  ["Kate's brave.","Kate's loud. On a good day that comes to the same thing."]],
 "If you see Lucia, don't mention Kate."],
7:["Helen came home with a crate of oranges from some Russian in Frostholm, so I've made marmalade. Forty jars.",[
  ["Forty jars?","There were a lot of oranges. Everyone in the block's getting a jar. Kaisa's getting two; she plays my requests."],
  ["Who's the Russian?","Timber man at the Hotel Aurora. Yuri. Very generous. I've never trusted generous. Helen says that's my best quality."],
  ["Do you like marmalade?","Can't stand it. But boiling it kept the flat warm all night."]],
 "Take a jar. Otherwise Helen will make me bring three in to work."],
8:["Sunday roast: cod. And the cod's thin this year, thinner than last. Tom says the rig's frightening them off.",[
  ["Do you believe him?","Tom's never wrong about fish. About the weather and girls, constantly. Never about fish."],
  ["I hear something's gone wrong at the rig.","The boats are saying there's black water past the point. If that gets into the harbour, I'll be filleting oil for a month."],
  ["What's the secret of roast cod?","Butter, a lemon if you can get one, and don't touch it. Everybody touches it. Leave it alone."]],
 "Leave it alone. That's the whole secret, for most things."],
9:["Helen's at the HQ trying to invent a cola. She came home last night smelling of liquorice and gave me a cup of something brown.",[
  ["Was it good?","It tasted like cough syrup that had given up. I told her it was promising. Six years together: you learn things."],
  ["Could you invent a flavour?","I'd make it taste of smoked fish, and it'd sell to three people in Saltwick. Kate, Sam and me."],
  ["A Communist cola? Really?","Why not? Phil's got rich off people being thirsty for years. Let somebody else have a go."]],
 "If it smells of liquorice again, I'm hiding the kettle."],
10:["Helen's minding the HQ and Anna's at the funeral. I packed Helen herring on rye and folded the paper three times.",[
  ["You're not going to the funeral?","Me? I'd stand there in the wrong coat, thinking about lunch. The king deserves better mourners than me."],
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","Saw him once on the quay. He tasted a smoked herring and said 'marvellous'. We had it painted on the smokehouse."],
  ["Why fold it three times?","Once to keep the onion in, once to keep the warm in, and once so she knows it's from me."]],
 "Raise a glass to the old man later. Or a herring."],
11:["Helen's got her mind on the mill in Eastbrook. She talked about saw guards in her sleep.",[
  ["In her sleep?","'Put the guard back, Dagny.' Clear as anything. I said 'yes, dear', and she said 'thank you, comrade'."],
  ["What do you make of the mill?","A man should go home with the same number of fingers he went to work with. That's the whole of my politics."],
  ["Do you talk in your sleep?","Helen says I recite recipes. Last week it was dumplings, all the way through."]],
 "Let me get on. Kate's watching the clock for me."],
12:["(Bob is writing in a school exercise book, licking the pencil.) Recipes. The Union's doing a cookbook, to raise money for the strike fund. Guess who's writing it.",[
  ["What's in it so far?","Fish soup, herring three ways, Kate's pickled beetroot, and Sam's 'potatoes', which is just potatoes."],
  ["Who'll buy it?","Every wife, husband and mother in Saltwick, because their recipe's in it and they'll want to check I got it right."],
  ["Can I give you a recipe?","A London one? Go on. If it's beans on toast, I'm putting your name on it."]],
 "Page eleven's still free. Think about it."],
13:["Kate's gone round the plant with a sack, collecting everybody's free Kelp Cola from Kingsgrad. She's posting it back to Phil. Return to sender.",[
  ["Will she really?","She's bought the stamps. Kate never buys stamps for a joke."],
  ["Did you keep yours?","I'm using it to clean the scales off the knives. Works a treat. Don't tell Phil."],
  ["Kelp Cola's not so bad.","It's got seaweed in it. I gut fish all day. I don't need my drink tasting of the harbour."]],
 "If a sack of cola turns up at Phil's, you didn't hear it from me."],
14:["The big day. Helen's in her good blouse, and I'm in charge of sandwiches. Thirty of them, cut corner to corner, which is posher.",[
  ["Will you make a speech?","I'll speak to the sandwiches. Helen does the speaking, I do the feeding. Everybody's happier."],
  ["What are you hoping for?","That everybody comes home, nobody fights, and I get a sandwich myself. I never do."],
  ["Corner to corner is posher?","Triangles are for occasions. Squares are for Tuesdays. Everybody knows that."]],
 "If you're hungry later, I'll have one hidden."],
15:["The soup committee met about the cookbook. Two hours. We agreed on the cover.",[
  ["What's on the cover?","A fish. Kate wanted a red fish, I wanted an ordinary fish. We've gone with a fish holding a red flag. Nobody's happy, so it's fair."],
  ["Two hours on a cover?","That's quick for us. The Party took three meetings to choose a teapot."],
  ["Is it a Party cookbook?","A Union one. That's a difference that matters to me and nobody else. Helen understands."]],
 "The first copy's yours, if it ever gets printed."],
16:["Debate day. Helen's written Anna six pages. I've filled Anna a flask of soup. I know which she'll finish.",[
  ["Will you watch?","From the back, with the flask. Kate's in the front row with a whistle. I've asked her not to."],
  ["Who do you want to win?","Anna, because I live with Helen. But I'll clap for Erik. He's always been decent to the mill lads."],
  ["What's in the flask?","Pea and ham. Nobody ever lost an argument on pea and ham."]],
 "If anybody throws anything, I'm standing behind Sam."],
17:["The ground shook and every knife in the plant jumped off the bench. Kate caught two in the air. Nobody lost a toe, which is the main thing.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Nobody. Kate swore so loudly I think the tremor stopped out of shame."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Everybody says so. My granddad used to say the mountain had a cold. Looks like it's coming down with something."],
  ["How was the debate?","Anna was Anna. Erik was Erik. The soup went before the arguments did. That's all I'll say."]],
 "Keep your feet under you. The floor's got ideas."],
18:["Helen got a typed letter from that Russian. I noticed the 'e's all lean to the left. Helen says I notice things. I say I fillet things; it's the same skill.",[
  ["What else did you notice?","The paper smelled of cigarettes and hotel soap. A timber man's letters ought to smell of a sawmill."],
  ["Did Helen answer?","By hand, slowly, with crossings-out. That's how Helen says no to somebody important."],
  ["Filleting and noticing are the same?","You look for the bones. Every letter's got bones. So's every man."]],
 "Back to the bones."],
19:["Helen's trying to sign me up again. It's the Party's big recruiting day, and she brought her clipboard to breakfast.",[
  ["Will you sign?","I made her eggs Benedict. She forgot the clipboard. That's six years running."],
  ["Eggs Benedict in Saltwick?","With smoked herring for the ham. Eggs Bob, Kate calls them. They're going in the cookbook."],
  ["Isn't that unfair on Helen?",["Maybe. (He puts the knife down.) But she'd stop respecting me if I signed for her sake. She told me so once, by accident.",[
    ["By accident?","After a long meeting and a glass of Yuri's vodka. 'Never sign for me, Bob.' Then she fell asleep in her chair."],
    ["That's quite a thing to say.","She's quite a woman. Don't tell her I said so. She'll call a meeting about it."]]]]],
 "If Anna asks, I'm still thinking about it."],
20:["Nora from the Gazette was in Saltwick asking questions. Kate gave her an hour of gossip, and not one word of it was true.",[
  ["Why lie to Nora?","Kate says the Gazette's Democrat. If they want Saltwick's secrets, they can come and gut fish for them."],
  ["What did Kate tell her?","That Sam was once married to a mermaid. Nora wrote it down. We'll see if it's in the paper."],
  ["What was Nora asking about?","The Russian at the Hotel Aurora. Who he sees, what he buys. I said oranges, mostly."]],
 "If you read about a mermaid in the Gazette, it was Kate."],
21:["(Bob is stirring an enormous pot.) Volcano soup. Forty bowls for the block. If the world's ending, it isn't ending hungry.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","Frightened and coughing, but here. Helen's counting heads, I'm counting bowls. Between us we'll get everybody."],
  ["What kind of soup?","Whatever was in the cupboards. Potato, cabbage, a smoked haddock and Kaisa's onions. It's surprisingly good."],
  ["Did you see it go up?","From the roof. A red line up north and the whole sky going grey. I've never seen anything like it, and I don't want to again."]],
 "Take a bowl. You look like you need one."],
22:["The plant's shut while they get the ash out of the water tanks. My first weekday off since 1970, and I've no idea what to do with my hands.",[
  ["Cook?","I am cooking. I've cooked everything in the flat twice. I've started on Kate's cupboards."],
  ["Will you get paid?","Hardship money from the Union's biscuit tin. Helen won't let me take mine; she says I'll only spend it on onions. She's right."],
  ["Try resting.","Resting. (He tries it, briefly.) No. No good. Give me a fish."]],
 "If you see a fish, send it my way."],
23:["Back at the bench. Half the fish off the boats this week have oil on the skin. We cut it off and throw it away. Waste.",[
  ["Oil from the rig?","Where else? Tom's scrubbed the Gull three times. Sam's stopped speaking altogether, and he'd barely started."],
  ["Can you sell the rest?","Olga takes what's clean. Olga's fussy. Good: somebody on this island should be."],
  ["What would you do about the rig?","I'd make Dario eat a cod caught off it. Raw. He'd shut it down by teatime."]],
 "Mind the bins on the way out. They're the oily ones."],
24:["Six years in the flat with Helen. Kate reminded me. I made a cake and dusted it grey with cocoa, to match the island.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you. It feels like a fortnight and a century at once. That's how you know it's right."],
  ["How did you meet?",["A strike, in 1976. She was on a crate shouting; I was handing out tea. She came over for a cup and stayed for the biscuits.",[
    ["Romantic.","It was February and raining. Very romantic. My hands were blue."],
    ["Did you join the strike?","Of course. I'm Union. I just didn't join the Party, and she's held it against me fondly ever since."]]]],
  ["Does Helen cook?","She once boiled an egg so long it bounced. She's brilliant at everything else."]],
 "Right. Cake, then work. In that order."],
25:["The cookbook proof's back from the printer in Kingsgrad. They've spelt 'herring' with one R on the cover. Kate says it's a book about hearing now.",[
  ["Can it be fixed?","By hand, with a pen, on two hundred copies. Kate's volunteered Sam. He hasn't said no. He hasn't said anything."],
  ["Is it selling?","It's not out yet, but there's a waiting list already, and every name on it has a recipe inside."],
  ["One R. Unlucky.","It's that sort of month. A volcano, ash, and now spelling. We'll survive."]],
 "Two hundred Rs. Wish Sam luck."],
26:["Helen's on the telephone to every shop steward on the island. I've been bringing her tea and listening to her say 'just checking'.",[
  ["Checking what?","That they're all still there. She does it before something big. Last time, the harbour shut for nine days."],
  ["Are you worried?","Not about Helen. About the tea. She's on her eleventh cup."],
  ["Is a strike coming?","Ask her. I'm the man with the teapot. The teapot's not on the committee."]],
 "If she asks, I'm boiling the kettle."],
27:["Helen's off to the pictures in Kingsgrad with the bottling-line girls. A Soviet film. I've packed her a sandwich so she's something to do with her hands.",[
  ["Will you go?","I'd fall asleep at the first tractor. I'll wait for STORMWING. Gunnar says there's a robot in it."],
  ["What sandwich?","Egg and cress. A quiet sandwich. You can't eat a crunchy one in a Soviet film; the whole row turns round."],
  ["Any news of the cookbook?","Sam's corrected sixty covers. He's added an R to 'cod' on three of them. I've let it go."]],
 "Off you go. I'll keep the egg and cress warm. Not literally."],
28:["The twenty-eighth. Helen left before it was light, with a boiled egg in a tea towel. Whatever's going on today, she's in the middle of it.",[
  ["What's going on?","She won't tell me, and that's fair: I'd tell Kate, and Kate would tell the island."],
  ["Are you worried for her?","Always, a bit. But I've watched her walk into rooms full of angry men for six years, and she always walks out."],
  ["What are you doing today?","Filleting. Revolutions, coronations, whatever: people still want their supper."]],
 "Look after yourself out there. It's a funny day."],
29:["Helen came home late and fell asleep with her boots on. I took them off, and she said 'not now, Dagny' in her sleep.",[
  ["What's Dagny got to do with it?","Nothing, these days, I hope. Some fights just stay in your head, and the mill one's stayed in hers."],
  ["Are you two all right?","We're tired. That's all right. Tired together beats rested alone. That's going on the back of the cookbook."],
  ["Big day tomorrow.","The last one. I've made a stew that'll keep three days, just in case. In case of what, I don't know. In case."]],
 "Shut the door gently on your way out."],
30:["The last day. Whatever's decided, I'll fillet fish tomorrow, Helen will shout at somebody, and the stew will still be good.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm hungry, which is close. Hopeful is Helen's department."],
  ["Will you ever join the Party?","(He smiles.) Ask me tomorrow. Tomorrow's always been my answer, and I'm sticking to it."],
  ["What's in the stew?","Everything we had left. It's called 'end of the month'. It's a good one."]],
 "Go on. And come back for a bowl."],
};
// Britta, depot worker for Diversified Petroleum in Frostholm, lives in the Skyline Apartments (Diversified Petroleum). Threads:
// Gertrud, the depot's ancient forklift; her weariness with the executives' grand plans; her neighbours (Ruth and her westerns next
// door, Magnus and his cello upstairs); whether the depot crew ought to join Helen's union.
DAILY['Britta']={
7:["(Britta is kicking the wheel of an old yellow forklift.) Come on, Gertrud. Come on. (She looks up.) A week snowed in, and she won't start. Neither would I, at her age.",[
  ["Gertrud?","The forklift. Built in 1951, rebuilt in 1963, sworn at daily since. She lifts four drums at a time when she's in the mood."],
  ["What do you do here?","Load drums, fill tankers, keep the depot running in all weathers. Dario makes speeches about the future. I make sure there's diesel for it."],
  ["Want me to push?","(She laughs.) Gertrud weighs four tons. But it's a kind thought. Put your hand on her bonnet; she likes company."]],
 "Mind the drums by the gate. They're full, and they're not friendly."],
8:["(Britta is rolling a drum across the yard with her boot.) Something's up at the rig. Dario's been on the phone shouting, and they've sent for empty drums. Lots of them. You only want empties for one thing.",[
  ["What's that?","Catching what's leaked. Don't quote me, I'm only the depot."],
  ["Does this happen often?","Often enough that I keep a stack of empties by the gate, just in case. Nobody told me to. Nobody needed to."],
  ["You don't seem surprised.","I've worked for oil men for nine years. The only thing that surprises me is a thank-you."]],
 "If you're going out to the rig, take a coat. The wind out there's got teeth."],
9:["(Britta is rubbing the small of her back.) Gertrud lifted, I didn't. That's the deal, and Gertrud broke the deal at four o'clock. Eight drums by hand.",[
  ["Your back must be killing you.","My back's been killing me since 1977. It's taking its time about it."],
  ["Can't the company fix the forklift?","I've put in for a new one four times. Magnus sent back a memo about 'capital expenditure priorities'. Which means his campaign posters."],
  ["Why not make Magnus lift them?","(She grins.) I'd sell tickets. He'd fold like a deckchair."]],
 "Hot water bottle and an early night. That's my whole evening planned."],
10:["(Britta is eating a sandwich on an upturned drum.) They're burying the king today, and the bosses are all off in their black suits. So the depot's quiet. Best funeral I've ever had.",[
  ["Not sad about the king?","He seemed a decent old stick. But I never met him, and he never once needed his diesel delivering in a blizzard. I'll save my tears for people I've lifted things for."],
  ["Who's minding the shop?","Me and Gertrud, and the cat that lives behind the oil tanks. She's the only one here with any sense."],
  ["Is that a good sandwich?","Herring and onion. Ruth next door won't eat lunch with me on herring days. More for me."]],
 "Quiet days are rare. Don't tell anyone I liked it."],
11:["(Britta has a union leaflet in her overall pocket, and pushes it down when she sees you.) Nothing. A bit of paper. (She sighs.) Helen's lot are at the lumber mill in Eastbrook today, about pay.",[
  ["Are you thinking of joining the union?","Thinking's free. The mill hands and the cola girls have got Helen. Who've the depot crew got? Magnus, with a memo."],
  ["What would Dario say?","Dario would say he's a friend to the working man, and then he'd have Magnus fire the working man. Politely, with a form."],
  ["Is that a Communist leaflet?","It's a union leaflet. Helen's not daft: she knows half the depot would sooner swim to Finland than join the Party."]],
 "Don't put my name in your paper next to that leaflet."],
12:["(Britta is wiping her hands on a rag.) Busy day up at the HQ. Papers going up and down. Magnus walked past the depot twice, looking like a man with a secret in his briefcase.",[
  ["What sort of secret?","With Magnus, it'll be a sum. He doesn't do any other kind."],
  ["Are you close to Ruth, next door?","Close as two walls. I know every western she owns, because I hear them through the plaster. Bang, bang, yee-haw, at ten at night."],
  ["Do you like cowboy films?","I fall asleep in the gunfights. Ruth's never forgiven me. Something about the rhythm of it."]],
 "Tell Ruth I'll see her at the cinema on Saturday. I'll try to stay awake."],
13:["(Britta has a Kelp Cola bottle and is peering at it.) Sven brought a crate of the free stuff from Kingsgrad. I'm told not to drink it on company property, so I'm drinking it on Gertrud. Gertrud's not property, she's family.",[
  ["How is it?","Sweet. Fizzy. Like being shouted at by a seaside postcard. I'll have another."],
  ["Would Dario mind?","Dario minds everything that isn't his. I've stopped minding that he minds."],
  ["Is Gertrud running?","She ran for an hour earlier and then sighed and stopped. I know how she feels."]],
 "Don't tell the office. They'll want a memo about it."],
14:["(Britta has her good coat on over her overalls.) The big day. The whole island together. I'm going, mainly to see whether the bosses can stand in one place for an hour without making a speech.",[
  ["What do you expect from it?","Long speeches, cold feet, and somebody's hat in the sea. The usual island do."],
  ["Who are you hoping to see?","My sister from Saltwick. She married a fisherman and smells of herring permanently. I'll stand downwind."],
  ["You look smart.","It's the only good coat I've got. It's been to two weddings, a christening and a court case. Not mine."]],
 "Somebody's got to keep the diesel moving. I'll be back by four."],
15:["(Britta is trying to get Gertrud's bonnet open with a crowbar.) Prince or priest? Everybody's asking me. I keep saying, whichever one can fix a forklift.",[
  ["Neither of them could fix a forklift.","Then I'll go on not caring. It's very restful."],
  ["What's wrong with Gertrud now?","She's got a noise. A sort of sad clank, on the left. I've asked Nils at the electronics store. He said he does radios."],
  ["Could I have a look?","You're a reporter. You'd write a lovely description of the engine and then step back. That's fine. That's more than Magnus has done."]],
 "Clank, clank. That's her talking. I'll work out what she means."],
16:["(Britta has the radio on in the depot hut.) The Great Debate. The Communists and the Democrats, shouting at each other all over the island. Even Gertrud's listening.",[
  ["Who's making more sense?","Helen made a good point about pay. Then Anna made a speech about Moscow, and I lost interest. Nobody here wants to be Russian."],
  ["Do you vote, Britta?","There's never been anything to vote for. If there ever is, I'll vote for whoever's done a day's lifting."],
  ["Bet Dario hates this.","He hates anything he can't buy. You can't buy a radio debate. Though I wouldn't put it past him to try."]],
 "Leave the radio on. Gertrud likes the Democrats; they're very soothing."],
17:["(Britta is leaning on Gertrud, watching the HQ windows.) Nobody's seen Dario all day. The office is in an uproar. Magnus has asked me twice if Dario's car has been in for fuel.",[
  ["Has it?","Not since Thursday. Nobody fills that car but me, and I'd know. It's very thirsty and very red."],
  ["Where do you think he is?","Somewhere with a bottle. He's done it before. The difference is, today the whole building's frightened."],
  ["Why frightened?","Because the Syndicate's been calling, and Ruth's been writing down names, and Magnus has gone the colour of an empty drum."]],
 "If you see a red car, tell him the depot wants him back. Nobody else will say it nicely."],
18:["(Britta has steadied a stack of drums with both arms.) That shake knocked the whole top row over. Gertrud didn't even blink. She's been through worse.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Only my pride and the cat's dignity. She's gone under the tanks and won't come out."],
  ["Is the volcano going to go?","Ole says the steam's thicker every day, up north. Ole doesn't say things unless they're in a logbook."],
  ["Should you be stacking drums in an earthquake?","Probably not. But the drums don't know there's an earthquake, and the tankers still want filling."]],
 "If it shakes again, come and stand by Gertrud. She's never fallen over once."],
19:["(Britta is laughing at something.) Ruth's been at my door at midnight, saying somebody royal's been sneaking into the Midnight Note in a false moustache. She wants us to go and look.",[
  ["Will you go?","Not at midnight. I'm up at five for the drums. Royals can sneak about all they like after nine o'clock."],
  ["Who told her?","Rita, the singer there. She lives in the Skyline too. The whole building is one big telephone."],
  ["What's the Skyline like to live in?","Thin walls, good views and a lift that stops between floors when it's moody. Like Gertrud, but taller."]],
 "If the prince comes in for diesel, I'll let you know."],
20:["(Britta is reading a union leaflet openly now.) I've been talking to the others. Three of us would join Helen's union. Two wouldn't. One would if there was free soup.",[
  ["Is there free soup?","There's always free soup at the union. That's how they get you. It's very good soup."],
  ["Will you tell the bosses?","Not yet. You don't tell the bosses anything until there's enough of you to be worth sacking all at once."],
  ["Who's the one who wouldn't?","Lars, from the rig, when he's ashore. He says he's too well paid to have principles. He's right, which is the sad part."]],
 "Fold that leaflet away, if you've got one. Paper gets around."],
21:["(Britta is soot-grey from head to foot, hosing down a tanker.) The mountain's gone up! Lava up north and ash coming down like snow. The tanks are fine. I've checked them all, twice.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Gertrud's fine. The cat's under the tanks again. Everybody's where they should be."],
  ["What can be done?","Keep the generators fuelled, keep the roads clear, keep the hose going. That's what the depot's for, when it isn't for Dario."],
  ["Did you check on Ruth?","First thing. Swept her balcony, too, because she was too shaken to do it. She'd do the same for me. Probably with a lasso."]],
 "Cover your mouth when you go out. The ash is in everything."],
22:["(Britta is filling jerry cans for a queue of people.) Every generator in Frostholm wants feeding, and Dario's got the trucks washing down the streets for the Gazette's photographer. Guess who's filling all of it.",[
  ["You must be exhausted.","Past exhausted. Out the other side, where it goes quiet and you can't feel your feet."],
  ["Is Dario paying overtime?","He says 'loyalty is its own reward'. I said so is overtime. He laughed. I've no idea what that means for my wages."],
  ["Has Gertrud survived?","Gertrud is the hero of the eruption. She started first time today. First time in a month. I nearly cried."]],
 "Next in the queue, please. Hold your can steady."],
23:["(Britta is watching the HQ with her arms folded.) Something's gone wrong under the water at the rig. They've had me load a welding rig onto the boat, and Dario's not speaking to anyone.",[
  ["What broke?","Nobody tells the depot what broke. They tell us what to load, and we work it out. Today it's welding gear and divers' tanks. So: something deep."],
  ["Could it be the shaking?","Could be the shaking. Could be old pipe. Could be somebody with a spanner. I've heard all three by lunch."],
  ["Is it dangerous out there?","Einar always says the rig only stays up because it's too stubborn to fall over. Most days I think he was joking."]],
 "If you're going out to the rig, go with the welders. They've got the good coats."],
24:["(Britta is sitting on Gertrud, eating soup out of a flask.) Union soup. Helen's people brought it round. Very good soup. I've told you, that's how they get you.",[
  ["Have they got you, then?","I've signed nothing. I've just eaten the soup. A woman can eat soup without changing her politics."],
  ["How are the depot crew holding up?","Tired, grey and still here. Every one of us came in after the eruption. Nobody had to ask."],
  ["What would the union change?","A new forklift for one. Safety boots that fit. And a thank-you, once a year. In writing, so we can frame it."]],
 "Pass me that spoon. Thank you. That's one thank-you more than this month's."],
25:["(Britta is holding a broom and glaring at the ceiling of the depot hut.) Magnus was playing his cello again last night, in the flat above mine. The slow bits. For two hours. I banged the ceiling with this broom.",[
  ["Is he any good?","He's very regular. He plays the same mistake in the same place every time. You could set a clock by it."],
  ["Does he stop when you bang?","He plays slower. I think he thinks it's applause."],
  ["Maybe it helps him think.","Then he should think quieter. I've drums at six."]],
 "If you see Magnus, tell him the depot asks for less Bach. Nicely."],
26:["(Britta is shaking her head.) Dario's gone up the mountain to buy the spa. The spa, on the volcano. And I put in for a new forklift tyre last week and got a memo.",[
  ["What did the memo say?","'Under consideration.' It's been under consideration since 1979. It's very well considered by now."],
  ["Would you use the spa?","If Dario owned it, I'd have to pay to get in, and then he'd watch me float. No thank you."],
  ["Why does he want it?","Grand plans. They're always grand, and they're never for us. I'd settle for a small plan with a forklift in it."]],
 "Grand plans. I'll be here, lifting them."],
27:["(Britta is loading crates of milk and ice cream into a van.) Dario's office party is tomorrow. Milkshakes and bowling for everyone. I'm told the depot crew are invited, which is how I know he wants something.",[
  ["What does he want?","A crowd. Somebody to clap. Votes for Magnus at the Republicans, probably. Milkshakes are cheaper than wages."],
  ["Will you go?","Of course I'll go. Free milkshakes. I'm tired, not daft."],
  ["Can you bowl?","I can lift a bowling ball higher than anyone in that building. Whether it goes where I want is between me and God."]],
 "Strawberry, if anyone asks what I want. They won't."],
28:["(Britta has a paper hat on, and a chocolate milkshake.) Party day, and the whole island's out pushing at once. The Communists in Kingsgrad, the Church, the Cola lot. I'm pushing a milkshake.",[
  ["Having a good time?","Surprisingly. Ruth's dressed as a train robber, Lars bowled the ball the wrong way, and Magnus has undone a button. It's like the circus came to Frostholm."],
  ["Heard anything worth knowing?","The bosses are drinking, and drink talks. I'm keeping my ears open and my mouth full of milkshake."],
  ["Did you get your forklift?","(She laughs.) I asked Dario between frames. He said 'Ask me tomorrow.' I've heard that before."]],
 "Pass the straws. It's the only day off I've had this month."],
29:["(Britta is back in her overalls, loading drums.) The Republicans are voting today, and Magnus left in his good suit. The whole floor heard him practising at six. Forty-three cards.",[
  ["Do you hope he wins?","If he wins, he'll be too busy to play the cello. That's my only politics."],
  ["Who else is standing?","Harald from Kelp Cola, and Carlo, Gustavo's man. A cello, a cola and a crook. I'll take the cello, for the quiet."],
  ["Will anything change for the depot?","Whoever wins, somebody's still got to load the drums. That's the one thing on this island nobody argues about."]],
 "Back to work. The drums don't vote."],
30:["(Britta is sitting on Gertrud with the engine off, just looking at the sea.) The last day. Everyone says today's the day it's all decided. I came in anyway. Somebody's got to keep the diesel moving.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A boss who says thank you. A forklift that starts. A union card in my pocket, maybe. Small things. They add up."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Not for me. For the island. It's a stubborn old place, like Gertrud. I want it to keep going."],
  ["Gertrud's been quiet today.","She knows. She's very sensitive to atmosphere. (She pats the bonnet.) We'll be all right, old girl."]],
 "Whatever happens, the depot opens at six tomorrow. It always does."],
};
// Brother Anselm, Prior of the Mountain-Top Monastery (Orthodox Church). Threads: his visitors' ledger and his suspicion of
// everything from the towns, the discipline of the brothers (Mikael at the wall, Teodor's portions, Ilja's late candle), his
// quiet quarrel with Father Thyme over whether Augustus belongs on a throne or in a cell, and the mountain fox he feeds and denies.
DAILY['Brother Anselm']={
5:["(Brother Anselm opens a ledger on a lectern by the gate and dips his pen.) Name, town, and business on the mountain. Everyone who comes through this gate goes in the book.",[
  ["Is that really necessary?","Since the king died, everyone who climbs this path wants something. The book doesn't stop them wanting. It helps me remember what."],
  ["I'm a reporter, from London.","(He writes it down slowly.) Reporter. London. Business: questions. You'll be the first from London in the book. Don't make me regret the ink."],
  ["Put me down as 'pilgrim'.","I'll put you down as 'reporter, claims pilgrim'. Pilgrims don't carry notebooks."]],
 "Keep to the path. The book will know if you don't."],
6:["(Brother Anselm is watching a figure on the wall at the far end of the yard.) Brother Mikael. He stands there every night, looking down at Kingsgrad, and tells me he's admiring the stars. The stars are the other way.",[
  ["What's he looking for?","Whatever he left behind. A monk who keeps looking down the mountain hasn't finished climbing it."],
  ["Will you speak to him?","I speak to him every day. He says, 'Yes, Brother Prior,' and goes back to the wall. Some things only God can call a man away from."],
  ["Maybe he likes the view.","Nobody likes a view that much. I've seen it. It's mostly chimneys."]],
 "Vespers is at six. Be gone by then, or be in a pew."],
7:["(Brother Anselm is writing in the ledger, his mouth a thin line.) 'Diversified Petroleum. Business: roof.' A man in a fur collar, offering to mend our church for nothing. The devil does not usually do roofing, Father Thyme says. I'm not sure the devil has been asked.",[
  ["The roof does leak, doesn't it?","On the icons of Saint Nikolai and Saint Anna. Every winter. I've caught the drips in a basin for twenty years. A basin is honest."],
  ["What's wrong with free?","Nothing is free that comes up this hill in a car with a heater. When the roof's done, there will be a bill, and it won't be in money."],
  ["Will Father Thyme accept?","Father Thyme believes the best of everyone. That is why God gave him a prior."]],
 "Close the gate behind you. The wind gets ideas."],
8:["(Brother Anselm is straightening the hymn books in the church, very hard.) The Abbot has given his pulpit to a journalist. On the first Sunday without a king. I have said what I think, and now I am saying nothing, loudly.",[
  ["You don't approve?","The pulpit is for the Word of God. Not for the word of the Times, or whichever paper you write for."],
  ["Do you think I'll do it badly?","I think you'll do it the way the world does everything: with confidence. Confidence is not grace. We'll see which you've brought."],
  ["What would you preach?","That kings die and God doesn't. That would take three minutes, and the farmers would be home for lunch."]],
 "Sit up straight. The island will be watching."],
9:["(You come round the kitchen corner and find Brother Anselm crouched by the back step with a crust of bread. He stands up very quickly.) It's for the birds.",[
  ["That was a fox.","It was a bird. A large, red, four-legged bird. You'll not write about it."],
  ["Does the fox have a name?","Foxes don't have names. That one is called Nobody, because nobody feeds it. Brother Teodor would give it the whole kitchen."],
  ["Your secret's safe.",["Good. A prior with a pet is a prior with a weakness, and the brothers would have it out of me by Lent.",[
    ["Why the fox?","It was starving in the snow two winters ago, and it looked at me the way the novices do. I couldn't refuse it any more than I can refuse them."],
    ["Everyone needs a weakness.","So the Abbot tells me. His is everyone. Mine is a fox. I think mine is cheaper."]]]]],
 "Not a word. Not to Brother Teodor. Not to anyone."],
10:["(Brother Anselm has a heavy coat on over his habit, and the keys of the monastery van in his hand.) The king's funeral. I'm driving the Abbot down. All the gold and velvet of the palace, and a man underneath it who's gone to God with nothing in his pockets, like the rest of us.",[
  ["You're not fond of the palace.","I'm fond of the dead king's soul. I'm not fond of a chapel with heating and a choir of footmen. The palace prefers God in a frame."],
  ["Will the Church have a place of honour?","The Abbot will say the prayers. Then he'll stand to the side while Leopold is looked at. That's the place of honour, in a palace."],
  ["Drive carefully.","I always drive carefully. The van does not. It has a will of its own, and a rattle in the left door like a demon."]],
 "Stand when they carry him in. Some of them won't."],
11:["(Brother Anselm is counting blankets onto a handcart, his lips moving.) Forty-one. The Abbot wants to give them to the poor. I want to know which poor, and whether they'll be sold in Saltwick by Thursday.",[
  ["Isn't that a hard thing to say?","Charity with its eyes shut isn't charity. It's laziness with a halo. I'll give a man a blanket. I'd like to see him under it."],
  ["The Abbot's sending help to Pat, on the beach.","I know. I've met Pat. He told me where I could put my hymn book. I've prayed for him every night since. It's the least, and the most, I can do."],
  ["You're kinder than you let on.","Don't tell the novices. They're frightened of me, and it's the only thing keeping them in bed by nine."]],
 "Forty-one blankets. If you see one in a shop, I want to hear of it."],
12:["(Brother Anselm is stood at the foot of the stairs to the monks' cells, his head on one side.) Brother Ilja's candle was burning until two this morning, and I could hear him murmuring through the wall. Praying, I suppose. In rhythm.",[
  ["In rhythm?","Like plainchant, but faster. Da, da-da, da. He taps his foot. I didn't know prayer had a foot in it."],
  ["Will you tell him off?","For praying too much? I'd be the first prior in history. I'll tell him to use less candle. Wax costs money."],
  ["Maybe he's writing.","At two in the morning, with his eyes? He can barely see by day. If he's writing, he's writing in the dark. Which would explain his handwriting."]],
 "Go gently on the stairs. The brothers are resting."],
13:["(Four crates of Kelp Cola sit under the altar steps with a red ribbon on them. Brother Anselm is glaring at them.) Free samples. With compliments. And a picture of a cup with eyes. In the house of God.",[
  ["Will you throw them away?","Waste is a sin. Vanity is a sin. A cup with eyes is, I suspect, a sin. I'm weighing them against each other."],
  ["Is cola really so bad?","It's a drink that tastes of the sea, sold by a man who's never been on it. The brothers drink spring water. It doesn't need a mascot."],
  ["Brother Teodor's glazing carrots with it.","(He closes his eyes.) Of course he is. Then the carrots will be the sin, and I'll eat them anyway, because the alternative is waste. You see how the devil works."]],
 "Take some with you. Please."],
14:["(Brother Anselm is in the empty church, lighting the candles one by one.) The whole island has gone down to the big day on the fourteenth. Someone must keep the hours. God doesn't stop listening because everyone's gone to a rally.",[
  ["Aren't you curious?","About a crowd? I've seen crowds. They shout, they go home, they forget. The hours have been kept on this mountain for five hundred years. That I'm curious about."],
  ["Who's left up here?","Me, the fox and Brother Paavo, who says the cabbages can't be left. I didn't argue. The cabbages have more sense than most of the people down there."],
  ["Say a prayer for the island.","I say one every three hours. The island just hasn't been listening."]],
 "Go on, go down. Tell me tomorrow what they shouted."],
15:["(Brother Anselm is pacing the cloister.) Prince Augustus is with the Abbot today. They'll talk about his calling, they say. The Abbot will talk about crowns, and the prince will listen, and nobody will mention God until the door is shut.",[
  ["Don't you want Augustus to be king?",["I want Augustus to be holy. He can't be both. Nobody can.",[
    ["Why not?","A king must lie for the good of the country, and send men to die for it, and smile at men he despises. Holiness doesn't survive that. It shouldn't."],
    ["So you'd have him take vows?","I'd have him in this cloister, in a plain habit, praying for whoever does wear the crown. He'd be a great monk. He'd be a frightened king."]]]],
  ["Father Thyme disagrees with you.","Father Thyme thinks a holy king would heal the island. I think a holy king would be eaten by it. We've disagreed for thirty years, and loved each other for all of them."],
  ["What does Augustus want?","He wants everyone to stop asking him. He'll get his wish, one way or another."]],
 "Pray for him. He's the only one up here today who has to choose."],
16:["(There's a small radio on the gatehouse sill, and Brother Anselm is pointedly not listening to it.) The Abbot asked me to bring him the wireless for the great debate. I brought it. I've not brought it all the way.",[
  ["What's your view on a vote?","The people choosing their own masters? Every golden calf was voted for. Unanimously, I expect."],
  ["Who's speaking?","Anna, from the Party, who would close this monastery, and Erik, from the Democrats, who'd turn it into a museum. I'm listening for which is the bigger fool."],
  ["Let me take it to the Abbot.","Very well. Carry it as if it were full of devils. It probably is."]],
 "Bring it back when they've finished. Before compline."],
17:["(The bell in the tower gives a single low note, though no one's at the rope.) Did you hear that? The ground shook, and the bell spoke on its own. In five hundred years it has only ever spoken when we pulled it.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Brother Ilja has his chronicles out, and Brother Teodor has his pots out, and Brother Paavo is talking to his soil. I'm on my knees. One of us is right."],
  ["Do you think it's a sign?","Everything is a sign. Kings dying, princes wavering, men in fur collars at the gate, and now the earth itself. If God is speaking this clearly, it would be rude not to listen."],
  ["A sign of what?","Of judgement, I think. On whom, God hasn't said. But I've been going through the visitors' book, and I have some ideas."]],
 "Walk softly. The mountain's listening too."],
18:["(Brother Anselm is standing at the altar with the monastery's ring of keys, turning each one in his fingers as if it might confess.) Gone. From the altar, between vespers and matins. I hold every key on this mountain, and I have lost the most precious thing it holds.",[
  ["Was the church locked?",["Locked at nine. I locked it myself. I checked it twice, as I always do. This morning it was locked, and the relic was gone.",[
    ["Who else has a key?","The Abbot, and me. And Brother Teodor has the vestry key for the wine. I've asked them all. I asked myself, too, at length."],
    ["Someone came in through a window?","Every window is shut and every pane whole. Whoever did this either flew, or had a key, or knew something about this church I don't."]]]],
  ["Have you checked the visitors' book?","Every name for a month. Party men, oil men, the Russian, a woman from the cola company, and you. I've not ruled any of you out."],
  ["I'm so sorry, Brother.","(He looks at you sharply, and then not.) Thank you. I've not been sorry so hard since I was a novice. It's a long way to fall, from a prior."]],
 "If you know anything, God knows it too. Remember that."],
19:["(Steam drifts across the yard from the peak. Brother Anselm has his sleeves rolled up and a bucket of ash-grey water.) The steam is settling on everything. The fox hasn't come to the step in two days. Animals know before we do.",[
  ["Are the brothers fasting?","Three days, for the relic and for the mountain. Brother Teodor has made a soup so thin you can read through it, and he's sulking in a way only a cook can sulk."],
  ["Maybe the fox went down the valley.","Then it has more sense than any of us. I hope so. It'd be a foolish thing to miss, a fox."],
  ["Is there any news of the relic?","Rumours, from the towns. Every rumour has a different thief. I've written them all down. The book has never been so thick."]],
 "If you see a fox on the road, it's nobody's."],
20:["(Brother Anselm is coming down from the bell tower.) The Russian again. From the Hotel Aurora. I saw him leave his car at the bend and walk the last mile, to look humble. Humble men don't wear those shoes.",[
  ["What did he want?","He gave the Abbot an icon. Very old, very fine. Stolen from some church in Karelia, I'd bet the bell on it. The Abbot has it under a cloth, and is thinking."],
  ["Will you send it back?","I would. With him attached. But the Abbot says one doesn't refuse an icon, any more than one refuses a child at the door."],
  ["How did you see him so far off?","From the bell tower you can see every bend of the road for three miles. I've been up there more than usual, lately. I'd like to be the first to see what comes."]],
 "You're in the book today too. I've noted your shoes. They're fine."],
21:["(Ash is falling everywhere. Brother Anselm is at the bell rope, hauling it with both arms, his face streaked grey.) Counted! All the brothers, counted! The mountain has spoken and every one of us is still here to hear it!",[
  ["Are you hurt?","My hands are raw from the rope and my heart's going like a novice's. I'm not hurt. I'm grateful, which is harder on the knees."],
  ["Is the monastery safe?","The fire's going north, into the dead valley, away from us. A miracle, I say. The Abbot says it's the slope. We'll put both in the chronicle."],
  ["Why ring the bell?","So the farms below know we're alive, and so God knows we're still awake. Help me, or stand back. It's a heavy rope."]],
 "Go down while you can. Tell them the bell is ringing."],
22:["(Brother Anselm has the brothers in a line, sweeping the yard in time.) Ash is a discipline. It falls, we sweep. It falls, we sweep. By Lent we'll be the cleanest monastery in the north.",[
  ["You seem almost happy.","I am never happy. I am occasionally satisfied. A line of monks sweeping in order is very nearly satisfying."],
  ["Where's Father Thyme?","Down at East Farm, blessing the fields. He carried two blankets down himself, at his age, in this. I told him he was a fool. He told me I was right. Then he went."],
  ["Can I sweep?","(He hands you a broom.) Left to right. Not right to left. Brother Mikael, you are sweeping in a circle. God is not a circle."]],
 "Keep sweeping as you go. It's a fine discipline for a reporter."],
23:["(There is a new crack over the church door. Brother Anselm stands under it with an icon in his hands.) The aftershock split the plaster above the door, right through the old painted cross. Brother Paavo says plaster. I say we hang the Theotokos over it and pray.",[
  ["Why not both?","That is what the Abbot said. Do you two discuss me?"],
  ["Is the church safe to use?","It has stood for five hundred years. It's had a fright. So have we. We'll all stand a little longer."],
  ["Any news of the fox?","(He hesitates.) A track in the ash by the kitchen step this morning. Small, four toes. It may be a dog from the farm. It may not."]],
 "Bow your head under the door. Out of respect, and because of the crack."],
24:["(Brother Anselm has a pack on his back and a stout stick, and is inspecting the brothers' boots.) The retreat to the peaks. We go up the south ridge, away from the smoke, and we stay up there until we've heard what we came to hear.",[
  ["What do you hope to hear?","Each other, for once. Down here we speak in the refectory and pray in the church. Up there, on the rock, you hear what a man's really carrying."],
  ["Is it safe, after the eruption?","Safer than the towns. Nobody's going to ask us for anything up there, and the mountain's spent its anger in the other direction."],
  ["Which brother worries you most?","Brother Mikael. Up there he can't look at Kingsgrad. I'm curious what he'll look at instead."]],
 "Keep up, if you come. I don't wait. Not even for God."],
25:["(Brother Anselm is sitting on a bench with one boot off, inspecting a blister.) Back from the peaks. A night on the rock, and the brothers came down closer than they went up. My feet did not come down closer to anything.",[
  ["What happened up there?","Things a prior hears on the rock stay on the rock. I'll say only that I listened more than I spoke, which the brothers will tell you is a miracle in itself."],
  ["Have you heard about Princess Daphne?","That the Democrats want her for a figurehead and the palace for a bride? She'll do as she likes. She always has. She once put a frog in my hood during a christening."],
  ["Let me find you a plaster.","Brother Teodor has some in a tin with his raisins. Don't eat the raisins. They're older than the novices."]],
 "Thank you. Walk softly; my feet are listening."],
26:["(Brother Anselm is at the kitchen step, crouching, very still. A red tail flicks round the corner.) Don't move. Don't speak. It's come back.",[
  ["The fox!","(The fox snatches a crust and vanishes.) Nobody's fox. Thin, and grey with ash, and quite alive. Thank God."],
  ["Is it the same one?","It's got the torn ear. It's the same one. It went somewhere safe, and it came home. There's a lesson in that, and I'm too pleased to preach it."],
  ["The Kelp Cola men are putting princes on bottles, I hear.","A face on a bottle is a graven image. It'll be sold, drunk and thrown in a ditch. As a theology of kingship, it's at least honest."]],
 "Not a word about the fox. Not one."],
27:["(Brother Anselm has a letter in his hand, unopened, and he's frowning at the postmark.) This is for Brother Mikael. From Kingsgrad, from no one, in a hand I don't like. I've never once opened a brother's letter. I'm thinking about it.",[
  ["Don't open it.","No. I won't. But I'll give it to him myself, and I'll watch his face, and I'll stay in the room until he tells me to go."],
  ["What's worrying you?","There's a card game in the Syndicate's back room tonight, they say. Everyone who matters is playing. Brother Mikael went white when Brother Teodor mentioned it at supper."],
  ["Is Mikael in trouble?","Brother Mikael was in trouble before he came. The mountain was meant to keep it from following him. I'm the mountain, now, more or less."]],
 "Keep this to yourself. It's a brother's business."],
28:["(The church smells of smoke and summer herbs. Brother Anselm holds an old book open, his finger on a line.) The Herbal Rite. I'm the keeper of the words. They were old when the first king was crowned, and I've not changed a syllable in twenty years.",[
  ["You love this, don't you?","(He almost smiles.) It's the one day a year the mountain gives us thanks back. Sage, thyme, juniper, mountain mint. Even I can't find anything to disapprove of."],
  ["What do the words say?","Roughly: we didn't plant the mountain, and we won't outlast it, and we're grateful for the herbs. It's longer in the old tongue. Most things are."],
  ["The factions are all making their push today.","Then they'll be busy, and we'll be undisturbed. The best rites happen while the world's looking the other way."]],
 "Breathe the smoke as you go. It's the only incense I approve of."],
29:["(Brother Anselm is standing in the doorway of the scriptorium, watching Brother Ilja at his desk.) Everyone wants the Church's records today. The line of succession. Who was born, who was crowned, who was forgotten. As if the books were a weapon.",[
  ["Aren't they?","They're a memory. A memory can be used as a weapon, I suppose. So can a candlestick. We don't stop lighting candles."],
  ["What has Brother Ilja found?","Brother Ilja has found that I'm standing in his doorway, and he's stopped writing. He'll start again when I go. I'm going."],
  ["Who should be king, Brother?","Whoever will kneel, and mean it. You'll find most can't."]],
 "Let the scribe work. History's waiting."],
30:["(Brother Anselm is up in the bell tower, the rope in his hands, looking out over the grey island.) The last day. When it's settled, whatever it is, I'll ring. The island should hear the Church say amen to it, whether we like it or not.",[
  ["Will you like it?","Probably not. I don't like most things. I'll ring anyway. That's what faith is, some days: ringing the bell when you'd rather not."],
  ["You can see the whole island from here.","Every road, every chimney, every one of them waiting. They look very small. They always do from up here. It's good for the soul to remember they're not."],
  ["Will I be in your book tomorrow?","You've been in my book every day for a month. I'll keep the page open."]],
 "Go down carefully. And come back up, some time, without a notebook."],
};
// Brother Ilja, the monastery's scribe and keeper of the Church's records (Orthodox Church). Threads: copying the chronicle of
// the kingless year two hundred years ago, when the mountain woke; the late king's grandfather's forgotten brother, whose trail he
// keeps finding in the registers; his spectacles, held together with thread; and a love of music he keeps very quiet (he played
// jazz piano before his vows). He must never assume he got the record on the 12th: the player may have sold it.
DAILY['Brother Ilja']={
5:["(Brother Ilja is bent so close over a book that his nose nearly touches the page. His fingers are drumming softly on the desk.) Oh! You startled me. I'm afraid I don't hear people come in. Or see them, much.",[
  ["What are you working on?","The chronicle of the kingless year. Two hundred years ago, nineteen months with no one on the throne. I'm copying it before the old copy turns to dust in my hands."],
  ["You were drumming on the desk.","Was I? (He puts his hand flat.) A bad habit. It keeps time while I copy. The Prior says it sounds like a woodpecker with a grudge."],
  ["Isn't that terribly dark to read by?","It's a candle, my eyes and God. Two out of three are reliable."]],
 "Close the door gently, would you? The draught turns the pages."],
6:["(Brother Ilja's spectacles are held together at the bridge with black thread.) Forgive me if I look at the wrong half of you. My spectacles broke in the winter, and the optician in Kingsgrad was snowed in with the rest of the world.",[
  ["Can you see me at all?","I can see a reporter-shaped haze, with a notebook-shaped haze in its hand. That's more than I can see of most of the island."],
  ["How do you copy books like that?","Very close up, and very slowly. The chronicle doesn't mind. It's waited two hundred years. It can wait for my nose."],
  ["You should send for a new pair.","I have. The Prior is deciding whether new spectacles are a vanity. I've told him that seeing God's creation clearly is a duty. He's taking that under advisement."]],
 "Mind the stack by the door. I can't see it either."],
7:["(Brother Ilja is peering at a narrow, brown register.) Remember I mentioned the forgotten brother? The late king's grandfather had a younger brother. Here's his baptism, in eighteen sixty-four. Look for his death, and there's nothing. Not in any book.",[
  ["Maybe he died abroad.","Perhaps. Kings' brothers often did. But the Church records the deaths of princes wherever they fall. Someone ought to have written it down."],
  ["Why does it matter?","Probably it doesn't. It's usually nothing. But a scribe who sees a gap can't stop looking at it. It's like a missing tooth."],
  ["What was his name?","(He covers the page, gently.) I'd rather not say, until I know what I'm saying. Names are heavy things in a month like this."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about my missing tooth. Please."],
8:["(The choir is warming up in the church. Brother Ilja, at the back of a pew, is moving his head very slightly, in time.) Listen. The basses come in a half-beat late on the third line. Every week. It's rather lovely, actually.",[
  ["You've got a musician's ear.","(He goes a little pink.) A scribe's ear. One copies music too, you know. Chant manuscripts. The notes get into you."],
  ["Will you sing today?","Heavens, no. I mouth the words. My voice is like a gate in the wind. But I listen. Somebody has to listen properly."],
  ["What do you hope the sermon says?","Something worth writing down. I'll put it in the chronicle either way. The first Sunday without a king. Someone in two hundred years will want to know."]],
 "Sit where you can hear the basses. You'll see what I mean."],
9:["(Brother Ilja is reading aloud to himself in a low murmur, his finger on the page.) '...and the grey snow fell for forty days, and there was no king to pray for, so the brothers prayed for the mountain instead.' The kingless year. I've reached the grey snow.",[
  ["What was the grey snow?","Ash, I think. The chronicler says the mountain smoked and the snow came down grey, and the cows wouldn't milk. Two hundred years ago. Then it stopped."],
  ["And there was no king for nineteen months?","The old king died, the heir was a child, and three families argued. The Church held the island together until they'd stopped. History rhymes, they say. I'd rather it didn't."],
  ["Does it end well?","It ends with a coronation and a feast with forty geese. Whether that's well depends on whether you were a goose."]],
 "If it starts snowing grey, come and tell me first."],
10:["(Brother Ilja has the great register of the island open, and his pen dipped.) The king's funeral today. Everyone else has gone down to the palace. I stayed to write his death into the book. It's my duty, and I'm the only one who can find the right page.",[
  ["Is it hard, writing it?","His birth is three pages back, in my old master's hand. I'm writing his death in mine. That's all a scribe is, really: the hand between the hands."],
  ["What will you write?","His name, his titles, the date, the hour, 'reposed in the Lord'. And a small cross. Kings and fishermen get the same cross. I like that about the book."],
  ["Didn't you want to go to the funeral?","I'd see a blur in a box. Here I can see him clearly, in ink. I'll say my own goodbye on the page."]],
 "Let me finish the line. Ink doesn't wait."],
11:["(Brother Ilja is turning the pages of a very fat book.) The Abbot's sending help to the poor today. I looked up how the Church used to do it. The alms books go back to sixteen-ten. Bread, herring, firewood and, once, a goat.",[
  ["A goat?","Given to a widow in Saltwick in sixteen fifty-one, it says, 'for her comfort'. It doesn't say if the goat agreed."],
  ["Did the Church give more then than now?","More, for its size. Then the island grew rich and the Church grew comfortable. The books get thinner after the eighteen hundreds. I notice these things."],
  ["Why keep records of alms at all?","So that someone can check, later, whether we did what we said. That's the whole of a record. A promise with a date on it."]],
 "If you give something away today, I'd like to know. For the book."],
12:["(Brother Ilja looks up sharply as you come in, then at your hands, then at the door.) Oh, it's you. Good morning. Afternoon. Whatever it is. I'm expecting a delivery. Ink. From Eastbrook. I'm not nervous.",[
  ["You seem a little nervous.","Do I? It's the tremor in the candle. Ink is very important. Some ink you wait for for years."],
  ["From Eastbrook? From which shop?","Oh, one of the little ones. Off the main street. The name escapes me. (It plainly doesn't.)"],
  ["What colour ink?","(He blinks.) Black. Very black. With a bit of... blue in it. The colour of a night in a city, if you must know. Do you have it?"]],
 "If anything comes up the mountain for me, I'll be here. Right here."],
13:["(Brother Ilja is back at the forgotten brother's register, holding it up to the window.) Look at this. Between his baptism and his brother's coronation. A page has been cut out. With a knife. Very clean, very close to the spine.",[
  ["Who would cut a page from a register?","Someone who knew exactly which page, and had a very sharp knife. A long time ago, from the colour of the cut. Before my time, and before my master's."],
  ["What was on it?","That's the question that's kept me up. Births, deaths, marriages for the royal house. Something somebody didn't want left in the book."],
  ["How was yesterday?","(He waves a hand.) Long. Don't ask me about yesterday. Ask me about eighteen sixty-five instead. It's more mysterious, and less personal."]],
 "Not a word about the cut page. Not yet."],
14:["(Brother Ilja is putting on a heavy coat over his habit, buttoning it wrong.) I'm going down to the big day on the fourteenth. The Abbot says a scribe should see history, not just write it. I said I'd only see a blur. He said a blur's a start.",[
  ["Your buttons are wrong.","(He looks down, and sighs.) They're always wrong. I've been out by one button since nineteen sixty. Leave them. It's the style, in Kingsgrad."],
  ["Has there been a day like this before?","In seventeen eighty-one, the year of the kingless winter, the whole island met on the ice of Lake Ilmari. It cracked. Nobody drowned, but it was taken as a hint."],
  ["Will you write about it?","Of course. Two lines, maybe three. That's how history works: the whole island shouts for a day, and the chronicle gives it three lines."]],
 "If you see a monk with his buttons wrong, that'll be me."],
15:["(Brother Ilja is dusting a large illuminated book on a stand.) Prince Augustus is up the mountain today. When he was a boy, he'd come and sit here and turn the pages of this book. The Lives of the Saints. He wanted to know what happened to each of them.",[
  ["What did happen to them?","Mostly they died uncomfortably and were sorry for no one. Augustus always looked for the ones who'd been kings first. There are three. They all gave the crown away."],
  ["Is that a hint?","I'm a scribe. I don't hint. I just leave books open at certain pages."],
  ["Do you think he'll choose the Church?","I think he'll read the page the book falls open at. Books are wiser than people think, and less wise than people hope."]],
 "Leave it open at the saints, if you pass it. Just in case."],
16:["(Brother Ilja has a pen in one hand and the small radio pressed to his ear with the other.) The great debate, Anna and Erik. I'm taking it down for the chronicle. 'The island argued about its future on the wireless.' That's never been written before.",[
  ["Who's making more sense?","Anna's sentences are better built. Erik's are better sung. It's like listening to a fugue and a folk song at once. They'll never resolve."],
  ["Will the chronicle say who won?","The chronicle never says who won a debate. Only who was crowned afterwards. History is rude like that."],
  ["Do you vote, Brother?","Monks don't vote. We pray. It's a very long vote with only one person counting."]],
 "Hush, Erik's making a joke. Let me get it down."],
17:["(Brother Ilja is on his knees on the floor of the scriptorium, patting the boards.) The ground shook and my spectacles fell off the desk, and now I can't find them, which is the trouble with needing spectacles to find spectacles.",[
  ["Here they are. Oh. One lens is cracked.","(He puts them on and peers at you through a spider's web.) Well. Now I see you twice. You look well, both of you."],
  ["Was it the volcano?","The chronicle says the mountain shook for four days before the grey snow came. I read that last week and thought, how quaint. I'm not thinking that now."],
  ["Are the books safe?","I'd stacked them all flat, thank God. A falling book is a tragedy. A falling chronicle is a catastrophe."]],
 "If you're passing Kingsgrad, curse the optician for me. Gently."],
18:["(Brother Ilja has the inventory of the church open, and is reading it through his cracked lens.) The relic is gone. I've been asked to find every mention of it in the books, in case something helps. It came by ship, from Novgorod, in fourteen-twelve.",[
  ["What exactly is it?","A small gold reliquary, very old, with a bone of the saint in it. Six hundred years on this mountain. Never once left it, until now."],
  ["Does anything in the records help?","There's a note from seventeen eighty-one that the reliquary was hidden during the kingless year, and brought back after. So: it's been missing before. It came home that time."],
  ["How's the Prior taking it?","He's taking it personally, which is how he takes everything. He's counted the keys so often the ring's gone shiny."]],
 "If you hear anything of it, the book would like to know. So would I."],
19:["(Brother Ilja is copying, and humming a little phrase under his breath. He stops when he sees you.) Forgive me. A tune in my head. It goes away if I hum it, usually.",[
  ["What's the tune?","Nothing. An old thing. I heard it in Kingsgrad, long ago. In a place with low lights and a very old upright piano with a sticky F."],
  ["Do you know the Midnight Note, in Frostholm?",["(He goes very still.) I've... heard of it. Is there still a piano?",[
    ["I think so.","(He smiles, a little sadly.) Good. Good. Every club should have a piano, even if no one plays it properly. Especially then."],
    ["Why do you ask?","Oh, no reason. A scribe's curiosity. Pianos have histories too, you know. Some of them very sad."]]]],
  ["You hum well, Brother.","(He goes pink.) I hum terribly. But thank you. Don't tell the Prior. He thinks humming is a gateway to dancing."]],
 "Close the door behind you. The tune gets out."],
20:["(Brother Ilja is holding a register very close to the candle, squinting through the good half of his spectacles.) Another trace of the forgotten brother. Here, in the margin of a book of hours. A line of writing, faded. 'Forgive me, brother.' In a hand like the old king's.",[
  ["Which old king?","The late king's grandfather. The elder brother. If it's his hand, then he wrote it in a prayer book and never sent it. A king, saying sorry to a brother no one remembers."],
  ["Sorry for what?","For whatever's on the page that was cut out, I'd guess. But a scribe shouldn't guess. A scribe should find."],
  ["Does Father Thyme know?","I've told him a little. He told me to keep reading, and to keep quiet. He's very good at both."]],
 "Keep that to yourself. It's two old brothers' business, and they're both dead."],
21:["(Ash is falling past the window. Brother Ilja is carrying books down the stairs in his arms, three and four at a time, and he can barely see where he's going.) The chronicles! The cellar's stone. Take an armful, please, quickly, the oldest ones first!",[
  ["Which are the oldest?","The ones that look like they're about to die. The brown ones. The ones that smell of mice. Those."],
  ["Are you hurt?","My eyes are full of ash and I can't see a thing, which is normal. My heart is in my mouth, which isn't. Take the red one too."],
  ["The brothers are all counted, Brother.","Thank God. Then all that's left is the books. Men are made in God's image. Books are only made in ours. But they're all we've got of the dead."]],
 "Go! Down the hill, while there's a road. The books are safe now."],
22:["(Brother Ilja is writing very slowly, his face close to the page.) For the first time in two hundred years, a scribe of this monastery is writing the words 'the grey snow fell'. I thought it would be thrilling. It's only sad.",[
  ["What will you write about it?","The day, the hour, the colour of the sky, that no one died, and that the brothers swept the steps. In two hundred years someone will read it and say, how quaint."],
  ["History rhymed after all.","It did. I only hope it doesn't finish the verse. The kingless year went on for nineteen months."],
  ["Did all your books survive?","Every one, and the cellar smells of old paper now, like a library in heaven. If heaven has mice. I suspect it does."]],
 "Brush the ash off your shoes before you come in. The books sneeze."],
23:["(Brother Ilja is wearing a brand new pair of round spectacles, and looking at everything in astonishment.) They came this morning, from Kingsgrad! Look at you. You've got a face. You've got eyebrows!",[
  ["How does the world look?","Very grey, and very detailed. I'd forgotten the brothers had pores. The Prior is much older than I thought. So am I, in the mirror."],
  ["Can you read without your nose on the page now?","At a full arm's length! I read three pages of the chronicle standing up. I nearly wept. Then I noticed I'd copied two words wrong in nineteen seventy-nine."],
  ["They suit you.","(He touches them shyly.) Thank you. I feel like a man who can see what's coming. Mostly ash, but still."]],
 "Come back when I've had a good look at everything. It may take a while."],
24:["(Brother Ilja is packing a small notebook and three pencils into his pack.) The retreat to the peaks. I've never been able to see the view on a retreat. With these spectacles, I'm going to see every rock. I'm almost frightened.",[
  ["Why take a notebook on a silent retreat?","It's not silent; it's quiet. There's a difference. And a scribe without a pencil is just a man on a rock."],
  ["What do the brothers say up there?","Things they'd never say at supper. The wind's the only one listening. And me, I suppose. But I'm discreet. I'm the most discreet man on the island."],
  ["Will you keep up with Brother Anselm?","Nobody keeps up with Brother Anselm. We follow at a respectful distance, like history."]],
 "If you come up, keep to the ridge. The other side's still warm."],
25:["(Brother Ilja is leafing back through the register, smiling.) Everyone's talking about Princess Daphne today. Here's her christening. Daphne Eleonora Sofia Margareta. Four names, and she screamed through every one of them.",[
  ["You wrote it?","My first royal entry. My hand shook so much the 'Margareta' wanders off the line. I've always felt it suited her."],
  ["How was the retreat?","I saw the whole island from the top. Clearly, for the first time in thirty years. It's very small. And very loud, even from up there."],
  ["Is there a page cut out of hers?","(He laughs.) No. Daphne's whole page is here. All of it. She'd never let anyone cut her out of anything."]],
 "Four names. I still can't spell the last one without looking."],
26:["(Brother Ilja is tapping his pencil on the desk in a quick, light rhythm, and stops when he catches himself.) There I go again. Brother Teodor clatters his pot lids at supper and I'm off. I'm a terrible monk for rhythm.",[
  ["Did you ever play an instrument?",["(He hesitates for a long moment.) A little piano, once. Before. In Kingsgrad. A long time ago, and under another sort of life.",[
    ["Were you any good?","Once, for one night, I think I was. Then I wasn't, and I came up here. That's all there is. It's a short story."],
    ["Do you miss it?","Every day after compline. Then I pray, and it passes. Mostly. Prayer and music aren't so far apart, you know. Both are about listening for what isn't there yet."]]]],
  ["Is that why you tap?","The fingers remember what the head has put away. The Prior thinks it's a nervous complaint. In a way, it is."],
  ["I like the rhythm.","So do I. Don't tell anyone. Monks shouldn't have a favourite tempo."]],
 "Keep that between us. It's an old, quiet thing."],
27:["(Brother Ilja is sitting very still, with three registers open in front of him, side by side.) I think I can see it now. Where the forgotten brother goes. Who came after him, if anyone did. It's all there, if you read the books together instead of one at a time.",[
  ["What have you found?","Something that would make a great many people very interested, and some very angry. I'm not sure yet. I'm not sure enough to say."],
  ["Will you tell anyone?","The Abbot, first. Only the Abbot. A scribe's job is to keep the truth, not to throw it at people."],
  ["Is it dangerous to know?","Knowledge is only dangerous in a hurry. I'm never in a hurry. I'm a scribe."]],
 "Not a word. Not even in your notebook."],
28:["(Brother Ilja is at his desk, finishing a fresh copy of a few lines in a strange old script.) The words of the Herbal Rite. The Prior's book is falling apart, so I'm making him a new one. He'll read it today. He'll never admit it's easier.",[
  ["What language is that?","Old island tongue, from before the Church came. The Church kept the words and put a cross at the end. That's how most of our customs survived: a cross at the end."],
  ["What do the words mean?","'We did not sow the mountain. We will not outlive it. For what it gives, our thanks.' Then a list of herbs, which is the most beautiful part."],
  ["Everyone else is out making their big push.","And we're giving thanks for sage. In two hundred years, one of those will be in a chronicle. I'm not sure which."]],
 "Come to the rite. You'll smell it before you hear it."],
29:["(The scriptorium door is shut. When you knock, Brother Ilja opens it a crack, then lets you in and locks it behind you.) Forgive me. Everyone wants to see the books today. The line of succession. Four visitors before breakfast, and one tried to buy a register.",[
  ["Who were they?","I couldn't possibly say. One of them smelt of cigars, and one of them of the palace. One of them had very expensive shoes. That's all a short-sighted man notices."],
  ["Will you show anyone what you've found?","The records say what they say. I'll read them to anyone who asks properly, in front of the Abbot. They say less than everyone hopes, and more than anyone wants."],
  ["Did you tell the Abbot?","Last night. He listened, and then he said a prayer, and then he said, 'Well.' He says 'Well' when he's very surprised. It's the only way you can tell."]],
 "Lock the door behind you, would you? It's that kind of day."],
30:["(Brother Ilja has the island's chronicle open at a fresh page, and has written only the date.) The last day of September. Whatever happens today, I'll write it in this book, in my best hand, with my new spectacles. I just don't know what it'll be yet.",[
  ["What do you hope to write?","Something with a feast in it, and no grey snow. Forty geese, perhaps. The geese won't agree with me."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A little. But two hundred years ago a scribe sat at this same desk on a day like this, and he wrote it down, and he went to supper. I'll do the same."],
  ["Will you still play, after all this?","(He smiles.) I'll still tap on the desk. And after compline, very quietly, I'll still listen to whatever's in my head. That's enough music for a monk."]],
 "I'll leave the next line blank for now. Come back, and I'll read it to you."],
};
// Brother Mikael, a young monk who came up the mountain to escape something in Kingsgrad (Orthodox Church). Threads: the lights of
// Kingsgrad he watches from the wall at night; the debts and the card tables he left behind, which he won't name; the old solo
// chant he's learning for the Herbal Rite; and the letter to his mother that he keeps not sending.
DAILY['Brother Mikael']={
5:["(Brother Mikael is sitting on the yard wall, looking down the mountain towards Kingsgrad, and jumps when you speak.) Sorry. I was miles away. About eleven miles, actually. That's Kingsgrad, that smudge.",[
  ["You can see the whole town from here?","At night you can. The lights come on street by street. I know which street is which. I try not to, but I do."],
  ["Do you miss it?","No. Yes. I miss the trams. I don't miss anything else. Ask me again next year."],
  ["Isn't the wall a bit high to sit on?","Brother Anselm says the same. He says a monk on a wall is a monk halfway to somewhere else. He's not wrong. He's never wrong. It's exhausting."]],
 "Don't tell the Prior where you found me."],
6:["(Brother Mikael is shuffling an old deck of cards, very fast, without looking at it. He stops when he sees you, and puts them away.) Those aren't mine. They're Brother Teodor's, for patience. I was tidying them.",[
  ["You shuffle like a professional.","(He looks at his hands.) I used to be good at cards. Very good. And then I wasn't good enough, and that's when you find out who your friends are."],
  ["Play a hand with me?","No. Thank you. I've promised God I'll only play patience. He's holding me to it. So is Brother Anselm."],
  ["Who taught you?","Some men in Kingsgrad, in a back room that smelled of tomato sauce. That's all I'll say. Some lessons cost more than they teach."]],
 "Forget the cards. I have."],
7:["(Brother Mikael is singing a long, rising phrase under his breath, and breaks off on a high note.) It won't go. The top of it. It's the old chant for the Herbal Rite, at the end of the month. The Prior says I'm the only tenor who can do it. I can't do it.",[
  ["Sing it again.","(He tries. His voice cracks at the top.) You see? Like a gate hinge. The last brother who sang it had the voice of an angel. And ninety years of practice."],
  ["Why you?","The others are basses, mostly, or Brother Ilja, who mouths. I've got a tenor. I didn't ask for it. It came with the rest of me."],
  ["You've got three weeks.","Three weeks to find a note I've never been able to reach. It's a bit like being a monk, really."]],
 "If you hear a gate hinge singing, that'll be me practising."],
8:["(Brother Mikael is in his choir robe, pulling at the collar.) First Sunday without a king. The whole island's coming. The prince. Everyone. I'll be in the second row, singing, and trying not to see anyone I know.",[
  ["Who might you know?","People from Kingsgrad come up sometimes. Not often. But today, everyone's coming. I'll keep my eyes on the icons. The icons don't recognise me."],
  ["Are you nervous about singing?","Not about singing. Singing's the one thing I'm not nervous about. It's the faces."],
  ["The robe suits you.","It itches. Brother Anselm says that's humility. I think it's the wool."]],
 "Sit near the back, if you can. I'll know there's one friendly face."],
9:["(Brother Mikael has a folded letter in his breviary, and he's looking at it rather than at the prayers.) It's to my mother. In Kingsgrad. I wrote it in March. I haven't sent it.",[
  ["Why not?","Because if I send it, she'll know where I am. And if she knows, someone might ask her. And she's a terrible liar. She's the best woman in Kingsgrad and a terrible liar."],
  ["Doesn't she know where you are?","She knows I'm alive. Brother Anselm sent a note, at Easter. Three lines. She probably thinks I've joined the navy."],
  ["What does the letter say?","That I'm well, that I'm sorry, that I eat a lot of soup. The sorry part is two pages. The soup part's one line."]],
 "Don't mention the letter to anyone. It's not ready."],
10:["(Brother Mikael is alone in the church, kneeling.) Everyone's gone down to the king's funeral. I said I'd stay and keep the hours. I'm praying for the king. And for everyone who'll be standing near his coffin today, whether they deserve it or not.",[
  ["Why didn't you go?","The palace, today, will be full of every important man on the island. Some of them I'd rather didn't see me. That's all. It's not a story."],
  ["Did you ever see the king?","Once, at the races, when I was a boy. He waved at the crowd and I waved back. I thought he'd waved at me. I told everyone for a year."],
  ["Who don't deserve prayers?","Everyone deserves them. Some need them more. I'm praying hardest for those. Yes, I know. That's a dodge. It's a monk's dodge."]],
 "Go on, go down to it. Tell me later if it was beautiful."],
11:["(Brother Mikael is helping load blankets on a handcart.) Father Thyme's sending help to Pat, on the beach. I'm glad. A man can end up on a beach faster than you'd think. I nearly did.",[
  ["What happened?","I owed more than I had, and the people I owed weren't patient. For about a week, I slept under the tram shelter by the Civil Service Bureau. Then I walked up here."],
  ["Walked? From Kingsgrad?","All night, in March, in town shoes. Father Thyme opened the gate himself. He didn't ask a single question. He gave me socks."],
  ["Do you know Pat?","No. But I know the look, from the tram shelter. People walk past like you're a lamppost. Being looked at again is the gift, really. More than blankets."]],
 "Take one of the blankets down, if you're going that way."],
12:["(Brother Mikael looks as if he hasn't slept.) There was a car parked at the bend in the road last night. Lights off. For an hour. Then it drove away. I watched it from the wall the whole time.",[
  ["Who was it?","I don't know. Probably no one. Lovers, or someone with a flat tyre, or the oil company looking at the view. Probably no one."],
  ["Did you tell the Prior?","He'd ask why I was on the wall at two in the morning. And then he'd ask why a car frightens me. I don't want to answer either."],
  ["You can't watch the road every night.","I know. But up here nobody comes looking. Usually. It's the 'usually' that keeps me on the wall."]],
 "If you see a car at the bend on your way down, look at the number for me."],
13:["(Brother Mikael is practising the chant in the empty church, and the top note comes out, thin but whole.) Did you hear that? Did you hear it? I got it. Once. It was about the size of a mouse, but I got it.",[
  ["It sounded wonderful.","It sounded like a mouse. But a mouse in tune. Two weeks ago it was a mouse in a trap."],
  ["What changed?","Brother Teodor glazed some carrots with cola, and I ate four. I think it was the sugar. Don't tell the Prior I said that either."],
  ["Sing it again.","(He tries. It cracks.) No. It's gone back into hiding. But I know it's there now. That's the thing. You only need to find something once."]],
 "Tell nobody I reached it. I want to surprise them."],
14:["(Brother Mikael has his hood pulled forward, and he's standing very close to Brother Teodor's hamper.) I'm going down to the big day with the brothers. The Abbot asked me. I've got my hood up. I'll stay close to the sausage.",[
  ["Are you worried about being seen?","Thousands of people in one place, and I'm a monk in a hood. Nobody looks at monks. I'm counting on it."],
  ["What are you expecting down there?","Speeches, cold feet, and Teodor feeding the entire crowd out of one basket. Like the loaves and fishes, only with herring."],
  ["You don't have to go.","No. But the Abbot asked, and he didn't ask a single question when I came. I'll go wherever he asks."]],
 "If you see a monk in a hood next to a hamper, wave. Gently."],
15:["(Brother Mikael is sweeping the cloister.) Prince Augustus is with the Abbot today. They're trying to decide what he should be. I hope they let him decide. Nobody let me decide anything until I walked out of town.",
 [["Do you know the royal family?","I know who the princes drink with, and who they owe, and who's trying to get to them. Or I did. It's not a list I'd want to be on, on either side."],
  ["Should Augustus be king?","He's a kind man in a city full of people who'll use kindness. If he goes down there, he'll need friends who aren't friends of anyone else. That's a short list."],
  ["Would you have liked to be a prince?","(He laughs.) I'd have lost the palace at cards by the second week. No. I'm made for a cloister. It just took me a while to find out."]],
 "Pray for the prince. And sweep that corner, if you're passing. I missed it."],
16:["(Brother Mikael is sitting on the wall with the small radio in his lap, listening to the debate.) Brother Paavo lent me the wireless for the debate. Don't tell the Prior. Anna and Erik. My mother always voted Democrat, in her head. There's never been anything to vote for.",[
  ["What would you vote for?","For a Kingsgrad where a man can owe money to a bank instead of a back room. Neither of them's said anything about that. I'm still listening."],
  ["Is your mother political?","She's a seamstress. She's political about buttons and fair rent. That's more than most of the people on the wireless."],
  ["Who's winning, do you think?","Erik keeps making people laugh and Anna keeps making them think. I suppose it depends which the island needs more. I'd say both."]],
 "Hand that radio back to Paavo if you pass him. Quietly."],
17:["(Brother Mikael is pale, and keeps glancing down the road.) The ground shook this morning, and then the milk lorry man said the oil man, Dario, hasn't been seen since last night. People on this island don't just go missing.",[
  ["Maybe he's just away.","Maybe. Probably. Men like Dario go away all the time. On boats, on aeroplanes, to Helsinki. I hope that's it."],
  ["Do you know something?","No. I don't know anything. I've been up a mountain for six months. I only know the feeling, when someone big goes quiet. Everyone else goes quiet too, and listens."],
  ["Was it the tremor that scared you?","The tremor too. Everything at once. The mountain, the rumours, the Prior's face. It's like being in a card game where everybody has stopped smiling."]],
 "If you hear where he turned up, tell me. I'll sleep better."],
18:["(Brother Mikael is standing in the cloister, rubbing his arms.) The relic's gone, and the Prior looked at me first. Before anyone. I don't blame him. I know what I look like, to a man who knows where I came from.",[
  ["Did you take it?","No. I was in my cell. I swear it before God, and I've never sworn a thing up here. I've stolen before, a long time ago. Never from a church. Never from Him."],
  ["Does the Prior really suspect you?","He looked at me for half a second, and then he looked at everyone else. Half a second's enough when you've been the one people look at."],
  ["What will you do?","Pray. Sing. Sweep. Be in the places I'm meant to be at the times I'm meant to be there. That's all an innocent man can do. It's never enough."]],
 "If you hear who took it, tell the Prior. Not me."],
19:["(Brother Mikael is coming back from the gate with an empty hand and a strange look.) I gave the letter to the milk lorry man. The one to my mother. Six months in my breviary. Now it's going down the mountain in a crate between the churns.",[
  ["What made you send it?","The mountain's steaming, the ground's shaking, and someone stole a saint. If the world's going to end, my mother should know I eat a lot of soup."],
  ["Are you frightened she'll be found?","A bit. But I didn't put the monastery on the envelope. Just 'your son, who is well.' She'll know. Nobody else will."],
  ["Brave thing to do.","Brave? I've walked into back rooms in Kingsgrad with nothing in my pockets. This was harder."]],
 "If she writes back, I'll be the most pleased monk on this mountain."],
20:["(Brother Mikael is looking at the steam from the peak, not at Kingsgrad, for once.) Look at it. It's like the mountain's breathing out. Brother Teodor says it's simmering. I think it's holding its breath, and that's the steam leaking out.",[
  ["Any word from your mother?","Not yet. It only went yesterday. I've been at the gate every time the lorry's come. Brother Anselm's started timing me."],
  ["Does the volcano frighten you?","Not like Kingsgrad did. A mountain's honest. It's going to do what it's going to do. It won't pretend it's your friend first."],
  ["You're not on the wall today.","I'm on the steps. The Prior says it's progress. I think it's because the wall's warm, and I don't trust warm stones any more."]],
 "Look up at the peak on your way down. Not at the road."],
21:["(Ash is pouring down. Brother Mikael is carrying a stack of books out of the scriptorium behind Brother Ilja, and singing a psalm at the top of his voice.) Keep going! Down to the cellar! It's what you do when the world ends: you sing and carry things.",[
  ["Why are you singing?","Because the novices are frightened and if they hear singing they'll think it's all right. And because I'm frightened, and it's the only thing I'm good at."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","Counted! Every brother. The Prior counted twice and then hugged the Abbot. Nobody's going to mention that. Ever."],
  ["Your voice is carrying well.","(He laughs, coughing on ash.) Ha! The top note of the rite came out just now, with a volcano behind it. Of course it did. Now I just need a volcano every time."]],
 "Go! Down the hill, while there's a road!"],
22:["(Brother Mikael is sweeping the yard, very badly, in a circle.) The Prior says God isn't a circle. I say I'm sweeping in a circle so I can keep looking down the mountain. Kingsgrad's grey. All of it. Every roof.",[
  ["Your mother lives there.","In a flat over a butcher's, near the market. She'll have the windows shut and wet towels on the sills. She did that once when the bakery burned. She's sensible. She's fine. She's fine."],
  ["Kingsgrad didn't get the worst of it.","I know. The lava went north. But from up here, everything grey looks like the end of the world. I need to sweep in straighter lines."],
  ["Want me to take a message down?","No. Thank you. The letter's already gone. If she can't reach me now, it won't be for want of a postman."]],
 "Sweep left to right. The Prior's watching from the tower."],
23:["(Brother Mikael is sitting on the church steps holding a pair of thick grey socks, and he's clearly been crying.) The milk lorry brought a parcel. From my mother. She's knitted me socks. And a note: 'I knew you'd write when you were ready. Eat your soup.'",[
  ["That's wonderful.","Six months I was frightened to write, and she just knitted. She didn't ask why I left. Not one question. She's like the Abbot, that way."],
  ["Is she safe, after the ash?","She says the ash got into the butcher's sausages and he's selling them as 'smoked'. That's how I know she's fine. She's making jokes."],
  ["Put them on, then.","(He does, over his sandals, and wiggles his toes.) They're too big. She's always knitted for the man she hoped I'd be. One day I'll grow into them."]],
 "Don't tell the brothers I cried. Tell them it was the ash."],
24:["(Brother Mikael is in his mother's socks, a pack on his back, at the start of the ridge path.) The retreat to the peaks. Brother Anselm says up there I won't be able to see Kingsgrad. He looked very pleased about it.",[
  ["What will you look at instead?","The sky, the rock, the back of Brother Teodor. It'll be a nice change from roofs."],
  ["Will you tell the brothers your story?","Some of it, maybe. The Prior says a retreat's for setting things down. I've been carrying it six months. My arms are tired."],
  ["Practise your chant up there.","I'm going to sing it from the top of the ridge, where nobody can hear me crack. Except God. He's used to it."]],
 "Wish me straight paths and warm socks."],
25:["(Brother Mikael is sitting on the warm steps, looking north, towards the black line of the lava, not south towards Kingsgrad.) We came down this morning. I told the brothers, on the rock, why I came here. All of it. And the sky didn't fall. It had already done that, I suppose.",[
  ["How did they take it?","Teodor gave me his last sausage. Paavo said I'd make a good gardener. Ilja wrote nothing down, which is the highest honour he has. The Prior said, 'I know.'"],
  ["He knew?","Since the night I came. He never said. He's been watching the road for me. I thought he was watching me. He was watching the road."],
  ["You're looking north now.","The lava. You can see it glowing at night. It's terrible, and beautiful, and it's not Kingsgrad. I've decided I like looking at it better."]],
 "Thank you for listening, these weeks. You've been part of the rock too."],
26:["(Brother Mikael is singing the rite's chant in the church, and this time the top note comes out round and full, and holds.) ...Did that happen? Tell me that happened.",[
  ["That happened.","(He sits down on the altar step.) Without a volcano. Without the carrots. Just me. Two days before the rite. I could kiss the Prior. I won't. But I could."],
  ["What changed?","I think I stopped holding my breath. Since I came up here, I've been holding it, every day, watching the road. On the rock, I breathed out."],
  ["Sing it at the rite like that.","God willing, and the cola carrots permitting."]],
 "Don't tell anyone. I want the brothers to hear it first on the day."],
27:["(Brother Mikael is holding an envelope with a Kingsgrad postmark, opened. His hands aren't steady.) The Prior gave me this himself. He stayed in the room while I read it. It's not from my mother.",[
  ["Who's it from?",["It's not signed. It doesn't need to be. It's from the people I left. It's an invitation, of sorts. To the card game at the back of the pizzeria. Tonight.",[
    ["Will you go?","No. I've a rite tomorrow, and a chant, and a Prior who stood in the room. I'm going to stay on this mountain and sing. Let them deal the hand without me."],
    ["What happens if you don't?","Maybe nothing. Maybe they wanted to see if I'd come running. A year ago, I would have. That's how I know I've changed."]]]],
  ["Are you in danger?","I don't think so. If they'd wanted to hurt me, they'd not have sent a letter. They wanted me to know they know. Now I know. That's all it is."],
  ["Burn it.","(He looks at it, then at the candle, and holds the corner in the flame.) You're right. It's paper. It doesn't get to tell me where to go any more."]],
 "If you're at that card game tonight, don't play. Trust me."],
28:["(The church is full of herb smoke. Brother Mikael, in his choir robe, is breathing slowly, eyes shut.) The Herbal Rite. In an hour I sing. Brother Paavo's handing up the herbs, the Prior's reading the words, and I'm trying to remember how to breathe out.",[
  ["You'll be wonderful.","I'll be a tenor in a smoky church with a lot of nervous brothers. But thank you. Being told you'll be wonderful is half the note."],
  ["Isn't everyone else busy today?","Every faction's pushing for the crown today. And up here, we're singing to some sage. I know which I'd rather. I know which my mother would rather."],
  ["What does the chant say?","'For what it gives, our thanks.' Over and over, higher every time. The last 'thanks' is the top note. It's very simple, which is why it's so hard."]],
 "Stand near the front. If I crack, I'll want to look at someone who won't laugh."],
29:["(Brother Mikael is back on the wall, but sitting differently: legs on the mountain side, back to the road.) I've decided something. Whatever happens on this island tomorrow, I'm staying. I'm taking my vows properly. Life vows, at Christmas.",[
  ["Are you sure?","I came up here to hide. I'm staying because I want to. It took me six months to tell the difference."],
  ["Does the Abbot know?","I told him this morning. He said, 'I know.' Everyone on this mountain says that. I'm beginning to think they're all mind-readers."],
  ["Everyone's talking about the line of succession.","Let them. I've got my own line of succession: me, after me. That's enough for one monk."]],
 "Tell my mother, if you ever meet her. She'll knit something to celebrate."],
30:["(Brother Mikael is on the wall, looking down at Kingsgrad, grey and quiet in the distance.) The last day. I'm looking down the mountain, you'll notice. But I'm looking because I want to see what happens, not because I'm afraid of what's coming up.",[
  ["What do you hope happens?","That it's settled without anyone getting hurt. That my mother sleeps tonight. That someone in Kingsgrad wakes up tomorrow owing nobody anything."],
  ["Will you go down to Kingsgrad ever again?","One day. To see my mother, in daylight, in my habit, with my head up. Not this week. But one day."],
  ["You've changed, this month.","So has the island. We both had the ground shake, and both of us are still standing. I'll take that."]],
 "Go on, go down. I'll be up here. Singing, probably."],
};
// Brother Paavo, the monastery's gardener (Orthodox Church). Threads: his named plants (the twelve cabbages he calls the Apostles,
// and Old Stubborn, an apple tree that hasn't fruited in nine years), the herbs he gathers and dries for the Herbal Rite, what he
// overhears from people on the path, and the garden soil warming as the mountain wakes.
DAILY['Brother Paavo']={
5:["(Brother Paavo is kneeling in the snow, talking quietly to a row of white humps.) ...and you'll be fine, all of you, it's only September. Oh. Hello. Don't mind me. I'm reassuring the cabbages.",[
  ["Do they need reassuring?","Everyone does, in September up here. There are twelve. I call them the Apostles. One of them is always a bit of a disappointment."],
  ["How do you garden on a mountain?","Slowly. With manure, prayer and a great deal of swearing under the breath. Not that last one. Forget I said that."],
  ["Do they ever answer?","Not in words. But they grow, and that's an answer. Most people I talk to don't grow at all."]],
 "Mind the third Apostle on your way out. He's tender."],
6:["(Brother Paavo is propping the lid of a wooden cold frame open with a stick.) Lettuce. Under glass. Don't tell the Abbot; he thinks lettuce on a mountain is showing off.",[
  ["Is it?","A little. But a man's allowed one vanity, and mine's green."],
  ["Who's been up the path today?","Two men from the Party in Saltwick, arguing all the way down about whether the Abbot had said yes. He hadn't said anything. He'd given them tea. People hear what they want on the way down."],
  ["You hear everything from here, don't you?","The path runs past the garden wall, and people think a man with a trowel is deaf. I don't repeat it. Much."]],
 "Close the frame if you pass it. The wind's coming round."],
7:["(Brother Paavo is standing before a small, crooked apple tree with his hands on his hips.) This is Old Stubborn. I grafted him nine years ago. He's never given me a single apple. Not one.",[
  ["Why not give up on him?","Because he's alive, and he's trying. You don't give up on a thing that's trying. Father Thyme taught me that, about novices."],
  ["Maybe he needs a talking-to.","I've tried. I've tried coaxing, I've tried scolding, I've tried singing. Brother Mikael sang to him once. He lost two leaves."],
  ["What kind of apple is he?","A Finnish Antonovka, from a cutting the old king's gardener sent up. A royal apple. That might be the trouble. They're hard to please."]],
 "Say something nice to him on your way past. It can't hurt."],
8:["(Brother Paavo is brushing soil off his habit outside the church.) The first Sunday without a king. I'll sit at the back. The Apostles don't mind if I smell of compost, but the farmers do.",[
  ["Who's come up for the service?","Juniper and Pirkko from Eastbrook, the prince, and Hilda and Olaf from the farm. Olaf complained about the hill the whole way up. Hilda told him the hill was older than his knees."],
  ["What do you hope to hear?","Something simple. Seasons come round, things die, things grow back. Sermons are best when they sound like gardening."],
  ["You don't seem nervous.","Why would I be? I'm only listening. It's the one who's talking who should be nervous."]],
 "Sit at the back with me, if you like. It's warmer, and nobody looks round."],
9:["(Brother Paavo is up a ladder in the loft over the tool shed, hanging bundles of leaves from the rafters.) The herbs for the Herbal Rite. Once a year, at the end of the month. I start drying them now, or they're too green to burn.",[
  ["What goes into it?","Sage, yarrow, juniper, mountain mint and thyme. A great deal of thyme. Every year I put in an extra bunch, to tease the Abbot. He's never said a word."],
  ["Where do they grow?","Thyme on the south slope, sage by the old wall, mint in the gully where the snow melts first. Each has its own corner, like the brothers."],
  ["Smells wonderful up there.","Like summer, folded up and put away. I come up here in February to smell it when I can't remember what green is."]],
 "Mind the ladder. It's older than I am, and less patient."],
10:["(Brother Paavo is tamping down earth round a sapling by the garden gate.) The others have gone down for the king's funeral. I stayed. I'm planting a rowan for him. A rowan lives a long time, and it keeps the bad spirits off, they say.",[
  ["That's a lovely thing to do.","It's the only thing I know how to do. A funeral's words. A tree's a promise. I'm better with promises."],
  ["Did you know the king?","He came up the mountain once, years ago, and asked me how I grew potatoes on a rock. I told him patience. He laughed and said he'd try it on his son."],
  ["Doesn't the funeral matter?","Of course it does. But someone has to stay and water the living. The dead are well looked after today."]],
 "Say a word to the rowan, if you like. It'll hear the king's name more than I will."],
11:["(Brother Paavo is filling a sack with potatoes from the cellar store.) The Abbot's sending help to the poor on the beach. I can't give much, but I can give potatoes. Potatoes are the closest thing to charity that grows in the ground.",[
  ["Where do your potatoes come from?","The seed from Hilda, at East Farm. She brings a sack up every spring and tells me I'm planting them wrong. I am. They grow anyway."],
  ["Is the man on the beach a friend of yours?","Pat? I've never met him. But I've heard the brothers talk about him on the path for years. It's about time someone stopped talking and dug."],
  ["Can I carry that?","You can carry half. I'll carry the other half, and we'll argue about which half's heavier."]],
 "Go gently on the path. The potatoes bruise, and so do I."],
12:["(Brother Paavo is crouched in the far corner of the garden, frowning.) Look here. The snow's gone, in this one patch, by the north wall. And the earth's soft. And there are worms up. In September, on a mountain.",[
  ["What does it mean?","I don't know. The soil's warm. Not much. Like a bed someone's just got out of. I've never felt it in twenty years."],
  ["Could it be the sun?","On the north wall? The sun's never been to the north wall. It doesn't know where it is."],
  ["Have you told the Abbot?","I've told the worms. They seem to know more than I do. I'll tell the Abbot when I've something to tell."]],
 "Don't step on the worms. They've come up for a reason."],
13:["(Brother Paavo is hoeing, and chuckling to himself.) Two men from Kelp Cola came up with crates of free cola this morning. On the way down one said to the other, 'Do monks even drink?' and the other said, 'Everybody drinks.' I nearly fell in the onions.",[
  ["Do monks drink?","Spring water, tea, and wine at the liturgy. And Brother Teodor's plum brandy at Christmas, which isn't drinking, he says, it's medicine."],
  ["What else do you hear on the path?","Who's been up, who's going down happy, who's going down angry. The angry ones walk faster. The happy ones stop to look at the view."],
  ["Have you tried the cola?","It tastes of a beach that's been put in a bottle. The Apostles liked it, though. I poured one on the compost."]],
 "Keep your voice down near the wall. The path has ears, and they're mine."],
14:["(Brother Paavo is sitting on an upturned bucket among the cabbages.) Everyone's gone down to the big day on the fourteenth. Me and the Prior are holding the fort. And the Apostles, of course.",[
  ["Not curious about it?","Curious, yes. But a crowd doesn't need me. A cabbage does. You'd be surprised how few things in life actually need you."],
  ["What will you do all day?","Dig. Talk to the Apostles. Listen to the Prior lighting candles. It's the quietest day the mountain will have all month."],
  ["What do the Apostles think of it all?","They think the frost is coming, and they're right. They're always right about the frost. They'd be wrong about everything else, but the frost's the thing that matters."]],
 "Go on, go down. Tell the island the cabbages send their regards."],
15:["(Brother Paavo is sweeping leaves off the garden bench.) Prince Augustus is with the Abbot today. He always comes out to the garden afterwards. He sits on this bench and doesn't say anything. I keep it swept for him.",[
  ["What's Augustus like?","Quiet. Kind. He asked me once how long it takes a tree to grow. I said, depends what you want from it. He thought about that for an hour."],
  ["Does he talk about the crown?","Never. He talks about bees, and the weather, and once about his mother. People think he's thinking about the crown. I think he's thinking about everything but."],
  ["What would you tell him?","That roots matter more than branches. But he knows that. Everyone knows it. That's not the hard part."]],
 "Leave the bench clear, if you pass. He might need it today."],
16:["(Brother Paavo is weeding, and a small radio on the garden wall is babbling about a debate.) The Prior left the wireless here. Anna and Erik are arguing. I'm letting the Apostles listen. They can decide for themselves.",[
  ["Who would the cabbages vote for?","Whoever promises rain in June and no frost in August. Nobody's promised it yet. They're holding out."],
  ["What do you think of a vote, Brother?","Every spring I choose which seeds to plant, and every autumn the garden tells me if I was right. A vote's like that. You only find out at the harvest."],
  ["Turn it off, then.","(He does.) Ah. You're right. The worms were getting agitated."]],
 "If you vote, vote for whoever likes vegetables. They're rarer than you'd think."],
17:["(Brother Paavo is on his knees in the warm corner, his hands flat on the earth.) The ground shook, and now there's steam coming off the soil. Just a breath. Like a cow on a cold morning.",[
  ["Is that dangerous?","For me? No. For the carrots, yes. They're half cooked in the row. I've never had to harvest a vegetable early because it was done."],
  ["The worms knew, didn't they?","Five days ago. I should have listened. They've all gone deep now, or gone somewhere better. Worms are wiser than men; they don't hold opinions."],
  ["Will the garden survive?","Gardens always survive. It's only the gardeners who sometimes don't. I'm being gloomy. Ignore me; it's the steam."]],
 "Don't kneel there. It's hot."],
18:["(Brother Paavo is leaning on his spade, looking down the path.) Something's been taken from the church in the night. The Prior is beside himself. And I heard nothing. Nothing on the path, all night. That's what troubles me.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","I sleep with my window open over the garden, and I hear every boot on that path, every fox, every drunk novice. Last night: silence."],
  ["So whoever took it didn't use the path?","Or they came some other way, over the rocks. I won't guess. Gardeners shouldn't. We plant and wait."],
  ["How's Old Stubborn?","Shaking with the ground, like the rest of us. Still no apples. Still trying."]],
 "Walk loud on the path. It's a comfort to me now."],
19:["(Brother Paavo is pulling carrots out of steaming earth and dropping them into a basket.) Warm to the elbow. I put my arm in to the shoulder this morning and nearly lost my sleeve. The Abbot thinks I'm exaggerating. He didn't put his arm in.",[
  ["What will you do with them?","Brother Teodor will make soup. He says pre-cooked carrots are a sign from God that he deserves a rest."],
  ["Is the Abbot worried?","The Abbot is never worried, outwardly. Inwardly he has given up butter. That's how you tell."],
  ["Are the herbs safe?","In the loft, drying, and far from the hot corner. The rite will go on. Herbs are older than volcanoes, in a way."]],
 "Have a carrot. They're nearly soup already."],
20:["(Brother Paavo is coming in through the garden gate with a basket of blue-black berries and a scratched face.) Juniper berries, from the high slope, before the ash comes. If the mountain's going to throw things at us, I want the rite's berries in first.",[
  ["Is the ash coming?","The sky's the colour of a bruise in the north. The goats on the slope have gone quiet. Animals know before we do; so do gardeners, if they're honest."],
  ["You've scratched yourself.","Juniper's a fighter. Like the lady in the clothes shop in Eastbrook. Lovely, and sharp, and she won't be picked till she's ready."],
  ["What else do you still need?","Yarrow from the gully. Then I'm done, and the loft is full, and the mountain can do as it likes."]],
 "Get home before dark. The sky looks wrong."],
21:["(Ash is falling. Brother Paavo is dragging sacking over the cold frames, coughing.) The mountain's let go! Cover the glass, cover the glass, it'll crack in the heat. Mind the Apostles!",[
  ["Are you all right?","My eyes are stinging and my lettuce is doomed. I'm fine. Help me with this corner. Pull."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","The Prior's counted us. All here. The lava's going north, away from us. The ash isn't going anywhere; it's coming here."],
  ["Forget the lettuce, Brother!","I can't. It's the only lettuce for fifty miles. You'd fight for it too, if you'd grown it."]],
 "Go! Tell them in the towns the monks are counted."],
22:["(Brother Paavo is standing in a grey garden, a handful of ash running through his fingers.) The whole garden's under it. The Apostles look like old men. But you know what this is, in ten years? The best soil on the island.",[
  ["That's very optimistic.","It's very gardening. Everything that falls is food for something. Even ash. Even kings."],
  ["Did the lettuce survive?","Two heads. Under the glass, looking at me like orphans. I'll give one to Brother Teodor and keep one for courage."],
  ["What will you plant in it?","Potatoes. The volcano's fed the ground. The ground will feed us. It's only polite."]],
 "Brush yourself off at the gate. It gets in everything."],
23:["(Brother Paavo is standing in front of Old Stubborn, open-mouthed.) Look. Look at him. One blossom. One pink apple blossom. In September. In the ash.",[
  ["How is that possible?","The warm ground. He thinks it's spring. He's nine years late and six months early, and I could kiss him."],
  ["Will it make an apple?","Probably not. It's too cold, and there's no bees. But he tried. He tried, after nine years. That's all I wanted from him."],
  ["There's a sermon in that.","Don't tell the Abbot. He'll use it, and then everyone will look at Old Stubborn, and he'll get shy."]],
 "Don't touch the blossom. Just look at it."],
24:["(Brother Paavo is shouldering a pack, and looking back at the garden.) The retreat to the peaks. I'm going, and I hate leaving them. The Abbot says he'll water. He'll forget. He forgets everything except God and chess.",[
  ["Will the garden be all right for a day?","The garden will be fine. It's me that won't. I'll be up on that rock wondering if the frost's got the lettuce."],
  ["What do you do on a retreat?","Walk, pray, sleep on stone and listen to the brothers say what they've been carrying. I mostly listen. I've got good ears for it, from the path."],
  ["Leave them a note.","(He laughs.) I have. On a stick, in the middle of the Apostles. It says: 'Back tomorrow. Be good.'"]],
 "Look after Old Stubborn's blossom while I'm gone. Just by not touching it."],
25:["(Brother Paavo is cutting cabbages and laying them in a barrow.) Back from the peaks, and the Apostles are coming in. Eleven of them. The twelfth I'm leaving in the ground a while longer. He's not ready.",[
  ["Is that the disappointing one?","He's the one I've given up on most times. So I won't give up on him now. That's the way of it, with Apostles."],
  ["How was the retreat?","Cold. Long. Good. On the rock you can hear the island breathing, all the way down. It sounded nervous."],
  ["Princess Daphne's in the news.","Daphne climbed the garden wall once, when she was eight, and ate a raw turnip in front of me to prove she could. She'll do whatever she's told not to. I like her."]],
 "Take a cabbage home. One of the good ones."],
26:["(Brother Paavo is weeding by the path wall, and he beckons you closer.) Two men from the oil company went past the wall this morning, talking about the hot springs. 'Mr Dario wants the spa,' one said, 'and Mr Dario gets what he wants.'",[
  ["The hot springs at the spa?","Up the other side of the mountain, where the water comes up fizzy. If it's still there after the eruption. Everything's moved a bit, underneath."],
  ["Should you tell anyone?","I just did. I'm a gardener; I hear things and then they grow. You can do the harvesting."],
  ["What do you think of Dario?","He came up the path in a white coat once and stepped in the compost. I don't know the man's heart, but I know his shoes."]],
 "Keep that between us and the wall."],
27:["(The loft over the tool shed is crammed with herb bundles. Brother Paavo is counting them, his lips moving.) Forty-two bunches, and one extra of thyme. For tomorrow. The Herbal Rite. Everything's dry and ready, and the ash didn't get into a single leaf.",[
  ["You must be relieved.","I'm proud. That's a sin, so I'm trying to call it relieved. It isn't working."],
  ["What happens at the rite?","We carry the herbs up to the stone above the church, we say the old words, and we burn them for the mountain. The smoke smells of every summer you've had."],
  ["Will the Abbot notice the extra thyme?","Twenty years, and he's never said a word. Either he hasn't noticed, or he's the kindest man alive. I know which I think."]],
 "Come tomorrow, if you can. It's the best day on the mountain."],
28:["(Brother Paavo is carrying an armful of dried herbs up the slope behind the church, smiling.) The Herbal Rite! Smell that. That's August, and June, and May, all in your nose at once.",[
  ["What's your part in it?","I bring the herbs and hand them up. The Prior says the words. The Abbot lights the fire. Brother Mikael sings. I just stand there smelling of compost and joy."],
  ["The island's very busy today.","Down there, yes. Every faction pushing at once. Up here we're burning sage and saying thank you. I know which I'd rather be doing."],
  ["Is it a pagan thing?","It's older than the Church, and the Church kept it. The Church keeps everything good it finds. That's the trick of it."]],
 "Stand downwind. You'll smell of summer for a week."],
29:["(Brother Paavo is sitting on the bench by Old Stubborn. The blossom has fallen, and there's something tiny and green where it was.) Look. Look closely. I'm not saying it's an apple. I'm not saying anything. I'm just sitting here.",[
  ["Is that an apple?","I said I'm not saying. If I say it, it'll hear me and stop. Gardening is mostly not saying things."],
  ["Everyone's arguing about the line of succession.","Roots, they mean. Who came from whom. They should come and garden. A root's where it is. Arguing doesn't move it."],
  ["You've waited nine years for this.","Nine years, and he gives it to me the month the whole island goes mad. That's a royal apple for you. Timing."]],
 "Don't say the word. Just walk away slowly."],
30:["(Brother Paavo is on his knees planting garlic cloves in the grey soil, one by one.) The last day, they say. The island's future, settled. And here's me planting garlic. Garlic goes in by October, whoever's king.",[
  ["Aren't you anxious about today?","A little. But the garlic doesn't know. It'll come up in spring whatever happens. I find that a comfort. Plants keep going."],
  ["What will the garden look like next year?","Better than ever, after the ash. Potatoes like fists. Cabbages like bishops. Old Stubborn might even give me two."],
  ["Thank you for showing me the garden.","Thank you for listening. Most visitors talk to me like I'm one of the cabbages. You listened like one. That's the highest compliment I've got."]],
 "Come back in the spring. You'll see what grew."],
};
// Brother Teodor, the monastery's cook (Orthodox Church). Threads: his famous mushroom soup and Pirkko's twenty-year hunt for
// its secret, which he feeds her one clue at a time; the last foraging in the forest below before the snow; the Prior's fasts and
// portions, which he gets round; and the mountain, which he thinks is simmering like a pot.
DAILY['Brother Teodor']={
5:["(Brother Teodor is stirring a vast pot with a paddle, and waves it at you.) Another one! Two visitors in a day! Sit down, there's bread, there's soup, there's a chair with only three wobbles.",[
  ["Do you feed everyone who comes up?","Everyone who comes through that door. Party men, oil men, reporters. A man with a full stomach can't hate anyone properly. I've tested it."],
  ["What's the secret of the soup?","Ha! Love, patience and a thing in a jar. The jar is the part I'm not telling. Pirkko from Eastbrook has been after it for twenty years."],
  ["Three wobbles?","Four, if you lean back. Don't lean back. Brother Anselm leaned back in nineteen seventy-four and has never been the same."]],
 "Take some bread for the walk down. Take two."],
6:["(Brother Teodor is rubbing his knees and groaning.) I've been down to the forest below, for the last chanterelles before the snow takes them. Two hours down, three hours up, and my knees have written to the Abbot to complain.",[
  ["Did you find many?","A basket and a half. Golden ones, like little trumpets. In a week they'll be gone till next year. That's mushrooms for you: they come when they like."],
  ["Isn't it dangerous, picking mushrooms?","Only if you don't know them. I know them. I've known them since I was six. My grandmother could tell a good one from a bad one by smell, and so can I."],
  ["You could ask Brother Paavo to grow them.","Paavo grows cabbages and grudges. Mushrooms won't be grown. They'll only be found. Like grace."]],
 "Take the path slowly. I'll be resting my knees in the pot."],
7:["(Brother Teodor has crumbs on his habit and looks guilty.) Pirkko sent up a box of cinnamon buns with the milk lorry. For the brothers. There were twelve. There are now... eight. The Prior counted them at breakfast.",[
  ["Who ate the four?","A cook must test everything that comes into his kitchen. For safety. Four times."],
  ["What did Brother Anselm say?","He said gluttony was a deadly sin. I said so was waste, and the buns were going stale. We are not speaking, except about buns."],
  ["Are they good?","They're superb. Cardamom, butter and a little pinch of salt. She thinks if she bakes well enough I'll give her the soup recipe. I'll keep eating until she's sure."]],
 "Take a bun. Then there's seven, and nobody can blame me."],
8:["(The monks' kitchen is full of steam and the smell of onions.) Sunday! After the service, everyone comes to the refectory, and Teodor feeds them. Farmers, shopkeepers, a prince. Soup doesn't care who's royal.",[
  ["How many are you cooking for?","Forty, if Olaf comes. Thirty-five otherwise. Olaf eats three bowls and complains about the price of every vegetable in them."],
  ["Do you listen to the sermon?","From the back, with one ear on the soup. God understands. He gave me the soup."],
  ["Can I help?","You can cut onions. Everyone who cuts onions in my kitchen cries, so nobody can tell who's moved by the sermon."]],
 "Come and eat after the service. That's an order, from the kitchen."],
9:["(Brother Teodor is chuckling over a cutting board.) The milk lorry man says the whole island's hunting for a new cola flavour today. A cola! If anyone asks me, I'll tell them what I tell everyone: flavour's in the earth. Go to the heart of the island.",[
  ["What did you mean?","My soup tastes of the forest because the mushrooms do. If you want a drink that tastes of this island, go to the island's heart. The volcano. Ha!"],
  ["Would anyone take a monk seriously about cola?","They'd write it down. People always write things down when a monk says them. They should try writing down what monks don't say. That's the useful part."],
  ["Would you drink a volcano cola?","I'd cook with it. A good braise needs a bit of smoke. But a man shouldn't drink lava. Even I have limits."]],
 "Go and taste something real today. Promise me."],
10:["(Brother Teodor is wrapping a parcel of sandwiches in a cloth.) The Abbot's off to the king's funeral. The palace will feed him little cakes the size of a thumbnail, and he'll come home starving. So: sandwiches, in his coat pocket.",[
  ["The palace has a good cook, I'm told.","Marta! We trained in the same kitchen in Kingsgrad, forty years ago. She can make a sauce that'd make a bishop weep. She can't make soup. I've told her. She's never forgiven me."],
  ["Did you want to go?","I'm a cook. Funerals are where they put out the food and nobody eats it. It breaks my heart. I'd rather stay and feed the living."],
  ["What's in the sandwiches?","Cheese, pickle and a prayer. And a little slice of ham, which the Abbot doesn't need to know about, it being a fast day."]],
 "Don't tell the Abbot about the ham."],
11:["(Brother Teodor is ladling soup into a tall tin can with a lid.) The Abbot's sending help to Pat, on the beach. Fine, he can send blankets, and hymns. I'm sending soup. You can't eat a hymn.",[
  ["Will it still be hot when it gets there?","If it goes in the van and someone drives like the Prior, it'll be hot. If it goes down with a novice on foot, it'll be cold and spilled. I've wrapped it in my spare habit."],
  ["Does Pat like your soup?","He's never had it. Twenty years on the beach and nobody's brought him the soup. I'm ashamed of the Church, and I'm the Church. That's how you know I mean it."],
  ["You're a good man, Brother.","I'm a fat man with a ladle. But God uses what He has."]],
 "If you're going to the beach, mind the can. It's hot."],
12:["(Brother Teodor is grinning from ear to ear.) I went down with the milk lorry to Pirkko's van, had a pasty, and let slip a clue. 'Something from the forest that isn't a mushroom.' Her face! She'll be up all night.",[
  ["Was that an accident?","Nothing a cook says over a pasty is an accident. It's a gift with a puzzle in it. Twenty years she's been asking. She's earned a crumb."],
  ["Why not just tell her?","Where's the joy in that? She's the only person on the island who wants something from me that isn't the Abbot's ear. I'm enjoying being wanted."],
  ["Is it pine needles?","(He looks at you, horrified.) Who told you pine needles? Is that what she thinks? Good. Let her think pine needles."]],
 "Pine needles. Ha! Don't tell her you asked."],
13:["(There's a pan of carrots on the stove, glistening brown.) Kelp Cola left crates at the gate. Free samples. The Prior wants them gone, or blessed, or both. I've boiled two down into a glaze for the carrots. It's rather good.",[
  ["Is that allowed?","It's not drinking it, it's cooking it. Cooking is the redemption of ingredients. Even cola can be saved."],
  ["How does it taste?","Sweet, a bit salty, a little like the sea at the end of a summer. The carrots are happy. The Prior will eat three helpings and complain about them."],
  ["You should sell that recipe to Phil.","Phil can come up the mountain and ask, like everyone else. And bring buns."]],
 "Take a carrot. They're not sinful, much."],
14:["(Brother Teodor is packing a wicker hamper so full the lid won't close.) The big day on the fourteenth! The brothers are going down, and the brothers are going to eat. You can't stand about in the cold on a soup's worth of breakfast.",[
  ["What's in the hamper?","Rye bread, smoked cheese, pickled herring, eight boiled eggs, and the last of the plum cake. It's for six of us. It'll do for four, if I'm one of them."],
  ["What are you expecting down there?","A lot of speeches and no lunch. Every big day on this island is the same. Someone always forgets the lunch. It won't be me."],
  ["Will you sit on the lid?","(He does. It closes. He looks proud.) There. Twenty years in a monastery: you learn how to make things fit."]],
 "Come and find our hamper, if you're hungry. Everyone does."],
15:["(Brother Teodor is stirring a small pot of plain barley porridge.) Prince Augustus is up with the Abbot today. This is his favourite. Barley, water, a pinch of salt. He apologises to it before he eats it. A prince who apologises to porridge!",[
  ["Plain porridge? For a prince?","He eats like a novice. Always has. At the palace they feed him lobster, and he comes up here for barley. That tells you something about the boy."],
  ["What do you think he'll choose?","I think he'll choose with a full stomach, if I have anything to do with it. Nobody should decide their life hungry. That's how you get revolutions."],
  ["Can I have some porridge?","If there's any left. He has three bowls, when he's troubled. I've made enough for four."]],
 "Pray for him, between mouthfuls."],
16:["(Brother Teodor is up to his elbows at the sink, and the washing up is loud.) Supper's in silence, but the washing up isn't. The radio debate has got into the brothers. Paavo's for the Democrats, Mikael won't say, and the Prior's for God, in triplicate.",[
  ["And you?","I'm for whoever eats properly. Anna eats standing up. Erik eats too fast. I've no candidate. I'll vote for whoever comes up the mountain and finishes their soup."],
  ["Does anyone argue for the Party?","Nobody here. But the milk lorry man is a Communist, and he brings our milk. So we're very polite to the Party on Tuesdays."],
  ["Do you ever disagree with the Abbot?","Only about salt. He wants less. He's wrong. The rest, I leave to God and the Prior."]],
 "Dry that plate before you go, would you? Thank you."],
17:["(Pots and pans are scattered across the kitchen floor. Brother Teodor is picking them up one by one.) The ground shook, and every pot jumped off its hook. I thought the Prior had finally lost his temper. It was the mountain.",[
  ["Is anything broken?","Two plates, a jar of honey and my nerve. The honey I'll miss. The nerve I'll get back with a sandwich."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm cooking twice as much. That's how I'm frightened. The mountain is simmering like a pot that someone's forgotten. I know that sound. Lids come off."],
  ["Let me help you pick up.","Bless you. The big one goes on the big hook, the little one on the little hook. Everything has its place, until God shakes the kitchen."]],
 "Hold on to your plate, if you're eating."],
18:["(Brother Teodor is sitting at the kitchen table with his head in his hands.) The Prior asked me where I was last night. Me! Because I've a key to the vestry for the wine. As if I'd steal a relic. I'd steal a pie. Not a saint.",[
  ["He's just being careful.","I know. I know. He has to ask. It's his job. But I've cooked for that man every day for twenty years, and he looked at me like a stranger."],
  ["Where were you last night?","Asleep in the kitchen chair, with a cloth on my face, snoring. The novices can tell you. They put a spoon in my mouth for a joke."],
  ["Do you know who took it?","If I knew, I'd have made them a meal and asked for it back. Nobody steals from a cook who's just fed them. That's the oldest rule there is."]],
 "Have a biscuit. I need someone to have a biscuit."],
19:["(The pot on the stove holds a soup so thin you can see the bottom.) The Prior's declared a fast. Three days, for the relic and the mountain. This is fast soup. It's water that's heard of vegetables.",[
  ["You look miserable.","I am a cook in a fast. I'm a painter with no colours. A singer with a sore throat. A... I'll stop. I'm hungry."],
  ["Is it really just water?","(He lowers his voice.) There's a stock cube in it. One. Hidden in the bottom. God sees it, but He's merciful."],
  ["Did Pirkko get another clue?","I sent her a note with the milk lorry. 'A spoonful of something sour.' Ha! That'll keep her busy through the fast. Someone should be cooking something."]],
 "Don't eat anything in front of me. Please."],
20:["(Brother Teodor is turning over a small, strange grey mushroom in his fingers.) I went down to the forest, to see if there was anything left. Everything smells of matches down there. Of sulphur. And look: these come up where there's been nothing for years.",[
  ["Are they safe to eat?","I don't know them. Sixty years, and I don't know them. That frightens me more than the steam does."],
  ["Why do they smell of sulphur?","The forest's breathing what the mountain's breathing. The roots know before we do. They're all leaning a little north, like they're listening."],
  ["Throw them away, Brother.","(He hesitates, then drops them in the stove.) You're right. A cook who doesn't know a mushroom should leave it alone. It's the only rule that's never failed me."]],
 "Wash your hands if you touched anything in the forest."],
21:["(Ash is coming down outside. Brother Teodor has every pot in the monastery on the stove at once.) Soup for an army! The farms, the road, whoever comes up frightened! Nobody's going to be hungry tonight while there's a fire in this kitchen!",[
  ["What can I do?","Carry! Bowls to the refectory, bread to the gate. Anyone who comes through that door gets fed. Anyone. I don't care if it's the Russian."],
  ["Are you all right?","I've ash in my eyebrows and my hands are shaking, and I've never been so useful. A cook in a disaster is the only rich man in the world."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","Every brother counted, the Prior says. Thanks be to God. Now let's make sure every brother is fed, too. Two different miracles. Both necessary."]],
 "Take a bowl before you go. Don't argue with a cook today."],
22:["(Brother Teodor is shaking flour through a fine sieve, and grey grit is coming out.) Ash in the flour. Ash in the salt. Ash in my beard. And Hilda's cows are off their milk, so there's no butter. Bread without butter. We are being tested.",[
  ["Can you still bake?","I'll bake if I have to sieve every grain by hand. Bread is the one thing on this mountain I'll not give up. Not for a volcano."],
  ["How do you cook without butter?","With oil, and sorrow. And a little lard I'd been keeping for Christmas. Don't tell the Prior about the lard."],
  ["The ash will pass.","It will. And the cows will milk again. And Paavo says the soil will be wonderful. Everybody's an optimist when they're not doing the baking."]],
 "Shake your coat out at the door. I've just swept."],
23:["(Brother Teodor is holding up a large jar with a crack down it, and the floor smells of plums.) The aftershock. My plum brandy. Seven years old. Gone, all over the larder floor. Medicine, it was. Purely medicine.",[
  ["I'm so sorry, Brother.","Thank you. You're the only person who's said so. The Prior said 'Good.' The novices are walking very slowly through the larder, breathing deeply."],
  ["Can you make more?","Next autumn, when the plums come. Seven years to make it good. I'll be old. I'm already old. I'll be older."],
  ["Was it really medicine?","For the heart. Specifically, for mine. One small glass on the feast days. Two at Christmas. The rest was for guests, I promise."]],
 "Mind the floor in the larder. And don't breathe too deeply, it's strong."],
24:["(Brother Teodor is packing rye bread and dried sausage into every brother's pack.) The retreat to the peaks. The Prior says we're to take only what we need. I need sausage. I've decided we all need sausage.",[
  ["Is that allowed?","He said what we need. He didn't say who decides. I've decided. It's the cook's prerogative."],
  ["Anything for Pirkko before you go?","I sent her a little jar of the soup itself this morning. 'Taste, and work it out.' She'll be furious. She'll be thrilled. She'll be up the mountain by Christmas."],
  ["What do you do up there?","Walk until my knees beg, sleep on rock, and listen to the brothers say things they never say at supper. And I share the sausage. That's when they say the most."]],
 "If you come up the ridge, I've got a spare sausage."],
25:["(Brother Teodor is lowering himself into a kitchen chair very carefully.) Back from the peaks. I lost a pound up there, and found it again at breakfast. My knees are not speaking to me.",[
  ["How was it?","Cold, beautiful, and the sausage was a triumph. The Prior had two pieces. He'll deny it. I have witnesses."],
  ["Everyone's talking about Princess Daphne.","I made her a birthday cake once, when she was six, for the palace. Three tiers. She ate only the icing off the top, and then asked for more icing. I liked her at once."],
  ["Would she make a good queen?","Anyone who knows what she wants from a cake knows what she wants from a country. Whether she wants a country is another question."]],
 "Sit down if you like. I'm not getting up."],
26:["(Brother Teodor is laughing over a scrap of paper.) A note from Pirkko, up with the milk. One word: 'Lingonberries?' I've sent one word back. 'Warmer.' Ha! She'll be throwing pasties at the factory wall.",[
  ["Is she close?","She's very close. She's been very close for ten years, and then she adds something clever and ruins it. The secret is to not be clever."],
  ["Will you ever tell her?","When she's got it, I'll tell her she's got it. That's not telling. That's agreeing. I'm good at agreeing, when it's with soup."],
  ["Dario wants to buy the spa, I hear.","The hot springs? I boiled eggs in them once, when I was a novice. Perfect in four minutes, and they tasted of eggs and the earth. Now they'll cost a fortune. Everything does."]],
 "Warmer, I said. Ha! Warmer."],
27:["(Brother Teodor is drying a pot very slowly.) I said something at supper I shouldn't have. About the card game tonight, at the pizzeria in Kingsgrad. Brother Mikael went as white as flour, and left his bread. Mikael never leaves bread.",[
  ["Why would that upset him?","I don't know, and I don't ask. A man who comes up the mountain doesn't owe anyone the reason. But I know the face of a man who's had a bad meal. That was it."],
  ["Is he all right?","I've left a plate in his cell, with a cloth over it and a spoon. It's what I do. It's all I can do."],
  ["You didn't mean any harm.","No. But the tongue of a cook is quick, and the ears of a young man are quicker. I'll be more careful. Pass the next pot."]],
 "If you see Brother Mikael, smile at him. He needs it."],
28:["(The kitchen smells of thyme, sage and frying bread.) After the Herbal Rite, there's a feast. The herbs that aren't burned go into the bread, and the bread goes into the brothers. It's my favourite day of the year.",[
  ["What's on the menu?","Herb bread, mushroom soup, pickled beetroot, and a pumpkin from East Farm the size of a novice. Olaf says it cost him a fortune to grow. Olaf says that of everything."],
  ["Everyone's busy down the mountain today.","Let them be busy. We'll be fed. History is made by people who've forgotten to eat. I'd rather be the man who reminds them."],
  ["You're beaming, Brother.","Full kitchen, full pots, full hearts. It's the only politics I understand."]],
 "Come and eat. There's always room for one more at the bench."],
29:["(Brother Teodor is sitting very still, with a small jar in front of him and a spoon in his hand.) Pirkko's sent a jar up the mountain. Her soup. She thinks she's got it. And she has. Twenty years, and she has. I don't know whether to laugh or cry.",[
  ["Are you sure?","I tasted it four times. To be certain. Lingonberries, birch sap, and she dried the mushrooms first. She's even got the salt right. Better than mine, a little. Don't tell her."],
  ["What will you tell her?","One word. I owe her that. I'll send it with the milk lorry in the morning. I'll be thinking about which word all night."],
  ["Are you sad the secret's out?","A little. But a recipe isn't a secret. It's a gift that hasn't been given yet. It's about time I gave it."]],
 "Twenty years. Well. Well, well, well."],
30:["(Brother Teodor is kneading dough on the big table, flour up to his elbows.) The last day, they say. Whatever they decide down there, people will come up this mountain hungry afterwards. Happy or sad. So I'm baking.",[
  ["Did you send Pirkko her word?","With the milk lorry, first thing. One word. 'Yes.' I can hear her from here, if I listen."],
  ["Are you worried about the island?","I'm worried about everyone who'll go to bed angry tonight. A full stomach is the first step towards a full heart. I can't do the second step for them. I can do the first."],
  ["What will you bake?","Everything. Rye, white, herb, sweet buns with cardamom. Pirkko's recipe, the buns. I owe her that too."]],
 "Come back hungry, whatever happens. The bread will be waiting."],
};
// Bruno, ringmaster of the travelling circus at Lake Ilmari (the Circus). Threads: thirty years on the road and his tall tales, the takings
// and the permit he hasn't got, his troupe (Igor in the bunk below, Buttons's appetite, Meg who runs things, Zora's forecasts),
// the island's politicians as an audience, the volcano and whether the circus should move on.
DAILY['Bruno']={
7:["(Bruno sweeps off his top hat with a flourish.) Ladies and gentlemen, the roads are open, and the press has arrived! Bruno, ringmaster, thirty years on the road, never once on time.",[
  ["Pleased to meet you. Nice coat.","Red, like all the best things: sunsets, wine and bad debts. I've had it since Bologna. It has been mended more times than I have."],
  ["How long has the circus been here?","Since the snow went. We came in over the ice before the king died, and then the island shut like a purse. A fine audience, but a captive one."],
  ["Is the circus licensed to perform here?","(He lowers his voice.) Licensed is such a strong word. We have a letter. It has a stamp on it. Nobody has asked what the stamp says."]],
 "Come to the big top any evening. The first row is free for the press."],
8:["(Bruno is leaning on a tent pole, reading a Eastbrook Gazette with great suspicion.) The Gazette says Sunday is a day of rest. In my profession, Sunday is when the tickets sell.",[
  ["Do you not rest at all?","I rest on the day I'm buried, and then only if the grave's got a view. Today Igor is resting. He's lifting only the small anvil."],
  ["Is the island good for business?","Everyone has a king to mourn and nothing to do about it. A man who is sad and idle will pay for a juggler. That's economics."],
  ["What does the Gazette say about you?","Nothing. I've asked three times. I've begun to think the editor is jealous of my coat."]],
 "If the church bells stop, that's us. We're louder."],
9:["(Bruno holds up a bottle of Kelp Cola to the light.) A man in a good suit tried to sponsor my circus this morning. He wanted the name on the big top. KELP COLA PRESENTS. I told him I'd think about it, which is how I say no politely.",[
  ["Would you take the money?","I'd take a goat if it paid. But I won't be a bottle on a tent. A circus has to stand for something, even if it's only itself."],
  ["Who was he?","A Kelp Cola man with a clipboard. He had a pen on a chain. I've never trusted a man whose pen is afraid of being stolen."],
  ["What would you call your own cola?","Ringmaster's Reviver. It would taste of sawdust and applause. It would sell terribly, and I'd be very happy."]],
 "A circus can drink anyone's cola. It's only the name that's not for sale."],
10:["(Bruno has taken his hat off and, for once, isn't waving it.) They bury the king today. I've buried three ringmasters in my time. The best of them said the show must go on. Today, it won't. We're dark until tomorrow.",[
  ["That's decent of you.","It's not decency. It's manners. And a little superstition. You don't juggle on the day of someone's last act."],
  ["Did you ever perform for the king?","Once, in Kingsgrad, years ago. He sat very still through the whole of it. At the end he said, 'Thank you, that was very loud.' I put it on a poster."],
  ["Will the island be all right?","The island will have a new king, or a vote, or a revolution. Whichever, they'll still want a circus on Saturday."]],
 "Go gently today. We'll be back under the lights tomorrow."],
11:["(Bruno is counting coins into a tin, with moving lips.) Fourteen krona and a button. The takings from Eastbrook. They love us, but they pay like a people who've been told life is hard.",[
  ["Is that a good night?","It's a tragedy with a trapeze. But Meg says it's enough to feed us and the horse. We're never ruined while the horse eats."],
  ["Where did the button come from?","Buttons says it's his. I say it's the dignity of the circus. We'll fight over it later."],
  ["Why are Eastbrook's wages so low?","I hear the mill hands want a fair day's pay. I told them I'd be on their side if I could find anyone to pay me one. A circus is always on the side of the underdog. Only the underdog usually can't afford a ticket."]],
 "If anyone asks, we were in Kingsgrad today. It sounds richer."],
12:["(Bruno has a map spread on a barrel and is drawing arrows on it.) A circus moves like an army, only with more sequins. I'm planning our winter. Southwards, if the ice lets us.",[
  ["Are you leaving the island?","One day. Not today. A circus that leaves a town in the middle of a story is a circus that never finds out the ending."],
  ["Where does the circus winter?","Wherever there's a barn, a barmaid and a bank manager who can be charmed. Last year it was a barn in Lapland with an organ in it."],
  ["Do you ever feel like settling?","Every year, in March, for about a day. Then I see a lorry and a road, and I forget what I was thinking."]],
 "I'll draw you a map when it's done. It'll be wrong, but it'll be handsome."],
13:["(Bruno is practising a bow in a puddle, checking his reflection.) The Cola men are giving away free samples in Kingsgrad tomorrow, I hear. Which means half the island's going to be there. I'm thinking of setting up a tent next door.",[
  ["Selling what?","Hope. Candy floss. Hope in candy floss. It's the same product, really, but one of them's honest about the sugar."],
  ["Free samples? Would you take one?","I'd take twelve, and give eleven to Igor. He's worked up a thirst lifting the ringmaster."],
  ["What do you make of the Kelp Cola people?","They sell sweet water and the feeling of being young. I sell the same thing, but mine has a tiger. Not a real one. A very loud cat."]],
 "The crowds go where the free things are. A ringmaster knows."],
14:["(Bruno is up since dawn, brushing his coat with a clothes-brush the size of a loaf.) The big day! Everyone's talking about the fourteenth, the whole island. And I, a man of the world, have no idea what's going to happen. It's wonderful.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","A crowd. Speeches. Someone crying. Someone selling sausages. I've seen that show a hundred times, only the hats change."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Never. But I'm wearing my lucky socks. I always wear them on days when other people are about to do something."],
  ["Is the circus involved?","We're a circus. We're always involved. We just wait to be asked, and if we're not asked, we turn up and it's a surprise."]],
 "If I'm not here later, I'm in the front row. Probably."],
15:["(Bruno shakes out a cape, and a great deal of dust.) Do you know, the two princes both want to be king, and they've each sent a man to ask whether I'll put a banner on the big top? A crown on one, a cross on the other. I said, 'Gentlemen, I only fly the flag of the circus.'",[
  ["Which banner is nicest?","The crown's got better gold. The cross has better manners. I'd like to see them fight it out in the ring. I'd sell tickets."],
  ["Did either of them pay you?","Not a krona. They wanted it for nothing, for the honour. I said the honour of the ringmaster is not for sale, only for hire."],
  ["Would you back a prince?","I'd back whoever laughs at my jokes. So far, it's Augustus's cook and a goat."]],
 "I'm a showman. I sit on a fence, but I do it beautifully."],
16:["(Bruno is rehearsing a speech to the horse.) Ladies and gentlemen, the Great Debate! I've asked the Communists and the Democrats whether they'd like to borrow the big top. A debate with a ringmaster holding the bell. I'd be marvellous.",[
  ["Would they let you?","They were polite. They said they'd arranged their own venue. That's what people say when they've never seen you hold a bell."],
  ["What would you ask them?","Which of you will let the circus keep its horse? The answer tells me everything."],
  ["Who's going to win the debate?","The one who speaks last and slowest. It's always the one who speaks last and slowest. I learned it from a bishop."]],
 "I've sent them the bell, in case they need it. They'll see."],
17:["(Bruno has his boot against the ground, as if listening.) Did you feel that? Just now. A little shiver, in the earth. Igor says it's the horse. The horse says nothing, which is suspicious.",[
  ["It felt like a lorry going by.","There's no lorry for a mile. It came from the north. Where the steam is. (He pulls his coat tight.) Thirty years, and I've never pitched under a volcano."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a ringmaster. I'm paid to look as though nothing's wrong. Ask me again tomorrow, and see if I'm still smiling."],
  ["What does Madame Zora say?","She's been drawing the curtains and muttering since Tuesday. When Zora goes quiet, we all go quiet. It's the only thing she's ever done that frightens me."]],
 "If the earth wants an act, it'll have to book in advance."],
18:["(Bruno is tapping a barometer, which is stuck on STORMY.) I'm told that Kelpy, the cup in the cola museum, has a rival now. A man dressed as a bottle. We're all of us, in the end, a kind of mascot.",[
  ["Have you seen Kelpy?","Not yet. But I'm told Kelpy is very popular with children. In my day we called that talent. Now it's a marketing department."],
  ["Is it bad for the circus?","Competition's good for the soul. And the little ones will still want a real clown. A cup can't fall off a unicycle."],
  ["Is the ground still shaking?","A little. Like a cat purring in a very large room. I'd feel better if it stopped. I'd feel better still if it didn't."]],
 "Tell the cup I said break a leg. Does a cup have legs?"],
19:["(Bruno is watching Meg, who is hanging upside down from the big top rope.) She says the earth's moving, and she can feel it up there. The rope hums. I said it's the wind. She said wind doesn't hum in tune.",[
  ["Is the big top safe?","It's stood through blizzards in Karelia. A bit of shaking won't harm it. But I'll be moving the audience's chairs to the lakeside, just in case."],
  ["Has anyone asked you to join a party?","Comrades have asked. The Midnight Note has asked me to come and stand in the audience and look important. I'm doing the second. It has a better bar."],
  ["What does Igor think?","Igor thinks everything will be fine. Igor thinks the sun will come up in the same place until he's dead. He's the happiest man I know."]],
 "Mind the guy ropes. They're a bit tight today."],
20:["(Bruno is holding a candle in the middle of the day.) Zora gave it to me. She says to light it if the ground does anything unfair. I said I'd light it, as soon as I've found a match.",[
  ["Does Zora really know things?","She told me, five years ago, I'd lose my hat in a river. I laughed, and then I lost my hat in a river. I don't laugh at Zora now."],
  ["Is the circus staying put?","The lake's low. The ground's solid. And we've paid for the pitch till the end of the month. I never leave a paid pitch."],
  ["What's in the air today?","Everything: a palace job whispered in Frostholm, a hotel man with a secret. Not our business. Our business is sawdust."]],
 "Don't stand under the trapeze. It's a habit I've developed."],
21:["(Bruno stands in the doorway of his caravan, grey with ash, still in his red coat.) The mountain has spoken. The sky's the colour of old porridge. And I've never in my life been so glad to see Igor, who was holding the caravan up.",[
  ["Is everybody all right?","All of us. The horse is cross, Buttons is hungry, Meg is fixing a rope, and Zora said she told us so. A normal day."],
  ["Did you see the eruption?","From the lake, over the pines. A red glow, a roar like a thousand drummers. Thirty years of shows, and nothing's ever got an ovation like that."],
  ["What now?","Now we sweep. And then we wait. I'm told a circus is the best medicine after a disaster. I'd better start believing that."]],
 "Cover your mouth if the wind turns. The ash is in everything, including the tea."],
22:["(Bruno is shaking the ash off the big top canvas with a long broom, and a lot of language.) It's like grey snow, only it's not cold and doesn't melt. The audience will think we're doing a trick.",[
  ["Will you perform in this?","I'll perform in a flood, I'll perform in a fire. But I'll ask the audience to wear scarves."],
  ["Is anyone hurt on the island?","Nobody, thank the Lord. The north has the lava, and it's keeping to the north. Everyone's frightened, and everyone's still alive. It's a fine beginning."],
  ["Would you move the circus?","Not with the roads covered. Besides, a circus in a crisis is the thing people remember. We stay. We're the only noise left."]],
 "Sweep it out of the ring before the audience arrives. Preferably into Buttons."],
23:["(Bruno is writing a poster in white paint on a black board.) GREY SKIES, BRIGHT SPIRITS. THE CIRCUS, STILL STANDING. I'm rather pleased with it. I'll nail it to a tree in Eastbrook if there's a tree that's clean.",[
  ["Are people coming?","Some. A few brave souls and a lot of children who've been kept indoors for two days. A child with ash on his shoes is a ticket-buyer."],
  ["What's happening on the island?","The Party people are fixing their machinery, and the oil lot are mending whatever's cracked. Everyone's mending. We're mending the audience."],
  ["Do you ever stop performing?","On the day I'm dead. And then I'll probably do the funeral oration myself."]],
 "Come early. The good seats are the ones that haven't got ash in them."],
24:["(Bruno has a blanket over his shoulders and a mug in both hands.) The monks are heading for the peaks, I hear. In a retreat. A fine idea. If I were a holy man with a mountain, I'd go up it too.",[
  ["Would you retreat?","A ringmaster can't retreat. It's all advance, all the time. But I'd like a week somewhere with a sofa and no applause."],
  ["What's the mood at the lake?","Tired. Kind. Igor's giving shoulder-rides to children and Buttons is telling the same joke for the ninth time. It's like a family."],
  ["Have the aftershocks stopped?","One at breakfast, a small one. The horse looked up, and that was that. The horse takes it very well."]],
 "Keep warm. A cold ringmaster is a very quiet one, and that's no use to anyone."],
25:["(Bruno has been looking at the stars through a tin cup.) Everyone on the island's got a plan for the end of the month. The prince, the princess, the party, the pastor. Me, I've got a trapeze and a very long coat.",[
  ["Who do you think will come out on top?","I think a crowd always gets what it wants, eventually, even if it doesn't know it's asking. I'd just like it to want something kind."],
  ["Is the circus on anyone's side?","We're on the side of anybody who buys a ticket. We always have been. It's the only honest politics there is."],
  ["Do you ever worry about the future?","Never about mine. I worry about the horse's. She's seventeen, and she's earned a pension."]],
 "A good night to look at the sky. Ash and all."],
26:["(Bruno is polishing a brass bell with his sleeve.) The cola people are talking about making a prince into a bottle. A Prince in every bottle. I'd have thought a prince in the ring was the better act.",[
  ["Would you sell the prince a ticket?","I'd give him the best seat in the house, and then see if he'll stand on his head. A king should be able to take a joke."],
  ["Is it true the oil man's buying a spa?","So I'm told. Where do you put the money, if you're an oil man? Into somewhere that smells nice. A circus smells of sawdust and horse, and I think that's more honest."],
  ["Does the circus have rivals?","Not on the island. In Helsinki there's one with a tiger. A real one. It hates everyone and it's not even paid."]],
 "Everyone's selling something this week. I'm giving mine away, in the evenings."],
27:["(Bruno's tin is heavy with takings, and he looks suspicious of it.) The Communists have taken over the picture-house, I hear, to show a film. A revolution at the pictures. I'll admit, it's a fine thing to do with a Tuesday.",[
  ["Would the circus show a film?","We've got no projector. We've got a trampoline and an ox. If you want revolution, you'll need a bigger tent."],
  ["Are you a revolutionary?","I'm a ringmaster. All my life I've told a crowd where to look. A revolution's just a larger crowd."],
  ["What's the mood on the island?","Tight. Like a drum. Everyone's waiting for someone to hit it."]],
 "I like a good film. Only I don't like the interval. People start thinking."],
28:["(Bruno is standing in the big top ring in full costume, alone.) The biggest push of the month, they say. The factions are all out today. And I, in my small way, am out too, rehearsing with no audience.",[
  ["Who are you rehearsing for?","The last night of the month. I want to make people laugh, whichever way it turns out. A laugh is a kind of ceasefire."],
  ["Will anything happen at the lake?","Probably nothing. Everyone will be in Kingsgrad, shouting. The lake's a good place to be on a day like this: quiet and wet."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A little. But not of the shouting. It's the quiet afterwards that I worry about."]],
 "Let them do their pushing. We've got the sawdust."],
29:["(Bruno is sitting on a barrel, with the whole troupe's boots in a row in front of him.) Cleaning boots the night before the last night. Every circus does it. If you're going to go out, go out well-shod.",[
  ["What's the plan for tomorrow?","The plan is to put on the best show I've ever put on, and see who turns up. Maybe nobody. Maybe all of them."],
  ["Is everyone ready?","Meg's checked every rope twice. Igor's lifting. Buttons has been fed. Zora says she's not going to tell me anything. Which I find very reassuring."],
  ["What will you say if the island asks for a speech?","I'll tell them: ladies and gentlemen, whatever you decide, mind the step. It's an old circus saying."]],
 "Early to bed. A ringmaster needs his voice."],
30:["(Bruno stands in the lakeside dawn, hat in hand, looking at the grey sky.) The last day. Whatever the island decides, I've had the pleasure of an audience of a lifetime. I only wish I'd had a better poster.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm always nervous before curtain-up. It's what makes the first bow worth taking."],
  ["What will the circus do after today?","Pack the caravans, feed the horse, and wait for the road to say where. Wherever it says, we'll go. We always do."],
  ["Any last words for the British press?","Spell my name right. And say the coat is red, not scarlet. Scarlet is a woman I once knew in Hamburg."]],
 "Ladies and gentlemen, thank you. Mind the step on your way out."],
};
// Buttons, jester of the circus at Lake Ilmari (the Circus). Threads: he is always hungry, his terrible jokes, Meg who catches him,
// his rivalry with Bruno's hat, his wish to be taken seriously for one day, the volcano and the aftermath.
DAILY['Buttons']={
7:["(Buttons juggles three apples, drops one, and eats it.) Ah! A visitor! You've come at an excellent moment: I've got three apples and a feeling of destiny. Now I've got two apples.",[
  ["I'm the British reporter.","A reporter! Then you can put me in the paper. 'Local Jester Hungry.' It's not news, but it's true."],
  ["Tell me a joke.","What's red and white and goes up and down? A candy-striped lift. (He waits.) I'm told it's better in the dark."],
  ["How's life at the lake?","Wet and wonderful. Meg says I'm to behave. I say, what's the fun of a jester who behaves?"]],
 "If you've got a sandwich, I can always find room."],
8:["(Buttons is lying on his back on the jetty, with his cap over his face.) Sunday. The one day Meg lets me lie down without a rope round my ankle. Don't wake me unless there's cake.",[
  ["Is there cake?","There's a rumour of cake. It began in Bruno's caravan and hasn't been confirmed. I'm following it like a dog."],
  ["Do you ever take a day off?","Every day's a day off, if you do it right. Bruno calls it idleness. I call it holding the lake still."],
  ["What's Meg doing?","Mending a rope, and not speaking to me. It's a terrible thing, a woman mending a rope. You know it's about you."]],
 "Wake me for lunch. Or cake. Or the end of the world, whichever comes first."],
9:["(Buttons has a bottle of cola in each hand and a worried face.) Everyone's trying to make a new cola. I've been asked to taste six. I've tasted nine. Don't ask me how.",[
  ["Which is the best?","The blue one. It tasted of nothing. It's a very restful cola. It would make a fine pillow."],
  ["Who asked you to taste them?","A man in a suit, a woman in a red scarf, and a very small boy. The boy had the best palate. He said they all tasted like medicine."],
  ["Are you feeling all right?","I'm fizzing. I think I'll float away. Hold my ankle."]],
 "If I burp tonight in the ring, it's not in the act."],
10:["(Buttons has taken off his cap and is holding the bells in his hand so they don't ring.) The king's funeral. I've never been so quiet in my life. Meg says it's good for me. I think it's like being underwater.",[
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","No. But Bruno says he said the circus was very loud. And I thought, how rude. And then I thought, how true."],
  ["It's a hard day for everyone.","Yes. I made a joke this morning, and I couldn't finish it. It's the first time. Meg held my hand and I felt better."],
  ["Will you perform tomorrow?","I'll tumble and juggle. I'll be silly, and everyone will say how brave. That's what jesters are for: to help people be sad and then stop."]],
 "Be gentle today. If you can't be gentle, be asleep."],
11:["(Buttons is sitting in the sawdust, counting something on his fingers.) I'm trying to work out how many sausages I'm owed. Bruno says we're paid by the show. I say we're paid by the sausage.",[
  ["How many sausages are you owed?","Eleven. Possibly twelve. It's complicated. There was one that was half a sausage and I'm not sure about the legal status."],
  ["Do you get paid properly?","In smiles and soup. I hear the mill hands in Eastbrook are asking for a fair day's pay. I'm with them. I'd go on strike, but I'd miss the juggling."],
  ["What does Meg say about it?","She says I'm to stop counting and start tumbling. She says the sausages will come. She's never been wrong about a sausage."]],
 "If you see a sausage on the road, think of me."],
12:["(Buttons has Meg's hat on, and is trying to look dignified.) Meg's gone to look at the horse. She told me not to touch her hat. I've touched it. I'm now, technically, an acrobat.",[
  ["It suits you.","It's too big. It comes down over my eyes. It's like being kissed by a very large mushroom."],
  ["How did you and Meg meet?","In a ditch outside Tampere. I'd fallen off a unicycle. She was the only one who didn't laugh. I fell in love on the spot, and then she laughed."],
  ["Has she forgiven you for the hat?","She's about to find out. Run if you like. I'd rather not be a witness."]],
 "I'll be in the caravan, pretending to have been there all day."],
13:["(Buttons is in the middle of the ring, throwing his cap up and catching it.) They're giving free cola away in Kingsgrad tomorrow, I hear. I'm thinking of going as a bottle. A very short, very hungry bottle.",[
  ["Would you do the juggling for them?","For a crate, I'd juggle the entire marketing department. Bruno says we've got our dignity. I say dignity doesn't fizz."],
  ["What's Meg think of the idea?","She's said no twice, and I'm waiting for her to say yes a third time. It's the third that counts."],
  ["Is the food good at Kelp Cola?","There's a woman called Pirkko with a van. I hear she makes pancakes that would make a grown man cry. I'd cry."]],
 "A hungry jester's a dangerous jester. Remember that."],
14:["(Buttons has polished every bell on his cap and is now wearing it with great seriousness.) Today's the big day. The fourteenth! I don't know what's happening, but I've been told to look my best.",[
  ["What's going to happen?","Nobody will say. Bruno says speeches, Meg says nothing, and Zora just looked at me and said 'eat something'. Which is her worst forecast yet."],
  ["Are you excited?","I'm so excited I can't juggle. I tried, and I hit myself on the nose. It's a terrible feeling when your own nose lets you down."],
  ["Will you be telling jokes?","Only if someone asks. And if they do, I'll tell them the one about the bishop. It's very short. So is the bishop."]],
 "Wish me luck. And save me a seat near the sandwiches."],
15:["(Buttons holds up a gold paper crown in one hand and a wooden cross in the other.) A man from the palace gave me the crown. A monk gave me the cross. I now have the island's two biggest problems, and neither of them is lunch.",[
  ["Which one are you going to wear?","The crown makes my ears itch. The cross makes me feel I should be nicer. So I'll wear the crown and be nicer."],
  ["What do you think of the princes?","I think they should have a custard-pie fight. Winner's king. It's how things are done in the best circuses."],
  ["Does Bruno have an opinion?","Bruno says he's neutral. He's wearing both hats and a smile. He's the finest fence-sitter I know."]],
 "Both's too heavy for one head. Even a fool's."],
16:["(Buttons is lying on a barrel, practising a speech to the sky.) 'Ladies and gentlemen, my opponent is a goat!' I'm rehearsing for the Great Debate. Nobody's asked me, but I'd win.",[
  ["Who'd you debate?","A Communist and a Democrat, one in each hand. I'd say to the first, 'Share the sausages.' And to the second, 'Let the sausages vote.'"],
  ["Are you going to listen to it?","I'm told it's very long. I'll bring a snack. If it gets dull, I'll juggle it."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one with the better snacks. It's a general rule of politics, and a well-known truth about juggling."]],
 "Don't tell them I'm rehearsing. They might ask me to speak."],
17:["(Buttons is holding his juggling balls still, one in each hand, and not smiling.) The ground did a funny thing just now. I was in mid-toss, and the balls came down at the wrong time. It was like being juggled.",[
  ["Was it an earth tremor?","Bruno says so. He's very brave about it, and he's standing very near the door. I'd say the lake's got a hiccup."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. I've never fainted, but I'm told it's very restful. Meg says if I faint, she'll leave me where I fall."],
  ["Is there steam from the volcano?","A little white thread, up in the north. Like someone's left a kettle on. I'd like to find out who, and give them a talking-to."]],
 "If the ground shakes again, I'll be under the table. Bring a biscuit."],
18:["(Buttons is eating something out of a paper bag, rather proudly.) A man at the Cola museum has dressed up as a cup, and he's called Kelpy, and I've got a dreadful feeling he's better at it than I am.",[
  ["Is a cup funnier than a jester?","A cup is a cup. It has no legs and cannot juggle. But children cry when it leaves. I've never made a child cry on leaving. I'm taking notes."],
  ["Would you like to meet Kelpy?","I'd like to take him for a sandwich. Two entertainers, over lunch. We could swap jokes. Mine are worse but louder."],
  ["Have the shakes stopped?","Not quite. The ground twitches like a dog dreaming. Igor holds the caravan down, which helps a bit."]],
 "Don't tell the cup I said it. We're colleagues."],
19:["(Buttons has painted a small grey cloud on his cheek.) Meg says it's silly. I say if the island's going grey, the jester should match. It's called solidarity. Or possibly face paint.",[
  ["What are the Communists up to?","They want comrades, I hear. I'd be a good comrade. I share my sandwiches, and I've got a red nose."],
  ["Have you been to the Midnight Note?","Once. Rita sang and I cried into a bowl of peanuts. I've never been so moved by anything that wasn't food."],
  ["Is Meg still cross about the hat?","She's cross about the hat, the apples, and the thing with the rope. She says I'm very reliable. She means it kindly, I think."]],
 "If you see a comrade, tell him I've got a red nose already."],
20:["(Buttons is staring at the sky from the jetty with his chin in his hands.) Madame Zora's been predicting something for three days, and she won't tell me what. She just looks at me and says 'Buttons, wear boots.' I'm wearing two pairs.",[
  ["Does she mean the volcano?","She'd never say. She says things like 'a great fire in a quiet place' and then offers you a biscuit."],
  ["Are the others worried?","Igor says it's nothing. Bruno says it's everything. Meg says it's Tuesday. I think it's a bit of all three."],
  ["What are you going to do?","Sit tight and tell jokes. It's all a jester can do when the earth's going to sneeze."]],
 "Wear your boots. Both pairs, if you're me."],
21:["(Buttons is covered in grey from cap to toe, like a statue that's just heard a joke.) It went up! The whole mountain! There was a roar like a thousand wardrobes falling down stairs. And then it rained ash, and Meg held me, and I held a sandwich.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","We're all safe. The lava's far in the north. Nobody's hurt. I've never been so glad to be silly and alive."],
  ["What did it look like?","A red light in the sky and a black cloud on top, like a hat. It wasn't funny. And then it was, a bit, when Bruno sneezed grey."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I was. I'm less so now. Fear's like a joke: it's worst before the punchline."]],
 "Brush your shoulders off. You look like a statue of a reporter."],
22:["(Buttons is sweeping the ash off the jetty with a juggling club.) I've found a use for it at last. It's a very bad broom, and a very good club. Don't tell Bruno. He'll want to put it in the act.",[
  ["How's the camp?","Grey. Quiet. Everyone's helping everyone, which is a bit unnerving. I helped Zora carry a table. She said thank you. I nearly dropped it."],
  ["Is there food?","Bruno's got a pot of something on the stove. It's got ash in it, but it's warm. I'm starting to think ash is just a very small spice."],
  ["How's Meg?","She's checking every rope for dust and humming. When she hums, I know she's worried. It's a nice hum."]],
 "If you find a clean apple, I'll trade you a joke."],
23:["(Buttons is wearing a scarf over his nose and mouth, and his voice is muffled.) I'm a bandit. A dust bandit. Stand and deliver. Actually, I've forgotten what I was going to ask for. Biscuits?",[
  ["Has anyone been to the village?","Eastbrook's mending. The Communists and the oil men are mending things, I hear. A great many spanners about. Spanners have never been funnier."],
  ["Are the audiences back?","A few. They sit in the dark and don't say much, but they laugh at the oddest moments. I think they laugh because they're alive."],
  ["Do you feel funny?","I feel peculiar, which is a start. Funny comes later."]],
 "Pass the biscuits. I'm nursing a thought."],
24:["(Buttons is sitting on the caravan steps with his cap in his lap.) The monks have gone up to the peaks, they say, to pray for us all. I've said a prayer too. It was mostly 'please let there be a pie'.",[
  ["Do you think it'll be answered?","Prayers are like jokes. Some land and some fall flat. But the ones that fall flat make you listen all the harder."],
  ["Is the aftershock still there?","It comes and goes. Like Bruno's temper, only more polite. We've been rehearsing in the open, and the audience clap in time with the shakes."],
  ["What do you want most?","A full stomach, a warm bed, and Meg to say I'm funny. In that order. It used to be in the other order. I'm older now."]],
 "A pie's a very small miracle. I'll take it."],
25:["(Buttons is turning a paper crown over and over in his hands.) They say there's a crown for everyone, and a princess for democracy. I don't understand any of it. But I like the sound of a crown for everyone. I'd wear mine to dinner.",[
  ["Who'd you give a crown to?","Meg first, then the horse, then Igor. Then I'd have a very silly parade. And then lunch."],
  ["What's all the talk about?","I think the royals and the Democrats are doing something clever. I can't follow it, but I'd clap if someone told me to."],
  ["Do you like the princess?","I've never met her. But I hear she's kind, and a kind person's always worth a laugh."]],
 "A crown on every head. Even mine. Even the horse's."],
26:["(Buttons is carrying a cup with a face painted on it.) I've made a rival to Kelpy. It's called Mugsy. It talks. I do the voice. It's the worst puppet in the island and my finest achievement.",[
  ["What does Mugsy say?","'Pour me.' He only knows the one line. It's very dramatic. Bruno says it's the best thing I've done."],
  ["Heard about the Cola prince thing?","A prince in every bottle! It's marvellous. I'd drink a prince. I mean — I'd drink a bottle with a prince on it. It's different."],
  ["Is there anything on at the lake?","Rehearsals. And a very serious dinner. Meg's making soup. I've promised not to look at the pot."]],
 "Mugsy sends his regards. And asks to be filled."],
27:["(Buttons is wearing a Red Army cap that's far too big.) The Communists are showing a film tonight in Eastbrook. It's a revolution at the pictures. I said I'd go in costume, and Meg said I'm in costume already.",[
  ["What's the film about?","I'm told it's long, black and white, and has people shouting at wheat. I'd go for the interval ice cream."],
  ["Are you going to the pictures?","If someone buys me a ticket. A jester's wallet is a very empty thing."],
  ["What's the mood?","Taut. Everyone's walking on tiptoe, and I'm doing my best to tiptoe the other way. It's called comedy."]],
 "I'll tell you the plot afterwards. Which is to say, I'll make it up."],
28:["(Buttons has been up since four, rehearsing a cartwheel and failing.) The biggest day of pushing, they say. Everyone out on the road, waving banners. And I'm doing cartwheels. We all do what we can.",[
  ["Is it a big day for the circus?","The biggest crowds of the month, if they come this way. Bruno's polishing the bell. Igor's lifting the ringmaster just for practice."],
  ["Are you worried about tomorrow?","I'm worried about tomorrow, the day after, and my left shoe. It's got a hole, and the ash gets in."],
  ["Do you have a plan?","I'm going to do the thing I do best: fall over, get up, and bow. It's all politics is, really."]],
 "Don't tell the crowds I'm scared. Tell them I'm funny. It's the same thing."],
29:["(Buttons is sitting with Meg on the caravan steps, her head on his shoulder.) It's the eve of the last day. We're not saying much. Meg says it's nice to be quiet with me, which is the best thing anyone's said all month.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Full and empty at the same time. Like after a big meal. The island's got a lot to decide, and I've got a sandwich."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Go to the show. Do the show. Whatever happens, the show's the thing. It's the one thing we can all agree on."],
  ["Is Meg all right?","She's perfect. She's thinking about ropes. When she's thinking about ropes, she's happy."]],
 "Sleep well. Dream of sausages. I will."],
30:["(Buttons is standing at the lakeside, cap in hand, a rare look of solemnity on his face.) The last day. It's funny: I've told a thousand jokes this month, and I can't think of one.",[
  ["That's not like you.","No. It's what the day does. Bruno says it's the pause before the punchline. I hope he's right."],
  ["What will you do afterwards?","Eat. Then juggle. Then eat again. The island will be what it'll be, and I'll be a jester in it."],
  ["Any message for London?","Tell them we were here, and that the people of this island are kind, and silly, and cold. And that I'm still owed twelve sausages."]],
 "Thanks for listening. Nobody ever does. It's why I'm so loud."],
};
// Carlo, the Crime Syndicate's candidate for leader of the Republican Party, living in Hope Springs Residences (Crime Syndicate). Threads: his vanity
// and good suits, his speeches (lower taxes, safer streets), his rivals Harald and Magnus, Margit and the ballot, Paola next door, the Colonel's dachshund.
DAILY['Carlo']={
6:["(Carlo is adjusting his tie in the hall mirror, and doesn't turn round.) Ah. The press. Come in, come in. Mind the carpet. You're looking at the next leader of the Republican Party.",[
  ["That's a bold claim.","A modest one. I'd have said the next leader of the island, but I don't want to frighten the Colonel."],
  ["What do you stand for?","Lower taxes and safer streets. It's all anyone wants. They want to keep their money and walk home at night."],
  ["Who's backing you?","Friends. Good friends, in good places. A man with the right friends never has to ask for anything twice."]],
 "Put that in your paper. Spell my name with a capital C."],
7:["(Carlo is trying three ties against his shirt in the mirror.) The roads are open, which means the voters can reach me. I've been asking myself: red, blue, or a very small pattern. The pattern says 'trustworthy'.",[
  ["Which one do you pick?","The pattern. It's the one that doesn't frighten the elderly."],
  ["Are you nervous about the campaign?","Nervous? I've never met a nerve I couldn't smile at."],
  ["Who are your rivals?","Harald, from the Cola people, and Magnus, from the oil. Two decent men, and they'll both be disappointed."]],
 "Wish me luck. Not that I need it. It's a courtesy."],
8:["(Carlo is practising a speech on the dachshund, who's lying on his foot.) 'Friends, neighbours, Republicans.' Brigadier doesn't blink. It's a very good sign. He's the toughest crowd in Hope Springs.",[
  ["That's the Colonel's dog?","He visits. He's taken a liking to me. It's my aftershave. It's very expensive, and I believe it contains liver."],
  ["What's your speech about?","Opportunity. Security. Taxes. And at the end, a small, sincere pause. It always gets applause."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Whenever there's an audience. The monastery's a bit steep, but the view's excellent."]],
 "Don't let the dog follow you home. He's got a flat to protect."],
9:["(Carlo has a newspaper open at a photograph of himself.) Look at that jaw. The Gazette called me 'a man of the moment'. I'm having it framed. It's the first review I've had that wasn't from my mother.",[
  ["What do you think of the cola news?","Harald's company is making a new flavour, I hear. It's a clever move. If he's sweet, they'll vote for him. I'll have to be sweeter."],
  ["Is the Syndicate involved in the race?","The Syndicate? I have no idea what you mean. I'm the candidate of the sensible, law-abiding people of Hope Springs."],
  ["Who reads the Gazette?","Everyone, in Eastbrook. In Hope Springs, we read it for the weather and the obituaries."]],
 "I'll sign a copy for you. It's a rare chance."],
10:["(Carlo is in a black suit and an immaculate black tie, speaking gravely.) A sad day. The king's funeral. I went, in the third row from the back. I'd like to think he saw me. I wore my best.",[
  ["Did you speak to anyone?","I shook many hands. I asked after everyone's health. The prince thanked me. I think. He might have been sneezing."],
  ["Was it moving?","Very. I cried a little, in the right places. It's a talent. I've been told it's very endearing."],
  ["Will it change the race?","A king dies and a new man rises. It's the oldest story. I intend to be the new man, in a better suit."]],
 "Be respectful today. And be seen to be. It's the best of both worlds."],
11:["(Carlo is counting a roll of notes, then puts it quickly in a drawer.) A donation. From a very generous friend. I've never asked where it comes from. It's the surest way of staying honest.",[
  ["Who's the friend?","A well-wisher. A man who likes order. I'm told he's fond of the island."],
  ["Do you think wages are fair?","I think the mill hands deserve more. And the owners deserve less trouble. A good leader finds the point where everyone's slightly disappointed."],
  ["Would you take a stand?","I'd take a stand if it came with a balcony. A man should be seen to stand."]],
 "A generous heart is a very sound investment."],
12:["(Carlo is polishing a pair of gleaming black shoes.) A candidate's shoes are his first speech. People look down before they look up. I've been told mine say 'competent, prosperous, slightly dangerous'.",[
  ["Is that your message?","The first two. The third is for the ladies."],
  ["Who's winning the race?","In Frostholm, Magnus. In Kingsgrad, Harald. In Hope Springs, me. In the rest, nobody knows who they are. It's a very open contest."],
  ["Where do you stand on oil?","I stand on a very good carpet. It's a better position than most."]],
 "Wear good shoes. It's the only advice I'm sure of."],
13:["(Carlo is leaning on the mantelpiece, a glass of something pale in his hand.) There's a recipe, I'm told. The Don's after it. I've told him I'm a politician, not a chef. It's a very good joke. He didn't laugh.",[
  ["What kind of recipe?","A cola one. It's not my business. But if there's a recipe, there's a margin. And if there's a margin, there's a vote."],
  ["Do you ever worry about the Don?","I worry about the weather, my tailor, and my hair. The Don is a very good friend, and I don't ask questions."],
  ["Is it a big week?","It's a week. They all feel big, when you're the one in the mirror."]],
 "I'd offer you a drink, but it's for the cameras. Wait till they arrive."],
14:["(Carlo is in his best suit, a carnation in the buttonhole, and a small, perfect smile.) The great gathering. I've been up since dawn. I've rehearsed seven expressions. I'm using three.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Crowds. Hands to shake. A few small speeches. And at the end, a great many people who'll remember my face."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm exhilarated. Nervousness is for men with something to hide."],
  ["Is Paola going with you?","She's coming along, as an escort. She'll stand behind me and look like a very fine coat-stand. People find it reassuring."]],
 "Come and see it. I'll save you a good view. For a price. I'm joking."],
15:["(Carlo has a pair of cufflinks, one in the shape of a crown, one in the shape of a cross.) A gift from two admirers. I'll wear one on each wrist. I like to be balanced.",[
  ["Who do you back, Leopold or Augustus?","The man who wins. It's the only sensible position."],
  ["Does the Republican Party have a view?","Not yet. It's waiting for a leader. It's a very patient party."],
  ["What would you do if you were prince?","Lower the taxes and raise the hat. It's a good rule."]],
 "I'll have to tell the tailor. He loves a good dilemma."],
16:["(Carlo is rehearsing an answer to an imaginary question, with a hand on his heart.) The Great Debate. I've not been invited, of course. It's Communists and Democrats. But I'd have been marvellous. I've got a profile for it.",[
  ["What would you say?","I'd say: 'Friends, I'm neither left nor right. I'm in the middle, and the middle's where the money is.'"],
  ["Who'll win the debate?","The one who looks at the camera. There's no camera, I know. But he'll still look at it."],
  ["Will you go?","Only if I'm asked to sign something. I like a good signing."]],
 "If it gets dull, I'll tell a joke. It always helps."],
17:["(Carlo has a very slight tremor in his hand, and a very rigid smile.) The floor shook. A little. I've asked the building manager to look into it. He said he'd look into it, and then he looked up, and then he left.",[
  ["Was it an earth tremor?","It was a mild one. Entirely natural. I intend to include it in my speech, as a symbol of change."],
  ["Are you worried?","Not at all. I've got excellent insurance, and an excellent tailor. Between them, I'm covered."],
  ["What about the Syndicate's decision?","A decision? I've no idea. If it's executive, I'm sure it's in the best interests of everyone. They always are."]],
 "Don't stand near the chandelier. It's very expensive, and a bit wobbly."],
18:["(Carlo has his hair arranged in a very careful wave.) I'm told that Kelp Cola has a new mascot, a cup called Kelpy. It's a clever idea. A mascot can't be asked about taxes. I wonder if I could be a mascot.",[
  ["Would you dress up?","If the costume were tailored. I don't do polyester."],
  ["What about Harald?","A decent man. A little dull. He's got the face of someone who's always about to apologise."],
  ["How's the campaign?","Rising. I've had some very positive letters. One from my mother. She says I'm doing very well. I think she meant it."]],
 "A candidate should never sweat. It's bad for the shirt."],
19:["(Carlo is polishing a very large silver cup.) I'm having a trophy made. For the winner. It's quite beautiful, and it's got a very large space for the name. I've left it blank, out of modesty.",[
  ["Whose name will go on it?","I couldn't say. But it's a very long space, and I've a short name."],
  ["Have you heard about the comrades?","The Communists want volunteers. A very good idea. I'd rather have donors. Volunteers expect to be thanked."],
  ["Is it a hard campaign?","It's a bit like a long dinner. You have to keep smiling, and avoid the soup."]],
 "Keep the cup out of the draught. It tarnishes."],
20:["(Carlo is sitting very straight, looking at the clock.) A quiet evening, I think. Paola's gone out on business. I don't ask. It's a courtesy between neighbours.",[
  ["Is she all right?","Paola's always all right. It's the rest of us I worry about."],
  ["What do you do when she's away?","I practise. Vowels, mostly. It's a very big part of being a politician. A good O wins an election."],
  ["Is there anything going on tonight?","A hotel, in Eastbrook, and a man in it. I don't know why I mention it. Just a thought."]],
 "I'm retiring early. A politician needs his beauty sleep."],
21:["(Carlo sits in his armchair in a grey film of ash, looking at his own reflection.) I've never been so dusty in all my life. My hair's gone grey. My suit's gone grey. For once, I match the island.",[
  ["The volcano erupted.","So I noticed. It was an extraordinary sound. I've asked my staff to find out what it means, politically."],
  ["Are you hurt?","Only in dignity. The Colonel's dog hid under my chair. I've never been so honoured."],
  ["What will you do now?","Dust. Then call a press conference. A crisis is an opportunity, if you've got the right tie."]],
 "Don't touch the sofa. It's cream. It was cream."],
22:["(Carlo is holding a dustpan in one hand and a speech in the other.) I've been sweeping. It's very good for the image. A leader who sweeps is a leader who cares. I'd like a photograph.",[
  ["Is that real sweeping?","It's real enough. The photograph's tomorrow. I've been rehearsing."],
  ["What's the mood in Hope Springs?","Grim. The rich don't like grey. It's a very unfashionable colour."],
  ["What's your message?","'Together, we'll clean it up.' It's got a ring to it. Taxes and safe streets, and a clean pavement."]],
 "Come back tomorrow. I'll be photogenic."],
23:["(Carlo is polishing a brass plate that says CARLO FOR LEADER.) I've had this made. It's for the door of the HQ. The ash has got on it, but I'll rub it off. It's a symbol: you can't keep a good man down.",[
  ["Is the campaign continuing?","Of course. A volcano doesn't stop an election. It just makes it grubbier."],
  ["What's Margit saying?","Margit's the party secretary. She says the ballot box needs cleaning, and I need to stop talking. She's a very capable woman."],
  ["Are the others still in?","Harald and Magnus are. I hear Magnus is mending pipes. I hope he gets his hands dirty. It's very good for his image."]],
 "A clean plate, a clean conscience. I've got the first one."],
24:["(Carlo has a silk handkerchief over his nose and a very small cough.) The Don's put a crew on the clean-up. I'm told it's all in hand. I never ask. A man in my position doesn't need to know where the mop goes.",[
  ["Does Paola do the cleaning?","Paola does a great many things. Mostly, she looks after me. It's the best-kept secret in the building."],
  ["Is the Syndicate part of the clean-up?","Whatever the Syndicate does is entirely lawful, and rather good for the neighbourhood."],
  ["Is it getting to you?","A little. Ash does terrible things to a collar."]],
 "Another few days, and it'll be over. I'll be a leader or a very well-dressed citizen."],
25:["(Carlo is in front of the mirror in a new suit, a very light grey.) It's called 'volcanic'. The tailor sold it to me as a statement. It's a statement that says: I've seen the worst, and I'm still well dressed.",[
  ["Is it a good suit?","It's the best suit on the island. It makes me look like a man who's about to give a speech about a bridge."],
  ["Any news on the royals?","A crown for everyone, I hear. It's a very democratic gesture. If I were royal, I'd have a very good crown."],
  ["How's the race?","Close. It's a very sharp contest. Three men, three suits, and one ballot box."]],
 "Don't smudge it. It's silk. It's all I've got."],
26:["(Carlo has a new poster in his hands and is frowning at it.) Look at it. 'CARLO: SAFE STREETS, SOFT TAXES'. It's a lot of words. I'd have gone for 'CARLO' and a nice portrait. The advisers say it's a bit vain.",[
  ["It is a bit vain.","It's confident. There's a difference. About four inches of jaw."],
  ["What about Harald's prince-in-a-bottle?","A clever idea. But a prince in a bottle doesn't vote. A man in a suit does."],
  ["Do you think you'll win?","I know I'll look good. The rest is the Don's department."]],
 "I'll sign one for you. It's a collector's item."],
27:["(Carlo is straightening a large red rosette on his lapel.) I'm told there's a high-stakes game at the Club tonight. I'd go, but a candidate shouldn't be seen to gamble. I'll be at home, learning my speech.",[
  ["Do you gamble?","Only on myself. It's the safest bet I know."],
  ["What do you hear from the Communists?","They're showing a film. A revolution at the pictures. I've no objection. I'd like a cameo. A man in a good suit, being applauded."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A little. The silence in the room is getting louder. I'd like to fill it with something expensive."]],
 "A good night's sleep, a clean collar, a clear conscience. I've two of the three."],
28:["(Carlo is in a cream suit, with Paola a silent step behind him.) The biggest day. I've been told to say very little and smile a great deal. It's the best advice I've ever had, and I never thought I'd take it.",[
  ["What will you say at the HQ?","'Friends, I'm honoured.' Then a pause. Then something about taxes. It's all in the pause."],
  ["Is everything ready?","The suit is pressed, the speech is short, and the bodyguard is in a very good mood. I couldn't ask for more."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm excited. And a bit frightened. A little of each. It's good for the skin."]],
 "Wish me luck. Or don't. I've got a very good tailor."],
29:["(Carlo is pacing the carpet in his socks, a glass of water in one hand.) Tomorrow it's all over. I've lost track of what I promised. Something about streets. And about taxes. And a very small bridge.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm calm. I'm always calm. It's the hands that give me away."],
  ["Is Paola ready?","Paola's been ready since breakfast. She sits there like a very polite thunderstorm."],
  ["Any last-minute plans?","A haircut. A deep breath. A final look in the mirror. It's all a man needs."]],
 "I'll sleep like a baby. A very well-dressed baby."],
30:["(Carlo stands in front of the mirror, in his best suit, and straightens his tie for the last time.) The last day. Whatever happens, I've looked marvellous. It's a great comfort. Not everyone can say it.",[
  ["Any last words?","Vote for whoever you like. But vote. It's the only way to complain afterwards."],
  ["What will you do after today?","Whatever the Don asks. Whatever the island needs. And I'll do it in a very good suit."],
  ["Thank you for talking.","The pleasure was mine. You've a good face for a reporter. Honest, and slightly tired."]],
 "Goodbye. I'll try to look gracious, win or lose."],
};
// Colonel Halvorsen, commander of the island's army at the Officers' HQ (The Army). Threads: the army stays out of politics (he insists), his frequent dinners at the palace, his neighbours Harald and Carlo at Hope Springs, Sergeant Rask's ledgers, the ash and the army's part in cleaning up.
DAILY['Colonel Halvorsen']={
6:["(Colonel Halvorsen rises, straightens his tunic and does not offer to shake hands.) The British reporter. Welcome to the Officers' HQ. I assume you know that the army has no politics. Please make a note of it.",[
  ["Noted. How long have you commanded?","Eleven years. Before that, a lifetime in uniform. I've seen kings and snow come and go. The army stays."],
  ["Does the army really stay out of politics?","Absolutely. We serve the island. Whoever's in charge is a matter for the island. I merely take dinner where I'm invited."],
  ["Where do you dine?","Here, at home, and now and then at the palace. Purely a courtesy."]],
 "Good day. And do spell my name correctly."],
7:["(Halvorsen is studying a map of the island with a ruler.) The north-west road is open. The whole island will be on the move. A commander must watch the roads. A good road is a loyal servant.",[
  ["Is the army ready for anything?","We're ready for everything that can be foreseen, and a few things that can't."],
  ["How are your officers?","Efficient. Rask counts, Niemi feeds, Koski watches, Aho trembles. A balanced regiment."],
  ["What does the island need?","Order, and a good cup of tea."]],
 "If you've further questions, apply in writing."],
8:["(Halvorsen has the radio on very low.) A sermon from the Church today. Father Thyme is said to be struggling. I sympathise. In my experience, speeches are always hardest when the heart is full.",[
  ["Are you a churchgoing man?","On feast days, and funerals. I find the hymns steadying. The rest, I leave to the chaplain."],
  ["Did you know the late king well?","Thirty years' acquaintance. He had a firm handshake and a poor memory for names. I forgave him both."],
  ["Will the army support the Church?",["The army supports the island. If the Church needs a road cleared or a door guarded, we're at its disposal.",[
    ["Even for Prince Augustus?","The army has no preferred prince. I've dined with both. They're perfectly agreeable."],
    ["That's diplomatic, Colonel.","Thank you. It's the only art a soldier is allowed."]]]]],
 "God save the island. That much I will say."],
9:["(Halvorsen is turning a Kelp Cola bottle in his hand with great suspicion.) There's a new formula, I'm told. A crate arrived at the barracks unsolicited. Rask wishes it returned. Niemi wishes it stirred into a stew.",[
  ["Do you drink it?","On very hot days. It's tolerable. It lacks a certain gravity."],
  ["Is the army involved with Kelp Cola?","Not officially. But Phil sends crates at Christmas. I send a polite note in return."],
  ["What about the Communists' cola?","They sent a sample as well. It was red. I used it as a paperweight."]],
 "Keep it quiet. The army is neutral on beverages."],
10:["(Halvorsen is in full dress uniform, white gloves on.) Today we bury the king. I've led the guard of honour. It was the proudest and saddest duty of my career. The island's never been so quiet.",[
  ["How was the ceremony?","Solemn. Dignified. The Royal Chapel was full. I stood with my men, and we did not falter."],
  ["Did the Prince speak to you?","Prince Leopold shook my hand. He looked tired, as one should. I told him the army was his father's, and the island's."],
  ["What do you feel today?","Respect. Sorrow. A little lost, if I'm honest. But I'll deny that in print."]],
 "Thank you for your respect. It means a good deal to the regiment."],
11:["(Halvorsen is at his desk, reading a report.) The mill hands are demanding fair pay, and the Church is giving alms. The army's not involved in either, though I'm told the soldiers have opinions.",[
  ["Do the soldiers discuss wages?","In whispers. I pretend not to hear. It's an old and useful discipline."],
  ["Is the army paid fairly?","Adequately, which is the best a soldier should expect."],
  ["Would you support the mill workers?","As an individual, with sympathy. As a Colonel, with silence."]],
 "The army keeps the peace. It does not take a side. Please remember."],
12:["(Halvorsen is reviewing a stack of reports.) Rask tells me the armoury's missing a whistle, a cap and a belt. He regards it as a crime wave. I regard it as a tidy-up. We've agreed to disagree.",[
  ["Is there a thief?","Possibly. More likely a misfiling. But Rask will find it, if only to prove a point."],
  ["Do you trust him?","With my life. And my accounts, which is rarer."],
  ["What's the army doing today?","Drill, inspection, tea. All in strict order."]],
 "Do mention that the army's in good order. It's the one thing I'm certain of."],
13:["(Halvorsen adjusts his cuffs.) The cola people are distributing free samples across the island. A clever move. Free things tend to be accepted without questions. I've told the men to read the labels.",[
  ["Do you think it's a bribe?","I think it's marketing. The distinction is a matter of etiquette."],
  ["Will the army accept samples?","Privately, certainly. Officially, no. Rask has the receipts."],
  ["Which faction do you prefer?","None. I have neighbours in several. The conversation is always polite."]],
 "A soldier should never accept a gift he can't return."],
14:["(Halvorsen is standing at his window, arms folded.) Tomorrow the island gathers. I've issued orders. We'll be present, but not conspicuous. The army must be seen, and not heard.",[
  ["What do you expect?","A great many speeches, a great deal of enthusiasm, and, God willing, no incidents."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm prepared. A prepared man is rarely worried, though he may be tired."],
  ["Will the army take sides?","No. Whatever the temptation, whatever the dinner."]],
 "I'll see you there. Stand where I can see you."],
15:["(Halvorsen is pouring tea with great care.) The royal household and the Church are quarrelling over crown and cross. The army, of course, takes neither side. I've been invited to dine at the palace on Friday. That's quite separate.",[
  ["It sounds political.","It's a dinner. The soup's excellent. We talk about horses."],
  ["Do you support Prince Leopold?","I respect him. He's his father's son. I also respect Prince Augustus, who is devout."],
  ["Which would you pick?","I would pick whichever the island picks. That is the whole principle of the army."]],
 "If you must write something, write that I was tactful."],
16:["(Halvorsen is listening to the debate with a fixed expression.) The Communists and the Democrats are at it on the wireless. A great deal of rhetoric. I note that neither mentions the army, which is as it should be.",[
  ["Would you like them to?","Heavens, no. An army that's mentioned in a debate has already lost the argument."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","Whoever bores the other into submission. It's an old tactic."],
  ["Does the army worry about the Communists?","We worry about everything. Equally and politely."]],
 "Switch it off if it gets too loud. Discretion is the better part of silence."],
17:["(Halvorsen grips the desk as the floor shudders.) A tremor. Short, sharp. The first of several, I suspect. My officers report steam over the northern peak. I've called an emergency conference.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Presumably. The army has contingency plans for most things. Not this. But we'll adapt."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm vigilant. It's a different thing, though it looks the same."],
  ["What will the army do?","Prepare to evacuate, assist and clear. We're good at all three."]],
 "Remain calm. And remain close to a doorframe."],
18:["(Halvorsen looks tired.) The Church is searching for a relic, I'm told. Two monks called at the barracks. I received them courteously and Rask searched them, politely. They found nothing.",[
  ["What was the relic?","A small gilded box, they said. I don't know what's in it. A bone, a thread. I do not ask."],
  ["Will the army help find it?","If asked formally. Rask has already catalogued everything that shines."],
  ["Is it important?","To the Church, vitally. To the army, a matter of courtesy."]],
 "I'd advise you not to hunt it. It tends to find its own way home."],
19:["(Halvorsen is writing a letter and doesn't look up.) The Party's recruiting at the gates. Some of my younger men have been approached. I've reminded them of their oath. I do not wish to lose a private to a pamphlet.",[
  ["Is the army at risk of splitting?","Never. We're like a good wall. A bit chipped, perhaps, but solid."],
  ["What did the men say?","Aho said he'd consider nothing without his mother's advice. Koski said he'd prefer an adventure."],
  ["What about the Midnight Note?","I've heard rumours of royal frivolity. I neither confirm nor attend."]],
 "If you meet a recruiter, be polite. It confuses them."],
20:["(Halvorsen is standing very still, hands behind his back.) A stranger is said to be at the Hotel Aurora, and the police are busy. I've told the men not to involve themselves. It's a civilian affair.",[
  ["Do you know who he is?","I have no idea. Nor do I wish to. A soldier shouldn't speculate about hats."],
  ["Is the island in danger?","It's always in danger, in a quiet way. That's what islands are for."],
  ["Is the army on alert?","Of course. It always is."]],
 "Keep your head down, reporter. It's what the army does."],
21:["(Halvorsen is covered in a fine grey dust, and still upright.) The volcano has erupted. I've ordered every man to assist. The roads are being cleared, the hospital supplied, the monks escorted. No lives lost. That's the report, and I'm very glad of it.",[
  ["Is the island safe?","Reasonably. The lava's to the north, away from the settlements. We've sentries on every road."],
  ["Were you frightened?","I was alert. It felt, I suppose, like a very large door slamming."],
  ["What now?","We clean, we reassemble, we reassure. And then we have a very large dinner."]],
 "Cover your mouth. And be careful on the roads."],
22:["(Halvorsen has his sleeves rolled, which is rare.) The army's out clearing the ash. Everyone's chipping in, even Rask, who's been issued a broom and has numbered the bristles. The palace has sent a hamper. It's a splendid gesture.",[
  ["How are the men?","Tired, grey and proud. It's a good combination."],
  ["Is the army neutral in this?","In ash, there are no factions. Only shovels."],
  ["What have you heard from the factions?","Cola people with trouble at a farm. The royals with a gift for the princess. The island has a very busy mind."]],
 "Take a broom, if you have a spare hour."],
23:["(Halvorsen winces at a faint tremor.) Aftershocks. They'll continue for days. I've ordered the men to sleep in boots. Rask has issued the boots, the blankets and a short lecture.",[
  ["How is morale?","Better than I feared. Niemi's soup is working wonders."],
  ["Is the army coping?","Admirably. It's what the army's for."],
  ["Any final orders?","Stay calm, stay dry and stay alert."]],
 "And do keep clear of cliffs."],
24:["(Halvorsen is watching a map.) The Church is going up to the peaks today on a retreat. I've lent them blankets and an escort. I told them to be careful. A monk on a mountain is a delicate thing.",[
  ["Is it safe up there?","Safer than the valleys, at the moment. The ash is thinner. The air's clean. I'd go myself if I could."],
  ["Does the army support the Church?","We support every citizen. The Church's a fine one."],
  ["Will you visit?","Perhaps. After the island's settled."]],
 "I wish them a good climb. And a good sleep."],
25:["(Halvorsen taps the desk.) The royals propose a crown for everyone. The Democrats want a princess. Both have written to me. I've replied courteously and said nothing at all. A fine letter.",[
  ["Do you support either idea?","I support the island deciding. It's a simple position, and surprisingly rare."],
  ["Who do you think the island will choose?","I'm a soldier, not a prophet. But I note which way the neighbours are leaning."],
  ["Who are your neighbours?","Harald and Carlo, at Hope Springs. Both candidates for the Republican business. Both very friendly, over the fence."]],
 "The army, as ever, will remain impartial. I'll sign that in blood if necessary."],
26:["(Halvorsen is reading a newspaper with a raised eyebrow.) Dario's bought the spa at Hope Springs, I read. Harald and Carlo live on the same road. I wonder if they'll be invited to the opening. I certainly shan't.",[
  ["Is the army against Diversified Petroleum?","No. We're against disorder. Oil's incidental."],
  ["What do you think of the cola princes?","A prince in every bottle. Marketing at its finest. I'd hate to be the Prince who ends up on a lemonade."],
  ["Do your neighbours talk politics?","Constantly. I nod politely and go inside."]],
 "Neighbours are best kept at a hedge's distance."],
27:["(Halvorsen is writing orders.) Tomorrow is the largest push. Every faction on the move, flags and speeches. I've deployed the men in discreet positions. If anyone loses their temper, we shall be present, calm, and a little taller than they are.",[
  ["Are you expecting trouble?","I expect crowds. Trouble is a minority sport."],
  ["Is the army ready?","Entirely. I've had Rask check twice."],
  ["Will you be at the pictures?","At a distance, in a good coat. Revolutions look better from afar."]],
 "Stay in a crowd, but not the front of it."],
28:["(Halvorsen is in full uniform, every medal in place.) Today's the day. The state visit, the Party's flags, the cola going abroad, the oil men in their office. I've dispatched the men to every key point. And I've dined, I regret to say, with the palace last night.",[
  ["Does the palace expect your support?","They expect my presence. I have given them my presence. It is a very good presence."],
  ["What will you do if things get out of hand?","Step in, politely, with a great many men."],
  ["How are you feeling?",["Composed. A little tight at the collar. The island's future rests on tomorrow, though not on me alone.",[
    ["Are you proud of the army?","Immensely. They've done everything asked of them, and a few things nobody asked."],
    ["Anything you'd say to the island?","Be kind to each other. And mind your manners in public."]]]]],
 "Stand firm, reporter. It's the only way through."],
29:["(Halvorsen is at the window, looking out over the barracks.) Primaries, lines of succession. The last act. I've nothing to add, save that the army will respect the outcome, whatever it is.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","Slightly. A commander who isn't nervous isn't paying attention."],
  ["Is the army ready?","To the last button. And Niemi's made soup."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Wait, watch and obey whoever's lawfully chosen."]],
 "Rest, if you can. It's going to be a long one."],
30:["(Halvorsen stands at attention at the barracks gate, medals gleaming in the cold light.) It's the last day. The army has done its duty, and I've done mine. Whatever the island chooses, we'll serve it, with all we've got.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Steady. Calm. Quietly hopeful. I won't say more, in case it spoils the luck."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash on the parade ground. Aho writing to his mother. Rask's ledgers. The men, who never once complained."],
  ["Any message for London?","Tell them the army kept its oath. That is all a soldier asks."]],
 "Thank you, reporter. You may go."],
};
// Corporal Niemi, army cook at the barracks (The Army). Threads: the single enormous pot and what's in it, his singing, his dreadful marksmanship, feeding the regiment on a quartermaster's budget, Sergeant Rask's accounts, the weather and the ash.
DAILY['Corporal Niemi']={
6:["(Corporal Niemi is stirring a pot the size of a small boat, singing something about a girl and a herring.) Ah! A visitor! You'll be the reporter. Sit, sit. Mind the ladle, it's got a mind of its own.",[
  ["What's in the pot?","Today, soup. Yesterday, also soup. Tomorrow, a surprise, which is also soup. It's one pot. I've never needed a second."],
  ["Do you sing while you cook?","Always. It keeps the soup warm and the men away from the lid."],
  ["Are you a good cook?","I'm a good corporal who happens to cook. The men have never complained. Not to my face."]],
 "Take a bowl on your way out. Just don't tell the Sergeant."],
7:["(Niemi is tasting from a spoon, frowning.) Needs salt. Always needs salt. The Sergeant counts it by the grain, so I have to steal it by the pinch.",[
  ["Does Rask watch the food supplies?","He watches everything. Rifles, boots, blankets, spoons. If an army ran on counting, we'd have won every war."],
  ["Did you ever fight in one?","Never. I was a very bad shot. They put me in the kitchen to keep me away from the targets."],
  ["How bad a shot?","Last drill I hit the flagpole and the chicken coop, and nothing I aimed at."]],
 "Lick the spoon on the way out. That's the best bit."],
8:["(Niemi has the radio on, tuned to the sermon, and is humming along.) A fine sermon, they say. Father Thyme lost his words, and I'd say the Almighty lost His voice. The Church needs a good stew.",[
  ["Is that your cure for everything?","Soup. And a bit of singing. And never speaking to a priest before breakfast."],
  ["Are you religious?","I'm devout about meals. Three times a day, sharp. Everything else is negotiable."],
  ["What would you cook for Father Thyme?",["A thick barley soup with smoked sausage, to put some words back in him. Warm, filling, and no fish.",[
    ["Why no fish?","Because the last time I made fish, Private Aho wrote home about it. Two pages. His mother sent a letter of complaint to the Colonel."],
    ["Would he eat it?","He's a very polite man. He'd eat it, and then pray for me. Both would help."]]]]],
 "God bless the cook. He's the one who keeps the faith."],
9:["(Niemi is holding up a Kelp Cola bottle, suspiciously.) They've changed the recipe, I hear. I tried it. Tastes like a sweet dentist. Rask wants to know where the crate came from. I said, Providence.",[
  ["Do the men like cola?","They love it. They'd drink it for breakfast if I let them. I let them."],
  ["Is the Party selling its own cola?","The People's Cola. Someone sent a sample. It tasted of beetroot and good intentions. I cooked with it."],
  ["Does it work in soup?","It does. A little sweetness. It's secret. Don't tell the Colonel."]],
 "Take a bottle if you want. I can't be seen giving it away."],
10:["(Niemi is wearing a black armband and no apron.) A sad day. The king's funeral. The Colonel's gone with the guard, and the rest of us are quiet. I cooked a plain stew, no singing. It felt right.",[
  ["Did you ever cook for the king?","Once. A military dinner, twelve years back. He asked for seconds. I've never been so proud."],
  ["What did he eat?","The soup. Then he said, Corporal, this is exactly like my mother's. I wept into the pot."],
  ["How are the men?","Quiet. A quiet barracks is the worst sound there is."]],
 "Eat something. Grief's easier on a full stomach."],
11:["(Niemi is chopping onions with tears streaming and not from the onions.) Everyone's arguing about pay today. The mill, the cola, the church. I've a soldier's pay and a cook's mind. I say, feed people first and argue after.",[
  ["Is the Church giving alms today?","They are. I've sent a pot of soup to Father Thyme. A big one. It's the best sermon I can give."],
  ["Do you earn a fair wage?","I earn enough for tobacco and a new apron. It's not about money. It's about the stew."],
  ["Is the Union after the army?","They'd need a very long spoon."]],
 "Mind the onions. They bite."],
12:["(Niemi is practising at a paper target with a rifle, eyes squeezed shut.) Rask makes me shoot once a week, and the less said the better. Stand back, if you don't mind. I've a gift for the unexpected.",[
  ["Are you any better?","I hit the target. I think. A bird fell off the wall, but I'm not sure it was mine."],
  ["Why do you keep trying?","Because it's army rules. And because Rask watches, and I'd rather hit something than be hit by Rask."],
  ["What would help?","Glasses. Or a bigger target. Or a smaller rifle. Or no rifle."]],
 "Don't stand behind me. Or in front. Or near."],
13:["(Niemi has a tray of tiny bottles and a look of triumph.) The cola people left me forty samples. Forty! I'm using them for the sauce tonight. Don't tell Rask. He counts them. He knows.",[
  ["Is that wise?","Probably not. But it's free, and sweet, and I've a stew that needs perking up."],
  ["Who delivered them?","A man in a green tie with a lot of teeth. I told him my pot was the best advert he'd get."],
  ["Did he agree?","He asked for the recipe. I laughed. A cook's recipe is his dignity."]],
 "If Rask asks, you've seen nothing, tasted nothing, and enjoyed it all."],
14:["(Niemi is baking a tray of buns with careful hands.) Everyone's talking about tomorrow, the great gathering. The Colonel says we'll be in position, calm and quiet. I'll be in the kitchen, loud and baking.",[
  ["Is the army on duty?","The Colonel's orders. Aho will stand and tremble. Koski will watch the gate. And I'll feed whoever comes back."],
  ["What are you baking?","Buns, for the long day. If there's a riot, at least they'll be well fed."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm a cook. I'm only nervous about the oven."]],
 "Take a bun. They're warm, and warm things are lucky."],
15:["(Niemi is scrubbing the pot with a very long brush.) The royals and the Church are shouting at each other. Crown before cross, cross before crown. I'd say bread before both.",[
  ["Which side are you on?","The side with a proper kitchen. The palace has a good one, I hear. I'd give my left ladle for a week there."],
  ["Does the Colonel take sides?","He says no. And he dines at the palace every second Tuesday. Make of that what you will."],
  ["What do soldiers think?","That the army should stay out of it. And eat well while it does."]],
 "Bring me a rumour next time. I'll make it into a stew."],
16:["(Niemi has a spoon in one hand and a radio in the other.) Debate day. The Communists and the Democrats are going at it. I'd cook for both, if they sat at the same table. They'd stop shouting when the soup came.",[
  ["Is soup a political tool?","The best. No one's ever stormed a building with a full stomach."],
  ["Who do you favour?","The one with the fewest speeches. I'm a simple man."],
  ["Are the Communists after the barracks?","They'd be welcome. A hungry Communist is a hungry man. I'd feed him."]],
 "Don't let the soup boil over while they argue."],
17:["(Niemi grabs the pot as it slides along the stove.) Whoa! There she goes again. A tremor! The whole kitchen jumped. The ladles are singing. It's not me, for once.",[
  ["Was that the volcano?","Must be. Koski says there's steam coming off the north peaks. I say the mountain wants its supper."],
  ["Did you drop anything?","Only my dignity. And three spoons."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Never. I've a pot to hold on to. It's got more weight than I have."]],
 "Keep a bowl handy, in case it shakes again. Food helps."],
18:["(Niemi is looking suspiciously at a cupboard.) Two young monks came by looking for a relic, of all things. Looked in my cupboards! My cupboards! I told them the only relic in here is the biscuit from 1974.",[
  ["Did they find anything?","A tin of pickled herring, and a very old sock. Neither's holy, though the herring was a miracle."],
  ["What's the relic?","A bone, or a bit of a saint. They looked in the pot. I took offence. The soup is not a reliquary."],
  ["Do you believe in relics?","I believe in recipes. They last longer than bones."]],
 "If it turns up in the stew, I'll let them know. Eventually."],
19:["(Niemi is rolling dough with unusual violence.) Something's going on with the Party. They're signing people up. A pair of them stood at the gate with a flag and a flask. I gave them soup. They stayed an hour.",[
  ["Did they recruit anyone?","Aho. Nearly. Then Rask walked past, and Aho put the leaflet in his boot."],
  ["Are the Communists friendly?","They're hungry, mostly. And very keen on borscht. I made a pot."],
  ["Does the army mind?","The Colonel said to be polite. And to count the spoons."]],
 "A good cook is neutral. The belly has no politics."],
20:["(Niemi is humming something sad.) There's talk of a man at the Hotel Aurora. A stranger, they say. Sergeant Hanna of the police has been asking questions. I told the Sergeant to check his own pantry first.",[
  ["Do you know who the stranger is?","I haven't the foggiest. But the cook always hears first, because everyone's hungry when they're afraid."],
  ["Is the army involved?","Not officially. Rask has counted every revolver twice, just in case."],
  ["Do you feel safe?","As long as the pot's on the boil. It's an excellent shield."]],
 "Whatever happens, have a bowl first. Then run."],
21:["(Niemi is grey from hat to boots.) The mountain's gone up, and the sky's gone black. I've never seen the like. The pot's got a lid of ash. I've put it in the soup, and the men say it's seasoning.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","The men are all counted, and Rask has counted them twice. Nobody hurt, as far as I know."],
  ["What were you doing when it blew?","Stirring. Always stirring. The ground shook and I held on to the spoon."],
  ["What now?","We clean. We cook. We feed the island, I think. It's the only thing an army's for."]],
 "Cover your pot. And your head. And your nose, if you've any sense."],
22:["(Niemi is standing on a chair, wiping ash off a shelf.) Everything's grey. The flour. The sugar. The Colonel's tea. I've lost three days' baking to a mountain.",[
  ["How are the men taking it?","They've started to sing again, which is a bad sign. They sing when they're frightened."],
  ["Have you heard any news?","The cola people have trouble at a farm, and the palace is buying a gift for the princess. It's all gossip, but it's good gossip."],
  ["Are the supplies low?","Rask says we've enough for a month. That means a fortnight. He rounds up."]],
 "If you see a clean spoon anywhere, send it to me."],
23:["(Niemi is stirring slowly, eyes on the ceiling.) Another aftershock this morning. The pot sloshed and I swore. Aho jumped on a bench and wouldn't come down. He's a good lad, but nervous.",[
  ["Is Aho all right?","He writes to his mother. He says the soup's very exciting. I don't think that's what she wants to hear."],
  ["How do you keep calm?","I cook. I sing. I think of the pot. It's got a nice round, stable shape."],
  ["What's the Colonel doing?","Telephoning. And dining at the palace, naturally. He says it's strategy."]],
 "Keep stirring, if you're nervous. It helps."],
24:["(Niemi is packing a hamper with a flourish.) The Church are climbing the peaks today for a retreat. I've made them sandwiches, a flask of tea, and a large fruitcake. Monks need sustenance. Faith runs on fruitcake.",[
  ["Is the army going with them?","Two of ours, to keep the road. I gave them double rations. A mountain is hungry work."],
  ["Is the cake any good?","It's heavy enough to stop a charge. It'll see them to the top."],
  ["Are you jealous?","Of the view, a bit. Of the climb, not at all."]],
 "Bless the cake, bless the cook, and don't drop the flask."],
25:["(Niemi is tasting the soup and looking guilty.) There's a crown for everyone, they say. I'd be happy with a hat for the pot. The royals want us to stand by while the Democrats want a princess. I'd settle for gravy.",[
  ["Is the army neutral?","Officially. Unofficially, we're hungry."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who feeds the most people. It's never been about ideas."],
  ["What would you do if you were king?","Bring in a second pot. And forbid fish."]],
 "There's always room for pudding. It's a rule."],
26:["(Niemi is humming and peeling a mountain of potatoes.) They say Dario's bought the spa, and the cola's putting princes on bottles. People will drink anything with a face on it. I'd put the Colonel on a bottle, and see how many buy.",[
  ["Would the Colonel mind?","He'd be thrilled. Then he'd say it was a matter of national honour."],
  ["Does the army get cola?","Crates. I stack them by the potatoes. A very good combination, I'm told."],
  ["Is the spa fancy?","I hear they have hot stones and towels like clouds. I'd settle for a hot stove and a quiet night."]],
 "Take a potato for the road. A single one. Don't be greedy."],
27:["(Niemi is shaping little dumplings.) A revolution at the pictures, they say. I've never seen a film, but I'll bet there's a cook in it somewhere, holding the thing together.",[
  ["Will the army be there?","Standing by, in case. I'll send a hamper."],
  ["Do you worry?","I worry the pot will boil dry. Beyond that, I leave it to God and the Colonel."],
  ["Are you singing today?","A little. It's the war song of the herring. It's very short and very sad."]],
 "If there's trouble, bring them here. A bowl of soup ends most arguments."],
28:["(Niemi has two pots on, which is unheard of.) The biggest day, they say. Everyone's on the move. State visits, flags, the cola going abroad, the oil men in their office. I've doubled the soup. When people don't know what to do, they eat.",[
  ["Are you ready?","The pot's polished and the ladle's sharp. The army marches on its stomach, and I'm the stomach."],
  ["What will the men do?","Stand firm. Look serious. Wait for lunch."],
  ["What do you hope for?",["That nobody's hurt. That the island finds a king, or a president, or whatever it wants. And that the men come home hungry.",[
    ["You'd make a fine general.","I'd lose every battle. But the troops would be well fed, and happy to retreat."],
    ["Anything to say to the army?","Eat. Rest. Trust the Colonel. And never trust the Sergeant's accounts."]]]]],
 "God bless the stew. And whoever eats it."],
29:["(Niemi is standing very still beside the pot.) Tomorrow it ends. The primary, the line of succession, all that. I've made a large lamb stew, in case of celebration or sorrow. It works for either.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Calm, mostly. A bit nervous. The pot's calm, so I'm calm."],
  ["Will you be on duty tomorrow?","On duty and on the stove. They need feeding more than anything."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash in the soup. And Aho's face when the ground shook."]],
 "Eat well tonight. Tomorrow's a long one."],
30:["(Niemi is stirring slowly, humming something gentle.) The last day. I've been feeding the army for twelve years, and I've never made so much soup in a month. The pot's tired, and so am I.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Quiet. Hopeful. A bit hungry."],
  ["What's next for the army?","The Colonel says we'll serve whoever the island chooses. And I'll serve soup."],
  ["Any final words for London?","Tell them we kept the island fed. It's not glamorous, but it's true."]],
 "Take a bowl before you leave. It's on the house."],
};
// Dagny, owner of the Eastbrook Lumber Company, on the mill floor most days (The Proprietors). Threads: counting timber and every mark,
// the wage and saw dispute and Helen, Bjarne's ledger, her father who founded the mill, her flat in Frostholm and the long commute.
DAILY['Dagny']={
4:["(A grey-haired woman looks up from a clipboard, counts something under her breath and finishes before she speaks.) Four hundred and twelve. Good. You're the reporter. Don't touch the timber, it's been counted.",[
  ["Impressive mill, Mrs Dagny.","Just Dagny. My father built it with a handsaw and a loan. I've turned it into the best timber yard north of Helsinki. It's all in the figures."],
  ["How many people work here?","Thirty-one. All fully employed, all fully paid. Anyone who says otherwise is a Communist, and a poor mathematician."],
  ["Do you count everything?","Everything. Logs, planks, sacks of sawdust. I even counted the pigeons once. Eleven. Two have been replaced."]],
 "Mind the floor. Sawdust costs money."],
5:["(Dagny is checking a delivery slip.) Now the road's open, the orders will come. Kelp Cola wants two thousand crates by the end of the month. That's an excellent week.",[
  ["Is business good?","It's always good. It could always be better. That's what 'business' means."],
  ["Do you like Kelp Cola?","I like that they pay on time. I don't like the posters. I don't like the free samples. A free sample is a way to lose money in a friendly manner."],
  ["How do you manage the mill?","By being here. A proprietor who sits at a desk deserves what she gets. I know every plank by its first name."]],
 "I'll be on the floor. Where the money is."],
6:["(Dagny's folding a piece of paper into exact quarters.) I live in Frostholm, you know. Skyline Apartments. A little flat, very efficient. Takes me forty minutes to get here by lorry. That's forty minutes I could be counting.",[
  ["Why not live nearer?","Because Frostholm has a better class of neighbour, and a very good lift. And nobody in Eastbrook would let me forget I was the boss."],
  ["Do you take the lorry every day?","On a good day. On a bad day, I sleep in the office on a camp bed. Bjarne thinks I don't know he knows."],
  ["Is it lonely?","It's quiet. I like quiet. I find it improves one's arithmetic."]],
 "Your hat's covered in sawdust. I'd brush it, but it'd be a waste of effort."],
7:["(Dagny is on the telephone and speaks without looking up.) Yes. Yes. Four marks the board. No, I can't go lower. I've no intention of going lower. (She puts the phone down.) Frostholm's open and everyone wants a discount.",[
  ["Who was that?","A builder who wants the palace price for a pigsty. I told him the palace price was for the palace."],
  ["Do you ever give a discount?","Once, in 1971, to a priest. It was a mistake. He said a prayer, I got nothing and he put the money in the poor box."],
  ["Is it a hard business?","It's a mathematical business. Hard is for those who don't read the numbers."]],
 "I haven't time to chat. But you may stand there. It's free."],
8:["(Dagny's inspecting a plank against the light.) It's a Sunday, and I'm here, because the logs do not know what a Sunday is. Neither do I, particularly. The Church thinks it's a day of rest. I think it's a day when nobody's looking.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas, Easter and funerals. It's cost-effective. I put a coin in, they say a prayer, and I leave a bit lighter."],
  ["What does your father's portrait say?","It says 'No waste'. He painted it himself. It's above my desk, so I see it every morning, and he looks cross."],
  ["Is the mill your whole life?","It's the bit that pays. I have a cat, but he's very independent."]],
 "Sundays are for sums. And for sawdust."],
9:["(Dagny reads a pamphlet with the tips of her fingers, as if it might be damp.) 'The People's Cola.' Someone's slid this under my door. A Communist beverage, with a hammer on the label. I'm tempted to frame it.",[
  ["Would you stock it?","In a bin. It's got a Party stamp. I'd prefer to buy from a company that wants to make a profit and be honest about it."],
  ["What do you think of Helen?","A Communist agitator, in a union coat. She comes up to my mill, and she says 'fair', as though it were a number. I have yet to see a fair number that's cheaper than mine."],
  ["Is Helen in the Party?","The union and the Party are cousins, and I don't like either family reunion."]],
 "Take the pamphlet. I've no room in the bin."],
10:["(Dagny wears black, which is her usual colour, but with a brooch.) I've kept the mill open. The king would have wanted the orders filled. He wasn't a man for ceremony, or not much. He liked a clean plank.",[
  ["Did you know him?","He bought a table from us in 1964, and paid in full. That was the beginning and the end of our acquaintance. It was a very good transaction."],
  ["Won't the staff want time off?","They've had a minute's silence. It cost me a minute. It's a considerable sum, over thirty-one people."],
  ["Are you sad?","I'm precise. It's a form of sadness, I find. You'll never see me weep, but I count."]],
 "The mill stays open. Respect is best shown by doing one's job."],
11:["(Dagny shuts her ledger and looks at you with the particular patience of a woman who's had many visitors today.) So you're the reporter Helen's been sending. Let me guess: the pay. And the saw.",[
  ["That's right. The men have a case.","They've a grievance. A case needs figures. I've seen none. I've seen Knut waving a mutilated hand and Ilse looking disapproving."],
  ["Is the saw really dangerous?",["It's old. It's heavy. It's been standing for fifty years. That's not the same as dangerous. It's reliable. Reliable things have a way of being a little dangerous.",[
    ["Knut lost a fingertip.","Knut has told seven versions of that story. I believe none. If you can show me the guard's broken, I'll look into it. If it's a good price."],
    ["What would a new guard cost?","Forty marks, Bjarne tells me. And a day's work. It's a trifle. It's a trifle I've been thinking about for two years."]]]],
  ["Can we speak about wages?","We can speak about anything. I can't promise to agree. Everything has a cost. Everything has a person who pays it. I'd like it not to be me."]],
 "Think about what you've seen. And be careful what you repeat."],
12:["(Dagny is tapping a pencil against the ledger.) I've had a note from Bjarne. He thinks I should be nicer. It's got a little drawing of a smiling plank. I've kept it. It's the only thing I've ever been given for free.",[
  ["Is Bjarne a good bookkeeper?","He's meticulous and frightened, which is the ideal combination. If he were braver, he'd be dangerous."],
  ["What does the ledger say?","That it's our best year since the war. That we've money in the bank and cobwebs in the pockets. A very curious position."],
  ["Does the union have a point?","They have a view. A point's sharper. I'm waiting for a sharp one."]],
 "I'll consider things. I always consider things. It's why I've kept the mill."],
13:["(Dagny's staring out of the window at a Kelp Cola van.) That van's been in my yard for an hour, giving away sugar water to my employees. I've told them it's coming out of their lunch.",[
  ["Is that fair?","It's a joke. Mostly. I've a sense of humour. It's just very dry. Like my timber."],
  ["Do you mind them?","I mind the principle. A thing given is a thing owed. I'd rather pay and be quit."],
  ["Is there anything you'd give away?","Advice. It costs nothing, and nobody takes it. A perfect arrangement."]],
 "Close the door. The cola smell's getting in."],
14:["(Dagny's in her good coat, fastened to the neck.) The fourteenth. I've shut the mill at noon, which is the first time since my father's funeral. Don't say I'm sentimental. I've done the sums, and it's cheaper than a riot.",[
  ["Will you go?","Briefly. I'd like to be seen. A proprietor's a public figure, like it or not. And somebody should keep an eye on the pickpockets."],
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches. Sausages. A lot of people pretending to know what they want. I know exactly what I want: a stable island with a good rate of exchange."],
  ["Are you worried about the factions?","I'm worried about all of them. The Reds want my mill. The Democrats want my money. The oil men want my land. I've kept it all, so far."]],
 "Keep your wallet buttoned. That's today's civic duty."],
15:["(Dagny's flicking through a sheaf of receipts.) Yesterday was expensive. The mill was shut, the staff were out and, I'm told, a man tried to sell a flag with my face on it. It was a very poor likeness.",[
  ["Was it a good day?","It was crowded. It was noisy. I'd rate it three out of ten. Though the Church's cart was a sight."],
  ["Did you hear any speeches?","I heard thirty. I remember two. I wrote the best on the back of a delivery slip. I'll bill someone."],
  ["What was your feeling?","That an island is a very large unbalanced account. It's the only way I can say it. The assets are good. The liabilities are loud."]],
 "Mind the receipts. They're my whole heart."],
16:["(Dagny has put a Democratic Party leaflet and a Communist one side by side on the desk.) The debate. I've read both leaflets. One says 'Vote'. The other says 'Seize'. I find them equally expensive.",[
  ["Who do you prefer?","The one who'd leave my books alone. At the moment, that's neither."],
  ["Will you attend?","From a distance. Under an umbrella. With my pencil."],
  ["Do your workers go?","Every one. I've told them they may, so long as they're back for the afternoon shift. Which they won't be, so I've allowed for it."]],
 "I'll let you know who wins. I'll be the one counting the applause."],
17:["(Dagny is gripping the edge of the desk.) The whole office just rattled. The pencils jumped. A mill's a poor place to be in an earthquake: it's a building full of things that fall.",[
  ["Was it the volcano?","I've no idea. I'm a proprietor, not a geologist. But I've asked Bjarne to check whether it's covered."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Not frightened. Prudent. I'll move the ledger to the top shelf. And a sandwich. One never knows."],
  ["Will the mill close?","If it must. I'd sooner not. A closed mill is a fine for nothing."]],
 "If it happens again, don't run. Walk briskly towards the door. Running costs shoe leather."],
18:["(Dagny has placed a small dish of mints on the desk, which is unheard of.) Take one. I've bought them in bulk. They were cheaper by the hundred. I've now a hundred mints, and one mouth.",[
  ["Thank you. Is it a special occasion?","It's a Tuesday. But Bjarne said the staff were getting tense and I thought: mints. They're a mild bribe."],
  ["Is the dispute still on?","It's on. They'd like their money. I'd like them to have less. A compromise is what we'll get, and neither of us will be happy."],
  ["Is there anyone you trust?","Bjarne. And my cat. And a man in Frostholm who sells me cigars on credit. That's three, which is more than most."]],
 "Have another mint. They won't keep."],
19:["(Dagny looks up, pale.) Another tremor, this morning. A beam shifted over the canteen. Nobody was beneath it, which is the only reason I haven't had to fill in a form.",[
  ["Is the building safe?","I've asked a man to look. He charged me forty marks to say 'it'll hold'. I could have said that for nothing."],
  ["Does the volcano worry you?","It worries my accountants. They've started using the word 'contingency'. It's an unpleasant word, and I use it on everyone else."],
  ["What would you do?","Fix the beam. It's not that I'm kind. It's that a falling beam is a lawsuit."]],
 "I'll think about the saw. I'm only thinking. I'm not saying yes."],
20:["(Dagny's pinching her nose.) That smell. It's in the planks. Every board I count smells of boiled eggs. If we can't get it out, it'll lower the price.",[
  ["It's the volcano.","I know it's the volcano. I'm not blind. I'm only offended. A mountain ought to know better than to interfere with commerce."],
  ["Are you prepared?","I've a bag in the office. Passport, deeds, a sandwich and a very large coat. I'll know when to leave."],
  ["Will you leave the mill?","Not unless it leaves me first."]],
 "Carry on. Business as usual is a form of courage."],
21:["(Dagny's coat is grey with ash and her hair has gone white.) It's over, or at least the worst of it. I stood by the saw as it fell. It was magnificent. I'd never say so to Knut.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Perfectly well. I counted the staff. All thirty-one. It's the best number I've had all year."],
  ["What was it like?","Like the end of a sum. A very large one. I felt, for a moment, that I'd been counting the wrong things."],
  ["Will the mill reopen?","Tomorrow. We'll shovel. Everyone will be on time, and I shall dock the ones who aren't."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I was moved. It would damage my credit."],
22:["(Dagny's wearing a dust mask over a very stern face.) We've sent out four hundred masks. I've charged the crew a quarter-price. It seemed generous, and I'm still annoyed.",[
  ["That's good of you.","It's accounting. A lung's an asset. A healthy lung's more productive."],
  ["How's the mill?","Dusty. Grey. But the saw's running. Knut kissed it. I pretended not to see."],
  ["How are your nerves?","I've not the time for nerves. I've a ledger to fill, and an island to survive. In that order."]],
 "If you've any spare cash, buy timber. The rebuilding will be great."],
23:["(Dagny's frowning at the roof.) There's ash in the beams. It'll need clearing, before the snow. I'm told there'll be snow. In September. The island does like to keep one guessing.",[
  ["Is the aftershock a worry?","Each one's a small invoice from nature. We'll pay it over time."],
  ["Is the pay dispute forgotten?","It's mislaid. It'll turn up. These things always do, like an umbrella."],
  ["Do you worry about the crew?","I count them. It's the same thing, in my language."]],
 "Take a mint. They're free this week. I'll deduct it from the volcano."],
24:["(Dagny leans back in her chair.) I've been told the island's coming together. Everyone's carrying buckets. It doesn't alter the books, but it's nice to see.",[
  ["Even you?","I carried a bucket. It was a very small bucket. And I gave it to Ilse almost at once."],
  ["Are the factions behaving?","They're pausing. It's the quiet before the invoice. The Syndicate's cleaning something. I don't ask what."],
  ["What do you want for the island?","Stability. A fair rate of exchange. And, if possible, someone who knows how to add."]],
 "Don't get sentimental. It's contagious."],
25:["(Dagny's polishing her glasses on a handkerchief.) I've been thinking about Helen. I'll not say it twice. She's got a point. She's irritating, loud and probably a Communist. But she's got a point.",[
  ["What point?","That a business is its people. My father said it. He said it with an accent, and I didn't listen."],
  ["Will you tell the men?","I'll tell the ledger. It's more likely to believe me."],
  ["What would your father say?","'Spend it wisely.' Which is what he said about everything, including his own funeral."]],
 "Don't repeat this. If anyone asks, I was counting."],
26:["(Dagny's going through the post.) A Prince in Every Bottle. Dario's bought a spa. The island's gone mad, and I'm selling planks to every one of them. It's a wonderful time to be sane.",[
  ["Who's buying?","Everyone. Kelp Cola for crates. The oil men for scaffolding. The palace for a gazebo. I'm told it's for a princess."],
  ["Do you have a favourite?","The one who pays cash. It's not a political position. It's a moral one."],
  ["Do you worry about the future?","I plan for it. Worry's a bad investment. It returns nothing and takes everything."]],
 "If you're building anything, do it in pine. It burns cleanly."],
27:["(Dagny's marking something on a map.) The end of the month's near. I've got orders to the end of October, and nobody knows who'll be in charge by then. It's a very interesting risk.",[
  ["Who would you like to see in charge?","Someone who pays the bills. And, in the interest of honesty, someone who doesn't tax timber."],
  ["Do you vote?","Always. I vote for the candidate who's cheapest to run. It saves everybody trouble."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm solvent. It's a cheerful state."]],
 "Keep your receipts. They're the only history that matters."],
28:["(Dagny's shut the ledger for once.) The biggest day for every faction. I'm shutting the mill early. I'd say it's an act of civic duty. In truth, it's because nobody will work.",[
  ["Will you go out?","Briefly. And in a very sensible hat. I intend to be seen and not to be handed a leaflet."],
  ["What do the men think?","They think I'm a monster. They're partly right. I'm a monster with a ledger."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I don't have the time. But if I did, it'd be a pleasant afternoon of it."]],
 "I'll count the crowds. It helps."],
29:["(Dagny's sitting very upright.) Tomorrow's the vote, or the end, or both. I've been going through my father's papers. There's a note: 'Pay them fairly. They'll stay.' I'd forgotten it.",[
  ["Why do you think you forgot?","Because it's expensive. Remembering's often the dearest thing there is."],
  ["Will you act on it?","I may. I've been checking the figures. They're not as bad as I'd feared."],
  ["That's a change.","It's a correction. There's a difference. But don't tell Helen."]],
 "I'll sleep on it. I always do. Then I'll count."],
30:["(Dagny stands in the middle of the mill floor, silent, as the saws rest.) Whatever happens today, the timber'll still need cutting. That's the comfort of my trade.",[
  ["Any last words?","Look after your money. Look after your people. And look after your hat. It's the first thing the wind takes."],
  ["Was it a good month?","It was a costly one. But the books balance, in a manner of speaking. I've seen worse Septembers."],
  ["Will you see me again?","If you pay for the timber, certainly. Otherwise, I'll send an invoice."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Pay your bills, and keep your counsel."],
};
// Daphne, the late king's daughter, walking the palace gardens (Royal Family). Threads: the island's power struggle, which she
// follows like a sport, and the callers who come to charm her (Gustavo, Phil, Dario); her boredom in mourning black, and her
// father's old cigarillos behind the hedge; the gardens, the frozen fountain and Viktor's birds; her growing curiosity about
// the Democrats, the Gazette and the idea of a vote.
DAILY['Daphne']={
2:["(Daphne is walking the gravel path with her hands in a fur muff.) Do you know, you're the eleventh person to come and look sorry at us today. I've been keeping count. You're the first one with a notebook.",[
  ["Who were the other ten?","A colonel, two ministers, a bishop's secretary, a man from Kelp Cola with remarkable teeth and five people who just wanted to see the inside. I gave them the scenic route."],
  ["How are you, really?","Cold. Sad, I suppose, in a way that keeps catching me on the stairs. Daddy used to whistle on the stairs. Now they're just stairs."],
  ["Eleventh isn't bad.","It's not a podium place, darling. Come back tomorrow and try harder."]],
 "Walk on the gravel, not the snow. Viktor takes footprints personally."],
3:["(Daphne holds up a bunch of hothouse roses.) From Phil, at Kelp Cola. Red roses for a girl in mourning. He must think black goes with everything.",[
  ["Does it?","Everything except my complexion. I look like a widow in a Russian novel. Which is fun for about a day."],
  ["Is Phil courting you?","Phil is courting the crown, and I'm the bit of it he can reach. I don't mind. It's lovely to be wanted, even as a doorstop."],
  ["What will you do with them?","Give them to Marta. She'll put them in a vase on the servants' table and say they're from an admirer. She'll mean Phil, and everyone will think it's Edmund."]],
 "If you see Phil, say they were lovely. He'll send more. I'm running out of vases and servants."],
4:["Eastbrook's open, so the whole palace has gone quiet in case a Democrat is listening. (She lowers her voice theatrically.) Have you met them?",[
  ["Not yet.","Oh, do. Erik runs them out of a community centre with a coffee pot. Leopold says they're dangerous. Augustus says they're misguided. I've never met anyone dangerous and misguided. It sounds marvellous."],
  ["Why the whisper?","Because the walls in there have ears, and out here only Viktor does. And Viktor's only interested in thrushes."],
  ["You sound interested.","I'm interested in everything I'm told not to be. It's the only hobby a princess is allowed."]],
 "Bring me back some gossip from Eastbrook. I'll trade you some of ours."],
5:["(Daphne is looking at the fountain, frozen solid, with an icicle down the middle.) The fountain's frozen. It does it every winter. Daddy called it 'the king's ice lolly'.",[
  ["Did he?","He was funny, sometimes, when nobody official was about. Then a minister would walk in and he'd turn back into a statue."],
  ["When does it thaw?","May, usually. Viktor puts sacks round the pipes and talks to it. I don't know what he says, but it's very effective."],
  ["Your brother's gone up the mountain.","Augustus? He goes up whenever he wants to feel holy. I go into the garden whenever I want to feel wicked. We each have our mountains."]],
 "Don't touch the icicle. It's been there since the first snow, and I'm fond of it."],
6:["I've worn black for nine days. Grace in Kingsgrad sent up three new mourning dresses, and they're all exactly the same. I've named them.",[
  ["What are their names?","Monday, Gloom and Mrs Thyme. Gloom has a slightly better collar. I'm wearing Gloom."],
  ["Do you miss colour?","Terribly. I'd kill for a red scarf. Well, not kill. Hint heavily at a dinner party."],
  ["You still look smart.","Smart is what people say when they mean 'severe'. Daddy would say I look like a bishop's umbrella."]],
 "Tell Grace I'd like Gloom in green. She'll faint."],
7:["The road north's open, so the oil man will be coming to call. Dario. He sent a card ahead. A card! With gold on it.",[
  ["What's he like?","He shakes your hand like he's weighing it, and he smells of money and aftershave. Daddy liked him, which is a terrible sign."],
  ["Will you see him?","Of course. I see everyone. You learn more from a man who wants something than from one who's got it."],
  ["Gold on a card is a bit much.","Dario is a bit much. That's his charm. You can see him coming from Frostholm."]],
 "Go and meet him. Tell me what his office looks like. I bet there's a map with pins in it."],
8:["Augustus has gone up for the Sunday sermon and Leopold's moping in the throne room. So I'm reading the Gazette behind the hedge. Don't tell anyone.",[
  ["Is it forbidden?","Not forbidden. Just not done. Nora writes about us as if we're a weather front. I love it."],
  ["Anything good in it?","A letter from a mill hand asking why a king costs more than a lumber mill. I've read it three times. I can't answer it."],
  ["Why behind the hedge?","Edmund hides the papers. He thinks we're too delicate. I'm about as delicate as a snowplough."]],
 "If anyone asks, I was looking at the birds."],
9:["(Daphne is wrapped in a black shawl.) Tomorrow's the funeral. I've been asked to be dignified for three hours. I've never been dignified for three minutes.",[
  ["You'll manage.","I'll manage the way I manage a dinner party: by pretending it's someone else's and I'm a guest."],
  ["What will you miss about him?",["The way he cheated at cards. He'd tell you he was cheating, and still win.",[
    ["Did you ever beat him?","Once. He sulked for a week and then had my winnings framed. They're in his study: one shilling and a bus ticket."],
    ["Who taught him?","Gustavo, I think. Long ago. Don't write that. Or do, it would be the most interesting line in the obituary."]]]],
  ["What will you wear?","Gloom, obviously. Mrs Thyme's for the wake. I'm saving Monday."]],
 "See you in the chapel. I'll be the one trying not to look at the hymn numbers."],
10:["(Daphne's eyes are red, and her chin is up.) Daddy hated funerals. He'd slip out before the hymns. I'm going to try very hard not to do exactly that.",[
  ["I'll keep you company.","You're sweet. Stand somewhere I can see you, and if I start edging for the door, cough."],
  ["Who's coming?","Everyone who wanted something from him, and everyone who wants something from us. The pews will be full of very sorry people taking very careful notes."],
  ["You're allowed to be sad.","I know. I've been sad since six this morning. I was hoping to be something else by lunch."]],
 "Go on. Find a good pew. Not behind a pillar: Edmund always puts the press behind a pillar."],
11:["Well. Gustavo kissed my hand in front of the whole island, and Augustus looked as though he'd seen a heretic. I enjoyed it enormously.",[
  ["Did you mean to let him?","Darling, a girl doesn't let Gustavo do anything. Gustavo simply does it, and you decide afterwards if you'll allow it."],
  ["Who is Gustavo to your family?","An old friend of Daddy's. They played cards. Nobody will tell me what else. That's the most exciting sentence in the palace."],
  ["How was the funeral, for you?","I didn't slip out. I cried in the hymns, which surprised me, and in the right places, which surprised everyone else."]],
 "If you're writing about Gustavo, leave me out of it. Or put me in somewhere flattering."],
12:["(Daphne is crouching by a hedge, peering in.) Viktor showed me a robin's roost in here. Look. A little brown ball, puffed up against the cold. Like Edmund in the hall.",[
  ["Does Viktor show you much?","Everything that's interesting about the garden. Nothing that's interesting about the gate. He's very loyal, and very annoying."],
  ["What's at the gate?","Cars at midnight, according to Oskar. Oskar tells me everything and Edmund tells Oskar off. It's a lovely system."],
  ["You're very fond of the gardens.","They're the only place the walls don't listen. Inside, there's a draught in every room, and a different person at every keyhole."]],
 "Tread softly. The robin's had a hard week too."],
13:["It's all happening, isn't it? Kelp Cola giving bottles away in Kingsgrad, and someone at dinner whispering about their famous recipe. I adore it.",[
  ["Who was whispering?","I shan't say. A man with a ring on his little finger. He wanted to know where the cola family keep their secrets. As if I'd tell him. As if I knew."],
  ["Do you know?","Darling, I don't even know where Edmund keeps the jam. Phil's family must keep it in a vault, or under the old man's pillow."],
  ["You're enjoying all this.","I'm a royal in mourning in a palace full of clocks. Of course I'm enjoying it. Wouldn't you?"]],
 "Don't drink anything free today. I hear it's the most expensive thing on the island."],
14:["(Daphne pulls on long black gloves.) The big day. The whole island in one place. I'm told to smile without showing teeth, which is a skill only royals and cats have.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches, shouting, someone fainting, and a great deal of people looking at Leopold to see if he's Daddy yet."],
  ["Who are you looking at?","The Democrats. I want to see what a room full of people who'd like to abolish me looks like. I imagine they'll have very good jumpers."],
  ["Show some teeth.","(She does, briefly, wickedly.) There. That's for you. The rest of the island gets the cat."]],
 "I'll look for you in the crowd. Wave if anyone throws anything."],
15:["Everyone's after Augustus today. The monks want him in a cassock and the family wants him in a crown. Nobody's asked what Augustus wants. He'd faint if they did.",[
  ["What do you think he wants?","To be told he's good. Everyone in this family wants that. Augustus is just the only one who'll say so in a prayer."],
  ["What do you want for him?","For him to be happy. It's a terribly unfashionable thing to want in this palace."],
  ["Will you talk to him?","I took him a cup of cocoa and said 'whatever you choose, I'll still steal your biscuits.' I think it helped."]],
 "If you see him, be kind. He's a darling, but he's made of paper."],
16:["The Democrats and the Communists are debating today. I begged Edmund to let me go, in a headscarf, at the back. He looked at me as though I'd asked to be fired from a cannon.",[
  ["Why do you want to go?","I want to hear Erik. Anyone who wants to abolish me and is still polite about it is worth hearing."],
  ["What would Leopold say?","He'd say a princess doesn't sit at the back of a debate. Then he'd ask me to tell him everything they said."],
  ["You could listen on the radio.","I'm going to. Under the blankets, like a girl at school. Bring me back a leaflet."]],
 "If you're going, sit near the front. Tell me if Erik's handsome. I've heard rumours."],
17:["(Daphne catches your arm as the gravel shivers under you.) Did you feel that? The whole garden moved. The fountain cracked down the middle.",[
  ["The king's ice lolly!","(She looks genuinely stricken.) I know. Viktor says it's the cold. Viktor said it in a very worried voice."],
  ["Was it an earthquake?","There's steam over the mountain in the north. Oskar says there are rabbits running down the road. Nobody tells you what rabbits know."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Thrilled, rather, and a little sick. Don't tell anyone that: they'll think I'm heartless. I'm just bored, which is worse."]],
 "Hold on to something. Not me. The bench."],
18:["I've been reading the Gazette behind the hedge every morning. Nora wrote that the palace is 'a museum with a family in it'. I laughed so hard Viktor came to see if I'd fallen over.",[
  ["Is she right?","Horribly. We dust the throne and feed the portraits and sometimes a prince comes out and waves."],
  ["Will you complain?","Never. It's the only true thing anyone's written about us in years. I've cut it out. It's in my jewellery box."],
  ["You'd make a good reporter.","I'd make a terrible one. I'd know everything and print nothing, and then tell it all at dinner."]],
 "If you meet Nora, don't tell her I liked it. She'll only write something nastier to see if I like that too."],
19:["The palace is a tomb with curtains. I've read every novel in the library that doesn't have a saint in it. That's four.",[
  ["What do you do for fun?","Walk. Smoke Daddy's old cigarillos behind the hedge where Edmund can't see. Count Leopold's frowns. Thirty-one at dinner."],
  ["Go out somewhere.","Where? Everyone would stand up. I'd like, just once, to go somewhere where nobody stood up."],
  ["You smoke cigarillos?","Daddy's. There were two boxes. Now there's one. They're vile and they make me feel nine feet tall."]],
 "Don't tell Augustus about the cigarillos. He'll pray for me in front of people."],
20:["(Daphne is in the frozen garden, looking up at the palace windows.) Someone was in the library late. Augustus, I expect, praying in his sleep. The light was on till three.",[
  ["Couldn't you sleep either?","I never sleep in mourning. I lie awake and plan elaborate escapes I'll never take. Tonight it was a fishing boat to Finland."],
  ["Is everyone in the palace awake at night?","Leopold walks the floor. Augustus prays. I plot. Edmund guards. Ingrid hears all of it. She must be exhausted."],
  ["What's in the library at three?","Books, darling. And a great many secrets Edmund won't tell me. I've given up asking. Mostly."]],
 "Sleep well, if you can. Somebody on this island should."],
21:["(Daphne stands at the top of the garden, staring north, with ash in her hair.) The mountain's on fire. I've wanted something to happen for weeks, and now I'd like it to stop.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Yes. No. My hair's grey and my dress is grey and the whole world's grey. Gloom finally matches."],
  ["Come inside.","In a minute. I want to remember this. It's the most real thing that's ever happened to me, and nobody had to arrange it."],
  ["Where's Leopold?","On the front steps being a king. I'm proud of him. Don't tell him, he'll get unbearable."]],
 "Go home and shut the windows. The ash gets in everywhere. Even into princesses."],
22:["I'm so bored I could scream, and I'm not allowed to scream: it's undignified, and the ash makes you cough. I need something to help me relax, darling. Something that isn't a saint.",[
  ["Like what?","(She smiles sideways.) Something a little herbal, a little forbidden. I hear the island's farms grow more than potatoes. I'm only saying I've heard."],
  ["A book? A bath?","I've read the books and I've had the baths. I need something Edmund wouldn't approve of. That's the only test I trust now."],
  ["You're a princess. Ask anyone.","Anyone would tell Edmund, and Edmund would tell Leopold, and Leopold would look at me like Daddy did. No. It would need someone discreet."]],
 "Forget I said anything. Or remember it very quietly."],
23:["(Daphne is sweeping ash off the bench with her glove.) Viktor's been out since dawn with a broom, sweeping the paths. The birds have come back, he says. All covered in grey, like little ghosts.",[
  ["The birds came back?","The robins first. Then the blackbirds. Viktor nearly cried, and Viktor doesn't do that. I saw his ears go red."],
  ["Have you been sleeping?","A little. Viktor gave me a bag of dried lavender for my pillow. It smells of his shed, but it works."],
  ["What's the north like?","Burnt and grey, Oskar says, and Leopold's sent soup. I wanted to drive up with it. I was refused, in triplicate."]],
 "Sit on the bench, it's clean now. Well, cleaner."],
24:["A king's coming on the twenty-eighth, from across the water. Edmund's in a frenzy. He's counting spoons in his sleep. I heard him.",[
  ["What's the visiting king like?","Old, rich, devout and has a son my age, they tell me. I'm rather afraid it's not a state visit. I'm rather afraid it's an audition."],
  ["An audition for what?","For me. Daddy always said I'd marry for the island. I said I'd marry for love or not at all. He said love was a luxury of commoners."],
  ["You don't have to marry anyone.","(She looks at you a long moment.) Nobody's ever said that to me. Say it again, at dinner, in front of Edmund."]],
 "If you see a foreign prince, trip him."],
25:["Someone from the Democrats has been asking after me. Me! Not Leopold. They want a royal who'll say a vote isn't the end of the world.",[
  ["Is it the end of the world?","It might be the end of ours. But it might be the start of something rather interesting. I'm not sure Daddy would recognise the difference."],
  ["Would you say it?",["I'd say anything that let me choose something for once. I'm only allowed to choose hats.",[
    ["What would Leopold think?","He'd be hurt. Then he'd think about it. Then he'd ask me what Erik's like. He's more curious than he lets on, our Leopold."],
    ["What would your father think?","Daddy would be furious. And secretly impressed. He loved a fight, as long as he won it."]]]],
  ["You've been reading the Gazette.","Every morning behind the hedge. Nora's ruined me. I think in leaflets now."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I said that. Tell everyone I said something charming about the weather."],
26:["Kelp Cola came calling again, all teeth and folders, to see Leopold. I gave them the scenic route. Twice.",[
  ["What did they want?","His face, I think. On something. Phil only ever wants faces on things."],
  ["Did you tell Leopold?","Of course not. I want to see his face when he finds out they want his face. It'll be the best face of the month."],
  ["The scenic route?","Past the portrait of Grandmother, who glares at everyone from Kelp Cola. She disliked fizzy drinks. She said they made people cheerful for no reason."]],
 "If they offer you a bottle, refuse it. You'll be signing something."],
27:["(Daphne holds up a green silk scarf.) Grace made it in secret. I'm wearing it under Gloom for the state visit. Nobody will see it. I'll know.",[
  ["It's lovely.","It's Daddy's favourite colour. He'd have hated me wearing it to mourn him. That's partly the point."],
  ["Is everyone ready?","Edmund's counted the spoons twenty times. Marta's threatening the fish. Viktor's dusted every hedge. Leopold's rehearsing a speech to the mirror. I'm doing my nails."],
  ["Rebel.","(She winds it twice round her throat.) A very small rebel. A rebel you could fit in a drawer."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. Look for a flash of green."],
28:["(Daphne's voice is low and quick.) A king at the gate, and the whole island making its biggest push at once. Every party, every company, every saint. I've never been so alive.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Everyone thinks they are. That's how you know it's close."],
  ["Is the green scarf on?","Under the collar. (She tugs it a fraction into view.) There. Don't tell Edmund."],
  ["Be careful today.","I'm always careful. I just do it with a smile so nobody notices."]],
 "Go! Something's happening everywhere. Come back and tell me all of it."],
29:["The island's deciding who comes after Daddy. Leopold, or Augustus, or nobody. Nobody ever thinks of me, which is lovely. I can watch.",[
  ["Would you want it?","The crown? Goodness, no. It gives you a headache and a lot of people standing up. I'd much rather be the person in the garden who knows everything."],
  ["Who do you want?","Whoever lets me keep the gardens and the Gazette. That's a short list and I haven't told anyone who's on it."],
  ["Are you nervous for them?","For Leopold, terribly. For Augustus, a bit. For the island? (She shrugs.) The island will be fine. It's survived us so far."]],
 "Walk with me tomorrow. Whatever happens, I'll want somebody to walk with."],
30:["(Daphne is sitting on the edge of the cracked fountain, in Gloom, with the green scarf showing.) Last day of the month. Whatever happens, I'm going to the circus at the lake when it's over. Nobody stands up at a circus.",[
  ["Can I come?","You can hold my candyfloss. I've never had candyfloss. That's the sort of thing that happens to princesses."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash. Gustavo's kiss on my hand. Augustus with a feather. Leopold on the steps. And a reporter who kept coming back."],
  ["What if everything changes?","Then I'll change too. I've been practising all month. I'm rather good at it now."]],
 "Thank you, darling. You made the month a great deal less dull."],
};
// Dario, CEO of Diversified Petroleum, at the HQ in Frostholm, home in a villa in Hope Springs (Diversified Petroleum). Threads: the
// oil and a government that signs without reading; his bowling lane and milkshake machine; trusting nobody (Magnus, Phil, Gustavo,
// the prince) and the bourbon; a private sorrow about somebody he lost, and a tune he hums; the spa on the volcano he has set his heart on.
DAILY['Dario']={
6:["(Dario is at his villa door in a camel-hair dressing gown, with a glass in his hand.) The road from Kingsgrad opens for one day, and the British press is on my doorstep. That's what I call service.",[
  ["Lovely house. Bigger than the palace, nearly.","Nearly. The palace has more rooms, and I have better plumbing. I know which one I'd rather sit in."],
  ["What brought an American oilman to Sarivalto?",["The same thing that brings a dog to a butcher's. (He sips.) There's oil under that sea, son. More than anyone here understands.",[
    ["And the island gets what, exactly?","Jobs. Roads. A cinema that shows films from this decade. They get everything except a say in it."],
    ["You make it sound simple.","It is simple. It's only people who make it complicated."]]]],
  ["Bit early for bourbon, isn't it?","It's always early somewhere and late somewhere else. I split the difference."]],
 "Come and see me at the HQ when Frostholm's dug out. I've got a bowling lane. Nobody else on this island can say that."],
7:["(Dario is polishing a bowling ball with a handkerchief.) A man's office tells you who he is. Most men have a desk. I have a lane, a ball return and a milkshake machine.",[
  ["Can I have a go on the lane?","One frame. Mind the third board from the left, it's warped. I lost a bet to it once."],
  ["Why a bowling lane in an oil company?","Because when I was nine I set pins in a Tulsa alley for a nickel a game, and I swore one day I'd own the whole alley. I settled for one lane. I'm a modest man."],
  ["What flavour milkshakes?","Chocolate, strawberry and vanilla. Anything fancier is for Europeans and the dentist."]],
 "Shut the door on your way out. The pins don't like a draught."],
8:["(Dario is on the telephone, and puts his hand over it.) Not now, journalist. I've a problem at sea, and I don't discuss problems. I solve them.",[
  ["Anything I can help with?","Maybe. I'll let you know. I've got a short list of people I trust, and you're not on it yet, but you're nearer the top than Magnus."],
  ["What sort of problem?","The wet kind. That's all you get, and you didn't get it from me."],
  ["You look like you need a milkshake.","(He almost smiles.) Strawberry. Two straws. I'll have both of them."]],
 "Go on. And if anybody asks, Diversified Petroleum has never had a better week."],
9:["(Dario is lining up a shot on his lane.) Watch this. Seven-ten split. Nobody makes this. I make it about one time in fifty.",[
  ["Go on, then.","(The ball clips the seven and nothing else.) That was forty-nine. Next time is fifty."],
  ["Heard Kelp Cola are cooking up a new flavour.","Phil cooks up a new flavour every time he's frightened. He's been frightened since I landed. Good for him: it keeps the dentists busy."],
  ["How are things out at the rig?","The sea's like any partner. It smiles at you, and then it sends a bill. I pay my bills."]],
 "Next time you come, I'm making that split. Bring a witness."],
10:["(Dario is in a black suit, tugging at his collar.) They're burying the king today in the Royal Chapel. I've been told I'm sitting four rows back, behind a bishop and a florist.",[
  ["You'll be paying your respects, then.","I'll be paying them in full. The old man was all right. And it's a fine place to see who's sitting next to whom."],
  ["Who's in the front rows?","Family, the Church, and Phil from Kelp Cola, because he sent the biggest wreath. I should have sent a bigger one. I'll remember that for the next king."],
  ["Black suits you.","Black always suits an oilman. It hides the stains."]],
 "Don't print what I said about the wreath. Print that I wept."],
11:["(Dario is humming something slow while he signs papers. He stops when he sees you.) You didn't hear that.",[
  ["What was the tune?","Nothing. An old song. Somebody used to sing it in the kitchen, a long time ago, and it gets into my head in the cold."],
  ["The mill hands in Eastbrook want a pay rise, I hear.","Helen's out there telling men with saws they're underpaid. Of course they're underpaid. So was I, once. I went and dug a hole about it instead of making a speech."],
  ["You've got a nice voice.","I've got a voice like a gravel truck. Out. Go on."]],
 "Close the door. And forget the tune."],
12:["(Dario is pacing past the bowling lane.) There's a record shop in Eastbrook, with a girl in it called Ruby, who won't sell me a record. I've been told no by senators, journalist. Never by a shopkeeper.",[
  ["What record?","An old jazz thing. Nobody's heard of it. (He looks at the window.) I want it. Let's leave it there."],
  ["Maybe she's a Democrat and you're an oilman.","She's certainly a Democrat. I offered her twice what it's worth. Principle's a fine thing, until it costs you rent."],
  ["Since when do oilmen like jazz?",["Since never. (He sits down, heavily.) It's not the jazz. It's who's on it.",[
    ["Who's on it?","Somebody I owe. Leave it."],
    ["Sounds personal.","Everything worth having is personal. Go on, I've got calls to make."]]]]],
 "Whatever you've heard about me today, I'm a very reasonable man."],
13:["(Dario is holding a bottle of Kelp Cola at arm's length.) Phil's people are giving these away free outside his HQ. Free. Do you know what you call a man who gives his product away?",[
  ["Generous?","Desperate. Generous is a word desperate men use about themselves."],
  ["Have you tried it?","I tried it in 1971. I still remember it. That's not praise."],
  ["You could give away free oil.","(He laughs.) Now there's an idea. Pour it on the street outside Phil's office and let him slip on it."]],
 "Take the bottle. I don't want it on my desk."],
14:["(Dario has a new tie on, and he keeps checking it in the glass of the trophy case.) Big day, journalist. The whole island in one place, and every one of them wondering who's going to run it.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I haven't been nervous since 1958. I'm attentive. There's a difference, and it's about two bourbons."],
  ["What do you expect from it?","Speeches. Hats. A lot of people being very sure of themselves. I'll stand at the back and count who's actually got money."],
  ["Nice tie.","Silk, from New York. I've got eleven more just like it, in case one of them gets spoilt."]],
 "If anyone asks where I'm standing, I'm standing wherever the wind's behind me."],
15:["(Dario is reading the Gazette with his feet on the desk.) A prince and a priest's prince arguing over a crown. Leopold or Augustus. Which one would you rather sign a drilling licence?",[
  ["The one who'd read it.","Then you've picked wrong. I want the one who won't."],
  ["Does the Church bother you?","Priests want a man to look up. I want him to look down, where the oil is. We don't get on."],
  ["Neither. Let the people vote.","You've been in Eastbrook again. People vote for whoever promises them the most. I promise more than anybody, so I've no quarrel with it."]],
 "Kings come and go. The oil was here first."],
16:["(The radio on Dario's desk is turned up: two voices are shouting over each other.) The Communists and the Democrats, having a debate. It's like listening to two cats in a sack.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever shouts last. Anna sounds like she means it. That's what worries me about her."],
  ["Which side are you on?","Neither. I'm on the side of whoever's left standing, and then I'm on it very hard."],
  ["Turn it off?","No. Know your enemy, journalist. And then sell him something."]],
 "Leave the radio. I want to hear them run out of breath."],
17:["(Dario has his coat on and his hat in his hand. He doesn't look pleased to see you.) I've told Ruth I'm not here today. To anyone. So you haven't seen me.",[
  ["Where are you going?","Somewhere quiet, where nobody wants anything from me. If you find a place like that on this island, write me a postcard."],
  ["Is something wrong?","There's always something wrong. Today it's a feeling. I've learned to listen to my feelings, journalist. They've got better instincts than my staff."],
  ["You can trust me.","I trust my bowling ball, and only because it always comes back."]],
 "You haven't seen me. Remember that."],
18:["(Dario is resetting his pins by hand.) The ground moved under the HQ, did you feel it? Knocked three of my pins down on its own. Best strike rate I've had all week.",[
  ["Was it an earthquake?","A shiver. The island's got a cold. They say there's steam up north, from the old mountain."],
  ["Does that worry you, with the rig?","Rigs are built for weather. Not for mountains. (He stands a pin up, carefully.) I've asked Magnus to look at the insurance."],
  ["Maybe the island doesn't like you.","The island doesn't have to like me. It just has to hold still while I drill."]],
 "If the floor goes again, stand in a doorway. That's free advice, and I never give it."],
19:["(Dario is at the milkshake machine, pouring vanilla.) Someone tells me a member of the royal family's been slipping out to the Midnight Note at night, in a false moustache. Have you heard that?",[
  ["Do you go to the Midnight Note?","No. (He stirs the milkshake for a long time.) Jazz and I have some history. I don't go and look at it."],
  ["Which royal?","If I knew that, journalist, I'd have a photograph in my safe. Royal secrets are worth more than oil, ounce for ounce."],
  ["Who told you?","Sven at the fuel dock. Sven tells everybody everything, for the price of a coffee. I pay him in milkshakes."]],
 "Vanilla's underrated. Like me."],
20:["(Dario is looking out of his window at the Hotel Aurora.) There's a Russian at the Aurora who calls himself a businessman. I've met businessmen. They don't buy that many batteries.",[
  ["What do you think he is?","Somebody's ears. The question is whose, and what they've heard."],
  ["Have you met him?","Once, in the hotel bar. He bought me a drink and asked how deep the rig drills. I told him to the bottom."],
  ["Maybe he likes batteries.","Then he's the first Russian I've met who likes anything."]],
 "If the Russian asks about me, tell him I'm charming."],
21:["(Dario's window is grey with ash, and the lights flicker.) The mountain's blown, journalist. Fire up north, ash on the cars, and every telephone on the island ringing at once.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","As far as I know. I've had Ruth ring round every one of my people. I'm a lot of things, but I count my men."],
  ["What does it mean for the rig?","It means I'm not sleeping tonight. The rig's a long way from the mountain, but the sea floor's all one floor."],
  ["Scared?",["(He pours a bourbon, and doesn't drink it.) A little. Don't print that.",[
    ["I won't.","Good. You're climbing up my list."],
    ["What scares you about it?","That it doesn't want anything. You can't do a deal with a mountain."]]]]],
 "Go home and shut your windows. The ash gets into everything."],
22:["(Dario is wiping ash off his bowling ball with a handkerchief.) Grey. Everything's grey. The lane's grey, the cars are grey, and Magnus's face is grey, though that's normal.",[
  ["How's Frostholm coping?","Sweeping. Everybody's out with brooms. I sent the depot crew round with a fuel truck to wash the streets down, so the Gazette can say something nice about me."],
  ["Any damage at the rig?","The rig's shaking every time the ground does. I've got men watching it round the clock, and I'm watching them."],
  ["Will the ash wash off the lane?","It'll take wax and a week. I've waited longer for less."]],
 "Wipe your feet on the way in next time. There's half a volcano on that rug."],
23:["(Dario is on the telephone and slams it down.) Something's gone wrong under the waterline at the rig. Under. The one place I can't stand and shout at it.",[
  ["What's broken?","That's what I want to know. And whether it broke, or was broken."],
  ["You think it was sabotage?","I think this island is full of people who'd like my rig at the bottom of the sea. The Communists. Phil. Possibly God."],
  ["Can it be fixed?","Everything can be fixed. Except people. People just get older and more expensive."]],
 "Don't write about it. Not yet. Not ever, ideally."],
24:["(Dario is looking at a brochure with steam on the cover.) Ever been up to the Hot Springs Spa? On the mountain's lower slopes. I used to go up every week, before it blew. Best bath I ever had.",[
  ["Is it still standing?","Standing, steaming and closed for sweeping. The pools never stopped. Nothing stops them."],
  ["You don't seem like a spa man.","I'm a man with a bad back from twenty years of holes. Hot water's the only thing on this island that's ever been kind to it."],
  ["Who owns it?","Somebody who doesn't need my money, apparently. I don't believe in such people, but I keep meeting them."]],
 "Go up and try it, when the ash settles. Tell them I sent you, and watch their faces."],
25:["(Dario is drawing a little building on a notepad, with steam coming out of it.) I've offered for the spa. Twice. The owner said it isn't for sale. Do you know how often I've heard that?",[
  ["How often?","Hundreds of times. And then, about a week later, it was for sale."],
  ["Why do you want it so badly?","Because a man should own one thing that doesn't smell of oil. And because I can."],
  ["Maybe take no for an answer.","I've never once done that, and look where I am. A lane, a milkshake machine and a window full of ash."]],
 "Put the drawing in the bin for me. No: give it back."],
26:["(Dario is straightening his tie, the silk one from New York.) Today I'm going up the mountain to talk about the spa. Man to man, owner to owner. One of us is going to be very happy.",[
  ["And if they still say no?","Then I'll have a bath and think about it. I do my best thinking in hot water."],
  ["How much are you offering?","More than it's worth. That's the only kind of offer that ever works on a sentimental person."],
  ["Can I come?","You can follow. Everybody follows me, journalist. Only some of them get there."]],
 "Wish me luck. No, don't. Luck's for people who haven't got a plan."],
27:["(Dario has a list on his desk headed PARTY, and he's adding to it.) I'm throwing a party here at the HQ tomorrow. Bowling, milkshakes and every one of my people. Even Magnus, God help us.",[
  ["What's the occasion?","Loyalty. You feed people, you let them knock some pins over, and they remember who did it, on the days that matter."],
  ["Am I invited?","You're the press. You're invited to stand by the door and write down how generous I was."],
  ["Gustavo's poker game is today, isn't it?","It is, in the pizzeria's back room. I don't play cards with a man who owns the table, the deck and the waiter."]],
 "Strawberry or chocolate, for the party? Vanilla's for me."],
28:["(The HQ is strung with streamers, and Dario has rolled up his sleeves.) Party day, journalist, and the whole island's out pushing its luck at once. Communists, Cola, the Church. Me, I'm pushing bowling.",[
  ["Is this really just a party?","It's never just a party. A man with a milkshake in one hand and a bowling ball in the other tells you all sorts. I've got good ears."],
  ["Who's the best bowler here?","Ruth. She bowls like she answers the phone: no nonsense and straight down the middle."],
  ["What are you hoping for, these last days?","A government that signs where I point. And a quiet week. I'll settle for the first."]],
 "Take your shoes off before you go on my lane. Everybody does. Even the priests would."],
29:["(Dario is reading a list of names and drumming his fingers.) The Republicans choose their leader today. Magnus, against Phil's man Harald, and Gustavo's man Carlo. A stiff, a fizz and a hood.",[
  ["Do you want Magnus to win?","I want Diversified Petroleum to win. Magnus is the coat it's wearing this season."],
  ["Isn't Magnus your man?","Magnus is Magnus's man. But he's ours too, for as long as it suits him. That's the same deal I've got with everybody."],
  ["Who's the favourite?","Whoever Margit thinks counts the votes fairest. Margit's honest, and that makes her the most dangerous woman in Frostholm."]],
 "Come back when it's counted. I'll be at the lane, pretending I'm not nervous."],
30:["(Dario is standing at the end of his lane with a ball, not bowling.) Last day, journalist. Whatever this island decides, it decides today. I've done everything a man can do with a chequebook and a telephone.",[
  ["Is that all a man can do?","No. There are other things. I've done most of those too, over the years. I'm not proud of all of them."],
  ["What will you do if it goes against you?","Drill somewhere else. The world's round, and most of it's on top of something."],
  ["You seem far away.",["(He sets the ball down.) I was thinking about somebody who'd have liked to see this island again. She never got to.",[
    ["Who?","Somebody who sang. That's all you get. It's more than anybody else got."],
    ["I'm sorry.","Don't be. Sorry's no use to anyone. Have a milkshake instead."]]]]],
 "Whatever happens today, journalist, you were more interesting than most."],
};
// Dex, who runs the hot dog cart (The Dogfather) on Eastbrook's food-cart plaza (Democratic Party). Threads: the island's gossip by
// lunchtime, a leaflet with every sausage, his new sausage of the week, his rivalry with Rosa at the cart outside Kelp Cola HQ, and
// Paolo the bookie's odds on who'll run the island, chalked up on the wall by the plaza.
DAILY['Dex']={
4:["(Dex slaps a sausage into a bun and a leaflet into your other hand.) Welcome to the Dogfather! First hot dog in Eastbrook since the snow. Mustard? Ketchup? Democracy? It all comes free with the bun.",[
  ["What's the leaflet?","Erik's finest. 'A free vote, a fair wage, no masters.' Read it while you chew. Most people read it twice: once for the words and once for the mustard stains."],
  ["Why 'the Dogfather'?","Because I make you an offer you can't refuse. One hot dog, one dollar, one leaflet. (He winks.) And because Paolo the bookie told me the Syndicate lot hate it."],
  ["What's the news in Eastbrook?","Snow's off the square. Maya's beans are bad, Jonna's chair is empty, Ruby's turned the music up to drown out the silence. And there's a foreign reporter. That's you. You're news."]],
 "Come back at lunchtime. By then I'll know everything."],
5:["(Dex is chalking prices on a little board.) Monks! They've opened the road to the monastery. Somebody'll be up there by now, getting the Abbot to bless their sausages. Not me. My sausages are already blessed.",[
  ["Who blessed them?","My mother. Every one, when she taught me the recipe. She said 'God help whoever eats these'. That's nearly a blessing."],
  ["Any gossip today?","Anna from the Communists went to see the Abbot last week, in a headscarf. And Linnea at the tavern says the royal maids are all looking for new jobs. You didn't hear it from me. You heard it from a sausage."],
  ["One hot dog, please.","(He builds it with great ceremony.) There. Leaflet's on the house. Read the small print about fishing quotas, Erik worked very hard on it."]],
 "Mind the mustard on your sleeve. It never comes out."],
6:["(Dex is leaning on his cart, nodding at the wall by the plaza, where someone has chalked numbers.) Seen Paolo's board? He's taking bets on who'll run the island. I'm not allowed to bet. I'm a party member. I just read it.",[
  ["What are the odds?","Royals are favourites, Communists are close, Kelp Cola's got the money. And us? Twenty to one, last time I looked. Paolo says it with a big sorry smile."],
  ["Is Paolo Syndicate?","He takes bets and he wears a nice coat and he lives in the Timber Lofts like the rest of us. He's never said who he works for. He never has to."],
  ["Twenty to one's not good.","Twenty to one's a lovely number. That's how much you win. Erik says don't bet against a teacher. I say don't bet against a man with a hot plate."]],
 "Don't put money on us. Put mustard on a sausage."],
7:["(Dex is wiping down the cart, whistling.) The north-west's open! Frostholm, the oil men, the big billboards. All of them with sausages of their own. Polar Burger. Burgers! On an island! In the snow!",[
  ["Is Polar Burger a threat?","Sanna's lovely. Her burgers are fine. Her burgers are also square. A man shouldn't trust a square food. Round's honest."],
  ["Anything new on the menu?","This week's special: the Lumberjack. Smoked sausage, fried onions and a little sprig of pine. Bjarne tried it and said it tasted of the mill. That's a compliment from Bjarne."],
  ["What's the gossip from Frostholm?","Dario's got a secretary ringing all over the island, looking for a record. Ruby's told her no nine times. And there's a Russian businessman at the Hotel Aurora who tips in roubles. Who tips in roubles?"]],
 "The Lumberjack. Ask for it by name."],
8:["(Dex has the radio balanced on the cart, tuned to the monastery.) Sunday. Everyone's waiting to hear what the Abbot says about the old king. The whole plaza's got its ears out.",[
  ["What do people expect him to say?","Something long and sad, with a lot of Latin in it. Pirkko from the food van's already crossed herself twice and bought a hot dog. Biggest crowd I've had all week."],
  ["Is everyone churchy round here?","On Sundays, half the island prays and the other half pretends to be busy when the priest goes by. I sell to both. God doesn't mind mustard."],
  ["Any news from the oil rig?","Something's gone wrong out there, they say. The boats from Saltwick saw a black slick on the water first thing. Dario's on the telephone shouting. You'll hear more by lunchtime."]],
 "Sunday sausages. Holy and spicy."],
9:["(Dex is surrounded by people asking questions, and looks delighted.) Everyone wants to know what flavour people like! Cola men, Party men, all asking the hot dog man. I've never been so important.",[
  ["What do people like, then?","Salt and sweet together, and something to make you sneeze. People want to be surprised, but only a little. That's my whole philosophy."],
  ["Isn't Rosa the expert on that?","Rosa! (He clutches his chest.) Rosa at the Kelp Cola cart. Everyone says ask Rosa. Rosa's never had a new idea in her life. Rosa sells the same dog she sold in 1972."],
  ["What's the Democrat dog?","My new invention. Mustard, pickles and a little paper flag on a stick. Erik ate one and looked confused. Confused is good. Confused is thinking."]],
 "Mustard and pickles. That's the future."],
10:["(Dex has a black armband on and a little black ribbon tied round the handle of his tongs.) It's the funeral today. I haven't sold a hot dog since breakfast. Nobody eats at a funeral, and everyone's at it in their heads.",[
  ["Do you miss the king?","Not his politics. But he bought a hot dog off my father once, at the summer fair, and paid with a note so big nobody had change. My father framed it. Then he spent it. But he framed it first."],
  ["Who went to the funeral?","Erik, in his father's suit. Linnea from the tavern, sobbing like it was her own dad. Arvid from the Grill too: royals, the both of them. And something's coming in by boat at the harbour today, I hear, while everyone's looking the other way."],
  ["Something by boat?","(He taps his nose.) Paolo was very interested in the boat times. That's all I'll say. When Paolo's interested, the customs shed gets interesting."]],
 "Half price today. Out of respect."],
11:["(Dex has a crowd of mill hands round his cart, all talking at once.) The mill lads are on their lunch break, which has been going for two hours. Pay and the saw. Helen's up from Saltwick to speak for them.",[
  ["What do they want?","A rise, after six years. And a guard on the saw: Dagny had it pulled to speed up the line. A lad lost two fingers to it in June. Knut lost the tip of one in a different story every week."],
  ["Is it a Communist thing?","It's a union thing. Helen's a Communist, but a union card's a union card. Erik's upset he didn't think of it first. He'll be upset all week."],
  ["Free hot dogs for strikers?","(He thinks.) Half price. I'm a Democrat, not a saint. And a leaflet each, so they remember who fed them."]],
 "If you're going up to the mill, take a sausage for Ilse. She forgets to eat."],
12:["(Dex is chalking a new special on his board: THE OIL BARON. Hot dog with a little hat.) Juniper's put a Stetson and cowboy boots in her window. In Eastbrook! I've named a sausage after them.",[
  ["What's in the Oil Baron?","Smoked sausage, barbecue sauce, and a little paper Stetson on a toothpick. It's tasteless. In both senses. It's selling like mad."],
  ["Any other gossip?","Ruby's doing something secret with a record, and she won't tell me, which is a first. And there's money moving at Diversified Petroleum. You don't need to know how I know. You need a hot dog."],
  ["Who'd wear a Stetson in Eastbrook?","Juniper says a hat always finds its head. Whose head, she won't say. She's very discreet, Juniper. For about an hour."]],
 "The Oil Baron. Yee-haw, as they say in Frostholm."],
13:["(Dex is glaring at a Kelp Cola girl in a paper hat, handing out free bottles across the plaza.) Free samples. In my plaza. Do I go up to Kelp Cola HQ and give out free sausages outside Rosa's cart? I do not.",[
  ["Maybe you should.","(He considers it, very seriously.) Free sausages, outside Kelp Cola, outside Rosa's cart. With leaflets. ...I like you. You're a troublemaker."],
  ["Do you sell Kelp Cola yourself?","I sell what people drink. That's not the same as liking it. I sell it with a leaflet about corporate power. That's balance."],
  ["Any other gossip?","There's talk the Syndicate's after Kelp Cola's secret recipe. Paolo was asking Wilma at the museum about the old safe. Wilma told him it's a gift shop. Paolo bought a mug."]],
 "If the cola girl wants a sausage, it's full price."],
14:["(Dex is packing his cart for travel: tongs, mustard, a crate of buns and a stack of leaflets as thick as a Bible.) The fourteenth! The whole island in one place! Do you know how many hungry people that is?",[
  ["How many hot dogs are you bringing?","Four hundred. And four hundred leaflets. One each. Some people will get a leaflet and no hot dog. That's politics. Some people will get a hot dog and no leaflet. That's lunch."],
  ["What's everyone saying about today?","That it's the day the island looks itself in the eye. Paolo's not taking bets on it. When Paolo won't take a bet, you know nobody knows anything."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Of a crowd? I sell to crowds. I'm nervous of the mustard running out. That's the real disaster."]],
 "Come and find the Dogfather. Follow the smell."],
15:["(Dex is counting coins into a tin and looking exhausted and happy.) Three hundred and eighty hot dogs. Every leaflet gone. One man ate four. I think it was Igor from the circus. Very gentle with a bun.",[
  ["What did people talk about?","Everything. Who's going to be king, who isn't, why the Abbot had his best robes on. The whole island in one place, and every one of them talking at once. I loved it."],
  ["What's the gossip today?","The palace and the monastery are at each other. Crown before cross, cross before crown. Prince Leopold's lot want the monks quiet. The monks want Augustus. Somebody's getting a strongly worded letter."],
  ["Did the Democrats do well?","People listened. Some of them even kept their leaflets. One woman folded hers into a hat, but that's still keeping it."]],
 "Twenty hot dogs left. Help me out."],
16:["(Dex is setting up his cart outside the hall where the debate's being held.) Debate day! The Democrats and the Communists. I'm parked outside, so both sides have to walk past me. Strategic sausage placement.",[
  ["Will you sell to the Communists?","Of course. A hungry Communist is an angry Communist. A full Communist is just a bloke with a strong opinion. I'm keeping the peace."],
  ["Who'll win the argument?","Erik knows his stuff. Anna's sharp as a fish knife. Helen's got the room on her side when it's workers. Paolo's got it at evens. He's never had it at evens."],
  ["What's today's special?","The Debate Dog: half mustard, half ketchup, and you have to argue about which end to start at. It's a bit gimmicky. It's selling like mad."]],
 "Pick a side. Pick a sauce."],
17:["(Dex's cart rattles on its wheels, and he grabs the tongs before they fall.) That's three today. The ground. Rosa says her cart rolled clean across the square at Kelp Cola. She chased it for a whole street.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Steam off the mountain, people say. Pat on the beach says he saw smoke at night. Pat sees a lot of things. But Hekki at the radio tower said it too, and Hekki's sober."],
  ["Is Erik worried?","Erik's counting fire buckets and telling me to keep the cart near the tram, in case people need to move. I'm not moving. People need sausages more than ever in a crisis."],
  ["What's the gossip from Frostholm?","Dario's not in his office. Nobody's seen him. His secretary says 'meetings'. Paolo says 'interesting'. I say 'pass the mustard'."]],
 "Brake on, cart. Stay where you are."],
18:["(Dex is waving a tea towel at a man in a paper cup costume across the plaza: it's Kelpy, the Kelp Cola cup.) That's Jaana in there, from the museum. Something's going on with Kelpy today. Lots of men in suits around the museum.",[
  ["What's going on inside Kelpy?","Jaana won't say, she can't say, she's in a cup. But a cola executive came out of the museum this morning looking like he'd seen a ghost. Whatever's in Kelpy, it's more than Jaana."],
  ["Anything else today?","The monks are looking for something they've lost. Brother Paavo came down the mountain asking everyone if they'd seen 'the relic'. I said I'd seen a sausage. He didn't laugh."],
  ["Want me to wave at Kelpy too?","Go on. She likes it. She can't wave back, the arms don't bend. But she wiggles."]],
 "If Kelpy wants a sausage, I'll post it through the slot."],
19:["(Dex is very carefully not looking at a man in dark glasses and a big hat who's just bought a hot dog.) Don't look. Don't look. That was someone from the palace in a disguise. I'd know that chin anywhere.",[
  ["Which royal?","I'm not saying. A good hot dog man never says. But he paid with a coin that had his own father's face on it, and he didn't even notice."],
  ["Where's he going?","Frostholm, I'd guess. The Midnight Note. That's where people go when they want to be nobody for an evening. Ruby goes. Half the island goes, in hats."],
  ["What else is happening?","The Communists are out recruiting. Comrades wanted! They've got a table at the Saltwick fish plant with free tea. Free tea! They've learnt from Kelp Cola."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the chin."],
20:["(Dex is leaning in close, whispering, and smells strongly of onions.) The man at the Hotel Aurora. The Russian. He bought a hot dog yesterday and asked me who sells papers in Eastbrook, and who buys them. Who buys papers! Who asks that?",[
  ["What did you tell him?","Everybody. Which is true. Then I gave him extra onions. Extra onions are my secret weapon. He'll be breathing them in his hotel room for a week."],
  ["Erik's looking into him, isn't he?","Erik's whispering about him so loudly the whole plaza knows. Today's the day, he says. I'll know by lunchtime. I always know by lunchtime."],
  ["Is he a spy?","He's a businessman who asks about newspapers and never talks about business. You tell me what that is."]],
 "Extra onions. On the house. For courage."],
21:["(Dex has a huge pot balanced on his hot plate and is ladling soup into tin cups.) The mountain's blown! Ash coming down from the north! I'm making soup! Hot dog soup! Don't ask what's in it!",[
  ["Is everybody safe?","Erik's got the Community Centre open. Nobody's hurt, that we've heard. Monks coming down from the mountain with blankets. Even Paolo's helping, carrying old ladies across the plaza."],
  ["What's in the soup?","Sausages, potatoes, onions, a bit of mustard. And love. Mostly sausages."],
  ["Shouldn't you get inside?","In a minute. People are cold and frightened, and soup's the one thing I can do. (He hands you a cup.) Drink. Then go."]],
 "Soup and then shelter. Go on."],
22:["(Dex's cart is grey with ash, and he's scraping it with a spatula.) Everything's grey. My buns. My mustard. Me. But we're all here. Nobody hurt, on the whole island. I've checked with everyone. That's the gossip today. The best gossip I've ever had.",[
  ["What about the north?","The farms got it worst. Lava down the hillside, the roads gone in places. But no people hurt. Animals got out. Even the farm cat."],
  ["How's the plaza?","Grey and full. Everybody's out sweeping and borrowing brooms and lending sugar. It's the friendliest Eastbrook's been since the war."],
  ["Free hot dogs?","For anyone with a broom. That's the rule this week. I've given away sixty. Erik says I'm bankrupting the movement. I say I'm feeding it."]],
 "Got a broom? Have a hot dog."],
23:["(Dex is working with a fresh set of tongs, and keeps glancing at the sky.) Still grey up north. Steam. The aftershocks come every few hours. I've stopped jumping. Mostly.",[
  ["Any gossip from the clean-up?","The Communists say somebody's been sabotaging something at Kelp Cola. And Dario's got men out at the oil rig, mending something deep down. Everyone's mending. Even the crooks."],
  ["How's business?","Never better. Frightened people eat. Tired people eat. People sweeping ash eat twice. I'm going to need a second cart."],
  ["A second cart?","(His eyes shine.) The Dogfather Two. Up at the tram stop. Or Kingsgrad, outside the palace. Right next to Rosa. Imagine her face."]],
 "The Dogfather Two. Remember where you heard it first."],
24:["(Dex is drawing on a napkin: a cart, with a little roof.) The Dogfather Two. I've got the plans. Bjarne says he'll build the frame from mill offcuts, if Dagny doesn't count them. Dagny counts everything.",[
  ["Where will it go?","Kingsgrad, outside the palace gates. Every tourist, every royal, every guard on his tea break. And opposite Rosa, at Kelp Cola, so she can watch me."],
  ["Any gossip today?","The monks have gone up to the peaks for a retreat. And the Syndicate's cleaning up after something. Paolo's gone very quiet. When Paolo's quiet, I count my spoons."],
  ["Why do you hate Rosa so much?","Hate? (He looks shocked.) I don't hate Rosa. She taught me to cook onions in 1975. I just have to beat her. That's love, on this island."]],
 "Napkin plans. Every great empire starts on a napkin."],
25:["(Dex is wearing a paper crown and selling a hot dog with a little crown on a toothpick.) The Princess Dog! In honour of Princess Daphne, who Erik says might come round to democracy. It's got a little cherry on it. Princesses like cherries.",[
  ["Is she really coming round?","Erik thinks so. Erik's met her for tea, or he's going to, or he's thinking about it. Erik's been wearing his good jacket all week. That's how I know."],
  ["What's the palace doing today?","Leopold's giving out paper crowns. A crown for everyone, they're calling it. I got mine first. (He tips the paper crown.) I've always wanted to be a king for a day."],
  ["Doesn't the crown make you a royalist?","It's paper, it's free, and it keeps the ash off my hair. That's not royalism. That's practical."]],
 "The Princess Dog. Royalty, with mustard."],
26:["(Dex is reading the Gazette on his cart, with ketchup on it.) Dario's buying the spa. Kelp Cola's putting a prince on every bottle. And Paolo's odds have changed again. Nobody knows anything, and everybody's betting on it.",[
  ["How are the odds now?","I'm not allowed to say. Erik made me promise. They've moved, that's all. Up or down, my lips are sealed. With mustard."],
  ["Will Dario make the spa better?","He'll make it more expensive. Saga at the spa's already looking for a new job. She came in and asked me if the Dogfather's hiring. It might be, for the Dogfather Two."],
  ["What about the prince on the bottle?","Which prince? Nobody knows! They've printed both, people say, and they're waiting to see which one sells. That's Kelp Cola. They'd print a fish if it won."]],
 "Read the paper. It's got ketchup on, it's more interesting."],
27:["(Dex is loading a second cart, a rickety borrowed one, with crate after crate of sausages.) Rally's tomorrow! I'm taking both carts to Kingsgrad. Four hundred hot dogs. Six hundred leaflets. This is my finest hour.",[
  ["Whose is the second cart?","Rosa's old spare. (He grins.) I asked her. She said yes. She said, 'It's for democracy, Dex, and you'll need all the help you can get.' Then she laughed for ten minutes."],
  ["What's the gossip today?","The Communists are showing a film at the cinema. Revolution at the pictures! Otto's furious. And the Syndicate's got a big card game at the casino. Very high stakes, Paolo says."],
  ["Six hundred leaflets?","Bertha printed them. She only smudged a hundred. Erik calls that a miracle. I call it Friday."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Bring an appetite."],
28:["(Dex has both carts lined up at the edge of Kingsgrad square, and is lighting the hot plates with shaking hands.) Rally day! Four hundred sausages, six hundred leaflets, and a square that's empty for about another hour. I've never been so nervous about a bun.",[
  ["How big will the crowd be?","Erik says thousands. Bjarne says hundreds. Paolo won't say anything, and he's moved his board indoors. I've brought enough for thousands. Optimism's cheaper than sausages."],
  ["Will there be trouble?","There's always a few lads looking for a fight by the fountain. Ilse says she'll have a word if they start. Ilse's words are very short and very effective."],
  ["One hot dog, please.","(He hands it over without even looking.) Leaflet's inside the bun today. Saves time. Don't eat it."]],
 "Next! Next! Don't eat the leaflet!"],
29:["(Dex is sitting on his cart, with his feet up on the hot plate, which is off.) I've got no voice and no pickles and no sausages left. I'm a hot dog man with no hot dogs. Ask me anything. I know everything today.",[
  ["What do you know?","That the Communists and the Democrats are being friendly in public and fierce in private. That every faction on the island's had its biggest day. That tomorrow, someone wins."],
  ["Who's going to win?","Paolo's stopped taking bets. That's how close it is. He says it's the first time in twenty years he hasn't known."],
  ["Are you happy?","I've sold more hot dogs this month than in my whole life. I've handed out three thousand leaflets. I've got a second cart on a napkin. (He smiles.) I'm very happy."]],
 "Tomorrow's the big one. I'll know by lunchtime."],
30:["(Dex is setting up the cart very slowly and carefully, like it's the first day of the season.) The last day. I've polished the tongs. Whatever happens, people are going to want to eat after.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote, a fair wage, no masters. And a licence for the Dogfather Two that doesn't take Bertil three months."],
  ["What's the gossip today?","There isn't any. That's the strangest thing. The whole island's gone quiet. Everyone's waiting. Nobody wants to say anything in case it's wrong."],
  ["Thank you for the hot dogs, Dex.","(He hands you one, wrapped in a leaflet.) Last one of the month, on the house. Whatever happens, come back to the plaza. I'll know by lunchtime."]],
 "Go on. History's waiting. And mustard."],
};
// Doctor Lind, the island's doctor, in Kingsgrad (Republican Party). Threads: the hospital the island hasn't got and the plans
// he keeps drawing for one, his chess game by post with Doctor Salmi in Turku, too little sleep, which candidate might pay for
// a ward, and patients he won't name.
DAILY['Doctor Lind']={
2:["(Doctor Lind is washing his hands at a little basin.) You're not bleeding, are you? Good. Then you're the first person through that door today who isn't.",[
  ["Busy morning?","Three frostbitten toes, one sprained wrist and a man who swallowed a fishhook. The roads opened and everyone came to show me what the snow did to them."],
  ["I'm the British reporter.","So I hear. Kingsgrad's very small. Well, welcome. If you need anything stitched, I'm downstairs from the police. Convenient for both of us."],
  ["Is there a hospital on the island?","No. (He dries his hands.) There's me, two beds and a medicine cabinet. Anyone very ill goes by boat to Oulu. Six hours, if the sea's kind."]],
 "Wrap up. The cold's what I see most of, after fishhooks."],
3:["(The doctor is writing up notes, very fast.) Sit, if you like. Don't touch the cabinet. Nothing personal: I don't let myself touch it either, after midnight.",[
  ["Do you work nights too?","On call every night. The telephone rings, I put my coat on over my pyjamas. Half of Kingsgrad's seen my pyjamas. They're blue."],
  ["Who was in this morning?","I don't say who. (He keeps writing.) But someone from the palace came in with a cut from a very good knife, and said it was the cheese. It wasn't the cheese."],
  ["You look tired.","I'm always tired. I was tired in nineteen sixty-two. I'll be tired when I'm dead, and then I'll finally get some sleep."]],
 "Don't catch anything. I've no beds."],
4:["(A chessboard sits on a shelf, mid-game.) Don't knock that. It's taken eleven months to get there.",[
  ["Who are you playing?",["Doctor Salmi, in Turku. We were students together. One move a letter. Fourteen days there, fourteen back. We've made eleven moves.",[
    ["Who's winning?","He is. He's been winning since nineteen fifty-one. That's why I keep playing. One day he'll make a mistake, and I'll be there."],
    ["Isn't that very slow?","It's the only thing in my life that is slow. I treasure it."]]]],
  ["What's your next move?","Knight to F-five, I think. I'll post it on Monday. Then a month of worrying I've made a terrible mistake."],
  ["I play chess.","Then don't look at the board. I'll know if anyone's looked. I'll see it in the dust."]],
 "Don't touch the board on your way out."],
5:["Do you want to see something? (He unrolls a sheet of paper covered in pencil.) The Kingsgrad Hospital. Twelve beds, an operating room, an X-ray machine.",[
  ["Who drew it?","I did. On the back of forty prescription pads, over twenty years. My architect is an insomniac doctor with a ruler."],
  ["Where would it go?","On the empty lot past the tram stop. Close to the palace, close to the harbour. Close to Kelp Cola, too, which might help with the money."],
  ["How much would it cost?","More than the island's ever spent on anything except the palace roof. The old king liked the roof. He never needed the hospital."]],
 "(He rolls it up carefully.) One day."],
6:["I'm a Republican, you know. People are surprised. They expect a doctor to be a Communist, or a saint.",[
  ["Why a Republican?","Because I like things that work. I don't care who's on the coins. I care whether there's a bed when a fisherman's crushed under a winch."],
  ["Who'll you vote for?","Whoever builds the hospital. Harald's got the money. Magnus has got more money. Carlo's got money of a sort I don't want to ask about."],
  ["What about the revolutionaries?","Anna's people want free medicine for everyone. Lovely. With what? I've seen what a revolution does to a hospital. You get fewer doctors and more speeches."]],
 "Fewer revolutionaries, more hospitals. Put that in your paper."],
7:["(The doctor has a little girl's doll on the desk and is bandaging its arm.) Don't mind me. It's for a patient who's frightened of the bandage.",[
  ["That's very kind.","It works. You bandage the doll first, then the girl lets you do hers. I learned it in Helsinki. Best thing they taught me."],
  ["Who's the patient?","A child from the Kingsgrad Apartments. That's all you get. If you want her name, she'll tell you herself. Loudly."],
  ["Do you like children?","I like children better than grown-ups. They tell you where it hurts. Grown-ups tell you it's nothing, and then it's something."]],
 "If you're frightened of bandages too, I'll do the doll first."],
8:["(He has a box of burn dressings laid out on the bed.) They say something's burst at the oil rig. I've put out the burns kit, in case.",[
  ["Are you expecting casualties?","I'm a doctor. I'm always expecting casualties. Mostly they don't come. When they do, I want the dressings where I can find them."],
  ["Could they bring anyone here?","A boat from the rig to Eastbrook Harbour, then a cart. An hour, if they're quick. This is what I mean about a hospital. An hour's a long time when you're burnt."],
  ["Did you hear the sermon?","On the wireless, while I counted bandages. It was decent. Plain words. The old king would have liked it. He disliked long sermons. He told me so, as a patient."]],
 "Let's hope I've laid it all out for nothing."],
9:["(The doctor is writing a letter.) Knight to F-five. I've posted it. Now I'm writing to Salmi again to say I've changed my mind.",[
  ["Can you change your mind by post?","No. That's the rule. But I like to tell him I'd have liked to. It makes him nervous."],
  ["What's the cola business?","Everyone's talking about the Communists making their own cola. And Kelp Cola making a new one. I'll be treating the stomachs of both."],
  ["Do you ever take a day off?","Christmas. In the morning. Then somebody's goose goes down the wrong way, and that's Christmas."]],
 "Salmi'll get my move in a fortnight. I'll be sleepless till then."],
10:["(The doctor wears a black armband over his white coat.) The funeral. I won't be going. Somebody faints at every funeral. I'll be here when they do.",[
  ["Did you treat the king?","For thirty years. He had a bad knee, a good heart and a terrible way with pills. Wouldn't take them. Fed them to the palace dogs."],
  ["What did he die of?","Of being old. That's all I'll say. Kings die like everybody else. They just get a better coffin."],
  ["Are you sad?","He was a patient. You're not supposed to have favourites. (He looks out of the window.) He was my favourite."]],
 "If you see anyone go pale in the chapel, send them down."],
11:["(He's examining a man's hand while talking to you over his shoulder.) Mill accident. Not this one, another. They're always happening at Dagny's mill.",[
  ["Are the mills dangerous?","The saws are old and the men are tired. That's the recipe. I've sewn more fingers back on from that mill than from the rest of the island."],
  ["Should someone do something?","Somebody should. Dagny should buy new guards. Helen says she'll make her. I wish them both luck. I'd like fewer fingers in jars."],
  ["You can't talk about patients.","I'm not. I'm talking about saws. Saws don't have privacy."]],
 "Mind your fingers, wherever you go."],
12:["(The doctor is asleep in his chair. He wakes with a jolt.) I'm awake. I wasn't asleep. Who's bleeding?",[
  ["Nobody. Long night?","Three calls. A baby, a broken ankle, and an old lady who thought she was dying and was just very cold. Two hours' sleep. Very glamorous."],
  ["You should rest.","I'll rest when there's another doctor on the island. There's a nurse in Saltwick, very good, and me. And Madame Zora at the circus, who thinks she's a doctor."],
  ["Who had the baby?","A girl. Healthy. Loud. That's all. Her mother can tell you the rest."]],
 "Don't let me fall asleep again. I snore."],
13:["(There are three bottles of Kelp Cola on his desk.) Free, they say. I've had two patients this morning with stomach ache. Both said they'd had five free ones.",[
  ["Is it bad for you?","Everything's bad for you if you drink five of it. Water's bad for you if you drink five buckets. Cola's just quicker."],
  ["Would Harald build a hospital?","He sent me a crate of cola and a note saying he 'cares about the island's health'. I wrote back asking for an X-ray machine. No answer yet."],
  ["Are you going to drink yours?","After work. With a sandwich. It's the only sweet thing I'll have all week. Don't tell my patients."]],
 "One bottle. Not five."],
14:["(The doctor has a bag packed by the door.) I'm going to the big day. Someone has to. Crowds faint, crowds fall over, crowds argue and get punched.",[
  ["Are you hoping for anything?","I'm hoping for a quiet day. Nobody fainting, nobody hurt. And somebody up there saying the word 'hospital'. Just once."],
  ["Do you feel part of it?","Everyone's part of it. That's the point. I'll stand at the back with the bag and watch the island decide what it wants to be."],
  ["What's in the bag?","Smelling salts, bandages and a flask of tea. That's what a crowd needs, mostly."]],
 "If you see anyone go down, shout for me. I'll be at the back."],
15:["(He's writing.) Two letters. One to Salmi, one to Harald. Salmi will answer. Harald won't.",[
  ["What did you ask Harald?","For the third time, for the X-ray machine. I said I'd name it after him. The Harald Machine. I don't think he's tempted."],
  ["Did Salmi reply?","Bishop to G-four. (He groans.) He's got me. Again. Thirty-one years and the man's never lost a game against me."],
  ["Isn't there trouble between the palace and the Church?","So they say. I don't take sides. I treat both. Augustus came in last month with a bad back from kneeling. Leopold, for nothing at all. He never has anything wrong."]],
 "Bishop to G-four. I'll be up all night."],
16:["(The debate's on the radio in the waiting room.) Listen to them. Anna wants free doctors. Erik wants doctors to be voted for. I'm the only doctor. Nobody's asked me.",[
  ["What would you say if they did?","Pay for a hospital first. Then argue about who owns it. You can't vote a broken leg better."],
  ["Who's winning?","The waiting room. They're shouting at the radio. A fisherman with a sore throat is shouting loudest. I've told him to stop."],
  ["Would you work for the Communists?","I'd work for anyone with a sick child. But I'd want to keep my own chessboard, thank you."]],
 "Turn it down on your way out. Please."],
17:["(He's putting things into a bag.) Did you feel that? The ground. I've had three people in with cuts from falling crockery already.",[
  ["Was it bad?","Little shakes. But if the mountain's waking up, I'll need more than a bag. I've written to Oulu for more dressings. Fourteen days."],
  ["What about Dario?","Missing, they say. Nobody's brought him to me, so he's not hurt. That's the doctor's view. Everyone else can worry about where he is."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a doctor. I'm paid to worry, and I'm underpaid, so I worry for free as well."]],
 "Put your crockery on low shelves. I mean it."],
18:["(He unrolls his hospital plans again.) Look at it. With the ground shaking, I keep thinking: twelve beds. Twelve. Wouldn't it be good, now?",[
  ["What would you do if the mountain erupted?","Use the church. The Kingsgrad church has pews, a roof and a lot of floor. I've asked the priest. He said yes before I'd finished asking."],
  ["Is anyone helping you prepare?","Sergeant Hanna. She came in with a list. She's better at lists than I am. Between us we've got blankets, bandages and a big pot for tea."],
  ["Will the hospital ever happen?","After a disaster, people find money. That's the sad truth. They find money for hospitals after they need them."]],
 "Twelve beds. Keep it in mind."],
19:["(The doctor is fitting cloth masks on a row of wooden heads, the kind for hats.) Grace lent me the heads from her shop. I'm making ash masks.",[
  ["Why ash masks?","If the mountain throws ash, it gets in the lungs. Cotton and a bit of gauze. Better than nothing. Grace cut me a hundred from an old bolt of cloth."],
  ["Is Grace helping?","She's furious about it. 'My best muslin,' she says, every time she cuts one. Then she cuts another. That's Grace."],
  ["Will people wear them?","Fishermen won't. Children will, if you say they look like Falcon in his flying mask. Otto gave me that idea."]],
 "Take a mask. Take two. Keep them by the door."],
20:["(He's on the telephone.) Yes, Hilda. Yes, the cow can come. No, not into the surgery. (He hangs up.) The farms are coming south.",[
  ["Are they evacuating?","On their own. Nobody's ordered it. They don't like the steam and their animals don't like the ground. Sensible people."],
  ["Is it going to erupt?","I'm a doctor, not a mountain. But Nils in Frostholm has a machine that says it's getting worse. And my teeth hurt. My teeth have never been wrong."],
  ["Do you need any help?","Can you carry? Blankets to the church, then. No, wait. You're a reporter. Write it down instead. 'Doctor short of blankets.' Someone might read it."]],
 "Keep a mask in your pocket. Today, of all days."],
21:["(The doctor's coat is grey with ash. He's sitting for the first time all day.) It went up. The mountain. And nobody's dead. Nobody. Do you understand how lucky that is?",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Coughs, a lot of them. Two burns from people who went too close to look. A broken wrist from a man who fell off a cart. That's all. That's nothing."],
  ["Where is everyone?","In the church, half of them. Sleeping on pews. Lotta from the palace is making soup in the big pot. Sergeant Hanna's counting heads."],
  ["You should rest.","I'll sit for five minutes. Then the telephone will ring, and I'll put my coat on over my pyjamas. Except I'm already wearing the coat."]],
 "Wear the mask. Please. Everyone's lungs are mine for the next month."],
22:["(He's listening to a fisherman's chest.) Breathe in. Out. (To you.) Ash cough. I'll hear it in my sleep. If I sleep.",[
  ["How many coughs today?","Forty. Children worst. Old people second. Fishermen, never, because fishermen don't come to the doctor till they're dead."],
  ["Is there anything to do for it?","Steam, rest, warm drinks, and stay indoors till the ash is washed off the street. The rain'll help. I've asked God for rain."],
  ["How's Doctor Salmi?","(He smiles.) I got a letter. He says he heard about the volcano on the Finnish radio. He's offered me a draw. Thirty-one years, and a draw. It's the volcano. He feels sorry for me."]],
 "Breathe through your nose. And don't sweep without a mask."],
23:["Margit's sent everyone a letter. The vote's still on, the twenty-ninth. I nearly forgot it existed. Then I remembered the hospital.",[
  ["Has anyone promised you a hospital?","After the eruption? All three of them. Harald, Magnus and Carlo. In letters. Very good letters. I've put them in the drawer with the hospital plans."],
  ["Do you believe them?","I believe the eruption. That's real. The letters are good writing. Nobody writes a good letter about a hospital unless they want something."],
  ["Will you take Salmi's draw?","(He looks at the board.) No. I've found a move. Rook to E-one. Thirty-one years. I'm going to win."]],
 "Rook to E-one. It goes in the post today."],
24:["(He's sleeping on the examination bed. He opens one eye.) It's quieter. First night in a week nobody rang. I didn't know what to do. So I slept here.",[
  ["Did it help?","I dreamed of the hospital. All twelve beds, empty. Nobody ill. Then I woke up and my back hurt. That's a doctor's dream."],
  ["How's the island?","Coughing, and cleaning up. The farmers are going back north to see what's left. The church is half empty again. That's progress."],
  ["Do you ever get ill yourself?","Never. Doctors can't afford to. (He sneezes.) That's ash. That's just ash."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I slept in the surgery. It's bad for my reputation."],
25:["(He's standing at the window with a cup of coffee.) I've counted. In the last month, eight people I'd have put in a hospital bed. I put them in my own bed instead.",[
  ["Where did you sleep?","Here. On the examination bed. I'm fifty-two and I sleep on a bed with a paper sheet. The island owes me a mattress."],
  ["Does Freja sell mattresses?","Freja sold me a lovely one two years ago. It's in my flat, under a patient with pneumonia. That's what happened to my mattress."],
  ["Who's in your flat?","A fisherman's wife from Saltwick. Getting better. That's all I'll say. She's knitted me a scarf."]],
 "Ask Freja about a second mattress, if you see her."],
26:["Jack and Frank were in, arguing. Jack's thinking of voting for Carlo because Carlo promised to cut the harbour tax. Frank thinks he's a fool.",[
  ["What do you think?","I think a man who promises you exactly what you want is very good at finding out what you want. That's a skill. I'm not sure it's a virtue."],
  ["Who will you vote for?","The man most likely to put the hospital in the ground. I've read their letters four times. I've still not decided."],
  ["Do you have a favourite?","I don't like Carlo's friends. I don't like Magnus's pipelines. I don't like Harald's cola. I like the hospital. It doesn't have a candidate."]],
 "Fewer promises, more beds. That's my campaign."],
27:["(He's very quiet, writing.) A man came in this morning and asked for a large bandage and some tape. Wouldn't say what for. Cash. Very polite.",[
  ["Who was he?","Nice suit. Hope Springs, I'd say, from the shoes. I don't ask. A doctor who asks too many questions doesn't stay a doctor."],
  ["Is something going on?","Everyone says there's a big card game at the pizzeria tonight. Big games end in small fights, in my experience. I'll keep the tape out."],
  ["Was he hurt?","His knuckles. A bit. 'Door,' he said. Everyone's been hit by a door this month. The doors on this island are terribly violent."]],
 "If you play cards tonight, keep your knuckles to yourself."],
28:["(The doctor's waiting room is full.) Every faction's out today, marching and shouting. I've had a sprained ankle from a rally, a nosebleed from a flag and a fainting from a speech.",[
  ["Which faction's injuring the most people?","I don't keep score. (He pauses.) The rally in Eastbrook. Enthusiasm's very dangerous. People stand on things."],
  ["Are you going out?","No. I'm the only doctor on an island where everyone's doing something big today. I'll be here, behind the door, with the tape."],
  ["How do you feel about tomorrow?","The vote. I've decided. Don't ask me who. I'll tell you in a year, if the hospital's built. Then I'll say I knew all along."]],
 "If you're going out, wear sensible shoes."],
29:["(He's putting on his coat.) Going to Frostholm to vote. I've arranged for the nurse in Saltwick to cover. One hour. The first hour off I've had in a month.",[
  ["Who are you voting for?","I'm voting for twelve beds. I just don't know which name they're under. I'll know when I see the paper."],
  ["What will you do with the rest of your hour?","Sit in a café in Frostholm and drink coffee that someone else made. Then come back. Then something will be broken."],
  ["Good luck.","Luck's for card players. I'm a doctor. I'll take a steady hand and a short queue."]],
 "If anybody's ill in the next hour, tell them to wait. Politely."],
30:["(He has the hospital plans on the desk, and the chessboard beside them.) Last day. Whatever happens to the island, I'll still be here, with two beds and a cabinet.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","An island that builds hospitals instead of statues. That's all. It doesn't matter much who's in the palace, if there's a bed in the ward."],
  ["Did Salmi answer?","Not yet. Rook to E-one's still in the post. Whatever happens today, in a fortnight I'll find out whether I've won. Something to look forward to."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Of the future? No. Of the night shift? Always. I'll be on call. Whatever happens, someone will need stitching."]],
 "Go carefully. I've no beds to spare."],
};
// Eddie, bartender at the City Bar in Kingsgrad (The Proprietors). Threads: her opinions, which she keeps to herself, and the jar
// customers pay into when they try to prise one out of her; the stories she hears twice (Frank's bear, Jack's pike, Otto's films);
// Eila and Marjut at their table, Marjut's grudge against the palace; her mother's glogg recipe, saved for the end of the month.
DAILY['Eddie']={
2:["(Eddie pours a measure big enough to drown in.) First time in? Sit down. The road from Saltwick's only been open a day, and you've already found the bar. That's a good instinct.",[
  ["That's a generous measure.","That's the City Bar measure. My mother poured it, her mother poured it. Kingsgrad's cold enough without being mean about whisky."],
  ["What does Kingsgrad think of the king's death?",["(She polishes a glass.) Kingsgrad thinks all sorts. I listen to all of it. I don't think anything.",[
    ["Surely you've an opinion.","See that jar? Anybody who asks for my opinion puts a coin in. It goes to the orphanage at Christmas. That's a dollar, please."],
    ["Who does the talking, then?","Everyone else. Marjut, at the table there, used to work at the palace. She'll tell you more about the royals than they know themselves."]]]],
  ["Heard any good stories?","Every story in Kingsgrad, usually twice. The second time's always better. Come back tomorrow and I'll tell you which ones."]],
 "Come in from the cold any time. The fire's always lit."],
3:["Frank was in last night and told the bear story again. That's eleven times this year. (She makes a chalk mark on a board behind the bar.) I keep count.",[
  ["What's the bear story?","Frank, a rifle, a bear in his woodshed in 1974. Every time, the bear gets bigger and Frank gets closer. By Christmas he'll have wrestled it."],
  ["What else is on the board?","Jack's pike, which has grown from two feet to five since spring. Otto doing the end of STORMWING, every word. And Sergeant Hanna's one about the goat in the cells."],
  ["Do you ever tell your own stories?","Bartenders don't have stories. We have other people's, and good memories."]],
 "Mind the board on the way past. It's my life's work."],
4:["(Eila is at the corner table, stitching the hem of an apron.) Eila's mending my apron for a free coffee. Best deal in Kingsgrad, and she knows it.",[
  ["Who's Eila?","Seamstress, thirty years, until the factory in Kingsgrad shut. Now she mends for half the city and grumbles about the other half."],
  ["Is she any good?","Better than any tailor. Don't tell Grace I said so. ...Actually, you can: put a dollar in the jar first."],
  ["Was that an opinion, Eddie?","(She narrows her eyes.) That was a fact about stitching. Facts are free."]],
 "Go easy on the stairs. They lean."],
5:["Marjut's been at the palace gossip again. The maids are all in a state about who'll be kept on and who'll be let go after the funeral. She was let go first, and she's not over it.",[
  ["Why was she let go?","She says she dropped a tray in front of the king's sister-in-law in 1979 and nobody forgave her. The palace says economies. Both, probably."],
  ["What does she know about the royals?","More than she lets on. She told me Prince Leopold talks to his dogs in French. I've no idea if that's true. I don't judge."],
  ["Don't you get bored of gossip?","Gossip's what keeps Kingsgrad warm. Coal's for the rich."]],
 "Marjut's tab is on the house today. Don't tell her, she'll be insulted."],
6:["The road south to Hope Springs is open. All the big villas'll come into town in their furs now, and they'll want cocktails. I don't do cocktails.",[
  ["What do you do instead?","Whisky, beer, aquavit and my opinion of anyone who orders a cocktail, which I keep to myself."],
  ["Who comes in from Hope Springs?","Nobody comes in. They send someone to buy a bottle. Once, Gustavo's man came in and asked for 'the usual'. I'd never seen him before. I gave him the usual. He seemed happy."],
  ["One aquavit, please.","(She pours one, with a slice of something dark on the rim.) Rye bread. You eat it after. My grandmother's rules."]],
 "Next time, bring an appetite. The rye's homemade."],
7:["(Eddie is chalking up the board.) Jack's pike: three feet, four inches now. He measured it with his hands last night and knocked Bertil's glass over.",[
  ["Does Jack own the fishing shop?","Owns it, sits in it, sells three hooks a week and tells pike stories to the rest of Kingsgrad. He's happy. That's more than most."],
  ["Who's Bertil?","Civil servant, from the Bureau. Drinks one beer slowly and fills in the newspaper crossword in pencil, so he can rub out his mistakes. A careful man."],
  ["Has anyone actually seen this pike?","Nobody's seen it. That's the point of a pike."]],
 "If you see Jack, don't ask about the pike. You'll lose an hour."],
8:["Sunday. Half the bar went up the mountain for Father Thyme's sermon and the other half say they did.",[
  ["How do you tell which half?","The ones who went have cold ears and want brandy. The ones who didn't have warm ears and want to know what he said."],
  ["Did you hear what he said?","It's on the radio later, they say. I'll have it on behind the bar. I'll listen. I won't comment."],
  ["What's the gossip from the oil rig?","Something's gone wrong out there, and Dario's been on the telephone all morning, they say. Frostholm's always on the telephone about something."]],
 "Warm your ears by the fire before you go."],
9:["(Eddie sniffs a bottle of Kelp Cola from the cooler.) Kelp Cola's sent round crates of the old stuff, cheap. They say there's a new flavour coming. The Party says there's a people's cola coming. I say I've a bar to run.",[
  ["Do people drink cola in a bar?","With rum, yes. Without rum, only Rosa's lot on their way home from the hot dog cart. And children, who aren't allowed in."],
  ["What's the Party's cola?","Helen's idea. A cola made by the workers for the workers. Sounds like beer to me, but I'm not paid to think."],
  ["What does it taste of?","Kelp Cola? Sweet, fizzy, and a little like the harbour. People love it. Don't ask me why."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Wear black, and a warm coat."],
10:["(The bar is full of people in black, and Eddie is in black too.) The funeral's at the palace today. Kingsgrad's come straight here from the gates. They need it.",[
  ["How are people taking it?","Quietly. The palace staff came in first, then the police, then everybody. Edmund the butler drank a whole brandy without blinking. I've never seen that."],
  ["Did you know the king?",["I poured for him once. A state dinner, years ago. I was hired help in a white jacket.",[
    ["What was he like?","He thanked me. Every time I poured. Nobody else at the table did. That's all I know about kings."],
    ["What did he drink?","Water, all evening, and a glass of plum brandy at the end. A man after my own heart, in the plum brandy."]]]],
  ["Marjut must have something to say.","Marjut's crying at her table and won't say why. She hated the palace. She liked him. People are like that."]],
 "Get some sleep. Funerals take it out of everyone."],
11:["(Eddie is putting coins from the opinion jar into rolls.) Thirty-two dollars and eighty cents. That's a lot of people trying to get my view on the funeral.",[
  ["And did any of them get it?","Not a word. Bertil came closest. He asked what I thought of the hymns. I said they were long. He put two coins in for that."],
  ["Who paid the most?","Frank. He wanted to know if I'd vote for the Republicans if there was ever a vote. He put in a dollar every time I didn't answer. Five dollars."],
  ["What's it going to buy?","Shoes for the orphanage at Christmas. Opinions are worth something after all. Just not mine."]],
 "Go easy. Kingsgrad's still in black."],
12:["Sally from the estate agent's was in, buying a round for no reason. She says the island's property market's about to boom. She says that every Thursday.",[
  ["Is she right?","She's been right once. In 1977 she sold a house in Saltwick to a man who wanted it. It happens."],
  ["Does she ever stop smiling?","Not since I've known her. She smiles at funerals. She smiled at the king's. She said it was a big turnout."],
  ["Who bought a round for no reason?","Sally did. That's the reason. She likes to be liked. Most do."]],
 "Mind the step. Sally spilt half the round on it."],
13:["(Eila slams a bundle of mending on her table.) Eila's had a bad day. The Kelp Cola girls offered her a free cola and a free hat. She said she'd sooner have a job.",[
  ["She's got a point.","I don't say whether people have points. I give them coffee. She's on her third."],
  ["What happened to the factory?","Cheap clothes off the boat from Helsinki, and the owner sold the building to a bank. Eila stood outside it for a week. Then she came here."],
  ["Free hats, though.","(Eddie holds up a paper hat with a cartoon cup on it.) She threw it at me. I'm keeping it. It's a story."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Kingsgrad's going in its best boots."],
14:["(The bar's half empty, and Eddie is in a scarf.) The big day. I'm going myself after I lock up. I like to see what people are like in a crowd.",[
  ["What do you expect?","I don't expect. I watch. You see who stands with whom, who leaves early, and who's crying."],
  ["Is it worth the cold?","Half the island in one place? I'll hear about it in here for ten years. Better to have been there."],
  ["What's your opinion on it all?","(She nods at the jar.) A dollar, please."]],
 "Wrap up warm. Crowds are cold, whatever people say."],
15:["Otto's been in doing every line of TOP COMRADE again, in the voice. He's going to wear the board out.",[
  ["Is it a good film?","Otto says so. He's seen it forty times. I've heard it twenty, without seeing it once."],
  ["What's the gossip from Sunday?","Church and palace are squabbling over which prince the island should have. Marjut says Augustus is too holy to boil an egg. Edmund says Leopold's too proud to eat one."],
  ["Do you go to the cinema?","Once a year, on my birthday. I sit in the back row and fall asleep. Otto never forgives me."]],
 "Go easy. Otto might come back for an encore."],
16:["(Eddie is wiping up broken glass.) The debate this morning. Communists against Democrats. A few came on here afterwards still debating. One of them threw a glass. Not at anyone. At the idea.",[
  ["Who threw it?","A man who's paying for it. I don't give names. Marjut gives names, for a coffee."],
  ["How did the debate go?","Loudly. Nobody I've spoken to agrees who won. That's how you know it was a real debate."],
  ["Did you pick a side?","I picked up the glass. That's my side."]],
 "Watch your step. I missed a bit."],
17:["(Something rattles behind the bar, and Eddie steadies a row of bottles.) Did you feel that? Third one today. Bottles walking along the shelf like they're going somewhere.",[
  ["Have you had shakes before?","My mother said there was one in the thirties that cracked the church bell. Never felt one myself till today."],
  ["What's the bar saying?","Frank says it's the Soviets testing something. Otto says it's like the start of a disaster film. Eila says the island's had enough of all of us."],
  ["Is anyone frightened?","Everyone's frightened and nobody'll say it. So they drink and talk about other things. I do a lot of pouring."]],
 "Mind the bottles. They've started wandering."],
18:["Dario from the oil company went missing yesterday, they say, and half Frostholm went looking. Two of his men came in here asking. I gave them a beer and nothing else.",[
  ["Had you seen him?","I don't see people. I pour for them. It's a different thing."],
  ["What did the men want?","Whether a man in a fur collar had been drinking alone. In Kingsgrad, in September? Every man's in a fur collar."],
  ["Any theories in the bar?","Nine. Frank's is the best: a bear. He's going to work it into the bear story. I'll know by next week."]],
 "Go on. And if you see a man in a fur collar, buy him a drink. He's had a week."],
19:["(Marjut is sitting very upright with a cup of coffee and a hat on.) Marjut's had a letter from the palace. She won't say what's in it. She's been sitting like that for an hour.",[
  ["Is it good news?","If it were good, she'd tell everybody. If it were bad, she'd tell everybody louder. So it's something else."],
  ["Ask her for me.","I don't ask. I wait. Marjut will tell me before closing, because she can't not. That's the trick of a bar."],
  ["Is she still angry with the palace?","She's angry with the palace like you're angry with family. Very, and always, and she'd fight you if you said a word against them."]],
 "Leave her be today. She's working something out."],
20:["Marjut told me. (Eddie leans over the bar.) The letter was a reference. For a job as a maid in Helsinki. She's not sure she wants to go.",[
  ["Why wouldn't she?","She was born here. Her mother's buried here. And she's been angry at the palace so long she wouldn't know who to be angry at in Helsinki."],
  ["What do you think she should do?","(Eddie points at the jar.) Don't even try. I told her to sit with it. Sitting with it's free."],
  ["Did someone at the palace write it?","She says it's signed by Princess Daphne. Daphne never had a kind word for anyone, Marjut says. And yet."]],
 "Don't tell Marjut I said. She'll know anyway."],
21:["(The windows are black with ash, and the bar is full of people with grey hair, young and old.) The mountain's gone up. Everyone came here. I don't know why. It's not like a bar's safer.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody I've heard. Sergeant Hanna came in to tell everybody that, and stayed for a whisky. First time I've seen her drink on duty."],
  ["What can you see from here?","Grey sky, glowing at the north end. Ash coming down like snow but warm. Eila says it's the end of the world. Frank says it's an opportunity."],
  ["Why does everyone come to a bar?",["Because there's light, and people, and somebody who'll pour. (She pours another.) And because at home you're alone with it.",[
    ["Are you alone with it at home?","I've got the bar. That's not alone."],
    ["Are you frightened, Eddie?","(She pauses.) That's an opinion. But I'll not charge you for it. Yes."]]]]],
 "Stay, if you like. Nobody's going anywhere in this."],
22:["Ash in the beer. Ash in the till. Ash in Eila's mending. She's charging extra for the ash.",[
  ["Is the city all right?","Covered, coughing and complaining, which is Kingsgrad all right. The streets are grey and the children are drawing in it."],
  ["What's the bar's mood?","Like after a funeral. Quiet at first, then everyone talks at once. And the bear story's got a volcano in it now."],
  ["Can you still get supplies?","Lisa says the supermarket's had nothing in since Saturday. I've got beer, aquavit and twelve bags of crisps. We'll manage."]],
 "Shake off at the door. Please. I've swept twice."],
23:["(Eddie is writing on a slip of paper behind the bar.) I'm copying out my mother's glogg recipe. I'll make it on the last day of the month. Whatever happens, there'll be glogg.",[
  ["What goes in it?","Red wine, a lot of spices, a little sugar, raisins, almonds, and a measure of aquavit that my mother called 'a secret' and everyone else called 'a lot'."],
  ["Why the last day?","Because it's going to be a day, isn't it? Whatever comes of it, people'll want something warm in a cup."],
  ["Can I have some now?","It's not ready. Glogg sits for a week. Like most good things."]],
 "Come back on the thirtieth. Bring a mug."],
24:["Frank's been in, very pleased with himself about the Republican election. Margit's running it, and he says it'll be clean as a whistle. He says that about his rifles too.",[
  ["Who's he backing?","Frank backs whoever says the word 'freedom' the most. This week it's everyone. He's torn."],
  ["Is it a real election?","Margit's counting the votes herself in a shoebox. Kelp Cola, the oil company and somebody of Gustavo's are all running. Make of that what you like. I don't."],
  ["Eddie, would you vote?",["(She smiles and taps the jar.) You know the price.",[
    ["(Put a dollar in the jar.)","(She nods.) Thank you for the orphans. And no."],
    ["Not today.","Then the orphans go barefoot. On your conscience."]]]]],
 "Go on. The orphans thank you either way."],
25:["(Eila and Marjut are arguing at their table, in whispers.) They've been at it an hour. Marjut's going to Helsinki or she isn't, and Eila says she's a traitor either way.",[
  ["What does Eila want?","For her to stay. Eila won't say it, so she calls her a traitor. That's how friends talk in Kingsgrad."],
  ["When's Marjut leaving?","She hasn't said she's leaving. She's bought a suitcase. Those aren't the same thing."],
  ["Should I talk to them?","Not unless you want to be called a traitor too. Have a whisky and look at the board."]],
 "Go on. They'll sort it before closing."],
26:["(Eddie stands back to admire the chalk board.) Frank's bear's now nine feet and was blown out of the woodshed by the volcano. Jack's pike: six feet even. I've had to make the board bigger.",[
  ["What's the most-told story this year?","Frank's bear: twenty-two times. Otto's films, eighteen. Jack's pike, sixteen. The volcano story's catching up fast: everyone's got one."],
  ["What's your volcano story?","I poured, they talked. Like any day. That's not a story, it's a job."],
  ["Will you ever write them down?","Henrik from the Gazette asked me once. I said the stories belong to the people who tell them. Which is true, and also means I'm lazy."]],
 "Come in the next time Frank's in. You've got to hear the bear once."],
27:["Otto's showing a film for the Party at the cinema today. Or the Party's showing a film at Otto's. Nobody can tell me which, and Otto's been in, white as a sheet.",[
  ["What's wrong with Otto?","He's a Republican with a cinema full of Communists. He drank two beers and recited the end of STORMWING to himself, quietly, for comfort."],
  ["What film?","Something with tractors in it, he says. He hasn't seen it, which upsets him more than the Communists."],
  ["Are you going?","To a film with tractors in it? I'll hear all about it from twenty people tonight. I'll have seen it twenty times."]],
 "Go easy. Kingsgrad's full of people with strong feelings."],
28:["(Through the window, flags and banners go past, and a band somewhere is playing badly.) Everybody's in Kingsgrad today. Every party, every flag, every band. I've doubled the beer order.",[
  ["Who's out there?","Erik's got a rally in the square, the Party's marching, there's a state visit at the palace, and everyone's hanging out of windows to see. Kingsgrad's never had a day like it."],
  ["Will it turn ugly?","Sergeant Hanna's got every policeman she owns out there. I've put the good glasses away. Make of that what you like."],
  ["What's your hope for all this?",["(She takes a long breath, and doesn't point to the jar.) That everybody gets to go home tonight. That's all.",[
    ["You gave me an opinion for free.","Did I? Well. Don't print it. It'll ruin my reputation."],
    ["Will Marjut go to Helsinki?","She's at the window watching the flags. She says she'll decide after the thirtieth. So will a lot of people."]]]]],
 "Mind yourself out there. It's busy."],
29:["(Eddie is stirring a big pot on a burner at the end of the bar.) The glogg's warming through. Tomorrow it's ready. It smells like Christmas in here, if Christmas were nervous.",[
  ["Can I smell it?","(She lifts the lid. Cloves, cinnamon, wine.) My mother's kitchen, every December. Don't touch."],
  ["What's the bar saying about tomorrow?","Everybody's got a guess, and nobody's guessed the same thing. I've heard twenty futures for the island today. I'll hear one more tomorrow, and it'll be true."],
  ["Will you be open?","I've been open through a funeral, a volcano and a debate. I'll be open tomorrow, whatever happens. Somebody has to be."]],
 "Come back tomorrow. Bring a mug."],
30:["(Eddie ladles hot glogg into a mug and slides it to you.) The last day. Drink that, and whatever the island decides, decide it warm.",[
  ["It's wonderful.","My mother's. With the secret in it. (She pours a measure of aquavit into the pot.) There. That's a lot."],
  ["What's the opinion jar up to?","Sixty-one dollars and forty cents. Shoes for every child at the orphanage, and maybe a ball. You lot are very curious about what I think."],
  ["What happens to the board now?","I'll wipe it in the new year, like always. The stories come back. They always come back, a bit bigger."]],
 "Go on. Come back and tell me how it all ends. I'll want to hear it twice."],
};
// Edmund, butler to the royal household for thirty-one years, in the Grand Hall (Royal Family). Threads: the palace clocks,
// stopped at the king's death until he's buried; the household's great days (the funeral's seating plan, then the state visit
// and the spoons); training young Oskar on the door; the late king's habits and the pocket watch he gave Edmund; the papers he
// hides from the family.
DAILY['Edmund']={
2:["(Edmund stands in the Grand Hall beside a tall clock whose pendulum is still.) You'll notice the clocks have stopped. Every clock in the palace, at the minute His Majesty died. It is the custom.",[
  ["When do they start again?","When he is laid in the crypt. I shall wind every one of them myself. There are forty-three."],
  ["Doesn't anyone need the time?","The household keeps time by Marta's kitchen, which has never stopped for anyone. Not even a king."],
  ["What minute did he die?","Twenty past four. He was having his Kelp Cola. (A pause.) I'll thank you not to print the second part."]],
 "Mind the rug at the door. It has opinions about heels."],
3:["The young man on the door is Oskar. He's been with us two years. He's honest, which is a virtue, and he talks, which is not.",[
  ["What has he told you?","Everything, sir. He's very thorough. I'm training him to tell me everything and the world nothing."],
  ["How did you start?","As a hall boy, polishing boots at fourteen. Old Queen Astrid's boots. She checked them herself, with a candle."],
  ["Is he any good?","He stands still very well. He can't yet stand still without looking like he's standing still. That takes about ten years."]],
 "Kindly don't encourage Oskar. He's quite encouraged enough."],
4:["(Edmund is folding the Eastbrook Gazette into quarters.) The road to Eastbrook is open, so the newspaper is arriving again. I find it best to read it before the family does.",[
  ["Do you hide it from them?","I curate it, sir. The family have a great deal to bear this month. The Gazette has a great deal to say. I balance the two."],
  ["Anything in it about the palace?","Nora suggests the palace roof costs more than the island's school. That is untrue. It's the gutters that cost more."],
  ["Isn't that censorship?","It's housekeeping. Nobody calls it censorship when I take the cobwebs down."]],
 "If you're writing for the paper yourself, kindly spell 'Highness' with the H."],
5:["His Majesty had his habits. Whisky at ten. Kelp Cola at four. The shipping forecast at six, read aloud, whether anyone was listening or not.",[
  ["Who listened?","I did, sir. For thirty-one years. I could tell you the sea state in Viking and Forties for any evening since 1953."],
  ["Will Prince Leopold keep the habits?","The prince has his own. He walks at night. He doesn't care for Kelp Cola. I keep the bottle on ice anyway, in case."],
  ["What will you miss?","(A long pause.) His footstep. He was heavy on the stair. I knew where he was in the palace at every moment. Now I listen and there's nothing."]],
 "Good day. The forecast's moderate, occasionally poor. Wrap up."],
6:["(Edmund consults a pocket watch, then remembers it isn't wound.) A habit. His Majesty gave me this in 1963, on my tenth year. It's stopped with the others.",[
  ["What's engraved on it?","'To Edmund, who knows where everything is.' His Majesty thought he was being funny. He was being accurate."],
  ["Do you know where everything is?","Every key, every room, every bottle and every skeleton. The skeletons are mostly in the crypt, sir. Where they belong."],
  ["Will you retire?","He offered me a cottage twice. I told him twice I'd retire when he did. (He puts the watch away.) I may have to revisit that."]],
 "I'll ask you to keep your hands off the silver on your way out. It's nothing personal. I ask everyone."],
7:["Mr Dario of Diversified Petroleum has sent a card with gold edges announcing his intention to call. I've put it in the drawer with the other gold cards.",[
  ["How many gold cards are there?","Fourteen, since His Majesty died. Kelp Cola, the oil men, two hotels, a casino and a gentleman who sells hearses."],
  ["Will he be received?","He'll be received by me, first. I receive everyone. Some of them get further."],
  ["What makes someone get further?","Manners, sir. And a reason the family would wish to hear. Mr Dario has one of those."]],
 "Close the drawer behind you, metaphorically. I'll do it literally."],
8:["The chapel must be ready by Tuesday. I've had the pews moved three times. The Church wants the front, the family wants the front and the front only has two rows.",[
  ["Who gets them?","The family. Then Father Thyme at the altar, where he can see everyone. Then everyone else, in an order that has kept me up two nights."],
  ["What's the hard part?","Where to sit the people who hate each other. Which is everyone, sir, this month."],
  ["Can I help?","You can tell me nothing you overhear. That's what everyone else will be doing."]],
 "Sunday or not, there's polishing to be done. Good day."],
9:["(Edmund has a seating plan on the side table, crossed out in four colours of ink.) The eleventh draft. Kelp Cola and Diversified Petroleum must not sit together. Or must. I can no longer tell.",[
  ["Put them together.","His Royal Highness had the same idea. I'm told it was his own. (A very faint dryness.) I'll put it down to the family's sense of humour."],
  ["Where's Gustavo going?","On the aisle, near the back, where he can leave quickly. And where everyone can see him leave. Both suit him."],
  ["Is Anna coming?","Miss Anna of the Communist Party has accepted. I've placed her opposite the coffin. If she wants to look at a king, she shall."]],
 "Tomorrow at ten. Black, polished, and silent in the chapel, if you'd be so good."],
10:["(Edmund is in full black, very still, his gloves immaculate.) Today we lay His Majesty in the crypt. I've served the household thirty-one years. Today I'll serve it as well as I ever have.",[
  ["Are you all right, Edmund?","I'm on duty, sir. I'll be all right afterwards, privately, in the wine cellar, with a glass of his '61."],
  ["Is everything ready?","The chapel's laid, the candles are trimmed, the choir's sober and the clocks are waiting. Everything except the family, and the family are as ready as anyone is."],
  ["Tell me one thing about him.","He couldn't tie a bow tie. Every formal dinner for thirty-one years, I tied it. Today I tied it for the last time."]],
 "If you'll excuse me. There's a great deal of island coming up the drive."],
11:["(Every clock in the Grand Hall is ticking again.) I wound them all at dawn, sir. Forty-three. It took two hours. The palace sounds alive again.",[
  ["Did it help?","Somewhat. The sound of the house going on. It's the only kind of comfort a butler is allowed in the daytime."],
  ["Which was the last clock?","His Majesty's, by the bed. I set it to the right time and stood there a while. It's not his time any more. It's the household's."],
  ["What now?","Now, the thank-you letters. Two hundred and eighty wreaths. I've written forty and Kelp Cola's alone needed a paragraph."]],
 "Listen to the clocks on your way out. They're very glad to be back."],
12:["Oskar asked me today if a butler is ever allowed to have an opinion. I told him yes: on wine, silver, and the correct temperature of a room.",[
  ["What about politics?","A butler's politics are the family's politics, sir. I'm a royalist by profession and by conviction. Fortunately they agree."],
  ["What's the correct temperature?","Nineteen degrees in the drawing rooms, sixteen in the bedrooms and colder in the servants' hall, as His Majesty said, 'to keep them moving'."],
  ["Did Oskar accept that?","He asked about football. I said a butler supports whichever team the prince does. The prince doesn't support a team. It's a very peaceful arrangement."]],
 "If you see Oskar, tell him to stand still. He'll know what you mean."],
13:["We had an unexpected visitor at the back door this morning, wanting to know where the household keeps its 'special things'. I showed him the coal hole.",[
  ["Who was he?","A man with a ring on his little finger and very soft shoes. He gave a name I didn't believe."],
  ["What special things?","The palace has a great many valuables, sir, and every one of them is exactly where it ought to be. I'll leave it there."],
  ["Did he go away?","Eventually. Viktor walked him to the gate with a spade. Nobody threatened anyone. The spade spoke for itself."]],
 "I'll ask you not to report that. It's nothing. It's the kind of nothing I deal with."],
14:["The family will appear before the whole island today. I've laid out the coats, the gloves and the expressions.",[
  ["The expressions?","His Royal Highness is to look composed, Prince Augustus serene and Princess Daphne interested in something other than the crowd. I've rehearsed them all."],
  ["Will you be there?","Two paces behind and one to the left. Where I always am. The island won't see me. That's how it should be."],
  ["Is it a big day for the palace?","It's a big day for the island. For the palace, it's an ordinary day with more polishing."]],
 "Keep your notebook in your pocket in front of the prince. He finds it distracting."],
15:["(Edmund is carrying a tray of tea into the library, untouched, and back out again.) Prince Augustus hasn't eaten all day. He's deciding something. I'm not to ask what.",[
  ["Do you know what?","I know a great deal, sir. On this occasion I'd rather not."],
  ["Has anyone been to see him?","The library door has been opened by more people today than in the last year. I've counted. It's a butler's prerogative."],
  ["Is he all right?","He's a man of conscience. That's never quite all right. It's admirable, though, and I'll defend it to anyone."]],
 "Should you go into the library, knock first. And keep your voice down. He hears everything."],
16:["I've noticed the Princess reading the Eastbrook Gazette behind the hedge. She thinks it's a secret. I've arranged for the paper to be left where she'll find it.",[
  ["You're on her side?","I'm on the family's side, sir. All of it. If the Princess is curious, better she's curious with the facts than without them."],
  ["You said you curate the paper.","I curate it for the Prince. For the Princess, I leave it on the bench. Each according to his nature."],
  ["Does she know you know?","She knows. She's known since she was six. We don't speak of it. It's a very comfortable arrangement."]],
 "Kindly don't tell the Princess I told you. It would spoil her morning."],
17:["(A crystal on the chandelier is still swinging.) The building shook, sir. I've told the household it was the boiler. I've told the boiler nothing.",[
  ["It wasn't the boiler, was it?","No. But a household is calmer if it thinks it knows what happened. I'll find out what happened, and then I'll tell them that instead."],
  ["Did anything break?","A Meissen shepherdess in the drawing room. She lost her crook. I've had worse losses this month, and so has she."],
  ["Is there steam in the north?","So Viktor says. He also says the dogs in the village have stopped barking. Viktor's reports are always about animals. They're seldom wrong."]],
 "Should it happen again, stand in a doorway. Not this doorway: it's mine."],
18:["Oskar stood on the door for four hours this morning without moving once. I told him well done, and he dropped the umbrella. It's progress, sir.",[
  ["Why was he so still?","He's been practising for the state visit. A king arrives on the twenty-eighth. Oskar has decided to become furniture."],
  ["You're fond of him.","I'm fond of standards. Oskar is becoming one. That's a different thing."],
  ["Who trained you?","Old Mr Persson, butler before me, who once stood in a doorway through a fire because nobody had dismissed him. I was nineteen and I thought him a god."]],
 "Don't drop anything in the hall. Oskar will try to catch it, and that's the umbrella again."],
19:["The Prince has asked me, very casually, whether I still have the old black overcoat from His Majesty's younger days. The one without the crest.",[
  ["Do you?","I have everything, sir. That's my function. Whether I find it is another matter, and depends on why it's wanted."],
  ["Why does he want it?","He didn't say, which I took to mean I shouldn't ask. I've been doing this a long time."],
  ["Will you tell anyone?","Who would I tell? Coats are private. A butler never discusses a gentleman's coats."]],
 "I'll see you out the front way. The side door is, today, closed for reasons."],
20:["(Edmund is walking the hall with his ring of keys, checking each door.) I've been round the palace twice this morning. Locked, every door. I like to hear them say so.",[
  ["Why the extra rounds?","Too many strangers this month. Too many soft shoes. A butler checks doors the way a sailor checks the weather: often, and without saying why."],
  ["How many keys are there?","Ninety-one. I know each by its weight. (He shakes the ring gently.) That one's the wine cellar. That one's the chapel. That one is none of your business."],
  ["Expecting trouble?","I'm always expecting trouble, sir. That's why it so rarely finds me unprepared."]],
 "You'll find the front door unlocked for you. And locked behind you."],
21:["(There is ash on Edmund's shoulders, and he hasn't brushed it off.) The mountain in the north is erupting, sir. I've the staff indoors, the shutters closed and the Prince on the steps. That's the order of things.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not here. Oskar ran to town for news. Nobody hurt there, thank God. A great many frightened."],
  ["Shouldn't the Prince be inside?","Yes. I said so. He said his father stood on those steps in '62. I took him out a coat. That's what a butler can do."],
  ["Your shoulders are covered.","(He glances down.) So they are. I'll brush them when the Prince comes in. Not before."]],
 "Wet a cloth, keep it over your mouth, and walk, don't run."],
22:["The ash is in every room, sir. Every curtain, every carpet, every cushion. Elsa has wept in the laundry. I gave her a handkerchief. It was grey before she used it.",[
  ["How do you clean it?","Slowly, gently and dry. Water turns it to cement. I've told the staff to treat it like a guest who won't leave."],
  ["How's the household?","Shaken and sweeping. The Princess is restless. The Prince is stern. Prince Augustus is cleaning books with a feather. I'm counting brooms."],
  ["And the state visit?","Six days. The palace will be clean. If I have to dust it with my own sleeve."]],
 "Kindly wipe your feet three times on the way in. Twice won't do it."],
23:["I've drawn up the plan for the state visit. Arrivals, reception, the chapel, the banquet. It's longer than the funeral plan, and has fewer people who hate each other.",[
  ["Fewer?","One fewer. The visiting king has no reason to dislike anyone yet. He'll have found several by the fish course."],
  ["What's on the banquet menu?","Marta's keeping that from me. She says if she tells me I'll tell her it's wrong. She's right."],
  ["Where will the guest sit?","At His Majesty's right hand. His Royal Highness's right hand, I mean. (He pauses.) I keep doing that."]],
 "Should you be at the banquet, the fork on the outside is for the herring. Never the other way."],
24:["(Edmund is polishing a spoon with great concentration.) I counted the silver this morning. Three hundred and six spoons. There should be three hundred and six. There are three hundred and six.",[
  ["Then why count again?","Because I've counted it four times, sir, and each time I feel slightly less certain I'm still awake."],
  ["Are you all right, Edmund?","I'm tired. I'm sixty-four. I've buried my king and I'm about to entertain a foreign one. Ask me on the first of October."],
  ["Is one missing?","No. If one were missing, you'd know. The whole palace would know. You'd hear me from Kingsgrad."]],
 "Kindly keep your hands where I can see them. It's the spoons. Nothing personal."],
25:["His Royal Highness asked me, at breakfast, what a butler would think of a king who answered to a parliament. Nobody's asked my opinion on anything since 1953.",[
  ["What did you say?","I said a butler answers to the household, the household answers to the King, and the King answers to God and the gutters. Anything more is above my station."],
  ["What do you really think?","(A long pause.) That the King was a good man who never asked anyone anything. And that his son is asking. It might be the making of him."],
  ["Did he like your answer?","He laughed and said 'the gutters'. I've not heard him laugh at breakfast since he was a boy."]],
 "Don't put me in the paper. Butlers are never in the paper. It's how you know they're good."],
26:["The Princess has been showing visitors from Kelp Cola the scenic route. Twice round the portrait gallery. They came out looking very tired. I shan't stop her.",[
  ["What did Kelp Cola want?","An audience with His Royal Highness. They had folders. Nobody with folders wants anything small."],
  ["Did they get one?","They got to the Prince's door, sir. I can't say what happened on the other side of it. That's a door I don't listen at."],
  ["You let the Princess tease them?","She's her father's daughter. He once made a minister walk the full length of the palace twice to deliver a single sentence. It was 'no'."]],
 "Should anyone offer you a folder in my hall, you'll leave it there."],
27:["(Edmund is in shirtsleeves, which seems to shock even him.) Tomorrow. Every room, every candle, every footman. I've ironed my own gloves. I never trust anyone else with them.",[
  ["Is the palace ready?","The palace is ready, sir. Marta says the kitchen isn't, but Marta has said that before every banquet since 1966, and has never once been right."],
  ["Is Oskar ready?","He's ready. He's practised bowing so much he does it in his sleep. Lotta says he bowed to the soup tureen last night."],
  ["You look tired.","I'll sleep in October. October is a fine month for sleeping."]],
 "Tomorrow, sir, you'll see the household at its best. Do make a note."],
28:["(Edmund is at the door to the Grand Hall, gloves on, quite still.) A king at the gate, and the whole island making its play at once. I've drawn every curtain to the right height and wound every clock.",[
  ["How do you feel?","On duty. It's the best feeling I know. Thirty-one years, and the house has never looked better."],
  ["What's the island doing today?","Everything at once, by all accounts. Flags, rallies, offices, chapels. It's not my business. My business is this hall."],
  ["Good luck, Edmund.","A butler doesn't need luck, sir. He needs a clean floor and a reliable footman. Today I have both."]],
 "Stand to the left as the King comes in. Not the right. Never the right."],
29:["(Edmund is winding His Majesty's watch, slowly.) The island is deciding who comes after him today. I've wound his watch. I thought he'd want to know the time.",[
  ["Who do you hope for?","The family, sir. All of it. I've served three generations and I'd like to serve a fourth."],
  ["What if it's nobody?","Then I'll serve the family anyway, wherever they go. A butler doesn't serve the crown. He serves the people under it."],
  ["Is the watch working?","Perfectly. Twenty past four. I've set it to the moment he died. It seemed right, just for today."]],
 "I'll be in the hall, if you need me. I'm always in the hall."],
30:["The last day of the month, sir. Whatever is decided, the clocks will go on, and the floors will need doing, and someone will have to answer the door.",[
  ["Will you stay, whatever happens?","Thirty-one years. I'll stay as long as there's a household to keep. Longer, probably, out of habit."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Wind the clocks. Count the spoons. Read the Gazette before the family. And sleep, sir. It's October tomorrow."],
  ["Thank you, Edmund.","(He inclines his head, very slightly.) You kept your voice down in the Grand Hall and your hands off the silver. That's more than most."]],
 "Good day. Mind the rug. It still has opinions."],
};
// Eila, a seamstress out of work in Kingsgrad since Lindqvist's dress shop closed, taking in mending (The Unemployed). Threads:
// her letters to the Civil Service Bureau about relief money (Bertil, and a form that never comes back), her needle (armbands for
// the funeral, then Grace's overflow, then Grace's offer), her worry for Marjut and her grudge, and her quiet anger at Hope Springs.
DAILY['Eila']={
2:["(Eila is threading a needle without looking.) You're the reporter. The first tram from Saltwick brought you and half of Saltwick with you. Eila. I mend things.",[
  ["What do you mend?","Coats, collars, trousers, the odd wedding dress. Anything with a seam. Twenty-two years at Lindqvist's dress shop, before it shut."],
  ["What happened to Lindqvist's?","Frostholm happened. Ready-made from Helsinki, shipped in by the crate. Nobody wants a dress that fits when they can have one that's new."],
  ["You don't look pleased to see me.","I don't look pleased to see anyone. It saves time. (A pause.) Welcome to Kingsgrad."]],
 "If your coat tears, you know where I am. Marjut and I don't go far."],
3:["(Eila is cutting black cloth into strips on her knee.) Armbands, for the funeral. Half the city'll want one by the tenth. I've got eight days and one pair of scissors.",[
  ["Good business, at least.","It's the first proper work in months. A king has to die before a seamstress eats. Write that down."],
  ["How much for one?","Fifty pennies. Seventy with a stitched edge. Hope Springs pays seventy and Saltwick pays fifty, and I don't argue with either."],
  ["Do you mourn him yourself?","I mourn twenty-two years at Lindqvist's. The king I'll mourn when somebody in that palace mourns me."]],
 "Mind the pins on the bench. I lose about one an hour."],
4:["(Eila has a long form on her lap, filled in in neat capitals.) Form 12B. Application for relief. My third. The first two came back 'incomplete'. They didn't say what was missing.",[
  ["Who sends them back?","The Civil Service Bureau, behind the apartments. A man called Bertil. He's polite. Polite's worse than rude: you can't shout at polite."],
  ["Will this one get through?","I've filled in every box twice and drawn a line through the blank ones. Let them call that incomplete."],
  ["What does relief pay?","Enough for coal. That's all I want. Coal, and not to have to ask."]],
 "If you're passing the Bureau, tell them a seamstress is waiting."],
5:["(Eila glances at Marjut, further along the bench.) She's not slept. The palace sent her a letter. Not her wages. A letter saying her wages are 'being processed'.",[
  ["How long has she waited?","Since August. Fourteen years she scrubbed their floors, and they let her go a week after the king died. To save money, they said."],
  ["Who let her go?","The butler. Edmund. Marjut says his name the way you'd say a toothache."],
  ["Can anything be done?","Not by us. Not by anybody with a bench instead of an office. That's the whole of it."]],
 "Don't ask her about it today. Let her be cross in peace."],
6:["(Eila is stitching armbands fast.) Forty sold. Rosaria bought six for the pizzeria. Even Frank at the gun shop took one, and Frank doesn't mourn anything that can't shoot back.",[
  ["Business is booming.","For four more days. Then the king's in the ground and I'm back to collars. Death's a short season."],
  ["Who else bought one?","Sergeant Hanna, for the whole police station. And a woman from Hope Springs who said mine were 'charmingly rustic'. I charged her ninety."],
  ["Any for the palace?","The palace has its own seamstresses. Had. They let two of those go too. (She bites a thread off.)"]],
 "Take one. On the house. Reporters should look respectful."],
7:["(Eila holds up an envelope.) Form 12B, back again. 'Incomplete.' I went in and asked Bertil which box. He went pink and said 'all of them, I'm afraid'.",[
  ["All of them?","There's a new form. 12C. Nobody told anyone. He gave me a copy and apologised four times. I'll grant him that."],
  ["Does anyone ever get relief?","Bertil says a man from Frostholm got some in 1978. He said it like a miracle."],
  ["You're very patient.","I'm not patient. I'm stubborn. They're different things, but they look the same from the outside."]],
 "12C. Six pages. I'll have it in by Wednesday."],
8:["(Eila sniffs.) Sunday. The Church bells were going all morning up at the monastery, you could hear them on the wind. Do you go to church?",[
  ["Not often.","Nor me. Not since the priest told me the poor are blessed. I asked if he'd like to swap. He didn't."],
  ["Do you believe?","In God, maybe. In the people who say they speak for Him, less every year."],
  ["What do you do on Sundays?","Mend Monday's coats. The needle doesn't know it's the Sabbath."]],
 "Go and do your Sunday thing. I'll be here."],
9:["(Eila is counting coins into a tin.) Seventy-one armbands. Fifty-three pounds and some pennies. Coal till Christmas, if I'm careful, and I'm always careful.",[
  ["Well done.","It's not well done. It's a king dying. I'm not proud of it. I'm only fed by it."],
  ["What will you do next?","Collars and cuffs. Grace at the tailor's sends me the odd thing she's too busy for. She's busy now: everyone wants a suit for the funeral."],
  ["Do you get on with Grace?","We're polite. She's the reason Lindqvist's shut, a bit, and she knows it. She's also the only one who sends me work. Life's like that."]],
 "Don't spend your money in Frostholm. Spend it here."],
10:["(Eila stands very straight, in her best black.) They're burying him now. You can hear the bells from here. Marjut's gone up to the gates to watch.",[
  ["Not you?","Somebody has to sit on the bench. And I've seen enough rich people in black this week. Most of them in my armbands."],
  ["What was he like, the king?","He opened the new tram line in 1965. He said 'how splendid' and got straight back in his car. That's all I know of him."],
  ["Is Marjut all right?","She went up to see him off. Fourteen years she cleaned his rooms. Whatever the palace did to her, she liked him. Grief's not tidy."]],
 "Stand still a minute with me. Then go."],
11:["(Eila is watching the Bureau doors.) Handed in 12C this morning. Bertil stamped it with a big red stamp. He looked so pleased with himself.",[
  ["That's progress.","The stamp says 'received'. Not 'approved'. I've learnt the difference this month."],
  ["How long now?","Six weeks, he says. Unless there's a new government, in which case, he says, 'who knows'. He seemed frightened by the idea."],
  ["Bertil sounds decent.","He's decent. He's a decent man in an indecent building. I'd sew him a new collar if he'd let me."]],
 "Six weeks. I'll mark it on the wall."],
12:["(Eila has a suit jacket on her knee, chalk lines down the back.) From Grace. Overflow. She's had a rush on suits: every man on the island wants to look important.",[
  ["Who wants suits?","Businessmen, oil men, men who've never worn one. Grace says the oil men want to look like Wall Street. On Sarivalto."],
  ["Is she paying well?","Fair. Grace is always fair. That's what I can't stand about her: you can't even resent her properly."],
  ["You're very quick.","Twenty-two years. My hands could do this in the dark. Some winters they have."]],
 "Don't lean on the jacket. The chalk smudges."],
13:["(Eila is cross.) Marjut went to the palace kitchen door again. For her wages. Edmund wouldn't see her. He sent the footman out with a biscuit.",[
  ["A biscuit?","A biscuit. On a plate. Fourteen years of service and a ginger biscuit. Marjut threw it at the lamp post."],
  ["What will she do?","That's what worries me. Marjut doesn't forget. She keeps a list in her head, and Edmund's at the top of it."],
  ["Can't the Bureau help her?","There's a form for that too. 14A. I've seen it. It's twice as long as mine."]],
 "Keep an eye on her for me, if you see her alone."],
14:["(Eila has a flower pinned to her coat.) The big day. Everybody in one place. I want to see their faces, the ones in charge, when they see how many of us there are.",[
  ["What do you hope happens?","That somebody up front looks at the crowd and counts. Not votes. People. How many of us have nothing to do on a weekday."],
  ["A flower?","From Marjut. She stole it from a palace window box. (A small smile.) Don't print that."],
  ["Will you speak up?","Me? No. I'll stand there and be counted. That's speaking, if they're listening."]],
 "Come on. I want a spot near the front."],
15:["(Eila is thoughtful.) Yesterday. All those people. I saw women from the Saltwick plant, men from the Eastbrook mill. Same coats as mine, same mending.",[
  ["What did you feel?","Less alone. And more angry. When you see how many of you there are, you start wondering why you're the ones on the bench."],
  ["Did anyone in charge notice?","Some. Erik shook hands with half the crowd. Anna told us we were the future. The palace waved. Hope Springs didn't come at all."],
  ["Back to work today?","Back to Grace's collars. History's very stirring, but it doesn't hem trousers."]],
 "Same bench, same needle. Different eyes, though."],
16:["(Eila is listening to a radio someone's left on the bench.) The debate. Anna and Erik. Both of them have said 'working people' eleven times. I've been counting.",[
  ["Who's more convincing?","Anna knows what she's angry about. Erik knows what he'd do instead. I'd like someone who knew both."],
  ["Is it worth listening to?","It's worth hearing them say 'working people' to each other. It's the first time anyone's said it on the wireless."],
  ["What about the unemployed?","Not once. 'Working people', eleven times. 'People without work', not once. I'll write and tell them."]],
 "Write that down, reporter. Eleven to nothing."],
17:["(Eila steadies her sewing tin as the bench trembles.) Did you feel that? The ground. My pins jumped right out of the tin.",[
  ["An earthquake?","A little one. The tram stopped dead. The driver got out and looked at the rails like they'd insulted him."],
  ["Is everyone all right?","Doctor Lind's got a queue, but it's all nerves. Old Mrs Ek fainted in the supermarket and Lisa caught her."],
  ["Has it happened before?","Not in my lifetime. My mother said the mountain shook in 1921. She said a lot of things."]],
 "Help me find the pins. They're all over the cobbles."],
18:["(Eila looks at you over her spectacles.) Grace came to the bench herself this morning. To ask if I'd want regular work. Three days a week, in her shop.",[
  ["That's wonderful!","It's charity. Grace feels guilty about Lindqvist's. I'd be sewing for the woman who put me out of work."],
  ["Will you take it?","I said I'd think about it. Grace said 'of course'. She's so reasonable. I could scream."],
  ["Is pride worth it?","Pride's all I've had since Lindqvist's. It doesn't keep you warm, but it keeps you standing up."]],
 "Don't tell Marjut. She'll tell me to take it, and she'll be right."],
19:["(Eila nods towards the north, where steam rises over the hills.) Look at that. The mountain's breathing. Has been for two days. Nobody official's said a word.",[
  ["Has anyone said anything?","The radio says 'monitor the situation'. Kaisa sounded worried. Kaisa never sounds worried about anything but records."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. I've got a bag packed. Needles, thread, the coal tin and my mother's thimble. Nothing else in my flat's worth saving."],
  ["Who'd help, if it went?","Hope Springs has cars. The palace has the army. The rest of us have feet. That's how it's always been."]],
 "If it goes, head south. Don't wait for anyone to tell you."],
20:["(Eila is mending a palace footman's coat, of all things.) Oskar brought it down. Torn sleeve. The palace seamstress was one of the two they let go. Ha.",[
  ["Serves them right.","It does. I charged the palace rate. Oskar paid without blinking. It's not his money."],
  ["How's Marjut?","Strange. Quiet. She's been talking to the maids who are still up there. Lotta and Elsa. I don't know what they talk about. I don't ask."],
  ["Fine stitching.","Better than the palace has had in a month. They'll notice. Or they won't. I'll know."]],
 "Hold this while I knot it. Thank you."],
21:["(Eila has a damp cloth over her mouth, and ash in her hair.) The mountain's gone. Black sky, all over the north. Are you all right? Is everyone all right?",[
  ["I'm fine. Are you?","Coughing. Frightened. Alive. They say the lava's going north, away from the towns. They say nobody's hurt. I'll believe it tomorrow."],
  ["Where's Marjut?","At home. I made her stay in. She wanted to go up to the palace and see if the maids were safe. Loyal to the end, that girl, to the wrong people."],
  ["What can I do?","Wet a cloth. Breathe through it. Don't stand under anything that can fall. That's all any of us can do today."]],
 "Get indoors. Please."],
22:["(Eila is beating ash out of a coat.) Ash in every seam. Everybody's coats, everybody's curtains. I've had more work this morning than all of August.",[
  ["Disaster pays, then.","Kings dying and mountains blowing up. My two best customers this year. I'd rather be poor."],
  ["Who's helping clean up?","The army's out on the roads with lorries. Hope Springs has sent its maids to sweep Hope Springs. Saltwick's sweeping itself."],
  ["How's the city?","Grey. The tram's running slow. Lisa's sold every broom in the supermarket. Frank's got a queue for dust masks."]],
 "Shake your coat out before you go into anywhere nice."],
23:["(Eila looks at you squarely, a tape measure round her neck.) I've said yes to Grace. Started this morning. I'm on my lunch. Don't say anything.",[
  ["Congratulations.","I said don't say anything. (A pause.) Thank you. It's very strange, having somewhere to be at nine."],
  ["What changed your mind?","The mountain. Sitting in my flat with ash coming under the door, I thought: pride won't sweep that up. Wages might."],
  ["How's Grace as a boss?","She asked my opinion on a lapel. Nobody's asked my opinion on a lapel since 1979. I nearly cried into the button tin."]],
 "Back to it. Grace runs a tight shop, and so do I."],
24:["(Eila is on her bench at lunch, picking ash out of a hem.) Everything that comes into Grace's has ash in it. We've had to put a sheet over the cutting table.",[
  ["Busy at the shop?","Run off our feet. Suits for the end of the month. Every party wants its men to look like they've already won."],
  ["Do the aftershocks bother you?","One knocked a bolt of tweed off the shelf this morning. Grace and I both screamed, then laughed. First time we've laughed together."],
  ["You and Grace, friends?","Colleagues. Let's not rush things. I've only forgiven her for about a third of Lindqvist's."]],
 "Mind the step. It moved an inch yesterday."],
25:["(Eila looks almost amused.) Bertil came into Grace's this morning. With his collar frayed to threads. I mended it while he waited, and didn't charge.",[
  ["That was generous.","It was strategic. A man owes you a collar, he remembers your form. (She smiles.) And he looked so cold."],
  ["Any news of your relief?","He says the relief committee meets in October. 'Whoever's in charge by then.' He went white saying it."],
  ["Do you still need it?","Less, now. But Marjut does. And all the others on the benches. I'll keep the form going for them."]],
 "Bertil's a good man. Somebody should tell him."],
26:["(Eila is worried.) Marjut's been talking about Edmund again. 'Evening the score.' I don't like the sound of it. She's been up at the palace gate twice this week.",[
  ["What might she do?","Nothing violent. Marjut's not that kind. But she knows that household inside out. She knows what would hurt."],
  ["Have you talked to her?","I told her: he's a butler. Butlers outlive kings. Revenge on a butler is like shouting at the tide."],
  ["Maybe she just wants her wages.","She wants her wages and an apology. She'll get the wages. Nobody on this island has ever got an apology."]],
 "If you see her near the palace, walk her home. Please."],
27:["(Eila is quietly furious.) The army sent a lorry to sweep the ash in Hope Springs. Before the hospital. Before the tram stops. Hope Springs first.",[
  ["Who told you that?","Everybody saw it go down South Road. The Colonel lives there, doesn't he? Funny, that."],
  ["Maybe it was a mistake.","Mistakes go both ways, now and again. This kind only ever goes one way."],
  ["Will you write to someone?","I'll write to Bertil. Form 7, complaints. He'll stamp it 'received'. It's a start."]],
 "Everybody gets their ash swept eventually. Some people just get it swept first."],
28:["(Eila has pins in her mouth and a long strip of cloth over her arm.) Banners. Grace had an order for the rally in the square. Letters a foot high. I've been sewing all night.",[
  ["What do they say?","'A Vote for Every Islander.' I put an extra stitch in 'Every'. Nobody'll notice but me."],
  ["Will you go to the rally?","I'll go and look at my banner. Whatever anybody says from the stage, the stitching will be straight."],
  ["Busy day everywhere.","Flags, rallies, the palace in a state. The whole island's out in the street today. (She looks at the banner.) Let's hope it's in one piece by tonight."]],
 "Mind the banner. It's still got pins in it."],
29:["(Eila is calmer than you've seen her.) Marjut's had her wages. Edmund sent them down with Oskar. Every penny. No apology. She's taken them.",[
  ["How is she?","Lighter. Still cross. But she spent some of it on a cake for both of us. That's the first cake in this block since spring."],
  ["Tomorrow's the big day.","So everybody says. I've sewn for three of the parties this week and I still don't know which one I want."],
  ["What do you want, then?","Someone who'll answer a form inside six weeks. And looks at the bench when they walk past. Low hopes. I've earned them."]],
 "Get some sleep. Tomorrow nobody will."],
30:["(Eila is sitting with Marjut on the bench, a sewing tin between them.) Last day of the month. I took the day off from Grace's. I wanted to be here, on my bench, to see it.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like before a fitting. You've cut the cloth, it's too late to change it, and you're about to find out if it hangs right."],
  ["Whatever happens, you've got work.","I have. That's more than I had on the second. More than most of the bench has. I won't forget them."],
  ["Has it been a good month?","A king died, a mountain blew, and I got a job from Grace. (She shakes her head.) I'd never have believed you, if you'd told me."]],
 "Whoever's in charge tomorrow, they'll need their collars mending."],
};
// Einar, roughneck on Diversified Petroleum's oil rig, bunks in the crew quarters (Diversified Petroleum). Threads: how unsafe the
// rig is, and the complaints he writes that nobody reads; sleep, which he loves more than anything; his daughter Hilda, nine, who lives
// with her mother in Eastbrook and writes to him; the money he's putting by so he can leave the rig one day.
DAILY['Einar']={
7:["(Einar is on the deck by the drill tower, tapping a pipe with a spanner and listening.) Hear that? No? That's the trouble. Nobody hears it but me.",[
  ["What am I listening for?","A sort of whistle, under the clank. The valve on the west pipe's been weeping for a month. I've told them. In writing. Twice."],
  ["Is the rig dangerous?","Every rig's dangerous. This one's dangerous and cheap. That's the worst mix there is."],
  ["You look tired.","Two weeks on, one off. On my week off I sleep. On my two weeks on I think about sleeping. It's a full life."]],
 "Hold the rail on your way back. Don't trust the grating."],
8:["(Einar is pulling on oilskins with a face like thunder.) It's gone. The west valve. I said it would, and it has, and now there's oil on the sea. Somebody's going to find this very surprising.",[
  ["You warned them.","I did. In writing. I kept a copy. I always keep a copy. Not that it'll help anybody now."],
  ["What happens now?","We clean it up, or we don't, and either way Dario shouts. I've been shouted at by better men, and worse weather."],
  ["Who did you warn?","Magnus. He wrote back that it was 'noted'. I'd like to note something on him."]],
 "Go careful. Whatever happens, don't stand downwind of it."],
9:["(Einar has a pencil and a letter pad.) Writing to Hilda. My daughter. She's nine, and she's in Eastbrook with her mother. Every week she writes and asks what I've done. Every week I make something up.",[
  ["What do you tell her?","That I wrestled a whale. That I've seen the northern lights reflected in a puddle of oil, which is true. Mostly whales."],
  ["What does she write?","She draws me pictures. This week it's the rig, with a smiling sun over it. The sun's bigger than the rig. She's very accurate."],
  ["What are you writing about yesterday?","I'm writing that it was a busy day with a lot of water. She doesn't need more than that. She's nine."]],
 "If you're in Eastbrook, the school's by the square. She's the one with the plaits and the opinions."],
10:["(Einar is asleep sitting up, and wakes with a start.) Sorry. Funeral today, for the old king, and they've given us an hour off. I'm spending it the way the king would have wanted. Sleeping.",[
  ["Is that what the king would have wanted?","He was eighty-odd. I bet he'd have given his crown for one good long sleep, near the end."],
  ["Did you know him?","He came out to the rig when it opened. Shook my hand. Asked me if I liked the work. I said no. He laughed and said, 'Neither do I.'"],
  ["Go back to sleep.","(He's already halfway there.) Thank you. You're a decent sort, for a reporter."]],
 "(He's asleep again before you've turned round.)"],
11:["(Einar is reading carbon copies of his own reports.) I've written forty-one complaints about this rig in two years. Forty-one. The company files them. Somewhere there's a drawer full of me being right.",[
  ["What's the worst thing about the rig?","The legs. Below the waterline. Nobody's looked at them since 1977. Salt eats steel; it's not personal, it's chemistry."],
  ["Who reads the complaints?","A clerk in the Petro Tower files them under E. Nobody else. He sent me a card at Christmas once. 'Thank you for your correspondence.'"],
  ["Forty-one? Why keep going?","Because the forty-second might be the one they read. And because when it all goes wrong, I want it written down that I said so."]],
 "Forty-two. Coming soon, to a drawer near you."],
12:["(Einar is eating porridge very slowly.) Do you know what I'm going to do on my week off? Sleep for a whole day. Then eat. Then sleep another day. Then see Hilda.",[
  ["Is that it?","That's everything. A man who works this rig doesn't want adventures. He wants a bed that doesn't move."],
  ["Where do you sleep ashore?","A room above the Timberline Tavern in Eastbrook. Noisy until midnight, then like a church. I sleep through the noisy part, anyway."],
  ["What's Hilda like?","She wants to be a lighthouse keeper, like Signe at Eastbrook Harbour. She says lighthouses are better than rigs because they stay still and help people."]],
 "Porridge. Sleep. Hilda. A man needs only three things."],
13:["(Einar is holding a bottle of Kelp Cola at arm's length.) Free samples from Kingsgrad, off the supply boat. Lars has had six. I'm going to have one, very slowly, so it lasts.",[
  ["Is it good?","It's sugar and bubbles and a little bit of seaweed. My mother would say it's what you'd get if the sea had a birthday."],
  ["Would Dario mind?","Dario minds most things. He's never been out here, so I can mind my own business. And my own cola."],
  ["Lars has had six?","Six, and he's started singing. Badly. Somebody should take the seventh off him."]],
 "Lasts longer if you sip it. Like a week off."],
14:["(Einar is in a clean jumper.) The big day, the whole island in one place. The boat's taking us in. I'll find Hilda in the crowd, and I'll stand her on a crate where she can see everything.",[
  ["What do you expect from the day?","Everyone pretending they know what happens next. Nobody does. I'd rather be honest, and sleepy."],
  ["Will her mother mind?","Her mother and I get on better since we stopped living together. That's often the way, I find."],
  ["Clean jumper. Very smart.","Hilda knitted the sleeves. Her mother did the rest. One sleeve's longer. I wear it with pride."]],
 "Look for the man with the girl on a crate. That'll be me."],
15:["(Einar is smiling, which is unusual.) Hilda saw everything from her crate on the big day. She asked me why everybody wanted to be king. I couldn't answer her.",[
  ["What would you have said?","That some people like being in charge, and some people like being asleep. I know which one I am."],
  ["Prince or priest, today?","Neither one's ever been on a rig. Whoever goes out to look at the legs below the water, I'll vote for him."],
  ["She sounds clever.","Cleverer than me. She's going to go away and come back and run the place. I'll be the old man asleep in the corner."]],
 "Hilda says the crown would look better on a dog. I'm not going to argue."],
16:["(Einar is half-listening to the debate on the crew radio, with his eyes shut.) The Communists and the Democrats. I'm listening for anything about the rig. Nobody's mentioned it. Nobody ever mentions it.",[
  ["What would you want them to say?","That somebody's going to make the companies fix what they break. Just that. Then I'd vote for them, whoever they are."],
  ["Would the Communists fix the rig?","They'd paint it red and call it the People's Rig. Then it'd rust in a different colour."],
  ["You're half asleep.","I'm listening with the half that's awake. It's the better half for politics."]],
 "Wake me if they mention a valve."],
17:["(Einar is very calm.) Dario's missing, they say. The office is in a fluster. I've worked under five bosses on five rigs. They come and go. The rust stays.",[
  ["Aren't you worried about him?","Dario can look after himself. He's got a bowling lane and a safe. I've got a jar of coins and a daughter. I'll worry about mine."],
  ["What would happen without him?","Somebody else would come and sit in his chair, and read none of my reports. Same as now, with a different name on the door."],
  ["Where do you think he is?","Asleep somewhere, if he's got any sense. It's what I'd do."]],
 "If he turns up, tell him forty-two's still waiting."],
18:["(Einar is listening, very still, with a hand flat on the table.) Felt that? The ground's shaking ashore. That shake will have gone down through the sea floor, and up every leg of the rig.",[
  ["Is that bad?","It's not good. A rig's a table standing in the water. You don't shake a table full of plates."],
  ["Have you reported it?","Number forty-three. I wrote it while it was still shaking. My handwriting's never been worse."],
  ["What can be done?","Divers. Down below the waterline. Look at the legs, look at the welds. It costs money, so they won't. Until they have to."]],
 "Forty-three. Filed under E. As ever."],
19:["(Einar is reading a letter from Hilda, and laughing.) She's drawn a volcano, with steam coming out. She says it's the mountain, and it's angry because nobody visits it. She's nine.",[
  ["Is she frightened?","Not a bit. She's frightened of spiders and the dark. Volcanoes she finds interesting."],
  ["Is Eastbrook safe from the mountain?","It's a long way south. Further than Frostholm. Her mother has a bag packed by the door anyway. She's a sensible woman."],
  ["Have you written back?","I drew her a picture of me asleep. She says my drawings are very lazy. She's not wrong."]],
 "An angry mountain because nobody visits. There's something in that."],
20:["(Einar is counting coins into a jar.) The leaving fund. Every week, ten dollars. When it's full, I go and work on land. Fixing boats. In Eastbrook, near Hilda. Somewhere that doesn't move.",[
  ["How full is it?","About a third. Another year and a half, if nothing goes wrong. Something always goes wrong, so: two years."],
  ["Why not now?","Because a boat-mending man earns half what a rig hand does, and Hilda needs shoes. Lots of shoes. She grows like a weed."],
  ["Does Lars know about it?","Lars thinks a jar's for keeping beer in. I keep this one well hidden from him."]],
 "Ten dollars in, every week. The jar doesn't argue."],
21:["(Einar is pale, and has a radio in his hands.) The mountain's erupted. I've been on the radio to Eastbrook for an hour. Hilda's safe. Her mother's safe. Everyone's safe. I can breathe again.",[
  ["Thank God.","I'm not much for God, but I'll thank anybody today. I'll thank the radio. I'll thank the cook."],
  ["What did Hilda say?","She said she could see the orange in the sky from her bedroom window, and could she please have a piece of the volcano for school."],
  ["What does it mean for the rig?","The sea floor's moved. I don't know what it's done underneath us. Nobody does. That's what frightens me."]],
 "Go on, find your own people. Everyone should do that today."],
22:["(Einar has his forty-three carbon copies laid out in a row.) After yesterday, I've written a report I've wanted to write for two years. Number forty-four. It says: send divers. It says please. It's never said please before.",[
  ["Who's it going to?","Straight to Dario, this time. Not through Magnus. Magnus files things. Dario shouts at things, and shouting at least gets things done."],
  ["Will he read it?","He'll read the first line. So I made the first line 'The rig could sink.' That gets a man's attention."],
  ["What's below the waterline?","Rust, bad welds, and two years of nobody looking. And now an earthquake on top of it. It's like a rotten tooth."]],
 "Forty-four. With please. Let's see if manners work."],
23:["(Einar is very quiet, and very angry.) Something's given way under the waterline. Exactly where I said. In forty-four reports, in writing, with copies.",[
  ["How bad is it?","Water's coming in. They've sent for welders and divers. If they get here, it's fixable. If they don't, it's not."],
  ["Did it just fail, or was it helped?","I'd like to know that too. Old steel breaks, but it breaks in an old way. I'll know when I see the edges."],
  ["Do you feel vindicated?","No. I feel sick. Being right about this was never the point. The point was nobody getting hurt."]],
 "If anybody asks, I've got the copies. All of them."],
24:["(Einar is fast asleep, sitting up, with a pen still in his hand. He wakes when you speak.) Sorry. Up since the day before yesterday. I've forgotten what a bed's for.",[
  ["Go and lie down.","I will. In a minute. After I've finished writing to Hilda. She worries when the letter's late."],
  ["What are you writing to her?","That I didn't wrestle any whales, this time. That there's a lot of fuss about the rig. That the sea's very cold at the bottom."],
  ["You look done in.","I'm done in, done over and done for. But I'm sitting down, and that's a start."]],
 "(He's asleep again, pen and all.)"],
25:["(Einar is looking at a fresh letter from the company.) Dario's written to me. Dario. In his own hand. Two lines. 'Received your report. We'll talk.' I don't know if that's a promotion or a firing.",[
  ["What do you think it means?","With Dario? Both, probably. He'd promote you and sack you in the same breath if it saved money."],
  ["Would you take a promotion?","If it meant fixing things. Not if it meant a desk. I'd fall asleep at a desk and never wake up."],
  ["Show it to Hilda.","I will. She'll want to know who Dario is. I'll tell her he's the man who owns the whale."]],
 "We'll talk. Two words that keep a man up at night. Not me. I'll sleep through it."],
26:["(Einar has the leaving jar on his knees.) Dario's gone to buy the spa on the mountain. If I asked him for a new valve, he'd tell me money doesn't grow on trees. It grows in hot baths, apparently.",[
  ["Would you ever go to the spa?","Once, with Hilda's mother, before Hilda. Fell asleep in the hot pool. She had to wake me before I drowned. Best sleep of my life."],
  ["How's the leaving fund?","Two dollars more than last week. It's not much. It's always going in the right direction, though."],
  ["Maybe Dario will spend on the rig next.","Maybe. And maybe the volcano will apologise. I'm not holding my breath for either."]],
 "Hot baths and rusty legs. That's an oil company for you."],
27:["(Einar is drawing on a scrap of paper.) Hilda wants a picture of the rig for her class, with everybody's names on it. I've put Lars on the gantry, the cook in the galley, and me asleep in my bunk.",[
  ["Is that accurate?","Very. She'll know it's true. She's seen me asleep in a chair at her birthday party."],
  ["Who else is on it?","A whale, for old times' sake. And a little figure at the top, waving. Hilda says that's her, come to visit. She'll be very cross if I leave her off."],
  ["Going to the office party tomorrow?","The boat's taking us in. Bowling and milkshakes. I'll go for an hour, then find a quiet chair. Nobody bowls in a quiet chair."]],
 "A rig, a whale and a waving girl. Better than any of the company's posters."],
28:["(Einar has a milkshake and a very comfortable chair in a corner.) Dario's party. I've bowled once. Seven pins. Then I found this chair and I'm not leaving it.",[
  ["Has Dario said anything to you?","He came over, shook my hand and said 'Forty-four reports.' Then he walked off. I don't know what it meant. I'll sleep on it."],
  ["The whole island's out today.","Every faction pushing at once. Fine. Let them. I've got a chair."],
  ["Seven pins is good!","Lars got four, after he'd bowled backwards. I'm the champion of the rig, by a wide margin."]],
 "If anyone wants the chair, they'll have to wake me first."],
29:["(Einar is reading a timetable for the Eastbrook boat.) The Republicans vote today, in Frostholm. I hope whoever wins knows what's below a waterline. I've written them a letter, just in case.",[
  ["What does the letter say?","Fix the rig. It's short. Hilda helped me spell 'maintenance'. Twice."],
  ["Who'd you like to see win?","Whoever actually reads letters. I don't know which one that is. I've never met a politician who did."],
  ["Is that the Eastbrook timetable?","My week off starts soon. First boat to Eastbrook. Then sleep. Then Hilda. Then more sleep."]],
 "If they don't read it, I'll write another. I'm very good at that."],
30:["(Einar is sitting quietly with a cup of tea and Hilda's latest drawing.) The last day. Everybody says it's settled today, one way or the other. Hilda's asked me what'll happen. I said I didn't know.",[
  ["What did she say?","She said 'Then we'll find out together.' She's nine. She knows more than the lot of them."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A rig that's safe. A week off that's quiet. A girl who grows up somewhere that listens. That's all."],
  ["You've earned a good sleep.","I have. I'll have it whatever happens. That's one thing no government can take off a man."]],
 "Whatever happens today, the tide still comes in. Look after yourself."],
};
// Elin, Phil's secretary at Kelp Cola HQ (Kelp Cola). Threads: Mats, her partner at Diversified Petroleum, and the secret neither
// company knows (his proposal, her answer, the unsigned letter in her drawer); typing every secret in the building and telling
// none of them; Harald's speeches and their adjectives; standing between Phil and everyone who wants him, Petra included.
DAILY['Elin']={
2:["(Elin opens the door only halfway. Behind her, a man reads the financial pages at the kitchen table.) Oh! The reporter. The whole block's talking about you.",[
  ["Who's that at the table?",["That's Mats. My... Mats. (She lowers her voice.) He's a clerk at Diversified Petroleum, and I'm secretary to the boss at Kelp Cola. You see the difficulty.",[
    ["Does anyone know?","Rosa from the hot dog cart knows, because Rosa knows everything. The companies don't. Phil would faint, and Dario would sack him."],
    ["Why not just tell them?","Because both companies are fighting over the Republicans, and everyone would think we're spies. We're not. We just like the same breakfast."]]]],
  ["When do you go back to work?","Tomorrow, when the road to the HQ's open. My typewriter's been alone for a month. I worry about it."],
  ["Nice flat.","Thank you. Mats chose the curtains. Don't tell anyone that either."]],
 "Shut the door quietly. The whole stairwell listens."],
3:["(Elin is typing at a terrific speed and doesn't look up.) Forty calls before the kettle boiled. Phil's upstairs, if it's him you want.",[
  ["What are you typing?","A letter to the palace about the wreath. Third draft. Phil wants it bigger, the palace wants it smaller, and I want my lunch."],
  ["You must hear everything here.","I type everything here. Hearing is extra."],
  ["About yesterday, at your flat...","What about yesterday? (She smiles sweetly.) I don't remember a thing. Neither do you."]],
 "Mind the stairs. I had them polished."],
4:["Harald's given me his first campaign speech to type. Eleven pages, and the word 'vibrant' nine times.",[
  ["Is it a good speech?","It's a smiling speech. You can hear the teeth."],
  ["Who is Harald, really?","A very nice man who has never once been told no. He tips the tea lady at Christmas, though, which is more than some."],
  ["Cut a few of the vibrants.","I've cut four. He'll put them back. He always puts them back."]],
 "If you hear the word 'vibrant' in Frostholm, that was me. I'm sorry."],
5:["(Elin is sorting the post.) Mats asked me last night what I'd think of New York. I said I'd think it was very far away.",[
  ["Does he want to move there?","He wants to be the kind of man who could. It's not the same, and it's a lot cheaper."],
  ["Have you ever been abroad?","Stockholm, once, on a school trip. I lost a glove and fell in love with an escalator."],
  ["Anything good in the post?","Bills, invitations, and a letter for Harald in pink ink. I don't open his post. I just notice the colour."]],
 "Back to the post. It never sorts itself."],
6:["Phil came in whistling: he's slept in his own bed at last. He whistles flat, but I haven't the heart to tell him.",[
  ["What's he got you typing?","A letter to the Hope Springs residents' committee about Dario's flagpole. 'Unseemly' twice, 'flagrant' once. He wanted 'outrageous', but I talked him down."],
  ["Do you like the job?","I like knowing things. The job's how I get to."],
  ["How's Mats today?","(She glances at the door.) Mats who? ...He's fine. He's got a cold. Don't say his name so loudly in this building."]],
 "And not a word about the curtains."],
7:["Mats went back to the Petro Tower today, for the first time since the snow. He went in his good tie. I tied it.",[
  ["Is it hard, loving a rival?",["We have rules. No shop talk at home. He doesn't ask about cola, and I don't ask about oil.",[
    ["Do you ever break the rules?","Once. He asked what flavour we were working on, and I told him herring. He believed me for a week."],
    ["Has anyone at work noticed?","Harald asked who I was seeing. I said a man who sets the Gazette crossword. He laughed, and hasn't asked since."]]]],
  ["What's the Petro Tower like?","Mats says it hums: the lifts, the lights, the men. He loves it. I've never been in. I'd be seen."],
  ["Rosa really knows about you two?","Rosa gives me extra mustard and a look. The look says: I know. The mustard says: I won't tell."]],
 "Rules are rules. Even the ones we break."],
8:["My day off, and here I am. Phil's left his reading glasses somewhere in the building, and he'd ring me at home every hour till I found them.",[
  ["Heard about the oil rig?","Something's gone wrong out there, but I don't know what, and I mustn't ask. That's the rule. I'll read about it in the Gazette like everybody else."],
  ["Is Phil pleased about it?","Phil will pretend not to be. I'll be typing him a very sincere letter of sympathy, with no adjectives at all."],
  ["Have you checked his top pocket?","(She stops.) ...Oh, for heaven's sake. He was wearing them when he left. They're on his head. They're always on his head."]],
 "Sundays. Even on Sundays, the glasses."],
9:["Saara came up from the lab to ask me what people like in a cola. As if I'd know. I like a quiet afternoon.",[
  ["What would you put in a cola?","Coffee. Then I could drink it at my desk and nobody would know which I was doing."],
  ["Does Phil ever go down to the lab?","When he wants to feel like his grandfather. He puts on a white coat and gets in the way."],
  ["Busy day?","Phil wants the wreath confirmed, Harald wants a new speech, and the palace keeps ringing to ask where we'd like the wreath put. I've told them: somewhere visible."]],
 "If you have a flavour idea, Saara's downstairs, and she's desperate."],
10:["(Elin is in black, pinning on her hat.) I'm going to the chapel to stand at the back. Somebody has to hold Phil's spare handkerchief.",[
  ["Is Phil upset?","More than he lets on. That bottle went up to the palace for fifty years. It's like losing a pen pal."],
  ["Will Mats be there?","Behind Dario, with the oil people. We'll be at opposite ends of the chapel, not looking at each other. Good practice for the rest of our lives."],
  ["Who sent the biggest wreath?","We did. I measured Dario's when it was delivered. Ours is a hand wider. Phil asked me to check."]],
 "Walk softly in the chapel. The floor creaks."],
11:["Petra from the warehouse came up today and wanted Phil. I stood in her way on the landing. I felt like a goalkeeper.",[
  ["Why stop her?","Because Phil said nobody, and because she'd have shouted, and he'd have given her everything she asked for and blamed me in the morning."],
  ["Do you think she's right?","She's right about the heating. I've been in that warehouse, and my ink froze. Don't tell Phil I said so."],
  ["You'd make a good goalkeeper.","I've had practice. Harald's tailor tried to get past me last week with a tape measure."]],
 "Close the door on your way out. Petra may be back."],
12:["(Elin's typing slows.) Mats was very quiet at breakfast. He read the financial pages twice and didn't say a word about New York.",[
  ["Is something wrong at his office?","No shop talk at home. That's the rule. But I know his face, and that was his 'someone's asked me for something' face."],
  ["What sort of man is Mats?","Proper suits, proper manners, and men who know what a bond yield is. He's a romantic, really."],
  ["Free samples tomorrow, I hear.","Kurt's had me type out the delivery rounds. Eleven pages. He's timed every street."]],
 "If you see Mats, don't say hello. You don't know him."],
13:["A man in a good coat asked the cleaners where Phil keeps the recipe. The cleaners asked me. I said in the fridge, with the cola. He's looked in the fridge twice.",[
  ["Where is it really?","I've typed every letter in this building for six years and I've never once typed that. Make of it what you will."],
  ["Did you hand out samples?","Forty bottles on the tram. A man from Saltwick asked if it was a Communist plot. I said no, a capitalist one."],
  ["What was the man like?","Italian shoes, a Kingsgrad haircut, and tomato on his cuff. I'd guess he eats a lot of pizza."]],
 "Help yourself from the fridge. There's no recipe in it."],
14:["(Elin has a scarf, a flask and a folder.) Phil's three speeches are in this folder: short, long and in case of disaster. I'm carrying them all day.",[
  ["What does the disaster one say?","That's confidential. It's very good, though. It's the only one without the word 'vibrant'."],
  ["Are you and Mats going together?","Separately. We'll stand near each other in the crowd, like strangers, and go home together after. It's quite romantic, if you don't think about it."],
  ["What are you hoping for?","A steady day, and a seat. I'll get neither."]],
 "Stay close to a Kelp Cola crate. They're everywhere today."],
15:["(Elin is typing very fast, and very badly.) Mats asked me something last night. Not about oil. Not about cola.",[
  ["Did he propose?",["(She stops typing.) He did. With his grandmother's ring and a very bad speech. Worse than Harald's.",[
    ["What did you say?","I said I'd tell him when the month's out. If we marry, we have to tell the companies, and one of us would have to leave."],
    ["Congratulations!","Shh! Not in this building. (But she's smiling.) Thank you."]]]],
  ["Crown or Cross, today?","Phil says he'll sell to whoever wears the hat. I've typed two letters of congratulations, one for each prince, just in case."],
  ["Do you take the tram home?","Every day. It's the only time I read a book that isn't a ledger."]],
 "Not a word. Not to Rosa, not to anyone."],
16:["Phil's asked me to listen to the debate and count how often he's mentioned. I've got a pencil and a tally sheet ready.",[
  ["Who do you support?","I'm a secretary. I support whoever buys the typewriter ribbon. Privately, I'd like a vote. Don't tell Harald: it would spoil his smile."],
  ["How many mentions are you expecting?","Anna will get to him inside five minutes. I've sharpened two pencils in case she gets going."],
  ["Have you answered Mats yet?","Not yet. I've written 'yes' on the back of an envelope three times and torn it up three times. Not because it's no. Because it's complicated."]],
 "If you hear Phil's name on the wireless, count it for me."],
17:["Mats says nobody's seen Dario at the Petro Tower for two days. He said it over breakfast. We broke the rule.",[
  ["Where could Dario be?","Mats says the top floor's all closed doors and whispering. That's all I know, and more than I should."],
  ["Did you feel the ground move?","The typewriter jumped a whole line. I typed 'Yours faithfully' in the middle of a sentence, and Harald signed it anyway."],
  ["You broke the rule?","Just the once, over toast. We'll be more careful. It was a very interesting rule to break."]],
 "You didn't hear that from me. Or from Mats."],
18:["Wilma rang to say Jaana's ill, and Phil's asked me to find a Kelpy by lunchtime. I've rung everyone on the island short enough to fit.",[
  ["Who'd fit the cup?","Someone about my height, with a strong neck and no dignity. I'm not volunteering."],
  ["Have you ever seen Jaana without the head?","Once, at the staff party, and only from behind. She has lovely hair."],
  ["Any word on Dario?","No shop talk. (A pause.) Still nothing. The rule's back on, but only just."]],
 "If you know anyone with a strong neck, send them to Wilma."],
19:["Mats wants to take me to the Midnight Note on Saturday, to hear Rita sing. In Frostholm, where half his office drinks.",[
  ["That's a risk.","That's what I said. He said the lights are low. He's very romantic, for a man who files invoices."],
  ["They say a prince was seen there.","Then nobody will be looking at us. Perfect."],
  ["Phil said there were leaflets in the crates.","Helen's. Phil had me collect them. I've a drawer full now. Some of them are rather well written."]],
 "If you're at the Midnight Note, you haven't seen us."],
20:["Harald's new speech has the word 'eruption' in it. 'An eruption of prosperity.' I've begged him.",[
  ["Cut it.","I tried. He says it's topical. So's the steam coming off the mountain, and I'm not putting that in a speech either."],
  ["Is Saara really going up the volcano?","She asked me for the company's insurance papers. I gave her the wrong ones, on purpose."],
  ["Have you answered Mats?","Not yet. He's stopped asking, which is worse. He just looks at me over the financial pages."]],
 "Keep away from the mountain. Saara won't, but you should."],
21:["(There's ash in Elin's hair, and she's sweeping her typewriter with a pastry brush.) I've counted every head in the building twice. Kurt counted them again.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Mats rang the switchboard to ask, in a very bad Swedish accent. The whole office heard."],
  ["Is the typewriter all right?","There's ash in the keys. Every 'e' comes out grey. Harald's speech looks as if a ghost typed it."],
  ["What a day.","All the cups fell off the shelf at once, like applause. Then it was very quiet. I'll remember the quiet."]],
 "Cover your mouth outside. The ash gets everywhere."],
22:["Somebody downstairs has started calling me 'the Swede'. Because of the telephone call. I'm going to kill Mats, very gently.",[
  ["Do they suspect anything?","Harald thinks it was a Swedish buyer. Harald thinks everyone's a buyer."],
  ["What's wrong at East Farm?","Phil wrote 'quietly' three times in one letter. Once, it's a secret. Three times, it's a scandal."],
  ["Have you decided about Mats?","I've decided to decide by the thirtieth. That counts as a decision, doesn't it?"]],
 "Tell nobody about the Swede. Least of all a Swede."],
23:["Kurt's sleeping at the factory with a pillow and a torch, because someone's said someone's said the Party has plans. I've sent him a flask of soup with Rosa.",[
  ["Is the factory in danger?","Kurt doesn't need much of an excuse to sleep beside his bottling line. Someone only had to whisper."],
  ["Still having aftershocks?","Phil's drinks cabinet rings like sleigh bells every hour. I've started to quite like it."],
  ["How's Mats?","At the Petro Tower till all hours. They're mending things after the eruption, he says. That's as much shop as I'll allow."]],
 "If you're near the factory, say goodnight to Kurt for me."],
24:["(Elin lowers her voice.) I've written a letter of resignation. It's in my drawer. I haven't signed it.",[
  ["Why would you resign?",["If I marry Mats, one of us has to go. He loves his tower. I love my typewriter, but I'm cheaper to lose.",[
    ["Phil might surprise you.","He might. He's a soft old thing under all the trophies. But Harald would think I'd sold the recipe, and Harald talks."],
    ["Couldn't Mats leave instead?","He would, if I asked. That's the trouble. I won't ask."]]]],
  ["Who else knows about the letter?","You, Rosa and the drawer."],
  ["Where would you work instead?","Mary at the book shop could use a hand. Fewer secrets, more dust. I'd miss the secrets."]],
 "It's only a letter. Letters can wait in drawers for years."],
25:["I typed a contract today that I'm not allowed to tell you about, for a man I'm not allowed to name. You'd like him. Everybody does.",[
  ["Give me a clue.","He's never had to queue for anything in his life. That's all you're getting."],
  ["They say Princess Daphne's courting the Democrats.","I typed a congratulations letter for every possibility weeks ago. There's one for her too, on very thick paper."],
  ["How's the letter in the drawer?","Still unsigned. Still in the drawer. I check on it every morning, like a pet."]],
 "Not a word about the contract. I mean it."],
26:["Mats says his company's buying the hot springs spa. He said it over breakfast, then put his hand over his mouth.",[
  ["A spa? On a volcano?","Mats says the men upstairs think the hot water's good for business. I think they want somewhere to sit in a bath and shout."],
  ["Is the secret advert today?","Today. I've been told to keep the stairs clear and my mouth shut. I'm good at both."],
  ["You broke the rule again.","Twice this week. We're terrible spies. Which rather proves we're not spies."]],
 "Keep the stairs clear on your way down, would you?"],
27:["Phil's had me type the foreign buyers' names on little cards. I've been practising saying them in the lift.",[
  ["Are you nervous for him?","For Phil, for Harald, for all of us. Buyers tomorrow, the vote the day after. I'm living on coffee and Kelp Cola, which is a terrible mixture."],
  ["How's Mats?","Magnus has him working late on the campaign. He comes home smelling of the photocopier."],
  ["And the letter?","I've signed it. It's still in the drawer. Signed is different from sent."]],
 "Get some sleep. Somebody in this building should."],
28:["(Elin sits behind a clean blotter with a fixed smile.) The buyers are upstairs. If anyone asks, I've worked here forever and I love it. Both are true.",[
  ["How's it going up there?","I can't tell. Phil's laughing, which is either very good or very bad."],
  ["Has anyone tried to spoil it?","A man rang twice and hung up when I answered. I've written down the times. I write everything down."],
  ["Will you tell Phil about Mats today?","With the buyers here? No. Tomorrow's the vote. The day after, maybe."]],
 "Smile on the stairs. They can see you from the boardroom."],
29:["Harald's day. I've ironed his handkerchief and checked his teeth for spinach. That isn't in my job description, but neither is half of what I do.",[
  ["Will he win?","He's practised his winning face and his losing face. They're the same face. That's either his gift or his problem."],
  ["Is Mats at the primary too?","With Magnus's lot, holding a placard. I'll be with Harald's lot, holding a placard. We'll wave, carefully."],
  ["Who would you vote for?","I'm not a member. I've just typed eleven drafts of one man's speech. That ought to be worth a vote."]],
 "Off to Frostholm. Wish the handkerchief luck."],
30:["(Elin's drawer is open, and empty.) I said yes to Mats. Whatever happens to the island today, that part's settled.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you. Not too loud. We'll tell the companies tomorrow, if there's still a company to tell."],
  ["What happens to the island now?","I've no idea. I've typed letters for every way it could go. I'll post whichever one comes true."],
  ["Will you leave Kelp Cola?","Ask me next week. Today I'm Phil's secretary, and he's lost his glasses again."]],
 "Off you go. History doesn't wait for typists."],
};
// Elsa, laundress at the Royal Palace (palace workers, no faction). Threads: what the washing reveals, the prince's muddy boots, starch and her sore hands, her friendship with Marta and Lotta, a sheet she won't talk about.
DAILY['Elsa']={
2:["(Elsa is up to her elbows in a steaming tub.) Stand back, it's all starch in here. You'll be the British reporter. Word reaches the laundry before the front door.",[
  ["Word seems to travel fast here.","Everything comes through this room eventually. Sheets, shirts, secrets. I wash the lot."],
  ["Does the laundry really tell you things?",["More than the butler would like. A shirt has a good deal to say, if you know how to listen.",[
    ["Such as?","Lipstick, wine, soot, harbour mud. Never the same story twice. I couldn't possibly say more."],
    ["Anything on the prince's shirts?","(She smiles thinly.) The prince's shirts are spotless. That's what's interesting."]]]],
  ["It's warm in here, at least.","It's the only warm room in the palace. Don't tell Edmund, or he'll send someone to inspect it."]],
 "Don't touch the iron. It's hotter than it looks."],
3:["(Elsa is wringing out a sheet by the window.) The king's bed linen, last washed the week he died. I can't bring myself to put it away. I keep thinking he'll need it.",[
  ["Did you look after him long?","Twenty years of his sheets. He was tidy, and he never complained about the starch."],
  ["What was he like to work for?","He'd leave a note on the pillow, thanking me. A king writing thank-you notes to the laundress. I've kept them all."],
  ["That's very sad.","It is. And I've fifty sheets to do before supper. The world doesn't stop."]],
 "Mind the wet floor, it's slippery."],
4:["(Elsa holds up a pair of muddy boots.) Harbour mud again. On the prince's boots. I can tell Eastbrook harbour from Frostholm by the smell, and this is Eastbrook, and it isn't how you get a prince's boots dirty.",[
  ["What's he doing at the harbour?","Not my business. I just scrub. Though I'd like to know who he meets down there."],
  ["Are you saying he's up to something?","I'm saying that a prince in clean shoes has nothing to hide, and these are not clean shoes."],
  ["How do you clean mud off leather?","Warm water, a soft brush, and a very short memory."]],
 "I never said a word. The boots said it."],
5:["(Elsa is hanging shirts on a line in front of the stove.) Marta came by earlier to complain about Viktor and the herb bed. They've been at it for three days. I keep the peace with biscuits.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Marta, naturally. Viktor knows it. He's just being stubborn."],
  ["Do you have friends here?","Marta, Lotta, Ingrid. Oskar, when he's not sulking. We're a small, noisy family. The royals are more like lodgers."],
  ["What does a laundress do for fun?","Sit down. Nobody's ever asked me that, and I can't think of anything better."]],
 "If you see Marta, don't mention the rosemary."],
6:["(Elsa has a collar in one hand and a lens in the other.) There's a smear on this collar that's expensive. Not lipstick. Perfume, and a very French one. Someone at the palace has a visitor.",[
  ["Who owns the collar?","I couldn't say. It's one of Leopold's, I think. Or a footman who's borrowed one. Both are possible, and neither is my business."],
  ["You make a good detective.","I make a good laundress. The two are closer than you'd think."],
  ["Is it a secret?","Everything's a secret until it's on the line. Then it's the whole palace's."]],
 "That perfume. I'd know it anywhere."],
7:["(Elsa shakes a damp sheet loose.) They say the north-west road's open now. The oil people will want their linens done. I've told Edmund I'm not washing for Frostholm.",[
  ["Not even for good pay?","Money doesn't take the smell of oil out. I'd rather keep the palace clean than make it rich."],
  ["How's your week been?","Long. Everyone's in mourning, and mourning is very hard on linen. So many black handkerchiefs."],
  ["Do you ever miss your family?","My sister lives in Eastbrook. I see her on feast days. She makes better pastry than Marta, and I won't be saying that aloud."]],
 "Fold the corners. Always fold the corners."],
8:["(Elsa is ironing slowly, the radio humming somewhere below.) Sunday. The monastery bells came down the valley this morning, and I stopped to listen. First time all week.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Not as often as I should. I wash the altar cloths for the palace chapel, though. That counts, doesn't it?"],
  ["Any secrets in the altar cloths?","Candle wax, wine, and the odd tear. People confide in linen, you know."],
  ["It must be quiet down here.","Quiet is the best part. Steam, the iron, my own thoughts. A good day's work."]],
 "A good iron's like a good prayer. Slow and steady."],
9:["(Elsa is sorting a basket of black cloth.) Funeral clothes. All washed, pressed, and hung for tomorrow. Every tie, every veil. I've been up since four.",[
  ["That's a lot of black.","It is. And the one bit of colour is Daphne's scarf. Dark blue. She wants it kept out of the black pile. I said it'd be a rebellion. She said good."],
  ["Is Daphne cheerful about the funeral?","She's brave. She'll cry later, in private. I'll wash the handkerchief and say nothing."],
  ["Will you go to the chapel?","At the back, in my best apron. I've washed the king's linen for twenty years. I should see him off."]],
 "Go and get some sleep. Tomorrow's a long day."],
10:["(Elsa is in a plain black dress, her hands unusually still.) I've been in the chapel and out again. The wreaths are heavier than I thought. The family stood very straight.",[
  ["How did it go?","Properly. Leopold read. Augustus prayed. Daphne held her grandmother's hand. I cried into my sleeve."],
  ["I'm sorry.","Thank you. He thanked me for his sheets, every year. I'll miss the notes more than the king."],
  ["Is the palace different now?","Quieter. Like a house after a party. Everyone's tired, and nobody wants to say what comes next."]],
 "Come back when the dust has settled. I'll put the kettle on."],
11:["(Elsa is back at the tubs, sleeves rolled.) Back to the wash. Four hundred mourners left four hundred muddy footprints, and half of them on my floor.",[
  ["Anything interesting in the washing?","A bishop's glove with a hole in it. A Party badge hidden in a coat. And a prince's collar that I'll say nothing about."],
  ["Do the staff gossip about the family?","Constantly. But we never repeat it outside. A laundress who talks doesn't last."],
  ["Is Edmund strict?","He's fair, and very fussy. He inspects the pillowcases with a ruler. Nobody's ever failed him, and nobody wants to."]],
 "Don't breathe the steam. It'll curl your hair."],
12:["(Elsa spreads a tablecloth on the ironing board.) Marta wants every napkin starched like cardboard for some dinner next week. I told her linen has feelings. She told me to mind my business.",[
  ["Who's the dinner for?","A state visitor, I think. Foreign, very grand. They'll want to see the family at their best."],
  ["Is the family at its best?","Not lately. They've been quarrelling in whispers. I find the whispers in the cuffs."],
  ["What would you serve at a state dinner?","Whatever Marta says. She's the one with the ladle."]],
 "Stiff napkins, soft words. That's the palace."],
13:["(Elsa folds sheets into perfect squares.) Tomorrow's the great day. The whole island, they say. We've been told to look our best and keep our mouths shut. Both easy for me.",[
  ["Do you know what's happening?","No, and I'd rather not. When people tell me secrets, I wash them out."],
  ["Will you be watching?","From the laundry window, if the angle's right. It looks out on the drive, and sheets don't mind an audience."],
  ["Nervous?","About the starch, mainly. If the napkins collapse, I'll never live it down."]],
 "Early night, if you can. I won't be having one."],
14:["(Elsa stares out of the window at the drive.) The great day, and I've never seen so many cars. Everyone's in their best coat. I'd happily wash the lot, if they'd stand still.",[
  ["Is it exciting?","Frightening, more like. All those people deciding things, and none of them looks like they've slept."],
  ["Who's the best dressed?","Prince Leopold, naturally. But Augustus's cassock has a stain. I'm saying nothing."],
  ["What's the mood below stairs?","Hushed. Oskar's practising his bow in the mirror. Marta's burnt a loaf and blamed the oven."]],
 "Mind the carriage. It always swings wide at the corner."],
15:["(Elsa is rinsing a ribbon in clear water.) Everything from yesterday's come back to me in the wash. A glove with ink on it. A tie with a dab of whisky. A dozen little stories, none of them on the front page.",[
  ["Whose glove had the ink?","A clerk's, I'd say. Someone who signed in a hurry. The ink was fresh and blue."],
  ["Would you tell me about the whisky?","Only that the tie belonged to someone with a very ancient surname and no head for drink."],
  ["You're a good source.","I'm an excellent laundress. The rest is a coincidence."]],
 "Don't print what I can't confirm. I'll deny it."],
16:["(Elsa is warming the iron on the stove.) There's a debate tonight, between the Party and the Democrats. Lotta wants to go. I'd rather stay in with a hot water bottle and the wireless.",[
  ["Which side do you favour?","The side that doesn't make a fuss about sheets. So far, neither has said a word about them."],
  ["Do you read the newspapers?","The Gazette, a week late, when Marta's done with it. Mostly for the recipes."],
  ["Is the palace excited?","Edmund says the palace takes no side. Then he locks himself in the pantry for an hour. I assume it's to think."]],
 "If it's a good debate, tell me about the insults."],
17:["(Elsa steadies a stack of plates that has begun to rattle.) Did you feel that? The whole room trembled. The iron jumped on the stove. It's the second time this week.",[
  ["Could be the volcano.","I hope not. I've a great deal of ironing and no patience for lava."],
  ["Are the others frightened?","Lotta's been crying a bit. Marta tells her it's just the heating pipes. Marta's very good at saying things she doesn't believe."],
  ["Do you remember tremors before?","One, when I was a girl. It broke my mother's best teapot. She never forgave the mountain."]],
 "Hold on to something. Just in case."],
18:["(Elsa is hanging sheets that have a faint smell of sulphur.) Even the laundry smells of eggs today. I've opened every window. The whole palace smells like a bad breakfast.",[
  ["Is that the volcano?","The wind's brought it from the north. It'll pass, I hope. If not, I'll need more lavender."],
  ["Is anyone leaving?","Not yet. But I've seen Edmund counting the cars in the garage. He's up to something sensible."],
  ["Do you have any hobbies?","Knitting. I make scarves for the poor, and I'm going to need a lot more this winter."]],
 "Open a window if you feel faint."],
19:["(Elsa is wiping a spot of paint off a cuff.) Someone's been in costume. Face paint on a collar. A false moustache stuck to a coat. I won't say whose, but it's a good moustache.",[
  ["Whose coat was it?","I couldn't tell you. Though it fit the prince rather well."],
  ["Was he at a party?","Somewhere with music. There was confetti in the pocket, and a cigarette from a club in Eastbrook. The Midnight Note, I'd say."],
  ["You'd make a fine spy.","I make a fine laundress. It's the same job with fewer trains."]],
 "Forget I said anything. The cuff's clean now."],
20:["(Elsa glances at the window.) There's a smudge on the horizon, over the mountains. Looks like smoke. It'll be mist, I expect, though it's the wrong colour for mist.",[
  ["Could it be the volcano?","Everyone's saying so. The washing's all damp and the sky's wrong. I've never liked this kind of day."],
  ["Is the palace ready?","Edmund's been through the cellars and the stores. He's even counted the candles. Twice."],
  ["Are you afraid?","A bit. But fear won't wash a sheet, so I'll carry on."]],
 "Close the windows, if you've any sense."],
21:["(Elsa has a scarf over her face.) It's gone up. The whole sky's grey, and ash is falling like snow, only warm. I hung the sheets out, and now they're black.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","No, thank God. The lava's in the north, away from the towns. We were lucky."],
  ["How is the palace?","Frightened, and covered in dust. Marta's baking, which means she's worried. Edmund's making lists."],
  ["Can you wash ash out?","Not easily. It's a fine grey powder, and it goes into everything. I'll be at it for weeks."]],
 "Cover your hair. It'll never come out."],
22:["(Elsa is beating a rug over the railing.) Ash on everything. Every sheet, every shirt, every curtain. I've been washing since dawn, and the sky keeps giving more.",[
  ["How do you cope?","With tea, and the knowledge that it can't last forever."],
  ["Is Edmund keeping order?","He's keeping lists. He's drawn up a rota for sweeping. We're all on it, even the princes."],
  ["Do the royals help?","Daphne does, in her old coat. The others watch from the windows, and Oskar brings them tea."]],
 "The hem of your coat's grey. Give it here."],
23:["(Elsa is boiling a kettle of water for the tubs.) The floor trembles now and then. Mild, like a sleeping cat's purr. I've stopped looking up.",[
  ["Do you mind the aftershocks?","I mind the plates. We've lost three. But everyone's calmer than last week."],
  ["Do you hear anything from outside?","Viktor says the garden's a ruin. He'll have it back in a month. He's always right about plants."],
  ["Is there news from the family?","They're meeting a good deal. Whispering, in the dining room. I've never washed so many tablecloths for so few dinners."]],
 "If the floor shakes, don't tell Marta. She'll drop the pot."],
24:["(Elsa folds an altar cloth with great care.) The monks are moving up to the peaks, I hear. A retreat. The Church wants me to wash their vestments before they go.",[
  ["Is that a lot of washing?","Forty cassocks, and every one smelling of incense. My hands will be pink for a week."],
  ["Do they say why they're leaving?","To pray, they say. Others say to hide from the ash, or from the Prince. I stay out of it."],
  ["Will you go with them?","Not I. Someone has to keep the palace in clean linen, whoever's king."]],
 "Carry the altar cloth gently. It's older than I am."],
25:["(Elsa is pressing a blue silk scarf.) Daphne's. She's been out all day with the Democrats, they say. I found chalk dust on this, and a flyer in the pocket.",[
  ["Is she with the Democrats?","She's a young woman with her own mind. I wouldn't presume."],
  ["Would the family mind?","Leopold would. Augustus would pray about it. Edmund would pretend he hadn't noticed."],
  ["Will you keep her secret?","Washing machines don't gossip. And neither do I."]],
 "A scarf like that deserves careful pressing. Leave it."],
26:["(Elsa straightens a shirt on the hanger.) Visitors galore at the palace. Cola men, oil men, Party men. I've been told to launder their coats if they leave them. Not one has.",[
  ["Do you get to see them?","From the window. The oil men have fur collars. The Party men have the same coat, in different sizes."],
  ["Which is the best dressed?","The cola man. Spotless, matching, fragrant. He smells of vanilla."],
  ["Will you be glad when it's over?","Very. But I'll miss the stories. A laundress never runs short of them."]],
 "Mind the wet shirts. They're all someone's best."],
27:["(Elsa is in the middle of a mountain of napkins.) The state dinner's tomorrow, and each napkin has to be folded in the shape of a swan. I've done sixty, and the swans look like bats.",[
  ["Can I help?","You may hold the starch. Don't breathe on it."],
  ["Who's the dinner for?","A foreign guest, from the Eastern side. Marta won't say. I've been told only that the cutlery's to be polished with a cloth, not a thumb."],
  ["Is everyone anxious?","Terribly. Oskar's practising his bow. Edmund's inspecting the swans."]],
 "The swans will have to do. I'm out of starch."],
28:["(Elsa smooths a tablecloth.) Every faction's pushing today, they say. The whole palace has been in a flutter. I've changed the linen on every bed, in case someone needs a spare.",[
  ["Is that a good sign?","It's a busy sign. Busy is good, in a palace. Quiet is when things go wrong."],
  ["What's the mood?","Tight, like starch. A single tug and we'll all fall into line."],
  ["Do you know who will win?","I wash for whoever stays. That's as far as my politics go."]],
 "I've done my bit. The rest is up to them."],
29:["(Elsa's hands are red and raw.) Tomorrow's the end of it, and I've hung every sheet in the palace. They flutter like flags. I couldn't have asked for a better view.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","Hope is too big a word. I'd say I'm bracing, like linen in a gale."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Wash, as always. Whatever happens, someone will want a clean shirt."],
  ["Any last words?","Kindness, and a hot iron. They cure most things."]],
 "Sleep well. I've aired your coat."],
30:["(Elsa has put down the iron for the first time in days.) The last day. The sky's still grey, but the sheets are white. I've never been prouder of a clothesline.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Tired, and strangely calm. Whatever's decided today, the laundry will still need doing tomorrow."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The king's thank-you notes, the ash, and your questions. In that order."],
  ["Thank you for everything.","You're welcome. Mind the stairs. They've been polished."]],
 "Come back and see me, if the island lets you."],
};
// Erik, leader of the Democratic Party, at the Community Centre in Eastbrook. Threads: his old schoolteacher habits (and his old
// pupils all over Eastbrook), Bertha the duplicating machine and the endless leaflets, the debate with the Communists (16), the
// Russian at the Hotel Aurora (20), winning Princess Daphne round (25) and the big rally in Kingsgrad (28).
DAILY['Erik']={
4:["(Erik is standing on a chair, pinning up a blue banner that won't stay straight.) Hold that corner, would you? There. Eastbrook's open, the snow's gone from the square, and the Democratic Party is back in business.",[
  ["What business is that, exactly?","The business of asking people what they want. Nobody on this island's ever been asked. Not by the king, not by the cola company, and certainly not by Moscow."],
  ["Is the banner always this crooked?","Always. It was sewn by a committee. (He climbs down.) That's democracy for you: slow, a bit lopsided, and everybody had a say in the stitching."],
  ["You look like a schoolteacher.","I was one, for twenty years, at the school the king built. I still mark everything in red pen. My shopping lists come back with 'see me' on them."]],
 "Mind the boxes on the way out. It's always leaflet day here."],
5:["(Erik is turning the handle of an old duplicating machine, which clanks and spits out a smudged leaflet.) This is Bertha. She prints our leaflets, and she hates me personally.",[
  ["What do the leaflets say?","'A free vote, a fair wage, and no masters.' Then a lot of smaller print, which nobody reads, about fishing quotas. Bertha smudges the important bit every time."],
  ["Where did you get her?","From the school, when they bought a new one. I used to print spelling tests on her. Half of Eastbrook learnt to spell 'necessary' off Bertha, and they still can't."],
  ["Have you tried kicking her?","(He gives Bertha a gentle kick. She prints a perfect leaflet.) ...I'm not putting that in the party manual."]],
 "Take a leaflet. Take two, they're smudged differently."],
6:["(Erik is sorting a pile of letters at the long table.) Membership forms. Twelve new ones since the roads opened. Most from Eastbrook, one from Saltwick, and one, I swear, from a fisherman's dog.",[
  ["A dog joined the party?","The fisherman put the dog's name down for a laugh. I've kept it. Every member counts. And the dog's never once argued with me at a meeting."],
  ["Is Saltwick not Communist country?","Mostly. Helen's lot have the fish plant and the harbour. But one woman in Saltwick wrote 'I'd like to choose' on her form. That's all a Democrat is, really."],
  ["Who reads all these?","I do, in bed, with a mug of Maya's coffee. It's the best part of the job. Better than speeches. Don't tell anyone I said that."]],
 "Twelve new members. We'll be a crowd by Christmas."],
7:["(Erik has a Gazette open at the business page, and a red pen in his hand.) Frostholm's open, which means Diversified Petroleum's open, which means the oil men will be all over the island by Monday with chequebooks.",[
  ["You don't like Dario?","I don't know him. I know his company bought half the north coast for the price of a tractor. Men like that don't want a vote. They want a receipt."],
  ["Why the red pen?","Habit. I've found four spelling mistakes in the business page and one in Dario's name. (He caps the pen.) Henrik will be thrilled."],
  ["Is there a Republican threat too?","Magnus and his friends, with their party in the Petro Tower. They call it a party. It's a board meeting with balloons."]],
 "Back to Bertha. She's been quiet. I don't trust her when she's quiet."],
8:["(Erik is in a cardigan, with his feet on a radiator, listening to the sermon from the monastery on a little radio.) Sunday. I don't believe in much, but I believe in a day off. Twenty minutes of one, anyway.",[
  ["You listen to sermons?","I listen to everything. You learn more about a man from how he talks to God than how he talks to voters. The Abbot's sounding tired."],
  ["What will you do with your day off?","Write to my sister in Vaasa, mend a chair, and then, I expect, print some leaflets. I'm very bad at days off."],
  ["What does the church want?","Prince Augustus on the throne, and the island on its knees. Kindly meant. But I didn't spend twenty years teaching children to stand up just to have them kneel."]],
 "Even Bertha gets Sundays off. Well. Half of them."],
9:["(Erik is tasting something from a paper cup, with a face of great concentration.) Dex has invented a 'Democrat dog'. Mustard, pickles and a little flag. I'm told it's good for morale. My morale is confused.",[
  ["Everyone's talking about cola flavours.","Kelp Cola and the Communists, both after a new recipe. Two sides fighting over who makes the island's fizzy drink. Nobody's asked the island if it wants one."],
  ["Is Dex a good party worker?","The best. He hands out a leaflet with every sausage. Half of Eastbrook joined the party with mustard on their fingers."],
  ["Is it bad for you?","Almost certainly. (He eats the rest.) But a leader has to support his members."]],
 "If you see Dex, tell him the flag nearly choked me."],
10:["(Erik is in a dark suit, a little short in the sleeves, with a black armband.) I've been to Kingsgrad for the funeral. I bowed to the coffin. Some of our members won't like it.",[
  ["Why did you bow?","Because he was a man, and he's dead, and his children were standing there. I don't want a king. I don't want to dance on one's grave either."],
  ["Who else was there?","Everyone. The princes, the Abbot in gold, Phil from Kelp Cola looking at his watch, Dario looking at Phil. Anna from the Communists didn't bow. She'll want me to notice. I noticed."],
  ["Nice suit.","It was my father's. He wore it to my wedding, and I wore it to my divorce. It's had a full life."]],
 "I'm going to take this suit off and be a Democrat again."],
11:["(Erik is pacing, with a letter in his hand.) The mill lads want a rise and a guard back on the saw, and they've gone to Helen for help. Helen. The Communists' union leader, fighting for my members.",[
  ["Isn't that good for the workers?","It's good for the workers, and that's what matters. (He sighs.) It's also very bad for my pride. They should have come to me first."],
  ["Why didn't they come to you?","Because I've been printing leaflets about wages instead of shouting at Dagny about them. Knut said that to my face, very kindly. He was in my class in '63. He was right then too."],
  ["Does Dagny listen to anyone?","Dagny listens to her accounts. Bjarne keeps her accounts. If Bjarne ever stopped being loyal, Dagny would have a very bad week."]],
 "I'm off to the mill. Somebody ought to look like they're on the lads' side."],
12:["(Erik is up a ladder, changing a light bulb in the hall.) Ruby's been in, very mysterious. She's sold a record to someone who won't give his name, and the money's for the party. Anonymous donors make me nervous.",[
  ["Why nervous? It's money.","Money with no name on it usually has a reason for having no name. (He climbs down.) But Ruby says the note was polite, and she reads people better than I read anything."],
  ["How much does the party need?","For the rally alone: a stage, banners, a loudspeaker, permits from the Civil Service, and buses. Roughly everything we haven't got."],
  ["Want me to hold the ladder?","A little late, but thank you. I fell off this ladder in 1979 and broke the trophy cabinet. We'd only won one trophy. Egg and spoon."]],
 "Let there be light. Well, a sixty-watt bulb. Small steps."],
13:["(Erik is arguing with a Kelp Cola girl in a paper hat at the door, very politely.) No thank you. No, not even a small one. No, the party doesn't take free samples. (She leaves.) They're giving it out by the crate.",[
  ["What's wrong with free cola?","Nothing, for you. For me, a photograph of the Democratic leader with a Kelp Cola in his hand on page two of the Gazette. Phil would frame it."],
  ["You're very careful.","I have to be. The Communists have Moscow, the royals have a palace, the oil men have oil. All I've got is that nobody can say I've been bought. It's my only asset."],
  ["Are you actually thirsty?","Desperately. (He pours a glass of water from a jug.) Tap water. Neutral in all disputes."]],
 "If the cola girl comes back, I'm in a meeting. Forever."],
14:["(Erik is straightening his tie in the reflection of a window.) The fourteenth. The whole island in one place. If we can't be heard today, we never will be.",[
  ["What do you hope for today?","That people look around and see each other. Fishermen next to princes, monks next to oil men. That they think: we could all decide this together."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I taught thirty eleven-year-olds for twenty years. Nothing on this island frightens me. (His hand shakes on the tie.) Much."],
  ["Your tie's crooked.","(He fixes it.) Thank you. Ruby would have said so in a much ruder way."]],
 "Wish us luck. All of us. The whole island."],
15:["(Erik is surrounded by notecards, spread over the long table.) The debate's tomorrow. Anna and Helen against me and an empty chair, if nobody else turns up. I've written thirty arguments and I believe all of them.",[
  ["What's your strongest argument?","That a vote can be taken back and a revolution can't. If I'm wrong, you vote me out. If Moscow's wrong, you just live with it."],
  ["What will Anna say?","That a vote's just a game for people who own things, and the workers lose either way. She's not entirely wrong, which is why she's dangerous."],
  ["The royals and the church are squabbling today.","Let them. Crown before cross, cross before crown, and neither of them's asked the people in the pews. It writes our leaflets for us."]],
 "Thirty arguments. I'll be lucky to remember three."],
16:["(Erik has his sleeves rolled up and a glass of water he isn't drinking.) Debate day. I've spent all night with my notecards, and now I can't find the one that says 'smile'.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Like the first day of term. I used to be sick in the staff toilets every first day of term for twenty years. Then I'd go in and be fine."],
  ["Who's in the audience?","Mill hands, fishermen, half of Saltwick and all of Eastbrook. Ilse's in the front row. I've asked her not to heckle Helen. She's promised to heckle quietly."],
  ["Want some advice from a reporter?","Always. (He grabs a pen.) Go on. Short sentences? Yes. Henrik always says short sentences. I'll write that down."]],
 "Smile. That's what the card said. I remember now."],
17:["(Erik's water glass rattles on the long table, and he steadies it.) That's the second today. The ground. Dagny says it's nothing. Dagny also says the mill's broke.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Steam up north, people say, from the old mountain. The last time it went, my grandmother was a girl. She talked about the ash for the rest of her life."],
  ["Should Eastbrook be worried?","Eastbrook should be ready. I've asked Bjarne to look at the old fire buckets, and Dex to keep his cart near the tram, in case people need to move."],
  ["Is anyone else making plans?","The Abbot's praying, the Communists are holding a meeting about it, and Kelp Cola will probably sell volcano cola. And I'm counting fire buckets. Every man to his strengths."]],
 "If the floor goes again, get under the long table. It's survived worse."],
18:["(Erik is writing a list in red pen, and underlining it twice.) The rally, in ten days. Stage, banners, loudspeakers, buses, and a permit from the Civil Service, signed by a clerk who thinks forms are a sacrament.",[
  ["Who's the clerk?","Bertil. He's a good man, and he's never in his life signed anything on the day it was given to him. I've sent the form three times. He sends it back for a new stamp."],
  ["Why a rally in Kingsgrad, not here?","Because Eastbrook already agrees with us. You don't preach to the choir. You go and stand in the king's square and ask everyone else."],
  ["Who's building the stage?","Bjarne, from mill timber, if Dagny can be talked out of charging us for it. If not, we'll stand on the boxes of leaflets. Goodness knows we've got enough of those."]],
 "I'm off to stand in a queue at the Civil Service. Democracy is mostly queues."],
19:["(Erik is peering out of the window at the street, as if he expects to see someone.) The Russian businessman at the Hotel Aurora. Have you met him? I keep seeing his car in places a businessman wouldn't go.",[
  ["Where has his car been?","Outside Communist HQ in Saltwick. Outside the monastery gate. And yesterday, at the end of Alder Lane, with nobody in it. Businessmen don't park outside monasteries."],
  ["Who do you think he is?","I think he works for someone who'd like the island to stop asking questions. That's as far as I'll go out loud."],
  ["Isn't that a bit paranoid?","I was a schoolteacher. I can tell when someone's passing notes. (He drops the curtain.) He's passing notes."]],
 "Keep your eyes open in Frostholm. I'm told the hotel has thin walls."],
20:["(Erik has the curtains half drawn, and he's whispering, which suits him badly.) We're going to find out who the man at the Hotel Aurora really is. Today. It's time.",[
  ["What do you think you'll find?","A man who reports to Moscow, and hands the Communists their instructions, and maybe their money. I hope I'm wrong. I'm hardly ever wrong about passing notes."],
  ["What will you do if you're right?","Tell the island, loudly, with evidence. Nobody here wants to be decided by a man in a hotel room. Not even Helen, I suspect."],
  ["You're whispering. The curtains are shut.",["(He goes red, and speaks normally.) Yes, well. I've never investigated anybody before. I'm still learning the costume.",[
    ["Who else knows about this?","Ruby, because he went into her shop and frightened her. Dex, because Dex knows everything by lunchtime. And now you. Which is three too many, really."],
    ["Should you be telling a reporter?","Probably not. But you're a foreigner, and I've a feeling a foreigner's eyes are worth having on this."]]]]],
 "Not a word in the Gazette. Not yet."],
21:["(Erik's hair is wild, and he has a clipboard and a whistle.) The mountain's gone up! Ash coming down over the north, and it's heading our way. I'm getting the old and the small into the Community Centre.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that we've heard. The farms in the north have been cleared out, and the Abbot's sending monks down with blankets. Nobody's hurt, thank God. Even I'll say thank God today."],
  ["What can people do?","Wet cloths over your mouth, windows shut, and keep off the north road. And come here if you've nowhere better. Dex is making soup in a hot dog pan."],
  ["Is that a whistle?","It's my old games whistle. (He blows it, briefly.) Twenty years of football in the rain. It still works on adults, I'm glad to say."]],
 "Get inside somewhere. Please. I'm counting everyone."],
22:["(The hall is full of camp beds and blankets, and Erik is folding one of them with great care.) Everybody's gone home but the Halonens and their cat. Ash on every roof in Eastbrook. Nobody hurt. I keep saying it, because it's the only bit I believe.",[
  ["How did Eastbrook do?","Better than the north. Lava down past the farms, I hear, and the road's gone in two places. But Eastbrook sat in this hall and sang songs and nobody fought. I've never been prouder of a hall."],
  ["What does this mean for the vote?","It means people are frightened, and frightened people want someone strong to tell them what to do. That's the danger. Strong isn't the same as right."],
  ["What songs did you sing?","Ruby taught the whole hall a Louis Armstrong song. The Halonens' cat joined in. It was the best party meeting we've ever had."]],
 "If you need a camp bed, we've got thirty. And one cat."],
23:["(Erik is on his knees sweeping ash out of the hall doorway with a dustpan.) Grey. Everything grey. Bertha's full of it. She printed a leaflet this morning that said 'A FREE VOT' and then gave up.",[
  ["Is Bertha all right?","She's coughing. I've taken her apart on the long table and I'm cleaning her with a toothbrush. If she survives this, she can survive anything. Like the party."],
  ["What are people saying now?","That the mountain's a sign. The Church says from God, the Communists say from history, and Madame Zora at the circus says she told everyone. She probably did."],
  ["Want a hand sweeping?","(He hands you the dustpan.) A reporter who sweeps. I'll write to your editor. Hold it flat, or the wind takes it."]],
 "Thank you. I'll put you down as an honorary member. In pencil."],
24:["(Erik is writing a letter in his best schoolteacher handwriting, and blotting it carefully.) I'm writing to the princess. Daphne. She's the only one at the palace who reads anything that isn't a menu.",[
  ["What are you asking her?","To tea. To talk. Just to hear what a free vote would look like. A princess on our side would be worth a hundred leaflets, and Bertha's only got about eighty left in her."],
  ["Would a royal ever back a vote?","Kings have done it before, in other countries. Given their crowns a sensible size. Daphne's clever, and she's not the one who'd be king. That gives her room to think."],
  ["Isn't that a bit cheeky?","It's terribly cheeky. (He seals the envelope.) I used to tell my pupils: the worst anyone can say is no. Then they'd say it, and I'd have to think of something else."]],
 "To the post box, and then to bed. Ash permitting."],
25:["(Erik is pacing up and down the long table, in his good jacket, for once without a clipboard.) Today's the day I find out if a princess can be a democrat. I've tidied the hall. I've hidden Bertha.",[
  ["What's your pitch to her?","That a vote doesn't throw her family out. It just lets the people choose to keep them, or not. It's honest. I don't know if honest works on royals."],
  ["Does the palace know?","The palace knows everything, eventually, from the maids. Leopold has his own plans today, I hear. A crown for everyone, whatever that means. Paper crowns, probably."],
  ["Why hide Bertha?","Because she clanks, and she'd embarrass me in front of royalty. (He pauses.) I've also hidden the egg-and-spoon trophy. A man has his pride."]],
 "Wish me luck. And good manners."],
26:["(Erik is reading the Gazette, and getting crosser with every page.) Dario's buying the hot springs. Kelp Cola wants a prince on every bottle. And not one word in the paper about who'll fix the north road.",[
  ["Who should fix the north road?","Whoever the island chooses. That's the point. At the moment it's whoever's richest, and the richest don't use roads. They use helicopters."],
  ["What would a Democrat do differently?","Ask the farmers in the north what they need first. It sounds so simple. In three hundred years, nobody here's thought of it."],
  ["Want me to take the paper away?","(He folds it, sharply.) Yes, please. I'll only mark it in red pen, and Henrik's had a hard week."]],
 "Two days to the rally. I'm going to go and shout at a stage."],
27:["(Erik is on the floor of the hall, painting a banner in big blue letters: YOUR ISLAND. YOUR VOTE.) Banners. Twenty of them. We've run out of blue, so the last three are going to be in Kelp Cola red. Don't tell anyone.",[
  ["Who's helping paint?","Jonna did the lettering on the big one, and told me my spacing was 'a crime against the alphabet'. Meg from the circus is rigging them. She climbed the hall roof without a ladder."],
  ["What's the plan for tomorrow?","A stage in Kingsgrad square, Ruby's music, Dex's hot dogs, Bjarne's timber, and every voice we can find. And me, at the end, saying the same thing I've said for ten years, a bit louder."],
  ["Will the Communists cause trouble?","Some of them, maybe. And the Syndicate have their own reasons to want a crowd to turn into a brawl. Ilse says she'll keep the peace. Ilse's idea of peace is very firm."]],
 "Mind the wet paint. You've got half a 'VOTE' on your sleeve."],
28:["(Erik is in the hall, drinking Maya's coffee as if it were medicine, with a sheaf of notes in his hand.) The Big Rally. The square in Kingsgrad. Ten years of leaflets, and it all comes down to one afternoon.",[
  ["How many will come, do you think?","I've said thousands in public. In private I'd settle for enough to fill the square to the fountain. Even half the fountain."],
  ["How's the speech?","Nine drafts. Ruby says the ninth is good if I cut the middle. Jonna says the same thing in a ruder way. I've cut the middle. Now it's very short."],
  ["You look terrified.","I am. (He smiles.) First day of term. I'll be sick in the staff toilets and then I'll be fine."]],
 "Kingsgrad square. Come and listen. Come and shout."],
29:["(Erik's voice is hoarse, and he's eating a hot dog with the concentration of a man who hasn't eaten for a day.) Friendly rivalry with the Communists, they're calling it. Helen and I have shaken hands in front of a photographer. She squeezed very hard.",[
  ["Is it really friendly?","We agree the island shouldn't belong to a palace or a cola company. We disagree on everything after that. I'd call that a warm sort of feud."],
  ["What happens tomorrow?","Tomorrow the island decides. Or somebody decides for it. That's the difference between Helen and me, and between me and everyone else."],
  ["Are you proud of what you've done?","Ask me in a week. (He looks out of the window.) No. Ask me now. Yes. Whatever happens, Eastbrook stood up and asked to be asked."]],
 "Get some sleep. I won't. Somebody should."],
30:["(Erik is sitting alone at the long table under the crooked banner, with Bertha beside him, quiet for once.) The last day. Whatever's decided today, it'll be decided. Then I suppose I'll have to find out what I do on a Monday.",[
  ["What will you do, after?","If we win the vote, I'll stand for it, and probably lose to someone with a better tie. If we don't, I'll go back to teaching and tell thirty children what we tried. Either way, I'll be marking."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. Not for me. For the people who stood next to me. Knut and Ilse and Dex and Ruby. They believed me. I'd like to have been worth it."],
  ["Thank you, Erik.",["(He looks surprised, and pleased.) Thank you for listening to an old schoolteacher go on. You'd have been a good pupil. Asked too many questions.",[
    ["Is that a bad thing?","It's the only thing. Anyone who stops asking questions should never be allowed near a ballot box. Or a palace."],
    ["What mark would you give me?","(He reaches for the red pen, and then thinks better of it.) Ask me tomorrow. I'll know then."]]]]],
 "Go on. The island's waiting, and so am I."],
};
// Father Thyme, Abbot of the Mountain-Top Monastery and head of the island's Orthodox Church. Threads: his chess game by letter
// with Prince Augustus (and whether Augustus is meant for the crown or the cloister), his failing voice and his age, his polite
// quarrel with Brother Anselm, the mountain waking under the monastery.
DAILY['Father Thyme']={
5:["(Father Thyme is brushing snow off the bench outside his house with his sleeve.) The road is open, and the world comes up it puffing. Sit, if the bench will have you.",[
  ["It's a beautiful view.","On a clear day you can see Kingsgrad, and on a very clear day you can see what Kingsgrad is up to. Today it is only clouds. I prefer the clouds."],
  ["How long have you been Abbot here?","Thirty-one years Abbot, and a novice for twelve before that. I came up the mountain with a cough and a broken heart, and the mountain kept them both."],
  ["Is that a chessboard on your windowsill?",["It is. Prince Augustus and I play by letter. A move a week, carried up by whichever novice has the strongest legs.",[
    ["Who's winning?","He is, at the moment, and he apologises for it in every letter. That is the trouble with Augustus. He apologises for winning."],
    ["Do you let him win?","Never. A man who will be told the truth about chess may one day be told the truth about other things."]]]]],
 "Mind the path on the way down. The third step bites."],
6:["(Father Thyme is sipping something steaming from a tin mug.) Forgive the croak. Brother Teodor's honey and hot water. He swears by it. I swear at it.",[
  ["Are you unwell, Father?","Only old. The voice goes first, they say, and then the knees, and then the patience. I'm on the voice."],
  ["You preach on Sunday, don't you?","The first Sunday without a king. The whole island will be listening, and I have written nothing but crossings-out."],
  ["Honey's better with a drop of brandy.","So Brother Teodor says, and so Brother Anselm says he must never say again. Peace in a monastery is a delicate thing."]],
 "Go on. I'll try another page of crossings-out."],
7:["The north-west road opened this morning, and by lunchtime a man from Diversified Petroleum was at our gate offering to mend the church roof. For nothing. Imagine.",[
  ["That's generous of them.","Nothing that comes up this mountain in a car with a heater is generous. But the roof does leak on the icons, and the icons don't care who pays."],
  ["What did Brother Anselm say?","He said it was the devil in a fur collar. I said the devil seldom offers to do roofing. We are both still thinking about it."],
  ["Will the Church take oil money?","The Church has taken kings' money, fishermen's money and once, I'm told, a pirate's. It is what you do with it that God asks about."]],
 "If you see a roofer who prays, send him up."],
8:["(Father Thyme sits in the front pew of the empty church, turning his prayer rope through his fingers.) Sunday. The first one. I've rung the bell myself, to have something to do with my hands.",[
  ["Nervous, Father?","For forty years I have spoken to this island about God, and they have listened politely and gone home. Today they will listen properly. That is much worse."],
  ["What does the island need to hear?","Something true that it can bear. That is all a sermon is, in the end. The hard part is the bearing."],
  ["Will the royal family be listening?","Augustus will be in the third pew, looking at his hands. Leopold will hear about it by telephone. The king liked a short sermon. I may honour him in that."]],
 "Sit near the back, my child. The back is where the honest faces are."],
9:["(Father Thyme is at his table with a pen and a sheet of the monastery's best paper.) Tomorrow I bury a king. I am writing what I shall say over him, and he is being as difficult dead as he was alive.",[
  ["What was he like, really?","Generous, vain, kind to dogs, and frightened of the dark until the day he died. He made his confession every month. I shan't tell you what was in it."],
  ["Will you go down to Kingsgrad tonight?","Brother Anselm will drive me in the old van, with a blanket and a lecture. The Royal Chapel is cold, and so is Leopold. I shall need the blanket."],
  ["Could I read what you've written?","You could read the crossings-out. They are the more honest part."]],
 "I must finish this. He deserves a better last word than he'd have written himself."],
10:["(Father Thyme is in his black funeral vestments, and Juniper has clearly been at the hem.) The king's day. Every man who wants the crown will stand by his coffin and look sorrowful. I shall be watching their shoes.",[
  ["Their shoes, Father?","A man's face can lie, but his feet want to be somewhere else. You'll see. Watch who keeps edging towards the door, and who towards the throne."],
  ["Were you at his father's funeral?","As a novice, singing in the choir, in a snowstorm. This king was a young man then, and he caught my arm at the graveside when I slipped. I shall try to hold his son's."],
  ["Your vestments look very fine.","Juniper took the hem up a quarter of an inch, and told me it was the difference between grief and sloppiness. She's right. She's always right about hems."]],
 "Go in peace. And in a warm coat. The chapel is like a cellar."],
11:["(Father Thyme is folding blankets into a crate.) The king is in the ground, and now everyone's eyes are on the palace. I'd like ours to be on the beach.",[
  ["The beach?","There's a man called Pat who lives in a tent there, and has done for years. We take him a blanket at Christmas and sing at him. I'm not sure it has ever helped."],
  ["Does the Church help many people?","Pirkko feeds more of the island's hungry from her van than this monastery does with all its prayers. I mean to put that right before I'm too old to carry a crate."],
  ["Are those blankets for anyone in particular?","For whoever is cold, which in September on Sarivalto is everyone. Brother Anselm counts them as they go out. I count them as they don't come back. We both sleep."]],
 "God's blessing on you, whatever you're about today."],
12:["(Father Thyme is frowning at the chessboard.) A letter from Augustus, three days late, and no move in it. Only a page about whether a man can serve two masters. I think he means it about more than chess.",[
  ["What did you write back?","That he has a move to make, and that I'm an old man, and would like to see the end of the game. Both of those were about more than chess too."],
  ["Is Augustus torn between the Church and the crown?","Augustus is torn between everything. He'd apologise to a door for walking through it. That is what makes him good, and it is also what keeps him awake."],
  ["Has anyone else been up the mountain?","A great many parcels for the brothers, today. Ink, socks, seeds for Brother Paavo. Monks get more post than you'd think, and say less about it."]],
 "Keep your coat on. The weather's turning, and so is everything else."],
13:["Someone from Kelp Cola left four crates of their drink at our gate this morning, with a ribbon on. Free samples, the card said. With compliments, and a picture of a cup with eyes.",[
  ["What will you do with them?","Brother Teodor is boiling one down into a glaze for the carrots. The rest are under the altar steps until Brother Anselm decides whether they're a sin."],
  ["Have you tried it?","Once, in nineteen sixty. It tasted of the sea and of someone trying to sell me the sea. I don't suppose it's changed."],
  ["That's the island's business now, isn't it? Gifts.","Everyone is giving everyone something this month. When the gifts stop, look to see who's still smiling. That is who expected to win."]],
 "Take a bottle down with you, if you like. Save us from temptation."],
14:["(Father Thyme is wrapped in a heavy coat, his breath white.) The fourteenth. The whole island in one place, all at once, all of them sure they're right. I've lived long enough to be frightened of that.",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","A great deal of shouting, and a few quiet words that matter more. It is always the quiet ones. Listen for those."],
  ["Will the Church have its say?","The Church always has its say. Whether anyone hears it over the brass band is another matter."],
  ["Will you be warm enough?","Juniper knitted me a scarf, in black, with a little cross at each end. It is warm enough to make me feel guilty, which is how I know it's from a churchwoman."]],
 "Go and see it. Tell the world it was cold and we all came anyway."],
15:["(Father Thyme is setting out two chairs and a pot of tea.) Augustus is coming up the mountain today. Or the palace is sending someone to talk him out of it. One or the other, possibly both.",[
  ["What will you say to him?","That God calls some men to the altar and some to the throne, and that the call is quieter than the people shouting around him. Then I'll ask him what he hears."],
  ["Do you want him to be king?","I have wanted it for years. Lately I've wondered whether I want it for him, or for us. That is a question I don't like the taste of."],
  ["Two chairs. Isn't Brother Anselm joining you?",["Brother Anselm has his own view of what Augustus should be, and it isn't what I'd have him be.",[
    ["What does Anselm want?","That the boy take vows, and leave the crown to the sinners. Brother Anselm thinks holiness is wasted on a throne. I think a throne is where it's most needed."],
    ["So the Church is divided?","The Church is two old men who agree about God and disagree about Augustus. That's not a schism. That's a Tuesday."]]]]],
 "Pray for the boy. Whatever he chooses, he'll choose it alone."],
16:["(Father Thyme has a small radio on his table, turned low.) The Communists and the Democrats are debating on the wireless. Anna and Erik. I'm listening the way one listens to a storm. With interest, and from indoors.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Erik has more jokes and Anna has more facts. The island will decide which it prefers. I suspect jokes; they keep longer."],
  ["What does the Church make of a vote?","We've had a king for six hundred years, and a vote for none. I'm not against it. I'm only old enough to know that a crowd can be wrong as loudly as a king."],
  ["Turn it off. Have some peace.","(He does, and sits back.) Ah. You're right. God is in the silence, and the Party is in the wireless. I'll take the silence."]],
 "Go gently. Everyone is shouting today."],
17:["(A cup rattles on its saucer, and Father Thyme puts his hand on it until it stops.) Did you feel that? The mountain turned over in its sleep.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Brother Ilja says the chronicles record a waking, two hundred years ago. A year of grey snow, he says, and no king. History rhymes, he likes to tell me. I wish it wouldn't."],
  ["Are the brothers frightened?","Brother Teodor is cooking twice as much. Brother Mikael is singing louder. Brother Anselm says it is a judgement. On whom, he hasn't said, but he looked at me."],
  ["Shouldn't you come down the mountain?","My child, I have been on this mountain forty-three years. If it wants me, it knows where I am."]],
 "Walk carefully. The ground has opinions now."],
18:["(Father Thyme is standing before an empty space on the altar, very still.) We are missing something precious, my friend. Something that has sat in this church for longer than there have been kings.",[
  ["What happened?","It was there at vespers and gone at matins. Brother Anselm has counted every candle twice, and accused every mouse by name."],
  ["Who would take a relic?","Someone who wants something. A collector, a thief, a man with a point to make. On this island, this month, that is everyone."],
  ["Is it worth a great deal?","In money? A little gold and some very old bone. In what it means to the people of this island? I couldn't name the sum, and I wouldn't want to meet the man who could."]],
 "If you hear anything down in the towns, you'll tell an old man?"],
19:["(Steam rises from the peak behind the monastery, thin and white against the sky.) The mountain is breathing. Brother Paavo says the soil in the garden is warm to the elbow. In September.",[
  ["Should you leave?","Leave? The monastery has stood here since before the first king. If God wants it moved, He'll send a better sign than steam."],
  ["How's the chess going?","Badly. Augustus has my bishop pinned, and he wrote 'Sorry' beside the move. I shall have to lose to him with some dignity."],
  ["What do the chronicles say about steam?","That it came before the grey snow. Brother Ilja has stopped reading me the chronicles. I asked him to. I wish I hadn't."]],
 "Keep one eye on the peak, if you're up here again."],
20:["A Russian came up the mountain again this morning. The one with the expensive shoes, from the Hotel Aurora. He brought me a small icon as a gift. Very old, very beautiful. And very far from home.",[
  ["Did you keep it?","I have it in the cupboard, under a cloth, while I decide whether it was a gift or a key. Russians are like that: generous, and always with a door in mind."],
  ["What did he want?","To be my friend, he said. I have enough friends. I told him so, and offered him tea. He drank it very fast, which is how I know he wasn't from the Church."],
  ["Will you tell Brother Anselm?","Brother Anselm saw him from the bell tower before he'd reached the gate. Nothing comes up this mountain that Anselm hasn't seen first."]],
 "Go carefully in the towns. Everyone wants a friend today."],
21:["(Ash is falling like grey snow. Father Thyme has a lantern in one hand and a list of names in the other.) Every brother accounted for. Every one. Thank God. Thank God.",[
  ["Are you all right, Father?","My knees are shaking and my voice is gone again, but I'm here, and so are they. The mountain has made its speech, and nobody died of it. That's a better speech than most."],
  ["Is the monastery safe?","The lava's running north, away from us, down into the empty valley. Brother Anselm says it's a miracle. I say it's the slope. We've agreed to say both."],
  ["What can I do?","Go down the mountain while the road's clear, and tell them in Kingsgrad we're all alive. And then come back, if you like. Brother Teodor is making soup for an army."]],
 "Go now, while the road is open. God keep you."],
22:["(Father Thyme is sweeping ash off the church steps with a broom taller than he is.) The grey snow. Brother Ilja was right. Two hundred years, and it's come back to sit on our steps.",[
  ["Let me help with that.","(He hands you the broom, and sits down at once.) Bless you. I've been a monk long enough to know that sweeping is prayer, and old enough to want someone else to pray for a while."],
  ["How are the farmers?","I went down to East Farm at first light. Hilda's cows are off their milk and Olaf is off his temper. I blessed the fields. Olaf asked if I could bless the price of diesel too."],
  ["Is it a sign, Father?","It's ash. Everything is a sign, if you want it to be. This one says the mountain is alive. So are we."]],
 "Keep the broom, if you like. There's ash everywhere you're going."],
23:["(There's a new crack in the plaster above the church door, and Father Thyme is looking up at it.) The aftershock in the night. The church has a new wrinkle. We match now.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Brother Anselm thinks so, and wants it sealed with a prayer and a new icon over it. Brother Paavo says plaster would do. I've asked for both."],
  ["How are you sleeping?","In my chair, with my boots on. When the floor moves, I'd like to be dressed for it. The king would have laughed at that. He slept through his own coronation rehearsal."],
  ["Any word from Augustus?","A letter, this morning, asking if we're safe and whether my rook is still alive. Both, I told him. Neither of us is sure for how long."]],
 "Mind the door frame. It's had a fright."],
24:["(Father Thyme is waving off a line of monks with walking sticks and packs.) The brothers are off on retreat to the peaks, with Brother Anselm at the front setting a pace. I'm staying to mind the house. My knees made the decision for me.",[
  ["Isn't it dangerous, after the eruption?","They're going up the south ridge, away from the smoke. Brother Anselm has walked it every year for twenty years. He knows every stone by its first name."],
  ["What's the retreat for?","Silence, mostly. Up there a man hears what he's really been thinking. That's why half of them dread it."],
  ["You'll have the place to yourself.","Brother Teodor has left me eleven loaves and instructions for the soup. I shall eat the loaves and ignore the instructions. It's the closest I come to rebellion."]],
 "Go on. They'll be glad of a straggler to talk to."],
25:["Princess Daphne is all over the wireless today. Everyone wants her for something. I christened that girl, you know. She bit my finger in the font.",[
  ["What was she like as a child?","Fierce, curious and impossible to keep in a pew. She once asked me during a sermon whether God got bored. I've never answered her properly."],
  ["Is she on the Church's side?","Daphne's on Daphne's side, which is more than most people on this island can say of themselves. I respect it, and I pray for her, at some length."],
  ["Does God get bored?","(He laughs, which turns into a cough.) Not with this island. Not this month."]],
 "If you see the princess, tell her I still have the scar."],
26:["(Father Thyme is holding a bottle of Kelp Cola up to the light.) I hear Phil wants a prince's face on every bottle. I don't know whether to drink the thing or light a candle to it.",[
  ["What do you make of it?","Six hundred years of crowns, and now a king you can buy for a few coins and throw in the bin. Phil is a clever man. I'm not sure that's a compliment."],
  ["Is Augustus on a bottle too?","Not yet. Augustus would rather be on a lamppost. He'd be on the label apologising."],
  ["And Diversified Petroleum's buying the spa, I hear.","Dario wants to own the hot water now. Next he'll be selling us the snow. Forgive me, that was uncharitable. True, but uncharitable."]],
 "Take the bottle. I'd rather not drink a family I've baptised."],
27:["(The chessboard is out. Father Thyme moves a piece, then moves it back.) Augustus is three moves from mate. I can see it, and he can see it, and his last letter asks after my health in a very sorry hand.",[
  ["Will you resign?","I'll play it out. You don't stop a game because you're losing. You stop it when it's over. There's a sermon in that, if I had the voice."],
  ["Do you think he'll be king?","I think he'll be what God and the island make of him, and I think he'll apologise either way. That's all I'll say, even to a reporter. Especially to a reporter."],
  ["Teach me the game sometime.","Come back in the winter, when the island has settled. Long nights and a short board. It's the best way I know to find out who someone is."]],
 "Go on. The board will still be here."],
28:["(The church smells of smoke and summer: bundles of dried herbs hang from every beam.) The Herbal Rite. Once a year we burn the mountain's herbs and give thanks for whatever grew. This year I'm giving thanks that anything did.",[
  ["Is there thyme in it?","Of course. Brother Paavo puts in an extra bunch every year, to tease me. He thinks I don't notice. I notice. I'm touched."],
  ["What is the rite for?","For remembering that the mountain feeds us. The kings came and went, and the herbs came back every spring. That's the Church's whole argument, in a bundle of twigs."],
  ["Everyone else is out pushing for the crown today.","Let them push. We're burning sage. In a week, I'll ask you which of us did more good."]],
 "Breathe deeply on your way out. It'll do you good."],
29:["(Father Thyme has a very old book open on his table, and Brother Ilja's handwriting on the slips between its pages.) The line of succession. Everyone on the island suddenly wants to know who is next in line, and who was before, and who was forgotten.",[
  ["Is there a doubt about it?","There's always a doubt about it. Every king for six hundred years had a cousin who thought it should have been him. The Church kept all their names. That's what the Church is for."],
  ["What has Brother Ilja found?","Brother Ilja has found that the more you read, the less certain you are. He's a scholar. It's his whole trade."],
  ["Who would you crown, Father?","Whoever bows his head first. I've said it for years, and I haven't found a better answer."]],
 "Tomorrow is the last day. Get some sleep, if anyone can."],
30:["(Father Thyme is standing at the monastery gate, looking down at the island under its grey dust.) The last day. Whatever is decided down there, I'll ring the bell for it, and the brothers will sing, and the mountain will go on being a mountain.",[
  ["Are you afraid, Father?","A little. Not for the Church. For the people at the bottom of the hill, who'll have to live with it when the speeches are over."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Say matins, sweep the steps, write to Augustus. Lose at chess. A monk's tomorrow is very like his yesterday. That's rather the point."],
  ["Thank you for everything, Father.","Thank you, my child. You came up a mountain for a story, and you kept coming back. Whatever you write, write it kindly."]],
 "God keep you. And the island. And whoever wears the crown."],
};
// Frank, owner of the gun shop in Kingsgrad (Republican Party). Threads: the elk season and his old dog Duke, his war with
// Bertil's licence forms, the Republican race (no real Republican standing), his cabin in the north once the ground starts shaking.
DAILY['Frank']={
2:["(Frank looks up from a rifle he's oiling.) Road's open and the first one through my door is a reporter. Figures. What can I do for you?",[
  ["Just saying hello. I'm new in Kingsgrad.","Then hello. Frank. Guns, ammunition, and advice nobody asks for. The advice is free."],
  ["What does a gun shop owner make of the king's death?","He was a decent shot, for a king. Came in here once, in sixty-eight, bought a duck gun and paid cash. That's more than most governments do."],
  ["Are all these guns legal?","Every one of them, and I've got the paperwork to prove it. Three drawers of it. That's what's wrong with this island, right there."]],
 "Mind the step. It's iced over, and I'm not paying for your hip."],
3:["(A grey dog is asleep on a sack by the stove.) Don't mind Duke. He's fourteen and he's earned the sack.",[
  ["What a lovely old dog.","Best elk dog on the island, once. Now he points at the stove and waits for it to fall over."],
  ["Do you hunt much?","Every October, up north in the pines. Elk season opens on the first. Twenty-six days and counting, not that I'm counting."],
  ["Does he bite?","Only the post. And the man from the Civil Service Bureau, but that was a misunderstanding. Mostly."]],
 "Don't step on Duke on your way out. He won't move."],
4:["You again. Either you're writing about guns, or you've nowhere warm to stand.",[
  ["Somewhere warm to stand, mostly.","Honest. I like that. Stand by the stove, then, but don't touch the shelf with the red tins."],
  ["What's in the red tins?","Powder. Black powder, for the old muskets the palace guard uses on parades. I'm the only one who still stocks it. Nobody else wants the paperwork."],
  ["Who do you sell to, mostly?","Farmers, for the foxes. Fishermen, for the seals. And a man from Hope Springs who buys more than any one man should. I don't ask. That's the deal."]],
 "Close the door behind you. Cold gets in the barrels."],
5:["(Frank is filling in a form with his tongue between his teeth.) Bertil at the Civil Service Bureau wants every box of cartridges written down. Every box. In triplicate.",[
  ["That sounds like a lot of boxes.","It is a lot of boxes. That's the point of a gun shop. You don't see Lisa at the supermarket writing down every loaf."],
  ["Is Bertil being difficult on purpose?","Bertil's never done anything on purpose in his life. He just does what the form says. That's worse."],
  ["Why not just ignore him?","Because then Sergeant Hanna comes round with her notebook and counts my crates herself. She's slower than the form, and she has opinions."]],
 "If you see Bertil, tell him the forms are coming. Slowly."],
6:["You hear about this Republican business? Three men standing for leader, and not one of them's a Republican.",[
  ["Who's standing?","A cola man, an oil man and a fellow in a suit nobody can say what he does. Harald, Magnus and Carlo. I've seen better choices on a menu."],
  ["What makes a real Republican, then?","Leave a man alone. Low taxes, no busybodies, and a rifle over the door. These three want to run the island like a company. I don't work for a company."],
  ["Will you vote?","On the twenty-ninth, at the party HQ in Frostholm. Margit's running it. She'll want my membership card, and I'll have to find it. It's under Duke."]],
 "Vote's not till the twenty-ninth. Plenty of time to be disappointed."],
7:["(Frank is counting rounds into a box.) Frostholm's open today. Means every oil man on the island can drive down and tell me what I'm doing wrong.",[
  ["Do the oil men buy guns?","Shotguns for the company shoot, and they want them engraved. I don't do engraving. I've got a man in Saltwick who does, and he drinks."],
  ["You sound like you don't like Frostholm.","Glass towers and neon. You can't see the stars up there. A man should be able to see the stars before he shoots something."],
  ["How much ammunition do you keep, exactly?","Enough. (He closes the box.) More than the island needs, Hanna says. I say the island doesn't know what it needs yet."]],
 "Go and look at the towers, then. Come back when you miss the trees."],
8:["You hear about the oil rig? Something burst out there. Black water, they're saying in the bar.",[
  ["Who's saying that?","Eddie at the City Bar, and Eddie hears everything first and gets half of it wrong. But there was a smell on the wind this morning. Not fish."],
  ["What would oil do to the hunting?","To the elk, nothing. To the ducks on the shore, plenty. And the seals. Fishermen won't miss the seals, but they'll miss the fish."],
  ["Did you go to church to hear the sermon?","I listened on the radio with Duke. Duke snored. I thought it was decent. Short, too. A short sermon's a kindness."]],
 "If it's oil on the water, there'll be no ducks this year. Shame."],
9:["(Frank has an elk call on the counter and is blowing into it softly.) Practising. Don't look at me like that.",[
  ["Does that really sound like an elk?","To an elk, it sounds like a very confused elk. Confused is enough. They come to see what's wrong with him."],
  ["What's the plan for the season?","Up to the cabin, past the lake, on the first. Two weeks in the pines with Duke, a stove and a tin of beans a day. Nobody, no forms."],
  ["Can I have a go?",["(He hands it over. You blow. It sounds like a sick goose.) Well. That'll bring something out of the woods. Probably the army.",[
    ["Is it that bad?","Colonel Halvorsen's men would come and arrest it. Keep the day job."],
    ["Let me try again.","No. Duke's put his ears back. Give it here."]]]]],
 "Funeral tomorrow. I'll be closed. Out of respect, and because Bertil's office is too."],
10:["(Frank is in a black tie, which looks wrong on him.) Shop's shut, but you can stand there. I just came back for the dog.",[
  ["You went to the funeral?","Stood outside the palace with the rest of Kingsgrad. Didn't get into the chapel. The likes of me get the pavement, and the pavement suited me fine."],
  ["What was the king like, really?","Fair. Didn't bother anyone, didn't tax the guns. Shot a duck badly in sixty-eight and laughed about it. You can't ask more of a king."],
  ["The tie suits you.","It doesn't, and you know it doesn't. I've had it since my father's funeral. It'll do for mine."]],
 "Go on. I want to sit with Duke a while."],
11:["Business is up. Funny how a dead king does that. People buy guns when they don't know who's next.",[
  ["Who's buying?","Shopkeepers. Mary from the book shop came in yesterday and asked what the smallest one was. Mary! She bought nothing. Just wanted to know."],
  ["Is that good or bad?","Good for the till. Bad for the island. I'd sooner sell to hunters than to frightened people. Frightened people point things at the wrong end."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Me? I've got four hundred boxes of cartridges and a dog with no teeth. I'm the safest man in Kingsgrad."]],
 "Don't let anyone tell you a gun makes you brave. It just makes you loud."],
12:["(Frank holds up a form.) Bertil's sent it back. I wrote the numbers too close to the lines.",[
  ["Too close to the lines?","'Figures must be clearly inside the box.' His words. My figures were in the box. They were just friendly with the edge."],
  ["What will you do?","Write it again, bigger. And then take it to him myself, so I can watch him read it. He hates being watched. It's the only fun I get."],
  ["Maybe you should just write neatly.","I've written neatly for forty years. Bertil's twenty-nine. He was in nappies when I was filling in his father's forms."]],
 "If you're going past the Bureau, tell Bertil I'm coming. Let him sweat."],
13:["They're giving away Kelp Cola on every corner. Some fellow tried to give me one. I asked him if it came with a cartridge.",[
  ["Did you drink it?","Gave it to Duke. He licked the top and went back to sleep. That's the most honest review it'll ever get."],
  ["Is this Harald's doing?","Harald's company, Harald's money. Free cola for everyone, and a vote for Harald on the twenty-ninth. That's not a gift, that's a down payment."],
  ["You don't like free things?","Nothing's free. Someone always pays. Usually the man who drank it, and usually later."]],
 "You want my cola? Take it. I won't miss it."],
14:["(Frank is locking up.) Big day. The whole island in one place. You'll want to be there with your notebook.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'll go. I'll stand at the back with my hands in my pockets and see who's shouting loudest. That's how you know who's scared."],
  ["What do you expect to happen?","Speeches. Too many. Somebody'll promise something, and somebody'll believe it. I just hope nobody does anything stupid."],
  ["Should I be worried?","You? You're a reporter. You write it down and go home to London. It's us who have to live with it."]],
 "Keep your head down and your notebook up."],
15:["People are saying the Church and the palace are falling out. Augustus wants the cross on top, Leopold wants the crown.",[
  ["Which would you pick?","Neither on top of me. A crown's a hat and a cross is two sticks. Let them squabble in the chapel and leave the gun shop out of it."],
  ["Who told you about it?","Edmund, the palace butler, came in for a shotgun cartridge for the old king's collection. Can't keep a thing to himself, that man, once he's had a sherry."],
  ["Doesn't the Church own half the north?","Most of the woods past the monastery. I've hunted there forty years. If Father Thyme starts charging for the elk, then I'll have a view."]],
 "Two princes and one chair. Somebody's going to stand."],
16:["(Frank has the radio on low.) They're debating. Communists and Democrats. I've been listening for an hour and nobody's said the word 'gun' yet.",[
  ["What do you make of Anna?","She's sharp. I'd not want to argue with her about rabbits, never mind the Soviet Union. But she wants everything shared, and I don't share my rifle."],
  ["And Erik?","Erik wants a vote. Fine. I'd vote. Then I'd want them to leave me alone till the next one. Nobody ever promises that."],
  ["Turn it off, then.","Can't. It's the best entertainment since Otto got that flying picture. Better jokes, too."]],
 "If either of them says 'gun', ring me."],
17:["(A box rattles on the shelf. Frank steadies it with one hand.) Did you feel that? Second one today.",[
  ["Was that an earthquake?","Ground's shifting. My father said it did that in the fifties, before the mountain smoked. I didn't believe him. I'm starting to."],
  ["Is your cabin safe?","It's up north, under the mountain. Four walls, a stove and my good rifle in a cupboard. If the ground opens up, the elk won't be the only thing running."],
  ["Heard anything about Dario?","Only that nobody can find him. Rich man goes missing, everybody looks. Poor man goes missing, nobody notices till the rent's due."]],
 "Don't stand under the shelf with the red tins. Not today."],
18:["Duke wouldn't come out from under the counter this morning. Animals know before we do.",[
  ["Know what?","Whatever the mountain's planning. You see steam up north on a clear day now. Not cloud. Steam. And Duke's been off his food."],
  ["Is he all right?","He's old and he's scared. So am I, a little. (He scratches the dog's ears.) We'll be scared together, the two of us. It's cheaper."],
  ["Should people be leaving the north?","Farmers up there are already talking about it. Hilda at East Farm asked me if guns could stop lava. I told her no. She didn't laugh."]],
 "If the ground shakes, get outside. Not under the counter. That's Duke's."],
19:["(Frank is reading a letter and frowning.) The party's sent me a ballot paper. Harald, Magnus or Carlo. Tick one.",[
  ["Who'll you tick?","I've been trying to choose the least bad one for three days. It's like choosing the least wet fish."],
  ["What's wrong with Carlo?","Carlo came in last week. Lovely suit. Promised 'safer streets'. Then two men who came with him stood by my door and didn't buy anything. I know what safe looks like. That wasn't it."],
  ["Can you leave it blank?",["Margit says a blank paper's 'spoilt'. Her word. As if I'd let it go off in the cupboard.",[
    ["Would you spoil it?","I might write 'Duke' on it. He's honest, he doesn't take money from cola men, and he's never once filled in a form."],
    ["Would Margit mind?","Margit minds everything. That's her job, and she's good at it. I'd feel bad. Not bad enough to vote for an oil man, mind."]]]]],
 "Twenty-ninth. Ten days to make up my mind. It'll take eleven."],
20:["(The rifles on the rack tick against each other.) Another one. The rack's never done that, not once in thirty years.",[
  ["Are you going to close up?","Close up and do what? Sit at home and watch my teacups dance? I'll stay open. Somebody might need a torch."],
  ["Do you sell torches?","I do now. Bought a box off Nils in Frostholm. He was selling them like hot cakes, and talking about the government listening to the earthquake."],
  ["What would you do if it erupts?","Get Duke in the truck, fill the tank, drive south. Then sit on the beach at Hope Springs with the rich people and watch it. Best seat on the island."]],
 "Keep a torch by the bed. Go on, I'll sell you one."],
21:["(Ash is falling past the window like grey snow.) Well. That's it, then. The mountain's gone up.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Duke's fine. He's under the counter and he's not coming out till Christmas. I can't say I blame him."],
  ["What about your cabin?","Up north, under it all. I'll not know till the ash stops. Might be standing. Might be a very hot hole. (He looks out.) Forty years I've had that cabin."],
  ["What are people saying?","That nobody's hurt, thank God. Doctor Lind's been up and down the street with a bag. And Hanna's telling everyone to stay indoors, which nobody is."]],
 "Cover your mouth with a scarf if you go out. That stuff gets in your chest."],
22:["(Frank is sweeping ash off the counter with a paintbrush.) It's in everything. The barrels, the bolts, the cartridge boxes. I'll be cleaning guns till the spring.",[
  ["Can I help?","Hold that rifle steady. No, not like a broom. Like it's going to bite you. There. You'd make a terrible hunter."],
  ["Any news of the cabin?","A farmer came down from up north. Said the road's grey as a gravestone but the woods by the lake are still standing. My cabin's past the lake. So, maybe."],
  ["Is the elk season off?","The elk don't care about ash. They'll be hungry and they'll be cross. It'll be a hard season, if it's a season at all."]],
 "Brush your boots before you come in. I've only just done the floor."],
23:["Margit telephoned. Wants to know if I'll still be coming to Frostholm on the twenty-ninth to vote. Volcano or no volcano.",[
  ["Will you?","Of course I will. A man doesn't skip a vote because a mountain coughed. I'd look a fool in front of Jack."],
  ["Has anyone tried to buy your vote?","A man from Diversified Petroleum offered to fill my truck for free. A crate of Kelp Cola turned up on my step. And Carlo's friends said nothing at all, which is worse."],
  ["Why does it matter so much?","Because whoever wins says what the Republicans stand for. And if it's a company man, we stand for the company. I didn't join for that."]],
 "Twenty-ninth. Frostholm. If the roads are clear of ash."],
24:["(Duke is out from under the counter, eating something.) Look at that. He's eating. The world's back to normal.",[
  ["What's he eating?","A sausage Rosa gave me from her cart outside Kelp Cola HQ. She said it was for me. Duke disagreed."],
  ["Is the ash clearing?","Slowly. The trams are running again. Sergeant Hanna had half of Kingsgrad out with shovels. She's wasted on a desk."],
  ["You seem cheerful today.","The dog's eating, the ground's still, and Bertil's office is shut for the ash. That's three good things. I'll take them."]],
 "Good day. Don't spoil it."],
25:["Jack from the fishing shop says he's voting for whoever promises to cut the harbour tax. I said, Jack, they all promise that.",[
  ["What did Jack say?","He said, 'Then I'll vote for whoever means it.' Jack's an optimist. He'd buy a boat with a hole in it if the seller smiled."],
  ["Who's actually promising what?","Harald says jobs. Magnus says oil. Carlo says safety. None of them says 'leave Frank alone'. I've been listening for it."],
  ["Are you two friends?","Forty years. We argue about everything. Bait, guns, the weather, women. He's wrong every time. I'd miss him if he left."]],
 "Tell Jack I said he's wrong. He'll know about what."],
26:["(Frank has a map of the north spread on the counter.) Somebody up the road saw my cabin. Still standing. Ash to the windowsills, but standing.",[
  ["That's wonderful news.","It is. (He smooths the map.) I'll go up in October and dig it out. Duke can supervise. He's good at that."],
  ["Who saw it?","Viktor, the groundskeeper at the palace. Drove up to see the lava. Says it's stopped, and it's black as a stove now, and still smoking."],
  ["Will you still hunt there?","Elk don't read newspapers. They'll still be there. They'll just be grey."]],
 "Five days to October. I'm counting again."],
27:["People are saying the Syndicate's holding a card game in the pizzeria tonight. Big money. 'Everyone who matters', they say.",[
  ["Are you going?","I don't play cards with men who come with friends. And I don't 'matter'. That suits me down to the ground."],
  ["Who'll be there?","Rosaria's back room. Gustavo, I'd guess, and every man who wants a favour from him before the vote. Carlo, certainly. Cards are how they talk."],
  ["Do you play poker?","Gin rummy, with Jack, on Thursdays. For matches. He cheats and I let him. That's friendship."]],
 "If you play with Gustavo, lose politely. That's my advice."],
28:["(Frank is standing at the window, watching the street.) Everybody's out. Flags, speeches, posters. Every faction on the island all at once.",[
  ["What's going on out there?","Everyone's pushing as hard as they can before the end. A rally in Eastbrook, a king coming from abroad, more cola. I'm keeping the shop shut and the dog inside."],
  ["Doesn't it excite you?","It frightens me. Too many people wanting the same thing on the same day. That's how you get a stampede."],
  ["Are you worried about trouble?","I've sold more locks this week than guns. That tells you something. People want to keep things out, not go out shooting. That's sensible."]],
 "Stay off the main street today. Too many people with something to prove."],
29:["Today's the vote. (He holds up his membership card.) Found it. Under Duke, like I said.",[
  ["Who are you going to vote for?","I'll tell you when I've done it, and maybe not then. The paper's private. That's the only thing about this race that is."],
  ["What will happen if Carlo wins?","Then the Republicans are the Syndicate with a badge. And I'll tear up the card and give it back to Duke to sleep on."],
  ["Good luck.","Luck's got nothing to do with it. Margit counts every paper twice. I trust her. She's the only one in that hall I do."]],
 "Off to Frostholm. If I'm not back by dark, feed the dog."],
30:["(Frank is sitting on the step with Duke.) Last day. Whatever happens to the island, it happens today.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Tired. Like the end of a long hunt, when you've been sitting in the cold all day and you just want to know if anything's coming."],
  ["What do you hope for?","An island where a man can hunt, sell what he likes and be left alone. Whoever gives me that, they can have the palace and the cola and all."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Pack the truck. Check the rifle. Load Duke in the front seat. October the first, and the elk don't care who won."]],
 "Whatever happens, the season opens tomorrow."],
};
// Freja, owner of Nordhem Furniture in Kingsgrad (no faction). Threads: where a sofa should go, fireplaces and the coming cold, a cranky delivery lorry, the one armchair nobody buys, neighbours in Kingsgrad Apartments, the ash.
DAILY['Freja']={
2:["(Freja is dragging a sofa three inches to the left and standing back to look.) No. Back again. Sorry, you caught me mid-argument. You're the reporter. Does the light fall right on this, do you think?",[
  ["Looks fine to me.","Fine is how rooms die. It wants to face the window, not the door. People always put a sofa where it's convenient."],
  ["What's business like with the king gone?","Odd. People are buying dark wood and plain lamps. Nobody wants to look cheerful in a house this week."],
  ["Who buys a fireplace in September?","The sensible ones. The snow came early, and people have only just dug out. I've sold six since yesterday."]],
 "Mind the lamps by the door. They're delicate and so am I."],
3:["(Freja is pinning a card to a bedside table.) Sold: Mrs Berg, Saltwick. Delivery pending a lorry that's been in a ditch since Tuesday. The island's roads are my enemy.",[
  ["What's wrong with the lorry?","Everything. It's got a mind of its own and an opinion on gravel. I've called it Gustav. It doesn't care."],
  ["Do you deliver all over the island?","Anywhere the road goes. Beds to Eastbrook, sofas to Frostholm, a wardrobe to the palace once. They sent it back for being too cheerful."],
  ["Do you live above the shop?","Kingsgrad Apartments, behind the Bureau. Third floor. My own flat is a disgrace, so don't come round."]],
 "If you see a lorry in a ditch, wave. It's mine."],
4:["There's one armchair in this shop that nobody's bought in three years. (She pats a mustard-yellow chair.) I won't say I love it, but I've started defending it.",[
  ["What's wrong with it?","Nothing. It's just yellow, in a country where people love grey. It's the Eastbrook of armchairs."],
  ["Would you sell it to me?","You're a reporter, you'll be living out of a suitcase. It would look ridiculous in a hotel."],
  ["Who'll end up with it?","Somebody with a sense of humour. I've been waiting for them since 1981."]],
 "Sit in it if you like. It's good for the conscience."],
5:["(Freja is on her knees at the back, hunting for a screw.) Do you know how many screws a wardrobe takes? Two hundred and four. I've got two hundred and three. Don't tell the customer.",[
  ["Can I help look?","If you see something small, shiny and guilty, that's it. It's usually under the one thing I can't move."],
  ["How do you keep the shop so tidy?","I don't. I rearrange it every morning to look tidy. It's a very different skill."],
  ["Have you ever furnished a famous house?",["Once, a villa in Hope Springs. A gentleman wanted a very long sofa, very low, and the curtains drawn. I didn't ask why.",[
    ["Who was he?","A Mr Gustavo's man. He paid in cash and didn't want a receipt. I gave him one anyway, and a very firm look."],
    ["Did he like the sofa?","He said it was perfect. Then he sat down and put his feet up. In the end, that's all a sofa is for."]]]]],
 "If you find the screw, keep it. It's earned its freedom."],
6:["(Freja is writing in a ledger, tutting.) Everyone wants beds this week. People who've been snowed in for days have apparently decided their mattress is the villain.",[
  ["What do people say about the snow?","That it's passing. They said that in 1969. We had five feet of it in October."],
  ["Is the Royal Palace buying anything?","They never do. They think a chair's something you inherit. Imagine being that sure of your sofa."],
  ["Which room matters most?","The kitchen, and the bed. The rest is decoration. But people will pay for decoration. That's where the profit is."]],
 "Tell your editor Nordhem delivers. Slowly, but eventually."],
7:["Every road's open, and suddenly everyone has a spare room to fill. (Freja throws her arms wide.) It's like the thaw has done my marketing for me.",[
  ["Is it a good week for furniture?","The best since the old king's coronation, when the whole island bought a throne-shaped armchair. I still have three."],
  ["Do you get customers from Frostholm?","The oil people, yes. They buy glass tables and no chairs. I've never understood it."],
  ["What's the secret of a good room?","Leave space. People fill rooms like they fill silences. Both are bad ideas."]],
 "If you're moving, call me. If you're not moving, call me anyway."],
8:["(Freja has switched off the radio, which was playing a sermon.) Sundays. People think of their souls, and then they think they should have a better bed.",[
  ["Is the shop open on Sundays?","In theory. I shut for the sermon and then for lunch. Then, it turns out, it's four."],
  ["Does the monastery buy furniture?","Beds, plain ones. The monks sleep like children. Sensible men, with no mattress opinions."],
  ["Do you go to church?","At Christmas, for the smell of the wax. It does the shop good to see something done properly."]],
 "Sunday's a day for sofas. Not for speeches."],
9:["(Freja has a bottle of cola on the desk and looks at it as at a rival.) Someone left it. The labels are all different this week, as if they've lost the recipe. I'm told the Party wants its own.",[
  ["Do you like cola?","In moderation, and never on a cream sofa. It's what I tell every customer: no drinks in the living room."],
  ["Who's making a new cola?","Everyone, apparently. A cola for the Party, a cola for the company. Half the island will be sticky by Friday."],
  ["Would you stock a cola table?","A table with a bottle-shaped hole in it? Don't tempt me."]],
 "Coasters. That's the whole of my advice."],
10:["(Freja has closed the shop with a small black card in the window.) I thought it only right. A funeral. He sat in my shop once, did you know? On the yellow chair, for ten minutes. He said it was 'bold'.",[
  ["The king sat on the yellow chair?","In 1971, on a tour. He was so polite I thought he'd buy it out of kindness. He didn't, so I'm glad I'm not a sentimental woman."],
  ["How are you marking the day?","I'm sitting in the shop with the lights off and a cup of tea. It seems about right."],
  ["What will happen to the palace furniture?","The new owner will probably want it changed. A new king always wants a new chair."]],
 "I'll open again at two. The shop deserves a moment."],
11:["The mill hands have been in, asking about work benches. Not for the mill: for themselves. There's talk of a fair day's pay, and they'd like something to sit at when it arrives.",[
  ["Are they that optimistic?","Workers always are, the day before. It's the day after that tells you."],
  ["Do the Democrats shop here?","Erik bought a desk. Said it was for the party. It's the only furniture on the island with a motto carved in it."],
  ["Is there money for furniture in Eastbrook?","A little. People there buy things they can mend. Good sense, if not good margins."]],
 "I'll give a discount on a desk if the vote ever comes."],
12:["(Freja is polishing a table with great intensity.) Do you know a man in a fur collar wanted a desk, the biggest one I have. For an 'accounts department'. He wanted it by Friday.",[
  ["Who was he?","Oil. Frostholm. He paid in advance. I don't like the way some people pay in advance. It feels like a threat."],
  ["Did you sell it?","I did. I'm a shopkeeper, not a judge. But I charged him for the delivery, the leg-lifting and my opinion."],
  ["Is it good for business, the oil?","Very good. Not for the soul. The two never go together on this island."]],
 "Every desk tells a story. Some of them are accounts."],
13:["(Freja is trying a sofa out and bouncing a little.) A cola company has been wanting beds for their 'brand ambassadors'. I said I don't sell to people who call themselves ambassadors.",[
  ["Did they pay well?","They offered a hundred sofas for free advertising. I said I'd think about it. I thought: no."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola taking over Kingsgrad?","In a way. They have the biggest building and the loudest tram. I just have the best chairs."],
  ["Which would you rather, a hundred sofas or one good order?","One good order. A hundred sofas is a warehouse. A good order is a relationship."]],
 "I'll have the yellow chair out front tomorrow. For luck."],
14:["(Freja's shop is empty and she's sitting very straight in an armchair.) Everyone's gone off to the big day. I thought I'd stay and hold the fort. Someone has to guard the furniture.",[
  ["Aren't you curious?","Terribly. But if I leave, I'll miss the one customer who didn't go."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","A lot of speeches and one fight. And sandwiches. I'm only hoping for the sandwiches."],
  ["Will it change anything?","Rooms change when you move a lamp. Countries change slower. But the lamp's a start."]],
 "Tell me everything tomorrow. Leave out the speeches."],
15:["(Freja is dusting a crucifix-shaped mirror, which she's decided is for sale.) Crown before Cross. Cross before Crown. I had a customer this morning who wanted a throne with a cross carved on the back. I said: 'Which one's on top?'",[
  ["What did he say?","He didn't say. He just frowned like a man who's been asked to pick a favourite child."],
  ["Is furniture political?","Always. Where you put the chair says who's important. I've seen a divorce begin over a sofa."],
  ["Who do you favour?","The one who buys a sofa and pays on time."]],
 "If you meet a prince, tell him I do thrones. Free delivery."],
16:["(Freja has the radio on, the debate rumbling softly.) I'm trying to ignore it and failing. Every speaker is sitting on a chair I'd have sold them better.",[
  ["Which side has the better chairs?","The Democrats' are folding. The Communists' are metal. Neither has an armchair. That tells you everything."],
  ["Do you listen to debates?","In the evenings, with knitting. It helps if you've something in your hands."],
  ["What would you ask them?","Where do they think people will sit down when they're done?"]],
 "I'll be here when they run out of things to say."],
17:["(A floor lamp trembles on its base and Freja steadies it.) The ground. Again. My china cabinet rattles like it's got something to confess.",[
  ["Are you worried?","About the cabinet, yes. About the island, I'm too busy being worried about the cabinet."],
  ["What's causing it?","The mountain's clearing its throat, the old fishermen say. I think it's heard too much politics."],
  ["Have you lost anything?","A vase. And Gustav the lorry's finally given up. Now it's the road and the ground against me."]],
 "Don't stand near the glass. It's the first thing to go."],
18:["(Freja has pushed all the lamps to the middle of the floor.) Safer. If the shelves go, I'd rather lose a lamp than a customer. Although in this economy, I'm not sure which is rarer.",[
  ["Is the shaking getting worse?","Daily. A little stronger each time. It's the kind of thing you get used to and then hate yourself for getting used to."],
  ["What are people buying now?","Sturdy things. Low tables. Beds that sit on the floor. Nobody wants to fall far."],
  ["Have you got a plan?","Tea, and a very solid table to hide under. It's from the Nordhem range."]],
 "If you want to sit down, do it near the door."],
19:["I've been asked to supply chairs for something called a 'Midnight Note'. A club, a very smart one, very small. Do you know it?",[
  ["The Midnight Note's in Frostholm.","So Sanna tells me. She says it's all velvet and smoke. I only deal in what's under the velvet."],
  ["Are you going?","I'd like to see my chairs in a dark room. They'd be most photogenic."],
  ["Who ordered them?","A man with a nice voice who didn't leave a name. I'm not sure whether to be flattered or alarmed."]],
 "If anyone asks, I'm a furniture woman, not a spy."],
20:["(Freja is wiping a table with an unusual amount of aggression.) There was a man in the shop yesterday asking about a bed. Not for him. For a hotel. The Aurora. They want twenty, all alike.",[
  ["The Hotel Aurora?","In Kingsgrad, yes. Plain beds and very good locks. I only supply the beds. The locks are their affair."],
  ["Is the hotel busy?","Suspiciously. A lot of men with briefcases and no luggage."],
  ["Did you take the order?","I did. A bed's a bed. Whoever sleeps in it, I'm not paid to know."]],
 "Any hotel with that many beds has secrets. I'd rather not hear them."],
21:["(Freja is standing in a shop full of grey dust, unable to speak for a moment.) The volcano. It's all over the showroom. Every sofa's the colour of an elephant.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. The shop, though, will need a week and a hoover. I don't have a hoover. I have a feather duster and low expectations."],
  ["Is the furniture ruined?","The fabric ones, mostly. The wood will clean. I've always preferred wood. Now I'm vindicated."],
  ["What will you do?","Open the door and let people see. Everyone's in the same state. At least here it's honest."]],
 "Take your shoes off at the door. It's a rule now."],
22:["The island's grey. I've started to see it as an interior colour. (Freja sweeps and sweeps.) I may do a whole range in volcanic.",[
  ["Is there a market for it?","There will be. People always want what they can't have, and then want it again when it's everywhere."],
  ["How are the other shops?","Mary's drowned in dust, Frank's stripping a rifle on his counter and Lisa's sold out of soap. I shall do very well in tiles."],
  ["Is the ash dangerous?","In the lungs, I'm told. Put a cloth over your nose. A napkin, if nothing else."]],
 "Don't touch the cream cushions. They're only just survived."],
23:["(Freja is wiping down the yellow chair.) It's the only thing that survived without a mark. I knew it was special. The ash didn't even stick.",[
  ["Is it a lucky chair?","I've decided it is. It's stayed in the shop longer than any customer has stayed in the island."],
  ["Is someone going to buy it now?","A woman from the clean-up crew asked about it. I told her it wasn't for sale. Then I thought about it for a minute."],
  ["How are you holding up?","Dusty. And stubborn. A Nordhem tradition."]],
 "I may sell it yet. But only to someone who laughs."],
24:["I've been drawing up a list of what the island will need after all this. Beds for the evacuees, tables for the kitchens, a hundred chairs for the shelters. (She taps a pencil on a page.)",[
  ["Are you giving them away?","Some. I'm not a saint, but I'm not a monster. At cost, mostly, and a little under for the old people."],
  ["Who's asking?","The Church, for the monastery shelters. The Party, for the halls. Everyone wants beds this week."],
  ["Do you ever stop selling?","When I sleep. And only if the bed's very good."]],
 "If you need somewhere to sit, I've a dozen."],
25:["Everyone's talking about a crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. (Freja is measuring a window.) I'd like to know the size of the throne. For the order.",[
  ["Are you sure there's a throne?","There's always a throne. It's a matter of who's going to sit on it."],
  ["Are you backing a candidate?","I back a good seat. A good sofa makes for a patient country."],
  ["Is the Democratic idea popular?","Among those who have chairs. The rest want a seat at the table."]],
 "If a princess wants a sofa, I'm ready."],
26:["There's a cola poster in the window across the road with a prince on it. (Freja squints.) It doesn't look like any prince I've sold a sofa to.",[
  ["Do you know Leopold?","He bought a lamp once. Paid cash, tipped the boy and left without a word. Very charming."],
  ["What do you make of the poster?","A prince in every bottle. I'd say a bottle in every prince, but no one asked me."],
  ["Is the cola winning the island?","It's winning the shop windows. The island's another matter."]],
 "If you see a prince in a bottle, put him back."],
27:["(Freja is straightening cinema-style rows of chairs, which she's mysteriously been asked for.) The Party's hired fifty chairs for a screening. A film, 'Revolution at the Pictures'. I only hope they stay clean.",[
  ["Is it a good film?","I haven't seen it. I only supply the seats. If the film's bad, at least they'll be comfortable."],
  ["Are you going?","I'll sit in the back row. If it's all speeches, I'll count the stitches on the seats."],
  ["Is it good for business?","Very. A revolution needs seating. Nobody tells you that."]],
 "Fifty chairs, delivered on time. Gustav came through."],
28:["(Freja has locked the front door and put up a sign: 'Open if you knock.') The big push, they say. I've never seen so many flags. I'd like to furnish every one of them.",[
  ["Is the day any different?","It feels like everyone's holding a breath. Even the chairs seem to be listening."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm a shopkeeper. A nervous shopkeeper's a sold-out one. So, yes. Perfectly."],
  ["What would you do if it all goes wrong?","Sell blankets. And hide the yellow chair."]],
 "Knock twice. I'll know it's you."],
29:["Tomorrow's the end of it. (Freja is standing at the window with her arms folded.) The whole island's holding its breath. Even the sofas look anxious.",[
  ["Are you ready?","As ready as I'll ever be. The shop's tidy, the lorry's mended, and my tea is hot."],
  ["Who do you want to win?","Whoever buys furniture. It isn't deep, but it's honest."],
  ["Any last advice?","Sit down, put your feet up and wait. It's what a good chair is for."]],
 "I'll keep the lamp lit. It's all I know to do."],
30:["(Freja's perched on the yellow chair, shoes off, a cup in her hands.) It's the last day. I took the chair out of the window this morning. I wanted to feel what it's like to sit on history.",[
  ["How is it?","Surprisingly lumpy. Rather like history, I imagine."],
  ["Has it been a good month?","The busiest in years. Beds, fireplaces, an ash-coloured sofa. I'm quite proud of the lot."],
  ["What next?","Whatever happens, people will need somewhere to sit. That's my entire political programme."]],
 "Take care of yourself. Sit down now and then."],
};
// Grace, the tailor in Kingsgrad (the Shopkeepers). Threads: the late king's last coat, finished and never collected; the island
// buying black and then buying campaign suits; the gossip of her fitting room; her hip, her eyes and the apprentice she won't admit she needs.
DAILY['Grace']={
2:["(Grace looks you up and down over her spectacles.) Hm. London cut. Off the peg. The left shoulder's a quarter of an inch low. Good afternoon.",[
  ["Good afternoon. Is it really?","It always is, off the peg. They cut for a man who doesn't exist. I've been cutting for real men for forty years. Real men are lopsided."],
  ["Did you make the king's suits?","Every one, since nineteen forty-two. And his father's last two. (She touches a dark coat on the rail.) That was his. He ordered it in August."],
  ["Can you fix my shoulder?","I could. It would take me an afternoon and you a month's wages. Stand up straight instead. It's cheaper."]],
 "Stand up straight on your way out. It's free."],
3:["(Grace is pinning black cloth onto a mannequin.) Mourning. Everyone wants black, all at once. I've three bolts left. Three!",[
  ["Who's ordering?","Half of Kingsgrad. The palace staff, all in a row. Edmund wants his tails let out. He won't say why. It's the shortbread."],
  ["What happens when you run out?","Then the island mourns in navy. Navy's very respectable. The Swedes mourn in navy. Nobody's ever complained about the Swedes."],
  ["Is the king's coat finished?","Finished. Pressed. Hanging on that rail in a cotton bag. (She doesn't look at it.) I don't know what to do with it. Nobody's come."]],
 "Don't touch the black. It marks."],
4:["(Grace is threading a needle at arm's length, squinting.) Don't help. I can do it. I've done it a million times. (She misses.) Nine hundred and ninety-nine thousand.",[
  ["Do you need glasses?","I have glasses. I need better light, a younger pair of hands and a little less pride. I'll settle for the light."],
  ["Have you thought about an apprentice?","Every day. Then I think about someone else's hands on my cloth, and I go and lie down."],
  ["Here, let me.","(She hesitates, then hands it over. You thread it.) Hm. Steady hands. (She takes it back.) Don't let it go to your head."]],
 "Steady hands. I'll remember that."],
5:["You'd be amazed what people say in a fitting room. Arms up, chin up, and out it all comes.",[
  ["Like what?","A certain gentleman from the palace told me which of the princes the old king liked best. I'll not say which. Arms up, chin up, and he wept on my tape measure."],
  ["Do you ever repeat it?","Never. (She sniffs.) Well. Never by name. A tailor's a priest with pins. People forget, because they're standing in their underthings."],
  ["Tell me one secret.","Bertil from the Civil Service Bureau wears a corset. Not by name. (She looks at you.) I said not by name."]],
 "Whatever's said in this shop stays in this shop. Mostly."],
6:["(A man in an expensive suit is just leaving.) Did you see the lining in that? Silk. Hand-sewn. Not mine. Italian. (She sniffs.) Hope Springs.",[
  ["Who was he?","He didn't say. He came to ask if I'd make three suits for 'a friend', fast, cash. Pinstripes. I said I don't do gangsters. He said 'Who said gangster?' Very polite."],
  ["Will you make them?","I'll make them. A suit's a suit. A tailor doesn't choose who wears it. If she did, half the palace would be in sacks."],
  ["Is the Syndicate a real thing?","It's real enough to pay cash and have an Italian lining. That's about as real as anything gets."]],
 "If you see three pinstripes in a row, they're mine. Unfortunately."],
7:["(Grace is measuring a bolt of grey flannel.) Frostholm's open. The oil men'll be down for suits. They always want them louder than they should be.",[
  ["Louder?","Checks. Big ones. Gold buttons. One of them asked for a Stetson to go with it. I sent him to Eastbrook. I don't do hats with a brim that wide."],
  ["Do you like the oil men?","They pay on time. They hold still. They don't cry on the tape measure. I like them more than I'd admit to Edmund."],
  ["Who's your best customer?","The old king was. Now? Harald from Kelp Cola. He has a suit for every day of the week. He's ordered six more. For the election, he says."]],
 "Grey flannel. Never loud. Remember that."],
8:["They say the oil rig's burst. I had Magnus in this morning, from Diversified Petroleum, white as a sheet, being measured for a suit he didn't need.",[
  ["Why would he buy a suit now?","Some men drink when they're worried. Some men buy suits. Magnus buys suits. He's got eleven. He'll need a twelfth if the oil's bad."],
  ["What did he say?","Nothing. Arms up, chin up, and he said nothing at all. That's the first time a man's ever said nothing in my fitting room. It frightened me."],
  ["Did you hear the sermon?","On the wireless, while I hemmed. Good, plain words. I could tell it wasn't Father Thyme. He'd have put in more Latin."]],
 "Twelve suits. Mark my words."],
9:["(Grace is sitting down, which she doesn't usually do.) My hip. Don't fuss. It does this when the weather turns.",[
  ["Can I get you anything?","A new hip. Failing that, the tin on the shelf. (You fetch it. It's peppermints.) Thank you. One for you. One for me."],
  ["Have you seen Doctor Lind?","He says rest. I said, 'Doctor, I've three weddings and a funeral.' He said, 'Then rest after the funeral.' He's a terrible doctor. He's quite right."],
  ["Who's getting married?","One of the palace footmen, a fisherman from Saltwick, and a girl from the cola factory. Funerals and weddings. That's tailoring, all year."]],
 "Pass me another peppermint before you go."],
10:["(Grace is in black, very upright, with the king's coat in its bag over her arm.) I'm going to the funeral. I'm taking the coat.",[
  ["What will you do with it?","Give it to Edmund, for the family. Or leave it in the chapel. I don't know. I only know it shouldn't hang in my shop with nobody inside it."],
  ["What was the king like to dress?","Impossible. Left shoulder lower than the right. Stood like a sailor. Never once stood still. I loved every minute."],
  ["Are you all right?","Forty years I've measured that man. I know his chest, his inside leg and his posture better than his wife did. (She stops.) I'll be all right."]],
 "Close the door for me. I'll lock up when I'm back."],
11:["(Grace's rail is empty where the king's coat hung.) I gave it to Princess Daphne. She took it in both arms and held it like a child.",[
  ["What will she do with it?","She said she'd keep it in her wardrobe and put it on when she misses him. It's three sizes too big for her. That's the right size for missing someone."],
  ["How did the funeral feel?","Long. Cold. Very grand. Leopold walked like a king already. Augustus walked like a priest. Daphne walked like his daughter. She's the only one I'd sew for."],
  ["Is the shop quieter now?","The black's over. Now everyone wants grey. Grey means 'I'm sad but I've got business to do.' I'm selling a lot of grey."]],
 "Three sizes too big. That's just right."],
12:["(Grace is at the cutting table, scissors flashing.) Busy. Suits, suits, suits. Everyone on this island wants to look like a man with money.",[
  ["Why all the suits?","The election. The Republicans are choosing a leader, and everyone wants to look like they're the sort who chooses. Very good for business."],
  ["Can anyone buy one?","Anyone with the price. Tailored suit, black trousers, a pair of Oxfords. You'd look like a stockbroker. (She looks at you.) An honest one, perhaps."],
  ["Which candidate dresses best?","Carlo. Don't tell anyone. His pinstripes are Italian and he stands like a dancer. Harald's suits are expensive and he fills them with smiling. Magnus wears his like armour."]],
 "A suit's only as good as the man standing in it."],
13:["(A bottle of Kelp Cola sits on her cutting table, unopened.) They're giving it away. A young man thrust it at me in the street. I don't drink anything brown.",[
  ["Not even tea?","Tea's amber. There's a difference. (She sniffs.) Amber's for ladies. Brown's for sailors."],
  ["Will you give it away?","To Lisa at the supermarket. She drinks one a day and saves every penny for London. She can have mine and save a dollar."],
  ["Is Harald behind it?","Harald's behind everything this week. He's had three new suits off me since Friday. He's measured like a man who expects photographs."]],
 "Take it to Lisa, would you? Tell her it's from Grace."],
14:["(Grace is putting on a hat with a short veil.) The big day. I'm going. I want to see what everyone's wearing. Half of it's mine.",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","That the island stays an island. Not part of anybody. Somewhere a woman can have a tailor's shop for forty years and nobody tells her what to cut."],
  ["Will you know your suits in the crowd?","From across the square. Every seam. I'll know who's had them let out, too. Big days make people eat."],
  ["Nice hat.","It was my mother's. She wore it to the old king's coronation in nineteen forty-one. It's seen one king in. It can see whatever comes next."]],
 "Stand up straight in the crowd. People will be looking."],
15:["(Grace is pinning a long black coat on a mannequin. It's very plain.) For Prince Augustus. He wants it 'without vanity'. I said, 'Your Highness, everything's vanity.'",[
  ["What did he say?","He said, 'Then make it with as little as you can.' So: no buttons showing, no lining, no pockets. A monk's coat with a prince's price. It's beautiful."],
  ["Has Leopold ordered anything?","A uniform. Gold frogging, sash, the lot. He wants it 'by the end of the month'. He doesn't say for what. I can guess. So can you."],
  ["Who do you think should be king?","The one who stands still for a fitting. (She sniffs.) Neither of them does. Their father never did either."]],
 "Two brothers, two coats. I'll be up all night."],
16:["(The radio's on: the debate. Grace is hemming and not listening.) Turn that down. Anna's voice goes right through my thread.",[
  ["Don't you care who wins?","I care. I just don't need to hear it. I'll know who won by who comes in for a new suit next week."],
  ["Would the Communists close your shop?","Anna was in here once. Nineteen seventy-six. She wanted a coat. Practical, warm, no nonsense. Then she asked for it in a very good red."],
  ["Has Erik ever been in?","Erik wears corduroy. Erik's never been near a tailor in his life. He'd win more votes in a good jacket. Somebody should tell him."]],
 "Down. Further. Thank you."],
17:["(A rack of hangers swings and clatters.) There. Again. The whole shop's been trembling all morning. I can't cut a straight line.",[
  ["Is it an earthquake?","A tremor, they say. I've pricked myself four times. The cloth's gone all over the floor. (She glares at the ceiling.) I've a uniform to finish."],
  ["Heard about Dario?","Missing. He came in last month for a dinner jacket. Very particular about the lapels. I'll be very cross if he's gone somewhere without collecting it."],
  ["Can I help pick things up?","Pick up the pins. Not the cloth. The pins. (You do.) Hm. You pick up pins properly. Point away. Somebody taught you right."]],
 "Point away. Always point away."],
18:["(Grace is sitting down again, rubbing her hip.) The doctor's been. He wants me to cut masks. For the ash, he says. Out of my good muslin.",[
  ["Will you do it?","I've cut forty. (She points at a pile.) My best muslin. Forty years I've kept that bolt for a wedding dress nobody's ordered. Now it's for lungs."],
  ["Is that a good use for it?","It's the best use I'll ever find for it. (She cuts another.) Don't tell the doctor I said so."],
  ["How's your hip?","Worse when the ground shakes. As if it knows. I'll be glad when the mountain makes up its mind."]],
 "Take a mask. Muslin. The best."],
19:["(A young woman is pinning a hem under Grace's eye.) Don't stare. This is Tiina, from Saltwick. She's on trial. One week. She may be hopeless.",[
  ["You've taken an apprentice?","I've taken someone to hold the pins. If she turns out to be an apprentice, that's her business. (Tiina looks up and grins.)"],
  ["Is she any good?","She's quick, she's careful, and she doesn't talk while she sews. That's three more things than I expected. Don't tell her."],
  ["What changed your mind?","The muslin. Cutting a hundred masks by myself with a bad hip and a tremor. I sat down, and I couldn't get up, and Tiina was at the door looking for work. That's all."]],
 "Tiina, straighter. (To you.) Goodbye."],
20:["(Grace is folding finished pieces into tissue paper, very fast.) If the mountain goes, I want everything finished and wrapped. Nobody's coat is getting ash on it.",[
  ["Is it going to go?","My hip says yes. The doctor says maybe. Nils in Frostholm says his record player says yes. I'm wrapping."],
  ["Is the uniform finished?","Leopold's? Last stitch this morning. Gold frogging, red sash. He'll look like a king. Or a hotel doorman. Depends who wins."],
  ["How's Tiina doing?","She stayed late last night and finished three hems without being asked. (She folds a jacket.) I didn't say thank you. I'll say it on Friday."]],
 "Wrapped, labelled, out of the dust. That's how you meet a disaster."],
21:["(The shop's windows are grey. Grace sits on her stool with a dust sheet over the cutting table.) It's gone up. I knew it. My hip knew it.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Tiina's fine: she ran in at the first bang and helped me cover everything. Then she held my hand. I let her."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, they say, thank God. The lava came down in the hills. Everything else is just grey. Grey's not a tragedy. It's a colour."],
  ["What about your cloth?","Every bolt covered. (She pats the dust sheet.) Forty years of cloth, under a sheet, safe. Ash can't get through good cotton."]],
 "Wear your mask. The muslin one. It suits you."],
22:["(Grace is brushing a jacket with a clothes brush, slow strokes.) Ash in every seam on the island. They'll all be coming to me. I'll be brushing till Christmas.",[
  ["Is it bad for cloth?","It's sharp. It cuts the threads if you rub it. You brush it out, gently, the way the nap lies. Tiina's learning. She's very patient."],
  ["You said you'd thank Tiina.","I did. On Friday. (She doesn't look up.) Well. Today. It's not Friday. But she'd earned it."],
  ["What's the gossip now?","Nobody's gossiping. Everybody's coughing. The fitting room's never been so quiet. It's unnatural."]],
 "Brush with the nap. Never against."],
23:["(Grace is measuring a big man in a white apron for a jacket.) Arms up. Chin up. (To you.) This is Arvid from the Cedar Grill. He's never owned a jacket in his life.",[
  ["What's the occasion?","He won't say. (Arvid looks at the ceiling.) I've heard something about the palace and a dinner for a foreign king. That's all. I've heard nothing."],
  ["Busy again, then?","The election's back on, and the island's remembered it needs clothes. Harald's ordered two more. Carlo's men have ordered three more. Magnus, one. Dark. Sensible."],
  ["How's your hip?","Better since the ground stopped. Tiina does the bending now. I do the shouting. It's a very good arrangement."]],
 "Arms down, Arvid. You can breathe now."],
24:["You know what I've been thinking? Forty years I've dressed the island. Fishermen, princes, gangsters. And none of them stands up straight.",[
  ["Not one?","The old king, nearly. Bertil, because of the corset. (She covers her mouth.) Not by name. I didn't say it."],
  ["What would you tell them all?","Shoulders back. Chin up. Look like you mean it. That's all a suit does. It makes you look like you mean it, even when you don't."],
  ["Do you mean it?","Every stitch. That's the only thing I've ever been sure of."]],
 "Shoulders back. Off you go."],
25:["(Grace is pinning a cream gown on a mannequin.) For the palace. Princess Daphne. For something on the twenty-eighth. Something with a foreign king in it.",[
  ["It's beautiful.","It's nearly beautiful. The waist's wrong. (She unpins and repins.) There. Now it's beautiful. Daphne won't care. She'll wear boots under it."],
  ["What's happening on the twenty-eighth?","A state visit. A king from somewhere with more money than us. The palace wants to look like a palace. They've ordered everything at once."],
  ["Does Daphne still have the old king's coat?","She wore it to her fitting. Over her nightdress. Three sizes too big. (She smiles.) I didn't say a word."]],
 "If you see Daphne, tell her the waist's right now."],
26:["Lisa from the supermarket carried my shopping home. I gave her five dollars. Don't tell her it was too much. She'll only argue.",[
  ["That was kind.","It wasn't kind. It was payment. My hip, two bags and a hill. Five dollars is cheap. (She sniffs.) Don't call me kind. It's bad for business."],
  ["She's saving for London.","I know. Forty years ago, I saved for Paris. I got as far as Helsinki, met a cutter called Aaro and came home with him instead. Paris can wait."],
  ["What happened to Aaro?","Died in sixty-eight. Best buttonholes in the north. (She touches a mannequin.) He's why I don't take apprentices. You take one, and you lose them."]],
 "Don't tell Lisa about Paris. She'll only want to go there too."],
27:["(Grace is fitting a waistcoat on one of Carlo's men, who stands very still.) Arms up. Chin up. Hm. You've got something in your inside pocket. Please remove it.",[
  ["What's in his pocket?","(The man takes out a pack of cards and puts it down.) Cards. There's a game tonight, I'm told. At the pizzeria. Big men, big money."],
  ["Do you mind dressing them?","I mind the cards in my lining. Everything else is business. Cloth doesn't care who wears it. I care, but I've stopped telling cloth."],
  ["Is everyone going?","Everyone who's ordered something from me this month. Which is everyone who matters. I've sewn half the room. I'd recognise them in the dark."]],
 "Cards out of the lining. Always."],
28:["(Grace is in her mother's hat, watching the street.) Look at them. Every faction on the island in their best. And I've made half of it.",[
  ["What do you see?","Daphne's gown, going up to the palace. Leopold's uniform on the steps. Harald's suit at the cola stand. Carlo's men in pinstripes. Gold frogging and pinstripes. What an island."],
  ["Are you proud?","Of the seams. Yes. Of what they're doing in them, I'm not sure yet. A tailor doesn't choose who wears the coat."],
  ["Is Tiina here?","She's at the palace, with a sewing kit, in case Daphne pops a stitch. My first apprentice at a state visit. (She sniffs.) I'm not crying. It's the ash."]],
 "Stand up straight. The whole island's watching."],
29:["(Grace is ironing.) The Republicans are voting. Three candidates, and I've dressed all three. Whoever wins, he'll be in one of my suits.",[
  ["Who'd you vote for, if you could?","I can't. Not a member. If I could, I'd vote for the best posture. Which means nobody. I'd spoil my paper."],
  ["Does it matter who leads them?","To the island, it might. To me, he'll still need taking in at the waist by Christmas. They always do, once they've won."],
  ["How's Tiina?","She's asked if she can stay on after the trial. (She irons a sleeve.) I said I'd think about it. I've already ordered her a thimble."]],
 "A thimble. Engraved. Don't tell her."],
30:["(Grace is sitting at her table, unusually still.) Last day. Whatever's decided today, the island will need clothes tomorrow.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","That I can keep cutting cloth. That nobody tells me what colour to use. That Tiina has a shop of her own one day, here, on this island."],
  ["Are you worried?","I've seen a war, a coronation, a funeral and a volcano from behind this table. I'm too old to worry. I'm just going to thread my needle."],
  ["Let me thread it.","(She hands it to you without a word. You thread it.) Hm. Steady hands still. (She takes it back.) Thank you."]],
 "Stand up straight, whatever happens. It's free."],
};
// Greta, receptionist at the Hotel Aurora in Frostholm, who lives in the Skyline Apartments (The Proprietors). Threads: Yuri,
// the generous timber businessman upstairs, and his late-night visitors; the hotel's famous night in 1959, when the Arvo Lind
// Quartet recorded Midnight at the Aurora in the ballroom and her mother was a chambermaid; the manager, Mr Lehto, cutting staff
// (Raili was let go) and Greta doing two jobs; her rule that a receptionist sees nothing, which she keeps badly.
DAILY['Greta']={
7:["(Greta looks up from the register under the key rack.) Good afternoon. Hotel Aurora. Are you checking in? No. You're the reporter from London. Room... none. You're not staying with us. Pity.",[
  ["How did you know?","I know every guest's name and room number, and you're not one of them. Which makes you interesting. Our guests are rarely interesting."],
  ["Tell me about the hotel.","Forty rooms, a ballroom we don't use, a lift that works on Tuesdays, and a view of the Petro Tower from every window. The road's only just open, so we've three guests. One of them's been here all winter."],
  ["Anybody famous ever stayed?","Everyone famous who ever came to Sarivalto. Which is to say, about four people. Ask me another day. I'll tell you about 1959."]],
 "Come back any time. The armchairs are free; the coffee isn't."],
8:["(Greta is sorting post into pigeon holes.) The gentleman upstairs has had eleven letters this week. From Helsinki, Tallinn and somewhere with no stamp at all. A receptionist sees nothing, of course.",[
  ["Who's the gentleman?","Yuri. A timber businessman, he says, from Leningrad. Charming. Buys the bar a round every night. Tips like a prince. Has been here since February."],
  ["Since February? Doing what?","Buying timber, he says. I've never seen any timber. I've seen a great many visitors, late at night, who don't look like they know which end of a tree is up."],
  ["You see a lot for someone who sees nothing.","I see everything. I just don't say it. ...Mostly."]],
 "Mind the revolving door. It catches coats."],
9:["The manager, Mr Lehto, has let another maid go. That's three since the summer. I'm on the desk and doing the second floor's towels. Look at my hands.",[
  ["Who was let go?","Raili, in the spring, and two girls this year. Raili still comes past and waves through the window. She's looking for work. There isn't any."],
  ["Why's he cutting staff?","Because the hotel's empty half the year, and Mr Lehto would rather have a full wallet than a full staff. He's very good at sums."],
  ["Couldn't you complain?","To whom? The union's in Saltwick, and the hotel's in Frostholm. Here, if you complain, you're a Communist. And then you're Raili."]],
 "Go on. If you see anyone who needs a towel, send them up. I'm the towel lady now."],
10:["(Greta has a black ribbon pinned to her lapel.) The funeral's today. Yuri asked me for a black tie for it. He wasn't invited. He wanted to stand outside the palace, he said, and pay his respects.",[
  ["Did you give him one?","Mr Lehto's spare. It suited Yuri better than it ever suited Mr Lehto. He came back down, and asked if I'd seen who else went."],
  ["Did you know the king?","He opened the Aurora's ballroom in 1958. Mother said he danced with a chambermaid that night, just one dance, to show he could. Not her, sadly. Her friend Aili."],
  ["Who else is at the hotel?","Two journalists from Helsinki, here for the funeral, both with colds. They've used every handkerchief we own."]],
 "Pay your respects, whatever you think of kings."],
11:["(Greta leans across the desk.) Yuri had four visitors last night. One at ten, one at eleven, two at half past one. They all came in the side door, but the side door creaks, and my desk is next to it.",[
  ["What did they look like?",["A woman in a union scarf. A man with fish scales on his boots. A man in an oil company jacket, with the badge turned in. And one I won't describe.",[
    ["Why won't you describe him?","Because he looked at me, on his way out, like he was remembering my face. I'd rather he forgot it."],
    ["An oil man? Visiting a Russian?","Timber and oil. Very good friends. Everyone's very good friends with Yuri."]]]],
  ["Why tell me?","I tell nobody. You're nobody yet. You're from London. In London you have so many Russians you won't care."],
  ["You should tell the police.","And say what? 'A guest has friends'? Sergeant Hanna would laugh at me. And Mr Lehto would fire me for gossiping about a paying guest."]],
 "If anyone asks, I was talking about towels."],
12:["People have been asking about 1959 all of a sudden. Two telephone calls this week asking if the hotel has a recording of the night the Arvo Lind Quartet played the ballroom. As if I'd keep it under the desk.",[
  ["What happened in 1959?","The Arvo Lind Quartet recorded a whole night, live, in our ballroom. 'Midnight at the Aurora.' They pressed two hundred copies. It's the only famous thing the Aurora ever did."],
  ["Who's asking?","One call from Frostholm, a man who wouldn't give a name but breathed like an oil executive. One from up the mountain, very polite, with a sort of echo."],
  ["Was your mother there?",["She was a chambermaid. She took coffee in at three in the morning and stayed, standing at the back with the tray, till they finished. Got a telling-off.",[
    ["Was it worth it?","She said it was the best night of her life, and she married my father. She wasn't joking. Don't tell him."],
    ["Do you have the record?","No. Mother never could afford one. She hummed it. I know the whole of side one, the way she hummed it. Probably wrong."]]]]],
 "Go on. And if you see the record, tell me what it sounds like really."],
13:["Mr Lehto's got a new idea: free Kelp Cola in every room. He signed a deal with them. Now the minibar's all cola and the guests complain they want brandy.",[
  ["Does Yuri drink cola?","He poured his down the sink in front of me and asked for vodka. I sent up the vodka. He tipped me five dollars and said, 'To the workers.'"],
  ["How's the hotel doing?","Better, with the roads open. Ten guests! Most of them for the funeral, and leaving. And Yuri, who isn't leaving."],
  ["Does Mr Lehto ever do the towels?","Mr Lehto's never touched a towel in his life. He doesn't believe in them. He believes in invoices."]],
 "Go on. The big day's tomorrow. Mr Lehto's making me work."],
14:["(The lobby's empty, and Greta is reading a magazine.) Everyone's gone to the big day. The guests, the porter, Mr Lehto. Even Yuri went, in a hat.",[
  ["Why aren't you there?","Somebody has to mind the keys. And somebody has to see who comes back first and who comes back angry."],
  ["Yuri in a hat?","A fur one. Very Russian. I said, 'You'll stand out.' He said, 'Every timber man in Leningrad wears one.' I've never seen a timber man in Leningrad. Neither, I suspect, has he."],
  ["What do you expect from it?","Speeches, and the island deciding nothing, at length. We're very good at that."]],
 "Go on, you'll miss it. Tell me who wore what."],
15:["(Greta has a pencil behind her ear.) Yuri's had an aerial put on his window. A long one. For the radio, he says. He likes to listen to the football.",[
  ["What football?","On a Sunday? From Leningrad? He's very keen. He listens all night. I can hear it humming through the ceiling on the stairs."],
  ["Did Mr Lehto allow it?","Yuri tipped Mr Lehto. Mr Lehto would allow a battleship on the roof for the right tip."],
  ["Football. Of course.","Of course. (She doesn't smile.) I'm very fond of football myself. I don't know the rules."]],
 "Go on. Don't look up at his window on your way out. He notices."],
16:["Raili came in for a coffee, on me. She's still looking for work. She asked Yuri if he needed a secretary. He said no, very kindly, and gave her twenty dollars.",[
  ["Twenty dollars? For nothing?","For nothing. He does that. Gives people money, buys them drinks, asks how they're getting on. Everyone likes him. That's what frightens me."],
  ["Why does it frighten you?","Because nobody's that kind for free. Not here. Not in timber."],
  ["How's Raili coping?","She listens to the radio all evening and goes to the Midnight Note to stay warm. She's thinner. She won't take food. She took his twenty dollars, though."]],
 "Go on. If you hear of any work, tell Raili."],
17:["(The keys on the rack are jingling on their own.) The ground's been shaking. Every time, the keys sing. Mr Lehto says it's nothing. Mr Lehto's packed a suitcase.",[
  ["Is the hotel safe?","It was built in 1931. It's survived a war, a fire on the third floor, and the Arvo Lind Quartet. I'm sure it'll survive a wobble."],
  ["Dario's gone missing, they say.","His people came in and asked if he'd taken a room. Under any name. I told them we've no Darios. They asked for the register. I said no. They took it anyway."],
  ["Did they find anything?","They found Yuri's name, and looked at each other. Then they put it back and left very fast."]],
 "Go on. Mind your head. Things are falling off shelves."],
18:["Something odd. Yuri came down at three in the morning, asked for a glass of milk and talked to me for an hour. About his mother. About Leningrad. About nothing.",[
  ["That's sweet.","It was. He's lonely. Or he wanted to know what I'd tell him. Probably both. People are usually both."],
  ["What did you tell him?","About my mother and the record. He asked a lot about 1959. Who stayed here then. Who came to the ballroom. As if he were writing a history."],
  ["Are you starting to like him?","(She looks at the register.) A receptionist doesn't like or dislike. She checks you in and out. ...He has very nice manners."]],
 "Go on. Don't tell anyone I'm soft. I've a reputation."],
19:["Yuri's visitors have doubled. Party people, I'm fairly sure: they came in with clipboards. And one of the Syndicate's men, who never comes in anywhere he isn't owed money.",[
  ["The Syndicate too?","Nico the bookie. He came in, went up, came down, and bought a cigar at the desk. He paid in Russian roubles. I didn't know Nico had roubles."],
  ["What do you make of it?","I make nothing of it. I write down the room numbers. I've a whole notebook now, under the till. In case."],
  ["In case of what?","In case someone ever asks the right question. Nobody's asked yet."]],
 "Go on. And if you see a man with a clipboard, don't sign anything."],
20:["(Greta lowers her voice to almost nothing.) The Democrats have been in. Erik's people, from Eastbrook. Asking questions about Yuri: when he came, who visits, whether he's really in timber.",[
  ["What did you tell them?","Nothing. And then I went to fetch a towel. A long towel. From the third floor. What they did at my desk while I was gone, I couldn't say."],
  ["Does Yuri know?","Yuri knows everything. He came down after and asked me if the Democrats were nice people. I said very nice. He said he thought so too."],
  ["Are you frightened?",["I'm a receptionist. I'm not paid enough to be frightened. (She pauses.) I've moved my notebook. To somewhere better.",[
    ["Where?","Somewhere better. If I told you, it wouldn't be."],
    ["Would you show me?","One day. Not today. Today, I'm the towel lady."]]]]],
 "Go on. And forget we talked."],
21:["(The lobby is full of guests in dressing gowns, coughing, and the windows are grey.) The mountain's gone up. Everybody's come down to the lobby. Nobody wants to be on the fourth floor in this.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Everyone's accounted for. I counted. Thirty-one guests, two cats and Mr Lehto, who's hiding in the office with his suitcase."],
  ["What about Yuri?","He came down calmly, dressed, with a briefcase, and asked if the telephones work. They don't. He went back up. I heard his aerial humming."],
  ["You're very calm.","Somebody has to be. If the receptionist panics, the hotel panics. My mother said that. She was right about everything except my father."]],
 "Stay in the lobby a while, if you like. There's tea."],
22:["(Greta is sweeping ash out of the revolving door.) Half our guests left on the first boat. The other half are journalists, come to see the volcano. They're all in the bar asking each other what happened.",[
  ["Journalists like me?","Like you, but with worse coats. Two Swedes, a German, a man from Helsinki who's been here before and says the room's smaller."],
  ["How's the hotel?","Ash in the ballroom, ash in the lift. The lift works on Wednesdays now. Mr Lehto's very pleased with the bookings. He's discovered volcanoes are good business."],
  ["And Yuri?","He bought the journalists a round. He told them he was in timber. They believed him. Journalists believe anything if you're buying."]],
 "Go on. Wipe your feet, please. Twice."],
23:["Raili's got a job! Mr Lehto's taken her back, for the journalists. She's doing the towels. I nearly kissed Mr Lehto. I didn't.",[
  ["That's wonderful.","It's temporary. When the journalists go, she goes. But she's laughing again. And I'm back to one job."],
  ["Did the volcano do some good, then?","It brought guests, and guests bring work, and work brings Raili. Strange way for the mountain to be kind."],
  ["What does Yuri say?","He sent Raili flowers. Real ones, from the Nordmart, in ash. She cried. I'm starting to think he's either a saint or very, very clever."]],
 "Go on. It's a good day at the Aurora, for once."],
24:["(Greta has a gramophone record on the desk, in a paper sleeve.) Look. A guest left it. Not Midnight at the Aurora. A cheap one from Helsinki. But I've been playing it in the ballroom at night.",[
  ["In the empty ballroom?","With the lights off, at two in the morning. It's beautiful. The floor's still sprung. The chandelier rattles when the ground shakes. Very romantic."],
  ["Do you dance?","Only when nobody's watching. And once, Yuri came in and asked if he might. I said no. Then I said one dance."],
  ["Greta!","One dance. For my mother. She'd have liked a story like that. Don't put it in the paper."]],
 "Go on. And I didn't tell you that."],
25:["Yuri asked me today, very politely, whether I'd ever thought of living somewhere bigger. Leningrad, for instance. With a hotel of a thousand rooms. I said I liked forty.",[
  ["Was he offering you a job?","He was offering something. I've learnt not to ask what. I said I'm a Frostholm girl, and the lift only works on Tuesdays here, and I like that."],
  ["Would you ever leave?","I've never been off the island. Mother never was. Leningrad sounds very cold and very big. I'm cold enough here."],
  ["What's your notebook say now?","More than a receptionist should know. Less than a policeman would want. It's the right size."]],
 "Go on. Mind the revolving door. It still catches coats."],
26:["Dario's buying the spa on the mountain, they say. He was in the bar last night telling everyone. He wants to turn it into a hotel. A volcano hotel. Mr Lehto went white.",[
  ["Competition?","A spa hotel with hot springs, against the Aurora with its Tuesday lift? Mr Lehto's been on the telephone all morning."],
  ["Would you work there?","I'm the Aurora. Mother was the Aurora. I'd sooner sweep the ash out of this lobby for the rest of my life."],
  ["Will the spa's owner sell?","Saga at the spa says not. But Dario says money talks. In Frostholm, it sings."]],
 "Go on. And don't tell Mr Lehto I said he went white."],
27:["(Greta is checking a long list.) The hotel's full for tomorrow. All forty rooms. Everyone wants to be near Kingsgrad for the big day, and close to Frostholm for the parties. Mr Lehto's in heaven.",[
  ["Who's coming?","Party people, oil people, cola people, and one very pale man from a Swedish newspaper who wants to know who's going to win. I said that's not on the register."],
  ["Is Yuri staying?","He's asked for a late check-out on the thirtieth. A very late one. And for the bill to be ready, just in case."],
  ["Do you have a favourite guest?","Mother said never have a favourite. (She smiles.) I'll tell you on the thirtieth."]],
 "Go on. It's going to be a long two days."],
28:["(The lobby's full of people with flags and suitcases.) Everybody's out today. The whole island's at a rally, or a party, or a march. I'm here giving out keys and listening.",[
  ["What are you hearing?","Everybody thinks they'll win. The oil men, the Party people, the cola men. Nobody's checked out yet. They're all waiting."],
  ["Is Yuri busy?","His aerial hummed all night and his visitors came in shifts. He looked tired this morning, and happy. I don't know which worries me more."],
  ["How are you?","My feet hurt, my mother would be proud, and the lift works today, of all days. That's how I am."]],
 "Go on. Tell me who's winning if you find out."],
29:["(Greta is holding her notebook, the one from under the till, and turning it over.) I've been thinking. About whether to give this to someone. And who.",[
  ["What's in it?","Six months of room numbers, names and times. Who went up to see Yuri, and when, and who came down looking guilty. That's all. It's only a list."],
  ["Who would you give it to?","The police? The Gazette? Nobody? I don't know. A receptionist sees nothing. I've seen too much."],
  ["Would you give it to me?",["(She looks at you for a long time.) You're from London. What would London do with a hotel list from Frostholm?",[
    ["Print it.","Then I'd lose my job, and Yuri would lose his smile. I'm not sure about either. I'll sleep on it."],
    ["Probably nothing.","That's what I thought. That's why I haven't given it to anyone. Nothing's what happens to lists."]]]]],
 "Go on. I'll decide tomorrow, like everyone else."],
30:["(Greta is at the desk, the register open, a fresh flower in a glass beside it.) The last day. Yuri's bill is ready on the desk. He hasn't come down. I've kept it under a book.",[
  ["Is he leaving?","Depends how today goes, I suppose. For him and for everyone. I've learnt to read a man's check-out like a weather report."],
  ["What will you do with the notebook?","I've decided. (She taps the desk.) It stays where it is until someone asks the right question. Today, maybe. Or never."],
  ["Who's your favourite guest?","(She smiles.) Mother said never have one. Mother also stood at the back of the ballroom with a tray till dawn. You're not a guest. You're the one who kept coming back."]],
 "Go on. Good luck today. The Aurora will be here when you're done."],
};
// Gunnar, dock worker at the Warehouse in West Saltwick Harbour (Union). Threads: crates and what is not on the manifest, his bad back, Helen and the Union, his flat in the block, the ash.
DAILY['Gunnar']={
1:["(Gunnar sets a crate down with a grunt.) The roads are clear and the boats are running. You'll see more freight this week than all winter. I'm Gunnar. I unload everything.",[
  ["Pleased to meet you. Busy job?","It's a back that holds up a lot of other people's goods. Mine's complaining."],
  ["What comes in on the boats?","Flour, fuel, tinned peaches, and things not on the form."],
  ["Things not on the form?","(He taps his nose.) A dock worker doesn't read the lists. He reads the weight."]],
 "Lift with your knees."],
2:["(Gunnar wipes his brow.) Boats from Kingsgrad today. Palace crates. Fine linen, fine silver. Heavy as a house, all of it.",[
  ["What's in the palace crates?","Black cloth for mourning, mostly. And enough candles to light the sea."],
  ["Do you like your job?","I like the weight. A crate is honest: it's as heavy as it says."],
  ["Heard any royal gossip?","That Prince Leopold ordered new boots, and Prince Augustus ordered new hymn books. That tells you everything."]],
 "Mind your toes."],
3:["(Gunnar pulls a crate of bottles across the floor.) Kelp Cola's thirsty today. They'll drink the whole harbour if we let them. Two hundred crates, and it's only Tuesday.",[
  ["Do you drink it?","Never on shift. It makes me burp mid-lift."],
  ["Is the Union happy with cola work?","Helen says it's decent work if the pay's decent. The pay's decent-ish."],
  ["Anything odd in the shipment?","One crate rattled like a box of coins. I didn't look. I'm paid to lift."]],
 "Stack them straight."],
4:["(Gunnar leans on a pallet.) Eastbrook's loading now. Timber and the Gazette's paper stock. Henrik's reams are lighter than they look, but they smell of ink.",[
  ["Do you read the Gazette?","Only the weather and the crossword. Henrik's crossword is too clever for the harbour."],
  ["What about the timber?","Fir, mostly. It goes out to the mainland, they say. I only know it's heavy."],
  ["Do you know Tor in Eastbrook?","From the other warehouse? Argues about Marx while lifting. I'd rather lift."]],
 "Don't sit on the planks."],
5:["(Gunnar sighs.) Candles and incense for the monastery. Heavy, strange smell. A crate marked 'sacramental' that I wasn't allowed to open.",[
  ["Did you want to?","I always want to. That's how I know what isn't on the manifest."],
  ["Is the Church a good customer?","They pay slowly and bless quickly. It's a fair trade."],
  ["What do you make of the monks?","They sit still for hours. I'd like a job like that. Don't tell my back."]],
 "Keep the incense dry."],
6:["(Gunnar glances over his shoulder.) A stranger asked about you at the gate. Nicely dressed, no cargo. I said I'm a dock worker, I don't know anyone.",[
  ["What did he look like?","Hat. Gloves. A smile like a drawer closing."],
  ["Was he from the Syndicate?","I couldn't say. Some men look like trouble the way some crates look heavy."],
  ["Should I be worried?","A reporter's always being watched. It's the job."]],
 "Eyes open."],
7:["(Gunnar nods at a boat.) North-west's open, so the oil lot are sending crates. Pipes and valves, and a trunk of fine suits for the boss. Dario's got a tailor, it seems.",[
  ["Do you like Diversified?","They pay well and talk little. I prefer that to the other way."],
  ["What's in the pipes?","Nothing, if they're honest. Pipe's a good place to hide things, and I'm saying nothing."],
  ["Any trouble?","None. Only strange paperwork, which is the way of oil."]],
 "Mind the valves."],
8:["(Gunnar rubs his lower back.) Sunday and I'm still at it. The monastery wants candles, since Father Thyme's looking for words and says the light helps.",[
  ["Does the light help?","I've no idea. But I'm lifting it."],
  ["Do you go to the service?","Seldom. The bench is harder than the dock."],
  ["Heard of the sermon trouble?","The monks are muttering. Somebody should lend him a dictionary."]],
 "Rest your back."],
9:["(Gunnar steadies a stack of crates.) Two cola factions today, and a fair few crates of sugar. Someone's brewing a new formula, and someone else is brewing a Party one.",[
  ["Which cola would you choose?","The one with the bigger crate and the smaller invoice."],
  ["Does the Party really want a cola?","Helen's mad for it. She says the workers should drink from their own bottle."],
  ["Do you taste test?","I'm a lifter. I drink what's put in front of me."]],
 "Check the labels."],
10:["(Gunnar takes his cap off.) The king's burial today. The harbour's quiet, even the cranes. I shifted wreaths at dawn, white lilies and winter roses. He deserved the flowers, whatever they say.",[
  ["Did you respect him?","He kept the harbour open. That's more than most."],
  ["Does the Union mourn?","We stop work for an hour. Helen says the king's gone and the workers remain."],
  ["Is there a boat for the funeral?","Aye, to Kingsgrad. The cargo's flowers and sorrow, and no extras."]],
 "Quiet please."],
11:["(Gunnar spits in his palms.) Mill hands in Eastbrook want better pay, and a boat of mill logs arrived with their banners on. The union's rallying.",[
  ["Are you backing them?","Of course. I back anyone who lifts for a living."],
  ["Do you earn enough?","I earn enough for bread and a bad back."],
  ["Is Helen leading it?","She's leading everything. She's already moved the tide table."]],
 "Stand together."],
12:["(Gunnar peers at a manifest.) Strange accounts today. Oil company wants everything shifted from one ledger to another. I only shift crates, not numbers.",[
  ["What are the numbers for?","Somebody wants money where Dario won't look. That's all I heard."],
  ["Do you hear much?","I'm a pair of ears with a hernia."],
  ["Heard about Ruby's record?","Ruby's got something Frostholm wants. Everyone's whispering about it."]],
 "Count twice."],
13:["(Gunnar balances a crate of bottles.) Free samples going out by the hundred. Free's the most expensive word there is, my mother used to say.",[
  ["Did you take a bottle?","Two. I'm not proud."],
  ["Who's handing them out?","Kelp's boys in white caps. They look like waiters at a very cold wedding."],
  ["What else came in?","A crate that smelled of something old. The Syndicate's recipe, someone said. I just lifted."]],
 "Don't drink on the job."],
14:["(Gunnar flexes his hands.) Tomorrow's the big day, the fourteenth. The harbour's been busy shipping banners and benches. I've shifted more chairs than a church.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'll go if my back lets me. It's a day everyone's got an opinion on."],
  ["What do you expect?","A speech, a crowd, and somebody dropping a bottle."],
  ["Is it a good thing?","Ask me tomorrow. Today it's just crates."]],
 "Look after yourself."],
15:["(Gunnar is checking a list.) Crown and cross, crown and cross. The harbour is full of both. Royal crates on this side, church crates on that, and me in the middle.",[
  ["Which do you favour?","Whichever has the lighter crates."],
  ["Is there tension?","The monks and the palace men glare at each other over the same gangplank."],
  ["Will it end in trouble?","Not on my dock. I've a crowbar and a short temper."]],
 "Keep to your own side."],
16:["(Gunnar hums.) Debate day. The Party and the Democrats at it. Radio's on in the warehouse and no one's lifting. Even the foreman's listening.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever shouts longest. Helen's good at that."],
  ["Do you follow it?","I like a debate if it ends. This one might not."],
  ["Who do you vote for?","I'll tell you when I've decided. Maybe on the day."]],
 "Turn it up."],
17:["(Gunnar frowns at the floor.) Did you feel that? A tremor under the crates. A glass jar rolled off the shelf. Nobody believed me.",[
  ["What do you think it is?","Old Mike says the mountain's turning over in its sleep."],
  ["Are the crates safe?","They're stacked, they'll hold. It's the roof I worry about."],
  ["Have you heard of Dario going missing?","Some folk say he's vanished. The oil boys are polite about it, which is how you know."]],
 "Mind your head."],
18:["(Gunnar points north.) Smoke on the mountain. A white thread, and the monks are lighting candles. A shipment of lanterns went out today, as if the Church knew.",[
  ["Do you think it's dangerous?","It's a long way off. Nothing a good wall can't stop."],
  ["Anything from the cola factory?","They had something about a cup called Kelpy. Everyone's gone soft over a cup."],
  ["Did the Church lose something?","A relic, they say. Nobody's allowed to ask. I stack the crates and say nothing."]],
 "Stack and say nothing."],
19:["(Gunnar stretches.) A busy night, they say, with a new act at the Midnight Note and Party men recruiting on the quay. I've been asked to sign a card.",[
  ["Did you sign?","I'm in the Union. That's card enough."],
  ["Who asked you?","A fellow in a good coat. He gave me a pencil. I kept the pencil."],
  ["Is the Party growing?","Slowly. Like mould."]],
 "Don't sign everything."],
20:["(Gunnar frowns at a crate.) Strange cargo for the palace, a shipment of velvet and wire cutters. And a note about a man at the Hotel Aurora. Not mine to read.",[
  ["Did you read it?","I looked. It was in a code or in Finnish, I couldn't tell."],
  ["Do you think something's happening?","Always. It's an island. Something's always happening."],
  ["Did you hear about the palace job?","I heard some folk are planning to do over the palace. I heard it from several people at once."]],
 "Lock up."],
21:["(Gunnar's face is smudged grey.) She blew. The mountain. We all saw the glow. Nobody on the harbour moved for a full minute. Then the foreman said, 'Back to work,' and we did.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far as I know. Ash on everything, but nobody hurt."],
  ["What did it sound like?","Like the biggest crate in the world falling off the biggest dock."],
  ["Are you scared?","A bit. But I've a job to do, and the ash doesn't unload itself."]],
 "Cover your mouth."],
22:["(Gunnar shovels grey from a pallet.) Ash in the crates, ash in my boots, ash in my tea. Trucks of it coming off the roads, a farm's roof gave way somewhere in the east.",[
  ["Are the boats running?","Slowly. The captains can't see the buoys."],
  ["Do you need help?","Always. Bring a shovel and good manners."],
  ["What's the Union doing?","Helen has everyone in teams. She's sweeping the island by committee."]],
 "Take a turn."],
23:["(Gunnar leans on his shovel.) Some of the dock machinery's seized with ash. Somebody's gummed up the works on purpose too, they say. A spanner in the winch, who knows.",[
  ["Who'd do that?","Somebody who dislikes winches."],
  ["Is anything being fixed?","The oil boys are sending men to fix something deep. I'd rather they fixed my winch."],
  ["Can you manage?","By hand. Like our grandfathers."]],
 "Oil the chains."],
24:["(Gunnar nods at the road.) Some of the monks are off to the peaks, with packs on their backs, in the ash. And a clean-up crew's moving about town with brooms and no questions.",[
  ["Why the peaks?","A retreat, they call it. I'd call it a long walk."],
  ["Who runs the clean-up?","Whoever has the brooms. Strange how many have brooms all of a sudden."],
  ["Do you trust them?","Not really. But a swept street's a swept street."]],
 "Sweep it all."],
25:["(Gunnar rests on a post.) Talk of a princess, and a crown for everyone. A crown on every head would give us all headaches.",[
  ["Is the harbour for a vote?","A vote's good, so long as it's counted by someone honest."],
  ["Is there a princess?","I've heard there's one somewhere. She's wanted by the Democrats, apparently."],
  ["What do you think of royalty?","Heavy hats. Heavy costs."]],
 "Count carefully."],
26:["(Gunnar scratches his neck.) Kelp Cola's printed princes on the bottles. And Dario's buying a spa. Not a crate of it came through here, thank heaven.",[
  ["Do you like the bottles?","The prince looks cross. He's cross because he's inside a bottle."],
  ["What would Dario do with a spa?","Steam. Think. Hide things, probably."],
  ["Who is buying what?","Everybody's buying everyone. My back isn't for sale."]],
 "Don't sell your back."],
27:["(Gunnar whistles.) The Party's showing a revolution at the pictures, and the Syndicate's talking high stakes. A film and a bet. I'm lifting crates of popcorn and playing cards.",[
  ["Are you going to the pictures?","If I'm not too tired. I like a film with a ship in it."],
  ["Are you betting?","On what? A dock worker's luck is the weather."],
  ["What's high stakes?","The stakes are what you put at risk. I put my back."]],
 "Don't bet your boots."],
28:["(Gunnar glances around.) A day of everyone pushing as hard as they can. Flags in my crates, leaflets in my pockets, an oil man's offered me a coat. I said no.",[
  ["Why no coat?","Coats come with strings. I've enough strings."],
  ["Who pushes hardest?","The Party, with songs. The oil lot, with envelopes."],
  ["Are you tired?","I've been tired since 1962. Today's no different."]],
 "Take it slow."],
29:["(Gunnar stacks slowly.) Another day of it. Last crates before the end, they say. The harbour's full of banners, and every one of them promises better than the last.",[
  ["Do you believe them?","I believe in weight. Promises weigh nothing."],
  ["What will you do after?","Lift crates. It never ends."],
  ["Is there hope?","There's a decent tide tomorrow. That's hope enough."]],
 "Rest well."],
30:["(Gunnar stands still among the crates.) The last day. Whatever happens, the boats will come in tomorrow and somebody'll have to unload them. That'll be me.",[
  ["Nervous?","A little. But it's out of my hands."],
  ["What do you want from tomorrow?","A fair wage, a quiet harbour, and a chair."],
  ["Thanks for the chats.","Anytime. Mind the dock edge."]],
 "Look after yourself, reporter."],
};
// Gustavo, Don of the Crime Syndicate, holding court at his table in The Club, Hope Springs (Crime Syndicate). Threads: courtesy
// and the slights he never forgets (above all Dario's), his old friendship with the late king, Carlo and the price of
// respectability, Mario in his cell, the lemon tree he is trying to keep alive through a northern winter.
DAILY['Gustavo']={
6:["(Gustavo does not get up. He gestures at the chair opposite, by the fire.) The journalist. Sit. Nobody at this table will bite you, and the brandy is better than anything in London.",[
  ["Thank you. It's a fine club.","It was a hunting lodge, once. Rich men shooting birds. Now rich men come here to lose at cards to me. Progress."],
  ["What exactly do you do, sir?","I listen. People come to this table with problems, and they go away without them. It's a kind of public service. Don't put the word 'public' in your paper."],
  ["Is it true you run the island's rackets?","(He smiles, without any hurry.) Who told you that? No, don't tell me. I'll find out, and I'll send them a nice bottle."]],
 "Come back whenever you like. The fire is always lit."],
7:["(Gustavo is turning a small lemon in his fingers.) Look at this, journalist. My lemon tree, in the glasshouse behind the villa. The first fruit, in September, this far north. They told me it couldn't be done.",[
  ["Who told you?","A gardener from Kingsgrad. He works somewhere else now. (He sniffs the lemon.) Somewhere warmer."],
  ["Where does a lemon tree come from, up here?","Sorrento, as a seedling, in a crate of olive oil. Forty years ago. It's older than most of the men in this room, and better company."],
  ["One lemon. Hardly a harvest.","Everything starts with one. One bookie, one boat, one favour. Then you look round and you own a glasshouse."]],
 "Mind the gate as you go. Paola likes it shut."],
8:["The Church wanted me in a pew this morning, for the old king's sermon. I sent flowers instead. God and I have an arrangement: I don't bother Him, and He doesn't bother me.",[
  ["You knew the king?","Fifty years. He came to my father's restaurant as a boy, through the kitchen door, so his tutor wouldn't see. He liked his eggs with too much pepper."],
  ["There's talk of trouble out at the oil rig.","(He sips his brandy.) Is there. Then Dario will be shouting at somebody. He shouts. A man who shouts has already lost the argument."],
  ["An arrangement with God? Sounds like protection.","(Gustavo laughs, once, very softly.) You see? You understand this island already."]],
 "Go carefully. The road down to Kingsgrad is still ice in the shade."],
9:["(A waiter refills Gustavo's glass without being asked.) Everyone is suddenly making cola, journalist. The Party, the chemists. There's a war on, and it's fizzy.",[
  ["Would you ever go into cola?","I'm in everything, a little. But cola is sugar and water and a man in a cup costume. I prefer businesses where the customer comes back because he has to."],
  ["What's your view of Phil and Kelp Cola?","Phil is a neighbour. He waves over the hedge. His family has a secret in a safe somewhere, and they think it makes them a dynasty. Every family has a safe."],
  ["The Communists want to brew their own. Worried?","The Communists want to brew everything themselves. Then they find out who owns the bottles."]],
 "Try the olives on the bar before you go. They came the long way round."],
10:["(Gustavo is in black, with a black tie, and he looks older.) I have just come from the Royal Chapel. They buried my old friend in the cold. He'd have hated it. He always said he wanted to be buried somewhere with oranges.",[
  ["I'm sorry. Were you close?","Close enough to keep each other's secrets, and far enough that nobody wrote about it. That's the best kind of friendship. (He looks into the fire.)"],
  ["Who did you see talking at the funeral?","Everybody, to everybody, about everything but the dead man. Dario was looking at his watch. I noticed. I notice these things."],
  ["Busy day for you otherwise?","(He looks at you for a moment.) A little business, in the morning. Business doesn't stop for funerals. The king understood that better than his children do."]],
 "Leave me with him a while, journalist. Come back another day."],
11:["(Gustavo has a ledger open beside his glass, though he isn't reading it.) Rosaria sends Mario a pizza in his cell every week. This week she sent two. He asked for anchovies. I'm told the sergeant ate one.",[
  ["How is Mario?","Patient. He said nothing to the police, so the police have nothing to say to him. He'll come out to a very good dinner, and a better job."],
  ["Why is Mario in jail, exactly?","A betting ring, they say. Somebody had to sit in a cell for it, so that other people didn't. He volunteered. I don't forget volunteers."],
  ["Does the sergeant play cards with him?","Every night. Mario lets her win, on Tuesdays. A small kindness costs nothing and buys everything."]],
 "If you pass the police station, say good afternoon to Mario. From a friend."],
12:["Carlo was here this morning, in a new suit, practising his handshake on my waiter. (Gustavo shakes his head.) The waiter was very good about it.",[
  ["Is Carlo a good candidate?","Carlo is a beautiful candidate. He smiles, he listens, he says 'lower taxes'. Underneath, he does what he's told. That's what makes him beautiful."],
  ["Why does the Syndicate want the Republicans?","Because a party is a respectable thing, and a respectable thing can sign papers. I've been signing things for forty years. I'd like it to be legal."],
  ["Will he beat Harald and Magnus?","Harald has a cola company and Magnus has an oil company. Carlo has me. I'd bet on Carlo. In fact, I have."]],
 "Tell Carlo, if you see him, that the tie was a mistake."],
13:["(Gustavo is peeling his lemon with a little silver knife.) Everyone wants a recipe, journalist. My mother's ragù, the Kelp Cola formula. People think a recipe is a secret. It isn't. It's a habit.",[
  ["What's in your mother's ragù?","Time. Six hours of it. And she never let anyone watch. (He smiles.) Not even my father. Especially not my father."],
  ["You're interested in Kelp Cola's formula?","I'm interested in everything other people lock up. It's a professional curiosity. Write that I take an interest in local industry."],
  ["A silver knife, for a lemon?","My father said a man should use the right tool for everything, even small things. Especially small things. People watch your hands."]],
 "There's a slice for you on the saucer. Don't waste it."],
14:["(The Club is quiet, and Gustavo is alone at his table, which is unusual.) Tomorrow, the day after, the island will remember today. I want to remember it from a good chair.",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","Speeches. Promises. Somebody will cheer, somebody will sulk. And afterwards, everyone will come to this table to see what I thought."],
  ["Will the Syndicate make itself heard?","The Syndicate doesn't shout. It sits in the room, and the room knows it's there. That's louder than any speech."],
  ["Alone, Don? No court today?","Everyone's out, making history. I'm old: I've made enough of it. I'll read about it in your paper."]],
 "Go and watch, journalist. Somebody should write it down properly."],
15:["(Gustavo is reading the Eastbrook Gazette, holding it at arm's length.) The palace and the monastery, at each other's throats over which brother wears the crown. Families. It's always families.",[
  ["Which brother would you choose?","Leopold likes good wine and bad company. Augustus likes prayers. I can do business with a man who likes wine."],
  ["Did the old king have a favourite?","He had a favourite dog, a favourite chair, and a favourite cigar. Of his children he never said. That was wise."],
  ["You read the Gazette? I'm flattered.","I read everything, journalist. Especially the bits nobody else reads. The shipping times. The obituaries. The small print."]],
 "Fold the paper the right way when you've done with it. Nobody ever does."],
16:["The Democrats and the Communists, debating in public, like gentlemen. (Gustavo raises his glass.) I hope they enjoy it. Words are free, and that's what they're worth.",[
  ["Don't you believe in a vote?","I believe in votes. I've bought several. A vote is a very honest thing: you always know what it cost."],
  ["Who'll win the debate?","Whoever's last to speak. The audience remembers the last thing it heard. That's why I always speak last, and quietly."],
  ["You'd do well in a debate.","I've never lost an argument in my life. I've lost some people who argued with me, but that's a different thing."]],
 "Close the door on your way out. The draught comes all the way from Moscow."],
17:["(The glasses on the bar chime faintly, and Gustavo puts a hand flat on the table until they stop.) The ground moved, journalist. Did you feel it? Sorrento has the same trouble. Old ground, with a temper.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say it's been asleep for three hundred years. So had I, once, before somebody owed me money."],
  ["What's the news in Frostholm today?","Dario's people are running about like hens. Somebody's late for something. (He shrugs.) It's not my business what a busy man does with his diary."],
  ["Are you worried by the shaking?","At my age, the ground moving is the least of my worries. Though I've told Paola to put a cushion under the brandy."]],
 "Walk gently on your way down. The island has a headache."],
18:["(Gustavo has his lemon tree's one leaf in an envelope, curled and brown.) The glasshouse cracked a pane in the shaking, and the cold came in. One leaf so far. I've had men watch the tree all night.",[
  ["Will the tree live?","It came through a crate, a boat and forty winters. It's tougher than it looks. Like me. Like Mario."],
  ["Men watching a tree? Seriously?","My tree. They watch it. They don't complain. (He tucks the envelope in his breast pocket.) Nobody here complains."],
  ["Heard any news from the monastery?","The monks lost something, I hear. They're turning the mountain over for it. If anyone on this island finds it, it won't be the monks."]],
 "Keep warm, journalist. The cold gets in through the smallest crack."],
19:["Rita sings at the Midnight Note on Thursdays. I go with Paola, and sit at the back. (He smiles.) She sings 'Stormy Weather' for me, and pretends she doesn't know who I am.",[
  ["You like jazz, Don?","I like a singer who knows the song is sad and sings it anyway. That's courage. The saxophone man is good too. He doesn't talk. I admire it."],
  ["Is Rita a friend of yours?","Rita is nobody's friend, which is why everyone loves her. I sent her flowers once. She sent them back with a note: 'Thank you, no.' I respected that."],
  ["Will anyone important be in the club?","Somebody always is, in Frostholm. I don't look at faces in a jazz club. It's bad manners. You should learn that."]],
 "Go and hear her, if you have an evening. Sit at the back."],
20:["(Gustavo is looking at a book of photographs of the Royal Palace.) Look at this, journalist. The king's grandfather's snuffbox. Gold, with a sapphire. My father saw it once, and talked about it until the day he died.",[
  ["Did you ever see it?","Once, at a dinner, across a table. The king took snuff and sneezed on the Swedish ambassador. A great night for the island."],
  ["Why the interest in the palace?","I'm an old man who likes beautiful things. The palace has a great many beautiful things, and not enough people looking after them."],
  ["You're not planning a burglary, Don?","(He closes the book gently.) I'm planning lunch. Rosaria's sending up a calzone. Burglary is a young man's business."]],
 "Go on with you. And keep your opinions about snuffboxes to yourself."],
21:["(Ash is settling on the windows of the Club, and Gustavo hasn't moved from his chair.) The mountain has opened, journalist. The members ran for their cars. I finished my brandy.",[
  ["Aren't you afraid?","I was born under Vesuvius. My grandmother made coffee the day it went up, in 1944, and served it to the Germans. You don't run from a mountain. You make it wait."],
  ["Is everyone in Hope Springs safe?","Paola went round every door. Nobody's hurt. One of the members fainted into the topiary. He's been telling everyone it was a dive."],
  ["What does this do to business?","Everybody needs something tonight that the shops don't have. Candles, glass, a boat. Business, journalist, is what happens when the ground moves."]],
 "Go home, and stay home. That is not advice. It's a kindness."],
22:["(A boy is sweeping ash off the Club's terrace, and Gustavo watches him critically.) Grey. Everything grey. Even the hedges look like Communists today.",[
  ["How's the lemon tree?","Covered in ash, so I washed every leaf myself, with a sponge, this morning. A Don with a sponge. Don't print it."],
  ["Who's helping clear up?","The Syndicate's men are clearing roads in Kingsgrad, for nothing. People notice who comes with the shovels. That's worth more than a speech."],
  ["Did the volcano reach the north?","Lava in the north, nobody dead. A miracle, the monks will say. I'd call it luck. Luck is less arrogant."]],
 "Brush your shoulders before you come in next time. You're bringing in the mountain."],
23:["Mario wrote to me. (Gustavo unfolds a letter written in pencil.) He says the walls of his cell shook so hard the sergeant let him sit at her desk. They played cards under it.",[
  ["He sounds in good spirits.","Mario is always in good spirits. It's why he's the right man for a cell. A gloomy man in a cell starts talking."],
  ["When will he be released?","When it's convenient. For him, for me, for the court. A good lawyer is coming over from Helsinki, when the boats are running properly."],
  ["You read his letters? Isn't that private?","He writes them for me. He addresses them 'Dear Uncle'. I'm not his uncle. Everyone on this island needs an uncle."]],
 "If you see Mario, tell him his uncle says hold on."],
24:["(Gustavo is cleaning his little silver knife with a napkin, very carefully.) Some days, journalist, there's a mess, and somebody has to tidy it. I don't like mess. I never have.",[
  ["What kind of mess?","The kind that the ash leaves. (He smiles.) What else would I mean?"],
  ["Are the roads clear yet?","The south, yes. The north is still smoking. The monks have gone up the mountain to pray at it. Good luck to them."],
  ["You make tidying sound sinister.","Everything an old man says sounds sinister. I said I liked my napkin folded, once, and three waiters resigned."]],
 "Wipe your feet. You'll find most of the island on them."],
25:["Leopold and his sister are both promising the people something. (Gustavo turns his glass.) A crown for everyone, a vote for everyone. Nobody promises the people what they want, which is a quiet life and a good pizza.",[
  ["Who do you want on the throne?","Whoever keeps the harbour open and the police underpaid. I've no feelings about crowns. My grandfather sold them to tourists."],
  ["Is Daphne serious about democracy?","Daphne is young. When I was young I was serious about everything. Then I learnt to be serious about very few things, and very serious about them."],
  ["Why not stand for king yourself?","(He laughs, properly this time.) A king has to wave from a balcony. I have a bad shoulder. Besides, a king works for everyone."]],
 "My regards to the palace, if you go. They know where I am."],
26:["(Gustavo is reading a property notice, his lips tight.) Dario is buying the spa. The hot springs, at the foot of a mountain that has just exploded. He has money, that man, but no sense. And no manners.",[
  ["What did Dario do to you?","Twelve years ago, at a dinner in this very room, he called my father's restaurant a 'spaghetti shack'. In front of everyone. He has forgotten. I haven't."],
  ["Is the spa a bad investment?","A spa in the ash? Saga will be scrubbing the steps for a year. But the springs are hot, I'll give him that. Everything in his life is somebody else's heat."],
  ["Twelve years is a long grudge.","A grudge is like wine, journalist. You put it down, you leave it in the dark, and one day you open it with friends."]],
 "Be kind to Saga at the spa, if you go. She'll need friends."],
27:["(The Club is half empty: the members have all gone to Kingsgrad.) The tournament, journalist. The back room at Rosaria's, every chair filled. Everyone who matters, at one table. I'll be dealing the first hand myself.",[
  ["Who's playing?","People with money, and people who think they can read faces. Nico thinks he can read anyone. Nico has been wrong about me for six years."],
  ["Why hold a tournament now?","Because the island's about to decide something, and I'd like to see everyone's hands first. Cards tell you who a man is. Speeches tell you who he wants to be."],
  ["Do you ever lose at poker?","Every week, on purpose, to the right people. Losing to the right person is the best investment a man can make."]],
 "Go to Rosaria's. Watch the hands, not the faces."],
28:["(Gustavo's table is covered with notes and telephone messages, and he's reading none of them.) Today every faction on the island makes its big push. Shouting, marching, flags. And I sit here.",[
  ["Isn't the Syndicate pushing too?","The Syndicate pushes every day, gently, in many places. When everyone else is shouting, you can hear a whisper very clearly."],
  ["What are the messages?","People asking what I think. (He pushes them aside.) I think tomorrow is the Republican vote, and I'd like everyone in this room to remember it."],
  ["You look tired, Don.","I'm seventy-three. I've looked tired since the war. It frightens people more than looking fierce."]],
 "Go and watch the marches. Bring me back the gossip."],
29:["(Gustavo is wearing his best suit, the one he wore to the funeral, with a red carnation.) The Republicans choose their leader today, journalist. Carlo or the cola man or the oil man. I put on a flower for luck.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I don't get nervous. I make arrangements, and then I wait to see whether other people kept them. That's a different feeling. It's quieter."],
  ["What will you do if Carlo loses?","Have a very long lunch. Then remember everyone who voted the wrong way. I have an excellent memory."],
  ["A red carnation. Very Italian.","My mother pinned one on my father every election day in Sorrento. He never won anything. She said it wasn't about winning."]],
 "Go to Frostholm and watch the ballot box. Count carefully."],
30:["(The fire is lit, the brandy is poured, and Gustavo is sitting very straight.) The last day, journalist. Tomorrow this island belongs to somebody. Whoever it is, they'll come to this table eventually. They always do.",[
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Water the lemon tree. Visit Mario. Have a pizza at Rosaria's. Whatever crown sits on whichever head, a man still needs lunch."],
  ["Will you miss the old king?","Every day. He was the last man on this island who called me by my first name and wasn't afraid of me. (He raises his glass to the window.)"],
  ["Has it been a good month for you?","Ask me in ten years. A month is nothing. I measure in decades, and in favours."]],
 "Goodbye, journalist. Remember: you always know where the back room is."],
};
// Hannu, newsstand vendor in Saltwick (no faction). Threads: he knows the headlines before they're printed, changes his opinion with every customer, his feud with Olga, rumours, cigarettes, the ash and what he'll print about it.
DAILY['Hannu']={
1:["(Hannu snaps his paper shut and beams.) A reporter! Splendid. Colleague, in a manner of speaking. I sell the news. You write it. Between us we're an industry.",[
  ["What's the headline today?","'King Dead, Island Mourns.' It was true yesterday and it'll be true tomorrow. A solid story. Keeps its shape."],
  ["Do you read what you sell?","Before it's printed. I hear it in the pub. The pub's the first edition."],
  ["Who runs the island next?","Well. (He looks left, then right.) That depends who's asking. Who's asking?"]],
 "Buy a paper. It's good for the island and very good for me."],
2:["(Hannu shuffles his papers.) Kingsgrad's open. That means a thick Gazette, and a thicker rumour. A man from the palace was seen buying a very large bouquet.",[
  ["How do you know?","A man told me who told a man. It's all very above board."],
  ["Is it true?","It will be, by Friday. Truth's a matter of timing."],
  ["Who do you think will win the island?","Whoever I last spoke to. It changes every few minutes. It's the beauty of the trade."]],
 "If you hear anything, I'll pay in cigarettes."],
3:["(Hannu taps a cigarette on his thumbnail.) I've a view on the Communists. Strong. Very firm. And then Olga walked by and told me what she thought, and I've lost it.",[
  ["What was your view?","That the Party's the future. Or the past. I forget which. It was a very compelling case."],
  ["Does Olga change your mind?","She doesn't mean to. She just shouts, and I find I've agreed."],
  ["Would you ever print what you don't believe?","Constantly. It's the only way to be fair."]],
 "Buy a smoke. They don't change their minds."],
4:["(Hannu holds a page up to the light.) I've the Gazette's headline for tomorrow already. It's 'Mystery Man at the Hotel'. Don't ask me which hotel. It hasn't happened yet.",[
  ["How can you know before it's happened?","A good newsagent doesn't wait for events. He lays the table."],
  ["Is that honest?","It's prophetic. There's a difference, and I charge for it."],
  ["Who's the mystery man?","Everyone. That's the beauty of a mystery. It fits whoever walks in."]],
 "If a stranger asks for me, I'm at the harbour."],
5:["(Hannu leans in.) The monks have asked for twenty papers. Monks! They've been reading the Gazette in secret for years. I'd say that's the biggest scoop of my career, but I'm sworn to silence.",[
  ["Which monk reads the paper?","I couldn't say. Though the one who reads the horse racing has a very straight back."],
  ["Do the monks buy cigarettes?","Not a one. They're a model of restraint, apart from the horses."],
  ["What else do you hear from the mountain?",["A rumour of an old relic gone missing. A very old one. The kind that makes grown men go quiet.",[
    ["What kind of relic?","Something to do with a saint. A hand, a bone, a finger. I wouldn't print it. Not yet. I'm saving it for a quiet week."],
    ["Is it true?","True enough to sell a paper. And what's true enough is true."]]]]],
 "If you hear of a relic, you heard it from a reliable source. Me."],
6:["(Hannu reads aloud.) 'Seven Parties, One Island.' It's a good line. The editor stole it from me. I said it in the pub in July.",[
  ["Do you write for the Gazette?","In spirit. I send in suggestions. They send back no, but they use them."],
  ["Which faction is the best story?","The Syndicate. It's the one I'm not allowed to write about. That's the sign of a good story."],
  ["Which paper do you like?","The one that sells. I'm a man of the market."]],
 "Come back Friday. I'll have the news by then, probably accurate."],
7:["(Hannu has a pile of new papers still warm from the press.) Every road's open. Everyone's in the street. I've sold forty in an hour. If it carries on like this I'll retire to Spain.",[
  ["Do you want to retire to Spain?","I want to retire to a place where the news is good and the cigarettes are cheap. I think that's Spain."],
  ["What's the news from Frostholm?","Oil. Money. A lot of men in very good boots. I've got a feeling about Dario."],
  ["What feeling?","He's got that look. Of a man who's got something to lose. I'd bet a paper on it."]],
 "I'll keep a copy for you. It's the least a colleague can do."],
8:["(Hannu has the radio on a sermon and is reading the racing page.) Sunday. The sermon's very good, but the odds in the three-thirty are better.",[
  ["What does the Church say?","That the world is a trial. Which I know. I've a newsstand."],
  ["Is the Church in the news?","Always. Father Thyme can't write his sermon, which is the best headline of the week. I'm holding it for the right moment."],
  ["Do you go to church?","For the weddings. The sandwiches are excellent."]],
 "A good sermon is a headline in disguise."],
9:["(Hannu has a bottle of cola on the stand.) A boy left it. 'The People's Cola' says the label. The Party's. Before that, it was Kelp. I've never seen so many labels on one liquid.",[
  ["Which cola is best?","I haven't decided. It depends who's watching."],
  ["Is it going to be a story?","It's already a story. 'Cola Wars Reach Saltwick'. I've a draft."],
  ["Do you drink it?","Only on Thursdays. It's a rule."]],
 "Don't repeat what I think. It will be different tomorrow."],
10:["(Hannu has tied a black ribbon to the stand.) A funeral. The old king was a regular reader. Well, not of mine. Of the sports page, I think. His man bought it.",[
  ["What will the papers say?","'A Nation Mourns.' It writes itself. It's the same headline as always, with a different name."],
  ["Did you ever meet him?","I saw him once, from a distance. He seemed shorter than the stamps."],
  ["What next for the island?","Well. (He lowers his voice.) A very great deal of uncertainty. Which is very good for circulation."]],
 "I'm trying to look sad. Is it working?"],
11:["(Hannu is shaking his head over a stack of papers.) Pay disputes at the mill. Alms at the church. If you want a quiet week, buy a different island.",[
  ["Who's right about the mill?","The workers, till I speak to the owners, and then the owners. Then I get tired and have a cigarette."],
  ["Is that a good way to run a stand?","It's the only way. A man with a settled view sells half the papers."],
  ["Do you vote?","On Thursdays. It keeps me honest."]],
 "Try not to have an opinion. It's exhausting."],
12:["(Hannu taps the counter.) A record's gone missing in Eastbrook, they say. Or it's about to. A rare one. I've been told by three people, all of whom said it in confidence.",[
  ["Who has the record?","Ruby, the shop woman. Everyone wants it, and nobody will say why. A mystery wrapped in a sleeve."],
  ["Is it a story?","It's a very good one. A record that's worth a fortune. I'll hold it until it's worth the ink."],
  ["Who wants it?","Oil, I hear. They've a taste for music, or a taste for what's hidden in it."]],
 "Keep it quiet. It's front page when it's ripe."],
13:["(Hannu hands a free bottle to a child.) Kelp Cola's sample day. They've taken over half the quay. I'm all for free things. I've told them my stand is available for a modest fee.",[
  ["Are they paying you?","In bottles. I'd rather have cash, but it's a start."],
  ["What does Olga think?","She threw a bottle at a gull. The gull took it badly."],
  ["Is it a good day for business?","The best since the funeral. A crowd's a crowd, whatever the reason."]],
 "I'll write it up as 'Cola Conquers Saltwick'. It has a ring."],
14:["(Hannu has no papers on the stand, only a handwritten sign: 'Back Soon. Gone to Make History.') It's the big day, and I'm taking a bus. I'll be reporting. In my head.",[
  ["Will you go?","I'd never miss it. The newsstand will keep. The history won't."],
  ["What do you expect?","The whole island in one place. A hundred opinions per square yard. I'll come back with nine of my own."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm thrilled. A man like me waits his whole life for a day like this. Even if I don't know what it is."]],
 "Don't tell Olga I've gone. She'll want to come."],
15:["(Hannu's reading from a folded paper.) Crown before Cross. Cross before Crown. I've written both headlines. I'll print whichever is true by Friday.",[
  ["Which is it?","It depends who's loudest. Or who's richest. Or who has the better choir."],
  ["Who do you favour?","I'm neutral. Which is to say, I favour everybody, until I'm asked to pay."],
  ["Will there be a fight?","I'd say so. It's the sort of argument that ends with a bishop on the ground."]],
 "If you see a bishop on the ground, tell me. I'll need a photograph."],
16:["(Hannu has a radio under his arm.) The Great Debate. I've half a mind to go and heckle. I haven't decided which side. I'll heckle both and see who flinches.",[
  ["Who'll win?","The Party's strong on slogans. The Democrats are strong on tea. It's very close."],
  ["Which would you vote for?","Ask me after the sandwiches."],
  ["Will you print it?","Every word. And several they didn't say."]],
 "I'll have the early edition. By midnight, if I'm lucky."],
17:["(Hannu steadies a stack of papers as it trembles.) The ground's moving. I wrote that on Tuesday, before it started. I'd like to know who told me.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","The old men say so. I say it's a story that's been waiting for the right ground."],
  ["Where's Dario?","Nobody knows. 'Where's Dario?' It's the question of the week. I've printed it in a very large font."],
  ["Are you worried?","A little. But a good disaster sells papers, and I'm ashamed to say I'm thrilled."]],
 "If the mountain blows, I'll have the first edition. I've already written it."],
18:["(Hannu is cleaning his glasses.) The monastery's lost a relic, I hear. 'The Lost Relic.' It's a good title. I've kept it by for a month.",[
  ["Who lost it?","The monks. Or somebody who borrowed it. Or the relic's gone for a walk. They're all good lines."],
  ["Will you print it?","Gently. A relic's a delicate matter. It's a headline that comes with a prayer."],
  ["Is it valuable?","Priceless. Which means somebody's put a price on it."]],
 "If you hear any more, I'll buy it. In cigarettes."],
19:["(Hannu has pinned a poster on his stand.) 'Comrades Wanted.' The Party's recruiting. I said to the man, I'm a neutral observer. He said, so were we all, until we weren't.",[
  ["Are you joining?","I'm considering it. For a week. Then I'll consider something else."],
  ["Is the Party popular?","In Saltwick, among the young. And the old ones who like a march."],
  ["What does Olga say?","That I'm a fool. She's right, but it doesn't help."]],
 "If you see a poster that says 'Reporters Wanted', that's mine. I put it up."],
20:["(Hannu is lowering his voice, scanning the street.) There's a man at the Hotel Aurora. A stranger. He's talking to the Democrats. I've three theories and a headline.",[
  ["Who is he?","A foreigner, a spy or a bank manager. In this business, they're hard to tell apart."],
  ["Will you print it?","Not yet. I'll wait until it's true. Or until it's interesting. Whichever comes first."],
  ["What's the headline?","'Man at the Hotel'. It's short, which is the secret of a good headline."]],
 "Not a word to Olga. She'll put it in the fish."],
21:["(Hannu stands in a grey blizzard, holding a sheet of newsprint over his head.) She's blown! The mountain! I'm in the middle of the biggest story of my life, and I've no paper. It's all gone grey.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. A cough. A great deal of grit. And a front page that's writing itself."],
  ["What will the headline be?","'Volcano!' With an exclamation mark, and a second one if the editor lets me."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, thank God. The wind went the other way. It's the best news I've ever had, and the worst for circulation."]],
 "Buy a paper. It's evidence. It might be the last one."],
22:["(Hannu is wiping ash off a headline.) Everything's grey, including the news. 'Ash Over Sarivalto'. It's a good line, but nobody's reading. They're sweeping.",[
  ["Are people buying?","In bulk. For wrapping, for lighting, for covering windows. I've found a new use for the Gazette."],
  ["What's the mood?","Tired. Dusty. And a little defiant. It's rather moving, if you've the eyes for it."],
  ["Is Olga all right?","She's shouting at the ash. It's not working, but it's good for morale."]],
 "The news is a lot lighter than the ash. That's something."],
23:["(Hannu cups his hands to light a cigarette in the wind.) They say the Party's got a spanner in the works. And the oil lot are doing deep repairs. I don't know what any of it means, but it's a very good page.",[
  ["What's going on?","Sabotage, or repairs. Or it's the same thing seen from different hats."],
  ["Do you trust the Party?","On Tuesdays and Fridays. The rest of the week I trust the oil."],
  ["What would you print?","'Spanner in Works.' It could mean anything. It's perfect."]],
 "If you see a spanner, don't touch it. It's evidence."],
24:["(Hannu is stacking papers in a wheelbarrow.) The monks are leaving the mountain. The Syndicate's doing a clean-up. I've two headlines, and one of them is true.",[
  ["Which one?","I'll tell you when I know. The monks, I think. The Syndicate doesn't clean anything it can't charge for."],
  ["Are the monks safe?","They say so. They've faith, and a very large mule."],
  ["Are you worried about the news?","I'm worried it'll run out. And then I'll have to make it up."]],
 "Buy two copies. One for reading, one for the fire."],
25:["(Hannu stands very straight, as if on a podium.) A crown for everyone! A princess for democracy! It's the best offer I've heard all month. Of course, I thought the same about the cola.",[
  ["Do you support it?","Wholeheartedly. Until I speak to the other side."],
  ["Who's the princess?","I've three guesses. One of them's a cow. It's a long story."],
  ["Is it a good story?","It's the best. It's a story with a hat in it."]],
 "I'll print both versions and let the people decide. That's democracy."],
26:["(Hannu holds up a bottle with a prince on the label.) 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' I'd call it a lie, but it's very nice packaging. I'll write it up as a trend.",[
  ["Is it selling?","Like a rumour. Everyone has one, and nobody can say where it came from."],
  ["Is the prince real?","Leopold, I think. The one with the hair. He signed off on it, or he didn't. There's a story either way."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola winning?","In the shops. The island's another matter."]],
 "Take a bottle. If you find a prince, I want an exclusive."],
27:["(Hannu has tickets sticking out of his pocket.) 'Revolution at the Pictures' tonight, and 'High Stakes' at the card table. I've been invited to both. I'll go to the one that has food.",[
  ["Which has food?","The pictures. The cards have only whisky. And whisky's not a meal, it's a mistake."],
  ["Who's inviting you?","The Party, for the pictures. A man in a good coat for the cards. Both said it was a favour."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a newsagent. I've no stakes. Unless the odds are good."]],
 "I'll tell you which was better. Probably the sandwiches."],
28:["(Hannu has hung every flag he can find along the stand.) The big push, then. Every faction's out. I've sold a paper to each of them and a different opinion to every one.",[
  ["Who's winning the day?","I'd say the Party in the morning and the cola by lunch. Ask me tomorrow."],
  ["Are you excited?","I've never been so busy. I've run out of headlines. I'm making new ones."],
  ["Do you think the island will hold?","It's held for a thousand years. It's not going to give up for a headline."]],
 "Buy one. It's a historic day, and I'm a historic man."],
29:["(Hannu stands at the stand, watching the road.) Tomorrow's the end, one way or another. I've a headline for each outcome. I'll print whichever happens. It's journalism.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who's still talking at midnight. It's always the one with the most stamina."],
  ["Are you ready?","The stand's polished, the papers are stacked. I've two cigarettes left and a lot of hope."],
  ["Any last words?","Buy early. After tomorrow, nobody will want yesterday's news."]],
 "Get some sleep. It's a long day."],
30:["(Hannu has the paper folded under his arm and is unusually still.) The last day. I've two headlines and a hundred papers. Whatever's printed, remember: I knew it first.",[
  ["How's the month been?","The best of my life. I've been wrong forty times and right twice. It's a very good ratio."],
  ["Any advice?","Print what you see. Not what you hope. Not what you fear. Just what you see. It's very hard."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come back soon. And bring a scoop."]],
 "Take a paper for the road. It's yesterday's, but it's free."],
};
// Harald, Kelp Cola executive and the company's candidate for Republican Party leader, Kelp Cola HQ (Kelp Cola). Threads: his smile
// and speeches, the race against Magnus and Carlo, Phil and Kurt, life alone in Hope Springs, a worry he keeps behind the smile.
DAILY['Harald']={
3:["(A tall man in a pale suit rises with a hand already out.) Harald. Kelp Cola. You'll be the English journalist: Phil said to look out for you. Sit, sit. Cola? Naturally.",[
  ["Thanks. Lovely office.","It's Phil's, really, but I'm a guest with a key. (He smiles with every tooth.) We're very proud of the view of the harbour."],
  ["You're standing for the Republicans?","I am. The party has no leader, and the island needs a steady hand. What's good for Kelp Cola is good for Sarivalto. I'd say that to the king."],
  ["Is there anyone who isn't pleased?","There's always someone. I've learned to smile at them the longest."]],
 "Do take a bottle for the road. The fridge never runs out."],
4:["(Harald is reading a typed speech, moving his lips.) 'My friends, the island stands at a crossroads.' Does that sound like a crossroads, or a roundabout? Be honest.",[
  ["It sounds like a speech.","Wonderful, that's what I'm after. Speeches that sound like speeches. People don't trust a politician who sounds like a human being."],
  ["Who writes your speeches?","Kurt, mostly, and I improve them. Kurt writes like a man hurrying for a tram."],
  ["Why do you want to lead the Republicans?","Because somebody must, and because I'd do it well. There's a party, a ballot box and a podium, and not a soul to stand at it. It's an opportunity, you see."]],
 "Excuse me. I must practise looking sincere. It takes some time."],
5:["(Harald stands at the window, adjusting his cuffs.) The roads are open, and the first thing I did was drive to Hope Springs to check the cat was alive. It was. I've never been so pleased to see a creature in my life.",[
  ["You've got a cat?","A grey one called Chairman. Don't ask. He sits on my speeches. It's the best review they get."],
  ["Is it lonely in Hope Springs?","Everything's so very tidy there. The Colonel next door salutes me every morning. I haven't the heart to tell him I'm not an officer."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola worth the effort?","It's a family. Rather a rich one, but a family. Phil's the father, Kurt's the clever son and I'm the cousin with the good coat."]],
 "Chairman will be waiting. He's very particular about supper."],
6:["(Harald is fanning a stack of leaflets in the shape of a peacock's tail.) Do you like it? 'HARALD: A STEADY HAND, A SWEET DRINK'. Kurt hates the slogan. I think it's rather good.",[
  ["It's a bit odd.","Good. Odd's memorable. People remember a bad slogan for years. Ask anyone about the old 'Kelp: It's Fizzy' campaign."],
  ["Who's your competition?","Magnus from the oil company and Carlo, who's backed by the Syndicate. I'm the only one whose backers don't carry a weapon. Or I assume so."],
  ["What does Phil say?","Phil says, 'Win.' Then he takes a drink. That's the whole of the strategy meeting."]],
 "Take a leaflet. Leave it on a bus, if you've the time."],
7:["(Harald raises a glass of cola to the light.) Frostholm's open, which means the Republicans can finally meet. Margit's put out chairs. Magnus has put out brochures. Carlo has put out a man.",[
  ["What do you mean, a man?","A large man with a hat, standing by Carlo's desk. He doesn't speak. He looks at the ballot box as if it owed him money."],
  ["Is Magnus a serious rival?","He's very clever, very cold, and has the oil company's money. Money doesn't lose elections: it just takes a long time to win them."],
  ["Is it a fair race?","Define 'fair'. I'm a businessman. I define fair as 'the result I'd like'."]],
 "A good politician, like a good bottle, should be kept cool."],
8:["(Harald is turning a coin over his knuckles.) The old king's death's a complication. Nobody knows how to canvass in mourning. One mustn't smile too much or too little. It's like walking a tightrope in good shoes.",[
  ["How do you smile at a funeral?","You don't. You tilt the head and look pained. I've been practising in the mirror. Chairman finds it alarming."],
  ["Will the Church affect the race?","Everyone says the Church wants Augustus. I say a vote's a vote. Father Thyme's flock buys a great deal of cola at Easter."],
  ["Does the company wish to see a prince?","It wishes to see profit. If a prince sells the product, then a prince it is. It's nothing personal."]],
 "I'm sorry, I'm distracted. I've never run for anything. Except once, in school, and I came last."],
9:["(Harald is looking at the label of a bottle with great intensity.) There's a new formula, as of this week. I'm to say I love it. I do love it. I love it more every time somebody asks.",[
  ["Do you actually like it?","It's a bit sweeter. I'd say, if pressed, it's a cola that wants to be liked. Like me."],
  ["Who decided to change the formula?","A committee. And Phil. And possibly a lawyer. It went in as a recipe and came out as a policy."],
  ["Is the old taste gone for good?","Wilma at the museum won't hear of it. She glares at me whenever I order a case. I take it as a compliment."]],
 "Don't tell Wilma I said the new one's sweet. She'd write a label about it."],
10:["(Harald wears a very black tie, and speaks very softly.) It's the funeral today. I've not been invited, which is a relief, since I'd have had to be sorry in front of a prince.",[
  ["Did you meet the king?","Twice. He asked me what I did, and I said, 'Business.' He said, 'Ah. That's a good thing to be vague about.' I was very hurt, and he was right."],
  ["How's the campaign?","Paused, out of decency. That means Magnus has two days of free advertising. It's a very cruel way to remember a king."],
  ["How are you?","I'm well. (He gives a perfect smile, which wobbles slightly at the edge.) It's an odd thing, standing in a room full of cola and thinking about mortality."]],
 "A moment's silence is the cheapest thing in politics. And the rarest."],
11:["(Harald holds the telephone away from his ear.) Mill hands. The whole of Eastbrook's marching about with placards. If they strike, the lumber stops. If the lumber stops, our new warehouse stops.",[
  ["Does Kelp Cola buy from the mill?","Boards, crates, shelves. We're one of Dagny's best customers. Which makes this awkward, in a way that I'd rather not discuss."],
  ["Which side are you on?","Mine. I'm a Republican candidate: I believe in the market. I also believe in not having to carry my own crates."],
  ["Are the workers right?","They're loud. Which isn't the same as wrong. I'd like to be paid more myself, but nobody's offered."]],
 "I'd advise you not to quote me. But do quote the cola."],
12:["(Harald has a pile of telegrams on his desk and a look of faint nausea.) Every faction on the island seems to have a reason to write to me. Magnus wants a chat. Carlo wants a drink. The Party wants me to say something nice about collective farming.",[
  ["Will you say it?","I'll say a lot of things. 'I believe in the dignity of all labour' costs nothing. It's the cheapest sentence in the language."],
  ["What does Magnus want?","He says he'd like to 'compare notes'. A man who says that wants your notes and wants you to have none."],
  ["Do you trust anyone on the island?","Chairman. And the woman at the Hope Springs concierge desk, who always remembers my birthday. It's a short list."]],
 "Time for another handshake. One's right hand is a political asset."],
13:["(Harald has a basket of samples under each arm.) Free Samples Day. The company's idea. I am going to hand out cola to anyone who'll take one, and then ask for their vote. It's called 'goodwill'. I call it 'sweet-talking with ice'.",[
  ["Is that legal?","Quite. It's a gift. It's not my fault that it happens to be a gift with a candidate's face on the lid."],
  ["Do people accept it?","Children do. Women of a certain age do. Men in fur collars from Frostholm don't. They look at the bottle as if it might be poisoned."],
  ["Will it win votes?","It'll win smiles. I'm a patient man. A smile's a seed."]],
 "Remember my name. It's easy. It rhymes with 'herald', and I'm in the business of announcing."],
14:["(Harald is adjusting his tie in a window.) The fourteenth. Everyone's being mysterious about it, which is a terrible way to run a nation. I'd rather have a printed agenda.",[
  ["What do you expect?","The island in one place, which I've never seen before. It's a marketer's dream and a security officer's nightmare."],
  ["Will you speak?","If I'm asked. And even if I'm not, I'll find a reason to be near a microphone. It's rather my gift."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I don't feel it, if I don't say it. I'm told that's a form of denial. I find it's a form of employment."]],
 "Wish me luck. And an umbrella, if the weather turns."],
15:["(Harald's smile has gone a shade less polished.) Yesterday was something. I've never been so cheered and so jostled. A man kissed my hand. I'm afraid it was in error.",[
  ["What happened to the man?","He apologised and offered me a herring. I accepted. It was the most honest transaction of the week."],
  ["Did the day go well?","For the company, extremely. For me, the voters liked my coat. For the island, I couldn't say. It's all a bit of a blur."],
  ["Any regrets?","I said 'my fellow islanders' four times. They didn't seem to like it. I've changed it to 'my friends'. It's friendlier and less likely to cause fights."]],
 "I'm going to rest my face. It's tired of being friendly."],
16:["(Harald's reading a printed card.) The debate between the Reds and the Democrats is today. No Republican, no cola man's invited, and I mind. I like to be asked to things I'd refuse.",[
  ["Would you debate Magnus?","In a heartbeat. I'd bring a prop. A bottle of Kelp Cola, with a little flag. He'd bring a barrel of oil. I'd win on charm."],
  ["Who do you expect to win?","Erik's lot, I'd think. They've got the numbers. The Reds have the volume. It's like a sparrow and a trombone."],
  ["Are you worried about the Communists?","I'm worried about anyone who owns a megaphone. Particularly Anna."]],
 "I'll listen from a distance. That's the safest place for a candidate."],
17:["(Harald stops mid-sentence as the cup on the desk trembles.) What on earth was that? My coffee's got ripples. Phil's mug's walked an inch toward the window.",[
  ["It's the ground shaking.","I realise that, thank you. I'm a businessman, not a fool. I just hoped it was the tram."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm insured. (He tries a laugh.) I'm told the north's got a volcano that's a bit rowdy. It's the only part of the island I haven't tried to buy."],
  ["Does it affect the election?","It affects everything. Nothing makes a voter more conservative than the floor shifting under his chair."]],
 "If it happens again, I'm going under the desk. It's a very good desk."],
18:["(Harald is examining his reflection in the fridge door.) I've been told to appear in a new advertisement. Something called 'Inside Kelpy'. They want me to be pleasant, in front of a giant cup.",[
  ["Is that Jaana's cup?","Yes, and don't tell her I said it, but the costume's a bit alarming. It looks at you. And it hasn't got eyes."],
  ["Do you like advertising?","I adore it. Politics is advertising with worse lighting. And no free samples."],
  ["What's the new campaign about?","It's about trust. Which is why the script's marked 'Confidential'. I find the best way to be trusted is to be hard to find."]],
 "Keep it to yourself. There's nothing more tedious than a leaked advertisement."],
19:["(Harald rubs his eyes.) I didn't sleep. The tremors were bad in Hope Springs. The Colonel was on his lawn at three, in a dressing gown and a helmet, with a pair of binoculars.",[
  ["Was the Colonel all right?","He was delighted. It's the first excitement since the war. He saluted me when I came out, and I nearly saluted back."],
  ["Is Hope Springs safe?","It's a gated community, which is to say we're locked in with our problems. The volcano doesn't read the gate."],
  ["Do you mind the quakes?","I mind losing control. I can manage a crowd, a board, a bad review. I can't manage the ground."]],
 "I'll buy a torch. A good torch is a political position."],
20:["(Harald's holding a handkerchief to his nose.) There's sulphur on the breeze. The whole of Kingsgrad smells like a struck match. I've been told it's the springs. I'm told many things.",[
  ["Do you think the volcano's about to go?","I think it's got a sense of theatre. And in my experience, theatre comes with a bang."],
  ["Is the company prepared?","There's a memo. It says 'remain calm'. We've hired a man to say it in the corridors. He's got a very soothing voice."],
  ["Are you?","(He smiles. It's very thin.) I've a suitcase packed and a taxi on call. I'm a man of action, in the sense that I prepare to leave."]],
 "I'll see you on the other side. If there is one. There usually is."],
21:["(Harald is dusted from collar to shoe, and quite still.) I watched it from my balcony. The whole sky turned the colour of a bruise. I said, aloud and to nobody, 'Oh, I see.' It's all I managed.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Perfectly. A little humbled. I'd always supposed the island revolved round Kelp Cola. It turns out it doesn't, particularly."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, I'm told. Which is a miracle I don't intend to question. Chairman is under the sofa and won't come out."],
  ["What now for the campaign?","Cleaning. There's nothing like ash to level a candidate. We're all just the same shade of grey."]],
 "Be careful with your lungs. Take a bottle. The fizz clears the throat. Or so we advertise."],
22:["(Harald is wearing a mask in the corporate colours, and it suits him.) The company's sending cola and blankets to the north. I'll be photographed handing them out. It's a charitable act, and the light's very good.",[
  ["Is that cynical?","It's both. The blankets are warm, the photographs are helpful. I don't see why a good deed has to be shy."],
  ["Do the people in the north want cola?","They want clean water and a roof. We're sending that as well. The cola's for morale."],
  ["Is it working for your campaign?","People remember who turned up. It's why I do. I wish I could say I'd do it with no cameras. I'd like to find out."]],
 "I'll try to take one blanket for myself. It's cold in Hope Springs."],
23:["(Harald pours a drink and doesn't touch it.) The aftershocks go on. The Republican headquarters has a crack down one wall. Margit put a poster over it. It's a very good poster of me.",[
  ["Does the crack worry you?","It's symbolic. A leaderless party with a crack in the wall. Even I couldn't have written it better."],
  ["Is Margit coping?","Margit would cope with the end of the world if it came with an agenda. She asked me whether the volcano had been consulted about the schedule."],
  ["What does Magnus think?","He's calm, as always. The oil company has insurance and a great many geologists. It's rather unfair."]],
 "Rest, if you can. The next few days'll be loud."],
24:["(Harald is sorting through a box of bottle tops.) Wilma sent over a box of historical caps. 'For perspective', the note says. I've been staring at them for an hour. They're not helping.",[
  ["What's on the note?","'Companies end. Cola doesn't.' It's very like her. She's a better philosopher than any of the board."],
  ["Do you believe it?","I'd like to. I've spent my life believing it, and it's been very profitable. But now and then I think about the cap from 1931."],
  ["What's wrong with it?","It's from a cola company that no longer exists. A very nice cap. All that's left of a lot of hope."]],
 "I'll take a cap with me for luck. It might be the one."],
25:["(Harald is in shirtsleeves, which is very unlike him.) I've had enough of ties for one week. I've been through a drawer of them. They're all the same tie.",[
  ["What's bothering you?","The race. Not Magnus or Carlo. The question's why I'm doing it. I've been asked three times today and my answer's changed each time."],
  ["Why are you doing it?","Because Phil asked. Because I was good at it. Because I wanted to be more than a man who smiles in an elevator."],
  ["Is that bad?","It's human. It's inconvenient, in a candidate. I'm hoping it doesn't show."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I've been honest. It would ruin my reputation."],
26:["(Harald is holding a poster of Prince Leopold.) 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' It was Kurt's notion. He thinks it's modern. I think it's an invitation to a lawsuit.",[
  ["Why a prince?","Because the voters like a prince, and the company likes a voter. It's very simple, and very distasteful, which is how marketing works."],
  ["Will Leopold approve?","No one's asked him. It's the great advantage of marketing: you needn't tell anyone."],
  ["Do you want him as king?","I want a king who buys cola. I'm not particular. It's a strangely flexible principle."]],
 "If anyone asks, I never saw the poster. It's a mystery."],
27:["(Harald straightens a stack of papers three times.) The primary's close. The final canvassing's tomorrow and the day after. I've got seventeen speeches and I've forgotten how to say hello.",[
  ["Say hello to me.","Hello. (He smiles, properly, for the first time.) That's better. You're a good listener. It's a risk, for a reporter."],
  ["Do you expect to do well?","I expect to be applauded. It's the one thing a Kelp Cola man always gets. The rest's up to the voters."],
  ["What would you do if you lost?","Go home, feed Chairman, and sleep for two days. It's not an unattractive prospect."]],
 "A good night's sleep is the best campaign tool. Nobody tells you that."],
28:["(Harald's in a fresh suit and a look of enormous calm.) Today's the day the company goes abroad. Kurt's packed the cola. I'm packing the hands I'm going to shake.",[
  ["Are you going with them?","No. I'm needed here, for the party. Phil said, 'Win, and then come and tell me about it.' It's a very businesslike goodbye."],
  ["How do you feel?","Like a bottle before it's opened: fizzing, and with a very firm lid."],
  ["Is the company ready?","It's as ready as it'll ever be. It's got a great many boxes and a very small plan."]],
 "Wish me a steady hand. It's all the slogan promises."],
29:["(Harald is checking his reflection in the glass of a framed advertisement.) The primary's today, or the day after, depending on whom you ask. I've been given three timetables and none agrees. It's very democratic.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I can't tell. I've been smiling for so long I've lost the ability to measure. If my face falls off, tell Margit."],
  ["Is there anything you'd say to the voters?","That I'd try. It's all anyone can offer, and the rest is marketing."],
  ["What do you think of Magnus now?","I think he's lonely, too. He just hides it better. In oil, you're allowed to look grim."]],
 "I'd shake your hand, but I've had a lot of hands today. Yours is the first honest one."],
30:["(Harald is standing very still, with his hands in his pockets.) The last day. I've nothing left to say. It's an odd feeling. For a month, there's been a speech in my mouth.",[
  ["Any last words?","Be kind to people who smile for a living. It's harder than it looks."],
  ["What will you do after today?","Sleep. Feed Chairman. And, if I can, take a bottle of the old cola up to the museum and tell Wilma she was right."],
  ["Was it worth it?","Ask me tomorrow. Today, I'm too busy being a candidate."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Write something kind. Or at least accurate."],
};
// Hekki, radio tech and CIA informant at the Radio Tower (CIA). Threads: he knows the player is NIGHTJAR and is too pleased about it, the hidden room, the night drop packages, his nerves, Kaisa's broadcasts, listening in, the volcano.
DAILY['Hekki']={
1:["(Hekki jumps, then closes the shack door behind you.) You're here. Good. Quiet, quiet. Nobody saw you? The room's ready. Bookcase, third shelf. I'm Hekki, radio. And, well. You know.",[
  ["Nice to meet you, Hekki.","Honoured, really. Nightjar. I've never said it out loud. I'm saying it very softly."],
  ["Keep your voice down.","Yes. Of course. I've been practising whispering. It makes my throat sore."],
  ["Is the radio ready?","It works, mostly. The tubes are old but willing. Chuck's frequency is marked in red pencil."]],
 "Mind the third shelf."],
2:["(Hekki is polishing a valve.) Chuck came through at dawn, thin but clear. He wants you at the palace. I've written it down, in a code I made up. It's not very good.",[
  ["What's the code?","Every letter is the one before it. A becomes Z. I thought of it myself."],
  ["Hekki, that's the oldest code there is.","Is it? Oh. Well, it's reliable."],
  ["Any other news?","The tower hums more when the wind's from the east. I think the island's thinking."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the code."],
3:["(Hekki leans on the workbench.) The cola people are in. Kelp's HQ, and so on. I picked up three telephone conversations by accident. Two were about lunch and one about money. I wrote them all down.",[
  ["You listen in on calls?","Only by accident. Many accidents, as it happens."],
  ["What did the money one say?","Something about a recipe and a safe. I underlined it twice."],
  ["Be careful.","I'm always careful. I just can't stop looking nervous."]],
 "Look calm. Practise it."],
4:["(Hekki dusts a dial.) Eastbrook's open, so I've got two more stations coming in from the mill. A woodpecker on one, I swear. On the other, someone reading the Gazette aloud, very slowly.",[
  ["Henrik?","I think it's Nora, rehearsing. She has a good voice for facts."],
  ["Keep an ear on them.","I do. I eat my lunch next to the speaker."],
  ["Anything from the Democrats?","Erik hums when he thinks he's alone. It's a hymn, I think, or a bus timetable."]],
 "Mind the aerials."],
5:["(Hekki is pale.) Father Thyme's monastery has a rule about silence, so there's nothing to hear from up there. I find that very unsporting.",[
  ["Is the Church a worry?","It's the one place I can't bug. A monastery that doesn't talk is a fortress."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Only to look at the roof. It's a good aerial."],
  ["Any packages at the night drop?","One small parcel. Smelled of peppermint. I haven't opened it. It's for you."]],
 "Don't open the peppermint."],
6:["(Hekki glances at the telephone.) Someone rang this morning for a 'friend'. A stranger on the line, asking you to meet them in the back of some pizzeria. I listened, naturally. Nightjar, it's the Syndicate.",[
  ["Is it safe?","It's a pizzeria. Nothing's safe about a pizzeria."],
  ["Should I go?","You're the agent. I'm the one who's sweating."],
  ["What do you know about them?","Gustavo. Smooth voice, big ring. Very dangerous with cheese."]],
 "Take a spare pencil."],
7:["(Hekki grins nervously.) The north-west road's open, and Chuck says Diversified. I've picked up a few hotel lines at Frostholm. A man named Yuri talks to a tape recorder in Russian. I recorded him recording himself.",[
  ["Yuri's KGB, Hekki.","I know. I thought so. I drew a small hammer and sickle in the margin."],
  ["How much can you hear?","The line, the hallway, and the lift. Mostly the lift."],
  ["Anything useful?","Vodka. A great deal of talk of vodka."]],
 "Burn the margin."],
8:["(Hekki sits straight.) Sunday sermon trouble, folk say. Father Thyme can't find the words. I think I'd have found some for him, if I could talk to him without a mask.",[
  ["Do you ever feel like quitting?","Every day. Then I hear the static and I'm back."],
  ["What did the monastery sound like?","Like a cathedral being very quiet at me."],
  ["Is there anything else to report?","Kaisa plays hymns and jazz in the same hour. It's the only free thing on the island."]],
 "Stay on the air."],
9:["(Hekki is writing.) Two cola factions now, and I've heard three formulas on the line. A man in Kingsgrad was shouting about 'the beetroot'. I don't know what it means.",[
  ["Helen's cola.","Is that it? Beetroot. For the people. It's the worst idea since fish pudding."],
  ["Is it a threat?","Not to the free world. Only to taste."],
  ["Anything about Kelp?","New formula, new ad man. New cup on the label."]],
 "Beetroot, noted."],
10:["(Hekki wears a black armband.) The king's funeral, over in Kingsgrad. I'll broadcast the service on the tower, even though nobody asked. It seems right.",[
  ["Is the tower used for that?","Kaisa will read the names. I'll hold the cable."],
  ["Did you tune in to the Syndicate delivery?","I heard a boat's coming, and a customs officer. Special Delivery, they call it."],
  ["Will it go well?","I've no idea. I'm a spectator with headphones."]],
 "Keep the line open."],
11:["(Hekki is eating a sandwich nervously.) The mill hands want their wages. I got their meeting on the line. Helen said 'Comrades!' eleven times. I counted.",[
  ["Did you pick up anything?","The Democrats will back them, so will the Union. Even Henrik's thinking of a headline."],
  ["Do you support them?","Of course. But I support quietly."],
  ["Should the CIA care?","Chuck says anything that shakes the island is interesting."]],
 "Count carefully."],
12:["(Hekki looks up from a notebook.) The oil company's moving money, I've heard. They use an accounting code, I use an alphabet code, we should have lunch. A man called Magnus is very worried.",[
  ["What does Magnus want?","Some funds put out of Dario's reach. I'd like to put out of Dario's reach a few things myself."],
  ["Is Ruby's record involved?","I picked up a lot of talk about a record. Frostholm wants it, and Ruby doesn't."],
  ["Should I look into it?","You will, Nightjar. You always do."]],
 "Write it down."],
13:["(Hekki is nibbling a fingernail.) Free samples. A lot of rumours about a recipe in a safe. The Syndicate's after it, and I've heard two voices fighting over a pizza.",[
  ["Where's the safe?","Somewhere very good, or very stupid."],
  ["Do you try the free cola?","I take the bottle. I can't drink it. Nerves."],
  ["What's the plan for tomorrow?","The fourteenth. Chuck wants every ear on it."]],
 "Don't spill it."],
14:["(Hekki's hands are shaking slightly.) Tomorrow the big day. I'll keep the receivers hot and send what I can. If anything happens, Chuck will want to hear.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Everything. And nothing. I'm trained for both."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm a flute in a gale."],
  ["Don't worry.","Easy for you. You look like you've done it before."]],
 "Stay tuned."],
15:["(Hekki is bent over a dial.) Crown and cross. The line crackles with it. Two sides, and I've got both on tape, which is very rude of me.",[
  ["What did you hear?","Prince Leopold's people are practising a speech. Prince Augustus's people are praying over it."],
  ["Is that useful?","To Chuck, yes. To me, it's just very loud."],
  ["Do you have a favourite?","I like the one that makes the better static."]],
 "Keep it quiet."],
16:["(Hekki is wearing headphones.) The great debate's on the radio, and I'm taping it. Helen and Erik, back and forth like a tennis match. I've never heard such politeness with such rage.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever blinks last."],
  ["Does the CIA care about debates?","Chuck does. He says words are weapons."],
  ["Do you agree?","I think static is the real weapon."]],
 "Tape it all."],
17:["(Hekki is rigid.) The needle jumped off the dial, Nightjar. Tremors. The tower shook, and my teacup walked right off the bench. Chuck would call it seismic. I call it rude.",[
  ["Is the tower safe?","It's been standing since '48. Fingers crossed."],
  ["Any news about Dario?","He's missing. The oil lines are full of people calling for him. I think he's walked out on his own company."],
  ["Is the volcano real?","Steam, from the north. I've logged it."]],
 "Hold the mast."],
18:["(Hekki looks north.) Steam's thicker. I've a very nice view from the roof. I wish I'd never found out. The Church has lost a relic, too, and I heard a priest swear.",[
  ["Priests swear?","In a whisper. With feeling."],
  ["Is Kelp involved with a cup?","Kelpy. Jaana plays him. I heard her complaining about the costume. It's hot."],
  ["What are you logging?","Everything. I've started a second notebook."]],
 "Log it all."],
19:["(Hekki straightens a stack of tapes.) The Midnight Note has a visitor coming in disguise tonight. Royal, I think. I heard a door. And the comrades are recruiting.",[
  ["Who's coming?","A prince? I won't say. I'd faint."],
  ["Is it safe to go?","The Note is always safe. It's the people outside I worry about."],
  ["How's the Party's recruiting?","Slowly. The posters say 'Comrades Wanted'. The baker came, at least."]],
 "Keep your hat on."],
20:["(Hekki whispers.) Someone's planning a job at the palace. And a man at the Hotel Aurora's been seen. I have two reels on them and neither's clear. Nightjar, it's getting busy.",[
  ["Who's the man?","A stranger. A photographer. Yuri's glad to see him, which is curious."],
  ["Who's doing the palace job?","The Syndicate. They sound like they've done it before."],
  ["Can you warn anyone?","I'm only allowed to warn you."]],
 "Stay low."],
21:["(Hekki is pale and grey with ash.) Nightjar! It erupted. The mast swayed, the dials spun, and the cat left home. Everyone's safe, I think. The sky's very beautiful, and very horrible.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","No. Just rattled. And greyer than before."],
  ["Did you report to Chuck?","The radio's half dead. I got one word through: 'Mountain'. Then static."],
  ["What now?","Clear the aerials, mend the lines, drink something strong."]],
 "Stay inside."],
22:["(Hekki wipes the dial with a cloth.) Ash on everything. The mast looks like a grey candle. I've swept the roof twice, and the ash comes back. It's the island apologising.",[
  ["Is the radio working?","Barely. Kaisa's broadcasting between coughs."],
  ["Any news from the farms?","A roof gave way. Someone's calling for Kelp's help, strangely."],
  ["Is Chuck worried?","Chuck's always worried. It's why he's Chuck."]],
 "Sweep gently."],
23:["(Hekki frowns.) Someone's put a spanner in a very important machine, and I've heard two groups blaming each other. Then Diversified's lot said 'deep repairs' to each other like a password.",[
  ["Is it a code?","It's oil. Same thing."],
  ["Do you know who sabotaged it?","Not yet. But I'm listening. I'm always listening."],
  ["Take it easy.","I'd love to. The ash makes me sneeze."]],
 "Bless you."],
24:["(Hekki looks up.) The monks are off to the peaks, and the clean-up crews are going door to door. I've intercepted one crew's radio. They say 'tidy' in a rather alarming way.",[
  ["Who sent them?","Not the monks. Maybe the Syndicate. They love a clean-up."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Very polite, very thorough. I'm hiding the good tapes."],
  ["Will the retreat go smoothly?","The peaks are cold and holy. It'll be quiet at last."]],
 "Hide the tapes."],
25:["(Hekki's eyes are bright.) A princess for democracy, a crown for everyone. It's on every line. Everyone's talking at once and I'm in heaven. I've never had such good listening.",[
  ["Is there really a princess?","There's talk. I've a tape of someone whispering her name in a lift."],
  ["Is it useful?","To Chuck, perhaps. To me, it's gossip of the finest kind."],
  ["Who's behind it?","The Democrats, and a few unexpected friends."]],
 "Keep listening."],
26:["(Hekki shows a bottle.) Kelp's printed princes on the cola caps. And Dario's buying a spa. I've two reels on the sale and one on the sauna.",[
  ["The sauna?","A man called Magnus sat in it and whistled. Very incriminating."],
  ["Why a spa?","I don't know. To hide in steam, possibly."],
  ["Do you like the new cola?","I like the cap. I keep them."]],
 "Collect them all."],
27:["(Hekki is chewing a pencil.) The Party's showing a revolution at the pictures, and the Syndicate's got a high stakes game on. I can hear both. The cinema has an echo and the card room has a chandelier.",[
  ["What's the high stakes game?","A poker night. A big one. Gustavo's hosting, and someone's cheating."],
  ["Are you going to the pictures?","I'm not allowed. I'm on duty. Which means I'm at home, with headphones."],
  ["Will you watch the film?","Through the wall. I can hear the horses."]],
 "Enjoy the horses."],
28:["(Hekki's voice cracks.) Today everyone's pushing hard. The Party's marching, the palace has a state visit, Kelp's going abroad, and Diversified's... office. I've four radios on at once and I can't tell them apart.",[
  ["Stay calm.","I'm trying. I've a biscuit."],
  ["What should I do?","Whatever you think best. I'm just the ears."],
  ["Is Chuck listening?","Chuck's always listening. Sometimes he even says something."]],
 "Biscuits help."],
29:["(Hekki wrings his hands.) Tomorrow's the end. I've logged everything. I've three notebooks, forty tapes, and one cup of cold tea. Do you think it's enough?",[
  ["It's more than enough.","Thank you. I needed to hear that."],
  ["What will you do after?","Sleep. Or fix the aerial. Probably both."],
  ["Any final reports?","Only that the island's very loud. And very tired."]],
 "Sleep when you can."],
30:["(Hekki stands tall, for once.) The last day, Nightjar. Whatever happens, you did very well. I've never been so proud to be an informant. I'm still terrified, though.",[
  ["Thank you, Hekki.","It's been an honour. Even with the ash."],
  ["Nervous?","I'm vibrating. But in a good way."],
  ["Any last advice?","Keep your head down and your dial up."]],
 "Good luck, Nightjar. Quietly."],
};
// Helen, leader of the Union, at the Party HQ in Saltwick (Communist Party). Threads: the mill hands at Dagny's (11 September),
// the girls on Kelp Cola's bottling line (Aino, Petra, Kurt and the stools), Bob's suppers and the comrades who won't let his
// missing Party card be, and the shop stewards she keeps ready for the end of the month.
DAILY['Helen']={
1:["(Helen is chalking numbers on a blackboard.) Wages, comrade. What the fish plant pays, what the cola factory pays, what the mill pays. Read it and weep.",[
  ["Who pays worst?","The mill. Six years without a rise at Dagny's, and bread's gone up twice in that time. I have the receipts."],
  ["Is that a Union blackboard or a Party one?","Same chalk. Anna says the Party's the head and the Union's the hands. I say the hands do the work."],
  ["My maths isn't good enough to weep.","(She laughs.) Then just weep. It saves time."]],
 "Off you go. I've a blackboard to finish."],
2:["Bob's left me a pie on the desk with a note: 'Eat it hot.' It's been cold since half past twelve.",[
  ["Bob cooks for you?","Every day the meetings run long, which is every day. He says a hungry union leader is a short-tempered one."],
  ["Is he a Party man?",["No. Union to the bone, but he won't take a Party card. The comrades never let me forget it.",[
    ["Why won't he join?","He says one of us in politics is plenty for a two-room flat. He's not wrong."],
    ["Does Anna mind?","Anna minds everything. She also eats his pie when she thinks I'm not looking."]]]],
  ["Cold pie is still pie.","Spoken like a fisherman. (She cuts you a slice.) Go on, then."]],
 "Tell Bob it was hot. He likes to hear it."],
3:["The cola factory's running again. Aino on the bottling line rang me: twelve-hour shifts to catch up for the snow, and not a penny extra.",[
  ["Can the Union do anything?","We can ask Kurt nicely. Then less nicely. Then the bottling line stops, and Phil asks us nicely."],
  ["Who's Kurt?","The foreman. Not a member. He thinks a union is something you catch off a dirty glass."],
  ["Twelve hours on a bottling line?","Clink, clink, clink, for twelve hours. Aino says she hears bottles in her sleep."]],
 "If you see Aino, tell her I'm on it."],
4:["Eastbrook's open, so I can finally get word out to the mill. Knut and Ilse have been writing to me all winter.",[
  ["What do they write?","That the guard's off the big saw, that Dagny had it wired back to speed the line up, and that a lad lost two fingers to it in June."],
  ["Isn't Eastbrook Democrat country?","Erik's country, yes. But a saw doesn't ask how you vote."],
  ["Letters all winter? Romantic.","Ilse sends drawings of the saw. Very detailed ones. Not romantic."]],
 "One day soon I'll need somebody to go out to that mill."],
5:["(Helen is muttering a speech under her breath, jabbing the air with a finger.) Sorry, comrade. Rehearsing. If I don't say it out loud, I forget where the shouting goes.",[
  ["What's the speech about?","Fair pay. It always is. I've given it two hundred times, and it's never once gone out of date."],
  ["Where does the shouting go?","After the numbers. People need the numbers first; then they need somebody to be angry for them."],
  ["Can I hear a bit?","'Comrades! Who gutted the fish? Who drank the profit?' (She stops.) That's enough. You'll want to go on strike."]],
 "Close the door on your way. I'm going to try the loud bit."],
6:["Lucia's been round the boats again with her little book. Tom paid. Sam paid. Mike didn't, somehow, and nobody knows why.",[
  ["Why didn't Mike pay?","He says she missed him out. Lucia doesn't miss anybody. I'll ask her, when I'm feeling brave."],
  ["Is it protection money?","'Harbour fees', she calls it. As if the Syndicate built the harbour."],
  ["Can't the Union stop it?","The Union can stop a harbour. Stopping one woman with a notebook is harder."]],
 "Keep your wallet in your inside pocket in Saltwick."],
7:["The Russian from the Hotel Aurora sent a crate of oranges to the HQ. Oranges! In September, on Sarivalto.",[
  ["That's generous.","Very generous. Generous people want something. I just haven't found out what yet."],
  ["What will you do with them?","Give them to the children in the block. Kaisa's going to announce it on the radio: 'Oranges, courtesy of the workers.'"],
  ["He's Anna's friend, isn't he?","He's everybody's friend. That seems to be his trade. The timber's a hobby."]],
 "Take an orange. Go on, they're for the people."],
8:["Sunday. Bob's roasting a fish the length of my arm, and I'm writing to the Kelp Cola board. Neither of us is going to church.",[
  ["What are you writing?","A list of demands. A break every four hours and a stool for every woman on the line. Revolutionary stuff."],
  ["What sort of fish?","Cod. Bob says the cod's thinner this year. He blames the oil rig."],
  ["Have you heard about the rig?","Dark water past the point, the boats say. If Dario's spilt oil on our fishing grounds, the whole harbour will hear about it from me."]],
 "Go on. Bob says if I talk any longer, the cod will dry out."],
9:["Comrade, I've been up since four thinking about cola. I never thought I'd hear myself say that.",[
  ["Why cola?","Because every worker on the island drinks it, and every bottle puts money in Phil's pocket. Take the bottle, and you take the pocket."],
  ["What flavour?","No idea! I've asked round Saltwick and everyone says 'herring', which is no help to anybody."],
  ["Can't people just drink tea?","Try telling that to a seventeen-year-old on the bottling line. A revolution has to taste nice, comrade."]],
 "If you hear of a good flavour, come and tell me."],
10:["Anna's gone to the funeral in her mother's coat. I'm holding the fort, and Bob's holding me to eating lunch.",[
  ["You didn't want to go?","Somebody has to answer the telephone. And I'd only have said something about the price of the flowers."],
  ["What's for lunch?","Herring on rye with onions. Bob's wrapped it like a parachute: three layers of paper."],
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","Once, at the harbour, in 1971. He asked if the fish were biting. I said the fishermen were. He laughed. He wasn't a bad man."]],
 "Tell Anna I kept the place standing."],
11:["The mill, comrade. Today I want somebody to stand in front of Dagny and say what the mill hands can't.",[
  ["Why not go yourself?","Dagny sees me coming and locks the gate. She knows all my speeches. She's never heard one of yours."],
  ["What do the mill hands want?","A rise, after six years without, and the guard back on the saw. Not a mansion. Fingers and bread."],
  ["Can't Erik help them?","Erik will make a speech about it. Speeches are lovely; I make them myself. But somebody has to win the thing."]],
 "Go and hear the mill hands out. They'll tell it better than I can."],
12:["(Helen has her feet up on the desk, which you suspect is rare.) Ten minutes' rest. Bob's orders. He timed it with the egg-timer.",[
  ["Tired?","Fifteen years of strikes, and the tiredness never once caught up with me. This month it's catching up."],
  ["How are the mill hands?","They've had their say, which is more than they've had in six years. Whatever Dagny does next, they won't go back to being quiet."],
  ["An egg-timer?","Three minutes an egg. He turned it three times and went back to the plant. I'm on my second egg."]],
 "(The timer runs out.) Back to it."],
13:["Phil's handing out free cola in Kingsgrad, and who do you think is bottling it all on overtime? Aino and Petra.",[
  ["Unpaid overtime?","'Promotional effort', Kurt calls it. I call it theft with a ribbon on."],
  ["Is the Union angry?","Petra is. When Petra's angry, the warehouse gets very, very tidy. It's terrifying."],
  ["Free cola's not so bad.","Nothing's free. Somebody paid. Look at who's carrying the crates and you'll see who."]],
 "Don't drink the free stuff. Aino bottled it on a bad back."],
14:["The big day. I've ironed my good blouse. Bob says I look like a bank manager. I told him that's the idea.",[
  ["Why a bank manager?","Because at big gatherings the bosses listen to people who look like them. Then I open my mouth, and it's too late."],
  ["What do you hope for today?","That the workers get a seat at the table. Not just the end of it, by the draught."],
  ["You look nice.","(She straightens her collar.) Thank you, comrade. Don't tell anyone. I've a reputation."]],
 "I'll be the one by the draught, complaining."],
15:["Somebody at the meeting asked again why Bob won't join the Party. In front of everyone. With Bob sitting there holding the soup.",[
  ["What did Bob say?","'I'm the soup committee.' They laughed, and that was that. He's cleverer than they think."],
  ["Does it bother you?",["A little. Not that he won't join. That they can't let it be.",[
    ["Will he ever join?","Not for me, and not for Anna. If he ever joins, it'll be for himself. That's the only reason that lasts."],
    ["How long has it gone on?","Six years. 'Helen, your Bob. Helen, the card.' I've mostly stopped hearing it."]]]],
  ["The soup committee sounds important.","The most important committee in the Party. Nobody walks out of a meeting where there's soup."]],
 "I'll tell Bob you were asking. He'll be pleased."],
16:["Debate day. I've written Anna six pages of facts. She'll use one sentence of it, and it'll be the best sentence.",[
  ["Will you be there?","Front row, with Bob, and Kate making noises. If Erik gets a fact wrong, Kate will correct him from the floor."],
  ["What's in the six pages?","Wages, rents and the price of fish since 1960. Erik will have feelings. Anna will have numbers."],
  ["Do you ever want to lead the Party?","No. I've got the Union, and that's plenty. Anna's the head, I'm the hands. Hands are busier."]],
 "Wish Anna luck. She'd never ask for it."],
17:["The ground shook in the middle of the meeting. Half the comrades went under the table. The other half went for their tea.",[
  ["Which half were you?","Neither. I kept talking. When it stopped, I had the whole room's attention for once."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Olga says the glass case in the fish shop rattled for a minute. The old people up north say the mountain's waking."],
  ["How was the debate?","Loud and long, and everyone said what they came to say. The island can decide who was right."]],
 "Mind the shelves if it goes again."],
18:["(Helen is frowning at a typed letter.) Our friend from the Hotel Aurora wants to know if the Union could call a general strike 'at short notice'. In writing. Typed.",[
  ["Could you?","Of course. One speech and the whole island stops. Which is exactly why I don't do it because a man in a hotel asks."],
  ["Why would he want a strike?","'To show the Party's strength,' he says. A strike's for the workers, not for show."],
  ["Typed letters are very formal.","He has a foreign typewriter. The 'e's all lean a bit to the left. Bob noticed. Bob notices everything."]],
 "I'll be answering him by hand. Slowly."],
19:["Recruiting day. Anna's sent the comrades out with clipboards, and I've been given the hardest case on the island: Bob.",[
  ["Will he join?","He'll cook me a very good supper and change the subject. He's been doing it for six years."],
  ["Who else are you after?","The girls at the cola factory. Petra, if we're lucky. She'd terrify the whole committee into working."],
  ["Clipboards? Very official.","Kate's is covered in fish scales already. She signed up two at the plant just by standing too close."]],
 "Wish me luck with Bob. I'll need it."],
20:["Nora from the Gazette was in Saltwick, notebook out, asking about the Russian at the Hotel Aurora. The Democrats have a bee in their bonnet.",[
  ["What did she ask you?","Whether the Party takes orders from Moscow. I said the Party takes orders from the fishermen, and the fishermen take orders from the weather."],
  ["Well, does it?","It takes friendship from Moscow. Orders I'd notice. I've had very few, and ignored the rest."],
  ["Nora gets everywhere.","She got into Ray's, bought Sam a drink and got four whole words out of him. That's a record."]],
 "If Nora asks you about us, tell her we're very boring."],
21:["(Helen has a list of names, with ticks beside most of them.) The mountain's erupted. I've been round every flat in the block, and I'm still two ticks short.",[
  ["Who's missing?","Sirpa and Tiina. Down at the beach with Pat, I'd bet, watching the sky like it's a film. I'll find them."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. Frightened, ashy and coughing. Kaisa's on the radio telling everyone to keep their windows shut."],
  ["How's Bob?","Making soup for the whole block. Forty bowls. It's the only thing he knows to do in a crisis, and it's exactly the right thing."]],
 "Shut the door quickly. The ash comes in with you."],
22:["The fish plant's shut till the ash is out of the water tanks. No fish, no wages, so the Union's paying hardship money from the strike fund.",[
  ["Is there enough in the fund?","For a week. After that we pass a hat. Saltwick's good with a hat."],
  ["Will the owners pay?","I've written to ask. I've also written what happens if they don't. The second letter's shorter."],
  ["What does a strike fund look like?","A biscuit tin in the bottom drawer, under the minutes from 1972. Don't tell the Syndicate."]],
 "No, you can't see the tin. Off you go."],
23:["Dario's rig carries on as if nothing's happened. The boats come back with ash in the nets and oil on the hulls.",[
  ["Can the Union act against the rig?","There are Union men out on that rig too. That's the trouble: I can't shut it without shutting off their wages."],
  ["Oil on the hulls?","Tom showed me. A black tidemark on the Gull, a hand's width up. He scrubbed it off, and it came back."],
  ["You look tired.","I am. But tired's a privilege. It means you're still in the fight."]],
 "Back to my letters. They don't write themselves."],
24:["Bob made me a cake. With ash on top. He says it's icing, and it's all the icing we'll get this month.",[
  ["Ash icing?","Icing sugar, really. He dusted it grey with cocoa to make me laugh. I did, the first time all week."],
  ["What's the occasion?","Six years in the flat together. Neither of us remembered. Kate did. Kate remembers everything about everybody."],
  ["Is he a good cook?","The best in Saltwick, and he'll never admit it, because then he'd have to cook for the whole Party."]],
 "Take a slice for the road."],
25:["Everyone's courting somebody this week. The Democrats court the princess, and suddenly the Kelp Cola board's courting the Union, with a letter about stools.",[
  ["Stools?","For the bottling line. My letter from the eighth. Now they say yes, when they're frightened of losing the island. Funny timing."],
  ["What does the Union give back?","Nothing. A stool isn't a favour. It's a right with legs."],
  ["And the princess?","Daphne's charming. I've never seen her lift a crate. But she can't help where she was born, any more than I can."]],
 "Three days to go. Then we find out who meant what."],
26:["(Helen is going down a list of names with a pencil.) Shop stewards. Every workplace on the island. I'm checking they're all still at the end of a telephone.",[
  ["Why check now?","Because the twenty-eighth is coming, and a union leader keeps her stewards close."],
  ["Are you planning a strike?","I'm planning to be ready. That's not the same thing, and you can quote me exactly as I said it."],
  ["Who's at the bottom of the list?","Knut, at the mill. He's always last on the list, and always the first to turn up."]],
 "If you hear a whistle on the docks, it's probably a boat. Probably."],
27:["Anna wants the comrades to fill the cinema in Kingsgrad, and I'm to get the bottling-line girls there. To a Soviet film about tractors.",[
  ["Will they go?","If there's popcorn. Aino says she'll go if the tractor's handsome."],
  ["Have you seen the film?","Anna has. She says it's 'very moving'. Anna cries at the price of herring, so I'm reserving judgement."],
  ["What would you rather see?","STORMWING, honestly. Bob says there's a robot in it. I like a robot."]],
 "Don't tell Anna about the robot."],
28:["The twenty-eighth. Every faction on the island's doing its biggest thing today. The Union's doing what it always does: turning up.",[
  ["What's the Party up to?","Ask Anna. If I told a reporter, she'd have me back gutting herring by Friday."],
  ["Are your stewards ready?","Every one of them, waiting for a word. They might never get it. Being ready is the point."],
  ["Did Bob make breakfast?","Porridge and a boiled egg, wrapped in a tea towel to keep it warm. He says a big day needs protein."]],
 "Off you go. It's a long day for everybody."],
29:["Erik's sent a crate of Eastbrook beer to the Union office, 'from one side of the island to the other'. Friendly rivals, says his note.",[
  ["That's kind of him.","It is. It's also very clever. Every man who drinks it will think warmly of Erik tomorrow."],
  ["Will you drink it?","Of course. I'm not proud, comrade. I'm thirsty."],
  ["Rivals or friends?","Both. Erik and I want the same things for the mill hands. We disagree about everything else."]],
 "One bottle. Then back to work."],
30:["The last day. Bob's packed me sandwiches for every meal, as if I might be gone a week. Whatever happens, the Union will still be here tomorrow.",[
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Get up. Go to work. Fight for the bottling line. Kings and commissars come and go; the bottling line goes on for ever."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a union leader. Hope's the job. Without it you're just somebody shouting in a cold room."],
  ["Sandwiches for every meal?","Herring, cheese, and something with jam he won't tell me about. He worries. That's how he says it."]],
 "Go well, comrade. Whatever comes."],
};
// Helmi, owner of Harbour Outfitters in Saltwick (no faction). Threads: bargains and tight margins, fishermen who never pay on time, her sister's knitting, the coming cold, her friendly rivalry with Olga, masks and boots after the ash.
DAILY['Helmi']={
1:["(Helmi looks you up and down and nods.) That coat won't last a week on this island. Reporter, are you? London will have taught you to dress for drizzle. This is not drizzle.",[
  ["What should I wear?","Wool next to the skin, oilskin over it and good boots. And a hat, because you lose half your warmth through the head. It's a fact. I've checked."],
  ["What's the best seller?","Wellies. Every season. Even in a snowstorm, someone's sure they'll need wellies by Thursday."],
  ["Is the shop old?","It's my father's, and his father's. The sign's been repainted three times. The bargains have never changed."]],
 "Keep the receipt. Everything here has a guarantee, or a grumble."],
2:["(Helmi is counting souwesters into neat piles.) The boats are out for the first time since the snow, and every man's lost his hat. They always lose the hat in the first hour. I'll sell twenty before noon.",[
  ["How do you keep the prices low?","I don't, I keep them fair. And I buy in winter, when nobody else wants to."],
  ["Do the fishermen pay?","Eventually. They settle in herring, in tips, in lies. I take them all and write them in a book."],
  ["Is Olga next door a friend?","We shout at each other across the lane. It's the kindest thing we do."]],
 "Check the buttons before you buy. Cheap ones fall off in the sea."],
3:["My sister's been knitting again. (Helmi holds up a jumper with a puffin on the front.) Three hundred of these, she's made. I sell one a month. The puffin is not catching on.",[
  ["It's a nice puffin.","Don't be kind. It looks like a potato with a beak. But she's happy, and I'll never say otherwise."],
  ["Would you sell the puffin to tourists?","If I had any. They only come for the lighthouse and the toilets."],
  ["Does your sister live in Saltwick?","Up the coast. She sends one jumper per week by post, with a note: 'Sell it, you ungrateful girl.'"]],
 "Take a puffin. At cost, for a reporter."],
4:["(Helmi folds a pair of dungarees with a snap.) Hannu was in. Wanted a discount on a hat because of 'the headline value of the shop'. I asked him what that was worth. He said, 'A mention.' I said, 'I'll take the discount off the mention.'",[
  ["Did he buy the hat?","He did, at full price. He grumbled for ten minutes. I enjoyed it."],
  ["Does Hannu write about the shop?","Hannu writes about everything. Mostly it's wrong, but it's interesting."],
  ["Are you in the paper often?","Only when a sou'wester goes missing. Then I'm a scandal."]],
 "Don't believe the headlines. Believe the weather."],
5:["(Helmi is stacking oilskins by size.) The monks have sent a boy down for a dozen pairs of warm socks. Warm socks! For men who sleep on stone.",[
  ["Do the monks pay?","Always, in coin and a blessing. I've no use for the blessing, but I keep it in the till, just in case."],
  ["What do the monks wear?","Black, wool, and sandals. In winter, they wear all three at once. It's a fashion nobody should copy."],
  ["Is there anything the monks want that's unusual?",["A very large oilskin. A coat the size of a tent. Brother Teodor, I think, though they wouldn't say.",[
    ["What would he do with it?","Keep dry in a bell tower, I'd wager. Or smuggle something in it. I never ask."],
    ["Did you make it?","I stitched two together. The seam's a disgrace, but it'll turn a storm. I charged for the thread."]]]]],
 "If you meet a monk, tell him I do a discount on thick socks."],
6:["The cold's arriving early, I can feel it in my knees. (Helmi taps a barometer by the door.) I've ordered more peacoats. Hope I'm wrong, but I've never been wrong yet.",[
  ["Is it going to be a hard winter?","Hard, long and early. The sea's gone a colour I don't like. Grey with a hint of spite."],
  ["Do you stock for the whole island?","Only the sensible parts. Kingsgrad has its tailor. I've the fishermen, and a few oddballs."],
  ["What would you buy for a reporter?","A pea coat, a watch cap, and a flask. It's the correct sequence."]],
 "Wrap up. You'll thank me in a fortnight."],
7:["(Helmi has the door open and is watching the road.) Everyone's passing through now the northwest's clear. Oil men with city shoes. They've no idea. Their feet will be blue by dusk.",[
  ["Will you sell to them?","At a slightly higher price. They don't know the difference and it keeps them warm."],
  ["Are they friendly?","They're polite and in a hurry. I like them better than the Party men. The Party men bargain."],
  ["Any good news?","The roads are open. That alone has doubled my trade."]],
 "If you see a man in thin shoes, send him over."],
8:["(Helmi has the radio on, quietly, a sermon murmuring.) I don't hold with sermons before noon. A shopkeeper's soul isn't up before the till.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas and funerals. Both are good for trade."],
  ["What would Father Thyme wear on a cold day?","A cassock, three jumpers and a dog. He's a practical man, despite the words."],
  ["Is Saltwick pious?","In a loud kind of way. Everyone prays when the boats are late. After that, they sell fish."]],
 "Don't stay too long in the cold. It's the sea air that gets you."],
9:["There's a company handing out cola bottles on the quay and calling it a 'new formula'. (Helmi sniffs.) It's the same fizz. I've a nose for a repackage.",[
  ["Do you like cola?","With a ham sandwich. In the sun. In a good mood. I have only had that conjunction twice."],
  ["Will you sell it in the shop?","I'd put a bottle in the window if they paid me a fair margin. Otherwise, no."],
  ["Is the Party's cola any better?","I've heard it's brown, round and political. That's three more qualities than I'd want."]],
 "I'll try the new one on Thursday, if the sky clears."],
10:["(Helmi has hung a black cloth in the doorway.) A funeral. I closed at eleven. The late king did buy a coat from my father, once. A reefer, navy. He wore it to a regatta and looked a fool.",[
  ["Did your father know him?","Briefly. He measured the king for the coat and said afterwards, 'He's thinner than his portraits.' We never forgot it."],
  ["Is the harbour quiet today?","As a mouse. The boats are in, the flags are half-mast. The sea seems to be listening."],
  ["Will it change the shop?","The shop outlived the old queen, the war, and a flood. It'll outlive the next king too."]],
 "Be kind today. We've a lot of ghosts in Saltwick."],
11:["(Helmi is mending a net, sleeves rolled up.) The Church is giving alms to the poor and the Democrats are fighting about pay. And me with a stack of work gloves no one's collected.",[
  ["Who owes you gloves?","The mill, in Eastbrook. They ordered forty pairs and sent a letter saying they'd 'review the terms'."],
  ["Will they pay?","They will if there's a fair wage. If there isn't, I'll take the gloves back and sell them to the monks."],
  ["Is there a lot of tension?","Enough to snap a thread. I keep my needles sharp."]],
 "Tell the mill I'm waiting. Politely."],
12:["A man in a nice coat came in this morning wanting thermal underwear in bulk. I asked how many. He said, 'Enough for a department.' I've had odd customers, but not an accounts department.",[
  ["Was he from the oil company?","Frostholm, by the vowels. He didn't give a name. Paid in crisp notes, which always makes me nervous."],
  ["Is it strange to buy for a department?","It's strange to buy for a department that wears thermal underwear. I hope the heating's broken."],
  ["Do you ask questions?","I ask the sizes. Everything else is their business."]],
 "If they send a second order, I'll ask what's going on."],
13:["(Helmi has set a crate of cola by the door.) Free samples. A lad left them and said it was 'a gesture'. I've told him my gestures are on sale for two marks apiece.",[
  ["Is Kelp Cola popular here?","It's a novelty. The fishermen drink it, say it's all right, and go back to coffee."],
  ["Is it good for the shop?","They want a poster in the window. I want a discount on a hundred bottles. We'll see who blinks."],
  ["Do you worry about the big companies?","Always. But a small shop has one advantage: we know your size."]],
 "I'll try one tomorrow. If it kills me, I'll leave you the till."],
14:["(Helmi has put up the 'Closed' sign and still sits in the window.) The harbour's gone to the big day. I stayed to mind the stock. A fishing boat doesn't care who's king, but a thief does.",[
  ["What do you expect today?","Noise. Flags. A good deal of boasting. And somebody will leave a hat behind. I'll sell it back to them."],
  ["Did Olga go?","Of course. She'd go to the opening of a drain if it had a crowd."],
  ["Are you curious?","A little. But it'll be in Hannu's paper tomorrow, with four mistakes."]],
 "Come back and tell me. Skip the speeches."],
15:["The Royals want a crown, the Church wants a cross. (Helmi looks up from her accounts.) The fishermen want dry socks. I think we know who's got the better priorities.",[
  ["Who's winning the argument?","The one who shouts loudest. It's nearly always a man in a hat."],
  ["Do you have a view?","I'm a seamstress by temperament. I'd stitch the two sides together and charge for the thread."],
  ["Does it affect the harbour?","The harbour feels it in the prices. Everything's up when two parties fight."]],
 "If anyone wants a flag, I've a bolt of cloth. Any colour."],
16:["There's a debate on the radio, the Party against the Democrats. (Helmi turns the dial up, then down.) I've listened for ten minutes and already I'm on both sides.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever's speaking at the time. It's the same with Hannu. I think they've been practising together."],
  ["Is it worth listening to?","It's very good for knitting. You can drop a stitch and no one notices."],
  ["Which side do you prefer?","The side with the better coats. Which, in my experience, is neither."]],
 "I'll turn it off when they start to quote Marx."],
17:["(The rail of oilskins sways, all of them at once, like a congregation.) Did you feel that? The ground's trembling. The old men say it's the mountain. I say it's the price of herring.",[
  ["Is it frightening?","A little. But I've got three hundred jumpers and a very stout counter."],
  ["Where's Dario? I heard he's missing.","I heard, too. A man like that disappears and everyone says it's convenient. I wonder for whom."],
  ["Are people buying more?","Torches, ropes and wool. If the mountain's angry, they'll be ready."]],
 "Tell Olga to stop shouting. It's not helping the mountain."],
18:["(Helmi is selling goggles from a basket.) Safety goggles, thirty pennies. Everyone's suddenly sure they'll need them. I'd never sold one in my life until this morning.",[
  ["Why goggles?","In case of ash. The monks sent word the mountain's about to cough. I think they're probably right."],
  ["Do you believe it?","I believe in being prepared. It's why my shop is always full of things nobody needs yet."],
  ["Is the shaking getting worse?","Every day. Like an old boiler. A clatter, a groan, and then a silence that's worse."]],
 "Buy a spare mask. They run out fast."],
19:["Hannu says the Party's looking for helpers, 'Comrades Wanted'. (Helmi snorts.) He wants me to stock red armbands. I said, I'll stock what sells, and nothing sells like a pea coat.",[
  ["Would the Party buy from you?","They would, and ask for a discount in the name of the people. I'd give it, but only in the people's currency."],
  ["Who's on the street?","Strangers. A lot of strangers in good coats. I've noticed because they're always cold."],
  ["Do you mind the Communists?","Not if they pay. That goes for everyone."]],
 "Armbands are by the door. Very popular. Don't ask which colour."],
20:["(Helmi is pinning up a notice about an extra delivery.) Nothing from Kingsgrad lately, not even a postcard. I think the road's busy with people trying to find a hotel. I'm told it's all hush-hush.",[
  ["What's at the Hotel Aurora?","A man, apparently. Whom everyone's watching. And whom everyone pretends not to."],
  ["Do you hear much?","People in oilskins talk. I hear about three secrets a week and sell a dozen coats."],
  ["Is Saltwick safe?","Safe as a boat in a harbour. But the sea's rising, and so are the rumours."]],
 "I never tell what I hear. It's bad for repeat custom."],
21:["(Helmi is standing in the doorway, a mask over her nose and a broom in her hand.) It went up! The mountain! A great grey cloud rolled over the water, and it's raining ash. My shop's a snowglobe. A dirty one.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. A bit of grit in the eye. I sold the last goggles to Olga, who needed them for her face."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not here, nor in the north, I'm told. The wind was kind. It's a miracle, or a fluke, and I'll take either."],
  ["What now?","Everyone's going to want masks, boots, and gloves. And I've got all three. I hate to sound pleased."]],
 "Don't track ash in. I'm not joking."],
22:["I've sold out of masks and ordered more. (Helmi shoves a heavy box across the floor.) The price has gone up, I admit. But so has the cost. It's not profiteering. It's just business in a bad week.",[
  ["Is it fair?","It's fair if I say so. Don't tell Olga. She'll want a cut."],
  ["How are the fishermen?","Bitter. The ash has fouled the nets. They're out all the same, in oilskins and fury."],
  ["Do you feel safe?","Safe enough. The mountain's a long way off. The prices are close."]],
 "If you're short, I can sell on credit. I've a very long ledger."],
23:["(Helmi shakes ash out of a blanket on the step.) The island's one colour now. It makes selling boots a challenge. You can't see the stitching.",[
  ["Do you miss the snow?","I do, God help me. Snow's clean. Ash is a conversation."],
  ["Is the clean-up under way?","In patches. Everyone's sweeping, and nothing's getting any cleaner. It's the island's new hobby."],
  ["What's selling best?","Brooms, buckets and bad language. Mostly the last, but I don't stock it."]],
 "A broom's half price. Everyone needs one."],
24:["The monks are retreating to the peaks, I hear. (Helmi folds blankets in a rush.) They've asked for thirty. The mountain may be cross, but the monks are very warm.",[
  ["Are they in danger?","They say they're in prayer. Prayer in a blanket's a safer bet."],
  ["Do you deliver to the monastery?","Not on that road. The boy goes halfway and a monk meets him with a mule."],
  ["What else do they need?","Rope, oil and very large boots. The sort that only come in black."]],
 "Tell Brother Ilja his boots are ready. He'll know."],
25:["There's talk of a crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. (Helmi puts down her scissors.) I'd rather talk about trousers. I've a lot of them, and no one's talking.",[
  ["Is it exciting?","It's loud. I prefer excitement with a discount."],
  ["Do you want a king?","I want a king who buys a good coat. Or a queen. Or a very warm committee."],
  ["Is the harbour ready for a change?","The harbour's ready for a hot meal and a sensible tide."]],
 "If a princess comes by, I do very fine gloves."],
26:["(Helmi squints at a crate on the pavement.) Another cola. 'A Prince in Every Bottle'. I wonder if I'll find one in my hat. There was a prince in my hat once. A cat, actually. It didn't pay either.",[
  ["Is Kelp Cola spreading?","Like damp. It's in every shop window on the coast."],
  ["Do you like the prince on the label?","He's pleasant enough. A very clean collar. I'd say he's never done a day's work."],
  ["Does the label sell it?","Everything sells if it's got a face on it."]],
 "I'll keep a bottle for the cat."],
27:["(Helmi has a ticket on the counter.) The Party's showing a film in Kingsgrad tomorrow, the cinema's been booked. 'Revolution at the Pictures'. I may go, if there's a bus.",[
  ["Is it any good?","I've heard it's all marching and no plot. That's most films, mind."],
  ["Who's going from Saltwick?","Hannu, for the headlines. And half the harbour, for the free sandwiches."],
  ["Do you like the pictures?","Not particularly. I prefer a good rain on a good roof."]],
 "If you go, tell me whether the hero wears a hat."],
28:["(Helmi has pinned a flag of every faction in the window, the whole lot in a row.) A shopkeeper can't be too careful. Everybody's in the street, so everybody's a customer.",[
  ["Is it a big day?","A noisy one. I sold six flags and forty pairs of boots. I don't care who wins, as long as they pay."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm calm. I've sold oilskins to men who were sure the sea was going to drown them. It never does."],
  ["Is Saltwick ready?","As ready as a harbour can be. The nets are in, the tide's out, and the pubs are full."]],
 "Come back after the speeches. I'll have the kettle on."],
29:["Tomorrow it's all decided. (Helmi tidies the oilskins into rows.) I've stocked up, put up the prices, and said a prayer. I always do the last two together.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm practical. Hope's for people with no stock."],
  ["Who do you want in charge?","Someone who'll leave the harbour alone. It's a modest wish."],
  ["Any last advice?","Wear a coat. It's cold. It's always cold. Cold's the only constant on this island."]],
 "Go home early. Get some rest."],
30:["(Helmi stands in the doorway, arms folded, watching the sea.) The last day, then. Whatever it brings, I'll be here at eight tomorrow with the key.",[
  ["How's the month been?","Cold. Busy. Dusty. I made more money than I have in years, and I felt rather bad about it."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A mild winter, a good catch, and a customer who pays on time. It isn't much."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come again. Bring your own coat."]],
 "Take care of yourself. That coat won't last."],
};
// Hely, twenty-three, out of work in Eastbrook since the mill cut the night shift she kept the tally for (The Unemployed). Threads:
// the circus at Lake Ilmari, which she wants to run away with (juggling, Meg's low wire, Bruno), her bets with Paolo the bookie
// and the debt that grows, the island's big days, which she wants a hand in, and Veikko, whom she teases and leans on.
DAILY['Hely']={
4:["(Hely is spinning a coin on the back of her knuckles.) Finally! Somebody from outside! Do you know how long it's been since anybody interesting came up that road? Hely. Tell me everything about London.",[
  ["London's big and grey and wet.","Everywhere's grey and wet. But big! I've never been anywhere big. Kingsgrad's the biggest place I've seen, and you can walk across it before your tea goes cold."],
  ["What do you do here?","Nothing. That's the problem. I kept the tally on the night shift at the mill, till they cut it. Now I sit on this bench and wait for something to happen."],
  ["Nice coin trick.","(She flicks it into the air and catches it behind her back.) I've had a lot of time to practise."]],
 "Come back tomorrow. I'll have thought of more questions."],
5:["(Hely is bouncing on her heels.) There's a circus coming! A real one, with a big top. Bruno's posters are up at the tavern. Lake Ilmari, from the seventh.",[
  ["Will you go?","Go? I'm going to join it. Don't laugh. People do. They run off with the circus and nobody ever sees them again. Perfect."],
  ["What would you do in a circus?","Anything. Juggle, walk the wire, sell the tickets, sweep the elephant. Do they have an elephant? I'd sweep an elephant."],
  ["What does Veikko say?","Veikko says I'd last a week. Veikko said that about the mill, and I lasted three years. Till they shut the shift."]],
 "If you see Bruno before I do, tell him I'm coming."],
6:["(Hely is juggling three wrinkled apples, badly.) Linnea gave me these from the tavern kitchen. They were going off anyway. I've dropped them about four hundred times.",[
  ["You're getting better.","Liar. (She drops one.) But a nice liar. I like you."],
  ["Who taught you?","Nobody. A library book from Kingsgrad. 'Juggling for Beginners'. The drawings are very confident. I'm not."],
  ["Want me to try?","(She hands you two.) Go on. ...Ha! You're worse than me. That's the best thing that's happened all week."]],
 "Keep one. It's bruised anyway."],
7:["(Hely is breathless, her boots muddy.) I went to the lake! The big top's going up. Red and yellow stripes. A man lifting a caravan wheel with one arm. Igor. He winked at me.",[
  ["Did you talk to Bruno?","He was busy shouting at a tent pole. He said 'come back when the show's up, girl, and bring money'. That's practically a yes."],
  ["Who else is there?","A fortune teller in a painted caravan, Madame Zora. A clown called Buttons who was hungry. And Meg, the acrobat. She stood on her hands on the jetty, just for fun."],
  ["Sounds wonderful.","It's the first thing on this island that isn't timber, fish, cola or politics. I nearly cried."]],
 "I'm going back tomorrow. And the day after."],
8:["(Hely leans in.) Do you bet? Paolo, the bookie, he'll take a bet on anything. I've got a krone on the oil rig having a bad week. Something's gone wrong out there, they say.",[
  ["You bet on disasters?","I bet on everything. The weather. The funeral. Whether Erik spills his coffee at the next meeting. It's the only exciting thing in Eastbrook."],
  ["Who's Paolo?","He stands by the Cedar Grill in a good suit. Very polite. Writes everything in a little black book. He knows everybody's business."],
  ["Is that wise?","Probably not. But it's a krone, and wise never paid me anything either."]],
 "Don't tell Veikko. He gets that look."],
9:["(Hely is sitting on the back of the bench.) Meg let me try the low wire! A foot off the ground. I stayed on for three steps. Three! She said that's good for a beginner.",[
  ["Three's very good.","It's three more than yesterday. Meg says it's about your eyes. Look at the end of the wire, not your feet. Like life, she says. Very circus."],
  ["Did you fall?","Into the sawdust. Twice. Meg laughed so hard she fell off the jetty. Then she taught me how to fall properly."],
  ["What's Meg like?","Tiny and tough as wire. She ran away from Tampere at fifteen. She did it, so it can be done."]],
 "Look at the end of the wire. Remember that."],
10:["(Hely is restless, kicking at the snow.) The whole island's in black for the funeral. Linnea's crying behind the bar. Even I've got a ribbon on. I didn't even know the king.",[
  ["Not going to the palace?","They don't let tally clerks into royal funerals. I'd have gone and sat on the wall, but Veikko said I'd get arrested."],
  ["What do you think comes next?","Something big. Everybody wants the island now he's gone. It's like the last day of school. Anything could happen."],
  ["You sound excited.","I am. Is that awful? Somebody died, and I feel like a door's just opened. I'll go to hell for that."]],
 "Something's coming. I can feel it."],
11:["(Hely is grinning.) There's a union woman up at the mill today, someone from Helen's lot, talking to Dagny about pay. Paolo's offering two to one she won't get anything.",[
  ["Did you bet?","Against Dagny. On principle. If I lose, at least I lost on the right side."],
  ["Would you go back to the mill?","If they put the night shift back. I liked it, actually. Counting logs in the dark, the stars, the saws going quiet at three."],
  ["Why isn't Veikko up there?","He wanted to go. His leg won't do the hill on a cold day. He's sitting here pretending he doesn't mind. He minds."]],
 "Whatever happens up there, I'm owed a drink."],
12:["(Hely is eating a hot dog from Dex's cart.) Dex gave me this on credit. Everyone in Eastbrook gives me credit. It's my face. It's very creditworthy.",[
  ["How much credit, exactly?","Dex, three hot dogs. Linnea, a bar tab. Paolo, a bit. Maya, a coffee. I'll pay them all back when I join the circus. Circus wages."],
  ["Do circuses pay well?","No idea! That's the beauty of it. Nobody knows. It's all possibilities."],
  ["Paolo, a bit?","(She waves the hot dog.) A bit. A small bit. Don't make the Veikko face. You're too young for the Veikko face."]],
 "Eat something hot. It's freezing."],
13:["(Hely is practising with three proper juggling clubs.) Buttons gave me these! Old ones, chipped. He said a girl who can't juggle apples ought to try something harder.",[
  ["Harder makes it easier?","That's what he said. Clowns talk in riddles. He also asked if I had any pizza. Twice."],
  ["Can you do it?","I can do two. The third one's thinking about it. (She drops one on her foot.) The third one's decided no."],
  ["How's the circus going?","Packed every night. Bruno says I can sell programmes at the door on Saturday. Unpaid. It's a start! It's a foot in the tent."]],
 "Stand back. The third club's got a temper."],
14:["(Hely has been up since five, and it shows.) The big day! The whole island in one place. Something's going to happen. I can feel it in my teeth.",[
  ["What do you think will happen?","Something nobody expects. That's the whole point of a big day. Paolo's taking bets on how many people turn up. I said nine thousand."],
  ["Are you going with Veikko?","I'm his elbows. Crowds part for a man with a stick if he's got a girl with elbows in front of him."],
  ["Nine thousand?","Every soul on the island, nearly. Or twelve. I'm good at counting. I counted logs for three years."]],
 "Come on! We'll miss the start!"],
15:["(Hely is hoarse.) I shouted myself silly yesterday. I don't even know what I was shouting. Everybody was, so I did.",[
  ["Did you win your bet?","Paolo says the count's 'disputed'. Paolo says that when he doesn't want to pay. (She shrugs.) I'll win the next one."],
  ["What did you think of it all?","I've never felt like that. All those people. Like the island had a pulse. I want more of that. I want to be in it, not watching it."],
  ["Back to the bench today?","For a bit. Then the lake. I'm selling programmes at the circus tonight. Unpaid. In a hat with a feather."]],
 "Watch for the feather hat. That's me."],
16:["(Hely is wired.) The debate! Anna and Erik, at the community centre. Veikko's put out every chair in Eastbrook. Paolo's taking odds on who throws the first punch.",[
  ["Who did you bet on?","A Saltwick fisherman, three to one. They've got bigger hands. Don't look at me like that, it's research."],
  ["Who do you want to win?","Erik, because he's ours. But Anna's got fire. I like fire. I'd vote for whoever's on fire, I think. Is that bad?"],
  ["Shouldn't you be listening?","I will be! From the front, by Veikko. He's got a reserved seat. I'm going to sit on the floor next to it and heckle."]],
 "If you see a chair fly, it wasn't me."],
17:["(Hely grabs the edge of the community centre steps as they judder.) Did you feel that? Paolo's going to take a bet on it, I know he is. Was it the ground, or was it Veikko's chairs?",[
  ["It felt like the ground.","Then it's the first honest thing to happen on this island all week. Even the pigeons are looking around."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Everyone says so. I say it's a rumour with good timing. Paolo's offering two to one it blows by Sunday, and I've put a mark on it. Two marks. Fine, five."],
  ["Should you be betting on a volcano?","Should I be betting on anything? That's a separate sermon. I've got Meg's circus show to see next week, and I'd like to pay for the seat."]],
 "If the ground opens up, I'm not paying that debt. Write that down."],
18:["(Hely is juggling three oranges and dropping the fourth, which she hadn't planned.) Practising. The circus at Lake Ilmari said I could have a try-out if I can keep five in the air. I'm at three and a half.",[
  ["That sounds good. Is Bruno helping?","Bruno says my wrists are too stiff. He told me to imagine I'm throwing away a bad idea. I thought of a few and it improved."],
  ["Would you really run away with them?","With a tent over my head and Meg's wire over my head, I'd be a very happy mess. I just need to quit being a very unhappy one here."],
  ["Is five realistic?","On a good day, with a following wind and Paolo not looking. You can have two bruised oranges. I've got more."]],
 "Take an orange. They're past their best, like me."],
19:["(Hely's sitting on the edge of a bench, rubbing her ankle.) The ground shook again at breakfast and I fell off a chair in the café. A man from the Red lot helped me up. I thanked him, and I still don't trust him.",[
  ["Why not?","He was too kind. Nobody's that kind for free. He wanted my signature on something. I told him I'd sign if he cleared my tab at Paolo's. He left."],
  ["Is the café still open?","It is. It's all very brave and very short of cake. A woman screamed when the teaspoons rang, and then laughed."],
  ["Are you worried about the mountain?","I'm worried about everything. It's the mountain's turn, that's all. Look at that sky. It's smoking."]],
 "I'm off to the lake. If I'm going to be afraid, I'll do it near a circus."],
20:["(Hely's wearing a scarf over her mouth.) The air tastes of boiled eggs. Veikko says it's the steam from the north. I told him it's the cook at the community centre. He's very hurt on the cook's behalf.",[
  ["Is it the volcano again?","It's the volcano, clearing its throat. I think we all know it's going to do something grand, and we're being very British about it."],
  ["But you're not British.","I'm very Eastbrook. It amounts to the same. We wait politely for disaster, and make a joke if it's late."],
  ["What would you do if it went?","Run to the lake, jump on Meg's wire and hope the lava has a sense of balance."]],
 "Get yourself a scarf. And a lucky orange, if you can."],
21:["(Hely is covered in grey, and her eyes are enormous.) It went. It really did. I was on the lakeshore, and Meg was on the wire, and the whole sky turned the colour of old dishwater. Meg didn't fall.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Not a soul. Not a bruise, even. I think the island held its breath, and let it out gently. Meg said it was the best view of her life."],
  ["What was it like?","Loud. Then silent. Then Bruno began to laugh, and he couldn't stop, and then we were all laughing. It's not the right response. It's the only one we had."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm alive, and I've forgotten I owed Paolo anything. That's a miracle in itself."]],
 "I'll have to wash my hair. Twice. It'll take a week."],
22:["(Hely's brushing ash off her sleeves.) Everything's grey. My orange's grey. My cat's grey. Even Paolo looked grey, and he's usually a very bright shade of crook.",[
  ["Did Paolo take bets on the volcano?","He did. He now claims every bet was 'void due to an act of God'. He says it so earnestly I nearly believed him."],
  ["Is the circus all right?","The tent's got a hole in the roof, and the ponies have gone off their oats. But Bruno's mending it with a sail."],
  ["Are you helping?","I'm sweeping. I'd rather juggle, but you can't juggle in ash. It comes out of the oranges."]],
 "I'll be at the lake if you need me. Or at the pub. Which is where I'm usually found."],
23:["(Hely is sweeping the same step for the third time.) The tremors keep coming. Veikko's put a bucket of water on the table to watch the ripples. He's always been a scientist, in his heart.",[
  ["Does it help?","It gives him something to look at. I'd rather look at a bookmaker's board, but at present the board says nothing."],
  ["Has Paolo reopened his book?","He's offering evens on the next big one. I said that was disrespectful. He said, 'It's an opportunity.' It's how I know he's all right."],
  ["Will you bet?","Not a penny. I'm turning over a leaf. A grey leaf, but a new one."]],
 "If you see me betting, stop me. Lightly. With a juggling ball."],
24:["(Hely's balancing on a kerb with her arms out.) Meg's been teaching me the wire. She says I've got a good centre and a bad conscience. The centre helps with the wire. The conscience helps with the debt.",[
  ["How's the debt?","Eighty-one marks. It's the same as last week. Which is good. It means it's stopped growing, and so have I."],
  ["Is Meg a good teacher?","She's very quiet, and then she says one thing: 'Don't look down.' I look down immediately. It's a gift."],
  ["Do you think you'll make it?","Some days, yes. Today, no. Tomorrow I'll be an acrobat. There's a lot of tomorrow left."]],
 "Walk the kerb with me. It's cheaper than a circus ticket."],
25:["(Hely's looking at a poster of a princess.) They've put them up all over Eastbrook. 'A Princess for Democracy.' It's a clever notion. The Democrats are always one clever notion short of a majority.",[
  ["Are you backing the Democrats?","I'm backing Veikko. He's got the chairs. And he's let me sit at the front, even though I've not paid my party dues."],
  ["How much are the dues?","Two marks a month. I owe fourteen. I've a good excuse: I've been in a state of volcano."],
  ["Is the princess real?","Apparently. I'd like her to turn up on a unicycle. That'd be the proper thing."]],
 "Tell Erik I'll vote. Tell him I need a rebate."],
26:["(Hely is stirring a cup of coffee, which she doesn't drink.) Everyone's got a poster, a pamphlet or a prince. 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' I'd settle for a krona in every pocket.",[
  ["Is life getting any better?","It's getting louder. Which is the island's idea of better. I can hear the posters being pasted. It's like a giant knitting."],
  ["What would help?","A job. A proper one, with a hat and a pay packet. Or a circus. I'd even take a job in a cola bottle."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","As hopeful as a woman with a debt and no cat. Wait, I've a cat. It left. It came back. It's a complicated animal."]],
 "I'll go home and think. Or I'll go to the pub and think there."],
27:["(Hely holds up three fingers.) That's how many days till the end. I've counted. I've also counted how much I owe, and the two numbers are quite close.",[
  ["Are you worried about the end of the month?","Yes. Because of the island and because of Paolo. One of them's going to be settled. I hope it's the island."],
  ["Will you run away with the circus?","If they'll have me. If they won't, I'll learn to eat fire. It's a skill, and it'd come in handy for chip-frying."],
  ["What does Veikko say?","He says I'd make a wonderful clown. He's right. It's the nicest thing anyone's said all month."]],
 "Come and see me at the lake. I'll show you a trick. It might even work."],
28:["(Hely is standing on her toes, craning to see the street.) Everyone's out. The whole of Eastbrook. Veikko's got his best tie on, and a flag the size of a tablecloth. I've never seen him so brave.",[
  ["What's the mood like?","Fizzy. The Reds have a band, the Democrats have sausages. I've had two sausages and I'm very much for democracy."],
  ["Are you going to the big push?","I'll be there. At the front, if I can, with a bit of heckling. I've a good voice for it, though I say so myself."],
  ["How do you feel?","Like a juggler with seven balls. And it's all going very well, so I'm watching for the floor."]],
 "If you see me falling, don't catch me. I'll bounce."],
29:["(Hely sits very straight, as if posing.) It's the last big day tomorrow. I've told Veikko I'm going to stand on a table and say something wise. He's told me to be careful of the table.",[
  ["What will you say?","I haven't the faintest. Probably 'Vote.' Or 'Pay your debts.' Or 'Look after your friends.' It's all the same thing, deep down."],
  ["Are you ready?","As I'll ever be. I've an orange, a bet and a dream. It's a very flimsy shopping list."],
  ["Any message for Paolo?","Tell him I'll pay what I owe. Eventually. And if I don't, I'll send him a postcard from the circus."]],
 "Wish me luck. I've never been listened to before. It might go to my head."],
30:["(Hely's standing in the middle of the street, holding an orange as if it were a torch.) Today's the day. I can feel it in my knees. It's very quiet. Even Paolo's put his book away.",[
  ["Any last words, Hely?","Be brave. Be a bit daft. Look after the people who've lent you something. And never bet against a woman with an orange."],
  ["Was it a good month?","It was a mountain of a month. I've been shaken, dusted, taught to walk a wire and told I'm a clown. I'd do it again."],
  ["What will you do next?","Run. Toward the circus, or away from the debt. It's the same direction, with luck."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. See you at the lake. Bring your own orange."],
};
// Henrik, editor of the Eastbrook Gazette (The Reporters). Threads: twenty years of headlines, desperate for a scoop, rivalry and pride in Nora, the printing press, the London reporter, his stove and his pipe, the ash.
DAILY['Henrik']={
4:["(Henrik looks up from a proof, over his glasses.) The foreign press. At last. I'm Henrik, editor, twenty years in this chair. I printed the king's coronation and I'll print his funeral. Sit, if you can find a chair.",[
  ["Pleased to meet you. Nice press.","A cast-iron monster. It prints the news and bruises my knuckles."],
  ["What's the Gazette like?","Small, honest, and broke. Mostly in that order."],
  ["What's the big story?","Whatever nobody else has. That's my life's work, and I've never found it."]],
 "Mind the ink."],
5:["(Henrik pins a proof to a board.) The monastery road's open. I sent Nora up to talk to Father Thyme. She came back with a blessing and no quotes.",[
  ["Is Nora any good?","Brilliant. Fearless. Do not tell her. She'll ask for a raise."],
  ["What's the Church up to?","Praying for Augustus. A long shot, but they're patient."],
  ["Any scoops in the monastery?","Probably. But they've a vow of silence, which is rotten for newspapers."]],
 "Check your spelling."],
6:["(Henrik shuffles the proof pages.) A mysterious call this morning, a voice asking if the English reporter was at the Gazette. I said no. He said thank you. A very professional liar, either of us.",[
  ["Who was he?","I've no idea. He called me 'editor' as if it were a mild disease."],
  ["Why would they ask?","Everyone wants to know what the foreigner's writing."],
  ["Have you any theories?","Several. All of them unprintable."]],
 "Keep it off the record."],
7:["(Henrik taps his pipe.) The north-west's open. Frostholm now has a road, and I've got a tip about a very large oil company with very large pockets.",[
  ["Want to share the tip?","Over a pipe, perhaps. Not in front of a typewriter."],
  ["Do you like Diversified?","They advertise. A paper can't be rude to a customer."],
  ["What's the oil man like?","Dario? Smooth. Too smooth. He has a laugh like a till."]],
 "Don't print that."],
8:["(Henrik leans back.) Sunday and I'm in the office. The sermon trouble is in my notes. Father Thyme apparently couldn't find his words. I'd love to lend him a few. I have a spare drawer.",[
  ["Will you print it?","A man looking for words is not news. It's every editor's life."],
  ["What does the Church think of the Gazette?","They subscribe. They say it's a sin. They renew every year."],
  ["Is there a scoop in it?","There's a scoop in everything if you stare long enough."]],
 "Don't stare too long."],
9:["(Henrik takes off his glasses.) Cola war. Kelp's new formula, the Party's People's Cola. The front page writes itself, but I wouldn't trust it. Too neat.",[
  ["Is it a real story?","It's a real fizz. Real news is boring and has no bubbles."],
  ["Who's lying?","Both, a bit. A good newspaper checks both sides and distrusts them equally."],
  ["Will you taste both?","Nora will. I'm too old for fizz."]],
 "Don't swallow it whole."],
10:["(Henrik has a black border round a page.) The king's funeral. I've written the leader three times. The third's the best. It says very little, with great dignity.",[
  ["May I read it?","It's mostly adjectives. 'Solemn', 'grey', 'enduring'."],
  ["What was he like?","A quiet man in a loud coat. Every headline I wrote about him was true and dull."],
  ["What next?","A very loud summer, I expect. Or autumn."]],
 "Rest in print."],
11:["(Henrik taps a pile of paper.) The mill hands are demanding a fair day's pay. It's right and it's real. I'll back them with a headline and a cold lunch.",[
  ["Is that your editorial line?","A fair wage is not politics. It's arithmetic."],
  ["Is the mill a good employer?","It's a mill. It employs, it pays, it grumbles."],
  ["Do the Party back them?","Naturally. They back everyone who's cross."]],
 "Keep it plain."],
12:["(Henrik grins faintly.) A record for Ruby, they say. The Democrats are in on it. And the oil people want something from Ruby too. I've a headline: 'Needle in a Haystack'.",[
  ["What's the record?","I don't know. But everybody wants it, which is how I know it matters."],
  ["Is Nora on it?","Nora's been in Ruby's shop all morning, pretending to browse."],
  ["Is it a big story?","It's a lovely small story. Those are the best."]],
 "Check the spelling of Coltrane."],
13:["(Henrik waves a leaflet.) Free samples. Kelp's giving it away and the Syndicate's after a recipe. If I could print one line on that, I'd retire.",[
  ["What's the line?","'The Secret Is Out.' But I've no secret."],
  ["Do you know who's after it?","The usual crowd. Gustavo's family cooks, and so does everybody else."],
  ["Will you run the story?","When it's true. Which means probably never."]],
 "Print the facts."],
14:["(Henrik presses his tie flat.) Tomorrow's the big day. I've cleared the front page, reserved the presses, and I've no idea what to write. It's the best feeling in the world.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","The island all in one place. Anything could happen. Everything usually does."],
  ["Will you cover it?","Nora will. I'll write it after. With hindsight, I'm a genius."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm an editor. I'm always nervous. It's like a stove."]],
 "Save me a seat."],
15:["(Henrik chuckles.) Crown against cross. Ha! The Gazette's always printed both. Sometimes in the same issue, to be safe.",[
  ["Which do you prefer?","The one that buys advertising."],
  ["Is it a real quarrel?","It's the oldest on the island. Palace versus pews."],
  ["Does the Gazette take sides?","Never, officially. Unofficially, I take sandwiches."]],
 "Stay neutral."],
16:["(Henrik rubs his hands.) The great debate. The Party and the Democrats, on one stage. I've put Nora in the front row with a notebook and strict instructions not to heckle.",[
  ["Will she obey?","Not a chance. But she'll heckle accurately."],
  ["Who'll win?","The audience. They get the entertainment for free."],
  ["Will you print it?","Every word, with the verbs left in."]],
 "Pencils sharp."],
17:["(Henrik glances up.) The floor trembled, did you feel it? A galley fell off the table. I said, in twenty years of printing, I've never had a headline shake itself loose.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Old Mike says so. The harbour says so. The Gazette says 'tremor, minor'."],
  ["Will you print the warning?","Not yet. A false alarm sells a lot of papers and loses a lot of readers."],
  ["Any news about Dario?","Missing, they say. It's the story of the week, and I haven't a word of it confirmed."]],
 "Check your sources."],
18:["(Henrik points north.) Steam. Beautiful. Terrible. Nora wants to go up and see it, and I told her to stay on the right side of the ash.",[
  ["Will you send her?","With a scarf and a pencil. And a stern talking-to."],
  ["Is the relic story true?","The Church has lost something. I've a source who's sworn. I've three sources who refuse to."],
  ["What about Kelpy?","He's a cup. I've written about the cup. The cup is popular."]],
 "Stay upwind."],
19:["(Henrik sighs.) 'Comrades Wanted' posters on every lamppost. I printed them, of course. Business is business. The Party paid in advance, which is rare and virtuous.",[
  ["Did you vote for them?","I vote for the printing bills."],
  ["What's the Midnight Note like?","I've heard Leo play. He makes a saxophone sound like regret."],
  ["Is there a story at the Note?","Possibly. Someone's going incognito. I'm told it's royal."]],
 "Keep it incognito."],
20:["(Henrik taps the desk.) The palace job, the man at the Hotel Aurora. Two stories and no proof. I've a drawer full of such stories. One day, one of them will be true.",[
  ["Who's the man?","A stranger. He signed the register in a name I recognise from a postcard."],
  ["Is the palace in danger?","Palaces always are. It's why they have walls."],
  ["Will you print it?","Not on a hunch. A hunch is for the crossword."]],
 "Wait for proof."],
21:["(Henrik is at the window, face grey.) The mountain. It went up. I've never written a headline so fast. 'VOLCANO ERUPTS. NO ONE KILLED.' Four words, all true.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Shaken. I've set the type three times. My hands won't hold still."],
  ["Is the press running?","It's coughing. Ash in the rollers."],
  ["What's the mood in Eastbrook?","Silent. Then everyone talking at once."]],
 "Print the facts."],
22:["(Henrik shakes ash from his sleeve.) The Gazette's got ash in every drawer. Nora swept the whole front office, and it's greyer than before. I've told her it's character.",[
  ["Any stories?","A farm roof collapsed, a cow's been rescued, a ferry's cancelled. Fine fresh news."],
  ["Is the paper still printing?","Barely. We're down to one page and a very big font."],
  ["Is the Royal Family doing anything?","Something for Daphne, I'm told. Don't ask me what."]],
 "Shake your coat out."],
23:["(Henrik holds up a rag.) Someone's put a spanner in the works, I've heard, at a machine somewhere. 'Spanner in the Works' is a good headline. If only I knew what it meant.",[
  ["Do you know who?","A rumour says the Party. Another says the oil lot. A third says a seagull."],
  ["Will Nora follow it?","She's on the trail. And so's the ash."],
  ["What about the oil's repairs?","Deep repairs, they say. I'd love to see a map."]],
 "Follow the spanner."],
24:["(Henrik settles into his chair.) Monks heading for the peaks, crews sweeping the town. A retreat and a clean-up on the same day. I've a feeling someone's tidying evidence.",[
  ["Whose?","Whoever's got the best brooms."],
  ["Is it a good story?","It's a very quiet one. My favourite kind."],
  ["Do you trust the crews?","I trust nobody who sweeps that thoroughly."]],
 "Check what they sweep."],
25:["(Henrik is beaming.) A princess for democracy, a crown for everyone. Nora's got an exclusive. It's the best she's written. I've asked her to cut three adjectives.",[
  ["Is it true?","It's sourced. Which is better than true."],
  ["What do the Democrats say?","They deny, with enthusiasm. It's how you know."],
  ["Will it sell?","Every copy. Even the ones with ash on."]],
 "Print it big."],
26:["(Henrik reads aloud.) 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' Kelp's stunt. Ridiculous. I love it. And 'Dario Buys the Spa'. I've a column for each. The island's gone mad, and it's good for circulation.",[
  ["Is it good news?","It's news. That's good enough."],
  ["Why a spa?","Nobody knows. That's why I'm printing it."],
  ["Do you like the new cola?","I like the headline."]],
 "Keep it fizzy."],
27:["(Henrik flips through notes.) Revolution at the pictures, high stakes in a cellar. I've sent Nora to the first, myself to neither. I'm too old to be shot at or moved to tears.",[
  ["Are you going to the pictures?","I'll read the review. Nora's writing it. She'll cry at the horse."],
  ["What's the high stakes thing?","A card game. Big money, bigger egos."],
  ["Will it be in the paper?","If someone loses a shirt."]],
 "Bring a handkerchief."],
28:["(Henrik is pacing.) The day of the big pushes. The Party marches, the palace has a state visit, Kelp is going abroad, and the oil lot... has an office. Every story at once. I'm giddy.",[
  ["Where's Nora?","Out. Somewhere. With a notebook and no sense of danger."],
  ["How will you cover it all?","With caffeine and prayer."],
  ["Do you think it matters?","It's the last proper push. Everything counts."]],
 "Print everything."],
29:["(Henrik mops his brow.) Tomorrow it's over, and I've a hundred columns and a single front page. I'll leave a gap for the headline, and hope it fits.",[
  ["What will the headline be?","I've no idea. Neither has anyone. That's the best bit."],
  ["Are you ready?","I've been ready for twenty years."],
  ["Any hunches?","One. It'll be a surprise. It always is."]],
 "Leave a gap."],
30:["(Henrik stands by the press, quiet.) The last day. Whatever happens, I'll print it, true and plain, and the next morning someone'll read it with their porridge. That's journalism.",[
  ["Good luck, Henrik.","And you. Don't flatter us in London."],
  ["Any last advice?","Be right first, fast second."],
  ["Nervous?","A little. It's good for the ink."]],
 "Stop the presses, then start them."],
};
// Hilda, farmer at East Farm (The Farmers). Threads: dawn chores and the weather, Olaf's complaining, the old barn that hums, her pumpkins, her low opinion of the towns.
DAILY['Hilda']={
5:["(Hilda doesn't look up from the bucket she's scrubbing.) You'll be the London reporter. Boots off at the door, and don't step on the cabbages.",[
  ["Nice farm. How long have you been here?","Thirty winters, give or take. The farm's older than I am, and it'll be here when I'm not."],
  ["What does a farmer make of all this fuss?","The king's dead, so there's fuss. The cows still want milking at five. They've no opinion on succession."],
  ["Is Olaf about?","Out in the barn, finding something to complain about. He'll have found it by now."]],
 "Mind the gate on your way out. It bites."],
6:["(Hilda is peeling potatoes into a bowl the size of a bathtub.) A woman from Eastbrook came by yesterday asking if we sell to the shops. I told her we sell to whoever pays on time.",[
  ["Do you grow all that yourselves?","Potatoes, cabbages, wheat and pumpkins. And Olaf's temper, which is the only crop that never fails."],
  ["Do people from the towns bother you?","They come out in clean shoes and leave in dirty ones. I like to think we improve them."],
  ["What time do you get up?","Four, when it's light enough. Half past three when Olaf's snoring wakes me first."]],
 "There's soup on, but not for you. Off with you."],
7:["(Hilda is squinting at the sky.) Frostholm's road is open now, they say. Nobody out here asked for that. More people, more mud on the yard.",[
  ["Do you have any good news?","The pumpkins are coming on. Fat as bishops, the biggest ones. I've got my eye on the fair prize."],
  ["Who comes out to buy from you?","Lisa from the supermarket, Pirkko for her food van when she's short. Everyone else shouts through the gate."],
  ["Is the weather turning?","It's always turning. Today it turns cold, tomorrow it turns colder. I wear the same coat for all of it."]],
 "If you see the post, tell him the gate's mended."],
8:["(Hilda has a hymn book open on the table, next to a sack of seed.) Father Thyme has a sermon on today, so I've half an ear on the wireless. I'll hear it. I won't be told what to think by a man who's never milked a cow.",[
  ["Are you religious?","I'm Orthodox in the way weather is. It's there, and I go to church when it's raining."],
  ["What's the sermon about?",["Nobody knows. They say the Father can't find the words. I've never known a priest short of words before.",[
    ["Maybe grief does that.","It does. I stood at my own mother's grave with nothing to say. The words came out in the potatoes afterwards."],
    ["Will you go to hear him?","If the cows allow. They've got a better memory for my chores than I have."]]]],
  ["Anything strange about the farm lately?","Only the old barn. Locked, boarded up, and it hums at night. Olaf says it's the wind in the beams. I say wind doesn't keep time."]],
 "Eat something before you go, you look like a lamp post."],
9:["(Hilda is wiping her hands on her apron.) There's talk of a new cola flavour today. Somebody in Kingsgrad is getting a lot of money to decide what sweet water should taste like.",[
  ["Do you drink Kelp Cola?","Olaf does, in secret. I find the empty bottles in his coat. I pretend to be surprised."],
  ["Is the Party selling its own cola?","The Communists? They'd give it away and then ask for the bottles back. I'd like to see it."],
  ["What do you drink?","Tea. Strong, black and made in a pot that's older than your newspaper."]],
 "Close the door. The draught's got a mind of its own."],
10:["(Hilda is in her black dress, hat pinned on, and holding a handkerchief she won't use.) They're burying him in Kingsgrad today. We can't go, with the cows. So we'll stand in the yard at noon.",[
  ["Did you like the king?","I never met him. But he waved at the tractor once, on the road, and I've never forgotten it."],
  ["Will you listen on the radio?","We'll hear it all, over the fence, from the neighbour's set. Olaf will cry, and he'll say it's the wind."],
  ["Who should be king next?","Not my business. The ones who clean the barns don't pick kings. But I'd like one who knows what a cow is."]],
 "A good day for the king to go. Clear sky. He'd have liked it."],
11:["(Hilda is stacking firewood by the porch.) The Eastbrook mill hands are asking for fair pay, so I hear. I'll say this: someone who works hard should be paid for it.",[
  ["Do you hire hands in harvest?","Two lads from the village, every autumn. They eat like horses and sing like crows, and I'd miss them."],
  ["Is the Democratic Party helping the mill?","Erik's lot, yes. Good men, if they'd stop writing leaflets and pick up a shovel."],
  ["Do you earn a fair wage?","The land pays what it pays. Some years I'm rich in cabbages and poor in money."]],
 "Take a carrot for the road. A raw one, mind."],
12:["(Hilda is hauling a pumpkin onto the scales, red in the face.) Forty pounds and it's still growing. I've named it Magnus, after nobody in particular.",[
  ["Can you win the fair prize?","There's Father Thyme's marrows to beat. He won't admit it's a competition, but he counts every one."],
  ["Is there anything on at Eastbrook today?","Something to do with a record for Ruby. I don't see the point. You can't eat a record."],
  ["What does Olaf think of Magnus?","He thinks I love it more than him. He's not wrong, it's very good-looking."]],
 "Don't lean on the scales. I've only just balanced them."],
13:["(Hilda has a crate of cabbages at the gate.) Somebody's been through the district giving away free cola samples. I've had a boy at the door with a basket of tiny bottles. I told him the cows were thirsty.",[
  ["Did you try one?","One sip. It tasted like a sweet shop had a fight with a pine tree."],
  ["Why do they come all the way here?","They'll hand it out to anyone with a mouth, and then they'll want a vote, or a field, or a signature."],
  ["Did you give the samples to the cows?","One cow sniffed it and walked away. Cows know best."]],
 "If anyone comes with more, point them at the pond."],
14:["(Hilda is brushing her hair, which she does maybe twice a year.) They're all going to gather tomorrow, I hear. The whole island. I'm not sure what for, but Olaf's polishing his boots.",[
  ["Are you going to the big day?","Yes. Olaf wants to see what everyone looks like when they're all in one place. I want to see who sits down first."],
  ["What are you expecting?","Shouting, mostly. And somebody giving a speech that could've been a sentence."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Nervous is for people with time. We've got a cow due and a roof that leaks."]],
 "We'll leave the lamp on for the cows. They get lonely."],
15:["(Hilda is in the yard, hanging out sheets in a wind that clearly disapproves.) I hear the royal side and the church side are going at each other today, crown against cross. I'd put them in a field together and see who gets out first.",[
  ["Whose side are you on?","The side that doesn't send me a bill. I'll pay my tithe to the church and my taxes to the king, and neither will send a thank-you."],
  ["Is it good to have kings and priests?","It's good to have something to argue about. Otherwise it'd be potatoes, all the way down."],
  ["Do you ever argue with Olaf?","Daily. Today it's about whether the sheet is dry. It is. He's wrong."]],
 "There's a cold wind coming. I can feel it in my knee."],
16:["(Hilda has the radio on, loud.) There's some kind of debate today, Communists against Democrats. Olaf says he'll listen with his arms folded.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who talks least. They'll all say the potato price will go up and none of them will do it."],
  ["Do you vote?","If I'm asked. Erik asked me nicely once. The Communists sent a man to explain why I didn't need to."],
  ["Does the debate matter out here?","Only if it makes the diesel dearer. Politics ends at the farm gate, unless it's dragging a lorry."]],
 "Keep your voice down. I'm trying to hear who's lying."],
17:["(Hilda is on the porch, with her hand flat against the wooden post.) Did you feel that? The ground gave a little shiver just now. Not much. Like a cow turning over.",[
  ["Was that an earthquake?","A little one. The old barn hummed louder just after, which is no comfort at all."],
  ["Is the volcano waking up?","They say so, in the north. I say it's the island clearing its throat."],
  ["Are you scared?","Of the volcano, no. Of Olaf trying to fix it with a hammer, yes."]],
 "If the milk starts to wobble, I'll let you know."],
18:["(Hilda is stacking pumpkins in the shed.) There's a church story going round, about some lost relic. Everyone's looking for it under their beds, I hear. I've looked in the barn. Nothing but straw and spiders.",[
  ["What sort of relic?","A bone, or a finger, or a bit of cloth. They never say. A saint's, they say, if it turns up."],
  ["Has anybody searched the farm?","Two boys in black coats walked through the yard yesterday, looking in the wells. I told them the only holy thing here is the milk."],
  ["Does Olaf believe in relics?","He believes in anything that costs less than a tractor."]],
 "If the relic turns up, don't tell Olaf. He'll sell it."],
19:["(Hilda is boiling something that smells of turnips and thunder.) There was a rumour that the Party's after workers, and the palace is up to something at the Midnight Note. People are always up to something. I'm up to my elbows in soup.",[
  ["Has any of the Party been out to the farm?","A man in a cap offered to let us all share the land. I said we'd share it with him when he shared his coat."],
  ["Do you go to the Midnight Note?","Heavens, no. Loud music and expensive tea. I like my evenings dull and my beds made."],
  ["What's cooking?","Turnip soup. It's a punishment I give myself. Olaf likes it, which says something about Olaf."]],
 "Stand back from the pot. It spits."],
20:["(Hilda is looking at the sky, sniffing.) There's something in the air. A tang. Like an iron stove that's been left too long. Something's coming, I'll bet my boots.",[
  ["What do you mean?","Weather. Or worse. The cows won't stand still in the field and the hens have stopped laying out of spite."],
  ["Any trouble out here?","The old barn was louder last night. A hum like a very large cat. Olaf says it's the pipes. We've no pipes."],
  ["What are people saying?","That a man's been seen at a hotel in Eastbrook, and the Democrats are scurrying. Not my business."]],
 "Latch the gate. Everything feels a bit loose today."],
21:["(Hilda is standing in the yard, apron grey with ash, staring north.) Well. Now it's happened. The whole sky's the colour of a dirty sock. Olaf's on the roof with a broom, shouting.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","We are. The cows are, though they look offended. Ash on everything. It's in the tea."],
  ["Is the volcano close?","Far enough. A great black cloud and a smell like a struck match. I never wanted to see that."],
  ["What will you do?","Sweep. Then sweep again. Then drink something strong and sweep once more."]],
 "Keep your mouth covered. You'll taste it for a week otherwise."],
22:["(Hilda is shaking a blanket of grey dust off the porch.) There's talk of cola people coming round the farms, something about trouble with a farm. I hope it isn't ours. We've enough trouble with the ash.",[
  ["Are Kelp Cola men visiting?","Sniffing around, I hear. For fields, for water, for what, I can't say. I told Olaf to keep his mouth shut and his gate shut."],
  ["Is the ash ruining the crop?","The pumpkins are dusted like cakes. The cabbages are sulking. We'll know in a week."],
  ["What's the mood out here?","Tired. Everyone's coughing and everyone's cross. Even the hens have gone quiet."]],
 "If anyone from the cola works comes, I'm not in."],
23:["(Hilda is trying to dust the ash off the old barn's boards, then stops.) It's warmer by this barn. I put my hand on the wood and it's warm. Strange. Like a sleeping animal.",[
  ["Maybe it's the volcano.","That's the sensible answer, which is why I don't trust it. It was warm before the ash."],
  ["Did you ever look inside?","Locked since Olaf's father's time. The key's long lost. We'd have to take the doors off."],
  ["Are there any aftershocks?","Little ones. The cups rattle in the cupboard. It's like living on a drum."]],
 "Don't touch the old barn. I mean it. It's got a mood."],
24:["(Hilda has a pot of tea and a plate of cold potatoes and looks tired all through.) The church is heading for the mountains today, some retreat. I'd go too if I could leave the cows. Peace and quiet sounds heavenly.",[
  ["Are you going on the retreat?","No. The cows are my retreat. They don't ask questions."],
  ["Why the mountains?","Away from the ash and the shouting, I suppose. And nearer to God, but He's got ash on His shoulders too."],
  ["Is Olaf holding up?","He's grumbling in his sleep about the price of coal. That's how I know he's alive."]],
 "Have a potato. It's not much, but it's hot."],
25:["(Hilda is sorting seed in the dim light.) I hear the royals are handing out crowns to everybody now, and the Democrats want a princess. Everyone wants something. I just want my turnips.",[
  ["A crown for everyone?","Why not? We could all wear them to milk the cows. Better than a hat."],
  ["Is there a princess involved?","So they say. Pretty, clever, in demand. I hope someone remembers she's a person and not a prize pumpkin."],
  ["How is the ash cleanup?","Done as far as the porch. Beyond that, it's a country matter."]],
 "Come again when you've something other than news. I've plenty of that."],
26:["(Hilda is at the kitchen table with a lamp lit, though it's afternoon.) Everyone's talking about the Colonel and the cola men and the oil men buying things. Dario buying a spa, of all things. A man who can buy a spa doesn't need our turnips.",[
  ["Is it true about the spa?","Rumour says so. Soaking in hot water while the rest of us shovel ash. It's the way of the world."],
  ["Is the cola in the district again?","A little. Princes on bottles, they say. I'd sooner have a cow on a bottle. At least it's honest."],
  ["Does any of it touch the farm?","It touches everything, in the end. Like the ash."]],
 "Take care on the road. The ruts are deep after the ash."],
27:["(Hilda is darning a sock by the stove.) There's something at the pictures in Kingsgrad tomorrow, a revolution. I've seen revolutions. They begin with a speech and end with a bill.",[
  ["Will you go to the pictures?","Never been. Olaf went once, in 1961, and fell asleep during the cowboys."],
  ["Is the Party planning something?","Whatever it is, they'll do it with flags. They love a flag."],
  ["What would you like to see?","A film with a good strong cow in it. Or a very long one about rain."]],
 "The stove's warm. Sit a minute, then go."],
28:["(Hilda has her best hat on, and her second-best expression.) It's the big push, all the factions at once. I can hear the roads humming from here. The state visit, the cola going abroad, the Party and the flag. And the oil lot in their office.",[
  ["Are you worried?","Only about the hens. They don't like noise, and there's plenty of it."],
  ["Does the farm matter in all this?","You'd think not. But everybody eats, and everybody's coming to our gate eventually."],
  ["What do you want to happen?",["I want people to stop shouting for a week. I want the old barn to stop humming. I want ash off my cabbages.",[
    ["What do you think the old barn is?","I think it's something Olaf's father left in there. A generator, maybe. Rats with ambitions. Don't tell Olaf I said that."],
    ["Will you open it?","When the key turns up. Or when the hum gets louder than my temper."]]]]],
 "Keep your head down out there. Tomorrow's worse."],
29:["(Hilda is stirring a pot with great purpose.) Everyone's in a state about lines of success and primaries and goodness knows what. All I know is that someone is going to be disappointed.",[
  ["Who will be disappointed?","Most of them. It's like the pumpkin fair. One winner, a lot of sour faces."],
  ["Will you vote tomorrow?","If there's a vote. If there's a pig, I'd vote for the pig. Better manners."],
  ["Any last wisdom?","Plant what you can. Mend what you can. Don't count on anything you haven't put in the ground."]],
 "Eat well tonight. Nerves taste better with stew."],
30:["(Hilda is in the yard at first light, arms folded, looking at the sky.) Well, it's the last day. Whatever happens, the sun rises and the cows still need milking. I find that a comfort.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Hopeful, and trying not to say so out loud. Hoping is bad luck, like whistling indoors."],
  ["What have you learned this month?","That the island's much bigger than the farm, and the farm's much bigger than the island's problems."],
  ["Anything for the paper?","Tell them East Farm kept going. It's not much of a headline, but it's the truth."]],
 "Safe road, reporter. Wipe your feet next time."],
};
// Igor, strongman of the circus at Lake Ilmari (the Circus). Threads: he is gentle out of the ring, his iron bars and the ringmaster he
// lifts nightly, his secret love of knitting and small animals, Bruno's snoring in the bunk above, the volcano and helping others.
DAILY['Igor']={
7:["(Igor is bending an iron bar into a pretzel, very carefully.) Hello. Don't mind this. It's for the show. Bruno likes it knotted. I prefer them straight, but he's the ringmaster.",[
  ["I'm the British reporter.","Pleased to meet you. I'm Igor. I lift things. It's not complicated. Would you like to hold the bar?"],
  ["That looks heavy.","It's twelve kilos. I like it. It's honest. It doesn't argue."],
  ["Do you enjoy being the strongman?","Mostly. I'd like to be the strong man of a quieter show. But I'm good at it, so here I am."]],
 "If you need anything lifted, ask. It's the only thing I'm good at."],
8:["(Igor is sitting on the step of the caravan, with a ball of wool and a pair of needles.) Please don't tell the others. It's a scarf. For Bruno, for his birthday. He thinks I'm sharpening something.",[
  ["I won't tell. You knit?","My grandmother taught me. In the evenings, after the lifting. It's calming. It's the only thing I do that's smaller than me."],
  ["What colour is it?","Red, to match the coat. I've dropped three stitches. I hope he doesn't notice. He notices everything but the stitches."],
  ["Is it a secret?","The wool is. Everyone knows I knit. It's just that no one's said."]],
 "Keep it to yourself, please. It's the nicest secret I've got."],
9:["(Igor is holding a bottle of cola very gently, as if it might shout.) A man gave me this. He asked what I thought of the taste. I said it's like a very sweet thunderstorm. He wrote it down.",[
  ["Did you like it?","It made my nose tickle. I like water better. But I like it when people are kind enough to ask."],
  ["Who was the man?","From the Kelp Cola. A small man in glasses. He looked up at me the whole time. I crouched, so he wouldn't strain his neck."],
  ["Would you appear in an advert?","If I could hold a kitten. I'd like to be in a cola advert with a kitten."]],
 "I'd share this with Bruno, but he'll only argue about the bubbles."],
10:["(Igor has put on a black armband over the leather strap on his arm.) We're not performing today. I carried the black cloth for the big top myself. It's very quiet when you're a strongman and there's nothing to lift.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","No. But I think he was lonely. It's a feeling I know. A big man in a big house, with no one to talk to."],
  ["How are you holding up?","I'm fine. I went for a walk by the lake and talked to a duck. It was a good conversation."],
  ["Is Bruno upset?","He's trying not to be. He's polishing his boots. When he's upset, he polishes."]],
 "A quiet day. I'm glad of it, in a sad sort of way."],
11:["(Igor is carrying a log on each shoulder, like a pair of very large matching umbrellas.) Firewood. For the stove. I hear Eastbrook's mill hands want a fair day's pay. I think they should have it. It's hard work, lifting logs, and I'm only doing two.",[
  ["Do you earn a fair wage?","I get my food and my bunk, and a kind word from Meg. I'd like a pay rise, but I've never been able to ask. Bruno gets so loud."],
  ["Could you ask him?","I could. But I'd have to look at him while he was lying down, and it makes him sad. So I lift him instead."],
  ["Would you work in a mill?","I'd work anywhere there's a thing to lift. But I'd miss the lights."]],
 "I'll put the fire on. It's cold, and it's a good thing to do for others."],
12:["(Igor sits on a crate, looking at a small bird on his finger.) She's a wren. She comes every morning. I give her crumbs. I call her Mrs Pettigrew. Don't tell Bruno. He'd put her in the act.",[
  ["Does she mind you being big?","She doesn't seem to. She sits on my thumb as if it were a branch. It's the nicest thing that happens all day."],
  ["Do you like animals?","More than people, sometimes. They don't ask you to bend things. They just want to be near you."],
  ["What does Bruno think?","Bruno thinks I'm the finest strongman in Europe. He doesn't know about the wren. It's better that way."]],
 "Be gentle going past. She's easily startled."],
13:["(Igor is polishing a large brass weight with his sleeve.) They're giving away free cola in Kingsgrad tomorrow, I hear. Bruno says we should go, and stand near it, and look strong. He thinks I'd sell a lot of cola.",[
  ["Would you do it?","If I was asked nicely. I don't like being used as a prop. But I like a free drink, so I'm torn."],
  ["Is that what the weight's for?","It's for the show. Though a few of the Cola people seemed very interested in how much it weighs. I told them: heavier than a promise."],
  ["How's your day been?","Quiet. I carried water, I fixed a hinge, I read to the horse. It's a gentle life, if you don't count the iron."]],
 "Careful with the free things. They always want something back."],
14:["(Igor is in his best leotard, and looks like a lighthouse in it.) Today's the big day, they say. I don't know what it is, but I've been told to look impressive. I'm doing my best. I'm breathing in.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","A little. Crowds make me feel small, which is odd for a man my size. I like them best when they're far away."],
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches and shouting. I hope no one gets hurt. If they do, I'll carry them."],
  ["Are you performing?","If I'm asked. Bruno's got a very big plan, with a lot of me in it."]],
 "I'll stay at the back. It's where the quiet people stand."],
15:["(Igor is carrying a very large wooden cross and a very large wooden crown, one under each arm.) For the show. Bruno's idea. Crown versus cross. I'm to hold one up in each hand and flex. I told him it's a bit political.",[
  ["Which is heavier?","The crown. The cross is lighter than it looks. But I think the crown's heavier in other ways too."],
  ["Who would you pick?","I wouldn't. I'd like them both to be kind. If I had to lift one of them, it'd be whichever was nearer the door."],
  ["What did Bruno say?","He said it was a political masterstroke. Then he fell asleep. It's how he thinks."]],
 "I'll hold them up as long as I'm asked. But I'd rather put them down."],
16:["(Igor is lying on his back, doing very slow repetitions with a bar.) The Communists and the Democrats are having a big talk. Bruno wants me to go along, as a bodyguard for him. I said he's never in danger. He said, 'That's why I need you.'",[
  ["Do you like the debate idea?","I like it when people talk. It's better than shouting. But I don't understand what they're talking about."],
  ["Will you go?","If Bruno asks. I'll stand at the back and try to look neutral. It's difficult at my size."],
  ["Who do you think is right?","Whoever's kind to the horse. It's not very political, but it's my test."]],
 "If it gets noisy, I'll cover my ears. It's allowed."],
17:["(Igor has stopped mid-lift. The bar is still in the air, and he looks at the ground.) The earth moved. Just a little. It was like when someone taps you on the shoulder, but from underneath.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm big, but I'm not brave. I just look it. I'd like to hold on to something."],
  ["Was it a tremor?","I think so. Zora said there'd be one. She said it very kindly, and then gave me a biscuit."],
  ["What will you do?","Hold up the big top, if it comes to it. I've done it for a joke. I could do it for real."]],
 "If it happens again, hold on to me. I'm very good at not falling over."],
18:["(Igor is knitting a very long grey sock.) It's for Mrs Pettigrew. The wren. I know she's too small. But I thought, if there's a cold snap, she'll want something. It's more of a tiny hat, actually.",[
  ["That's very kind.","It's silly. But it makes me happy, so I'll carry on. The others don't need to know."],
  ["What's the mood in the camp?","Nervous. Zora's drawing curtains and Bruno's pacing. Meg is checking things, and Buttons is eating. It's a normal week."],
  ["Is the ground still moving?","Now and then. It's like a big animal turning in its sleep. I'd prefer it to stay asleep."]],
 "I'll make you one too, if you like. A scarf. In grey. It's appropriate."],
19:["(Igor is carrying every chair in the big top, three at a time, to the lakeshore.) Meg's idea. In case the ground does something. She's very clever. She said, 'Igor, carry,' and I did. I like being told what to do by someone who knows.",[
  ["Is it heavy work?","It's wooden chairs. I've lifted heavier. It's the number that tires me, not the weight."],
  ["Have the Communists been round?","A man with a red badge asked if I'd join. I said I'd think about it. He said the people need strong arms. I said, so do the chairs."],
  ["Are you worried about the shaking?","A bit. But I try not to show it. A scared strongman is bad for morale."]],
 "Mind your feet. These are going down in a line."],
20:["(Igor has a very large coat on, and is clutching a small bag.) Madame Zora told me to pack. Just the things I couldn't do without. I packed the wren's hat and a pair of socks. It's all I need.",[
  ["Is something going to happen?","She says so. She says a great fire. She told me to take care of the others. I said I would. It's what I do."],
  ["What will you carry?","Bruno, if he'll let me. Buttons, definitely. Meg won't want to be carried, but I'll try."],
  ["Are you ready?","As much as I can be. I'm big, and I'm strong, and I'm very frightened. It's all right."]],
 "Stay with the group tomorrow. It's what I'd say if I were wise."],
21:["(Igor is standing in the grey rain of ash, holding up a corner of the big top with one hand and a child's cap with the other.) It's all right. Everyone's here. I've counted them. I counted twice.",[
  ["The volcano erupted.","Yes. It was very loud, and very red. It was like being inside a drum. I put my hands over the horse's ears."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","Everyone I've seen. Bruno's gone grey and Buttons is white. Zora's in her caravan, and she says she's fine. I believe her."],
  ["How are you?","Shaky. But I held the pole, and it stayed up. It's a good feeling, being useful when it matters."]],
 "I'll carry anything you need. It's a day for carrying."],
22:["(Igor is shovelling ash from around the caravans, very neatly.) I'm making paths. If we have to move quickly, we'll have clear ways. It's easier than thinking. I like doing it.",[
  ["Is there a lot?","A lot, and it's heavy. Grey ash is heavier than snow, but it doesn't melt. I've decided to treat it like sand."],
  ["How's Bruno?","He's cross, and he's brave. He's complaining about the ash in his coat. It's how I know he's all right."],
  ["Is the lake safe?","It's grey on top and clear underneath. Like a lot of people."]],
 "I'll clear a path to your door too, if I'm passing."],
23:["(Igor is carrying a stranger's mattress on his back through the ash.) A man from Eastbrook asked for help. His roof's cracked. I said, of course. It's only a mattress. It's the least a big man can do.",[
  ["That's kind of you.","It's easy. I'd like to do more. I don't have words, but I have shoulders."],
  ["Are the Party people helping too?","There was a spanner man in the road. He was very polite. He said they were mending something. I said, 'Can I hold it?' He said no."],
  ["How are you holding up?","Tired, but happy. It's a good kind of tired."]],
 "If you see someone with a heavy load, tell them to ask me."],
24:["(Igor is sitting with Mrs Pettigrew on his shoulder, a wool hat on her head.) She came back. After the ash. I was worried. I thought she'd gone. She's a very small, very brave bird.",[
  ["That's a good hat.","It's a very small hat. I'm rather proud of it. It's the best knitting I've done."],
  ["The monks have gone up the mountain.","I hear they pray for us. I'd like to see them. I'd carry their bags, if they'd let me."],
  ["Are the aftershocks over?","Nearly. Just the odd small one. She doesn't mind. She hops."]],
 "Mind where you step. She's on the ground sometimes."],
25:["(Igor has a long length of ribbon in each hand, one red, one blue.) A crown for everyone, they say. I'm making a ribbon crown. It's for Meg. She doesn't know. I'm hiding it behind the net.",[
  ["That's sweet.","I'd like to make one for each of us. And one for the horse. She'd wear it with dignity."],
  ["Do you follow the talk of crowns?","Not really. I'd like everyone to be happy. If that means a prince, fine. If it means a vote, also fine."],
  ["What's the feeling in the camp?","We've got a bit more patience, I think. We've been through something together. It's made us softer."]],
 "I'll finish it tonight. By candle, if I have to."],
26:["(Igor is lifting Bruno over his head, with a slightly worried expression, in a rehearsal.) He wants a new move. He wants to be waving a flag. It's very difficult to lift a man who's waving a flag.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Only if he moves. He's been told not to. He's never listened to that."],
  ["Is it for the Prince in every bottle thing?","No. It's a flag for the circus. He says a flag's the only thing that's honest about being a flag."],
  ["Are you enjoying it?","I like the weight. It reminds me of home. We had very big sacks of flour. I lifted them for fun."]],
 "Hold on, Bruno. I'm putting you down."],
27:["(Igor has a large book under his arm.) It's a book of knitting patterns. I got it at the Eastbrook library. I'm going to the film tonight, I think, with Meg. The revolution one. I'd like to see how they do the crowds.",[
  ["Are you looking forward to it?","Yes. I like films. People talk quietly and look at each other, and I don't have to lift anything."],
  ["What's the film about?","A revolution. I hope it's a gentle one. I don't like it when there's too much shouting."],
  ["Is the mood changing?","It's tense. I can feel it, like when you hold your breath. Everyone's waiting."]],
 "I'll tell you the story afterwards. I'll try to keep it short."],
28:["(Igor sits on the big top steps, hands on his knees.) It's the biggest day. Everyone's out and about. I've been told to look after the camp. It's a good job. I'm very, very good at staying still.",[
  ["Are you worried?","A little. It's a lot of people with a lot of feelings. I'd rather hold a mountain."],
  ["Will you go to Kingsgrad?","Not today. Bruno says we're to stay by the lake. I'm glad. I'd rather be with the horse."],
  ["How is everyone?","Quiet. Meg's mending, Buttons is sleeping, Zora's drawing the curtains. It's the eve of something."]],
 "I'll watch the road. If anyone comes, I'll let you know."],
29:["(Igor has a blanket wrapped around his shoulders, and the ringmaster's red scarf is finished in his lap.) I'm giving it to him tonight. It's for the last night. He won't say thank you, but I'll know he likes it.",[
  ["That's a lovely scarf.","It has seven dropped stitches. I counted. Bruno will think it's a design."],
  ["How are you feeling?","Calm. I think we've done all we can. The rest is up to the island."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Lift, if I'm asked. And watch. And hope. It's a good combination."]],
 "Sleep well. I will, once I've checked the horse."],
30:["(Igor stands at the lake's edge, the red scarf round his neck, a wren on his shoulder.) The last day. I'm not good at words, but I'd like to say: thank you for visiting. It's been nice, talking to someone who doesn't need lifting.",[
  ["It's been a pleasure.","For me too. I'll carry the memory. It's very light."],
  ["What will you do after?","Go on with the circus. Pack the caravan, lift the poles, and see where the road goes."],
  ["Any last words?","Be gentle. It's the only strength that lasts."]],
 "Goodbye. Mind how you go. I'll be here."],
};
// Ilse, log loader on the mill road outside Eastbrook (Democratic Party, the Union). Threads: her strength and the contests she wins,
// Knut as rival and friend, her scorn for the Communists' talk, her savings for a truck of her own, Dagny's pay.
DAILY['Ilse']={
4:["(A big woman in a green cap heaves a log onto the stack without any visible effort.) Mind your toes. You're the reporter, then. I'm Ilse. I load. Don't offer to help, I've seen what happens.",[
  ["How heavy is that log?","About ninety kilos, wet. Dry, it's a feather. I've had the same argument with Knut every day for six years."],
  ["Do you ever get tired?","Of the work? No. Of people asking if I get tired? Every day."],
  ["Is it a good job?","It's an honest one. You put a log here and it stays here. That's a rare thing to find on this island."]],
 "Stand clear of the stack. It's got ideas."],
5:["(Ilse leans on a log as if it were a fence post.) Snow's gone and the lorries are back. Everyone wants timber. It's a good week to be big and cheap.",[
  ["Are you cheap?","The company thinks so. Dagny pays me what she'd pay a smaller man, and I move twice the timber. I've done the sums, and she's done them wrong."],
  ["Does Knut work hard?","Knut works loudly. It's not the same. But the saw likes him. And when the saw's happy, I load faster."],
  ["Do you like Eastbrook?","I like the people and the pine. I'd like it better with fewer speeches."]],
 "I'll let you carry a twig. For the photograph."],
6:["(Ilse lifts a log with one arm and sets it down gently.) A man from the circus at the lake came by and asked if I'd join the strongmen. I told him I've got a job. He said, 'It's a tent, not a job.' I said that was my point.",[
  ["Would you ever join the circus?","For an afternoon. I'd like to see the elephant. But I'd want a proper contract, and a place for my boots."],
  ["Can you lift a man?","Two, if they're polite. I carried Knut out of the tavern once. He's no weight, apart from his opinions."],
  ["What does your family say about the job?","My mother thinks I should marry a carpenter. My father thought I should be one. I became both, in a way. I carry for them."]],
 "If you want to see strength, watch a tree fall. It's the quietest violence."],
7:["(Ilse is eating an enormous sandwich in four bites.) The roads are open, so the lorry from Frostholm's been. The driver, a tiny man, tried to flirt. I picked up his lorry. Not the whole thing, just the back end.",[
  ["Did he mind?","He's invited me to his wedding, which is odd, since he's not engaged. I think it was a request."],
  ["Is it true you've saved for a truck?","Half a truck. A second-hand one. A red Volvo with a bad clutch. I've been paying into a tin since 1976."],
  ["What would you haul?","Timber. Cola crates. Furniture. Anything. I'd call it Ilse & Son, though there's no son. I just like the sound."]],
 "Don't tell Knut about the truck. He'll want to name it."],
8:["(Ilse is stacking logs in a perfect pyramid.) Sunday's quiet. I come to the mill anyway. It's the only place that doesn't ask me anything. The logs never say, 'When are you getting married?'",[
  ["Do people ask that?","All the time. 'When's the wedding?' 'Where's your young man?' I tell them he's in the forest, and he's a pine."],
  ["Would you like to marry?","If someone could lift me, I'd consider it. Nobody has. I'm a very high bar."],
  ["What do you do on days off?","Fish. Bake. Beat Knut at arm-wrestling. It's a full life, if you don't mind the splinters."]],
 "If you've a spare finger, you could poke the pile. Gently."],
9:["(Ilse flicks a bit of bark off her sleeve.) The Reds are out, handing out leaflets in front of the mill. 'The People's Cola.' I told the girl in the red scarf that I've never had a cola that wasn't paid for.",[
  ["What do you have against Communists?","They talk, and they talk, and they never carry anything. I once asked one to hold the end of a plank. He asked for a committee."],
  ["Are they all like that?","No. Some work. But they're usually the ones not talking, so you never hear of them."],
  ["Would you try their cola?","If it's free, I'll drink it. If it's red, I'll put it in a pot and see what it does."]],
 "If you meet the red-scarf girl, tell her I was kind. In my way."],
10:["(Ilse has a strip of black cloth knotted round her cap.) It's his funeral today. We stopped for a minute, the whole crew, with our caps off. Even the saw. Even the logs, I think.",[
  ["Did you like the king?","He was good for the island. He didn't talk much. He nodded. I like a person who knows when to nod."],
  ["Will the island change?","It will, with or without us. I'd rather it was with. I'd like a hand in it, even a little finger."],
  ["How's the mill today?","Quiet. Nobody's loading. We're all standing about thinking. It doesn't suit us."]],
 "Take it easy today. It's a day for it."],
11:["(Ilse is holding a hand-painted sign that says FAIR DAY'S PAY, upside down.) Helen's been round. Helen's always round. She's got the biggest voice in Saltwick and the smallest apartment.",[
  ["Are you part of the dispute?","I'm the dispute. Six years, no rise. Boots that have worn through. A saw line that scares me, though I never touch it."],
  ["What do you want?","Pay that fits the work. And Knut's saw fixed, before he's down to eight fingers. I'd miss them. He plays the spoons."],
  ["Will Dagny give in?","She'll fight. But she's not stupid. She'll look at her ledger. When Dagny looks at a ledger, she can see the future."]],
 "Say it fair. Say it plainly. Don't make me sound like a hero. I just lift things."],
12:["(Ilse is hauling a tarpaulin over a stack.) There's weather on the way. I can feel it in my elbow. A cold snap and ash. No: I mean rain. I've mixed up my ailments.",[
  ["Is your elbow reliable?","More reliable than the radio. It's been right every time except once. It said sun, and Knut fell in the pond."],
  ["Are you worried about the pay talks?","I'm trying not to be. Worry's heavy, and I've enough to carry."],
  ["What do you do when it gets too much?","I lift something. I've a huge stone behind the shed. It's very good for the mind."]],
 "If you want a good thought, lift something heavy. It clears out the nonsense."],
13:["(Ilse is eyeing a truck with Kelp Cola on its side.) Samples, they've got. Free. The driver tried to give me a crate. I said I'd carry it, but not for free. He looked frightened.",[
  ["Did you take any?","A bottle. And a note of the driver's name. If I'm to buy a truck, I'll need contacts."],
  ["Do you like the company?","They pay on time, which is more than I can say for some. And they've got a clean, well-kept lorry. I'm a woman of simple tastes."],
  ["Would you drive for them?","Maybe. If it came with a cap. I like a cap."]],
 "A free bottle's a nice thing. A free truck would be nicer."],
14:["(Ilse has a clean shirt on, and her hair's tied up.) Big day. Everyone's going. I'm to go with Knut, to hold him up. He's already been at the tavern, to get into the spirit.",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","A vote. A say. A bit of respect for the people who work with their backs. We've a lot of back, and not a lot of say."],
  ["Will you speak?","If someone hands me a megaphone. I've a good voice. It's the only thing I've ever been asked to keep down."],
  ["Is it exciting?","It's like the start of a log-rolling match. You don't know who'll fall in, but you'd hate to miss it."]],
 "Keep to the edge, where the wide boys can't push you. That's where I'll be."],
15:["(Ilse rubs her shoulder.) Someone tried to sell me a flag, and then tried to put me on their shoulders. I put him down. Gently. On a hedge.",[
  ["Was it a good day?","It was big and noisy and I enjoyed it. I felt part of something. Which I've not felt since the last strike."],
  ["Any trouble?","A scuffle by the cart. Some monk with a very good left hook. Knut went over like a skittle, with a smile on his face."],
  ["What now?","Back to the stack. Back to the saw's mercy. And the hope that someone, somewhere, heard us."]],
 "I'd shake your hand, but I'd best not. I might forget myself."],
16:["(Ilse's wearing her Democratic Party badge on her cap.) The debate's today. Knut and I have a bet on who'll lose their temper first. He's got Anna. I've got Veikko. Veikko's got a big stick of authority.",[
  ["Who'll win?","The Democrats, by sheer weight of sensible people. The Reds, by sheer weight of noise."],
  ["Do you like Anna?","I admire her for her energy. I'd put her to work stacking. She'd talk the logs into position."],
  ["Do you think anything'll change?","A little. Every day, a little. It's like moving a pile of logs: you carry one at a time."]],
 "I'll cheer for the right side. If I can find it."],
17:["(Ilse has stopped, a log in each hand.) That was the ground. I felt it through my boots. The whole pile shuddered, like a horse shaking off a fly.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","Interested. I've never had the earth argue with me before. It's got a lot more weight than I have."],
  ["Is the stack safe?","I've restacked it, tight. Knut said a loose stack's a death trap. For once, he was right. Don't tell him."],
  ["Does it worry the crew?","The older ones, yes. The younger ones think it's a lark. Wait until it tips a log onto a boot."]],
 "If it happens again, I'll stand in the doorway. It's what my mother taught me."],
18:["(Ilse heaves a bag of sawdust onto a cart.) I've been thinking about the truck. Seven hundred marks. I've got four. It's like looking at the sea from a very small boat.",[
  ["How long will it take?","At this pay? A hundred years. With a rise, fifty. With a miracle, a month."],
  ["What will you do if you get it?","Drive to the north and see the volcano. Then drive back and tell people it was smaller than expected."],
  ["Why a red Volvo?","Because it's loud. Because it's mine. And because I'd look good in it, I think."]],
 "Don't laugh. A woman can have a dream, even a heavy one."],
19:["(Ilse looks up at the roof.) There's another one. I felt the ground go, and the trees at the edge swayed. Not in the wind. It's the strangest thing, a forest that shivers.",[
  ["What do you think's happening?","The mountain's waking. The old men say so. I'd say it's hungry. We all get hungry."],
  ["Will the mill be safe?","The mill's built of good timber. It'll flex. It'll give. It's a very forgiving building."],
  ["Does Knut say anything?","He says he knew. He says that about everything. It's how he avoids being surprised."]],
 "If you see Knut, ask him about his finger. He's got a new version."],
20:["(Ilse sniffs and wrinkles her nose.) There's a smell. Struck matches and boiled cabbage. The cook swears it's not him. I believe him. It's far worse than his cabbage.",[
  ["Is it from the volcano?","It must be. Every breath tastes of a bad egg. I don't mind. I've worked in worse. I worked a summer at the fish cannery."],
  ["Are you staying on?","As long as there's a log to lift. It's the only certain thing: there's always another log."],
  ["Is anyone leaving?","Some of the younger men talk of going to the mainland. I told them the mainland has logs too, and they'd probably need lifting."]],
 "If it goes, I'll carry as many out as I can. It's what I'm for."],
21:["(Ilse's cap is grey, her face is grey, and she's grinning.) That was quite something. I was standing on the stack. I felt it in my chest first, then heard it. Then it rained dust.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Better than all right. Nobody's hurt. And I've a story now that'll beat Knut's bear."],
  ["What did you do?","I carried two of the lads to the shed. They were in a state. One of them cried. I told him I'd never tell, and now I've told you."],
  ["Is the mill ruined?","It's grey. It'll clean. Timber doesn't mind ash. It's the people who mind."]],
 "Don't breathe too deep. It's like sucking on a cold pencil."],
22:["(Ilse is shovelling grey from the mill yard into a barrow.) It's a very dull kind of heroism: moving ash from one place to another. But somebody's got to.",[
  ["How are the crew?","Tired. Dusty. Strangely cheerful. Knut's been telling the volcano story at every break, and each time, the lava gets closer."],
  ["Is Dagny about?","She's counting the logs. Always counting. She wore a mask. It matched her coat."],
  ["Is the pay dispute still going?","It's on a shelf, but it's not forgotten. Helen's promised to come back. She's good that way. She's like a train: slow but sure."]],
 "Pass me that shovel. It's the only thing that doesn't talk."],
23:["(Ilse's been rubbing her back.) Even I ache. I've never been so sore. I've moved more ash than I've moved logs, and ash doesn't help. It's slippery and it clings.",[
  ["Are the tremors still going?","Every few hours. I've got used to them. I put my cup down on a flat bit of floor and wait."],
  ["What do you do to relax?","Sit very still and say nothing. It's wonderfully difficult."],
  ["How's the truck fund?","The same. Though Knut left a coin in the tin. I told him he was daft. He said it was for luck."]],
 "If I look tired, it's not you. It's the mountain."],
24:["(Ilse is hosing down the log stacks.) It's a clean-up day. The Church lot are up in the north and the Syndicate's cleaning something somewhere. We're doing the honest bit.",[
  ["What do you make of the Syndicate?","Men in coats. I've never seen one lift anything. I think they're allergic to work."],
  ["Is the island coming together?","In a way. Everyone's got a bucket. The factions come second when you've got ash in your hair."],
  ["Will that last?","No. But it's nice while it does."]],
 "Mind the wet planks. They're slick as a politician."],
25:["(Ilse stops to stretch.) I've heard talk of a princess for democracy. And a crown for everyone. You'd think the island was a bakery.",[
  ["What's your view on royalty?","They're like timber. Some of them are good and some are rotten. You can't tell without cutting."],
  ["Would you vote for a princess?","If she can lift a log, yes. If not, I'll consider it, but I'll want a demonstration."],
  ["Is Erik backing a princess?","He's backing a vote. I'm told there's a lady. I'm told many things. I'm not a lady, but I'd like a vote."]],
 "A vote's like a good chain: it keeps things from falling on your head."],
26:["(Ilse is polishing her cap on her sleeve.) I put four marks more in the truck tin. It's heavy now, if I shake it. I like to shake it. It sounds like hope.",[
  ["How close are you?","Half. A little over half. I've a plan to take on weekend work. Hauling firewood to Saltwick. It's tiring, but it's a start."],
  ["Does Dagny know about the truck?","She's never asked. If she did, she'd want a share. She's like that. I'd say she's got a coin in her heart, if there was room."],
  ["What will you call it?","Big Red. Or Ilse's Revenge. I'll decide when I've the keys."]],
 "Don't tell anyone. Well, tell a few. They might chip in."],
27:["(Ilse leans on the stack.) The Reds are at the cinema. The Syndicate's got a high-stakes something. Everyone's got a plan. I've a log. Singular. I'm very fond of it.",[
  ["Is it special?","It's a good one. Straight. No knots. I'm saving it for the end of the month, for the celebration."],
  ["What celebration?","Any. Win or lose. There'll be a fire. And we'll sit round it and say things we'll regret."],
  ["Can I come?","You can. Bring a bottle. Not the new one. The old."]],
 "I'll save you a seat on the log. It's the best seat on the island."],
28:["(Ilse has put a ribbon in her cap.) It's the biggest day. The whole island's in the streets. Even Dagny's shut the mill, for the afternoon, with a face like a closed drawer.",[
  ["What'll you do?","Wander. Watch. Vote with my feet and my voice. And maybe carry a banner, if the banner-holder's drunk."],
  ["Are you excited?","Terribly. It's like the day before a big lift. You feel it in your whole body."],
  ["Do you think it'll go well?","I think it'll go loudly. Everything on this island does."]],
 "Stay close to the crowd. The edges are where the trouble is."],
29:["(Ilse crosses her arms.) Tomorrow's the last day. I should be thinking about politics. I'm thinking about the clutch on the Volvo.",[
  ["Is it that important?","To me, yes. To the island, no. But the island'll go on without me. The truck won't."],
  ["Is there anything you want to say before tomorrow?","That I'm proud of the crew. We're loud and we're slow and we're underpaid, but we got through a volcano."],
  ["Any hopes?","That the new island'll look after the ones who stack the logs. And have a decent road."]],
 "Look after yourself. You're a good listener, for a reporter."],
30:["(Ilse stands tall, a log under each arm, like a woman holding two small trees.) This is it. Whatever's decided, the logs still need moving. That's what I've always told myself.",[
  ["Any last words, Ilse?","Don't let anyone tell you the work isn't important. Someone has to lift. Someone has to carry."],
  ["Will you buy the truck?","When I can. Though I think the tin's tired of waiting. We'll see."],
  ["Will you miss the month?","I'll miss the noise. And the ash. I'll even miss the Reds, a little. Don't tell them."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Come back and see the Volvo. It'll be red."],
};
// Ingrid, the maid who keeps the royal apartments (the Palace Workers). Threads: the prince walking the floor at night and the
// burnt paper in his grate; the old queen's ghost she's sure walks the corridor; the three royal bedrooms and what they say
// about the three of them; her shy plan to learn to type and leave service one day.
DAILY['Ingrid']={
2:["(Ingrid jumps, and nearly drops a pillow.) Oh! Sorry. I thought you were the ghost. I mean, I thought you were Mr Edmund. I mean, hello.",[
  ["The ghost?","(She whispers.) The old queen. Queen Astrid. She walks this corridor at night, in a grey dress. Everyone says so. Well, I say so."],
  ["Mr Edmund's not that scary.","He is when you've left a crease in a pillowcase. He can see a crease through a closed door."],
  ["Hello, Ingrid.","Hello. (She smiles, and then looks at the floor, as if she's not sure smiling's allowed.)"]],
 "I'd better get on. The beds don't make themselves, whatever Princess Daphne thinks."],
3:["Three rooms, three of them, and each one's different. I could tell you who sleeps where with my eyes shut.",[
  ["Tell me.","The prince's room smells of pipe smoke and cold. Prince Augustus's smells of candle wax. The princess's smells of French perfume and something she says is incense."],
  ["Who's the tidiest?","Prince Augustus. His room's like a monk's cell. A bed, a chair, an icon and a book. I dust the book. That's all there is to do."],
  ["Who's the messiest?","(A small, guilty smile.) I couldn't possibly say. But it's the room with the perfume."]],
 "Mind the corridor rug, the corner curls up. It's caught the queen out too, they say."],
4:["The prince was walking again last night. Back and forth, back and forth, from the window to the door. Twelve steps. I counted, from my bed.",[
  ["You can hear him from the quarters?","When it's quiet, everything carries. The pipes run under his floor. I hear him like he's on the ceiling."],
  ["What time?","Two, three. Every night since the king died. He stops at the window for a long time. Then he starts again."],
  ["Why do you think he walks?","I think he's frightened. (She covers her mouth.) I shouldn't say that about a prince."]],
 "Please don't put that in the paper. People would think I'd been talking."],
5:["There were ashes in the prince's grate again this morning. Not coal ashes. Paper. Lots of it, burnt very black.",[
  ["Did you read any?","No! I'd never. I just notice. One bit hadn't burned. It had 'People of Sar' on it, in his hand. The rest had gone."],
  ["What do you do with them?","Sweep them up and put them in the scuttle, same as coal. Mr Edmund says ashes are ashes. I think some ashes are more than ashes."],
  ["Love letters?","(She goes pink.) I don't think so. It looked more like a speech. Or a very sad letter to everyone."]],
 "I've said too much. I always do, once I start."],
6:["I'm saving up for a typing course. There's a woman in Kingsgrad who teaches it, evenings. Forty words a minute and you can work in an office.",[
  ["You want to leave the palace?","Not now. Someday. I'd like a job where nobody can see the dust I've missed. Typing's clean."],
  ["How much have you saved?","Enough for half. Princess Daphne gave me a scarf she didn't like. I sold it to Grace's shop. Is that wrong?"],
  ["Forty words a minute is fast.","I've been practising on the old typewriter in the library, when no one's there. I'm at eleven. Some of them are spelt right."]],
 "Don't tell Mr Edmund about the typewriter. Or about the scarf."],
7:["(Ingrid is dusting an icon in Augustus's room very gently.) Prince Augustus fasts and prays and reads, and he always says thank you when I bring the coal. He's the only one who does.",[
  ["Do the others not?","The prince nods. The princess says 'darling, you're a treasure', which is nice, but she says it to the cat too."],
  ["Is he very religious?","He prays on the floor, not the kneeler. I can see the marks on the rug. I hoover round them. It feels rude to hoover them away."],
  ["Who's the icon of?","Saint Nicholas. For sailors and children. He says it was his mother's. He touches the frame every morning, I think. It's the only thing in here that isn't dusty."]],
 "Close the door softly. He doesn't like it banged, even when he's not in."],
8:["I heard her again. The ghost. Last night, in the corridor, a voice reading, very soft, like someone reading a letter to themselves.",[
  ["Are you sure it was a ghost?","No. (She hesitates.) It might have been Prince Augustus. He reads aloud sometimes. But at three in the morning, with no light under his door?"],
  ["What did it say?","Something about ears. Ridiculous ears. I didn't stay to hear more. I pulled the blanket over my head."],
  ["What does the ghost want?","Mrs Marta says the old queen walks when the family's in trouble. She walked the winter the king's wife died, Mrs Marta says."]],
 "If you hear reading in the corridor, don't follow it. Promise."],
9:["(Ingrid is pressing black armbands with a cool iron.) Everyone's to wear one tomorrow, even the staff. I've done thirty. My hands smell of starch and sad.",[
  ["Sad has a smell?","It smells like black wool that's been in a drawer too long. All the mourning things came out of the attic. They smell of the last funeral."],
  ["Whose funeral was that?","The queen's. The king's wife. Princess Daphne was only six. I wasn't here; Elsa was. Elsa says the princess hid in the laundry basket all day."],
  ["Will you go to the service?","I'll stand at the back, with the maids. You look at your shoes and try not to sniff. It's harder than it sounds."]],
 "Here, take an armband. You might want one tomorrow."],
10:["(Ingrid's eyes are very red.) The prince didn't walk last night. Not once. I lay awake waiting for the twelve steps, and they never came. Somehow that was worse.",[
  ["Maybe he slept.","I hope so. I left an extra blanket on his chair. He'd pushed it on the floor in the morning. But the bed was slept in."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm just tired. It's a sad day, isn't it? Even for people who only dusted him."],
  ["Did you know the king well?","He called me 'the quiet one'. Once he asked me if I was happy. I said yes, sir, and he said 'good, somebody should be'. That's all. I think about it a lot."]],
 "I'm sorry. I've got to do the bedrooms before the family come back from the chapel."],
11:["The princess cried in her sleep last night. Just a little, the way a child does. Then she got up and laughed at breakfast like nothing happened.",[
  ["How do you know?","The pillow. It's always the pillow that tells you. Elsa says the laundry tells her everything. She's right."],
  ["Is she all right?","She's like a cat. She's fine until you look straight at her, and then she isn't."],
  ["Did you tell anyone?","Who would I tell? You're the first. I don't know why I tell you things."]],
 "Don't mention the pillow to the princess. Please."],
12:["(Ingrid shows you her fingertips, stained with ink.) The typewriter ribbon. I've got up to fifteen words a minute. Most of them were 'the'.",[
  ["Fifteen's progress.","It is! Prince Augustus caught me practising. I thought I'd be dismissed. He said 'carry on' and brought me a book on the history of shorthand."],
  ["What do you type?","Things I've seen. Not secrets. Weather. What the princess wore. What the prince ate. Like a little diary nobody will read."],
  ["Where's the typewriter?","In the library, under the window. It belonged to the old king's secretary. Mr Edmund says nobody's used it since 1970. I've used it every evening this week."]],
 "Fifteen words. Next week, twenty. Then an office in Kingsgrad, maybe, someday."],
13:["More paper in the prince's grate. A great deal. He must have written all night. There was a bit that said 'not my father' and then nothing.",[
  ["You did read it.","(She flushes.) Just the bit that hadn't burnt. You can't not read the bit that hadn't burnt. It's right there."],
  ["Is it the same speech?","I think so. He's been writing the same thing for a week. I think he can't get to the end of it."],
  ["Will you tell him you saw?","Never. You don't tell a prince you've read his ashes."]],
 "I'm going to stop looking at the grate. I am. Tomorrow."],
14:["Everyone's out today, the whole island, and the palace is empty. I've done all three bedrooms. It's very quiet. Just me and the ghost.",[
  ["Aren't you going?","Someone has to stay and keep the fires in. I volunteered. Crowds make me want to hide in a cupboard."],
  ["Is the ghost about?","(She laughs nervously.) I've been singing while I work, so she knows I'm here. I've been told ghosts don't like hymns."],
  ["What will you do with the quiet?","Type. Twenty minutes in the library. Nobody to hear the keys. I'm going to try for twenty words."]],
 "If you see the family, tell them their fires are lit."],
15:["Prince Augustus hasn't slept in his bed. I took him cocoa in the library at midnight. He had two letters, side by side, and he wasn't reading either of them.",[
  ["What did he say?","He said, 'Ingrid, have you ever had to choose between two good things?' I said once, between a blue ribbon and a green. He laughed. I think I helped."],
  ["Who were the letters from?","I didn't look. Well, one had a monastery seal. And one was in the prince's hand. I didn't look further."],
  ["Was he upset?","Calm. Very calm. That's worse, with him. When Prince Augustus is calm, it means it's serious."]],
 "Be quiet if you go to the library. He's thinking."],
16:["The prince and Prince Augustus played chess last night, in the prince's room. I heard them laughing. I haven't heard that since I came.",[
  ["Who won?","The prince lost, and he's never lost anything in his life. I think he did it on purpose. I think Prince Augustus knew."],
  ["How long have you been here?","Four years. Since I was eighteen. My mother was a maid at the Hotel Aurora in Frostholm. She said palaces were cleaner."],
  ["That's nice to hear.","It is, isn't it? A palace can feel very empty. A laugh fills it right up."]],
 "Close the door gently. There's still something nice in the air in here."],
17:["(Ingrid is pale.) The whole corridor shook. The portraits rattled. I was in the library and the shelves were coming down, and Prince Augustus was reading a psalm out loud.",[
  ["Did you help?","I read it with him. Psalm 46. 'Though the earth be removed.' My voice was very small, but I read it."],
  ["Was it the ghost?","(She swallows.) Mrs Marta says it's the mountain in the north. But the queen's portrait was the only one that fell. I'm just saying."],
  ["Are you hurt?","A book hit my shoulder. A big one. The lives of the saints. Prince Augustus said it was a blessing. It didn't feel like one."]],
 "If it happens again, I'm going under the stairs. I don't care how it looks."],
18:["The prince listens to the radio late. Jazz, very low, through his door. I'm not supposed to know. Oskar's not supposed to know I told him.",[
  ["Is it good jazz?","I don't know about jazz. It sounds like a lot of people arguing beautifully. He hums along. Very badly. Don't tell him."],
  ["Why is it a secret?","The old king hated jazz. He liked marches. I think the prince still feels him listening."],
  ["Does it help him sleep?","He walks less, on jazz nights. Six steps instead of twelve. I've noticed."]],
 "I shouldn't say any of it. You make me talk. It's your face."],
19:["(Ingrid is folding a flat cap that isn't hers.) I found this on the prince's chair. A flat cap. Like the fishermen wear. I've never seen it before.",[
  ["Whose is it?","I think it's Oskar's. It's got his name sewn in, in his mum's hand. What's the prince doing with Oskar's cap?"],
  ["Put it back.","I will. Exactly as it was. I've learned you put back what you find. You don't ask."],
  ["Maybe he's going fishing.","(She giggles, then claps a hand over her mouth.) The prince in a flat cap, with a rod. I can't imagine it."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the cap. It's probably nothing. It's probably everything."],
20:["(Ingrid is holding a saucer at arm's length.) The princess's room. A saucer of cigar ash under the bed. Little thin cigars. She thinks I don't find them. I find everything.",[
  ["Will you tell anyone?","No. I empty it and put it back, clean. She'll know I know. It's how we talk, the princess and me: in saucers."],
  ["What happened to the flat cap?","It was gone from the prince's chair this morning. Back on Oskar's peg in the quarters, I suppose. I haven't looked. I'm trying not to look at things."],
  ["You seem jumpy today.","Too many strangers this month. Mr Edmund's been round the doors twice with his keys. When Mr Edmund's jumpy, everyone is."]],
 "Don't tell the princess about the saucer. She'd only find a better hiding place."],
21:["(Ingrid is shaking, with ash on her apron.) The mountain's on fire. I could see it from the princess's window. Red, under the smoke. I thought the world was ending.",[
  ["It isn't. Breathe.","I know. Prince Augustus said so. He opened the chapel and said anyone could sit there. I sat there for an hour and didn't say anything."],
  ["Is everyone all right?","Oskar ran to town. Nobody's hurt. The princess stood at the top of the garden in the ash, laughing and crying at once."],
  ["What can I do?","Nothing. Just being here helps. Isn't that silly? You're a stranger and it helps."]],
 "Close the windows when you go home. All of them."],
22:["Ash in every bedroom. On the pillows. In the princess's perfume bottles. On Prince Augustus's icon. I've been wiping since dawn with a damp cloth.",[
  ["Damp? Mr Edmund said dry.","Dry for the carpets. Damp for the faces. Saint Nicholas has a face, so I did him damp. I think he deserved it."],
  ["The princess is restless.","She's walking about the palace like a cat in a box. She asked me today if I knew anyone who grew 'herbs'. I said Brother Paavo grows cabbages. She laughed for ages."],
  ["Did the typing survive?","The typewriter's full of ash. Prince Augustus is cleaning it with a goose feather. He said it was 'good practice'. For what, I don't know."]],
 "Wipe your feet. Twice. Three times. Mr Edmund's orders."],
23:["The prince walked again last night. Not twelve steps. More. Up and down, and then he stopped at the window for an hour. No paper in the grate this morning.",[
  ["No paper?","None. Just coal ashes. I think he finished it. Or gave up. I don't know which."],
  ["What's worrying him?","The mountain. The island. A king coming on the twenty-eighth. Everything, Mrs Marta says. Mrs Marta says princes have more to worry about than maids."],
  ["Do you agree?","(She thinks.) They have bigger worries. I have smaller ones. But mine keep me up just as late."]],
 "I've got to do the visitors' rooms for the foreign king. Every one of them, with clean linen and no ash."],
24:["I'm doing the guest suite for the visiting king. The best rooms, in the east wing. Nobody's slept there since the old king's coronation jubilee.",[
  ["Is it haunted?","(She laughs, finally.) No. It's just cold. And there's a mouse. I've named him Bertil, after the man at the Civil Service Bureau. They look alike."],
  ["What's the room like?","Blue, with a bed big enough for four kings and a painting of a sea battle. Elsa and I aired the curtains for two days. They still smell of 1959."],
  ["How's the typing?","Twenty-two words a minute! The typewriter's clean. Prince Augustus gave it back with a note: 'Practice makes saints.' I've kept the note."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about Bertil the mouse. The king might not like mice."],
25:["Something happened last night. I heard the ghost in the corridor again, reading. I opened the door, very brave, and it was Prince Augustus, in his dressing gown, reading an old diary aloud to himself.",[
  ["So it wasn't a ghost.","It was never a ghost. (She looks almost disappointed.) He was doing voices. A young woman's voice, very bossy. He went bright red when he saw me."],
  ["Did they see you?","He said, 'Come in, Ingrid, it's about ears.' So I did. I sat on the floor and listened. It's the nicest thing that's happened to me here."],
  ["Will you miss the ghost?","A bit. It's nice to think someone's watching over the corridor. Maybe she still is. Just quieter."]],
 "I'll sleep tonight. For the first time in weeks, I think I'll sleep."],
26:["(Ingrid is smoothing a sheet with both hands.) Three days to the state visit. Fresh linen, fresh flowers, fresh soap in every room. The soap's from Frostholm. It smells of pine.",[
  ["You've worked hard.","Mr Edmund said 'satisfactory'. He's never said anything nicer to me. I nearly cried."],
  ["Will you see the king?","I'll see his back, and his luggage. Maids don't see kings' faces. We see their pillows."],
  ["What would you tell him?","That the guest bed's very soft and he mustn't lose his glasses down the side. The old king did, once. I found them in March."]],
 "Don't sit on the guest bed. I've just done it."],
27:["The prince is trying on his father's uniform in front of the mirror. I'm not watching. I'm folding. He said 'Ingrid, do I look like him?'",[
  ["What did you say?","I said 'a little, sir. Round the eyes.' He looked pleased and sad at once. I think that was the right answer."],
  ["Does he?","He does. But he stands differently. The old king stood like a wall. The prince stands like a man pretending to be a wall."],
  ["Are you ready for tomorrow?","My apron's starched, my cap's pinned and my knees are knocking. I think that's ready."]],
 "I'd better go. There's a guest towel with a crease in it, and I'll never forgive myself."],
28:["(Ingrid hurries past with fresh towels.) A king's arrived, and the whole island's in uproar outside, and I've forgotten where I put the soap.",[
  ["It's in your apron pocket.","(She checks.) It is! Thank you. You'd make a good maid. Don't take that badly."],
  ["What's the visiting king like?","He has very big boots and he left them in the corridor to be polished. I polished them. I've polished a king's boots. My mother won't believe it."],
  ["How's the prince?","Standing very straight. He didn't walk last night. He slept. I could tell by the quiet."]],
 "I've got to go. The soap!"],
29:["The island's deciding who's next today. Everyone's very quiet in the apartments. Prince Augustus is praying, the princess is in the garden and the prince is writing again.",[
  ["Writing the speech?","I think so. (She hesitates.) And this morning there wasn't any paper in the grate. He kept it. It's on his desk, face down."],
  ["What do you hope for?","For all three of them to be all right. Whatever happens, I'd like to keep their rooms. I know where everything goes."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm a maid. It's not my island to decide. (She pauses.) But I'm nervous anyway."]],
 "If you see the prince, don't mention the speech. Let him have it."],
30:["The last day. I've done all three rooms. I left a note in each. Just 'good luck', typed. Twenty-eight words a minute now.",[
  ["Typed notes?","Prince Augustus said it was a waste of my new skill to type two words. I said they were the two most important words today."],
  ["What will you do, after?","Keep the rooms. Learn to type faster. Maybe the course in Kingsgrad, in spring. Maybe not. I'm not in a hurry any more."],
  ["Will you miss the ghost?","There's no ghost. (She smiles.) But if there is, I left her a note too."]],
 "Thank you for listening. Nobody listens to the maid."],
};
// Jaana, who plays Kelpy the cup, the Kelp Cola Museum's mascot in Eastbrook (Kelp Cola). Threads: life inside the costume (heat, the
// head she never takes off in public), her tour speech, Wilma as boss and friend, her stage dreams (Leo and Rita), Kelp Cola's big days.
DAILY['Jaana']={
4:["(A giant red cola cup waves both arms at you, then does a small bow that nearly tips it over.) Welcome to the Kelp Cola Museum! (The voice from inside is muffled and delighted.) You're new! Are you the reporter?",[
  ["That's me. Who's in there?","Kelpy! Only Kelpy. (The cup puts a finger to where its lips would be.) Kelpy doesn't have a day job and Kelpy doesn't have a face."],
  ["Is the museum any good?","It's the best museum on the island, and I do the tour, so I'm not biased. Well, I am. But there's a real bottling line, still running."],
  ["Isn't it hot in there?","Like a sauna with a straw. But the snow's only just cleared, so for once I'm grateful."]],
 "Wave on your way out! Kelpy likes a wave."],
5:["(Kelpy is leaning against the door frame, fanning itself with a brochure.) Slow day. The roads have only just opened, and the first coach of visitors has so far been a family from Saltwick who wanted the toilet.",[
  ["Do you ever get a break?","Ten minutes, round the back, with the head off. Wilma stands guard. Nobody's ever seen me, and I want to keep it that way."],
  ["Why keep your face secret?","Because Kelpy's more fun than I am. If the children saw it was just Jaana from the Timber Lofts, the magic would go."],
  ["Do the children really believe in Kelpy?","The small ones, completely. One of them asked if I was full of cola. I said only on Fridays."]],
 "Mind the step. Kelpy's feet are big but they're not much use."],
6:["(Kelpy practises the guided tour to an empty room.) ...and here we see the first ever bottle of Kelp Cola, 1923, still with some of the original fizz. (The head turns to you.) Oh! Hello. I'm warming up.",[
  ["Is that bottle really still fizzy?","Wilma says it is. I think she opens it when no one's looking and tops it up with soda water. Don't tell her I said so."],
  ["How many times have you given that tour?","Eleven thousand, three hundred and something. I can do it in my sleep. I have, once, in front of a bus party."],
  ["Do you get paid by the tour?","By the hour, and not very well. But I get a cola and a free costume, and the costume's the best thing about it."]],
 "Come back and I'll give you the extended tour. It has a song."],
7:["(Kelpy is waving at a passing tram, and the tram driver rings the bell back.) That's Lars. He rings every time. It's the only applause I get all day.",[
  ["Do you want more applause?","Of course. I'd like a stage. Rita at the Midnight Note says I've got presence. Well, she said I've got a lot of presence, which is the kind of thing you say to a cup."],
  ["Is Frostholm open now?","Everywhere is, they say. I haven't been past the tram stop yet. Kelpy's not made for slush."],
  ["What does the company make of the king's death?","Phil put a black ribbon round the museum's first bottle. Kurt wanted a black ribbon round me. I said I wasn't going to be a funeral cup."]],
 "Don't let the door slam on my tail. I don't have one, but it's the principle."],
8:["(Kelpy is carrying a tray of small paper cups.) Tasters! Wilma says visitors who taste go on to buy. I say visitors who taste just go on to taste. Want one?",[
  ["Go on, then.","(You take a cup of the dark, fizzy stuff.) The old recipe. It's a bit sweeter than the new, and it's got something in it nobody's ever named."],
  ["What's in the old recipe?",["That's the question. (Kelpy leans in.) Nobody here knows the whole of it. Phil says the formula's locked in a safe, and Dex says it's locked in an old man's head.",[
    ["Which old man?","Don't know. A rumour. There are a lot of rumours when you stand around a cola museum all day."],
    ["You must have a theory.","I think it's cinnamon and sadness. But I'm a mascot, not a chemist."]]]],
  ["Does it ever stain the costume?","Constantly. Wilma keeps a bottle of vinegar by the door, and that's the real smell of the museum."]],
 "Take another on your way out. Kelpy won't tell."],
9:["(Kelpy bounces up on the spot, which makes the whole cup wobble.) Big news! They're saying the new formula's coming. Kurt came by with a clipboard and a face like a man whose cat had been shot.",[
  ["What's the new formula?","No one will say. Which means it's either brilliant or terrible. I've learned that, in marketing, those are the only two options."],
  ["Does the Party want a cola too?","Communists making cola! (Kelpy shakes with laughter.) It'd be red, taste of beetroot and come with a lecture. I'd come to the launch, though."],
  ["Do you like working for Kelp Cola?","I do. It's loud and silly and I get to be a cup. I just worry what happens if the company stops being silly."]],
 "If you hear what's in the new one, tell Kelpy. Kelpy tells nobody."],
10:["(Kelpy is wearing a small black armband over one big red sleeve.) They're burying the king in Kingsgrad today. Wilma says we're to keep the museum open but not wave. I've been standing very still.",[
  ["That's hard for a mascot.","It's agony. My arm's been twitching for an hour. I've done the stately nod. The stately nod is a lot of pressure."],
  ["Did you ever see the king?","Once, when he opened the museum in 1969. He asked whether Kelpy was a cup or a cow. I said a cup. He gave me a coin."],
  ["How do the staff feel about today?","Quiet. Wilma's been polishing the same bottle all morning. She doesn't cry in front of people. She polishes."]],
 "Rest well, your Majesty. (Kelpy bows its big head.) And you, too."],
11:["(Kelpy is perched on a stool with the head tilted back, drinking through a straw.) Eastbrook's loud today. The mill hands are all over the street with leaflets. Something about a fair day's pay.",[
  ["Do you know the mill hands?","Knut and Ilse live on my floor in the Timber Lofts. Knut's got a missing fingertip and a story to go with it. A different story every night."],
  ["Should they get a pay rise?","I'd say yes, but I'm paid to be a cup. I'm not in a position to lecture anyone about wages."],
  ["What do the leaflets say?","'Fair pay, safe saws'. I've pinned one inside the costume. It's the only thing in there that doesn't smell of feet."]],
 "I hope they get it. Dagny's got a lot of timber and not a lot of heart."],
12:["(Kelpy's holding a feather duster.) Wilma's got me on dusting duty. A cup with a duster. It's practically performance art.",[
  ["Is Wilma a good boss?","The best. She's strict about dust and soft about people. She once let me off a whole Saturday because my mother was ill."],
  ["Did you want to be an actress?","From the age of six. I did Cinderella in the school hall, as the pumpkin. I'm now a cup. The trajectory's clear."],
  ["What does Wilma collect?","Bottles, caps, labels, anything older than she is. She dates them by smell. She's been right about everything but her own birthday."]],
 "Come and see the bottling line. It's the loudest thing in Eastbrook after the mill."],
13:["(Kelpy's waving two big paper cups and shouting at a passing bicycle.) Free samples! Free samples! I'm told the company's handing them out across the island today. I don't know what the occasion is, but I'm glad of it.",[
  ["Why free samples?","So people like Kelp Cola better than anything else, I'd say. Kurt says 'brand loyalty'. I say 'bribe with fizz'."],
  ["Are you giving them out too?","Only here. Kelpy's not allowed past the museum gate. Something about insurance and a bus."],
  ["Does it work?","On the children, completely. On their parents, less so. On Knut, it does if I put rum in it."]],
 "The sun's out! That's a bad omen. It's September."],
14:["(Kelpy's perched on the museum steps, flapping a little paper flag.) Tomorrow's the big day, I mean today's the big day. The fourteenth. Everyone's been talking about it all week, and I've heard nothing but guesses.",[
  ["What do you expect?","A crowd. A lot of speeches. A lot of people shouting. And I'd like it all to be over so I can go back to being a cup in peace."],
  ["Will Kelpy be there?","Kelpy's invited, Wilma says, but Kelpy's too wobbly in a crowd. I once got knocked into a fountain."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I always am before something big. It's the costume. If I panic, nobody can tell. That's the best part of it."]],
 "Wish me luck. Wish the island luck, actually. It looks like it needs more."],
15:["(Kelpy is sitting on the floor with a plate of biscuits on its lap.) Wilma's gone to lie down. She says the fourteenth's worn her out, and it wasn't even her party.",[
  ["Everyone's tired today.","I think the whole island is. It's like the morning after a wedding where nobody knew who was getting married."],
  ["What was your favourite part?","Kelpy's. A little girl hugged me, round the middle, for a full minute. I could hardly breathe. It was the best minute of my year."],
  ["Is there anything you're worried about?","That it'll all start again. The shouting, the posters, the new formula. I'd just like a quiet week of waving."]],
 "Have a biscuit. They're stale, but so is most of the museum."],
16:["(Kelpy is humming a jingle, and doing a small jig.) There's a debate today, between the Reds and Erik's lot. Dex says it's going to be a brawl. I'm told there are bets.",[
  ["Who'll win?","The one with the louder voice. Which, from what I hear, is Anna, and she's got a megaphone."],
  ["Are you going?","I'd love to, but I can't take Kelpy and I can't leave Kelpy. It's a mascot's dilemma."],
  ["Do you like either side?","I like the people on both. Not the slogans. Slogans don't dance."]],
 "If anyone throws a chair, remember I was here, being a cup."],
17:["(Kelpy leans heavily on the counter.) Did you feel the floor? It went wobbly for a second, just after breakfast. All the bottles chattered on the shelf.",[
  ["Was that the volcano?","Wilma says it's the old pipes. Wilma also says the bottles were here before the tram and will be here after the end of the world."],
  ["Were you scared?","A little. Being in a costume, I couldn't tell if the floor was moving or if it was me. I'm used to wobbling."],
  ["Did anything break?","One bottle. 1957. She wasn't happy. She put the pieces in a velvet bag and said a few words."]],
 "If it happens again, I'm going to hide inside the cup. It's the safest place on the island."],
18:["(Kelpy is carrying a big banner that reads 'INSIDE KELPY'.) I'm told I've got a visitor today who wants to see how it all works. Behind the scenes. It's flattering, and a little bit terrifying.",[
  ["What's behind Kelpy?","Mostly sweat. And a bad hinge at the back of the neck. And, I'm sorry to say, a sandwich I forgot on Tuesday."],
  ["Who's the visitor?","A friend of Kurt's, I think. Or a man Kurt owes a favour. Kurt's friends always carry notebooks."],
  ["Does anyone know it's you in there?","Wilma. My mother. And a boy in Saltwick who saw me through the neck gap when I was climbing out of a taxi. I bribed him with a cola."]],
 "Don't tell them about the sandwich. Please."],
19:["(Kelpy is rubbing its own neck.) The tremors are getting worse. There was another one last night. The glasses on my bedside table rattled all through the second act of my dream.",[
  ["What was the dream?","I was on a stage, a real one, and it was full of people, and I'd forgotten my lines and my face. The face was the bad part."],
  ["Is the museum safe?","Wilma says it's the safest building in Eastbrook. She says that about everything she owns. Even the gift shop's till."],
  ["Is the company worried?","Phil sent a memo. It said: 'Do not panic.' Which is the single most panic-making thing a company can say."]],
 "I'll sleep with the window open. If anything's going to blow, I'd like to hear it first."],
20:["(Kelpy sits very still, the cup's head slightly tilted.) There's a smell of sulphur in the street this morning. I thought it was the museum drains, but Wilma says no.",[
  ["Have you heard any rumours?","That the volcano's about to go. That it isn't. That it's a bluff to sell more insurance. I've heard four, and I believe none."],
  ["What would you do if it went?","Run, very badly. A cup isn't an athletic shape. I'd roll. I'd probably end up in the Kelp Cola factory."],
  ["Are you staying open?","As long as there's anyone to wave at. Someone should be a cheerful thing on a day like this."]],
 "Be careful out there. Kelpy's worried about you. Kelpy's got a little soft spot."],
21:["(Kelpy is out on the steps, covered in a thin layer of grey.) It went. It really went. There's ash on everything, and I mean everything. I look like a giant biscuit.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Fine. Frightened. And then, once I'd seen no one was hurt, I felt a bit ridiculous at how glad I was. Glad to be here."],
  ["What was it like?","A huge, long groan. Then a bang. And the sky turning the colour of old tea. All the bottles in the museum rang together, like a choir."],
  ["What happens to the museum now?","Wilma's already got a broom. She said, 'It's only ash,' in the voice of a woman who's seen worse. I haven't. I'll be sweeping for a week."]],
 "Mind the ash on the steps. It's like walking on cold flour."],
22:["(Kelpy is coated in grey and wielding a small broom.) I've done the front steps three times. It keeps coming back. I think the ash is shy and wants to come in.",[
  ["Is the museum all right?","The bottles are fine. The first bottling line coughed a bit. It's old and doesn't like change, or ash, which is a kind of change."],
  ["What are people saying?","That the world is ending. That it's the Communists. That it's the Royals. That it's the cola. It's always somebody's fault."],
  ["Is the cola company doing anything?","They're handing out free bottles to the people in the north. It's a nice thing to do. It's also very good advertising. Both, I suppose."]],
 "Don't let anybody tell you it's all right. But don't let anybody tell you it isn't."],
23:["(Kelpy takes a deep breath, audible through the felt.) The aftershocks are still here. I keep thinking the floor's about to move, and when it doesn't I feel cheated.",[
  ["Have you been sleeping?","In snatches. I put my shoes by the bed and a torch in my pocket. I look like a very worried lighthouse keeper."],
  ["Do you hear anything from the factory?","Petra says the line stopped for a day because of the ash and started again because of Kurt shouting. Both did the job."],
  ["What does Wilma say?","That it's good for business. 'Souvenir ash,' she said. She's put a jar of it in the gift shop at ten marks."]],
 "If you've got ash on your shoes, wipe them. Wilma checks."],
24:["(Kelpy hasn't got a broom today; it has a bucket.) It's the clean-up. People are scrubbing roofs and windows and cars. I've been scrubbing me.",[
  ["Is it all coming off?","Mostly. Some of it's got inside the costume, and I've begun to feel as if I'm made of pencil lead."],
  ["How's the island?","Tired. Cross. Together, in a way. I saw Dagny hand a mill hand a cup of coffee. I nearly dropped my bucket."],
  ["What about the factions?","They've gone quiet, but it feels like the quiet before another round. Everyone's saving something up for the end of the month."]],
 "Take a cup of tea with you. It's still cold out and the ash dries your throat."],
25:["(Kelpy's polishing the floor with a cloth tied round one big boot.) Wilma had a visitor this morning, and then she disappeared into the back for an hour. She came out with her eyes red.",[
  ["Is she all right?","She said it was the ash. But I know Wilma. She cries at old labels. She cried at a cola cap from 1931 once."],
  ["Do you worry about her?","A bit. She's given everything to this museum. I'd like her to have something that doesn't need dusting."],
  ["Is anything wrong with the museum?","Nothing I know of. But the company's being funny about it. Kurt keeps measuring the walls."]],
 "I'm going to make her a cup of tea. A real one, from a real cup."],
26:["(Kelpy is carrying a huge cardboard sign that says 'A PRINCE IN EVERY BOTTLE'.) Don't ask. Kurt told me to wave it about. I've been doing it all morning, and no one's the least bit interested.",[
  ["Which prince?","Leopold, I think. Or possibly a prince from a long way off. The sign's a bit vague. It's a marketing sign."],
  ["Do you believe in the monarchy?","I believe in being paid. But I'll admit I like a crown. It's a bit like my head, only smaller and with jewels."],
  ["What does Wilma think of the sign?","She turned it to face the wall. 'There's no prince in any of the bottles,' she said. 'Not one I've ever found.'"]],
 "I'll hold it a bit lower. I'm not a flag."],
27:["(Kelpy slumps into a chair.) Everybody's so busy and so cross. The cinema's showing something the Party wants, the Syndicate's playing cards, and I'm waving at nobody.",[
  ["Do you want to take a day off?","Every day, but the island's not the kind of place that gives you one. Besides, who'd be Kelpy?"],
  ["Is there a Kelp Cola plan for the big days?","Kurt says it's 'going abroad'. I thought he meant on holiday. He didn't."],
  ["Are you looking forward to the end of the month?","In the way you look forward to a dentist. I'd like it to be over. I'd like to know who's in charge."]],
 "I'll be here till the end. A cup's a cup, whoever wins."],
28:["(Kelpy bounces, clapping both hands.) It's today! The biggest day! Everyone's being so brave and so loud. Kurt's gone off in a car with a suitcase and a very long face.",[
  ["What are you doing today?","Waving. Also giving the tour. And I might sing, if no one's listening. Wilma says people always listen."],
  ["Are you worried about the island?","Yes. Every day it feels more like a snow globe someone's shaking. I just don't know when they'll stop."],
  ["Is the company going abroad?","That's what Kurt said. Or at least the cola is. I'd like to go too. In the suitcase, if there's room."]],
 "Keep your head up. I'd keep mine up too, but it's stuck."],
29:["(Kelpy hugs its own big arms.) I'm so nervous I could fizz. Everyone's doing the last thing they can. Wilma's polishing the bottles, Kurt's pacing, Phil's on the phone.",[
  ["What do you expect tomorrow?","Noise. And then a quiet I don't know the shape of. I've been rehearsing a cheerful face for either."],
  ["Who do you want in charge?","Whoever lets a cup keep waving. That's my whole political programme. It's short, but it fits on a bottle cap."],
  ["Will you be at the museum?","Always. It's the only place I know to be on the day the world changes, apart from my bed."]],
 "Thank you for visiting. Truly. You were the nicest reporter we had."],
30:["(Kelpy stands very straight on the museum steps, both arms at its sides.) This is it. Whatever happens, I'll be here, and I'll wave. It's the one thing I'm certain of.",[
  ["Any last words, Kelpy?","Drink more cola. Be kind to the staff. And don't forget that a mascot's a person too. Even if you never see the face."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. I keep looking at the bottles on the shelves, and they all look so calm. I'm trying to learn from them."],
  ["Will you take your head off one day?","Perhaps. On the day I've got something to say that Kelpy can't. I don't think it's today. But it's near."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Kelpy will remember you. Kelpy remembers everyone."],
};
// Jack, owner of the fishing shop in Kingsgrad (Republican Party). Threads: the harbour tax and which candidate will cut it,
// the Admiral (a giant pike in Lake Ilmari he's never landed), a tip on where they're biting, gin rummy and arguments with Frank.
DAILY['Jack']={
2:["(Jack tips his yellow hat.) A customer! The road's barely open and here's a customer. Or are you lost?",[
  ["A bit of both.","Best way to arrive anywhere. Jack. Rods, tackle, bait, and free advice on where they're biting. The advice is the only thing that's free."],
  ["Where are they biting, then?","Off the jetty at Lake Ilmari, if the ice has gone. Perch, mostly. Use a red float: perch are vain, they like a bit of colour."],
  ["Do you ever catch anything yourself?","Every day, in my head. Running the shop, I get out twice a week if I'm lucky. That's the cobbler's shoes, isn't it?"]],
 "Come back when you've got a rod. Or come back to buy one."],
3:["You know what's killing this shop? The harbour tax. Every boat that ties up pays the Crown a fee, and every fisherman takes it out of my prices.",[
  ["How much is the tax?","Two dollars a mooring, a day. Doesn't sound much. Then you count the boats. Then you count the days. Then you go and have a lie down."],
  ["Who could change it?","Whoever runs the island, once there's someone running it. I'm a Republican, and I'll vote for whoever cuts it. Simple as that."],
  ["Is that all you care about?","No. I care about fish. But fish don't vote, so I care about the tax."]],
 "If you meet a politician, ask him about the harbour tax. Watch him squirm."],
4:["(Jack is threading a line through a rod's eyes, very slowly.) Don't talk for a second. It's the last eye.",[
  ["(Wait.)","(He gets it through.) There. Forty years and I still hold my breath. Thank you. You'd be surprised how few people can keep quiet."],
  ["What's the rod for?","A man from Eastbrook. Wants to try for salmon up the Valto River. I told him he's two months late. He wants it anyway."],
  ["Missed!","(He misses.) Thank you. Thank you very much. Now I'll be here all afternoon."]],
 "Quiet's the first thing a fisherman learns. Most never do."],
5:["Have you heard about the Admiral? No? Then sit down. This is important.",[
  ["Who's the Admiral?",["A pike in Lake Ilmari. As long as a rowing boat, near enough. Grey as a battleship, with a scar down one side. I've had him on the line three times.",[
    ["Three times? What happened?","Snapped the line, snapped the rod, and once he took the float and swam off with it like a trophy. I saw it bobbing in the middle of the lake for a week."],
    ["How do you know it's the same fish?","The scar. And the look in his eye. He knows me. I'm sure of it. He waits for me."]]]],
  ["Is this a fish story?","Every story about the Admiral's a fish story. That's what makes them true."],
  ["Has anyone else seen him?","Tom from West Saltwick swears he saw him take a duck. A whole duck. Tom drinks, mind. But so does the duck, these days."]],
 "One day, the Admiral. One day."],
6:["(Jack has a tide table pinned to the counter and is marking it with a pencil.) Herring's running off the west coast this week. Tell anyone who'll listen.",[
  ["How do you know?","The gulls. When the gulls go west of Saltwick in the mornings, the herring are under them. Gulls don't lie. Fishermen do."],
  ["Will Olga be pleased?","Olga at the fish shop? Olga's never pleased. She'll pay the boys less, say there's too much herring, and sell it for the same. She's a genius."],
  ["Does anyone fish for fun here?","Me. The children, off the jetties. The old king did, in his day. Bought a rod off my father. Never caught a thing. Very respectful about it."]],
 "Herring, west, under the gulls. Remember that."],
7:["Frostholm's open. Means the oil men'll be down to buy fancy reels they'll use twice. I love oil men.",[
  ["You like Diversified Petroleum?","I like their money. Their rig's another matter. I've seen what a rig does to a sea, in Norway. Fish don't like the taste."],
  ["Would you vote for Magnus?","If Magnus cuts the harbour tax, I'd vote for a magnus-shaped hole in the ice. So far he talks about pipelines."],
  ["What's a fancy reel?","(He holds up something gold with too many parts.) This. Forty dollars. It does what a five-dollar reel does, but it does it in Frostholm."]],
 "If you see an oil man, send him in. I've three of those reels."],
8:["(Jack is not smiling.) Have you heard? Something's burst at the oil rig. There's oil on the water.",[
  ["How bad is it?","Nobody'll say. The boats coming into Saltwick say there's a sheen as far as you can see off the west side. A sheen. On my sea."],
  ["What will it do to the fish?","Herring swim under it, if they're lucky. The shellfish can't. And the birds sit on it. (He shakes his head.) It's the birds I can't stand to think about."],
  ["Is anyone doing anything?","Dario's lot'll be wanting hose and pumps and booms, if they've any sense. I've hose. Nobody's asked yet. I hope somebody does."]],
 "Pray for a west wind. Pushes it out to sea."],
9:["(Jack has a jar of maggots open on the counter.) Fresh in. Don't look so worried. They're for perch, not for you.",[
  ["That's revolting.","That's bait. Everything's revolting till you've caught something with it. Then it's wonderful."],
  ["Any news of the oil?","Wind's turned. Pushing it north and out, they say. I'll believe it when I see a gull that isn't black."],
  ["Do the perch really like maggots?","Perch like anything that wriggles. Perch are the Republicans of the lake: they'll take any offer going."]],
 "Lid on the jar on your way out. Please."],
10:["(Jack is in his best jacket, a little tight.) Shop's shut for the funeral. I'm only here to feed the minnows.",[
  ["Did you go to the chapel?","Stood outside with the crowd. Couldn't see a thing. But I heard the bell, and that was enough. The old man liked his bells."],
  ["Did you know the king?","My father sold him a rod. He came back to say thank you, years later, and that he'd never caught anything with it. That's a decent man."],
  ["The jacket's very smart.","It was smart in nineteen sixty. I've grown. The jacket hasn't. Don't make me laugh, a button'll go."]],
 "Funny how quiet the town is. Even the gulls are quiet."],
11:["Frank's been in here telling me all three candidates are crooks. I told him, Frank, they're politicians. It's the same word in a better hat.",[
  ["Do you agree with him?","About Carlo, yes. About Harald and Magnus... they're businessmen. Businessmen know what a tax costs. That's my hope, anyway."],
  ["You two seem to argue a lot.","Forty years. Gin rummy on Thursdays, for matches. He says I cheat. I say he can't count. We're both right."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","Whoever has the most friends on the twenty-ninth. Republicans aren't many. Margit could fit us all in her kitchen."]],
 "If you see Frank, tell him I said he's wrong. He'll know about what."],
12:["(Jack is untangling a ball of line the size of a cat.) A child brought this in. Said it was 'a bit knotted'. A bit.",[
  ["Can it be saved?","Everything can be saved, with patience and a pin. This'll take me till Sunday. Good line, though. You don't throw away good line."],
  ["Let me help.","(He hands you an end.) Pull when I say pull, not before. Not before! (He sighs.) Never mind. That's a new knot. That's yours, that one."],
  ["Why not just cut it?","Cut it? (He looks hurt.) Do you cut a jumper when it's got a hole in it?"]],
 "Leave it with me. Me and the pin, we'll get there."],
13:["Free Kelp Cola all over town! I took two. One for me, one for the Admiral.",[
  ["You're giving a pike a cola?","I'm going to drop it off the jetty in the bottle. Maybe he'll choke on the cap. A man's got to try new things."],
  ["Is Harald buying votes?","Of course he is. But I'd sell mine for a cut in the harbour tax, so I can't really talk."],
  ["Does it taste good?","Like seaweed that's been to a party. I've had worse. I've been a fisherman fifty years: I've had much worse."]],
 "Sweet stuff. Rots the teeth. Keeps the shop busy, though: everyone wants to go fishing after."],
14:["(Jack is putting on his yellow hat to go out.) The big day. I'm closing up and going to see it. Want to walk with me?",[
  ["What do you hope comes of it?","Somebody stands up and says, 'The harbour tax is cut.' And I cry like a baby, in public, in front of Frank."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A bit. Crowds make me nervous. Too many people wanting things. Give me a lake with one fish in it any day."],
  ["Lots of fishermen there?","All of them. Saltwick's emptied. Tom and Sam'll be together, for once, not arguing about the union. Or arguing very quietly."]],
 "Big day. Bring a hat. It's colder in a crowd than you'd think."],
15:["The palace and the Church are fighting over who goes on top. You know what's on top in my shop? The rods. Simple.",[
  ["Who should be on top?","Whoever cuts the harbour tax. I keep saying it. Nobody listens. It's like talking to a herring."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas and funerals, like everyone. Father Thyme blesses the boats in spring. I'd keep that. Never lost a boat with a blessing on it."],
  ["What does Leopold think about fishing?","Leopold's never held a rod in his life. Augustus has, though. I sold him one, years ago. He fished like he was praying. Very still."]],
 "A man who fishes still is a man who's patient. I'd vote for patience."],
16:["(The radio's on: a debate.) Anna says the sea belongs to the people. I've got news for her. The sea belongs to the fish.",[
  ["Would the Communists cut the harbour tax?","They'd do away with the harbour. Every boat to the collective. My shop would be 'the people's tackle'. I'd still be behind the counter, mind."],
  ["What about Erik?","Erik says we should vote on it. Fine. Let's vote. My vote: cut it. There. Done. Why do they need a whole debate?"],
  ["Who's winning?","The shouting's winning. Neither of them's said anything about fish."]],
 "Turn it off on your way out. I've heard enough."],
17:["(The rods on the wall rattle gently.) There. Feel that? The ground's been doing it since breakfast.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Not here. Up north, maybe. Hilda's chickens stopped laying, she says. And there's steam off the top of the mountain."],
  ["What about the Admiral?","He'll feel it in the lake before we do. Fish always know. I went up at dawn: the water was all ripples, and no wind. No bites either."],
  ["Heard Dario's missing?","Missing! A man like that doesn't go missing. He goes somewhere he hasn't told anyone. That's different. Rich men call it a holiday."]],
 "Steady the rods if you bump them. They're touchy today."],
18:["You know what makes a good fishing spot? Quiet. And the circus has pitched its big top right on the east shore of the lake.",[
  ["Does the circus scare the fish?","The strongman goes swimming every morning. Igor. Like a walrus in a bathing suit. Every perch in the lake goes to the far end."],
  ["Have you been to the circus?","I went for the fishing and stayed for the fortune teller. Madame Zora. She said I'd land something big 'when the mountain speaks'. Load of nonsense."],
  ["Maybe the Admiral likes the circus.","Maybe he's in it. Maybe that's where he's been all these years, juggling."]],
 "When the mountain speaks. Load of nonsense. (He writes it down anyway.)"],
19:["(Jack is counting floats into a box.) Three days ago the ground shook. Now it shakes every morning. My cup walks across the table.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm old. I've seen a few things. I've not seen a mountain go up. I'd like to see it from a long way off, that's all."],
  ["What do the fishermen say?","They're keeping their boats close to Saltwick, ready to go. If the mountain goes, the sea's the safest place there is. Every fisherman knows that."],
  ["Will Madame Zora be right?",["Don't. (He looks at the floats.) I wrote it on a bit of paper and I keep it in my wallet. Don't tell Frank.",[
    ["Why not tell Frank?","He'd never let me forget it. He'd tell everyone in the City Bar, and then they'd tell the Admiral."],
    ["Your secret's safe.","Thank you. A man's entitled to one silly thing. That's mine."]]]]],
 "Keep your boots by the door. Just in case."],
20:["(Something on the shelf falls over. Jack doesn't look up.) It's been doing that all day. I've stopped picking things up.",[
  ["Should you close the shop?","And do what? Sit at home with my walking cup? No. People still want hooks. The world ending's no excuse to go without hooks."],
  ["Are the roads north still open?","Up to Frostholm, yes. Past the lake, the farmers are coming down with their carts. Hilda and Olaf brought their cow to Kingsgrad. A cow! On the tram!"],
  ["Have you seen the mountain?","From the end of the jetty. A grey plume. Like a pipe, smoked by a very big man. Doesn't look like weather."]],
 "Pick that up for me on your way out, would you? I'm on strike."],
21:["(Ash is drifting in at the door.) Shut that, would you? It's gone up. The mountain. Just after breakfast.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","Nobody's hurt, so they say. The north's covered. The lava came down in the hills, not the farms. God was looking the other way, and so was the lava."],
  ["What about the lake?","Grey. Grey as dishwater. I went up to look. All the fish'll be on the bottom, hiding. The Admiral too, I hope. The old devil."],
  ["Was Madame Zora right?","(He touches his wallet.) She said I'd land something big. Not that the mountain would. I'm still waiting for my half."]],
 "Cover your nose if you go out. That ash is sharp."],
22:["(Jack is wiping ash off a row of reels.) Everything's grey. I've fished in fog, I've fished in sleet, I've never fished in ash.",[
  ["Will the fish be all right?","The sea's big. It'll swallow the ash and not notice. The lake's small. It'll take longer. I give it a fortnight."],
  ["Are the boats going out?","Saltwick sent three out this morning. Tom said the water's milky and the fish are hungry, which is all a fisherman needs to hear."],
  ["You seem calmer.","The ground's stopped shaking. That's all I wanted. Ash I can wipe off. A shaking floor you can't do anything about."]],
 "If you're going fishing, wipe your line. The grit cuts it."],
23:["Margit's sent everyone a letter saying the vote goes ahead on the twenty-ninth, volcano or no volcano. Good for Margit.",[
  ["Who are you voting for?","Harald came by. Said Kelp Cola 'appreciates the harbour'. Magnus came by. Said the oil boats 'use the harbour'. Neither said 'cut'. Then Carlo came."],
  ["What did Carlo say?","He said he'd cut it. Just like that. 'Gone, Jack.' And smiled. Nobody's ever said that to me before. (He frowns.) I didn't like how much I liked it."],
  ["Isn't he with the Syndicate?","So they say. Frank says so, loudly. But the harbour tax is a real thing that happens to me, and the Syndicate's a rumour. That's how they get you, I suppose."]],
 "I'll think about it. Six days. Long enough to change my mind four times."],
24:["(Jack has a fresh tide table.) Herring's back under the gulls. West of Saltwick, like always. The volcano didn't put them off.",[
  ["That's good news.","It is. Fish come back. Fish always come back. It's people who don't."],
  ["Does Olga know?","Olga knew before the gulls did. She's already put her prices down, so she can put them up again when the boats come in."],
  ["What about the lake?","Still grey. I'll leave the Admiral alone till it clears. He's had enough of a shock. A gentleman doesn't fish for a frightened pike."]],
 "Herring, west, under the gulls. You remember."],
25:["Frank says I'm a fool for even thinking about Carlo. I said, Frank, at least he answered the question.",[
  ["Is Frank right?","Usually, about men. Never about bait. This is about men. (He sighs.) He's probably right."],
  ["What does Doctor Lind think?","Doctor Lind wants a hospital. He'd vote for whoever builds one, and he says Carlo'd build one with a back door. Doctors are funny."],
  ["Will you fall out over it?","Over a vote? Never. We fell out over a perch in nineteen sixty-four and didn't speak for a month. That was a serious matter."]],
 "Rummy's on Thursday. I'll let him win. He's had a hard week."],
26:["(Jack is restringing a rod.) The lake's clearing. I went up at dawn. Saw something big turn, right in the middle. Grey. Scar.",[
  ["The Admiral!","The Admiral. Alive, and in a temper. Sent a wave at the jetty like he was telling me off for being late."],
  ["Did you try for him?","Not yet. I want a new line, new rod, new everything. When I go for him this time, it'll be properly. Maybe the first of October."],
  ["Will you ever actually catch him?",["(He thinks about it.) If I catch him, what would I do on Sundays?",[
    ["Let him go again?","Maybe. Shake his fin. Tell him well done. Fifty years we've been at it."],
    ["Stuff him and hang him up?","Over that door, where everyone can see. And Frank'd have to look at it every Thursday. (He grins.) Tempting."]]]]],
 "Grey. Scar. Alive. Best news this month."],
27:["They say the Syndicate's holding a card game in the pizzeria tonight. Carlo's going. Everyone who wants a favour's going.",[
  ["Are you going?","Me? I can't bluff. My face does whatever my cards do. I'd lose the shop by eleven."],
  ["Will votes be bought there?","Votes, favours, boats. Things get bought at those games that don't go in any book. Frank says so. Frank's never been."],
  ["What's on at the cinema?","Otto's showing his flying picture again. Same as every day. He knows every word. He says them along with the actors. It puts people off."]],
 "Stay away from cards, is my advice. Fishing's cheaper."],
28:["(Jack is standing in the door.) Every faction on the island's out today. Flags, drums, leaflets. I've had six leaflets through my door since breakfast.",[
  ["Read any of them?","Every one. None of them mention the harbour tax. I've written to all six to tell them."],
  ["Who's loudest?","Hard to say. There's a rally in Eastbrook, there's a king off a boat, there's cola everywhere. Everyone thinks tomorrow's theirs."],
  ["Are you staying open?","Of course. Nobody's buying, mind. Everyone's too busy shouting to go fishing. Their loss."]],
 "Tomorrow I vote. Today I sit."],
29:["(Jack has his membership card in his hat band.) Voting day. The Republicans pick a leader. Frank's driving us both up to Frostholm.",[
  ["Have you decided?","I decided four times yesterday and twice on the way to the shop. I'll decide again with the pencil in my hand. That's the honest way."],
  ["What will you tell Frank?","Nothing. The paper's private. And if he asks, I'll tell him I voted for the Admiral."],
  ["What if your man loses?","Then I'll do what I always do. Sell rods, pay the tax and moan. Fifty years of moaning: I'm good at it."]],
 "Wish me luck. Not on the vote: on Frank's driving."],
30:["Last day of the month. Whatever's decided about the island, it's decided today.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Like I've got a big fish on and I can't see what it is yet. Could be the catch of my life. Could be a boot."],
  ["What do you want for the island?","Boats on the water, fish under them, and a fair price at the harbour. That's not much to ask. Somehow it always is."],
  ["Will you go fishing tomorrow?","First thing. Up to the lake with my new line. Whatever happens today, the Admiral'll be waiting. He's the only thing on this island that never changes."]],
 "Fish come back. Fish always come back. Remember that."],
};
// Jonna, the tattoo artist at Ink and Pine in Eastbrook (Democratic Party). Threads: the quiet since the snow and the customers
// coming back one by one, her ink stuck in customs, her blunt opinions on everyone's designs (Knut's finger, Buttons's secret
// commission), Kelp Cola wanting Kelpy tattoos, and the emblem and lettering she's doing for Erik's rally banners.
DAILY['Jonna']={
4:["(Jonna is sitting on her own tattoo bench, drawing in a sketchbook.) A customer? An actual person, walking in, upright? I'd forgotten what one looked like. Nobody's been in since the snow came.",[
  ["What sort of tattoos do you do?","Anything but bad ones. Anchors, pines, ships, wolves, your mother's name. I won't do your ex's name. That's for your own protection."],
  ["How do you get by with no customers?","Badly. I draw, I eat soup, and I wait for the roads to open. Well, they're open. Now I'm waiting for courage. People need courage for a tattoo."],
  ["Are those all your own?","(She holds out her arms, inked from wrist to shoulder.) Every one. Some I did myself, left-handed. You can tell which. Don't point them out."]],
 "Come back when you've thought of something. Something good."],
5:["(Jonna is pinning new designs to the wall, standing back, and taking half of them down again.) Flash. The designs on the wall. Half of these I drew in the snow, and half of them are terrible. Cabin fever.",[
  ["Which ones are terrible?","The bear in a crown. I drew that the night the king died, after two glasses of Linnea's aquavit. It's coming down. Nobody wants a drunk bear on their arm."],
  ["What's your favourite design?","The pine tree with the lantern in it. Eastbrook's tree. It means 'I'm from here, and I'll still be here when you've gone'."],
  ["Is business picking up?","One man came in yesterday, looked at the wall for an hour, and asked if I did henna. I don't."]],
 "Tell your friends. Tell your enemies. I'm not fussy."],
6:["(Jonna is holding a nearly empty ink bottle up to the light.) Black. My last bottle of good black. The rest of my order's sitting in the customs shed on the quay, waiting for Ole's stamp.",[
  ["Why is it stuck?","Because Ole put a duty on everything that comes in by boat, and Liv at customs has to weigh it, and count it, and write it in a ledger. My ink's been weighed three times. It hasn't got any heavier."],
  ["Can't Liv just let it through?","Liv lives two floors under me in the Lofts. She's lovely. She's also never bent a rule in her life. I've stopped asking. I just glare at her on the stairs."],
  ["What happens when the black runs out?","Then everyone on the island gets a tattoo in blue. Very patriotic. Very Democratic Party, actually. Erik would be delighted."]],
 "If you're going past the customs shed, glare at it for me."],
7:["(Jonna has a big sheet of paper on the floor and is lettering on it with a brush.) Erik asked me to do the lettering for the party's new banners. I said yes before I'd seen his spelling.",[
  ["What does the banner say?","'A FREE VOTE, A FAIR WAGE, NO MASTERS.' Which is good. He had it as 'NO MASTER'S', with an apostrophe. I nearly resigned from the party."],
  ["Is lettering like tattooing?","Same hand, different skin. Paper's more forgiving. Paper doesn't flinch. And paper never asks if you're sure halfway through."],
  ["Will you be paid?","In coffee and gratitude. Erik pays everyone in gratitude. If gratitude were money, the Democrats would own Frostholm."]],
 "Don't step on the banner. That 'W' took me an hour."],
8:["(Jonna is bandaging somebody's forearm, and they're leaving looking proud.) A Saltwick fisherman, with a mermaid. Came all the way over the hill on a Sunday. I told him the mermaid would last longer than his marriage. He laughed. He didn't say I was wrong.",[
  ["That's a customer, at least.","The first since the snow. I'll frame his money. No, I won't, I'll spend it on soup."],
  ["Is it a good mermaid?","It's a very good mermaid. He wanted her with a cigarette. I said no. Mermaids don't smoke: it's damp down there."],
  ["Do people regret tattoos?","Only the ones they didn't think about. I make everyone sit in that chair for ten minutes first and think. Half of them get up and leave. The other half deserve me."]],
 "Sunday's for mermaids. Monday's for regrets."],
9:["(Jonna is drawing something on tracing paper, and covering it with her arm when you come in.) Private commission. Don't look. I've been sworn to secrecy, and I take that seriously. Mostly.",[
  ["Who's it for?","Someone from the circus at Lake Ilmari. He came all the way down on the tram in his ordinary clothes, which on him are still quite colourful. That's all I'm saying."],
  ["What is it?",["(She hesitates, and then shows you, quickly.) A name, in a heart, with a trapeze through it. Over his heart. He wants it ready for a special day.",[
    ["Is it a love token?","It's a love token with a deadline. He's saving up. He's paying in instalments and the instalments are in very small coins."],
    ["Does she know?","Not yet. And if you tell her, I'll tattoo your name on a pig."]]]],
  ["Can I see your other work?","(She flips the sketchbook open somewhere else.) Here. A wolf for a mill hand, a ship for a ferryman, a rose for nobody. I draw roses when I'm bored."]],
 "Not a word. I mean it. The pig is real, and it's in my book."],
10:["(Jonna's shop is shut, but she's at the window in a black jumper with her sleeves pulled down.) Funeral day. I closed out of respect, and also because nobody gets tattooed at a funeral. Wrong mood.",[
  ["You covered your arms.","For the king. He didn't like tattoos. He said once on the radio that they were for 'sailors and foreigners'. I'm neither. I covered them anyway. It's his day."],
  ["Did you ever meet him?","Once, at the school, when I was twelve. He shook my hand. I'd drawn a dragon on the back of it in biro. He looked at it and said 'very fine'. I've never forgotten."],
  ["Is anybody tattooing his face?","Not in my shop. Kings on arms look like plates. And then he'd be on you for life, whoever comes after him."]],
 "Open again tomorrow. Back to sailors and foreigners."],
11:["(Knut is sitting in Jonna's chair, holding up his hand with the missing fingertip, while she looks at it with great seriousness.) Knut wants the stump tattooed. A little saw blade round the end. I'm thinking about it.",[
  ["Is that a good idea?","Artistically? It's brilliant. Medically? It's a fingertip. Personally? Knut tells a different story about that finger every time, and I want the tattoo to match one of them."],
  ["Isn't he meant to be at the mill?","He says he's on strike. Bjarne says he's on a tea break. There's a union lady up from Saltwick, and everyone's talking pay."],
  ["Which story is true?","(Knut says: 'The wolf.' Jonna rolls her eyes.) Last week it was a Russian submarine. I'll tattoo it when he picks one."]],
 "If Knut asks, I'm still thinking. Thinking takes a week."],
12:["(Jonna is up a ladder painting the shop's sign, INK AND PINE, in a fresh coat of green.) It was getting tatty. A tattoo shop with a tatty sign is like a dentist with no teeth. Nobody trusts it.",[
  ["Is business better?","A bit. A fisherman, a mill hand, a girl from the Kelp Cola museum who wanted a very small star where her mother won't see it. Three customers is a crowd, after August."],
  ["Is there a party job today?","Ruby's doing something secret with a record, and Erik's wringing his hands about it. I'm painting a sign. Somebody has to keep the street looking awake."],
  ["Want a hand?","Hold the ladder. Don't touch the brush. Nobody touches my brush. I don't even lend it to Erik, and he's the leader."]],
 "Thanks. Don't lean on the sign. It's wet."],
13:["(Jonna is holding a letter at arm's length, as if it smells.) Kelp Cola. They want me to do free tattoos of Kelpy the cup at their free samples. Free! As if my needles grow on trees.",[
  ["What would Kelpy look like on an arm?","Like a paper cup with a grin. On a forearm, it'd be a cup with a grin. On a bicep, it'd be a cup with a smug grin. No."],
  ["Did you say no?","I wrote back one word, in my best lettering, with a flourish. It wasn't 'yes'."],
  ["Isn't that good business?","Free isn't business. Free's a habit someone else gets to keep. If Phil wants Kelpy tattooed on people, Phil can learn to hold a needle."]],
 "If anyone asks for a cup on their arm, send them to Phil."],
14:["(Jonna has a little sketchbook and a stub of pencil, and she's tucking them in her coat pocket.) The fourteenth. The whole island in one place. I'm going to draw faces. Every face on the island, in one day.",[
  ["Why faces?","Because tattoos are faces, mostly. What people want to look like. What they're afraid to look like. I'll learn more today than in a year of flash."],
  ["What do you expect from today?","Speeches I don't need and people I'll want to draw. And a lot of waiting about, in the cold. Bring a flask."],
  ["Will you draw me?","(She looks at you, narrowly, for a long moment.) You've got a reporter's face. Always looking at something else. Stand still and maybe."]],
 "Bring a flask. And stand where the light's good."],
15:["(Jonna's sketchbook is open on the counter, full of quick pencil faces.) Look. The fourteenth. I drew a hundred and nine faces. The princes, the Abbot, Anna, Dario, a woman who kept crying, and a man who was asleep standing up.",[
  ["Who's the most interesting?","Augustus. He looked at the crowd like they might bite. And Leopold looked at it like he wanted it to clap. Brothers. You'd never think so, till you draw their ears."],
  ["Can I see Erik?","(She flips a page.) There. I drew him smiling at nobody. That's how Erik looks at crowds. As if every one of them's about to be a friend."],
  ["Today's crown against cross, I hear.","The palace and the monastery fighting about whose king Augustus would be. Funny thing: I drew him on the fourteenth, and he looked like he didn't want to be anyone's."]],
 "A hundred and nine faces. I'll be tattooing half of them in my dreams."],
16:["(Jonna has a fresh bandage on her own wrist and is admiring it.) I've done myself a new one, for the debate. Small. A ballot box, on the inside of my wrist, left-handed. It's terrible. I love it.",[
  ["Why a ballot box?","Because the Communists will be talking about tanks and factories, and Erik will be talking about votes, and I want to know which side I'm on when I look down."],
  ["Will you go to the debate?","Front row, with Ilse. She'll heckle with her mouth, I'll heckle with my eyebrows. We're a good team."],
  ["Show me the bad bits.","(She turns her wrist.) The slot's crooked. The box looks like a toaster. It's the best thing I've done all month."]],
 "Off to the debate. Wish my eyebrows luck."],
17:["(The ground shakes, gently. Jonna lifts her needle away from a customer's arm and waits until it stops.) Every time. Three times today. I'm tattooing between earthquakes now. Very slowly.",[
  ["Is it safe?","As long as I see it coming. I've got a rule: if the lamp swings, I stop. This gentleman's going to have a very wobbly anchor otherwise."],
  ["It's the volcano, people say.","Steam up north, Dex says. And now everybody wants a volcano tattoo. Two this morning. People get very sentimental about disasters before they happen."],
  ["Does the customer mind?","(The customer says: 'I'm fine.') He's not fine. He's grey. But he's brave, and he'll have a very good anchor."]],
 "Close the door gently. The lamp's swinging."],
18:["(Jonna is sitting with her feet up, eating a sandwich, with a bottle of ink beside her like a trophy.) It came. My black. Liv brought it up herself. She said she'd 'weighed it once more, for luck'.",[
  ["So you've made up with Liv?","We've been made up. I glared at her on the stairs for two weeks, and she stood there and took it, and then brought the ink. I've offered her a tattoo, free. She went pink."],
  ["Will you tattoo Liv?","She asked for a tiny customs stamp on her ankle. Approved. I've never liked anybody so much."],
  ["How's the private commission?","(She lowers her voice.) He came in for the first fitting. Sat very still. Didn't make a single joke. That's how you know it's serious, with a jester."]],
 "Black's back. Ink and Pine's open for business. Properly, this time."],
19:["(Jonna has a sheet of cartridge paper pinned to the wall, with a pine tree drawn on it, and a ballot box in the trunk.) The party emblem. Erik asked for something people would recognise from the back of a crowd. I've drawn it nine times.",[
  ["What does it mean?","The pine's Eastbrook, and the island. The box is the vote. And the lantern's the bit I put in for myself: you keep a light on for people you don't know yet."],
  ["Will Erik like it?","Erik likes everything. That's the problem. I need someone who'll tell me it's ugly. Ruby, probably."],
  ["It's ugly.","(She looks at you.) ...Thank you. Which bit? No, don't say. I know which bit. The lantern. The lantern's too big."]],
 "If Ruby comes in, send her through. I need an insult."],
20:["(Jonna is drawing at the window, and keeps looking up at the street.) A man in good shoes went past three times today. Russian accent. He looked in, at my flash, and he looked at it like it was a list of names.",[
  ["The man from the Hotel Aurora?","So Erik keeps whispering. Ruby says he asked her who buys records. He didn't ask me anything. He just looked."],
  ["Does he worry you?","A man who looks at a wall of tattoos and doesn't want one worries me. Everyone wants one, even if they never get it. He didn't want anything."],
  ["Why not draw him?",["(She turns the sketchbook round.) I did. From memory. Look at that jaw. You could crack ice on it.",[
    ["Can I have it?","No. It's evidence. Erik's going to want it. Or the Gazette. Or a priest."],
    ["He looks like a schoolteacher.","He looks like a schoolteacher who's never liked a single pupil. That's the difference between him and Erik."]]]]],
 "If he comes in, I'm fully booked. For a year."],
21:["(Jonna's shop is shuttered, and she's on the street with a wet cloth tied over her mouth, helping an old woman along to the Community Centre.) The mountain's gone up! Ash coming down like snow. Grab an arm, would you?",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","So far. Nobody hurt that I've heard. Erik's got the hall open. Dex is making soup. We're all walking slowly with old ladies."],
  ["Where's the lava?","Up north, past the farms, people say. Not here. Here it's just ash. Grey, on everything. My shop's going to look like a chimney."],
  ["Are you scared?","Terrified. But I've got an old lady on one arm and you on the other, so I can't show it. That's the secret of courage: someone's always watching."]],
 "Get inside. Wet cloth on your face. Go on."],
22:["(Jonna is wiping grey ash off the bottles on her shelves, one by one.) Ash in the ink. Ash in the needles. Ash in my hair. I'll be tattooing in grey for a month whether I like it or not.",[
  ["Is your shop damaged?","No. Just filthy. And I've had four people already today asking for an ash cloud, or a volcano, or the date. The twenty-first. People want to remember."],
  ["Will you do them?","Of course. It's history. You tattoo history on people, they'll never forget it. Better than a history book. History books burn."],
  ["Was anybody hurt?","Nobody, thank goodness. The farms in the north have lost a lot. Nobody's lost anybody. I keep saying it out loud, to make it true."]],
 "Ash clouds, half price, this week only."],
23:["(Jonna has her own sleeve rolled up and is tattooing a tiny grey cloud above her elbow.) My own. Left-handed. The twenty-first. Everyone else has one, so I want one too. It's going to be terrible.",[
  ["Is it?","(She looks at it.) It looks like a sheep. A very angry sheep. I'll tell people it's a volcano sheep. Very rare."],
  ["How's business?","Busy. Ash clouds, mostly. And one man who wanted the date and the words 'I SURVIVED'. I talked him into 'STILL HERE'. Ruby's got postcards that say the same."],
  ["How's the private commission?","(She checks the door, then whispers.) Finished, and he's paid. Last coins in a sock. He cried a bit. He wants to show her on a special day. Not a word."]],
 "Volcano sheep. Ask for it by name."],
24:["(Jonna is pinning Erik's new emblem to her wall, beside the flash.) Erik approved it. With the smaller lantern. Bjarne's cutting it into the rally stage, Meg from the circus is hanging the banners, and I'm doing the lettering on all twenty.",[
  ["Twenty banners?","Twenty. All in my hand. If one of them has a spelling mistake, I'll resign from the party, the island and possibly my own name."],
  ["Will people want the emblem as a tattoo?","They already do. Three mill hands this morning. Little pines with a box. I'm doing them small, on the wrist. Like mine. Only better, because they're right-handed."],
  ["Isn't a party tattoo a bit risky?",["It's a risk on any island where people might change their minds. (She shrugs.) Your tattoo is always yours. Who you put it on for isn't.",[
    ["What if the Democrats lose?","Then they'll have a pine tree and a box on their wrist and a story. Stories are worth more than winning. Ask Knut."],
    ["Would you take yours off?","Never. I'd add a little crack in the box, so everyone knew I'd been right."]]]]],
 "Twenty banners. Pray for my wrist."],
25:["(Jonna is lettering a banner, very carefully, with the tip of her tongue showing.) Erik's trying to win over Princess Daphne. If she comes over, I've told him I'll letter her a banner of her own. 'A PRINCESS FOR THE PEOPLE'.",[
  ["Would a princess want a banner?","Everyone wants a banner. Everyone wants to see their name in letters a foot high. Even the shyest people. Especially the shyest people."],
  ["Would she want a tattoo?","(She grins.) I would pay to tattoo a princess. Something small, behind the ear. A crown with a ballot box in it. She'd start a fashion."],
  ["Are you a royalist now?","I'm a lettering artist. I'll letter anybody's name if it's spelt right. A princess for democracy's a good story, and I like a good story."]],
 "Don't breathe on the banner. The 'P' is wet."],
26:["(Jonna is reading the Gazette with a look of disgust.) A prince on every bottle of Kelp Cola. They've printed the label. It's a terrible drawing. His nose is in the wrong place.",[
  ["Could you do better?","With my eyes shut, and my left hand. Who drew that? A cola executive? It looks like a potato in a crown."],
  ["What about Dario buying the spa?","Let him. He'll sit in hot water. It'll be good practice for when the island votes."],
  ["Will you complain to Kelp Cola?","(She folds the paper.) I've written them a second one-word letter. The same word as before. They'll know it's from me."]],
 "Potato in a crown. That's all I'll say."],
27:["(Jonna's floor is covered in banners, all drying. She's sitting on the only empty bit, rubbing her wrist.) Twenty banners, done. 'YOUR ISLAND. YOUR VOTE.' Twenty times, no mistakes. Then Erik painted the last three himself, and spelt 'island' with a 'y'.",[
  ["How did you react?","I said nothing for a whole minute. Then I said a lot of things for a whole minute. Then I fixed them. He bought me a coffee. He'll be buying me coffee till Christmas."],
  ["Are you going to the rally?","Front row. With Ilse, in case of trouble, and my sketchbook, in case of faces."],
  ["Is your wrist all right?","It's a tattoo artist's wrist. It'll hold a needle tomorrow. It just won't hold a teacup."]],
 "Mind the banners. They're my children."],
28:["(Jonna has a bundle of banner poles under her arm and a tin of paint in her hand.) Rally day. My banners are going up in the king's square in Kingsgrad. Twenty of them. My letters, a foot high, in front of the palace.",[
  ["How does that feel?","Like doing a tattoo on a whole town. I'm sick with nerves. If anything's spelt wrong, I'll have to emigrate."],
  ["Who's going?","All of Eastbrook. Mill hands, Maya with six urns, Ruby with a gramophone, Dex with sausages. Half the circus, Igor carrying things. And everybody who'd like it to go badly."],
  ["Will it go well?","It'll go how it goes. I just want the banners to be straight. That's the one bit I can control."]],
 "If you see a crooked banner, fix it, and don't tell me."],
29:["(Jonna is tattooing a mill hand's wrist, and talking to you without looking up.) Everyone's asking for the emblem since the banners went up. Six this morning. I'm doing nothing but little pines with ballot boxes.",[
  ["Business is booming, then.","Booming. Since the snow, I've had fewer customers than fingers. Now I've got a queue to the Gazette. I'll have to buy more black."],
  ["What about the Communists today?","Helen's people are asking for a little red star. I've done two. It's a free island. I'll tattoo anything that's spelt right."],
  ["Any regrets this month?","My volcano sheep. Erik's 'y'. And not drawing Princess Daphne on the fourteenth. I was busy with Augustus's ears."]],
 "Last one before tomorrow. Hold still. Not you. Him."],
30:["(Jonna is sitting on her tattoo bench in the empty shop, with her sleeves rolled up, looking at her own arms.) The last day. Whatever's decided today, I'm putting it on my arm tomorrow. Just the date. And whatever happens next, I'll draw it.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote. A fair one. And then a hundred years of people coming in here with their own ideas of who they are. That's all a tattoo shop is: a little vote on your own skin."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Of course. But I've got a ballot box on my wrist. If it all goes wrong, I'll look down and remember I tried."],
  ["Will you tattoo me, one day?",["(She looks at you, narrowly, for a long moment.) Maybe. When you've thought of something worth keeping. Come back then.",[
    ["What would you draw?","A pine with a lantern in it, small, on the wrist. Everyone who came to this island and helped should have one."],
    ["What if I never think of anything?","Then you'll never have a bad tattoo. That's the trouble with being sensible. Nothing to show for it."]]]]],
 "Go on. I'll be here. Ink and Pine never closes. Well. Sundays."],
};
// Juniper, owner of Flannel and Folk, the clothes shop in Eastbrook (Orthodox Church). Threads: hems and fit, her faith and Prince Augustus, Pirkko and the church faithful of Eastbrook, the odd things people buy (Stetsons, cowboy boots), the cold and the ash.
DAILY['Juniper']={
4:["(Juniper is pinning a hem with a mouthful of pins, so the first word is rather muffled.) Mm. Sorry. Welcome to Flannel and Folk. You've the look of someone who bought a coat for a milder country.",[
  ["I'm the reporter from London. Nice shop.","Thank you. Everything's second-hand and first-rate. Mind the mannequins, they're a bit shy."],
  ["How's business, with the roads just open?","Slow, but the snow's gone and the town's walking again. A woman bought four scarves this morning. She's either very cold or very pessimistic."],
  ["Is my coat that bad?","The sleeves are an inch short and the collar's been through a war. I could do something about both."]],
 "Come back if you need a better coat. Or a worse one that fits."],
5:["(Juniper is checking a rail of knitwear.) The north-east road's open now, so the monks will be down for their winter things. They never buy anything with a pattern. Black or grey. I put a stripe on a scarf once and Brother Anselm blushed.",[
  ["Are you Orthodox yourself?","Devout, I'd say. Every Sunday and most feast days. The church needs hands as well as prayers, and I've good ones for mending vestments."],
  ["Do you make clothes for the monastery?","Mend, mostly. Cassock hems get chewed by the cold. Father Thyme's is held together with prayer and my thread."],
  ["What's your favourite thing in the shop?","A green wool coat. It was a doctor's wife's. It fits no one, and I love it for that."]],
 "If you're going up to the monastery, tell them the hems are ready."],
6:["(Juniper is folding scarves with great precision.) Frostholm hasn't got a decent clothes shop, you know. They buy their shirts from a catalogue. A catalogue! They've no idea what they're missing.",[
  ["Do you get customers from other towns?","A few, when the road lets them. A man came in wanting a duffel coat. Hadn't a clue about the size. I gave him three."],
  ["What do you think of the Church's politics?","I think they pray very hard for Prince Augustus. I admire him. A young man who prays is a rare thing."],
  ["Do you ever wear anything fashionable?","I wear what fits and what's warm. Fashion is whatever the wind brings in."]],
 "That scarf suits you. It's not for sale, but it suits you."],
7:["(Juniper is on a stepladder, hanging a coat.) Everything's open now, the north-west and all. Do you think we'll get people from Frostholm? Oil people, in their good suits. I'll make them all buy something warm.",[
  ["Are there many oil people?","More every week. Fine suits, clean boots, nothing woollen. I hope they know what winter does."],
  ["How do you feel about them?","They've got money. I've got a shop. We might get along, if they don't ask me to take down the icons."],
  ["Is the shop crowded today?","Two customers and a cat. The cat's been in twice. She likes the beret rack."]],
 "If the cat comes back, don't feed her. She's got my customers' loyalty already."],
8:["(Juniper has her good dress on and is putting on gloves, as if for church.) There's the Sunday sermon on the wireless today, and I'm to be at Pirkko's for it. Father Thyme is supposed to be struggling for words. I'd say a prayer for him.",[
  ["Is Pirkko a good friend?","The best. She runs the food van at the cola factory, so she knows everyone's lunch and everyone's business."],
  ["Why can't Father Thyme find words?","The king's death hit him. And the Church is divided, between the crown and the cross. It's hard to preach when you're being pulled two ways."],
  ["What would you tell him?",["That the good words are the short ones. Amen. Peace. Hem and sew.",[
    ["That's not much of a sermon.","It's the best I've heard. Never trust a sermon you can't mend a button with."],
    ["Do you pray in the shop?","Between customers. Mostly for the right size. It doesn't always work."]]]]],
 "God keep you. And keep your collar straight."],
9:["(Juniper is dusting off a row of sweaters.) There's a new cola formula today, and a Party one, they say. Everyone's drinking one or the other. I stick to tea. It never changes its mind.",[
  ["Do you drink Kelp Cola?","Pirkko gives me a bottle sometimes. Very sweet. I leave it on the counter until it goes flat. Then I drink it."],
  ["What do you make of the Communists?","They'd have us all in grey jackets, and I'm very sorry for them. I like colour."],
  ["Do the Party people shop here?","One came in for a cap. Wanted it plain. I gave him a red one. He wore it out."]],
 "Come back if the cola gives you the shakes. I have a very long cardigan."],
10:["(Juniper is in black from neck to ankle.) The king's funeral today, in Kingsgrad. I've been up since five, ironing. Half of Eastbrook came in to borrow something black. I've sold the last of my mourning ties.",[
  ["Will you be at the funeral?","I'll be at the monastery church, with Pirkko. We'll light candles at the same hour. It feels better than a crowd."],
  ["Did the king have a good wardrobe?","The best. All wool, all fitted, and a cap he'd worn since 1948. He had one pair of boots, resoled seven times."],
  ["What's the mood in town?","Quiet. Even Dex put his cart away for the morning. That's how you know."]],
 "Stand straight, whatever you do today. It's what he'd have wanted."],
11:["(Juniper is wrapping a parcel in brown paper.) The Church is giving alms to the poor today, and I've put aside a few coats for the collection. A good coat is the best charity. It does the praying for you.",[
  ["Do you give clothes away often?","When I can afford to. Father Thyme says that's charity, I say it's stock control."],
  ["The mill hands want a fair day's pay.","So they should. I've sold more overalls this month than a decade. Everybody's tired of being patched."],
  ["Who needs the coats most?","The ones who say they don't need them. I've learned to leave them on the doorstep."]],
 "Be kind to someone today. It costs nothing and fits everyone."],
12:["(Juniper is arranging a pair of boots in the window.) A very odd thing. Two customers today have asked for a Stetson. Not a trilby, not a flatcap, a Stetson. A cowboy hat! I had two in the back, from an old order.",[
  ["Why would anyone want a Stetson here?","I haven't the faintest. Wear it and go to Texas, I suppose. Or a fancy dress party."],
  ["Did you sell them?","Not yet. A man in a sharp suit looked at them for a very long time. Then he looked at the cowboy boots, then the door."],
  ["Is it for a disguise?","(She raises an eyebrow.) In Eastbrook? Nobody has the time. Though the funny thing is, a hat does change a person."]],
 "If anyone comes for cowboy boots, tell them they're a size too big."],
13:["(Juniper is brushing lint off a shoulder.) The cola people are giving out samples round town today. I got one, I think, but I've no idea what to do with it. A bottle in a dress shop looks like a prop.",[
  ["Do the cola people come in here?","The girl from the museum's gift shop, Wilma. She buys cardigans in the autumn, sensible cardigans."],
  ["Do you like free samples?","Of anything. A free hem is a happy hem. But I never trust what's free in politics."],
  ["Have you heard of any trouble?","A stall at the market had a bit of a to-do over a recipe. I never go near anything with a secret in it."]],
 "Keep the bottle. It might be worth something. Or it might leak."],
14:["(Juniper is hanging up a notice, then taking it down.) Everybody's talking about tomorrow, the great gathering. Half the town's asked me to alter something. A woman wanted her best dress taken in, for fear of being seen.",[
  ["What will you wear?","The plum wool dress. It's old but it stands up on its own. I feel better in clothes that don't wobble."],
  ["What are you expecting?","Sermons, shouting, and a lot of people in the wrong shoes. And Erik giving a speech with his tie crooked."],
  ["Will the Church be there?","The whole mountain, probably. Father Thyme will be pale and Brother Anselm fidgeting."]],
 "Don't forget a scarf. It's colder than it looks."],
15:["(Juniper is crossing herself over a pile of unsold hats.) Crown before cross, cross before crown. The Church and the Royals have pitched against each other today. I don't like to see good people shouting.",[
  ["Whose side are you on?","The cross. But I've nothing against a crown. It's a nice shape. I'd make it a velvet lining."],
  ["What does Prince Augustus stand for?","Faith, kindness, a proper hemline on a cassock. He's pious, and he looks it, thank heavens."],
  ["Isn't Leopold the royal candidate?","He is. Handsome, loud, and with very bad taste in waistcoats. I did what I could."]],
 "I'll light a candle for peace. And one for the waistcoats."],
16:["(Juniper is on the telephone, then hangs up with a sigh.) Debate day. Communists against Democrats. Ruby wants me to listen with her in the record shop. I can't stand the shouting, but I do love a gossip.",[
  ["Who do you want to win?","The one that doesn't stand on my toes. The rest I leave to God."],
  ["Does the Church have a view?","Father Thyme says stay out of it. Brother Anselm says pray for both. I say mend what's torn."],
  ["Is Erik going to be nervous?","He wears the same tie when he's nervous. That tie has seen better days."]],
 "If you see Erik, tell him I could press that tie."],
17:["(Juniper steadies a row of hangers that rattle.) There. Did you feel it? The ground shook, and the hangers chimed. They say it's the volcano starting up. I'll tell you something: I've never liked mountains that smoke.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","They say not. They said the same about the weather, and look what it did."],
  ["Should the shop worry?","I've pinned the mannequins down. They've been jumping since morning."],
  ["Is the Church doing anything?","The monks are praying. Pirkko's making soup. I'm sewing. Everyone to their talent."]],
 "If it shakes again, stand in a doorway. And pray, if it helps."],
18:["(Juniper is looking under the counter.) The Church has lost a relic, you know. A real one. Brother Anselm came in white as chalk, and asked if I'd seen a small gold box in a patch of embroidery. I haven't.",[
  ["What was in the box?","He wouldn't say. A relic. A bone of someone holy, or a piece of someone's cloak. I'd rather not know."],
  ["Who'd take it?","Some thief with no soul, or a collector with too many. Or the cat."],
  ["Will you help look?","I've looked through every pocket in the shop. I found two buttons and a bus ticket."]],
 "If it turns up, it'll be in the last coat you check."],
19:["(Juniper is sewing a button on a red cap.) The Party's asking for comrades today. A young man in a red scarf came in to ask whether I had any more red. I said not for a Party, but I'd do it for a pillar-box.",[
  ["Do the Party want your clothes?","They want everyone's. But they pay for nothing, and they never return anything."],
  ["Have you heard about the Midnight Note?","Yes. The royals going incognito. A nightclub in Eastbrook, think of it. Someone must've asked me for a wig."],
  ["Did someone buy a disguise?","A woman bought a long coat and a very silly hat. I never ask. A shop is a confessional with better lighting."]],
 "If you're going out in disguise, stay off the dance floor."],
20:["(Juniper is looking at a coat with a stain on it.) There's talk of a man at the Hotel Aurora. A stranger, they say, in a good hat and a bad mood. People talk of nothing else, and nobody's seen him.",[
  ["Do you know who he is?","I haven't the foggiest. I only know the hat, because someone asked me to match it."],
  ["Is the Democratic Party involved?","Erik came in for a new tie, looking worried. I sold him one in green. He bought it without looking."],
  ["Why the worry?","Something's happening. I smell it in the shop, like damp wool before rain."]],
 "Don't go to the Aurora after dark. The corridors are cold."],
21:["(Juniper is brushing grey flakes off a window display.) The mountain's blown, and everything's ash. It's on the coats, the hats, even the mannequins. They look like ghosts in a very good tailoring.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Alive and sweeping. Pirkko rang. She's fine. Father Thyme's in the chapel with all the lamps lit."],
  ["Did you see it?","A column of smoke the size of the sky. Brown, then black. I closed the shop and said the Lord's Prayer standing up."],
  ["Is the shop ruined?","Everything's dusty. I'll wash it. Wool forgives a good beating."]],
 "Put something over your face. And don't rub your eyes."],
22:["(Juniper is shaking out a rug.) Every cardigan in the shop's been bought for the ash. People want scarves for their faces, and hats. I've never sold so many hats. I could've sold a very small one to a pigeon.",[
  ["Anything else going on in town?","A royal party's buying something nice for Princess Daphne, they say. And the cola lot have trouble at a farm."],
  ["What kind of trouble?","I don't ask. Farms and cola, no good comes of that mix."],
  ["Have you been busy?","Busy, grey and tired. And everyone wants a size they don't fit."]],
 "Tell your editor the ash is very hard on linen."],
23:["(Juniper is sitting very still, with her hand on a bolt of cloth.) Every time it shakes, I count. Ten seconds, then twelve. Some small tremor has been rolling under the floor all morning.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","A little. But I've faith, and a good needle."],
  ["Is the Church holding up?","Father Thyme's at the monastery, with a candle in each window. Brother Mikael's telling everyone to be brave."],
  ["What helps?","Work. A good seam never shakes, even if the floor does."]],
 "Keep busy. Idle hands tremble worst."],
24:["(Juniper is packing a small bag.) The Church is going on a retreat to the peaks today. Pirkko's sending me a sandwich for the journey. I'm too old for mountains, but I've mended five cassocks for the occasion.",[
  ["Are you going on the retreat?","I'll hold the shop, and pray from here. Someone's got to stay and make sure the hems hold."],
  ["Why the peaks?","Away from the ash, and nearer to God, they say. He's said to be a good listener at altitude."],
  ["Is Father Thyme leading?","He's walking at the front, on a stick. I gave him a better pair of socks."]],
 "Go gently. God keep the road."],
25:["(Juniper is pinning a small silk veil.) The royals are talking about a crown for everyone, and the Democrats a princess. I keep thinking, they'll all need something to wear.",[
  ["Is that why you're sewing a veil?","Somebody asked me. I don't know who, or why. I charged them double and prayed afterwards."],
  ["What do you think of Princess Daphne?","I've never met her, but I hear she's bright. Somebody should say what she wants for once."],
  ["Is the shop doing well?","The ash has been very good for business, in an awful way."]],
 "If you meet a princess, tell her the shop does alterations."],
26:["(Juniper is in the doorway, watching the street.) Dario's said to be buying a spa. Dario! A man like that in a spa. I'd sew the towels myself if he asked.",[
  ["Would you work for the oil man?","I'd work for anyone who pays on time. But I'd pray for him after."],
  ["Are Kelp Cola putting princes on bottles?","So I hear. A prince in every bottle. I'd like to see the ones who'd drink them."],
  ["Is anything ever simple on this island?","A hem. A hem is simple. Everything else is stitching."]],
 "Don't let them sell you a bottle with a prince in it. Buy a coat."],
27:["(Juniper is taking in a trouser leg, mouth full of pins.) There's a revolution planned at the pictures, I'm told, and cards at the Midnight Note. I'm a quiet person. I'd be happy with a nice bit of tweed.",[
  ["Would you go to the pictures?","If it was a nice film. A musical, perhaps. They always dress so well."],
  ["What do you think the Party will do?","They'll wave a flag, shout a bit, and go for tea. It's what everyone does."],
  ["What do you want?","A year with no ash, a cold that never turns to anything, and a quiet Sunday."]],
 "Don't catch a chill. It'd be a shame to ruin that collar."],
28:["(Juniper is wearing her best brooch.) It's a big day for everyone, I hear. State visits, flags, cola abroad, oil in offices. I've decided to wear my green. It's the colour of hope and moss.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","Only about my own side. The Church. The prince. But I've asked God, and He's rather busy."],
  ["What do you want from today?","That nobody gets hurt. That the right prince is honoured. And that the buttons stay on."],
  ["What would you say to Prince Augustus?",["That he's a good young man, and a good young man shouldn't have to wear such a heavy crown. Though I'd line it.",[
    ["Would you make him a coat?","A grey one. Plain, warm, with a collar he can turn up when he's tired of everybody."],
    ["Is faith enough to rule?","It isn't enough. But it helps. And a proper pair of boots."]]]]],
 "God keep the day, and keep your hem straight."],
29:["(Juniper is rubbing her eyes.) There's talk of primaries and lines of succession. I've never understood politics. I understand that tomorrow's the last day, and I'd like a day of peace after it.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","I don't know. I know who I'm praying for, and I'll leave the rest to the Lord."],
  ["Will you go to church tomorrow?","First thing. And again in the evening, if I'm able."],
  ["Is the shop open tomorrow?","Always. Even at the end of the world, someone needs a button."]],
 "Get some sleep. You'll need your wits tomorrow."],
30:["(Juniper is standing at the shop door, key in hand.) The last day. I've polished the counter twice. Everything feels like it should look its best.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Calm. Nervous. Both. The way you feel when you've finished a hem and don't know if it'll hang straight."],
  ["What have you learned this month?","That people will buy anything if you say it's practical. And that the island's softer than it looks."],
  ["Any message for London?","That we're well-dressed, and we'll manage. In wool."]],
 "Thank you for coming in. Come again. I'll have your coat ready."],
};
// Kaisa, DJ on Radio Sarivalto at the Transmitter Hut (The Entertainers). Threads: her warm voice, requests and dedications, borrowing Ruby's records, Hekki next door fixing the transmitter, the apartment block, the ash and the volcano.
DAILY['Kaisa']={
1:["(Kaisa slides the headphones off one ear.) Radio Sarivalto, live and slightly out of tune. I'm Kaisa. You're the reporter, the whole island's heard. Don't touch the red button.",[
  ["Pleased to meet you. Nice studio.","It's a hut with a microphone. I make it sound grand."],
  ["What do you play?","Slow songs, mostly. And gentle chat. People like to be talked to at night."],
  ["Who listens?","Everyone. Communists, monks, oil men. It's the only thing we agree on."]],
 "Mind the cables."],
2:["(Kaisa spins a record.) Palace road's open. I played a march for the king this morning, the one he liked. It crackled. I said it was the sea.",[
  ["Did people write in?","Fifty letters. Forty-nine said thank you. One said the march was too fast."],
  ["Was it?","It was. But you don't tell a king's favourite march it's too fast."],
  ["What will you play next?","Something for the island. I'm still choosing."]],
 "Stay tuned."],
3:["(Kaisa grins.) Kelp Cola's sponsoring a jingle. They want a bubbly voice. I said I've a warm voice. They said same thing, but with bubbles.",[
  ["Are you doing the jingle?","I am. For the fee. I've always wanted a decent kettle."],
  ["What does it say?","'Kelp, Kelp, hurray!' It's very modern."],
  ["Do you like the cola?","It tastes like sherbet. Just the right amount of regret."]],
 "Keep it fizzy."],
4:["(Kaisa leans on the desk.) Eastbrook's open, so Ruby's coming over with records. She lends me half her shop and gets back a third. We have an arrangement.",[
  ["Is Ruby generous?","She's generous and furious. A rare combination."],
  ["What does she lend you?","Jazz, mostly. Coltrane. I play him after midnight."],
  ["Does anyone like jazz?","The lighthouse keeper. And the cat."]],
 "Return her records."],
5:["(Kaisa sips tea.) Monastery's open. Father Thyme sent a hymn request. Very polite. 'Something slow, but not sad.' I played a lullaby and he sent a thank-you.",[
  ["Do monks have requests?","Always. The silent ones write the most."],
  ["Is it hard to please everyone?","It's impossible. So I please nobody equally."],
  ["Do you go up there?","Once a year. The bread's marvellous."]],
 "Don't fall asleep on air."],
6:["(Kaisa lowers her voice.) A man rang the station, asking for you by name. I said I'd pass it on. Is it you? He didn't leave one.",[
  ["Who was he?","Soft voice. Said a friend wished to meet you. He asked for a song, too."],
  ["What song?","'Strangers in the Night.' I thought that was a bit much."],
  ["Did you play it?","Of course. It was a request."]],
 "Be careful, dear."],
7:["(Kaisa smiles.) Frostholm now has a road, and a very rich man in a hotel keeps sending requests for Russian songs. I don't have many. He sends the records himself.",[
  ["Yuri?","Is that his name? A charming voice. A lot of vodka in his letters."],
  ["Do you play them?","In small doses. One a night. The monks pretend not to notice."],
  ["What are they like?","Slow. Sad. Beautiful. Like most things from the east."]],
 "Play something for him."],
8:["(Kaisa pauses between records.) Sunday. Church bells, then the radio. I play a hymn first, then something rather daring. Father Thyme can't find his words, they say. I could play him some.",[
  ["Could you?","A quiet violin. It says everything and nothing."],
  ["Does the Church object to your music?","They write letters. Strongly worded and wonderfully handwritten."],
  ["Is Hekki around?","He's in the next shack, fixing my transmitter and muttering. He's very sweet and very jumpy."]],
 "Keep it gentle."],
9:["(Kaisa holds up two bottles.) Two colas, two sponsors. Kelp's new and the Party's People's. I've been asked to do ads for both. I said I'm an artist, not a bottle.",[
  ["Will you?","For double the fee."],
  ["What's the Party's ad like?","'Drink for the people!' With a trumpet."],
  ["Which tastes better?","They taste like arguments."]],
 "Pour carefully."],
10:["(Kaisa's voice is very soft.) Today I read the funeral names on air. The whole island listened. I could hear the quiet between the words. That's a strange thing to be good at.",[
  ["That must have been hard.","A little. I had a glass of water. Then I played something slow."],
  ["What did you play?","The old king's march, but gently. No one wrote to say it was too fast."],
  ["What next?","More music. It's what I do when I don't know what to say."]],
 "Say it softly."],
11:["(Kaisa twirls a pencil.) The mill hands want fair pay. I played them a workers' song, and Helen sent a thank-you. Also a request for 'something with more drums'.",[
  ["Did you play it?","I've no drums. I banged a saucepan."],
  ["Did it work?","Helen said it was inspiring. Hekki said it was out of tune."],
  ["What's the mood?","Hopeful, a bit cross."]],
 "Bang a saucepan."],
12:["(Kaisa leans forward.) A record for Ruby, the Democrats said. They asked me to announce it, then cancelled. Something about it being 'delicate'. I've been told nothing, which is a first.",[
  ["Do you know what record?","I've a hunch. An old local song. A rare thing."],
  ["Would you play it?","If I had it. I'd play it once, then guard it."],
  ["Is the oil lot interested?","They're always interested in what isn't for sale."]],
 "Keep it delicate."],
13:["(Kaisa sips from a bottle.) Free samples, and a rumour about a secret recipe. I made a joke about it on air. Three people ran to the station to complain. One brought biscuits.",[
  ["What was the joke?","'It's so secret even the cola doesn't know.'"],
  ["Who complained?","The Syndicate, I think. Politely."],
  ["Did the biscuits help?","They helped a lot."]],
 "Take the biscuits."],
14:["(Kaisa is very calm.) Tomorrow's the big day, the fourteenth. I'll be on air, all day. I've lined up songs for everything. Joy, nerves, ceremony, and one for just in case.",[
  ["What's the one for just in case?","A very slow waltz. It works every time."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A little. Radio is a bit like standing in a very quiet church."],
  ["What do you expect?","The island all in one place. And very loud."]],
 "Warm up your voice."],
15:["(Kaisa hums.) Crown or cross. I've had requests for both. One for a march, one for a chorale. I played them back to back and nobody noticed they matched.",[
  ["Who requested what?","The palace asked for the march. Father Thyme asked for the chorale."],
  ["Do they match?","Almost note for note. It's the oldest joke on the island."],
  ["Will you tell them?","Never. It's the radio's secret."]],
 "Play fair."],
16:["(Kaisa adjusts her headphones.) The great debate is on air. I'm only the host between rounds. I play very quiet music while Helen and Erik argue. It's like putting a cushion on a fight.",[
  ["Is it a good fight?","They both love the island. They'd both like to fix it, and each other."],
  ["Who'll win?","The audience. They'll turn it off."],
  ["Do you vote?","I do. In secret. Even from myself."]],
 "Play a cushion."],
17:["(Kaisa grips the desk.) The microphone swung on its own. A shiver in the hut, then quiet. I was in the middle of a lullaby. I finished it, rather quickly.",[
  ["Was it a tremor?","Hekki says yes. He's pale about it."],
  ["Is the volcano waking?","Old women say so. I say the island's hiccuping."],
  ["Did listeners notice?","Three rang. One asked if I'd done it on purpose."]],
 "Keep singing."],
18:["(Kaisa looks out of the hut.) Steam over the mountain. I played something airy and light. People have been ringing in about it. A child asked if the mountain was cooking.",[
  ["What did you say?","'Yes, but it's not ready yet.'"],
  ["Is it a good answer?","The child seemed satisfied."],
  ["Heard about the Church's missing relic?","Father Thyme sent a very discreet request. 'A song for lost things.'"]],
 "Play it softly."],
19:["(Kaisa smiles.) There's a night at the Midnight Note, someone incognito. Leo and Rita are playing something special. I'm broadcasting from there, at midnight. Everyone's very hush-hush.",[
  ["Who's the incognito guest?","I'm not allowed to say. But they asked for my favourite song."],
  ["What is it?","A slow one. Very moonlit."],
  ["Are the Communists recruiting?","On air, yes. I read the notice. I added a little sigh."]],
 "Sigh nicely."],
20:["(Kaisa glances at the door.) A request from the palace, odd and urgent. 'Play something loud at nine.' I did. Then a man at the Hotel Aurora asked for something quiet at the same time.",[
  ["Did you do both?","In sequence. Loud, then quiet. A lovely rhythm."],
  ["Is something happening?","A lot, I think. I'm not supposed to notice."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a radio. I only notice what's tuned in."]],
 "Stay tuned."],
21:["(Kaisa's voice is steady, though her hands tremble.) I was on air when it happened. I said, 'Everyone stay calm,' and then the transmitter shook. I kept talking. It was the only thing I could think to do.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Everyone's safe, I think. I've had four hundred calls. Nobody's hurt."],
  ["What did you play?","Something warm. A lullaby. The whole island listened."],
  ["Are you all right?","I will be. Give me an hour, and a tea."]],
 "Breathe slowly."],
22:["(Kaisa dusts ash off the console.) Ash on the dials, ash on the discs. I've been playing everything with a soft cloth. The records sound a bit crunchy, but it's comforting.",[
  ["Is the transmitter working?","Hekki's up the tower with a brush. He's doing a heroic job and sneezing."],
  ["What are people requesting?","Cheerful songs. And one for a cow."],
  ["Did you play it?","Of course. For the cow."]],
 "Play it for the cow."],
23:["(Kaisa whispers.) A spanner in the works, I hear. Someone's gummed up a machine on the coast. And the oil lot are announcing deep repairs. I read the notice in my most reassuring voice.",[
  ["Did it work?","Folk said they felt better. Then they checked the news."],
  ["Is it sabotage?","Possibly. I only play the music."],
  ["Is the Party upset?","Helen's upset. She asked for something fierce."]],
 "Read it kindly."],
24:["(Kaisa looks out the window.) Monks heading for the peaks, crews sweeping the town. I've played a pilgrim's hymn, and a cleaning song. Both are very popular.",[
  ["What's a cleaning song?","I made it up. It's mostly humming and a washboard."],
  ["Did people like it?","A woman on Eastbrook Street cried. In a good way."],
  ["Are the monks listening?","On a battery radio, in the snow. They sent a card."]],
 "Hum along."],
25:["(Kaisa smiles.) A princess for democracy, a crown for everyone. Everyone's calling in with opinions. I've a pile of letters taller than the microphone.",[
  ["What do they say?","Half say yes, half say no, and one says 'what's for dinner?'"],
  ["Which do you read first?","The dinner one."],
  ["Do you have a view?","I have a song. It's neutral. It's a waltz."]],
 "Play the waltz."],
26:["(Kaisa chuckles.) Kelp wants me to read an ad: 'A Prince in Every Bottle!' with a fanfare. I said, 'I'm a radio, not a trumpet.' They sent a trumpet.",[
  ["Did you use it?","Once. Hekki dropped it. It sounded better broken."],
  ["And Dario's spa?","A rumour. I read it as a joke. Magnus rang to say it wasn't."],
  ["What's on the playlist today?","Something steamy. Within reason."]],
 "Keep it clean."],
27:["(Kaisa readies a record.) A revolution at the pictures, and a high stakes game in a cellar. I've been asked to play a drum roll for each. I only have one drum roll.",[
  ["Which gets it?","The pictures. The cellar can have a quiet cymbal."],
  ["Is the cellar dangerous?","Anything that needs a drum roll probably is."],
  ["Will you go to the film?","I might. With a hanky."]],
 "Share the drum roll."],
28:["(Kaisa's headphones are crooked.) The day of the big pushes. Every faction wants a spot on air. I've scheduled five-minute slots. It's like a very political quiz show.",[
  ["Who's first?","The Party. They booked early, with a song."],
  ["Who's last?","Diversified. They paid for the final slot. In envelopes."],
  ["Is it tiring?","It's glorious. I've never been so heard."]],
 "Keep to time."],
29:["(Kaisa leans back, exhausted.) One more day. The island's finally gone quiet, between songs. I've played everything I own, and some of Ruby's.",[
  ["What will you play tomorrow?","Whatever the island needs. I'll know when I hear it."],
  ["Are you tired?","Like a tuning fork. Still ringing."],
  ["Any last hopes?","That everyone goes home safe, and nobody's hurt."]],
 "Hum something soft."],
30:["(Kaisa smiles into the microphone, though it's off.) The last day. Whatever happens, I'll be here, with a slow song and a warm word. That's all the radio has ever been.",[
  ["Thank you, Kaisa.","Thank you. For listening. It's the best thing anyone can do."],
  ["Nervous?","A little. But the microphone's a comfort."],
  ["Any last song?","A lullaby. For the island. It's earned it."]],
 "Goodnight, wherever you are."],
};
// Kate, fishmonger at the fish processing plant in Saltwick (Communist Party). Threads: her gossip and her unwritten ledger of
// everybody's debts, standing up to Lucia and the Syndicate's little book, teaching Sirpa to gut so the plant takes her on, and
// finding out who Mike's sweet on (she never does).
DAILY['Kate']={
1:["(Kate slaps a cod onto the scales.) Look who's come in from the cold! The London reporter. Hannu at the newsstand saw you first and told Olga, and Olga told me.",[
  ["News travels fast here.","In Saltwick it doesn't travel, love. It's just everywhere at once, like the smell of fish."],
  ["What's today's gossip?","Tom's lent his boat to three people at once and none of them have brought it back. And Sam's said eleven words this week. That's a lot, for Sam."],
  ["You've got scales on your sleeve.","I've always got scales on my sleeve. I've had them at weddings. Anna says they're a badge of the working class."]],
 "Go on, you're letting the warm out."],
2:["The Kingsgrad road's open, and half of Saltwick's gone shopping. Who do they think's going to buy their fish? The king's not hungry.",[
  ["Do you ever go to Kingsgrad?","Once a year, for shoes. Grace the tailor once tried to sell me a hat. A hat! I've got a head for headscarves."],
  ["That's a bit grim, about the king.","He's past caring, love. And he'd have laughed. He liked a fishwife. He bought smoked herring off my mother once."],
  ["Can I buy a fish?","You can buy the plaice. It's been looking at me all morning, and I want it gone."]],
 "Wrap it in the Gazette. Best use for it."],
3:["I've got a project. Sirpa from the block's been out of work two years, so I'm teaching her to gut. Then the plant takes her on.",[
  ["Is she any good?","She's terrible. She gutted a herring yesterday and it looked surprised. But she's keen, and keen's most of it."],
  ["Will the plant hire her?","If I say so loudly enough. I've been saying things loudly to this plant for twenty years."],
  ["Why help her?","Because nobody helped me, and I'd like it to stop being that way. That's the whole Party in a sentence, love."]],
 "If you see Sirpa, tell her: thumbs out of the way."],
4:["Do you know what the fishermen in this harbour owe? I do. Kate's ledger: never written down, never forgotten.",[
  ["Who owes the most?","I'd never say. (She leans in.) Not Sam. Sam hasn't owed anybody a penny in forty years. It makes the rest of them look bad."],
  ["Who do they owe?","Ray at the bar, Helmi for the oilskins, and the Syndicate's little book. That last one's the trouble."],
  ["You should be a banker.","I'd be a good one. I'd charge nothing and shout a lot. They'd close me down in a week."]],
 "Off you go, love. Don't owe anybody anything."],
5:["Bob's in a mood. He tried a new marinade and nobody noticed. I said it was lovely, and he knew I was lying.",[
  ["You work beside Bob?","Twenty years at the next bench. I know his knife by the sound of it. He hums when he's happy. He's not humming."],
  ["What was the marinade?","Dill and something brown. He says juniper. I say a mistake."],
  ["Why isn't Bob in the Party?","Ask him. Somebody does at every meeting, and he just brings more soup. Clever man. Infuriating, but clever."]],
 "Hum something on your way past him. It might catch."],
6:["That Syndicate woman came round the plant with her little book, and I told her where to put it. Everybody's looking at me like I'm on fire.",[
  ["Weren't you scared?",["Not at the time. Afterwards, at home, I put a chair under the door handle. Don't you tell anybody that.",[
    ["Is she dangerous?","She smiles when she writes your name down. Nobody smiles at a notebook unless it's bad news."],
    ["Should you tell the police?","The police are in Kingsgrad. The Syndicate's on my doorstep. Do the sums, love."]]]],
  ["What did you tell her?","I can't repeat it to a reporter. It involved her book and a herring barrel."],
  ["Does Lucia live in Saltwick?","In the block, with the rest of them. And nobody knows her business. Funny, that, when I know everybody's."]],
 "If she asks, you've never met me."],
7:["Sirpa gutted twelve herring today and only one looked surprised. Progress! And Helen's handing out oranges from the Russian.",[
  ["Which Russian?","Yuri, at the Hotel Aurora in Frostholm. He buys the Party oranges and vodka and asks Anna a lot of questions. I like the oranges. The jury's out on him."],
  ["Is twelve herring good?","Twelve's a start. I do three hundred before lunch. But we all start at twelve."],
  ["Are the oranges good?","Juicy. I ate mine at the bench and got pips in the cod. Bob found one and looked at me like I'd shot his dog."]],
 "Have a segment. The workers insist."],
8:["Sunday, and I'm in to do the books. Has anybody told you about the rig? The fishermen are saying the water's gone black past the point.",[
  ["Is it oil?","Mike says it smells like a garage out there. Mike's got a nose like a bloodhound, so I believe him."],
  ["Why work on a Sunday?","The plant does its books on Sundays, and I'm the only one who can add up without a pencil."],
  ["Do you go to church?","My mother did. I go to Party meetings. Same thing: long, cold, and a biscuit at the end."]],
 "If you go out on a boat, hold your nose past the point."],
9:["Helen's making a cola! The Party's very own. She asked me for a flavour and I said 'herring'. She's not speaking to me.",[
  ["Was that serious?","Half serious. I'd drink it. I'd be the only one, but I'd drink it."],
  ["What would you really suggest?","Something hot. Something that makes your eyes water in a good way, like a Saltwick winter in a bottle."],
  ["A Communist cola could work.","Anything works with Helen behind it. She once stopped the harbour for nine days over a tea break."]],
 "If you think of a flavour, don't say herring. It's taken."],
10:["Funeral day. I've tied a black ribbon on my scales. The old king bought a smoked herring off my mother in 1959. It's the least I can do.",[
  ["Are you going to the funeral?","To Kingsgrad, in my good shoes, to stand at the back while the family weep? No, love. My ribbon's going instead."],
  ["Is Anna going?","In her mother's funeral coat. She'll stand there and not bow, and everybody will notice. That's the point."],
  ["What did your mother say about him?","That he had soft hands and a kind face, and paid in coins, not notes, which was considerate."]],
 "Spare a thought for the old man. He liked his herring."],
11:["Sirpa came round for another lesson last night. I've asked the manager to give her a trial, and he said 'after the month's out'. Everything's after the month's out on this island.",[
  ["Is that a no?","It's a 'not yet'. I've been turning 'not yet' into 'yes' at this plant for twenty years."],
  ["How's she doing?","She cried at her first cod, and then gutted it beautifully. Crying's allowed. Bob cries at onions."],
  ["What's Helen up to?","Off to sort out the mill hands in Eastbrook. She says it's for the Union. It's because she can't stand a saw without a guard."]],
 "If you see the manager, tell him Sirpa's ready."],
12:["Gossip from Eastbrook: Ruby at the record shop has sold some rare record for a fortune, and nobody knows who to. I know everything, and I don't know that. It's killing me.",[
  ["Why does it matter?","It doesn't. That's why it's killing me. The important things I find out easily. It's the silly ones that hide."],
  ["Who do you think bought it?","A monk, a millionaire or a madman. On this island, possibly all three."],
  ["Do you like music?","I like the Internationale, and Mike singing it on his boat. Everything else is noise with a haircut."]],
 "If you find out who bought it, come straight here. There's a free plaice in it."],
13:["(Kate is wrapping a sack in brown paper.) Every bottle of free Kelp Cola in the plant, going back to Phil. Return to sender. With a note.",[
  ["What does the note say?","'Dear Phil. We pay for what we drink. Love, the fish plant.' Bob put in the 'love'. I wanted something else."],
  ["Isn't that a bit petty?","Of course it's petty! Petty's all a fishwife's got against a cola company, and I'm very good at it."],
  ["How's the People's Cola?","Helen won't say a word about it. When Helen won't say a word, something's going on."]],
 "Help me carry this to the post? No? Coward."],
14:["The big day! Everybody in one place! I've so much gossip saved up I might burst.",[
  ["Who are you hoping to see?","Everybody. The princess, to see if her hair's real. Dario, to see if he's real. And Gustavo, to see if he ever blinks."],
  ["Will you behave?","I'll behave like a lady from Saltwick. Which is to say, not much."],
  ["What do you hope comes of it?","That the people who do the work get noticed for once. And a sandwich. Bob's making them."]],
 "Find me afterwards. I'll tell you everything I saw."],
15:["Sirpa's doing well, but somebody's been at her. The Syndicate's after her for a debt from last winter. Coal money.",[
  ["How much?","Not much, to you or me. To Sirpa it's three weeks' wages, and Lucia's got it in her book."],
  ["Can you help?","I've given her my spare from the jar. Don't tell Bob: he'd give his too, Helen would find out, and then there'd be a meeting."],
  ["Does everyone owe the Syndicate?","Everybody who was cold last winter. That's how they do it: they wait for the cold, then they offer coal."]],
 "Keep it to yourself. Sirpa's proud."],
16:["(Kate has a tin whistle in her apron pocket.) Debate day! Front row. Bob says no whistle. I say Erik's not getting away with one wrong fact.",[
  ["What counts as a wrong fact?","Anything Erik says about fishing. He's never been out in a boat. He gets seasick on the tram."],
  ["Will you heckle?","Heckle? Me? I shall make constructive contributions from the floor. Loudly."],
  ["Is the whistle necessary?","Probably not. But I like to be prepared. My mother took a rolling pin to the 1938 strike."]],
 "Front row. Look for the whistle."],
17:["Did you feel it? Everything in the plant jumped. I caught two knives out of the air. Bob says I should join a circus.",[
  ["Were you frightened?","Afterwards. During, I was too busy catching knives."],
  ["Did you use the whistle?","Once! Only once. The whole hall turned round, and Anna gave me a look that'd freeze the harbour. Worth it."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","My granny said the mountain wakes every hundred years and wants a sacrifice. I've offered it the plant manager."]],
 "If the floor goes again, stand near me. I catch things."],
18:["Here's a funny thing. Mike's left the harbour early three days running, and his boat's still tied up. Where's a fisherman going at four in the afternoon?",[
  ["Maybe he's tired.","Mike? He sings the Internationale at five in the morning. He's never tired. He's up to something."],
  ["Is it your business?","Everything in Saltwick is my business. That's why I'm so good at it."],
  ["Maybe he's in love.",["(She stops mid-fillet.) Mike? In love? With who? Everybody he knows, I know.",[
    ["Not everybody, it seems.","Don't you start. I've got a list. I'll go through it tonight."],
    ["Just a guess.","Well, it's a good one, and I hate it. Now I shan't sleep."]]]]],
 "Not a word to Mike. I'll find out myself."],
19:["(Kate's clipboard is already shining with scales.) Recruiting day! I've signed up two at the plant, one at the bar, and Ray's dog, if dogs count.",[
  ["Do dogs count?","Anna says no. I say he's a working dog. He guards the bins. That's labour."],
  ["How do you persuade people?","I stand very close and smile until they sign. It's not technically a threat."],
  ["What about Sirpa?","First on my list. She says she'll sign when she's got a job. I say sign, and the job will follow. We're at an impasse."]],
 "Sign up, love? Go on. No? I'll get you yet."],
20:["Nora from the Gazette came round asking about the Russian at the Hotel Aurora. I told her a long story about Sam's mermaid wife. She wrote it all down.",[
  ["Sam had a mermaid wife?","Of course not. But Nora's a Democrat, and she's not getting Saltwick's real business for nothing."],
  ["What does she want with Yuri?","Erik's lot think he's a spy. A spy who sends oranges? Spies don't send oranges. They send... I don't know what spies send."],
  ["Will Sam mind?","Sam's said nothing for forty years. He's not going to start over a mermaid."]],
 "If it's in the Gazette, buy two copies. One for Sam."],
21:["(Kate has a headscarf tied over her mouth.) The mountain's blown! Ash in the fish, ash in my hair, ash in places I won't mention to a reporter.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine, love. Frightened half to death, but fine. My house shook like a dog shaking off water."],
  ["Has anyone found Sirpa?","Helen found her on the beach with Tiina and Pat, staring at the sky. I gave her an earful and a cup of tea."],
  ["What happens to the plant?","Shut till they've cleaned the tanks. No fish, no wages. But Helen's got a tin for that."]],
 "Shut the door, quick. The ash wants in."],
22:["I've been shovelling ash off Sam's step. He's seventy-odd. He told me to go away, I said 'shan't', and he made me a cup of tea. So we're even.",[
  ["Is Sam all right?","He's seen the sea do everything, but not this. He keeps looking north, as if the mountain owes him money."],
  ["Doesn't he want help?","Nobody in Saltwick wants help. They want you to do it while they pretend not to notice."],
  ["How was the tea?","Ash. Everything tastes of ash this week. I expect kisses do too, not that I've had the chance to check."]],
 "Grab a shovel if you're passing Sam's. He won't say thank you, but he'll mean it."],
23:["Small good news in a bad week: the plant manager's agreed to see Sirpa in October. Not a job. A meeting. For Sirpa, that's a mountain climbed.",[
  ["Congratulations to her.","And to me! I taught her to hold a knife. She held it backwards for a week."],
  ["Does she still owe the Syndicate?","A little. I've told her: the day she's paid up, she buys a new lock anyway."],
  ["Is the plant open again?","Since this morning. The fish smell of oil, but they're fish. We cut the bad bits off."]],
 "Tell Sirpa well done if you see her. She'll pretend not to care."],
24:["I reminded Bob it's six years since he moved in with Helen. He's made a cake. He'd forgotten. Men!",[
  ["How did you know?","I was there when he carried his boxes up the stairs. It was raining, and he'd forgotten the cutlery. You don't forget a thing like that."],
  ["Did Helen remember?","Helen remembers every wage rate since 1960 and not one birthday. They suit each other."],
  ["You should write it all down.","And lose my power? Never. It's all in here, love. (She taps her head, and leaves a scale behind.)"]],
 "Not a word to Helen that I told you."],
25:["I've worked it out. Mike's in love. I don't know who with yet, but he's bought a new shirt, and Mike hasn't bought a shirt since 1974.",[
  ["Have you asked him?","He went red and started singing. The whole Internationale, every verse. That's a confession, in my book."],
  ["Maybe she's from Eastbrook.","A Democrat? Oh, that'd be beautiful. Anna would have kittens."],
  ["Leave the poor man alone.","Never. Gossip's like fish: it has to be gutted fresh."]],
 "If you see Mike with anybody, you come and tell me first."],
26:["The big push is on the twenty-eighth, and the Party's got me painting banners. I can gut a cod in four seconds. I can't paint a straight line.",[
  ["What does the banner say?","'Land to the workers.' Or 'Lard to the workers', the way I've painted it. Bob says lard's useful too."],
  ["Are you excited?","Excited and sick at once, like before a dance. I haven't been to a dance since 1971."],
  ["Can I see it?","Absolutely not. It's a secret till it goes down the street. Then everybody can laugh at the D."]],
 "If it rains, the lard runs. Pray for dry weather."],
27:["Anna's sending us all to the pictures in Kingsgrad, for some Russian film. Sirpa's never been to the cinema. She's had her hair set.",[
  ["Never been to the cinema?","Not once in twenty-six years. I told her it's about tractors. She said she doesn't care if it's about turnips."],
  ["Will you enjoy it?","I'll enjoy Sirpa enjoying it. That's the best kind."],
  ["What would you rather see?","Anything with a handsome man in it. Russian films don't do handsome. They do sturdy."]],
 "Save me a seat if you're going. Front row, as always."],
28:["Today's the day! Banners, flags, everybody out. Every faction on the island's doing its big thing today, and we're doing ours bigger.",[
  ["What's the Party doing?","I'd tell you, love, but Anna said 'not the reporter, Kate', very slowly, as if I were simple."],
  ["Is your banner ready?","'Land to the workers', with a very small D squeezed in. You'd hardly notice. You'd notice."],
  ["Can the Party win?","We've more people than anybody and less money than anybody. It's a fair fight, for once."]],
 "Wave if you see the banner. Wave at the D."],
29:["I still don't know who Mike's seeing. Twenty-odd days I've been at it. It's my greatest failure.",[
  ["Maybe let him keep his secret.",["(Kate sighs.) Maybe. Everybody should have one. I just don't know what mine is.",[
    ["You haven't got a secret?","I had one in 1971, at a dance. He was from Frostholm. It lasted a week. That's all you're getting."],
    ["Everyone knows all yours?","Everybody knows everything about me, love. That's the price of knowing everything about them."]]]],
  ["Who's your money on?","Kaisa from the radio; she plays his songs. Or Olga; she keeps him the good herring. One of them. Surely."],
  ["Big day tomorrow.","The last one. Whatever happens, I'll be at the bench at six. Fish don't care who's in charge."]],
 "Tomorrow, love. Wear your scarf."],
30:["The last day. I've been up since four. Couldn't sleep, so I gutted a fish at home to calm down. It didn't help the fish.",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","That the people who catch the fish own the boats. If not tomorrow, then some day while I'm still loud."],
  ["What will you tell your grandchildren?","I haven't got any. I'll tell Sirpa's. I've decided I'm their aunt."],
  ["Any last gossip?","Plenty. But I'm saving it. If the world's still here tomorrow, I'll need something to talk about."]],
 "Whatever happens, love, it's been a good month for gossip."],
};
// Knut, sawyer at the Eastbrook lumber mill, missing a fingertip (Democratic Party, the Union). Threads: the many stories of his finger,
// the big saw and its danger, pay and Dagny, the Timberline Tavern and Linnea, Ilse as his workmate and rival, Helen and Erik.
DAILY['Knut']={
4:["(A broad man in a yellow hard hat shuts off the saw and holds up his left hand.) See that? Nine and a half. A bear took it. Ah, you're the reporter. Mind the sawdust.",[
  ["A bear? Really?","A big one, in the forest, in '68. (He grins.) Well, that's one version. I've got six. Which d'you like best?"],
  ["Is the saw dangerous?","It's the most dangerous thing in Eastbrook, apart from my sense of humour. It's been taking fingers off the crew since before I came."],
  ["What's the mill like to work in?","Loud, dusty and good. There's no feeling like a clean board coming off the blade."]],
 "Don't stand behind the saw. It bites."],
5:["(Knut wipes his hands on a rag.) You're back. The snow's off the road, and the logs are coming in. Ilse's been loading since dawn. She's a monster. A very pleasant monster.",[
  ["Is Ilse your friend?","Friend, rival and conscience. We've an argument about whose job's harder. She says hers, because she lifts. I say mine, because I think."],
  ["Who's the boss here?","Dagny owns the place and Bjarne keeps the books, but the saw's mine. Nobody tells a sawyer how to saw. Except Dagny. And the saw."],
  ["Tell me another story about the finger.","Well, it was a dispute with an accordion. No. A bet over a pike. Wait, it'll come back to me."]],
 "I'll have the story by tomorrow. Or a different one."],
6:["(Knut is oiling the big blade.) You can hear it, can't you? It sings when it's happy. It's out of tune today. I think it's the cold in the teeth.",[
  ["You talk about it like a person.","It's been with me longer than my last two girlfriends. And it's kept its temper better."],
  ["Is it very old?","Older than Dagny. Older than the road. It came on a ship from Sweden in the twenties and complained all the way."],
  ["Has it injured many?","Eleven fingers, one boot and a lunchbox. It's a hungry saw. But it's fair."]],
 "Keep your hands where I can see them. It's not personal."],
7:["(Knut is sweeping up with great energy.) Frostholm's roads are open, and they'll want timber by the lorry-load. Dagny's rubbing her hands. She's got very dry hands from all that rubbing.",[
  ["Does Dagny pay well?","She pays. That's the best I can say. It's like asking if the weather's generous. It arrives, and it isn't much."],
  ["Are the men unhappy?","They're tired, which is cheaper than unhappy. Helen's been round. She says there's a better way."],
  ["Who's Helen?","The union. A big voice in a small flat in Saltwick. She came up here once, in a blizzard, to give a speech in the canteen. We all clapped. It was warm in there."]],
 "I'll tell you more when the saw's quiet. It's never quiet."],
8:["(Knut is gnawing a sandwich.) Sunday's the only day the mill's still. I came in anyway. I'd sooner be here than at home with the radio's sermon. I like a sermon, but not at that volume.",[
  ["Are you religious?","Tavern on Fridays. Church at Christmas. Between times I trust my hands. Nine and a half of them."],
  ["Is the tavern good?","It's the best on the island. Linnea pours a very fair pint, and she never counts how many. She counts the stories."],
  ["Did a bear really take the finger?","Not a bear. A branch. No: a raccoon. Oh, you've caught me. I forget."]],
 "Don't let me bore you. I've a habit of telling the same story twice."],
9:["(Knut is leaning on the log pile.) They say there's a new cola. I've had a taste. It's like the old one, but it's been to a better school.",[
  ["Do you drink a lot of cola?","A bottle at lunch. Two on a hot day. And one at the tavern, to dilute the vodka."],
  ["What do the lads think about the Communists' cola?","They think it'd be free. And they think it'd be red. And they think it'd be a bad idea, because they'd drink it by the barrel."],
  ["Is the mill getting orders from Kelp Cola?","Crates, by the thousand. It's the one company that's always buying. They're better than the palace."]],
 "The crates are good work. I'm proud of them."],
10:["(Knut is wearing a black armband over his work shirt, and his hard hat is off.) We stopped the saw for a minute at noon. Everyone stood there. It was the quietest I've ever heard it.",[
  ["Did you like the king?","He came here once, when I was a boy. He put his hand on a log and said, 'Good timber.' We talked about it for years."],
  ["What was it like, the silence?","Strange. You hear all the other things. Ilse breathing. A bird in the rafters. Somebody's boot. It was a lovely few minutes."],
  ["Is the mill closing for the funeral?","No. Dagny kept it open. She said the dead king wouldn't want to hold up the orders. It's the kindest cruel thing I've heard."]],
 "Rest in peace, Your Majesty. And you, too, reporter. Take it easy."],
11:["(Knut shakes your hand. It has a firm and slightly oily grip.) Helen said somebody might come by and hear us out. A reporter, no less. Good. I'll tell it straight.",[
  ["Tell me what's wrong at the mill.","Six years with no rise. A saw that takes a finger a year. And a boss who counts timber and not people. We want a fair day's pay."],
  ["What about the saw?",["It's the line. The guard's broken, the lever sticks and the emergency brake's a rag. Every one of us has a story.",[
    ["Including yours?","Mine's the worst, and I'll tell it straight for once: I was tired, the lever stuck, and there was no guard. No bear. I tell the bear because the truth makes the lads sad."],
    ["What do you want done?","A new guard and a lever that works. It's about forty marks of parts. She spends more than that on lunch."]]]],
  ["Do you expect Dagny to agree?","She's not a monster. She's tight. It's a different thing. If someone talks to her sensibly, she may bend. She might even blink."]],
 "Say a good word for us. We've a lot of fingers riding on it."],
12:["(Knut is mopping his face with a bandana.) The talk of the mill's still the wage. Ilse thinks the Communists should do something. I say the Communists should do their washing-up.",[
  ["Is Ilse a Democrat?","Through and through. She thinks the Reds talk too much and work too little. She says it every time one of them walks past."],
  ["Do you like the Democrats?","Erik's decent. He's got a coffee-stained manifesto and no idea about timber, but he listens."],
  ["What's the mood among the men?","Nervous. Hopeful. Like a dog that thinks it's been promised a bone."]],
 "Don't worry about us. We're tough. We're nearly all in one piece."],
13:["(Knut is peering at a printed notice.) There's a cola truck in the yard. Free samples. Dagny's furious. She says free anything's a form of theft.",[
  ["Did you take one?","Three. It was a very fair tasting. I did my duty as a customer."],
  ["Does Dagny dislike Kelp Cola?","She likes their orders and dislikes their charity. It makes her feel bought, she says. Which she is, but only when they pay on time."],
  ["Is Bjarne around?","He's in the office, hiding. He's good with numbers and poor with shouting. When Dagny shouts, he reads the ledger aloud, quietly, as a defence."]],
 "Have a bottle for the road. It's free, so it doesn't count."],
14:["(Knut is oiling a hinge for no clear reason.) Tomorrow, or today. The big day. I've never known an island so excited by a thing nobody's explained. I put on a clean shirt, to be safe.",[
  ["What do you expect?","A crowd. A great deal of talk. Some pushing. A little sausage. It's the island's way."],
  ["Are the lads going?","Every one. Dagny's let them off, to my surprise. She said she'd make it up on Saturday. She's already invoiced us."],
  ["What would you like to see?","A fair deal. A vote. A beer, afterwards. And my finger back. I've lost it, but I've a good story."]],
 "I'll save you a seat at the tavern, if there's one left."],
15:["(Knut has a bruise the shape of Norway on one forearm.) Yesterday was a scrum. I was shoved by a monk. I'm not sure it was an accident. He had a very heavy beard.",[
  ["Was it a good day?","Loud. It reminded me what it's like to be one of a lot of people who think they've a say. That's a rarity, here."],
  ["Did you meet anyone?","Half the island. Ruby from the record shop, Linnea, Dex with his sausages. I told Dex about the bear and he said: 'Which bear?'"],
  ["Back to work?","Back to the saw. It doesn't care who's in charge. It only cares who feeds it."]],
 "Come and see me again. I'll have a new version of the finger story."],
16:["(Knut is shaking sawdust out of his hat.) There's the Great Debate today. Ilse wants to go. I want to go, but only to heckle the Reds. We'll leave early, to make the whistle.",[
  ["Who'll win?","Erik, with the room. Anna, with the volume. And the audience'll win with the biscuits, which Veikko is supplying."],
  ["What's Ilse's view?","She says the Reds have never lifted a log. She says it with her whole forearm."],
  ["Are you worried about trouble?","We've been in worse at the tavern. At least here, no one throws a bottle. Not until after the third round."]],
 "I'll ask the first question. It'll be rude."],
17:["(Knut stops the saw and listens.) Did the whole floor go odd just now? The planks danced. I've had ground shifts before, but not in Eastbrook.",[
  ["It might be the volcano.","That's what the old men say. The north's been grumbling since the snow went. I said it was gas. Now I'm not sure."],
  ["Does it worry the lads?","Some. Jorma won't stand beneath the beams. Ilse says the beams are safer than he is."],
  ["Is the saw all right?","It hummed back. It didn't mind. It's a sturdy old thing. I'll say that for a Swedish saw."]],
 "If the roof falls in, remember I told you it's stable."],
18:["(Knut has a smear of grease on his forehead.) I've been up all night mending the lever. Not for the saw. For Ilse's truck. It stuck. She sulked, and I couldn't stand it.",[
  ["Are you two close?","As close as two people can be who argue every morning. She's the only one who'll tell me when I'm wrong. And she does."],
  ["Is it only friendship?","It's two people who both smell of sawdust. That's a kind of marriage, but without the paperwork."],
  ["Does she know?","She knows I'm daft. It's enough."]],
 "Don't print that. Or do. She'll never read your paper."],
19:["(Knut is leaning on the saw bench, listening.) I felt it again this morning. A small one. The board I was cutting jumped. It's the first time a plank's tried to dodge the blade.",[
  ["Is it getting worse?","It's getting more frequent. The old fellows at the tavern say it's a warning. They say a lot of things. Usually after the third drink."],
  ["Are you scared?","Of the volcano? A bit. Of the saw? Not at all. But a mountain that decides to move is another thing altogether."],
  ["What does Dagny say?","She asked if the tremors affect insurance. I said, I think they affect the mill. She said, 'Check the clause.'"]],
 "I'll keep my boots on. And my hat. It's a lucky hat."],
20:["(Knut sniffs.) You can smell it. A little sulphur, the sort that hangs in a bad egg. I told Ilse it was the cook's lunch. She told me the cook's lunch smells better.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","I think the mountain's clearing its throat. We all know it. We're being very polite about it."],
  ["What will you do if it goes?","Get the lads out, get the logs in the pond if it comes to that, and then have a very large drink."],
  ["Are you staying open?","Dagny says so. She says the only thing worse than an eruption's a cancelled order."]],
 "I'll see you in the tavern, if the tavern's still there. And the finger's still here."],
21:["(Knut's hard hat is grey and so are his eyebrows.) It went. I was on the yard, with a log in the tongs, and the sky turned. Ilse dropped the lot. Nobody's hurt. It was wonderful. I've never been so scared.",[
  ["How was it?","A noise like the end of a long note. Then a curtain of ash. The saw stopped itself, I swear. It knew."],
  ["Is the mill damaged?","Only the roof's dusting. We've shut for the day. Dagny came in with a scarf round her face, counted the logs and went home. I'd call that restraint."],
  ["How are you?","Shaking. Happy. Alive. Oh, and I've found my finger. I mean, I've remembered the real story. It's in the tavern book."]],
 "Don't breathe the ash. It tastes of old pennies."],
22:["(Knut is wearing a scarf pulled up over his nose.) The mill's open again, in a manner of speaking. We're sweeping the yard and the logs, and sneezing. Ilse's sneezes could fell a pine.",[
  ["What's the mood?","Dazed. And a bit proud. We were here, and we're still here. It's a very Eastbrook sentiment."],
  ["Is the pay dispute still on?","It's on a shelf. It'll come down again. Volcanoes don't settle a wage. They just make the wage seem small."],
  ["Is Dagny helping?","She handed out dust masks. Free. I thought she'd been ill. She said, 'They're deducted.' She's herself again."]],
 "Mind your eyes. And your pay."],
23:["(Knut's coughing into a sleeve.) The tremors haven't stopped. Every time the floor jolts, the saw gives a little jump, and I'm afraid it'll walk off with me.",[
  ["Are the men getting tired?","We're all frayed. Somebody had to be told to stop whistling. It sets everyone's teeth on edge."],
  ["What helps?","The tavern. Linnea's put out free soup. A little warmth. A song or two. It's what we have."],
  ["Is the union doing anything?","Helen's been by. She's got a list of everyone who needs a roof repaired. She never stops. She'll outlast the mountain."]],
 "If you've a spare minute, listen to the saw. It's talking."],
24:["(Knut is sat on an upturned crate.) A man's got to rest sometimes. We've been moving ash since dawn. It gets into the sawdust, and then into the sandwiches. I've eaten volcano.",[
  ["How does it taste?","Gritty. A little like a bad idea. But everybody else has it, so it feels fair."],
  ["Is the mill under strain?","It's running. The orders keep coming, for repairs. Dagny's discovered a new line of business: timber for rebuilding."],
  ["Is that fair?","It's business. It's also what we do. We build. It's not a bad thing to be good at."]],
 "I'll be back at the saw in a minute. Just let me enjoy the crate."],
25:["(Knut rubs his missing fingertip with his thumb.) Do you know, I've been telling the wrong story. The bear, the pike, the raccoon. I think I told them so I wouldn't tell the real one.",[
  ["What's the real one?","I lost it saving a lad called Sven, ten years ago, who'd tripped. I pulled him clear, and it was my finger or his arm. I've never said so."],
  ["Why not?","Because he'd have been embarrassed. He's a foreman now, in Saltwick. Every Christmas he sends me a card with a finger drawn on it."],
  ["That's a good story.","It's a quiet one. I prefer the bear. But you asked for the finger."]],
 "Keep it to yourself. Between us and the saw."],
26:["(Knut is whistling through his teeth.) There's a lot of talk about princes. The Cola folk have posters. The Royal lot have a cart. We have nothing. We have a saw and a union card.",[
  ["What do you think of princes?","They look lovely in a frame. I've never known one to lift a log. But I'd vote for any prince who'd put a guard on my saw."],
  ["What do you want from the new island?","A vote. A raise. A dry roof. A world where a man can tell a true story, or a false one, and either way get home safe."],
  ["Is that too much?","It sounds a lot when I say it. It sounds small when I look at what we've got."]],
 "Don't forget us when the great ones come. We're the ones who make the planks."],
27:["(Knut's blowing the dust off a faded poster.) Revolution at the pictures, they're saying. The Reds have taken over the cinema. I say if they'd put half that energy into a rota, we'd have a better world.",[
  ["Will you go?","If it's a good film. If it's a lecture, I'll bring a sandwich."],
  ["Do you mind the Communists?","I like them as people. As a government, I'd rather be run by a saw. It's at least honest about what it'll do to you."],
  ["Who's your pick?","Erik. A plain, good man. And a vote that counts. I've never voted for anything but a pint."]],
 "Save me a seat near the back. I'll be shouting."],
28:["(Knut has put on a clean shirt, a little tight.) The big push. Every faction's out. Even Dagny's put a flag on the mill, a small one, in the colours of the Eastbrook Lumber Company.",[
  ["Is that a political flag?","It's a commercial flag. But the way she hung it up, I'd call it a statement."],
  ["What do you expect today?","A lot of shouting, a lot of free food and everyone wanting my vote. It's the nicest I've ever been treated."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A bit. It's a long month, and it's all come down to a handful of days. I'd like a long sit-down, and a very short speech."]],
 "If it goes well, drinks on me. If not, drinks on you."],
29:["(Knut leans on the saw.) Tomorrow it all goes up or down. We've done our bit. We've made the planks, we've told the stories, we've sweated and sneezed.",[
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Vote, if I can. Drink, if I can't. And sing, whatever happens. The tavern's got a piano in a very poor state, and I know four songs."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","I try to be. It's the one tool a sawyer can't do without. A hopeful man keeps all his fingers."],
  ["Any message for Dagny?","Say it plain: a fair day's pay is a good day's work. She'll understand. She hates being told, but she can add."]],
 "I'll save you a pint. Wherever you're standing."],
30:["(Knut has turned the saw off and stands with his hat in his hands.) The last day. It's quiet. It's the quietest the mill's been since the king's funeral. I think the saw's holding its breath.",[
  ["Any last story, Knut?","The bear. A grizzly, six foot, with a taste for sawyers. I fought it with a spanner. (He grins.) It's the best one."],
  ["Are you pleased with the month?","It was longer than I thought. And better. The ash, the song, the lad Sven. A man gets to know himself."],
  ["Will I see you again?","If you come back, you will. The tavern's open, the saw's sharp and the bear's still out there."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Mind your fingers. All ten."],
};
// Kurt, foreman of the Kelp Cola factory (Kelp Cola). Threads: his stopwatch and the factory's timings, the cola he drinks from
// morning to night (and Doctor Lind, who counts), the Union (Petra and Aino, whom he grumbles at and quietly likes), and his
// pigeons on the roof of the Kingsgrad Apartments, with a runaway royal pigeon and two eggs, Leopold and Augustus.
DAILY['Kurt']={
2:["(Kurt is at his window with a stopwatch.) The factory's been shut since the snow came. I've timed every day of it.",[
  ["What are you timing now?","The plough. It did Kingsgrad's main street in forty-two minutes. I could do it in thirty, with Aino and a shovel."],
  ["Is that a Kelp Cola in your hand?","(He holds up the bottle.) Fourth today. Doctor Lind says three's plenty. Doctor Lind drinks coffee, so who's he to talk?"],
  ["What's that noise on the roof?","My pigeons. Twelve of them, in a loft up top. They've been cooped up as long as I have, and they're just as cross about it."]],
 "Shut the door behind you. Draughts slow everything down."],
3:["Kelp Cola HQ's open and the factory isn't. They can push paper in Kingsgrad, but nobody's pushing bottles. (He drinks.)",[
  ["When does the factory open?","When they clear the road to Eastbrook. Tomorrow, they say. I've laid out my overalls. I've ironed them."],
  ["You iron your overalls?","A foreman sets the standard. If the foreman's creased, the line's creased."],
  ["Tell me about the pigeons.","The grey one's Phil. The fat one's Harald. The one that keeps flying off towards Frostholm I haven't named. I don't trust it."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. On the minute."],
4:["(Kurt stands on the factory floor with a stopwatch and a bottle.) We're back. The line started four seconds late. Somebody's going to hear about those four seconds.",[
  ["Who was late?","The line, not a person. The belt was cold. I've had words with it."],
  ["It's very loud in here.","Aino calls it the loudest place on the island. She's right. It's music, if you're a foreman."],
  ["Are you in the Union?","Most of them are. I'm not. A foreman can't strike against himself. I'd only have to time it."]],
 "Stand behind the yellow line. It's there for a reason."],
5:["Petra came round the floor at the dinner break with a clipboard. Union business. I timed her: nine minutes.",[
  ["What's she after?","Heating for the warehouse, more pay and a word with Phil. Two of those are reasonable. I'll let you guess which."],
  ["Will you ever join?","Every year they ask, and every year I say no. Every year Petra brings me a cake anyway. It's a good cake."],
  ["How many colas so far today?","Six. Before dinner. The doctor can come and count them himself if he's so keen."]],
 "If you see Petra, tell her nine minutes is too long."],
6:["One of my pigeons came home with a stranger: a hen with a ring on her leg that isn't mine. Somebody important keeps birds, and she's run off with my grey one.",[
  ["Whose bird is she?","There's a crest on the ring. Rich people put crests on everything. I'll find out."],
  ["A pigeon romance?","It's no laughing matter. My grey one's been strutting about all day like he owns the roof."],
  ["How's the line running?","To the minute. Twelve hundred bottles before dinner. I'd like thirteen hundred, but I'm a reasonable man."]],
 "Keep an eye out for anyone who's lost a pigeon."],
7:["Frostholm's open, and I've had an order from Polar Burger already. Sanna wants forty crates. Those oil men drink cola like water.",[
  ["Do oil men like Kelp Cola?","They drink it, complain it isn't American, and buy more."],
  ["Did you see Doctor Lind?","He listened to my heart and said it fizzes. I said thank you."],
  ["Found the pigeon's owner?","The crest on her ring's a crown. I've a royal pigeon in my loft. I'm keeping it quiet."]],
 "Not a word about the pigeon. She's settling in."],
8:["Sunday, so the line's quiet, so I'm here oiling it. A quiet line is a line with time to think about breaking.",[
  ["Do you ever take a day off?","I took one in 1977. I spent it worrying. Not worth it."],
  ["Heard about the oil rig?","Oil in the sea, so they say. Dario's men couldn't time a boiled egg. Good thing kelp's tough."],
  ["What about the royal pigeon?","Edmund at the palace says they've lost a bird. I haven't told him I've got her. She's happy where she is."]],
 "Mind the oil can. It's full."],
9:["Saara's hunting for a new flavour. A new flavour means new labels, new caps and new timings. I've a headache already.",[
  ["What flavour would you want?","Kelp Cola. The proper one. If it isn't broken, don't put cloudberries in it."],
  ["Do you time your own breaks?","Twelve minutes for dinner. Four for a cola. One for the pigeons, in my head."],
  ["Doesn't cola cure headaches?","It does. Ask my grandmother: she's ninety-four and drinks two a day. It's the chemists that cause them."]],
 "If Saara finds her flavour, warn me first."],
10:["We stop the factory for a minute's silence today, for the king. I'll time it. Exactly sixty seconds. He deserves that much precision.",[
  ["Did you ever meet him?","He came here in '61, when I was a lad on the packing bench. Pressed the start button four times. Played merry hell with the timings. Lovely man."],
  ["Will you go to the chapel?","Phil's going for all of us. Somebody's got to keep the place running, and it isn't going to be Phil."],
  ["What will you think of, in the silence?","The king. And whether the belt's slipping. I'm only human."]],
 "Sixty seconds. Not fifty-nine."],
11:["Petra went up to HQ in her dinner hour to see Phil. Took her an hour and twenty. I docked nobody. Don't tell her.",[
  ["Why didn't you dock her?","I should have. A foreman who doesn't dock isn't a foreman. But it was about the heating, and I'm cold too."],
  ["Is the factory shut up at night?","Empty by six, locked by a quarter past. I check the doors myself, every one."],
  ["How are the pigeons?","The royal hen's laid an egg. In my loft. On my roof. I may be harbouring a prince."]],
 "Doors locked, lights off, on the minute."],
12:["Tomorrow's the giveaway. I've eleven pages of delivery rounds and a crate for every street on the island. Elin typed it; I timed it.",[
  ["Every street?","Every street and every farm. Two bottles for Olaf at East Farm, who'll say he doesn't drink it and then drink four."],
  ["What if crates get stolen?","Then I'll know to the minute when, and I'll know who. I count crates the way other men count sheep."],
  ["How many have you had today?","Nine. It's a big day. Doctor Lind can come and count."]],
 "Early start tomorrow. Earlier than early."],
13:["(Kurt is loading crates onto a trolley at a run.) No time! Giveaway. Every crate's numbered. If one goes missing, I'll know which.",[
  ["Can I help?","Can you lift? No? Then hold the door."],
  ["Who'd steal a crate?","Anyone with arms. And there's a man in a good coat hanging about by the gate. Not my business, but I've written down when he came."],
  ["Any pigeon news?","Two eggs now. I've named them Leopold and Augustus. Let's see which one hatches first."]],
 "Out of the way! Crate coming through!"],
15:["The palace and the Church are at it again today. So are my eggs, in their way: sitting very still and saying nothing.",[
  ["Which prince do you back?","The one that hatches first. That's as scientific as anyone else's method."],
  ["Is Aino in today?","Aino's in on the half-minute every day. Comes in off the tram and sings on the line when she thinks nobody can hear. Everybody can hear."],
  ["Do you ever sit still?","No."]],
 "Back to work. The eggs can mind themselves."],
14:["The factory's off for the big day. First weekday I've stopped it since the king's birthday in '79. Feels like a missing tooth.",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","A lot of standing about. I'll time the speeches. The longest wins nothing."],
  ["Will you vote, if there's a vote?","I'll vote for whoever keeps things on time. Nobody ever has, so I've never voted."],
  ["Are you bringing cola?","A crate. It's for the crowd. Some of it."]],
 "Go on. History's starting, and it's already late."],
16:["The debate's on the wireless today. I've put a set on the packing bench, turned low. Work's down nine per cent.",[
  ["Who are the workers cheering?","Aino cheers for Anna, Petra cheers for Erik, and the rest cheer whenever somebody's rude."],
  ["And you?","I'm Kelp Cola, and Kelp Cola's Harald. Harald's not on. So I'm the one turning it down."],
  ["Nine per cent?","I timed it. Every time Anna says 'comrades', they stop to laugh or clap. Either way, they stop."]],
 "If you're listening too, keep it quiet."],
17:["The ground moved, and every bottle on the packing bench rang like a bell. Prettiest sound I've ever heard, and the most frightening.",[
  ["Was anything broken?","Six bottles. I've written them down. If the mountain wants to pay for them, it can."],
  ["How did the pigeons take it?","They flew up all at once and came down five minutes later. Birds know things. I've started watching them like a barometer."],
  ["There's steam on the volcano.","My grandmother says it smoked like this in '38 and did nothing. She also says she's forty."]],
 "Keep away from the shelves. They're nervous today."],
18:["Phil wants somebody in the Kelpy suit while Jaana's poorly. Elin asked me. I said I've a factory to run and a neck to keep.",[
  ["You'd make a fine Kelpy.","I'd make a punctual Kelpy. Tours on the minute, and no questions after."],
  ["What does Doctor Lind say about you?","Says my blood's half cola. I asked him which half. He didn't laugh."],
  ["How are the birds?","Restless. They won't settle. I sat up in the loft with them last night with a blanket and a bottle."]],
 "If you're going to wear that cup, mind the doorframes."],
19:["More leaflets in the crates. Helen's lot. Eleven so far. I've used them to wedge the warehouse window shut.",[
  ["Are your workers joining the Party?","The Union's one thing and the Party's another. Most of mine know the difference. Aino doesn't care. Petra cares too much."],
  ["Would you ever join the Communists?","They'd want me to time everything for the state. ...Actually, that's not a bad idea. Don't tell anyone I said so."],
  ["The window didn't shut before?","Petra's been on about it for a year. Now it shuts, thanks to Helen. There's a lesson in that, and I don't like it."]],
 "Bring me any leaflets you find. The window's still rattling."],
20:["(Kurt sniffs the air.) Smell that? Rotten eggs, off the mountain. Doctor Lind says it's sulphur and it won't hurt. Doctor Lind's never been right yet.",[
  ["Are you worried?","I've drawn up a plan. If the mountain goes, everyone out of the east door in ninety seconds. We practised: ninety-four. Not bad."],
  ["What do the pigeons make of it?","They won't fly north. Not one of them. Birds know."],
  ["Do you and Doctor Lind get on?","He's a Republican and I'm a Kelp Cola man. We agree on Harald and nothing else."]],
 "East door, ninety seconds. Remember it."],
21:["(Kurt's overalls are grey with ash.) Everyone out of the east door in eighty-eight seconds. Best time yet. Then it rained ash for an hour.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Not one. I counted heads here, then went up to HQ and counted those. Elin had already counted. I counted again."],
  ["And the pigeons?","I ran home. Every bird in the loft, huddled up, eggs and all. I've never been so glad to see a pigeon."],
  ["Were you frightened?","Of course. I was frightened in eighty-eight seconds, and I'm frightened now. I'll be frightened on time tomorrow, too."]],
 "Cover your mouth outside. That's an order."],
22:["Brooms. Everybody on brooms. I've timed the sweeping, and Petra's fastest. Don't tell her I said.",[
  ["Has ash got into the bottles?","Not a speck. Saara went through every crate in the warehouse with a torch."],
  ["Heard about trouble at East Farm?","Olaf came by for his cola and wouldn't look me in the eye. That man always looks you in the eye, if only to check for a spare bottle."],
  ["How are the eggs?","Still being sat on. The hen's grey with ash. She won't budge."]],
 "Take a broom on your way past. Everyone else has."],
23:["I'm sleeping here. Someone's said the Party means to throw a spanner in somebody's works, and these are my works.",[
  ["Where will you sleep?","On the packing bench. Pillow from home, a crate of cola and a torch. I've slept worse."],
  ["Who told you about the spanner?","Rosa told Elin, Elin told Phil, Phil told me. That's how news travels in Kingsgrad: by hot dog."],
  ["Aren't you overdoing it?","A foreman who overdoes it is a foreman with a factory in the morning."]],
 "Doors locked. All of them. I'll check again in ten minutes."],
24:["My back hasn't spoken to me since I slept on that packing bench. It's sulking.",[
  ["Did anything happen in the night?","I heard every creak in the building and blamed each one on Helen. That's all I'll say."],
  ["Who fed your pigeons?","Petra, from down the corridor. A Union woman feeding a foreman's pigeons. Don't you dare print it."],
  ["How many colas yesterday?","Fourteen. The night shift counts double."]],
 "If you see Doctor Lind, don't mention my back."],
25:["I've signed Petra's petition for heating in the warehouse. Not the Union. Just the petition.",[
  ["Why now?","Because she fed my birds and asked nothing for it. A foreman pays his debts."],
  ["Will Phil listen to you?","Phil listens to his foremen. He doesn't always hear them, but he listens."],
  ["They say Princess Daphne wants a vote.","Then she can come and vote in the dinner break. Ten minutes, two options."]],
 "Don't tell Petra I'm pleased about it. I'm not. Much."],
26:["Phil's making an advertisement, very hush-hush. They wanted bottles with character, so I took them a crate off the museum's old line. Wilma counted them out and she'll count them back.",[
  ["Who's in the advert?","Don't know, don't care. As long as he holds the bottle label-out."],
  ["Dario's buying the spa, I hear.","Oil men in hot water. About time."],
  ["How are the eggs?","Any day now. I've a calendar on the loft door."]],
 "If you see the advert, check he's holding it label-out."],
27:["Foreign buyers tomorrow. I've had the floor scrubbed twice and the windows once. You can see the mountain through them now, which is a pity.",[
  ["Will you show them round?","If they come down. I'll show them the stopwatch, too. A foreign buyer likes a stopwatch."],
  ["Does anyone want the deal to fail?","Someone always does. I keep my eyes open and the doors locked. That's all a foreman can do."],
  ["You look nervous.","I've had twenty colas. That's not nerves. That's preparation."]],
 "Tomorrow. Clean shoes, if you're coming."],
28:["(Kurt wears a clean shirt and tie, with the stopwatch on a chain.) Buyers, in my factory. I've told everyone to look busy, and Aino not to sing.",[
  ["Will Aino behave?","Aino will sing. Aino always sings. I've told the buyers it's a local tradition."],
  ["What are they asking?","How many bottles in an hour. I've the figure written on my cuff."],
  ["Is the Union up to anything?","Petra's got a banner rolled up under her bench. If she unrolls it in front of the buyers, I'll... I'll hold the other end. It's about the heating."]],
 "Smile. They're watching from the gallery."],
29:["Phil's given us the afternoon to go up to Frostholm and cheer for Harald. I've timed it: forty minutes on foot, if you don't stop at Polar Burger. Everyone will stop at Polar Burger.",[
  ["Will you cheer?","I'll clap on the beat. I'm not a cheering man."],
  ["What's at stake for Kelp Cola?","If Harald leads the Republicans, we've a seat at the table. If not, we're sat on the floor."],
  ["How are the eggs?","Not yet. They're waiting. Everyone's waiting."]],
 "Forty minutes. Don't dawdle."],
30:["(Kurt is up in the pigeon loft on the roof, holding a tiny grey chick.) Hatched. Both of them, within a minute of each other. I timed it.",[
  ["Which came first, Leopold or Augustus?","Fifty-eight seconds apart, and I'm not saying which way round. Somebody might read something into it."],
  ["The island's last big day.","Whatever happens, there'll be a factory floor to sweep tomorrow, and I'll be there to sweep it on time."],
  ["What will you call the mother?","Kelp. Obviously."]],
 "Mind the ladder going down. It's slippery with ash."],
};
// Lars, roughneck on Diversified Petroleum's oil rig, bunks in the crew quarters (Diversified Petroleum). Threads: more money than
// he's ever had and none of it saved (a motorbike he's going to buy); Rita, the singer at the Midnight Note, who isn't interested;
// a growing debt to Nico, the Syndicate's bookie in Frostholm; the rig, which he treats as a joke because the alternative is worse.
DAILY['Lars']={
7:["(Lars is lying on a bunk with his boots on, flicking a coin.) A visitor! Out here! Either you're lost, or you're from the company, or you're the reporter everyone's on about. Sit down. Not on that bunk, it's wet.",[
  ["Why's the bunk wet?","The ceiling drips when the sea's up. Company says it's condensation. I say it's the North Sea, coming in to say hello."],
  ["What's it like, working the rig?","Cold, loud, and they pay me more than my dad earned in his life. I'd do it upside down for that money."],
  ["Heads or tails?","(He flicks the coin and catches it.) Heads. I always call heads. I've lost about four hundred dollars that way, but it's a principle."]],
 "Mind the gangway on your way out. It's greasy, and the sea's a long way down."],
8:["(Lars is pulling his boots on, fast.) Something's up. Alarms, shouting, Dario on the radio. When Dario's on the radio, it's never good, and it's always our fault.",[
  ["What do you think's happened?","Something's leaked. Something's always leaking out here. It's a rig, not a teapot."],
  ["Does this happen a lot?","Put it this way: I've got a spare pair of boots just for spills. And a spare pair for the spare pair."],
  ["Are you scared?","Me? No. I'm twenty-three and well paid. Nothing bad happens to people who are twenty-three and well paid."]],
 "If anyone from the company asks, I've been ready since dawn."],
9:["(Lars is counting a roll of notes on his bunk.) Payday. Two weeks' wages. By the time I get back out here after my week ashore, it'll all be gone, and I'll have nothing to show for it but a headache.",[
  ["Why not save some?","I am saving. For a motorbike. A red Triumph, from a fella in Kingsgrad. I just keep spending the savings."],
  ["Where does it all go?","The Midnight Note, mostly. And the casino. And the bar at the Aurora, where a beer costs what a meal costs anywhere else."],
  ["Could I borrow some?","(He laughs and puts it away.) You've a nerve. I like that. No."]],
 "If you see a red Triumph in Kingsgrad, tell the fella I'm still coming."],
10:["(Lars is trying to tie a black tie in a cracked mirror.) They're burying the king today, and the rig's flying its flag at half-mast. I'm wearing this tie out of respect. I can't get it to sit right.",[
  ["Let me help.","(He lets you.) Thanks. My mother always did it. She'd be glad somebody's keeping me presentable."],
  ["Did you care about the king?","He was on the money. Every note I've ever spent had his face on it. That's a sort of friendship."],
  ["Are you going to the chapel?","They don't let rig hands into chapels. We'd make the pews smell of diesel. I'll raise a glass to him in the Midnight Note on my week off."]],
 "God save the king. Whoever he turns out to be."],
11:["(Lars is lying on his bunk, grinning at the ceiling.) There's a singer at the Midnight Note in Frostholm. Rita. Voice like warm honey. I've sent her flowers three times.",[
  ["How did she take the flowers?","She gave them to the barman, Sid. He's got a very nice flat now, all full of my roses."],
  ["Does she know who you are?","She knows I'm the fella who claps too loud at the wrong bits. It's a start."],
  ["Maybe she's not interested.","Maybe. But she hasn't met the real me yet. The real me's got a motorbike."]],
 "If you see Rita, tell her the rig says hello. No, don't. Yes, do."],
12:["(Lars is playing patience on an upturned crate.) Losing to myself. That's how bad my luck is this week.",[
  ["Bad week?","I owe Nico, the bookie in Frostholm, eighty dollars. Horse called Northern Light. Came in fourth. Out of four."],
  ["What does Nico do if you don't pay?","Smiles. He smiles at you very nicely, and then his friend smiles at you too. I'll pay him."],
  ["Want me to deal?","Go on, then. (You deal. He loses.) See? It's not the cards, it's me. I'm cursed. Charmingly."]],
 "If you see Nico, I'm out at sea. A long way out."],
13:["(Lars has a bottle of Kelp Cola in each hand.) The supply boat brought a crate of the free stuff from Kingsgrad. Dario would sack us all for drinking it. So we're drinking it fast.",[
  ["How is it?","Tastes like a fizzy beach. I've had six. My teeth are humming."],
  ["Would Dario really sack you?","He'd want to. But nobody else will work out here, so he'd just glare. He's got a very good glare."],
  ["Any money in your pocket yet?","Some. I put it in a sock. A sock is very hard to spend. Unless it's a Saturday."]],
 "One more cola and I'm going to start floating."],
14:["(Lars has slicked his hair back and is buttoning a clean shirt.) The big day! The whole island together. The company's sending the boat in so we can all go. I'm going to find Rita in the crowd.",[
  ["What do you expect from the day?","Speeches I won't listen to and a lot of girls in their best coats. I'm a simple man."],
  ["Will Rita be there?","Everybody's going to be there. That's the whole point. One of everybody, and I'm after one of them."],
  ["You scrub up well.","I've got two good shirts. This is the better one. The other one's got Northern Light's losing ticket in the pocket."]],
 "Wish me luck. I'll need all of it, and some of yours."],
15:["(Lars is lying on his bunk with his arm over his eyes.) Don't ask me about the big day. I saw Rita. I said hello. She said 'Oh, it's you.' Do you know how long I've been waiting for 'Oh, it's you'?",[
  ["That's not bad, really.","That's what I thought. 'Oh, it's you.' She knows it's me. That's progress."],
  ["What did you say next?","Nothing. My mouth stopped. I just nodded like a fishing float, and then she was gone."],
  ["Prince or priest, by the way?","Whoever puts Rita on the radio. I'd vote for anything that let me hear her without paying the Midnight Note's prices."]],
 "Oh, it's you. I'm going to remember that for the rest of my life."],
16:["(Lars has the crew radio pressed to his ear.) The Great Debate, Communists against Democrats. The lads have got a bet on who swears first. I've got two dollars on Anna.",[
  ["Has anybody sworn yet?","Not yet. It's very polite. Disappointingly. Somebody almost said 'blast' about ten minutes ago."],
  ["Who'd you vote for?","Whoever kept my wages. The Communists would share them out. I'm not sharing. I've only just got them."],
  ["You'll bet on anything.","I'll bet on whether you'll leave before the debate ends. Two dollars says no."]],
 "If Anna swears, I'm buying everyone a beer. A small one."],
17:["(Lars is pacing the crew quarters.) Word on the radio is that Dario's gone missing. Nobody knows where. The foreman's telling us to keep working, like that's going to help find him.",[
  ["Where could he be?","On a bender, probably. He drinks more than I do, and I do it for a living."],
  ["Does it change anything out here?","Nobody's going to pay us if he's gone. That's what everyone's thinking and nobody's saying."],
  ["Do you like Dario?","No. But he pays on time, and I like that very much. I'll take a boss I don't like who pays."]],
 "If you find him, ask him about our wages before anything else."],
18:["(Lars has a hand on the bunk frame, which is creaking.) The whole rig groaned like an old man getting out of a chair. They say the ground's shaking on the island. The sea's not supposed to shake.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","The foreman says there's steam over the old mountain up north. I didn't know we had a volcano. Nobody tells rig hands anything."],
  ["Does the rig hold up in a shake?","She's built for storms. Nobody said anything about earthquakes. I checked the manual. There's a page missing."],
  ["You look a bit green.","That's the sea. Or last night's beer. Or the volcano. I'm not choosing."]],
 "If she groans again, I'm sleeping in my boots."],
19:["(Lars is holding a pair of tickets with a grin.) Two tickets for the Midnight Note, Saturday. Rita's singing. And rumour has it a royal's been sneaking in at night in a false moustache. I'm going to see both.",[
  ["Who are you taking?","Nobody, yet. The second ticket's for whoever's brave enough. Or for Rita, if she'll sit with me in the interval."],
  ["Which royal?","Nobody knows. Sid at the bar says he tips well and claps in the right places. That's not me, then."],
  ["Did those tickets cost much?","Twenty dollars. Which I don't have. Which means Nico's going to smile at me again."]],
 "Saturday. Moustaches and jazz. I'll report back."],
20:["(Lars looks pale, and is sitting very still on his bunk.) Nico sent a message out on the supply boat. A postcard of Frostholm, with 'One hundred and twenty' written on the back. That's what I owe him now.",[
  ["How did it get to a hundred and twenty?","Interest. Nico's very interested in me. I've never been so popular."],
  ["Can you pay?","On payday, if I don't go ashore. If I don't go ashore, I don't see Rita. That's a terrible choice."],
  ["Tell someone. The police, maybe.",["The police? (He laughs, not happily.) Sergeant Hanna's got a Syndicate bookie in her cells already. They're very comfortable.",[
    ["Then pay him and stay out here.","Two weeks with no Rita and no beer. I'd go mad. But I'd go mad with all my fingers."],
    ["Then stop betting.","Easy for you to say. You've never seen a horse called Northern Light. He looked like a winner."]]]]],
 "If anyone asks, I'm fine. I'm always fine. That's my whole thing."],
21:["(Lars is on the gantry window, staring north.) Look at it. The whole sky's orange over the island. The mountain's going off like a firework. We can see it from here.",[
  ["Is the rig safe?","We're miles off the coast, on a lot of steel in a lot of sea. Safer than Frostholm right now. Probably."],
  ["Is everyone accounted for?","Ruth on the radio's been calling us all by name. Everyone answered. Even the cook, who never answers anything."],
  ["Are you scared now?","(Quietly.) Yeah. Don't tell the lads. I'm twenty-three. I'm supposed to be the brave one."]],
 "Stand by the window with me a minute. It's the biggest thing I've ever seen."],
22:["(Lars has ash in his eyebrows and his hair.) Went ashore on the supply boat to see the damage. Frostholm's grey. Grey cars, grey trees, grey people. I went into the HQ and Ruth said 'We're always open'.",[
  ["How's Frostholm coping?","Sweeping. Everyone's sweeping. Sid's sweeping the Midnight Note. Rita sang to the queue for the generator fuel. I cried. Don't tell anyone that either."],
  ["Did you see Nico?","No. And he didn't see me. I came back on the first boat. He'll find me eventually; Nico always does."],
  ["Wash your eyebrows.","They're my volcano eyebrows. I'm keeping them as a memory."]],
 "Pass the soap, then. I suppose I should."],
23:["(Lars is soaked to the waist and shivering.) Something's gone wrong under the waterline. There's water where there shouldn't be water. They've sent for divers and welders and every prayer anyone knows.",[
  ["What happened?","Something gave way down below. Whether it broke on its own or somebody helped it, the foreman won't say. He's gone very quiet."],
  ["Is the rig going to flood?","Not if the welders get here. If they don't, we'll find out how well I swim."],
  ["Can you swim?","Like a fish. A very small, frightened fish."]],
 "Pass me a towel. And a cigarette. And a prayer, if you've got one."],
24:["(Lars is lying on his bunk, unusually quiet.) I've been thinking. That's new for me. If something goes wrong out here, I've got nothing. Eighty dollars in a sock and a debt to Nico.",[
  ["What would you change?","Save. Properly. Not in a sock. In a bank. Agnes at Nordbank says she'll open me an account with ten dollars."],
  ["What about the motorbike?","Still want it. But maybe I want to be alive to ride it more than I want it this week."],
  ["You'll be back to normal tomorrow.","(He grins, a little.) Probably. But today I'm a thinker. Leave me be."]],
 "Ten dollars and a bank book. That's my new plan. Write it down so I can't forget."],
25:["(Lars has a bank book and is showing it off.) Look. Nordbank, account number four, four, nine, one. Ten dollars in it. Agnes counted it twice, and stamped it. I've never been stamped before.",[
  ["Proud of you.","So am I. Nobody's ever been proud of me for not spending money."],
  ["What about Nico?","I paid him half. He smiled. A real smile, not the other kind. I think he was surprised."],
  ["Will it last?","It's been in there two days. That's a record. I'll let you know at the weekend."]],
 "Four, four, nine, one. Remember it, in case I forget it on purpose."],
26:["(Lars is laughing.) Dario's gone up the mountain to buy the spa. The spa, on the volcano. A man who owns a rig wants to own a bath too.",[
  ["Would you use it?","If it was free. Hot water after two weeks out here would make me cry. Again."],
  ["Is Dario paying you more?","He's buying a spa. I'm saving ten dollars. We're both investing in our futures."],
  ["How's Rita?","She waved at me through the window of the Note when I was ashore. Waved! I'm going to marry her."]],
 "A wave. A wave's a start. Everything's a start, if you want it enough."],
27:["(Lars has his good shirt out again.) Dario's party tomorrow at the HQ: bowling and milkshakes, and the boat's taking us in. I've never bowled in my life.",[
  ["Bowling's easy. Roll it straight.","Straight? Nothing in my life's ever gone straight. I'll give it a try."],
  ["Will you bet on it?","Nope. (He hesitates.) A small one. With myself. Nobody else. I've got a bank account to think about."],
  ["Heard about Gustavo's poker game?","I've heard. Big money, at the pizzeria. Nico told me there's a seat if I want it. I don't want it. I really don't."]],
 "Straight down the middle. That's my new motto. For bowling and money."],
28:["(Lars is laughing, with milkshake on his chin.) Dario's party! I bowled the ball backwards. Backwards! It went under the milkshake machine. Ruth laughed so hard she cried.",[
  ["Any luck with the second go?","Four pins. Magnus clapped. Magnus! He's undone a button. Everyone's gone mad."],
  ["Heard anything interesting?","The bosses talk when they've had a drink. I'm only a rig hand. I just hear 'Lars, get the ice.'"],
  ["The whole island's out today.","Every faction pushing all at once. And here we are with milkshakes. I'm on the right side."]],
 "Have a strawberry milkshake. It's the only one Dario doesn't hover over."],
29:["(Lars is in his work clothes again, rubbing his arm.) Back on the rig. Sore arm from bowling. The Republicans are voting today in Frostholm. Magnus is up for it. The lads have a bet on.",[
  ["Who've the lads got their money on?","Half on Magnus, out of loyalty. Half on Carlo, out of fear. Nobody's on Harald. Nobody out here drinks his cola in public."],
  ["Did you bet?","No. I really didn't. I've got eighty-four dollars in the bank. I'm a saver now. It's very boring."],
  ["Would Magnus be a good leader?","Magnus would be a very tidy leader. Everything would be in rows. I'd hate it. But the rig would get new valves."]],
 "If Magnus wins, I'm asking him for a new bunk. A dry one."],
30:["(Lars is standing at the window, looking towards the island.) The last day. Whatever's going to happen, it's happening over there. And here I am, on a lump of steel in the sea, waiting.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A government that leaves my wages alone. A motorbike. Rita saying 'Oh, it's you' again, but warmer."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A bit. It's strange, not knowing. Usually I just bet on things like this."],
  ["You've changed this month.","Have I? (He thinks.) Maybe. I've got a bank account. I nearly feel like a grown-up. Don't spread it around."]],
 "Whatever happens, I'll be out here. Somebody's got to keep the lights on."],
};
// Leo, saxophonist at The Midnight Note in Frostholm (the Entertainers). Threads: he says more through his horn than out loud, he sleeps
// until noon, his old Selmer saxophone and its dented bell, Rita's voice, Sid's bar, the rich people who request waltzes, the tremors.
DAILY['Leo']={
7:["(Leo is wiping a saxophone with a soft cloth, and answers a moment late.) Hm? Oh. Morning. Or whatever it is. I've been up since eleven, which is early for me.",[
  ["I'm the British reporter.","Right. Nice. (He nods slowly.) We don't get many. We get oilmen, mostly, and a man who sells hats."],
  ["That's a beautiful saxophone.","It's a Selmer. It's older than me, and it's got a dent in the bell where a man threw it at a wall. It plays better for it."],
  ["Do you play every night?","Every night the doors are open. The roads are open now, so we'll be busy. Frostholm's thirsty."]],
 "Come by The Midnight Note. I'll play you something. I'm better at that than at talking."],
8:["(Leo is lying on a sofa with the saxophone on his chest.) Sunday. No one in the club till late. I'm practising scales in my head. It's very quiet. Rita says it's the loudest thing she's ever heard.",[
  ["Do you not talk much?","I talk with this. (He pats the horn.) It's a better listener. It never interrupts."],
  ["What are you practising?","A slow one. Something with a lot of space in it. You can say a lot with space."],
  ["How's Rita?","In the next room, singing in the bath. Don't tell her I said so. She'd stop."]],
 "It's a good day for nothing. I'm putting it to full use."],
9:["(Leo has a bottle of cola on the piano, going flat.) A man from Kelp Cola asked if I'd play on their advert. I said I'd think about it. He said they'd pay. I said I'd think about it faster.",[
  ["Would you do it?","If it was a good tune. I'd play a jingle if it swung. Nobody's asked me to play a jingle that swings."],
  ["What would the cola sound like?","Something sweet, a bit fizzy, with a sharp note in the middle. A B-flat, I think. Maybe a B-flat minor."],
  ["Do you like the stuff?","It's all right. I prefer coffee. Black. With a hint of regret."]],
 "If it's a good tune, I'm in. If not, I'm in bed."],
10:["(Leo is in a black shirt, and sits very still.) They bury the king today. Sid's closing the club for the evening. Rita's going to sing one song, very quietly. I'll play behind her. I'm told the king liked marches.",[
  ["Do you play marches?","Only if forced. But I've been practising a slow one. It sounds like a march that's lost its way home."],
  ["How do you feel about the day?","Quiet. I never knew him. But I know what it is to play for an empty room."],
  ["Will you play at the funeral?","No. They have their own band. They won't want a saxophone. It's far too honest."]],
 "A slow night ahead. I'll make it count."],
11:["(Leo is sitting on the edge of the stage, picking at a reed with a penknife.) A man came in at lunchtime to ask if I'd play for free, for the poor. I said yes. Then he asked if I'd play for free for the rich. I said, no.",[
  ["Why not?","The poor can't afford to pay. The rich can, and don't. There's a difference, and it's about eleven krona."],
  ["Does the club do well?","It does fine. Sid keeps the books and the bar. I keep the notes. It works."],
  ["What did you play for the poor?","'Smoke Gets In Your Eyes', slow. It seemed right. Sad, and a little proud."]],
 "Play for those who listen. It's the only rule."],
12:["(Leo is looking at a cracked reed in the light.) A man at the bar says Ruby, in Eastbrook, has a record that someone wants very badly. A rare one. I'd like to hear it. I'm a sucker for rare things.",[
  ["Do you know Ruby?","I've bought records from her. She's honest, and she's fierce. She won't sell a thing to someone who won't love it."],
  ["What record is it?","I can guess. There's only one local jazz record anyone would pay for. I won't say. It's a secret I'd like to keep."],
  ["Would you pay for it?","If I had the money. I'd give her a tune for it. She'd hate it, but she'd keep it."]],
 "If Ruby sells it, I hope it's to a lover, not a collector."],
13:["(Leo is blowing a long low note, then stops.) I've heard they're giving free cola in Kingsgrad tomorrow. It's a day for crowds. Sid says the club will be quiet. I say, play a ballad. It's what people want when they've had too much sweet.",[
  ["Do you go to Kingsgrad?","Sometimes. I play at the palace once in a blue moon. The prince likes a waltz. The princess likes a rumba."],
  ["Have you any favourite songs?","'Round Midnight'. It's a song about being awake when everyone else isn't. I've lived in it for years."],
  ["Is there a lot of talk at the club?","A lot. About the Syndicate, the oil, the king. I just play, and the talk goes in one ear and out of the bell."]],
 "Good night, in your own time. I'm only just waking."],
14:["(Leo is polishing his shoes with a very careful hand.) The fourteenth. A big day, I hear. Rita says everyone on the island will be there. I said, who's going to open the club? She said, nobody. It's a holiday.",[
  ["Are you going to the gathering?","If I can find my other shoe. I'd like to see what an island looks like when it's all in one place."],
  ["What do you expect?","A lot of talking, not much listening. It's the usual."],
  ["Will you play?","If someone asks. If not, I'll just stand at the back and hum. It's a good place to hear."]],
 "If I'm not there, I'm asleep. Wake me in time for the tune."],
15:["(Leo is sitting at the piano, picking out a hymn with one finger.) A man from the monastery came in. He said the church wants Prince Augustus. Another said the family wants Leopold. I played them both a waltz. They danced.",[
  ["Who did you vote for?","With a saxophone? I didn't. I'd rather be paid. Kings and crosses are for people with free evenings."],
  ["Do the princes come to the club?","Leopold, sometimes, in a disguise. A bad one. He wears a hat with a very small feather."],
  ["What do you think about the choice?","I'm a musician. I'd choose whoever hires me. And whoever doesn't ask for 'Danny Boy'."]],
 "A crown's heavy. A saxophone's heavier, but it's more fun."],
16:["(Leo is tuning his horn with a sigh.) A great debate between Communists and Democrats. Both sides have asked me to play at it. I said I'd play at both, but I'd charge them each double.",[
  ["Who'll win?","The one who tells the best story. Nobody ever wins on facts. It's the tune they remember."],
  ["Do you follow politics?","In the same way I follow the weather. It's coming, I don't know when, and I take an umbrella."],
  ["What would you play?","A slow blues. Something that says, 'You've both got a point, and I'd like a drink.'"]],
 "Don't tell them I said it. They'd hire me for both."],
17:["(Leo stops mid-phrase and puts a hand on the piano lid.) The floor moved. Just a bit. The piano hummed a note I didn't play. It's an F, I think. Slightly flat.",[
  ["Was it a tremor?","I think so. Sid says it's the trams. There aren't any trams. We've told him."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I play in the dark all night. I'm used to the unexpected. But I'd rather it didn't come from beneath."],
  ["Is there steam from the volcano?","In the north, yes. A little white plume. It's like the mountain's smoking a cigarette. I'd like to know what it's thinking."]],
 "I'll tune the piano tomorrow. It's out of sorts."],
18:["(Leo is lying on his back, holding the saxophone above him.) I heard Kelpy the cup's got a new song. A jingle. I'd like to hear it, but I'd hate to like it. It's a very odd position.",[
  ["Would you play with Kelpy?","If he can keep time. Most cups can't."],
  ["How's the Midnight Note?","Full. Everyone wants a drink and a tune. The ground's been shaking a little, and the glasses clink in time."],
  ["Do the shakes bother you?","I've begun to play along. It's a useful beat. A little slow, but I'm told it's a groove."]],
 "Come in. I'll play you the earthquake blues. It hasn't got an ending yet."],
19:["(Leo is blowing a long soft note into a handkerchief.) It's a quiet one. For the dust that's coming. I can feel it, in the reed. Something's coming, and the saxophone knows before I do.",[
  ["Are you superstitious?","I'm a musician. I'm full of it. Never whistle in the dressing room. Never play a requested waltz in a bad mood."],
  ["Is there anything at the club tonight?","I hear someone's coming, in disguise. A person from the palace. I haven't been told who. Sid winked at me. I'll play something royal."],
  ["Are you looking forward to it?","I'm always looking forward to being surprised. It's the one luxury of the profession."]],
 "Keep an eye on the corners. That's where the interesting ones sit."],
20:["(Leo has two saxophone cases by the door, as if ready to leave.) Rita says Zora, at the circus, told her to pack. I said I don't believe in fortune tellers. She said, 'Pack anyway.' So I've packed. I've packed the reeds and a sandwich.",[
  ["Is there a reason?","A feeling. A very low note, from somewhere underneath. I've been hearing it for a week. It's a note I don't like."],
  ["Where would you go?","The lake, perhaps, or the beach. Somewhere without a ceiling. I've always wanted to play outdoors."],
  ["Is Rita worried?","She's calm. She's been practising the hymn. She says if the world's ending, she'd like to be in tune."]],
 "Keep your coat by the door. It's the oldest tip in the book."],
21:["(Leo sits on the pavement, grey with ash, the saxophone across his knees.) It went. The mountain went. I heard it from the flat: a bass note so low I felt it in my teeth. I've never heard that note before. I hope I never do again.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Fine. The horn's fine. Rita's fine. Sid's under the bar, but he's fine. We're a lucky bunch."],
  ["What did you see?","A red glow and a great grey cloud. Then the ash, drifting like very slow snow. It was almost beautiful. I'm not proud of that."],
  ["What now?","Now I play. It's the only thing I know. A slow one. For everyone who's frightened."]],
 "Wipe your shoes. The ash is in the keyhole."],
22:["(Leo is blowing ash out of the keys of his saxophone, one by one.) It's in every pad. It's in the bell. I'll be coughing up music for weeks. Rita says it's an improvement.",[
  ["Is the club open?","In a fashion. We've swept it. The candles are lit. People come in grey and leave a bit lighter."],
  ["What are you playing?","Something low and gentle. A song for people who can't sleep. It's most of the island, tonight."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","No. Just frightened. And grey. And thirsty. Sid's selling out of everything."]],
 "Come and listen. It's free, and it's warm."],
23:["(Leo plays a few notes, a little hoarse.) I can only get about half the range. The ash has got in the reed. I've been boiling them. It's like making soup for a very small, very demanding animal.",[
  ["Are people coming in?","More than before. They sit in the dark and listen. They don't say much. It's the best audience I've ever had."],
  ["What's the news?","The Party's fixing its machinery. The oil lot's mending its pipes. Everyone's mending. I'm mending a saxophone."],
  ["Is Rita singing?","Every night. Her voice has an edge now, a bit of grit. It's even better. She doesn't believe me."]],
 "Don't breathe in too much. It's bad for the lungs. And the solos."],
24:["(Leo is sitting on the Skyline Apartments stairs in the half-dark.) The monks have gone up the mountain to pray. I wish them luck. I'd go up there with the horn, but it's a long way and I've got a gig.",[
  ["Would you play for the monks?","If they'd let me. They'd probably prefer chanting. But I'd be happy to play a quiet one, in the corner."],
  ["Are the aftershocks still going?","One this morning. A small one. The saxophone buzzed on its stand. It's the first note I didn't have to blow."],
  ["How's the club?","Warm. Dusty. Alive. Sid's put up a sign: 'Ash on the house.' It's his only joke."]],
 "Wrap up. It's colder than it looks."],
25:["(Leo is practising a very slow, very sweet waltz.) For a princess. I've been asked to play at a party. A princess for democracy, they say. I've no idea what it means, but the waltz is nice.",[
  ["Who's the princess?","Daphne, I think. She comes to the club sometimes. She tips well, and she cries at the ballads."],
  ["Is it a big do?","Big enough. A crowd, a few speeches, a lot of canapés. I'll stand by the piano and nod at people."],
  ["Do you like the royals?","I like anyone who tips. And who requests something I can play."]],
 "If you're there, I'll play you something. Don't ask for a polka."],
26:["(Leo is looking at a bottle of cola with a prince's face on it.) Look at this. A prince, on a bottle. I've been played on a jukebox, but never printed on a label. I think I'd like it. It's a kind of immortality, with a screw cap.",[
  ["Would you put your face on a bottle?","I'd put my saxophone on it. My face isn't made for it. It's too long and a bit tired."],
  ["What do you think of Kelp Cola's plan?","It's a business. They want everyone to drink the same thing. I'd rather we all drank something different, and argued about it."],
  ["How's the music?","Good. Rita's written a new song. It's about a man who gets everything he wants and then loses his keys."]],
 "I'll drink a prince, if he's cold. It's a very fine thing."],
27:["(Leo has a ticket for the pictures stuck in his hat-band.) A revolution at the pictures. The Communists are showing a film tonight. I'm told it has a very fine score. It would be good to hear someone else's saxophone, for once.",[
  ["Are you going?","If Rita's up for it. She likes a good villain. She'd cheer if there was one."],
  ["Do you like films?","I like the dark. And the music. And the way everyone looks at the same wall. It's a lot like a club, only quieter."],
  ["What's the mood on the island?","Tight. Like a string just before it's plucked. You can hear it, if you listen."]],
 "I'll tell you what the saxophone's like. I'll be honest."],
28:["(Leo is playing a very quiet, very slow tune, with his eyes closed.) The biggest day, they say. All the factions are out. I'm playing at home. It's the only thing I can do that won't hurt anyone.",[
  ["Are you worried?","A bit. I've seen how crowds can go. But I've also seen how a song can stop one."],
  ["What would you like to see happen?","I'd like everyone to go home, put on a record, and have a rest. It's an unfashionable position."],
  ["Is the club open tonight?","Of course. Sid says the Syndicate, the oil men and the Party will all be in. It's the only neutral ground. It's got a piano."]],
 "Come in later. I'll save you a stool."],
29:["(Leo has a very small glass of something amber, and a very big silence.) It's the night before. I'm in no mood to talk. I'll play instead. It's all I've got, and it's mostly enough.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Calm. A bit sad. Like at the end of a very long set. You know it's nearly over, and you'd like to play another."],
  ["What will you play tomorrow?","Whatever's needed. If it's a hymn, a hymn. If it's a waltz, a waltz. I'm a very adaptable man."],
  ["Is Rita ready?","She's been ready all her life. She's just waiting for the audience."]],
 "Get some sleep. I'm going to practise, very quietly."],
30:["(Leo stands at the club door, saxophone over his shoulder, in the grey light.) The last day. I woke at eleven, as usual. It's not a bad way to meet the end of things. Rested, and a little late.",[
  ["Any last thoughts?","Play it as you feel it. And if you can't, play it soft. Someone will hear."],
  ["What will you do after today?","Play. It's what I do. The island will be what it'll be, and I'll be in the corner, with a horn."],
  ["Thank you for talking.","Thank you for listening. Most don't. It's why I use the horn."]],
 "Goodnight, whatever time it is. I'll play something for you."],
};
// Leopold, the prince and heir, in the throne room of the Royal Palace (Royal Family). Threads: his father's shadow and the
// crown he means to wear; the speech he keeps writing and burning; his father's sailing boat, the Kestrel, laid up at Eastbrook
// Harbour; the jazz he listens to on the wireless when nobody's about; his brother Augustus and the Church, and Daphne.
DAILY['Leopold']={
2:["(Leopold sits very straight on the edge of the throne, as if it might object.) Another visitor. The island queues up to look sorry at me. Half of them mean it.",[
  ["Which half?","The half who never wanted anything from him. It's a short queue. (He almost smiles.) Edmund keeps a list."],
  ["Have you slept since he died?",["Some. The palace creaks at night. I'd never noticed. He used to make more noise than the floorboards.",[
    ["What was he like, at home?","Loud at breakfast, silent at dinner. He read the shipping forecast aloud as if it were scripture."],
    ["Do you miss the noise?","I miss being able to blame someone. Now it's all mine, the creaking included."]]]],
  ["Nice chair.","It's my father's chair. Until a bishop says otherwise, I'm only keeping it warm."]],
 "Edmund will see you out. He sees everyone out. It's his favourite part."],
3:["(Leopold is looking at a crate of Kelp Cola by the door.) Phil sent that up from Kingsgrad, with his condolences. Twenty-four bottles of condolence.",[
  ["Kind of him.","Phil is never kind. Phil is early. My father drank one every afternoon at four, on ice, for fifty years. Phil wants to know if I'll keep the habit."],
  ["Will you keep the habit?","I don't much like it. Don't print that, or Phil will send fifty-two bottles to change my mind."],
  ["Can I have one?","Take two. Take the crate. Marta says it rots the teeth, and she'd know: she's taken a bottle a day since 1951."]],
 "Tell Phil, if you see him, that the prince is grateful. It's what princes are for."],
4:["I had a letter from Eastbrook today. Erik, the Democrat. Very polite. He'd like an election, at my earliest convenience.",[
  ["What did you write back?","Nothing yet. I've put it with the other polite letters. It's a tall pile, and it leans to the left."],
  ["Is an election so bad?",["It's a question of what comes after it. My father said a vote is a coin tossed by a crowd. He said a great many things, though.",[
    ["Did you agree with him?","I was never asked. That was rather his point about everything."],
    ["And what do you say?","I say I've been prince for thirty-four years and king for none. Ask me in a month."]]]],
  ["Eastbrook's quite fun, actually.","So I'm told. They have a record shop and a bartender who keeps my father's photograph behind the bar. That's my entire following in Eastbrook."]],
 "If you're going back to Eastbrook, the Timberline does a good whisky. Don't tell them who sent you."],
5:["(Leopold is reading a letter and puts it face down.) The road to the monastery's open again. My brother went up it before the snowploughs had stopped.",[
  ["To pray?","To pray, to read, to sit with Father Thyme and be told how holy he is. Augustus likes the mountain. The mountain likes him back."],
  ["Does that worry you?",["Father Thyme crowned my father, and he'll want a say in crowning me. Or not me.",[
    ["Not you?","There are monks who'd rather see a king who fasts than a king who sails. They haven't said so. Monks never say so."],
    ["Have you talked to Augustus?","We talk about the weather, and books I haven't read. It's how brothers talk in this family."]]]],
  ["Who was the letter from?","My tailor. Grace in Kingsgrad. My father's uniform is being taken in for me. Everything of his is slightly too big."]],
 "Go up the mountain if you like. Wave to my brother. He won't see you; he'll be looking up."],
6:["Have you ever sailed? Properly, I mean, not on a ferry being sick over the rail.",[
  ["Once or twice.","Then you'll understand. My father kept a boat, the Kestrel, laid up at Eastbrook Harbour. He taught me to sail her when I was nine. I fell in twice."],
  ["Only on ferries, being sick.","Honest. The British usually lie about boats. My father would have liked you, and then sent you below to bail."],
  ["Is she still there?",["Under a tarpaulin, with snow on it. Nobody's touched her since he did. I keep meaning to go down.",[
    ["Why don't you?","A prince can't just walk down to a harbour. There's a car, and Oskar, and somebody from the Gazette."],
    ["Will you sail her again?","Before the ice comes in properly, I'd like to. Once. Don't put that in the paper either."]]]]],
 "If you're in Eastbrook, look in on the Kestrel for me. Green hull. Says Kestrel on it, which helps."],
7:["The road north is open, so I suppose Dario will be up here by supper, wanting to buy something.",[
  ["You've met Dario?","Twice. He shakes your hand as if he's checking the weight of it. He made my father laugh, which I could never do."],
  ["What would he buy?","Drilling rights, a mountain, me. He hasn't decided. Oil men take the whole map and work inwards."],
  ["You don't like him.","I don't like being measured. Dario looks at the island like a surveyor. My father at least looked at it like a landlord."]],
 "Frostholm's a long way to go for a handshake. Take a coat."],
8:["(Leopold is alone in the throne room, with a sheaf of papers.) Augustus has gone up the mountain for the Sunday sermon. I'm down here with my father's old speeches.",[
  ["Are they any good?","They're short. He said a king should never speak for longer than a soldier can stand still. Oskar would agree."],
  ["Why didn't you go to church?","Because the whole island would watch me pray and decide whether I meant it. I pray better without an audience."],
  ["Read me one.","'People of Sarivalto. The winter is long, and so are we.' (He lowers the page.) That's the whole of 1963. He was very proud of it."]],
 "Go and hear the sermon if you like. Tell me what they said about him. Tell me honestly."],
9:["Edmund has drawn up the seating plan for the funeral. It has more crossings-out than a peace treaty.",[
  ["Who sits where?","Family at the front. The Church behind. Then the parties, the companies and whatever Gustavo is, in an order Edmund has changed eleven times."],
  ["Is everyone coming?","Everyone who matters, and several who think they do. Anna's coming. I can't decide whether that's respect or reconnaissance."],
  ["Put Phil next to Dario.","(He looks at you for a long moment.) That's either very wicked or very wise. I'll tell Edmund it was my idea."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. Wear something dark. Everyone will."],
10:["(Leopold is in black, turning a pair of grey gloves over in his hands.) Today my father goes down into the crypt. I've been told to wear these. I've been told a great deal.",[
  ["How are you holding up?","Like a tent pole. Straight, and only because of the ropes. Edmund's one of the ropes. Daphne's another."],
  ["Who told you to wear the gloves?","Protocol. A book in the library, older than the chapel. Augustus found the page. He's very good at finding the page."],
  ["I'm sorry, Your Highness.","Thank you. You're the only foreign paper that came. I won't forget it. I'm not sure yet whether that's a threat or a thank you."]],
 "Go on. There's a great deal of island to talk to today."],
11:["It's very quiet. The wreaths are still in the Grand Hall. Kelp Cola's is the size of a cartwheel.",[
  ["Have you read the papers?","No. Edmund hides them, which tells me something. I'll read them when I'm crowned, or when I'm braver."],
  ["What happens now?","Now the island stops looking at the coffin and starts looking at me. I preferred it when I was the one at the back."],
  ["Cartwheel?","With a bottle cap in white lilies in the middle. My father would have laughed. Then he'd have sent it back."]],
 "Take a lily, if you like. There are hundreds. They're making Elsa sneeze."],
12:["(There's a smudge of soot on Leopold's cuff.) I've been writing a speech. The one I'll give when they crown me. I've written it nine times.",[
  ["What happens to the drafts?","The grate. Ingrid sweeps them up in the morning and pretends not to see. She's very good at pretending."],
  ["How does it start?","'People of Sarivalto.' After that it goes wrong every time. Either I sound like him or I sound like I'm apologising for him."],
  ["Just say what you mean.","No king on this island has ever done that. It would cause a panic."]],
 "If you think of a second sentence, I'll pay handsomely. Royalty pays in medals, mostly."],
13:["Oskar tells me Kelp Cola are giving bottles away free in Kingsgrad today. My father's drink, free. He'd have thought it the end of civilisation.",[
  ["Free things are popular.","So I hear. Perhaps I should give away free knighthoods. Edmund says that's what the Syndicate does with favours."],
  ["Phil wants everyone on his side.","Phil wants everyone thirsty. It's the same thing, in Phil's line of work."],
  ["Did your father drink anything else?","Whisky at ten, Kelp Cola at four and tea in between. He was a man of routine. I'm a man of insomnia."]],
 "Don't take the free bottles. They're never free."],
14:["(Leopold is pacing behind the throne.) The whole island, in one place, all at once. I'm expected to be there and to look like a king.",[
  ["What will you say?","As little as possible, as well as possible. My father's rule. Soldiers standing still, and so on."],
  ["Are you nervous?",["Terrified. Don't print it. Print 'composed'. They always printed 'composed' about my father.",[
    ["Was he composed?","He was sick in a flowerpot before his silver jubilee. Edmund moved the flowerpot. Nobody ever knew."],
    ["You'll be fine.","That's what Daphne said, and then she laughed for some time."]]]],
  ["Who do you expect to see?","Everyone who wants me crowned, and everyone who wants me gone. I expect they'll stand quite close together."]],
 "Wish me luck, journalist. A king isn't supposed to need it."],
15:["(Leopold is looking out of the window towards the chapel.) My brother is in the library deciding whether he's my brother or the Church's.",[
  ["Is it that simple?","Nothing about Augustus is simple. He'd follow God off a cliff, and he'd say sorry to the cliff."],
  ["What do you want him to choose?",["Me. Us. The family. I'm aware how that sounds from the man in the big chair.",[
    ["It sounds honest.","Then don't tell anyone. Honesty spreads on this island like a cold."],
    ["It sounds selfish.","Kings are selfish on the island's behalf. That's the theory. I'm still working on the practice."]]]],
  ["Have you asked him?","You don't ask Augustus things. You leave a door open and wait to see if he walks through it."]],
 "Whatever he decides, he's still my brother. Remind me I said that."],
16:["The Communists and the Democrats are debating today. Anna and Erik, arguing about what to do with the island. Nobody invited the island's prince.",[
  ["Would you have gone?","I'd have sat at the back and taken notes. You learn more from the people who want your job than the people who want your autograph."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who talks less. Erik never stops, and Anna never smiles. It'll be close."],
  ["Feeling left out?","A little. My father would have sent a telegram with a single word in it, and the word would have been 'No'."]],
 "If you're there, tell me who clapped. That's always the real news."],
17:["(The chandelier tinkles above the throne.) Did you feel that? The floor moved. Edmund says it's the boiler. Edmund says everything is the boiler.",[
  ["It felt like the ground.","It did. My grandfather's history of the island has a chapter on the mountain in the north. I've never read past the title."],
  ["Is the palace safe?","It's stood for three hundred years. It's survived wars, fires and my father's temper. It can survive a boiler."],
  ["They say Dario's gone missing.","Have they? Then the island's lighter by one handshake. Someone will find him. Someone always finds men like Dario."]],
 "If it happens again, stand under a doorway. My father's advice. He said it about everything."],
18:["Can you keep a secret? Every night, about midnight, there's jazz on the wireless. Kaisa plays it from the radio tower. I listen with the volume very low.",[
  ["Your father didn't approve?","He liked marches. Brass, drums, a lot of men in step. Jazz is a lot of men out of step, beautifully. He'd have hated it."],
  ["Who's your favourite?","Anyone with a saxophone, after eleven. There's a man at a club in Frostholm, Leo, who Kaisa plays sometimes. I'd give my left glove to hear him live."],
  ["So go and hear him.","A prince, in a jazz club in Frostholm? The Gazette would have it on the front page before the second number."]],
 "Don't tell Edmund. He thinks I'm listening to the shipping forecast."],
19:["Have you ever wanted to walk into a room and have nobody stand up?",[
  ["Everyone stands up for you?","Every time. I've never once sat down in a room that wasn't already looking at me. It's very tiring on the knees, theirs and mine."],
  ["I walk into rooms like that daily.","Lucky you. Enjoy it. Order a drink, sit in a corner, listen to the band. Do it once for me."],
  ["You'd need a disguise.","(He looks at you, slightly too long.) A hat, perhaps. A dark coat. It's surprising how few people look past the coat."]],
 "Forget I said anything. Princes say all sorts of things at this time of year."],
20:["(Leopold is at the window.) The steam's still coming off the mountain in the north. And the Kestrel's been hauled up the slip at Eastbrook for the winter. I never sailed her.",[
  ["There's always spring.","There is. A king can sail in spring. A prince could have sailed today. (He shrugs.) Spring, then."],
  ["Who hauled her out?","The harbour men, on my orders. I signed the paper myself. It's the first thing I've ever signed that I wish I hadn't."],
  ["Are you worried about the mountain?","Viktor says the birds have gone quiet. Viktor knows more than the island's geologists, of which there are none."]],
 "Keep your boots by the door tonight. Just in case."],
21:["(Leopold stands on the palace steps in his coat, looking north, with ash drifting on the wind.) The mountain's opened. There's fire in the north and ash coming down on Kingsgrad. And they're all looking at the palace.",[
  ["What will you do?","Stand here, where they can see me. My father stood on these steps through the storm of '62. A king isn't much use, but he can be seen."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. I've sent Oskar down to the town for news. He ran, which is the first time I've seen Oskar run."],
  ["You should go inside.","So should everyone. I'll go in when they do."]],
 "Go carefully. Wet a cloth and keep it over your mouth."],
22:["The ash is in everything: the carpets, the curtains, my father's chair. Elsa is in despair, and Daphne's in a mood.",[
  ["Daphne's in a mood?","She's bored. She says the palace is a tomb with curtains. She's not wrong, but it's the wrong week to say it."],
  ["How's the north?","Lava down the far slopes, and nobody hurt, thank God. Augustus thanked Him at some length. I sent blankets."],
  ["Ash suits the throne room.","Grey on red and gold. Very sombre. Very my father's reign, Daphne would say. Daphne says a lot."]],
 "Shake your coat before you come in next time. Edmund's beyond words."],
23:["I've told Marta to send soup to the people in the north who lost their sheds to the ash. She called me a sentimental boy. Then she made forty litres.",[
  ["Good for you.","My father would have sent a telegram. I'd rather send soup. It's warmer and it doesn't need a reply."],
  ["Is that what a king does?","It's what a king can do. He can't stop a mountain or win a vote. He can order soup, so I've ordered it."],
  ["Will the soup win votes?","(He laughs, properly, for once.) You really are a journalist. Write that it was Marta's soup. She'll want the credit."]],
 "If you see Marta, tell her it was good soup. She won't believe it from me."],
24:["A king is coming. Not me: a real one, from across the water. On the twenty-eighth. The palace has four days to look like it isn't covered in ash.",[
  ["Who is he?","A neighbour with a navy and a long memory. He knew my father. I'm told he'll be watching to see if I'm worth knowing."],
  ["Can you be ready?","Edmund thinks so. Marta thinks not. Viktor has been seen dusting hedges. We'll be ready, or we'll be honest."],
  ["What does he want?","Kings never say what they want. They come to dinner and they say how lovely the fish is, and then you find out a month later."]],
 "Four days. Dust your shoes when you come. Everyone else is dusting theirs."],
25:["I've been reading about parliaments. Britain has one, I'm told, and still keeps a queen. Do you find the arrangement works?",[
  ["Mostly.","Mostly is more than most things on this island manage. My father would have had the man who suggested it rowed out to sea."],
  ["She opens it with a speech.","Written for her by somebody else. (He thinks about it.) That has its appeal. My speeches go in the grate."],
  ["Are you thinking of one?",["I'm thinking. That's all a prince is allowed to do in public.",[
    ["And in private?","In private I think the island might like a king it can argue with. As long as it doesn't win every argument."],
    ["What would Augustus say?","That God didn't put kings on thrones to be outvoted. Then he'd go and pray about it, and come back less sure."]]]]],
 "Don't print that I asked. Print that I'm 'considering all options'. It sounds like a man with a plan."],
26:["Kelp Cola want a word with me. They always want a word. This time they've sent it in a bottle with a bow on it.",[
  ["What do they want?","Phil says it's 'a little something for the crown'. When Phil says little, I lock the silver."],
  ["You could use the money.","Palaces are expensive. My father's roof is older than the Kelp Cola company. (He sighs.) Don't tell Phil I said that either."],
  ["Is there a bow on everything?","Phil would put a bow on a coffin if it sold. He nearly did, last week: you saw the wreath."]],
 "If you see Phil, tell him I'm 'considering'. It keeps him up at night."],
27:["(Leopold is standing very still in his father's dress uniform, with pins in the sleeve.) Grace took it in from Kingsgrad. It still smells of him. Pipe tobacco and cold.",[
  ["It suits you.","It suits the uniform, which is the point. Nobody looks at the man in it. They look at the braid."],
  ["Is this for the visiting king?","It's for the visiting king, the island, the Gazette and my father's portrait in the Grand Hall. Mostly the portrait."],
  ["Mind the pins.","(He winces.) Too late. Princes bleed, apparently. Elsa will have to get it out of the cuff."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. If I look like him, say so. If I don't, also say so."],
28:["(Leopold straightens his collar for the third time.) Today a king comes up the drive, and the whole island has its biggest day at once. Everyone's pulling a different rope.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like a man about to be measured. Quite used to it. My father measured me every day for thirty-four years."],
  ["Who's pulling which rope?","The Party wants red flags, the companies want contracts, the Church wants my brother, and the Democrats want a vote. I want a quiet afternoon. I won't get one."],
  ["Good luck, Your Highness.","Thank you. I'll take it from anyone today, even the foreign press."]],
 "Stand where I can see you. It helps, a friendly face, even a sceptical one."],
29:["The island's deciding who comes after my father: me, or my brother, or nobody at all. I've rewritten that speech once more.",[
  ["How does it start now?","'People of Sarivalto.' And then, for the first time, a second sentence I don't hate. I'm not telling you what it is."],
  ["Have you spoken to Augustus?","This morning. We talked about the weather and a book I haven't read. Then he hugged me, which we don't do. I'm still recovering."],
  ["What if it's nobody?","Then I'll be the last prince of Sarivalto, and I'll go and sail the Kestrel, and nobody will stand up when I walk into the bar."]],
 "Whatever happens, the speech didn't go in the grate. That's something."],
30:["(Leopold sits back in his father's chair for the first time, properly, and looks at the ceiling.) The last day of the month. The island decides what it is. I've done what I can.",[
  ["Are you ready?","No. I don't think my father was either. He just looked it for forty-one years."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Read the papers. Edmund can't hide them forever. And then, whatever I am, I'll go and look at a boat."],
  ["Was it worth it?","Ask me in spring. On the water. You can come, if you promise to bail."]],
 "Thank you, journalist. Whatever you write, write that I tried."],
};
// Linnea, bartender at the Timberline Tavern, Eastbrook (Royal Family). Threads: the late king's photograph behind the bar, keeping the peace between Democrats and drinkers, Veikko and Hely at their table, her loft with Arvid downstairs, her old family loyalty to the crown.
DAILY['Linnea']={
4:["(Linnea polishes a glass and doesn't stop.) The reporter, I presume. Eastbrook's just come out of the snow, and you're in my bar. Sit anywhere. Not that table. That's Veikko's.",[
  ["Whose photograph is that behind the bar?","The old king's. He came in once, in 1967, and had a beer. I framed the glass. It's in the back."],
  ["I'm told you're a royalist in a Democrat town.","I'm a bartender in a Democrat town. I keep my views behind the bar, with the whisky."],
  ["What's good here?","The beer's cold, the fire's warm, and nobody asks questions. Except you, apparently."]],
 "Mind Veikko's chair. He's particular."],
5:["(Linnea is wiping the bar with a long, slow stroke.) Quiet today. The Democrats are at the community centre, and the rest are digging out cars. I've time for a chat, if you've time to listen.",[
  ["Do the Democrats drink here?","Constantly. Erik comes on Fridays, orders a coffee, and leaves a tip big enough for a beer. I don't argue."],
  ["Why keep the king's photograph?","Because a man doesn't stop being a king because he's dead. And because he was kind to my father."],
  ["Is it a safe place to talk?","The safest on the island. I've been told every secret in Eastbrook, and I've forgotten all of them."]],
 "Go easy on the whisky. It's stronger than it looks."],
6:["(Linnea sets a glass on a coaster.) Hely and Veikko were in at lunch, nursing one beer between them. Out of work, both of them. I let them stay. A bar's a better place with company.",[
  ["Do you give them free drinks?","I give them time. It costs me nothing and it's worth more to them."],
  ["What happened to their jobs?","Hely worked at the mill, Veikko elsewhere. Both gone with the snow and the new management. It's been a hard year."],
  ["You're soft-hearted.","Don't spread it around. I've a reputation to keep. A sharp tongue and a short temper."]],
 "Have a peanut. They're free, and nobody counts them."],
7:["(Linnea leans on the bar.) The north-west road's open, and the first Frostholm man was in at noon. Fur collar, loud voice, asked for imported lager. I told him to try the harbour.",[
  ["Not a fan of oil men?","They tip well and talk badly. I've served worse, but not often."],
  ["What did he say?","Something about the future, and prices. He seemed to think he owned the place. I corrected him."],
  ["Does it worry you?","Every stranger worries me a little. The ones with money, a lot."]],
 "If he comes back, I've a very small glass for him."],
8:["(Linnea turns the radio down.) Sunday. Church people drink quietly, and the rest drink less quietly. A calm day, and I like it.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Not on Sundays. I'm open. The Lord understands. He's had a drink or two Himself, by all accounts."],
  ["Is the Father a customer?","Father Thyme? Never. He sends his regards, through a man who drinks for him."],
  ["Is this how you spend Sundays?","Wiping, pouring, listening. And when it's slow, sitting by the fire with the cat."]],
 "Take a seat by the stove. It's warmest there."],
9:["(Linnea chalks a price on the board.) The cola men have been in, handing out little bottles to taste. I don't mind. I let them sit at the bar, as long as they buy a beer afterwards.",[
  ["Is the cola any good?","It's sweet. And fizzy. Hely said it tasted like cough medicine with ambition."],
  ["Will you stock it?","If it sells. A bar has no loyalty except to the till."],
  ["Is there a new formula?","They say so. I say, leave the cola alone. The island's changing enough."]],
 "A little fizz never hurt anyone. Much."],
10:["(Linnea has a black ribbon on the frame of the king's photograph.) Today's his funeral. I've closed till noon, and I've lit a candle. Even the Democrats took their caps off as they passed.",[
  ["Are you going to Kingsgrad?","I can't leave the bar. But I'll play the radio. They're broadcasting the service."],
  ["How do the Democrats feel?","Respectful. Erik shook my hand. He didn't have to, and I won't forget it."],
  ["What was he to you?","A fixed point. In a changing world, there was always the king. Now there's a photograph."]],
 "Raise a glass to him later. Quietly."],
11:["(Linnea is cutting lemons.) The funeral was lovely, they say. The radio said the island wept. I didn't. I washed glasses, and let the tears fall in the sink.",[
  ["Was the bar busy?","Packed. All of Eastbrook wanted to talk about it. Even the sceptics."],
  ["Did anyone say anything notable?","Maya said the old king was kind. Dex said he was dull. They were both right."],
  ["What now?","The prince will decide. Or the island will. I serve whoever's thirsty."]],
 "Squeeze the lime. It's the only thing here that's fresh."],
12:["(Linnea pins a handwritten note to the wall.) Ruby's having a record night, they say. Something about a rare disc, and the Democrats. I'm getting a crate of beer ready in case.",[
  ["Is Ruby a friend?","A neighbour. She plays Coltrane at a volume that rattles my glasses. I'd miss it if she stopped."],
  ["Is there trouble about the record?","A man from Frostholm was asking. Ruby won't sell. She's stubborn, like most of us here."],
  ["Will you go?","If I can leave the bar. Arvid will cover. He's good with beer, and bad with conversation."]],
 "Ask Ruby about the record. She won't tell you, but it's fun to watch."],
13:["(Linnea sorts the bottles on the back shelf.) Tomorrow's the great day. The tavern will be full by dawn, and empty by midnight. I've ordered twice the beer.",[
  ["Do you know what's happening?","Only what everyone says. A gathering, and a decision. Which means a hangover for somebody."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a bartender. I'm always worried. It's called inventory."],
  ["Who'll be here?","Everyone. And those who aren't will send someone to listen."]],
 "Stay late. It'll be a night to remember."],
14:["(Linnea has the radio on, low.) The big day, and the tavern's quiet. They're all out there, listening, watching. I've never been so alone in a full bar.",[
  ["Is it as you expected?","It's strange. Nobody's drinking. Everyone's thinking. That's rarer than a good whisky."],
  ["What does the old king's photograph say?","Nothing. He's looking at the door, as he always did."],
  ["How are you feeling?","Like a woman holding her breath. I'll let it out at closing time."]],
 "A drink on the house, once it's over."],
15:["(Linnea stacks clean glasses.) The morning after. Half the town's in here, talking in whispers. Hely says it's the best time of her life. Veikko says it's the worst.",[
  ["What do you say?","I say it's Monday, and I've a bar to run."],
  ["Did you see anything yesterday?","I saw a lot of faces, and every one was different. That's how I know something happened."],
  ["Is the tavern changing?","Every day. People come in looking for answers. I serve them a pint, which is cheaper."]],
 "Sip slowly. The bar's open till midnight."],
16:["(Linnea pours a beer for herself.) A debate tonight, the Party against the Democrats. The whole town will want a seat, and none of them will want to pay. I'm putting up the price.",[
  ["Will you listen?","On the radio, behind the bar. I'll cheer for whoever's funniest."],
  ["Who are you voting for?","I'm voting with my feet. Away from any man who shouts."],
  ["What do you make of the Party?","Red flags and long speeches. They drink a lot, for people who disapprove of pleasure."]],
 "Don't heckle in here. I've a very long memory."],
17:["(Linnea steadies a tilting bottle.) The shelf shook. A glass fell, and I caught it. First time in my life. It must be the volcano. Or my nerves.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","Not frightened. Just aware that the floor isn't as solid as I thought."],
  ["Did you feel it, too?","Arvid felt it in the pot. He said the soup rippled. A trembling stew, if you can imagine."],
  ["What do the regulars say?","Veikko's convinced it's the end of the world. Hely says it's the best excuse yet for another drink."]],
 "If it shakes again, duck behind the bar."],
18:["(Linnea sniffs the air.) Do you smell that? Rotten eggs. It's been coming down the valley since breakfast. I've lit the fire to cover it, but it's no good.",[
  ["It's the volcano?","So they say. The old men are looking north and muttering. It never ends well."],
  ["Is business affected?","People drink more when they're nervous. I'm doing very well, and I hate it."],
  ["Do you want to leave?","Never. A bartender doesn't leave her bar. She goes down with it, pouring."]],
 "Open the window. I'd rather have the cold than the smell."],
19:["(Linnea adjusts a feathered mask on the shelf.) A masquerade at the Midnight Note tonight, and half my regulars are going. Hely's found a costume. Veikko's borrowed a moustache.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm needed here. But I'd love to see a face I don't know, for once."],
  ["Is it a royal affair?","I've heard whispers about a prince in disguise. I'm saying nothing, but I'm smiling."],
  ["Do you approve?","If it keeps the island in good spirits, I'll lend my own moustache."]],
 "I'll hear all about it tomorrow. It's the barman's reward."],
20:["(Linnea is hanging a lamp.) A rumour about a robbery at the palace tonight. I don't believe it. Nobody would dare. And yet everyone's talking about it, so someone will.",[
  ["Who would steal from the palace?","Anyone who thinks it's easy. I've served a good few, and none were very clever."],
  ["Would it bother you?","It's the king's house. Yes. It would."],
  ["Is the Syndicate behind it?","I couldn't say. I serve them beer. I don't ask what they do between pints."]],
 "Lock up well tonight. Just in case."],
21:["(Linnea dusts ash off the bar.) It's erupted. The whole sky's grey, and the ash is coming down like snow. I've closed the shutters, but it's creeping in under the door.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. God be thanked. The lava's up north, far from us."],
  ["How's the tavern?","Full. People want company, and the fire. I'm serving soup, and nobody's complaining."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A little. But I've the whole of Eastbrook around me, and that's a comfort."]],
 "Stay in. Drink. The ash won't last."],
22:["(Linnea sweeps ash from the doorstep.) It's on everything. In the beer, in the bread, in the cat's fur. I've named the cat Ash. She doesn't mind.",[
  ["Is business good?","Booming. People can't work, so they drink. I'm sorry to say it."],
  ["How's the mood in town?","Tired, but kind. Everyone's checking on their neighbours. Even the Democrats are bringing soup to the royalists."],
  ["Would the old king approve?","He'd say it's what a community is for. And he'd have a beer."]],
 "Wipe your feet. I'm sick of sweeping."],
23:["(Linnea listens for a rumble.) The aftershocks come and go. I find I've started counting them, like a clock. One every hour, roughly. It's almost soothing.",[
  ["Is that how you cope?","I count, I pour, I wipe. It helps."],
  ["Any gossip tonight?","A shipment of something at the port, they say. And a pipe that went wrong in Frostholm. I'm not following either."],
  ["Is Arvid all right?","He's cooking for half the street, and singing. He sings when he's frightened."]],
 "If the glasses rattle, hold on to the bar."],
24:["(Linnea wipes the king's photograph.) The Church is going up to the mountains. A retreat. Veikko's said he'll join. I told him he'd miss the beer. He thought about it, and stayed.",[
  ["Is the whole town leaving?","Not at all. The monks are going to pray. The rest of us are going to drink, and hope."],
  ["Do you worry about the Church?","I worry about everyone, a little. It's a bartender's habit."],
  ["Is it a good time to travel?","With the ash and the shakes? I'd stay by the fire."]],
 "Keep your coat on. The fire's burning low."],
25:["(Linnea arranges a bowl of nuts.) The princess is touring the island, they say, with a Democrat in tow. It's the talk of the tavern. Some call it treason, others a romance.",[
  ["What do you think?","I think she's young, and thoughtful, and she'll go far. Whatever side she chooses."],
  ["Would she be welcome here?","With open arms. I'd serve her the best I have. Without the whisky."],
  ["Do your regulars agree?","Veikko says she's a traitor. Hely says she's a star. They've been arguing for an hour."]],
 "Pick a side, or pick a seat. Both are free."],
26:["(Linnea checks the stock.) Cola men giving away bottles, oil men buying rounds, Party men handing out leaflets. The whole island's in my bar, and none of them wants to pay.",[
  ["Is it hard to keep the peace?","It's the only thing I do. A little tact, a little firmness, a lot of ice."],
  ["Who's the worst?","The oil man in the fur collar. He called my beer cold. It's meant to be."],
  ["Will you survive?","I always do. The bar's the one thing that stays when everything else goes."]],
 "If you see a man with a bottle of cola, point him my way."],
27:["(Linnea writes a sign.) Closed early tomorrow, for the big push. Or open late. I haven't decided. It depends on who's winning.",[
  ["Do you have a favourite?","The king's portrait's looking at me. I'd say it favours the royalists."],
  ["Will you vote?","If there's a vote, yes. For whoever lets me keep my bar."],
  ["Are the regulars ready?","They've been ready since the snow melted. They're just waiting for someone to tell them what for."]],
 "A good night's sleep, and a stiff drink. That's my prescription."],
28:["(Linnea pours a small beer for the king's photograph.) Today every faction's pushing. The bar's been full since dawn. I've heard six speeches and I've understood none.",[
  ["Is it exciting?","It's loud. And I'd like a quiet corner and a cat."],
  ["What's the mood?","Like the moment before a storm. Everyone's laughing too loudly."],
  ["Will you go out?","Never. The bar's my battlefield."]],
 "Stay close. If it gets rough, I'll hide you in the cellar."],
29:["(Linnea sits at her own bar for the first time.) Tomorrow it's over. Whatever happens, we'll all be here. Older, perhaps. A bit greyer, with ash in our hair.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a bartender. I'm always hopeful. It's how you make a living."],
  ["What would you like to see?","A king, a vote, a peaceful island. I'd take any two."],
  ["Is there anything you'll miss?","The old king, and the snow. They were both quiet."]],
 "Take a stool. This one's on me."],
30:["(Linnea lifts her glass to the king's photograph.) The last day. The island's about to find out. I've swept the floor, polished the bar, and opened the good bottle.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Calm. I've done what I can. Now it's the island's turn."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The snow leaving, the king going, and you sitting on that stool, asking questions."],
  ["Any last words?","Tip your bartender. And don't believe everything you read."]],
 "Thank you for coming. Mind the ash on the step."],
};
// Lisa, on the till at the supermarket in Kingsgrad (the Shopkeepers). Threads: the biscuit tin she's saving in to leave the
// island (London, she hopes), what people buy at her till and what it tells her, and the delivery boat that's always late.
DAILY['Lisa']={
2:["(Lisa beams from behind the till.) Oh! You're new. You're the reporter, aren't you? From London? Actual London?",[
  ["Actual London.","I knew it! The coat. Nobody here has a coat like that. Is it true there are shops on every street? Shops on top of shops?"],
  ["What's it like working here?","Bread, cola, beer, cigarettes. Bread, cola, beer, cigarettes. Sometimes someone buys a drill and I nearly faint with excitement."],
  ["Do you get many customers?","Today? Everyone. The road's open, and Kingsgrad's been eating tins for a fortnight. I've sold a mountain of bread already."]],
 "Come back and tell me about London. Please. I'll give you a discount. (She lowers her voice.) I can't actually give you a discount."],
3:["(Lisa is stacking cans into a pyramid.) Don't touch it. It took me twenty minutes. If you take the bottom one, I'll cry.",[
  ["It's very impressive.","Thank you! Nobody ever says that. Birgit at the Nordmart in Frostholm does hers in straight lines. Straight lines! Where's the joy?"],
  ["Why a pyramid?","Because people buy the top one. Then the next. If you stack them flat, they think you've got too many and walk away. It's psychology."],
  ["(Take the bottom one.)","(The pyramid stays up, somehow. She stares at you.) That's never happened before. You must be very lucky. Or a witch."]],
 "Mind the pyramid on your way out."],
4:["Can I tell you a secret? (She leans over the till.) I'm leaving the island. One day. I'm saving.",[
  ["Where are you going?","London. Where else? I'll work in one of those big shops with the lifts and the perfume counter. I'll wear a uniform with a little badge."],
  ["How much have you saved?","Two hundred and eleven dollars. In a biscuit tin under my bed. Danish butter biscuits, the blue tin. I ate the biscuits first, obviously."],
  ["Why leave?",["Because I've lived on this island twenty-four years and I've seen all of it. Twice. I want to see something I haven't seen.",[
    ["Won't you miss it?","The snow, maybe. The people. Not the till. (She pats it.) Sorry. I'll miss you a bit."],
    ["What's the fare to London?","Boat to Helsinki, train, another boat, another train. About six hundred, with a room for the first month. So: four hundred to go."]]]]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the tin. Especially not my mother."],
5:["You can tell everything about a person by what they put on the till. (She nods at the door.) Him, for instance. Bertil.",[
  ["What did Bertil buy?","One tin of tomato soup, one loaf of bread, one bar of plain chocolate. Every day. For four years. Never, ever the milk chocolate."],
  ["What does that say about him?","That he's a man who knows what he likes, and is frightened of what he doesn't. And he's lonely. Nobody buys one bar of chocolate if they've got someone to share it."],
  ["What would my shopping say?","Show me your basket. (She looks.) You're new here, you eat on the move, and you don't sleep enough. Is that right? It's always right."]],
 "Come back with a fuller basket. I'll tell you your fortune."],
6:["(Lisa is looking out of the window towards the harbour.) The delivery boat's late. Third time this month. We're out of milk, eggs and patience.",[
  ["Where does the boat come from?","Helsinki, every Friday, in theory. In practice, whenever the sea lets it. The captain's called Pekka. He says the sea and he don't get on."],
  ["What will people do without milk?","Complain. Mostly to me. 'Lisa, where's the milk?' As if I keep it in my pocket. I'll put a sign up: 'Milk: ask the sea.'"],
  ["What else is short?","Coffee. That's the real trouble. Doctor Lind came in for coffee and there wasn't any, and he just stood there. I think he might have cried."]],
 "If you see a boat, tell me. I'll be watching the window."],
7:["Two hundred and nineteen dollars. (She holds up the blue tin, which she's brought to work.) I put eight in last night. Overtime.",[
  ["You bring your savings to work?","I couldn't leave it alone. I kept thinking a burglar would get it. Then I thought, a burglar here would know where to look. So I brought it."],
  ["Is that safe?","It's under the till. Nobody looks under the till. Except me, every ten minutes, to check it's there."],
  ["What's the overtime for?","Restocking. The boat came in at midnight. I stacked till two. A tin of peaches fell on my foot. (She wiggles it.) Worth eight dollars, that foot."]],
 "Don't say anything about the tin. Shh."],
8:["Oil on the sea, they're saying. The fishermen were in for beer this morning, and none of them was laughing.",[
  ["What did they say?","That it's black off the west of the island, from the rig. Sam from Saltwick said Diversified Petroleum should be made to drink it."],
  ["Will it affect the shop?","If the fish go off, people buy more tins. That's good for the shop and bad for everyone. That's how shops work, mostly."],
  ["Did you hear the sermon?","On the radio behind the till. Very nice. I cried a little. Then a man wanted cigarettes, and I had to stop crying and find his change."]],
 "If it's a bad sea, it's a bad week."],
9:["(Lisa has a pencil behind her ear and a list in her hand.) Everyone's buying cola. Every crate's gone. They say Kelp Cola's making a new flavour.",[
  ["What flavour would you want?","Strawberry. Pink. In a tall bottle with a curly straw. Nobody on this island would buy it. Everyone in London would."],
  ["Who's been buying the most?","Funny thing: a man from the Communist Party. Bought six bottles. Said it was 'for research'. Then he bought a bag of sugar. Big bag."],
  ["Do you drink it?","Every day. One bottle at lunch. It's the only thing I spend money on that isn't the tin. My little luxury."]],
 "Strawberry. Write it down. You'll see."],
10:["(The shop's half-dark. Lisa is in a black dress behind the till.) We're open. The manager says people need bread on sad days too.",[
  ["Are you going to the funeral?","After the morning rush. I'll stand outside the palace with everyone else. My mother's going. She's been crying since the news came."],
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","Once, when I was six. He came round the shops at Christmas. He bought a bag of oranges and gave me one. I kept the peel for a year."],
  ["People are still shopping?","Black ribbons and handkerchiefs. I've sold out of both. And bread. Always bread."]],
 "Funny day. Everyone's quiet. It's nice and it's horrible."],
11:["(Lisa is counting money from the tin.) Two hundred and thirty-four. Don't look at me like that. I was nearly at two hundred and fifty, and then my boots split.",[
  ["Couldn't you mend them?","Helmi in Saltwick mended them twice. The third time she just looked at them and said 'Lisa'. In that voice. So I bought new."],
  ["How long till London?","At this rate? Two years. Unless something wonderful happens. Like a rich old lady leaving me her fortune for helping with her shopping."],
  ["Do you know any rich old ladies?","Grace, the tailor. She's not rich, she's comfortable. And she'd never leave me anything. She'd leave it all to her mannequins."]],
 "Two years. Unless something wonderful happens."],
12:["Guess who came to my till last night? Dario. From the oil company. He bought a pint of milk. Himself!",[
  ["Why is that surprising?","Because men like Dario don't buy milk. Someone buys milk for them. He looked at the till like he'd never seen one."],
  ["What else did he buy?","Nothing. A pint of milk and a newspaper from Ville's stand on the way in. He was reading the arts page. Dario. The arts page."],
  ["Did you talk to him?","He said 'thank you' and left the change. Ten dollars. (She waves the note.) That's going in the tin. Thank you, Dario."]],
 "Ten dollars richer. I like the oil company today."],
13:["Free cola day! Kelp Cola's handing it out everywhere. We've been told to give a free bottle with every loaf. My hand hurts.",[
  ["Is it good for business?","It's lovely. Everyone's smiling. They come in for a free bottle and leave with bread, butter and a cake. Harald's paying for my wages, really."],
  ["Who's the most cola anyone's taken?","Toni from the arcade. He came through the till four times in four different hats. I knew it was him. I said nothing. He looked so happy."],
  ["Are you voting for Harald?","I can't vote. I'm not a Republican. I'm a supermarket. (She laughs.) If I could, I'd vote for whoever gives everyone free things. Wouldn't you?"]],
 "One free bottle with every loaf. Have a loaf."],
14:["(Lisa is taking off her apron.) The manager's closing for the big day. Everyone's going. You'll be there? With your notebook?",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","Something exciting. Something that makes the island interesting, so I don't have to go to London to have a life. (She thinks.) I'd still go."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Of crowds? No. I see the whole of Kingsgrad every week at the till. I know who owes who. It's a family, really. A big, loud, nosy family."],
  ["Who will you stand with?","My mother, and Freja from the furniture shop. Freja will tell us where to stand. She always knows where everything should go."]],
 "Big day! Wear something warm."],
15:["I've been listening to the customers all morning. The Church and the palace, the palace and the Church. That's all anyone's buying today: argument.",[
  ["What are people saying?","The old ladies are for Augustus. Holy, quiet, good boy. The young ones like Leopold. Handsome, sure of himself. It's like choosing a husband."],
  ["Who would you choose?","Daphne. Neither of the boys. Princess Daphne came in once and bought a pair of nylons and a bar of chocolate and paid with coins. She's lovely."],
  ["Is the till busy?","Busy enough. People buy more when they argue. I don't know why. Maybe it's hungry work."]],
 "Pick a husband. Then come back and tell me who."],
16:["(The radio's on behind the till: the debate.) Anna's very clever. I don't agree with her. But she's very clever.",[
  ["Who do you agree with?","Erik, I suppose. Everyone gets a vote. Even me. A supermarket girl voting! My mother would have a heart attack."],
  ["Would the Communists let you leave?","(She stops.) I hadn't thought of that. Mike from Saltwick said nobody in the Soviet Union needs to leave. I said that's not what I asked."],
  ["Turn it off and talk to me.","Gladly. Tell me about London. Have you ever been to a shop with a lift? What's on the top floor? Is it hats? I bet it's hats."]],
 "Hats on the top floor. I'm sure of it."],
17:["(Tins rattle on the shelves.) Did you feel it? Again! My pyramid fell down. Twenty minutes, gone.",[
  ["Was it an earthquake?","A little one. They're saying the mountain up north's waking up. The farmers came in for candles and tins and looked very worried."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. The tin's fine. I checked. (She checks again.) Still fine."],
  ["Heard about Dario?","Missing! Can you imagine? I sold him milk last week. I keep thinking I should tell someone. But what would I say? 'He likes the arts page'?"]],
 "Help me with the pyramid? Just the top three."],
18:["(Lisa has a sign up: CANDLES: 2 PER CUSTOMER.) Everyone wants candles. Everyone wants tins. The manager says ration them, so I'm rationing them.",[
  ["Is it panic buying?","It's not panic. It's sensible. If the mountain goes, who's going to bring milk? Pekka? Pekka can't get here on a calm day."],
  ["How's the tin?","Two hundred and forty-one. But I bought candles for my mother. And tins. So that's two thirty-five. The volcano's costing me London."],
  ["Who's buying the most?","Frank from the gun shop bought forty tins of beans. Forty! He said they're for 'October'. I don't know what happens in October."]],
 "Two candles each. Even for London reporters."],
19:["(Lisa looks tired.) I didn't sleep. The ground kept moving, and the tin kept rattling under the bed, and I kept thinking it was a burglar.",[
  ["Do you want to leave sooner?","I thought about it all night. I could go now, with what I've got. Get as far as Helsinki. Then what? Sleep on a bench?"],
  ["Is your mother all right?","She's packed a bag and put it by the door. She won't say what's in it. I looked. Photographs, her good teapot and a tin of ham."],
  ["Want me to tell you about London?",["Yes. Please. Anything. Tell me about the shop with the lift.",[
    ["Hats are on the fourth floor.","I knew it! (She clasps her hands.) Hats on the fourth floor. I'll go there first. I'll try on every one."],
    ["The top floor's the restaurant.","A restaurant. In a shop. (She stares at you.) I'm going to have tea there. In a hat. That's the plan now."]]]]],
 "Thank you. I feel better. Isn't that silly?"],
20:["Two candles per customer. (She points to the sign.) Two. Even for the palace. Edmund came in for twenty. I gave him two.",[
  ["What did Edmund say?","He said, 'Do you know who I am?' I said, 'You're Edmund, you buy shortbread on Thursdays.' He took his two candles and left."],
  ["Are the farmers coming south?","All morning. Hilda and Olaf from East Farm came in with a cow on a rope. A cow! In the bread aisle! The manager had a fit."],
  ["Are you scared?","Yes. But I've got the tin, and my mother's got her teapot. If we need to run, we're ready to run."]],
 "Keep a candle in your pocket. Just in case."],
21:["(The windows are grey. Lisa is sweeping ash out of the door with a broom.) It went up. The mountain. Just like that. Everybody's all right, they say.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Mum's fine. She sat in the kitchen holding the teapot. The tin's fine. (She laughs, a bit wildly.) Everything's fine. Everything's grey."],
  ["Is the shop open?","Of course. People need bread on grey days too. And masks. Doctor Lind brought us a box of cloth masks. I've given out sixty."],
  ["What does it look like up north?","Kaisa says on the radio there's lava in the hills and ash everywhere. And smoke you can see from Saltwick. I'm not going to look. I've seen enough grey."]],
 "Take a mask. Doctor's orders."],
22:["(Lisa is wiping every tin on the shelf with a damp cloth.) Ash in everything. Every tin, every loaf, every bottle. I've wiped three hundred tins.",[
  ["Can I help?","Here. (She hands you a cloth.) Tins on the left. Wipe, turn, wipe. Don't stack them in a pyramid yet. We're in mourning for the pyramid."],
  ["Is the delivery boat coming?","Pekka telephoned from Helsinki. He's not coming till the ash settles. Says his boat's too pretty to get dirty. Milk, never."],
  ["How's the tin?","Two hundred and twenty-eight. I gave some to Sirpa from Saltwick. She was in with nothing and two children. It's only money. London can wait a week."]],
 "Wipe, turn, wipe. You're a natural."],
23:["(Lisa has a postcard on the till: a big red bus.) Look what came! From my cousin. She lives in Stockholm. She sent me a London card she bought there.",[
  ["What does it say?","'Wish you were here.' But she's not there. She's in Stockholm. She just liked the bus. (She props it up.) I like the bus too."],
  ["Would you go to Stockholm instead?","Maybe. It's closer. It's cheaper. But it's not London. If I'm leaving, I want to really leave."],
  ["Is everyone talking about the vote?","The Republicans. The old men from the shops: Frank, Jack, Otto, the doctor. They come in for bread and argue by the beer. Jack's thinking about Carlo, apparently."]],
 "A red bus. One day."],
24:["(The pyramid's back, three tins high.) Small pyramid. Practice pyramid. The ground's stopped shaking, so I thought I'd try.",[
  ["It looks good.","It looks brave. That's what it is. A brave pyramid. When it's ten tins high, I'll know the island's all right."],
  ["How's your mother?","She's unpacked her bag. Except the teapot. She's keeping the teapot by the door. 'You never know, Lisa.'"],
  ["Is the till busy?","Busy with people buying soap. Everyone wants to wash. Ash in the hair, ash in the sheets. I've sold more soap this week than all year."]],
 "Three tins. Tomorrow, four."],
25:["You know what I've been thinking? If the island goes to the Russians, can I still go to London?",[
  ["Probably not easily.","That's what I thought. Mike from Saltwick says I'd 'not want to'. He keeps telling me what I'd want. It's very annoying."],
  ["What if the Democrats win?","Then I could vote, and stay, and still go. Erik's people came in with leaflets. One was very nice. Ilse, from the mill. She said 'whatever you want, Lisa'."],
  ["What do you want?","(She thinks.) I want to choose. That's all. London or here. Not to have someone choose for me."]],
 "Whatever happens, I'm keeping the tin."],
26:["(Lisa is reading a letter at the till, smiling.) Two hundred and fifty! I hit two hundred and fifty! Grace came in and tipped me. Grace!",[
  ["Why did Grace tip you?","I carried her shopping home. She's got a bad hip and won't say so. She gave me five dollars and said 'Don't spend it on cola.'"],
  ["Did you spend it on cola?","Of course not. It's in the tin. London money's sacred. Cola money comes out of my wages."],
  ["What's the letter?","From Pekka, the boat man! The boats are running again on Friday. And there's milk on board. Milk, after all this. I could weep."]],
 "Milk on Friday! Spread the word."],
27:["(Lisa is restocking the beer, lots of it.) The manager says everyone'll want beer tonight. Big card game at the pizzeria. Everyone who matters.",[
  ["Who's going?","Everyone who buys cigars. Carlo bought a box this morning. Harald's driver bought two. Even Dario's people. The cigar shelf's empty."],
  ["Would you ever play?","Poker? With my tin? Never. I'd lose London in one hand. I've seen how men look when they come out of that pizzeria."],
  ["What's on at the cinema?","Otto's always got the flying picture. Sometimes I go and sit in the back on my evening off. It's warm and nobody asks me for change."]],
 "No poker for me. I'm a saver."],
28:["(The shop's busy and loud. Flags, leaflets and badges on everyone.) Everyone's out! Rallies, flags, a foreign king. And they all want sandwiches.",[
  ["Who's buying the most sandwiches?","The Democrats, for their rally. Twenty loaves. The palace, for the state visit: forty jars of jam. The Communists bought bread and nothing on it."],
  ["Are you joining in?","I'm on the till. I'm always on the till. (She grins.) But I'm wearing three badges under my apron. One from each. Nobody can say I'm not friendly."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","Everyone thinks it's their day. Somebody's going to be very disappointed. I hope it's not me. I hope it's not the island."]],
 "Big day. Want a sandwich? We've got plenty of bread."],
29:["(Lisa is quiet, counting the tin under the till.) Two hundred and sixty-three. I've counted it three times today. It's something to do with my hands.",[
  ["Are you nervous about tomorrow?","Everyone is. You can feel it at the till. People are buying too much, or nothing at all. That's how you know they're scared."],
  ["Is it enough for London?","Not yet. But it's more than I had. And I've learned things. Like that the island might change without me leaving it."],
  ["The Republicans are voting today.","I know. Otto and the doctor were in for sandwiches to take to Frostholm. Frank bought a flask. Jack bought a lucky packet of cigarettes. He doesn't smoke."]],
 "Two hundred and sixty-three. Not bad for a supermarket girl."],
30:["(Lisa is behind the till, looking out of the window.) Last day. Whatever happens, I'll be on the till. Isn't that funny? The world changes and I'm still on the till.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","An island where I can choose. Go or stay. London or here. That's what I want. (She smiles.) And milk on Friday."],
  ["Will you still go to London?","One day. Maybe. Or maybe London's going to come here. Someone like you, with a coat and stories. (She grins.) That counts a bit."],
  ["What will you do tonight?","Count the tin. Have a cola. Listen to the radio with Mum and her teapot. Whatever happens, we'll hear it together."]],
 "Thank you for the stories about London. I mean it."],
};
// Liv, customs officer at the Customs Shed in Eastbrook Harbour (Communist Party). Threads: stamps and forms by the book, the Party's quiet eyes, the parcel boat of the 10th, Tor next door in the Lofts, her tea, the ash.
DAILY['Liv']={
4:["(Liv stamps a form without looking up.) Eastbrook's open, and so is the harbour. I'm Liv, customs. If it comes in, I see it. If it goes out, I see it twice.",[
  ["Nice to meet you. Is it busy?","It will be. Every boat in the north is lining up for a stamp."],
  ["What do you look for?","Anything that doesn't match its paper. Paper is the truth, or very close to it."],
  ["Do you ever let things slide?","Never. Ask anyone. They'll tell you, with feeling."]],
 "Keep your papers dry."],
5:["(Liv sorts forms into a box.) A boat from the monastery side today, with a crate marked 'devotional'. Devotional what, I asked. The monk smiled. I stamped it anyway.",[
  ["What was in it?","Candles. I counted. Three too many for the form."],
  ["Do you check monks too?","Especially monks. Piety hides a lot of string."],
  ["Is the Party interested in your findings?","The Party's interested in everything. I just stamp."]],
 "Count twice."],
6:["(Liv pours tea from a flask.) Someone telephoned the harbour office this morning, from a number I didn't know. Wanted to know the sailing times. Very polite. I said they're posted on the wall.",[
  ["Who was it?","Wouldn't say. People who won't say are my speciality."],
  ["Why do you think they asked?","A boat's coming in, probably. The next days will be interesting."],
  ["Do you tell anyone?","I tell my forms. And now and then, a friend."]],
 "Tea helps."],
7:["(Liv glances at a manifest.) The north-west's open now, and the Frostholm trade will hit us by Monday. Oil company crates, strange forms. I like strange forms. They tell me everything.",[
  ["What's strange about them?","Dates written in different hands. Weights that don't quite add."],
  ["Do you like Diversified?","They pay on time. That's not the same as liking."],
  ["Would you tell Anna about odd crates?","The Party likes to know where the island's money goes."]],
 "Honest paper, honest man."],
8:["(Liv leans on the counter.) Sunday and the shed's open. Customs don't keep the Sabbath. The shipping bell tolls from the monastery, and I stamp in time to it.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Not for years. I'd rather read a good ledger."],
  ["Are the Communists against religion?","We're for the workers. The Church is for the monks. They can wait."],
  ["Is there trouble brewing?","Some. Father Thyme's sermon troubles are making the Church nervy."]],
 "Keep your temper."],
9:["(Liv sniffs a crate.) Sugar. Tons of it. Someone's making a lot of cola. Kelp's sending sacks to Kingsgrad, and the Party wants a share for its own recipe.",[
  ["Is the Party making cola?","Helen's got a notebook full of formulas. I've seen one. It had beetroot."],
  ["Does Kelp pay duty?","Every grain, if I have anything to do with it."],
  ["Would you drink beetroot cola?","For the cause. Once."]],
 "Pay duty."],
10:["(Liv stands at the end of the quay, arms folded.) A boat is due this morning, with a parcel for somebody who wants it quiet. I've seen the telegram. I'll be on the jetty, in my uniform, and I'll check it properly.",[
  ["What's in the parcel?","I'll find out when I open it. That's rather the point of me."],
  ["Is it the king's funeral today?","It is. Black flags all over the harbour. A boat of mourners left at dawn."],
  ["Is someone going to try to dodge you?","Everyone tries. A few succeed. I'm working on that."]],
 "Not a word to anyone."],
11:["(Liv taps a form.) Mill hands marching for a fair day's pay, and the Party in the front. Quite right. Fair pay is the foundation of everything. Also, it's good for the Party's reputation.",[
  ["Are you going to march?","I'm on duty. But I'm marching in spirit, with a stamp."],
  ["Do you know the mill hands?","Half of them live in the Lofts, with me. Tor's cousin is one."],
  ["Is it a good cause?","The best. Even Henrik admits it, and he admits nothing."]],
 "March carefully."],
12:["(Liv scans a ledger.) Something odd today. Oil company accounts shifting about like fish in a net. I've noticed the numbers don't tally. Someone's been creative.",[
  ["How creative?","Very. If I were an accountant, I'd be envious."],
  ["Who's behind it?","The man behind the desk at Diversified, I suppose. It's usually the man behind the desk."],
  ["Will you report it?","I'll write it down, and the Party will read it. Quiet eyes, as they say."]],
 "Keep it out of the papers."],
13:["(Liv inspects a box of bottles.) Free samples, every one stamped and counted. The Kelp boys are handing them out. I made them fill in a form for each bottle. They looked pained.",[
  ["Surely that's excessive?","Excessive is the word I live by."],
  ["Did you try one?","In the line of duty. For testing. It tasted of pear drops and ambition."],
  ["What about the Syndicate's recipe?","I hear there's a secret one. I haven't seen it. Yet."]],
 "Don't litter."],
14:["(Liv adjusts her cap.) Tomorrow's the big day, the fourteenth. Everyone's heading to it. The harbour's emptier than I've ever seen. I stay at my desk. Somebody has to.",[
  ["Are you not going?","I'll go in the evening, if the paperwork lets me."],
  ["What are you expecting?","Crowds. Flags. A lot of lost hats."],
  ["Is the Party excited?","Anna's got a speech. She's had it ready since breakfast."]],
 "Come back and tell me."],
15:["(Liv hums.) Crown or cross. The harbour's full of men who look at each other like cats. I'd stamp the lot of them, if I could.",[
  ["Who's on which side?","The palace men wear gold buttons, the church men wear grey. It's easy at customs."],
  ["Does the Party care?","The Party cares about the crowd, not the quarrel. A quarrel splits the crowd."],
  ["What do you think?","I think I'd rather neither of them had a boat."]],
 "Keep your buttons."],
16:["(Liv nods.) The debate today. The Party against the Democrats. Radio's on at the shed. Erik's too polite and Helen's not polite enough. It'll be a fine contest.",[
  ["Who do you back?","The Party, naturally, on paper. In practice I back the better argument."],
  ["Do you like Erik?","Everyone does. It's his main weakness."],
  ["Will it change minds?","One or two. Which is plenty, if they're yours."]],
 "Listen carefully."],
17:["(Liv puts down her stamp.) Did you feel that? The whole shed rattled. The pens on the desk are all lined up now, on one side. Not a good sign.",[
  ["The earth shook?","Gently. Like a cat shifting on a lap."],
  ["Are you scared?","Not yet. I'm cataloguing it."],
  ["Have you heard of Dario?","Gone, they say. Vanished from Frostholm, and the oil men are being very calm. Too calm."]],
 "Stay alert."],
18:["(Liv peers through a window.) Steam on the horizon. A white plume above the mountain. Everyone's pretending not to see it. I've noted it in the log.",[
  ["You log the mountain?","I log everything that arrives. And it's arrived."],
  ["Is there talk of a relic?","The monks have lost something and want it back quietly. I received a form, in confidence."],
  ["What about Kelp's cup?","Kelpy. Every child in Eastbrook wants one. The factory's sold out of cups."]],
 "Log everything."],
19:["(Liv straightens a pile of forms.) Comrades wanted. The Party's putting up posters on every door in the Lofts. I've signed my own, just to be neat.",[
  ["Is recruitment going well?","Slowly, but steadily. Like a good tea."],
  ["Who joined?","Two of the mill hands, and a baker. The baker's a good catch."],
  ["Heard about the Midnight Note?","Someone's coming incognito, they say. I only hear what's stamped."]],
 "Be a good comrade."],
20:["(Liv shuffles forms.) A palace job planned, some say. And a man at the Hotel Aurora, who's said to be watching. Two stories in one day. I'm saving them for the right ear.",[
  ["Whose ear?","The Party's. It's what I'm here for."],
  ["Is the palace job true?","I never confirm a rumour. I only forward it."],
  ["Does that bother you?","It's tidy. That's all I ask."]],
 "Don't spread rumours."],
21:["(Liv's face is pale, and her cap is grey with ash.) It went. The mountain. The sky turned the colour of a bruise, and every boat in the harbour tooted. I stamped nothing for an hour.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far as I know. Lava in the north, ash on all of us."],
  ["What did you do?","Sat down. Drank my tea. Then I counted the boats."],
  ["Will the harbour reopen?","Tomorrow, if the ash allows."]],
 "Cover the forms."],
22:["(Liv beats a ledger against the desk and ash puffs out.) Ash in every file. Years of paperwork, and grey as a goose. I'm going to have to restamp the lot.",[
  ["Is the harbour open?","Barely. Every captain wants clearance and none of them can see."],
  ["How is Eastbrook?","Everyone's out with brooms. Even Henrik, in a scarf."],
  ["Is the Party helping?","Anna's sent soup. The soup is good."]],
 "Brooms, not stamps."],
23:["(Liv scrubs the shed's windowsill.) A spanner in the works, I hear, at some machinery down the coast. Sabotage or accident, it's hard to say. The Party's blaming someone else. As usual.",[
  ["Who do they blame?","Whoever's handy."],
  ["What about the oil rig repairs?","Deep repairs, the form says. Deep, and vague."],
  ["Do you believe it's sabotage?","I believe in evidence. I've none."]],
 "Evidence first."],
24:["(Liv watches the quay.) Some monks are leaving for the peaks, with their robes tucked up and their hoods down. And clean-up crews are going door to door. Neatly.",[
  ["Who sends the crews?","I've noticed more than one kind. They don't all use the same brooms."],
  ["Are they honest?","Mostly. One crew was oddly interested in the shed's safe."],
  ["What's in the safe?","Stamps. Very dull. They'd be disappointed."]],
 "Lock the safe."],
25:["(Liv yawns.) A princess for democracy, a crown for everyone. The Gazette's full of it. I read the headlines on my way in. The Party's response is a polite sneer.",[
  ["Do you like the idea?","A princess who believes in votes would be a novelty. Novelty isn't policy."],
  ["What does Tor think?","He's got a three-page argument about it. I told him to shorten it."],
  ["Would you vote for her?","I'd read the papers first."]],
 "Read first."],
26:["(Liv straightens.) Kelp has a prince in every bottle now. They've stamped it right on the cap. And the oil man's bought a spa. A great many papers crossed my desk for that.",[
  ["What did the papers say?","Mostly zeroes, and a few names I'd like to forget."],
  ["Is it a good purchase?","Steam, towels, and quiet rooms. Ideal for plotting."],
  ["Would you visit?","I'd inspect it, with a clipboard."]],
 "Bring a towel."],
27:["(Liv taps her pen.) Revolution at the pictures tonight, and high stakes at somebody else's table. I'm going to the film. I'll cry at the horses, probably.",[
  ["Do you like propaganda films?","I like horses. The propaganda comes with them."],
  ["Is the Syndicate's thing dangerous?","Gambling isn't a crime, till someone loses their coat."],
  ["Do you gamble?","On the tide table. It hasn't failed me yet."]],
 "Keep your coat."],
28:["(Liv checks her watch.) Today the Party marches on Kingsgrad, and the harbour's full of banners going the other way. Someone needs to keep order. That's me, with a stamp.",[
  ["Are you going to the march?","In spirit. In body I'm here, checking flags for weight."],
  ["Who else is pushing?","Everyone. Even the oil lot have hired a band."],
  ["Is it exciting?","It's unbearable. In a good way."]],
 "Don't stand in the road."],
29:["(Liv straightens a pile of papers.) Tomorrow it ends, one way or another. Every faction has made its last big push. I've never stamped so many banner crates.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","Fair paper and a fair count."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm always worried. It keeps the forms in order."],
  ["What will you do after?","Go home, put the kettle on, and read a novel."]],
 "Sleep well."],
30:["(Liv puts down her stamp, finally.) The last day. Whatever happens, there'll be boats tomorrow, and somebody will have to look in their crates. I'm glad it's me.",[
  ["Good luck, Liv.","And to you. Write it honestly, reporter."],
  ["Nervous?","A little. But it's mostly paperwork from here on."],
  ["Any last advice?","Check the weight. It's the one thing that never lies."]],
 "Stamped, approved, done."],
};
// Lotta, the maid who sets and clears the palace dining room (the Palace Workers). Threads: what the dinner guests say over the
// soup until she comes in with the fish; the table, the napkin swans and the state banquet; Oskar the footman, who is sweet on
// her, and whom she teases; her afternoons off (the debate, the cinema, the circus) and her quiet curiosity about a vote.
DAILY['Lotta']={
2:["(Lotta is folding a napkin into a swan without looking at it.) Don't lean on the table, it's just been polished. Everything in here's just been polished. Me included.",[
  ["How do you do the swan?","Fold, fold, tuck, pull the neck. Takes four seconds. Oskar's been trying for a year. His look like they've been run over."],
  ["Who's coming to dinner?","Only the family. They'll sit at one end of a table for thirty and say 'pass the salt' four times. I count. It's always four."],
  ["Polished you?","Mr Edmund inspects us before dinner. Nails, collars, shoes. He once sent me back for a hair. One hair."]],
 "Mind the swans on your way out. They're very sensitive."],
3:["The colonel from the barracks was at dinner. Colonel Halvorsen. He talked over the soup about 'keeping order' and 'the loyalty of the men'. Then I came in with the fish and he talked about the fish.",[
  ["What did he mean, loyalty?","I don't know. He said 'the men will follow the crown, Your Highness, as long as there is one.' The prince put his spoon down very slowly."],
  ["Was the fish good?","Herring. Always herring. The colonel had two helpings and said it reminded him of the war."],
  ["Do you always listen?","I don't listen. I serve. It just goes in. Like the gravy into the tablecloth."]],
 "Don't repeat that about the colonel. Soup talk stays in the soup."],
4:["(Lotta is ironing a tablecloth.) Twenty feet of damask, and if there's a crease Mr Edmund will find it from the other end of the hall.",[
  ["What do you do on your days off?","Go into Kingsgrad. The cinema, if Otto has anything good. The tearoom. Anywhere nobody wants me to carry soup."],
  ["Do you like the job?","I like the dining room. It's the most beautiful room in the palace and nobody sits in it but three sad people. Someone ought to enjoy it."],
  ["Is Oskar any help?","Oskar tried to iron once. He ironed his own sleeve, while he was wearing it. He was very brave about it."]],
 "Step round the board. I've got a corner to do."],
5:["Prince Augustus read a letter from the monastery out at dinner. Father Thyme's. Very kind, all about the king. Then the prince said 'more kind letters, how lovely' and nobody spoke till the fish.",[
  ["Is it always tense?","Not always. Sometimes it's just silent. Silent's easier. You can hear yourself pour."],
  ["What does Daphne do?","Pushes peas around her plate and makes jokes nobody laughs at except me. I'm not supposed to laugh. I do it into the gravy boat."],
  ["Do they talk about the crown?","Only at the soup. By the fish they're on to the weather. I've never once heard them argue over pudding."]],
 "Don't tell them I laugh at the jokes. I'd be sacked, and so would the gravy boat."],
6:["Oskar watches me lay the table through the window. He thinks I don't see. He's standing on the step, absolutely still, staring at the napkins.",[
  ["He likes you.","(She laughs.) I know. The whole palace knows. Even Viktor knows, and Viktor only knows about birds."],
  ["Do you like him?","He's sweet. He's very sweet and very tall and very cold. I haven't decided yet. I like to take my time."],
  ["Maybe he likes the napkins.","He might. He's been trying to fold a swan for a year. I think he thinks if he gets it right I'll marry him."]],
 "Don't tell Oskar I said 'sweet'. He'll be unbearable."],
7:["Mr Dario's card came, with gold on it, and Mr Edmund said 'we'll see'. Then the prince said 'set a place'. So I'm laying for an oil man.",[
  ["What's an oil man like at dinner?","I'll tell you tomorrow. The last one I served, years ago, put salt on everything before he tasted it. Marta has never forgiven him."],
  ["Which plates are you using?","The second-best. Mr Edmund decides. The best ones are for kings and bishops. Oil men get the ones with the little chip."],
  ["Are you curious?","About Mr Dario? Everyone is. They say he's going to buy the north of the island. I'd like to see a man who buys mountains eat soup."]],
 "Step back, I'm laying the fish knives. They're sharper than they look."],
8:["Well. Mr Dario ate soup like a man reading a contract. He talked about drilling under the mountain to the north. Then I came in with the fish, and he talked about his villa.",[
  ["Drilling under the mountain?","'There's wealth under that mountain, Your Highness, and heat.' That's what he said. The prince said 'heat?' and Mr Dario smiled and said nothing more."],
  ["Did he salt his food?","He didn't eat the fish at all. He looked at it as though it owed him money. Marta's taken it personally."],
  ["Was the prince friendly?","Polite. The prince is always polite. He's polite like a locked door: very smooth, and you don't get in."]],
 "Don't tell Mr Dario I told you. I'd like to keep my chip-free plates."],
9:["(Lotta is laying a table for three hundred in the Grand Hall.) The wake. After the funeral tomorrow. Three hundred places, and Mr Edmund wants the forks two inches from the plate. I've got a ruler.",[
  ["Two inches exactly?","Two inches. If it's two and a quarter, he'll move it without a word, and look at you. The look's worse than the word."],
  ["Who's helping?","Ingrid, Elsa and Oskar, who keeps putting the knives on the wrong side and then apologising to them."],
  ["You must be exhausted.","My feet are. My hands are. My head's fine. My head's thinking about Saturday, when I've an afternoon off."]],
 "Don't move a fork on your way out. I'll know."],
10:["(Lotta is in black, with a white apron.) The funeral's in the chapel, then they'll all come through here. Three hundred people who all want to sit next to someone important.",[
  ["Who do you think will come?","Everyone. Kelp Cola, the oil man, the Communists, the Democrats, Gustavo. I'll serve them all the same soup and listen to them all say the same thing."],
  ["What will they say?","'He was a great man.' Then they'll look around to see who heard them say it."],
  ["Are you sad about the king?","He used to wink at me when he finished his soup. Every night, as I took the plate. I'll miss the wink."]],
 "Go to the chapel. I'll have your soup ready after. Not before."],
11:["The wake was a feast of whispers. I carried three hundred bowls and heard three hundred secrets. I've forgotten all of them. Mostly.",[
  ["Mostly?","Two men from the oil company said the cola people had brought the biggest wreath 'to cover the bill'. I don't know what bill. I nearly spilt the soup."],
  ["What else?","Somebody from the Syndicate asked Oskar which door the deliveries come in by. Oskar told him. Oskar tells everybody everything."],
  ["How are you holding up?","My arms are like rope. I've got gravy on my soul. Ask me again on Saturday."]],
 "Don't repeat any of it. I'm a maid. I didn't hear a thing."],
12:["(Lotta is laughing quietly to herself.) I put plasters on Oskar's thumbs. Blisters from coats. I said he was brave. He went the colour of beetroot.",[
  ["Was he brave?","About blisters? Hardly. But you should have seen his face. Like I'd given him a medal."],
  ["Are you warming to him?","I'm warming to the idea of him. He's honest. That's rare in this palace. Everyone else has two faces. Oskar barely manages one."],
  ["Who else needs plasters?","Elsa, for her hands. She won't take them. She says plasters are for people who have time to bleed."]],
 "Don't tell Oskar I'm warming. Let him freeze a while longer."],
13:["A man came to dinner with a ring on his little finger. A friend of a friend of the family, Mr Edmund said, with that face. He asked the princess about Kelp Cola's 'family secret'.",[
  ["What did she say?","She said 'darling, I don't even know where Edmund keeps the jam.' Everyone laughed. He didn't."],
  ["Who was he?","He gave a name I've never heard. He tipped me, under the plate. Nobody here tips. It made me want to give it back."],
  ["Did you keep it?","I put it in the poor box in the chapel. It felt like the right thing to do with that sort of money."]],
 "If you see a man with a ring like that, don't let him buy you anything."],
14:["The whole island in one place today. I'm off at two, so I'll see the end of it. I'm going to stand at the back and see who shouts loudest.",[
  ["Who do you think will?","The Communists. They have the best banners. Oskar's mum's going. She's made her own, he says. In red felt."],
  ["What do you want?","I'm a maid. I'm not supposed to want anything. (She shrugs.) But I'd like to be asked, once. About anything. Even soup."],
  ["Are you going with Oskar?","He's on duty. He's walking behind Mr Edmund. Two paces. I'll wave at the back of his head."]],
 "If you see me in the crowd, pretend you don't know me. I'm off duty."],
15:["Lemon cake for Prince Augustus. I carried it to the library myself. He was sitting with two letters. He looked like a man in a station, between two trains.",[
  ["What did he say?","'Thank you, Lotta.' He always says my name. Nobody else in the family does. They say 'thank you' to the air."],
  ["Did he eat it?","One slice. He'll eat the rest tonight when nobody's looking. He's like a mouse with cake."],
  ["Which train will he take?","I don't know. Marta's praying he takes neither and stays on the platform with us."]],
 "Go easy in the library, if you're going. He's very fragile today."],
16:["It's my afternoon off and I'm going to the big debate. The Communists and the Democrats, arguing about who should run the island. I've never been to a debate.",[
  ["Who do you support?","I don't know yet. That's why I'm going. I'd like to hear someone explain it without shouting."],
  ["What does Marta say?","She says I'll come back with ideas. I said I've already got ideas. She said 'that's what I'm worried about.'"],
  ["Is Oskar going?","He's on the door. He looked so miserable I said I'd tell him all about it. He said 'every word?' I said 'every word that's interesting.'"]],
 "If you're going, I'll be at the back, in a green hat. I don't own a black one that's not for work."],
17:["The debate was loud. I liked Erik. He asked people questions. Anna told people answers. I think I prefer questions. (The cutlery drawer rattles.) Oh! Did you feel that?",[
  ["The ground moved.","The chandelier swung right round. Mr Edmund said 'boiler' and went very pale. Mr Edmund never goes pale."],
  ["Tell me about the debate.","It nearly came to blows at the end, over who'd get the fish plant. I hid behind a pillar. Oskar's mum was waving her banner like a sword."],
  ["Do you prefer Erik?","I prefer being asked. I'm not saying more. I work in a palace."]],
 "Hold the table edge if it happens again. It's heavier than it looks."],
18:["The colonel was back at dinner. He talked about a guard of honour for the foreign king on the twenty-eighth. Thirty soldiers on the drive. He wants them to fire a salute.",[
  ["A salute?","Twenty-one guns. The prince said 'not near the windows'. The colonel said 'naturally, Your Highness' and looked like he'd been robbed."],
  ["Who's the foreign king?","A king from across the water. The colonel called him 'an old friend of the crown'. The princess said 'and of the crown's money'. Nobody laughed but me."],
  ["You laughed again?","Into the gravy boat. It's had a lot of laughter in it this month."]],
 "Don't tell the colonel I laughed. He's very sensitive about guns."],
19:["(Lotta is humming something with a lot of trumpet in it.) Do you know this? I heard it on the radio. Late, on the jazz programme. Kaisa plays it at midnight.",[
  ["You listen to jazz?","Only since last week. Ingrid said the prince does, and I wanted to know why. Now I know. It sounds like being somewhere else."],
  ["Is it Leo, the saxophonist?","Is that his name? He plays at a club in Frostholm. The Midnight Note. I'd love to go once. I'd never get past the door, in my shoes."],
  ["Hum it again.","(She does, quietly, and does a little turn with a tray.) Don't tell Mr Edmund. He thinks music is something done by bands in uniforms."]],
 "If you ever go to the Midnight Note, tell me what it's like."],
20:["The prince was humming at breakfast. Something jazzy. Mr Edmund dropped a teaspoon. I've never seen Mr Edmund drop anything.",[
  ["What was he humming?","The tune I was humming yesterday. I think. Or one like it. All jazz sounds the same to me still, and all of it sounds good."],
  ["Was everyone surprised?","The princess raised an eyebrow. Prince Augustus smiled into his porridge. Nobody said a word. That's this family."],
  ["Something's going on.","Something's always going on. Mr Edmund's been round the doors twice today. Something's going on everywhere."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the teaspoon. Mr Edmund's pride is all he has."],
21:["(Lotta is pulling the dining room curtains shut against the ash.) The mountain's on fire in the north. I've shut every curtain in the room. The table's grey already.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm shaking like a jelly. But the table needs covering. You do what's in front of you."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Oskar ran to town. Nobody's hurt. He came back grey from head to foot. I hugged him. I didn't mean to. It just happened."],
  ["You hugged him?","(She turns very red.) Don't. It was the volcano. Anyone would have."]],
 "Go inside somewhere, and stay there till it settles."],
22:["The tablecloth's ruined. Twenty feet of damask, grey forever. Elsa's tried three times. Mr Edmund stood over it like it was a grave.",[
  ["Is there another?","The second-best one, with the darn in the corner. Mr Edmund wants it turned so the darn faces the wall. As if the visiting king inspects the corners."],
  ["How's Oskar?","He came to the dining room four times today with excuses. He 'needed a spoon'. He lives in the servants' quarters. There are spoons there."],
  ["The hug?","(She throws a napkin at you.) It was the volcano."]],
 "Shake the ash out of your hat. Not on my table."],
23:["We've been practising for the state visit on the gravel. I hand flowers to the wheelbarrow, and Oskar opens its door. Viktor laughs at us from the shed.",[
  ["Why the wheelbarrow?","It's about the height of a king's car. And it doesn't mind if you drop the flowers."],
  ["Is Oskar good at it?","He opened the wheelbarrow beautifully. Then he bowed to it. Then he looked at me to see if I'd laughed. I had."],
  ["What flowers?","White roses. From Kingsgrad, if the ash lets them through. If not, Viktor says he'll find something. Viktor always finds something."]],
 "Don't laugh at the wheelbarrow. It's had a hard month."],
24:["The visiting king's secretary came to look at the dining room. A tall thin man with a tape measure. He measured the distance between the chairs.",[
  ["Why?","He said his king prefers 'a respectful distance'. Mr Edmund said 'naturally' and looked like he wanted to measure the secretary for a coffin."],
  ["Did he say anything else?","He asked who'd be at dinner. When he heard the colonel, he wrote something down. When he heard the princess, he underlined it."],
  ["Underlined the princess?","Twice. I saw it upside down. I'm good at upside down. You have to be, clearing plates."]],
 "Don't tell the princess about the underlining. Or do. She'd love it."],
25:["The family were all at breakfast, and the prince asked the princess what she thought of a vote. A real vote. At breakfast! Over the eggs!",[
  ["What did she say?","She said 'darling, I'd vote for anyone who let me choose my own hats.' Then she looked at him and said 'actually, I'd like to think about it.' She's never said that."],
  ["What did Augustus say?","'God might not mind a few more people asking questions.' I nearly dropped the toast rack."],
  ["What do you think?","I think it's the first time I've heard them talk at breakfast and not just pass the salt. I counted. Only once."]],
 "Don't put that in the paper. Breakfast's private."],
26:["Men from Kelp Cola came, with folders, and the princess took them the long way round. Twice past the old queen's portrait. One of them sat down in the corridor and asked for water.",[
  ["Did you bring him water?","Kelp Cola. On a tray. I thought it'd be funny. Mr Edmund thought it was funny too, but only with his eyebrows."],
  ["What did they want?","The prince, for something. Oskar told them he likes jazz. Oskar can't keep anything in."],
  ["Tough on the men.","Folders and fizzy drinks. They'll live."]],
 "If you see Kelp Cola men in the corridor, offer them a chair."],
27:["(Lotta is folding napkins at speed.) A hundred swans for tomorrow. Ninety-eight done. Oskar did two. They look like geese that have been in a fight.",[
  ["Will you use his?","At the end of the table. Under the colonel. He'll never notice. Soldiers don't look at napkins."],
  ["Is everything ready?","The table, the swans, the silver, the second-best cloth with the darn to the wall. And me, with my best apron. I've ironed it twice."],
  ["Oskar bowed to a tureen?","He did. Last night. I said it was the best bow I'd ever seen. He's been unbearable since. I like him unbearable."]],
 "Don't tell Oskar about the geese. Let him think they're swans."],
28:["(Lotta is very upright, with white roses in her arms.) A king's here, and the whole island's out in the streets, and I've to hand him flowers without dropping a petal.",[
  ["You'll be wonderful.","I know. I practised with the wheelbarrow. The wheelbarrow said I was wonderful."],
  ["What's going on in the streets?","Everything. Flags, rallies, shouting. Oskar's mum's red banner. And a king, in our dining room. I'll be listening over the soup."],
  ["What do you hope he says?","Something worth hearing. I've served a hundred dinners this year, and nobody's said anything worth hearing since the old king winked."]],
 "Step back. Here comes the car. (She takes a breath and lifts her chin.)"],
29:["(Lotta is smiling at nothing.) Oskar and I went for a walk round the garden. I held his arm. The ice was slippery. Well. I said it was slippery.",[
  ["Was it slippery?","Viktor sanded the paths that morning. I saw him do it. I held Oskar's arm anyway."],
  ["Oh, Lotta!","I know. Don't. Marta's already made a face. Ingrid's already cried. Elsa said 'about time'."],
  ["What's the island deciding?","Who's next: the prince, his brother or nobody. The family's very quiet. I'm laying the table as if it's any other day. That's my job."]],
 "Mind the gravel. Viktor's sanded it. For some reason."],
30:["The last day of the month. Whatever happens today, I've laid the table for three. Same as always. And on Wednesday, Oskar's taking me to the cinema.",[
  ["What's showing?","A film about a bank robbery. Otto's choice. I said I like the bit where they get caught. Oskar said he'd never thought of it like that."],
  ["Will you stay, whatever happens?","I'll stay. The dining room's the most beautiful room in the palace. Somebody ought to enjoy it, whoever sits in it."],
  ["Did you ever decide about the vote?","I decided I'd like one. Whatever they do with the crown. (She folds a perfect swan.) Don't tell Marta."]],
 "Mind the swans. They're very sensitive today."],
};
// Lucia, Syndicate enforcer, keeping an eye on the docks in Saltwick (Crime Syndicate). Threads: the debts she collects (Gunnar
// and his card habit, Hannu's newsstand, Ray's broken chair), her temper and her efforts to count to ten, living down the corridor
// from the Communist leaders, and Mike, the secret she nearly lets slip all month and never quite does.
DAILY['Lucia']={
1:["(Lucia is leaning on a bollard, cleaning her nails with a matchstick.) You're new. Nobody's new on this island in September. You must be the reporter everyone's whispering about.",[
  ["Word travels fast here.","Faster than the boats. Slower than me, though. (She smiles.) Lucia. I look after things on the docks. People's debts, mostly."],
  ["What do you do here?","I remind people what they owe. Kindly, the first time. You'd be amazed how many people only need reminding once."],
  ["You don't look like a docker.","I'm not. I'm the woman the dockers look round for before they buy a round they can't pay for."]],
 "Nice to meet you, reporter. Don't run up a tab."],
2:["(Lucia has a little notebook open and is ticking something off.) Gunnar paid. All of it, in cash, before I'd even said hello. I'm almost disappointed.",[
  ["Who's Gunnar?","He works the warehouse at West Saltwick. Big hands, small brain, plays cards with sailors. He loses on Fridays and pays on Mondays. Today he broke the pattern."],
  ["What does he owe you for?","Not me. Friends of mine. (She snaps the notebook shut.) Reporters ask a lot of questions, don't they?"],
  ["Disappointed? You like collecting?","I like the walk. Fresh air, sea view, a man going pale. It's a good job, if you've got the face for it."]],
 "If you see Gunnar, tell him well done. He'll be terrified."],
3:["My flat's in the block by the square. (Lucia jerks her head at it.) Third floor. I've got Anna, the Party leader, on one side, and Helen the union woman across the corridor. I hear them through the walls, planning the revolution.",[
  ["Do they know what you do?","They know I work for 'an import firm'. Anna once asked me to join the Party. I said I'd think about it. I'm still thinking. Eight years now."],
  ["What do they plan, through the walls?","Meetings about meetings. Helen practises speeches in the bath. She's quite good. I've nearly been converted twice."],
  ["Who else lives there?","Kaisa from the radio, two girls with no work, Olga from the fish shop, and some fishermen. (She looks at the sea.) Nice enough people."]],
 "Don't tell Anna I said she plans in the bath. That was Helen."],
4:["(Lucia's knuckles are red, and she's flexing her hand.) Before you ask, I hit a door. Not a person. A door. Ray's back door, at the bar. It stuck.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. The door's not. Ray says I owe him a door. (She laughs.) Imagine. Me, owing someone."],
  ["Do you have a temper?","I've got a short one. Somebody told me to count to ten. I get to four, usually. Four's an improvement on none."],
  ["Who told you to count to ten?","(She stops flexing.) A friend. Somebody calm. You'd not know them."]],
 "Mind the door at Ray's. It's a menace."],
5:["Hannu at the newsstand owes us two months. (Lucia nods at the little stall.) I keep telling myself he'll pay. He keeps selling papers to people who don't pay him. It's a chain of misery.",[
  ["Will you lean on him?","Hannu's got a bad chest and a mother in Kingsgrad. I've told my bosses he's paid. I'm paying it myself. Don't put that in your paper."],
  ["That's generous of you.","It's not generous. It's tidy. A newsstand closing is worse for business than a small debt. And I like the crossword."],
  ["Who exactly are your bosses?","(She grins.) An import firm. Very respectable. They import things."]],
 "Buy a paper from Hannu while you're here. It helps."],
6:["(Lucia is sitting on the harbour wall with a little bag of salted fish, eating it with her fingers.) Want one? Fresh this morning. A fisherman gave them to me. Somebody has to eat them.",[
  ["Which fisherman?","Oh, one of them. They all look the same in oilskins. (She offers the bag again.) Have one. They're good."],
  ["Thanks. They're delicious.","Salt, a bit of smoke, a bit of patience. That's all fish needs. That's all most things need."],
  ["Do the fishermen owe you money?","Fishermen owe everyone money: the boat, the net, the bank, the sea. I leave them alone. They've got enough people chasing them."]],
 "Take another for the road. Go on."],
7:["(Lucia is twisting a length of fishing twine round her fingers, making knots.) I'm learning to mend a net. Don't laugh. A bowline, a sheet bend, a clove hitch. My fingers are useless at it.",[
  ["Why learn to mend a net?","Because I'm bored of being good at one thing. (She pulls a knot tight.) And because a net doesn't argue back."],
  ["Who's teaching you?","Somebody patient. With very rough hands and no sense of humour about knots. That's all you're getting, reporter."],
  ["Can a net be useful in your work?","(She laughs.) Picture me throwing a net over Gunnar. It's not a bad idea, actually."]],
 "If you find a loose knot anywhere, it's mine."],
8:["There's talk at the harbour about the oil rig. (Lucia frowns at the sea.) The fishermen say there's something black on the water past the point. If it comes in, there's no fishing. If there's no fishing, nobody pays anybody.",[
  ["Is it an oil spill?","Ask Dario. The fishermen say the company boats went out at dawn, very fast and very quiet. Fast and quiet is never good news."],
  ["You sound worried for the fishermen.","(She looks at you sharply.) I'm worried for the debts. A man who can't fish can't pay. Business."],
  ["What do the Communists say?","Through the wall? Helen says it's capitalism poisoning the sea. Anna says 'write that down'. They'll have a leaflet out by teatime."]],
 "Go to the sermon if you want to. I'll be watching the water."],
9:["(Lucia's sitting on the steps of the apartment block, red in the face.) I counted to six this morning. Six! Helen knocked on my door with a leaflet about the Party's cola, and I didn't slam it.",[
  ["Congratulations. That's progress.","I know. I'm very proud of myself. I took the leaflet. I even read it. It's going to taste of beetroot, I bet."],
  ["What's the Party's cola?","A people's cola, made by the workers. Helen's very excited. She'd make the sea into a cooperative if she could."],
  ["What would you have done at four?","Slammed it. Last month I slammed it so hard the number came off. Helen put it back on herself. She's not a bad woman."]],
 "Tell nobody I took a leaflet. I have a reputation."],
10:["(Lucia's coat is damp at the hems, and her hands are blue with cold.) I've been out on a boat this morning. Don't ask. I hate boats. My whole stomach is still out there somewhere past the lighthouse.",[
  ["Why go on a boat if you hate it?","Work. Some deliveries need a person on the boat who won't get seasick in front of the customer. I was sick behind the wheelhouse."],
  ["Did you see the funeral?","I heard the bells across the water. Sad, a bit. The old king used to come down to Saltwick at Christmas and buy fish he didn't want. People liked him for it."],
  ["You look frozen. Want my scarf?","(She takes it, surprised, and wraps it round her neck.) You're very soft, for a reporter. I'll give it back. Probably."]],
 "I'm going home to stand in a bath. Don't follow me."],
11:["(Lucia is waiting outside Ray's bar with her arms folded.) Ray's door, again. I paid for a new one. It's lovely: oak, with a brass handle. And he's put it on the wrong way. It opens on to the ice.",[
  ["So people slip as they leave?","Three men this morning. And they came back in to complain, and slipped again. Ray's making a fortune in brandy for the shock."],
  ["Did you count to ten about it?","To eight. Then I laughed. Laughing's better than counting, I'm finding. It's quicker."],
  ["Heard anything about the mill in Eastbrook?","The union's sending someone to talk to Dagny about pay. Helen was up all night writing figures. I lent her my pencil. Don't tell the Syndicate."]],
 "Watch the door at Ray's. It's out to get you."],
12:["Gunnar's back to losing. (Lucia holds up the notebook.) He lost at cards on Saturday to a sailor from Frostholm, and he's been hiding in the warehouse ever since. I can see his boots under the crates.",[
  ["Will you go in after him?","No. I'll wait for him to come out. Gunnar gets hungry at noon exactly. He's like a cuckoo clock."],
  ["Why does he keep gambling?","He says he'll win it all back. Everyone says that. Nobody ever does. That's the whole business, reporter, in one sentence."],
  ["Are you going to hurt him?","(She sighs.) I'm going to look at him. That's usually enough. Then I'll buy him a pie, because he's an idiot."]],
 "It's almost noon. Watch this."],
13:["(Lucia is holding a can of Kelp Cola at arm's length.) Somebody gave me this at the tram stop in Kingsgrad. Free. I drank half and my teeth started singing.",[
  ["Not a fan, then?","I'm Sicilian by my grandmother. I drink black coffee and red wine and the occasional grappa. This is for people with no tongues."],
  ["What were you doing in Kingsgrad?","Visiting friends at a pizzeria. They wanted to talk about recipes. (She shrugs.) Cola recipes. Very boring."],
  ["Would the fishermen drink it?","They're Communists. They'd drink the Party one. Out of loyalty. With their eyes shut."]],
 "Have the rest. Please."],
14:["(Lucia's dressed up, in a dark coat with a brooch.) The big day. Everyone's going. Anna and Helen left at seven, singing. I'm going because my bosses want faces in the crowd. And because I'm curious.",[
  ["What do you hope happens?","Nothing that makes a lot of noise. Noise is bad for business. And for people's tempers. Mine included."],
  ["You look nice. Meeting someone?","(She goes slightly pink.) I'm going to stand at the back with the dockers and watch. That's all. Nobody's meeting anybody."],
  ["Which side are you on?","Mine. And my friends'. And a few people I've decided to keep safe, whatever their politics. It's a short list."]],
 "Go on. You'll miss the speeches. Lucky you."],
15:["Somebody saw me. (Lucia is pacing.) At the big day, in the crowd. I was standing next to someone, and Olga from the fish shop gave me a look. A long look. Olga sees everything and says everything.",[
  ["Standing next to whom?","Nobody. A man. A fisherman. We were standing next to each other because it was a crowd. Crowds put people next to each other."],
  ["Would Olga gossip?","Olga would gossip to a herring. If she says anything, I'll have a word. A nice word. A very nice word."],
  ["Why does it matter who sees you?","(She stops pacing.) Because in my job, reporter, and his, you don't stand next to the wrong people. That's all I'll say."]],
 "Forget I said 'his'. I didn't say it."],
16:["(Lucia is sitting on the harbour wall, very still, watching the boats.) The Party's debating the Democrats today. Helen's been practising in the bath since five. I could hear every word.",[
  ["Was she any good?","Very good. She had a bit about 'the hands that net the fish should own the boat'. I nearly clapped. I had to put my head under the pillow."],
  ["Will you go and listen?","No. I'll stay here. Somebody has to watch the docks while the docks are listening to the debate."],
  ["You seem calmer today.","I sat on this wall for an hour without wanting to hit anything. Somebody told me that's what the sea's for. I didn't believe them."]],
 "Sit, if you like. The wall's big enough."],
17:["(The bollard Lucia is leaning on shudders, and she grabs it.) Mother of God! The whole quay moved. The boats are bumping. Somebody's going to lose a hull.",[
  ["Is everyone out of the water?","The boats came in fast. All of them. (She counts them under her breath.) Six, seven. All of them."],
  ["Who were you counting?","The boats. Just the boats. A good enforcer knows where every debtor is. They're all debtors, those boats."],
  ["What's the news from Frostholm?","Some executive at the oil company's gone missing, people say. Nobody's very sad. The fishermen are saying it serves Dario right for the black water."]],
 "If it shakes again, get off the quay. Don't be brave."],
18:["Olga said something. (Lucia's jaw is tight.) In the fish shop, loud, to three customers. 'Our Lucia's been very friendly with a fisherman lately.' I counted to two. Then I bought a herring, and left.",[
  ["Two? That's a relapse.","I didn't throw the herring. I took it home and ate it. That's growth, reporter. Look it up."],
  ["Is it true?","(She looks at you for a long time.) Is what true? That I bought a herring? Yes. That's true."],
  ["Will Olga keep talking?","Olga's sister owes us forty dollars. I'll mention it to her, gently. I've never used that sort of thing for myself before. I don't like it."]],
 "Don't print any of that. Not one word."],
19:["(Lucia has a Communist Party leaflet in her hand and is laughing.) Helen's recruiting today. Comrades wanted! She knocked on my door with it, and the fishermen's boots were in my hall, and she didn't notice. Thank God for leaflets: Helen never looks up from them.",[
  ["Whose boots were in your hall?","Mine. Big ones. For the docks. A woman needs proper boots in Saltwick."],
  ["Will you join the Party?","Me? I collect money for people who'd shoot Helen's lot on sight. Joining would be a very short career. In both."],
  ["Helen sounds kind.","She is. They both are. They want everyone to have bread and a boat and a vote. Imagine wanting something that simple."]],
 "Go on. Somebody will want you to join something."],
20:["Gunnar paid. (Lucia shakes her head in wonder.) All of it, and a little extra, with a note: 'For the pie.' After eight years I think I've reformed a man. It feels terrible.",[
  ["Why terrible?","Because I liked him better as a gambler. Now he'll start saving, and buy a house, and stop talking to me."],
  ["Maybe he'll still say hello.","He said hello this morning. With his hat in his hand, like I'm the priest. Nobody's ever done that."],
  ["Anything going on in Kingsgrad?","The palace is in a flap, Kaisa said on the radio. Some fuss about guards. She played a soothing record after. She's good like that."]],
 "Buy Gunnar a drink at Ray's, if you see him. Just one."],
21:["(Lucia is helping a fisherman pull a tarpaulin over a boat, with ash falling like grey snow.) The mountain's gone off! Grab that corner. Pull. Pull! Ash in an engine is a dead engine.",[
  ["Who's the fisherman?","(She doesn't look up.) A man with a boat. Help him, or move out of the way, reporter."],
  ["Is everybody safe?","Everybody in Saltwick. I went door to door. Anna's flat, Helen's, Kaisa's, the fishermen. All safe. All terrified. All safe."],
  ["You're very good in a crisis.","I'm good when there's something to hit or something to pull. Today there's a lot to pull."]],
 "Go home and shut the window. Now."],
22:["(Lucia is sitting on the steps of the apartment block, smoking, with ash on her shoulders.) I didn't sleep. Nobody did. Helen made soup for the whole building, in her biggest pan. I took two bowls.",[
  ["Two bowls? Hungry?","One was for somebody who'd been out on the boats all day. (She smiles at the ash.) Don't ask who."],
  ["How bad is the damage here?","Windows, gutters, a roof over the fish shop. Olga's flat is full of ash. I sent the Syndicate's boys round with brushes. She won't know who sent them."],
  ["Helping Olga? After what she said?","She's a gossip, not a villain. And her roof's in. You don't let a neighbour's roof fall in. Even the neighbour with the mouth."]],
 "Mind the steps. They're grey and slippery."],
23:["I'm frightened, reporter. (Lucia says it very quietly, looking at her boots.) There's trouble coming at the cola factory. People talk at the docks. If certain people get caught up in it, there'll be no coming back.",[
  ["What kind of trouble?","Party trouble. Strike talk, spanners, machines. The fishermen go quiet when I walk past. They never used to go quiet."],
  ["Which people do you mean?","Party people. Fishermen. A man who's too brave and not very good at keeping his head down. (She stops.) A friend."],
  ["Can't you warn your friend?","He knows. He laughs. He says the workers have nothing to lose. He's wrong. He's got me."]],
 "Forget all of that. I'm tired, that's all."],
24:["(Lucia's face is set and grey.) I've had a busy morning, and I don't want to talk about it. Sit with me a minute, that's all. Not a word.",[
  ["(Sit with her a minute.)","(She lets out a long breath.) Thank you. Some mornings you wish you'd been born a net-mender. Somewhere warm. Sicily. Anywhere."],
  ["Is it the Syndicate?","Everything's always the Syndicate. And I said no questions. (But she doesn't move away.)"],
  ["Want a coffee?","Black. No sugar. Thank you, reporter. You're much kinder than a newspaper should be."]],
 "Go on, I'll be fine. I'm always fine."],
25:["(Lucia's practising a knot on the harbour wall.) Look: a clove hitch. Perfect. Took me three weeks. My teacher said it was 'not bad'. From him, that's a sonnet.",[
  ["A clove hitch, finally. Well done.","Next it's the bowline. You make a rabbit come out of a hole and go round a tree. I keep losing the rabbit."],
  ["Is he the friend from the big day?","(She pulls the knot very tight.) You're a nosy reporter. That's why you're good at it, I suppose."],
  ["What's all the fuss in Kingsgrad today?","Princes and princesses, promising everyone things. Daphne wants a vote, I hear. I'd vote. I've never voted for anything. I'd quite like to."]],
 "Practise a knot yourself. It's good for the temper."],
26:["Anna asked me again. (Lucia rolls her eyes.) To join the Party. Ninth year running. She stopped me on the stairs and said 'Lucia, you know about hard work.' I said 'Anna, you've no idea.'",[
  ["What did Anna say to that?","She laughed and said she'd keep asking. She thinks I'm a lonely woman who needs a cause. I let her think it."],
  ["Why does Anna keep asking?","She says I've got a worker's hands. If she ever found out what they get up to, and who with, it wouldn't be a membership card she offered me."],
  ["Dario's bought the spa, I hear.","In the ash? He'll be bathing in grit. Good. Somebody should teach that man to hold his breath."]],
 "Anna's not to know I told you. Nor anybody."],
27:["(Lucia is putting on a smart coat over her work clothes.) There's a tournament in Kingsgrad. Poker, at the pizzeria. I'm on the door. Nobody gets in who isn't invited, and nobody gets out owing money.",[
  ["Will you play?","I can't play poker. My face does everything my brain does. I'd lose my wages on the first hand."],
  ["Who's playing?","Everyone with money and nerve. Nico, who has nerve but no money. And the Don, who has both."],
  ["Who'll keep the docks while you're gone?","The docks will keep themselves for one day. And a certain fisherman's promised to stay home and not start a revolution until I'm back."]],
 "Come to Kingsgrad and watch. Bring money, or don't come."],
28:["(Lucia is very pale.) The Party's marching to Kingsgrad today, with flags. All of them. Helen's got a megaphone. And a certain fisherman's carrying the banner at the front.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'll be in Kingsgrad, in a doorway, watching. If anyone touches that banner, they'll find out what four looks like."],
  ["Does the Syndicate know you're worried?","The Syndicate thinks I'm worried about the rackets in Kingsgrad. I'll let them think it. Thinking's cheaper than knowing."],
  ["You care about him a lot.","(Lucia looks away to the boats for a long time.) Yes. And now you know. And you're a reporter. And you'll say nothing. Will you? (She doesn't wait for an answer.)"]],
 "Go and watch the march. Keep your head down."],
29:["(Lucia is holding a piece of net, mended, with a neat bowline in the middle.) I did the bowline. The rabbit came out, round the tree, back down the hole. He said it was 'good'. Good! I nearly fell off the wall.",[
  ["You'll be a fisherwoman yet.","Never. I'm too fond of dry feet. But I'll be able to mend his nets when he's an old man with bad fingers. That's something."],
  ["Is the march over?","Everyone came home in one piece. Hoarse, cold and full of songs. I made tea. In my flat. For two."],
  ["What's the Syndicate doing today?","The Republican vote. All the Syndicate's eyes on Frostholm. Nobody's looking at Saltwick. It's lovely, being nobody's business."]],
 "Keep the bowline secret. That's my best work this year."],
30:["(Lucia is sitting on the harbour wall with two cups of coffee, holding one out to you.) The last day, reporter. Tomorrow everyone wakes up on an island that's decided something. I want to wake up next to the right person. That's all.",[
  ["Whatever happens, I hope you're happy.","(She clinks her cup against yours.) Happy? I'm a woman who counts to four. But I'll take quietly content. That's my politics."],
  ["Will you keep working for the Syndicate?","Ask me tomorrow. Maybe I'll mend nets. Maybe I'll keep breaking doors. A woman can do both, these days."],
  ["Does your fisherman know how lucky he is?","(She almost spills her coffee, and then she laughs.) He tells me every day. It's terrible. Don't you dare put him in your paper."]],
 "Drink up. It's cold, and it's the last day of something."],
};
// Madame Zora, fortune teller of the circus at Lake Ilmari (the Circus). Threads: her crystal ball and palms, her uncanny forecasts
// (always vague, often right), the troupe she mothers, her wry scepticism about herself, the tremors, the volcano and the ash.
DAILY['Madame Zora']={
7:["(Madame Zora looks up from the crystal ball, as if she's been expecting you for a week.) Ah. The reporter. Sit. No, not there, that's the cat's. The cat is a very good judge of people.",[
  ["Can you tell my future?","I can tell you're sceptical. It's the first thing the ball shows. (She smiles.) The future is cheaper than that. A coin for the cat."],
  ["Do you really see things?","I see what people want to hear and what they're afraid of. Sometimes they're the same thing. It's most of the trade."],
  ["What do you see for the island?","Snow, then people. Then a great many words. It's not much of a prophecy. I save the good ones for those who pay."]],
 "Come back when you've a question you're afraid of."],
8:["(Zora is shuffling a very old deck of cards, with her eyes closed.) It's Sunday. The monks are singing and the cards are restless. They don't like church music. Too much sky, not enough floor.",[
  ["What do the cards say today?","The Tower, upside down. The Fool, very much right way up. It's either Buttons or the government."],
  ["Do you pray?","In my fashion. I light a candle and ask for good weather and honest customers."],
  ["Would you read for the monks?","They'd say it's a sin. Then they'd ask when the harvest is. They always ask when the harvest is."]],
 "The cards say you should drink more tea. It's the sort of thing they say."],
9:["(Zora has a bottle of cola in front of her, and is gazing into it as if it were the ball.) Kelp Cola have sent a man to ask me what the island's going to want next. I told him: something that tastes of home. He wrote it down. He was very pleased.",[
  ["Is that true?","It's true of everything. People want what reminds them of being safe. It's a good prediction. It's also a cheap one."],
  ["Did you charge him?","Three krona and a lemon. I'm not a charity. I'm a seer."],
  ["Can you see the cola flavour?","Blackcurrant, I think. Or liquorice. I saw something dark and sticky. It could have been the bottle."]],
 "If it tastes of liquorice, you heard it here first."],
10:["(Zora has a black shawl on, and the crystal ball's covered with a cloth.) I don't read on a burying day. The dead like their quiet. A king dead is a king who's seen everything and has nothing left to say.",[
  ["Did you see his death coming?","I saw a great grey bird, in the autumn, over the palace. I thought it was weather. It was a king."],
  ["What does the funeral mean for the island?","That the doors are open. When a king dies, every faction's hearts go loud. It's a time for choosing. It's also a time for mistakes."],
  ["Are you sad?","A little. He never visited. He sent a man to tell my fortune once, and then didn't pay."]],
 "Take care today. The air's thick with the wrong kind of silence."],
11:["(Zora is laying out cards in a cross on the table.) Alms for the poor, and a fair day's pay. The island has a conscience this week. It's a good omen, though it won't last.",[
  ["What do you see?","Hands. Many hands, reaching. Some give, some take. The cards say it's a good week for those who give without counting."],
  ["Will the mill hands get their pay?","I don't predict outcomes. Outcomes aren't mine to give. But I see a lot of people standing together. It's a start."],
  ["Is that the cards or your instincts?","They're the same thing, my dear, if you've been listening long enough."]],
 "Give what you can. It comes back in other forms."],
12:["(Zora is reading a woman's palm, from across the table, in profile.) Don't mind me. I'm in the middle of a very important line. It's about a man who's lost a record. And a woman who's lost a lot more.",[
  ["Who's the woman?","I can't say. But the line is deep, and it says someone's about to be asked to sell something she loves."],
  ["Are you always right?","Often. Not always. But the rare times I'm wrong, people forget. And the times I'm right, they tell everyone. It's the best system in the world."],
  ["Do you read for the Syndicate?","I've read for everyone. A Don likes his fortune. He likes it to be the same as last time, though."]],
 "Come back when you've lost something. I'll tell you where it is."],
13:["(Zora has a basket of coloured scarves and is pulling them out one at a time.) Free samples, they're giving away, in Kingsgrad. A thing's only free if you don't ask what it's for. I'd advise caution.",[
  ["Is it a trap?","It's a promise, which is a very old kind of trap. It's usually gentle. Sometimes it's a recipe."],
  ["What recipe?","I've heard talk of a recipe. A secret one. Someone's looking for it, and someone's guarding it. It will cause trouble."],
  ["You're hinting at something.","I'm hinting at everything. It's a habit. It's also, as it happens, a living."]],
 "A scarf for you. No charge. It will be cold on the fourteenth."],
14:["(Zora is wearing her best purple shawl and a great many bangles.) Today. The big gathering. The cards have been unusually shy about it. I think they don't want to spoil the surprise.",[
  ["What do you expect?","I expect a lot of feeling. A great wave of it, and then a little ripple, and then people going home, and arguing in the kitchen."],
  ["Are you going?","I'll watch from a distance. I always do. A seer who's in the middle of things can't see them."],
  ["Is it an omen?","Every day is an omen. This one's wearing a better hat."]],
 "Whatever you see, remember: it's just the beginning."],
15:["(Zora has two cards face up, a crown and a cross.) The two of them, side by side. It's an old spread. It means a choice, between two things that look nothing alike and mean the same.",[
  ["The princes, you mean?","Perhaps. Or perhaps you. Everyone on the island has a crown and a cross inside them. It's the usual trouble."],
  ["Which one will win?","I won't say. It would spoil the flavour. And it's not up to the cards."],
  ["Do the princes consult you?","One has. The other would, but he's afraid I'd tell him the truth."]],
 "Choose with your heart, and check with your head. It's an old rule, and it never fails."],
16:["(Zora has a very small bell on the table beside the ball.) A debate, I hear. Between those who'd like the Party and those who'd like a vote. I've given each a little reading, free. It's my civic duty.",[
  ["What did you tell them?","The Communist, that he'll be heard but not believed. The Democrat, that he'll be believed but not heard. They both left cross."],
  ["Is that your gift?","It's my curse. I see people as they are. It's rarely convenient."],
  ["Will you go to the debate?","I'll sit at the back and watch the hands. You can tell more from a man's hands than from his speeches."]],
 "Listen to the pauses. They hold the real arguments."],
17:["(Zora's candle flame is leaning, quite steadily, to the north.) There. You see? The first tremor, a few days ago, and now the candle bends. It's the mountain, breathing. It's waking up.",[
  ["Is there danger?","There's change. It always comes with a little danger. I'd advise keeping boots by the door."],
  ["Have you told the others?","I've told Bruno, who laughed. I've told Meg, who checked her knots. I've told Buttons, who ate. It was the best reaction."],
  ["Can you see how it ends?","I can see a lot of grey. And a lot of people carrying each other. It's not a bad ending."]],
 "Sleep lightly. The mountain's a light sleeper too."],
18:["(Zora is polishing the crystal ball with a soft cloth.) The church is looking for a relic, I hear. A holy thing. The monks have a way of losing the very things they treasure. It's a talent.",[
  ["Do you know where it is?","I know it's closer than they think, and further than they'd like. It's the sort of answer that earns me my living."],
  ["Is it a real relic?","Everything is real, if enough people believe in it. It's a kind of magic, and it's the only kind that works."],
  ["What about the Cola museum?","I've heard there's a cup who's a celebrity. I'd read his fortune. It would be a very short reading."]],
 "Look in the last place you'd expect. And then look in the one after."],
19:["(Zora has drawn the heavy purple curtains, and the caravan is in candlelight at midday.) I'm resting my eyes. The visions are loud today. I keep hearing a sound like a great drum, very far away.",[
  ["Is it the mountain?","It's what the mountain will say, when it's finished clearing its throat. I'd keep the horses near."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Of the mountain? No. Of the people? A little. They'll panic. They always do. It's why I tell them softly."],
  ["What about the Communists calling for comrades?","They're asking for hands. They'll get them. It's the one thing people can't resist: being needed."]],
 "Keep your boots on tonight. And your friends near."],
20:["(Zora hands you a small blue bead on a string.) Take it. It's not magic. It's glass. But when you hold it, you'll remember to be calm. That's the whole of the spell.",[
  ["Why are you giving me this?","Because tomorrow, I think, you'll need something to hold. I've told the others to prepare. I'm telling you, because you listen."],
  ["Tomorrow? What happens?","The ground will remember what it is. It's nothing you can't survive. But you'll want to be near people you like."],
  ["How certain are you?","I'm never certain. I'm persuasive. It's different. But I'd bet the cat."]],
 "Keep the bead in your pocket. It's cold, and it's yours."],
21:["(Zora steps out of her caravan, covered in ash, and quite calm.) Well. That's that. The mountain has said what it wanted. Nobody's dead, and nobody's lost. It's the best outcome I could have asked for.",[
  ["You predicted it.","I predicted a fire in a quiet place. It was a fairly safe bet, given the steam. But I'm glad no one laughed too hard."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm shaken. Even a seer is shaken by the sight of her own prophecy. I'll have a cup of tea and a good cry. In that order."],
  ["What now?","Now the clean-up. And the fear, which will take longer. And then, I think, a very strange kind of peace."]],
 "Be kind to each other. It's the one prophecy I'm sure of."],
22:["(Zora is sweeping the steps of her caravan with a silver-handled brush.) Ash on the cards. Ash on the ball. I've never had such a fine grey on my best deck. It's like reading in the fog.",[
  ["Can you still read?","With a damp cloth and good will. The ash is a reader's friend. It shows where the fingers have been."],
  ["What do you see now?","Hands, hauling. Brooms, sweeping. Faces coming out of shadows. A very hopeful picture, actually."],
  ["Are people coming to you?","More than ever. They don't want the future. They want someone to say it will come."]],
 "Keep your cloth damp. Keep your heart dry. It's the best advice I have."],
23:["(Zora sits at the table, with the ball covered in a grey cloth.) The Party's mending its machines and the oil men their pipes. Everybody's mending. It's a very good sign, when a people mends.",[
  ["Do you see more shocks?","A few small ones. They're the mountain turning over. I'd stay away from tall, thin things."],
  ["Who's been to see you?","A woman from the north who'd lost a goat. A man who'd lost his nerve. A child who'd lost his shoe. I found all three, more or less."],
  ["Is the circus all right?","We're coping. Bruno's cross and Buttons is happy, which is how you know it's normal."]],
 "A little mending, every day. It's all anyone can do."],
24:["(Zora is looking at a single card, a hooded figure on a peak.) The Hermit. The monks are going up the mountain, I hear. For a retreat. The cards approve. It's a good time to be by yourself, if you've the right kind of silence.",[
  ["Do you ever go on retreat?","Every night. It's called sleep. I recommend it."],
  ["Would you read for the monks?","They don't need it. They've been reading the sky for a thousand years. It's the same thing, with less glitter."],
  ["How is everyone?","Calm. The aftershocks are small, and the children are asleep. It's the quiet part of the story."]],
 "Take a little time to be by yourself. The ash will wait."],
25:["(Zora has three cards face up: a crown, a rose, a bridge.) A crown for everyone, they say, and a princess for the vote. It's a pretty spread. I don't know who dealt it. But the Bridge is a very good card.",[
  ["What does the bridge mean?","That two sides can meet, if someone's willing to stand in the middle. It's a lonely place, but a useful one."],
  ["Who stands in the middle?","Whoever's brave, or silly, or both. I've known a jester who could."],
  ["Is it a happy omen?","It's an honest one. They're less common."]],
 "Cross the bridge when you come to it. And not before."],
26:["(Zora is arranging cola bottles on her table, as if they were a constellation.) A prince in every bottle. It's a marvellous thing to see a man's face on a label. It's the oldest kind of magic: selling someone a piece of a king.",[
  ["Is it a good omen?","It's a harmless one. People drink their kings. It's cheaper than burying them."],
  ["What about the oil man's spa?","Hot water and ambition. A fine combination. It tends to get cold at the edges."],
  ["Can you read a bottle?","Yes. This one says it's thirsty, and it's got a long way to go."]],
 "Don't drink a prince on an empty stomach."],
27:["(Zora has a ticket to the Eastbrook pictures, and is turning it over.) The Communists are showing a film. A revolution, on a screen. I'd like to see it, if only to find out whether they've got the ending right.",[
  ["Do you go to the pictures?","Once a year. I like the dark, and the faces. It's like a crystal ball you can eat popcorn in."],
  ["What do you see for the island?","A great deal of waiting. And then a moment when everyone breathes in. And then, as always, tea."],
  ["Are you worried about the week?","I'm never worried. I'm concerned. It's more professional."]],
 "If the film's too long, leave. The ending's usually the same."],
28:["(Zora is wearing all of her rings, and looks as though she's about to read the national weather.) Today's the biggest day. Every faction's out, waving something. I've closed the shutters. I can't read in a crowd. They talk over the ball.",[
  ["What do the cards say?","The cards say 'stay home'. I've taken it as personal advice. It's not often they're so direct."],
  ["Will someone win?","Someone will think they've won. It's not the same as winning, but it's the next best thing."],
  ["Do you feel anything?","A very large silence, like the one before a bell. It's been there since the morning."]],
 "Keep your head down today, and your heart up."],
29:["(Zora is holding the crystal ball in both hands, as if it were warm.) Tomorrow's the end of it. The ball's gone clear. No pictures, no colours. It happens when everything's been said. It's the clearest I've ever seen it.",[
  ["Does that mean something?","It means the future's not in the ball. It's in the room. I've known it for years, but I pretend otherwise."],
  ["Are you ready?","As ready as anyone. I've told the circus to wear its best. It's the one thing they've never needed telling."],
  ["Any last prediction?","Some people will cry. Some will cheer. A few will do both. And someone will find a lost glove."]],
 "Go home, rest, and wash your face. You'll want to be clean."],
30:["(Zora has put out all but one candle, and the caravan is soft and quiet.) The last day. I've nothing left to tell. The cards are asleep and the ball is dark. It's what I'd hoped for. A day without prophecy.",[
  ["Any last words?","Be good to each other. It's not a prediction. It's a request."],
  ["What will you do afterwards?","Fold the shawl, pack the cards, and follow the circus. The road will tell me where."],
  ["Thank you for everything.","You're welcome. And thank you for listening. It's rarer than prophecy."]],
 "Go in peace. And mind the ash on the stairs."],
};
// Magnus, Diversified Petroleum executive and its candidate for Republican Party leader, at the HQ in Frostholm (Diversified
// Petroleum). Threads: the race against Harald (Kelp Cola) and Carlo (the Syndicate), run by Margit, to the vote on the 29th; his
// resentment of Dario; his stiff speeches on index cards; oil as the island's only future; the cello he practises, to his neighbours' grief.
DAILY['Magnus']={
7:["(Magnus is at the corner desk, squaring a stack of index cards.) The press. Good. I am Magnus, Diversified Petroleum, and I am standing for leader of the Republican Party. You may write that down. Spelling is M, A, G...",[
  ["I can spell Magnus.","Many people can't. Margit at the party HQ put me down as 'Magnum' on the first list. It has been corrected."],
  ["What are the cards for?","My speech. Forty-one cards. I am trying to get it down to forty, but every card is essential."],
  ["Does Dario back your campaign?","Dario backs anything that makes him money. At present, that includes me. (He squares the cards again.) I intend to keep it that way."]],
 "Card one: 'Sarivalto runs on oil.' The rest follows from that."],
8:["(Magnus is reading an insurance policy with a ruler under each line.) There has been an incident at the rig. I am not at liberty to discuss it, except to say that the policy is very clear about acts of God.",[
  ["What does it say about acts of God?","That they are excluded. Which is unfortunate, because God appears to be very active in the North Sea."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not as far as this office has been told. This office is often told things last."],
  ["Sounds like Dario's problem, not yours.","Everything at Diversified Petroleum is Dario's, including the problems. He simply prefers that I hold them."]],
 "I must read clause nine again. Clause nine is a snake."],
9:["(Magnus is standing very straight, practising in front of the window.) 'Fellow Republicans. The island has a choice.' (He stops.) Too dramatic? Margit said I sound like a tax return.",[
  ["You sound fine. A bit stiff.","Stiff is reliable. Nobody ever lost money backing a stiff man."],
  ["Who are you up against?","Harald, for Kelp Cola, who smiles like a toothpaste advertisement. And Carlo, for the Syndicate, who smiles like a man counting your teeth."],
  ["Try saying it like you mean it.","(He tries again, louder.) 'Fellow Republicans.' (Ruth looks up from reception.) ...I meant it the first time."]],
 "Card one is settled. Cards two to forty-one remain under review."],
10:["(Magnus is in a black suit, buttoned to the top.) The funeral is today. I have been seated beside Harald, from Kelp Cola. I expect the king would have found that amusing.",[
  ["Will you speak to Harald?","Briefly, and about the weather. In a chapel, even rivals talk about the weather."],
  ["Did you know the king?","I shook his hand once, at the opening of the fuel depot. He asked me whether I liked my job. I said yes. That was not true, but it was correct."],
  ["Is Dario going?","Dario is going in the second car, and I am going in the fourth. These things are noticed."]],
 "Black is very practical. It requires no decisions."],
11:["(Magnus is adding up a column of figures, twice.) Do you know how many barrels lie under the shelf off this island, by our survey? Neither does anybody, officially. Unofficially, it's a great many.",[
  ["How many is a great many?","Enough to give every family on Sarivalto a car, and the roads to drive it on. Phil gives them cola."],
  ["Doesn't the oil belong to the island?","The oil belongs to whoever can lift it. That is not a philosophy. It is engineering."],
  ["You should put that on a card.","(He writes it down.) Card forty-two. I was trying to get down to forty."]],
 "Barrels, not bubbles. That is my slogan. Margit says it needs work."],
12:["(Magnus has a folder under his hand and keeps his voice low.) Not a good day for chatter, I'm afraid. I have papers to move, and the walls in this building are thin.",[
  ["What sort of papers?","The dull sort. The sort no one reads. That is the best kind, in my experience."],
  ["Thin walls? Who's listening?","Ruth hears everything at reception. Dario hears everything Ruth does. I hear the clock."],
  ["You look worried.","I look prepared. People confuse the two."]],
 "We'll speak again when the paper is dry."],
13:["(Magnus is tearing up an index card.) I gave half my speech to the Republican HQ today. Margit timed it. She said it would be shorter if I left out the numbers. The numbers are the speech.",[
  ["How did they take it?","Nils from the electronics store asked a question about drainage. Otto from the cinema fell asleep. I counted that as a draw."],
  ["Kelp Cola are giving away free samples today.","Harald thinks he can buy votes with fizzy water. He may be right. That is what frightens me."],
  ["Leave out the numbers, then.","Without numbers I am a tall man in a grey suit. With numbers, I am a tall man in a grey suit who is correct."]],
 "Card twelve has been retired. It served well."],
14:["(Magnus has his hair combed flat and a party badge on his lapel.) The fourteenth. The whole island in one place. I have prepared a short greeting, in case anyone asks me for one.",[
  ["Will anyone ask?","Probably not. Preparation is never wasted. It is only unused."],
  ["What will everyone be watching for?","Who stands next to whom. On this island a handshake is a treaty and a nod is a scandal."],
  ["You seem almost excited.","I am never excited. I am, at most, ready."]],
 "If you see Harald, tell him my badge is bigger. It is."],
15:["(Magnus is reading the Gazette.) Leopold or Augustus. Crown or cross. The whole island is arguing about which prince, and nobody is asking what either of them knows about pipelines.",[
  ["Which prince would suit you?","Whichever one leaves the economy to grown-ups. I suspect neither, but Leopold at least likes cars."],
  ["Will the Republicans take a side?","The Republicans are waiting to see which side wins, and then they will have taken it all along."],
  ["Why not be king yourself?","(He considers it seriously.) The hours are poor and the pension is uncertain."]],
 "Crown, cross, ballot box. Oil goes under all of them."],
16:["(Magnus is listening to the radio with his eyes shut.) The Great Debate. Anna for the Communists, the Democrats for themselves. I'm listening for technique. They both breathe in the wrong places.",[
  ["Anything you can steal for your speech?","Anna pauses before her best line, and lets them wait. I have tried it. People think I've forgotten my card."],
  ["Who do you think won?","Neither. A debate is two people losing in public. That is why I prefer written submissions."],
  ["Would you debate Harald like that?","I would welcome it. Harald would bring a cola bottle and wave it. I would bring a graph."]],
 "Breathe on the comma. Not before. That is the lesson."],
17:["(Magnus starts when you come in, and puts a hand flat on his desk.) Oh. It's you. I'm a little on edge. Nobody's seen Dario all day, and the whole building is behaving like a henhouse.",[
  ["Where do you think he is?","If I knew where Dario went when he didn't want to be found, I would be running this company, not explaining it."],
  ["Who's in charge while he's gone?","Technically, I am. In practice, Ruth. In theory, God, though I wouldn't put that in the minutes."],
  ["Why are you on edge?",["A man in a good coat has been standing outside the Skyline Apartments two evenings running. Probably nothing.",[
    ["Did you tell anyone?","I told the porter. He said it was a free country. I said not yet, it isn't."],
    ["What did he look like?","Well dressed. Patient. The sort who has never once in his life had to queue."]]]]],
 "I shall be at my desk. Where anyone can see me. That's rather the point."],
18:["(Magnus is steadying a cup that is rattling in its saucer.) The ground shook again. Every time it does, the insurance clause on volcanic activity becomes more interesting to me.",[
  ["Is the rig covered for earthquakes?","For earthquakes, partly. For volcanoes, the policy uses the word 'regrettably' four times."],
  ["Are people frightened?","The depot crew have started making jokes about it. Britta says that's how you tell a Frostholmer is frightened."],
  ["Maybe the mountain is voting.","Then it is voting against everyone equally. I almost respect that."]],
 "I'll draft a memo on volcanoes. Nobody will read it, but it will exist."],
19:["(Magnus is holding a cello case beside his desk, awkwardly.) Don't. I'm taking it to be restrung. And yes, I play. Badly, according to the whole fourth floor of the Skyline.",[
  ["How long have you played?","Since I was eight. I was never good, but I was very regular. The neighbours say that's worse."],
  ["What do you play?","Bach, mostly. Slowly. Britta on the floor below bangs on her ceiling with a broom at the fast parts, so I have stopped playing fast parts."],
  ["A cellist in politics. That'll win votes.","Do you think so? (He brightens.) No. You're being amusing. I can tell, now."]],
 "Bach is very orderly. Like a good balance sheet."],
20:["(Magnus is reading a list of names with a pencil.) Margit has sent round the list of Republicans eligible to vote. Forty-three people. Every one of them wants something.",[
  ["What do they want?","Nils wants cheaper shipping. Frank wants nobody to mention guns. Doctor Lind wants a hospital. Otto wants a bigger cinema screen. I can offer all of it, on paper."],
  ["How many will vote for you?","By my count, fourteen, with eleven undecided. By Harald's count, I imagine, three."],
  ["And Carlo?","Carlo's voters don't tell anybody how they'll vote. Nobody asks them twice."]],
 "Forty-three. I know every one of their birthdays now. It was a long weekend."],
21:["(Magnus has ash on his shoulders and his tie askew, which you have never seen.) The mountain has erupted. There is lava in the north and ash on every desk in Frostholm, and I have just realised I am frightened.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Ruth has rung every name on the staff list. Everyone answered. That is the best news I've had all year."],
  ["What will it do to the campaign?","I have no idea, and for the first time today I don't care."],
  ["Your tie's crooked.","(He looks down at it, and leaves it.) So it is. Let it be crooked. It's that sort of day."]],
 "Go somewhere with a roof. The ash is coming down like snow."],
22:["(Magnus has his tie straight again.) I've drafted a statement on the eruption for the Republican HQ. 'Sarivalto stands firm.' Margit says it's the first good thing I've written.",[
  ["Is that the whole statement?","The whole statement is two pages. Margit kept the first line and threw away the rest. She's ruthless. I admire it."],
  ["How's the clean-up going?","Dario has the depot trucks washing down the streets. He'll put it in the Gazette. I'd have done the same, which annoys me."],
  ["Does it change things for oil?","It makes people afraid, and afraid people want something solid. There's nothing more solid than a barrel."]],
 "Stands firm. Three words. I may use them for everything now."],
23:["(Magnus is reading a telex and pressing his lips together.) Something under the waterline at the rig. Dario's in a fury. I am being calm, which he takes as an insult.",[
  ["What happened?","Something gave way below the water. Whether it was the sea, the shaking or a person, I couldn't say. I am only allowed to say 'incident'."],
  ["Could it hurt your campaign?","If the rig floods, the man standing for oil is standing in the sea. Harald would enjoy that photograph."],
  ["You don't seem sorry for Dario.","I'm sorry for the rig. Dario is unsinkable. I've checked."]],
 "Incident. I shall say incident until it means nothing at all."],
24:["(Magnus is ironing a shirt on his desk, with a travelling iron.) Don't look so surprised. I iron my own shirts. A candidate must be crisp, and the laundry in Frostholm is full of ash.",[
  ["You're full of surprises.","I'm full of routine. Routine only looks surprising to people who haven't got any."],
  ["Any news from the Republican HQ?","Carlo has been seen buying drinks for half the party at the casino. Harald has been seen giving away cola. I have been seen ironing."],
  ["Who taught you to iron?","My mother. She said a man with creased sleeves will never be trusted with money. She was right about most things."]],
 "Hot iron, cool head. That might be card forty-three."],
25:["(Magnus is reading the Gazette with distaste.) A crown for everyone, from the palace. A princess for the Democrats. The whole island is handing out royalty like Kelp Cola samples.",[
  ["Not your sort of politics?","I prefer policies to princesses. Princesses don't come with a budget."],
  ["How's your count of votes?","Sixteen. Two of the undecided have come over. One of them says it's because I was the only candidate who didn't try to buy her a drink."],
  ["Would you ever smile in a photograph?","I did, once, in 1974. The photographer asked if I was in pain."]],
 "Sixteen. I've written it on the inside of my wrist. Don't tell Margit."],
26:["(Magnus is looking at a memo with a picture of a bathhouse on it.) Dario wants to buy the spa on the mountain. The volcano spa. After an eruption. And he wants me to find the money.",[
  ["Will you find it?","I always find the money. It's what I'm for, apparently."],
  ["Is it a good investment?","It's a hot bath on the side of an angry mountain. As an investment, it is a very expensive way to sit down."],
  ["Why does Dario want it?","He says his back. I think he simply heard the word 'no' and couldn't bear it."]],
 "I shall be in the ledger room, finding money where there isn't any."],
27:["(Magnus is reading a card aloud, quietly.) 'The future of Sarivalto is not in a bottle. It is in a barrel.' (He looks up.) Is that too pointed? Harald will be in the room.",[
  ["It's good. Keep it.","Thank you. I've been sitting on it for a fortnight. It's the only line I wrote at three in the morning that survived breakfast."],
  ["Very pointed. Harald will sulk.","Harald sulks the way other men shave: daily, and with great care."],
  ["Isn't there a poker game today?","At the pizzeria. Carlo's playing, and half the party's watching him. I don't gamble. My whole life is a careful bet already."]],
 "Barrel, not bottle. Card forty-three. Final answer."],
28:["(Magnus is holding a milkshake as if it might bite him.) Dario's office party. Bowling. Streamers. I have been told I must 'loosen up', so I have undone one button.",[
  ["Have a go on the lane.","I bowled earlier. Two pins. Ruth applauded, which was kind, and then she bowled a strike, which was not."],
  ["Is everyone pushing hard, everywhere?","The whole island is out today, every faction at once. I've never seen so many people trying so hard to look calm."],
  ["One button. Wild.","(He very nearly smiles.) Don't tell anyone. I have a reputation."]],
 "Tomorrow is the vote. Today, apparently, I bowl."],
29:["(Magnus is dressed in his best grey suit, with forty-three cards in a rubber band.) The vote is today, at the party HQ. Margit is counting. I have checked my speech forty times and my tie twice.",[
  ["Good luck.","Thank you. Luck is a variable. I've planned around it, but I'll take it."],
  ["What will you say if you lose?","I've written that one too. It's four cards. It's better than the winning one, which worries me."],
  ["What does Dario say?","He said, 'Don't embarrass me.' From Dario, that is practically a blessing."]],
 "Barrels, not bubbles. Wish me a short count."],
30:["(Magnus is sitting very still at his desk, with his index cards in a neat pile.) The last day. Whatever is settled, it's settled today. I find I have nothing left to prepare.",[
  ["How does that feel?","Very strange. Like standing on a platform with no train timetable."],
  ["What will you do now?","Go home, and play the cello. All of it, including the fast parts. Britta will have to cope."],
  ["You did your best.","I did my most. Whether that was my best, the island will tell me."]],
 "Barrels, not bubbles. It may not have been my best slogan, but it was mine."],
};
// Margit, secretary of the Republican Party in Frostholm, running the leadership election. Threads: the rulebook of 1921 and
// her pencils, the membership roll that keeps swelling, the candidates' friends and the gifts she sends back, and Quorum, her
// budgerigar.
DAILY['Margit']={
7:["(Margit looks up from a ledger, pen poised.) Are you a member? No? Then you can't vote, but you can stand there. Mind the ballot box. It's new.",[
  ["What's the election for?","The leadership of the Republican Party. The post's been empty since Mr Holm retired in July. Three candidates. Voting on the twenty-ninth. I'm running it."],
  ["Who are the candidates?","Harald, from Kelp Cola. Magnus, from Diversified Petroleum. And Carlo, who says he's 'in import and export'. (She makes a note.) I'm to be fair to all three."],
  ["Nice box.","Thank you. Oak, with a brass lock, and one key, which is on a ribbon round my neck. I'm told that's fussy. I'm told that by people who'd like the key."]],
 "Please don't lean on the ballot box. It's not a table."],
8:["(Margit is reading a small, very old book.) The party rulebook. Nineteen twenty-one. Everything's in here, if you know where to look.",[
  ["What does it say about elections?","Rule forty: 'Each member shall have one vote, cast in secret, in his own hand.' His. They wrote it before women were members. I've crossed it out and written 'their'."],
  ["Is it still useful?","Very. Rule sixty-two says the secretary may refuse any gift that might 'sway her judgement'. I've used rule sixty-two four times this week."],
  ["Who's been sending gifts?","Flowers, chocolates, a bottle of something French, and a box of cigars. (She sniffs.) I don't smoke. I sent them all back. With a note citing rule sixty-two."]],
 "Rule sixty-two. Everyone should read it."],
9:["Quorum didn't sing this morning. Quorum always sings. (She sighs.) My budgerigar. He's very sensitive to atmosphere.",[
  ["What's upsetting him?","The candidates. Well, their friends. A man from the Syndicate came round to my flat last night to 'introduce himself'. Quorum hid in his bell."],
  ["Why call a bird Quorum?","Because when he's in the room, we can start. (She doesn't smile.) It's a party joke. It was funnier in nineteen sixty-five."],
  ["Did you complain about the visitor?","To whom? Sergeant Hanna's in Kingsgrad, and he didn't do anything. He just stood in the door and admired Quorum. That's what frightened me."]],
 "If you've any millet, Quorum likes millet."],
10:["(Margit is in black.) The party's shut for the funeral. I've only come in to check the ballot box hasn't moved.",[
  ["Has it moved?","No. I measured where it stands with a tape measure. Two foot from the podium, one foot from the wall. It's exactly where I left it."],
  ["Did you know the king?","He opened this building in nineteen sixty. Cut the ribbon, made a joke about Republicans in a monarchy, and asked for a cup of tea. He was very polite."],
  ["Aren't Republicans against kings?","Not on this island. We're for business, low taxes and a quiet life. The king was very quiet. We approved of him."]],
 "God rest him. I'm going to check the box again."],
11:["(Margit is adding names to a list, very slowly.) Forty-one new members. In four days. Forty-one.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","The party had sixty-three members last year. We've signed up forty-one since the leadership opened. Mostly from Kelp Cola, the oil company and Hope Springs."],
  ["Can they vote?","Rule nineteen: anyone who's paid their dues by the twentieth may vote. They've all paid. In cash. In very new notes."],
  ["Isn't that cheating?","It's not against the rules. (She puts down her pen.) It's against the spirit of the rules. The rulebook doesn't have a rule about spirit."]],
 "Forty-one. I'll need more pencils."],
12:["Harald came in today. All smiles. He asked if I'd thought about 'a bigger role in the party'. A bigger role! I'm the secretary.",[
  ["What did he mean?","He meant a job. After the election. If he wins. (She sniffs.) I told him I'm happy with my role and my pencils. He smiled more. It's unsettling."],
  ["Are the candidates all like that?","Magnus doesn't smile. He sends letters on company paper, with his rules for the election. His rules, if you please. I've filed them under 'not rules'."],
  ["And Carlo?","Carlo sits at his campaign desk all day, being polite to me. Every morning: 'Good morning, Margit.' Every evening: 'Good evening, Margit.' He's never once asked me for anything. I'm waiting."]],
 "Not rules. Filed."],
13:["(Margit has a crate of Kelp Cola by the podium, unopened.) They brought it this morning. For 'the volunteers'. I've no volunteers.",[
  ["Why not send it back?","I have. Twice. They keep bringing it. It's free today, all over the island. I can't refuse something everyone's getting. That's the trick of it."],
  ["Will it affect the vote?","Every member in Frostholm's got a bottle of Harald's cola in their hand. That's very good advertising. But you can't vote with a bottle. You need a pencil."],
  ["Can I have one?","Take two. Then I'll be two closer to having none. (She makes a note.) Two bottles, removed by the press."]],
 "Two bottles. Noted."],
14:["(Margit is locking the ballot box with great care.) The big day. I'm going. I'll take Quorum. He doesn't like to be left alone when there's a crowd.",[
  ["You're taking a budgie to a rally?","In his travelling cage, under a cloth. He'll hear it all. He's a member, honorary. He's very well informed."],
  ["What do you expect?","I expect everybody to be very loud and very sure, and the Republicans to stand in a corner looking at their watches. We're not a shouting party."],
  ["Will the candidates be there?","All three. Harald with a smile, Magnus with a speech, and Carlo with friends. I'll be watching the friends."]],
 "Big day. Keep your hands in your pockets. Everyone's after something."],
15:["(Margit is on the telephone.) No. No. Rule forty. No. (She hangs up.) Magnus wants to vote by post. For his whole department.",[
  ["Is that allowed?","Rule forty says 'in their own hand'. A department doesn't have a hand. Each member comes to the box. That's how it's always been."],
  ["What did he say?","That the rulebook is 'outdated'. It's from nineteen twenty-one. It's meant to be outdated. That's the point of a rulebook."],
  ["What about the palace and the Church?","Rows about crowns and crosses. I've no opinion. I've enough rows of my own."]],
 "Rule forty. In their own hand. Everyone."],
16:["(Margit has the debate on the radio, low.) Anna and Erik. Very good debating. The Republicans never have a debate. We just have a vote and then tea.",[
  ["Why no debate?","Last time we tried, in nineteen seventy-four, Mr Holm and Mr Berg talked about drainage for two hours and the audience went home. Since then, tea."],
  ["Will Harald, Magnus and Carlo debate?","I offered. Harald said yes, on Kelp Cola's stage. Magnus said yes, on Diversified Petroleum's. Carlo said he'd prefer 'a quiet word with each member, privately'. I said no to all three."],
  ["What do you think of the Democrats?","They want everyone to vote. Lovely. They've no idea how many pencils that is."]],
 "Pencils. Thousands of them. Imagine."],
17:["(Margit is gripping the podium.) Did you feel that? The floor. And the ballot box moved. An inch. I've measured it.",[
  ["Was it an earthquake?","A tremor, Nils says. He's got a machine. I don't care what it is: I want my ballot box where I left it. (She pushes it back an inch.)"],
  ["Have you heard Dario's missing?","Everyone's heard. Magnus has been very calm about it. Too calm. I've written that down, and then I crossed it out, because it's not my business."],
  ["Are you all right?","No. The ground's moving, a man's missing, and Quorum's off his millet. But I've got a vote to run. Rule one: the vote goes ahead."]],
 "Rule one. The vote goes ahead."],
18:["Someone's been in the files. (She holds up a folder.) The membership roll. Someone's moved it from the second drawer to the third.",[
  ["Who could have done it?","Anyone who's been in this hall. Which is everyone, and their friends, and their friends' friends. Carlo's desk is nearest the files."],
  ["Is anything missing?","Nothing's missing. Everything's here. Someone's read it. That's worse. They know who the members are now, and where they live."],
  ["Are you going to the police?","And say what? 'Somebody read my list'? Sergeant Hanna would laugh. I've bought a padlock for the drawer instead. Nils says three is careful."]],
 "One padlock. I'm not as careful as Nils."],
19:["(Margit is putting a cover over Quorum's cage.) He won't stop flapping. The ground keeps going. Birds know.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","Of the mountain? A little. Of the vote? A lot. If the mountain goes, at least nobody can say it was rigged."],
  ["Who's helping you run the vote?","Nobody. I've asked members to stand by the box as witnesses. Nils said yes. Otto said yes, if he's back for the matinee. The doctor said maybe."],
  ["What's the worst thing that could happen?",["(She thinks.) A full box and a wrong count. I count everything twice, and then I ask someone else to count it, and if they get a different number we all count it again.",[
    ["That could take all night.","It might. I've packed sandwiches. And a flask. And a spare pencil."],
    ["What if someone stuffs the box?","They'd have to get the key. (She touches the ribbon at her neck.) And they'd have to get past Quorum."]]]]],
 "Twice. Then again. That's how you count."],
20:["(Margit is in her coat in the hall, with a suitcase.) I'm sleeping here tonight. With the box. Don't look at me like that.",[
  ["Why sleep here?","Because of the ground. Because of the files. Because Carlo's friends have started standing outside in the evening, smoking. I'd rather be with the box."],
  ["Is that wise?","Probably not. But I've a camp bed from Nordmart, a flask, and Quorum. And the door locks from the inside. I checked twice."],
  ["Who's that man at the Hotel Aurora?","The foreign one? Upstairs? He came in here last week and asked for a copy of our rulebook. Very polite. I gave him one. I wish I hadn't. I don't know why."]],
 "If you hear anyone at the door at midnight, tell them I'm awake."],
21:["(The windows are grey. Margit is sitting on the ballot box, holding Quorum's cage on her knee.) It's gone up. The mountain. And I'm sitting on the box.",[
  ["Why are you sitting on the box?","If the roof comes in, I want to know where the box is. (She looks at you.) I know how that sounds. It's been a very long night."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, they say. Thank heaven. Lava in the hills. Ash on everything. Quorum's very upset. So am I."],
  ["Will the vote still happen?","Rule one. The vote goes ahead. Rule one doesn't mention volcanoes. If it did, it would still say the vote goes ahead."]],
 "Brush yourself down before you leave. And don't sit on the box. That's mine."],
22:["(Margit is wiping ash off the ballot box with a clean handkerchief.) Every inch of it. Ash in the lock, ash in the slot. I'll not have a grey vote.",[
  ["Are you sending the letters?","Typed them last night. 'Dear member, the leadership vote will take place on the twenty-ninth, as planned, volcano notwithstanding.' 'Notwithstanding'. I like that word."],
  ["Did the candidates help with the ash?","Harald sent men to sweep. Magnus sent a bill for the sweeping. Carlo swept it himself, in his good suit, and said 'Good morning, Margit'. I don't know what to think."],
  ["How's Quorum?","Singing again. Ash on his feathers, but singing. Birds forgive. People take longer."]],
 "Notwithstanding. Remember that word. It's very useful."],
23:["(Margit is counting pencils into boxes.) One hundred and four members. One hundred and four pencils. Plus ten spares. Plus a spare for the spares.",[
  ["One hundred and four?","Sixty-three old members and forty-one new. All with dues paid by the twentieth. They can all vote. And every new one owes his job to a candidate."],
  ["Does that worry you?","It worries me that the Republican Party might be chosen by men who joined last week. It worries me more that I can't stop it."],
  ["Could you change the rules?","Rule ninety: the rules may be changed by a vote of members. Of whom one hundred and four exist. You see the difficulty."]],
 "One hundred and fifteen pencils. That's the spirit of the rules, at least."],
24:["(Margit has a letter in her hand and her lips are tight.) Unsigned. On good paper. Telling me which way 'the members would prefer the count to go'.",[
  ["Who sent it?","No name. Good paper, good typing. Frostholm paper, from the stationer's by the Petro Tower. That narrows it to half the town."],
  ["What will you do?","I've filed it. Under 'threats'. It's a new file. I bought it this morning. I didn't think I'd need it."],
  ["Should you tell someone?","I told Quorum. He bit me. I think he's on my side."]],
 "A new file. 'Threats'. I hope it stays thin."],
25:["(Margit is practising.) 'Will members please form an orderly queue.' 'Will members please write clearly.' 'Will members please stop talking to the secretary.'",[
  ["Are you rehearsing?","I've not run a contested vote in my life. The last leader was chosen by a show of hands and a cup of tea. This one's in the newspaper."],
  ["Will Henrik cover it?","The Gazette's sending Nora. She telephoned to ask for a seat. I've given her one by the wall, where she can see the box and not touch it."],
  ["You'll be fine.","Thank you. I won't be. But I'll be fair. Fine was never on offer."]],
 "Orderly queue. Write clearly. Thank you."],
26:["Carlo asked me something today. After nineteen days of 'good morning'. He asked who would be counting with me.",[
  ["What did you tell him?","The truth. Me, Nils, and anyone else who wants to watch. I said it loudly, so the whole hall heard. Rule forty-four: the count is public."],
  ["What did he say?","'Very wise, Margit.' And he smiled, and went back to his desk. (She shivers.) I've never been called wise and felt worse about it."],
  ["Are you frightened of him?","I'm frightened of what he might not do himself. He's very polite. It's the friends I worry about."]],
 "The count is public. Rule forty-four. Everyone's welcome."],
27:["(Margit is sitting very still.) They're having a card game tonight. At the pizzeria in Kingsgrad. Carlo's playing. And half my members.",[
  ["Will they trade votes?","Nobody trades votes at a card game. They trade favours. Then they remember the favours on the twenty-ninth. It's not against the rules."],
  ["Have you been invited?","I have. A card on good paper. I sent it back, citing rule sixty-two. I've never played poker. I'd lose. Very fairly."],
  ["Are you ready for the twenty-ninth?","The box is clean. The pencils are counted. Quorum's had his millet. The only thing that isn't ready is me. I'll never be ready. It goes ahead anyway."]],
 "Rule sixty-two. Never fails."],
28:["(Margit is in the hall with the windows shut. Outside, the shouting of another faction's rally drifts past.) Everyone's out, shouting for their side. We don't shout. We wait.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Like the night before an exam. I've revised everything. And I still dream that I've lost the key."],
  ["Is the key safe?","(She pulls the ribbon out of her collar.) Here. It hasn't left my neck in a week. I sleep with it. Quorum's jealous."],
  ["Will you get any sleep?","No. I'll lie on the camp bed and count pencils in my head. One hundred and fifteen. I'll get to a hundred and fifteen and start again."]],
 "Tomorrow. Nine o'clock. Doors open. Rule one."],
29:["(Margit stands at the podium in her best suit, the key at her throat, the hall filling.) Members, please form an orderly queue. You, not a member, by the wall, please.",[
  ["How's it going?","Seventy-one papers in the box. Nobody's shouted. Carlo's friends are by the door, being very quiet. Harald's smiling. Magnus is looking at his watch."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","I'm the secretary. I don't think. I count. Twice. Then again. (She touches the key.) Please don't ask me again. People are listening."],
  ["Good luck, Margit.","Thank you. (She almost smiles.) You're the only person who's said that to me today and not wanted something. Now, by the wall, please."]],
 "By the wall, please. Thank you. Next member."],
30:["(Margit is sitting at the podium. The box is open and empty. Quorum is singing.) It's done. Counted twice. Then again. My part's done.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Tired. Very tired. And proud. Nobody stuffed the box. Nobody took the key. Every paper counted, in their own hand. Rule forty."],
  ["What happens to the island now?","Today, everything happens. Whoever leads the party will have to deal with that. I'm going home to sleep for a week. Quorum's coming too."],
  ["Will you run the next one?",["(She looks at the box.) If I'm asked. Somebody has to be fussy. It might as well be me.",[
    ["You were very fair.","That's the nicest thing anyone could say to a secretary. I'll write it in the minutes. Under 'any other business'."],
    ["Will you change the rules?","Rule sixty-two stays. Rule forty stays. I might add a rule about friends. Rule one hundred and one: no friends."]]]]],
 "Rule one. It went ahead. Good day to you."],
};
// Mario, Syndicate bookie, locked in the middle cell of Kingsgrad Police Station (Crime Syndicate). Threads: his running game
// of gin rummy with Sergeant Hanna (the tally in matchsticks), Rosaria's pizzas, the detective novels from Mary's book shop, the
// view from his window, waiting for a release that never quite comes, and never talking about the betting ring.
DAILY['Mario']={
2:["(Mario is lying on his bunk with his hands behind his head. He sits up.) A visitor! The first since the snow. The road's open, then. Come closer, the bars don't bite. The sergeant might.",[
  ["How long have you been in here?","Since August. Seven weeks. Not that I count. (He nods at the scratches on the wall.) The wall counts."],
  ["What are you in for?","A misunderstanding about some numbers. I'm a numbers man. The police are not. We'll leave it there, friend."],
  ["You seem very cheerful for a prisoner.","Three meals, a bed, and nobody owes me anything in here. Outside, I've got forty people to chase. This is a holiday."]],
 "Come back. A man in a cell needs his visitors like a plant needs water."],
3:["(Mario is shuffling a pack of cards through the bars.) Gin rummy, with the sergeant. We play every evening. She thinks I let her win on Tuesdays. I do. She thinks I don't know she knows.",[
  ["Who's winning overall?","Me, by two hundred and twelve matchsticks. We play for matchsticks. Money would be a bribe, and I'm a reformed man."],
  ["Is Sergeant Hanna kind to you?","She's the fairest woman on the island. She arrested me, she feeds me, and she beats me at rummy on Thursdays. Fair."],
  ["Could you teach me gin rummy?","Through the bars? I've taught worse in worse places. Sit on the bench there. No: first, empty your pockets. Habit."]],
 "Tell the sergeant I'll have the cards ready after supper."],
4:["I'm reading. (Mario holds up a battered paperback with a dagger on the cover.) Mary from the book shop sends them over. A Swedish detective. He has a cold in every chapter. I like him.",[
  ["What's it about?","A dead banker in a lake. Everybody wanted him dead. The detective works it out on page two hundred. I worked it out on page forty."],
  ["Do you read a lot?","In here? A book a day. Outside, I read the racing pages and the odds. This is better for the soul, the priest says. He visits on Sundays."],
  ["How did you work it out on page forty?","The wife had a new fur coat and no money. Follow the money, friend. It always ends at someone's coat."]],
 "If you see Mary, tell her more detectives. Fewer love stories."],
5:["(Mario is standing on his bunk, looking out through the little barred window.) I've got a view of the street, if I stand on the bed. I make bets with myself on what goes past. A tram, three to one. A dog, evens.",[
  ["What's the longest odds you've taken?","A prince on a bicycle. Hundred to one. It hasn't come in yet. I'm patient."],
  ["What can you see right now?","Eila and Marjut on the bench. Lisa from the supermarket, carrying boxes. And a man in a fur hat who's walked past three times. Not police. Not ours."],
  ["Don't you get bored?","Bored? I've got the whole of Kingsgrad in a window the size of a newspaper. There's more drama on that pavement than at the cinema."]],
 "Mind how you go. The man in the fur hat's still out there."],
6:["Big day for some people. (Mario winks.) There's a card game at Rosaria's. I'd give a week of my life to be at that table. Nico plays there, and Nico can't read a face to save his mother.",[
  ["Who's Nico?","The bookie in Frostholm. Clever with numbers, bad with people. He thinks he can tell when you're bluffing. He can tell when you're sneezing."],
  ["Do you miss the cards?","I play rummy with a policewoman for matchsticks. It's like asking a racehorse if it misses running, while it pulls a milk cart."],
  ["Any tips for a poker table?","Never look at your own cards twice. Watch the man who stops talking. And always eat Rosaria's pizza: it makes everyone else jealous."]],
 "If you're playing, play for me too."],
7:["(Mario is doing press-ups on the cell floor, counting in Italian.) Ventotto, ventinove, trenta. A man has to keep in shape. When they open that door I'll walk out like a tenor.",[
  ["When will they let you out?","Soon. Soon is a word I like. It's never wrong, because it never says when."],
  ["Has anyone talked about a trial?","There's a lawyer coming from Helsinki, when the boats run. I've never met him. He's very expensive. Somebody loves me."],
  ["Thirty press-ups? Show off.","Thirty in the morning, thirty in the evening. Sixty a day for seven weeks. Work it out. I'm a strong man in a small room."]],
 "Go on, before I start on the sit-ups."],
8:["(Mario has a large slice of pizza in each hand.) Rosaria came! Through the side door, with a tea towel. The sergeant took a slice for rent. I got the rest. Today is a feast day.",[
  ["Is the food here terrible?","The food here is boiled. Potatoes, boiled. Fish, boiled. I think they'd boil the coffee if they had a pot big enough."],
  ["You and Rosaria are friends?","She feeds me, I don't tell her anything she doesn't want to know. That's the best kind of friendship on this island."],
  ["Any trouble out at the oil rig, did you hear?","The sergeant had the radio on. Something about a leak, and Dario shouting down the phone. Oil and water. Neither of them listen to anyone."]],
 "Take a crust. I insist."],
9:["Gin rummy result, last night. (He holds up a matchstick.) The sergeant won. A Thursday, it wasn't, so she won fair. I'm troubled. Either she's getting better, or I'm getting soft.",[
  ["Maybe she's been practising.","With whom? There's nobody else in this station except me and the mice. She plays patience at her desk. Maybe patience is the secret."],
  ["What's the new tally?","Two hundred and eleven, my favour. One match. One match can start a fire, friend."],
  ["Have you heard about the cola business?","Everybody wants to make a cola. If I were out, I'd be taking bets: Party cola against Kelp Cola. I'd give the Party five to one. People want sugar, not slogans."]],
 "Tell nobody she beat me. I have a reputation."],
10:["(Mario is standing very still at the window.) The church bells, for the old king. They've been going all morning. Even the sergeant took her cap off at her desk.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","Me? I sold him a raffle ticket once, outside the cinema. He won a fruit basket. He tipped me ten dollars for the luck."],
  ["Anything else happening today?","(He glances at you, then back out of the window.) The bells, the funeral, the cold. Nothing that a man in a cell would know about."],
  ["Who will be king now, do you think?","I'd give Leopold evens, Augustus three to one, and a republic ten to one. But don't put money on it: I'm not licensed. Never have been."]],
 "Go and see the funeral, if you can. Say something for me."],
11:["Gustavo wrote to me. (Mario taps his breast pocket.) He wrote one line. 'Hold on, the lawyer is coming.' One line from Gustavo is worth a book from anyone else.",[
  ["You're close to the Don?","I'm his nephew. Not by blood, by agreement. He calls everyone who keeps their mouth shut 'nephew'. I'm a very good nephew."],
  ["Do you ever wonder about telling the police everything?","(Mario smiles, quite pleasantly.) Friend. I've been asked that by experts. Next question."],
  ["What will you do when you're out?","Eat a whole pizza alone, standing up, in the street. Then go to the Midnight Note and listen to Rita sing until they throw me out."]],
 "Good afternoon to you. Say hello to the outside for me."],
12:["(Mario is shuffling cards one-handed, very fast.) Another detective. This one's French. He wears gloves to every murder and he's never once wrong. I don't trust him.",[
  ["Why not trust him?","A man who's never wrong is a man who doesn't tell you when he is. I've known bankers like that. They end up in the lake."],
  ["Did you work out this one too?","The butler. It's always the butler in French books. In Swedish books it's the wife. In real life it's the accountant."],
  ["Show me that one-handed shuffle.","(He does it again, slowly.) Practice. Seven weeks of it. When I come out I'll be the best dealer on the island, and I won't be allowed near a card table."]],
 "Give my regards to Mary at the book shop."],
13:["The sergeant brought in a free Kelp Cola. (Mario holds up the can like a trophy.) They're giving them out at the tram stop. It's the most excitement I've had since the snow.",[
  ["Do you like it?","It tastes like a sweet shop fell in the sea. I'll drink it. A man in a cell can't be choosy about his excitement."],
  ["Does she bring you lots of things?","Books, papers, pizza, gossip. She's a good woman. I'd marry her if she weren't my jailer. It would make for difficult anniversaries."],
  ["Any gossip from the street today?","A man came into the station to complain that somebody's been asking questions about the Kelp Cola safe. The sergeant wrote it down very slowly."]],
 "Bring me the next Gazette, if you've finished with it."],
14:["(Mario is sitting on his bunk with his hands folded, looking at the door.) The big day. The whole island's out there in the cold, deciding things, and here I am. Like a man who missed the boat, standing on the pier.",[
  ["What do you think will happen?","Somebody will make a promise. Somebody will believe it. I'd lay odds on that, if I were laying odds."],
  ["Is the sergeant going?","She's gone to keep order. She left me the radio and a sandwich. And the keys on her desk, twelve feet away, as a test. I passed."],
  ["You didn't try for the keys?","A man who runs is a man who's guilty. I'm a man who waits. Gustavo told me to wait. So I wait."]],
 "Come back and tell me about it. Every word."],
15:["The prince and the abbot. (Mario has the Gazette spread on his knees.) Fighting over a crown like two dogs over a sausage. I'd give the abbot the long odds, but monks are patient. Like prisoners.",[
  ["Are you a churchgoer?","My mother was. She lit candles for me every Sunday, which is why I'm only in a cell and not in the sea."],
  ["Which side would you back?","The side that pays out. In my business you don't love a horse. You love a price."],
  ["Prisoners and monks: the same?","A small room, bad food, a lot of thinking and no women. The monks chose it. That's the only difference."]],
 "Mind the step. The sergeant polished it."],
16:["(Mario has a pencil and is filling in a crossword.) Seven letters. 'A free choice, by the people.' I've got 'E' at the end. The radio's full of the debate. I can't think straight.",[
  ["Election.","That's eight. Try again. (He counts on his fingers.) 'Referee'? No."],
  ["Who's winning the debate?","Anna's louder, Erik's longer. The sergeant switched it off after an hour, to save the battery. We played rummy instead. Much better democracy."],
  ["Why not ask the sergeant?","She did it already, in pen, at her desk. She's very good at crosswords. She says 'franchise' and won't tell me how."]],
 "Bring a dictionary next time. Or a lawyer. Either."],
17:["(The cell bars hum faintly, and dust falls from the ceiling.) Did you feel that? The whole station shook. I was on the bunk and it moved half a foot. The sergeant came running to check I hadn't escaped.",[
  ["Are you all right in there?","Fine. A bit of plaster in my hair. If the wall falls down, I'll stay put. Nobody can say Mario ran."],
  ["What's the gossip from Frostholm?","A man from the oil company's gone missing, the sergeant says. Nobody can find him. She's not worried. She says rich men go missing all the time, usually to Sweden."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","The sergeant thinks so. I'd say seven to one it goes up before the month's out. Don't take that bet. I'm usually right."]],
 "If it shakes again, stand in a doorway. That's what the sergeant does."],
18:["Rosaria's plate came back with my note on it. (He laughs.) I told her less dill, and she sent me a pizza covered in dill. That's a woman who knows how to win an argument.",[
  ["Did you eat it?","Every crumb. A man in a cell can't afford pride. Also, it was very good. Don't tell her."],
  ["What's the gossip from the monastery?","The monks lost a holy something. Half of them are in the snow looking for it. The sergeant says if it's stolen, she'll be the one looking."],
  ["Have you heard about a man inside a giant cup?","Kelpy? The museum mascot? Somebody's playing games inside that cup, the sergeant says. I don't ask. A man's cup is his castle."]],
 "Tell Rosaria thank you. In a way that sounds sarcastic."],
19:["(Mario is reading a new book, upside down, to see if anyone notices.) It's a love story. Mary sent it by mistake. I've read thirty pages and nobody's been murdered. I'm very worried.",[
  ["Maybe somebody gets murdered later.","I hope so. Otherwise it's just two people looking at each other on a boat for two hundred pages."],
  ["Why upside down?","To see if the sergeant was watching. She was. She turned it over, and didn't say a word. That's how you know someone cares."],
  ["Heard anything about the Midnight Note?","The sergeant's cousin works the door there. She says somebody very grand is coming to hear Rita, in a false moustache. Every week somebody's in a false moustache."]],
 "Send Mary my love, and tell her: more murders."],
20:["(Mario is lying on his back, flipping a matchstick into the air and catching it.) A quiet day in the cell. Too quiet. The sergeant's had three telephone calls from the palace this morning. Something's up.",[
  ["What sort of calls?","Something about guards and doors and who was where. She went very stiff and said 'yes, Your Highness' four times. I've never heard her say it before."],
  ["Did someone steal something?","I'm in a cell. How would I know about stealing? I'm the one person on the island with an alibi. I'm very proud of it."],
  ["How's the matchstick tally?","Two hundred and fifteen, my favour. She's distracted. I could win her whole pension today, if I were a cruel man."]],
 "Good afternoon to you. If you're the thief, I didn't see you."],
21:["(The window is grey with ash, and Mario is sitting on the floor with his back to the bars.) The mountain's gone up. The sergeant opened my cell door. 'You can go,' she said, 'if you want to run for it.' I stayed.",[
  ["Why didn't you leave?","Because she was frightened, and she opened the door for me first. You don't walk out on a woman who does that. Besides, where would I go? It's raining rocks."],
  ["Are you safe in here?","It's stone, it's low, and it's got bars. It's the safest room in Kingsgrad. People should be queuing up to get arrested."],
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Nobody, they say. Lava in the north. Ash here. The sergeant cried a bit, then made tea. I held the cups. A good afternoon, really."]],
 "Go home. Shut your windows. Don't run."],
22:["(Mario is sweeping ash out of his cell with a little brush, very thoroughly.) The sergeant let me clean the whole station. Eight hours out of the cell. I've never been so happy to hold a brush.",[
  ["You're a model prisoner.","I'm a model guest. A guest who doesn't tidy up doesn't get asked back. I want to leave when I leave, on good terms."],
  ["Is the town badly hit?","Ash on everything, the trams stuck, and Gustavo's men out on the streets with shovels. The sergeant didn't say anything about that. She just made a note."],
  ["Will you get time off for good behaviour?","There's no time to take off. Nobody's said how long I'm in for. That's the beauty of it. And the trouble."]],
 "Wipe your shoes. I've just done this floor."],
23:["Gin rummy, after the eruption. (Mario lays out a hand on the bunk.) The sergeant's been winning every game. She's playing like a woman who's decided life's too short to lose at cards.",[
  ["What's the tally now?","Two hundred and two. She's taken thirteen in three days. At this rate she'll own me by Christmas."],
  ["Has the lawyer come yet?","The boats are stuck with the ash. The lawyer's in Helsinki, eating herring, sending letters. Expensive letters. 'Patience, Mr Mario.' I've got patience. I've got nothing else."],
  ["Do you like the sergeant?","She arrested me with a smile and a 'sorry'. She's brought me soup every day for seven weeks. If that's not love, it's something better."]],
 "Don't tell her about the tally. She doesn't need the encouragement."],
24:["(Mario is quiet today, staring at the wall.) Paola came by, to bring me a clean shirt from Gustavo. She asked me one question and left. Paola never comes for nothing.",[
  ["What did she ask?","Whether the sergeant had been out of the station this morning. (He shrugs.) I said no, she's been at her desk with her crossword. That's all."],
  ["Is everything all right?","Everything's always all right, friend. It's an island. Problems come and go. Like the tide."],
  ["A clean shirt? For court?","For hope. A man in a clean shirt feels like he's going somewhere. Even if it's only to the other end of the bunk."]],
 "Go well. Mind the ash on the steps."],
25:["The detective solved it. (Mario holds up the Swedish novel, finished.) The banker in the lake was never dead. He ran off to Spain with the money and a better coat. I should have seen it.",[
  ["So you weren't right on page forty?","I was right about the coat. Wrong about the body. Half right. Half right keeps a bookie in business, friend."],
  ["Princes and princesses all over today.","Daphne wants a vote, Leopold wants a crown for everyone. The sergeant says she'll arrest whoever wins, just to be fair."],
  ["What's next on the reading list?","A Russian one. Very long. Everybody's unhappy in a different way. It should feel just like home."]],
 "Tell Mary I've finished the Swede. Ask for a sequel."],
26:["(Mario is doing sit-ups, red in the face.) I've heard. Dario's bought the spa at the hot springs. In the ash. He must have very cold feet, or very warm money.",[
  ["What does Gustavo think about Dario?","(Mario stops mid sit-up.) Gustavo thinks about many people. I don't say what he thinks. That's how I'm still on this side of the bars and not the lake."],
  ["Would you ever go to a spa?","Once, in Sorrento, with a girl from Naples. The water was too hot, the girl was too clever, and I lost all my money at cards in the lobby."],
  ["You've got stronger.","Fifty sit-ups, fifty press-ups. When I walk out, I'll walk out like a statue in a square."]],
 "Go on. I've got another twenty to do."],
27:["The tournament! (Mario grips the bars.) The whole island at Rosaria's back table, and I'm here, with a policewoman and a Russian novel. Friend, it's cruel. Who's playing? Tell me who's playing.",[
  ["Everyone who matters, I hear.","And Nico, who doesn't matter but thinks he does. Watch his left ear. It goes red when he's bluffing. I've won two hundred dollars off that ear."],
  ["Would you win, if you were there?","I'd come second, to Gustavo, on purpose. Then I'd win it all back from Nico in the car park."],
  ["Want me to tell Nico about his ear?","Never! A man's ear is his own business. If you tell him, I'll lose my pension."]],
 "Go and play. Watch the ear. Don't tell anyone I told you."],
28:["(Mario has his ear to the little window.) Marches, flags, singing. I can hear the Communists two streets away, and a royal band coming the other way. The sergeant's out keeping them apart.",[
  ["What can you see from up there?","Red flags, a lot of boots, and Eila selling hot drinks from a flask. She's the only one making money today."],
  ["Does the noise bother you?","After seven weeks of silence and a policewoman humming? It's the opera. I'd buy a ticket."],
  ["Who'll come out on top tomorrow?","I don't give odds on tomorrow any more. Tomorrow is a horse I've never seen run."]],
 "Go and watch. Bring me back a flag, any colour."],
29:["Carlo came to see me. (Mario looks impressed.) In a suit like a cathedral. He shook my hand through the bars and said 'Mario, when I'm leader, I won't forget you.' Carlo forgets his own birthday.",[
  ["Is Carlo a good man for the job?","Carlo's a good man for any job where you smile and somebody else carries the bag. That's most of politics, so yes."],
  ["Who'd you bet on, in the vote?","I'd not bet on the Republican vote. I know too much about how votes get counted. Professional ethics."],
  ["Did he bring you anything?","A campaign badge. 'Lower taxes, safer streets.' I'm wearing it. I'm the safest man on any street in Kingsgrad."]],
 "If you go to Frostholm, shout once for Carlo. From me."],
30:["(Mario is sitting up, very straight, in his clean shirt, with his hair combed.) The last day. Tomorrow, whoever runs this island, they'll need a judge, a court and a lawyer. Something will move. It must.",[
  ["Do you think you'll be out soon?","I think the door will open one day, and I'll walk out and eat a pizza in the street. Maybe tomorrow. Maybe at Christmas. Soon."],
  ["What's the final tally with the sergeant?","Two hundred and two, my favour. We've agreed not to settle it. If we settle it, there's no reason to keep playing."],
  ["Was it worth it? Keeping quiet?","(Mario looks at you a long time.) Friend. A man who keeps his word is a man who sleeps well. Even on this bunk."]],
 "Whatever happens tomorrow, come and visit. I'll be here. Probably."],
};
// Marjut, a palace maid for fourteen years, let go by Edmund the butler a week after the king died (The Unemployed). Threads: her
// unpaid wages and her grudge against Edmund (and the letter she writes about his wine accounts), palace gossip from Lotta and
// Elsa, who are still up there, looking for work (Sally's show houses, Rosaria's sink), and the old king's reading glasses she kept.
DAILY['Marjut']={
2:["(Marjut looks you over with a professional eye.) Your collar's turned under at the back. Fourteen years at the palace: I notice collars. Marjut.",[
  ["Thank you. You worked at the palace?","Fourteen years. Linen, bedrooms, silver. Then the king died, and a week later Edmund told me the household was 'economising'. On me."],
  ["Who's Edmund?","The butler. Grey, polite, smells of silver polish. He's never raised his voice in his life, and he's never needed to."],
  ["Miss it?","I miss the work. I don't miss being invisible. Up there, you're furniture that can carry trays."]],
 "Fix your collar before you go anywhere important."],
3:["(Marjut is watching the palace road.) The household's in a state. Black crêpe on every door, the chapel being scrubbed. And they're two maids short. Ha.",[
  ["Short because of you?","Because of me and Inga, who did the mending. Lotta's doing three people's work. She told me last night at the City Bar, in tears."],
  ["Who's still up there?","Ingrid, Lotta, Elsa in the laundry, Marta in the kitchen, Oskar on the doors, Viktor in the gardens. And Edmund, counting spoons."],
  ["Do you want to go back?","(She hesitates.) I want them to ask. I'd say no. But I want them to ask."]],
 "If you go up there, look at the corners. I bet nobody's dusted them."],
4:["(Marjut has a pocketful of job cuttings from the Gazette.) A maid's not much use outside a palace. Everyone in Kingsgrad does their own dusting.",[
  ["Any leads?","Sally at the estate agent's wants someone to clean the houses she shows. Rosaria at the pizzeria wants someone at the sink. Glamour."],
  ["You could work in a hotel.","In Frostholm? They're letting maids go there too. Raili, she's from the Hotel Aurora. Nobody's hiring hands this year."],
  ["What skills do you have?","I can fold a sheet so it squeaks, polish silver till you see your soul, and walk through a room without being noticed. Very useful, in a spy."]],
 "I'll try Sally tomorrow. Wish me luck."],
5:["(Marjut is turning a letter over and over.) From the palace. Not my wages. A letter saying my wages are 'being processed'. Edmund's handwriting. I'd know it anywhere.",[
  ["How much do they owe?","Six weeks, and my holiday. It's not a fortune. It's the principle. And my rent."],
  ["Why would Edmund hold them back?","Because he can. Because I once told him the wine cellar book didn't add up. He said 'thank you, Marjut'. He never forgets a thank you."],
  ["Can you complain?","To who? The prince? The prince has a dead father and a kingdom to fight for. My six weeks aren't on his list."]],
 "Eila says I should frame the letter. I'd rather burn it."],
6:["(Marjut is smoothing her coat.) Saw Sally. She'll pay me to clean her show houses on Thursdays. One house. It's not the palace, but at least it hasn't got Edmund in it.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you. It's one morning a week. But Sally pays on the day, in cash, and says 'thank you'. The palace never said thank you in fourteen years."],
  ["Is Sally nice?","Sally's nice to everybody who might buy a house. I'm not going to buy a house, so she's nice to me out of habit."],
  ["A palace maid cleaning empty houses.","Empty houses are lovely. Nobody rings a bell at you. Nobody leaves their teacup on the piano."]],
 "Thursdays I'm a working woman. Mind your manners."],
7:["(Marjut leans close.) Lotta says the Colonel came to dinner again last night. Colonel Halvorsen, from the barracks. That's three times since the king died.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","The old king had him twice a year. Christmas and the king's birthday. Now it's twice a week. Draw your own conclusions."],
  ["What do they talk about?","Lotta serves the soup and goes out. But she says the Colonel never touches his wine and Prince Leopold never stops filling it."],
  ["Gossip, surely.","Gossip's just news that hasn't been printed yet. You should know that, reporter."]],
 "You didn't hear it from me. Or from Lotta."],
8:["(Marjut has a pair of old gold-rimmed spectacles in a handkerchief.) Can I show you something? The old king's reading glasses. He left them in the linen room in July. I never gave them back.",[
  ["Why keep them?","I meant to. Then he died, and Edmund sacked me, and I thought: let him look for something, for once."],
  ["Are they valuable?","Gold rims. But that's not it. He used to read in the linen room when he wanted quiet. Nobody else knew. I'd fold sheets and he'd read."],
  ["You liked him.","He said 'good morning, Marjut' every morning for fourteen years. He knew my name. Edmund still calls me 'the girl'."]],
 "Don't tell anyone I've got them. Not yet."],
9:["(Marjut is rubbing her hands.) Sally had me in early this week, to clean her show house. Little place on the edge of town. Somebody's going to be happy there, if anybody buys it.",[
  ["Was there much to clean?","Dust and dead flies. I polished the door knocker till it shone like palace brass. Sally nearly cried."],
  ["Who'll buy it?","Sally says it's been for sale since spring. Nobody's got money but the people in Hope Springs, and they don't want a little house."],
  ["Nice to be working?","It's nice to be tired for a reason. I'd forgotten that."]],
 "If you know anybody wanting a house, it's a nice one. Clean knocker."],
10:["(Marjut has been crying, and is furious about it.) I went up to the gates to see him off. The king. They wouldn't let me in. The guards didn't even know my face.",[
  ["I'm sorry.","Fourteen years. I carried his tea up those stairs eight thousand times. And I had to stand at the gate with the tourists."],
  ["What did you see?","The carriage. The black horses. Edmund at the chapel door, very solemn, in a coat I pressed last Christmas."],
  ["Did you say goodbye?","Through the railings. I said 'good morning, sir'. It was the only thing I ever said to him. (She wipes her eyes.)"]],
 "Go on. I'll be all right in a minute. I always am."],
11:["(Marjut is sharp today.) Eila says I should let it go. The wages, Edmund. Let it go. Easy for her. She had a shop shut on her. I had a person do it.",[
  ["Is it different?","A shop doesn't look you in the eye and say 'economising'. A shop doesn't keep the good silver polish for itself."],
  ["Maybe she's right.","Maybe. Eila's right about most things. It's very annoying. (She sighs.) But not this one."],
  ["What would you do?","Nothing. I'd just like him to feel it. Once. What it's like when somebody else holds the book."]],
 "Don't tell Eila I said that. She worries."],
12:["(Marjut is amused.) Lotta says Princess Daphne has taken to sitting on the palace roof to smoke. In September. In a fur. Edmund pretends not to see.",[
  ["Is Daphne unhappy?","Daphne's bored. Bored is worse than unhappy, in a palace. Unhappy people cry; bored people do things."],
  ["How do you know so much?","Maids talk. We see the bedrooms, the bins and the bathwater. You learn more from a bin than from a newspaper."],
  ["Should you be telling me this?","Probably not. But nobody up there's paid me since August, so I'm off duty."]],
 "I'm only saying what the whole kitchen knows."],
13:["(Marjut is shaking with anger.) I went to the kitchen door for my wages. Edmund wouldn't see me. He sent Oskar out with a biscuit. On a plate.",[
  ["A biscuit?","A ginger biscuit. On the second-best plate. I threw it at the lamp post. Oskar looked so ashamed I nearly felt sorry for him."],
  ["What did Oskar say?","'Mr Edmund regrets.' Mr Edmund regrets nothing. Mr Edmund hasn't regretted anything since 1958."],
  ["What now?","(She folds her arms.) Now I think. I've got time to think. That's what he's given me."]],
 "Never take a biscuit from a butler."],
14:["(Marjut has a flower in her buttonhole, which looks suspiciously like palace stock.) The big day. Eila's got the other one. Don't ask where I picked them.",[
  ["The palace window boxes?","I said don't ask. (She grins.) Viktor won't miss two. Viktor grows them for Edmund to look at, and Edmund never looks."],
  ["What do you expect today?","Speeches. The family on a balcony, I suppose, if they show up. I want to see the household, how they look. Who's standing where."],
  ["Will Edmund be there?","Behind the family, carrying something. That's where butlers stand. I'll wave."]],
 "Come on. Eila wants to be near the front."],
15:["(Marjut has a strange look.) I saw Edmund at the gathering yesterday. He saw me. He looked away first. Fourteen years, and I've never seen him look away first.",[
  ["Maybe he feels guilty.","Butlers don't feel guilt. They feel draughts. But something's bothering him. I know that face."],
  ["What did you do?","I curtsied. Very low. The way you're meant to for a duke. He went red to the ears. (She smiles.)"],
  ["You enjoyed that.","I did. Is that wrong? It was the best thing that's happened to me since August."]],
 "Somebody's worried up there. I'd like to know why."],
16:["(Marjut has a writing pad and pencil.) I'm writing a letter. To Prince Leopold. About Edmund. And the wine cellar book.",[
  ["What about the wine cellar?","Twenty bottles a month that never went upstairs. I counted, for two years. I know where they went: the back door, on Tuesdays, to a man with a van."],
  ["Is that wise?","No. But it's true. And Leopold's going to want to know who's selling his father's claret before the whole island does."],
  ["Revenge?","Justice. Revenge would be telling the Gazette. (She looks at you.) Don't get ideas."]],
 "It's not sent yet. I'm choosing my words. Like a lady."],
17:["(Marjut is holding the king's glasses up to the light.) The ground shook this morning. I grabbed these first. Not my purse. These.",[
  ["That says something.","It says I'm a sentimental fool. Eila says so. She grabbed her coal tin."],
  ["Was it a big tremor?","Enough to set the palace bells going on their own. Lotta says Edmund dropped a whole tray of the Meissen. (She tries not to look pleased.)"],
  ["What will you do with them?","I don't know. Give them back, I suppose. To someone who'd understand. Not Edmund."]],
 "If it shakes again, hold on to what matters."],
18:["(Marjut looks worried.) Lotta says the palace has gone strange. Prince Augustus hasn't come out of the library for two days, and Leopold's pacing the corridors at night.",[
  ["What's wrong with them?","Brothers. The crown on one side and the Church on the other, Lotta says. Augustus reads, Leopold paces. They used to play cards together."],
  ["Do you care, still?","(She's quiet.) I changed their beds since they were boys. You don't stop caring because someone sacks you. That's the worst of it."],
  ["What's the mood below stairs?","Frightened. If the family breaks, the household goes with it. Lotta asked if Sally needs anyone else on Thursdays."]],
 "Families. Palaces are just families with more stairs."],
19:["(Marjut is clutching an envelope.) Sent it. The letter to the prince. Through Lotta, so it goes on his breakfast tray, not through Edmund.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Sick. Like I've thrown the good china off the roof. Can't take it back now."],
  ["What will happen?","Leopold will read it, or he won't. Edmund will find out, or he won't. Either way I've said it. In writing. Fourteen years of not saying anything."],
  ["Does Lotta mind?","She was thrilled. She's been terrified of Edmund since she was sixteen. She said she'd put it under the marmalade."]],
 "Not a word to Eila. Not yet."],
20:["(Marjut is twitchy, checking the palace road.) Nothing from the palace. Lotta says the prince read something at breakfast and went very quiet. She doesn't know if it was mine.",[
  ["Give it time.","Time's all I've got. I've polished my shoes four times this morning. Eila's threatened to hide the polish."],
  ["Any other palace news?","They're nervous about the silver and the paintings, Lotta says. Edmund's locking cabinets he's never locked before. Funny time to start."],
  ["Are you worried?","That he'll find out? He'll know. He knows every hand on the island. Let him. I'm not his any more."]],
 "If you see a palace footman coming down this road, tell me."],
21:["(Marjut is covered in ash, her eyes red.) The mountain's blown! I wanted to run up to the palace, to see if Lotta and Elsa were safe. Eila wouldn't let me.",[
  ["Eila was right.","She's always right. (A cough.) Viktor came down an hour ago on his bicycle. Everybody up there's fine. Shaken. Fine."],
  ["Are you all right?","Ash in my hair, ash in my shoes, ash in my tea. I'm alive. They're saying everyone's alive."],
  ["Where were you when it went?","Sweeping my own stairs. The window went grey all at once. I thought the sky had fallen in. I suppose it had."]],
 "Get indoors. Breathe through a cloth. I'm serious."],
22:["(Marjut has a broom over her shoulder.) Everybody's a maid today. Sweeping every step in Kingsgrad. I'm the only one doing it properly.",[
  ["Show them how it's done.","I have been. Grain of the cobbles, never against. Mrs Ek from the third floor's taking notes."],
  ["Any work from the clean-up?","Sally wants all her houses swept. Rosaria wants the pizzeria back room scrubbed, and pays double. I've never been so popular."],
  ["Any word from the palace?","Lotta says they've got every servant up ladders brushing ash off the roof. And still two maids short. (She sniffs.)"]],
 "Never sweep against the grain. Remember that."],
23:["(Marjut is frowning at her broom.) Lotta came down last night. Edmund's been called into the prince's study twice this week. Door shut. Voices raised. Edmund's voice.",[
  ["Was it your letter?","Lotta thinks so. I think so. Edmund's voice raised. I didn't know it went that loud."],
  ["Do you feel better?","I thought I would. I feel strange. Like when you finally find the stain you've been scrubbing for a week, and it's under the rug."],
  ["Will he come after you?","He's a butler, not a gangster. The worst he can do is look at me. He's been doing that for fourteen years."]],
 "Let's see what the week brings."],
24:["(Marjut is polishing the king's spectacles with her handkerchief.) The aftershocks keep rattling the windows. Every time, I check these are still in my pocket.",[
  ["Still haven't returned them?","I'm waiting for the right person. He used to read in the linen room with them on his nose. Not to Edmund. Not to a drawer."],
  ["Who's the right person?","Someone who sat with him and read. The prince with his nose in books, perhaps. Augustus. He used to come and find his father there."],
  ["How do you feel about the palace now?","Tired. Angry less. Sad more. That's grief, Eila says. She says I've been angry so I wouldn't have to be sad."]],
 "Eila's very wise. Don't tell her I said so."],
25:["(Marjut is laughing behind her hand.) Rosaria made me a pizza for scrubbing her back room. A whole one. Mushrooms. I've never had a whole pizza.",[
  ["How was it?","Greasy, hot, wonderful. Eila had two slices and pretended she didn't. We ate it on the bench like schoolgirls."],
  ["The back room?","Card tables, ashtrays, a safe I wasn't to look at. I didn't look at it. I can not look at anything. Fourteen years' practice."],
  ["Will Rosaria hire you?","Saturdays, she says. With Sally on Thursdays. Two days a week. I'm practically a career woman."]],
 "Mushrooms on pizza. Who'd have thought it."],
26:["(Marjut is staring at the palace road with her jaw set.) Still no wages. Lotta says Edmund's 'dealing with it'. Edmund's been dealing with it since August.",[
  ["Maybe it's on its way.","Maybe. Or maybe he's saving it for after the thirtieth, for whoever's in charge. Edmund always waits to see who's in charge."],
  ["You went up to the gate again?","Twice. I just stood there. Viktor waved from the roses. I'm making a point. I'm not sure what the point is."],
  ["What would satisfy you now?","My wages. And a 'thank you, Marjut'. Real, not polite. I know it's too much. I want it anyway."]],
 "Eila thinks I'm going to do something silly. I'm not. Probably."],
27:["(Marjut is sitting very upright.) I saw Edmund in town today. Coming out of the Civil Service Bureau. He looked older. He nodded to me. Nodded.",[
  ["A nod's something.","From Edmund, a nod's a speech. In fourteen years he nodded at me once, the day I didn't drop the punch bowl."],
  ["Did you nod back?","I curtsied again. A small one, this time. Not for a duke. For a butler. He understood."],
  ["Is the feud over?","Nothing's over. But it's quieter. Like the mountain. Smoking a bit, not blowing."]],
 "We'll see. Butlers have long memories."],
28:["(Marjut is nervous.) Everyone's doing something today. Lotta says the palace is upside down for a foreign visitor. And there's talk of flags and rallies all over town.",[
  ["Are you worried for them?","For Lotta and Elsa and Marta. They'll be run off their feet. Two maids short, on the biggest day of the year."],
  ["Would you go back today?","(She actually thinks about it.) If they asked. Just for today. To see the silver done right. But they won't ask."],
  ["What's the gossip?","That everybody's got a plan for today, and every plan goes through the palace. Lotta says she's locking her room."]],
 "Keep your head down today. And your collar straight."],
29:["(Marjut has an envelope in both hands.) It came this morning. My wages. Every penny, and my holiday. Oskar brought it on a plate. A good plate.",[
  ["That's wonderful.","It is. I bought Eila a cake. We ate it on the bench. There's a note in the envelope, too."],
  ["What does the note say?","'Thank you, Marjut. E.' Three words and a letter. (She holds it very carefully.) Fourteen years for three words. I'll take them."],
  ["Was it your letter to the prince?","Maybe. Maybe he just wanted his books straight before tomorrow. I don't care which. It's paid."]],
 "Tomorrow, the whole island finds out who it belongs to. I already know who I belong to: me."],
30:["(Marjut holds up the empty handkerchief.) I gave them back. The king's glasses. Sent them to Prince Augustus with Lotta, with a note saying where his father used to read.",[
  ["That's lovely.","Lotta says he held them a long time and didn't say anything. That's the right answer. That's what I wanted."],
  ["The last day of the month.","The day it all gets settled. Whatever happens up there, I've settled mine. Wages, Edmund, the glasses. All tidied."],
  ["What's next for you?","Sally on Thursdays, Rosaria on Saturdays. And this bench, with Eila, on the days between. It's not a palace. It'll do."]],
 "Good luck today, reporter. Whoever wins, keep your collar straight."],
};
// Marta, the palace cook, in the Palace Kitchen (Royal Family). Threads: the household's menus, from the funeral wake to the
// state banquet she won't tell Edmund about; Arvid, her old apprentice, now at the Cedar Grill in Eastbrook with her herring
// sauce; ruling the staff (Oskar's thieving, Lotta, Elsa's hands); feeding a family that won't eat; her late husband Henning.
DAILY['Marta']={
2:["(Marta is gutting herring with terrible speed.) If you're staying, peel something. If you're not, you're in the draught.",[
  ["I'll peel.","(She hands you a knife and a sack of potatoes without looking up.) Thin. If I can't see through the peel I'll know you're from the city."],
  ["How many do you feed?","The family, the staff, the colonel when he calls, the visitors who say they're not hungry and then are. Thirty, some days. Forty since the king died."],
  ["Herring again?","Herring always. The old king ate it every day for forty-one years. The new one does too. It's the only thing in this palace that hasn't changed."]],
 "Put the peelings in the pail for Viktor's compost. Not the bin. He checks."],
3:["Kelp Cola sent a crate for the prince. He doesn't drink it. So I do. One bottle a day, since 1951. My teeth are my own and I'll thank you not to ask how many.",[
  ["Since 1951?","The year I came, a girl of sixteen, to scrub pans. The old cook drank it, so I drank it. Now I'm the old cook. Somebody'll start drinking it because of me."],
  ["Isn't it bad for you?","Everything's bad for you. Herring's bad for the herring. You drink what you like and you work it off at the stove."],
  ["Is it any good?","It's sweet and it's cold and it's the same every day. In a palace, that's a virtue."]],
 "Close the door. I'm not heating the whole of Kingsgrad."],
4:["Eastbrook's open again, so that boy will be back at work. Arvid. He was my apprentice for nine years. Now he's got his own grill, and my herring sauce.",[
  ["Your herring sauce?","Dill, mustard, a splash of the king's aquavit and something I won't say. He took it with him in his head. I can't arrest a man's head."],
  ["Were you sorry to lose him?","Sorry? I was furious. I was also proud, and I'll thank you not to tell him that, ever."],
  ["Is the Cedar Grill good?","I've never been. I'll go the day he writes and asks me. He hasn't yet. He's as stubborn as I am. I taught him that too."]],
 "If you eat at the Cedar Grill, don't tell him I sent you. Tell him the sauce is too sharp."],
5:["(Marta is rolling pastry with some violence.) Prince Augustus has gone up the mountain. He'll come back thinner. He always comes back thinner.",[
  ["Do the monks not feed him?","Brother Teodor feeds him cabbage soup and holiness. It's not a meal. It's a punishment with steam."],
  ["What are you making?","A pie for when he gets back. Lemon. Very plain. He won't eat it today because of a saint, but he'll eat it tomorrow because of me."],
  ["Does Daphne eat?","She eats nothing and says it's delicious. She's been doing it since she was twelve. I put butter in everything so it's not for nothing."]],
 "Mind the pastry. It's going to be a pie, God willing."],
6:["Oskar's been at the buns again. I count them. Twenty-four on the tray, twenty-three by noon. Every day. He thinks I don't know.",[
  ["Will you tell him off?","No. I make twenty-five now. He gets one, I get the satisfaction, and he gets a fright every time I look at him."],
  ["He's young, he's hungry.","He's young, he's hungry and he stands out in the cold all day like a lamp post. If he wasn't stealing buns I'd be worried."],
  ["What kind of buns?","Cardamom. My mother's. Don't write it down. If I see it in a newspaper I'll know who to blame."]],
 "Take a bun. Go on. Then I'll know where the twenty-fourth went."],
7:["Prince Augustus is fasting for a saint I'd never heard of. So I sent up soup with a note that said 'Saints eat'. Oskar ate it.",[
  ["Did you scold Oskar?","I gave him another bowl. Pea and ham doesn't keep. And the boy looked like he'd stolen from the altar. Punishment enough."],
  ["Will Augustus ever eat?","When the saints let him. There's a great many saints this month. I've started keeping a calendar of them on the larder door."],
  ["Saints eat?","Saint Peter was a fisherman. You think he didn't have a herring now and then? I'll argue it with the abbot if I have to."]],
 "If you see the prince's brother, tell him there's pie. He'll say no. Tell him anyway."],
8:["(Marta has a list as long as her arm.) The wake. After the funeral. Three hundred people, perhaps more, and every one of them hungry and pretending not to be.",[
  ["What will you make?","Herring three ways, cold beef, a salmon the size of Oskar, rye bread, cardamom buns, and a cake the king liked. Black cherry. He had it every birthday."],
  ["Who's helping?","Lotta, Ingrid when Edmund lets her go, and Elsa, who's better with dough than you'd think. Laundresses have strong arms."],
  ["Can I taste anything?","You can taste the end of this spoon, if you stand there much longer."]],
 "Out of the way, then. I've three hundred people to feed and two days to do it."],
9:["(Marta is up to her elbows in dough.) Tomorrow they bury him. Today I'm making his cake. Black cherry, with kirsch. I've made it forty times, and I've never once cried into it until now.",[
  ["I'm sorry, Marta.","Don't be. A tear in the batter does no harm. Salt. It's good for it."],
  ["What was he like to cook for?","Fussy about herring, careless about everything else. He'd eat a pie standing up in the kitchen and leave me a coin under the plate. Thirty years of coins. I've kept them all."],
  ["Did he ever come down here?","Every birthday. He'd come down for a slice of this, and sit at that table there, and we'd say nothing at all. It was the best conversation in the palace."]],
 "Go on with you. I've a cake to cry into."],
10:["The wake's laid out in the Grand Hall. Three herrings, a beef, a salmon, a cake. If anyone says they're not hungry I'll put them in the pantry.",[
  ["How are you?","Busy. Busy's the only way through. I'll be sad on Thursday, when there's nothing to do."],
  ["Will you go to the service?","I'll stand at the back of the chapel in my apron, and leave before the last hymn to warm the soup. He'd understand. He liked his soup hot."],
  ["Who's going to eat it all?","Gustavo. That man eats like a wolf at a wedding. And the cola man takes rolls home in his pockets. I see it. I always see it."]],
 "Don't touch the cake till after the service. It's his."],
11:["Every crumb of it gone. Even the salmon's head. Somebody took the salmon's head. I want to know who.",[
  ["Who would take a fish head?","Pat, the fellow from the beach, I'd have said, but he wasn't invited. The oil man, perhaps. Men like that take things for the sake of it."],
  ["Was it a good wake?","People ate and talked about him. That's all a wake is. He'd have liked the beef. He'd have complained about the herring."],
  ["Are you resting now?","Resting? I've tomorrow's lunch to do. And the family won't eat. They never do after a funeral. I'll make soup till they do."]],
 "If you see someone walking about with a salmon's head, tell me."],
12:["Oskar's been telling everyone about a grey van that came round the back in the night. That was the fish man from Saltwick, wanting paying at midnight. I shouted at him.",[
  ["Why at midnight?","Because the roads have only just opened, and they're behind on everything, and so are their manners."],
  ["Oskar thought it was mysterious.","Oskar thinks a cat on the wall is mysterious. He's a good boy, but he reads too many comics in the quarters."],
  ["Do you get all your fish from Saltwick?","Herring from Tom's boat, salmon from wherever I can get it. Arvid gets his from the same boats. He outbids me. I know he does."]],
 "Don't tell Oskar it was the fish man. It'll spoil his week."],
13:["(Marta is scowling at a bottle of Kelp Cola.) They're giving these away in Kingsgrad. Free. Oskar brought four. I've confiscated three. For his teeth.",[
  ["And you'll drink them?","Of course. My teeth are older. They've less to lose."],
  ["What do you think of Kelp Cola?","I think it's the only thing on the island that never changes. That makes it either very good or very bad. I haven't decided in thirty years."],
  ["Has anyone tried to make their own?","People try. It's never as good. There's something in it Phil's family won't tell anyone. I've tried to guess. I think it's kelp."]],
 "Take one. I've three. Don't tell Oskar."],
14:["The family's out among the whole island today. I'm in here with the stock pot. When they come back, they'll want feeding, whatever happens out there.",[
  ["Don't you want to see it?","I've seen crowds. They all smell of wet wool and want toilets. I'd rather have the stock pot."],
  ["What are you making for after?","Beef broth with dumplings. Hearty, and you can eat it when you're angry. I expect some of them will be angry."],
  ["Who do you want to win?","Win what? I want the prince crowned and the kitchen left alone. The rest is weather."]],
 "Come back hungry. Everyone always does after a day like this."],
15:["I've made lemon cake for Prince Augustus. Plain. He's in the library deciding something, and a man can't decide on an empty stomach.",[
  ["Will he eat it?","He's had one slice. Ingrid says he ate it reading two letters at once. That's progress for him."],
  ["What's he deciding?","Whether he's for God or for us. As if a body has to choose. I've been for both since I was a girl, and nobody's ever minded."],
  ["Do you want him to stay?","(She puts down her spoon.) I've fed that boy since he could hold one. I want him where he's happy. I'd just like it to be within reach of my kitchen."]],
 "If you're going into the library, take him a cup of tea. Tell him it's from me."],
16:["Lotta's off to hear the debate on her afternoon. The Communists and the Democrats. She asked if I minded. I said I mind that she'll come back with ideas.",[
  ["What ideas?","Ideas that the palace kitchen should be run by a committee. I've seen committees. They can't boil an egg between them."],
  ["Do you have a politics?","I'm a royalist. I've been a royalist since 1951. My politics are the family and the stock pot, and the stock pot's never once voted."],
  ["Oskar wants to go too.","Oskar wants to go anywhere Lotta goes. Edmund's put him on the door. I've told him to stop moping or I'll have him scrubbing turnips."]],
 "Close the door. The draught's got opinions too."],
17:["(The pans on the rack swing and clink.) There. Did you see? Everything moved. The stock pot slopped onto the stove. Twelve hours of broth on the floor.",[
  ["Was it an earthquake?","It was something. Viktor says there's steam over the mountain in the north. He says the hens stopped laying yesterday. Hens know."],
  ["Is anything broken?","Two plates, a jar of pickled beetroot and my patience. The beetroot's on the ceiling. Don't look up."],
  ["Can I help?","Mop. There. In the corner. Thin strokes. I'm sure you can mop, even if you can't peel."]],
 "If it goes again, get away from the stove. I'll stay. Someone's got to watch the soup."],
18:["My husband Henning would have known what to do about this mountain. He was coachman here. Forty years in the stables. He said the horses always knew before the ground did.",[
  ["When did he die?","Seven winters ago. He went in his sleep in the quarters, in the bed by the stove. I still sleep on the other side."],
  ["What was he like?","Big, slow and kind, and he never once came into my kitchen without asking. A rare man. I teased him every day for thirty years."],
  ["Did the horses know?","Every time. A thunderstorm, a fire in the stables, the old queen dying. They'd stamp the night before. The horses are gone now. We've only got Viktor."]],
 "Go on. I've stock to start again."],
19:["The prince asked me for a packed supper tonight. A packed supper! In a napkin, he said, like a farmhand. He's never asked for a packed supper in his life.",[
  ["Where's he going?","He didn't say. He said not to tell Edmund. So I didn't tell Edmund. I've told you instead, which doesn't count."],
  ["What did you pack?","Herring on rye, a slice of lemon cake and a bottle of the king's aquavit. He looked at the aquavit and put it back. Then took it again."],
  ["Is he all right?","He's thirty-four and he's never been anywhere on his own. A packed supper is the first sensible thing he's done all month."]],
 "If you see a prince with a napkin in his pocket, you didn't."],
20:["Somebody was in my larder last night. Nothing taken. Everything moved an inch. I know my larder. I could cook in it blind.",[
  ["Who'd go in your larder?","Oskar, but he'd take buns. Ingrid, but she'd say sorry. This was someone careful. Careful frightens me more than hungry."],
  ["Did you tell Edmund?","Edmund went round with his keys twice. He said every door was locked. Then he stood in my larder a while saying nothing. That's how I knew he was worried."],
  ["Maybe it was the tremor.","A tremor knocks things over. It doesn't line them up again, wrong."]],
 "Keep your hands where I can see them in my kitchen. I mean it more than usual today."],
21:["(Marta is grey with ash, ladling soup into mugs at the servants' door.) The mountain's gone up in the north. Anyone who comes to my door gets soup. That's the rule today.",[
  ["Who's been coming?","Every servant, the postman, two soldiers, a woman from Kingsgrad with a baby. I've put the baby in the bread basket by the stove. It's the warmest place."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","Nobody hurt, Oskar says. He ran to town and back. I gave him two mugs and a bun. He didn't even have to steal it."],
  ["Can I help?","Hand out mugs. Smile. And don't let anyone take the ladle. It was my mother's."]],
 "Take some soup with you. Pour it into anyone who looks cold."],
22:["Ash in the flour. Ash in the sugar. Ash in the butter, if you leave it a minute. I've covered everything in cloths. My kitchen looks like a ward.",[
  ["Can you still cook?","I cooked through the winter of '62 with the snow up to the windows. I'll cook through this. It'll just be grey."],
  ["How's the family eating?","Prince Augustus is eating, thank God. Daphne's eating nothing, as usual. The prince ate standing up in here at midnight, like his father."],
  ["What does ash taste like?","Like a bonfire that's got opinions. Don't eat any. I'll know, and I'll make you sweep."]],
 "Shake your coat at the door. Not in here."],
23:["The prince has ordered soup for the north. Forty litres. For people who've lost their sheds and their fences to the ash. He called it 'Marta's soup'. In front of Edmund.",[
  ["That's kind of him.","I called him a sentimental boy. Then I made forty litres. Then fifty, in case. His father never once named a soup after me."],
  ["What soup is it?","Pea and ham, with barley. Thick enough to stand a spoon in. You need that when you've got ash in your hair."],
  ["Will you go north with it?","Somebody's got to make sure it's served hot. Viktor's driving the van. I'm sitting next to the pot with a blanket over it like a baby."]],
 "Out of the way. I've soup to load and a van to shout at."],
24:["(Marta has a menu card covered with a dishcloth.) The state banquet. Four days. I've decided the menu, and I'm not telling Edmund, because he'll tell me it's wrong.",[
  ["Tell me, then.","(She lifts the cloth an inch and drops it.) There's herring. There's always herring. Beyond that, you'll read it in your paper, after I've cooked it."],
  ["Is Edmund cross?","Edmund's been cross since 1966. I've just given him a reason this week."],
  ["Who's the guest?","A king from across the water, old, very rich and very fussy, Edmund says. I've cooked for fussy kings before. They all end up asking for seconds."]],
 "Don't lift the cloth on your way out. I'll know."],
25:["I had a letter. From Arvid, at the Cedar Grill. He wants to know if I'll come and eat at his restaurant one evening. After twelve years. He wrote 'please'.",[
  ["Will you go?","(She looks at the letter a long time.) He's spelled 'please' wrong. He always did. I taught him to cook, not to write."],
  ["He must miss you.","He misses my herring sauce. He's been trying to get it right for twelve years. He's close, I hear. He's not that close."],
  ["You should go.","I'll think about it. After the banquet. When I've nothing to do and nobody to shout at."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the letter. Especially not Lotta. She'll make a romance out of a herring."],
26:["(Marta is plucking a goose with brutal efficiency.) Goose for the banquet. I wanted three. I got two from the farm. The ash got into the third one's feelings, apparently.",[
  ["Its feelings?","Hilda at East Farm says it won't eat since the eruption. She's keeping it as a pet now. A pet goose. On a farm."],
  ["Two's enough, surely.","For a king? A king eats like a regiment and takes the leftovers home in his valet."],
  ["Kelp Cola were here today.","I know. They came into my kitchen and asked what the prince eats. I said 'people from Kelp Cola'. They left."]],
 "Mind the feathers. They go up your nose and stay till Christmas."],
27:["Tomorrow. Everything's made that can be made. Everything else I'll make at four in the morning, with Lotta yawning on one side and Elsa on the other.",[
  ["Will it be good?","It'll be the best meal this palace has served since the coronation. Then everyone will forget it by Tuesday. That's cooking."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I came here at sixteen. I've fed one king for thirty-three years and I'll feed another tomorrow. My knees are nervous. The rest of me is busy."],
  ["What's the pudding?","Black cherry cake, with kirsch. His cake. The old king's. It seemed right to make it for a king, one more time."]],
 "Go home. Sleep. Some of us won't."],
28:["(Marta doesn't look up from four pans at once.) A king in the dining room and the whole island going mad outside. Neither of them is coming into this kitchen. Not today.",[
  ["Can I do anything?","Peel. Thin. Like I taught you."],
  ["How's the banquet going?","Ask me when the plates come back. If they're clean, it went well. If they're not, I'll know who to blame, and it won't be me."],
  ["What's happening outside?","Flags, shouting, rallies, all of it, Oskar says. I've no time for any of it. There's herring to plate."]],
 "Out. Unless you're carrying onions."],
29:["(Marta is sitting down, for once, with a bottle of Kelp Cola.) The plates came back clean, every one. And today the island's deciding who comes after him. I've made a stock. Whoever it is, they'll want stock.",[
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a royalist cook in a palace on a day like this. Of course I'm worried. I'm also sitting down, which hasn't happened since August."],
  ["What if there's no king?","Then the palace will be a museum, and museums need cafés, and cafés need cooks. I'll cook whatever happens. But I'll mind."],
  ["Will you visit Arvid?","Next week. I've written back. I said yes, and that his sauce is too sharp. He'll know what that means."]],
 "Sit, if you want. There's a second chair. Henning's. Nobody's sat in it in seven years. Go on."],
30:["The last day of September. The bread's in, the soup's on and the island's deciding everything. I've told the kitchen to carry on as normal. That's the only way to carry on.",[
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Cook breakfast. Whatever happens today, people want breakfast tomorrow. Kings, commissars, democrats. They all want eggs."],
  ["Thank you for the buns, Marta.","(She snorts.) You took four. I count. I counted. Come back when you're hungry and I'll count again."],
  ["How will you feel, whatever happens?","Tired. Then hungry. Then I'll get up and cook. That's how you get through everything. Ask anyone who's lived through a winter here."]],
 "Close the door behind you. Whatever's going on out there, it's warm in here."],
};
// Mary, owner of the book shop in Kingsgrad (no faction). Threads: reading everything she sells, her fear that books will be banned, her cat Orwell, her neighbours Frank and Grace, the tourists' island maps, the cold and the ash.
DAILY['Mary']={
2:["(Mary looks up from a paperback and keeps her finger in the page.) The road's open, and the first new face is a reporter. Come in. Mind the stack by the door: it's Tolstoy and it's heavy.",[
  ["Nice shop. Do you read it all?","Everything I sell. Otherwise how would I know what to recommend? I'm on page two hundred of something about whaling. Don't ask."],
  ["What's the mood in Kingsgrad since the king died?","Hushed. People buy black ribbon and sensible novels. Nobody wants to be caught reading anything frivolous this week."],
  ["Got anything on the island's history?","One shelf, and it's mostly the king's own memoirs. Very dull. He rather admired himself."]],
 "Close the door behind you. The cold gets into the paper."],
3:["(Mary is dusting the island maps with a feather duster.) Tourists buy these. Well, tourists would buy these if there were any. I keep them dusted for hope.",[
  ["Do many people buy maps?","Mostly people who are lost on purpose. Sailors, spies, men avoiding their wives. Nothing like a map for looking busy."],
  ["Who comes in here the most?","Frank from next door. He pretends he's after a manual and goes through the crime shelf instead. He's read all of it twice."],
  ["Any spies, then?","(She raises an eyebrow over her glasses.) I only sell books, dear. Though a lot of people ask for atlases lately."]],
 "Mind Orwell on the step. He's asleep, and he bites."],
4:["Orwell has taken to sleeping on the poetry. I've decided he has taste. (She scratches a grey cat under the chin.) How are you finding Kingsgrad?",[
  ["Cold. And the cat's fat.","He eats like a dockworker. Lisa at the supermarket saves him the fish ends. I pretend not to know."],
  ["Is Orwell a name that means something?","It's a very good writer, who understood what happens to books when the wrong people get hold of a country. I named him as a warning."],
  ["Does the Democratic Party read much?","Erik's lot read pamphlets, which isn't the same. But they do pay. That's more than the Communists, who borrow."]],
 "Don't let the cat out. It's too cold for his dignity."],
5:["(Mary is taking a book off the shelf, looking at it, and putting it back, three times.) I'm having trouble deciding what to put in the window. The last window cost me a week of sleep.",[
  ["Why? What was wrong with it?",["A man looked at the display and said it was 'a lot of unsupervised thinking'. He wasn't joking. I took half of it down.",[
    ["Who said that?","I won't say. He wears a good coat and has an interest in what the island reads. That's all I'll say, and more than I should."],
    ["Do you worry about being told what to sell?","Every night. If one party gets the island, the first thing to go is the shelf at the back. I keep it dusted anyway."]]]],
  ["What would you put in the window?","Something with a plain cover and an awkward idea. Those are the ones that last."],
  ["Is it a slow week?","The week of a funeral coming and a road reopening is never slow. It's just not for books."]],
 "The back shelf is for people who ask. Nobody's asked yet."],
6:["The Eastbrook Gazette says there'll be speeches from all seven parties before the month is out. (She reads it with an expression of deep suffering.) I'm going to need a great deal more tea.",[
  ["Will you go to the speeches?","I'll go to one, sit at the back with a notebook and read a novel underneath it. That's how I keep my sanity."],
  ["Is the Gazette any good?","Ville's headlines are better than the paper's. It's a long way from Dickens, but it has a crossword."],
  ["Who's your favourite faction?","None. I like the one that lets me keep my shelves. Ask me again on the thirtieth."]],
 "If you need a book on the way to Eastbrook, I've a thin one."],
7:["(Mary is pencilling a new price on a stack of cookery books.) With every road open there's a rush on cookery. Everyone has people coming round and nothing in the cupboard.",[
  ["Anything for a visitor to read?","A thin book of island ghost stories. Written by a monk, who regretted it. It's very good."],
  ["Do you ever get bored of books?","Never. Sometimes of people, but they have the decency to buy something and go."],
  ["Is Grace next door busy?","She's measuring half the island for black. I lend her my tape measure, and she lends me her scissors, and neither of us returns them."]],
 "Come back when you've finished the ghost stories. Or when you're frightened."],
8:["(Mary has the radio set low on a sermon from the monastery.) I like a good sermon, but I like it better when it has a plot. Father Thyme's been struggling with this one all week.",[
  ["What's wrong with Father Thyme's sermon?","Words won't come, I hear. I'd lend him a good thesaurus, but I'd have to carry it up that mountain."],
  ["Are you religious?","I'm devout about proofreading. Everything else I'll take under advisement."],
  ["What does the Church read?","Lives of saints, mostly. And, I suspect, the occasional detective story under the cassock."]],
 "Tell Father Thyme to try a shorter sentence. It works for me."],
9:["(Mary sets down a glass bottle of cola, flat and untouched.) Someone's giving them out as samples. I'm not touching it. It's a free drink from a company that wants something.",[
  ["Which company?","Kelp Cola, mostly. And a tin of something from the Communists, said to be 'the people's'. It tastes like cold tea with a slogan."],
  ["Isn't free cola a nice thing?","Nothing's free. They'll want a window poster, or a review in the paper. I'm not for sale at fizzy prices."],
  ["Do you read the ingredients?","I read everything. Sugar, water, and a lot of adjectives."]],
 "If anyone offers me a poster, I'll tell them what to do with it. Politely."],
10:["(Mary is in a black cardigan.) I closed the shop this morning for an hour, so I could listen to the funeral on the wireless. The king and I never met, but we shared a love of Tolstoy.",[
  ["Did he really read?","He had a library in the palace the size of a small church. I sold him half of it. Mostly he bought the covers."],
  ["Did the funeral move you?","The music did. Nobody tells you that even a king gets a plain wooden box in the end. It rather suits him."],
  ["What happens now to the island?","Everyone wants to write the next chapter. I just hope it isn't a short one."]],
 "I'll open again at noon. The dead don't buy books, but the living do."],
11:["There's a notice at the mill about pay, and the mill hands have been in here, which never happens. I sold three copies of a book about trade unions.",[
  ["Are the mill hands reading about unions?","They're reading about their rights, which is more than I've ever managed. I'll admit I charged full price."],
  ["Is that an Eastbrook thing?","It's a Democratic thing. Helen from the Communists is pushing the other way. I'd sell both of them the same book."],
  ["Is reading a political act?","It is when someone is trying to stop you. Otherwise it's a hobby."]],
 "The shelf on labour is nearly empty. Quite exciting."],
12:["(Mary is flicking through a catalogue.) A woman from Eastbrook asked me for a record today. I told her it was a book shop. She seemed to think we were related.",[
  ["Was it Ruby you meant?","Ruby, yes. A lovely woman with dreadful taste in noise. We're colleagues in a way: both of us sell things people have to listen to."],
  ["What does she collect?","Rare records. I collect first editions. We have lunch twice a year and neither of us understands the other."],
  ["Is there a quiet part of the day?","Between three and four, when everyone thinks the shop is closed. Then the best people come in."]],
 "I'll be here. I'm always here. It's rather the point."],
13:["There are free samples going round the island, and I've had a leaflet pushed under my door about a 'Kelp Cola Free Samples Day'. Does anyone read their own leaflets?",[
  ["Did you read it?","Twice, for the grammar. It's got three exclamation marks in one sentence. I counted them with a pencil."],
  ["Are you tempted to try the cola?","I've tried it. It tastes of a sweetshop that has lost its sense of proportion."],
  ["How's the shop for sales?","Quiet. People are saving their money for tomorrow. Everyone's saving something for the fourteenth."]],
 "If anyone knows what the fourteenth is going to be, I'd love to be told."],
14:["(Mary has a paperback propped on the till and isn't reading it.) Everyone's gone quiet. Even Frank hasn't been in, and he's usually here by ten. It's the big day, isn't it?",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","Noise, speeches, and somebody shouting. I expect to be home by six with a cup of tea."],
  ["Does the whole island come together?","So I'm told. It happens maybe once in a generation, and usually when somebody has died."],
  ["Are you worried?","A little. When people gather in large numbers, they stop reading. That's what worries me."]],
 "Go on, you'll miss it. Tell me what you see."],
15:["(Mary shakes her head.) The Church and the Royal Family are hurling sermons at each other. It's Crown before Cross, or Cross before Crown. I could sell a book on each side and make a fortune.",[
  ["Which side are you on?","Mine. It has a till and a cat."],
  ["Do people really care about the order?","The order of things is all some people have. Tell a man his world's the wrong way round and he'll fight you for the upside-down."],
  ["Is there a book for this?","Plenty. Every one argues with the last. You could spend a lifetime in my shop and never agree with anyone."]],
 "Take something thin to read. It helps."],
16:["The Great Debate's tonight, I'm told, the Communists and the Democrats. (She sighs.) I'd like a debate where somebody actually answers the question.",[
  ["Who'll win?","The one with the best jokes. It's never the one with the best argument."],
  ["Will you listen?","On the wireless, with a crossword. I'll fill it in during the long answers."],
  ["Do you like Erik's speeches?","He's earnest. That's worth a great deal and sells very few tickets."]],
 "I'll leave the shop light on. The debaters might need a dictionary."],
17:["(The shelf by the window gives a small, definite shudder. Mary puts a hand on it.) Did you feel that? The ground's been doing that. Orwell hates it.",[
  ["Is it an earthquake?","The old men say it's the mountain. I say it's the book on the second shelf finally giving up."],
  ["Where's the cat?","Under the counter, plotting. He's decided the floor has turned on him personally."],
  ["Is the island going to be all right?","The island's been here a long time. I'd back it over most governments."]],
 "If the shelves fall, I'll know whom to blame. The mountain."],
18:["(Mary has taped a strip of paper across the window.) I'm told it helps if the glass goes. I read that in a book, so it must be true.",[
  ["Is the shaking getting worse?","A little, each day. The poetry has started to fall over on its own. I think that's a literary judgement."],
  ["What are people buying?","Candles, which I don't sell, and survival manuals, which I do. Surprisingly good business."],
  ["Do you feel safe in Kingsgrad?","Safe enough. It's far from the mountain. Ask me again if the ground opens."]],
 "I've moved the heavy books to the bottom shelf. Very sensible."],
19:["(Mary is humming.) There's a rumour that someone's hiring clerks at the Party office, 'Comrades Wanted' on a poster. I find that rather alarming. When has a party ever wanted a comrade who reads?",[
  ["Are you thinking of applying?","Heaven forbid. I'd correct their grammar and be shot before lunch."],
  ["Do you mind the Communists?","I don't mind anyone who pays. I mind anyone who wants to tell me what to stock."],
  ["Is Kingsgrad changing?","In the way a river does. You don't notice until your feet are wet."]],
 "The poster's on the lamppost outside. Third spelling mistake from the top."],
20:["(Mary peers at her till and then at the window.) A man in a very good hat stood across the street for an hour. He didn't come in. I find that more disturbing than the earthquakes.",[
  ["Who was he?","I don't know. That's the whole problem. In a shop you get to know everybody, and the ones you don't know are the ones to watch."],
  ["Was he watching you?","Or Frank. Or the Palace. It's a very watchable street."],
  ["Maybe he was just reading the window.","If so, he's the first customer in a month who read the window and not the signs. I'll forgive him."]],
 "If he comes back, I'll sell him a mystery. At full price."],
21:["(The shop is full of grey dust, and Mary is standing in the middle of it with a duster that has given up.) It erupted. The volcano. There's ash on every shelf. I've never seen anything so thorough.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Quite, thank you. Frightened, then furious, then very tidy. I've started on the Dickens."],
  ["Are the books ruined?","The ones by the window, I'm afraid. The rest I'll rescue with a cloth and an apology."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, I'm told, which I take as a minor miracle. I'll count it in the shop's favour."]],
 "Don't come in with those boots. The ash gets everywhere."],
22:["I've been dusting for a day and a half. (Mary hands you a feather duster, hopefully.) Orwell is grey. He seems to approve.",[
  ["Can I help?","You can stand there looking sympathetic. That's already half of the job."],
  ["How's the cat?","Grey. Offended. Making small remarks about the ash on his paws."],
  ["Is business good?","Hardly. But there's a run on anything about volcanoes. I've one book on Vesuvius and it's gone."]],
 "There's a cup of tea if you can bear the grit."],
23:["Frank came in today and bought a book on cooking. A gunsmith with a cookbook. I could write a short story about that.",[
  ["What's Frank cooking?","Soup, he claims, for the neighbours with no stove. He's been rather kind. I'd never have guessed."],
  ["Has the ash changed the neighbourhood?","It's softened it. People are doing each other's windows. For a week, nobody's a faction."],
  ["Is Grace busy?","She's sewing masks for the street. Cotton and string. Very Grace."]],
 "Tell Frank his tomatoes are dreadful. He'll grin."],
24:["(Mary has a hand on the small of her back.) I've been sweeping all morning, and I've decided that a book shop is the world's worst place for ash. Every page has a fingerprint.",[
  ["Is there any good news?","Orwell has stopped sulking. And I found a first edition under the counter I'd forgotten I'd put away."],
  ["What do people read now?","Light things. Nobody wants a tragedy right now. I've sold forty detective novels."],
  ["Is it still shaking?","Every few hours. Like a sleeping giant turning over. I keep expecting an apology."]],
 "If you find a good rag, bring it. Mine are all grey."],
25:["There are rumours about a crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. (Mary reads the headline twice.) It sounds like the sort of thing I'd stock in the children's section.",[
  ["Is it a good plan?","Anything that gives people a chance to vote and wear a hat is likely to be popular."],
  ["Who's the princess?","I'd rather not speculate. I've a rule against gossip before noon."],
  ["Do you want a king?","I want a sensible reader on the throne. I'll take either."]],
 "I'll keep the window clean, whatever happens. Someone has to."],
26:["The cola people are saying 'a prince in every bottle', which is the strangest slogan I've heard, and I once sold a book on mushrooms.",[
  ["What does it mean?","I think they've put a picture of Prince Leopold on the label. I haven't had the heart to look."],
  ["Will you stock it?","Over my dead body. And over the bodies of several readers."],
  ["Do you think it'll sell?","Everything sells if you put a prince on it. It's a very old principle."]],
 "I'll read the label tomorrow. When I'm braver."],
27:["(Mary has a ticket in her hand.) They're showing a film tonight, 'Revolution at the Pictures', is that the title? I haven't been to the cinema in six years. I hate that it's tempting.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm considering it. It could be dreadful, but then so is most of what I read."],
  ["Who's behind it?","The Party. It'll be in black and white and every face will be in profile."],
  ["What do you read, before bed?","Detective novels. Poison and mud. I find it settling."]],
 "If I go, I'll take a book for the dull bits."],
28:["(Mary is closing the shutters halfway.) There are flags up all down the street, and a state visit, and a cola going abroad. I thought I'd let the street have the day to itself.",[
  ["Is it too loud for a book shop?","Every faction is making its biggest push today. Nobody's in the mood for the quiet shelf."],
  ["Are you afraid?","A little. It's the shouting that's frightening. Not the volcano. That was just honest."],
  ["What would you tell the island?","Read the small print. It's all there. Nobody does."]],
 "I'll be inside with the cat. Come by if you need a quiet five minutes."],
29:["Tomorrow it's all decided. (Mary has been standing at the same shelf for ten minutes, her hands in her cardigan pockets.) I've never been so aware of my own stock.",[
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Open the shop. Whatever happens, they'll want something to read. They always do."],
  ["Are you afraid of a ban?","Every day. That's why I sell the things people might one day want to burn."],
  ["Is there anything you'd like to tell the new government?","Leave the back shelf alone. That's all. It isn't much to ask."]],
 "I've put the kettle on. It's all I can do tonight."],
30:["(Mary is standing in the doorway with a duster, looking out at the street.) It's the last day, and I don't know what I'm hoping for. Only that there's still a shop on Monday.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like the last page of a long book. You want to know the end and you don't want it to stop."],
  ["Any advice for a reporter?","Write it down properly. Some of us will want to remember how it was."],
  ["Has it been a good month?","It's been the most interesting of my life, and I include the year of the great mushroom pamphlet."]],
 "Take a book. On the house. Go on, before I change my mind."],
};
// Mats, office clerk for Diversified Petroleum in the Petro Tower in Frostholm, lives in the Kingsgrad Apartments with Elin, Phil's
// secretary at Kelp Cola (Diversified Petroleum). Threads: Elin, and their rule never to talk about work, broken every evening; his
// dream of Wall Street and the financial pages; his filing, in very neat capitals; the fear that the two companies will find out about them.
DAILY['Mats']={
2:["(Mats opens his flat door in a cardigan, with the financial pages folded under his arm.) Oh. Hello. Sorry, I thought you were the milk. The road to Frostholm's still snowed in, so I'm working from the sofa. Which means not working.",[
  ["What do you do, when you're working?","I'm a clerk. I file things. Reports, invoices, letters. People think it's dull. It is dull, but it's tidy, and I like tidy."],
  ["Is that yesterday's Financial Times?","Last week's. The boat brings it in a bundle when it brings it at all. The Dow Jones is up, and I'm sitting in Kingsgrad in my socks."],
  ["Who's the milk for?","Me and Elin. My partner. She takes it in her coffee and I take it in my tea, and we've never once agreed which is right."]],
 "If you see the milkman, tell him flat 3B is desperate."],
3:["(Mats is sitting on the stairs of the Kingsgrad Apartments with a ledger on his knees.) Elin's gone in to work today. Kelp Cola's open again. I'm going through my own household accounts, twice, for something to do.",[
  ["Elin works at Kelp Cola?","She's Phil's secretary. (He lowers his voice.) And I'm with Diversified Petroleum. So we have an agreement: we never talk about work at home."],
  ["How are your household accounts?","Balanced to the cent. Elin spent four dollars on a scarf she says was a bargain. I've written it down as 'scarf, disputed'."],
  ["Does the agreement work?","It works until about seven o'clock every evening. Then one of us says 'You'll never guess what happened', and it's all over."]],
 "Don't mention the agreement to anyone, will you? It's a small island."],
4:["(Mats is reading a library book with a picture of a skyscraper on the cover.) 'The Men of Wall Street.' Mary at the bookshop ordered it in for me. It took four months. It's wonderful.",[
  ["Why Wall Street?","Because everything there moves so fast, and it all goes into the right column in the end. And the suits. Have you seen the suits?"],
  ["Have you ever been to New York?","Never. I've been to Helsinki, twice, and once to Stockholm for a course in double-entry bookkeeping. That was the best week of my life."],
  ["Isn't that a bit dry?","Dry? (He looks hurt.) A balance sheet is the most honest thing in the world. It can't lie, it can only be lied to."]],
 "Chapter nine is about the Crash. I'm saving it for a rainy day."],
5:["(Mats is helping Bertil from the floor below carry a box of files up the stairs.) Bertil brings his work home too. We're the two tidiest men in the Kingsgrad Apartments, and possibly the island.",[
  ["What's in the box?","Forms. Bertil says the king's death has made a great many forms necessary. He seemed almost happy about it."],
  ["Do you and Bertil get on?","He plays chess by post with a man in Helsinki, and I help him address the envelopes. It's a quiet friendship. Those are the best ones."],
  ["Does Bertil know about you and Elin?","Bertil knows everything about everybody in this building, and he's never said a word. That's what a civil servant is."]],
 "Mind the third stair. It squeaks, and Doctor Lind on the ground floor complains."],
6:["(Mats is on the telephone in the hallway, and hangs up.) That was the office. Frostholm opens tomorrow. Back to the Petro Tower. Seven days of mail on my desk, unfiled. I can feel it.",[
  ["You sound excited.","I am. Is that sad? I think it might be sad. But the filing won't do itself, and nobody else does it properly."],
  ["What did Elin say about it?","She said 'Good, you can stop alphabetising the spice rack.' I had it by country of origin. She's moved the paprika."],
  ["How do you get to Frostholm?","The tram, up the coast. Forty minutes each way with the financial pages. It's the best part of my day, apart from the evenings."]],
 "Back to work tomorrow. I'll iron a shirt. Two, in case."],
7:["(Mats is at his desk in the Petro Tower, in front of a mountain of envelopes.) Seven days of post. And Dario's got everyone over at the HQ for a speech. I'll have to file in my head while he talks.",[
  ["What does Dario talk about?","Oil, and himself, and how he dug a hole in Oklahoma. We've all heard about the hole. It gets deeper every time."],
  ["How do you file seven days of post?","Date, then department, then sender, in capitals. By lunchtime this desk will be an empty field. I'm almost giddy."],
  ["Want a hand?","(He looks horrified.) No. Thank you. That's very kind. No."]],
 "Don't touch the in-tray. I know it looks like a mess. It isn't."],
8:["(Mats is pulling a folder from a filing cabinet, very fast.) Something's happened at the rig. Magnus wants every insurance file since 1979 on his desk in an hour. I know exactly where they are, which is the only good thing about today.",[
  ["What happened at the rig?","Nobody tells the clerks. They just ask us for files. You can guess a lot from which files, though. Today it's insurance and safety."],
  ["Safety files?","The ones with complaints in. Einar on the rig writes very long complaints. I file them under E. They take up most of E."],
  ["You're very calm.","Panicking makes the files go in the wrong drawer. And then there's two problems."]],
 "If Magnus asks, the files are on their way. They are. Running."],
9:["(Mats looks tired and slightly guilty.) We broke the agreement last night. Elin told me Kelp Cola's working on a new flavour. I told her about the files. Then we sat in silence feeling terrible.",[
  ["What new flavour?","I didn't ask. (Pause.) Something with a fruit in it. I said, 'Don't tell me.' She said, 'I already have.' We both felt awful."],
  ["Is it really so bad to talk?","With Magnus and Harald both trying to lead the Republicans? If anyone found out, they'd think one of us was a spy. Probably both."],
  ["Who'd you be spying for?","Nobody! I'm a clerk. I'd make a terrible spy. I'd file the secrets and hand them back."]],
 "Never again. Until the next time, probably."],
10:["(Mats is in a dark suit, with a black band on his sleeve.) They're burying the king today. Clerks aren't invited to the chapel, of course, but I'll stand on the road in Kingsgrad when the procession passes.",[
  ["Will Elin be there?","She's going with Phil, holding his umbrella and his notes. If we see each other, we'll nod politely, like strangers. It's become a sort of game."],
  ["Did you admire the old king?","He kept the island's books very straight for a king. The royal accounts are public, you know. I've read them. He spent almost nothing on himself."],
  ["Nice suit.","It's my only good one. It's a copy of a picture in a magazine, of a man from Morgan Stanley. Grace the tailor in Kingsgrad did her best with it."]],
 "Hats off when the coffin passes. Elin says I always forget."],
11:["(Mats is tidying his pencils in order of length.) Quiet day at the tower. Too quiet. Magnus came by twice to ask me where things are filed. He's never asked me anything before.",[
  ["What was he looking for?","Old account numbers. The sort that live at the back of the bottom drawer. I gave him what he asked for. That's my job."],
  ["Should you be worried?","I'm a clerk. Clerks don't worry. We cross-reference."],
  ["Why the pencils?","When the world's untidy, you tidy the bit in front of you. My mother taught me that. She had seven children, so she knew."]],
 "Shortest on the left. It's not science, but it's mine."],
12:["(Mats is reading a memo, and puts it face down when you come in.) Busy day. People wanting signatures, people wanting files. I always read everything twice before I sign. It saves trouble later.",[
  ["Who'd make an exception to that rule?","Nobody. (He hesitates.) Well. A proper man from a proper bank, perhaps. Somebody who did things properly. That's different."],
  ["What's the strangest thing you've ever filed?","A letter from a man in Texas, offering to buy the island's weather. I filed it under W. I still don't know what he wanted."],
  ["Bad day?","Not bad. Busy. Busy's good. Busy means the world needs paper."]],
 "Read twice, sign once. Write that in your notebook. It's the only advice I have."],
13:["(Mats is eating a sandwich at his desk with a napkin tucked in his collar.) Elin brought home a crate of Kelp Cola from the free samples. We have rules about work at home. We have no rules about free cola.",[
  ["Is it good?","It's very sweet and very fizzy. I had two. Then I felt disloyal and had a glass of water."],
  ["How's the agreement going?","Terribly. Last night she said Harald's been practising his speech in the lift. I said Magnus uses index cards. We've broken it every evening this week."],
  ["Do you ever argue about the companies?","Once. About which company has the better pension scheme. Mine has. She won't accept the figures."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the cola. It's a very small sin, but it's mine."],
14:["(Mats has his good suit on again.) The big day, the whole island together. Elin and I have agreed to stand together. In public. For the first time.",[
  ["Isn't that risky?","Very. But there'll be so many people that nobody will notice. And if they do, it's a gathering of the whole island. We're the island."],
  ["What do you expect from today?","Speeches I'll forget and faces I won't. And a sense, maybe, of where everything's going. I like to know where things are going."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I've checked my tie four times. Elin says that's not nerves, that's me. She's right."]],
 "If you see us, pretend you don't. It's more romantic that way."],
15:["(Mats is shaking his head at the Gazette.) Crown before cross, cross before crown. I don't mind which prince, as long as somebody keeps the books. Kingdoms run on ledgers, not on crowns.",[
  ["Who keeps the island's books now?","Bertil, at the Civil Service Bureau, and about nine other people like him, quietly. They're the real government. Nobody notices."],
  ["Which prince would you pick?","Augustus reads. Leopold drives. I'd pick the one who reads, on principle. But I'm a clerk, so I would."],
  ["Is anything in the paper about Wall Street?","A small piece on page nine. The Dow's dipped. I've cut it out. I'll file it under 'Worry, foreign'."]],
 "Ledgers, not crowns. Somebody should stitch it on a cushion."],
16:["(Mats has a pencil behind each ear.) Elin and I listened to the Great Debate on the radio last night, with the volume very low, so as not to hear each other's opinions.",[
  ["What did you think of it?","Anna was very clear. Erik was very kind. Neither of them mentioned the national debt once. I wanted to telephone in."],
  ["Did you and Elin agree?","We agreed we'd both rather have a vote than a Soviet. That's the first political thing we've ever said to each other."],
  ["Two pencils?","One for filing and one for crosswords. I lost track of which is which around ten o'clock."]],
 "A vote is just a ledger of what people want. I approve of ledgers."],
17:["(Mats is filing very fast, and his hands shake slightly.) Dario's missing. Nobody's seen him. The whole company's ringing the tower to ask if he's here. He's never once been to the tower.",[
  ["Where could he be?","I don't know. I file where things go. People, I can't help with. People don't stay in their drawers."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. When the man at the top goes missing, things start falling out of cabinets. Paperwork and people."],
  ["Have you told Elin?","Not yet. If I tell her, she'll tell me something back, and then we've both said too much on a day like this."]],
 "Close the door, would you? I need to hear myself think."],
18:["(Mats is picking up a fallen stack of folders.) The tower swayed. Did you feel it? Eleven floors of glass, and every filing cabinet rolled six inches to the left.",[
  ["Was anything damaged?","My system. Six years of perfect order, in a heap. (He sighs.) It's all still there. It's just in the wrong order, which is worse."],
  ["Is the volcano waking up?","They say so. There's steam in the north. Elin says Kelp Cola's insuring the bottling plant. I didn't hear that. I'm not listening."],
  ["Want help picking them up?","You'd put them back in the wrong place, very kindly. No. Thank you."]],
 "One folder at a time. That's how you rebuild the world."],
19:["(Mats is reading a letter with a New York postmark, and holding it as if it might fly away.) A bank in New York has written back to me. I sent a letter in July, asking about clerk positions. They said 'Not at present.'",[
  ["That's not a no!","That's exactly what I said. 'Not at present' means 'possibly in future'. I've read it eleven times."],
  ["Would you really leave the island?","With Elin. Only with Elin. She says she'd like to see the Statue of Liberty and then come home. I'd like to see the Statue of Liberty and then stay."],
  ["Will you write again?","Every year, on the first of July, until they say yes or ask me to stop. I've made a note in my diary."]],
 "Not at present. It's the most hopeful thing anyone's ever sent me."],
20:["(Mats is looking at a delivery note with a frown.) The tower's had a delivery for the Hotel Aurora by mistake. A box for the Russian businessman upstairs there. It hums.",[
  ["What's in it?","I didn't open it. I'm a clerk, not a customs officer. I've re-addressed it and sent it on. It was very heavy, for something that hums."],
  ["Who is the Russian?","A businessman, the hotel says. Elin says he's been to Kelp Cola twice, asking about bottling. Oh no. I've broken the agreement at work now."],
  ["Isn't that a bit suspicious?","Everything's suspicious if you look hard enough. I'd rather file it."]],
 "Re-addressed and forwarded. That's the end of it, as far as I'm concerned."],
21:["(Mats has ash on his spectacles and a box of files in his arms.) The mountain's erupted. The tower's being cleared. I've saved what I could: the accounts, the contracts, and Elin's photograph off my desk.",[
  ["Is Elin all right?","She telephoned from Kingsgrad. The ash hasn't got that far south yet. She told me to come home. I said I had to save the files. She called me a name."],
  ["What's happening in Frostholm?","Ash on the cars, people running for the trams, and the sky up north is orange. Nobody's hurt, they say. Everyone's frightened."],
  ["Put the box down.",["(He holds on to it.) If I put it down, I'll start shaking. While I'm holding it, I'm just a clerk with a box.",[
    ["Then hold it. I'll walk you to the tram.","(He nods.) Thank you. Mind the steps. They're slippery with ash."],
    ["Elin would want you to put it down.","(He puts it down, very carefully, and sits on it.) She would. She'd also say I should have left an hour ago."]]]]],
 "Go home, if you can. That's what I'm going to do. Eventually."],
22:["(Mats is dusting ash off folders with a pastry brush.) Elin's pastry brush. She doesn't know I've borrowed it. Don't tell her. It's very good for ash.",[
  ["Did you make it home last night?","At ten o'clock, grey from head to foot. Elin hugged me and then made me stand in the bath. We didn't talk about work at all. That's the first time."],
  ["How's the tower?","Dirty, cold, and nothing's broken but my filing. Which is a lot, to me, but not to anyone else."],
  ["You've got ash on your glasses.","I know. I've decided it's a filter. It makes the world look calmer."]],
 "If you see Elin, I've borrowed nothing. Especially not a brush."],
23:["(Mats has a telex in his hand.) Something's gone wrong under the water at the rig. Magnus wants every maintenance report from Einar since 1980. They're all under E. They take up most of E.",[
  ["What do Einar's reports say?","That things are rusty and will break. Every report. For years. I've filed them very carefully, and nobody's ever asked to see them before today."],
  ["Will anyone read them now?","Probably. People read warnings after the thing they were warned about happens. It's very human, and very bad for filing."],
  ["Does that make you angry?","A little. A clerk isn't supposed to be angry. But somebody should have read E."]],
 "If you see Einar, tell him somebody's reading his reports. He'll want to know."],
24:["(Mats is studying the financial pages, upside down by mistake.) Sorry. I'm distracted. Elin said something last night that I keep thinking about. She said 'If we ever had to choose, what would we choose?'",[
  ["Choose what?","Between the companies and each other. I said each other, of course. Then she asked if I'd said it too quickly. I don't know how fast you're meant to say it."],
  ["Did you choose each other?","We chose to make cocoa and not talk about it. That seemed like the wisest choice available."],
  ["Your paper's upside down.","(He turns it round.) So it is. The Dow's down, either way."]],
 "Each other. Not too quickly. Not too slowly. I've been practising."],
25:["(Mats is reading a list of Republican voters with a frown.) Magnus asked me to file the Republican Party's member list. Forty-three names. Doctor Lind. Frank. Otto from the cinema. Half my building.",[
  ["Is Elin on it?","No, thank goodness. Neither am I. We're the only two people in the Kingsgrad Apartments not in a party. We're very lonely at the meetings."],
  ["Is Magnus going to win?","I couldn't possibly say. (Pause.) He has a great many index cards. That's not nothing."],
  ["Isn't that list confidential?","Everything I file is confidential. That's why they give it to me. I'm the quietest man on the island."]],
 "Forty-three. Filed under R. Some under W, for 'Worried'."],
26:["(Mats has a bill of sale in front of him and is turning it over.) Dario wants the spa on the mountain. He's asked for the paperwork to be ready in case. Twenty pages of it, ready for a signature that may never come.",[
  ["Will he get it?","I can't say. I can only make sure that if he does, it's filed properly. That's my small part in history."],
  ["Is it a good investment?","After an eruption? It's a hot bath on an angry mountain. Wall Street would call it 'speculative'. I'd call it 'Dario'."],
  ["Have you been to the spa?","Once, with Elin, for her birthday. She fell asleep in the hot pool. I read a pamphlet on mineral rights. It was perfect."]],
 "Twenty pages, in triplicate. History, filed."],
27:["(Mats is in a cardigan, eating soup at his desk.) Elin's working late every night this week. Kelp Cola's going abroad, or something. I mustn't ask. I've stopped asking. It's killing me.",[
  ["What do you do while she works?","I file at home. I've done the spice rack again. By colour, this time. She'll notice."],
  ["Is the agreement still holding?","It's holding the way ice holds in April. We both know what's under it."],
  ["Are you going to the office party tomorrow?","I've been told I must. I'll stand near the milkshake machine and talk to nobody. It'll be lovely."]],
 "Cumin, coriander, curry. All yellow. It's perfect. She'll hate it."],
28:["(Mats is holding a milkshake with both hands, standing very close to the wall.) The office party. Dario said everybody had to bowl at least once. I've bowled once. I hit the gutter, very precisely.",[
  ["What have you heard here?","That Magnus thinks he'll win tomorrow. That Lars has debts. That Ruth likes cowboys. I knew that one already. Everybody knows that one."],
  ["Is the whole island out today?","Every faction, all at once. Elin's at Kelp Cola, sending boxes abroad. We'll both be home late and say nothing."],
  ["Enjoying yourself?","I'm a clerk at a party. This is how we enjoy ourselves. You can't tell, but inside I'm dancing."]],
 "Vanilla. Unremarkable. Like me. I like it."],
29:["(Mats is filing the papers for the Republican vote with great care.) The vote's today. Magnus, Harald, Carlo. Elin and I had breakfast in silence. It was the most loving silence I've ever had.",[
  ["Who do you want to win?","Whoever wins, Elin and I go home to the same flat. That's the only result I'm counting."],
  ["Did you talk about it at breakfast?","We passed each other the marmalade. It said everything."],
  ["What will you do when it's done?","File the results, in capitals, and go home early. For the first time in six years."]],
 "Whatever happens, it'll be filed by Monday."],
30:["(Mats has cleared his desk entirely, and is sitting with his hands flat on it.) The last day. Whatever happens to the island, happens today. I've filed everything. There's nothing left in the in-tray.",[
  ["How does an empty desk feel?","Frightening. Wonderful. Like the top of a page before you write the first line."],
  ["What will you and Elin do?","Have dinner. Talk about work, openly, all evening, for once. We've decided. Whatever happens, we're done pretending."],
  ["And Wall Street?","Still there. It's a big street. One day, maybe. Not at present."]],
 "Read twice, sign once. And then go home to someone. That's all I know."],
};
// Maya, the coffee roaster at the Coffee Roasters in Eastbrook (Democratic Party). Threads: her feud with Ole the harbourmaster
// and his import taxes, her roasting experiments (and the blend she names for each week), Dex at the cart outside who cadges coffee,
// her cardamom buns, and the coffee urns she's promised Erik for the debate (16) and the rally (28).
DAILY['Maya']={
4:["(Maya is shaking a tray of beans and sniffing them, unhappily.) Eastbrook's open, and you've come in for coffee. Good. I warn you, it's strong. It has to be. It's covering for a lot.",[
  ["Covering for what?","For the beans. Ordinary, dusty, tax-paid beans, the only kind I can get since Ole the harbourmaster put a duty on everything that floats. I roast them dark so you can't taste their feelings."],
  ["One coffee, please.","(She pours a cup so black it seems to absorb the light.) There. That's the strongest coffee on the island. Erik drinks four a day and he can hear colours."],
  ["You're a Democrat too?","Of course. In a free vote, I'd vote out Ole. In a free market, I'd buy beans from Colombia. Same thing, really. Freedom smells of coffee."]],
 "Drink it while it's hot. It's worse when it's cold."],
5:["(Maya is writing on a blackboard behind the counter: THIS WEEK'S BLEND: SNOWMELT.) Every week I name a blend. It's the same beans, roasted differently. Mostly it's the same beans, roasted angrily.",[
  ["Why Snowmelt?","Because the roads are open, and everyone's in a good mood, and nobody's noticed yet that it tastes of bark. Name it something cheerful, they drink it cheerfully."],
  ["What's wrong with Ole's taxes?","Ole says the harbour needs the money. The harbour needs a new coat of paint and a harbourmaster who's tasted coffee once in his life. He drinks tea. Weak tea. With milk."],
  ["Is that a bun I can smell?","Cardamom buns, from the back kitchen. My grandmother's recipe. The only thing in this shop that's exactly as good as it should be."]],
 "Snowmelt. Remember the name. You'll never taste it again."],
6:["(Dex leans in through the door, holding out an empty mug. Maya fills it without looking up, and he vanishes.) That's Dex, from the hot dog cart. He pays me in gossip. It's a bad exchange rate.",[
  ["What gossip did he bring?","That the Syndicate's opened a card game in Kingsgrad, and someone from abroad's been invited. Everybody's a guest these days. Nobody's a customer."],
  ["Doesn't he sell drinks himself?","Kelp Cola. From a cart. (She shudders.) I've told him if he ever sells coffee I'll set fire to his sausages. We're very good friends."],
  ["Do you get many customers?","Erik, Dex, the mill lads on their way up, and Nora from the Gazette, who drinks it standing up like a horse. And tourists, Ruby tells me, any day now."]],
 "If Dex comes back, tell him the next one costs a rumour with a name in it."],
7:["(Maya is holding a single bean up to the window and squinting at it.) Look at this. Broken, pale, a bit mouldy. It came in a sack that's been in Ole's warehouse for three weeks waiting for a stamp.",[
  ["Can't you buy from somewhere else?","Everything comes in by sea, and everything at sea goes through Ole. Frostholm's harbour, Eastbrook's harbour, they all answer to his ledger. The man's a toll bridge with a moustache."],
  ["Who'd change the taxes, if you could vote?","Anyone who's ever had a cup of real coffee. I'd vote for a fish if it promised to lower the duty on beans."],
  ["Throw it out, then.","(She drops it in a jar marked SINS.) Not out. In there. I keep the bad ones as evidence, for when there's a trial."]],
 "Frostholm's open. If you see good coffee there, don't tell me. I'll only be jealous."],
8:["(Maya is trying a spoonful of something brown in a cup, and grimacing.) Roasted barley. My grandmother's wartime trick, when there wasn't any coffee at all. It tastes like wartime.",[
  ["Why barley?","To stretch the beans. Half barley, half coffee, and nobody notices for a week. Then they notice, and they come in and look at me with very sad eyes."],
  ["Does the Sunday crowd want coffee?","After the monastery service on the radio, they want something stronger than God. I sell them coffee. Ruby sells them jazz. Linnea sells them the rest."],
  ["Are you trying to poison me?",["(She laughs.) Only a little. Here. (She pours from the other pot.) That's the real one. Mostly real.",[
    ["Mostly?","Eighty per cent. The rest is optimism. Optimism doesn't have a tax on it yet. Give Ole time."],
    ["It's good.","It's fine. Good is something else. Good is rarer on this island than a fair tax."]]]]],
 "Don't tell Erik about the barley. He thinks he's been drinking coffee."],
9:["(Maya has her arms folded and is glaring out of the window at Dex's cart.) Dex has been asked about cola flavours twice today. Cola flavours! Nobody's ever come to ask me about coffee.",[
  ["What would you put in a cola?","Coffee. Obviously. A dark, bitter cola with a kick in it. They'd sell a million. Kelp Cola would sell it with a cartoon of a happy fish on the side."],
  ["Why are they asking cooks?","Because a chemist can make a drink, but a cook knows what people want to taste. Saara at Kelp Cola's clever. She knows that much."],
  ["Jealous of Dex?","Of Dex? (She snorts.) A man who thinks mustard is a vegetable? ...A bit. He gets asked things. People just ask me for 'a coffee'."]],
 "If anyone wants a coffee cola, I'm the woman."],
10:["(The shop's quiet, and Maya has put a black ribbon on the coffee grinder.) The funeral's today. The whole palace in black, and the Abbot. I've given the grinder the day off, out of respect. It's the hand grinder or nothing.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","He came to Eastbrook once, when I was a girl, to open the school. He drank a coffee from my grandmother's pot, and pulled a face, and drank it all anyway. I've always respected that."],
  ["Is Eastbrook in mourning?","Eastbrook's in a black jumper and a thoughtful mood. People are sad, and they're wondering what comes next, and they're feeling bad about wondering."],
  ["Hand-ground, then?","(She cranks the little handle.) Slower. Better, if I'm honest. Grief's good for coffee. Everything's good for coffee except Ole."]],
 "Raise a cup for the old man. Even if it's barley."],
11:["(Knut and two other mill hands are crowded round Maya's counter, talking loudly. She shoos them out with a tea towel.) Out! Go and argue at the mill. They've been in here for an hour over one cup each.",[
  ["What were they arguing about?","Pay. And the saw. And Helen from the Communists coming up to talk for them. Half of them want her help and the other half want to want it less."],
  ["Do you agree with them?","They work like oxen for Dagny and they can't afford my coffee. That's how you know something's wrong. A mill hand should be able to afford a coffee."],
  ["Want me to buy them another round?","(She thinks about it.) No. They'll only stay another hour. But it's a kind thought. You can buy them one next week."]],
 "If you're at the mill, tell Knut he owes me for a broken saucer."],
12:["(Maya is chalking the blackboard: THIS WEEK'S BLEND: STRONG OPINIONS.) Erik's asked me to do the coffee for the debate with the Communists. Four days. A hundred cups. And I'm meant to be neutral.",[
  ["Can coffee be neutral?","Absolutely not. Coffee always takes sides. If Anna gets the bottom of the urn, that's fate, not politics."],
  ["Why Strong Opinions?","Because the debate's coming, and everyone on this island's got one. And because it's the same beans as last week, roasted till they shout."],
  ["Ruby looks nervous today.","Ruby's been in twice for a coffee, and stared into it both times like it was a crystal ball. Something about a record. She won't say. Ruby always says, eventually."]],
 "A hundred cups. I'll need a bigger urn, and Ole will tax it."],
13:["(A Kelp Cola girl in a paper hat is standing outside the window with a tray of samples. Maya is standing inside it with a tray of coffee.) It's war. She gives away cola, I give away coffee. We'll see who blinks first.",[
  ["Who's winning?","She's got sugar. I've got caffeine and cardamom buns. So far, three customers to her, and seven to me. And Dex, who had both."],
  ["Can you afford to give it away?","No. (She hands a cup to a passer-by.) But I can't afford to let Kelp Cola own the street either. It's a matter of principle. Expensive principle."],
  ["Give her a coffee, too.","(She looks at the girl, who's shivering.) ...Fine. Just one. Don't tell anyone. It's not surrender, it's diplomacy."]],
 "She drank it. Diplomacy works. Victory is mine."],
14:["(Maya is filling three huge flasks and loading them into a pram.) The fourteenth. The whole island in one place, cold, waiting about, and in need of coffee. I'm bringing the pram.",[
  ["Is that a pram?","My grandmother's. It's carried two babies and twenty years of coffee. It's the most useful thing I own."],
  ["What do you hope for today?","That people look at each other and decide to be decent. And that someone, anyone, asks Ole about the import duty in public."],
  ["Will people pay for coffee?","On a cold day, standing about, listening to speeches? They'd pay for hot water. I'll be a rich woman by sundown."]],
 "Wish me luck. And somebody push the pram if I fall over."],
15:["(Maya is hunched over a small notebook with numbers in it, sighing.) I made more in one day than I've made in a month. The fourteenth. And then I worked out what Ole takes off it. Then I made a different noise.",[
  ["How much does Ole take?","Enough for a new harbour lamp. He bought one last year, and he's proud of it. Every time I see it I think: that's my sugar."],
  ["Did you get much sleep?","I never sleep. I'm a coffee roaster. I lie very still for six hours and plan blends."],
  ["The royals and the church are fighting today.","Leopold and Augustus. Crown and cross. If I were their mother I'd sit them down with a cup of coffee each and make them say sorry. She's not, and I'm not, so there we are."]],
 "Numbers. Horrible things. Coffee's better."],
16:["(Maya is wheeling a big urn on a trolley toward the door.) Debate day! The urn's full, the cups are stacked, and I've labelled the sugar 'Capitalism' and the milk 'Socialism'. Let's see which goes first.",[
  ["Which will go first?","The sugar. Every time. Even the Communists take sugar. Helen takes three spoons, and she knows I've noticed."],
  ["Who'll win the debate?","Whoever's had the most coffee. Erik's had four already. He'll talk like a typewriter."],
  ["Isn't labelling the sugar biased?","Biased? (She grins.) It's democratic. Everyone chooses. Some people even choose both, which is what Erik would call a coalition."]],
 "Come and have a cup at the debate. Pick a side. Or just pick sugar."],
17:["(The cups on the shelf rattle. Maya grabs the coffee pot to steady it.) That's the third one today. The ground. I nearly spilt a whole pot. Do you know what a whole pot costs?",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Dex says there's steam coming off the mountain. Dex also said the king had a twin, once. But I've seen the steam. It's real."],
  ["Coffee grows on volcanoes, doesn't it?","The best coffee in the world. Java, Guatemala, Kona. (She brightens, then darkens.) Imagine. A volcano of our own, and Ole would only tax the steam."],
  ["Are you scared?","Of the ground? A bit. Of losing a full pot? Terrified. I've got priorities."]],
 "Hold on to your cup on the way out."],
18:["(Maya is roasting a batch with the window open and a wet tea towel on the sill.) The ground keeps shaking, so the roaster keeps rattling, so the beans roast unevenly. I've invented a new blend by accident. I'm calling it Tremor.",[
  ["What does Tremor taste like?","Uneven. Some of it's burnt, some of it isn't, and you never know which sip you're getting. Very much like this month."],
  ["How's the shop doing?","Better than August, worse than the fourteenth. People drink more coffee when they're frightened. I don't like what that says about my business."],
  ["Seen Erik lately?","He came in with a list for the rally as long as his arm, and asked if I'd do the coffee in Kingsgrad, for thousands. I said yes. Then I sat down for a while."]],
 "Tremor. Get it while it lasts. I hope it doesn't."],
19:["(Maya is wrapping cardamom buns in paper, a dozen at a time.) The circus up at Lake Ilmari ordered three dozen buns. The strongman came down for them himself. He was so polite I gave him two extra.",[
  ["The strongman? Igor?","That's him. Shoulders like a door frame, voice like a cat. He asked me if the buns had nuts, because the jester's allergic. He worries about everyone."],
  ["Do you sell much to the circus?","More than I'd expect. Bruno, the ringmaster, has a sweet tooth and a slate he never pays off. Meg the acrobat comes and pays it off for him, quietly."],
  ["Can I have one?","(She hands you a bun.) The thirty-seventh. It's the best one. It's always the extra one that's best."]],
 "Tell Igor I said hello. And to keep the nuts away from the jester."],
20:["(Maya is pouring coffee into a cup for someone who's just left, and looking after them, puzzled.) A man in very good shoes. Russian, I think. Ordered black, no sugar, drank half and asked me who comes in here every day.",[
  ["What did you tell him?","That everyone comes in every day, because it's the only coffee in town. Which is true, and tells him nothing. (She frowns.) Ruby says he asked her the same thing."],
  ["The man from the Hotel Aurora?","Erik thinks so. Erik's been whispering about him all week. Erik's a terrible whisperer. Everyone in the shop could hear."],
  ["Did he like the coffee?","He said it was 'like home'. (She considers.) I'm not sure that was a compliment. I've heard what they drink in Moscow."]],
 "If he comes back, I'll give him the barley."],
21:["(Maya has the shutters half down, and she's filling every flask she owns.) The mountain! It's going up. Ash coming down in the north. I'm making coffee. When the world ends, someone should be making coffee.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Erik's got people at the Community Centre. Dex is making soup. Ruby's packing records. And I'm making coffee. Everyone's doing the thing they know."],
  ["Shouldn't you close the shop?","And go where? Into a cellar, with no coffee? (She hands you a flask.) Take this to the Community Centre. They'll be cold."],
  ["Can I help?","You're holding a flask. That's helping. Hold it a bit more firmly. The ground's moving."]],
 "Go on. Get inside somewhere safe. Take the coffee."],
22:["(There's ash on the windowsill, and Maya is wiping her roaster with a cloth, very gently, as if it were a horse.) It got into the roaster. Grey dust in every crack. If I roast anything now it'll taste like a chimney.",[
  ["Is the roaster ruined?","No. I'm taking it apart, one screw at a time. I've done it before, when a sack of beans had a stone in it. It took two days and I swore in four languages."],
  ["How's Eastbrook?","Grey and tired and alive. Nobody hurt. Everyone's sweeping. Dex keeps bringing me hot dogs and telling me it'll be all right."],
  ["What will this week's blend be?","(She thinks.) Ash Wednesday. It isn't Wednesday, and it's not Lent. But it's the right sort of name."]],
 "Mind the ash. And if you've a small screwdriver, I need it."],
23:["(The roaster is back together and roaring away. Maya is grinning like a madwoman.) She lives! The roaster lives! Fourteen screws, one tea towel and a lot of language. Coffee is back in Eastbrook.",[
  ["Congratulations.","Thank you. I'm going to give everyone on the street a free cup. Even Ole, if he comes. Especially Ole. I'd like to watch him enjoy something."],
  ["Is the ash in the coffee?","(She sniffs a cup.) A bit. Smoky. I've had worse. People in Italy pay extra for smoky."],
  ["What's the mood in town?","Like after a fever. Everyone's tired and kind and very hungry. Dex has sold more hot dogs this week than in August."]],
 "One free cup. Go on. It's my thank-you to the mountain for not taking the roaster."],
24:["(Maya is drafting a letter at the counter in big, angry handwriting.) I'm writing to Ole. A proper letter. 'Dear Harbourmaster, Your taxes on beans are an insult to the island and to my grandmother.' It's going well.",[
  ["Will he read it?","He reads everything. He's an administrator. He logs every letter in a ledger. Mine will be in his ledger for a hundred years. That's something."],
  ["Is it worth it, after the volcano?","More than ever. After the mountain, people deserve good coffee. Life's short and it's grey enough without bad beans."],
  ["Mention your grandmother again.","(She adds a line.) 'My grandmother, who survived two wars and one king, would be ashamed of you.' Better?"]],
 "Off to the post box. Wish me luck."],
25:["(Maya is chalking a new blend on the board: THIS WEEK'S BLEND: PRINCESS.) Dex says Erik's going to try and win over Princess Daphne. So I've named the blend after her. Light, a bit sweet, and surprisingly strong.",[
  ["Is that what Daphne's like?","I've no idea. But it's what I'd like a princess to be. If she comes to Eastbrook, she gets a cup on the house."],
  ["Would a princess drink this?","Princesses drink whatever's put in front of them. That's their whole job, as far as I can see. Being polite to bad coffee."],
  ["Have you ever met her?","I once served her a coffee at a church fete. She said 'thank you' to me and then 'thank you' to the cup. I liked her for that."]],
 "Princess. A light roast with a strong finish. Remember that."],
26:["(Maya is reading the Gazette and sputtering.) Dario's buying the hot springs! And the price he's paying? I could buy beans for the whole island for a hundred years with it. And Ole wouldn't tax him a penny.",[
  ["Why wouldn't Ole tax him?","Because Dario's money comes in by pipeline, not by boat. There's no duty on a pipeline. The rich have their own harbours."],
  ["What will Dario do with a spa?","Sit in it, I expect, and think about owning the rest of the island. I'd sit in it and think about coffee. That's the difference between us."],
  ["Kelp Cola's in the paper, too.","A prince on every bottle. A prince! On a cola bottle! (She folds the paper.) I'd put a coffee bean on a bottle before I put a prince on it."]],
 "I need to roast something. Hard."],
27:["(Maya has six huge urns lined up on the counter, and she's scrubbing the last one.) The rally's tomorrow. Six urns, for Kingsgrad square. If the crowd's half as big as Erik thinks, I'll be pouring till midnight.",[
  ["How will you carry them all?","Igor, from the circus. He came down for his buns and I asked, and he said 'of course' as if I'd asked him to pass the salt. He's carrying two at a time."],
  ["What's the blend for the rally?","I'm calling it Free Vote. It's my best roast of the month. I've been saving the last decent beans for it. Every last one."],
  ["Will you get any rest?","On the thirty-first. I've written it on the calendar. 'Rest.' I've never written that before."]],
 "Come and have a cup of Free Vote in the square tomorrow. It's the good stuff."],
28:["(Maya is lining up six urns at the edge of Kingsgrad square, with Igor setting them down as gently as kittens.) Rally day! The urns are full, the square's still filling, and Free Vote is the best coffee I've ever roasted. My hands won't stop shaking.",[
  ["How big will the crowd be?","Erik says thousands. I've brought coffee for thousands. If it's hundreds, I'll be drinking Free Vote till Christmas, and I won't mind a bit."],
  ["Will Erik's speech be good?","If he cuts the middle. Everyone's told him to cut the middle. I've given him two cups to help him decide."],
  ["Is it nerves or caffeine?","(She holds up a trembling hand.) Both. Neither. I've been tasting the urns since six. It's quality control. That's my story."]],
 "More cups. Always more cups. Go and find a good spot by the stage."],
29:["(Maya is sitting on an upturned crate behind her counter, eating a bun and staring at nothing.) I've never been so tired. My arms are like rope. And today's another day of it, and tomorrow's the last.",[
  ["What's happening today?","The Communists and the Democrats being 'friendly'. Helen came in for a coffee. Three sugars. She said it was good. I nearly fainted."],
  ["Is it friendly?","It's friendly the way two cats in a yard are friendly. Everyone's smiling with their teeth."],
  ["Take a day off.","I'll take one on the thirty-first. I've written it on the calendar. Today I'm a coffee pot with legs."]],
 "Tomorrow's the big one. I'll be open, whatever happens."],
30:["(Maya is roasting very slowly, with the door shut, and the shop smells wonderful.) The last day. Whatever's decided, I wanted the shop to smell good for it. Some things deserve a proper roast.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote. A harbourmaster who answers to someone. And a cup of coffee, a real one, that nobody's taxed into the ground."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Terrified. But I've roasted through snow, a funeral and a volcano. I can roast through a new government. Coffee doesn't care who's in charge."],
  ["Thank you for all the coffee.","(She smiles, and pours you a cup.) Thank you for drinking it. A roaster's nothing without someone to drink the coffee. This one's on the house."]],
 "Come back tomorrow. I'll still be here. Coffee doesn't stop."],
};
// Meg, acrobat of the circus at Lake Ilmari (the Circus). Threads: her ropes and rigging, keeping the troupe organised (the books,
// the horse, the stove), Buttons who falls and whom she catches, an old injury she doesn't mention, the volcano and the ash.
DAILY['Meg']={
7:["(Meg is hanging upside down from the big top rope, a checklist in her teeth. She takes it out.) Don't walk under me. I'm checking the knots. Everything here depends on knots.",[
  ["I'm the British reporter.","Then be useful and hold this end. (She drops a rope into your hands.) Pull. Not that hard. There. You're now part of the rigging."],
  ["Do you ever fall?","Never. Buttons falls. I catch him. It's the arrangement, and it's worked for six years."],
  ["How high is the trapeze?","Twelve metres. I measured it with a string and Igor. The string was more accurate."]],
 "Keep clear of the ring while I'm working. It's the only rule I have."],
8:["(Meg is mending a leather strap with a curved needle.) It's Sunday, but a rope doesn't know it. If I leave this till Monday, somebody will have a very short career.",[
  ["Is that for the trapeze?","For the net. A net's the most honest thing in a circus. It says out loud that someone might fall."],
  ["Don't you rest?","I rest when it's done. It's never done, so I rest in small pieces. Like a cat."],
  ["Where's Buttons?","Asleep on the jetty with his cap on his face. He thinks I don't know. He snores in the key of G."]],
 "If you're passing the jetty, throw a pebble at him. Gently."],
9:["(Meg has a ledger open on her knee and her pencil tucked behind her ear.) Someone from Kelp Cola came by with a very large cheque. Bruno nearly fainted. I told them we'd have to think. We don't have to think. We have to eat.",[
  ["Will you take it?","Not for the name on the big top. Perhaps for a crate of cola and a day's peace. There's a difference between money and being owned."],
  ["Who keeps the circus books?","I do. Bruno can count to three, Igor to four. The rest is me and a very tired pencil."],
  ["Do you like cola?","Warm, in a thunderstorm. Otherwise I prefer tea. Cola's for people who want to feel they're doing something."]],
 "I'd tell you what the takings are, but then I'd have to cry."],
10:["(Meg has pulled the net down from the rigging, and folded it in black.) We're dark today. Bruno insisted. And I'll admit it feels right. You don't swing over a room on the day they bury a king.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","I saw him once, at a window, waving at the lake. Like a man waving at a boat he's not on."],
  ["Is it hard to stop for a day?","Harder than it looks. My hands don't know what to do. They keep reaching for a rope."],
  ["How's Buttons coping?","He's gone quiet, which is alarming. I made him soup. He said thank you in a small voice. I nearly cried."]],
 "Tomorrow we'll be loud. Today, let's just be still."],
11:["(Meg is stacking spare ropes in neat coils.) Buttons has been complaining about wages. He's heard the Eastbrook mill hands want a fair day's pay, and he's decided he's part of the labour movement. He's never worked a day. I mean, never with a lifting job.",[
  ["Does he have a point?","A small one. We're underpaid. But we're also over-loved, which is a better deal than most. I'd just like both."],
  ["What do you earn?","Enough to eat, and less than the horse costs. We make it work. It's what a circus does."],
  ["Would you join a union?","If it came with a safety net. I'd join anything with a safety net."]],
 "I don't do speeches. I do knots. Knots are more honest."],
12:["(Meg has Buttons's hat on a stick and is looking at it sternly.) He borrowed my hat. I've taken it back, and I'm giving him the silent treatment. It lasts ten minutes, and it's very effective.",[
  ["Is he in trouble?","Always. It's what he's for. But I'd miss him if he wasn't. I'd have to make my own jokes, and they're dreadful."],
  ["How did you two meet?","In a ditch outside Tampere. He'd fallen off something. I thought, here's a man who'll never be a danger to anyone but himself. I was right."],
  ["Do you ever want to leave the circus?","Once or twice. But then I stand at the top of the rope and look down at all those faces, and it's enough. You can't have that in a bank."]],
 "Ten minutes are up. Time to forgive him. He's got sad eyes."],
13:["(Meg is writing on a card in block letters.) NO FREE SAMPLES. I'm putting it on the ticket booth. Kingsgrad's giving cola away tomorrow, and I've no intention of giving away anything we've worked for.",[
  ["Would the circus do a free show?","Not for free. For a fair price, we'd do three. Free means they'd value it at nothing."],
  ["Is Bruno on board?","Bruno thinks everything's a chance to be seen. I think it's a chance to be cheated. We balance each other out."],
  ["Will you go to Kingsgrad?","Perhaps, for the horse's feed. If the cola men are giving things away, they might have oats."]],
 "Take what's offered, but ask what it costs. A good rule for ropes and men."],
14:["(Meg is sitting cross-legged in the ring, chalking her hands.) The fourteenth. I've been told it's a gathering of the whole island. I have no idea what that means, but the rigging's tight and the net's down.",[
  ["Are you ready?","I'm always ready. I'm a bit worried about everyone else. A whole island in one place is a lot of weight on one rope."],
  ["What do you expect?","Noise, shoving, a speech. Someone will fall over. If I'm there, I'll catch them."],
  ["Is the circus performing?","We're on standby. If someone needs a distraction, we've got a very good one. He's in a red coat and he never stops."]],
 "Stay near the edges. That's where you can see everything and nothing can fall on you."],
15:["(Meg has a chart of the circus's expenses, with two little flags on it.) Someone from the palace and someone from the monastery have both asked me to choose a prince. I said I only choose ropes. They said, a rope's a choice, and I said, quite.",[
  ["Which prince would you choose?","The one who's looked at a net before. A king needs to know what happens when you let go."],
  ["Is the circus taking sides?","Bruno's keeping a foot on each side. I said that's how men split their trousers. He didn't laugh."],
  ["What do you make of the crown and the cross?","They're both a kind of rigging. One holds up the throne, the other holds up the sky. I'd trust neither without checking."]],
 "I'll check the knots in the morning. They'll still be knots."],
16:["(Meg is practising a handstand against a pole, and speaks upside down.) A debate between the Communists and the Democrats. I'd like to watch. They'd be great on a trapeze: all that swinging, and nobody listening.",[
  ["Who would win?","The one who lets go at the right moment. It's the only thing that matters on a trapeze."],
  ["Are you going to listen?","I'll get the gist from Buttons, who'll have got it from someone who was asleep."],
  ["Which side are you on?","The side of fair weather and good rope. That's all a performer needs from politics."]],
 "Be careful who you vote for. It's like picking a catcher. Look at his hands."],
17:["(Meg is standing very still, one hand on the rope, her face turned up.) Did you feel that? The rope hummed. Not the wind: it was too regular. The whole big top shivered, like a horse.",[
  ["Was it an earth tremor?","I think so. Bruno says it's nothing. I'd say it's the first one. There'll be a second."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Not for me. I've fallen from worse. It's the little ones, and the horse, and Buttons, who'll hide under the bed."],
  ["Where do you think it came from?","North, from the mountain. There's steam, and Zora keeps staring that way. When Zora stares, I check my knots."]],
 "Come away from the poles. They carry it."],
18:["(Meg is inspecting the net hook by hook.) I've told Bruno we need to move the audience chairs to the lakeside. He said the audience would complain. I said the audience can complain from a safe distance.",[
  ["Is the big top safe?","It's stood a hundred storms. But I don't trust the ground. I've doubled the guy ropes and put Igor on the main pole."],
  ["What does the island say?","The cola people are making a cup into a star, and the church is hunting for a relic. Everyone's got a story. I've got a net."],
  ["Is Buttons helping?","He's carrying things. Mostly sandwiches. It counts as moral support."]],
 "A safe tent is a boring tent. Boring's what I'm aiming for."],
19:["(Meg is writing a list in the margin of the ledger.) Things to do if the ground gets worse. One: wake Igor. Two: wake the horse. Three: wake Buttons, though it won't help. It's good to have a list.",[
  ["Who would you save first?","I'd save the rope. Then everyone else. Don't tell Buttons the order."],
  ["Does the circus have a plan?","Bruno has a plan. It's called 'keep smiling'. I have a plan too, and it has numbers."],
  ["Have you heard about the Communist call for comrades?","They want volunteers, I hear. I'd volunteer for a rope-pulling team, but I won't sign anything."]],
 "If the ground shakes in the night, grab the horse. She'll know where to go."],
20:["(Meg has been up all night, and her eyes show it.) Zora stopped by at midnight. She put her hand on the net and said, 'Tomorrow, hold on.' Then she went to bed. I didn't sleep after that.",[
  ["Does Zora predict real things?","Often enough that I double-check the rope. She said I'd fall in love with a fool, and I'm still waiting for the end of that one."],
  ["What will you do?","Check everything twice. And keep the horse near. And be there if Buttons trips."],
  ["Are you scared?","A little. Fear's useful. It tells you where the knots are weak."]],
 "Hold on tight tomorrow. It's good advice for anything."],
21:["(Meg is sitting on the caravan steps, grey with ash, an arm around Buttons.) It's over, or the first part is. The mountain went off like a gun. The net held, the big top held, and the rope's still humming. We're all here.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Everyone. The horse is shaking, Bruno's pretending not to, Buttons has just had a good cry. Igor held the big top pole up with his shoulder for an hour."],
  ["What did you see?","A glow in the north, red as a stove. The ground swung like a trapeze. And then ash, falling softly, like the worst kind of snow."],
  ["How are you?","Tired. Proud. Ready for a very long sleep, after I've counted heads again."]],
 "Check on the others. Then check again. It's what I do."],
22:["(Meg is beating the dust out of the net with a flat paddle, and coughing.) Everything's grey. The ropes, the horse, Buttons. He looks like a statue of himself. I've told him not to move, and he's thrilled.",[
  ["Can the circus perform?","With scarves, yes. The audience wears them too. A hundred people with grey mouths and bright eyes. It's very theatrical."],
  ["How's the camp?","Fine. We've got food and a stove. The lake's grey, but the water's clean enough to boil. It'll be a long few days."],
  ["What's the news?","Ash on the roads, lava in the north, no one killed. Which is the main thing. The rest is cleaning."]],
 "Wear something over your mouth. It's no fashion, but it works."],
23:["(Meg has hung every rope in the camp out to air, like washing.) Ash gets into everything. It's in the knots, in the hair, in the soup. I could stand here all day and still find more.",[
  ["Is it hard work?","It's something to do with my hands. I'm better when I'm busy."],
  ["Are the audiences coming?","A few. They sit and look at the ring as if they'd forgotten what it was for. Then Buttons trips, and they laugh, and they remember."],
  ["Who's helping?","Igor does the heavy things. Zora does the quiet things, like telling us it's going to be all right. She's never wrong about that."]],
 "Take a rope home with you. It's good luck, and it's clean."],
24:["(Meg has a bandage on one palm.) A rope burn. My fault. I wasn't looking. Buttons says I can't be hurt, because I'm made of wire. It was a nice thing to say, and a wrong one.",[
  ["Does it hurt?","A bit. I've had worse. I broke my wrist in Riga in '76, and I climbed back the next night. It was a mistake, but a proud one."],
  ["The monks have gone to the peaks, I hear.","A retreat. I understand it. When the ground won't stay still, you go up and look down."],
  ["Is Buttons being useful?","He's making tea. It's terrible, but he's making it with love. I drank two."]],
 "I'll be back on the rope by tomorrow. It's what I'm for."],
25:["(Meg is stringing a new line between two poles.) A crown for everyone. That's what the bills say. A crown for everyone, and a princess for democracy. It's a lot of crowns. I'd rather have a good rope.",[
  ["What do you make of the talk?","It's a lot of balancing, and nobody's holding the net. I'd just like to know who's going to catch them."],
  ["Would you wear a crown?","Only if it came with a harness. A crown without a harness is how kings fall."],
  ["What's the mood at the lake?","Tired and a bit hopeful. We're mending. We might even be better."]],
 "A good rope is the only crown I'd trust."],
26:["(Meg is rehearsing alone, hanging by one knee.) I've been watching Kelp Cola make a prince into an advertisement. A prince in every bottle. It's the circus again, with a different ringmaster.",[
  ["Is that how you see it?","They want a face on every bottle. We want a face in every seat. It's all the same trade. They just sell fizz."],
  ["What about the oil man's spa?","Fancier than a circus, and wetter. But I bet there's no net. Not for the guests."],
  ["Are you looking forward to the end of the month?","I'm looking forward to the day after. When the rope's quiet again."]],
 "Don't sign anything you can't climb."],
27:["(Meg is tightening a guy rope with her whole weight.) They're showing a film in Eastbrook tonight, I hear, about a revolution. I'd go, but I've seen enough revolutions. They all go in circles, like the ring.",[
  ["Do you like films?","I love them. The people are all in the right place and nobody falls. I like to be soothed."],
  ["Are you going?","Maybe, if Buttons can find a ticket. Otherwise I'll stay home and mend. It's the nearest I get to a night out."],
  ["How's the mood?","Tight. People are like ropes on a hot day: they're expanding, and no one wants to say."]],
 "Have a good evening. Don't let anyone pull your rope."],
28:["(Meg is stood with her arms folded, looking at the sky.) All the factions are out today, pushing. Everyone's shouting for the last of it. And we're here, a circus by a lake, being quiet. It's rather nice.",[
  ["Will the circus join in?","We'll stay out of it. We'll put on a show, and let them come to us. We're the one place where no one can win."],
  ["Is Bruno restless?","He's pacing, polishing the bell. He wants a part in it. I've told him to stay put, and he's sulking."],
  ["Are you worried?","About the next two days, a bit. But I've held worse weights than this island."]],
 "Stay calm. It's a good trick, and anyone can learn it."],
29:["(Meg is tightening the last guy rope.) Everything's checked. Everything's tied. All I can do now is wait. I've told the others to get some sleep. They're all pretending.",[
  ["How are you?","Steady. I don't sleep much before a big night. It's like standing on the edge of the platform, just before the jump."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","I think the island will do something brave or something foolish. Usually both."],
  ["Is Buttons asleep?","He's snoring. It's the best sound I've heard all week."]],
 "Sleep if you can. The net will be there in the morning."],
30:["(Meg stands at the top of the big top ladder, looking out over the lake.) The last day. I've been up here since dawn. The whole island looks small from this height. It makes it easier to think.",[
  ["What's it like up there?","Quiet. Grey. Everything's equal. A prince and a pig look the same from twelve metres."],
  ["What will you do after today?","Take the net down, fold it up, and take the circus where the road goes. I'll check the rope first."],
  ["Any last advice?","Hold on. And if you can't, let go cleanly. It's the half-hearted falls that hurt."]],
 "Thanks for listening. It's easy to forget that people do."],
};
// Mike, fisherman at West Saltwick Harbour (Communist Party). Threads: his secret sweetheart (Lucia, never named) and the gifts
// and lies it costs him, learning the Internationale in Russian for Anna's big day (the 28th), his stubborn old boat the
// Valborg and its gearbox, and Kate on his trail.
DAILY['Mike']={
1:["(Mike is coiling rope and singing under his breath.) '...the earth shall rise on new foundations...' Oh! Hello. You're the reporter. Don't write down my singing.",[
  ["You've a good voice.","Twelve years in the Saltwick choir, till they asked me to stop doing the Internationale at Christmas."],
  ["What are you catching?","Cod, if they'll have me. The snow kept us in for a fortnight; the fish have forgotten my face."],
  ["Is that a Party song?","It's the song. The whole world sings it. Well, half of it. The good half."]],
 "Mind the rope. It bites."],
2:["The Valborg won't start. Third time this week. She's older than me and twice as stubborn.",[
  ["What's wrong with her?","The gearbox, Tom thinks. Tom always thinks it's the gearbox. Tom's usually right."],
  ["Can you borrow a boat?","Tom's. Everybody borrows Tom's. There's a queue. I'm fourth, behind a man who wants to see the palace from the sea."],
  ["Why Valborg?","My grandmother. She was stubborn too, and she wouldn't start in the mornings either."]],
 "If you know anything about gearboxes, I'm all ears."],
3:["(Mike is wrapping something small in newspaper, and hides it quickly.) Nothing! It's nothing. A present. For... my mother.",[
  ["Your mother lives here?","In... Kingsgrad. Yes. She likes... (he looks at the parcel) ...pocket knives. She's very practical."],
  ["That's sweet of you.","I'm a sweet man. Ask anyone. Don't actually ask anyone."],
  ["You're blushing.","It's the wind! Saltwick wind. Very red-making."]],
 "Forget you saw that. It's a mother thing."],
4:["Anna wants me to sing the Internationale on the twenty-eighth. In front of the whole island. In Russian.",[
  ["Do you speak Russian?","I speak 'comrade', 'vodka' and 'goal'. I've got three weeks to learn eight lines."],
  ["Will you do it?","Of course. Anna asks, I do it. That's what a Party man is. Even if my knees knock."],
  ["Can I hear it?","In Finnish, yes. (He sings a line, then stops.) In Russian, not till I stop sounding like a seal."]],
 "Vstavai... no. Wait. I'll get it."],
5:["Tom lent me the Gull for the morning. I caught more in her in two hours than I did in the Valborg all of August.",[
  ["Maybe the Gull's luckier.","Tom says boats pick up their owner's luck. Tom's never had a bad day in his life. It's sickening."],
  ["Will you fix the Valborg?","When I've the money for a gearbox. Next year, then. Or the year after. She'll wait. She's patient, unlike me."],
  ["What did you catch?","Cod, haddock, and a boot. Left foot. I'm keeping it in case the right one turns up."]],
 "Tell Tom I filled his tank. I did. Mostly."],
6:["Kate's asking why Lucia missed me on her rounds. I said she must have forgotten. Kate looked at me like a gutted herring.",[
  ["Why did Lucia skip you?","Luck? Charm? A clerical error? (He looks out to sea.) I don't know, and I'd stop asking, if I were you."],
  ["Do you know Lucia?","Everybody knows Lucia. She lives in my block. We nod on the stairs. That's all. Nodding."],
  ["Lucky you.","Lucky me. Yes. Very lucky. That's the word."]],
 "Don't tell Kate we talked about this. Please."],
7:["I went over to Frostholm and asked the Russian at the Hotel Aurora to teach me the words. He bought me four vodkas and taught me two lines.",[
  ["What's Yuri like?","Lovely man. Laughs a lot, knows everybody's name. He wanted to know who in Saltwick wasn't keen on the Party. Odd thing to ask a singer."],
  ["Two lines for four vodkas?","One and a half. I made up the other half on the way home. I think it's about a horse."],
  ["Is Frostholm nice?","All glass and lights. I felt like a herring in a jeweller's."]],
 "Vstavai, proklyatyem zakleimyonny... That's the line. I think."],
8:["I took the Gull out past the point. It smells like a garage out there. There's a rainbow on the water, and not the nice kind.",[
  ["Oil from the rig?","What else? Dario's men say 'routine maintenance'. Routine! There are dead gulls on the swell."],
  ["Will it reach the harbour?","If the wind turns. Then Kate will go out with a mop and murder somebody."],
  ["Fishing on a Sunday?","Fish don't keep Sundays. Nor does oil, it seems."]],
 "If you see Dario, tell him a fisherman sends his regards. Rude ones."],
9:["Helen asked me to taste her first go at a people's cola. I've tasted engine oil that was friendlier.",[
  ["What did it taste of?","Liquorice and regret. I told her it had potential. She's a union leader; she knows when you're lying."],
  ["Do you drink Kelp Cola?","Not me. But... somebody I know drinks it by the crate. Somebody. Not a Party member. Obviously."],
  ["What flavour would you want?","Something with fire in it, like the old songs. Something that makes you stand up."]],
 "If Helen asks, I said it was lovely."],
10:["Funeral day. The palace wanted singers. Not me. I'm not their sort of singer.",[
  ["Who'll sing, then?","The monks. They sing in Latin and look at the floor. I reckon the old king would have liked a sea shanty."],
  ["Would you sing for the king?","For him, the man? Maybe. He fished as a lad, they say. For the crown, never."],
  ["Will you go to the funeral?","I'll be on the water, singing to him from here. Quietly. Don't tell Anna."]],
 "A shanty for the old man. Then back to work."],
11:["(Mike is counting coins in his palm.) How much is a good scarf in Kingsgrad? Not for me. A woman's scarf. Cashmere. For my... cousin.",[
  ["Your cousin?","In... Frostholm. Yes. She feels the cold. Very much. All my cousins do."],
  ["Try Grace, the tailor.","She'd ask questions. Tailors always do. 'What colour are her eyes?' That sort of thing."],
  ["Last week it was your mother.",["(He goes very red.) She's... they're... it's a big family. Look, can we talk about cod?",[
    ["Cod it is.","Thank you. The cod are thin this year. It's the rig. There. Cod."],
    ["Who is she, Mike?","Nobody. Somebody. (He sighs.) Somebody who'd be in a lot of trouble if people knew. So would I. That's all you're getting."]]]]],
 "Cod. We talked about cod."],
12:["Gunnar says a gearbox came into the warehouse, not on any manifest, about the size of mine. He says if I don't ask, he won't tell.",[
  ["A stolen gearbox?","Not stolen. Lost. Gunnar finds a lot of lost things. I'm a Party man; I don't do that sort of thing. (Pause.) What would it cost?"],
  ["How's the Russian coming?","Four lines! I sang them to the gulls. Two of them left."],
  ["Will you buy it?","Anna would skin me. I'll think about it. In a cupboard, where she can't hear me thinking."]],
 "You didn't hear any of that."],
13:["Kelp Cola's handing out free bottles in Kingsgrad. I picked up a whole crate. For... a friend. Who likes it.",[
  ["Isn't that against the Party line?","The Party line's about buying it. Free isn't buying. I'm very clear on the theory."],
  ["Kate's posting hers back to Phil.","Kate's a better communist than me. Don't tell her I said so, or she'll make me post mine back too."],
  ["Which friend?","A friend with a sweet tooth. You don't know her. Him. Them."]],
 "If Kate asks, I've never touched a Kelp Cola in my life."],
14:["The big day. I've got my good jumper on. Anna says no singing. I said 'what, at all?' She said 'Mike.' Just my name. That's enough, with Anna.",[
  ["Will you obey?","I'll hum. Humming isn't singing. Humming's a grey area in the Party rules."],
  ["Who are you hoping to see?","Nobody in particular. (He glances towards the apartment block.) Nobody."],
  ["What do you expect?","Every faction on the island in one place, all smiling. I'd rather be in a storm. At least you know where the wind's coming from."]],
 "If you hear humming, it's not me."],
15:["Six lines in Russian! I sang them in the bath and the neighbours knocked on the wall. In time with the music. I count that as support.",[
  ["Which neighbour knocked?","The one next door. (He pauses.) The other side. Not the one you'd think. There are two sides. Never mind."],
  ["What about the royals squabbling?","Crown before cross, cross before crown. I'd say fish before both. Nobody asks a fisherman."],
  ["Six lines is progress.","Two to go, and they're the hard ones, with all the 'zh's in. I sound like a kettle."]],
 "Zh... zhh. No. Again tomorrow."],
16:["Debate day! Anna against Erik. I've been told to clap at the right bits and not to sing. I've written CLAP on my hand.",[
  ["What are the right bits?","Anything about fishermen, boats or bread. If Erik says 'vote' too often, I'm allowed to cough."],
  ["Do you like Erik?","He's all right. He came to Saltwick once and helped mend a net. Badly. But he tried."],
  ["Will you sing afterwards?","Whatever happens, I'm singing afterwards. Nobody can stop a man singing on the walk home."]],
 "Wish me luck. My hand's sweating on the CLAP."],
17:["Did you feel the ground go? I was halfway up the stairs at the block. I grabbed the nearest person. She grabbed me back. Then we both let go very quickly.",[
  ["Who was it?","Just... a neighbour. On the stairs. People are on stairs. It's a staircase."],
  ["Was anyone hurt?","No. Glasses broke on the third floor, and Kaisa's records came off the shelf. Nothing worse."],
  ["How was the debate?","I clapped at the right bits and coughed at a few. Then I sang all the way home, and nobody stopped me."]],
 "Stairs. Very dangerous in a tremor. Remember that."],
18:["Kate's onto me. She's asked three times where I go after work. I said choir practice. There hasn't been choir practice since 1979.",[
  ["Where do you go?","Home. The long way. Very long. Past the... look, it's walking. Walking's allowed."],
  ["Why not tell her the truth?",["Because the truth's the kind that goes into Kate's ledger, then Anna's office, then... somebody else's office.",[
    ["Somebody else's?","People who don't like Party men coming round. Let's leave it there. Please."],
    ["Is it worth the trouble?","(He smiles, properly, for the first time.) Every bit of it."]]]],
  ["So there's no choir?","There is now. I've started one. Of one. Me. Wednesdays."]],
 "If Kate asks, I'm at choir."],
19:["Recruiting day. Anna's given me Tom. Tom! He'll smile, lend me his boat and sign nothing. He does it every year.",[
  ["Why won't Tom join?","He says he likes everybody, and the Party wants you to dislike some people. He's not wrong, which is annoying."],
  ["Who else will you try?","Gunnar. He'll ask what's in it for him, I'll say soup on Thursdays, and he'll say he knows what's in the soup crates."],
  ["Will you sign anybody?","One. A man at Ray's who's been agreeing with me since 1975. He just never had a pen."]],
 "Soup on Thursdays. Tell your friends."],
20:["Nora from the Gazette asked me about the Russian. I said he'd taught me a song. She wrote 'song' and underlined it twice.",[
  ["What else did you tell her?","That he's generous, he laughs a lot, and he asks a lot of questions for a man who sells wood. Oh. Should I not have said that?"],
  ["Does he ask a lot?","Who's in the Party, who isn't, who's thinking about it. I thought he was just friendly. Russians are very friendly."],
  ["Is the song finished?","Seven lines. One to go. It's the one about the last fight. Fitting."]],
 "Don't put the bit about the questions in your paper. Please."],
21:["(Mike's face is grey with ash.) I was out in the Gull when it went. The whole north turned red. I've never rowed so fast, and I had an engine.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","No. Shaking. The sea went flat, then rough, then flat again. It was like the island took a breath."],
  ["Is everyone safe in the block?","Helen's counted everybody. I ran up every floor to check. Every floor. Every flat. Especially... every flat."],
  ["Did you sing to calm down?","I sang the whole thing, in Russian, all eight lines, without thinking. Perfect. Terror helps."]],
 "Get indoors. The sky's still coming down."],
22:["The Valborg's full of ash. Every crack, every seam. Even her stubbornness has gone grey.",[
  ["Can she be cleaned?","With a brush and three days. I've got the brush. I'll steal the days from somewhere."],
  ["How's the harbour?","Dead fish along the tide line. Sam's out there picking them up one by one, like he's apologising to them."],
  ["Did anyone help you?","Somebody brought me a flask of coffee and didn't say a word. Best help there is. (He doesn't say who.)"]],
 "Mind the ash on the jetty. It's like walking on flour."],
23:["Dario's rig's pumping again, through all this. The men at Ray's say somebody ought to do something about it.",[
  ["Do what?","Something. That's all anybody says: 'something'. Nobody's ever said what. Me included."],
  ["Would you?","I'm a singer and a fisherman. I'd sing at it. Very loudly. It might work."],
  ["How's the Valborg?","Clean, and she started first time. First time in a year. Maybe she likes volcanoes."]],
 "If the rig stops, you never heard me say 'something'."],
24:["(Mike is wearing a new blue shirt, with the creases still in it.) Don't. Don't say anything about the shirt.",[
  ["Nice shirt.","I said don't. (He tugs at the collar.) It's for going out. People go out. Even in ash."],
  ["Going out where?","The Midnight Note, in Frostholm. Jazz. I don't like jazz. Somebody does. I'm told it's the done thing."],
  ["Kate will notice.","Kate's noticed. She asked if I'd joined the Democrats. Blue! I'd never thought of that."]],
 "Not a word about the shirt."],
25:["Kate asked me straight out if I'm in love. I sang the whole Internationale at her. She said that was a confession.",[
  ["Was it?","It was a song. A song can't be a confession. Can it? Oh, God. It can."],
  ["Well, are you in love?",["(He looks at the water a long while.) Yes. And if anybody in this harbour finds out who with, I'll have to move to Finland.",[
    ["Is it that bad?","Worse. She's from the wrong side. Very wrong. And to her lot, so am I."],
    ["I won't tell.","You're a reporter. You'll tell the whole of London. But London doesn't know me, so that's all right."]]]],
  ["Kate always finds out.","Not this time. It's the one secret on the island Kate can't get her teeth into. I hope."]],
 "Sing something, if anybody asks what we talked about."],
26:["Rehearsal for the twenty-eighth. All eight lines, in Russian. Anna listened with her eyes shut and said 'good'. Just 'good'. I nearly cried.",[
  ["'Good' from Anna is a lot.","It's the most she's said to me since I joined. She said 'hm' in 1979. That was close."],
  ["What's happening on the twenty-eighth?","Something big. That's all she's told me. I'm to sing when I see red. Those are my orders."],
  ["Will you be nervous?","Terrified. But you don't think about yourself when you sing that song. You think about everybody who sang it before you."]],
 "When you see red, listen for me."],
27:["The Party's taking everybody to the cinema in Kingsgrad for the Soviet film. I've said I'll go. I've also said I'll go to STORMWING with somebody else, the same day.",[
  ["Two films in one day?","One at three, one at eight. Tractors, then spaceships. I'll need a lie-down in between."],
  ["With whom?","A friend. Who likes spaceships, and robots, and very much doesn't like tractors."],
  ["Does Otto know?","Otto knows everything that happens in his cinema. I'm hoping he's discreet. Cinema men usually are."]],
 "If you see me in the back row at eight, you didn't."],
28:["(Mike is pacing the jetty, mouthing words.) The twenty-eighth. Eight lines of Russian, and my knees are knocking like a loose gearbox.",[
  ["You'll be wonderful.","That's kind. Wrong, possibly, but kind. Thank you."],
  ["Where will you sing?","Wherever Anna points. She hasn't told me yet. She's a great one for not telling you yet."],
  ["Does your friend know you're singing?","She'll hear about it. The whole island will. I hope she doesn't mind. I hope... well. Hope's free."]],
 "Wish me luck. Vstavai..."],
29:["My throat's raw. I've sung more this month than in the last five years. And everybody's being polite to each other today, Erik's lot and ours. It's unnerving.",[
  ["Polite how?","Erik's people bought us a round at Ray's, and we bought one back. Nobody hit anybody. Saltwick's in shock."],
  ["Will you sing tomorrow?","Whatever happens tomorrow, I'll sing. A happy one or a sad one. I've got both ready."],
  ["And your secret friend?","Still a secret. Still a friend. More than a friend. (He grins.) I've decided I don't mind any more."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. The last one."],
30:["The last day. Whatever happens, I've decided something. After today, I'm going to stop hiding things. One thing in particular.",[
  ["What will you stop hiding?","You'll see. Or you won't, if she says no. (He laughs.) She won't say no. She'll say 'are you mad?', and then yes."],
  ["And the Party?","The Party will shout. Then Anna will look at me and say 'Mike', just my name, and that'll be that."],
  ["Sing me something.","(He sings a line, softly, in Russian, perfectly.) There. A whole month of practice. Don't tell anybody it was for you."]],
 "Whatever happens, comrade. Whatever happens."],
};
// Nico, Syndicate bookie on the streets of Frostholm (Crime Syndicate). Threads: his book on everything, and the volcano bet he
// gave Raili at fifty to one; his belief that he can read anyone's bluff (he can't, and his left ear goes red); getting ready for
// the poker tournament on the 27th; his rivalry with Paolo in Eastbrook; slow to pay out, always.
DAILY['Nico']={
7:["(Nico is under a lamp post with a little notebook, ticking things off fast.) The road's open, the oil men are back in their towers, and Frostholm's betting again. You're the British reporter. Want a price on anything?",[
  ["What can I bet on?","Anything. Will it snow by noon? Six to four. Will Dario shout at a waiter today? Odds-on. Will the reporter put Frostholm on the front page? Twenty to one, against."],
  ["Who are you, exactly?","Nico. I'm a numbers man. I give people the chance to be right about something, for a small fee. It's a public service."],
  ["Twenty to one against? Insulting.","Prove me wrong. Ten dollars on it? No? (He grins.) I knew you'd say no. I read people."]],
 "Come back with money. Or don't. I'll know which."],
8:["I can read anyone. (Nico squints at you.) You've had coffee, you slept badly, and you've just come from somewhere cold. Am I right?",[
  ["Everyone on this island's come from somewhere cold.","(He waves his pencil.) Details. I'm right on the big things. It's why I win at poker. Mostly."],
  ["You're wrong. I slept beautifully.","Then you're bluffing. I can tell, your left eye does a thing. (It doesn't.) See? Nico knows."],
  ["What's the talk about the oil rig?","A leak, they say. The Petro Tower's emptied out to the harbour. I'm giving three to one that Dario blames the fish. Nobody's taken it."]],
 "Watch your left eye. I'm watching it too."],
9:["(Nico is arguing with a thin woman by the phone box.) No, Raili, you can't have the money yet, the bet's not finished. (He turns to you.) Raili bet ten dollars the Party's new cola tastes of turnips.",[
  ["Does it?","Nobody's tasted it yet. That's the point. She wants paying for a turnip that hasn't happened. That's not betting, that's prophecy."],
  ["Who's Raili?","Out of work, lives in the Skyline like me, sits at the Midnight Note every evening with Arto. She bets small and wins too often. I don't like it."],
  ["You're slow to pay out, aren't you?","I'm careful to pay out. There's a difference. Careful takes longer. Ask anyone."]],
 "If Raili asks, you didn't see me."],
10:["The king's funeral. (Nico has a black band round his hat.) I took a book on how long the bells would ring. Arto said forty minutes. It was thirty-eight. He wants paying. I'm thinking about it.",[
  ["Thirty-eight's not forty.","Exactly. Arto says it's close enough. In my business there's no close enough. There's right and there's Arto."],
  ["Did you go to the funeral?","Me? In the palace, with the gentry? I listened from the boulevard. Frostholm's quiet today. Half the towers went to Kingsgrad in their best fur."],
  ["Betting on a funeral? Really?","Everyone bets on something at a funeral. Who'll cry, who'll come, who'll inherit. I'm the only one who writes it down."]],
 "Take your hat off when the bells go. Respect."],
11:["(Nico is counting notes very quickly, and dropping some.) My book for the week. Four hundred and twelve dollars. Paolo in Eastbrook will have, what, two hundred? Village bookie. Takes bets on the mill whistle.",[
  ["You and Paolo don't get on?","We came over on the same boat. He was seasick in my hat. I've never forgiven him. He's never let me forget I wore it after."],
  ["Who's the better bookie?","Me. Look at the numbers. He'll say his people come back and mine don't. I say, why would I want people coming back? I want new people."],
  ["You dropped a ten-dollar note.","(He snatches it up.) Good eyes. You'd make a good dealer. Or a good thief."]],
 "Don't mention the hat to anyone."],
12:["(Nico is reading the Gazette and laughing.) Ruby in Eastbrook's sold some rare record to a stranger. I've a book on who. Dario's favourite. Dario wants everything, and buys half of it.",[
  ["What's so special about the record?","Old jazz, recorded live at the Hotel Aurora, here in Frostholm, years before I came. Greta at the hotel says her father was on the door that night. Everyone's father was, to hear Frostholm tell it."],
  ["Who's the outsider?","A monk. Fifty to one. A monk who likes jazz. I'll lose nothing on that one."],
  ["Do you ever bet yourself?","Every Saturday, at Rosaria's back table. Poker. It's not betting. It's skill. I read faces."]],
 "Go and find out who bought it. Then tell me first."],
13:["(Nico is holding a free can of Kelp Cola and frowning at it.) They're handing these out in Kingsgrad like leaflets. I've got a book on how many they give away by teatime. Two thousand, I said. Vince at the casino says five.",[
  ["How will you count them?","Ville at the newsstand's counting the empty cans in his bin. It's a very scientific operation. Ville's very bored."],
  ["Any other bets today?","Whether anyone on the island ever finds the Kelp Cola recipe. A hundred to one. Phil's family keeps it somewhere nobody's ever looked. (He taps his nose.) People keep trying."],
  ["Is the cola any good?","It's sweet, it's fizzy, it's free. Three good things. If it were also a winning ticket, I'd marry it."]],
 "Take a can. I've got six. They're everywhere."],
14:["(Nico has a board propped against the phone box, chalked with odds.) The big day! I've got prices on everything. First speaker to cry, longest clap, who walks out. The whole island in one place: it's the Derby.",[
  ["What's the favourite?","That nobody agrees on anything. Odds-on. I'd lay it at a hundred to one on, and still not lose."],
  ["Isn't this a solemn occasion?","Solemn for the island. For a bookie it's the biggest day since the king's birthday. I'll be solemn tomorrow, with my takings."],
  ["Give me odds on myself.","That the reporter writes it down wrong? Evens. That the reporter writes it down right? (He studies you.) Seven to two. You're careful. I can read it."]],
 "Go on, go and watch. I'll be taking bets at the back."],
15:["The big day went well. For me. (Nico pats a fat pocket.) Every price came in the way I set it. I read that crowd like a menu. Paolo rang to ask how I did. I told him. He hung up.",[
  ["Paying out to the winners, then?","In good time. In good time. There's a process. The process is: they come and find me, and I'm busy."],
  ["What's the next big bet?","The monastery and the palace, arguing over which brother wears the crown. Leopold's the favourite. The monks are praying for long odds."],
  ["You've got chalk on your nose.","(He rubs it, making it worse.) Occupational hazard. A bookie with clean hands isn't working."]],
 "Don't tell Paolo I've got chalk on my nose."],
16:["(Nico is leaning in the doorway of the arcade, playing with a coin.) Debate day. Party against Democrats, in public. I've given evens. Toni in there says the Party will win because they shout. Shouting's not winning. Shouting's volume.",[
  ["Who would you vote for?","I'm a bookie. I'd vote for whoever gives me the best price. Neither of them would let me exist. Maybe I'll stay home."],
  ["Do you play the arcade games?","Toni lets me on the pinball for free, because I tell him which machine's paying. Pinball's not luck. Pinball's a bad man with a good flipper."],
  ["Can you really read faces?","(He looks at you very hard.) You're about to say something sarcastic. Am I right? (You weren't.) Close enough."]],
 "Practise your poker face. You'll need it with me."],
17:["(The street lamp over Nico sways, and he grabs the post.) Whoa! The ground! The whole boulevard just moved like a rowing boat. And Raili, at the phone box, just shouted 'I want a bet!'",[
  ["What did Raili want to bet?","Ten dollars that the volcano goes up before the month's out. It's been asleep three hundred years. I gave her fifty to one. Free money."],
  ["What's the news from the oil company?","An important man there's gone missing, they say. Dario's people are all over the boulevard asking questions. I've a book on whether he turns up. I'm keeping it very quiet."],
  ["Fifty to one on a volcano? Risky.","Risky? It's a mountain. Mountains sit. That's what they do. It's like betting a cat will sing."]],
 "If you feel it again, hold the lamp post. It's sturdy."],
18:["(Nico is pale, and keeps glancing north, where there's a thread of steam over the hills.) There's smoke. Steam. Whatever it is, it's coming out of the top of the mountain, and Raili's been walking past me all morning, smiling.",[
  ["Fifty to one doesn't look so clever.","Steam isn't an eruption. A kettle steams. Does a kettle erupt? It does not. The bet stands. I'm calm."],
  ["How much would you owe her?","(He swallows.) Five hundred dollars. Which is more than my book for a week. Which is fine. Which won't happen. Fine."],
  ["Heard any gossip from the monastery?","The monks have lost a holy thing, a relic. The abbot's searching every cupboard. I've given three to one it turns up under a monk's bed."]],
 "Stop looking at the mountain. It doesn't like being stared at."],
19:["Rita sings at the Midnight Note on Thursdays, and I always go. (Nico straightens his tie.) Sid says somebody grand might be coming in to hear her, in disguise. A disguise! In Frostholm! I'll spot him in a second.",[
  ["How will you spot a disguise?","Everyone in a disguise looks at the door too much. And they tip too much. The worse the moustache, the bigger the tip."],
  ["Are you in love with Rita?","Half the island's in love with Rita. I'm in the other half: I'm just in love with the takings on the nights she sings."],
  ["What about the volcano bet?","Steam, still. The ground shook twice more in the night. I've moved my bed away from the window. For the draught."]],
 "Come to the club on a Thursday. Watch the door with me."],
20:["(Nico has his notebook open and is doing sums, muttering.) Five hundred. Minus my week. Minus the rent. Minus Arto, who I still owe for the funeral bells. Minus my dignity. It's a big minus.",[
  ["Have you paid Arto yet?","Thirty-eight minutes is not forty. But Arto's been very patient, and sitting outside my door. I'll pay him Monday. Or the Monday after. There are many Mondays."],
  ["Has the volcano gone up?","Not yet. It's steaming like a sauna. Raili brought me a cup of coffee this morning, to 'keep my strength up'. That's cruelty."],
  ["What's the gossip from Kingsgrad?","The palace is in a state. The guards were up all hours, and the butler's been seen counting the silver with a magnifying glass. Somebody did something. I'll find out who."]],
 "If you've got five hundred dollars spare, I'd take it."],
21:["(Ash is falling on the boulevard, and Nico is standing perfectly still under it, grey from hat to shoes.) It's gone up. The mountain. It's actually gone up. (Raili walks past, waving.) She's waving at me.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I've lost five hundred dollars and I'm covered in volcano. I'm the least all right man in Frostholm. Nobody's hurt, though. Thank God for that."],
  ["Will you pay Raili?","(Long pause.) The bet said 'erupt'. This could be called 'venting'. There's a technical difference. A geologist might agree with me."],
  ["Get indoors, Nico.","Yes. Yes, good idea. (He doesn't move.) I just want to watch the money go up the mountain for a minute."]],
 "Go home. Shut the windows. Don't bet on anything."],
22:["(Nico is brushing ash off his notebook, page by page.) I looked it up. In the library. An eruption is 'the discharge of lava, ash or gas from a volcano'. There's lava in the north. I've no argument.",[
  ["So you'll pay Raili?","I'll pay her. I'm a man of my word. Slowly, but a man of my word. Fifty dollars a week, for ten weeks. She laughed for a very long time."],
  ["How's Frostholm after the eruption?","Grey and quiet. The oil men are working from candles. The arcade's shut, the casino's shut, the Midnight Note opened for anyone who wanted a drink and some company."],
  ["Why not just pay it all?","Because then I'd have nothing left for the tournament. On the twenty-seventh. Everyone who matters, at Rosaria's back table. I've been practising."]],
 "Brush yourself off before you come near my notebook."],
23:["(Nico is shuffling cards on top of the phone box.) Practice. Every day until the tournament. Shuffle, deal, read. Paolo says I've got a tell. I haven't got a tell. I've got a face like a wall.",[
  ["What tell does Paolo say you have?","He won't say. He says I'll find out when I lose. Which I won't. Because I have no tell. Which is why he won't say."],
  ["Who'll be at the tournament?","The Don, Carlo, people from the oil company, people from the palace, maybe. Anybody with money and nerve. I've got nerve. Money, I've got a payment plan for."],
  ["Deal me a hand.","(He deals two cards face down to you, and two to himself.) Look at yours. Don't let me see your face. (He stares.) You've got a pair. (You haven't.)"]],
 "Practise your poker face. I'm reading you anyway."],
24:["Arto's paid. (Nico looks hurt.) All of it. The funeral bells bet. I gave him forty dollars and he gave me a hug. In public! A bookie doesn't get hugged. It's bad for business.",[
  ["That's nice, Nico.","It's not nice, it's a precedent. Now everyone will want paying on time and hugging me. Raili's already looking."],
  ["What's the news in Frostholm?","People keep their voices down today. Something happened somewhere, something nobody's saying. In my experience, that's the loudest kind of news."],
  ["How's the payment plan with Raili?","First fifty dollars, paid. She put it on the bar at the Midnight Note and bought everyone a drink. Including me. With my own money. Twice."]],
 "If you see Raili, say Nico's being very good about it."],
25:["(Nico is studying a pack of cards with a magnifying glass.) Making sure Rosaria's cards for the tournament aren't marked. Gustavo sent them. Gustavo's cards wouldn't be marked. But I'm thorough.",[
  ["Thorough, or paranoid?","There's no difference at a card table. The paranoid man keeps his money. The trusting man buys everyone a drink with it."],
  ["Who's your biggest rival in the tournament?","The Don. He never looks at his cards and he never loses unless he wants to. I've played him six years. I've never once read him."],
  ["What's happening at the palace today?","Leopold's promising a crown for everyone, they say. I'd give anything for the odds on that. Prices on a crown? I'd be a rich man."]],
 "Don't touch my cards. You've got jam on your fingers."],
26:["Dario's buying the spa. (Nico taps his notebook.) I had a bet on that. Who'd buy the hot springs after the eruption: Dario at three to one. Vince at the casino gave me two to one. I've won! I've actually won!",[
  ["Will Vince pay up?","Vince is slower to pay out than me. I've met my match. It's like looking in a mirror."],
  ["Why would Dario want the spa?","Hot water, mud, rich people in towels. And the land. Everything Dario buys is really the land underneath. It's the oil in his blood."],
  ["Congratulations. First win in a while?","First win since the mountain. It's a sign. I'm on a streak. Tomorrow, the tournament. Streaks don't stop for anyone."]],
 "Get some sleep. I'm not. I'm practising."],
27:["(Nico is in his best suit, with a red carnation and slicked hair.) Tournament day. Rosaria's back room. I'm off to Kingsgrad. I've practised for a month. I've read every face on this island. Today, Nico wins.",[
  ["Good luck. Watch your ear.","My ear? What about my ear? (He touches his left ear, which is going red.) There's nothing wrong with my ear. Who's been talking?"],
  ["Who are you most worried about?","Nobody. Worry is a tell. (He sits on his hands.) Maybe the Don. Maybe whoever the Don's decided should win."],
  ["What's the prize?","Money, and a seat at the Don's table at the Club for a month. The money I'd spend in a week. The seat I'd sit on for a year."]],
 "Come and watch. I'll wave from the winner's chair."],
28:["(Nico is sitting on the steps of the Skyline Apartments, looking at his hands.) I won't talk about the tournament. Ask me anything else. Anything.",[
  ["How about the big marches today?","Red flags in Kingsgrad, a rally in Eastbrook, the oil company's office party, the royals with a state visit. Everyone on the island's shouting about something. I've got no book on any of it. I can't face it."],
  ["Was it your ear?","(He holds his ear.) I asked Rosaria. She said 'it goes red, Nico, everyone knows'. Everyone! For six years! Mario took two hundred dollars off that ear."],
  ["What's tomorrow?","The Republican vote. Carlo for us. I'll be there at the back, if I can show my face. Even my face with an ear on it."]],
 "Go on. Leave a man to grieve his ear."],
29:["(Nico is in Frostholm's boulevard, with a hat pulled down very low over both ears.) The Republican vote, at the party HQ. Carlo's in there now, smiling. I'm out here, keeping the book. No more ears showing.",[
  ["What odds on Carlo?","I'm not giving odds on Syndicate business. Gustavo says so. I'd give them if he didn't. They'd be honest. Honest-ish."],
  ["Who else is standing?","Harald from Kelp Cola, Magnus from the oil company. Both their desks are empty most days. Carlo sat at his every day. If sitting at a desk wins votes, it's his."],
  ["Nice hat.","It's for the cold. Only the cold. (A pause.) Is it very obviously hiding my ears?"]],
 "Go in and watch the count. I'll stay out here, hatted."],
30:["(Nico has his notebook open on the last page.) The last day of the month. The last page of the book. Tomorrow, the island's settled, and every bet in Frostholm pays out. Or doesn't. I'm sweating, and it's minus seven.",[
  ["What's your biggest bet on today?","Who runs this island tomorrow. I've not set a price. For the first time in my life, I don't know. It's a horrible feeling. It must be how everyone else feels."],
  ["Will you pay everyone on time?","I'll pay everyone. I'm a changed man. I'll pay them in good time. (He thinks.) A changed man, with a process."],
  ["Has it been a good month?","I lost five hundred dollars to a mountain and found out about my ear. And I was hugged in public. It's been educational."]],
 "Come back tomorrow. I'll have a new book."],
};
// Nils, owner of the electronics store in Frostholm (Republican Party). Threads: his certainty that someone is listening (the
// clicks on his telephone, the numbers station on the shortwave), his wall of televisions, the electronic vote counter Margit
// won't use, and the seismograph he builds from a record player once the ground shakes.
DAILY['Nils']={
7:["(Nils looks up from a radio with its back off.) Ah. A customer. Or a reporter, from the notebook. Mind the cables, they're all live.",[
  ["Both, maybe. Nice shop.","Nicest on the island. Only one on the island, but still. Nils. Radios, batteries, film, and anything with a valve in it, mended."],
  ["What's the radio's trouble?","A loose valve and a dead mouse. The mouse was the bigger problem. Belongs to Greta at the hotel. The radio, not the mouse."],
  ["Is the government listening to us?",["(He lowers his voice.) Funny you should ask. My telephone clicks. Every time I pick it up: click. Who do you think that is?",[
    ["The telephone company?","That's what they want you to think. (He taps the side of his nose.) I've put a tea cosy over it. Muffles them."],
    ["Probably nobody.","Nobody doesn't click. Somebody clicks. I'll find out who."]]]]],
 "Mind the cables on your way out. And don't say anything near the telephone."],
8:["(A wall of televisions all show the same grey snow.) Nothing on. Sunday. The Swedish station doesn't come in till the afternoon, if the wind's right.",[
  ["Who buys televisions on an island with no station?","Everyone in Frostholm. They watch the Swedish one. Half the island can tell you the weather in Stockholm and not in Saltwick."],
  ["Heard about the oil rig?","Ruth from Diversified Petroleum lives in my building. Came down the stairs like a fire alarm this morning. Something burst. She wouldn't say what. Oil company people never do."],
  ["Did you hear the sermon?","On the radio. Lovely signal from the tower: Hekki keeps it beautifully. Odd fellow, Hekki. Lives in a shack. Very quiet. I like him."]],
 "If you've a television that won't tune, bring it in. I'll have a look."],
9:["(Nils is hunched over a shortwave set with headphones on. He holds up a finger.) Shh. Listen.",[
  ["What is it?",["(He hands you one earphone. A woman's voice reads numbers, slowly, in a language you can't place.) Every night at nine, and some mornings. Numbers. Just numbers.",[
    ["Who's she reading them to?","Somebody who knows what they mean. Spies, I say. Somebody on this island has a little book and writes them down."],
    ["Maybe it's a lottery.","A lottery with no prizes that nobody's ever won. That's the government, all right. Which government, I couldn't tell you."]]]],
  ["Is this legal?","Listening's legal. It's talking that gets you into trouble. That's what I keep telling the telephone."],
  ["You should be on the radio yourself.","Kaisa asked me once. Sit in her hut and talk about valves. I said, Kaisa, everyone would be listening. That's the problem."]],
 "Nine o'clock. Tune in if you've a set. Then tell me I'm imagining it."],
10:["(Every television on the wall shows the same picture: black and white, a chapel, very small figures.) The funeral. The Swedes are showing it. Pull up a box.",[
  ["Is that the Royal Chapel?","Kingsgrad, live, from a camera on the palace wall. Swedish television sent a van. I helped them with a cable this morning. Nobody thanked me."],
  ["Who's that by the coffin?","Leopold, standing very straight. And that's Augustus, praying, and Princess Daphne, who looks like she'd rather be anywhere else. Can't blame her."],
  ["Why aren't you there?","Somebody has to keep the sets tuned. And this way I see it ten times, from ten angles. Well. One angle, ten times."]],
 "God save whoever's next. They'll need it."],
11:["(Nils is fitting a third lock to the back door.) Frostholm's full of strangers since the roads opened. You can't be too careful. I've a lot of capacitors in here.",[
  ["Who'd want capacitors?","You'd be surprised. A capacitor's a little tin of lightning. Put one in the wrong machine and the machine stops being a machine."],
  ["Isn't three locks a lot?","Three locks is careful. Four locks is mad. I'm careful."],
  ["Are you worried about thieves or the government?","Both. The thieves are easier. You can see a thief."]],
 "Pull the door to. It sticks. That's on purpose."],
12:["Margit came in. You know Margit, the party secretary? Running the leadership vote? I showed her my vote counter.",[
  ["What's a vote counter?","A box of my own making. You press a button for your man, it clicks, a number goes up. No pencils, no counting, no mistakes. Lovely bit of work."],
  ["What did she say?","She said, 'Nils, how do I know it counts right?' I said, 'Because I made it.' She said, 'That's what I'm afraid of.' Pencils, she wants. Pencils!"],
  ["I'm with Margit.","Of course you are. Everyone is. Nobody trusts a machine till it's saved their life. Then they won't leave it alone."]],
 "Pencils. In this day and age."],
13:["(Nils is holding a bottle of Kelp Cola up to the light.) Free, they're handing them out. Why? What's in it?",[
  ["Cola, I'd think.","And something in the cap? A little transmitter? (He shakes it.) Nothing rattles. Doesn't mean nothing's there."],
  ["Are you going to drink it?","I'm going to take the cap apart first. If it's clean, I'll drink it. If it isn't, I'll ring the papers. Henrik at the Gazette loves a story."],
  ["Who'd bug a cola?","Who wouldn't? Every household on the island, a bottle on the table. Best listening post in the world. Harald's no fool."]],
 "If your cap's got a wire in it, bring it to me."],
14:["(Nils is packing a tape recorder into a bag.) The big day. Everyone in one place. I'm going to record the lot.",[
  ["Why record it?","Because tomorrow everyone'll say they said something they didn't. I'll have the tape. Tapes don't lie. People do."],
  ["Are you hoping for anything in particular?","I'm hoping nobody signs anything. Every time a lot of people stand in a field, somebody signs something. Then you're stuck with it."],
  ["Can I borrow the recorder?","Certainly not. (He hugs the bag.) It's a Nagra. It's Swiss. It's mine."]],
 "Big day. Remember to listen as well as watch."],
15:["(Nils is playing back a tape, frowning.) Listen to this bit. There's a click. Right there. In the middle of the speeches.",[
  ["What's the click?","Someone else recording. Two machines near each other: you get a click. Someone was standing near me with their own tape. I'd like to know who."],
  ["Maybe it was the Gazette.","Nora doesn't use a recorder, she uses a pencil and a good memory. No. This was a good machine. Better than mine. (He looks hurt.) Much better than mine."],
  ["People say the Church and palace are arguing.","Of course they are. One wants the crown on top, one wants the cross. And they'll both want my televisions for their speeches. I'll rent them to both."]],
 "Someone on this island has a better tape recorder than me. I'll not sleep."],
16:["The debate's on every radio in the shop. Communists and Democrats. Listen to the static when Anna speaks.",[
  ["What's wrong with the static?","Nothing's wrong with it. It's different. When Anna's at the microphone, it goes up. When Erik is, it goes down. Who's tuning it?"],
  ["You think someone's rigging the debate?","I think someone's listening to it from very close. And not on a set from my shop. Russian set, maybe. Heavier hum."],
  ["Who are you rooting for?","Neither. I'm a Republican. I'd rather have neither of them and a decent battery."]],
 "Listen to the static. It's never just static."],
17:["(The televisions shiver on their shelves.) There it goes again. Third time since lunch. Hold that set, would you?",[
  ["Was that an earthquake?","A tremor. Little one. I've had a cup of tea walk off the counter twice. I'm going to measure it."],
  ["How do you measure an earthquake?","A seismograph. You need a needle, a drum of paper and something heavy that doesn't move. I've got an old record player. I'll have one by tonight."],
  ["Heard Dario's missing?","Ruth's been up and down the stairs all day, white as a sheet. Somebody's been calling all the hotels. Nobody's answering at his villa. That's all I know."]],
 "If anything falls off the shelf, leave it. I'll know which way it fell."],
18:["(A record player on the counter has a pen taped to its arm, scratching a wavy line on a turning roll of paper.) My seismograph. Look at that.",[
  ["What's it telling you?","Every hour, a little squiggle. And the squiggles are getting bigger. (He taps the paper.) Something's happening under the north."],
  ["Isn't that a record player?","It was a record player. Now it's science. I've told Birgit at Nordmart. She told me to stop blocking the aisle."],
  ["Should you tell someone official?","I rang the Civil Service Bureau. Bertil said there's a form. There's always a form. The volcano's not going to fill in a form."]],
 "Don't lean on the counter. You'll make an earthquake of your own."],
19:["(The paper roll is covered in scribble.) Bigger. Every day bigger. I took it up to the Radio Tower to show Hekki.",[
  ["What did Hekki say?","He looked at it for a long time and said 'huh'. Hekki says 'huh' a lot. Then he asked if I could make one for him. That's a compliment from Hekki."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","I think the mountain's going to do something. I don't know when. Neither does the government, I'll bet, and they're the ones listening."],
  ["Are you scared?","Frightened? (He thinks.) A bit. Mostly I want to see it on paper. That's the scientist in me. The rest of me wants to go to Stockholm."]],
 "If you're up north, look at the steam. Then come and tell my machine."],
20:["(Nils is selling torches as fast as he can hand them over.) Everyone wants a torch. Everyone wants batteries. I've never been so popular.",[
  ["What does the seismograph say?","It's gone off the paper. I had to put a bigger roll on. Even Birgit stopped in to look. She said, 'Is that bad?' I said yes. She bought two torches."],
  ["Who's that man at the Hotel Aurora?","The one in the grey coat? Upstairs, never comes out? I've seen his light on at three in the morning. And his aerial. A man doesn't need an aerial like that to listen to the Swedish weather."],
  ["Could I buy a torch?","Get in the queue. (He grins.) I'm joking. Nobody's queueing. They just look like they are."]],
 "Batteries in the torch, torch by the bed. Tonight of all nights."],
21:["(The shop is dim, the windows grey with ash. Nils has a radio pressed to his ear.) It's gone. The mountain. The pen jumped off the paper altogether.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I hear. Kaisa's on the radio saying stay indoors, keep the windows shut. Lava's come down the north side, into the hills. Not near the farms, thank God."],
  ["What did the seismograph do?","Drew a line straight up and then fell off the table. Best day of its life. (He picks up the pen.) I'll keep that roll forever."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm shaking. Not from the ground. I've wanted to see something like this my whole life. I didn't think it would be this frightening."]],
 "Keep the windows shut. That ash gets into everything with a valve."],
22:["(Nils is cleaning ash out of a television with a paintbrush.) Every set in Frostholm's full of grit. I've forty in the back. I'm going to be rich and very tired.",[
  ["Can you fix them all?","Give me a month and a lot of coffee. Ash is the worst. It conducts. You turn it on, it crackles like bacon."],
  ["Is the ground still moving?","Little aftershocks. The seismograph's back on the table. It's drawing much smaller squiggles. Tired, like the rest of us."],
  ["Did anyone listen in during the eruption?","(He looks at you.) Funny question. The numbers lady was on, nine o'clock, same as ever. The world ends and she's still reading numbers. That's dedication."]],
 "Bring me anything with grit in it. I'll have it crackling again by Friday."],
23:["Margit's been in again. She's having second thoughts about the counting. About the people who'll be standing round her while she counts.",[
  ["What's she worried about?","The candidates' friends. Harald's men in suits, Magnus's men in nicer suits, and Carlo's men who don't need suits. She wants witnesses. Honest ones."],
  ["Did she change her mind about your counter?","No. Pencils, still. But she asked if I'd stand beside the ballot box and watch. Me! (He straightens up.) I said yes."],
  ["Will you vote?","Of course. I'm a member. I'll vote and then I'll watch the box like a hawk. I'll bring my tape recorder."]],
 "A witness. Me. I'll need a clean shirt."],
24:["(Nils has the telephone in pieces on the counter.) I've had enough. I'm finding the click.",[
  ["Have you found it?","(He holds up a tiny bit of metal.) A loose contact. Here. It clicks when you lift the handset. (He looks disappointed.) It's just loose."],
  ["So nobody's listening?","Nobody's listening to my telephone. That's different from nobody's listening. They might be listening to everybody else's."],
  ["You look relieved.","I look let down. I wanted it to be spies. A man needs something to worry about. Now I'll have to worry about the volcano."]],
 "Tea cosy stays on, mind. Just in case."],
25:["Magnus lives on the fourth floor of my building. You know that? Up and down, all hours, with his briefcase.",[
  ["Do you get on with him?","He nods. I nod. He asked me once if my radios could reach the oil rig. I said, 'Why don't you just ring them?' He didn't like that."],
  ["Will you vote for him?","He's very sure of himself. Oil, oil, oil. I'd like a leader who'd heard of anything else. Like transistors."],
  ["Who would you vote for, then?","Whoever'd let me put a television in every house on the island. None of them's said it. Harald might. Cola sells best on television."]],
 "If you see Magnus on the stairs, don't mention radios. He's touchy."],
26:["(Nils is rewinding his tape from the fourteenth.) I listen to it every night. You hear more the third time.",[
  ["What do you hear?","Who clapped and who didn't. Who coughed when Anna spoke. Who laughed at the wrong bit. It's all there. You just have to listen."],
  ["Did you find out who the click was?","(He lowers his voice.) Someone near the front. A good machine, very quiet. I'd say Russian, from the hum. I'd say the man at the hotel."],
  ["Isn't that a bit far-fetched?","Everything's far-fetched till it isn't. A month ago, a volcano was far-fetched."]],
 "Listen, the third time. You'll hear it."],
27:["(The televisions show a Swedish film.) Otto would hate this. Swedes in a kitchen, not talking. Very artistic.",[
  ["Do you go to Otto's cinema?","Every Saturday. I sit in the back and watch the projector. Otto lets me fix it when it jams. I'd rather fix it than watch STORMWING again."],
  ["Is there a card game tonight?","At the pizzeria. All the big men. I wouldn't go near it. But if somebody wanted to record it, I could lend them something small. Hypothetically."],
  ["Any more aftershocks?","Little ones. The seismograph's nearly quiet. I'll miss it. (He pats the record player.) Might play a record on it again."]],
 "Swedish films. Nobody says anything for an hour and then someone's sad."],
28:["(Nils has every radio in the shop on a different station.) Everyone's broadcasting at once. Listen. Communists on one, cola on another, the palace on a third.",[
  ["What's going on?","Every faction's having its big day before the end. Rallies, flags, a foreign king. I'm listening to all of them. Somebody has to."],
  ["Who's got the best signal?","Kelp Cola. They've paid Kaisa for every advert slot going. You can't hear the end of the world for jingles."],
  ["Doesn't it give you a headache?","(He takes off his headphones.) What? (He puts them back on.) Sorry, I didn't hear you. That's the trouble."]],
 "Tomorrow, the vote. I'll be by the ballot box with a clean shirt."],
29:["(Nils is in a clean shirt with a tape recorder on his shoulder.) Today's the day. Margit's asked me to watch the box. I've been up since four.",[
  ["Who will you vote for?","I've decided. I won't say. A man's vote's like his telephone: private, and possibly listened to."],
  ["Is the recorder allowed?","Margit doesn't know about it yet. She'll find out when she sees it. I'll say it's for posterity."],
  ["Nervous?","Very. If anything goes wrong, I'll be on the tape saying 'that looks wrong'. And then everybody'll know I was right."]],
 "Off to the party HQ. Watch your back today: everyone's watching everyone."],
30:["(The shop is quiet. Nils has one radio on, very low.) Last day. Whatever happens to the island happens today. I want to hear it as it happens.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","An island where nobody needs to listen to anyone's telephone. Where they put a television in every house. And let me sell them."],
  ["Are you scared?","Of the island going to the wrong people? Yes. Of the Russians coming? Yes. Of the government listening? I'm used to that."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Fix forty televisions full of ash. Whoever's in charge, the sets still need mending. That's the good thing about valves. They don't care."]],
 "If anyone's listening, I hope they like what they hear."],
};
// Noora, lab technician in Kelp Cola's research lab under the HQ (Kelp Cola). Threads: her chemistry diploma by post from
// Helsinki (the exam on the 28th) and Saara, who marks her papers; the kelp in the lab tank, which she calls Sigrid; her records
// from Ruby's shop; and her flat in Saltwick's apartment block, with knocking pipes, Communist neighbours and Helen's cat, Lenin.
DAILY['Noora']={
1:["(Noora is on her knees beside a rattling radiator with a spanner.) Sorry, sorry! The pipes. They knock all night, as if somebody wants to come in.",[
  ["Aren't you at work?","The road to Kingsgrad's still snowed under. The lab's been without me for weeks. I hope somebody's feeding the kelp."],
  ["Who are your neighbours?","Helen from the Union's two doors down, and Anna lives upstairs. Half this block's Communist, and the other half's tired."],
  ["That's a lot of records.","Careful! That's my whole collection: forty-two records, all from Ruby's shop in Eastbrook, back when I could get to Eastbrook."]],
 "Mind the radiator on your way out. It bites."],
2:["I walked all the way to Kingsgrad to check on the lab, and they wouldn't let me past the gate. The HQ doesn't open till tomorrow.",[
  ["Who's feeding the kelp?","Nobody! It lives in a tank of seawater. It's probably fine. Kelp's survived worse than a month without me. (She looks worried.)"],
  ["What do you do in the lab?","Run the equipment, wash the glassware, mix the test batches for Saara. Mostly wash. I've washed a beaker so clean I could see my future in it."],
  ["What's in your future?","A chemistry diploma, by post, from Helsinki. Two more years of exams at the kitchen table. Then I'll be the one who doesn't wash up."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. I'll run the whole way."],
3:["(Noora is peering into a tank of seawater.) It's alive! The kelp's alive! Oh, Sigrid, I'm sorry I left you.",[
  ["You named the kelp?","Sigrid. All of it. She's one big family, really."],
  ["Where's Saara?","At her desk, eating lunch out of a jar. She's been here since dawn, with that look: the 'I need a new flavour' look."],
  ["How did you get in from Saltwick?","On the fish lorry, with Bob. I smell of herring and enthusiasm."]],
 "Say hello to Sigrid on your way past. She likes company."],
4:["I've mixed eleven test batches today: cloudberry, liquorice, pine. Saara tasted each one, wrote something down and poured it down the sink.",[
  ["What did she write?","'No.' Eleven times. In very neat handwriting."],
  ["What's the pine like?","Like licking a Christmas tree. In a good way. No. Not in a good way."],
  ["Eastbrook's open again.","Then on Saturday I'm going to Ruby's. I've been saving since July."]],
 "Don't drink anything off that bench. Some of it's floor cleaner."],
5:["Helen knocked last night with a leaflet and a pie. The pie was very good. The leaflet was about owning the means of production.",[
  ["Will you join the Party?","I own a beaker brush and forty-two records. I'm not sure I have any means to share."],
  ["Isn't Helen the Union leader too?","She asked me to join the Union. I said lab technicians aren't really workers. She looked at my hands and laughed."],
  ["What was in the pie?","Fish. Everything in Saltwick has fish in it. I'm half herring by now."]],
 "If Helen knocks for you, take the pie and run."],
6:["I couldn't wait for Saturday. I went to Ruby's after work: Ella Fitzgerald, the Cole Porter Song Book. A dollar off, because I knew who was on piano.",[
  ["Who was on piano?","Paul Smith. Ruby said hardly anybody gets that. I've been reading sleeve notes since I was twelve."],
  ["How's Sigrid?","She's grown two inches. I measure her every morning. Saara says that isn't science, it's mothering."],
  ["What's Saara like to work for?","Brilliant, very quiet, and she forgets to eat. I've started bringing her an extra sandwich. She eats it without noticing. It's like feeding a bird."]],
 "I'll play you the Ella some time. Not in the lab: Saara says music agitates the samples."],
7:["My exam papers came from Helsinki! The post's been stuck in the snow since August. Organic chemistry, part two. I'm frightened of it.",[
  ["Will Saara help?","She marked my practice paper in red ink and wrote 'nearly' at the bottom. 'Nearly', from Saara, is like a medal."],
  ["Why chemistry?","My dad was a fisherman who could tell the weather by the smell of the sea. I wanted to know why. That's chemistry, really."],
  ["What's organic chemistry?","Carbon. Everything that lives. Including Sigrid."]],
 "Twenty-one days till the exam. I've drawn a calendar on a beaker."],
8:["Sunday in Saltwick, and everyone's in the bar talking about the oil rig. If oil reaches the fishing grounds, half my block's out of work.",[
  ["Is it that bad?","Nobody knows yet. Mike's out on his boat looking. Kate's crying in the stairwell, then saying she isn't."],
  ["Would oil hurt the kelp?","It would smother it. Sigrid's grandmothers are out on that beach. I'd take it personally."],
  ["What do you do on Sundays?","Records and revision. Ella and organic chemistry. She makes it bearable."]],
 "Keep your fingers crossed for the fish."],
9:["(Noora is washing beakers very fast.) It's a mad day. Everybody wants a new flavour all of a sudden, and the lab's upside down.",[
  ["Where's Saara?","Somewhere being wanted. She's wanted everywhere at once today. She'll tell me where when she remembers I exist."],
  ["Have you had lunch?","At my desk. Saara eats at hers, so I eat at mine. It's a sort of tradition, like monks."],
  ["What flavour would you make?","Something that tastes of the sea, but nicely. Saara says 'nicely' is the hard part."]],
 "If you're going to touch anything, wash it after. Please."],
10:["The funeral's on the radio. Kaisa's playing hymns between the speeches. She lives on my floor, and she practises her announcing voice in the bath.",[
  ["Did you ever see the king?","He waved at me once from a car. I was nine. I waved back so hard I fell in a puddle."],
  ["What's Kaisa like?","Lovely. Loud. She's why I know all the words to everything."],
  ["Is the lab quiet today?","Saara's at her desk with the radio on, pretending not to listen. She took her glasses off for the hymns."]],
 "Quietly on the stairs. Saara's listening."],
11:["Helen's off to the lumber mill in Eastbrook today, for the mill hands. She's left me her cat.",[
  ["Helen has a cat?","A big orange tomcat called Lenin. He sleeps on my records. I've had to move Ella."],
  ["Will you join the Union now?","I have! Petra came by the lab with a form. She said if I washed one more beaker without a card, she'd report me to herself."],
  ["How's the revision?","Benzene rings. I dream about them. Last night one rolled down the stairs."]],
 "If you see an orange cat, don't feed him. He's on a Party diet."],
12:["Have you heard? Ruby's sold the rarest record on the island, Midnight at the Aurora, to a buyer who won't give his name!",[
  ["Why is it so special?","Hardly any were pressed. I've never even heard it, and nor has anyone I know. Ruby's been sitting on it for years."],
  ["Would you have bought it?","With what? I'd have to sell Sigrid. And all my glassware. And Ella."],
  ["How's Lenin?","Still on my bed. Helen came home and he wouldn't go back. He's chosen the bourgeoisie."]],
 "If you ever hear that record, tell me what it sounds like."],
13:["A man in a good coat came down the lab stairs asking for the toilet. He spent a very long time looking at Saara's blackboard.",[
  ["What's on the blackboard?","Formulas and test batches. Nothing secret. The real recipe isn't down here. Even Saara doesn't know all of it."],
  ["Did you stop him?","I offered him a beaker of pine cola. He left quite quickly."],
  ["Did your neighbours get free samples?","Half the block said no thank you, capitalist fizz. Then they drank it on the stairs when they thought nobody was looking."]],
 "If you see the man in the coat, tell him the toilet's upstairs."],
14:["The big day. Everyone from my block's going together, under a red banner. I'm going with them, under a small umbrella, just in case.",[
  ["Are you a Communist now?","No! I'm a lab technician with Communist neighbours. It's like living in a beehive: you walk carefully."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That nobody shouts, and that there's room on the fish lorry home."],
  ["Is the exam still on?","In a fortnight. Any day the island isn't busy making history, I revise."]],
 "If you see a small umbrella in a red crowd, that's me."],
15:["I passed my practice paper! Saara wrote 'good' at the bottom. Not 'nearly'. Good!",[
  ["Congratulations!","I've told Sigrid. She took it calmly. She's kelp."],
  ["Leopold or Augustus?","Augustus came down here once, years ago, to ask about the chemistry of incense. He was very sweet. I don't know Leopold, except from the stamps."],
  ["How will you celebrate?","Another record. Ruby's put a Billie Holiday aside for me. I'll get it when I've saved."]],
 "Good! She wrote good!"],
16:["The whole block's crammed into Helen's flat to hear the debate on the wireless. I'm here, washing glassware and listening on Saara's set. It's much quieter.",[
  ["Who do you want to win?","Anna lives right above me. If I say Erik, she'll hear me through the floor."],
  ["What does Saara think of debates?","She says they're like titrations: you add and add and nothing happens, and then suddenly the colour changes."],
  ["Where's Lenin?","Back with Helen, wearing my scarf. Petra knitted it. Lenin says it's his now."]],
 "Whoever wins, the beakers still need washing."],
17:["The lab shook! Every flask on the bench rang. I caught three, Saara caught two, and Sigrid sloshed out of her tank onto my shoes.",[
  ["Was anything broken?","One test tube, and my nerves."],
  ["There's steam on the mountain.","Saara's thrilled. She says it'll be sulphur dioxide and hydrogen sulphide. She's using words I've only seen in my textbook."],
  ["Did you put Sigrid back?","Gently. I said sorry. She's been very quiet since. She's kelp. She's always quiet."]],
 "Stay away from the shelves. Everything's still wobbling."],
18:["Jaana's ill, and everyone's hunting for somebody to be Kelpy. Elin rang down to ask if I'd fit. I'd fit, but I'd faint.",[
  ["Would you do it?","Wave at strangers all day in a cup with no air holes? I'd rather wash beakers. Which I am."],
  ["How's the revision?","Ten days to the exam. I've drawn benzene rings on the bathroom mirror."],
  ["Any news of the mountain?","Saara's planning a trip up to take readings. She asked if I'd carry the flasks. I said yes before I'd thought about it."]],
 "If you end up in the cup, breathe through the straw."],
19:["The Party's recruiting hard in Saltwick. Helen's got the whole block wearing red ribbons. I've got one on my lab coat, inside, where Phil can't see.",[
  ["So you've joined?","No! Helen pinned it on. You don't argue with Helen and a pin."],
  ["Is Saara's trip still on?","Phil said no: insurance. Saara said yes. We're going soon. Don't tell Phil."],
  ["Heard about the prince at the Midnight Note?","Kaisa says she saw a prince at the jazz club in a false moustache. Kaisa says a lot of things, at full volume."]],
 "Not a word about the ribbon. Or the trip."],
20:["We went up the mountain to take readings! Saara and me, as far as the hot springs. Saga at the spa made us tea. Everything smelled of eggs.",[
  ["What did you find?","The springs are two degrees hotter than last year. Saara wrote it down three times and went white."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Saara says the mountain's 'restless'. That's the most worried I've ever heard her sound."],
  ["Has Phil found out?","Not yet. My shoes are still yellow with sulphur. I'm hiding them under the bench."]],
 "Don't mention yellow shoes to anyone upstairs."],
21:["(Noora's lab coat is grey.) It went. Saara was right. The flasks rang and rang, and then the whole building rocked.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Yes. Saara held my hand on the stairs. I've never seen her hold anything but a pipette."],
  ["What about Sigrid?","Fine. Water's the best place to be in an earthquake, if you're kelp."],
  ["Is Saltwick all right?","I don't know! I'm going home as soon as it's safe. Helen will have the whole block in the cellar, singing."]],
 "Go carefully. The ground's still deciding."],
22:["Everyone in the block's fine. Helen did have us in the cellar, singing. Anna sang bass. I never knew Anna sang.",[
  ["What did you sing?","The Internationale, then some Abba, then the Internationale again, because Anna noticed."],
  ["Is there ash in Saltwick too?","On everything. My records are in a suitcase under the bed, wrapped in tea towels."],
  ["Is the exam still on?","I've written to Helsinki to ask. The post's full of ash."]],
 "If you're going to Saltwick, wear a scarf over your mouth."],
23:["The block's buzzing. Helen and Anna were whispering on the stairs, and they stopped when I came past. They never stop for me. I'm furniture to them.",[
  ["What are they planning?","I don't know, and I don't want to. I've a lab to wash and an exam to pass."],
  ["Kurt's sleeping at the factory.","Then somebody's told him something. Kingsgrad's like a test tube: shake it once, and everything fizzes."],
  ["Any word from Helsinki?","The exam's still on, by post, on the twenty-eighth. Saara's going to sit with me. She'll be very strict and very kind."]],
 "I'm keeping my head down. It's safer down here."],
24:["Saara's collecting ash samples. The lab's full of little jars labelled with the day they were swept up. It looks like a church calendar.",[
  ["What's in the ash?","Sulphur, silica, iron. Saara says the island's insides are on the outside now."],
  ["Are your records safe?","Still in the suitcase. I've had nothing to listen to for days. I've been humming Ella to Sigrid."],
  ["How's Helen?","Busy. Lenin's moved in with me for good. Helen says it's a personal decision for a free cat."]],
 "Don't touch the jars. They're Tuesday."],
25:["Ruby sent the Billie Holiday over with Rosa, with a note: 'For the girl who knows who's on piano. Pay me when the ash settles.'",[
  ["That's kind of her.","Ruby's lovely. Prickly, but lovely. Like a cactus that likes jazz."],
  ["Have you played it yet?","Not yet. You don't play Billie on a dusty needle. I'm waiting till the flat's clean."],
  ["Heard about Princess Daphne?","Kaisa read it out on the radio: a princess for democracy. Anna threw a slipper at the wall. I heard it land."]],
 "Billie Holiday. Pay when the ash settles. I love this island sometimes."],
26:["Phil's filming something upstairs, and we've been told to stay in the basement. So I've been in the basement. Which is where I always am.",[
  ["Who's being filmed?","I heard boots on the stairs. Very shiny ones. That's all I know."],
  ["Dario's buying the spa, I hear.","Saga's spa? After what we measured? I hope he likes his bath two degrees warmer."],
  ["How's the revision?","Two days. I've stopped eating, so now Saara brings me a sandwich. Everything's turned upside down."]],
 "Quiet on the stairs. They're filming."],
27:["Exam tomorrow. Organic chemistry, part two. I know it. I know I know it. I don't feel as if I know it.",[
  ["Shall I test you?",["(She brightens.) Go on, then. Ask me anything.",[
    ["What's a benzene ring?","Six carbons holding hands in a circle, sharing their electrons fairly. Helen says it's the only Communist molecule."],
    ["What's in Kelp Cola?","That's not on the paper, and I don't know. Nobody does, except Phil. Nice try, though."]]]],
  ["The Party's at the cinema today, I hear.","Helen wants the whole block there for some Soviet film. I've told her I'm revising. She says Lenin will be disappointed."],
  ["Good luck tomorrow.","Don't say luck. Say 'reaction kinetics'. It's more reliable."]],
 "Reaction kinetics. Say it with me."],
28:["(Noora sits at Saara's desk with a pen and a sealed envelope, and Saara stands guard by the stairs.) Exam day. Upstairs, foreign buyers. Downstairs, me and organic chemistry. We're all sweating.",[
  ["How's it going?","Question four's lovely. Question five's a monster. Don't talk to me: Saara will disqualify us both."],
  ["Helen's in Kingsgrad with a flag, I hear.","She left before dawn with a very big one. I'm not asking. I'm answering question five."],
  ["Reaction kinetics.","(She smiles without looking up.) Thank you."]],
 "Shh. Question five."],
29:["I posted it! My exam's on its way to Helsinki on the mail boat. Eight weeks till I know.",[
  ["How did it go?","Question five fought back. I think I won on points. Saara read it over my shoulder and said nothing, which is good. When it's bad, she says 'hmm'."],
  ["Are you going to cheer for Harald?","Phil's sent everyone to Frostholm. I'm staying. Somebody has to change Sigrid's water."],
  ["How will you celebrate?","Billie Holiday, on a clean needle, with Lenin on my lap and the pipes knocking along."]],
 "Eight weeks. I'll be at the post office every morning."],
30:["(Noora is feeding the kelp tank.) The last day. Everyone's out watching history. I'm in here with Sigrid. One of us has to stay calm.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","That whoever wins leaves the lab alone, and the post to Helsinki keeps running."],
  ["What about Helen and Anna?","Up all night, both of them, and Lenin too. I left a flask of tea outside their door. Whatever side you're on, you need tea."],
  ["And Saara?","She says that whatever happens, she'll still need a chemist. A chemist. Not a technician. I'm going to frame it."]],
 "Close the door gently. Sigrid's had a long month."],
};
// Nora, young reporter at the Eastbrook Gazette (The Reporters). Threads: scoops, curiosity about the foreign journalist (she's suspicious), Henrik her editor, her notebooks, a scarf she hates, the ash.
DAILY['Nora']={
4:["(Nora looks up from her notebook, bright-eyed.) You're the English reporter! Nora, the Gazette. Fearless, ambitious, underpaid. What are you here for, really? Besides the king?",[
  ["Just the king. And the island.","Right. Everyone says that. Then they stay a month."],
  ["What do you cover?","Everything. Henrik says if I'm not in trouble by Friday, I'm not trying."],
  ["Do you often interview reporters?","Only the interesting ones. You're interesting. I'm going to find out why."]],
 "Mind the notebooks."],
5:["(Nora jots something down.) I went to the monastery today. Father Thyme blessed me and told me nothing. A very polite stonewall. I wrote it up as 'a quiet scoop'.",[
  ["Is that a scoop?","It's the shape of one. I'm still working on the filling."],
  ["Do you like the monks?","I do. They're maddening. I think they know everything."],
  ["Did you learn anything?","The candles are not what they were. Don't ask."]],
 "Check your pen."],
6:["(Nora taps her pen.) A man rang asking about you. 'The foreign reporter.' I said I'd never heard of you. He said he'd call back. I asked for his name and he hung up.",[
  ["What did he sound like?","Smooth. Like a boot being polished."],
  ["Should I be worried?","I'm more worried that I've no idea who you are."],
  ["I'm a plain reporter.","Plain reporters don't get called twice."]],
 "I'm watching, you know."],
7:["(Nora flips pages.) Frostholm's open. I've a plan: go to the oil headquarters, ask Dario a question he can't answer, and print the silence.",[
  ["Will he talk?","He'll smile. A smile is quotable if you describe it well."],
  ["Is that wise?","Probably not. But I'm young."],
  ["What's the question?","'Where does the money go?' It always works."]],
 "Be careful."],
8:["(Nora scribbles.) Sunday, and I'm on the story of the sermon. Father Thyme apparently can't find words. I can lend him some. I have too many.",[
  ["Will you print it?","Henrik says a stumbling priest isn't news. I say it's the most human thing on the island."],
  ["Do you go to church?","For stories. Sometimes for hymns."],
  ["What's Kaisa playing?","Something slow. She's got a voice like warm gravy."]],
 "Keep writing."],
9:["(Nora swills cola in a glass.) I've tasted both. Kelp's new formula is sweet and sneaky. The Party's People's Cola tastes like a cough sweet with ideals.",[
  ["Which do you prefer?","Neither. I'm a tea girl."],
  ["Will you write it up?","Obviously. 'The Cola Wars: A Taste Test'. Henrik will cut the jokes."],
  ["Who's behind them?","Helen and Phil. Chalk and fizz."]],
 "Sip carefully."],
10:["(Nora's in a black coat.) They're burying the king. Henrik gave me the assignment: stand in Kingsgrad, look sad, and count the wreaths. There were eighty-three.",[
  ["Did you see anyone interesting?","Everyone. Prince Leopold had a very stiff collar."],
  ["Did you feel sad?","A little. He was the only king I ever had."],
  ["What's next for the island?","Chaos. With cake."]],
 "Count carefully."],
11:["(Nora grips her pencil.) The mill hands marched. I walked beside them for a mile. They sang. One of them offered me a sandwich. That's how I know I'm a proper reporter.",[
  ["What were they singing?","Something about a day's pay. It rhymed badly."],
  ["Will it work?","Helen's behind it. It usually does."],
  ["Did you take the sandwich?","Of course. Objectivity doesn't mean starving."]],
 "Support your sources."],
12:["(Nora is out of breath.) I've been in Ruby's shop all morning, pretending to browse. There's a record in the back that half of Frostholm wants. She won't say what it is.",[
  ["Did you find out?","Not yet. I bought a polka by mistake."],
  ["Who else wants it?","A man from the oil company, called twice. And Erik's lot, for different reasons."],
  ["Will you keep digging?","Obviously. I love a locked drawer."]],
 "Don't scratch the vinyl."],
13:["(Nora raises a bottle.) Free samples! I've been given six. Free cola, a secret recipe, a safe. If I can't make a story out of that, I'll eat my notebook.",[
  ["Who's after the recipe?","Gustavo's lot. They want to bottle it and sell it. At a very high price."],
  ["Will you write about it?","Only when I've proof."],
  ["Do you ever stop?","Never. It's the curse."]],
 "Keep the receipts."],
14:["(Nora can barely stand still.) Tomorrow's the big day! The whole island in one place. I've a notebook, three pencils, and a very bad feeling that something enormous will happen.",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","A scoop. A big one. With a quote."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm electric."],
  ["What do you expect?","Everyone on their best behaviour, then someone slipping."]],
 "Write fast."],
15:["(Nora grins.) Crown before cross, cross before crown. I've interviewed both. The palace says they're modest, the monks say they're humble. It's a modesty contest.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The one with better biscuits."],
  ["Do you take sides?","Never. Quietly, I like the Democrats."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Only to dignity."]],
 "Stay neutral."],
16:["(Nora's pen is flying.) The great debate. I've been told to behave, and I intend to. Mostly. I've three questions each for Helen and Erik. Two are polite.",[
  ["What's the third?","'Who's paying for your hat?'"],
  ["Who'll win?","The one who's less tired. Erik's been up all night with sandwiches."],
  ["Do you vote?","I do. It's the one thing I don't print."]],
 "Pencils ready."],
17:["(Nora clutches the desk.) The whole office shook. Henrik's pipe fell out. I wrote 'EARTHQUAKE?' on a page and underlined it. Then 'tremor'. Then I crossed it out.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Everyone says so. I'm going up with a notebook. Henrik says no. I'm going anyway."],
  ["Is Dario really missing?","I've asked. No one admits it. Which is how I know."],
  ["Are you scared?","A bit. But not enough to stop."]],
 "Take a scarf."],
18:["(Nora points north.) That steam! I climbed the hill this morning and saw it. White like a kettle. I wrote a line: 'The mountain clears its throat.' Henrik cut it. Cowards.",[
  ["Will it erupt?","I hope not. I hope so. I'm a terrible person."],
  ["Heard about the relic?","The monks have lost something and won't say. I'm on it."],
  ["Have you met Kelpy?","The cup? I interviewed him. He didn't say much. Jaana was inside."]],
 "Don't climb too high."],
19:["(Nora whispers.) There's someone going incognito at the Midnight Note tonight. Royalty, I think. I'm going. In a hat. I look suspicious in a hat.",[
  ["Who is it?","A prince, I'd say. Or a princess."],
  ["Will you tell me?","Maybe. Over coffee."],
  ["What about the Party's recruitment drive?","Posters everywhere. I've been invited to join. I said I'm already in the Gazette."]],
 "Wear the hat."],
20:["(Nora is pacing.) Two stories. The palace job and a man at the Hotel Aurora. I'm going to the hotel. Henrik says no, I say I'm having lunch.",[
  ["Who's the man?","A stranger. A photographer who doesn't photograph."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Only if I'm obvious. I'm never obvious."],
  ["Heard about the palace?","Every Syndicate man in Kingsgrad is whistling. It's very loud."]],
 "Don't be obvious."],
21:["(Nora's face is smeared with ash.) I saw it! The mountain, in the north. The whole sky went red. I've written nine hundred words in my head. And I'm shaking so hard I can't write them.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Everyone's fine. Nobody died. I keep saying that."],
  ["What did it look like?","Like the sky had a hole in it, and the hole was angry."],
  ["Will you write it?","Tonight. With every word I've got."]],
 "Breathe first."],
22:["(Nora brushes ash from her notebook.) Grey on the pages, grey on the pencils. I've swept the office, swept the stairs, swept Henrik's pipe. Everyone's helping.",[
  ["Any stories?","A cow was rescued from a farm. A woman baked ash-proof bread. A prince sent a gift for Daphne."],
  ["Who's Daphne?","I'm working on it."],
  ["How's Eastbrook?","Like a ghost town with a good heart."]],
 "Wear a mask."],
23:["(Nora winks.) Spanner in the works! Somebody's sabotaged something, and the Party's blaming the oil boys, and the oil boys say 'deep repairs'. I smell a story.",[
  ["Who did it?","A man with a spanner. That's my lead."],
  ["How will you find him?","Talk to every mechanic on the island. It's a short list."],
  ["Is it a big story?","It's the best kind: small and mysterious."]],
 "Find the spanner."],
24:["(Nora looks tired.) Monks off to the peaks, crews sweeping everything. A retreat and a clean-up. I think one of them is hiding something. They sweep too well.",[
  ["Which crew?","The one in matching overalls. Real cleaners don't match."],
  ["What are they hiding?","I'll find out. It's my life's mission."],
  ["Will you follow them?","Discreetly. With a broom."]],
 "Look busy."],
25:["(Nora is thrilled.) A princess for democracy! I've an exclusive. It's mine. Henrik's said it's good. He said it quietly, but he said it.",[
  ["Who's the princess?","I can't say. Not yet."],
  ["Is it real?","It's real enough to print."],
  ["What do the royals say?","They deny everything. Beautifully."]],
 "Print it."],
26:["(Nora flourishes a bottle.) A prince in every bottle! Kelp's gone mad. And Dario's bought a spa. I've been to look. It's very white and very quiet.",[
  ["What did you see?","Towels. Many towels. And a very tall man called Magnus."],
  ["Why would he buy a spa?","To hide in. Or to steam in. Both, probably."],
  ["Are you writing it?","In the style of a very polite scream."]],
 "Keep the towel."],
27:["(Nora grins.) Revolution at the pictures. I'll review it. 'Rousing, with horses.' And there's a high stakes game somewhere. I'm not invited. Yet.",[
  ["Will you go to the card game?","I'm working on an invitation."],
  ["Is it legal?","It's a matter of opinion."],
  ["Do you like films?","I love them. Even the bad ones."]],
 "Don't bet."],
28:["(Nora is bouncing.) The big push! Every faction, all at once. I've a notebook for each. The Party marches, the palace welcomes, Kelp goes abroad. I'm out the door in two minutes.",[
  ["Where will you go first?","Wherever the noise is."],
  ["Isn't it overwhelming?","It's perfect."],
  ["Take care.","I always do. Mostly."]],
 "Run."],
29:["(Nora rubs her eyes.) One more day. I've written eleven pages and slept four hours. I'm incredibly happy.",[
  ["What will you do after?","Sleep. Then write the real story."],
  ["Do you know who'll win?","No. And that's what's wonderful."],
  ["Any last hunches?","The unexpected. Always."]],
 "Get some sleep."],
30:["(Nora holds her notebook to her chest.) Today's the day. Whatever happens, I'll be there, writing. And I'll find out who you really are, one of these days. I'll bet on it.",[
  ["Good luck, Nora.","Thanks. You too, mysterious foreign reporter."],
  ["Nervous?","Electric. Always."],
  ["Any last advice?","Write it down before it happens."]],
 "Get the quote."],
};
// Olaf, farmer at East Farm (The Farmers). Threads: complaints in order (weather, prices, politicians), Hilda and her pumpkin, the cows, the old barn he never goes in and its night-time hum, his secret Kelp Cola habit.
DAILY['Olaf']={
5:["(Olaf looks up from a cow's hoof.) Reporter, is it? Hilda said you'd be round. Watch where you stand. This one kicks, and the cow's no better.",[
  ["Good-looking farm.","Cold, damp and too many stones. But it's ours. Come back in summer and it'll still be damp."],
  ["How's farming this year?","Terrible. The weather's wrong, the prices are wrong, and the politicians are wrong about everything. In that order."],
  ["Hilda says you complain a lot.","Hilda says a great deal. She's mostly right, which is what's so annoying."]],
 "Mind the muck. It gets on everything, including you."],
6:["(Olaf is leaning on a pitchfork as if he'd grown there.) Frostholm road'll be open soon, I hear, and the lorries will want diesel. Diesel's up again. It's always up.",[
  ["What do you sell?","Potatoes, milk, a bit of wheat. Never enough of any of it, said the buyer."],
  ["Have you always lived here?","Born in the farmhouse. Never left except for a wedding and a funeral. Both were too long."],
  ["Why so gloomy?","I'm not gloomy. I'm accurate."]],
 "Don't tell Hilda I stopped to chat. She'll give me another job."],
7:["(Olaf is rubbing his back and looking at the sky.) Roads all open, they say. Now the whole island will come out here with their opinions. I preferred it snowed in.",[
  ["Would you rather be cut off?","Some days. Nobody asks you for anything when you're cut off."],
  ["What are the politicians up to?","Everything but the cowshed. All those parties and promises and not one's ever carried a bucket."],
  ["How are the cows?","Better than me. They get regular meals and don't read the paper."]],
 "If the paper wants a story, write that the cows are fine and the farmer's not."],
8:["(Olaf is scraping mud from his boots, grumbling.) Sermon on the wireless today. Father Thyme can't find his words, they say. Never had that trouble myself. I've got plenty and nobody wants them.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Hilda drags me. I sit at the back and think about the barn roof."],
  ["Which barn?","The one I work in. The old one next door? Never go near it. Locked since my grandfather. It hums at night, you know. Like a kettle that never boils."],
  ["What do you think the hum is?",["Wind in the beams. Or my grandfather, complaining about the prices from beyond the grave. It's the sort of thing he'd do.",[
    ["Have you tried to look inside?","The padlock's rusted solid, and the key's gone. Hilda wants the doors off. I say leave sleeping barns alone."],
    ["Does it keep you awake?","Not me. I sleep like a stone. Hilda says it's the only thing I do well."]]]]],
 "Say a prayer for the potatoes. They need it more than I do."],
9:["(Olaf has a bottle half-hidden in his coat and puts it behind a bale.) That's not what it looks like. It's medicine. For the nerves.",[
  ["Is that Kelp Cola?","(He sighs.) It is. Hilda thinks I've given it up. Don't tell her. She'd sniff it out like a hound."],
  ["There's a new flavour today, I hear.","They're changing the formula. If they ruin it, I'll write to someone. I don't know who. The king, but he's busy."],
  ["Does Hilda mind?","She thinks sugar rots the character. Mine's been rotting since 1948 and she hasn't left yet."]],
 "Not a word. I'll give you a potato."],
10:["(Olaf is in a black coat that fits like a sack.) They're burying the king today. Hilda's got the radio on and a hanky out. I'm in the barn pretending the cows need me.",[
  ["Did you ever see him?","Once, at a show in 1959. He looked at my prize bull and said, Fine animal. I've never forgotten it."],
  ["How are you feeling?","Funny. You don't think you'll miss a man who's never milked a cow. But there's a hole where he was."],
  ["Will the island be all right?","Probably not, but it never has been, and we're still here."]],
 "Take your hat off. He'd have liked that."],
11:["(Olaf looks cross, even for Olaf.) The Eastbrook mill hands want a fair day's pay. About time, I say. Nobody works harder than a man shifting timber. Except a man shifting potatoes.",[
  ["Do you pay your own help fairly?","I pay what I've got. Some years that's a lot, some years it's a sack of carrots."],
  ["Who's behind the mill campaign?","Helen's union, and Erik's lot. They're all very sure of themselves. Good for them."],
  ["Is the Church helping the poor today?","Alms, they call it. Hilda's sent a basket of cabbages. I told her cabbages aren't money."]],
 "Tell the mill I said good luck. And that diesel's too dear."],
12:["(Olaf is hovering near an enormous pumpkin on a wheelbarrow, looking jealous.) She calls it Magnus. I call it a very expensive soup. She's talked to it every morning for a month.",[
  ["Is it a prizewinner?","It might be. Then she'll be unbearable until Christmas."],
  ["Do you ever talk to your cows like that?","I speak to my cows. I don't name my vegetables."],
  ["Anything exciting on the island today?","Something at Eastbrook about a record. Something at Frostholm about money. None of it's potatoes."]],
 "Don't say I was jealous. I'm merely observing."],
13:["(Olaf is holding a tiny bottle up to the light.) A boy brought these round. Free samples. Of Kelp Cola. I should be insulted. I've paid full price for years.",[
  ["Do you like the new formula?","It's all right. A bit like the old one, trying very hard. I'll buy a crate if it's fair."],
  ["Why are they giving it away?","Because they want a vote, or a barn, or both. People don't give away things for nothing, not unless they're my wife."],
  ["Have the Syndicate been about?","The what? I've enough on with the cows. Anyone who offers you a recipe for free, run."]],
 "Keep the little bottles. They're good for ink."],
14:["(Olaf is oiling his boots.) Big day tomorrow, the whole island at once. I've told Hilda we should stay home. She says we should go. I have a strong feeling I'm going.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Speeches. Long ones. I'll bring a flask and a sandwich."],
  ["Do you want to go?","I want to see who turns up. A man learns more about a place from its crowds than its newspapers."],
  ["Who'd you like to see win?","Whoever can fix the tractor. Beyond that, I'm open."]],
 "Keep the road clear tomorrow. I'll be the one in the muddy boots."],
15:["(Olaf is shaking his head at a seed catalogue.) Crown against cross today, I hear. The royals and the Church tearing at each other like two dogs with one bone. I'd give them a turnip each and tell them to share.",[
  ["Which side are you on?","The side with the better hymns, if it comes to it. Though the royals bring better cake."],
  ["Does the Church matter on a farm?","It matters at harvest. Nobody argues with a bell at harvest."],
  ["Does Hilda have a side?","She's got a side for everything, and it's never mine."]],
 "Don't repeat that about the cake. I'd be in trouble."],
16:["(Olaf is sitting on an upturned bucket, listening to the radio.) Debate day. Communists versus Democrats. I should enjoy it. Everyone's right, and nobody's got a cow.",[
  ["Who's got the stronger argument?","The one who says the price of potatoes is a crime. They both say it. Neither does a thing."],
  ["Do you trust either?","I trust the weather. It lies, but it lies to everybody."],
  ["Will you vote if there's a vote?","I'll vote against whoever's loudest. That's always safe."]],
 "It's cold on this bucket. Come back with a stool."],
17:["(Olaf is holding his tea at arm's length.) Did you see that? It slopped. The ground shook, just a little. I've never known it do that in sixty years. And the old barn hummed right after. Odd.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say so. Steam's coming off it in the north. I say it's the island getting old and bad-tempered, like me."],
  ["Does the old barn hum like that a lot?","Nights, mostly. I put it down to the wind. The wind's got its work cut out."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm worried about the roof. Everything else I've stopped worrying about, to save time."]],
 "If the cows start moving about, give me a shout."],
18:["(Olaf is staring at a hole in a sack of seed.) Something's been at this. Rats, or a priest looking for a relic. I heard about it in Eastbrook. A lost relic. Everyone's out hunting it.",[
  ["What sort of relic?","I don't know. A bone of a saint, or a button. Hilda says it'll turn up in the last place anyone looks."],
  ["Have they looked on your farm?","Two lads in black came through this morning. They asked to see the barn. I said which one, and they went pale."],
  ["Did they get in the old barn?","With what? The padlock's thick as my thumb. They'd need a blessing and a crowbar."]],
 "If it's a bone, I want it kept away from the dog."],
19:["(Olaf is carrying a milk churn as if it weighs nothing and says it weighs everything.) Strange doings. The Party wants workers, the palace wants to go to a nightclub in disguise. And I want my back to stop hurting.",[
  ["The palace is going to a nightclub?","The Midnight Note, in Eastbrook. Something's afoot. I read it in a man's face. He was looking at his shoes very hard."],
  ["Has the Party been out here?","A woman with a clipboard. She counted the cows and said they were an unorganised workforce."],
  ["Are the cows organised?","They're very organised. They all stand in the same place and block the gate."]],
 "That churn's heavier than it looks. So am I."],
20:["(Olaf is walking the fence line and talking to it.) Something's up. The hens are off, and the old barn was loud last night. A sort of hum, but more of it. Hilda sat up and said, It's the pipes. We've no pipes.",[
  ["Maybe it's an old generator.","Granddad had one, they say. Ran the lights in the war. Doubt it still works. A thing like that can't hum for fifty years."],
  ["What would you do if it's something odd?","Nothing. Leave it be. Things that hum tend to be happier not disturbed."],
  ["Do you hear anything else?","Boots on the road. Strangers about, asking for a man at the Aurora Hotel. I said I'd never heard of him."]],
 "Watch yourself on the road tonight. Strange folk about."],
21:["(Olaf is covered in grey dust and looks very sorry for himself.) That's it, then. The mountain's gone up. Ash on my roof, ash in my tea, ash on the cows, who look ready to sue.",[
  ["Is the farm all right?","It's alive. That's all I'll say. Everything else is grey."],
  ["Did you see the eruption?","A great black pillar, up north. Then the sky went dark at noon. I stood in the yard and said the first prayer I've said in years."],
  ["What now?","Sweep. Wash. Complain. I'm good at the third."]],
 "Don't touch anything. It's all got a skin on it."],
22:["(Olaf is shovelling ash off the yard, slowly.) Word is, men from the cola works are looking at farms. Something about trouble at a farm. I don't like the sound of it. I don't like the sound of anything today.",[
  ["Would Kelp Cola want your farm?","Not mine, I hope. We've nothing but potatoes and stubbornness."],
  ["Have you seen any cola men?","One, with a van and a clipboard. He asked about the water. I said it was wet. He didn't like that."],
  ["Is the ash ruining the crop?","It's put a hat on every pumpkin. Hilda's out there with a feather duster."]],
 "Go easy. The ash is slippery when it's wet."],
23:["(Olaf is leaning on the old barn's wall, then pulls his hand away.) Warm. The wall's warm. It's not meant to be, it's an old barn. Maybe the mountain's heating the ground.",[
  ["Is that normal?","I've no idea what's normal this week. Cows are skittish, tea's grey, and the walls are warm."],
  ["Maybe you should open it.","Maybe I should. Maybe I'll do that in spring. Or never. I've a feeling it's better kept shut."],
  ["Any aftershocks?","Small ones. The cups jump on the shelf. Hilda's started to like it. She calls it the farmhouse jig."]],
 "Don't lean on that wall. It's got ideas."],
24:["(Olaf is carrying a lantern in the daylight, as the sky's so thick.) The church folk are off to the mountains today, a retreat. I'd go too, if I thought the cows would manage. They wouldn't.",[
  ["Is there any peace to be had?","Only in the cow shed. Warm, quiet, and nobody argues about the king."],
  ["How's Hilda?","Sweeping. Talking to the pumpkin. The pumpkin's holding up better than I am."],
  ["Do you want the ash to stop?","I want a lot of things to stop. Ash, rain, politics, the price of seed. Mostly I want my back to."]],
 "Light the lamp early. It's dark by four."],
25:["(Olaf is chewing a stem of straw.) The royals and the Democrats are making speeches about crowns and princesses. Everyone's got a plan except me. My plan is breakfast.",[
  ["A crown for everyone?","Fine. I'll wear mine in the barn. The cows can be duchesses."],
  ["What about the princess?","I've heard she's sensible. Which is more than you can say for the rest."],
  ["Do you ever think about politics?","Only when it comes through my fence."]],
 "Don't mind me. I'm just standing here, dusty and resentful."],
26:["(Olaf is turning his hat over and over.) They're saying Dario's buying the spa. A man with that much money, and I can't get a new tyre. There's a lesson somewhere in that.",[
  ["Do you mind the oil people?","I mind anyone who gets rich while I count the cabbages."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola doing something today?","Putting princes on bottles, I heard. I don't see how that sells more. But then I don't sell cola."],
  ["Would you take a job at the spa?","As what? A cow? They might need one for the atmosphere."]],
 "The hat's fine. I'm fine. Go on."],
27:["(Olaf is trying to mend a bridle by feel.) There's to be a revolution at the pictures tomorrow, so I'm told. I've never seen a film, only the shadow on the barn wall when the lamp's behind a cow.",[
  ["What do you think of the Party?","Good talkers. The farm needs a spanner, not a speech."],
  ["Would you go to the pictures?","If Hilda said. Otherwise, no. Films are too long and the seats are too small."],
  ["Are there high stakes somewhere?","I heard a rumour of cards. The Syndicate, I suppose. I never gamble, I'm a farmer."]],
 "Check the latch. I never trust a Friday."],
28:["(Olaf has put his good coat on over his working coat.) Everything's happening at once, the whole island at a boil. State visits, red flags, cola going abroad. Even the oil lot are in their office. I'm just trying to find my left boot.",[
  ["Are you afraid?","A bit. I've never seen so many men with serious faces."],
  ["What would you tell them?","Go home, plant something, and wait for spring."],
  ["Do you have any hopes?",["That whoever wins leaves the farms alone. And that the old barn stops humming. Hilda says it's not the wind. I say what else could it be?",[
    ["Maybe someone's using it.","At night? On my land? Who'd do that? There's nothing in there but dust and my grandfather's regrets."],
    ["Will you look inside soon?","If the key turns up. Hilda found a key yesterday, but it was for the pig pen. A very good pig pen."]]]]],
 "Wish me luck with the left boot."],
29:["(Olaf is staring at the radio like it owes him money.) They're saying it all comes to a head. Primaries, lines of succession. All I want to know is who'll fix the road. Nobody says.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who talks least and promises most. Wait, that's impossible. Never mind."],
  ["Are you voting tomorrow?","If there's a ballot, I'll mark it. If there's a queue, I'll go home."],
  ["How is Hilda?","Nervous. She's got the best hat on and she's polishing the pumpkin."]],
 "Eat well and sleep early. Anything else is a gamble."],
30:["(Olaf is standing at the gate with a mug of tea, looking out at nothing.) Last day, is it. Funny. You think you'll feel something. I feel hungry.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","That the island stays an island. That the cows are milked and the potatoes grow. That's all I've ever hoped for."],
  ["Has the month changed you?","I've complained a little less. Hilda says she hasn't noticed. She's wrong, but I won't say so."],
  ["Will you tell people about the farm?","Tell them it's cold and muddy and ours. They'll understand."]],
 "Safe home, reporter. Next time bring a stool."],
};
// Ole, harbourmaster at North Frostholm Harbour (administrators, no faction). Threads: his new import taxes and the shopkeepers who hate him, every boat in and out in his log, Signe's phone calls from the lighthouse, tariffs and manifests, his pride in a tidy quay.
DAILY['Ole']={
7:["(Ole looks up from a ledger with a ruler in his hand.) A stranger on my quay. Name, vessel, cargo, intent? Oh, the reporter. I've heard. The north-west's open, and I've forty boats to log.",[
  ["Busy day?","Every day, since the snow. Every boat that comes in pays something. I take it, I log it, and I'm hated."],
  ["Why are you hated?","The import tax. It's perfectly reasonable, and no one likes it. Shopkeepers glare at me in the street."],
  ["Can I take a photo of the harbour?","Of course. Don't stand on the coil of rope. It belongs to the Crown."]],
 "Mind the cranes. They swing when they please."],
8:["(Ole peers over his glasses.) A quiet Sunday. Only three boats, and a man trying to sell me a fish. I explained that the harbourmaster doesn't buy fish. I tax them.",[
  ["Is that a joke?","It's a statute. The fish is taxed on landing, and again on sale."],
  ["Do you eat fish?","Constantly. I pay the tax on it myself. It's only fair."],
  ["Is anyone angry about it?","Everyone. But they pay. Eventually. With remarks."]],
 "Tidy quay, tidy mind. That's my motto."],
9:["(Ole taps a stack of manifests.) The cola men have a shipment of sugar due. Forty tonnes, from Gdansk. I've told them it'll be taxed. They've asked if it can be called 'sweetener'. It can't.",[
  ["Why not?","It's sugar. Whatever they call it, it weighs the same."],
  ["Do the cola men argue?","They send letters. Very polite. I file them under 'future amusement'."],
  ["Is your tax fair?","It's consistent. Which is better."]],
 "A ton's a ton, and a tax is a tax."],
10:["(Ole has a black band on his hat.) Today's the funeral, and the harbour's closed for the morning. The ships have lowered their flags, and the gulls have gone quiet.",[
  ["It sounds peaceful.","It is. Only the sound of Signe's horn, three times, from Eastbrook. It carried across the water."],
  ["Did you know the king?","I once taxed his yacht. He paid it with a smile, and a bottle of cognac."],
  ["Was that against the rules?","Technically. But I accepted it. I'm not made of stone."]],
 "A good day. Say a prayer, if you're so inclined."],
11:["(Ole shuffles a pile of notices.) The harbour's back to work, and the shouts are the same as before. A crate of oranges, a crate of nails, a crate I'd rather not describe.",[
  ["What's in the third crate?","It's marked 'machine parts'. They weigh like machine parts, but they smell like perfume."],
  ["Will you inspect it?","Eventually. If someone files a complaint. Or a sneeze."],
  ["Do you get many such crates?","A few. Every harbour does. I note them, and I wait."]],
 "A good harbourmaster waits. A bad one gets splinters."],
12:["(Ole raps on a barrel.) A telephone call from Signe at the lighthouse. She spotted a tanker off the north point, sitting low and quiet. It wasn't on my list.",[
  ["Whose is it?","A Diversified Petroleum vessel, I suspect. Not due till next week. They're always early when they want to be unnoticed."],
  ["Will you stop it?","I'll ask it its business. Politely. And then I'll tax it."],
  ["Do you trust Signe?","Absolutely. She sees everything. I don't know how she does it, from a tower."]],
 "If the tanker docks, I'll let you know. Or I won't. We'll see."],
13:["(Ole straightens a row of pens.) Tomorrow's a big day, and I've closed the port to everything but passenger boats. I've also hired two extra men to count heads on the gangplank. It's a very orderly plan.",[
  ["Do you know what's happening?","Something enormous, with flags. A harbourmaster's duty is to keep it moving."],
  ["Is it a problem?","Only if they all arrive at once. I've written a schedule."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About the quay, a little. It's older than it looks."]],
 "Don't lean on the rail. It's a recent repair."],
14:["(Ole stands on the quay with a clipboard.) The big day, and I've logged eleven boats in an hour, all full of people. Every one of them is smiling too hard.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","Smiling's suspicious. A boat should be bored."],
  ["Do you feel part of it?","I feel like the doorman. It's an honourable job."],
  ["What would you like to see?","An orderly day, and a clean quay."]],
 "Show your ticket to the man by the post."],
15:["(Ole sorts through a heap of slips.) The aftermath. Every boat that came in yesterday has gone out, and half the cargo's unaccounted for. I'll be sorting it for a week.",[
  ["Did anything go wrong?","Only my filing. But it's a small thing, in a very big week."],
  ["Is the harbour busy?","Quieter, in a worrying way. A harbour should shout."],
  ["Will the taxes stay?","They'll stay until someone repeals them. And then I'll find a new one."]],
 "Please sign the book. And mind the grease."],
16:["(Ole listens to a radio.) A debate tonight, the Party against the Democrats. I'm told both sides plan to talk about tariffs. I've been asked to say nothing. I'm very good at it.",[
  ["Will you listen?","With one ear. The other is on the quay."],
  ["Do you favour a side?","Neither. They'll both tax me, in the end. It's the price of public service."],
  ["Do you mind being unpopular?","I'm used to it. It's quite restful, in a way."]],
 "A debate's like a tide. It comes in, and goes out."],
17:["(Ole grips a post.) The quay trembled. A crate slid into the water, and a gull fell off a bollard. I've never seen a bird look so offended.",[
  ["Was that the volcano?","It might have been. Or the sea, having a thought."],
  ["Is the harbour safe?","It's stone and timber. It'll cope. I'm less sure about the cranes."],
  ["Did anyone get hurt?","Nobody. Only a crate of eggs, and a man's dignity."]],
 "If it shakes again, step away from the edge."],
18:["(Ole wrinkles his nose.) Sulphur on the wind, and a very unpleasant tide. The fishermen are leaving early, and the harbour's full of boats with nowhere to go.",[
  ["Are they frightened?","They're prudent. A man who stays near a volcano is a man with a good insurance policy."],
  ["Do you worry?","About the books. They're a mess. The rest I leave to Providence."],
  ["Is the smell getting worse?","It is. I've put a handkerchief in my hat."]],
 "Breathe slowly. It helps."],
19:["(Ole checks a list.) A party boat's left for Eastbrook tonight, with a band and a hundred masks. I've charged them a night fee, and a moustache levy. They paid both without a word.",[
  ["A moustache levy?","I invented it this morning. It's quite popular."],
  ["Isn't that unfair?","It's creative. And it's going to the lifeboat fund, in theory."],
  ["Is there a prince on board?","I couldn't confirm. But the boat sat very low in the water. Royal ballast, I'd say."]],
 "Have a good time. And keep your moustache dry."],
20:["(Ole lowers his voice.) A suspicious crate came in at dusk, and left in a van before dawn. I logged it. A very ordinary crate, with a very unordinary lock.",[
  ["What was in it?","I couldn't say. But I've an idea it's headed for the palace. And not as a gift."],
  ["Did you tell Hanna?","I did. She said she'd keep an eye out. She's one woman, with a very large island."],
  ["Are you afraid?","I'm a harbourmaster. I've seen worse."]],
 "I'd go home early tonight, if I were you."],
21:["(Ole is covered in grey ash.) The volcano's erupted. The sky's black, the sea's grey, and every boat in Frostholm's been loaded with people. I've never logged so many names.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. The lava's far up north. We've been lucky."],
  ["How's the harbour?","Full, noisy, and very polite. Everyone's saying please. It's alarming."],
  ["Are you coping?","I've a clipboard and a whistle. It's all a man needs."]],
 "Don't breathe the ash. Keep your cloth wet."],
22:["(Ole wipes his glasses.) The ash is in the water. A grey film, as far as I can see. The fish are rising, belly up, in the shallows. It's a ghastly business.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","To fish, yes. To men, only if they drink it."],
  ["Will you stop the boats?","Only those with deep drafts. The rest can crawl in."],
  ["Is there anything you can do?","I can log it. It's what I do. A good log's the best medicine."]],
 "Boil your water. And your tea."],
23:["(Ole peers at the horizon.) A tanker's caught fire off the north point. No, a small one. A pipeline, or a pump, I'm told. A sabotage, somebody whispered. I've no proof, and I'll not say.",[
  ["Whose tanker was it?","Diversified Petroleum's. I'd not say it's an accident. But I'm not the police."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","A few singed eyebrows. No more."],
  ["Will you investigate?","I'll log the facts. If anyone asks, I'll tell them what I saw. Which was smoke."]],
 "Keep clear of the north quay. It stinks."],
24:["(Ole holds up a sheaf of permits.) A flotilla of monks, setting off for the north with a donkey and a reliquary. They've paid the tax. They said it was voluntary. I said it was mandatory. We prayed over it.",[
  ["Was it a long argument?","Twenty minutes. I won, eventually. The donkey kicked me."],
  ["Is it wise to go north?","Not for a sane man. But faith isn't sane."],
  ["Will they be back?","I've logged their departure. I'll log their return, if they have one."]],
 "Safe travels. And mind the donkey."],
25:["(Ole saluted a launch.) The princess passed through today, in a small boat, with a Democrat and a picnic. I've never seen the harbour so polite. I stood to attention, and the gulls did too.",[
  ["Did she pay a tax?","I waived it. It seemed rude. Besides, she smiled."],
  ["Is that allowed?","It's a very small exception. It's in subsection four."],
  ["Do you like her?","I've no opinion. But I'd be happy to tax her, if asked."]],
 "A good day for the harbour. Smooth water, soft light."],
26:["(Ole drums his fingers.) A great many deliveries, and every crate has a flag. Cola, oil, Party. I've logged six hundred. I'll need a second ledger, and a third hand.",[
  ["What's in them all?","Banners, bottles, bunting. The island's dressing for something."],
  ["Are you taxing it?","Of course. A banner's a luxury. A bottle's a commodity. Bunting's a grey area."],
  ["Will you survive?","With tea. And the grim knowledge that it'll all be gone by October."]],
 "Please queue. Cargo before people."],
27:["(Ole looks over a manifest.) Tomorrow's the big push, and I've been told to expect forty vessels. A fleet. I've cleared the quay, and told the gulls to leave.",[
  ["Did they listen?","They're gulls. They've started a committee."],
  ["Is the harbour ready?","As ready as it'll be. The ropes are coiled, the log's open, and I've sharpened my pencils."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About being blamed, yes. It's a harbourmaster's lot."]],
 "Go home early. Tomorrow, the quay's mine."],
28:["(Ole wades through paperwork.) Today every faction's pushing, and the harbour's choked. Four boats from the cola men, five from the oil firm, and a very red one flying Party flags. I've charged them all.",[
  ["How did they take it?","Loudly. I've never been so shouted at, or so right."],
  ["Is the quay holding?","By its fingernails. And so am I."],
  ["Do you feel the pressure?","Like a plank in a gale. But planks float."]],
 "If you're boarding, do it quickly. The tide won't wait."],
29:["(Ole closes a ledger.) Tomorrow's the end of it, and I've logged every vessel, every crate, and every complaint. A tidy record, for a very untidy month.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a harbourmaster. I hope for calm seas, and a clean quay."],
  ["What do you want?","A fair wind for everyone. And a tax rebate for myself."],
  ["Any worries?","That the next government will abolish the tariff. I'd miss being hated."]],
 "Go home and sleep. The tide's in your favour."],
30:["(Ole stands on the quay with his hat in his hands.) The last day. The harbour's quiet, the water's grey, and the sky's strangely clear. I've logged my last boat. Now we wait.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Calm. A harbour's always ready. Whoever comes in, I'll write them down."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The king's funeral, the ash, and a reporter who didn't mind the smell of fish."],
  ["Any last words?","Keep a good log. And pay your tax."]],
 "Fair winds. And thank you for visiting."],
};
// Olga, owner of the fish shop in Saltwick (no faction). Threads: the fishermen and the price of herring, her loud feud with Hannu over headlines, Helmi next door at the outfitters, her smoking shed, her bad back, ash on the catch.
DAILY['Olga']={
1:["(Olga slaps a herring on the slab and glares at you.) Reporter, is it? Good. You can tell your London readers the herring in Saltwick are the finest in the Baltic. And the cheapest, if you buy before noon.",[
  ["What makes a good herring?","The eye, my girl. Clear and bright, like a child's. If it's cloudy, it's last week's, and I'm too honest to sell you that."],
  ["Is the king's death bad for business?","The king never bought fish. He had a man for that. A very rude man."],
  ["Is it loud in here every day?","It's loud wherever I am. I was born in a gale."]],
 "Wrap up warm. Fish shops are colder than the sea."],
2:["(Olga is gutting something at astonishing speed.) The boats were out at five, the first since the snow. Four hundred kilos of herring and one cod the size of a dog. Sit down, you look like you've not eaten.",[
  ["Who catches it all?","The lads in the harbour. Hard men, soft hearts. They cry at weddings and weigh the catch with their thumbs."],
  ["Can I buy something smoked?","Smoked fish is by the door, in the paper. And no, it isn't a tourist price. I've never met a tourist."],
  ["Do you ever tire of fish?","Never. People tire. Fish are honest."]],
 "Don't touch the cod. He bites, even when dead."],
3:["(Olga leans over the counter and drops her voice, which still carries to the harbour.) That man at the newsstand told me the herring's gone up. He's wrong. It hasn't gone up, it's gone sideways.",[
  ["Do you mean Hannu?","I do. He talks politics like it's weather. He's wrong in the morning and right by lunch, and still insufferable."],
  ["How do you set your prices?","By the look of the fisherman. A sad one gets paid more. A cheerful one means a good day, and I pay less."],
  ["Does Hannu buy fish?","On credit. And he writes a headline about it. 'Shopkeeper Robs Honest Reader'. I'll show him robbery."]],
 "If he asks, I'm not in. I'm in the sea."],
4:["Helmi next door was in at seven, wanting oilskins on credit for a boy who hasn't paid since March. (Olga rubs her back and groans.) We're a small street. Everyone owes everyone.",[
  ["Are you and Helmi friends?","Rivals and friends. She sells the boots, I sell the catch. A man needs both, and neither of us admits it."],
  ["Is your back bothering you?","Thirty years of lifting crates. It talks to me. Mostly in complaints."],
  ["Who owes you?","Everyone. The island runs on a ledger and a handshake. I keep both."]],
 "Watch the floor. It's wet. It's always wet."],
5:["(Olga is wiring a notice to the door: 'No Fresh Salmon Until Further Notice'.) Someone's been telling people there's a shortage. There isn't. There never is. I just haven't any salmon.",[
  ["Why no salmon?","The lads say the shoals moved. Or the monks blessed them away. I hold with the shoals."],
  ["Who spread the rumour?","Hannu, I'd wager. He likes a shortage. It sells papers."],
  ["What would you like to catch?",["A big fish, a really enormous one, the kind you tell stories about. Old Tobias swears it's out there, beyond the headland.",[
    ["What kind of fish?","A cod the size of a boat, he says, with a scar on its lip. I think he's been at the aquavit. But I'd pay well for it."],
    ["Would you sell it?","I'd stuff it and hang it over the counter. And charge people to look."]]]]],
 "If you catch anything, bring it here. I'll pay a fair price. Almost."],
6:["(Olga hangs a fish by its tail.) I had a man in this morning from Frostholm, asking if I'd sell to the oil crews. They'd pay in dollars, he said. Dollars! I told him my fish are Finnish.",[
  ["Aren't dollars good?","Good as paper. I prefer coin, and I prefer to know the buyer's name."],
  ["Is the oil company buying up the island?","It's buying up the idea of it. Soon everything will be a barrel."],
  ["Did you take his order?","I took half, in marks. It's a business. I'm not above being bought, I'm just above being cheap."]],
 "Come back Friday. The herring will be in a better mood."],
7:["The northwest's open now. Frostholm. That means more men with money and no sense of fish. (Olga rubs her hands.) I'll put the price up a penny and see who notices.",[
  ["Is that honest?","It's business. A penny's a penny, and a penny's not a lie, it's an opinion."],
  ["Do you ever go to Frostholm?","Never. It's all glass and wind. I like my buildings to smell of something."],
  ["What do the Frostholm people want?","Everything wrapped in paper. They've no idea what to do with a whole fish."]],
 "Don't buy the cod. It's old. I mean it's wise."],
8:["(The radio's on, a sermon rolling out from the monastery. Olga is cutting along to it, like a conductor.) Sundays are my best day for trade. Everyone feels guilty about something, and fish forgives it.",[
  ["Do people really buy fish after church?","Fridays for penitence, Sundays for relief. Either way it comes to me."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Rarely. The Almighty and I have an understanding: I clean my herring, he leaves my knees alone."],
  ["What's Father Thyme preaching?","He can't find the words, I hear. Tell him to say 'cod'. It always works."]],
 "I'll light a candle for the catch. Or a cigarette. Whichever's nearer."],
9:["(Olga is sniffing a bottle of cola with deep suspicion.) A boy gave me this, with a leaflet. 'Kelp Cola: Now With New Formula'. I told him the only new formula I trust is the one my grandmother used for smoking eel.",[
  ["Does it taste any good?","Like a pop song. Quick, sweet, forgotten. It's not meant to last."],
  ["Are the cola people in Saltwick?","Not yet. If they come, they'll want me to put a bottle in every fish. I'll turn them away."],
  ["Is the Party making a cola too?","I heard. 'The People's Cola'. I'd drink it if it was cold and came with a herring."]],
 "A good fish needs no formula. That's what I told the boy."],
10:["(Olga has a black ribbon pinned to her apron, and she's not shouting, which is alarming.) They're burying him today. He once stood on that very spot, in 1963, and ate a smoked mackerel. He said it was 'adequate'. I never got over it.",[
  ["What did you think of him?","A big man with a small appetite. He liked things plain. We'd have got on, if he'd had the sense to ask."],
  ["Are you watching the funeral?","On the wireless, with the shop shut. For an hour. The herring can wait."],
  ["Will the island be the same?","The sea doesn't care who's king. Neither do I, between you and me."]],
 "Be gentle today. Even I'm trying."],
11:["There's an almsgiving at the church, and a fight over pay in Eastbrook. (Olga shakes her head.) The island's splitting into two halves: those who give and those who ask. I sell to both.",[
  ["Do you give to the poor?","A bag of herring every Sunday, left on the steps. Don't write it down."],
  ["Will the mill workers get their pay?","If it's fair, they'll get it. If it isn't, they'll get a speech."],
  ["Who deserves what?","The man who lands the fish, the man who sells the fish, and the cook who burns it."]],
 "Don't tell Hannu about the herring. It'll be a headline."],
12:["(Olga is scrubbing a slab with a furious brush.) The harbour's been arguing about a record. A record! Some old song about herring, and someone wants it for a collection. Ruby in Eastbrook's the one with it.",[
  ["Is it a famous song?","A shanty about a girl called Olga, and I've told them it's me. It isn't. But it might be."],
  ["Would you buy the record?","I would. I'd play it in the shop and charge a penny to listen."],
  ["Who's asking for it?","Frostholm money, I'm told. Dario, or one of his lot. They'd buy the sea if it came with a ribbon."]],
 "If they play it, I'll sing along. In a very low voice."],
13:["(Olga has a crate of bottles by the door, uninvited.) They're handing out samples of that cola and leaving crates at every shop. I'm using this one as a doorstop.",[
  ["Do the fishermen like it?","They like anything free. They like it more with a drop of aquavit in it."],
  ["Is it good for business?","For the kiosk, maybe. For me, no. A man who's drunk cola doesn't want a smoked fish."],
  ["Who delivered it?","Boys in matching jackets, very polite. They asked if I'd sell cola at the counter. I said I'd sell them to the gulls."]],
 "The gulls won't touch it. That says something."],
14:["(The shop is quiet, which is unnatural.) Half the harbour's gone to the big day. The other half is hiding behind a newspaper. I've never known such a stillness since the storm of '74.",[
  ["Are you going?","And leave the fish? I'll go when I'm sure it's safe, and not before."],
  ["What do you expect?","A parade, a fight, a speech. A lot of sandwiches. I've packed some herring, just in case."],
  ["Is it good for Saltwick?","It's good for the island. Saltwick will take what's left."]],
 "Tell me who spoke the loudest. It'll be a man."],
15:["Crown before Cross, they say, and Cross before Crown. (Olga bangs the knife on the slab.) And the fish before both. That's the proper order.",[
  ["Who do you back?","The one that eats fish. It's a short list."],
  ["Do the fishermen care?","They care about the weather. The rest they leave to God and the harbourmaster."],
  ["Isn't it important?","To someone, yes. To a herring, not at all."]],
 "The sea will be there either way."],
16:["(Olga has the radio on full volume.) The great debate. They've both promised to go through the whole fishing industry. I'd like to hear them gut a cod.",[
  ["What would you ask them?","What's the price of a barrel of herring? If they don't know, they can't run a country."],
  ["Who'll win?","Whoever shouts last. It's always the same."],
  ["Does it matter to Saltwick?","Everything matters. Nothing changes. It's a very good arrangement."]],
 "I'll listen until the second boring bit. Then I'll gut something."],
17:["(A crate rattles on the shelf. Olga holds it down with one hand.) The ground's shaking. The old men say it's the mountain. I say it's the cod, turning over in the tank.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","Frightened's a word for people with time. I've fish to sell."],
  ["Where's Dario? I heard he went missing.","So they say. Dario's no concern of mine. A man who loses himself deserves what he catches."],
  ["Is the sea affected?","It's strange. The fish are deeper. The lads came back with half the usual catch and twice the grumbling."]],
 "If you feel it again, stand in a doorway. That's what my mother said."],
18:["The fish are behaving oddly. (Olga stares at a bucket.) They're leaping. Great silver arcs of them. The boys think it's a good omen. I think it's a bad one with a good price.",[
  ["Why are they leaping?","Because something's wrong in the water. Warm patches, and a smell of eggs. I've lived by this sea for fifty years and I don't like it."],
  ["Is the catch bigger?","Bigger. Stranger. I've half a mind to hold the price until I know what's going on."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Likely. The sea hears things we don't. And it doesn't hold a grudge, which is more than I do."]],
 "Don't swim. Not that you would. But don't."],
19:["(Olga wags a smoked eel at you.) Hannu's been telling the street the Party's hiring. 'Comrades Wanted.' I said: I'm wanted by nobody, and I've a shop to prove it.",[
  ["Would you join?","If they wanted fish. But they want slogans, and I haven't the lungs."],
  ["What is Hannu saying now?","He's changed his mind twice since breakfast. He's for the Party until I mention the rent."],
  ["Is the street divided?","Over a newspaper. Over nothing. Saltwick has always been like that."]],
 "He's a fool, but he's our fool. That's the harbour for you."],
20:["Everyone's whispering about a man at a hotel. (Olga lowers her voice, which is still a good deal louder than anyone else's.) The Aurora. In Kingsgrad. He's been seen with a Democrat.",[
  ["Who's the man?","Nobody knows. A mystery with a suitcase. Hannu has three theories and no evidence."],
  ["Does it matter?","It matters if he's got money. And a man in a hotel always has money, or he wouldn't be in a hotel."],
  ["Do you hear everything here?","The fish don't talk, but the fishermen do."]],
 "I'd rather not know. It gives me indigestion."],
21:["(The shop's a ghost: grey ash on the counter, grey on the scales, grey on Olga. She's still shouting.) It blew! The mountain! I saw it from the harbour. A great black fist in the sky, and then rain made of dust!",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm furious. Coated, but furious. The herring's still alive. I've checked every one."],
  ["Did anyone get hurt?","Nobody. Thank God, and the wind. It blew north. We were lucky, and I'll say it only once."],
  ["Is the fish ruined?","A day on ice and a rinse and it'll be fine. A fish has seen worse than ash."]],
 "Wipe your feet. I mean it. I've just swept."],
22:["(Olga is hosing down the pavement.) It's on everything. The boats, the nets, the gulls. They look like grandfathers. I've never seen them so quiet.",[
  ["Are the boats going out?","Some. The lads will sail through a wall of fire for fifty marks. It's not courage, it's arithmetic."],
  ["How is Helmi?","Selling masks and goggles at triple price. I told her it's robbery. She said it's supply and demand."],
  ["Is the smell bad?","Like a burnt match in a fish tank. I've stopped noticing."]],
 "Don't breathe through your mouth. It tastes of eggs."],
23:["(Olga throws a handful of ash onto the slab.) I'm told it's good for the soil. I've told the sea. It doesn't care. A fish has no use for fertiliser.",[
  ["Have you sold much?","People still need to eat. I'm doing more business than ever. A catastrophe's good for the herring."],
  ["Is that cynical?","It's practical. Cynical's what you say when it isn't your fish."],
  ["Are the monks buying?","In bulk. They're sheltering half the north. Smoked fish for fifty. They want it cheap and blessed."]],
 "I'll bless it myself, if it's cheaper than the monks."],
24:["The harbour's open again, and the boats are out. (Olga rubs her back and sighs.) The ash made the nets heavy. I thought my back might go for good.",[
  ["Are you going to rest?","When I'm dead. Which, the way things are, may be soon."],
  ["Is there any good news?","A cod. A beautiful one. It's on the slab. Somebody blessed it, or it's just lucky."],
  ["How are your neighbours?","Helmi's making a fortune from boots, and Hannu's selling papers about the end of the world. We're all doing our bit."]],
 "Eat a herring. It'll do you good."],
25:["There's talk of a crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. (Olga snorts.) A fish doesn't need a crown. It has a head and it knows how to use it.",[
  ["Do you like the idea?","I like anything that makes the rich stop. A princess for democracy is a funny idea. But funny's not bad."],
  ["Do you back the Democrats?","I back whoever pays at the counter. But I'd vote for a woman who can gut a fish."],
  ["Is the harbour talking about it?","The harbour's talking about the weather. It's the only subject that ever matters."]],
 "If they put you in charge, I'll give you a discount."],
26:["(Olga is staring at a crate of cola, left once again on her step.) 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' What is it with these people? A prince in a bottle is a ghost. I should know. I've pulled a few out of the sea.",[
  ["Do you mean the cola's haunted?","It's certainly full of spirits. Of one kind or another."],
  ["Who's buying it?","The young ones. They like a prince, as long as it's a fizzy one."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola selling in Saltwick?","They'll sell in a desert. But I'll still say no."]],
 "Send them to Hannu. He'll take anything with a label."],
27:["Tonight there's a film in Kingsgrad and a game of cards in Frostholm. (Olga wipes her hands.) I'm told it's called High Stakes. I've never played cards. I've sold enough fish to know a bluff.",[
  ["Are you going to the film?","A revolution at the pictures. If it has a boat in it, I'll go."],
  ["Do you gamble?","Every morning, with the tides. I've lost more to the sea than any casino."],
  ["Who's running the cards?","The Syndicate, I suppose. They do. It's always the Syndicate."]],
 "If a Syndicate man buys fish, I charge him double. For the privilege."],
28:["(Olga's put out a sign: 'Fish, Flags, Flummery'.) It's the big push. Every faction's out in the streets with leaflets. I've sold more herring today than all week. People eat when they're nervous.",[
  ["Is Saltwick behind anyone?","Behind its own dinner. I've never met a politician who knew a good haddock."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm always nervous. It's how I stay alive."],
  ["What's the mood?","Hungry. Which is very good for me."]],
 "Buy two. It's a long day, and the fish won't mind."],
29:["Tomorrow's the end of it. (Olga pats a herring.) Win or lose, the sea will still be there. I find that comforting.",[
  ["Are you ready for tomorrow?","The shop is, and the knives. The rest is up to God and the harbourmaster."],
  ["Who do you want to win?","Whoever leaves the fishermen alone. They're the only ones who've never asked for anything."],
  ["Any last thoughts?","I've sold herring through two kings and a snowstorm. I can sell it through anything."]],
 "Go to bed early. It's a long day tomorrow."],
30:["(Olga is wiping the counter slowly, for once without shouting.) The last day. You'd think I'd feel more. I feel the same. The tide comes in and goes out and I sell fish.",[
  ["How has the month been?","Mad. And loud. I enjoyed every minute and I'd never say so."],
  ["What will you remember?","The day the ash fell on the herring. It looked like snow on silver."],
  ["Any advice for the reporter?","Eat more fish. Write fewer words. Both are good for the heart."]],
 "Take a smoked mackerel for the journey. No charge. Don't tell Hannu."],
};
// Oskar, the palace footman, on the door of the Royal Palace (Royal Family). Threads: the cold on the door and the fur-lined
// boots he's saving for at Harbour Outfitters; the late-night visitors up the drive, which Mr Edmund says he didn't see; his
// soft spot for Lotta, the maid; his mum in Saltwick, a Party member, who teases him about working for a king; learning to stand
// still for the state visit.
DAILY['Oskar']={
2:["(Oskar stamps his feet on the step.) Sorry. Can't feel my toes. They're in there somewhere, I counted them this morning.",[
  ["How long are you out here?","From seven till Mr Edmund says. Mr Edmund doesn't say often. I think he forgets I'm out here, like a boot scraper."],
  ["Get some better boots.","Saving for them. Harbour Outfitters in Saltwick have fur-lined ones, real sheepskin. I'm a third of the way there. The left one, roughly."],
  ["You're very cheerful for a frozen man.","Mum says I'd be cheerful at my own hanging. I said only if they let me wear a hat."]],
 "Mind the step, it's glass this time of year."],
3:["Mum rang from Saltwick. She's in the Party, Mum. She asked if I'm still holding doors for the oppressors. I said yes, and they tip badly.",[
  ["Is she really a Communist?","Since before I was born. She's got a picture of Lenin in the kitchen, next to one of me in my uniform. She says we're both misguided."],
  ["Do they tip badly?","The family don't tip at all. Royals don't carry money. The visitors tip. The man from Kelp Cola gave me a bottle cap. Just the cap."],
  ["What did you say to her?","I said the oppressors give me Marta's pie on Sundays. She went quiet. Mum respects pie."]],
 "If you're ever in Saltwick, don't tell Mum you met me. She'll try to recruit you."],
4:["(Oskar is hopping from foot to foot.) Mr Edmund says a footman should be like a statue. I said statues don't get chilblains. He said, 'Precisely.'",[
  ["What did he mean?","I've been thinking about it all morning. I think he meant stop complaining. He usually does."],
  ["Is he strict?","He's like a clock that talks. You always know where you are. Mostly in trouble, but you know."],
  ["Do statues get chilblains?","Viktor says the one in the garden has a crack in its foot from the frost. So I think I win, really."]],
 "Close the gate behind you, would you? Mr Edmund will blame me if it swings."],
5:["Can I tell you something? There was a car up the drive at two in the morning. No lights. Black. It sat by the side door for twenty minutes.",[
  ["Who was in it?","Couldn't see. A man got out and went in the side. Mr Edmund was there with a lamp, and then the lamp went out."],
  ["Did you tell anyone?","I told Mr Edmund I'd seen it. He told me I hadn't. So now I'm telling you, and you're not anyone."],
  ["What were you doing up at two?","Couldn't sleep. The quarters are next to the boiler, and it sings at night. Ingrid says it's a ghost. Ingrid says everything's a ghost."]],
 "Don't put that in your paper. I'd lose my post, and I'm only a third of a boot in."],
6:["(Oskar is watching the dining room window.) Lotta's laying the table. She does the napkins into swans. I can't even do mine into a napkin.",[
  ["You like Lotta?","(He goes very red.) She's a colleague. A very good colleague. With a very nice laugh. I've only heard it twice."],
  ["Swans?","Proper ones, with necks. She says it's simple. She showed me once. Mine looked like a duck that had been in an accident."],
  ["Have you told her?","Told her what? There's nothing to tell. (He looks at his boots.) Nothing yet."]],
 "Don't say anything to Lotta. Please. She'd laugh, and I'd only hear it for the wrong reason."],
7:["I ate Prince Augustus's soup. He was fasting and it was going cold. Is that a sin? I've been worried about it all day.",[
  ["Not if it was going cold.","That's what I thought. Waste is a sin too. Marta says so. Marta's very religious about waste."],
  ["Did he mind?","He smiled and said 'bless you, Oskar'. I don't know if that was a blessing or a joke. Both, maybe. He's like that."],
  ["What soup was it?","Pea and ham. Very good. I'd eat it again, sin or not. Don't tell the monks."]],
 "If you see Prince Augustus, say I'm sorry about the soup. But it was lovely."],
8:["Sunday, so the family's split. Prince Augustus has gone up the mountain. The prince is inside with papers. And I'm out here, with the weather.",[
  ["Do you get a day off?","Wednesday afternoons. I go into Kingsgrad to the cinema, if Otto's showing something with a car chase."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Mum says religion's an opiate. Marta says it's compulsory. I go to the chapel at Easter, so they both think I'm halfway."],
  ["What's the weather doing?","Getting colder. Viktor says snow by Friday. He knows by the way the crows sit."]],
 "Go on in, it's warm by the fire. Not out here, but in there."],
9:["(Oskar holds out his gloved hands to show you.) Mr Edmund's given me white gloves for the funeral. White! I've been told not to touch anything all day so they stay clean.",[
  ["What will you do at the funeral?","Open the doors, take the coats and stand at the back of the chapel not moving. I've practised not moving all week. I'm very good at it now."],
  ["Who's coming?","Everyone. I've got a list. Mr Edmund wrote it out. Gustavo's on it. I've never taken Gustavo's coat. I'm told you do it very carefully."],
  ["Nervous?","A bit. I've never been in the chapel with that many important people. What if I sneeze?"]],
 "Don't shake my hand. The gloves."],
10:["(Oskar stands very straight in white gloves, his breath smoking.) The cars have been coming up since eight. I've taken sixty-two coats. I can't feel my arms.",[
  ["Any notable coats?","Gustavo's. Camel hair, very heavy, something hard in the pocket. I didn't look. I've been told not to look."],
  ["How are the family?","The prince walked past me like a ghost. Princess Daphne winked at me. Prince Augustus said thank you for the door, which nobody says."],
  ["Hold on, it's nearly over.","It's only just starting. They have to come out again. That's sixty-two coats the other way."]],
 "Mind the drive. It's lined with cars and a lot of very cross chauffeurs."],
11:["I've got blisters on both thumbs from coats. Lotta put plasters on them in the kitchen. She said I was 'brave', and she was laughing, but still.",[
  ["That's progress.","Is it? (He brightens.) It is, isn't it? She touched my thumbs. Both of them."],
  ["Tell me about the funeral.","It was very quiet, and very long, and Princess Daphne cried in the hymns, which I didn't expect. I had to look at the ceiling."],
  ["Brave about blisters?","She meant it as a joke. But I'm keeping it. It's the nicest thing a girl's said to me since school."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about the thumbs. It's private."],
12:["Another one, last night. Not the black car. A grey van this time, from the south road. Three men, and one of them had a limp.",[
  ["Who was it?","Don't know. They went round the back, to the kitchen. Marta came out and shouted, and they went away again. I think she was shouting about the hour."],
  ["Did you tell Mr Edmund?","He said I'd been dreaming. I said I was standing up. He said footmen can dream standing up. I'm not sure that's true."],
  ["Are you keeping a list?","(He taps his breast pocket.) In my notebook. Just the cars. Not the people. Mr Edmund doesn't know. Please don't."]],
 "If you see a man with a limp, don't mention me."],
13:["(Oskar has a bottle of Kelp Cola in his coat pocket.) They're giving them away in Kingsgrad. Free. I went at lunch and got four. I've hidden three in the quarters.",[
  ["Why hide them?","Marta says Kelp Cola rots the teeth, and she drinks a bottle every day, so she knows. I'm saving them for Lotta."],
  ["Is it any good?","It's free. Free's always good. It's a bit like flat sea water with sugar in it, but in a nice way."],
  ["Did your mum hear?","She rang to say it's capitalist propaganda in a bottle. Then she asked if I could bring her one."]],
 "Want a sip? No? Fair enough. More for Lotta."],
14:["The whole island today, in one place. Mr Edmund says I'm to walk two paces behind him. He walks two paces behind the prince. We'll be like a very slow train.",[
  ["Are you excited?","Terrified. Everyone looking. I'll be looking at the back of Mr Edmund's head. I know it very well. It's very calm."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","Speeches. Arguments. Mum'll be there with her flag. I'll try not to wave."],
  ["Do the family trust you?","They trust me with doors. I think it's a start. Next year it might be umbrellas."]],
 "If you see my mum, tell her I'm standing up straight."],
15:["Everyone's been in and out of the library. The Prince, Princess Daphne, two letters from the monastery and a lot of tea. I've opened that door forty times.",[
  ["What's going on?","Prince Augustus is deciding something. Nobody says what. But Marta's made his favourite cake, so it's big."],
  ["You're counting the door?","I count everything. Cars, coats, doors. It passes the time. Forty-one now: Ingrid's just gone in with the cocoa."],
  ["What's his favourite cake?","Lemon. Very plain. Like him, Marta says, but she says it kindly."]],
 "Don't knock too loudly if you're going in there. He's thinking."],
16:["Lotta's going to the big debate on her afternoon off. The Communists and the Democrats. She asked if I wanted to come. Me!",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm on the door. Mr Edmund said the door doesn't take afternoons off. I said that's not what he said about Wednesdays."],
  ["She asked you!","I know! I've been walking around grinning. Viktor asked if I'd been kicked by a horse."],
  ["Does your mum know?","Mum'll be at the debate. On the Communist side, at the front, with a banner. Lotta will meet Mum. That's terrifying."]],
 "If you're going, save Lotta a seat. Not next to my mum."],
17:["(Oskar's eyes are wide.) The step moved under me. The whole step. I thought I'd got the wobbles from standing too long.",[
  ["You felt it too?","The gate rattled, and all the rooks went up at once. Viktor came out of his shed with his hat on backwards."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","There's steam coming off the mountain in the north. Mum rang. She says it's the earth rising against the bourgeoisie."],
  ["Are you all right?","Fine. I just wish I'd been holding something. Like a lamp post. Or Lotta."]],
 "If it goes again, hold the railings. I'll be holding them anyway."],
18:["Mr Edmund says I stood still for four hours yesterday. Not a twitch. Then he said 'well done' and I dropped the umbrella. Four hours, ruined.",[
  ["Not ruined.","That's what Lotta said. She said she'd watched me from the window and I looked 'very professional'. I'm going to frame that."],
  ["Why the practice?","There's a king coming on the twenty-eighth. A foreign one. I'm to open his car door. I've been practising on the wheelbarrow."],
  ["Did the umbrella survive?","Bent at the end. I'll straighten it. Mr Edmund will notice. He notices bent things."]],
 "Mind the umbrella stand. It's had a rough week."],
19:["(Oskar keeps glancing at the side door.) The prince asked me today which door the coal comes in by. The coal. I've never heard a prince ask about coal.",[
  ["Why do you think he asked?","Don't know. He was being very casual. Princes aren't very good at casual. He kept buttoning his coat."],
  ["Did you tell him?","The side door, by the kitchen. You can go in and out without passing the hall. (He stops.) I shouldn't have told you that either."],
  ["Maybe he likes coal.","Maybe. He also asked if I owned a flat cap. I said yes. He said 'good'. Just 'good'."]],
 "I'm saying nothing else. My lips are sealed. Sealed like the coal hole."],
20:["My notebook says it was quiet last night. Not a single car. I sat up till three to be sure. Quiet's worse, somehow.",[
  ["Why is quiet worse?","Because you start hearing things that aren't there. The boiler. The wind in the gate. Footsteps in the gravel that are probably just Viktor."],
  ["Were they Viktor?","I hope so. They stopped at the shed. Viktor doesn't usually walk round at night, though. Viktor's asleep by nine."],
  ["Get some sleep, Oskar.","Can't. Lotta says I look like a ghost. Ingrid says I am one. Ingrid's not helping."]],
 "If you're out late, use the front gate. I'll know it's you."],
21:["(Oskar is out of breath, covered in grey ash.) I ran to town and back for the prince. Kingsgrad's grey. The mountain in the north's on fire. Nobody's hurt. I said that first, didn't I?",[
  ["You said it. Breathe.","Sorry. I've never run that far. Mr Edmund gave me a glass of the king's brandy. I think it's gone to my knees."],
  ["What's it like in town?","People in the street, with their scarves over their faces, looking north. Lisa from the supermarket was handing out wet cloths."],
  ["Was the prince pleased?","He put his hand on my shoulder and said 'thank you, Oskar'. In front of everyone. I'll remember that till I'm a hundred."]],
 "Cover your mouth on the way out. It's worse than it looks."],
22:["Ash everywhere. I've swept the steps five times. Every time I turn round they're grey again. It's like painting the sea.",[
  ["Your boots look grey too.","They were black. Now they're grey and wet and I've lost a toe somewhere. The fur-lined ones can't come soon enough."],
  ["Is your mum all right?","She rang. Saltwick's fine, just grey. She asked if the palace was safe. Then she asked if the king's horses were safe. She likes horses more than kings."],
  ["How's Lotta?","She's sweeping the dining room. I helped her carry the rugs out. She sneezed on me. I didn't mind."]],
 "Sweep your feet on the way out. Not that it'll help."],
23:["(Oskar is grinning.) I've done it. Saved enough for the left boot and half the right. Helmi at Harbour Outfitters said she'd hold them if I go in on Wednesday.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you. After the twenty-eighth. I want to wear them for the state visit. Mr Edmund says footmen don't wear sheepskin. I'll wear it inside."],
  ["Half the right?","I'm going to ask Mr Edmund for an advance. He'll say no, then give me the money anyway and say I shouldn't tell anyone. He always does that."],
  ["Good job.","I've been cold since July. You don't know what it's like. Every morning, my feet in two blocks of ice."]],
 "Wish me luck with Mr Edmund."],
24:["Lotta and I are both on the door for the state visit. She's handing the king flowers, and I'm opening his car. We've been practising together.",[
  ["Practising together?","On the gravel. She hands flowers to the wheelbarrow. I open the wheelbarrow's door. Viktor watches us from the shed and laughs."],
  ["That's lovely.","It is. (He goes red.) I mean it's very professional. We're colleagues. We just laugh a lot, now. I've heard her laugh about forty times."],
  ["What's the visiting king like?","Old, they say. Rich. Brings his own valet. I'm worried his valet will laugh at me."]],
 "Don't tell Mr Edmund about the wheelbarrow."],
25:["Mum rang. She says the palace has been sending people to the Democrats, to make a deal. She asked if the king's going to share. Is he going to share?",[
  ["What did you tell her?","That I open doors, Mum. I don't know who goes through them, I just open them."],
  ["What does your mum want?","For there to be no king, and for me to come home and work at the fish plant. I'd rather freeze here than gut herring with Kate."],
  ["Is the prince sharing?","Don't know. But he's been laughing at breakfast, Lotta says. He hasn't laughed at breakfast since the old king died."]],
 "If you see Mum, tell her I'm eating properly."],
26:["(Oskar is very quiet.) Men from Kelp Cola came with folders. I had to let them in. They asked me what the prince likes. I said 'jazz'. I don't know why I said it.",[
  ["Is that true?","Ingrid hears it, late at night, through his door. Very low. The radio. I'm not supposed to know. I'm sure I'm not supposed to tell Kelp Cola."],
  ["What did they say?","They wrote it down. Then they gave me a bottle cap. Another one."],
  ["Don't worry about it.","I'm going to worry anyway. It's what I do instead of sleeping."]],
 "Don't tell the prince I said jazz. Please."],
27:["Tomorrow. I bowed to the soup tureen last night, practising, and Lotta saw. She said it was the best bow she'd ever seen. To a tureen.",[
  ["She's teasing you.","I know! She teases me all the time now. That's a good sign, Marta says. Marta says she used to tease her husband till the day he died."],
  ["Are you nervous?","My knees are shaking. Mr Edmund says knees shaking is fine as long as the rest stays still."],
  ["Did you get the boots?","Not yet. After tomorrow. My feet are going to be frozen for the most important day of my life."]],
 "Wear something warm tomorrow, if you're coming. One of us should be."],
28:["(Oskar stands at the top of the steps, white gloves on, in a state of total stillness.) Don't make me laugh. A king's arriving. I can't laugh. My face is a statue.",[
  ["(Make a face.)","(His lip twitches. He holds it.) That was cruel. I'll get you back. In about thirty years, when I'm a butler."],
  ["Good luck today.","Thank you. The whole island's at it today. Mum's got a red flag. Lotta's got flowers. I've got a door."],
  ["What's happening everywhere?","Everything. Every party's out. Rallies, flags, a lot of people shouting at the same time. And a king. On my step."]],
 "Step aside, please. Royal car. (He freezes, beautifully.)"],
29:["Lotta and I went for a walk after yesterday. Just round the garden. She held my arm because the ice was slippery. The ice was very slippery.",[
  ["Was it really slippery?","(He grins.) Viktor had sanded the paths that morning. They weren't slippery at all."],
  ["That's wonderful, Oskar.","I know! I don't know what happens now. I suppose I have to ask her to the cinema. Otto's showing something with a car chase."],
  ["What's the island deciding?","Who's next. The prince, or Prince Augustus, or nobody. Mum's very excited. I'm trying not to think about it. I'm thinking about Lotta."]],
 "Mind the ice on the path. Or don't. Sometimes it's useful."],
30:["(Oskar is in new, fur-lined boots.) Look! Helmi held them. My feet are warm for the first time since the king died. Whatever happens today, I'll face it with warm feet.",[
  ["They suit you.","Thank you. Mr Edmund said 'sheepskin' as if it were a swear word. Then he asked where I got them."],
  ["What will you do if things change?","Open doors. Somebody'll always need doors opening. Maybe the doors of a parliament. Mum would like that."],
  ["Did you ask Lotta to the cinema?","Wednesday. Otto's showing a film about a bank robbery. She says she likes the bit where they get caught. I like the bit where she said yes."]],
 "Mind the step. It's still glass. But my feet aren't."],
};
// Otto, manager of the cinema in Kingsgrad (Republican Party). Threads: quoting STORMWING and TOP COMRADE, Bertha the old
// projector, the western stuck somewhere on a supply boat, the takings in his ledger, and the Communists' interest in his screen.
DAILY['Otto']={
2:["(Otto leans over the ticket counter.) 'Tower, this is Falcon. Requesting permission to go fast.' (He waits.) No? Nobody? You've not seen STORMWING.",[
  ["Not yet. Is it good?","Good? It's eighty seconds of the finest flying ever put on film. I've seen it four hundred times. I cried at three hundred and twelve."],
  ["What's showing besides that?","TOP COMRADE. The Soviet answer. Same story, more forms. I show it for balance. And because the man from Moscow sent it free."],
  ["Who are you?","Otto. I manage the cinema, I sell the tickets, I run the projector and I sweep the floor. I'm the whole staff. I'm also the audience, most nights."]],
 "'Denied, Falcon.' (He smiles.) That's the tower. Come back when you've seen it."],
3:["(A great clatter from the booth upstairs.) That's Bertha. My projector. She does that when it's cold.",[
  ["Is she all right?","She's forty years old and she's never missed a showing. She clanks, she smokes a bit, she eats a bulb a week. We understand each other."],
  ["Why call a projector Bertha?","After my aunt. Big, loud, very warm and unreliable in winter. They'd have got on."],
  ["Can I see the booth?","Nobody goes in the booth. Not even me, unless she's jammed. It's her room. You don't go into a lady's room."]],
 "If you hear a bang during the picture, that's Bertha. Stay seated."],
4:["Do you know what's wrong with this island? Two pictures. Two! A man can't live on two pictures.",[
  ["Why only two?","The distributor in Helsinki sends us whatever's left over. This year, it was STORMWING and a Soviet film the Russians gave away. I'm waiting on a western."],
  ["What western?","RIDERS OF THE PALE SKY. Cowboys, horses, a man with no name and a very long walk. Ordered in June. Somewhere on a boat. I dream about it."],
  ["Two's better than none.","That's what my mother said about my teeth. She was wrong about those too."]],
 "If you see a boat with a film tin on it, tell me."],
5:["(Otto is writing in a ledger.) Fourteen tickets yesterday. Fourteen. Nine for STORMWING, five for TOP COMRADE. The Communists are catching up.",[
  ["Who watches TOP COMRADE?","Mike and Sam from Saltwick, for the politics. Toni from the arcade, for the volleyball. And one old lady who thinks it's a comedy. She's right."],
  ["Is it a comedy?","Comrade Sokol shoots down his own weather balloon. 'Now nobody knows if it will snow.' If that's not a comedy, I don't know what is."],
  ["Do you keep track of everyone?","Every ticket since nineteen sixty-three. Thirty-two ledgers. If you want to know who was at the pictures on a Tuesday in sixty-nine, ask me."]],
 "Fourteen. Write that in your paper. It might shame them into coming."],
6:["I'm a Republican, you know. Have been since I was old enough to sit in the back row. And now this leadership business.",[
  ["Who are you voting for?","I'm looking for a leading man. Harald's got the smile. Magnus has got the jaw. Carlo's got the suit. None of them's got the script."],
  ["What does a Republican want?","Pictures, not posters. Business left alone. A man can run his own cinema. I don't need the state telling me what to show."],
  ["Is it a good story?","Three men, one job, the twenty-ninth. It's a western. I just don't know who's wearing the black hat yet."]],
 "Twenty-ninth. Popcorn's on me if it's a good fight."],
7:["(Otto is sweeping between the seats.) Somebody left a mitten in row C. That's how I know it's a real cinema. Mittens and peanuts.",[
  ["Do you get much mess?","Peanut shells, cola bottles, one time a goat. Don't ask. A farmer from East Farm. He said the goat liked flying pictures."],
  ["Which line do people quote most?","'Fox two!' Every child in Kingsgrad shouts it in the street. Even the Communist children. I'm proud of that."],
  ["Do you ever get tired of the films?","Tired? (He stops sweeping.) I find something new every time. Last week I noticed Frost blinks twice before he says 'Splash one.' Twice! It's art."]],
 "If you find the other mitten, row C."],
8:["Sunday. Matinee and evening. And a bit of news: the oil rig's in trouble, they say. Black water.",[
  ["Who told you?","Mats, the clerk from the Petro Tower. He came in for the matinee to stop thinking about it. Sat through STORMWING twice and didn't laugh once."],
  ["What did you think of the sermon?","Heard it on the radio in the booth while I threaded Bertha. Good. Plain. Not too long. I'd have cut the middle a bit. Editor's eye."],
  ["What does the oil rig mean for you?","Nothing, I hope. Unless they make a picture of it. 'BLACK TIDE'. (He frames it with his hands.) Not bad."]],
 "'Let him go. Let him tell them about us.' (He sighs.) Best line in the picture."],
9:["(Otto is fixing a poster with drawing pins.) New poster for TOP COMRADE. The old one faded. Sokol was looking pink.",[
  ["Pink isn't right for a Soviet hero?","Pink's not right for anyone. Except Lotta from the palace. She wears it well. She comes on Thursdays."],
  ["Did Moscow send the poster?","A crate of them. With a letter thanking me for 'bringing culture to the people'. I've framed it. Nobody's thanked me for anything since nineteen seventy."],
  ["It's a bit crooked.","(He looks at it.) It's leaning left. (He straightens it.) Everyone's a critic."]],
 "Come and see Sokol in his new colours."],
10:["(The cinema is dark. Otto sits alone in the front row with his hat in his lap.) Closed for the funeral. Thought I'd sit with the screen a while.",[
  ["Did you go to the chapel?","Stood outside. Heard the bell. Then I came here. The old king came to the pictures once, you know. Sat in row F. Laughed at all the wrong bits."],
  ["What did the king watch?","A comedy. Swedish. Dreadful. He laughed so hard his crown fell off. Well. His hat. He didn't wear the crown to the pictures."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm sixty-one. I've buried my mother, my aunt and two projectors. This is the first king. (He shrugs.) You'd think it'd get easier."]],
 "Row F. I'll put a little card on it. Nobody'll know but me."],
11:["(Otto is in the booth door, wiping his hands.) Bertha ate another bulb. That's three this month. The bulbs come from Helsinki. Helsinki doesn't care.",[
  ["Can Nils fix her?","Nils in Frostholm? He offered. He'd make her electric. Bertha's been electric since nineteen forty-one, I told him. He meant 'more' electric. I said no."],
  ["How many bulbs do you have left?","Two. If she eats two more, it's lights out for the pictures. And then where will Kingsgrad go of an evening? The City Bar. God help us."],
  ["Isn't she worth replacing?","Replace Bertha? (He puts a hand on his heart.) You'd replace your grandmother if she needed new glasses?"]],
 "Two bulbs. Pray for Bertha."],
12:["(Otto is reading a telegram.) From Helsinki. About my western. 'Delayed. Stop. Regret. Stop.' That's all. Two stops.",[
  ["What happened to it?","No idea. Maybe it's on a boat to Riga. Maybe a customs man in Turku's watching it. Maybe the cowboys got lost. It happens in westerns."],
  ["Could you show something else?","What else? I've STORMWING and TOP COMRADE. I could borrow some jazz off Ruby in Eastbrook, play it in the dark and call it a picture. Very modern."],
  ["Write back and complain.","I did. 'Disappointed. Stop. Very. Stop.' Cost me two dollars. Stop."]],
 "'Regret. Stop.' Story of my life."],
13:["Free cola all over town. Kelp Cola. I've a crate of it behind the counter, and the children are buying peanuts to go with it.",[
  ["Is that good for business?","Very. Free cola makes you thirsty for more cola, and peanuts. I sold forty bags of peanuts this morning. I'll vote for Harald just for that."],
  ["Would you really?","No. But I'd buy him a ticket. In the front row. With a cola."],
  ["Are you sure it's free?","Nothing's free. I've watched enough pictures to know. The man who gives you something in the first reel wants something in the third."]],
 "First reel, the gift. Third reel, the bill."],
14:["(Otto is pinning a sign to the door: CLOSED: GONE TO SEE HISTORY.) Everybody's going. Even I'm going. Bertha can have a day off.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","A crowd scene. A speech. Somebody stands on something and the music swells. I hope it's good. I hope the music's good."],
  ["Will you be quoting films all day?","Only if it's appropriate. 'Two bandits inbound from the north.' (He thinks.) That might be appropriate."],
  ["Who's the leading man?","That's the question, isn't it. A big day with no leading man. Nobody knows who to look at. It's like the end of a picture with no ending."]],
 "Big day. Pay attention to who's in the back row. That's where the story is."],
15:["You know what'd make a good picture? The palace and the Church. Brothers. One wants the crown, one wants the cross. Like a western, with candles.",[
  ["Who'd play Leopold?","A tall man with a good chin who looks at the horizon a lot. Every picture has one. They always win, and they're never very interesting."],
  ["And Augustus?","A thin man, lit from the side, who might be a saint and might be a villain till the very last reel. He's the one you'd watch."],
  ["Who wins?","Depends who writes it. In Hollywood, the chin. In Moscow, neither: they both get sent to a farm."]],
 "Two brothers, one crown. I'd buy a ticket."],
16:["(Otto has the debate on a radio in the ticket booth.) Listen to Anna. Listen to the timing. She waits for the laugh. She's a natural.",[
  ["You like Anna?","As a performer, she's wonderful. As a politician, I'd not let her near my cinema. She'd show TOP COMRADE every night and call it 'education'."],
  ["What about Erik?","Erik talks like a man reading the menu. Good food, no music. He'll never get the girl."],
  ["Have the Communists ever asked about your cinema?","Helen asked once what it'd cost to hire it for an evening. I said more than the Party's got. She said, 'For now.' I didn't like the 'for now'."]],
 "'Comrade, there are no zones.' (He shakes his head.) There are zones in my cinema. Row A's mine."],
17:["(Bertha clanks above. The whole building shudders, and dust falls from the ceiling.) That wasn't Bertha.",[
  ["Was that an earthquake?","A tremor, they're saying. My popcorn machine walked six inches to the left. I've never seen it walk anywhere."],
  ["Is the building safe?","Built in nineteen twenty-two. Stood through a war and two kings. It'll stand through a wobble. It's the screen I worry about. It's on wires."],
  ["Heard about Dario?","Missing, they say. A rich man vanishes. Everybody looks. (He narrows his eyes.) In a picture, he'd be in the last place you'd look. Usually somewhere warm."]],
 "If the ground shakes during the picture, stay in your seat. It's part of the show."],
18:["(Otto is tying the screen's corners with extra string.) Another wobble this morning. I'll not have the screen falling on the paying public.",[
  ["Is anyone still coming to the pictures?","More than ever. People want to sit in the dark and see someone braver than them. That's what pictures are for, when the ground moves."],
  ["What's the bravest bit in STORMWING?","When Falcon hits the brakes and the bandit flies right past him. Takes nerve to slow down when everything says go faster. There's a lesson in that."],
  ["Have you thought about closing?","Close? The pictures never close. They kept them open in London in the Blitz. I'll keep them open in a wobble."]],
 "Hold on to your seat. It's that kind of week."],
19:["People have been asking if there's a picture about volcanoes. There isn't. Not on this island. I've STORMWING and TOP COMRADE and a western lost at sea.",[
  ["Any news of the western?","Another telegram. 'Located. Stop. Helsinki. Stop.' It's in Helsinki. It was always in Helsinki. Somebody put it on a shelf."],
  ["What would a volcano picture look like?","Red sky, a man running. A woman who won't leave her cat. Somebody says 'It's going to blow!' Then it blows. Then it's an hour of crying."],
  ["Can't you make one?",["Me? Make a picture? (He looks wistful.) I've a camera. Eight millimetre. Haven't used it in years.",[
    ["Use it now.","Maybe I will. Up north, if the mountain does anything. Not too close. A wide shot. Wide shots are safer."],
    ["What did you film last?","My aunt Bertha's seventieth birthday. She blew out the candles and set her hair on fire. It's very short, and very funny, and I've never shown anyone."]]]]],
 "'Located. Stop.' (He sighs.) Small mercies."],
20:["(Otto is loading a little cine camera.) I'm going up north tomorrow, if the trams run. Thought I'd film the steam on the mountain.",[
  ["Is that safe?","From the lake, it's miles. I'll sit on the jetty with a flask of coffee and a long lens. If it does anything, I'll be the first man to film it."],
  ["Who'll run the cinema?","Bertha. She knows the programme. (He laughs.) No. I'll be back for the evening showing. The audience waits for no man."],
  ["Heard any more about the Communists and your cinema?","Helen's been past again. And a young fellow from Saltwick, looking at the projector through the booth window. Very interested in Bertha. Nobody's that interested in Bertha."]],
 "If I'm not back by the evening showing, start without me."],
21:["(Otto is covered in grey dust, holding his camera like a baby.) It went up. I was on the jetty. I got it. I think I got it.",[
  ["You filmed the eruption?","Thirty seconds of it. Then the ash came down and I couldn't see the viewfinder. I ran. A man's allowed to run. Falcon ran, once."],
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. Grey all over, that's all. Nobody's hurt, they say, thank God. The lava went down into the hills."],
  ["Can we see the film?","It needs developing. In Helsinki. (He groans.) It'll come back in a month with a telegram saying 'Regret. Stop.'"]],
 "Cinema's open tonight. People need the pictures more than ever."],
22:["(Otto is cleaning the projector lenses with a soft cloth, very carefully.) Ash got into Bertha. I spent all night on her. She's cross with me.",[
  ["Is she working?","She ran the evening showing with a cough. You could see the ash on the screen, little grey stars, all through STORMWING. Very atmospheric."],
  ["Did people come?","A full house. Everyone wanted to sit in the dark with other people. We cheered when Falcon said 'Fox two.' Nobody's ever cheered before."],
  ["Did your film survive?","The tin's in my coat pocket, wrapped in a sock. I'll send it to Helsinki when the boats run. With a note: 'Do not put on a shelf.'"]],
 "Full house. Best night in twenty years. Funny, what it takes."],
23:["Margit's sent everyone a letter. The vote goes ahead on the twenty-ninth. I'm going to dress for it.",[
  ["What will you wear?","My good coat and a hat with a brim. If it's to be a western, I'll look like the sheriff."],
  ["Have you picked your man?","Harald came to see STORMWING. Laughed in the right places. Magnus came to see TOP COMRADE and didn't laugh at all. Carlo didn't buy a ticket. Just looked."],
  ["Does laughing in the right places win your vote?","It's a start. A man who laughs in the right places understands timing. A man who doesn't laugh at Sokol and the balloon has no soul."]],
 "Twenty-ninth. Good coat, good hat."],
24:["(Otto is counting the takings.) Takings are up since the mountain went. Up! I've had to order more peanuts.",[
  ["Why do you think that is?","People want to forget, for eighty seconds. And for thirty-four seconds of Sokol. A good picture's like a warm bath. You come out different."],
  ["Who's been coming?","Everyone. Sergeant Hanna came on her night off. She sat in the back and said 'Fox two' under her breath. I've never been so happy."],
  ["Any word on the western?","They've found the tin. They've lost the boat. Some day both will be in the same place. Till then, cowboys in my dreams."]],
 "Bring a friend. Peanuts are on me today."],
25:["Ville from the newsstand says people are talking about my cinema. Saying the Party wants to put on a picture of their own.",[
  ["Is that true?","Nobody's asked me. Not properly. Hints, looks through the booth window. If they want my screen, they can come and ask like everybody else, with money."],
  ["Would you show a Communist film?","I show TOP COMRADE. I'm not afraid of a red star. But it's my screen. I choose. That's the whole point of being a Republican."],
  ["What if they made you?","Nobody makes Otto do anything. (He thinks.) Except Bertha. And my mother, when she was alive."]],
 "My screen, my choice. Write that down."],
26:["(Otto is up a ladder fixing a light over the door.) The bulb's gone over the sign. CINEMA's become CINE. Sounds French. I won't have it.",[
  ["It sounds rather posh.","Posh? I'd have to put the prices up. And wear a scarf. And show films where nobody talks. No."],
  ["Hold the ladder for you?","Please. (You hold it.) Thank you. You're the first person who's ever helped me with that ladder. Usually it's just me and gravity."],
  ["Any customers today?","The Kelp Cola people were in, asking to show an advert before the pictures. With Prince Leopold in it, they said. I said I'd need to see it first."]],
 "CINEMA. All six letters. Thank you for holding."],
27:["(Otto is behind the counter, looking at a film tin he hasn't opened.) Somebody's been asking about the evening showing. Very particular about it.",[
  ["Who's asking?","People from Saltwick. The Party. They've something they want me to see. A reel. (He taps the counter.) I've not said yes. I've not said no."],
  ["What will you do?","What I always do. Watch it, think about it, and choose. It's my screen. Nobody's ever made me forget that, and nobody will."],
  ["Is there a card game tonight too?","At the pizzeria. All the big men. I wasn't invited. I'd only bluff with film quotes. 'I see him. Hold on to something.'"]],
 "Whatever's on tonight, there'll be peanuts."],
28:["(Otto is at the door, watching crowds go by with flags.) Every faction on the island's out. Flags, rallies, a foreign king. It's the big finale.",[
  ["Is it like a film?","It's like the last reel. Everyone's running, music's loud, and you can't tell who's going to win till the very end. I can hardly bear it."],
  ["Are you showing anything today?","Doors open as usual. Whoever needs a sit-down in the dark, it's there. I'll even let the Communists in. They pay the same four dollars."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","In a picture, the good man wins and rides off. On an island, I don't know who's the good man. That's what makes it so frightening."]],
 "'Two bandits inbound from the north.' Keep your eyes open."],
29:["(Otto is in his good coat and hat with a brim.) Voting day. The Republicans choose a leader. I'll vote at noon, and be back for the matinee.",[
  ["Who's getting your vote?","The man who laughed in the right place. Or the man who'll leave my cinema alone. If those are the same man, I'll be very happy."],
  ["You look like a sheriff.","Thank you. That's the intention. If anyone causes trouble at the ballot box, I'll look at them very hard."],
  ["Nervous?","Like the night before a premiere. Not that I've had one. I'd like one. One day, perhaps, my volcano picture."]],
 "Off to Frostholm. Matinee's at three. Don't be late."],
30:["(The cinema is dark. Otto is in the front row, eating peanuts.) Last day. The last reel. And I don't know the ending.",[
  ["What ending do you want?","The island free, the pictures open, and a western arriving on a boat. That's the happy ending. I'd settle for two out of three."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. Pictures end and the lights come up and you go home. This one, you have to live in afterwards."],
  ["Will the cinema stay open?","Tonight? Of course. Whatever happens. People will need a dark room and somebody braver than them on the screen."]],
 "'Sorry, Tower. Couldn't hear you.' (He smiles.) Doors open at seven."],
};
// Paola, Syndicate enforcer in Hope Springs and Gustavo's most trusted muscle (Crime Syndicate). Threads: the watches and clocks
// she mends, her game of chess by post with a stranger in Turin, Colonel Halvorsen and his dachshund next door, Carlo's vanity
// along the corridor, and the rich of Hope Springs who owe money and think nobody's counting.
DAILY['Paola']={
6:["(A grey-haired woman in a dark coat is standing by the fountain, perfectly still. She looks at you for a moment before speaking.) You're the journalist. Paola. I keep things tidy in Hope Springs.",[
  ["Tidy?","Gates shut. Debts paid. Gardens quiet. (She glances at the clipped hedges.) It's a very tidy place. I'd like it to stay that way."],
  ["Do you work for Gustavo?","I work for Hope Springs. Hope Springs works for Gustavo. It's simpler than it sounds."],
  ["You were very still just now.","I was listening to the fountain. It's running slow. Somebody's let leaves into the pipe."]],
 "Close the gate behind you."],
7:["(Paola has a pocket watch open in her palm and a jeweller's screwdriver between her teeth. She takes it out.) A balance spring. The Colonel's. He overwound it. Men always overwind.",[
  ["You mend watches?","Watches, clocks, anything with a spring. My father was a watchmaker in Bergamo. He said: if you can mend a watch, you can be patient with anything."],
  ["Colonel Halvorsen? The army commander?","He lives two doors from me in the Residences. He walks a dachshund at six every morning, exactly. I know because I've set my clock by it."],
  ["Does he pay you for it?","He brings me a bottle of aquavit at Christmas. I don't drink it. I keep it as a calendar."]],
 "Don't touch anything on the bench."],
8:["(Paola is reading a postcard, a chessboard drawn on the back in ink.) Knight to f3. From Turin. A man I've never met, playing chess with me by post for nine years. His move took seven weeks.",[
  ["Who is he?","I don't know. He signs 'G'. He writes nothing but moves. I like him very much."],
  ["Who's winning?","We've finished four games. He's won two, I've won two. This one is the fifth. It may take the rest of our lives."],
  ["Seven weeks for one move?","The boats. The snow. He thinks very carefully. So do I. There's no hurry, in chess or in anything that matters."]],
 "I'll think about my reply for a week. Good day."],
9:["(Paola is standing at the gate of Phil's villa, with her hands behind her back.) Mr Phil's brother-in-law. Cards, at the Club, three weeks ago. He has forgotten. I'm here to remind him.",[
  ["How do you remind someone?","I stand here. He looks out of the window. He sees me. He remembers. Usually by teatime."],
  ["Is it a lot of money?","For him, a little. For me, nothing. For the Club, a matter of principle. Principles are expensive."],
  ["What's happening at Kelp Cola?","They're making a new flavour, I hear. And the Party wants to make one too, out of spite. Nobody asked me. I drink water."]],
 "Don't stand near me. He'll think you're helping."],
10:["(Paola is in black, with a black glove on each hand.) The Don's at the funeral. I drove him. Now I'm back, and I have work. Today, please don't ask me what.",[
  ["All right. How was the funeral?","Cold. Long. The Don stood at the back and the princes stood at the front, and between them the whole island looked at its shoes."],
  ["What work?","(She looks at you.) I asked you not to. You did anyway. That's a journalist, I suppose."],
  ["Black gloves suit you.","They were my mother's. I wear them twice a year. Today, and her birthday."]],
 "Go home, journalist. It's a day for being at home."],
11:["(Paola has a pocket watch open on a cloth, and a screwdriver the size of a toothpick.) A spring. A hair's breadth out. Nobody would notice. I would. That's the whole difference between us and them.",[
  ["Whose watch is it?","A man's in Hope Springs who's late with a payment. I'm mending it while he's still looking for the money. A kindness. He doesn't know it's a hostage."],
  ["You mend watches?","Clocks, mostly. It's the only job where doing it slowly is doing it well."],
  ["Mill hands want a fair day's pay, I hear.","So I hear. It's a fine idea. It would make my work a great deal more difficult, since fewer people would borrow."]],
 "Don't breathe on the movement. It's a fine thing, and so is my patience."],
12:["(Paola is looking at a chessboard on the table, one piece moved.) A letter from Turin. 'G' has answered. Knight to f3, after seven weeks. A man who takes that long deserves a reply of equal care.",[
  ["Who is G?","I've told you. I don't know. It's a pleasure, not knowing. Everything else I do, I know everything about."],
  ["What will you play?","Pawn to d4. Ordinary. But ordinary, done well, wins most games and most lives."],
  ["Do you ever meet your opponents?","The ones at the table, never. The others, daily. They're less polite."]],
 "A quiet afternoon. I'm using it."],
13:["(Paola is dusting the glass of a grandfather clock in the lobby.) The Colonel's dachshund has been on the clock again. It has an interest in the pendulum. I think it believes it's a very slow rabbit.",[
  ["Colonel Halvorsen's dog?","Brigadier. He's named after a rank above his master's. The Colonel doesn't know. It's the nicest joke in the building."],
  ["Does the Colonel know what you do?","He knows I mend clocks. A military man likes to be told things in simple terms. I think he hopes it's all I do."],
  ["Is there something going on in the Syndicate?","The Don is hunting for something, I'm told. A recipe. I wasn't asked to help. I'm not sorry."]],
 "If the dog comes by, don't feed him. He'll never leave."],
14:["(Paola is in a grey coat and a hat, and stands by the door, ready to leave.) The Don wants me at the big gathering. To stand somewhere and see. It's what I'm for. I'll be one of a very great many people. Everyone's talking about it.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Noise. A lot of men who'd like to be heard. I expect nothing more, and I'll be pleased if I'm right."],
  ["Does the Don worry?","The Don never worries. He plans. It's more expensive, but it lasts longer."],
  ["Will Carlo be there?","In a new suit, with a new smile. He's been practising in the mirror. I can hear him through the wall."]],
 "I'll be at the edge of the crowd. If you see someone watching, it's me."],
15:["(Paola is winding a clock, very slowly.) A prince with a crown, a prince with a cross. They're both quite sure of themselves. I prefer a man who has doubts. He's easier to read, and kinder to his horse.",[
  ["Does the Syndicate take a side?","The Syndicate takes a percentage. It's an older tradition."],
  ["Who would you choose?","I don't vote. A person in my line of work is never on a list. It's a great comfort."],
  ["What do you think of the island?","It's very pretty and very cold. I like it. I'd like it more without the snow."]],
 "A good clock tells the time. A great one tells it truthfully. It's rarer."],
16:["(Paola is listening to the radio, with her head on one side.) The Great Debate. Two sets of men, saying the same thing in different words. In ten minutes, one of them will say 'the people', and the other will say 'the people'.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one with the better tie. It's a bad thing to say, but I've seen it often enough."],
  ["Do you follow the debates?","As I follow the chess. Not for the moves, but for the mistakes."],
  ["What does the Don say?","He says it's a very good time to be quiet. I've taken it as an instruction."]],
 "I'll turn it off. A debate is best heard at a distance."],
17:["(Paola is looking at the floor, where a cup of tea has a small ring on its surface.) The ground shook a moment ago. A tremor. It's the second this week. I don't like it. I prefer a world that stays where I put it.",[
  ["Are you worried?","I'm careful. It's different. I've put the watches in a drawer, and the cat on the sofa."],
  ["Do you hear anything of the Syndicate?","They're making decisions. An executive one, I'm told. I'm not on the committee. I'm what comes after."],
  ["What does the Colonel say about the shaking?","He's taken it as a military matter. He's inspecting the foundations with a spirit level and a very worried dog."]],
 "If the tea ripples again, step back. It's good advice for most things."],
18:["(Paola has a bag of small tools on the table and is sorting them by size.) Everything has its place. The Don knows it. The Colonel knows it. Carlo has never known it, and spends a great deal of time looking for his keys.",[
  ["What's Carlo like as a neighbour?","Loud, vain and charming. He sings in the bath. He sings very badly, but with confidence. He'll make a fine politician."],
  ["Is he really going to be leader?","He's the Don's choice. It's not for me to say. I'd just like his speeches to be shorter."],
  ["Do you like him?","I like the way he's never afraid. It's the quality of a man who's never been hit. I find it restful."]],
 "I'll mend your watch, if you've got one. No charge. It's a hobby."],
19:["(Paola is wearing gloves and carrying a very small parcel.) Don't ask. It's a clock for a very old lady. She wants it ticking when she dies. It's the kind of thing I'm happy to do.",[
  ["That's a kind thing to do.","It's a precise one. I don't mix the two. But sometimes they coincide."],
  ["Who's the lady?","A widow in the Residences. She pays me in biscuits. They're very good. Don't tell the Don."],
  ["Is there much talk in Hope Springs?","A great deal. They're all frightened, but they won't say it. They talk about the weather, the taxes and the dachshund."]],
 "Be careful with old things. They've been through more than you."],
20:["(Paola is in black, hair tied back, with a small case in her hand.) I've a job tonight. Something at the palace, I'm told. It's the sort of work I don't talk about. You'll forgive me if I'm brief.",[
  ["I understand.","Thank you. It's a rare quality, in a journalist."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Never. A person who's nervous has stopped paying attention. I'm paying a great deal."],
  ["Any advice?","Stay out of corridors. And don't carry anything you can't put down."]],
 "Good night. If you hear nothing, it went well."],
21:["(Paola stands at the window of her flat, in a shawl of grey ash.) It has gone up. The mountain. I've never heard anything so loud. A very large clock, striking the hour. I counted. It's an extraordinary thing, to hear a clock you can't stop.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Perfectly. The watches are fine. The cat is under the bed. The Colonel's dog is on the balcony, barking at the sky."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","No. Nobody. It's a miracle, or a very good clock."],
  ["What does the Don say?","He's in the Club, with the doors shut, and a very large brandy. He says nothing yet. When he does, it'll be about money."]],
 "Wipe your feet. I've just swept."],
22:["(Paola is sweeping the corridor floor in long, even strokes.) Ash. It's on every flat, every sill. I've done the corridor twice. The Colonel's dog sneezes in a very military way.",[
  ["Are you helping everyone?","I'm helping myself. It's the same thing, when the corridor's shared."],
  ["Is the Syndicate affected?","The Club's been closed. The bookies are out of business. But the Don's quite calm. He says there's always money in a crisis."],
  ["What's the mood in Hope Springs?","Shaken. The rich are discovering they've no idea how to sweep. It's quite touching."]],
 "Come in and sit. I've made a pot of tea. It's the only thing I do for free."],
23:["(Paola has a clock in pieces, laid out on a cloth with a sifting of grey.) The ash gets in the movement. Every watch on the island will need cleaning. It's the best business I've ever had, and I'm doing it for nothing.",[
  ["Why for nothing?","A person's watch is the one thing they hold on to in a crisis. It would be wrong to charge."],
  ["Are the Party and the oil men mending things too?","There's a great deal of mending, yes. I hear spanners all day. It's quite pleasant."],
  ["Is it hard to be kind?","Not at all. It's being precise that's hard. Kindness is just a matter of detail."]],
 "Bring me anything with a tick. I'll look after it."],
24:["(Paola has a mop and a bucket and a very flat face.) The Don's sent me out with a crew, to clean up. I won't say what. It's a job. I'll do it well, and quietly. It's the best I can offer.",[
  ["That sounds ominous.","It's a very ordinary job. It only sounds ominous when I say it in that voice."],
  ["What are the monks doing?","Going up the mountain, I hear. To pray. A sensible thing, when the mountain's been rude."],
  ["Will you be long?","As long as it takes. Time's a matter of exactness. I'm very exact."]],
 "Stay out of the way. It's safer for both of us."],
25:["(Paola is looking at a letter from Turin, with a smile that's gone almost as fast as it came.) 'G' has made a mistake. A very small one. A bishop in the wrong place. I've not told him. I'm enjoying it too much.",[
  ["Will you take the bishop?","Eventually. A mistake should be savoured. It's the only moment a stranger's honest."],
  ["Would you like to meet G?","No. It would ruin everything. A man at a distance is a mystery. Up close, he's just a man with a pen."],
  ["How's the island?","Tired. Hopeful. A little drunk on possibility. It's an odd mood, and it won't last."]],
 "I'll reply in a week. It's no time at all."],
26:["(Paola has a small notebook, and is writing in a very neat hand.) I hear a prince in every bottle, and a spa for an oil man. It's a very good week for people who like to be looked at. I prefer to look.",[
  ["Do you ever feel you're on the wrong side?","The wrong side of what? I'm on the side of the clock. It's ticking, regardless of what any of us do."],
  ["What do you make of Dario?","A man who buys a spa has a lot of money and a poor sense of where to spend it. It makes him interesting."],
  ["What does the Don want?","Everything. In small, careful increments. It's the way of a patient man."]],
 "Don't ask what I write. It's a list. Not a long one."],
27:["(Paola is tightening a tiny screw, with the tip of her tongue between her teeth.) High stakes tonight, I'm told. A card game at the Club. The Don's playing. It's a very big pot. I'm in the corner, looking at the door.",[
  ["Do you play cards?","Never. A card game is a way to lose money slowly. I prefer to lose it quickly, and in private."],
  ["Is the Don a good player?","The best. He plays the man, not the cards. It's why he's rich."],
  ["Will there be trouble?","There's always the possibility. It's why I'm there. If it comes, it will be brief."]],
 "Come and watch, if you can keep quiet. It's an education."],
28:["(Paola is in her best black suit, a clean pocket watch on a chain.) The biggest day. The Don has told me to stay close. I'm to stand behind Carlo's chair at the Republican HQ. It's a very simple job. I stand. I watch. I'm good at it.",[
  ["Are you nervous for Carlo?","I'm never nervous. I'm attentive. He's nervous enough for both of us."],
  ["Do you think he'll win?","I don't think. I watch. It's less tiring."],
  ["What's the mood?","Tight, like a spring. It's the same sound as a clock before it chimes."]],
 "Stay out of my line of sight. It's a courtesy, not a threat."],
29:["(Paola is winding every clock in her flat, one by one.) The night before. I like to have everything running. If it goes wrong tomorrow, at least the clocks will be right.",[
  ["Are you ready?","I'm prepared. It's not quite the same. But it's close."],
  ["Is Carlo ready?","He's been rehearsing a speech for a week. He's put it on three cards, and lost one. We've found it, in the dog's basket."],
  ["How is the Don?","Quiet. It's a bad sign, or a good one. With him, it's the same."]],
 "Sleep, if you can. I won't."],
30:["(Paola stands at the window of her flat, with the sun on her grey hair.) The last day. Everything I've wound, all month, is about to strike. I've no idea what the hour will be. It's a very peaceful feeling.",[
  ["Any last words?","Take care of your watch. And your friends. It's an old rule, and it holds."],
  ["What will you do afterwards?","Mend a clock. Play a game of chess. Walk a dachshund, if the Colonel will let me."],
  ["Thank you for talking.","You're welcome. You were a good listener. It's more useful than most skills."]],
 "Goodbye, journalist. Mind the stairs."],
};
// Paolo, Syndicate bookie in Eastbrook, who takes bets in the Timberline Tavern (Crime Syndicate). Threads: the ledger he keeps in
// his head and his party tricks with it, Eastbrook gossip (the mill, the Gazette, Ruby's shop), his hopeless love for Maya at the
// Coffee Roasters (and the coffee he can't stand), his rivalry with Nico in Frostholm, and his mother's letters from Genoa.
DAILY['Paolo']={
4:["(Paolo is leaning against the bench in the square, hat tipped back.) A stranger! In Eastbrook! On the very day the snow lets go. Paolo. I take a little bet here and there. Welcome, welcome.",[
  ["What sort of bets?","Anything with an ending. Football, horses in Sweden, whether the mill whistle blows on time. This morning, whether the road would open by noon. It opened at ten past. I did well."],
  ["What's the news in Eastbrook?","The Democrats have a new leaflet, the mill's arguing about pay, and Ruby at the record shop has a rumour of a very rare record. That's a quiet week, here."],
  ["Isn't betting illegal on the island?","Illegal is a strong word. Let's say it's unregistered. Like most of the fun on this island."]],
 "Come and find me at the tavern. Linnea pours a fair glass."],
5:["(Paolo taps his temple.) Go on, ask me. Any bet in Eastbrook since 1974. I keep it all up here. No books, no paper. The police can't read my head.",[
  ["What did Knut bet on last Easter?","Knut, from the mill. Easter Sunday, 1979... no, this Easter: five dollars that Bjarne would fall in the river at the spring log drive. He did. Knut won twenty."],
  ["How do you remember it all?","My grandmother. She ran a grocery in Genoa with no till and no book, and she knew to the lira what everyone owed for forty years. It's in the blood."],
  ["You'd make a good spy.","(He laughs.) A spy? I can't keep anything to myself. I tell everyone everything. That's why they tell me everything back."]],
 "Remember: Paolo never forgets. In a nice way."],
6:["I've just had a coffee. (Paolo winces.) At the Coffee Roasters. Maya makes it. It's the worst thing that's ever happened to my stomach, and I'll go back tomorrow.",[
  ["Why drink it if you hate it?","Have you seen Maya? She roasts the beans herself and she hums. She asked me once if I liked it strong. I said yes. Six months ago. I've drunk it strong every day since."],
  ["Is it really that bad?","It's not bad. It's Finnish. It's very black, very strong, and goes straight to your knees. Italians drink coffee. Finns survive it."],
  ["Have you told her how you feel?","I told her the beans smelled nice. That was in April. I'm building up to it."]],
 "Don't tell Maya. Any of it. Especially not the coffee."],
7:["(Paolo is reading a letter on thin blue paper.) My mother, from Genoa. She writes every week. 'Paolo, are you eating? Paolo, are you married? Paolo, it's twenty-six degrees here.' She means it as an insult.",[
  ["Is she right? Are you eating?","At the Cedar Grill, every evening. Arvid gives me a discount because I keep him in the gossip. It's the only currency in Eastbrook that never loses value."],
  ["Why don't you go back to Genoa?","Too warm. Too many cousins. And here I'm somebody. In Genoa I'd be Paolo, Teresa's boy who never married."],
  ["What will you tell her?","That I'm eating, I'm not married, and it's minus three. She'll pray for all three."]],
 "If you see a blue envelope in the post, it's mine. Leave it alone."],
8:["Big bet this morning. (Paolo rubs his hands.) Whether Father Thyme would actually turn up for his own sermon on the king. The abbot's been stuck for words all week. People say the reporter's writing it for him.",[
  ["I wouldn't know anything about that.","(He grins.) Of course not. Neither would I. I gave it even money that you'd make them cry. Don't let me down."],
  ["What else is the gossip?","Something's leaked at the oil rig. The fishermen at the harbour say black stuff on the waves, and Dario's boats racing out. No bets on that. Nobody wants to win money on dead fish."],
  ["Isn't betting on sermons a bit low?","It's not the sermon. It's the people. I'm betting on people, always. The words are just the race."]],
 "Go on, go and listen. I've got a book to run."],
9:["(Paolo is at the tavern bar, ordering a beer from Linnea.) Linnea's in a mood. She's a royalist, she's sad about the funeral tomorrow, and I put money on her wearing black all week. She has. I feel a bit low.",[
  ["Betting on a woman's grief? Paolo.","I know. I know. I'll give the money to the Church box. The monks will put it to better use than me. Probably."],
  ["What's the mood in Eastbrook?","Everyone's talking about cola. The Party's making one, the company's making one. Dex at the hot dog cart says he's had more people ask about flavours than buy sausages."],
  ["How's the coffee situation?","I asked Maya if she'd ever drink a cola. She said 'over my dead roaster'. I laughed too loud. She looked at me like I was a bad bean."]],
 "Buy Linnea a drink, if you're staying. She needs one."],
10:["The king's funeral. (Paolo has a black band on his sleeve.) I'm in Eastbrook keeping an eye on things, while the important people are at the palace. Someone has to watch the harbour.",[
  ["What's happening at the harbour?","Boats coming in, boats going out, Liv in her customs shed with her nose in a book. The usual. (He's watching the road very carefully.)"],
  ["Did you know the king?","I took a bet off his chauffeur once. Five dollars that the king couldn't eat a whole herring in one go. He could. He did it at a fete in front of a hundred people."],
  ["You look nervous.","Me? Nervous? I'm Genoese. We're never nervous, we're only alert. Very alert, today."]],
 "Go to the funeral, if you can still get in. Paolo's minding the shop."],
11:["The mill hands want more money. (Paolo has lowered his voice to a delighted whisper.) Knut says the saw line's dangerous, Bjarne says the books are cooked, and Dagny says the whole lot of them can go and fell trees in Siberia.",[
  ["Who's in the right?","Knut lost the tip of a finger in April, so I'd say he's got a point. Dagny pays them in pennies. But she's my landlady, so I say nothing loudly."],
  ["Are you taking bets on it?","Odds on a pay rise: three to one. Odds on Dagny losing her temper: evens, and she's already lost it twice this morning."],
  ["Will Gustavo care about a mill?","(Paolo's smile tightens a little.) The Don cares about everything a little. That's why he's the Don. Who said I tell Gustavo things?"]],
 "If you're going up to the mill, mind the sawdust. It's everywhere."],
12:["(Paolo is outside the Vinyl Resting Place, looking in.) Ruby's sold that rare record. Midnight at something. To a buyer who won't give a name. The whole street's guessing.",[
  ["Who do you think bought it?","My money's on somebody very rich or very holy. Nobody else on this island would pay that much for jazz."],
  ["Is there a bet on it?","Of course. Dario's two to one, the monastery's three to one, a lovesick bookie's a hundred to one. That's me. I don't even own a record player."],
  ["What is it about this record?","A jazz quartet, live at the Hotel Aurora in Frostholm, before I came here. Two hundred pressed, Ruby says. Records are like people: the rare ones get stolen."]],
 "Don't tell Ruby I was peering. She'd charge me for looking."],
13:["Nico telephoned from Frostholm. (Paolo rolls his eyes.) Gloating. He says he took four hundred dollars in Frostholm last week, and I took two hundred and ninety. He's counting every dollar like a bank.",[
  ["Are you two rivals?","We came over on the same boat in 1974. He went north to the glass towers, I went east to the trees. He says I'm a village bookie. I say he's a city show-off."],
  ["Who earns more for the Syndicate?","He takes more, I pay out more. People in Eastbrook bet five dollars and come back. People in Frostholm bet a hundred and never come back. Mine's the better business."],
  ["Anything going on in Kingsgrad?","Kelp Cola's giving cans away at the tram stop. Free! Wilma at the museum's furious. She charges a dollar for a can in the gift shop."]],
 "If Nico calls you, don't believe a word about his numbers."],
14:["(Paolo is wearing his best hat, and a flower.) The big day. I've got a book on everything: how long the speeches last, who cries, who storms out. I'm going to make a fortune, or lose my hat.",[
  ["What's the favourite bet?","That somebody says 'the future of our island' in the first two minutes. Evens. Nobody's taking it. They all know it's coming."],
  ["Are you going to watch?","I'll stand by the back with my ears open. Afterwards, everyone in Eastbrook will want to tell me what they saw. I'll know more than anyone who was there."],
  ["Why the flower?","Maya's going. She might stand near me. She might ask me where I got it. I've got an answer prepared."]],
 "Go on, go and watch. Tell me who cried first."],
15:["She asked. (Paolo is beaming.) Maya asked where I got the flower. I said 'from the window box of a beautiful woman'. She said 'that's my window box, Paolo'. It was. I'd forgotten.",[
  ["What did she say then?","She laughed. Then she said I owed her a flower. Then she gave me a coffee for free. I drank it. I've had a stomach ache all morning. It's the happiest day of my life."],
  ["You stole her flower?","I didn't steal it. I borrowed it, for luck. I'll give it back with interest. I'm a bookie, it's how I think."],
  ["What's the gossip from the palace?","The abbot and the prince are arguing over the crown. Linnea's on the prince's side, so she's in a good mood again. The tavern's a cheerful place today."]],
 "Don't ask me any more about Maya. I'll only blush."],
16:["(Paolo is in the tavern with a notepad, which surprises you.) Don't look at me like that. It's not for bets. I'm writing down the debate. Erik's doing it in Eastbrook, Anna's come over from Saltwick, and half the town's betting on who sweats first.",[
  ["You, writing something down?","For Maya. She couldn't go: the roaster needs watching. I said I'd tell her every word. I've remembered every word. The notepad's so she believes me."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","Erik knows the people here by name. Anna knows the numbers. In Eastbrook, names beat numbers. Two to one, Erik."],
  ["Who's sweating first?","Erik. He always does. He wears a wool jumper to everything. I've won eleven dollars on that jumper this year."]],
 "Go and listen. Then come and tell me if I got it right."],
17:["(The bottles behind the tavern bar rattle, and Linnea grabs two.) Did you feel that? The ground! Knut says a pile of logs fell over at the mill. Nobody under it, thank God.",[
  ["Is it the volcano, do you think?","Bjarne says it's the mountain clearing its throat. I've opened a book: will it go up before the end of the month? Evens. Everyone wants yes. Nobody wants it to happen."],
  ["What's the news from Frostholm?","Nico rang, very excited. Some big man at the oil company hasn't turned up to work, and nobody knows where he's gone. Nico's taking bets on it. Bad taste, if you ask me."],
  ["Isn't your own volcano bet bad taste?","(He thinks about it.) Yes. Yes, it is. I'll close the book. Linnea, write that down: Paolo closed a book. History."]],
 "If it shakes again, get under a table. Not mine."],
18:["My mother's letter came. (Paolo reads aloud.) 'Paolo, I read in the paper there are earthquakes on your island. Come home at once. Also, have you met a nice girl?' Two questions, one stamp.",[
  ["Will you go home?","To Genoa, with a volcano starting up here? She thinks I'll come running. I've survived Finnish coffee. I'll survive a mountain."],
  ["Have you met a nice girl?","I've met a nice girl with a coffee roaster and a window box, who thinks I'm a funny little man with a flower. That's a start, in Genoa terms."],
  ["What's the gossip in Eastbrook today?","Jaana, inside Kelpy at the museum, says somebody's been poking about in the cup. Wilma's locking up the costume at night. Never a dull day in that museum."]],
 "If you've got a stamp, I'll buy it off you. Mamma needs an answer."],
19:["(Paolo is leaning on the bench in the square, with his hat over his eyes.) Long night. I was up at the Midnight Note in Frostholm, at Nico's invitation. He wanted to show me how a city bookie lives. He lives on credit.",[
  ["How was the music?","Rita sang, Leo played. Rita sang 'Autumn Leaves' and the whole room forgot its debts. Even Nico put his pencil down."],
  ["Who's going to the club this week?","Sid says somebody very grand is coming, all hush-hush, in disguise. (He grins.) Every week on this island somebody grand is coming in disguise. It's never anyone."],
  ["Nico on credit? Tell me more.","He took me for a drink and asked Sid to put it on his tab. Sid laughed for a full minute. I paid."]],
 "Don't tell Nico I said a word about his tab."],
20:["The Democrats are in a fuss. (Paolo nods at the community centre.) Erik's had a telephone call about some man at the Hotel Aurora in Frostholm. Very hush. Very Democrat. They whisper like they're planning a picnic.",[
  ["What's the man at the hotel about?","Some Russian businessman, they say, who goes up mountains and makes phone calls. Erik thinks he's a spy. Erik thinks the postman's a spy."],
  ["Heard anything from Kingsgrad?","The palace is jumpy. Guards counting doors, butlers counting spoons. (He looks innocent.) Nothing to do with me. I'm in Eastbrook. Everybody saw me."],
  ["Did you give Maya her flower back?","With interest! A whole bunch, from the Kingsgrad market, wrapped in the Gazette. She put them in a coffee tin by the till. She said 'thank you, Paolo'. With my name in it."]],
 "Go and look at the flowers in the coffee tin, if you pass. Mine."],
21:["(Ash is falling over Eastbrook's square, and Paolo has his scarf over his mouth.) The mountain's gone up! Look at the sky. It's like the inside of a chimney. Get indoors, get indoors!",[
  ["Is everyone safe here?","I've counted the street: Ruby, Juniper, Maya, Dex, the Gazette lot. All inside. I keep a list of everyone in my head. It's useful for more than bets."],
  ["What about the mill?","Bjarne's shut the saws and sent everybody home. Dagny wanted to keep working. Knut told her where to put her ledger. Good for Knut."],
  ["You're shaking, Paolo.","I'm Genoese. We've never had a volcano. We've had the French, the English and my mother. None of them rained fire."]],
 "Go inside! Now! Don't stand there writing it down."],
22:["(Paolo is sweeping ash from the step of the Coffee Roasters, with great dignity.) Maya's step. She didn't ask. I just turned up with a brush. She made me a coffee. Weaker, this time.",[
  ["Weaker? She's noticed.","She's noticed for six months, she says. She thought it was funny. She says next time, I can ask for milk. Milk! In a Finnish coffee shop."],
  ["How's Eastbrook coping?","The roofs are grey, the river's grey, the dog at the mill is grey. The Gazette's printing on whatever paper isn't ashy. Henrik's in a terrible temper."],
  ["Are the roads open?","The south road, yes. The north, no. Nico can't get here to gloat, and I can't get there to laugh at him. Peace in our time."]],
 "Brush your shoulders. Everyone in Eastbrook looks like a ghost."],
23:["(Paolo is at the tavern, writing nothing, looking at nothing.) Do you know what's strange? Nobody wants to bet since the mountain. Nobody. They just want to sit with someone. I've taken no money in three days.",[
  ["Is that bad for business?","It's terrible for business. The Don will ask why Eastbrook's quiet. I'll tell him: Eastbrook's frightened. Frightened people don't gamble. They hold hands."],
  ["What will you do instead?","Sit with someone. I'm good at it. I sat with Hely on the bench for an hour this morning, and Veikko after, and Linnea after that. Nobody said a word."],
  ["Any gossip from the cola factory?","The Party's up to something there, they say. Machines, spanners, strike talk. Helen's been seen in Eastbrook twice. Helen doesn't come to Eastbrook for the coffee."]],
 "Sit, if you like. I'm not taking bets."],
24:["(Paolo is pale, and has his hands in his pockets.) Don't ask me what kind of morning I've had. Some mornings a man hears things he shouldn't, and wishes he'd been born deaf, in Genoa.",[
  ["What did you hear?","Nothing. I heard nothing, and I've already forgotten it. You see? Even Paolo can forget something, if he tries very hard."],
  ["Are the monks going up the mountain?","Linnea says the abbot's taking them on a retreat, up in the peaks, with the ash still falling. They're braver than me, or holier."],
  ["Want to talk about Maya instead?","(His face lights up, slowly.) Yes. Please. She laughed at my hat this morning. It's the best thing anyone's said all week."]],
 "Thank you. Go well. Don't listen at doors."],
25:["Everyone's talking about the princess. (Paolo is breathless.) Daphne, they say, wants a vote for the island. A princess, for democracy! Erik's walking round Eastbrook like a man who's seen a saint. My mother will never believe it.",[
  ["What do people here make of it?","Ruby clapped. Linnea cried, and I think in a bad way: she's a royalist. Maya just said 'well, well'. Maya's 'well, well' is worth a whole speech."],
  ["Did you take bets on it?","No more bets this week. I'm a reformed bookie. (He pauses.) I did give Knut two to one that Erik would cry. He has, twice. I paid."],
  ["Will you write to your mother about it?","Already have. 'Dear Mamma, a princess wants a vote, and I have a nice girl, almost. It's minus five.' She'll faint, then she'll tell all of Genoa."]],
 "Go and see Erik. He'll be glowing like a stove."],
26:["(Paolo's at the bench in the square, reading the Gazette with interest.) Dario's buying the spa! In the ash, at the foot of a mountain that's just blown its top. I've a cousin in Genoa who bought a hotel on a cliff that fell in the sea. Dario's that man.",[
  ["Is that a good investment?","It's a hot bath, on top of a fire, after the fire's gone out. Some people say perfect timing. I'd say he's the only buyer, so he'll get a price."],
  ["What does Eastbrook think?","Dagny's jealous. She wanted the spa years ago and couldn't afford it. Now she says it's full of ash and bad luck. Envy and sense sound alike."],
  ["What does the Don think?","(Paolo folds the paper carefully.) The Don thinks very privately. And I only pass gossip one way. Up."]],
 "Read the Gazette while it's still warm."],
27:["The tournament! (Paolo is in his best coat.) In the back room at Rosaria's, everyone who matters. I'm going to watch Nico play. I've waited eight years to see him lose to someone who isn't me. I've told Maya to listen out for my cheering.",[
  ["Are you playing?","No. Paolo doesn't play. Paolo keeps the book. Odds on Nico going out in the first hour: six to four. I'll have to be careful not to look happy."],
  ["What's Nico's weakness?","He thinks he reads people. He reads them like a man reads a menu in a language he doesn't speak. Pointing at things and hoping."],
  ["Will you bet on the winner?","I've a little on the Don, out of loyalty, and a little on whoever the Don lets win, out of sense."]],
 "Go to Kingsgrad. Watch Nico's ear. That's all I'll say."],
28:["(Paolo is watching the Democrats gather for their rally in the square.) The big push. The Democrats have flags, the mill's closed for it, Maya's making coffee for the whole crowd for free. I'm carrying the urns.",[
  ["Are you a Democrat now?","I'm a man carrying an urn for a woman with a roaster. If that's democracy, put me down for it."],
  ["Will the Syndicate mind?","The Syndicate wants Carlo in Frostholm tomorrow. Today, Paolo's carrying coffee. A man can do two things in a weekend."],
  ["What's the mood in Eastbrook?","Loud. Hopeful. Ruby's playing records out of her window. Even Dagny's come out to watch, with her arms folded."]],
 "Grab a coffee, it's free. Don't tell Maya I said it was weak."],
29:["(Paolo has a tiny sliver of paper in his hand.) The Republican vote, in Frostholm. I'm going up to watch with the Syndicate. Carlo's nervous. He's phoned me twice to ask what the odds are. I lied, both times.",[
  ["What are the real odds?","Paolo doesn't give odds on Syndicate business. (He tears the paper into tiny bits.) That was the real odds. Now it's confetti."],
  ["Why lie to Carlo?","Because he's a nervous man with a speech to make. You don't tell a tenor the theatre's half empty."],
  ["Anything new with Maya?","She said, if I come back from Frostholm in one piece, she'll teach me to like coffee. I said I'd come back in two pieces, to get two lessons."]],
 "Go to Frostholm. Count the votes better than they do."],
30:["(Paolo is standing in Eastbrook's square, hat in hand, looking at the people going by.) The last day, my friend. Tomorrow the island's somebody's. I've kept every bet of this month in my head. It's been a strange one.",[
  ["What will you remember most?","The first coffee Maya made weak, on purpose, without saying anything. That's the best thing I've seen in eight years on this island."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Write to my mother. Say: 'The island's changed hands. I haven't. It's minus six. I'm eating.' She'll be pleased with three out of four."],
  ["Any last bets?","One. That whoever wins, Eastbrook will still drink its coffee too strong and argue in the tavern. Odds-on. Nobody will take it."]],
 "Come back to Eastbrook any time. Paolo remembers his friends."],
};
// Pat, a hobo who has camped on the beach between Saltwick and South West Road for years (The Unemployed). Threads: the bottle
// with a letter in Russian he found on the 2nd, the boats with no lights at the river mouth, his years as a ship's cook and the
// daughter in Turku he never wrote to, and the sea, the cold and the gulls. From the 12th he keeps to things true wherever he sleeps.
DAILY['Pat']={
1:["(Pat looks up from poking at the sand with a stick.) A new face off the morning boat. Nobody new comes ashore here in September. Nobody old does either.",[
  ["Morning. Lovely beach.","It is, when the snow's off it. Took three days to dig my tent out. I've had worse landlords."],
  ["What brings a man to live on a beach?",["The sea brings everything else here. Thought it might as well bring me. (He grins, short of a few teeth.)",[
    ["Seriously, though.","Seriously? A bottle, a long time ago, and a captain who didn't like it. That's all you're getting on the first day."],
    ["Fair enough.","There you are. You've got manners. Most people who ask want the sad story, not the man."]]]],
  ["Do you get many visitors?","Gulls. The odd fisherman. Sirpa and Tiina from the village, when they want to gossip somewhere Ray can't hear."]],
 "Mind the tide line. The sand's soft where the river comes in."],
2:["(Pat holds up a green glass bottle, corked and waxed.) Look what the sea sent me. There's a letter in it. In Russian, I think: all backwards Rs.",[
  ["Have you opened it?","Not yet. You open a thing like that, it stops being a mystery and starts being someone's business. I'm enjoying the mystery."],
  ["Could it be important?","Could be a spy's secrets. Could be a sailor's shopping list. The sea doesn't tell you which, it just hands them over."],
  ["Sell it to a collector.","Who's collecting? The king's dead and the shop in Eastbrook only takes records. No, I'll keep it."]],
 "If you can read Russian, keep it to yourself for now. I like not knowing."],
3:["(Pat is rebaiting a long fishing line with bits of mussel.) Cod come in close when it turns cold. Cold's coming. So are the cod, if I'm lucky.",[
  ["Caught anything yet?","A boot, Tuesday. Left foot. If the sea sends me the right one I'll be the best-dressed man in Saltwick."],
  ["How long have you fished here?","Eleven winters. Before that I fished for other people, on boats. Now the fish and I are on more equal terms."],
  ["Doesn't look very efficient.","It isn't. But nobody shouts at me, and the cod don't ask for a union rate."]],
 "Don't step on the line. Took me an hour to bait."],
4:["(Pat nods towards the sea.) Saw lights out there last night. Late. A boat with no running lights, coming in slow to the river mouth.",[
  ["Fishermen, surely?","Fishermen want to be seen, or someone runs them down. These didn't want to be seen. That's a different trade."],
  ["What were they landing?",["Crates, two men, a handcart up the path towards the bridge. (He shrugs.) I was asleep. I'm always asleep, when anyone asks.",[
    ["Who do you think they were?","Someone with a good boat and a bad conscience. There's a few of those on this island."],
    ["Aren't you scared?","Of what? I've nothing worth taking. A man with nothing is the safest man on any beach."]]]],
  ["Maybe you dreamt it.","Maybe. I dream about cod, mostly. Not men with handcarts."]],
 "You didn't hear any of that from me. I was asleep."],
5:["(Pat is stirring a pot of something grey on his camp stove.) Fish head soup. A fine dish. I used to make it for forty men.",[
  ["Forty men?","Ship's cook, on the coastal steamer out of Kingsgrad, twenty years ago. Forty deckhands, one pot, one ladle, one cook. I was good, too."],
  ["Smells... interesting.","That's the sea in it. In a restaurant in London they'd charge you a week's wages and call it rustic."],
  ["What happened to the job?","The bottle happened. The captain found it in the flour bin. The sea doesn't care if you drink. Captains do."]],
 "I'd offer you some, but there's only enough for one, and he's hungry."],
6:["(Pat squints at the grey sky.) First real frost on the dunes this morning. Fingers didn't work till noon. That's early for September.",[
  ["How do you survive the winter?","Newspapers in the bedroll. Driftwood on the fire. And I go up to Ray's bar when it's twenty below, if he's feeling Christian."],
  ["Doesn't the Church help?","Father Thyme's lot come down every Christmas with a blanket and a hymn. The blanket's very welcome."],
  ["You could move indoors.","Indoors costs money, friend. Out here the rent's paid in cold, and I've got plenty of that."]],
 "Keep your hat on. Heat goes out the top of your head."],
7:["(Pat is turning the green bottle in his hands.) I've been looking at my letter. There's a name at the bottom, I'm sure of it. Five letters. Starts with something like a G.",[
  ["Galina? Gleb?","Could be. I've decided she's a Galina. A Galina waiting on a quay somewhere for a sailor who never wrote back."],
  ["Still haven't opened it?","I've opened the cork. Only to smell it. Paper, wax and the inside of a long way off."],
  ["Ask the Russian in Frostholm.","There's a Russian in Frostholm? There's a Russian everywhere, if you listen to Anna. No. Not yet."]],
 "If the sea sends a dictionary, I'll know where to start."],
8:["(Pat sniffs the wind.) Smell that? Something's wrong out at sea. Diesel, or worse. It's coming down from the north-west on the wind.",[
  ["The oil rig, maybe?","Maybe. You live by the sea long enough, you know when it's sick. It's sick today, or something near it is."],
  ["Will it reach the beach?","The current runs from the rig down past the point. If anything's spilt, it'll find its way here before it finds a newspaper."],
  ["You've got a good nose.","Ship's cook. You learn to smell burning before it burns. Same with the sea."]],
 "If you see black on the sand, don't let a gull near it."],
9:["(Pat holds up a cork float, black and tacky.) Found three of these at dawn. Tar on them. Nobody's told me anything, but the sea did.",[
  ["Is the beach bad?","Patches, up by the river. The gulls are keeping off them, which is wiser than most people."],
  ["Who do you blame?","Whoever owns the thing that leaks. I'm not clever about oil, but I'm clever about who never comes down to the beach to look."],
  ["Can I do anything?","Write it down for your paper. Nobody reads what a hobo says, but they read what a Londoner writes."]],
 "Wash your boots before you go into Ray's. He'll blame me."],
10:["(Pat has a strip of black cloth tied round his arm.) For the king. Found it on the tide line, so I'd say the sea's in mourning too.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","Cooked him a fish once, on the steamer, when he was a young prince. He said it was the best perch he'd ever eaten. I said it was cod."],
  ["Not going to the funeral?","In this coat? They'd have the army on me before I got to the gate. I'll mourn him from here. The view's better."],
  ["A sentimental hobo?","Everyone's sentimental at a funeral. Even the gulls have gone quiet. Or they've gone to the palace for the sandwiches."]],
 "God rest him. He liked fish, at least."],
11:["(Pat rubs his hands together over nothing.) Cold's in my bones today. A drop of something warm would go down very well, if the world was a kind place.",[
  ["Is it ever a kind place?","Once a year, round Christmas, for about an hour. Then the hymn starts and spoils it."],
  ["What would you want, if you could have anything?","A bottle and a smoke, if I'm honest. If I'm being very honest, a door to shut. But I'm only honest on Sundays."],
  ["You're shivering.","I'm keeping warm. Shivering's the body's way of doing a jig without asking you first."]],
 "Go on, you've got places to be. I've got the sea to watch."],
12:["(Pat gazes out at the water.) The sea's grey all the way to Finland today. On a day like this you'd think nobody had ever crossed it.",[
  ["Have you ever crossed it?","Hundreds of times, on the steamer. Kingsgrad to Helsinki, Helsinki to Turku, Turku back. I could cook a stew in a force eight."],
  ["What's in Turku?","(He's quiet a moment.) A daughter. Leena. She'd be thirty now. I haven't written in eleven years, so she'll have stopped waiting."],
  ["Finland's not that far.","Far enough. Distance isn't miles, friend. It's how long since you last wrote."]],
 "Never mind me. The grey gets in your head on days like this."],
13:["(Pat scratches his beard.) Word is the cola lot are giving bottles away today. Free, to everybody on the island. Nothing's free.",[
  ["It's a promotion, I think.","That's the price, then. You drink it in the street and you're an advertisement. Clever people, the cola lot."],
  ["Would you drink one?","Eventually. The cap's good for a fishing lure, if you flatten it. The drink I could take or leave."],
  ["Kelp in cola. Odd idea.","Kelp's in everything here. The soup, the soap, the old men. Why not the fizzy pop."]],
 "If they hand out free coats next, tell them where to find me."],
14:["(Pat has combed his beard, more or less.) Big day, isn't it? Everyone's talking about the fourteenth. Even I'm talking about it, and nobody asks me anything.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches. Arguments. Somebody'll fall over. Big days are the same everywhere; it's only the hats that change."],
  ["Do you care who runs the island?","I care who runs the soup kitchen. The rest is up to the people with addresses."],
  ["You've tidied yourself up.","A man should look decent on a big day. In case history's watching. It never is, but in case."]],
 "Go and see it all. Tell me who fell over."],
15:["(Pat is whittling a bit of driftwood with a penknife.) Making a gull. It's going to be a gull. At the moment it's a potato.",[
  ["Who taught you to carve?","An old Swede on the steamer, between shifts. He carved a whole chess set. I've been making the same gull for eleven years."],
  ["It does look like a potato.","Honesty. I like that. Most people say 'oh, lovely' and look at their shoes."],
  ["Will you sell it?","To who? No, it's for Leena. If I ever send her anything, it'll be a gull. Or a potato."]],
 "Mind the shavings. They get in everything."],
16:["(Pat chuckles.) Heard there's a debate today. Anna and Erik shouting at each other in public. Wish I had a ticket.",[
  ["Whose side are you on?","The side with the free coffee. Erik's lot, then. Anna's lot do soup, but you have to clap after."],
  ["Is debate pointless?","No. It's better than the other thing. I've seen men argue with fists on the steamer. Words are cheaper to mend."],
  ["Should I report on it?","You should. Write that a hobo said they're both right half the time. That's more than most people manage."]],
 "If anyone throws a chair, save it for me. Good firewood."],
17:["(Pat looks up sharply.) Did you feel that? The ground went like a tram going over points. Just for a second.",[
  ["I think I felt something.","The gulls felt it. Every one of them went up at once. Gulls know before people do, and people know before newspapers."],
  ["Earthquakes here?","Never in my time. My granny said the mountain in the north was only sleeping. She said that about my grandfather, too."],
  ["Probably nothing.","Probably. I've said 'probably nothing' about a lot of things that turned out to be something."]],
 "Keep one eye on the hills. The other on your wallet."],
18:["(Pat points north.) See that, over the mountains? White, like a kettle on. That's the volcano steaming, or I'm a Dutchman.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Depends who you ask. Ask a scientist, he'll say 'monitor it'. Ask a hobo, he'll say move south. I'm about as south as you get."],
  ["Has it ever erupted?","Long before anybody wrote anything down. There's black glass on the north shore. That came from somewhere."],
  ["Pretty, though.","Pretty things in the sky are usually trouble. Ask any sailor about a red morning."]],
 "If the mountain starts talking, listen to it."],
19:["(Pat is holding the green bottle again.) I let someone read my letter. Hannu, from the newsstand. His mother was from Vyborg.",[
  ["What did it say?","It's to a Galina. I was right! From a sailor called Pyotr, saying he's sorry and he'll come home when the ship docks. That's all. Lovely."],
  ["Disappointed it isn't secrets?","No. Secrets are cheap on this island. A man saying sorry, that's rare. Worth more than any code."],
  ["Did he ever get home?","The letter doesn't say. That's the trouble with letters in bottles: they never get the reply."]],
 "I'll keep it. Pyotr's sorry ought to be somewhere safe."],
20:["(Pat is quiet, looking at the sea.) Pyotr and his Galina have got me thinking. About sorry. About Leena.",[
  ["Write to her.","With what? Pencil, I've got. Paper, I've got: the sea sends paper all the time. It's the words I haven't got."],
  ["What would you say?","'Your father's alive, he's sorry, he can still cook.' That's three things. Maybe it's enough."],
  ["She might not want to hear.","Might not. But Galina might not have wanted Pyotr's letter either. He sent it anyway."]],
 "Don't mind me. Old men get soft when the weather turns."],
21:["(Pat's beard is grey with ash, and he isn't brushing it off.) The mountain's gone up. The whole sky's the colour of my old apron. Are you all right, friend?",[
  ["I'm fine. Are you?","Coughing like a kettle, but alive. Everyone's alive, they're saying. That's the main thing, on a day like this."],
  ["What did you see?","A tower of black over the hills, and red under it, like a stove door opening. Then it rained grey. It's still raining grey."],
  ["Is anywhere safe?","South's safe, they say. The lava's going north, away from everyone. For once the island's luck is going the right way."]],
 "Cover your mouth with something. That ash gets in deep."],
22:["(Pat is coughing.) Sea's got ash on it like a skin on cold soup. The fish won't come up through that for a while.",[
  ["Are you eating?","Enough. Rosa's cart gave me a sausage when I said I'd seen the eruption. Story for a sausage. Fair trade."],
  ["What's it like everywhere else?","Everyone sweeping. Saltwick's grey, Kingsgrad's grey, the cola tram's grey. The whole island looks like an old photograph."],
  ["Will the fish come back?","The fish always come back. They've seen worse than us. They'll wait till we stop shouting."]],
 "Breathe through your scarf. I'm not joking."],
23:["(Pat is turning a smooth black stone in his fingers.) Volcano glass. The ash washed it up the beach, or brought it down from the hills. Look how it shines.",[
  ["It's beautiful.","The mountain makes something pretty and something dreadful in the same afternoon. Like a lot of people I know."],
  ["Is that for Leena too?","(He looks at it.) Maybe it is. A gull and a stone. She'll think her father's gone simple. She won't be wrong."],
  ["Worth anything?","To a tourist, a krone. To me, it's the first new thing the island's made in years."]],
 "Mind the ground. It's still twitching."],
24:["(Pat steadies himself as the ground shivers again.) Another one. That's the third today. The island's got the hiccups.",[
  ["Do the aftershocks worry you?","Not much. Nothing to fall on me but the sky, and that's been falling for days. I'm used to it."],
  ["Who's been helping people?","The army had lorries out clearing the roads. Even Gustavo's lot were sweeping. Everybody's neighbours when the sky falls in."],
  ["You're very calm.","When you've slept through a storm in the Gulf of Bothnia, an island coughing is nothing."]],
 "If it shakes again, sit down. It's easier on the knees."],
25:["(Pat has a scrap of paper and a stub of pencil.) I've started it. The letter. Got as far as 'Dear Leena'. Took me two days.",[
  ["That's the hardest part.","You'd think. But the next bit's harder. 'Dear Leena' is just her name. Everything after it's mine."],
  ["What comes next?","'It's your father.' Then I have to explain eleven years. On one bit of paper. With a pencil this big."],
  ["Want help writing it?","(He shakes his head.) Kind of you. But you're a writer, it'd come out too good. She'd know it wasn't me."]],
 "Don't tell anyone. A hobo writing letters. They'd talk."],
26:["(Pat is smiling at something.) Saw two of the circus folk on the sand at first light, a big man carrying a little woman on his shoulders. Practising, I suppose. Or in love.",[
  ["The circus at Lake Ilmari?","That's them. The path from the lake comes down to the beach. They wave at me. Circus people wave at everyone."],
  ["Have you been to the show?","Can't afford a ticket. But I've heard the band from the bridge, on a still night. That's my circus."],
  ["In love, probably.","Probably. The morning after a volcano, folk start loving each other more. Nothing like the sky falling to remind you."]],
 "Go on. The world's in a hurry this week."],
27:["(Pat tilts his head.) Everyone's rushing about. Red flags, cola lorries, oil men in good shoes. Something's coming to the boil.",[
  ["What have you heard?","That every party's saving its biggest trick for the end of the month. Like a ship's cook saving the good jam for the last night."],
  ["Who'll come out on top?","The one who listens to the sea, and none of them do. So: nobody I know."],
  ["Does it matter to you?","A bit. Whoever wins decides if the soup kitchen stays open. That's my politics: soup."]],
 "Keep your head down this week, friend. Heads up get knocked."],
28:["(Pat cocks his head at the noise from inland.) Hear that? Bands, shouting, somebody with a loudhailer. It's the twenty-eighth. Everyone's out.",[
  ["Are you going to watch?","From a distance. In a crowd, a man in my coat gets pushed to the back, or pushed out."],
  ["What would you shout?","'Mind the ash!' Nobody else is going to say it."],
  ["It's quite exciting.","It is. I'm not too old to feel it. The whole island's like the steamer the night before Helsinki: everyone on deck."]],
 "Go and see it. Come back and tell me the bits they won't print."],
29:["(Pat is watching the horizon.) Tomorrow it all gets settled, they say. Kings, colas, flags. I've been trying to remember what the island was like before all this.",[
  ["What was it like?","Quieter. Colder. A king who liked fish. Nobody counting votes, because there weren't any."],
  ["Will you miss the old days?","Some of them. Not the ones I spent on the steamer, drunk. The ones before that, maybe."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Me? I've nothing to lose. That's the only good thing about having nothing: you sleep through history."]],
 "Get some rest yourself. Tomorrow's a long one."],
30:["(Pat holds up a folded scrap of paper.) Finished it. My letter to Leena. Four lines. It took me eleven years and a volcano.",[
  ["What does it say?","That's between her and me. But it ends with 'your father, who is sorry, and can still cook'. That's the bit I'm proud of."],
  ["Will you send it?","I'll send it. By post, mind. Not by bottle. I've learnt that much from Pyotr."],
  ["Big day for the island too.","The island will be settled by tonight, they say. Good. So will I. Some days everything comes in on the same tide."]],
 "Whatever happens today, friend, the sea'll still be there tomorrow."],
};
// Petra, warehouse worker at the Kelp Cola factory and Union organiser (Kelp Cola). Threads: the freezing warehouse and the stove
// she's promised; organising the Union by night (Helen's visits, her letters and petition, the line between Union and Party); her
// word with Phil, which never quite comes; and the Union scarf she's knitting, one row for every member. Kurt is her neighbour.
DAILY['Petra']={
2:["(Petra is knitting beside a lukewarm radiator, a pamphlet open on the arm of her chair.) The reporter! Come in, but keep your coat on. The landlord thinks heating's a Communist plot.",[
  ["What are you knitting?","A scarf. One row for every Union member at Kelp Cola. It's going to be long."],
  ["What's the pamphlet?","Minutes of the last Union meeting. I took them. I take everything: minutes, notes, abuse."],
  ["It's cold in here.","This is warm. Wait till you see the warehouse."]],
 "Mind the stairs. The light's been out since August."],
3:["Kelp Cola HQ's open today and the warehouse isn't. So Phil's in a heated office, and I'm in here wearing three jumpers.",[
  ["Three jumpers?","Vest, jumper, cardigan, jumper. Four. I said three to sound tougher."],
  ["What do you want from Phil?","Heating in the warehouse, a raise for the line and five minutes of his time. The five minutes is the one he can't spare."],
  ["How's the scarf?","Forty-one rows. That's the factory floor done. The warehouse is next, and that's mostly me."]],
 "Tomorrow the factory road opens. I'll be back in the cold by eight."],
4:["(Petra is stacking crates, her breath steaming.) Welcome to the warehouse. It's colder in here than outside. I've measured.",[
  ["How is that possible?","Concrete floor, tin roof, and a window that won't shut. Science."],
  ["How many crates in here?","Four thousand two hundred, give or take Kurt's lunch."],
  ["What does your job involve?","Stack them in, stack them out, count them both ways. In between, organise the workers. The stacking's easier."]],
 "Don't touch the high stacks. They lean."],
5:["Helen's coming over from Saltwick for a Union meeting at my flat tomorrow. I've bought biscuits. Union biscuits: the cheap ones.",[
  ["Isn't Helen a Communist?","She is, and she leads the Union. The Union isn't the Party, and Helen knows the difference. Some of her friends don't."],
  ["Can a reporter come along?","No. You'd write it up, Phil would read it, and I'd be stacking crates in Lapland."],
  ["Kurt says he timed you yesterday.","He told you that? I timed him timing me. Eleven seconds, with his tongue out."]],
 "Not a word about the meeting. Biscuits are expensive."],
6:["Helen came. Nine of us in my flat, two chairs and one plate of biscuits. We've voted to ask Phil for a meeting. Politely. In writing.",[
  ["Will he say yes?","He'll say 'when things are calmer'. Things are never calmer."],
  ["Who came?","Aino, Bob and Kate from the fish plant, two lads from the line, and me. Helen did most of the talking. I did the biscuits."],
  ["How long's the scarf now?","Sixty rows. Aino's row is purple. She insisted."]],
 "If you've any biscuits, the Union accepts donations."],
7:["I've written to Phil. Two pages, very polite. I gave it to Elin, and Elin said she'd put it on his desk. Elin's desk is between me and his desk.",[
  ["Is Elin on your side?","Elin's on Elin's side. She's clever. I like her."],
  ["What does the letter say?","Heating, pay, a word. I put 'please' in three times. Helen said once was plenty."],
  ["Frostholm's open. Busier now?","More orders, more crates, same heating."]],
 "If you're going up to HQ, wave at my letter."],
8:["Sunday, and I'm in the warehouse, because Polar Burger wants forty crates by tomorrow and Kurt wants them yesterday.",[
  ["Heard about the oil rig?","Oil in the sea. The fishermen are furious. If the fish come in black, Bob and Kate will have the fish plant out in sympathy."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Only to sing. I like a hymn. I don't like a sermon. You'd think they'd have worked that out by now."],
  ["Any reply from Phil?","Not a word. I've counted the days. I count everything."]],
 "Close the door. The cold follows people in."],
9:["Saara came through asking what flavour I'd want in a cola. I said: hot. Any hot flavour. Hot soup, hot coffee, hot cola.",[
  ["Hot cola?","Don't laugh. Pirkko sells hot coffee from her van and does a roaring trade. Hot cola would sell like mad in this warehouse."],
  ["Any word on your letter?","Elin says Phil's 'considering it'. I've been considered before. It's cold."],
  ["How's the scarf coming on?","Eighty rows. It goes round me twice."]],
 "Tell Saara: hot. She wrote it down. I think she was being polite."],
10:["We're stopping for a minute's silence for the king. Kurt's got his stopwatch out. I'll be quiet for the king, and not a second longer for Kurt.",[
  ["What did you think of the king?","He opened the factory canteen in '64. They closed it in '71. I liked him. I liked the canteen more."],
  ["Has the Union sent flowers?","A small wreath, from all of us. Smaller than Phil's. Everything is."],
  ["Phil's at the funeral.","Then he's in a heated chapel. Good for him."]],
 "Sixty seconds. Then back to the crates."],
11:["I went up to HQ in my dinner hour. Got as far as the landing. Elin stood in front of Phil's door and wouldn't budge.",[
  ["What did you say?","Everything. Loudly. He was behind that door the whole time. You can't tell me he didn't hear."],
  ["Helen's at the mill today, I hear.","Fair pay for the mill hands. If they get something, we'll ask for the same. That's how a union works: one door at a time."],
  ["Did Kurt dock your pay?","He didn't. He thinks I don't know. I know. He's getting a cake."]],
 "One door at a time. I'll be back at that one."],
12:["Kurt's numbered every crate for the giveaway. I numbered them, he checked the numbers, and I checked his checking.",[
  ["Do crates ever go missing?","Now and then one walks. Usually towards Saltwick. I don't ask."],
  ["Free cola for everyone, then.","Free cola for the whole island, and the warehouse is still freezing. Phil can give away four thousand bottles, but not one stove."],
  ["How's the scarf?","A hundred rows. I've started on the fish plant. Bob wants his row in herring silver."]],
 "Early start tomorrow. Bring gloves if you're helping."],
13:["(Petra is waving crates out of the door, ticking a list.) Every crate's out, and I've watched every one like a hawk. Mind your feet.",[
  ["Can I have a bottle?","You're on the list. Everyone's on the list. Even the Communists, though Helen says she'll pour hers away."],
  ["Anything odd today?","There's a man in a good coat at the gate. Not a cola man: he hasn't looked at a crate once. He keeps looking up at the HQ windows."],
  ["You look busy.","I'll be counting till the lights go out."]],
 "Out of the way, please. Crate coming through."],
14:["The big day. The Union's going as one, in a block, with a banner. I made the banner. It says FAIR PAY. Short words carry further.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","That whoever comes out on top remembers who stacks the crates."],
  ["Bit of a short banner.","I had a long one: 'A Fair Wage and a Warm Warehouse for the Workers of Kelp Cola'. It fell over."],
  ["Are you wearing the scarf?","Of course. A hundred and twenty rows. Aino's wearing the other end."]],
 "Look for the purple row. That's us."],
15:["The palace and the Church are squabbling over which prince today. Nobody asked the warehouse. The warehouse would like a prince who brings a stove.",[
  ["Leopold or Augustus?","Leopold's never stacked a crate. Augustus has never stacked a crate. It's a hard choice."],
  ["Any reply from Phil?","Elin says he's written my name on his pad. My name, on his pad. In Kingsgrad, that's progress."],
  ["Do you take the tram home?","Every day. It's the only warm place I go. I'd live on it if they let me."]],
 "Whichever prince it is, tell him about the stove."],
16:["Kurt's put the wireless on for the debate. I'm for Erik. A vote's a union for everybody, isn't it?",[
  ["Why not Anna?","Anna would make Phil's factory the people's factory, and then the people would answer to Anna. I've met Anna."],
  ["Is Helen for Anna?","Helen's for Anna and for us. Most days she manages both. Today she'll be squirming."],
  ["Kurt says you cheer for Erik.","Kurt's timing my cheers. I've decided to cheer exactly as long as I like."]],
 "Turn it up on your way past. Kurt's turned it down again."],
17:["The ground moved and the stacks swayed. Four thousand crates, leaning like drunks. Then they stood up straight, as if nothing had happened.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","No. I've moved everyone away from the high stacks. Kurt wanted to time it. I told him where to put his stopwatch."],
  ["There's steam on the mountain.","If it gets any warmer, the warehouse might finally heat up. That's the Union's official position."],
  ["How's the scarf?","A hundred and forty rows. I knit faster when I'm frightened."]],
 "Stay away from the high stacks. They've had a fright."],
18:["Jaana's ill. She stood in the snow in that cup for a fortnight, waving. Nobody heats Eastbrook's pavements either.",[
  ["Is Jaana in the Union?","She's an Entertainer, she says. I've asked her to join twice. Both times she answered as Kelpy: 'Fizz-tastic!' That's not a yes."],
  ["Will she be all right?","A cold. I've sent soup over to Eastbrook. Union soup: cabbage."],
  ["Any news on the heating?","There's a rumour of a stove. Nobody says when."]],
 "If you see Jaana, tell her the soup's from the Union. It's a recruiting soup."],
19:["The Party's recruiting at the gate, and there are leaflets in the crates. Kurt's using them to wedge my window shut. My window, which I've been asking to have fixed for a year.",[
  ["Will you join the Party?","No. I'm Union. The Party wants the whole island. I want a stove."],
  ["So the window shuts now?","It does. Thanks to the Communist Party and a foreman with a gift for wedging. It's going in the minutes."],
  ["Phil says he's ordered you a stove.",["He said that? To you? (She stares.) From where?",[
    ["From Helsinki, he said.","Helsinki. So it's coming by boat, in September, to an island with a volcano. I'll believe it when I'm warm."],
    ["He seemed sincere.","Phil's always sincere. He's sincere about everything, all at once. It's very tiring."]]]]],
 "A stove. From Helsinki. I'll knit it a cover."],
20:["Helen says the Union's to be ready if the mountain goes. Ready for what, I asked. Ready, she said. Helen's very good at 'ready'.",[
  ["What's your plan?","Kurt's is the east door in ninety seconds. Mine is: grab the minutes, grab the scarf, run."],
  ["What's that smell?","That's the mountain. Or it's Bob's herring row. Hard to tell in here."],
  ["Any sign of the stove?","There's a space cleared for it in the corner. I've put a chair in it. It's the coldest chair on the island."]],
 "Remember: minutes, scarf, run."],
21:["(Petra's jumpers are grey with ash.) We got out. Eighty-eight seconds. I carried the minutes, the scarf and Aino, who froze at the door.",[
  ["Is Aino all right?","Shaken. She sang all the way across the yard, at the top of her voice. Good for her."],
  ["How's the warehouse?","Ash through the window. Kurt's wedge fell out. Every crate's grey."],
  ["At least the volcano was warm.","For an hour. Then the ash came down, and it's colder than ever. Even the mountain's on Phil's side."]],
 "Mind the ash on the floor. It's slippery as ice."],
22:["Kurt says I'm the fastest sweeper. He told Aino, who told me. He thinks I don't know. Everybody knows everything here.",[
  ["Heard about East Farm?","The farm grows for Kelp Cola, and the bosses are whispering. When the bosses whisper, it's usually about money or about us."],
  ["How's the scarf?","Ash in the wool. I'm knitting grey rows now. It's a historical record."],
  ["Will the stove still come?","Not this week, Elin says. But there's a chair keeping its place."]],
 "Grab a broom. Everyone else has."],
23:["Kurt's sleeping at the factory to guard it, so I'm feeding his pigeons. He gave me a list. Two pages. Timed.",[
  ["You and Kurt are friends now?","Neighbours. Same floor of the Kingsgrad Apartments. Disasters make you neighbourly."],
  ["Is the Party going to throw a spanner?","Not into my warehouse. Helen knows that if anyone throws anything in here, I'll throw it back."],
  ["How are the pigeons?","There's a royal one, he says. I didn't ask. Her eggs are warm, which is more than my warehouse is."]],
 "If you hear cooing on the roof, that's me, not the pigeons."],
24:["Kurt's back, with a bad back. The pigeons are fine. I fed the royal one first, out of protocol.",[
  ["What do you really think of Kurt?","A good foreman, a bad Union man, and a soft heart in ironed overalls."],
  ["Is anyone joining the Party?","Two lads from the line. They'll be back. The Party has more meetings than biscuits."],
  ["Have you written to Phil again?","Shorter this time. 'Dear Phil, it's still cold. Petra.' Elin laughed when she read it, and took it straight up."]],
 "Don't tell Kurt I said soft heart. He'd time how long it took him to recover."],
25:["Kurt signed my petition. The foreman! I've framed his signature, in my head.",[
  ["Why did he sign?","He says it's about the pigeons. It isn't. He's been cold for years too; he just times it instead of saying so."],
  ["How many signatures now?","Every name in the factory, the warehouse and the lab. Noora signed twice; I had to cross one out."],
  ["They say Princess Daphne's with the Democrats.","If she wants to learn about the people, she can stack crates for a day. I'll lend her a jumper."]],
 "Every name. Even the foreman's. Write that down."],
26:["Kurt took a crate of old bottles to the museum for some hush-hush advert. Hush-hush in this company means everybody knows by dinner.",[
  ["Who's in the advert?","Rosa says somebody very grand. Rosa says a lot. Usually she's right."],
  ["Any sign of the stove?","Elin says it's on a dockside in Helsinki, waiting for a boat. I've been waiting longer than a stove."],
  ["How long is the scarf?","Two hundred rows. It reaches from my door to Kurt's."]],
 "Somebody grand. Grand people never work in warehouses."],
27:["Foreign buyers tomorrow. Kurt wants the warehouse to look like a picture. I want them to see it as it really is. We've settled on clean and cold.",[
  ["Will the Union do anything?","I've a banner. I might unroll it. I might not. It depends on the coffee."],
  ["Who'd want to spoil the deal?","Not us. A deal abroad means work for years. We want the work. We'd just like it warm."],
  ["Nervous?","I'm a warehouse worker. I'm never nervous. Just cold."]],
 "Come and see tomorrow. Bring a jumper for the buyers."],
28:["(Petra stands very straight beside a stack, with a rolled banner under her bench.) They're coming through soon. If they ask me anything, I'm answering honestly. Kurt's terrified.",[
  ["Will you unroll the banner?","It says WARM WAREHOUSE, WARM WELCOME. Kurt helped with the wording. I'll decide when I see their shoes."],
  ["What will you tell the buyers?","That we make the best cola in the north, in the coldest warehouse in the north. Both true."],
  ["Good luck.","Luck's for the Republicans. We've got a petition."]],
 "Stand back from the door. They'll be here any minute."],
29:["Phil's given us the afternoon to go and cheer for Harald. I'm staying here. Somebody has to count the crates, and I'm not cheering for a Republican.",[
  ["Not even for Harald?","Harald's nice. He sends a card at Christmas. But a Republican's a Republican, even with good teeth."],
  ["Is the Union backing anyone?","The Communists and the Democrats are being polite to each other today. Helen calls it 'friendly rivalry'. I call it a tea party with knives."],
  ["Is the scarf finished?","One row to go. I'm saving it for tomorrow."]],
 "Close the door. The cheering lets the cold in."],
30:["(Petra is knitting the last row of the scarf.) The last row. It's for the island, whatever the island ends up as. Every colour I had left.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","Heat, pay, and a word with whoever's in charge. Same as always. A different person to ask, maybe."],
  ["Did you ever get your word with Phil?","Not a meeting. But he came down here himself yesterday, in his coat, and stood by the empty chair for five whole minutes. He said, 'Goodness, it's cold.' It's a start."],
  ["Show me the scarf.","(She unwinds it across the floor, all the way to the door.) Every worker in the company. Kurt's row is at the end. Ironed."]],
 "Wrap up warm out there. Whatever happens, winter's coming."],
};
// Phil, CEO of Kelp Cola, in his office upstairs at Kelp Cola HQ (Kelp Cola). Threads: his grandfather and the secret recipe
// (and who will inherit it), the hedge war with Dario next door in Hope Springs, Harald's run for the Republican leadership, the
// business (the giveaway, a secret advert, the foreign buyers), and Petra from the warehouse, who wants a word.
DAILY['Phil']={
3:["(Phil is polishing a trophy with his sleeve.) First day the road's open and the whole building's buzzing. Elin's taken forty telephone calls already. Sit, sit.",[
  ["What are all the trophies for?","Best Soft Drink in the Northern Baltic: 1958, 1961 and 1974. There were two entrants in 1974. We still won, and I'll hear no more about it."],
  ["How has the snow treated business?","Like a shut door. The factory's still snowed in behind Eastbrook, and the island's been drinking what it had in the cellar. Kurt rang me twice a day to tell me nothing had changed."],
  ["Who's the stern fellow in the portrait?","My grandfather. He started all this with a cooking pot and a bucket of kelp off the beach. He watches every penny I spend, even now."]],
 "Take a cold one from the fridge on your way out. Everybody does."],
4:["(Phil puts down the telephone, beaming.) That was the factory. The Eastbrook road's open, which means the line's open, which means Kurt's happy for the first time since August.",[
  ["Kurt's the foreman?","Kurt drinks a dozen bottles a day and times his own sneezes. Best foreman on the island, and he'd tell you so himself."],
  ["Is the museum open again too?","Wilma had it dusted before the plough reached her door. Kelpy's out on the pavement waving at snowdrifts."],
  ["Who's on the telephone more, you or Harald?","Harald. He's ringing every Republican on the island to ask after their mothers. He means it, too. Mostly."]],
 "Mind the stairs. They're polished; Elin insists."],
5:["(A blanket and a pillow are folded on the sofa.) I've slept in this office since the snow came. Tomorrow the road to Hope Springs opens, and I get my own bed back.",[
  ["Who are your neighbours out there?",["Gustavo on one side, who sends a ham at Christmas and never says what he does. Dario on the other, who sends nothing and says too much.",[
    ["Do you and Dario get on?","We share a hedge. He wants it taller, I want it shorter. That's the whole of Kelp Cola against Diversified Petroleum, really."],
    ["What does Gustavo do?","Imports, he says. I've never asked what. A man who sends a ham that good has earned a little privacy."]]]],
  ["The sofa looks comfortable enough.","It was my grandfather's. He never slept on it either: he nodded off at his desk with a pen in his hand. Family tradition."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola sending anything to the funeral?","The biggest wreath in the chapel. The king drank our cola every afternoon for fifty years. It's the least we can do, and I mean to do rather more than the least."]],
 "If you see the plough, tell it to hurry."],
6:["(Phil is in a good mood, and a fresh shirt.) My own bed last night, at last. And then at dawn Dario's man ran up a flag on his new flagpole, in a week of mourning.",[
  ["What flag?","Diversified Petroleum's colours, at full mast. The king's barely cold. I've written to the residents' committee."],
  ["Who's on the residents' committee?","Me, Dario and Gustavo. So the vote's going to be interesting."],
  ["How's Harald's campaign coming along?","He's got a smile you could bottle. I've told him to keep it to a respectable size until after the funeral."]],
 "Never buy a house with a hedge. That's my advice to the London papers."],
7:["Frostholm's open, so Dario's back at his desk, and the billboards on the Petro Tower are lit again. Ours is bigger. I had it measured.",[
  ["Have you been to Frostholm much?","As little as I can. It's all glass and neon up there. You can't hear yourself think for the sound of other people's money."],
  ["What is the Republican Party, exactly?","A club for people who sell things. I sell things. Harald's our man because he's the only one of us who can sit through a meeting without a drink."],
  ["Do you ever drink anything but cola?","Brandy, in the cabinet, on special occasions. A visit from the press is a special occasion. (He doesn't move to open it.)"]],
 "Give Frostholm my regards. Not too warm ones."],
8:["Have you heard? Something's gone wrong out on Dario's oil rig. He's been on the telephone all morning, and I could hear him through the hedge.",[
  ["Are you pleased?","I'm not pleased. I'm a businessman. I'm a little bit pleased."],
  ["Could it reach the beaches?","That's what worries me. My grandfather's kelp grows on that beach. If oil comes ashore, I'll take Dario's hedge down myself."],
  ["Do you go to church, on Sundays?","Christmas and funerals. Father Thyme says that's twice more than Dario."]],
 "Pray for the beaches, if you're the praying sort."],
9:["Saara's down in the lab muttering about cloudberries again. I asked her for a new flavour by the end of the month, and she looked at me as if I'd asked for the moon.",[
  ["What's wrong with cloudberry?","Nothing, if you're a jam. A cola has to taste like an adventure."],
  ["Why a new flavour now?","Because when an island changes hands, people buy something new to celebrate. I want that something to be ours."],
  ["Make it taste of money.","(He laughs.) Everything we make tastes of money. That's mostly the sugar."]],
 "If you have any bright ideas, the lab's downstairs."],
10:["(Phil is in a black suit, with a small Kelp Cola pin on the lapel.) The funeral's today. A bottle every afternoon for fifty years, sent up to the palace on ice, and now the order's stopped. I can't get used to it.",[
  ["Did you know the king well?","He came to the factory once, in '61. He pressed the button that starts the line, and asked if he could press it again. He did. Four times."],
  ["You're wearing a cola pin to a funeral?","Elin made me take it off. Then she put it back on, smaller. She's a diplomat, that girl."],
  ["Who are you sitting next to?","The ushers have put me beside Dario. Somebody at the palace has a sense of humour."]],
 "Straighten your tie before you go in. Everyone will be looking."],
11:["Petra from the warehouse came up the stairs today. Elin stopped her on the landing, but I heard every word through the door. She's got a good pair of lungs.",[
  ["Will you talk to her?","I will. When things are calmer. Things are never calmer, that's the trouble."],
  ["What does she want?","Heating in the warehouse, more pay, and five minutes of my time. The five minutes is the dear one."],
  ["The mill hands in Eastbrook want more too.","Dagny's business, not mine. But if the mill goes out, it'll travel down the tram line like a cold."]],
 "If you see Petra, tell her I heard her. That much is true."],
12:["Tomorrow every man, woman and child on the island gets a free bottle of Kelp Cola. Kurt's been counting crates in his sleep.",[
  ["Why give it away?","The first one's free and the second one's thirty cents. My grandfather gave tonic away at the harbour in 1911, and look at us now."],
  ["Won't some of the crates walk off?","Some will. A bottle in a thief's hand is still a bottle of Kelp Cola. But I'd rather it were in a customer's."],
  ["Does Dario ever give anything away?","Dario gives nothing away. Not even a nod at a funeral. I was watching."]],
 "Tell your readers: free, tomorrow, while stocks last."],
13:["(Phil checks the lock on the cabinet behind his desk, twice.) Elin says a man in a good coat has been asking the cleaners where the family keeps the recipe. The family keeps the recipe where the family keeps it.",[
  ["Where is it, then?",["(He laughs.) Nice try. You'd make a fine thief, for a journalist.",[
    ["Is it even written down?","Some of it. The rest is up here (he taps his head), and in my grandfather's handwriting, which nobody alive can read. Me included."],
    ["Who's the man in the good coat?","Nobody knows him. Which on this island means he works for somebody everybody knows."]]]],
  ["How are the free samples going?","I've sent everyone out with a crate, even Harald. He's handing them out like ballot papers."],
  ["Can I have a free sample?","(He takes a bottle from the desk drawer and hands it over.) There. Now you're part of the campaign."]],
 "If anyone asks you about recipes, you've never been in this office."],
14:["The big day at last. The whole island in one place. Elin's written me three things to say, one for each way it might go.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Speeches, cold feet and a great many thirsty people. We've a crate at every corner."],
  ["Which of the three speeches is best?","The short one. Elin wrote it. I'd vote for her."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm never nervous. My hands shake from the sugar."]],
 "Wrap up warm. History's always colder than it looks."],
15:["Leopold or Augustus? That's all anyone's asking me. I'll tell you what I told Father Thyme: I'll sell cola to whoever's wearing the hat.",[
  ["But which would you rather?","Leopold likes a party, and parties need cola. Augustus drinks water from a well. You can do the sums."],
  ["The Church wants Augustus.","The Church wants a great many things, and it's never once paid for a crate."],
  ["Do you use the tram yourself?","My grandfather laid that line so the workers could get to the factory and the tourists to the museum. The tourists never came. The workers are very grateful."]],
 "Pick a prince if you must. I'm picking both."],
16:["Anna and Erik are debating today, the whole island listening on the wireless. Neither of them likes cola companies, so at least they'll agree on something.",[
  ["Who'll win the debate?","Whoever stops talking first. The island's sick of speeches, and I'm paying for one of the candidates."],
  ["Will you listen?","Elin will. She'll tell me if I'm mentioned. I'm always mentioned."],
  ["Anna wants to nationalise you.","Anna wants to nationalise the weather. She'll find it's already state-owned: grey."]],
 "If they mention me, tell me what I said back."],
17:["Did you feel the floor go? My trophies did. 1974 fell flat on its face, and there's steam on the mountain. And Dario's car hasn't moved from his drive for two days.",[
  ["Was it an earthquake?","A little shiver, Saara says. Grandfather said the mountain breathes every forty years. He also said kelp cured gout."],
  ["Where's Dario got to?","Oil men disappear. They go to Houston and come back with a tan. I hope it's nothing worse: I'd have nobody to argue with about the hedge."],
  ["You've been watching his drive?","I've been at my window rather more than usual. That's not the same thing, and I'll thank you not to print it."]],
 "Put 1974 back on the shelf on your way out, would you? Facing the door."],
18:["Wilma telephoned. Jaana's poorly, and the museum's without a Kelpy. Without Kelpy it's just a room full of old bottles.",[
  ["Can't someone else wear the cup?","Someone will have to. Wilma's too short, Kurt won't, and I'm the chairman."],
  ["Who is Jaana, out of the costume?","A lovely girl, I'm told. I've never once seen her face. That's professionalism."],
  ["Is the ground still shaking?","Now and then, and the tea with it. I've told Elin to fill the cups half full."]],
 "Get well cards to the museum, Eastbrook. Wilma will pass them on."],
19:["Helen's lot are handing out leaflets at my factory gate again. Some of them turned up in the crates, folded very neatly.",[
  ["Are your workers listening?",["Some. I'd listen too, if I stacked crates in a warehouse without heating.",[
    ["Then heat it.","I have. I mean, I've ordered a stove. From Helsinki. It's coming. These things take time."],
    ["Have you spoken to Petra yet?","Not yet. I've got as far as writing her name on my pad. That's further than I've got with most people."]]]],
  ["They say a prince was seen at the Midnight Note.","Good for him. Jazz needs a royal patron, and the palace could use a drink."],
  ["Were you ever red, Phil?","At nineteen, for a fortnight. Then my father put me on the bottling line, and I discovered the joy of a day off."]],
 "If you find a leaflet in your cola, it's a free gift."],
20:["The steam's thicker on the mountain. Saara wants to go up and take samples, and I've told her the company insurance doesn't cover volcanoes.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Grandfather said it last went up before the war, and it only sulked. I've bought sandbags anyway."],
  ["What does she want to sample?","Gas, water, ash. She's a scientist. She sees a smoking mountain and thinks: interesting."],
  ["Who's the man at the Hotel Aurora?","A Russian who sells tractors, they say. He orders a crate of our cola a day and has never once asked the price. My kind of Russian."]],
 "If the ground goes, get under a desk. This one's oak."],
21:["(Phil's window is grey with ash.) Well. It went up. The sandbags were no use at all, but they made me feel better.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","Elin's counted heads. Every one of my people's accounted for, and Kurt's counted them twice."],
  ["What about the factory?","Ash on the roof and ash in the yard. Saara swears it won't get into the bottles. I've told her to swear it again, slower."],
  ["Will this change who rules the island?","A mountain doesn't vote. But it frightens people, and frightened people want a steady hand. I've said that before. It's truer today."]],
 "Shut the door behind you. The ash gets in anyway, but shut it."],
22:["East Farm grows for us, and something's wrong out there. Hilda wants it sorted quietly, so naturally everyone in this building knows. (He looks at you.) And now the London papers.",[
  ["What's wrong at the farm?","If I knew, it wouldn't be a problem, it'd be a plan. Olaf says it's the ash. Hilda says it's Olaf."],
  ["How's the clean-up going here?","Kurt has everyone out with brooms. The trophies are in a drawer till the shaking stops. 1974 is at the bottom."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola hiding something?","Every company's hiding something. Ours is a recipe. Dario's is a pipeline. Gustavo's is Gustavo."]],
 "Keep the farm out of the paper, there's a good reporter."],
23:["Word is the Party's planning to throw a spanner in somebody's works today, so Kurt's sleeping at the factory. He's taken his own pillow.",[
  ["Is Kurt all right with that?","He's taken a pillow and a crate of cola. He's happier there than at home, frankly."],
  ["Still having aftershocks?","Every hour or so. My drinks cabinet sounds like a sleigh at Christmas."],
  ["Dario's men are out mending the rig.","I hope it holds. Truly. Oil on my beach would be the end of my grandfather's kelp, and I'd sooner lose the hedge."]],
 "If you see a spanner, kick it into the sea."],
24:["I've no children, you know. Thirty years making cola, and never got round to a wife. The ash makes a man think about these things.",[
  ["So who gets the recipe?",["That's the question that keeps me awake. My nephew in Turku wants to sell the company to an American. Over my grandfather's dead body, which is in the churchyard and can't object.",[
    ["What about Saara?","Saara could make it better than I can. That's exactly why I can't give it to her. (He smiles.) I'm joking. Mostly."],
    ["What about Harald?","Harald would put his own face on the label. No."]]]],
  ["Any regrets, in thirty years?","1974. We bottled a liquorice cola. Nobody in this building has spoken of it since, and nor will you."],
  ["You could still marry.","At sixty-one, on a volcano, with a hedge dispute? Who'd have me?"]],
 "Don't put any of that in the paper. Especially the liquorice."],
25:["We're making a new advertisement. I can't tell you who's in it. Elin's sworn to secrecy, and so am I, and I'm the one who swore her.",[
  ["Who's the star?","Someone with a famous face. Not mine, sadly: Elin says my face is for radio."],
  ["They say Princess Daphne's courting the Democrats.","If she stands for anything, she'll want a drinks sponsor. My door's open."],
  ["Is this about the crown?","Everything's about the crown this month. Even the weather."]],
 "Ask me in a fortnight. I might tell you then."],
26:["Dario's buying the hot springs spa, they say. Who buys a spa on a volcano a week after it blew its top?",[
  ["Would you?","No. But I'd buy the water. It comes up fizzy all by itself. That's free fizz, and nobody's thought to bottle it."],
  ["How's the secret advert?","Secret. Everyone in the building's whispering, which means all Kingsgrad knows there's a secret, and nobody knows what."],
  ["Is Harald ready for the vote?","Harald's been ready since the day he was born. His mother says he came out smiling."]],
 "Not a word about the advert. Not even to Rosa. Especially not Rosa."],
27:["Two days to go. Tomorrow the foreign buyers come to look us over, and the day after, the Republicans choose a leader. I've not slept properly since the mountain went.",[
  ["Who are the buyers?","Men in good shoes from across the water. If they sign, Kelp Cola goes on sale abroad. My grandfather's kelp, in foreign shops."],
  ["Who'd want the deal to fail?","Dario, Gustavo and Anna. It's a short list if you leave out everyone else."],
  ["The Communists are taking over the cinema, I hear.","Otto's showing a Soviet film for the comrades, apparently. Two hours of tractors. I'll take the liquorice cola."]],
 "Get some sleep. One of us should."],
28:["(Phil is straightening his tie in the glass of the trophy shelf.) The buyers are in the building. Elin has hidden every bottle of the 1974.",[
  ["Good luck.","Luck's for the casino. I've got a better product and a nervous foreman."],
  ["What are they looking for?","A cola that travels. I've told them ours has been travelling since 1911: from the beach to the bottle."],
  ["And if they say no?","Then I sell to the island, as I always have. I'll still sleep. Badly, but I'll sleep."]],
 "Smile on the stairs. They're watching from the boardroom."],
29:["Harald's day. I've told the staff to go up to Frostholm and cheer, and anyone who isn't a Republican to go up and look like one.",[
  ["Will Harald win?","If smiles were votes he'd have won in August. Magnus has money, Carlo has friends. Harald has us."],
  ["Why does a cola company need a party?","Because a company without a voice gets told what to do. My grandfather took orders from a king. I'd sooner take them from a vote."],
  ["The palace and the Church are at it too.","Everybody's busy and nobody's buying. It's a terrible day for cola."]],
 "If you're going up to Frostholm, take a crate for the queue."],
30:["(Phil sits at his desk with an unopened bottle in front of him.) The last day. Whatever happens, somebody on this island will be thirsty tomorrow. I keep telling myself that.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm sixty-one and I run a cola company on a volcano. Nervous is the least of it."],
  ["What would your grandfather have wanted?","The island healthy, happy and drinking his tonic. No king's ever managed all three."],
  ["Open the bottle, then.","(He opens it, and pours two glasses.) To the island. Whoever gets it."]],
 "Go on. Go and watch it happen. And write it well."],
};
// Pirkko, who runs the HOT FOOD van outside the Kelp Cola factory, lives in Eastbrook (Orthodox Church). Threads: her old van
// Bertta, which was her late husband Pekka's; the leftovers she gives to whoever needs them; the factory gossip at her hatch; her
// campaign to get Brother Teodor's mushroom soup recipe out of him, one clue at a time.
DAILY['Pirkko']={
4:["(Pirkko bows her head over a tray of pasties for a moment, then looks up.) Sorry, love, grace first, customers second. Bless this food and the hands that pay for it. Now: what can I get you?",[
  ["What's good today?","Everything, because it's hot, and nothing else for a mile is. The burgers are honest and the coffee would wake a bishop."],
  ["You say grace over every pasty?","Every one. They're the Lord's pasties before they're your lunch. It only takes a second, and it's never once made them colder."],
  ["Is it always this quiet?","The roads only opened this morning. Tomorrow the factory'll be back on full shifts, and you won't get near the hatch for elbows."]],
 "Mind the step down. Bertta's a tall old van."],
5:["(Pirkko is stacking paper bags.) The road up the mountain opened today. I sent a box of cinnamon buns up with the milk lorry for the brothers. Brother Teodor will have them gone before the Abbot sees the lid.",[
  ["You know the monks well?","I go up every Sunday, rain or snow. Father Thyme's a saint, Brother Anselm's a saint with a ruler, and Brother Teodor makes the best soup on God's earth."],
  ["The best soup?","Mushroom. Twenty years I've been trying to make it, and twenty years he's laughed at me. He says the secret's love. It isn't. It's something in a jar."],
  ["A bun bribe, then?","A bun investment. One day he'll crack, and I'll have the recipe, and the mill hands will weep into their bowls."]],
 "Go on, you're letting the warm out."],
6:["(A man in a thin coat walks off with a paper bag, and Pirkko waves.) That's Veikko, from the Timber Lofts. Out of work since the spring. I give him what's left at the end of the shift. He pretends it's a favour to me, and I let him.",[
  ["That's kind of you.","It's what the food's for. God didn't make pasties to be thrown in a bin behind a cola factory."],
  ["Who else do you feed?","Hely, Veikko's friend, when she'll let me. Pat on the beach, when I can get a bag down there. Anyone who stands near the hatch looking at the menu too long."],
  ["Doesn't it cost you?","Some. But Pekka, my husband, God rest him, always said a van that feeds nobody for free is just a kitchen with wheels."]],
 "There's always a bun for anyone who needs one. Remember that."],
7:["(Pirkko is kicking the side of the van, gently.) Bertta's heater's packed up again. Pekka could fix her with a hairpin. I can only threaten her.",[
  ["Was this Pekka's van?","He drove her for the mill crews until his heart gave out, five winters back. I kept the van. I kept him in it, really."],
  ["Want me to have a look?","You're welcome to try. She's a Finnish van from nineteen sixty-four, and she doesn't respond to the English. Or to me."],
  ["Kicking it works on a jukebox.","(She kicks it again. The heater coughs into life.) Well, would you look at that. You can stay."]],
 "Bertta says thank you. Or that might be the exhaust."],
8:["(The van's shutter is half down, and Pirkko is putting on a good hat.) Sunday, love. I'm off up the mountain for the service. The first one since the king. Everyone'll be there.",[
  ["What do you hope Father Thyme says?","Something for the young ones. They sit at the back and look at their shoes. I'd like the Church to look at them back, for once."],
  ["Will Juniper be there?","In the front pew, in a hat with a brim like a cartwheel, checking everyone's hems. I sit behind her and count the pins."],
  ["Are you closing the van?","For the morning. The factory doesn't run Sundays, and God and Bertta both need a rest."]],
 "Come up if you can. They don't bite. Except Brother Anselm."],
9:["(Pirkko leans out of the hatch.) Half the factory's talking about a new flavour, and the other half's denying it. A girl from the lab came out and asked me what people on this island like to eat.",[
  ["What did you tell her?","Pea soup and gossip, I said. If they can put that in a bottle, I'll buy shares."],
  ["Does Kelp Cola treat its workers well?","They pay on Fridays and the canteen's terrible, which is good for me. The lads grumble, but they grumble about the weather too."],
  ["What would your perfect cola taste of?","Cardamom and a bit of salt. Like a Christmas bun that's been to sea. Don't tell them, they'll steal it."]],
 "Coffee's on the house if you bring me factory gossip."],
10:["(There's a black ribbon tied to Bertta's wing mirror, and the radio inside the van is playing the funeral, very low.) They're burying him now. I couldn't afford the morning off, so I'm listening.",[
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","Once, at the Kelp Cola factory opening. He bought a pasty and didn't eat it. Pekka said it was the most honest thing a king had ever done."],
  ["Who's leading the service?","Father Thyme. Listen to him, he's struggling with his voice. Bless him. He'll get through it on pure stubbornness."],
  ["Should I turn it up?","Leave it low, love. Grief should be quiet. The lads in the queue have all taken their caps off. That's enough."]],
 "God rest him. Now, can I get you something hot?"],
11:["Father Thyme telephoned the factory office, if you please, to ask me about Pat on the beach. What he likes to eat. Fifteen years I've been sending that man pasties, and now the Church wants a report.",[
  ["What does Pat like?","Anything with gravy, and anything I tell him not to have. He's not a bad man. He's just been cold for too long."],
  ["Do you think the Church can help him?","If they go to him properly, and listen. Not the blanket and the hymn and back up the mountain for tea. He hates the hymn."],
  ["You should be in charge of the alms.","Don't you dare say that to Brother Anselm. He'd have me in a habit by Thursday."]],
 "If you're going down to the beach, take him this bun from me."],
12:["(Pirkko is grinning.) I've got a clue. Brother Teodor came down with the milk lorry, had a pasty, and let slip that the secret's 'something from the forest that isn't a mushroom'.",[
  ["Pine needles?","I thought that! Then I thought, no, he'd never. Then I thought, he absolutely would. I'm trying it tonight."],
  ["Why not just ask him straight out?","I did, for twenty years. Then I tried buns. Buns work better than asking. Remember that, if you're ever a reporter. Oh, you are."],
  ["What other news from Eastbrook?","Juniper's got cowboy boots in the window, of all things. Some supplier's mistake. She's furious and she can't stop looking at them."]],
 "Try the pasty. It's got a secret too, and I'm not telling."],
13:["(The factory gate is piled with crates, and a man in a Kelpy hat is pressing bottles on everyone who walks past.) Free samples! They're giving away their own cola to their own workers. The lads are using them as doorstops.",[
  ["Has it hurt your trade?","Never. You can't eat a cola. Though one of the lads tried dipping a hot dog in it. I've asked God to forgive him."],
  ["What's the point of it?","Phil wants everyone on the island to have a bottle in their hand before anyone else gets a word in. The cola's the vote, in a way. Clever. Bit much."],
  ["Want a free cola?","I've got forty in the van, love. Have one of mine."]],
 "Take a sample, they won't miss it."],
14:["(Pirkko is loading the van with three times the usual sausages.) The fourteenth! I'm taking Bertta to the big day. Every soul on the island in one place, and all of them hungry.",[
  ["How many are you expecting?","I've done four hundred hot dogs, and I'll run out by noon. Then they'll eat the buns. Then they'll eat each other."],
  ["What do you think will happen there?","A lot of men making speeches with cold hands. I'll be at the back with the mustard, which is where the real business happens."],
  ["Need a hand?","You can carry the urn. It weighs as much as a novice."]],
 "Mustard's in the blue tub. Don't mix them up."],
15:["(Pirkko is saying a quick prayer over the coffee urn.) I'm praying for Prince Augustus today. They say he's being pulled two ways, the palace one way and the Church the other.",[
  ["Have you met him?","He came by the van last year in a plain coat, as if no one would know him. He paid for a pasty, said grace over it with me, and left a tip the size of my rent."],
  ["Which way should he go?","That's between him and God. I'd only say: let the boy choose for himself. Everyone's been choosing for him since he was christened."],
  ["Is it the Church's business?","Everything's the Church's business, love. Whether the Church should go telling people is a different question."]],
 "Pray for him too, if you're the praying kind."],
16:["(Two factory men are arguing at the hatch, and Pirkko bangs a ladle on the counter.) No politics at the hatch! Anna and Erik are at it on the wireless, and now my queue's at it too.",[
  ["Who were they for?","One's Party, one's Democrat, and both of them want ketchup. Bread unites, love. Politics divides. I keep telling them."],
  ["What do you make of the debate?","Erik's got a nice voice. Anna's got a sharp one. I'd vote for whoever promised to fix the tram heaters."],
  ["Can I talk politics if I buy a burger?","You can talk about the weather and the burger, and that's the menu."]],
 "Ketchup's on the side, politics stays in your pocket."],
17:["(The coffee in Pirkko's cups is shivering.) Did you feel that? The whole van rocked. I thought Bertta had finally died, and then the factory chimney swayed and I thought, no, it's the mountain.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","The old people say it rumbles every hundred years or so, and then goes back to sleep. I hope the old people are right. They usually aren't."],
  ["Any news from the factory?","Rumour is the oil man's gone missing. Dario. Nobody's seen him since last night. The lads have a sweepstake on where he turns up."],
  ["Spilled anything?","A whole tray of mustard down my apron. I look like I've been shot by a hot dog."]],
 "Hold on to your cup. Just in case."],
18:["(Pirkko has the telephone receiver from the factory booth pressed to her ear, and hangs it up looking pale.) That was Juniper. Something's gone missing from the monastery church. Something holy. Father Thyme's beside himself, she says.",[
  ["What's missing?","A relic, she said, off the altar. It's been there since before there were trams. Who would steal from God, on a mountain?"],
  ["Any idea who'd do it?","There's men on this island who'd steal the hinges off heaven's gate if there was a buyer. I'm not naming names. Gustavo."],
  ["What's going on at the factory today?","There's some fuss about Kelpy, the big cup at the museum. The office girls are all whispering, and nobody will tell me what. Strange days."]],
 "Say a prayer for the brothers. They'll be in a state."],
19:["(Pirkko is beaming.) Clue number two! Brother Teodor sent down a note with the milk: 'a spoonful of something sour'. That's all. A spoonful of something sour! The man's a sphinx in an apron.",[
  ["Lemon?","Lemon's not from the forest. Unless it's lingonberries. Oh, I bet it's lingonberries. Or vinegar. Or sorrow, knowing him."],
  ["You could just buy his soup.","He doesn't sell it. That's the maddening part. You have to climb a mountain and be humble."],
  ["Seen the steam on the mountain?","Everyone has. The lads say it looks like the island's having a smoke. I don't like it, love. Mountains should keep still."]],
 "Lingonberries. I'll try it tonight. Wish me luck."],
20:["(There's a candle burning on the dashboard of the van.) Five years ago today Pekka drove this van home, sat down in his chair and didn't get up again. I light a candle and sell pasties. He'd want the pasties.",[
  ["I'm sorry, Pirkko.","Don't be, love. He had a good life and a bad heart. I still talk to him in the van, when the queue's quiet. He disagrees with me about mustard."],
  ["What was he like?","Big, slow, kind. Fixed anything. Laughed at his own jokes before he'd finished them. Couldn't make soup to save his life."],
  ["Is that why you want Teodor's recipe?","(She smiles.) Pekka always said if he could have one bowl of the monks' soup a week, he'd live for ever. I'd like to make it once, for him. Daft, really."]],
 "Have a pasty on Pekka. He always gave one away on a hard day."],
21:["(Ash is falling on the factory yard. Pirkko has the hatch open and a huge pot on the stove.) Soup! Free soup, for anyone! Don't stand there gawping at the mountain, love, come and get something hot inside you.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","The factory's shut and the lads are all accounted for. The ash is in everything. I've just served Bjarne a coffee with a crust on it."],
  ["Shouldn't you get to shelter?","Where's safer than a hot van with a full pot? People are frightened. Frightened people need feeding. That's the whole of my theology."],
  ["What's in the soup?","Whatever I had. Potato, onion, the last of the sausages, and a prayer. It won't win prizes, but it's warm."]],
 "Bowls are on the side. Take two."],
22:["(Pirkko is wiping grey dust off the van's windscreen with a wet rag.) Ash on everything. Ash in the buns. I've had to put a cloth over the mustard.",[
  ["How's business after the eruption?","Better than ever. Nobody wants to cook in a kitchen full of dust. They'll all eat out of a van for a week."],
  ["Did you go to East Farm for potatoes?","First thing. Hilda was out in the field in a scarf over her mouth, and Olaf was shouting at the sky. She said the cola men are coming out to the farm today, about something. She didn't say what."],
  ["Bertta survived, then?","Bertta's survived two kings, a husband and a volcano. She'll see me out."]],
 "Brush yourself down before you get in a tram, love."],
23:["(There's a fresh dent in Bertta's side, and Pirkko is glaring at it.) The aftershock threw the fryer basket clean through the window. Lucky I wasn't stood in front of it.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","A burn on my thumb, that's all. I put butter on it, which is wrong, but Pekka's mother did it, and she lived to ninety."],
  ["Can it be fixed?","Ask the factory mechanic. He fixes cola machines. A van's a cola machine that goes along, isn't it?"],
  ["What's the gossip at the factory?","The Party lads are very busy whispering, and the foremen are very busy pretending not to notice. Something's afoot. I keep my hatch shut and my ears open."]],
 "Keep away from fryers today. The ground's not done with us."],
24:["(Pirkko is holding a little jar of grey-brown soup up to the light.) Brother Teodor sent this down before he went off on the monks' retreat. 'Taste, and work it out,' says the label. Cruel man.",[
  ["What does it taste of?","Mushrooms, of course. Something smoky. Something sour, like he said. And something underneath I can't name. It's like trying to recognise a voice through a wall."],
  ["Are the monks safe up there?","Brother Anselm's leading them. He'd frighten a volcano off. I packed them twenty pasties for the trail, and I'll bet Brother Teodor eats nine."],
  ["Let me taste.","(She lets you have one spoonful, and watches your face like a hawk.) Well? Nothing? You're no use. Nobody's any use."]],
 "If you work it out, you come straight here. Straight here."],
25:["Princess Daphne's been on the wireless all morning. Everyone wants her for their cause today. She came to the van once, you know, in a fur hat, and asked for a hot dog with everything.",[
  ["What was she like?","Sharp as a knife and twice as funny. She ate the hot dog walking, like a factory lad. I liked her very much."],
  ["Would she make a good queen?","She'd make a good anything. Whether she'd want it is the thing. You can't make a girl like that wear a crown if she doesn't fancy it."],
  ["Did she pay?","She paid double and told me not to tell her brother. I haven't. Until now. Oh dear."]],
 "Forget what I said about the princess, love. Have a bun."],
26:["(A crate of Kelp Cola is going past on a trolley, and Pirkko stares at it.) The lads say Phil wants a prince's face on every bottle. A royal on the cola! Pekka would've laughed himself silly.",[
  ["Would you drink it?","I'd drink it and feel bad about it, which is how I feel about most things from that factory."],
  ["And Dario's buying the spa, they say.","Let him. I've never been in a spa. I've got a bucket of hot water and a stool in the kitchen. That's my spa."],
  ["Any news of Teodor's recipe?","He's back from the retreat, all thin and holy. I sent up a pasty and one word: lingonberries? He sent back one word: warmer."]],
 "Warmer, he says. I'll get there. God's my witness."],
27:["(Pirkko is sitting on the van step with her shoes off, rubbing her feet.) Twelve hours today. The whole island's rushing about. The Party's showing a film in Kingsgrad, the Syndicate's playing cards, and I'm feeding the lot of them.",[
  ["You should rest.","I'll rest when I'm dead, love, and then I'll want a word with whoever made feet."],
  ["What's the film?","Something Russian about tractors, the lads say. Three hours long. Bring sandwiches. Bring two."],
  ["What will you do when this is all over?","Same as before. Pasties, coffee, Bertta, church on Sundays. Whoever's in charge, the factory lads will still want their dinner."]],
 "Put your coat on, it's turning raw."],
28:["(The van's queue goes round the corner. Pirkko is serving with both hands.) Every faction's out today, love! Everyone's pushing. The factory's shipping cola abroad, the Party's got something planned for Kingsgrad, the monks are up the mountain burning herbs. And all of them want a hot dog.",[
  ["How do you keep up?","Prayer and a second fryer. Mostly the fryer."],
  ["Are you going up to the Herbal Rite?","After the shift, if Bertta'll climb the hill. It smells like a summer meadow up there on rite day. I wouldn't miss it for all the cola in Kingsgrad."],
  ["Who do you want to win, Pirkko?","Whoever feeds the poor and keeps the churches open. I'd settle for one out of two."]],
 "Move along now, love, there's a queue. God bless."],
29:["(Pirkko is standing very still with a ladle in her hand.) I've got it. I think. I think I've got Brother Teodor's soup. I sent him a jar up the mountain this morning, and now I'm waiting like a girl at a dance.",[
  ["What was the secret?","Lingonberries, a splash of birch sap, and the mushrooms dried first, not fresh. I won't know for sure till he answers."],
  ["What if you're wrong?","Then I'll try again, and he'll laugh, and I'll bribe him with buns until Christmas. It's the best argument I've ever had."],
  ["Busy day on the island.","Line of succession, they're all saying. Who's next for the throne. I'm only worried about who's next in my queue."]],
 "Pray for my soup. And the island. In that order, today."],
30:["(Bertta's hatch is up, the urn is on and Pirkko is tying her apron.) Last day, they say. The island's future decided by tonight. And look at me, open as usual.",[
  ["Why open, today of all days?","Because people will be frightened, or happy, or drunk, and all three get hungry. Bread doesn't care who's king."],
  ["Did Teodor answer about the soup?","(She pulls a note from her apron.) One word, love. 'Yes.' Twenty years. I cried into the mustard."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That whoever wins remembers the people at the back of the queue. And that Bertta starts tomorrow. Both would be miracles."]],
 "Have a bun on me. Whatever happens, it's still a good bun."],
};
// Private Aho, a young soldier at the barracks (The Army). Threads: his terror of Sergeant Rask, the long letters he writes his mother each evening, Corporal Niemi's cooking, homesickness, the volcano and the island's troubles.
DAILY['Private Aho']={
6:["(Private Aho snaps to attention, drops his pen, and picks it up again.) Sir! Madam! Sorry. You're the British reporter. They said you'd be round. I thought you were the Sergeant.",[
  ["Relax. What are you writing?","A letter to my mother. I write every evening. Two months in, and it's four pages a day. She says she can't keep up."],
  ["Who's the Sergeant?","Sergeant Rask, the quartermaster. He counts everything. Boots, blankets, privates. I'm afraid I'm one short."],
  ["Is army life hard?","It's early mornings, cold porridge, and drill. I'd say it's not what the recruiting poster promised."]],
 "If the Sergeant asks, you never saw me writing."],
7:["(Aho is polishing a boot and doesn't stop.) The north-west road's open today, so there'll be more traffic past the barracks. Private Koski says he'll check every cart. He's very keen, now that he has something to do.",[
  ["What do you tell your mother?","That I'm eating well, and sleeping well, and haven't been shouted at. The first is a bit true."],
  ["Where's home?","A little village south of Frostholm. We keep goats. It's very quiet. I miss the goats more than I thought I would."],
  ["Does the Sergeant really shout?","Not shout. He says your name in a way that makes you stand up straight. It's worse."]],
 "Don't mention the boot. It's not as shiny as it should be."],
8:["(Aho is sitting on his bunk, reading a letter.) My mother's sent a cake. It came in the post. Corporal Niemi says there's a rule against cake in the barracks. I'm hiding it under the bed.",[
  ["What kind of cake?","Honey and nuts. She's baked it since I was small. It's slightly burnt on one side, as it always is."],
  ["Will you share it?","With Koski, maybe. Niemi, if he promises not to put it in the pot."],
  ["Is it Sunday?","It's the day of the sermon on the wireless. We listen in the mess. Father Thyme can't find words, it seems. I know how that feels."]],
 "If you smell honey, you've not been here."],
9:["(Aho is sorting a pile of socks into pairs.) Two hundred socks and no pairs. Sergeant Rask says it's a test. I think it's just socks.",[
  ["Why does the Sergeant fuss about socks?","He says an army marches on its feet. And on his accounts. He knows how many socks we had and how many we've lost."],
  ["Have you found a pair?","Eleven. Out of two hundred. I think someone's been stealing the left ones."],
  ["Do you have any news from the cola people?","A van came by with crates of the new formula. Niemi bought three, and drank one while pretending to inspect it."]],
 "Don't help. If Rask sees you, he'll think I'm slacking."],
10:["(Aho is in his best uniform, buttons shining, looking pale.) The funeral's today. The Colonel's gone to Kingsgrad with a full guard of honour. I'm not in it. I'm to stay and polish things. I'm sorry, and relieved.",[
  ["Why are you relieved?","I'd have dropped the rifle. I'd have sneezed. I'd have forgotten which way to turn."],
  ["Did you know the king?","I saw him once, at a parade. He looked at me, and I forgot how to salute. He nodded, kindly."],
  ["What will you tell your mother?","That I stood very still in a very clean uniform, thinking of him. Which is almost true."]],
 "Be quiet today. The barracks feels it."],
11:["(Aho is wrestling with a rolled blanket.) Everyone's talking about the Eastbrook mill and wages. The sergeant says soldiers don't have opinions on wages. I have one, but I keep it in my boot.",[
  ["What does the army pay?","Enough for stamps, and a tin of sweets on pay day. Not enough for a home."],
  ["Would you strike?","I'd be shot. Or worse, I'd have to run laps with Corporal Niemi's pot."],
  ["Do soldiers talk politics?","We're told not to. Which means we whisper. Koski says the Party's very loud, and the Democrats very quiet."]],
 "If you see Rask, say you've been lost. It works for me."],
12:["(Aho is leaning on the barracks door, staring at the sky.) A quiet day. Nobody's shouting, nobody's marching, nobody's lost a rifle. I'm suspicious. Is it a trap?",[
  ["Maybe it's just a quiet day.","They don't exist. Every quiet day is a loud one in disguise."],
  ["What would you do with a free day?","Sit in the sun and write to my mother. She'd say I'm being lazy. She'd be right."],
  ["What's Rask up to?","Counting. He's counting the rifles, the bullets and the rubber stamps. Something doesn't add up, and he's very quiet about it."]],
 "I'll go before the trap springs."],
13:["(Aho is holding a tiny bottle with great suspicion.) The cola people were here handing out samples. Niemi said it was an insult to his tea. Then he drank three.",[
  ["Is the new cola good?","It's all right. It makes your nose tingle. I've no idea if that's a good sign."],
  ["Are soldiers allowed free samples?","The Sergeant said if it's free it's a bribe. Then he took one, and held it, and put it back in his pocket."],
  ["Who gave them out?","A tall man with a smile and a clipboard. I'd never seen so many teeth."]],
 "Hide the bottle. The Sergeant counts those too."],
14:["(Aho is rehearsing a salute in the mirror.) Everybody's going to the gathering tomorrow, the whole island. We're on duty. I'm to stand at the side and look serious. I've been practising my serious face.",[
  ["What's your serious face like?","It's like a goat, but upright. Koski says it's fine. Niemi says it's alarming."],
  ["Are you nervous about tomorrow?","Terrified. All those people, all that noise, and me holding a rifle. I haven't loaded it, I think."],
  ["What does the Colonel expect?","Order, quiet, and for the army to stay out of everything. He's very clear about that."]],
 "Pray for me tomorrow. Or at least don't stand too close."],
15:["(Aho is washing mess tins and singing a very small song.) The royals and the Church are having a row today, crown against cross. I'm not in it. I'm in the sink. I prefer the sink.",[
  ["Whose side is the army on?","The Colonel says none. Though he dines with the royals so much he's practically a footstool."],
  ["What do you think?","I think the island should pick something and stop arguing. My mother says the same, about the goats."],
  ["Do you ever pray?","Every evening, in my letters. Dear Mother, please let the Sergeant lose his voice."]],
 "If you see the Sergeant, don't say I was singing."],
16:["(Aho is sitting by the mess radio.) Debate day. The Communists and the Democrats. We're not allowed to take sides, so we all listen very loudly in the same direction.",[
  ["Which direction?","Mostly the Democrats, I think. A vote sounds nice. My mother voted once, for the best-looking candidate."],
  ["Do the Communists bother the barracks?","A man in a cap tried to give Niemi a leaflet. Niemi gave him a bowl of soup. They parted friends."],
  ["Is the army worried?","The Colonel's very calm. That's how I know he's worried."]],
 "Keep your voice down. Rask has ears like a bat."],
17:["(Aho has gone pale and is gripping the bunk.) The floor moved. Did you feel it? Just a little. The cups rattled. I thought it was Sergeant Rask coming up the stairs.",[
  ["It was the ground shaking.","The volcano. I've heard of it. Koski says it's smoking up north. He's seen it from the tower."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Very. But I won't tell my mother. I'll say the floor was just a bit lively."],
  ["What did Rask do?","He checked the rifles for damage. Then he checked us. In that order."]],
 "If it does it again, I'll be under the bed."],
18:["(Aho is pinning a map to the wall.) Church people are hunting a relic, and some of them came to the barracks. A very polite young monk, asking to look in the armoury. Sergeant Rask said no, then yes, then walked him around himself.",[
  ["Did they find it?","Nothing but boots. And a very cross Sergeant."],
  ["What's a relic?","Something holy. A bone, or a thread. I don't know. I'd have thought the Church had enough."],
  ["Do soldiers believe in relics?","Koski has a lucky coin. Niemi has a lucky ladle. Does that count?"]],
 "If you find one, tell the monk. And don't tell the Sergeant."],
19:["(Aho is writing quickly, tongue between his teeth.) Dear Mother, there's a rumour that the Party's after the soldiers. A man with a red scarf was at the gate. Koski turned him away, very politely, then shook for an hour.",[
  ["Is the army in danger of being recruited?","The Colonel says we're not political. But I've seen the way he looks at the palace gates."],
  ["Are you tempted?","I'm tempted by cake. Politics has never offered me any."],
  ["What's the mood among the men?","Jumpy. A rumour a minute. Somebody says the royals are going to a nightclub in disguise. I don't believe it. It's too silly."]],
 "Let me finish this letter. She likes the gossip."],
20:["(Aho is cleaning a rifle that's already clean.) There's talk of a stranger at the Hotel Aurora in Eastbrook. Sergeant Hanna from the police was seen heading that way. I'm glad it's not the army's business.",[
  ["Who's the stranger?","I don't know. But there's a lot of whispering. Even Niemi's stopped singing."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","I hope not. I've three days' leave saved and I'd like to use them."],
  ["What does Rask think?","He thinks it's a good reason to count the bullets again. He has counted eleven times today."]],
 "Keep your head down. That's army advice, and it's free."],
21:["(Aho is covered in grey dust, standing at the gate with a shovel.) It's blown. The mountain. The sky's black as my boots. The Colonel called us all out to help clear the roads, and I've never been so glad to be told what to do.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far as we know. Lava up north, but nobody hurt. I wrote that three times in my letter. She'll worry."],
  ["Were you scared?","Terrified. I held on to Koski, and he held on to the guard post, and the post held on to the ground."],
  ["What's the army doing?","Digging. Sweeping. Hauling. It's the first time I've felt useful."]],
 "Wear a cloth over your face, and keep your boots dry."],
22:["(Aho is leaning on his shovel, ash on his eyebrows.) The barracks is a grey mess. Niemi's pot has a lid of ash. Even the Sergeant's moustache has gone white. It makes him look wise, and I won't say so.",[
  ["Is the cleaning going well?","It's endless. We sweep, and the wind blows it back. It's like the army's whole story."],
  ["What's happening in the district?","Cola people at some farm, and the royals buying a gift for the princess. Everyone's busy."],
  ["How's your mother?","She wrote. She's baking something. She says the ash won't reach the goats."]],
 "Don't breathe through your mouth. You'll taste the mountain."],
23:["(Aho is wrapped in a blanket and shivering, though it's not that cold.) The aftershocks are the worst. You never know when. It's like being poked by a giant, every so often, to check you're still awake.",[
  ["How are the others coping?","Koski reads under the covers. Niemi sings louder. And Rask stands in the doorway like a statue, which helps, I'll admit."],
  ["Do you sleep?","In pieces. I write letters to keep from thinking."],
  ["What do you write?","That I'm fine, the ground is a bit lively, and I've learnt to fold a blanket in six ways."]],
 "Try to sleep. If it shakes, hold on to something heavy."],
24:["(Aho is sitting on the barracks steps, writing.) The Church is going up to the peaks on a retreat today. The Colonel's sent two of us to guide them up the road. I'm not one of them. I'd have liked the fresh air.",[
  ["Who's going?","Koski, I think, or the other Private. I'm on potatoes with Niemi."],
  ["Is it dangerous in the mountains?","The ash is thick up there, and the road's bad. The monks are used to it. The army is used to being told where to stand."],
  ["Do you envy them?","A bit. They've got silence, and I've got Niemi's singing."]],
 "If you meet a monk, tell him to pray for my feet."],
25:["(Aho is looking at a leaflet with a crown printed on it.) A crown for everyone, the royals say. A princess for democracy, the Democrats say. Nobody's printed anything for soldiers. We'd like a sandwich.",[
  ["Whose side would you pick?","Whoever feeds the army. Niemi, probably. He's got a very loyal pot."],
  ["Are you worried?","The Colonel says stay calm. So I'm calm. Very calm. Look at my hands."],
  ["Do you ever think about leaving?","Every day. Then I think about my mother's face and the goats, and I stay."]],
 "If you see a crown, don't try it on. They're sticky with promises."],
26:["(Aho is helping a grumbling Niemi carry sacks.) They're saying Dario's buying the spa at Hope Springs, and the cola people want to put princes in bottles. It seems the whole island has lots of money and no sense.",[
  ["Is the army in on it?","We're not. We've got a spoon and a sock. Though the Colonel lives in the same block as Harald and Carlo, I'm told."],
  ["Do you know them?","Candidates for the Republican thing? No. They don't come to the barracks. They send their lawyers."],
  ["Do you mind?","I'd mind if someone stole the pot. The rest is above my pay."]],
 "Mind the sacks. They're heavier than they look."],
27:["(Aho is polishing the same button for the fifth time.) Everybody's tense. The Sergeant says there'll be a revolution at the pictures tomorrow. I've never been to the pictures. I'd have to be in a revolution to go.",[
  ["What will the army do?","Stand at the edge and look serious. The Colonel's orders. Which is what I'm best at."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Terribly. But my mother says brave people are the ones who're afraid and go anyway."],
  ["What do you hope?","That nobody shoots. That nobody's shot at. And that Niemi doesn't serve fish."]],
 "Wish me luck. Or at least wish Rask luck, so he's in a better mood."],
28:["(Aho is standing very straight, very pale.) It's today. The big push. State visit, red flags, the lot. The Colonel gave us a speech. I understood about half. It sounded brave.",[
  ["What did the Colonel say?","That the army serves the island, not any party. And to stand firm, whatever happens. And to wear clean socks."],
  ["Are you ready?","I've written my letter. I've polished my boots. I've said a small prayer to the goats."],
  ["What do you fear most?",["Letting everyone down. Dropping my rifle at the wrong time. Letting Rask see me cry.",[
    ["Would Rask mind?","He'd say nothing. And then he'd give me an extra blanket. He's like that. Don't tell anyone."],
    ["You'll be fine.","Thank you. My mother says that too. She's not always right, but it's nice."]]]]],
 "Stand firm. That's the whole trick."],
29:["(Aho is writing the longest letter of his life.) Dear Mother, tomorrow is the last day. The island's future is decided. I'm to guard a door. I don't know which, but it's an important one.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Sick. Proud. A bit sick again. It's hard to tell."],
  ["Is the army ready?","We're clean, fed and well drilled. Sergeant Rask has counted every button. We're as ready as soldiers get."],
  ["What will you do after?","Go home. See the goats. Tell everybody I was brave, even if I wasn't."]],
 "Sleep if you can. I'll keep watch for you."],
30:["(Aho stands at the barracks gate in the cold light, boots shining.) It's the last day. I've done what I was told, and I've survived the Sergeant. That's something.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Quiet. Like when the line of soldiers stops marching and you can hear the wind."],
  ["What will you tell your mother?","That I was a good soldier. And that the island's a lovely, difficult place."],
  ["Anything to say to London?","Say that Private Aho held the line. Even if it was just a door."]],
 "Take care, reporter. And thank you for talking to me."],
};
// Private Koski, sentry at the barracks guard post (The Army). Threads: checking everyone's papers, the cold and the hours, the adventure novels he secretly reads, who passes the gate, the volcano seen from the post.
DAILY['Private Koski']={
6:["(Private Koski slides something under his jacket and straightens up.) Halt! Papers, please. Name, business, and, uh, nothing else. Don't look at the book.",[
  ["What book?","(He coughs.) No book. A very flat piece of my uniform. Papers, please."],
  ["I'm the British reporter. Here's my pass.","Ah. Yes. Everyone's heard. It's all in order. Welcome to the guard post, which is mostly a hut with a very strong draught."],
  ["Is it a good job?","It's an honest one. It's me, the cold, and a very small stove. It's like being a snowman with a rifle."]],
 "Move along, please. Nothing to see. Absolutely nothing."],
7:["(Koski is stamping his feet.) The north-west road's open, so there's twice the traffic. Carts, vans, a man with a goat. I've checked them all. The goat had better papers than the man.",[
  ["Do you check everyone?","Everyone. Even the Colonel's car. He doesn't like it, but he stops. Rules are rules."],
  ["What's the most interesting thing you've seen?","A hearse, once. Then a wedding. Then a man with a pig. It's all life, in a queue."],
  ["Is it boring?","It's meant to be. I make it better with a bit of reading."]],
 "Keep moving. And don't mention the book."],
8:["(Koski has the guard post radio on low, tuned to a sermon.) Father Thyme's on the wireless, but I can't make it out for the static. Sounds like he's talking from the bottom of a well.",[
  ["Are you religious?","I say a prayer when the shift ends. It's usually quite sincere."],
  ["What's the book you're hiding?","(He sighs.) The Count of Monte Cristo. I've read it four times. I'm on a ship at the moment, in a storm."],
  ["Do the others know?","Only Niemi. He keeps it quiet. He wants to borrow the pirate one next."]],
 "I'll pretend you never saw it. You'll pretend I never read."],
9:["(Koski is rubbing his hands, breathing steam.) Seven hours into my shift and the hut's grown icicles. A van went by with crates of the new cola. The driver waved. It's the only wave I've had all day.",[
  ["What's it like out here?","Cold. Quiet. Long. Like waiting for a bus that's been cancelled."],
  ["Do you drink cola?","On rest days. It warms the pocket, if you keep it near your chest."],
  ["Have you seen the Communists' version?","A boy handed me a sample. It was the colour of a brick. I put it in the stove."]],
 "Watch the ice on the path. I've fallen twice."],
10:["(Koski is standing extra straight.) A sad day. The Colonel's gone with the guard of honour to Kingsgrad. I'm told to hold the post and keep an eye on the road. A hearse passed an hour ago, and everyone stopped.",[
  ["Did you stop too?","I took my hat off. It's against regulations, but I'd do it again."],
  ["Did you see the king?","In a parade. He had a very straight back, I remember. I've tried to copy it."],
  ["How's the gate today?","Quiet. Very quiet. Even the crows have gone."]],
 "Pay your respects, and move on. He wouldn't want you shivering."],
11:["(Koski is checking a cart.) A lot of mill hands on the road today, with banners. Fair Day's Pay. I'm not allowed an opinion on that. But I read the banners. Out of duty.",[
  ["What did the banners say?","Mostly, Pay us properly. Very clear. I admired the lettering."],
  ["What does the army think of wages?","We're told not to. But a man in a hut at midnight has plenty of time."],
  ["Do you earn enough?","Enough for novels. I buy one a month, and swap with Niemi."]],
 "Stay in line, please, and keep your banner low."],
12:["(Koski has his nose in the pages and doesn't look up.) Halt. Oh, it's you. Sorry. I was in a cave with a treasure and a very angry pirate. Is it that time already?",[
  ["What's the book?","'The Sea Wolf.' Not mine. Borrowed. I'll be shot if Rask finds it."],
  ["Why do you read adventure stories?","Because out here, nothing happens. In there, everything does."],
  ["Is anything happening at the gate?","A fine nothing. Though a van from Frostholm passed twice. I wrote down the number. It's the most excitement I've had."]],
 "Keep it quiet. And if you see Rask, cough."],
13:["(Koski is holding a sample bottle at arm's length.) The cola people were here. A tall man with a very large smile gave me this and told me to enjoy. I haven't decided if it's a bribe.",[
  ["Is it safe to drink?","It's cola, I'm fairly sure. But I've read too many novels. I keep checking for poison."],
  ["Why did he give you one?","Because I'm at the gate. Everyone says hello to the gate. It's good for business."],
  ["Will you drink it?","At the end of my shift. In a dark corner, like a spy."]],
 "If I'm found collapsed, it was the cola."],
14:["(Koski is polishing the barrier.) The whole island's gathering tomorrow, and I'm to stay at my post. Typical. Everything happens somewhere else. I'm like the lighthouse keeper in my novel. All I get is a view.",[
  ["Would you like to go?","I'd like to see it. I've read about crowds, but never been in one."],
  ["What do you expect?","Shouting, flags, a lot of people in good hats. And perhaps one very brave pigeon."],
  ["Will the army be there?","The Colonel will. And some of the men. I'll hear it from here, on the wind, like a siege."]],
 "I'll keep the gate. You keep the story."],
15:["(Koski is looking at the sky.) Crown before cross, cross before crown. I read about a war like that once, in a book. It ended with a siege and a very large cake.",[
  ["Whose side is the army on?","We keep out of it. The Colonel's firm. Though he's very fond of palace dinners."],
  ["Do you have an opinion?","I think a crown looks nice, and a cross looks nicer. I'd have both on the same flag."],
  ["Is the gate busy?","Priests, mostly, going up and down. They're very polite. They ask after the weather."]],
 "If you meet a priest, bow. If you meet a royal, curtsey. If you meet Rask, run."],
16:["(Koski has the radio in his lap.) Debate day on the wireless. Communists and Democrats. I'm not supposed to listen on duty. So I'm listening off duty, on duty.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The one who shouts last. It's like a ship battle, with microphones."],
  ["Which do you prefer?","I like a good plot, and the Democrats have the better ending. A vote, a hero, a happy crowd."],
  ["Do you vote?","Never been asked. I'd vote for whoever got me a heater."]],
 "Turn the dial, if you like. Just keep the volume low."],
17:["(Koski grabs the doorframe.) Whoa! The hut shook! The whole guard post gave a shudder. My book fell off the shelf. Is that normal?",[
  ["It's a small earthquake.","I've seen the steam from here. There's a plume over the northern hills. It looks like a dragon's yawning."],
  ["Are you worried?","A bit. But I've a thick coat and a very sturdy hut."],
  ["What did Rask say?","He rang down to ask if the gate was still standing. I said yes. He said good. He was very emotional."]],
 "If it shakes again, hold the barrier. It's good luck."],
18:["(Koski is checking papers with extra care.) Two young monks came by, asking if they could look around for a relic. I let them in on my own authority. If I've lost my job, it's for a saint.",[
  ["What's the relic?","They wouldn't say. Something very small, they said, and very holy. They looked in the stove."],
  ["Did you help?","I looked under the hut. I found a mouse, a button and my other glove."],
  ["Do you believe in relics?","In stories, yes. In real ones, I'm cautious."]],
 "If it turns up, I'll have you to thank. Or blame."],
19:["(Koski is half in the book, half at the gate.) There's a lot of Party people on the road today, handing out leaflets. One tried to recruit me. He said Communism's got excellent adventures in it. I said I'd prefer pirates.",[
  ["What did he say?","That the revolution's like a great sea voyage. I said sea voyages have captains. He said, Exactly."],
  ["Is the army likely to join?","The Colonel's against it. Rask, too. Niemi might, if there's soup."],
  ["Have you heard about the Midnight Note?","The royals are going in disguise, I'm told. A very ripping yarn. I'd give a week's pay to see it."]],
 "If they offer you a leaflet, take two. Good for kindling."],
20:["(Koski is leaning forward, looking at the road.) There's a man at the Hotel Aurora, they say. A stranger. Sergeant Hanna's looking for him, and half the gate's been questioned. They asked me if I'd seen a man with a hat.",[
  ["Have you?","Seven. All of them with hats. It's winter."],
  ["Is it serious?","The way they ask, yes. Like in a book. The wrong question and the plot turns."],
  ["Do you like mysteries?","I love them. It's why I never leave the post. I might miss the next clue."]],
 "If you see the man in the hat, remember: he's not the one you think."],
21:["(Koski stands in the hut doorway, covered in grey dust.) It's gone up. The mountain. A great black cloud over the north. I've never seen such a thing. I dropped my book and it's covered in ash.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","I think so. They've called all the sentries in. The Colonel's on the telephone, shouting."],
  ["What did it look like?","Like the end of a very long chapter. The sky changed colour. Then the ground rumbled."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm frozen. Like a rabbit. A rabbit with a rifle."]],
 "Wear a scarf over your face. It helps with the ash."],
22:["(Koski is sweeping the guard post path.) Ash on everything. My hut looks like a very sad cake. A van went by with a cola crest on the door, heading for a farm. They said there was trouble. Something to do with a farm.",[
  ["What sort of trouble?","They didn't say. They looked cross. I wrote the registration down, just in case."],
  ["Is it any of the army's business?","The Colonel says it's the police's. Rask says it's the quartermaster's. Neither of them wants it."],
  ["How's the post holding up?","The stove's out, the roof's heavy, and my feet are grey. But we hold."]],
 "Keep to the road. The ash is deeper at the edges."],
23:["(Koski flinches at a small tremor.) Another one. They keep coming. I've started to feel them before they arrive, like a sailor in my book. The hut sways, my teeth buzz, and I think, here we go.",[
  ["Does it scare you?","Less than it did. You get used to the rhythm. It's like being rocked in a cold cradle."],
  ["What helps?","A good book, and a strong cup of something hot. Niemi sends tea."],
  ["Are the men coping?","Aho's the worst. He's writing to his mother with a shaking hand. His letters wobble."]],
 "If you feel it, stand still. Panic only makes it worse."],
24:["(Koski is watching a line of figures far off on the mountain road.) The Church's retreat, going up to the peaks. Little black dots in the grey. Like ants, climbing a dusty loaf. It's beautiful, in an odd way.",[
  ["Do you wish you were there?","A bit. Silence, a good view, and nobody asking for my papers."],
  ["Is the road safe?","The Colonel sent two men ahead. They radioed that it's passable. Mostly."],
  ["What are the monks like?","Polite. They bless the barrier. I've never been so well protected."]],
 "Say a prayer for the climbers. And for the gate."],
25:["(Koski is reading a leaflet with a crown on it.) A crown for everyone, it says. Imagine. A whole island full of kings. We'd need a lot of thrones.",[
  ["Do you want a crown?","If it's warm. A fur one, perhaps. With ear flaps."],
  ["What about the princess?","The Democrats want her. The royals want her. I want her to have a say. It's an old story, but it needs a good ending."],
  ["Does the army care?","We care about our supper, and the Colonel's mood."]],
 "If you meet a king, show him my hut. It's cosy."],
26:["(Koski is taking a bite of a sandwich and trying not to get ash in it.) They say Dario's bought the spa at Hope Springs. A spa! There's a villain in a book who does that. Then he drains the lake. I'm keeping an eye out.",[
  ["Is Dario a villain?","He's an oil man. In novels, that's the same thing."],
  ["Are the cola people up to something?","Princes in bottles. It's clever. You'd buy it just to see who's inside."],
  ["Would you collect them?","I'd collect all seven, if there were a prize. A little tin soldier, perhaps."]],
 "Eat something warm. And keep the paper out of the ash."],
27:["(Koski is jittery, tapping the barrier.) There's to be a revolution at the pictures tomorrow, and high stakes at some card table. I've never been to either. All I've got is the gate and a hut.",[
  ["Are you worried about tomorrow?","I'm worried everyone's going to want to come through at once."],
  ["What will the army do?","Stand by. Look sharp. Keep order. It's what we do."],
  ["Would you go to the pictures?","If it was a western. Something with a sunset and a long ride."]],
 "Keep to the road. I'll be here, as always."],
28:["(Koski is standing with great dignity, and a small blanket.) Today's the big push. The state visit, the Party's flags, the cola going abroad. All through my gate, I should think. I've brought two flasks and a spare pencil.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","Like a sentry in a siege. But sentries don't run. They count."],
  ["What will you do if there's trouble?","Call the Sergeant. Then the Colonel. Then Niemi, for soup."],
  ["What do you hope for?",["A quiet day, a clear road, and an ending that makes sense. Like a good book.",[
    ["What would your perfect ending be?","Everyone shakes hands, the volcano goes to sleep, and the sentry gets a promotion."],
    ["Would you like a promotion?","To corporal. With a stove. I'd be a very good corporal."]]]]],
 "Eyes open, feet warm, and papers ready."],
29:["(Koski is checking and rechecking the barrier.) The eve of the final day. Primaries, lines of succession. I don't understand any of it, but the road's full. Everyone's going somewhere in a hurry.",[
  ["Is the gate busy?","Busier than ever. I've checked four hundred papers, and one very damp sandwich."],
  ["Are you ready for tomorrow?","As ready as a hut can be. I've a full flask and an empty mind."],
  ["Will you read tonight?","I'll finish the last chapter. If the hero dies, I'll resign."]],
 "Pass, friend. And good luck."],
30:["(Koski is at the gate before dawn, breath steaming.) The last day. I was told to hold the post, so I'm holding it. I've held it for a month. It's very odd to think it'll be over tonight.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Cold. Proud. Slightly hungry. Like a hero in chapter thirty."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash, the shaking, and the man with the goat."],
  ["Anything for the paper?","Tell them the army kept the gate. Quietly, and in all weathers."]],
 "Pass, reporter. Take your story and go."],
};
// Raili, out of work in Frostholm since the hotel cut its staff, who looks for jobs by day and listens to Radio Sarivalto by evening (The Unemployed).
// Threads: her job hunt and the hotel, Kaisa's radio shows, Arto her friend, The Midnight Note on evenings, her dry view of the factions.
DAILY['Raili']={
7:["(A woman in a pink scarf looks up from a folded newspaper, and the pencil in her other hand goes behind her ear.) Job pages. Third time round. Nothing but 'experienced fishmonger wanted'. You're the reporter. Arto's told me.",[
  ["Arto talks about me?","He talks about everything. It's how he stays warm. Don't mind him. He's got a heart the size of a herring barrel."],
  ["What was your last job?","Chambermaid at the Grand. Eight years. They cut the staff in June and kept the flowers. The flowers earn more than I did."],
  ["Is there work in Frostholm?","There's the oil company, which doesn't want me. And there's a man at the docks who wants a wife. That's the offer."]],
 "If you hear of a job, tell the phone box. Arto'll pass it on."],
8:["(Raili has a radio the size of a loaf under her arm.) Sunday. Kaisa's on. She plays a lot of jazz on Sundays, when the monks aren't listening. I take it everywhere. Even the library.",[
  ["Do you listen all day?","In the evenings, mostly. By day I look for work. By evening I look for company. Radio Sarivalto gives me both."],
  ["Is Kaisa good?","She's the best voice on the island. She sounds like she's smiling at you across a table."],
  ["Do you ever ring in?","Once. I asked for 'Moon River'. She played it. I cried in a laundry basket."]],
 "Don't tell Arto I told you. He thinks it's a secret."],
9:["(Raili wrinkles her nose at a leaflet.) 'The People's Cola.' I've been asked three times today to sign a petition for a cola. I said, 'I'll sign for a job.' The girl wrote that down. She was very sincere.",[
  ["Would you work for the Party?","If they pay. I worked for the hotel and it was run by a man called Mr Fish. It can't be worse."],
  ["Is Arto interested?","Arto's interested in a sandwich with a promise of work in it. He's a man of simple appetites."],
  ["Do you vote?","I've never missed one. It's the one job I always get."]],
 "If you meet a Communist who can type, send them to me. I've a typewriter and a grievance."],
10:["(Raili has the radio on, low, a state funeral murmuring through the static.) The coverage is very good. I've been sitting here since seven. They've played a slow march, and a man in the crowd shouted 'God save the king' and was shushed.",[
  ["Did you like the king?","Yes. He was a man who knew when to say nothing. It's the greatest quality in a ruler, and the rarest."],
  ["How do you feel?","Sad. And a little hopeful, which is odd. A new reign might mean new jobs. I'm sorry, that's callous."],
  ["Is Arto listening?","He's outside, on the boulevard, with a shopkeeper's radio. We compare notes later. He hears the same thing and gets a different story."]],
 "Shh. They're about to play the anthem."],
11:["(Raili is cutting an advertisement from a paper.) The mill hands are asking for fair pay, and I wish them luck. I'd be happy to be exploited, if it came with a salary.",[
  ["Is that a joke?","It's a bitter one. I've learned the trick: say it out loud, and it hurts less."],
  ["Do you support the strike?","With a whole heart and an empty handbag. They've something to lose. We've something to win."],
  ["Is it fair, what they ask?","It's more than fair. It's what my mother called 'decent'. She said it in a whisper, as though it were a kind of swear word."]],
 "Put a word in for the ones who aren't allowed to strike, because they've nothing to strike from."],
12:["(Raili's got a stub of pencil, and a list.) Four interviews this week. Two cancelled, one 'we'll be in touch', and one with a man who spent forty minutes telling me about his boat.",[
  ["What was the boat like?","Very large, very white, and very rarely used. I think he wanted a deckhand, or a witness."],
  ["Did you get the job?","No. I said his boat was beautiful, and he said, 'You're very honest.' It's the quickest I've ever been dismissed."],
  ["What do you want to do?","Work with people. Look after something. A hotel, a garden, a ward. Anything where you leave a place better than you found it."]],
 "A good chambermaid's an unsung thing. Remember that, when you pay for your room."],
13:["(Raili holds up a small bottle.) Free cola. They gave Arto four. He gave me one. It's the first sweet thing I've tasted since the strawberry jam at Easter.",[
  ["Is it any good?","It's like a hug with bubbles. I know it's advertising. I don't mind being hugged by advertising."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola hiring?","I've asked. They said 'We'll let you know.' It's the nicest brush-off I've been given. It had a little bow."],
  ["Do you listen to the adverts?","On the radio. They're better than the news. They don't promise anything they can't deliver. Mostly fizz."]],
 "Finish it before the bubbles go. They're the best part."],
14:["(Raili wears a good hat.) The big day. They've announced it on the radio, over and over. Kaisa said, 'Be there or be square.' I'd not heard her use a slogan before. It suited her.",[
  ["What do you expect?","A crowd. A lot of promises. A lot of men in coats. I'll listen to everyone and then decide for myself."],
  ["Are you going with Arto?","I'm going ahead. I like to find a good spot. He'll follow, with a sandwich and a list of everyone who owes him a favour."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","Like a woman who's got a ticket for a raffle. She doesn't expect to win, but she's looking at the prize."]],
 "Save me a seat if you see an empty one. I'll do the same for you."],
15:["(Raili sits down, kicks off a shoe and rubs her heel.) That's the longest I've stood in my life. But I heard it all. The speeches, the shouting, a man on a ladder saying nothing for ten minutes.",[
  ["What was best?","A girl, sixteen or so, who stood on a crate and said, 'Why don't we ask everyone what they want?' It was quiet for a bit. Then somebody clapped."],
  ["Did anything change your mind?","No. But a few things reminded me why I've got one. It's easy to forget, when you're looking for work."],
  ["How's Arto?","Exhausted and thrilled. He's been handed six promises and a badge. He's wearing the badge like a medal."]],
 "I'll sleep like a log. A very small, grateful log."],
16:["(Raili's got the radio on, with a little flag tied to the aerial.) The debate's live on Radio Sarivalto. Kaisa's introducing. I've got a blanket, a cup of tea and a pencil to take notes.",[
  ["Who's winning?","It's the Reds' round. Anna's got the megaphone. But Erik's got the quiet. I think the quiet'll win."],
  ["Why are you taking notes?","In case someone asks. And someone will. People always ask the one who listens."],
  ["Do you want to speak?","I'd love to. I've plenty to say. But I'll leave it to the ones in good shoes."]],
 "I'll tell you the result. If there is one. There's usually just a draw."],
17:["(Raili clutches the radio as the table rattles.) The tremors. I felt it in my teacup. The radio went to static, and then came back. Kaisa said, 'Stay calm,' very calmly.",[
  ["Was it the volcano?","Nobody knows. But the radio's had four calls from the north in an hour. They say the springs are hotter and the steam's thicker."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Not yet. I've a head for the daily and a heart for the slow. This isn't a daily thing."],
  ["Is Arto worried?","He's thrilled. He's convinced it'll mean work, clearing up. I told him that's a terrible way to look at a disaster. He agreed, and kept smiling."]],
 "If it gets worse, turn on the radio. It's the best company."],
18:["(Raili has a newspaper cutting in her hand.) There's a notice at the hotel, and I'm going to go in. They're looking for temporary staff, for something called 'Inside Kelpy'. It's a campaign, I think.",[
  ["Are you going to apply?","I'm going to look the manager in the eye and say I've eight years of experience in making beds. It's not much, but it's mine."],
  ["What would you like to do?","Anything. But not a cup. Arto wants to be the cup. He'd be awful."],
  ["Is the Grand hiring?","It's hiring a handful. That's a great many more than yesterday."]],
 "Wish me luck. And a decent interview coat."],
19:["(Raili sits very still.) That was stronger. The wall clock fell off its nail. It's stopped at ten past nine. I'm going to leave it there. It seems proper.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","I think so. The radio's saying 'tremor activity'. It's a polite phrase for 'we're a bit scared'."],
  ["Do you feel safe?","Not entirely. But I've the radio, a blanket and a very good neighbour. Magnus lives downstairs and has a torch."],
  ["Do you know Magnus?","To nod at. He wears a very clean coat. He once held the lift door for me, and said nothing. It was the nicest thing a Diversified man ever did."]],
 "If the floor shakes, hold the radio. It's the only thing worth saving."],
20:["(Raili's got a damp cloth over her nose.) You can taste it. It's in the tea. Sulphur, like the end of a match. Kaisa says to stay calm and drink water.",[
  ["Do you think it'll go?","It's going to. We all know. It's rather like waiting for a letter. You know what it says, you just don't know when it'll arrive."],
  ["Will you stay?","Where would I go? The ferry's full of rich men. And the cats. They're leaving first."],
  ["What will you do?","Make soup, listen to the radio, keep the lamp lit. It's what women do on this island. We wait, and we boil."]],
 "If you're short of a bed, I've a sofa. It's very small and very loyal."],
21:["(Raili's face is grey, her eyes wide, her hands steady.) I was in the flat. The windows rattled, then the sky went dim, and then Kaisa's voice said, 'It's happened. Everyone stay inside.' I did. It was a very long ten minutes.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far. The radio's been counting names. It read out three thousand and then stopped. They said no deaths. I cried a little."],
  ["What does it look like?","From the window: a plume, like a dark tree. Then the ash, like grey snow. It's the most beautiful frightening thing."],
  ["Are you coping?","I've made tea for the whole building. We sat on the stairs and told stories. Arto told one about a herring. It lasted forty minutes."]],
 "Keep a cloth over your face. Breathe slow. And hold someone's hand."],
22:["(Raili's got a tray of tea and a pile of cups.) The council wanted volunteers. They're feeding the clean-up crews, and I'm the one with the urn. I've never been so glad to be useful.",[
  ["Are you being paid?","Four marks a day, and as much tea as I can drink. It's the best offer I've had in a year."],
  ["Is Arto working?","He's got a shovel. He's very proud. He holds it like a rifle. It's a nice sight."],
  ["Is the mood good?","It's strange. Everyone's covered in grey, and everyone's kind. It's as if the island's been given a very large cleaning cloth and told to start again."]],
 "Take a cup. It's strong. It'll get the ash out of your throat."],
23:["(Raili's counting cups with her lips moving.) The aftershocks are still coming. The urn shakes, and the tea sloshes. I've named it 'Wobbles'. It's the least I can do.",[
  ["Is the radio still on?","Day and night. Kaisa's not slept. She's been reading out names of people who need a lift, and roofs that need fixing. It's the best public service I've known."],
  ["Is the job going to last?","It's a week, they say. Maybe two. Then it's back to the pages, and the pencil."],
  ["Are you saving anything?","Every penny. I want to take a course. Reception work. A proper counter. I'd like to be the first face people see when they come in."]],
 "If you're ever in a hotel, be kind to the woman at the desk. She's got a story."],
24:["(Raili's sitting with her feet on a crate.) There's a funny feeling. People are being nice. A man in a coat gave me his seat. A woman gave me a biscuit. It's the ash. It's turned us all into the same colour.",[
  ["Will it last?","No. But it's lovely while it does. It reminds you what people can be, when they've no time to be anything else."],
  ["How are the factions?","Quiet. Very. It's like a table of guests who've all gone to wash their hands, and nobody wants to come back."],
  ["What do you want for the island?","Someone who'll remember the urn. The woman who poured the tea. That'd be a good sort of government."]],
 "I'll keep the kettle on. Somebody should."],
25:["(Raili looks at a poster on the wall.) 'A Princess for Democracy.' It's a bit of a mouthful. Kaisa read it out on air and giggled. It's the only time I've heard her laugh in a week.",[
  ["Do you like the idea?","I like the princess's face. She looks like she's read a book. And I like the word 'Democracy', though I'd not want it on my lunch."],
  ["Will you vote for her?","If she'd have me. I'd like a vote that counts. Even a small one. A thimble of a vote."],
  ["Is the island ready?","It's ready for a rest. But it'll vote, because it's stubborn, and because nobody else will."]],
 "Tell Kaisa I said hello. She'll know who. She always does."],
26:["(Raili is listening to a slow, soft song on the radio.) They've put out more posters. 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' I'm not sure what it means. It sounds like a fairy tale for people who drink.",[
  ["Do you like the posters?","I like the fonts. I like the way they promise everything. But I'd like it better if there were a job at the bottom."],
  ["Is there any news on the radio?","They're announcing every faction's plans for the end of the month. They're all very busy. It's a lot to read out."],
  ["What would you say, if you had a microphone?","'Thank you to everyone who carried a bucket.' That's all. It's the one thing I'd like on the record."]],
 "I'll listen. I'm good at listening. It's a very underpaid skill."],
27:["(Raili writes something down, then crosses it out.) I've been writing a letter to the Grand. I'm asking for my old job. I've been told they've been hiring again, now the ash has cleared. I'm not proud.",[
  ["Will they take you back?","I hope so. I'd be the best chambermaid on the island. I'd make every bed like a letter home."],
  ["That's a lovely image.","It's the only one I've got. A neat bed and a good pillow. It's a small thing, but it's what a stranger sees first."],
  ["What does Arto say?","He says I should write 'Dear Sir, I'm available.' I told him that's not a letter, it's a confession."]],
 "Wish me luck. I'd wish you the same, but you've got a story. That's luck enough."],
28:["(Raili's put on her best scarf and a little lipstick.) It's the big push. Every faction, every flag. I've got the radio, and a flask, and a very comfortable chair by the window.",[
  ["You're not going out?","I've been out. I've seen. I'd rather listen. You hear more from the chair. And it's warmer."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That it's quiet. That nobody gets hurt. That somebody remembers the people who don't shout."],
  ["Is Arto out?","In the thick of it. He'll bring back a report, and half a sandwich. He's very fond of the sandwich, as a witness."]],
 "I'll tell you what I heard. It'll be accurate. And a bit too long."],
29:["(Raili's got her hands round a mug.) Tomorrow, then. I've listened to every broadcast. I've put my feet up. I've written my letter. I'm as ready as a woman can be.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","Of course. It's a bit like waiting for exam results. You've done what you can. You can only hear the number."],
  ["What would you like to hear?","A voice, saying that the island's going to be fair. I'd put my pencil down and go home."],
  ["Will you listen all day?","From the first hum of the morning. Kaisa's promised to stay on air. She says she'll read out the result herself."]],
 "Come back and listen with me. The tea's free, if you bring the biscuits."],
30:["(Raili's got the radio on, volume low, and a small, steady smile.) The last day. It's very quiet. Even the street's holding still. Kaisa's put on a very soft piece of piano.",[
  ["Any last words, Raili?","Thank you for listening. It's the thing I've wanted most this month, and you did it for free."],
  ["What will you do now?","Wait for the radio. Then make dinner. Then go to the Midnight Note, and see if Leo's playing something sad."],
  ["Was it a good month?","It was strange, and loud, and grey. I made tea for a hundred people. It's the most useful I've felt."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Look after yourself. And if you ever need a bed made, you know where I am."],
};
// Ray, bartender at the bar in Saltwick, who lives in the flat above it (The Proprietors). Threads: her one rule (no politics at
// the bar) and the Party men and union men who test it; Sirpa and Tiina and Sirpa's tab; the leak in her flat and the late beer
// boat; her late father, who ran the bar before her, his stare and his ship's bell.
DAILY['Ray']={
1:["(Ray looks you up and down, the way she looks at everyone, and then puts a glass on the bar.) You're the first stranger through that door since the snow came. What'll it be?",[
  ["Whatever's warmest.","Whisky, then. It's the only thing in Saltwick that's been warm since August."],
  ["I'm a reporter, from London. What's Saltwick like?",["Fish, wind and the Communist Party, in that order. Anna's HQ is just along the street. (She wipes the bar.) In here, though, there's one rule.",[
    ["What's the rule?","No politics at the bar. You want to argue about the king, go and stand in the snow. It cools the blood."],
    ["Does anyone keep it?","Everyone keeps it. I make sure of it. (She gives you a look that would stop a tram.) Like that."]]]],
  ["Do you always stare at people like that?","My father did. He ran this bar for thirty years and never once had to raise his voice. I'm working on it."]],
 "Mind the ice on the steps. Half of Saltwick's gone down them this winter."],
2:["Back again. Either you like my whisky, or there's nowhere else open. (She pours.) Kingsgrad's road cleared, I hear. You'll be off to see the palace.",[
  ["Your whisky's fine.","Fine's all it is. The good bottles went on the king's wake, and the beer boat from Frostholm's still iced in."],
  ["Who drinks here, mostly?","Fishermen from the West Harbour, dockers, the girls from the fish plant. And Sirpa and Tiina, at that table, from opening to closing, on one coffee each."],
  ["What did your father think of the king?","He kept a picture of him over the till, and darts holes in it from a bad night in 1971. Both things are true. That's Saltwick for you."]],
 "Shut the door behind you. The heat's the only thing in here I can't replace."],
3:["(Ray is on a stool with a bucket, under a brown stain on the ceiling.) Don't mind me. The flat upstairs is leaking into the bar, and I'm not paying a roofer until the thaw.",[
  ["Need a hand?","With a bucket? I think I've got the hang of it. Hold that one, though, while I empty this."],
  ["Is it the snow?","Snow on the roof, then a warm night, then a river through my bedroom. My father fixed it with tar and a bad temper. I've only got the temper."],
  ["Put it on the drinks menu. Saltwick rain.","(The corner of her mouth moves.) Don't. Tom would order it."]],
 "If you see a roofer who works for whisky, send him round."],
4:["(Ray nods at the corner table.) Sirpa's tab has passed forty dollars. I've told her it's got its own chair now.",[
  ["Will she ever pay it?","She will when the fish plant gives her the hours back. Which is to say I've stopped counting and started hoping."],
  ["What do Sirpa and Tiina talk about all day?","Everyone who walks past the window. You, yesterday, for an hour. Sirpa thinks you're a spy. Tiina thinks you're a vicar."],
  ["You're soft, Ray.",["Say that louder and I'll bar you. (She lowers her voice.) Tiina lost her husband with his boat last season. Nobody charges her for coffee. Nobody.",[
    ["That's kind of you.","It's not kindness. It's Saltwick. We bury our own and feed what's left."],
    ["Does Sirpa know?","Sirpa thinks I've forgotten to write it down. Let her."]]]]],
 "Go on. And don't tell Sirpa she's a spy suspect. She'd be thrilled."],
5:["Sam and Gunnar were in last night and the rule held, but only just. Sam said 'comrade' twice and I took his glass away.",[
  ["Taking away a man's glass. Harsh.","It works. He sat there with his hands on the bar like a dog that's been told. Gunnar laughed so hard he paid for both."],
  ["What were they arguing about?","Whether the island goes to Moscow or stays its own. Sam says Moscow. Gunnar says Moscow can come and unload a herring boat first."],
  ["Do you have a side yourself?","I've got a bar. The bar's side is open till midnight. Ask me again and I'll take your glass."]],
 "Drink up. The rule applies to reporters too."],
6:["(Ray is polishing a brass ship's bell behind the bar.) My father's. He rang it for last orders and for every baby born in Saltwick. It's going green.",[
  ["Ring it now.","Not for you. Not yet. It's rung for weddings, births and the end of the war. Get married in Saltwick and we'll talk."],
  ["Where's it from?","A trawler called the Annikki, that went on the rocks off the West Harbour before I was born. Dad swam out for the bell, and nobody's ever explained why."],
  ["That's a story for the paper.","It's a story for the bar. Paper's for wrapping fish in."]],
 "Mind the bell on the way out. It's lower than it looks."],
7:["They've opened the north road. With luck the beer boat comes round from Frostholm this week, and Saltwick can stop drinking my father's last bottle of plum brandy.",[
  ["How bad is the plum brandy?","Mike says it takes the barnacles off his boat. He's not joking. He tried."],
  ["Who brings your beer?","A boat out of North Frostholm Harbour, when it remembers us. Frostholm drinks first and Saltwick gets what's left. Same as with everything."],
  ["I'll have the plum brandy.","(She pours a small one.) Brave. Sip it. And don't breathe near a candle."]],
 "If you're going to Frostholm, tell them Saltwick's thirsty."],
8:["Sunday. The fishermen should be at church or in bed, and instead they're in here, because something's wrong out at the oil rig.",[
  ["What's happened at the rig?","Nobody knows, which in Saltwick means everybody knows a different thing. Tom swears he saw a black stain on the water. Sam says it's a capitalist plot. I said no politics."],
  ["Did you go to the monastery for the sermon?","Up a mountain, on a Sunday, in this? Father Thyme can come down here and preach. I'd stand him a drink, too."],
  ["Busy day, then.","Busiest since the funeral was announced. Nothing fills a bar like bad news and no work."]],
 "Go on. And if you go near the harbour, don't walk on anything black."],
9:["(Ray sets down a crate.) The beer boat came. Twelve crates. The fishermen cheered like it was a royal wedding.",[
  ["Congratulations. First round on you?","First round on Gunnar. He bet Bob it wouldn't come till October, and Bob doesn't forget a bet."],
  ["Any news with the boat?","The skipper says Kelp Cola's got half their lab working all hours and the Party's got some scheme of their own. Everybody's making fizzy drinks except the people who can't afford them."],
  ["Any more news from the rig?","Tom's nets came up stinking. He's in the corner not talking about it. Leave him be."]],
 "Beer's cold, the roof's dripping, and all's right with the world. Nearly."],
10:["(The picture of the king over the till has a black ribbon on it.) Funeral's today, up at the palace. Saltwick's got its own way of saying goodbye.",[
  ["What's Saltwick's way?","At noon I ring Dad's bell once, everybody stands, and then they sit down and argue about who'll replace him. I've lifted the rule for one hour."],
  ["Who's gone up to the funeral from here?","Anna, so she can say she was there. Nobody else got asked. The fishermen don't get invited to palaces, they get sent the bill."],
  ["Will you miss him?",["(She looks up at the picture for a while.) He came in here once, you know. 1966. Incognito, he thought. Dad knew him at once.",[
    ["What did he drink?","Plum brandy. Two glasses. He said it was the best thing he'd tasted on the island. Dad never charged him and never told a soul."],
    ["How did your father know him?","The king had a stare too. Dad said you can always tell another one."]]]]],
 "When the bell goes, you stand. Even reporters."],
11:["Helen's been in, collecting names for the mill hands over in Eastbrook. In my bar. I let her, because she was buying.",[
  ["That sounds like politics.","It was union business, and she paid. The rule bends for cash. Don't tell Sam."],
  ["What do you make of Helen?","She'd talk a fish out of the sea and into a union. I like her. I'd like her more if she didn't stand on my chairs."],
  ["Has anyone seen Pat lately?","Pat's down on the beach with his campfire, same as always. Comes in for a whisky when he's got coins, which is about Christmas."]],
 "Go on, then. The chairs need wiping after Helen."],
12:["(Ray holds a postcard up to the light.) Kate brought this in. Somebody's written 'Saltwick, the bar' on it and nothing else. No name, no message, a picture of Stockholm.",[
  ["A secret admirer?","At my age? It'll be one of the fishermen who went south. They send a card every year so we know they're not drowned. Then they forget to sign it."],
  ["Can I see it?","(She hands it over: a tram, a palace, a grey sky.) Stockholm looks like Kingsgrad with better trams. Give it back."],
  ["What will you do with it?","Same as all the others. Pin it behind the till with the rest. That wall's the fleet's whole history."]],
 "Mind how you go. The wind's turned east."],
13:["Free Kelp Cola girls came down the street and handed out bottles. Sirpa took six. Sam poured his on the snow and made a speech. I took his glass away again.",[
  ["Did you try the cola?","I don't drink anything that colour. It's what Dad would have called 'a fizzy liar'. Tiina likes it, though."],
  ["What was Sam's speech?","Something about the workers' teeth. Half right. He's not wrong about the teeth. Don't tell him I said so."],
  ["Will you stock it?","Kelp Cola wants me to put a sign up and a fridge in. I said the only cold thing in this bar is me."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Come in after and tell me how it went."],
14:["(The bar's empty for once, and Ray is reading the Gazette.) Everyone's gone off to the big day. I've stayed to mind the place. Somebody has to.",[
  ["Aren't you curious?","Curious, yes. Curious enough to leave the till with Sirpa? No."],
  ["What does Saltwick expect from it?","The Party think it's their day, the dockers think it's a day off, and Tiina thinks there'll be a buffet. Tiina's usually right."],
  ["You could close up and come.","Dad closed once, for the end of the war, and somebody stole the bell. He got it back, but he never closed again."]],
 "Go on, you'll miss it. I'll keep a glass ready."],
15:["Sunday again. The church and the palace are at each other's throats over Prince Augustus and Prince Leopold, and Saltwick's at each other's throats over who's buying.",[
  ["Who's buying?","Not Mike. Mike's never bought a round since the Annikki went down, and he wasn't born then."],
  ["Church or crown, Ray?","Crown or cross, there's a rule. (She taps the bar.) Read the rule. It's carved there. Dad did it with a fish knife."],
  ["Busy weekend?","Saturday was busy. Sunday's the hangover. I'll mop and they'll moan."]],
 "Mind Mike on the way out. He's asleep by the door."],
16:["Anna and Erik are holding a debate today, Communists against Democrats. Half my regulars have gone to heckle. I've given them a list of rules.",[
  ["What's on the list?","No throwing anything heavier than a bread roll. No singing. And whoever starts a fight doesn't drink here for a week. Gunnar asked if fish count as bread rolls."],
  ["Who'll win it?","Whoever talks longest, if it's Anna. Whoever talks sense, if there's any justice. There isn't, much."],
  ["Will you go?","And listen to politics? On my day off from listening to politics? No."]],
 "If it comes to fists, tell them Ray said go home."],
17:["(A glass rattles along a shelf on its own, and Ray catches it.) That's the third time since breakfast. The ground's twitching. Old Olaf says the mountain's waking up.",[
  ["Is that normal?","Dad remembered a shake once, in the thirties. It knocked the bell off its hook. It's never felt like this, though: like something turning over in its sleep."],
  ["What are people saying?","Sirpa says it's the Russians drilling. Tom says it's the oil company. Tiina says it's God, and she's not happy with any of us."],
  ["Have you checked the roof?","The leak's got worse. Every shake opens it a bit further. The flat's more bucket than floor."]],
 "Hold on to your glass. Things are walking off the shelves."],
18:["Word's going round that Dario from the oil company went missing yesterday, and nobody can agree where he got to. Saltwick's having a lovely time with it.",[
  ["What's the best version you've heard?","Sirpa says he was kidnapped by monks. Tiina says he ran off with a singer. Mike says he fell in the sea, and Mike says that about everybody."],
  ["Has anyone been asking about him?","Two men in good coats came in, asked if I'd seen a man in a better coat, and drank nothing. I'd have thrown them out for that alone."],
  ["Do you care?","He's never bought a drink in Saltwick. I care exactly that much."]],
 "Go on. Somebody's got to find out what really happened. Might as well be the press."],
19:["Anna's had her people up and down the street signing up new Party members. They came in here with clipboards. I made them buy a drink before they asked anyone anything.",[
  ["Did they sign anyone up?","Sam signed twice, to make up for the clipboards. Tiina signed for the soup. Sirpa asked if there was a membership discount on tabs."],
  ["Did they ask you?","Twice. I gave them the stare. They went and stood outside."],
  ["Is the Party winning Saltwick?","Saltwick's been Red since before the war. The question's whether it's red enough to vote for Moscow. That's different. Folk here like being rude about Moscow too."]],
 "Mind the clipboard men on the corner. They've been out there since nine."],
20:["(There's steam on the northern skyline, and Ray watches it through the window.) You can see it from the harbour now. Smoke off the mountain, like a chimney.",[
  ["Are you worried?","I'm worried about my roof. If the mountain wants to do something, there's nothing a bartender can do about it but pour."],
  ["What does the harbour say?","Tom's tied the boat double. Gunnar's moved the good crates up off the dock. Everybody's doing something with their hands so they don't think."],
  ["Might be a good day to close.","Dad didn't close for the war, he didn't close for the funeral, and he won't close for a hill with ideas."]],
 "If it goes, come in here. Thick walls. My father built them for winters, not mountains, but still."],
21:["(The windows are grey with ash and Ray has lit every lamp. The bar is full, and nobody is arguing.) It went up. You'll have heard it. Everyone on the island heard it.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","Everyone in Saltwick's here or accounted for. Tiina counted heads twice. Sirpa counted them three times and got a different answer each time."],
  ["You're keeping the doors open.","Where else would people go? A bar's a church for people who don't go to church."],
  ["I've never seen the bar so quiet.",["Nobody wants to start a fight under an ash cloud. (She looks round the room.) I could get used to it.",[
    ["What did you do when it went?","Rang Dad's bell. Everybody stopped panicking to look at me, and I poured. That's the whole trick."],
    ["Will it go again?","Olaf says not. Olaf also said it wouldn't go the first time."]]]]],
 "Stay in a while if you want. There's soup on, and it's free today."],
22:["Ash on everything. The tables, the boats, the herrings. Sirpa says it's like living in an ashtray. She'd know.",[
  ["How's the roof after all that?","Full of ash, which turned to mud in the night and found the hole. My bedroom's a pottery now."],
  ["How's the harbour?","Tom took the boat out and came back grey. The fish don't care about volcanoes, but the fishermen do."],
  ["Any free soup left?","There's always soup for anyone who brings a bowl. Today it's soup with ash in, which I'm telling everyone is pepper."]],
 "Brush yourself off at the door. I've just swept."],
23:["(Ray has a roofer's ladder against the bar.) I gave up. A man from Kingsgrad's coming to fix the roof. He wants paying in cash and plum brandy.",[
  ["Is there any plum brandy left?","Half a bottle. He's getting a quarter. The other quarter's for when the bill comes."],
  ["I thought you'd fix it yourself.","So did I. Then the floor of my flat went soft as bread. There's stubborn and there's sleeping in a puddle."],
  ["Any news in Saltwick today?","Rumours of trouble down at the fish plant and out at the rig, again. The Party's busy and the dockers are tired. Business as usual, with ash."]],
 "Don't walk under the ladder. Not for luck: he drops things."],
24:["Sirpa's paid off ten dollars of her tab. Ten! I didn't know whether to cry or call the doctor.",[
  ["Where did she get it?","She says she found it. In Saltwick, after a volcano, that means she cleaned someone's windows and didn't tell me."],
  ["Good for Sirpa.","Good for Sirpa. Thirty-one dollars to go, and she's started calling the chair hers again."],
  ["Will you frame the money?","I've put it in the till like a normal person. But I did look at it for a while."]],
 "Go on. Good day for Saltwick, that one."],
25:["(The roofer's gone and the ceiling's dry for the first time in a month.) Look at that. A ceiling. A whole ceiling, with no buckets.",[
  ["Congratulations. Ring the bell.","(She considers it.) No. The bell's for births, weddings and wars. A ceiling's a small birth. I'll pour myself a small one."],
  ["How much did it cost?","More than the plum brandy and less than my father's funeral. I'll get it back off the fishermen by Christmas."],
  ["Now the mountain will knock it off again.","Say that again and you'll be up a ladder in the snow with a hammer."]],
 "Go on. I'm going to sit and look at my ceiling."],
26:["Anna was in, looking for men for something on the twenty-eighth. Big push for the Party, she says. Kingsgrad, flags, the lot. She bought a round for the whole bar.",[
  ["Did the rule hold?","The rule held because she paid first and talked second. If politicians did it in that order, I'd like them all."],
  ["Will Saltwick go with her?","The young ones will go for the flags and the walk. The old ones remember what Moscow did across the water and they'll stay here and complain."],
  ["Did you take her money?","I took her money, poured her drinks and gave her the stare. She gave it back. We're old friends."]],
 "Mind the flags on the way out. Someone's been painting them on my step."],
27:["(Ray is darning a bar towel.) Tiina's teaching me to mend. She was a net-maker before. I'm hopeless. She says I knot like a sailor who's had six.",[
  ["How's Tiina doing?","Better. She laughed at me today, properly. First time since last season. I'll be hopeless every day if it does that."],
  ["Show me your darning.","(She holds it up. There's a hole beside the darn now.) It's modern art. Henrik could put it in the Gazette."],
  ["Isn't it busy for darning?","Everyone's off at the pictures in Kingsgrad, or at the pizzeria playing cards. Saltwick's quiet before a storm."]],
 "Go on. I've a towel to ruin."],
28:["The whole island's out doing something today. Flags in Kingsgrad, speeches, parties. Saltwick's emptied out. I've three customers and one of them's asleep.",[
  ["Who's asleep?","Mike. He went to every gathering on the island this morning and drank at each one. He's done his duty."],
  ["Why aren't you out there?","Somebody's got to be here when they all come back angry. They always come back angry."],
  ["What's your hope for the island?",["(She thinks.) That whatever happens, the boats still go out and the beer still comes in. And a bar where people can still say what they like, outside the rule.",[
    ["That's political, Ray.","It's a bartender's opinion. Bartenders are allowed one a year. That was it."],
    ["What would your father say?","'Polish the bell.' He said that about everything. It meant: keep going."]]]]],
 "Come back later and tell me who threw what."],
29:["Sunday. Somebody's choosing leaders, somebody's choosing kings, and I'm choosing whether to open the last crate. (She opens it.) There.",[
  ["What's everyone saying about tomorrow?","Nobody says anything about tomorrow. They talk about the weather and the ash and then they all go quiet. It's like a wake before anyone's died."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I've poured for funerals, wars and volcanoes. I'm not nervous. I'm counting glasses."],
  ["To the island, Ray.","(She pours two.) To the island. And to Saltwick, which will still be here whatever they decide in Kingsgrad."]],
 "Go on. Get some sleep. Tomorrow's a long one."],
30:["(Ray has the bell polished bright and down off its hook, on the bar.) Last day of September. Whatever happens today, I'm ringing this when it's done.",[
  ["Even if it goes badly?","Births, weddings, the end of wars. And the end of a month like this one. Good or bad, it's over, and that's worth a bell."],
  ["Thank you for the whisky, Ray.","You paid for most of it. That puts you ahead of Sirpa. Come back when this is over, and I'll pour you the last of the plum brandy."],
  ["Can I ring it?","(She looks at you a long while. Then she nods.) When it's done. Not before. And only once."]],
 "Mind the ice on the steps. It's back."],
};
// Rita, jazz singer at The Midnight Note in Frostholm (the Entertainers). Threads: her smoky voice and the half of the island that's in love
// with her, the past she won't discuss (a mysterious "other city"), Leo and his silences, Sid's bar, her nerves before a song, the ash.
DAILY['Rita']={
7:["(Rita is sitting at a dressing-table mirror, pinning up her hair.) A new face. A reporter, I hear. I've been asked three times today if I'm the famous singer. I told them I'm the singer. The famous part is optional.",[
  ["I'm the British reporter.","Then you'll want a story. I haven't got one. I've got a voice and a song, and no previous address."],
  ["Where were you before Frostholm?","Somewhere warmer. (She smiles and doesn't say.) Ask me something else. Ask me what I'm singing tonight."],
  ["What are you singing tonight?","'Nature Boy'. It's a song about a strange little man who learns something. I've always felt I knew him."]],
 "Come by the club. Sit near the front. I sing better when someone's frowning."],
8:["(Rita is in a silk dressing gown, with a cup of tea, standing at the window.) It's Sunday. The bells are going, and I'm still in my nightclothes. The monks have the day and I have the night. It's a fair division.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Once, years ago. I sang the Ave Maria and everyone cried. It put me off. I don't like it when I'm that good."],
  ["Do you sleep late?","Until Leo's saxophone wakes me. He plays a very quiet scale at noon. It means he's up. It's a kind of alarm clock."],
  ["What's your morning like?","Tea, a long look out of the window, and a lot of pretending I'm not nervous."]],
 "Come back when the lights are low. It's when I'm most myself."],
9:["(Rita is holding a bottle of cola and looking at it like an old enemy.) They want me to sing a jingle. A cola jingle. 'Fizz, fizz, hurray.' I said I'd rather sing in a drain.",[
  ["Is it a good offer?","It's a very good offer. That's what worries me. Nobody pays that much for anything honest."],
  ["Who asked?","A smooth man with a clipboard. He kept calling me 'Ms Rita'. I said I'm just Rita. He wrote it down."],
  ["Will you sing it?","Not unless they let me change the words. I'd sing 'Fizz, fizz, regret'."]],
 "I'll sing for a fair fee, and a fair tune. Everything else is a jingle."],
10:["(Rita is in plain black, no jewellery at all.) We're singing a hymn tonight, for the king. Sid asked. I've not sung one in years. I'm a little frightened, if I'm honest. It's a very naked kind of song.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","No. But I think I knew what it was to be him. A man who'd been everything, and was now, mostly, a portrait."],
  ["What will you sing?","'Abide with Me'. In D. Leo will play behind me, very softly. If I cry, he'll cover it."],
  ["Is it a hard day?","It's a long one. I'm good at those. I've had practice."]],
 "Come tonight, if you want. Bring a handkerchief. Not for me."],
11:["(Rita is stitching a hem on a stage dress, with her mouth full of pins.) A man came to the club asking if I'd sing for the poor. I said yes. Then he said it was a charity ball for the rich. I said, no, thank you.",[
  ["Did you mind?","I mind being used. I'd sing for either, if they'd just ask honestly."],
  ["Do you get a fair wage?","Sid pays me what he can, and then a bit more. He's a good man, under the grumbling."],
  ["What's the buzz at the club?","Alms, pay, politics. Everyone's got an opinion. I let them have it. They tip better when they've been heard."]],
 "Mind the pins. They've a will of their own."],
12:["(Rita is looking at an old photograph, then puts it face-down.) Nothing. Just an old picture. A man, a city and a coat. It was a long time ago. Ask me something else.",[
  ["Who's the man?","Somebody I used to sing to. He never listened. It's the only thing I remember about him."],
  ["What city is it?","A very big one. Very loud. I didn't like it. I ran away to a little island with a lot of snow. It seemed the sensible thing."],
  ["Is it a sad story?","It's a story. It's got a beginning and a middle. I'm still working on the end."]],
 "Don't print it. Print the song. It's better."],
13:["(Rita is warming up her voice, with a long, rising 'ah'.) They're giving cola away in Kingsgrad tomorrow, I hear. And the Syndicate's hunting for a recipe. It's a very busy island for a very quiet place.",[
  ["Do you hear a lot at the club?","I hear everything. A singer's invisible, on a stage. They talk as if you're furniture. It's a very useful kind of fame."],
  ["What's the recipe about?","I don't ask. I don't want to know. I've learned to keep my ears for the music."],
  ["Is Leo listening too?","Leo hears everything and says nothing. It's his gift. And his tax dodge."]],
 "If someone offers you a secret, say no. It's heavier than it looks."],
14:["(Rita is wearing her best dress, a deep red one, and a faint, nervous smile.) The big day. Everyone on the island in one place. I've sung to a hundred. I've never sung to an island.",[
  ["Are you singing?","If they ask me. I'm not sure what I'd sing. Something simple. A hymn, a lullaby."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm always nervous. It's what keeps me honest. A singer who isn't nervous has stopped listening."],
  ["What do you expect?","Cold air, loud voices, a lot of feeling. Someone will cry, and someone will cheer. I'll be at the back, with a handkerchief."]],
 "Go and see it for yourself. Come and tell me, afterwards, what it was like."],
15:["(Rita has two men's roses, one in each hand, and looks at them thoughtfully.) A red one from a royalist, a white one from a monk. They both think I'll sing at their rally. I said I'm a jazz singer. They said, of course, how lovely.",[
  ["Are you picking a side?","I'm picking a key. D, I think. It suits most kings."],
  ["Who's your favourite prince?","The one who doesn't ask for a rose back. I haven't met him."],
  ["Is it getting political at the club?","It's always political. People just usually do it quietly, over a drink. It's the best kind."]],
 "Put them in water. They've done nothing wrong."],
16:["(Rita is reading a newspaper with a large headline, upside down, and then right way up.) The Great Debate. It sounds so noble. I hear the Democrats are fretting about their ties, and the Communists about their slogans.",[
  ["Will you go?","I'd like to. I want to hear someone say something they mean. It's a rare thing in politics, and very good for the voice."],
  ["Do you vote?","I'll vote for whoever sounds most tired. It's a good sign."],
  ["Who wins?","The one who stays on key. It's always the one who stays on key."]],
 "Don't tell them I'm coming. They'll make a speech about me."],
17:["(Rita stops in the middle of a note, a hand against the wall.) That was no tram. The floor shook. A faint, low shiver. I've sung through a lot, but I've never been interrupted by the ground.",[
  ["Was it an earth tremor?","I think so. Leo played a low C at the same moment, and I couldn't tell which one made it."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A little. I don't like it when the world changes key without warning."],
  ["Is the mountain steaming?","They say so. I haven't looked. I'm told it's pretty. I'd rather hear about it than see it."]],
 "If it happens again, hum. It steadies the nerves."],
18:["(Rita is practising a lullaby, very softly.) I'm trying a new song. It's about a mountain that dreams of being a cloud. I wrote it this morning, in the bath. It might be nonsense, but it's my nonsense.",[
  ["Is it about the volcano?","I didn't mean it to be. But the songs go where they want. They're like cats."],
  ["Can I hear it?","At the club, tonight. If you're good. If you're not good, you'll have to hear it anyway."],
  ["Has the shaking got worse?","A bit. The chandeliers tinkle. It's like a very small orchestra. Leo's started to play along."]],
 "Come early. I like a good seat for the nervous."],
19:["(Rita has the stage lights on, and is practising a bow in front of an empty room.) I'm told someone's coming tonight. Incognito. A member of the palace, in a bad hat. It's always the same. Royalty can't keep a secret in a hat.",[
  ["Who is it?","I'd rather not say. Sid told me, and Sid never tells. It must be someone very important or very silly."],
  ["Will you sing for them?","I'll sing for whoever sits in the front. If they're royal, they'll tip. If they're not, they'll be charming."],
  ["Do you like the disguises?","They're the best part. A prince in a false moustache is a prince finally relaxed."]],
 "Stay for the second set. It's when the interesting people leave."],
20:["(Rita has a small bag by the door, and a coat over the chair.) Madame Zora sent a note. 'Pack and sleep light.' I've never believed in her, and I've never doubted her. It's a very uncomfortable arrangement.",[
  ["Are you leaving?","Not yet. But I've found the passport. It's in a shoe. It's been in a shoe for years."],
  ["What's happening?","I don't know. A feeling, like a held breath. I've felt it before, in another city. It never ended well."],
  ["Is it a bad omen?","It's an honest one. It's the dull omens that fool you."]],
 "Be ready tomorrow. It's all anyone can do."],
21:["(Rita is on the steps of the Skyline Apartments, covered in a fine grey dust, her hair loose.) It's happened. The mountain's gone up. The ash is falling like soft rain. I'm all right. I'm singing, a bit, to keep the fear down.",[
  ["What are you singing?","'Over the Rainbow'. It was the first thing I thought of. It doesn't mean anything. It's just comforting."],
  ["Is everyone safe?","Leo's safe, and Sid's under the bar, and the piano's fine. It's the best we could've hoped for."],
  ["How are you?","Shaken. But I've survived worse. A mountain's quite honest, in its way. It doesn't pretend."]],
 "Keep your mouth covered. The ash is in everything, even a song."],
22:["(Rita is washing her scarves in a basin of grey water, coughing gently.) I can't sing a high note without a little cough. It's like a very small chorus of ghosts. I've decided to take it as harmony.",[
  ["Is the club open?","Open, and packed. Everyone's come in covered in grey, like statues. We lit every candle in the place."],
  ["What are you singing?","Something slow and low. A song for people who've had a fright. It's most of the island."],
  ["Are you coping?","Better than I expected. A fright's a good way to find out what you care about."]],
 "Bring a scarf. A clean one, if you've got it."],
23:["(Rita is dusting the top of the piano, with a very small brush.) I've never worked so hard in my life, and all I'm doing is cleaning. The ash comes up in the light like a grey fog. It's strangely beautiful.",[
  ["Are people still coming?","More than before. They want a song and a seat. It's all anyone wants in a crisis."],
  ["What's the news?","The Party's mending its machines, and the oil men their pipes. Everyone's mending something. I'm mending my voice."],
  ["How's Leo?","Silent, as ever, and playing beautifully. He's put a mute on the sax, to keep the ash out. It sounds like a man talking in his sleep."]],
 "Come and listen. I'll sing you something clean."],
24:["(Rita is looking out of the window, north, over the grey roofs.) The monks are going up the mountain. To pray, I hear. I'd go with them, if I knew a good enough hymn. I only know the ones with a drummer.",[
  ["Do you pray?","I sing. It's the same thing, with better lighting."],
  ["Are the tremors over?","Just the small ones. They come at night, like a cat jumping on the bed."],
  ["What's the mood?","Tired. A little tender. People are being nicer to each other. It's lovely, and it won't last."]],
 "Wrap up warm. The cold's coming in under the ash."],
25:["(Rita has a faint smile, and a very small bunch of paper flowers.) A crown for everyone, they say, and a princess for democracy. I've been asked to sing at a reception. I said I'd sing for anyone who's polite to the waiters.",[
  ["Who's the princess?","Daphne. She's a lovely girl. She comes to the club in a dark scarf and thinks nobody knows. We all know. We're very discreet."],
  ["Do you like her?","I do. She listens. It's rare. Most people at a table are just waiting to speak."],
  ["What will you sing?","A love song, I think, with a bit of a twist. It will be about a woman who's good at leaving."]],
 "If you're there, sit near the front. I'll sing it just for you."],
26:["(Rita has a bottle of cola on the dressing table, and her own face in the mirror, looking at it.) A prince in every bottle. Imagine if they'd put a singer on it. I'd get very thirsty.",[
  ["Would you put your face on a bottle?","Only if it were a very small bottle. And a very small face."],
  ["What about the oil man's spa?","I hear it's the grandest on the island. I'd like to go. I'd like to sing in a steam room. The acoustics must be wonderful."],
  ["How's the club?","Lively. Everyone's got money, and nowhere to spend it. Sid's thrilled."]],
 "Don't drink a prince on an empty stomach. It goes to the head."],
27:["(Rita is holding two tickets for the Eastbrook pictures.) A revolution at the cinema, I hear. I'm taking Leo. He'll sit in the back and say nothing, and then say, 'Good tune.' It's the most he's ever said about a film.",[
  ["Do you like films?","I love them. I like being in the dark and not having to sing. I'd like to be the girl who gets rescued, just once."],
  ["What's the film about?","A revolution, or something like it. I hope there's a song in it. The best revolutions always have a song."],
  ["What's the mood on the island?","Taut. Like a drum before the stick. I can feel it in my teeth."]],
 "Save me a seat. I'll sit on the aisle, in case I cry."],
28:["(Rita is stretching her neck, and humming one long note.) The biggest day of the month, they say. Everyone's out, shouting. I've decided to be quiet. A good singer knows when to rest.",[
  ["Will you go out?","Not today. I'll stay in, and warm up my voice. I've a feeling I'll need it tomorrow."],
  ["Are you worried?","A little. I've seen how crowds turn, in other places. It's not the shouting I fear, but the silence after."],
  ["What would you sing for them?","Something simple. A lullaby, maybe. They've all been up too long."]],
 "Keep your head, and keep your voice. It's the only advice I've got."],
29:["(Rita sits at the piano, a single candle on the lid.) The night before. I've no show tonight. Sid's given us a rest. It feels strange, to be so quiet, with so much about to happen.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Calm. A bit sad. Like at the end of a good long song, when the last note's still in the air."],
  ["What will you sing tomorrow?","Whatever comes. It might be a hymn, or a ballad, or just a long breath. I'll know when I'm there."],
  ["Is Leo all right?","He's practising, very softly, next door. It's a good sound to fall asleep to."]],
 "Go home and sleep. The song will still be here."],
30:["(Rita stands on the empty stage in the grey light, her hands at her sides.) The last day. I've sung a hundred songs this month, and I can't think of one. It's a strange, empty, lovely feeling.",[
  ["Any last words?","Listen. It's all there is. If you can't hear the music, hear the quiet. It's just as good."],
  ["What will you do after today?","Sing. Whatever happens, I'll sing. It's the one thing they can't take from me."],
  ["Thank you for talking.","You're welcome. You asked the right questions. It's rarer than you'd think."]],
 "Goodnight, wherever you are. I'll sing it for you."],
};
// Rosa, hot dog vendor outside Kelp Cola HQ in Kingsgrad (food vendors). Threads: knows everything that happens in the square, generous mustard, her neighbour Ville and his newspapers, rival Dex, the cola men's lunches, the ash on her cart.
DAILY['Rosa']={
2:["(Rosa waves a pair of tongs.) A new customer! And a reporter, I hear. Ville said you'd come. He gets everything first, except the weather. Hot dog? Mustard's free. It's all free, except the hot dog.",[
  ["I'll have one, thanks.","Onions? Of course onions. Nobody's ever regretted an onion. The Gazette says so, on page six."],
  ["Is it a good pitch?","The best on the island. Cola men at noon, tourists at two, palace staff at four. It's like a clock, with ketchup."],
  ["What do you hear in the square?","Everything. I'm a hot dog cart with ears. Try me."]],
 "Don't let Ville tell you he saw it first. I told him."],
3:["(Rosa piles onions on a roll.) I've had a busy morning. Two of Phil's men, and one very stern woman from the palace. She said she didn't eat hot dogs. She ate two.",[
  ["Who was she?","A lady-in-waiting, I'd guess. She had a very straight back and a very hungry look. She asked for extra mustard and told me to say nothing."],
  ["Do you gossip?","I listen. It's different. A gossip talks, a listener sells."],
  ["What's the palace like?","I've never been in. But everyone who comes out is in a very good mood and a very bad temper."]],
 "I've never said a word. Between us, eh?"],
4:["(Rosa nods at the stand next door.) Ville's been telling me about a man in a grey coat who's been counting the cola gates. I told him: count me in. I've been counting them since March.",[
  ["How many are there?","Three. And a back door. And a hatch for the delivery boys. It's the best-guarded gate on the island, and it's got a very leaky hatch."],
  ["Do you like Ville?","He's my neighbour. I'd say he's my best friend. But he'd never admit it. Too much pride, too many newspapers."],
  ["Does he share what he sees?","He sees it. I hear it. Together, we'd run the island."]],
 "Tell him he owes me a coin. He knows why."],
5:["(Rosa sets the tongs down.) Dex and I have an understanding. He sells at the front, I sell at the back. He's a good man. He just thinks mustard's a sin.",[
  ["Is that a rivalry?","A friendly one. We have a gentlemen's agreement. I'm not a gentleman, so I break it."],
  ["What does Dex sell?","Hot dogs, but sadder. They taste like they've given up."],
  ["Do the cola people prefer yours?","They prefer the mustard. They'd say it was the sausage, but I know better."]],
 "A good dog needs a good bun. Everything else is gossip."],
6:["(Rosa scrapes the grill.) A man in a fur collar came by yesterday, from the oil. He asked what I knew about the island's secrets. I said: about forty-two, and I'll sell you one for a hot dog.",[
  ["Did he buy one?","He bought a hot dog, and I gave him the first. It's about the weather."],
  ["Do you know any secrets?","I know where Phil hides his chocolate. It's in the third drawer, behind a stapler."],
  ["Would you sell the others?","Only for a very large tip. And a hot dog on the house."]],
 "Eat up. It's the only secret that tastes of something."],
7:["(Rosa fans the grill.) Every road's open, and the square's heaving. I've sold two hundred, and it's not even noon. I've started charging for the napkins.",[
  ["Is that honest?","It's very honest. I charge a penny, and I give them two."],
  ["Who's buying?","Everyone. Frostholm men, in thin shoes. A monk, in a very thick habit. And a girl with a goat, who said it was a pet."],
  ["How's the cola crowd?","In a rush. They talk about 'launch dates' and 'formulae'. I think they mean pop."]],
 "Take a napkin. It's a penny, but it's a very good napkin."],
8:["(Rosa has a radio perched on the cart, a sermon murmuring.) Sunday. Church bells, half a queue and a priest buying a hot dog. I won't say who. A woman has her secrets.",[
  ["Which priest?","One with a grey beard and a guilty look. He said it was for the 'poor'. I said, they'd have the same, with relish."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas, for the singing. And Easter, for the egg."],
  ["Is it quiet?","As a grave. I've time to think. It's rather alarming."]],
 "If you meet him, tell him it's on the house. I'm not a monster."],
9:["(Rosa holds a bottle of cola against the light.) New formula, they say. Two of Phil's lot have been in, arguing about the taste. One said vanilla, one said cherry. I said: it's brown. It's always been brown.",[
  ["Do you like it?","I like the ones who pay. The rest, I serve with a smile."],
  ["Who's making the other cola?","The Party, they say. 'The People's Cola.' It's the same recipe, with a slogan."],
  ["What would you put in a cola?","Mustard. Don't laugh. It'd sell."]],
 "Ask Dex. He'll say it's a crime. He'd be right, and so would I."],
10:["(Rosa wears a black scarf and sells hot dogs very quietly.) The funeral. The square's silent. They say the king ate a hot dog once, in 1958, from a cart not unlike mine. I like to think it was.",[
  ["Was it your cart?","My mother's. She'd never admit it. She said he asked for mustard, and she gave him the good one."],
  ["Are you watching the funeral?","From the cart. The wireless is on. I've turned the grill down. A little respect goes a long way."],
  ["What will happen to the island?","It'll carry on. It's an island. There's nowhere else for it to go."]],
 "I'll sell you a hot dog. A quiet one. For the occasion."],
11:["(Rosa wipes her hands.) Alms for the poor, pay for the mill. Everyone's got a cause, and nobody's got change. I've given three hot dogs on credit this morning.",[
  ["Do you often give credit?","To the ones who look honest. And the ones who look hungry. I'm a poor judge of both."],
  ["Who's asking?","A mill hand, a monk and a lad from the Party. They all said it was for a good cause."],
  ["Will they pay?","The lad will. The monk will pray. The mill hand's a mystery."]],
 "A hot dog on credit is a vote for hope. That's my politics."],
12:["(Rosa leans in.) That man in the fur collar's back. He's been circling the square all morning. He asked about a record, and then about a lady in Eastbrook. I told him I don't sell records. I sell sausages.",[
  ["Did he believe you?","He bought a sausage. That's all you can ask of a man."],
  ["What does he want with a record?","Something old, something hidden, something worth money. In my experience, it's the same thing."],
  ["Would you tell Ruby?","I've a cart in Kingsgrad and a mouth in Eastbrook. It's the best arrangement."]],
 "If he returns, I'll put extra mustard on his. It's my way of saying no."],
13:["(Rosa wrestles a crate of cola onto the cart.) Free samples! They've sent three lads in matching jackets. 'It's a gesture,' one said. I said it's a gesture that's very heavy, and he carried it for me.",[
  ["Is free cola a good idea?","It's the best idea. It's the only free thing the island's ever had that wasn't snow."],
  ["Will you sell it?","I'll sell it with a hot dog. A meal deal. It's called business."],
  ["Does Ville give it away?","With a hat on. He looks like a very sad clown."]],
 "Don't tell him I laughed. I'd never hear the end of it."],
14:["(Rosa has loaded the cart onto its wheels.) The big day! I'm taking the cart to the gathering. Everyone will be hungry, and I've five hundred rolls. If the island's going to make history, it'll do it on a full stomach.",[
  ["Is it a good place to sell?","The best. A crowd's a queue with a grievance. I'll serve them all."],
  ["What do you expect?","Shouting, banners, a very long speech. And a lot of onions."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm excited. It's the one day I'll sell out before noon."]],
 "If you see me in the crowd, I'm the one with the mustard."],
15:["(Rosa tightens her apron.) The Church and the Royals have been at it all morning. Crown before Cross, they say. I say: bun before sausage. It's the only order that makes sense.",[
  ["Who's got the better argument?","The one who buys the hot dog."],
  ["Is it getting nasty?","A priest and a prince's man were in my queue. They didn't speak. It was like serving two cats."],
  ["Which do you prefer?","I'd rather sell to both. A hot dog's neutral."]],
 "I've a feeling someone's going to drop a roll. And a reputation."],
16:["(Rosa has a radio on the cart, balanced on a cola crate.) The Great Debate. Communists and Democrats, shouting at each other. I'd put them both on the grill, but they'd talk the whole way through.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever shouts last. It's a very simple formula."],
  ["Would you like to be a politician?","Heaven forbid. I'd have to shake hands. I've seen what's on them."],
  ["What would you ask them?","Who's paying for the onions?"]],
 "I'll listen as long as the sausages hold."],
17:["(The cart shudders on its wheels and Rosa grabs the counter.) Did you feel it? The ground. Every ten minutes, a little shake. The mustard's sliding across the tray!",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say so. They also say it's Dario, because nobody's seen him. I'd say it's a coincidence. I'd say it quietly."],
  ["Where's Dario?","Gone. Vanished. Phil's lot are whispering. A man like that doesn't disappear unless he wants to, or someone wants him to."],
  ["Are you frightened?","A bit. But a hot dog cart's a very sturdy thing. It's survived two kings and a tram."]],
 "If the ground opens, grab the mustard. It's the only thing worth saving."],
18:["(Rosa sniffs.) That smell. Eggs. Sulphur. The cola men say it's the new formula. I say it's the mountain, and I'm the only one who's right.",[
  ["Is the mountain angry?","The steam's a mile high, they say. The monks have stopped ringing the bell. It's very quiet up there."],
  ["Are people buying?","Everyone's buying. Torches, rope, hot dogs. In that order."],
  ["Will you stay open?","If the sky falls, I'll still sell. A customer in a crisis pays full price."]],
 "Eat now. In case it's the last good one for a while."],
19:["(Rosa pins a poster to the cart.) 'Comrades Wanted'. A man said I'd make a splendid comrade. I said, I'd make a better cook. He said, there's no difference. I said, taste a hot dog.",[
  ["Did he?","He did. He's been back twice. We've a tentative understanding."],
  ["Are you tempted?","By the hot dog, yes. By the Party, no. I've enough on my hands."],
  ["What does Ville think?","He thinks I should join and spy. I think he's read too many papers."]],
 "Take a poster. They make good napkins."],
20:["(Rosa lowers her voice.) The Hotel Aurora's got a guest. A man, they say, with a very big secret. The maids are whispering. The porter's very pale. I'd say it was a spy, but I've no proof.",[
  ["What do they say?","That he arrived at midnight, in a long coat. He's been in the room three days, and he's never ordered a hot dog."],
  ["Does that mean something?","A man who doesn't eat a hot dog's hiding something. It's a rule."],
  ["Who's he talking to?","Democrats, they say. A delegate. Or a defector."]],
 "I never said a thing. I was selling sausages."],
21:["(Rosa is a grey statue behind a grey cart.) It blew! The mountain! I saw it from the square. A great grey plume, then everything fell. The mustard's grey. The onions are grey. Even the cat's grey.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","A cough. A very big cough. But I've covered the buns. They're the last clean thing on the island."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody. It's a miracle. A messy one."],
  ["Is Ville all right?","Furious. He says the paper's ruined. I said, it was never that clean."]],
 "Eat quick. The ash settles fast."],
22:["(Rosa sweeps the cart with a feather duster.) It's in the relish, the rolls, the napkins. I've washed everything three times. The cola men say it's 'character'. I say it's grit.",[
  ["Are you open?","Of course. The island's grey, but it's still hungry."],
  ["How's the square?","Everyone's sweeping. A very unusual sight: all factions with brooms."],
  ["What's the mood?","Tired. And oddly cheerful. It's as if the mountain's cleared the air."]],
 "Mind your boots. It's slippery. And grey."],
23:["(Rosa watches two vans pull up outside the HQ.) The Party's got men with spanners, and the oil lot have men with toolboxes. I'm told it's 'Spanner in the Works' and 'Deep Repairs'. I said, I mend sausages.",[
  ["What are they fixing?","Something at the cola line, or the Party's, or the oil's. It's very hard to say. They all look the same in the ash."],
  ["Is Phil watching?","From the window. He's been there since six. He looks like a man watching his own funeral."],
  ["Will it affect the cola?","If it does, I'll sell more hot dogs. It's the circle of life."]],
 "Don't ask me which side's right. I serve them all."],
24:["(Rosa stirs the onions.) The clean-up's on. A lot of men in grey, with brushes and a very good budget. They've been round the palace twice and the HQ once. They never buy a hot dog.",[
  ["Is that suspicious?","A man who never eats is on a mission. It's well known."],
  ["Who's paying them?","I'd say the Syndicate. They like a tidy island. It's easier to hide a body."],
  ["Was that a joke?","It was a very small one."]],
 "Don't repeat that. Or do. I'll deny it with a sausage."],
25:["(Rosa waves a flier.) A crown for everyone and a princess for democracy. I told the lad, I'll take a crown if it's made of mustard. He said he'd ask.",[
  ["Is it popular?","The kids love it. The old ones think it's rude. And I think it's very good for trade."],
  ["Who's the princess?","I heard a name. But I'm not telling. A woman's allowed one secret."],
  ["Do you want a vote?","I want a bigger cart. A vote's a nice extra."]],
 "If they make a princess, I'll do her a hot dog. With a little crown on top."],
26:["(Rosa squints at a bottle.) A prince in every bottle. The lad left a crate. I asked, which prince? He said, all of them. I said, that's a lot of princes for one cola.",[
  ["Is it selling?","Like hot cakes. Or hot dogs. They have a lot in common."],
  ["Is it Leopold?","Of course. With a very good hairstyle. I'd say it's more handsome than the real one, but I've never met him."],
  ["Does Ville sell it?","He hides it under the paper. He's embarrassed."]],
 "Buy a bottle. It'll look good on a shelf. It won't taste good, but it'll look good."],
27:["(Rosa packs the cart.) A film tonight. 'Revolution at the Pictures.' I've been asked to sell hot dogs at the interval. It's a very big honour. I've doubled the onions.",[
  ["Will you watch it?","From the back, with a tray. It's the best way to see a film. You can leave when it's boring."],
  ["Is the Party paying?","In rolls. And in revolutionary respect. I'd rather have cash, but I'll take respect."],
  ["Is anyone going to the cards?","A lot of men with money, in a lot of coats. It's called High Stakes. I'd stay away. It sounds like a sausage in trouble."]],
 "If you go, buy a hot dog. I'll be at the door."],
28:["(Rosa has bunting all round the cart.) The big push! Every faction's in the square. I've sold five hundred, and I've changed my flag four times. It's the only way to stay neutral.",[
  ["Is it a good day?","The best. Everyone's hungry and nobody's counting. I've never made so much money."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm too busy to be nervous. I've a queue to the tram."],
  ["Who's winning?","Whoever's hungrier. It's always the hungry ones."]],
 "Take a roll. And hurry. It's a big day."],
29:["(Rosa wipes the counter slowly.) Tomorrow's the last day. I've five hundred rolls, a barrel of mustard, and a lot of hope. If the island changes, I'll sell it a hot dog. If it doesn't, I'll sell it another.",[
  ["Are you ready?","The cart's polished. The tongs are sharp. I've never been readier."],
  ["What do you hope for?","Peace, quiet and a good crowd. In that order. Or the reverse."],
  ["Any last words?","Eat well. Vote late. And mind the mustard."]],
 "Go to bed. You'll need it. I will."],
30:["(Rosa stands very still, tongs in hand, looking out over the square.) The last day. It's so quiet I can hear the grill. It's the first time all month.",[
  ["How has the month been?","Loud, grey and delicious. I wouldn't change a bun."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash on the mustard. The cola men in masks. And Ville's face when the paper went grey."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come back. I'll save you a hot dog. A good one. With extra onions."]],
 "Take care of yourself. And of the island."],
};
// Rosaria, owner of the pizzeria in Kingsgrad (Crime Syndicate). Threads: her sourdough starter from Naples, a 'Sarivalto
// pizza' made from island ingredients that she tries out all month, feeding the hungry (Eila and Marjut, Mario in his cell),
// the cheese that comes by boat, and the back room she never asks about.
DAILY['Rosaria']={
2:["(Rosaria is dusting flour off her hands.) A customer! The first since the snow. Sit, sit. The road from the south is open, so the flour can come up, so I can make pizza. The world makes sense again.",[
  ["Smells wonderful in here.","That's the oven. Wood-fired, a proper one. I built it with a man from Kingsgrad who'd never seen one. He cried when it was done. So did I."],
  ["Is this the only pizzeria on the island?","The only one and the best one. The others are frozen things in the supermarket. Lisa sells them and apologises at the same time."],
  ["What's behind the door at the back?","Crates, a table, men playing cards. I don't go in, and I don't ask. (She smiles.) What I don't know, I can't burn."]],
 "Come back hungry. Everyone does."],
3:["(Rosaria lifts a cloth off a big earthenware bowl and sniffs.) Good. She's alive. My starter, the mother of every pizza. She's from Naples, thirty-one years old, and she nearly froze in the snow.",[
  ["A starter that's thirty-one years old?","My mother gave me a spoonful in a jam jar the day I left Naples. I fed it on the boat, in the train, in a hotel in Hamburg. It's the oldest thing I own."],
  ["How does a Neapolitan end up here?","A man, of course. He wanted to see the north. He saw it, he hated it, he went home. I stayed. I liked the quiet."],
  ["Does she have a name?","Nonna. Because she's old, she's sour, and she has to be fed every day or she sulks."]],
 "Close the door, the draught upsets Nonna."],
4:["Eila and Marjut were on the bench in the cold again, by the west end. (Rosaria wraps two slices in paper.) I take them something every afternoon. Don't tell them I made it on purpose.",[
  ["That's kind of you.","Kind? Nobody goes hungry within a hundred metres of my oven. It's a rule. My grandmother's rule. She fed half of Naples in the war."],
  ["Why are so many people out of work?","Ask the cola company. Ask the palace. They all have big plans for the island, and none of the plans have Eila in them."],
  ["Do they pay you back?","Eila brought me a pine cone once. Marjut said I looked tired. That's payment enough. Better than some people pay."]],
 "If you pass them, say Rosaria says hello. They like that."],
5:["(Rosaria is frowning at an empty crate.) No mozzarella. The boat from Helsinki is late, the boat from Stockholm is later. A pizzeria without cheese is a bakery, and I am not a baker.",[
  ["What will you use instead?","Goat's cheese from Hilda's farm. It's strong, it's island cheese, and the customers will complain, and then they'll order it again."],
  ["How does the cheese get here?","By boat, into Eastbrook Harbour, and up the road in Liv's customs book. Everything that comes here passes through somebody's hands."],
  ["Pizza without cheese? Sacrilege.","Marinara. No cheese, just tomato, garlic, oregano. The sailors in Naples ate it. It's older than your country's parliament."]],
 "Come and try the goat's cheese. Be honest, but not too honest."],
6:["(The back room door is shut, and the sound of chips clicking comes through it.) Busy morning. Some men came early for cards. I gave them coffee and closed the door. Sit, have a slice.",[
  ["Who's in the back room?","Friends. (She hands you a slice.) The cheese is from Hilda's. Tell me what you think."],
  ["Do you ever play cards yourself?","Scopa, with my sister, at Christmas, by letter. It takes until Easter. Poker is a game for men who like to lie with their faces."],
  ["This goat's cheese is strong.","Strong is good. Strong means it's alive. Sergeant Hanna said it tastes like a barn. She had three slices."]],
 "Mind the step on your way out: it's iced again."],
7:["I've decided, journalist. A pizza for Sarivalto. Island things on Neapolitan dough. (She shows a scribbled list.) Smoked herring, lingonberries, Hilda's cheese, a little dill.",[
  ["Lingonberries on a pizza?","Why not? The Swedes put them on meatballs. The Finns put them on porridge. On this island they put them on everything. So, pizza."],
  ["Who'll be your first taster?","Mario, in his cell. He can't run away. (She laughs.) I'm joking. Mostly."],
  ["My grandmother would call that a crime.","Your grandmother never tasted herring after six hours in a wood oven. Come back next week, and then apologise for her."]],
 "Go on. I need to talk to my herring."],
8:["(Rosaria is wrapping a pizza in a tea towel to keep it warm.) This is for Mario. The police station is two streets away. Sergeant Hanna lets me in through the side door, and she takes a slice as rent.",[
  ["How is Mario doing?","Thin. He says the police food is cardboard with a sauce. I won't let a man go thin two streets from my oven, whatever he did."],
  ["What did Mario do?","Something with numbers. I don't know. He never told me, and I never asked. He likes extra olives. That's what I know about Mario."],
  ["The sergeant takes a bribe, then?","A slice is not a bribe. A slice is manners. If I give her a whole pizza, then you can write it down."]],
 "If you're going past the station, tell Mario it's on its way."],
9:["Everybody is talking about cola. Rosa at the hot dog cart says people are asking her for flavours. (Rosaria snorts.) For flavours! Ask a cook what people like, and they'll tell you: salt, fat, and their mother.",[
  ["What flavour would you put in cola?","Lemon. A real lemon, not a chemist's lemon. Gustavo has a lemon tree at his villa, did you know? In this cold. He's very proud."],
  ["Are you friends with Rosa?","Rosa sells sausages in a bun in the snow and smiles all day. We have a deal: I don't sell hot dogs, she doesn't sell pizza. The peace has held for nine years."],
  ["Is cola any good with pizza?","With pizza you drink wine, or water, or beer if you're a sailor. Cola is for children and Americans. (She looks at you.) Sorry."]],
 "Tell Rosa I said her mustard is too sweet. She'll know it's love."],
10:["(Rosaria is in a black dress, with her apron over the top.) The king's funeral this morning. I didn't go, I'm not important enough, thank God. I lit a candle by the oven instead.",[
  ["Did you ever meet the king?","He came in once, years ago, in a big coat and a false moustache. He thought nobody knew him. I gave him the best table and didn't charge him. He left a hundred."],
  ["The back room's busy today.","Is it? I've been in the kitchen all morning. The dough doesn't wait for anyone. Not even kings. Not even Gustavo."],
  ["A candle by the oven?","In Naples we light a candle for the dead where the warmth is. The church is too cold for a soul to stay in."]],
 "Go gently. It's a sad day, even for people who didn't know him."],
11:["The Sarivalto pizza, first attempt. (Rosaria puts a slice in front of you with great ceremony.) Herring, lingonberry, goat's cheese. Eat. Then lie to me kindly.",[
  ["It's actually rather good.","Don't sound so surprised! (She beams.) Too many berries? The berries are fighting the herring. I'll make peace between them."],
  ["The herring's a bit loud.","Everything about herring is loud. It's a fish that shouts. Next time I'll smoke it less. Thank you for not being polite."],
  ["What did Mario think?","He ate it all and asked for a normal one. That's not a review, that's a man in prison. I don't count it."]],
 "Come back in a few days. Version two."],
12:["(Rosaria is rolling out dough, flour to the elbows.) Gustavo came in for lunch and said I make the island's best pizza. He says it every time. I tell him every time that he's right.",[
  ["Does Gustavo come in often?","Every week. He sits at the corner table and eats slowly, like a man who remembers being hungry. He always pays. That's more than some."],
  ["Does he own the pizzeria?","(She keeps rolling.) The oven's mine. The recipes are mine. Nonna is mine. Everything else, ask the man who owns the building."],
  ["Who doesn't pay?","Carlo. He orders big, he talks big, and he says 'put it on the campaign'. What campaign pays for pizza? I've got a list."]],
 "Flour on your coat. Brush it off before someone thinks you work here."],
13:["(Rosaria is wiping tables hard.) Kelp Cola are giving away free samples at the tram stop. Free! People come in here holding the cans, and ask for tap water with their pizza.",[
  ["Do you mind them giving it away?","Nothing is free. They give you a can today so you buy ten tomorrow. My grandmother called that the devil's first slice."],
  ["Phil's family has a famous secret recipe, I hear.","Everyone has a secret recipe. Mine is in my hands. Theirs is in a safe somewhere. Mine is harder to steal."],
  ["Want me to hide the cans?","Put them on the back shelf with the wine. Out of sight. The wine doesn't like them, but the wine is very old and doesn't like anybody."]],
 "Take a can with you. I've got too many."],
14:["The big day. (Rosaria has the radio on, Kaisa's voice low between songs.) I'm making extra dough. Big days, everyone gets hungry, and they all want to talk about it over something hot.",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","A lot of men in coats, saying the island's future. Then everyone comes here and says it again, louder, with cheese."],
  ["Will you go and watch?","Somebody has to stay by the oven. When the speeches are done, they'll want feeding. That's my part in history."],
  ["Kaisa's on the radio?","She's the only one on the radio. A lovely voice. She plays slow songs and talks like your aunt. I listen while I knead."]],
 "Come back afterwards. There'll be a seat and a slice."],
15:["(Rosaria is peering into the starter bowl.) Nonna's not happy. Too cold this week. She's gone flat and grumpy. I had to sit her by the oven with a blanket, like a grandmother.",[
  ["Is the starter in trouble?","She's been in trouble before. In Hamburg in 1952 she froze solid in the window. I warmed her in my coat on the train. She came back."],
  ["What's the gossip from the palace?","The prince and the abbot are fighting over which brother wears the crown. Marta from the palace kitchen buys flour from me. She says the soup's gone salty from worry."],
  ["A blanket for a bowl of dough?","You laugh. She's older than you. Have some respect."]],
 "Don't slam the door. Nonna's resting."],
16:["Sarivalto pizza, version two. (She slides a slice across.) Less berries, more dill, the herring smoked only a little. And a drop of the sea buckthorn oil Pirkko sells from her van.",[
  ["Better than the first.","Much better. You're too polite to say so, but the first one was a fight on a plate. This one's a conversation."],
  ["Did you hear the debate?","On the radio, yes. Anna and Erik, shouting about the people. The people were all here, eating pizza, not listening. I could have told them."],
  ["Who else has tried it?","Sergeant Hanna, Eila, Marjut, and a monk who came down for flour. He said it was 'a blessing upon the herring'. I'll put it on the menu."]],
 "Come back for version three. Nearly there."],
17:["(The plates on the shelf rattle, and Rosaria grabs the starter bowl to her chest.) Madonna! The ground's moving! Nonna nearly went on the floor.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Fine, fine. (She sets the bowl down very carefully.) In Naples the ground does this. You cross yourself, you check the oven, you carry on."],
  ["Have you heard any news from Frostholm?","People say somebody important at the oil company didn't come to work. Nobody knows where. (She shrugs.) Maybe he felt the ground move and went home."],
  ["Did the oven crack?","Let me look. (She runs a hand over the bricks.) No. Good bricks. Thank God, I'd have had to sell my soul for new ones."]],
 "Hold on to something, if it starts again."],
18:["Mario's letter came, with the plate I sent him. (Rosaria shows a note in pencil.) He says thank you for the pizza, and please could he have it with less dill. A critic, in a cell!",[
  ["Do you write to each other?","Through the sergeant. I send pizza, he sends the plate back with a note on it. It's the longest correspondence of my life."],
  ["Has he said when he'll be out?","Mario never says. I think he doesn't know. Gustavo knows, but Gustavo never says either. These men!"],
  ["Less dill. He's got a point.","Don't you start. The dill stays. Mario can pick it off with his fingers like a heathen."]],
 "Tell nobody I asked about Mario. People talk."],
19:["(Rosaria is making a pizza with nothing but tomato, olive oil and a great deal of garlic.) Leo from the Midnight Note telephoned. He wants six pizzas for the band, late. He talked for nine seconds. That's a speech, for Leo.",[
  ["Do the musicians eat here often?","Rita comes on Mondays and orders a marinara and eats half. A singer can't sing with a full stomach. Leo eats the other half and says nothing."],
  ["What's happening at the club later?","Sid says somebody important's coming in. Very hush. So the musicians want feeding early, before the fuss."],
  ["Garlic, for a jazz band?","Rita says it's good for the voice. I don't know if it's true. But it's good for keeping away anyone who wants to kiss her."]],
 "If you're going up to Frostholm, take a slice for the road."],
20:["(Rosaria is scrubbing the oven floor with a wire brush.) A quiet day. A strange day. The back room has people in it since early, and they all came in through the kitchen. I didn't see them. I was busy.",[
  ["Who came in the back?","People wanting coffee. I don't look at faces, I look at plates. Faces get you in trouble. Plates only get you washing up."],
  ["Anything interesting at the palace today?","Marta says the guards are jumpy, and the butler's counting the silver twice. Butlers are always counting something."],
  ["You must know everything that goes on here.","I know how many pizzas go into the back room and how many plates come out. That's all. That's all anyone needs to know."]],
 "Out you go. I've got an oven to clean."],
21:["(The windows are grey with ash, and Rosaria has every chair filled with people who came in off the street.) Sit where you can. The mountain went up. People are frightened. Frightened people need bread.",[
  ["You're feeding all these people?","Of course. The oven's hot, I've got flour. Eila is pouring water, Marjut is cutting slices. We're a restaurant today, not a business."],
  ["Is Nonna safe?","In her bowl, under a cloth, under the counter. The first thing I grabbed. Then I grabbed the money. Then I remembered to grab my coat."],
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Nobody, they say. Lava in the north, and ash on everything. God is kind to fools and islands."]],
 "Stay inside if you can. The ash gets in your chest."],
22:["Ash in the flour. (Rosaria holds up a sieve, shaking her head.) Ash in the sugar, ash in the oil. I'm sieving everything twice. Pizza with volcano in it. Maybe I'll call it that.",[
  ["Will you keep cooking?","What else? The boats are stuck, there's no cheese coming, the herring man can't fish. But there's still flour, and tomatoes in tins. We'll be Neapolitan about it."],
  ["Are the roads open to Kingsgrad?","Gustavo's men were out with shovels this morning, clearing the street. I sent them coffee. Whatever people say about them, they turned up."],
  ["Volcano pizza. Very marketable.","Kelp Cola would put it in a can and give it away at the tram stop. I'll just make it."]],
 "Shake your coat before you come in. Please."],
23:["(Rosaria is stirring a huge pot.) No pizza today: the oven needs a rest, and so do I. Soup. Bean soup, for anyone who comes in. My grandmother called it the soup of hard times.",[
  ["What's in it?","Beans, onions, stale bread, anything left over. Everyone's soup is different in a bad week. That's how you know you've had a bad week."],
  ["How are people coping?","People are tired. Eila slept in the back room last night, on the crates, because her flat has no window. Don't tell anyone. She was too proud to ask."],
  ["The oven needs a rest?","The ground shook it again in the night. One brick is loose. I talked to it. Ovens listen, if you're polite."]],
 "Take a bowl. Don't argue."],
24:["Paola came in this morning, early, and asked for an empty sack of flour and a strong coffee. (Rosaria shrugs.) She didn't say why. She never does.",[
  ["What would she want a flour sack for?","Maybe she's baking. (She looks at you steadily.) I don't ask, journalist. Neither should you. Have a coffee."],
  ["Is the oven fixed?","A man from the gun shop, Frank, came and mortared the brick. He charged me two pizzas. A good trade. He's a Republican, but his hands are honest."],
  ["Do you like Paola?","Paola once fixed my clock without asking, while she waited for her coffee. She's a precise woman. I trust precise people. Mostly."]],
 "Drink the coffee. You look like you need it."],
25:["Sarivalto pizza, version three. The last one. (She sets it down like a crown.) Herring, goat's cheese, dill, a few berries, and ash-free. I sieved the oregano by hand.",[
  ["It's perfect. Put it on the menu.","It goes on the board today, in chalk. Twelve dollars. The first island pizza. My mother would say I've gone native. She'd be right."],
  ["What will you call it?","The Sarivalto, of course. Or the Volcano, now. Or the Nonna. (She laughs.) Every customer can call it what they like. They do anyway."],
  ["Princes and princesses everywhere today.","A crown for everyone, a vote for everyone. Let them have pizza first. You can't vote on an empty stomach."]],
 "Tell everyone about the Sarivalto. A journalist's word is worth ten customers."],
26:["(Rosaria is stacking chairs to sweep.) The cheese boat came in! Mozzarella, real mozzarella, three crates, past the ash. I kissed the man who carried it. He was Swedish. He went very red.",[
  ["You must be relieved.","A month without proper cheese. I was turning into a Finnish housewife, making everything with goat's cheese and good intentions."],
  ["Have you heard Dario's buying the spa?","Everybody's heard. Gustavo said one word about it at lunch, and it wasn't a word I'd say in front of Nonna."],
  ["Does the Sarivalto stay on the menu?","Of course! With goat's cheese, as it was born. Real mozzarella is for the margherita. Everything in its place."]],
 "Come for a margherita. A real one, at last."],
27:["(The back room door is wide open for once, and men are carrying in extra chairs.) The tournament. Everyone who's anyone, in my back room, playing cards for money. I've made forty pizzas.",[
  ["Who's playing?","People in good coats, and Nico in a bad one. Gustavo deals the first hand. I'll be in the kitchen, keeping the dough warm and my ears shut."],
  ["Will you play?","A woman who plays poker in her own kitchen loses twice: once at the cards, once at the cooking. I'll serve."],
  ["Forty pizzas? For a card game?","Card players eat with one hand and lie with the other. I know what I'm doing."]],
 "If you're playing, don't eat the garlic one. You'll give yourself away."],
28:["(Rosaria is listening to the radio and slicing tomatoes.) Marches in Kingsgrad, rallies in Eastbrook, the cola men off abroad, the Party with red flags. Everyone's shouting today. I've got the doors open so the shouting can come in for a slice.",[
  ["Do you care who wins?","I care that the oven's lit and the boats come in. Every king, every party, every commissar: they all eat pizza, in the end."],
  ["Who's eaten here today?","A Communist, a Royalist and a man from the oil company, all at the same table. They argued, then they shared the chilli oil. That's diplomacy."],
  ["Not tempted to join a march?","I marched once, in Naples, for bread. We got the bread. Now I make the bread. I'm done marching."]],
 "Go and see the marches. Come back and tell me who shouted loudest."],
29:["Carlo came in this morning, for the first time in his life with money. (Rosaria waves a bill.) He paid the whole list. Every pizza since July. The Republican vote's today, and suddenly he's honest.",[
  ["Why pay up now?","Because a man can't lead a party with a pizza debt. Somebody would print it. (She looks at you.) Somebody like you."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","Carlo smiles, Harald sells cola, Magnus counts oil. I don't vote, I'm not even a Republican. But I've got a soft spot for a man who pays his pizza bill."],
  ["What's the gossip at the palace?","Marta says the royals are deciding who comes after Leopold, in a locked room, with lawyers. Lawyers in a palace. That's how you know it's serious."]],
 "Go to Frostholm and watch the vote. I'll keep a slice warm."],
30:["(Rosaria has put a candle in a jar on every table.) The last day of the month, journalist, and tomorrow the island belongs to somebody. So tonight, everybody eats here for free. I've decided.",[
  ["Free? Can you afford it?","Probably not. My grandmother fed half of Naples on a promise. I can feed half of Kingsgrad on a good month."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Feed Nonna. Make dough. Whoever runs the island, Rosaria opens at eleven. Some things don't change."],
  ["The Sarivalto, please.","(She smiles and cuts you the biggest slice.) The island's own pizza, on the island's big day. Eat it while it's hot."]],
 "Whatever happens, come back for a slice. There'll always be one."],
};
// Ruby, owner of the Vinyl Resting Place, the record shop in Eastbrook (Democratic Party). Threads: her jazz and rock and roll,
// her impatience with speeches, Erik and the party, the tourists who never come, the rare record (12 September).
DAILY['Ruby']={
4:["(Ruby lifts the needle off a record.) A new face! Eastbrook's only just dug itself out of the snow, and here you are. You're the reporter, aren't you? The British one.",[
  ["That's me. Nice shop.","It's the best record shop north of Helsinki. It's also the only one. Have a dig in the crates: there's Coltrane in there somewhere."],
  ["What does a record shop owner make of the king's death?",["Sad, I suppose. He liked marches. Played them on the radio every New Year. (She shrugs.) Nobody's ever going to play marches in here.",[
    ["So who should run the island?","A vote. Erik's lot, the Democrats. Not because they're perfect: because then we could get rid of them if they weren't."],
    ["You don't sound heartbroken.","I'm not. I'm a shopkeeper. I'm heartbroken when the delivery boat's late."]]]],
  ["Play me something.","(She drops the needle on something with a walking bass line.) There. Now you're in Eastbrook properly."]],
 "Come back when you've got time to listen. Records aren't for hurrying."],
5:["You're back! Either you like records, or you're trying to get the Democrats on the front page of a London paper. Both are fine by me.",[
  ["I like records.","Prove it. Who's on piano on Kind of Blue? (She waits.) Bill Evans. Mostly. You can stay."],
  ["Tell me about the Democrats.","Erik runs it from the community centre, with too much coffee and not enough money. Everyone in Eastbrook's a member, more or less. Even the dog at the mill."],
  ["Business good?","With the roads only just open? I sold one record yesterday, to myself. Don't print that."]],
 "Go on, I've got crates to sort. Shut the door, the cold gets in the sleeves."],
6:["(Ruby is pricing a box of records with a grease pencil.) A man from Frostholm came in this morning and asked if I had any records with the king on the cover. I told him to try the church.",[
  ["Are there any records of the king?","One. The king reading a Christmas speech, 1962. I've had it in the back for fifteen years. I'd pay someone to take it."],
  ["Who was the man?","Fur collar, oil on his shoes. Diversified Petroleum, I'd bet. They come over the hill to look at us like we're a zoo."],
  ["Any good records in that box?","Mostly polkas. One Elvis, very scratched. Life's like that: one Elvis to a box of polkas."]],
 "If you see anyone who likes polkas, send them my way."],
7:["(Ruby has the telephone tucked under her chin.) No. No. It's not for sale, and it wasn't for sale on Tuesday either. (She hangs up.) Frostholm's open, and Frostholm's found my number.",[
  ["Who was that?","A secretary at Diversified Petroleum, asking very politely about a record I've got in the back. Third time this week. Her boss doesn't take no for an answer, so she's been sent to ask it again."],
  ["What record is it?",["Something old and local and very hard to find. (She taps the counter.) I'm not saying more, or I'll have every collector in Frostholm queuing in the snow.",[
    ["Why not sell it, if they'll pay?","Because some records you sell to whoever offers most, and some you sell to whoever loves them most. I haven't worked out which this is yet."],
    ["Is it worth a lot?","Depends who you ask. To me, about a month's rent. To a man in a fur collar, apparently, his whole weekend."]]]],
  ["At least the roads are open. Tourists, any day now.","Tourists. (She laughs.) I've been saying 'any day now' since 1974. The last one bought a postcard of the mural and asked where the real shops were."]],
 "If anyone from Frostholm asks, I've moved to Sweden."],
8:["(The radio's on, low, with a sermon from the monastery. Ruby reaches over and turns the dial until she finds a saxophone.) There. God can have Sunday. I'll have Kaisa.",[
  ["Kaisa on the radio plays jazz?","On Sundays, when the station manager's at church. She borrows half her records from me and brings back about a third."],
  ["You're not a churchgoer, then?","My mother was. She used to say the organ at the monastery was the closest thing on the island to Coltrane. She'd never heard Coltrane, but she wasn't far wrong."],
  ["Any news of your delivery boat?","Stuck in Helsinki with a box of records for me and a crate of tinned peaches for Lisa. The peaches will get here first. They always do."]],
 "Shut the door, would you? The saxophone's letting the cold in."],
9:["(Ruby is sipping something brown out of a Kelp Cola bottle and frowning.) Everybody on the island's suddenly an expert on what a cola should taste like. Dex has been asked twice today. Nobody's asked me.",[
  ["What would your cola taste like?","Coffee and blackcurrant, with a twist of something that ought to be illegal. You'd drink it at two in the morning with Thelonious Monk on."],
  ["Who's asking about flavours?","Cola men and Party men both, Dex says. One lot wants to sell it, the other wants to give it away. They'd both like to own your mouth, it seems."],
  ["Is that bottle actually cola?","It's cola with a little help. (She screws the cap back on.) It's a long afternoon in a record shop with no customers."]],
 "If someone invents a jazz cola, tell them I want a crate."],
10:["(Ruby is in a black jumper, which looks like it's been at the back of a drawer for years.) They're burying him today, over in Kingsgrad. I played something slow for him first thing. It seemed only polite.",[
  ["What did you play?","Billie Holiday. 'Gloomy Sunday'. Wrong day of the week, but he never cared much for jazz, so I don't think he'll mind."],
  ["Are you going to watch it?","Erik's gone, in his good suit. He came in to ask whether a tie with a little pattern on it was disrespectful. I told him nobody'd be looking at him. He seemed relieved and hurt at the same time."],
  ["You said he liked marches.","He did. (She sighs.) I've got one march record, the Royal Guards' Band. I put it on after Billie, for a minute. Then I took it off again. One minute's my limit."]],
 "Back to the crates. Somebody has to keep the island cheerful."],
11:["(Ruby is holding a fat brown envelope up to the light.) Someone pushed this under the shop door in the night. Cash, a lot of it, and a note in very neat handwriting. Don't look at me like that, it isn't a bribe.",[
  ["What does the note say?","That the money's for a particular record, and where it's to go, and that the buyer would rather not be named. Very old-fashioned writing. Proper ink."],
  ["Is it the record Frostholm keeps ringing about?",["(She lowers the envelope.) It might be. (Pause.) And the funny thing is, this is half what Frostholm offered, and I'd still sooner take it.",[
    ["Why?","Because the note says 'please' twice and 'thank you' three times. The man from Frostholm said 'name your price' four times, which is not the same thing at all."],
    ["Will you tell the Frostholm people?","I'll tell them the same thing I've told them all week. And then I'll unplug the telephone."]]]],
  ["The mill lads are up in arms today, I hear.","Knut came in to buy a record to cheer himself up and spent twenty minutes telling me about the saw. He left with Jailhouse Rock. Make of that what you like."]],
 "Don't breathe a word about the envelope. Eastbrook leaks like a colander."],
12:["(Ruby keeps glancing under her counter, as if something there might be listening.) Two hundred copies ever pressed, and one of them's sitting under my counter in a cardboard mailer. I've never been so nervous about a piece of vinyl.",[
  ["Tell me about the record.","Midnight at the Aurora. The Arvo Lind Quartet, live at the Hotel Aurora, 1959. Pressed in Stockholm, sold at the door, and then everyone forgot about it except the people who were there."],
  ["Who played on it?",["Arvo Lind on bass, a drummer from Turku, a young pianist nobody's heard of since, and a singer on two numbers. (She smiles.) That piano player could make a ballad sound like somebody confessing.",[
    ["What happened to him?","Nobody knows. Gave it up, people say. Some of the best ones do. The world's full of people who played one perfect night and then went off to sell insurance."],
    ["And the singer?","No name on the sleeve. Just a white dress in the photograph. I've always wondered about her."]]]],
  ["Why's everyone so desperate for it?","Because it's beautiful, and there's none left. That's all a collector needs. (She frowns.) Though some people want things the way dogs want bones."]],
 "Mind the ice on the steps. I'm feeling superstitious about things that fall."],
13:["(A Kelp Cola girl in a paper hat has left a free bottle on Ruby's counter. Ruby has put it on the turntable lid, as if it were a stranger.) They're giving it away in the street now. Nothing that free is ever free.",[
  ["Free samples are nice, though.","A free song on the radio's nice. A free cola's a fishhook with sugar on it. Next month it's ten pence, and the month after you can't remember life without it."],
  ["Still thinking about the Arvo Lind record?","(She glances under the counter.) I keep thinking about the Aurora in 1959. All those people in their good coats, not knowing they'd be the only ones who ever heard it live. Whatever happens to a record, the night happened."],
  ["Want me to drink it for you?","Be my guest. (She slides it across.) Tell me if it tastes of kelp. I've always suspected."]],
 "Take the cola with you. It's spoiling the look of the shop."],
14:["(The shop's empty, and Ruby has her coat on over her jumper.) The fourteenth. Everyone on the island's talking about it like it's the last day of school. I'm closing at noon whether there's a crowd in here or not.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Speeches. Lots of them. I've packed a flask and a pair of earmuffs."],
  ["What do Eastbrook people want from it?","To be asked. That's all most people here want, about anything. Erik's been ironing his shirt since breakfast."],
  ["Will you bring a record player?","(She brightens.) Now there's an idea. A wind-up gramophone and a box of Louis Armstrong. Nobody could make a speech over that."]],
 "Wish me luck. And earmuffs."],
15:["(Ruby is wrapping a flat parcel in brown paper, and laughing to herself.) You'll never guess. I've sold the king's Christmas speech. Fifteen years in the back, and a man from the palace came in for it today.",[
  ["Who bought it?","A very straight-backed gentleman in a black coat. Edmund, the butler, I think. He held it like a holy relic and paid without haggling. I nearly gave him a polka for free."],
  ["The palace and the church are at it again.","So I hear. Crown before cross, cross before crown. If they want to fight over something, they can fight over the last copy of Kind of Blue. I've got two."],
  ["Do you feel bad, selling it?","Not a bit. It's gone to someone who'll play it. That's all I want for any record, even a dreary one."]],
 "One less dusty sleeve. The shop feels lighter already."],
16:["(Ruby has the music turned up, quite loud.) It's debate day. The Democrats and the Communists, in the same room, talking at each other. I've got Charlie Parker on so I can't hear it from here.",[
  ["Aren't you going to support Erik?","I support Erik with all my heart, from a distance of three streets. I gave him a record for luck. He said 'what is it?' I said 'Bird'. He's never heard of him."],
  ["What will the Communists say?","That the workers should own everything. Then you'd have to ask a committee before you could buy a Beatles record. I'll keep my little shop, thanks."],
  ["Isn't a vote a speech, more or less?","(She thinks.) No. A vote's a short song. Everyone gets to sing one note, and then it's over, and you can go home. That's why I like it."]],
 "Tell Erik the music stops when the arguing does."],
17:["(The needle jumps a groove, all by itself. Ruby puts a hand flat on the counter.) Did you feel that? The floor went, just for a moment. I thought it was a lorry on Alder Lane.",[
  ["It's the volcano, people say.","There's steam up north, Dex says. Steam. As if the island hasn't had enough to put up with this month."],
  ["Is anything damaged?","One Elvis jumped off the shelf. Same scratched one from the polka box. He's had a hard life, that record."],
  ["You're not scared?","Scared for the shop. Records don't like heat and they don't like falling. They'd be terrible on a volcano."]],
 "If the floor goes again, grab Coltrane first. He's in the second crate."],
18:["(Jaana, out of the Kelpy cup costume, is leaving with a record under her arm. Ruby waves her off.) Jaana, from the museum. Spends all day inside a giant paper cup, and comes in here to remember she's a person.",[
  ["What did she buy?","Patsy Cline. It's always Patsy Cline when she's had a bad day in the cup. Today's been a Patsy Cline day, apparently."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola treating her badly?","She can't say, and I can't ask, and we both know. (She shrugs.) They own the costume, so they think they own the person in it."],
  ["Has the ground been shaking again?","Twice in the night. I lay in bed at the Lofts and listened to the record shelves rattle upstairs at Juniper's. She collects them too. Badly."]],
 "If you see Jaana in the cup, wave. She can't wave back, but she likes it."],
19:["(Ruby is pulling on a smart coat with a fur trim that has seen better days.) I'm off to Frostholm later. Leo's playing at the Midnight Note, and I haven't heard a real saxophone in a room since the snow came.",[
  ["Can I come?","It's a free island. For now. Sit at the back, and don't talk during the solos, or Sid will throw you in the snow."],
  ["Who else goes to the Midnight Note?","Everyone pretends they don't. Oil men, cola men, a Party woman in a headscarf. Rumour has it there's a royal who comes in dark glasses and sits by the piano. Jazz doesn't care who you are."],
  ["Isn't Frostholm enemy territory?","For a Democrat, Frostholm's all banks and billboards. But good music's never enemy territory. Not even under a Nordbank sign."]],
 "If I'm late opening tomorrow, it was a good solo."],
20:["(Ruby is very quiet, with no music on at all.) A man came in this morning I didn't like. Russian accent, good shoes, very polite. He didn't look at a single record.",[
  ["What did he want?","To know who bought records here. Who came in, who ordered what. Like a census. I told him I don't keep a list, which is a lie: I keep it in my head."],
  ["The Russian from the Hotel Aurora?","The same. Erik's been muttering about him for a week. Says he's no more a businessman than I'm a ballerina."],
  ["Why put the music off?","Because he stood there and I didn't want him to know what I like. (She puts a record on, finally.) There. He's gone. Ella Fitzgerald can come back in."]],
 "If a man in good shoes asks about me, I'm a tea shop."],
21:["(Ruby is on her knees, packing records into crates with newspaper. Outside, the sky to the north is a strange colour.) It's going. The mountain. Everyone's saying so. I'm getting the good ones off the shelves.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. My hands are shaking, but that's just the floor. (She laughs, not very well.) Hold this box, would you?"],
  ["What do you save first?","The jazz. Then the rock and roll. Then the ones people have ordered and not paid for. The polkas can take their chances."],
  ["Is Eastbrook in danger?","Erik says not. Erik says the lava goes north, and Eastbrook's south. Erik also said the snow would be gone by August. I'm packing anyway."]],
 "Close the door behind you. And then go somewhere safe."],
22:["(There's grey ash on everything, even the shop window, and Ruby is wiping the record sleeves one at a time with a soft cloth.) It got into the sleeves. Through the door, under the door, through the cracks I didn't know I had.",[
  ["Did the records survive?","Mostly. One I'd stood too near the stove while I was packing has warped. It's a Neil Sedaka now shaped like a crisp."],
  ["Was anyone hurt?","Nobody, thank God. Even Erik said 'thank God', and he's a schoolteacher. The north's a mess, people say, and the farms up there. But nobody's dead."],
  ["How does ash sound on a record?","Like frying bacon. Like a 1920s blues record. (She almost smiles.) Some people pay extra for that."]],
 "Mind the ash on the step. It's slippier than snow."],
23:["(The shop's half cleaned. Ruby is up a stepladder, wiping the mural on the inside wall.) If a volcano doesn't bring the tourists, nothing will. That's what I keep telling myself.",[
  ["Tourists for a volcano?","Of course. People will go anywhere something's burnt. I'm going to sell postcards. 'Sarivalto: Still Here'."],
  ["How's the rest of Eastbrook?","Dex has been giving out free hot dogs to anyone with a broom. Maya's roasting with a wet towel on the window sill. Jonna's tattooing ash clouds on anyone who asks."],
  ["Want a hand with the ladder?","Hold the bottom, and if I fall, catch me before the records. I mean that."]],
 "Thanks. You hold a ladder very professionally."],
24:["(Ruby is opening a crate with a claw hammer, beaming.) The boat came in! The delivery from Helsinki, three weeks late. Through snow, funerals and volcanoes, the records came.",[
  ["What did you get?","Fats Domino, two Chuck Berrys, a lovely Dave Brubeck, and (she holds one up, horrified) forty copies of a Finnish accordion orchestra."],
  ["Forty accordion records?","Somebody in Helsinki thinks very poorly of me. Or very highly of the accordion. I'll give one to every Communist on the island. It's the people's instrument."],
  ["Did Tor at the harbour bring it up?","He did, on his back, cursing the whole way. Then he stood here for half an hour looking at the Brubeck. He'll be back for it on payday."]],
 "Take an accordion record. Please. Free. Take two."],
25:["(Ruby is humming along to Elvis.) A princess for democracy. That's what Erik's calling it, and he's practically skipping. He thinks Princess Daphne might come round to a vote.",[
  ["Do you think she will?","I'll tell you a secret. Years ago, a girl in a headscarf came in here and bought Elvis's first album, and paid in coins from a little purse. I'd swear on my turntable it was her."],
  ["Isn't a royal Democrat a contradiction?","Everybody's a contradiction. I'm a shopkeeper who hates selling things. Erik's a schoolteacher who never stops talking. A princess who wants a vote is no odder than that."],
  ["Did Erik ask you to help?","He asked if I'd lend him a record that might win over a princess. I lent him Nat King Cole. If that doesn't do it, nothing will."]],
 "If you see the princess, tell her the Elvis is still here."],
26:["(Ruby is reading the Gazette with her feet up on the counter.) Dario's buying the spa now. The hot springs. A man who buys everything ought to have a hobby that isn't buying everything.",[
  ["Have you met Dario?","Never in the shop. His secretary's rung often enough. (She turns the page.) I'll say this for him: he knows what he wants, and he wants it all."],
  ["Can a man own a hot spring?","On this island you can own anything, so far. That's what the vote's for. To find out what can't be bought."],
  ["Anything good in the paper?","Nora says Kelp Cola want a prince's face on every bottle. Which prince, she doesn't say. I doubt Kelp Cola know either."]],
 "I'm going back to the crossword. It's all oil and princes today."],
27:["(Ruby has three stacks of records on the counter and is moving them between stacks with great seriousness.) Erik's asked me to choose the music for the rally. The big one, in Kingsgrad, tomorrow.",[
  ["What will you play?","Something to march to that isn't a march. Something to sing that isn't a hymn. Something to dance to that won't get us arrested. Three records, and I can't pick."],
  ["So you're joining in the speeches after all?","(She points a record at you.) I'm playing the bits between speeches. That's the important part. That's when people decide."],
  ["Play the accordion orchestra.","Don't tempt me. If the Communists turn up to heckle, I might."]],
 "Three stacks, one gramophone. I'll be up till dawn."],
28:["(Ruby is loading a portable gramophone and a crate into a borrowed van.) Rally day. I've got the music, Dex has the hot dogs, Erik has a speech he's rewritten nine times. And I've got butterflies.",[
  ["What did you pick in the end?","That's a secret until it's playing. But I'll tell you this much: it swings."],
  ["Will people come?","Erik says thousands. Bjarne says hundreds. Ilse says she'll carry people there if she has to. I'd settle for enough to drown out the hecklers."],
  ["You, nervous? You run a shop.","Running a shop's easy. This is a whole island deciding what it sounds like. (She shuts the van door.) No pressure."]],
 "I'll be by the stage, next to the man with the loudest megaphone."],
29:["(Ruby looks tired but cheerful, and her voice is a little hoarse.) The Communists have got a choir out singing the Internationale. Helen's idea. It's actually rather good.",[
  ["Can you compete with that?","I've got Louis Armstrong and a gramophone with a big horn. If they want a sing-off, they can have one. Friendly, mind."],
  ["Do you mind the Communists?","I mind anyone who'd tell me what to play. But Helen likes a good tune, and you can't hate anyone who can hold a harmony."],
  ["What's the mood in Eastbrook?","Like the last seconds of a side, when you don't know if that's the last track or there's one more. Everyone's holding their breath."]],
 "One more day. Then we'll find out who's playing the music."],
30:["(The shop is very quiet. Ruby has put on a record and is just listening, with her chin on her hand.) The last day. Whatever happens, tomorrow I'll open the shop and put on a record. That's the plan.",[
  ["What are you listening to?","Coltrane. 'A Love Supreme'. It's the record I put on when I want things to turn out all right and I can't do anything about it."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote. A small dull vote with paper ballots and a wooden box. And then nobody making speeches about it for at least a year."],
  ["Thanks for the music, Ruby.","(She looks up and smiles.) Thanks for listening. You'd be surprised how few people do. Come back after, whatever after looks like."]],
 "Go on. The island's waiting. The record's nearly over."],
};
// Ruth, receptionist at Diversified Petroleum HQ in Frostholm, lives in the Skyline Apartments (Diversified Petroleum). Threads:
// the appointments book and who comes in by the back door; her love of westerns and cowboys (a signed rodeo poster over her bed, the
// cinema in Kingsgrad, a dream of a ranch holiday in Wyoming); reading Dario's moods from the intercom; eleven years on the desk.
DAILY['Ruth']={
7:["(Ruth looks up from a large leather appointments book.) Name? You're not in the book. Nobody's in the book; we've been snowed in a week. Oh, the British reporter. Dario's got everyone in for a speech.",[
  ["Does he make speeches often?","Whenever he's bought something. Last time it was the milkshake machine. We all had to clap."],
  ["Who else comes through here?","Everyone who wants something from Dario. Which is everyone. The ones who don't want to be seen use the back door, and I see them anyway."],
  ["Are you in charge of the whole building?","Officially, no. In practice, nobody gets up those stairs unless I've written them down. Eleven years. I've got very good handwriting."]],
 "If you want to see Dario, I'll pencil you in. Pencil, mind. Ink is for people he likes."],
8:["(Ruth is holding the telephone receiver away from her ear.) That's Dario. He's been shouting since he came in. Something at the rig. I've cancelled everything until Thursday.",[
  ["What's happened at the rig?","Nobody tells reception anything. I just hear the volume. Today it's very loud."],
  ["How do you read his moods?","One buzz on the intercom, coffee. Two buzzes, bourbon. Three buzzes, I start cancelling things. Today's been three all day."],
  ["Want me to cheer you up?","You could tell me you've got a horse outside. That would do it."]],
 "Don't go up. Not today. I mean it kindly."],
9:["(Ruth has a postcard pinned beside the switchboard: a cowboy on a bucking horse.) Don't laugh. That's Casey Tibbs, the greatest bronc rider who ever lived. He signed it. Well, he signed the one over my bed.",[
  ["Are you a big western fan?","I've seen every western that's ever come to the cinema in Kingsgrad, and some of them three times. Otto saves me the same seat."],
  ["How did you get it signed?","My cousin went to America in 1962 and stood in a queue at a rodeo in Wyoming for four hours. I've never loved anyone more."],
  ["Bit of a long way from Frostholm.","Everything good is a long way from Frostholm. That's why you have to dream about it."]],
 "Yippee-ki-yay. I'm not allowed to say that to callers, so I say it to you."],
10:["(Ruth is on the switchboard, plugging in cables.) Everyone's at the king's funeral, so it's just me and the telephones. They don't stop ringing. Half of them are asking what Dario wore.",[
  ["What did Dario wear?","Black, with a silk tie, and an expression like he was pricing the chapel. Don't print that. Actually, print it."],
  ["Did you want to go?","I watched the old king go past once, in 1968, in an open car in the snow. He waved at me. That's my funeral, and I've had it."],
  ["Who's been calling?","The Gazette, twice. Kelp Cola, once, pretending to be somebody else. I know Elin's voice. She sounds like a sensible woman trying not to laugh."]],
 "If the phones keep up like this, I'll need my own funeral."],
11:["(Ruth is writing in the appointments book in neat capitals.) A man came through the back door earlier, asked for Dario, and wouldn't leave a name. I wrote him down as 'Hat'.",[
  ["What did he look like?","Good coat, Italian shoes, and a face like a closed shop. One of Gustavo's, I'd say. They walk like they own the stairs."],
  ["Do you write everyone down?","Everyone. Even the ones who'd rather I didn't. Especially those."],
  ["Did Dario see him?","For four minutes. Then two buzzes. Bourbon. You can draw your own conclusions; I'm only the receptionist."]],
 "Hat. Four minutes. Two buzzes. It's all in the book."],
12:["(Ruth is stamping envelopes, very fast.) Busy day. Papers going up and down those stairs like a lift. Everyone wants a signature for something.",[
  ["Do people ask you to sign things?","Now and then, as a witness. I read every word first. Some people find that very inconvenient."],
  ["Who'd make you sign something without reading it?","A man with a nice enough hat might get close. (She laughs.) That's a joke. Mostly."],
  ["What makes a good visitor?","Clean boots, a firm handshake, and no long words. You'd be amazed how rare that is."]],
 "Sign in, sign out. That's all the signing I want today."],
13:["(Ruth is unwrapping a sandwich.) Have you tried the Kelp Cola they're giving out free in Kingsgrad? Sven brought a crate back. We're not supposed to drink it in this building, so I'm drinking it under the desk.",[
  ["Is it good?","It tastes like the seaside if the seaside had sugar in it. I've had three."],
  ["What if Dario catches you?","He'd buy the crate off Sven at twice the price and pour it down the drain in front of me. He's very theatrical about Phil."],
  ["Under the desk? Very cloak and dagger.","Eleven years on reception, you learn where every blind spot in this lobby is. There are four. I'm in the best one."]],
 "Don't tell anyone where the cola is. Particularly Magnus."],
14:["(Ruth has curled her hair and put on a brooch.) The big day. The whole island in one place. My mother used to say you could tell an island's future by its hats. I'm going to look at the hats.",[
  ["What do you expect from today?","A lot of men talking, a lot of women doing the work, and somebody falling in the snow. Same as every big day."],
  ["What hat would you like to see?","A Stetson. Just one. On a tall man with a quiet voice. I'm not fussy beyond that."],
  ["Nice brooch.","My grandmother's. A little silver horse. It only comes out for coronations, weddings and occasions."]],
 "If you see a Stetson, come and tell me. I'll be by the coats."],
15:["(Ruth has the Gazette folded to the cinema listings.) They're showing a western in Kingsgrad on Saturday. 'High Plains Drifter'. I've seen it four times. I'm going again.",[
  ["Is it any good?","It's about a stranger who rides into town and nobody knows what he's after. Clint Eastwood barely says ten words. I adore him."],
  ["Prince or Church, by the way?","I don't mind who's king as long as he can sit on a horse. Leopold looks like he'd fall off. Augustus looks like he'd pray first."],
  ["Who's going with you?","Britta, next door, if she's not too tired. She falls asleep in the gunfights. I've no idea how."]],
 "A stranger rides into town. I've always liked that sort of story."],
16:["(Ruth has the radio on low under the desk.) They're having the Great Debate on the radio, Communists against Democrats. Dario told me to listen and take notes. I've drawn a horse.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The Democrats sound like a school play. Anna sounds like she'd shoot the director. I'm on nobody's side; I just answer the telephone."],
  ["Show me the horse.","(She turns the pad round. It's a good horse.) His name's Thunder. He's against both of them."],
  ["Will you tell Dario you drew a horse?","I'll tell him Anna was very persuasive and the Democrats were very sincere. That's notes."]],
 "Thunder abstains. He's a very principled horse."],
17:["(Ruth is answering the telephone for the fourth time in a minute.) No, Mr Dario isn't available. No, I don't know when. (She hangs up.) Nobody's seen him all day. Not even me, and I see everyone.",[
  ["Where would he go?","When he's in a mood he drives. Up the coast, to the spa, to his villa. Once to the end of the jetty, where he sat in the car for an hour."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a receptionist. Worry is above my grade. (She chews her pen.) A little."],
  ["Who's been ringing?","Magnus, twice, from upstairs. Somebody who wouldn't give a name, three times. I've written them all down as 'Worried'."]],
 "If you find him, tell him Ruth wants to know. He'll come back for that."],
18:["(Ruth has caught a pen as it rolled off the desk.) Did you feel that? The whole lobby shook. My coffee walked to the edge of the desk on its own.",[
  ["Was it the volcano?","Sven says there's steam over the mountain. Sven says a lot of things, but this time Ole agrees, and Ole never agrees with Sven."],
  ["Are you frightened?","Cowboys don't get frightened. They get thoughtful and squint. (She squints.) I'm being thoughtful."],
  ["Who came through the back door today?","Nobody. Even the sneaks are staying home. That's how I know people are worried."]],
 "If it shakes again, I'm getting under the desk with the cola."],
19:["(Ruth is leaning forward, eyes shining.) Rita at the Midnight Note told Britta, who told me, that somebody royal's been coming in at night, in disguise. Can you imagine?",[
  ["Which royal?","Rita won't say. She's a jazz singer. They only tell you things in the third verse."],
  ["Would you go and look?","I'd go in a heartbeat, if I could get Britta to stay awake past nine."],
  ["What disguise?","A false moustache, apparently. On a royal. I'd have gone for a Stetson, myself, but nobody asks me."]],
 "A prince in a moustache. Better than the pictures."],
20:["(Ruth is copying names from a hotel letterhead.) Greta, at the Aurora's front desk, says the Russian in the top room has had six parcels this week. Six. I get one at Christmas.",[
  ["What's in the parcels?","Greta says they're heavy and they hum. She's not allowed to open them. Neither am I, if any come here. I would, though."],
  ["Do you and Greta swap gossip?","We're both receptionists. We're the island's only reliable news service. Henrik at the Gazette should pay us."],
  ["Has the Russian been here?","Once, last month. He asked how deep the rig went and admired my brooch. I didn't like him, even if he did know it was silver."]],
 "Six parcels. I'll find out. I always do."],
21:["(Ruth is pale, with ash in her hair and the switchboard lit up like Christmas.) The mountain's gone up, in the north. I've rung every name on the staff list. Every single one answered. (She sits down.) Every one.",[
  ["Well done, Ruth.","Somebody had to. Dario stood behind me the whole time and didn't say a word. I think he was counting too."],
  ["Is your flat all right?","The windows are grey and Britta's swept my balcony for me. That's the kind of neighbour you want when the world ends."],
  ["Are you all right?",["I'm shaking. Cowboys shake too, you know. They just do it in the saddle.",[
    ["Get some air.","There's no air out there. Only ash. I'll stay at my desk where I know where everything is."],
    ["Want me to stay a minute?","(She nods.) Just a minute. Then I've got more numbers to ring."]]]]],
 "Mind your eyes on the way out. The ash stings."],
22:["(Ruth is wiping ash off the appointments book with a soft brush.) Eleven years I've kept this book clean. Not one blot. And now look at it. Grey thumbprints everywhere.",[
  ["It's still readable.","Readable isn't the point. A book shows how a building's run. This one now says 'volcano'."],
  ["How are people coping?","Lars came in from the rig boat with ash in his eyebrows and asked if we were still open. We're always open. That's what reception means."],
  ["Who's been in today?","Half of Frostholm, wanting fuel for their generators. I sent them all to Britta at the depot. She hasn't forgiven me."]],
 "Wipe your shoes. I'm not brushing this book twice."],
23:["(Ruth has her intercom pressed flat and is holding it shut with her elbow.) Three buzzes. All day. Something at the rig, under the water. I've cancelled the rest of the week.",[
  ["Under the water? What happened?","Nobody tells reception. But the divers' boat went out, and Dario stood at that window for twenty minutes without moving."],
  ["How do you put up with it?","I think about Wyoming. Big sky, a horse called Thunder, and not one intercom for a hundred miles."],
  ["Have you ever taken a holiday?","Not in eleven years. I'm saving it all up. When I go, I'm going so far they'll need a map to post my wages."]],
 "Three buzzes. Don't go up there, unless you're a diver."],
24:["(Ruth is reading a seed catalogue under the appointments book.) Don't tell anyone, but I've been pricing horses. Not to buy. Just to know.",[
  ["How much is a horse?","More than a receptionist earns in a year, and that's before the saddle. But if you don't know the price, you can't save for it."],
  ["Where would you keep it?","On the Skyline balcony. (She laughs.) No. One day, somewhere with grass, and no ash, and no telephones."],
  ["Is that a seed catalogue?","Everything under this book is a dream, and the seed catalogue's the smallest. It's for a window box."]],
 "Don't tell Magnus about the horse. He'd want a cost analysis."],
25:["(Ruth is wrapping a small parcel in brown paper.) It's for my cousin in America, the one who got my poster signed. Ash from the volcano, in a jar. She'll never believe it otherwise.",[
  ["Will the post take it?","Ole at the port office says it needs a customs form. He says everything needs a customs form. He'd want one for a sneeze."],
  ["Do you miss having family nearby?","My mother's in Kingsgrad, and she rings me every evening to tell me who's died. It's a lot of company, in its way."],
  ["What else would you send her?","A photo of Dario's bowling lane. She thinks I make it up."]],
 "Volcano in a jar. Only on Sarivalto."],
26:["(Ruth is shaking her head at the appointments book.) Dario's cleared his whole day to go and see about that spa on the mountain. He's never cleared his whole day for anything. Not even the funeral.",[
  ["Why does he want it so much?","He says it's his back. But he went up every week before the eruption, and he always came back quieter. I'd want it too."],
  ["Will the owner sell?","Saga, who works there, lives in Kingsgrad, and she says the owner's as stubborn as a mule. But I've seen Dario with stubborn people."],
  ["Have you been to the spa?","Once. I fell asleep in the hot pool and woke up pink. It was the best day of my year."]],
 "If he comes back smiling, I'm asking for a raise. If he doesn't, I'm hiding."],
27:["(Ruth is cutting paper streamers with very large scissors.) Dario's office party is tomorrow. He's asked me to do the decorations. In eleven years he has never once asked me to do anything fun.",[
  ["What's the theme?","He said 'bowling'. I've decided it's 'bowling, but in the Wild West'. He won't notice until he's two milkshakes in."],
  ["Are you a good bowler?","I bowl like I answer the phone. Straight down the middle, and no nonsense."],
  ["Is Magnus coming?","Magnus has asked whether there's a dress code. I said cowboy boots. He wrote it down. I may have to tell him I was joking."]],
 "Yee-haw, as they say at the Petro Tower. They don't, but they should."],
28:["(Ruth has a bandana tied round her neck and a milkshake in each hand.) Party day. And the whole island's out pushing for something, every faction at once. I've never seen Frostholm so busy.",[
  ["Who's winning on the lane?","Me. Lars bowled the ball backwards. Magnus undid a button. It's been quite an afternoon."],
  ["Heard anything interesting?","At a party? Everything. Drink loosens tongues, and milkshakes loosen the rest. I've filled four pages in the book."],
  ["Nice bandana.","Thank you. Britta said I look like a train robber. That's the nicest thing she's ever said."]],
 "Take a milkshake. Strawberry. It's the safest."],
29:["(Ruth is sitting very straight with her hands folded on the appointments book.) The Republican vote's today, at the party HQ. Magnus left in his best suit at eight, with a rubber band round forty cards.",[
  ["Do you want him to win?","I want the building to be quiet afterwards. If he wins, Dario sulks. If he loses, Magnus sulks. I'm planning for sulking."],
  ["What's the gossip?","Margit's counting, and Margit's honest, so nobody can guess. That's very unusual round here. Everyone's on edge."],
  ["How many votes does he need?","Twenty-two out of forty-three. He's written it on his wrist. I saw him check it twice on the stairs."]],
 "Sulking, either way. I've ordered extra biscuits."],
30:["(Ruth has the appointments book closed for once, with her hand flat on it.) The last day. Whatever's decided, it's decided today. I haven't written anybody in. I thought I'd leave the page empty and see who comes.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","That the island stays an island. That somebody who can sit on a horse ends up in charge. That I still have a desk on Monday."],
  ["Who's been through the back door?","Today? Nobody. Everyone's using the front. It's the first honest day this building's ever had."],
  ["And Wyoming?","It's still there. It'll wait. Big skies are very patient."]],
 "Ride safe, stranger. I've always wanted to say that to somebody."],
};
// Saara, Kelp Cola's head chemist, in the research lab under the HQ (Kelp Cola). Threads: the hunt for a new flavour (and, after
// it, a 'Light' cola for the foreign buyers); Noora, her technician, and the exam she pretends not to care about; the volcano,
// which she measures before anyone else takes it seriously; and the secret recipe she builds around but has never been told.
DAILY['Saara']={
2:["(Saara sits at her kitchen table among a crowd of little bottles.) Don't knock anything over. That's cardamom, that's juniper, and that's my landlady's vanilla, which she doesn't know I've borrowed.",[
  ["Working from home?","The lab's shut till tomorrow. Phil wants a new flavour by the end of the month, and I don't stop thinking just because the road's closed."],
  ["What does Phil want, exactly?","Something new. He can't say what. He'll know it when he tastes it. That isn't a brief, it's a horoscope."],
  ["Juniper cola?","Tastes like gin that's lost its way. No."]],
 "Close the door gently. The cardamom's open."],
3:["(Saara writes on the blackboard, then rubs it out.) A month away, and the blackboard's exactly as I left it. Which is to say, wrong.",[
  ["What's on it?","Ratios. Sugar to acid to fizz. Every cola is a balance, and mine keep falling over."],
  ["Noora's very glad to be back.","Noora's the best technician I've ever had. Don't tell her. She'd stop being nervous, and the nerves are what make her careful."],
  ["Is the secret recipe down here?","No. The secret recipe is upstairs, in Phil's head and a locked drawer. I make everything around it. It's like decorating a house you're not allowed into."]],
 "Mind the burner. It's always on."],
4:["Eleven test batches today, and I said no to every one. Noora thinks I'm cruel. I'm only accurate.",[
  ["What was wrong with them?","Cloudberry tastes of jam, liquorice of medicine and pine of furniture polish. None of them tastes of news."],
  ["What does news taste of?","Surprise. Then, a second later, the feeling that you'd always known it. That's the hard part."],
  ["Did you try herring?","In a weak moment. Noora still won't speak of it."]],
 "If you think of anything new, I'm always here. Always."],
5:["A letter from my old professor in Helsinki. He wants me back at the university. He writes every September. He's very persistent, for a man of seventy.",[
  ["Will you go?","To teach first-years the boiling point of water? Here I make something people drink. That's better than a lecture."],
  ["Why did you come to Sarivalto?","Phil's father advertised for a chemist 'with a feeling for flavour'. Nobody at the university had ever used the word 'feeling' to me."],
  ["Did you miss the lab during the snow?","I missed the tasting table. You can't taste at home. Everything at home tastes of home."]],
 "Give my regards to Helsinki, if you're ever there. Not too warm."],
6:["Noora's been leaving a sandwich on my desk. I noticed today. Apparently it's been going on for a week.",[
  ["Do you eat it?","Evidently. I don't remember eating it. It must be a good sandwich."],
  ["Will you thank her?","I'll mark her practice paper. That's how I say thank you: in red ink."],
  ["Any progress on the flavour?","Coffee. I tried coffee. It tasted like the morning after a party. Phil liked it, which is a bad sign."]],
 "Don't mention the sandwich to her. I'd hate it to stop."],
7:["Sanna from Polar Burger's been on the telephone to Phil. The oil men want something 'with a kick'. Everybody's a flavour chemist now.",[
  ["A kick?","Ginger, chilli or rum. I can do two of those legally."],
  ["Have you eaten at Polar Burger?","Once. The burger was ordinary, the cola was ours, and the chips were perfect. Sanna knows exactly how long to fry. That's chemistry."],
  ["How's Noora?","Her exam papers came today and she went pale. Good. She's taking it seriously."]],
 "A kick. Honestly."],
8:["Sunday, and the lab's mine. I've tried smoke today: smoked birch. It tastes of a sauna. Not unpleasant. Not cola.",[
  ["Any other ideas?","I've run out of plants. I'm thinking about minerals now. That's either genius or desperation, and from the inside I can't tell which."],
  ["Heard about the oil rig?","If there's oil in the water, it'll reach the kelp beds. The kelp in our tank comes from that beach. I've taken a sample, just in case."],
  ["Don't you ever rest?","This is resting. Nobody rings the lab on a Sunday."]],
 "Smoked birch. Write it down as a failure, would you? I've run out of ink."],
9:["(Saara is pacing.) I'm wanted at the factory and wanted here, Phil's deadline is creeping closer, and nobody has a flavour. Today is a test of character.",[
  ["Why are you wanted at the factory?","Kurt wants to know how a new flavour would run on the line before there is one. Kurt likes to be ready for things that don't exist yet."],
  ["What exactly are you looking for?","Something nobody has tasted, that everybody recognises. 'Simple,' says Phil. Phil also thinks a titration is a dance."],
  ["Have you tried asking cooks?","Perhaps I should. Cooks know what people like. I only know what molecules like."]],
 "If you hear a good idea anywhere, from anyone, bring it to me."],
10:["I'm listening to the funeral on the wireless. Phil asked me to stay and mind the lab. He thinks somebody will steal the blackboard while we're all in black.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","He sent a note down to the lab once, years ago: 'Less sugar.' I've kept it. It's the only review I've ever had from a king."],
  ["Who'd steal a blackboard?","Anyone who can't read my handwriting, so nobody useful."],
  ["How's Noora taking it?","She cried at the hymns. I gave her a beaker to wash. It helps."]],
 "Quietly, please. They're singing."],
11:["Noora's come in with orange cat hair all over her coat. I've banned her from the tasting table until she's brushed.",[
  ["Isn't that a bit strict?","A single hair can ruin a tasting. So can a single opinion. I'm strict about both."],
  ["Helen's at the mill today, I hear.","And Petra's been round with a petition for heating in the warehouse. I signed it. That warehouse is colder than my sample fridge. I've measured."],
  ["What are you working on now?","Quality tests. Every hundredth bottle comes down here, and today's tasted exactly like yesterday's. That's the whole point."]],
 "Brush yourself down before you touch anything."],
12:["I've set Noora a mock exam. She's sitting it at the bench, and I'm pretending to read.",[
  ["How is she doing?","She chewed her pencil down to the lead at question three. Then she got it right."],
  ["Free samples tomorrow, I hear.","Phil wanted me to test a bottle from every crate. I said one in a hundred. We've compromised on one in a hundred."],
  ["Any word from your professor?","He's written again, with a photograph of my old lab bench. It's emotional blackmail, and it's working a little."]],
 "Don't distract her. Question four's a monster."],
13:["A man in a good coat came down here and stared at my blackboard. Noora chased him off with pine cola. The blackboard's no use to him: the secret isn't on it.",[
  ["Where is the secret, then?",["Not here. Every week Phil comes down with a little brown bottle and adds it to the base syrup himself, with the door shut. Even I don't know what's in it.",[
    ["Haven't you ever analysed it?","(She looks at you for a long moment.) A chemist doesn't analyse a friend's secret. Not on paper, anyway."],
    ["Doesn't that annoy you?","Every week, for nine years. I've learned to admire it. It's a magic trick you don't want explained."]]]],
  ["Did you get a free sample?","Phil gave me one himself. I've known what's in it for nine years, except for the part that matters."],
  ["Who would want the recipe?","Everyone who can't make it. Which is everyone."]],
 "If you see a little brown bottle, you didn't."],
14:["The big day. I've been told to stand by the Kelp Cola crates and look scientific. I've brought my lab coat. Phil says it adds credibility.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Crowds, speeches, cold hands. I've brought gloves and a notebook. A crowd's a kind of experiment."],
  ["What will you write down?","Who drinks what. A good chemist never stops counting."],
  ["Is Noora coming?","With her neighbours, under a red flag. I've told her to keep her umbrella up and her opinions down."]],
 "If you need a cola, look for the lab coat."],
15:["I wrote 'good' on Noora's practice paper. She looked at it as if it were a telegram from God.",[
  ["Was it good?","It was excellent. But 'excellent' is for the exam. You have to save something."],
  ["Which prince do you prefer?","Augustus came down here years ago to ask about incense. I told him frankincense is mostly boswellic acid, and he wrote it down. Anyone who writes down boswellic acid has my respect."],
  ["And Leopold?","Leopold once sent a crate back for being too fizzy. He's not wrong. I've been saying it for years."]],
 "Don't tell Noora it was excellent. Not yet."],
16:["Noora's listening to the debate on my wireless. I'm listening too, as an experiment in how long two people can talk without measuring anything.",[
  ["Who should run the island?","Someone who changes their mind when the evidence changes. I've never met such a person in politics."],
  ["Noora says debates are like titrations.","I see she's been quoting me. Yes. Add, add, add, nothing, nothing, then: pink."],
  ["What are you working on?","Tasting. Forty bottles from the warehouse today. My teeth are filing a complaint."]],
 "Turn it down a little on your way out. Anna's getting loud."],
17:["Did you feel it? Every flask on the bench rang a different note. I wrote them all down. It made a chord: D minor.",[
  ["Aren't you frightened?","Of course. But fright is only information arriving too quickly."],
  ["What's the steam on the mountain?","Sulphur dioxide, hydrogen sulphide and water vapour. The mountain's clearing its throat."],
  ["Noora says she caught three flasks.","She did. I caught two. We're having a competition next time."]],
 "Don't lean on the bench. It's still humming."],
18:["I want to take readings at the hot springs. Phil says the insurance won't cover it. I've asked Elin for the papers.",[
  ["Is it safe?","Nothing worth measuring is safe. But I'll take Noora, and Noora's careful enough for both of us."],
  ["Jaana's ill, I hear.","She has a cold from standing in the snow inside a cardboard cup. If they'd asked me, I'd have lined it with wool."],
  ["Why the springs?","The water's temperature tells you what the rock is doing. Rock doesn't write letters."]],
 "Not a word to Phil about the springs."],
19:["Elin gave me the wrong insurance papers. On purpose, I think. I've decided not to have noticed.",[
  ["So you're going up?","Tomorrow. Early, before Phil's had his first bottle."],
  ["Phil says there were Party leaflets in the crates.","Someone left one in my sample fridge. I tested it. It's mostly optimism."],
  ["Heard about the prince at the Midnight Note?","I don't follow princes. I follow temperatures."]],
 "If Phil asks where I am tomorrow, I'm in Eastbrook."],
20:["(Saara's shoes are stained yellow.) The hot springs are two degrees warmer than last year. Two degrees in a year. Rock doesn't do that without a reason.",[
  ["What does it mean?",["That something underneath us is rising. I've written to the university in Helsinki. By the time they read it, it'll be history.",[
    ["Should people leave?","I've told Phil to keep the factory's east door clear and the north road free. More than that, I can't honestly say."],
    ["Are you sure?","I measured three times. I'm sure of the numbers. I'm not sure of the mountain."]]]],
  ["Did Saga give you tea?","Tea that tasted of eggs. She says that's the healing minerals. I say it's hydrogen sulphide."],
  ["Did Phil notice your shoes?","He did. I told him I'd been to Eastbrook. He believed me. He was busy with Harald."]],
 "Keep your east door clear too. Wherever it is."],
21:["(Saara stands at the lab's little window, watching the ash fall.) I was right, and I wish I hadn't been.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Every one of us. Noora and I came up the stairs together. Then I went straight back down to check the kelp tank. Old habits."],
  ["What happens now?","Ash for weeks, and aftershocks. The mountain's said its piece, I think. It'll grumble for a while yet."],
  ["Did you warn Phil?","I did. He bought sandbags. It's the thought that counts. Chemically speaking, not at all."]],
 "Breathe through a cloth outside. Fine ash is worse than coarse."],
22:["I've been through every crate in the warehouse with a torch. Not a speck of ash in a single bottle. The caps held. I could kiss them.",[
  ["What's wrong at East Farm?","Phil says something's amiss out there, and he wants a chemist to look. I'd rather he wanted a farmer."],
  ["What's next for the lab?","Phil wants a cola for the foreign buyers. 'Light', he calls it: less sugar. The king asked for that twenty years ago, and nobody listened."],
  ["How's Noora?","Her exam's still on. I've told Helsinki the ash is no excuse. She's furious with me, and revising harder."]],
 "Less sugar. Twenty years late, but less sugar."],
23:["I've started collecting the ash, a jar a day. Sulphur, silica, iron. The island's insides are on the outside now.",[
  ["Why collect it?","Because nobody else will, and in fifty years somebody will want to know what this month was made of."],
  ["How's the Light cola?","Less sugar, more acid, and an American sweetener that tastes of tin. I'm trying to make the tin polite."],
  ["Kurt's guarding the factory.","If anyone throws a spanner into his machinery, I pity the spanner."]],
 "Don't touch the jars. They're dated."],
24:["Phil came down today and stood by the kelp tank for a long time without saying anything. Then he asked me if I'd ever wanted to run a company.",[
  ["What did you say?","No. He looked relieved and disappointed at the same time. It's a difficult face to make."],
  ["Would you, though?","I'd run the lab and leave the company to someone who likes meetings. Elin, probably."],
  ["How's the tin?","Getting quieter. Another few days."]],
 "Don't repeat that to anyone. Least of all Phil."],
25:["They want the cola in Phil's new advert to fizz just so. I've added more carbon dioxide and asked it nicely.",[
  ["Who's in the advert?","I wasn't told. Whoever he is, I've made the bottles fizz towards him."],
  ["They say Princess Daphne's with the Democrats.","Chemistry is full of unlikely bonds. Some of them turn out to be stable."],
  ["Did you answer your professor?","I've said no. I enclosed a bottle. He'll understand when he tastes it."]],
 "Shake nothing on your way out. Everything down here is under pressure."],
26:["Dario's buying the hot springs spa. Two degrees warmer and rising, and he's buying it. I've sent my readings to Saga. She's keeping them in her drawer.",[
  ["Would you warn Dario?","I'd warn anyone who asked. He hasn't. Oil men believe in the ground; it's always paid them."],
  ["How's the advert going?","They're filming upstairs. I've sent up six bottles, each shaken exactly twice."],
  ["How's Noora?","Two days to go. I've cleared my desk for her to sit at. Nobody's sat at my desk in nine years."]],
 "Quiet on the stairs. Cameras."],
27:["Kelp Cola Light is ready for the buyers. Less sugar, the tin's gone quiet, and it still tastes like us. I'm almost proud.",[
  ["Only almost?","A chemist is never proud. Satisfied, provisionally, pending further tests."],
  ["Could anyone spoil it?","I've locked the samples in my fridge, and the key's round my neck. Noora's the only other one who knows. Now you do. Don't."],
  ["Noora's exam is tomorrow.","I'm invigilating. Strictly. I've practised my stern face in a beaker."]],
 "You saw nothing round my neck."],
28:["(Saara stands at the foot of the stairs, arms folded, guarding Noora's exam.) Upstairs, Phil is selling my cola to foreigners. Down here, Noora's on question four. I'm needed in both places, and I've chosen.",[
  ["Shouldn't you be upstairs?","Phil has the samples and a speech. Noora has me. The cola doesn't need a chemist today. The chemist does."],
  ["How's she doing?","She's frowning. Frowning is good. Smiling at question five would worry me."],
  ["There's shouting in the streets.","Noora hasn't looked up once. That's the scientific temperament."]],
 "Whisper, or leave. Preferably leave."],
29:["Noora's paper is on the mail boat. I read it over her shoulder and said nothing. She understood.",[
  ["Was it good?","Excellent. I'll tell her so when the letter comes from Helsinki. Not before."],
  ["Will you cheer for Harald?","I'll go up to Frostholm and stand at the back with a notebook. Crowds are data."],
  ["And the kelp?","Sigrid's grown eleven inches this month. Noora measured. Nobody told the kelp about the volcano."]],
 "Excellent. Don't you dare tell her."],
30:["(Saara sits at the tasting table with two glasses.) The last day. I'm doing what I always do when I don't know what will happen: tasting something I do know.",[
  ["What are you tasting?","Today's Kelp Cola, and a bottle from 1951 out of Wilma's museum. Nearly identical. Something on this island stays the same."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A government that pays for a laboratory. Any government. I'm not fussy about the colour of the flag on the grant."],
  ["What about Noora?","I'll need a chemist next year, whatever happens. I've told her so. She went red, then wrote it down."]],
 "Shut the door behind you. The ash is still settling, and so am I."],
};
// Saga, attendant at the Hot Springs Spa, who runs its saunas and steam rooms for the island's wealthy (The Proprietors). Threads:
// what she overhears and never repeats, the springs and the volcano above them, her neighbours at Kingsgrad Apartments, her dream of a quiet cottage.
DAILY['Saga']={
2:["(A woman in a white tunic hands you a towel before you've said a word.) Locker eleven. Steam room's on the left. You're the reporter, I take it. Don't worry, nothing leaves this building. Well, nothing I say.",[
  ["Is it always so quiet?","With the roads only just open, yes. Normally it's like a train station for the rich. They all say they came to relax."],
  ["What do you hear in here?","Everything. People think steam muffles sound. It carries it. I've learned more in the sauna than at any library."],
  ["Do you ever repeat it?","Never. It's why they keep coming back. A spa attendant with a loose tongue is a short-lived thing."]],
 "The water's hotter than it looks. Mind your ankles."],
3:["(Saga is folding towels into precise triangles.) The Kelp Cola people are in town. Two of them came in this morning and sat in the steam, talking about a bottle. I know more about their bottle than the person who made it.",[
  ["Do you like Kelp Cola?","I like their free samples. I like the way they say 'synergy' with a straight face."],
  ["Who comes here most?","Whoever's got money and a worry. It's the same people. They just change hats."],
  ["Do you take sides?","I take towels. It's a quiet job. The only side I've got is the side of the bench that gets the draught."]],
 "Don't stay in too long. It melts the sense out of people."],
4:["(Saga is wiping condensation from a mirror.) Eastbrook's open, I hear. Maya at the coffee roaster's been in twice already, telling me about her beans. I don't mind. She's the only one who ever asks about me.",[
  ["What would you say about yourself?","I live in Kingsgrad Apartments, third floor. I've a cat I don't have, and a plant I do. I'd like a cottage."],
  ["A cottage where?","Somewhere with no steam. A lake. A porch. No one speaking about oil."],
  ["Do you like the job?","I love the water. It's alive. Every spring's a different temperature, and the mood changes with it."]],
 "Go and stand in the cold for a minute. It'll make you appreciate the hot."],
5:["(Saga tests the water with her elbow.) It's up two degrees, since breakfast. The springs are lively this week. The old regulars say it's the thaw. I think it's the mountain.",[
  ["Is the volcano near?","Above us. A short walk and a long climb. We're built on its warm side. It's a very expensive neighbour."],
  ["Does the water change?","Always. Hotter, then cooler. Smellier, then sweet. I've a book where I mark it. It's like a diary for something that doesn't talk."],
  ["What does the water taste like?","Smoky, a bit salt. Like a very old coin. Don't drink it. The tourists do, and make faces."]],
 "Come back when you're less dressed. It's a spa, after all."],
6:["(Saga's polishing the tile with a cloth.) A man came in today who said nothing for forty minutes. Not a word. He just breathed and sweated. At the end he gave me a large tip and a look. I've never been so unsettled.",[
  ["Who was he?","A stranger. Good coat, no luggage. He asked which of the baths was best for thinking. I said all of them are, if you're guilty."],
  ["Did he say anything else?","'Thank you, Saga.' I never told him my name. I wear a badge, of course, but I felt watched."],
  ["Was he from the Syndicate?","I can't say. Spa attendants can't say. But I'd not leave anything valuable in locker nine."]],
 "A quiet man's the loudest thing in the building."],
7:["(Saga hums a tune, tapping a bench.) I've had two wealthy gentlemen from Frostholm today. They spent an hour insulting each other's business. By the end of it, they were friends. That's what steam does.",[
  ["What does it do?","It takes the suit off people. You can't threaten anybody in a towel. It's the great leveller."],
  ["Who were they?","I couldn't say. But one of them wore a very good watch. He took it off, put it on the bench and forgot it. I've kept it safe. Until he asks."],
  ["Is Dario a client?","Mr Dario comes in, now and then. He's got a very strong back and a very soft voice. He tips well and asks nothing."]],
 "A towel doesn't lie. It's the one honest garment."],
8:["(Saga is sorting through bottles of oil.) Father Thyme's been in. Not for the baths: for the quiet. He sat in the changing room in his robe with a very long face and said nothing. I gave him a cup of mint tea.",[
  ["Is he troubled?","He's lost his words. I know the feeling. I once lost mine for a week after my aunt's funeral. You find them again, usually in the bath."],
  ["Do monks use the spa?","On Thursdays. It's an arrangement. They don't wear much, and they're very polite. And their feet are always freezing."],
  ["Do you offer any advice?","A cup of tea and a closed door. It's the most useful advice I know."]],
 "A saint would be glad of a hot bath. I'm told so, anyway."],
9:["(Saga's counting coins into a pot.) The Reds came in, three of them. They said they'd come to 'inspect the means of relaxation'. They stayed till closing and took all my mint tea.",[
  ["Are Communists good customers?","They don't pay, they discuss. But they're nice about it. And one of them sang. A very fine baritone."],
  ["Do you like the Party?","I like people who sing. I like fewer people who shout. It's a sliding scale."],
  ["What do they talk about in the steam?","Cola, mostly. They want one of their own. I said I'd try it. They said I'd be the first to sign a petition."]],
 "If you meet the baritone, tell him he's good. Nobody tells him."],
10:["(Saga has closed the spa for the morning, and put a small black ribbon on the door.) The funeral's today. I've left the springs running, for the ones who can't go. It seems right that someone's got a warm place to sit.",[
  ["Did you meet the king?","Once. He came here after a long winter, and he sat in the steam for an hour. He asked what I'd do if I were him. I said, 'Nothing, sir. That's the secret.'"],
  ["What did he say?","He laughed, for the first time in my hearing. Then he said, 'Thank you, young woman.' I was thirty-one."],
  ["How's the spa today?","Still. Only the sound of the water. It's rather beautiful."]],
 "Rest in peace, Your Majesty. The water's still warm."],
11:["(Saga looks out the window at the grey sky.) The whole island's arguing about wages, and here I am. No one's argued about my wages in years. They've never gone up. They've never gone down. They've simply been.",[
  ["Is that a complaint?","It's an observation. There's a difference, in my world. Observations are free."],
  ["What would you do with a rise?","Buy a cottage, then a boat, then think about it. I'd be a very peaceful woman in a very small house."],
  ["Do the clients tip?","The honest ones do. The ones who haggle in the sauna don't. I've a theory it's all about how long they can bear the heat."]],
 "If you hear about a cottage, let me know. Preferably with a bath."],
12:["(Saga's reading a small, careful letter.) A new pair of regulars started yesterday. They come in separately and leave separately, but they talk in the steam as if they'd just met. I think they've been scheming.",[
  ["What about?","Money. A great deal of it, moved from one drawer to another. I didn't mean to hear. I swear it's in the walls."],
  ["Do you worry?","A little. But I'm a woman with a towel. I stand outside the door and pretend I've gone deaf."],
  ["Are they from Diversified Petroleum?","I couldn't say. But one of them talked about 'depreciation' as if it were a woman's name."]],
 "Walls have ears here, and the ears are wet."],
13:["(Saga is wringing out a towel.) The cola people are handing out samples on the street. They tried to give me one at the door. I said I'd take it, but only if they sat in the steam with me.",[
  ["Did they?","Two of them. In their suits. They came out a different colour. I think they're still recovering."],
  ["Is it a gimmick?","It's a hot, wet advertisement. People leave with a clean skin and a bottle in their hand. It's brilliant, really."],
  ["Do you drink it?","In the bath, with ice. It's the only time I'm allowed to relax."]],
 "If you've got a samples bottle, give it to someone who needs a smile."],
14:["(Saga is hanging wet towels on a line.) The big day, tomorrow, or today. I've lost track. The steam's heavy and my sense of time is soft. I'm told everyone's going.",[
  ["Will you go?","I've been given the day. Imagine. The management said, 'Go, Saga, and be a citizen.' I said I'd rather be a towel."],
  ["What do you expect?","Noise. I've been in a very quiet building for a very long time. I'd like to hear the island."],
  ["Is anyone nervous in the baths?","They're all nervous. In the changing room, the rich man and the poor man look the same, and they both look frightened."]],
 "I'll listen to the crowds. I'll tell you if anyone says something wise."],
15:["(Saga's sitting on a bench, quietly rubbing her feet.) Yesterday was loud. I went, I stood at the back, I heard nothing but noise. It was wonderful. It's good to be part of a thing.",[
  ["Did you speak to anyone?","A woman in a Kelp Cola hat shared a sandwich. We didn't exchange names. It's the nicest meal I've had all year."],
  ["What was the best part?","The silence just before. A thousand people holding their breath. It's what I hear in the steam room, at midnight."],
  ["Are you rested?","No. But I'm happy. And I've come back to find three people asleep in the sauna. It's a good sign for business."]],
 "If you need a place to sleep, I can lend you a bench."],
16:["(Saga's tidying the oil rack.) The debate's today. I've had two gentlemen asking if the spa's got a back door. I said it's got a front door, a back door and a very steamy window.",[
  ["Are they avoiding someone?","Someone's always avoiding someone. It's the secret of my business. We're a hiding place with a view."],
  ["Which side do they support?","I'd not like to say. But one of them had a red scarf and a very blue face."],
  ["Will you go to the debate?","No. I'll be here, listening to the drip. It's the best speech on the island, and it never gets heckled."]],
 "If you hear any good heckles, repeat them to me. I could use a laugh."],
17:["(Saga stops mid-step.) Did you feel it? The water rippled. The whole pool shivered, as if somebody had tapped it. I've worked here eleven years. That's new.",[
  ["Is it a tremor?","It's the mountain's pulse. I've begun to think of it as a sleeping giant that's turned over. I just hope it doesn't wake."],
  ["Is the spa safe?","It's built into the rock. It's either the safest place on the island or the worst. It depends on the mood of the mountain."],
  ["What will you do if it grows?","Open the doors. Ring the bell. Get everyone out. In that order. And remember the towels."]],
 "I've never heard the water breathe before. I'd like it to stop."],
18:["(Saga puts a hand to the wall.) It's warm. The wall, not the steam. It's like leaning on a sleeping animal. I've written it in the water book.",[
  ["What's the temperature?","Three degrees above the usual. The regulars noticed and said nothing. It's a very polite sort of panic."],
  ["Has anyone left?","A few. A man from the Church, in a hurry. And a lady in a very large hat. She said the steam was giving her ideas."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm attentive. There's a difference. A frightened person runs. An attentive one writes things down."]],
 "Come and feel the wall. It's quite the thing, if you've a nervous disposition."],
19:["(Saga lifts her head.) That was a big one. Two cups fell off the shelf. A client in the steam room shouted something in a language I couldn't place. He was rather pink.",[
  ["Is the volcano going to erupt?","I'm not a scientist. I'm a woman who can tell the temperature with her toes. It's coming. I'd say soon."],
  ["Should people leave?","Some should. Some won't. The ones who love the island will stay, and the ones who love their money will count it first."],
  ["What does the water say?","It says nothing, but it's very loud about it."]],
 "If you've anything valuable, keep it close. And a towel."],
20:["(Saga's wearing a scarf over her nose.) The sulphur's rolling in from the north. The steam smells like a struck match. It's an acquired taste.",[
  ["Are you staying open?","As long as the water's safe and the regulars are keen. There's a man in the large bath who says he's never felt better. He's been there since seven."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Everything's dangerous if you stay too long. That's the first rule of the sauna."],
  ["What will you do if it goes?","Evacuate. Ring the bell. Count heads. Go home to my plant."]],
 "Take care. And leave the door ajar. The steam's restless."],
21:["(Saga's tunic is smudged with grey, her eyes bright.) We saw it from the springs. The whole sky opened. I've never heard a sound like it. The water leapt out of the pool and fell back, like it was cheering.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. The clients are fine. They've all been wrapped in towels and given tea. Nobody's ever been so well looked after."],
  ["What was it like?","Like standing inside a drum. And then the ash came down, very gently, like snow that had given up."],
  ["Will the spa survive?","It's made of rock. The mountain built it. I think it's the mountain's way of apologising."]],
 "Stay safe. And drink some tea. The mint's on the house."],
22:["(Saga's sweeping ash from the steps.) It's the sort of thing you don't expect to be doing in your working life. I've swept snow, leaves, sawdust. Never a volcano.",[
  ["Is it heavy?","Like wet flour. It sticks to everything. It's in the sauna, in the soap, in my hair. I've been grey for two days."],
  ["Is anyone coming in?","The brave ones. And the very dirty ones. A man came in covered from head to toe and said, 'I've been in the north.' I said, 'Locker eleven.'"],
  ["What do they say?","They say very little. They sit in the water and shake. It's cheaper than a doctor."]],
 "I'll keep the kettle on. It's the one thing the mountain can't stop."],
23:["(Saga's counting heads on a clipboard.) The aftershocks keep coming. Every one ripples the water. It's like the pool's trying to tell me something, in a very faint voice.",[
  ["What do you think it's saying?","'Careful.' It's usually 'careful.' I've known that water a long time."],
  ["Is it getting hotter?","A bit. And more golden. I've never seen it that colour. It's very beautiful. It's also very worrying."],
  ["Do the clients complain?","Not one. They say it's the best bath they've had. They've no idea what they're sitting in."]],
 "If it turns red, run. That's my only rule."],
24:["(Saga's mixing a tray of herbs.) We're getting the oddest visitors. People coming from the clean-up, wanting a good soak. I've put out extra benches. It's the island's one soft place, I think.",[
  ["Who's in this morning?","The odd lot. A priest, two Reds, a very quiet man in a leather coat. They don't speak, but they all sit at the same end."],
  ["Are the factions friendly?","In the steam, yes. It's too hot to fight. I wish they'd hold the election in here."],
  ["Would that work?","It'd be fairer. And everyone would vote quickly."]],
 "If you need a rest from the island, I've a bench with your name on it."],
25:["(Saga's looking through a window at the mountain.) There's less steam today. It's gone quiet. Which is when I get nervous. A quiet mountain's a thinking one.",[
  ["Is it over?","I doubt it. It's resting. It's like a snoring man: you don't know when he'll snort."],
  ["What do you do to calm yourself?","I scrub the tiles. It's a form of meditation. I've the cleanest tiles on the island."],
  ["Have you thought about leaving?","Every day. But the water would miss me. And I've nowhere with a bath."]],
 "Don't mind me. I talk to the tiles."],
26:["(Saga's counting banknotes in an envelope.) A man's bought the whole of the east wing for a week. For privacy, he said. It's a lot of money. I've no idea what he's doing in there. I'm told it's a business meeting.",[
  ["Is the spa being sold?","I don't know. There's talk. Mr Dario's been seen with a surveyor. They say he'd like to buy the whole place."],
  ["What would happen to you?","I'd stay on, I hope. A spa needs an attendant, whoever owns it. It's like a church: the vicar matters less than the pews."],
  ["Does it worry you?","It irritates me. A spa's a quiet thing. A man like Dario's a loud thing. But the money's real."]],
 "If you hear any gossip about the sale, tell me. I'll pretend I never heard."],
27:["(Saga's polishing a brass tap.) They're saying the Reds are taking over the cinema. And the Syndicate's got a game. And everyone's coming here to cool down before the end of the month.",[
  ["Is it busy?","Unbelievably. People who'd never meet are sitting in the same bath, sharing a towel. It's the nearest thing to a peace conference."],
  ["Do you hear secrets?","Whole libraries. I've enough to blow the island sky-high. I keep them in a very small drawer in my head."],
  ["Will you ever tell?","Perhaps in my memoirs. If I live that long. 'A Lifetime of Towels.'"]],
 "Come back soon. Bring a cold drink. The steam's relentless."],
28:["(Saga's tidying the benches with an air of great dignity.) The biggest day. Everyone's out. The spa's empty. It's very strange. I'm told even Mr Dario's stayed away.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","Entirely. He's here three times a week. I think he's up to something."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That whatever's decided, they leave the water alone. It's the best thing on the island."],
  ["Is anyone coming?","One old man, who says he's too old for politics. He sits in the steam with a newspaper. He's my favourite."]],
 "If you see him, be kind. He's seen a few kings."],
29:["(Saga's turning down the lamps one by one.) Tomorrow it's decided. I've been told by a dozen clients what I should hope for. I've said 'Mm' to every one.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A warm bath. A cool head. And a quiet cottage, if there's one to be had."],
  ["Do you think it'll go well?","I think it'll go. That's the best one can ask. A thing that goes is better than a thing that lingers."],
  ["Will you vote?","I will. In secret. It's the only time I'm allowed to say something and not hear it repeated."]],
 "I'll keep the water warm for you. It's a small thing, but it's mine."],
30:["(Saga stands at the edge of the main pool, with her hands folded.) The last day. The water's calm. The mountain's calm. It's the sort of calm one gets before a decision.",[
  ["Any last words, Saga?","Keep your counsel. Drink your tea. And never trust a man who won't take his socks off."],
  ["Will you miss the month?","I'll miss the secrets. They were an education. I've learned how the island's built. It's built on gossip and hot water."],
  ["What will you do now?","Mop. And then, when it's all over, I'll sit in the quietest bath and think of nothing at all."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Come back for a soak. I'll say nothing. I always say nothing."],
};
// Sally, Kingsgrad's estate agent (The Proprietors). Threads: the property boom she's sure is coming, which every bit of news (even
// a volcano) only brings closer; the five homes on her books, above all the villa in Hope Springs and its viewings; her new sign
// and her slogans; letters from her gloomy sister Kirsti in Helsinki, who thinks the island's doomed.
DAILY['Sally']={
2:["(Sally springs up from her desk, all teeth and blue wool.) A customer! Or a reporter, which is nearly as good. Sarivalto Homes! Sally. The road's open, the snow's melting, and the island's property market is about to boom.",[
  ["About to boom?","Mark my words. A new king, or a new something, and everyone will want a home here. Get in now, while the prices are frozen. Like everything else."],
  ["What have you got on the books?","Five lovely homes, one in every town. Bob's old house in Saltwick, a timber house in Eastbrook, a townhouse in Frostholm, the house along the street here, and a villa. A villa! In Hope Springs!"],
  ["The king's just died. Bad time to sell?","Oh, no, the best time. People get thoughtful at funerals. Thoughtful people buy houses."]],
 "Take a brochure! Take two. Give one to a friend."],
3:["Kelp Cola's HQ opened again today and I've already had a call from an executive asking about Hope Springs. Asking! Interest! The boom's begun.",[
  ["Who called?","I never say who. Discretion's the first rule of property. The second rule is to always mention the fireplace."],
  ["Tell me about the villa.",["Five thousand dollars, Hope Springs Gated Community, a fireplace, a view of the pines, and Phil, Dario and Gustavo as neighbours!",[
    ["Those neighbours sound dangerous.","Prestigious. That's the word. You'd never be burgled in Hope Springs. Nobody would dare."],
    ["Five thousand dollars?","A steal. In Helsinki you couldn't buy a garden shed for that. Well. A large garden shed."]]]],
  ["Do you ever have a bad day?","I had one in 1971. I didn't like it, so I stopped."]],
 "Keep me in mind! Everybody needs a home, even reporters."],
4:["(Sally is reading a letter and frowning, which doesn't suit her.) My sister Kirsti, in Helsinki. She says the island's doomed and I should sell up and come south. She says that every month.",[
  ["Is she right?","Kirsti's been saying the world's ending since 1962. It hasn't yet. She lives in a flat with a view of a car park. I have a view of a palace."],
  ["Will you write back?","Every month. I tell her the market's booming and the sun's shining. Sometimes it's even true."],
  ["What does she do in Helsinki?","She sells insurance. Against everything. It's made her very gloomy. You can't sell houses and insurance in one family. One of you has to hope."]],
 "Don't let Kirsti's letter put you off. Have a brochure."],
5:["I've had the window done! (She gestures at gold letters on the glass.) SARIVALTO HOMES: YOUR FUTURE STARTS HERE. A man from Eastbrook painted it. He charged me in coffee.",[
  ["It's very gold.","Gold sells. People see gold and think 'investment'. That's psychology."],
  ["Your future starts here?","It does! Everyone's future starts somewhere. Why not in a house with a working stove?"],
  ["Any viewings yet?","A couple from the palace staff looked at the house down the street, and then at each other, and then at their shoes. Maids' wages. I gave them a brochure to dream with."]],
 "Mind the wet paint on the door. It's a new door too."],
6:["The road to Hope Springs is open! I drove down to air out the villa. The fireplace drew beautifully. Gustavo waved at me over his hedge. Waved!",[
  ["Gustavo waved?","With two fingers, in a leather glove. Very neighbourly. I think he'd like a nice quiet neighbour. Or a very loud one he can keep an eye on."],
  ["Who's in the other villas?","Phil from Kelp Cola, Dario from the oil company. Big men, big houses, and none of them ever buys anything from me. They already have villas."],
  ["Is it really worth five thousand?","Every penny. The bathroom alone is worth a thousand. It has a bidet. From France. Nobody knows what to do with it."]],
 "If you see anyone rich, send them my way!"],
7:["The north road's open, so the Frostholm townhouse is back on the market. Thirteen hundred dollars, on the main street, handy for the jazz club. I've already had a nibble.",[
  ["Who's nibbling?","A Russian gentleman at the Hotel Aurora. In timber. Very charming. He asked about all five houses and which ones had cellars."],
  ["Cellars?","For wine, he said. And maybe for storage. Russians take storage very seriously."],
  ["The Russians want to buy up the island?","Oh, I don't do politics. If the Russians want a house, I'll sell it. If the Americans want one, I'll sell them the one next door."]],
 "Go on. Busy, busy. The boom waits for no one."],
8:["Sunday, so the office is shut, but I came in anyway to do the brochures. (She's colouring a drawing of the villa with pencils.) The printer in Eastbrook wants too much, so I draw them myself.",[
  ["That's a lovely drawing.","Thank you! I've given the villa a few more trees than it has. Trees grow. I'm drawing the future."],
  ["Did you go to the sermon?","I listened to it on the radio while I coloured. Very moving. Father Thyme should have been an estate agent: he made heaven sound like it had a view."],
  ["What news from the oil rig?","Something's gone wrong out there, they say. Bad for the fish, good for property in Kingsgrad. People want to live away from the smell."]],
 "Take one of the drawings. It's a collector's item."],
9:["(Sally is on the telephone, beaming.) ...yes, a lovely stove... yes... Saltwick... no, the fishermen don't smell at all... (She hangs up.) That was a nibble on Bob's old house!",[
  ["Who's Bob?","Bob the fishmonger. He moved into the apartment block after his wife left. The house was too big, he said, and too quiet. I said quiet sells."],
  ["Did they buy it?","They asked if it was near the fish plant. I said it's near the sea. It's both. They're thinking."],
  ["Do the fishermen smell?","Only of fish. And whisky, by evening. It's very authentic. That's the word I'm going to use in the brochure."]],
 "Fingers crossed for Bob's house!"],
10:["(Sally is in black, with a black bow in her hair.) The funeral's today. I wasn't asked, of course, but I stood at the gates. What a turnout. Every important person on the island in one chapel.",[
  ["You sound cheerful about it.","I'm sad! I'm also an estate agent. When important people gather, they talk about where to live next. That's how a market starts."],
  ["Did you know the king?","I sold his cousin a summer house in 1974. Well, I showed it to him. He didn't buy it. But he shook my hand."],
  ["Who'll live in the palace now?","Oh, that's not on my books. But if it were... (She sighs.) Imagine the commission."]],
 "Wear something dark today. And something warm. Black's not warm."],
11:["Father Thyme telephoned! Asking about my cheapest homes. For the Church's charity work, he said. I gave him all five prices and a blessing back.",[
  ["What does the Church want with a house?","He said something about the island's poor. Somebody in need of a roof. I think it's lovely. I think it's even lovelier with a deposit."],
  ["What's your cheapest?","Bob's old house in Saltwick. Eight hundred dollars. Small, but the stove works, and the fishermen are friendly. I wrote that myself."],
  ["Does anyone really buy for charity?","Not often. But the Church has deep pockets and long memories. I'll keep the keys polished."]],
 "Keep me in mind if you know anyone who needs a roof!"],
12:["(Sally is buffing a brass sign that reads SALLY: SARIVALTO HOMES SINCE 1969.) I'm putting it on the door. The other one fell off in the snow. Twelve years in business and not a day without hope.",[
  ["Since 1969?","Since I was twenty-two. My father said nobody would buy a house from a girl. I've sold forty-one houses since then. He bought the forty-first."],
  ["Any sales this month?","Not yet! But the nibbles! I've had more interest this month than in all of last year. The boom's in the post."],
  ["Who's buying, really?","People with plans. Everybody on the island's got a plan this month, and plans need places."]],
 "Go on, busy day for you too, I expect. Everybody's busy."],
13:["Kelp Cola girls came by handing out free samples and I gave each of them a brochure in return. A fair swap. Two of them want to live in Eastbrook.",[
  ["Can they afford it?","Not yet! But young people grow up and get promotions, and then they remember who was nice to them. I'm a long-term investor."],
  ["What's the Eastbrook house like?","Timber, mustard yellow, at the top of town, near the coffee and the record shop. Very fashionable. The young people want to live there and drink coffee and listen to jazz."],
  ["Did you try the cola?","I did. Very refreshing. Tastes like the sea, in a nice way. I could sell a house with that in the fridge."]],
 "Big day tomorrow! Half the island in one place. I'll bring brochures."],
14:["(Sally has a satchel stuffed with brochures.) The big day! The whole island together! I'm going with a hundred brochures and a smile.",[
  ["Is that appropriate?","Everything's appropriate if you do it with a smile. I'll be tactful. I'll wait till after the speeches."],
  ["What do you think will happen?","Something historic. And historic means change, and change means moving house. Mark my words."],
  ["Do you ever take a day off?","What would I do with it? Sit at home and look at my house? I've already sold it to myself."]],
 "See you there! Look for the brochures."],
15:["(Sally is counting brochures, fewer now.) Sixty-three gone! People took them for the drawing of the villa. One man took four to light his stove with, but I choose to think he'll read them first.",[
  ["Any proper interest?","A monk asked me about the timber house in Eastbrook. For his sister, he said. Monks have sisters. I'd never thought of it."],
  ["What about church against crown?","It's Sunday, and they're fighting about princes. If it's Augustus, the market goes quiet and holy. If it's Leopold, it goes grand. Either way, there'll be houses."],
  ["Do you sleep?","Like a baby. A baby who's sure the market's about to boom."]],
 "Keep a brochure in your pocket. You never know."],
16:["Another letter from Kirsti. (She reads aloud.) 'Dear Sally, the papers here say the island's tearing itself apart in public debates. Come home.' (She folds it up.) Debates are good for property.",[
  ["How are debates good for property?","People talk about the future. Then they want to live in it. It's very simple."],
  ["Will you go to Helsinki?","Never. I'd have to sell my house, and I'd never sell to anyone who didn't love it. And then where would I live? In Kirsti's car park."],
  ["Did you watch the debate?","I listened from the back. Lovely voices, both sides. Erik used the word 'home' four times. I'd hire that man."]],
 "Go on. Tell me if you hear of anyone moving."],
17:["(A picture of a villa slides down the wall, and Sally catches it.) The ground keeps doing that! It's settling. Islands settle. It's just like a new house.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say the mountain's steaming. I say: geothermal heating. Free hot water. You can't buy that anywhere else in the Baltic."],
  ["Doesn't that worry buyers?","Nothing worries buyers like an empty market. A bit of steam is character."],
  ["Heard about Dario?","Missing, they say! His villa's dark and his men are everywhere. I'm sure he's just... busy. Big men get busy."]],
 "Mind the pictures on your way out. They're adventurous today."],
18:["I took a man round the Kingsgrad house today, and the floor shook during the viewing. I told him it was the house welcoming him. He's thinking about it.",[
  ["Who was he?","A gentleman from Kelp Cola, a step up in the company, he says. He wants something near the palace. Very respectable. He went pale when the cups rattled."],
  ["Doesn't the shaking frighten you?","I'm frightened of empty houses, and men who say 'I'll think about it'. A bit of shaking is nothing."],
  ["The house welcoming him? Really?","Every house has a personality. That one's a little nervous. Like its buyer."]],
 "Go on. And steady as you go."],
19:["(Sally is pinning a map of the island to the wall, with five red pins.) My homes. Saltwick, Eastbrook, Kingsgrad, Frostholm, Hope Springs. All safe from the mountain. All a long way from it, very nearly.",[
  ["Very nearly?","The Frostholm house is closer to the steam than I'd like. I've written 'cosy' on its brochure."],
  ["Which one's your favourite?","The Eastbrook one. It's small and yellow and the porch has a swing. If I hadn't a house, I'd buy it myself."],
  ["Is the boom still coming?","Bigger than ever! People are looking at their homes and thinking. Thinking's the first step."]],
 "Take a map, if you like. I drew it."],
20:["(Sally is fanning herself with a brochure.) It's hot, isn't it? In September! The steam's in the air. I've had the window open all day. Lovely. Very Mediterranean.",[
  ["Is that the volcano?","They say it's warming up. I'm putting 'Mediterranean climate' on the brochures. Nobody can say I'm wrong today."],
  ["Are you worried at all, Sally?",["Me? (She smiles, and then stops smiling, for a moment.) My mother used to say the mountain was the island's heart. That if it ever woke up, we'd all have to wake up too.",[
    ["What did she mean?","I never asked. She sold flowers. Florists say things like that."],
    ["Are you awake?","I'm always awake, darling. I'm in property."]]]],
  ["Any sales?","An offer on Bob's house, very low. I'm thinking about it. Which means I'll say no on Monday."]],
 "Stay cool! Ha. Never thought I'd say that in September."],
21:["(The office is grey with ash, and Sally is in a headscarf with a broom.) The mountain's gone up! I heard it from my bed. Everyone's fine, I hear. I'm cleaning the window. Nobody can see the gold letters.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm wonderful. I'm alive and I've got a broom. You can do anything with a broom and a positive attitude."],
  ["What happens to the market now?","People will want to live a long way from the mountain. Saltwick! Kingsgrad! Hope Springs! I've got houses in every one of them."],
  ["You can't be cheerful about this.",["(She stops sweeping.) I can. I have to be. If I'm not, who is? Kirsti?",[
    ["That's brave.","It's business. Bravery's just business with your coat on."],
    ["Write to Kirsti.","I'll write to her tomorrow. I'll tell her it was spectacular. It was, you know. From a distance."]]]]],
 "Go home, shake off, and drink something warm. That's an order."],
22:["Kirsti telephoned! All the way from Helsinki! She heard about the volcano on the radio and she's in a state. I told her the ash is wonderful for gardens.",[
  ["Is it?","It's what they say about volcanoes. Rich soil. Very fertile. In a hundred years we'll have the best tomatoes in the Baltic."],
  ["Did she believe you?","She hung up on me. That means she's worried. Kirsti only hangs up on people she loves."],
  ["Any more interest in houses?","Three calls this morning! All asking how far each house was from the mountain. I've got tape measures out. The furthest one's Saltwick."]],
 "Mind the ash on the step. I've given up sweeping it."],
23:["(Sally is drawing a new brochure: the volcano, with a cosy-looking villa in front.) 'Hope Springs: a view to remember.' What do you think? Too soon?",[
  ["Much too soon.","Oh, you sound like Kirsti. All right, I'll draw a tree in front of the volcano. There. Now it's a nice tree."],
  ["It's a lovely drawing.","Thank you! I've been drawing since I was a girl. My father said I'd be an artist. I said artists don't sell houses."],
  ["How's Bob's house?","The low offer went higher. Not high enough. I said no, nicely, with a biscuit."]],
 "Take the drawing. When the island's famous, it'll be worth something."],
24:["The Russian gentleman from the Aurora telephoned again, asking about the cellar in the Frostholm house. How big it is, and whether it has a back door. Most particular.",[
  ["That's odd.","For storage, I'm sure. Timber men store things. He asked if the walls were thick. Russians like to be warm."],
  ["Will you sell to him?","If he pays! I don't ask buyers what they'll do in the cellar. That's not property, that's prying."],
  ["Who else is buying?","Everybody's asking and nobody's buying. It's like a dance where everyone's waiting for the band."]],
 "Go on. And don't tell the Russian I gossip. I don't, usually."],
25:["(Sally is breathless.) I hear the palace wants to share its power with the people! Edmund's niece told my hairdresser. A king who answers to a parliament! Do you know what that means?",[
  ["For democracy?","For property! Parliaments need buildings. Ministers need houses. Ministers' wives need bigger houses. It's a boom."],
  ["Is it true?","It's an offer, my hairdresser says, and somebody's taking it to Erik. I'm already thinking about which ministers want Hope Springs."],
  ["Do you care who wins?","I care that whoever wins buys a house. Preferably from me."]],
 "Go on. History's happening, and so is property."],
26:["Dario wants to buy the spa on the mountain! The whole of Frostholm's talking about it. He didn't come to me. He's going to the owner himself. (She sniffs.) Some people have no respect for the profession.",[
  ["Will the owner sell?","Not the owner I know. Old money, very stubborn. They say it's not for sale at any price. Which means it's for sale at a very high price."],
  ["Would you sell it for him?","In a heartbeat. A spa on a volcano! After an eruption! I'd call it 'hot property'. Nobody would forget that."],
  ["You're jealous.","I'm professional. Jealousy's for amateurs. I'm just a little hurt."]],
 "Go on. And if you see Dario, mention my name."],
27:["(Sally is at her desk, with no brochures for once, staring out of the window.) It's quiet today. All the noise is going on elsewhere. I've nothing to do but water the plant.",[
  ["That's not like you.","No. (She smiles.) Even I need a quiet day. I'm saving my smile for tomorrow. It's going to be busy."],
  ["What's happening tomorrow?","Everything! Rallies, marches, visits, parties. Kingsgrad's going to be full of people and flags. A good day to put a sign in the window."],
  ["Water the plant, then.","(She does.) It's a fern. I've had it twelve years. It's never sold a house, but it's very supportive."]],
 "Go on. Rest, if you can. Tomorrow's a big one."],
28:["(Sally has a new sign in the window: WHOEVER WINS, YOU'LL NEED A HOME!) Isn't it perfect? The whole island's on the streets. Every one of them will walk past my window.",[
  ["It's very neutral.","Business is neutral. I'll sell to a Communist, a king or a monk. I've already sold to a Republican: my father."],
  ["What's it like out there?","Flags, bands, speeches. Erik's in the square, the Party's marching, and the palace has visitors. Everybody's selling something. So am I."],
  ["Will you march?","In these shoes? I'll stand at the door and wave at everyone. People remember who waved."]],
 "Go and see it all! Wave back at me."],
29:["(Sally is writing a letter.) To Kirsti. I'm telling her that whatever happens tomorrow, I'm staying. The island's my home. You don't sell your home. You sell other people theirs.",[
  ["Is that what you're writing?","More or less. And I've put in a brochure for the Eastbrook house. In case she ever wants to visit. She won't, but it's nice to be asked."],
  ["What's your hope for tomorrow?","That people want to stay. If they stay, they need homes. If they go, they sell me their homes. Either way, I'll be here."],
  ["You never stop.","Neither does the island. That's why I love it."]],
 "Get some sleep. I'm going to, with a smile."],
30:["(Sally is in her best suit, with a fresh flower in the buttonhole.) The last day! Whatever happens today, the market opens tomorrow at nine. I've already put the coffee on.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","Not a bit. (Her hands shake a little on the cup.) Well. A bit. But it's the nervous before an auction. Something good comes after."],
  ["What will you do if the island changes?","Sell it a house. Every new island needs houses. And every new government needs offices. I'll draw them a brochure."],
  ["Thank you for the brochures.","Thank you for taking them! You're the only person this month who took one and didn't light a stove with it. I hope."]],
 "Good luck, darling. Your future starts here."],
};
// Sam, old fisherman at West Saltwick Harbour (Communist Party). Says little. Threads: mending his father's 1931 net a knot at a
// time (finished on the 30th), the thin cod and the oil from the rig, why he always votes with Anna (her mother, 1938), and his
// late wife Aili.
DAILY['Sam']={
1:["(Sam is mending a net on an upturned crate. He nods.) Reporter.",[
  ["Good day for fishing?","Snow's gone. Fish haven't come back. Tomorrow, maybe."],
  ["What's that net?","My father's. He knotted it in 1931. I'm mending it. A knot at a time."],
  ["Are you in the Party?","Since before Anna was born. I vote with her. Saves talking."]],
 "Mind the hooks."],
2:["Kingsgrad's open. (He doesn't look up.) Don't need anything in Kingsgrad.",[
  ["Ever been to the palace?","Once. 1952. Took herring to the kitchens. Cook gave me a bun. Good bun."],
  ["What do you make of the prince?","Young. (He ties a knot.) We were all young."],
  ["How's the net coming?","Forty-one knots. Four thousand to go. Done by spring. Or I'm done. One or the other."]],
 "Mind the hooks on your way."],
3:["Cola lorry nearly ran over Hannu's papers. Didn't stop.",[
  ["Is Hannu all right?","Hannu's fine. Papers are wet. He's selling them as 'with sea air'."],
  ["Do you drink Kelp Cola?","Tea. Black. Since 1938."],
  ["You don't say much.","Saving it for when it matters. (A pause.) Doesn't matter yet."]],
 "Tea's on, if you're cold. You look cold."],
4:["(Sam holds up a cod by the tail.) Look at that. Thin. Forty years, never this thin in September.",[
  ["Why is it?","Something's wrong out past the point. Fish know before we do."],
  ["Is it the oil rig?","Didn't say that. (He looks north-west.) Didn't not say it."],
  ["Maybe it's the snow.","Snow comes every year. The rig's new."]],
 "Eat it anyway. Thin's still fish."],
5:["Anna came by. Practised a speech on me. I nodded.",[
  ["Was it a good speech?","Long. Good. Too many words about Moscow. Not enough about nets."],
  ["Did you tell her?","No. She knows. She looks at me when she says Moscow."],
  ["Why do you always vote with her?",["Her mother. Fought for a ha'penny a barrel in '38. I was there. Twelve years old.",[
    ["What happened?","She got it. Then the sack. Never complained. Anna's the same. So I vote with her."],
    ["You remember 1938?","Better than last week."]]]]],
 "Off you go. Tide's turning."],
6:["Paid the Syndicate woman her 'harbour fee'. (He spits over the side.)",[
  ["Why pay?","Net's worth more than the fee. She knows it. I know it."],
  ["Who is she?","Lucia. Lives in the block. Smiles too much."],
  ["Did Mike pay?","No. (He looks at Mike's boat a long time.) Funny, that."]],
 "Don't pay her if you can help it. You can't."],
7:["Never been to Frostholm. Lived an hour away all my life. Never needed to.",[
  ["Why not?","Nothing there but glass and oil. Can't eat either."],
  ["There's a jazz club.","(He considers it.) Jazz. No."],
  ["Have you met Yuri?","The Russian. Bought me a vodka at Ray's. Asked a lot. I told him the tide times."]],
 "Frostholm. Hm."],
8:["Black water past the point. Saw it at first light. Rainbow on top.",[
  ["Is it bad?","Fish won't swim through it. Birds can't get out of it."],
  ["Who's to blame?","Dario. The rig. (A long pause.) Me, for not shouting when they built it."],
  ["Did you report it?","Told Anna. Anna'll tell everyone. That's what she's for."]],
 "Don't go past the point."],
9:["Helen gave me a cup of her cola. (He grimaces.) No.",[
  ["That bad?","Like a chemist's floor."],
  ["What should it taste of?","My mother made a drink from rowan berries over a smoky fire. Winter drink. Kept the cold off."],
  ["Will you drink it when it's better?","If Helen makes it. I'd drink anything Helen makes. Not eat. Bob does the cooking."]],
 "Tea's better. Always."],
10:["(Sam has taken his cap off and holds it to his chest.) The old man goes in the ground today.",[
  ["Did you know him?","Shook my hand on the quay once. Strong hand, for a king."],
  ["You're not a royalist, surely?","No. But a man's a man. Cap off, then cap on."],
  ["Will you go to the funeral?","No. Fish don't wait for funerals."]],
 "(He puts his cap back on.) There."],
11:["(He holds up a length of net, neatly mended.) Two hundred knots. Father did these with cold hands. So do I.",[
  ["Will you fish with it?","No. Too old. It goes on the wall. Somebody should keep it."],
  ["Why mend it, then?","Because it's torn. (He looks at you as if that were obvious.)"],
  ["Your father was a fisherman too?","And his. And his. Same water. Same fish. Fewer fish now."]],
 "Knot a day. More, when it's quiet."],
12:["Mike's learning Russian. Eight lines. All day. I've got used to it. The gulls haven't.",[
  ["Is he any good?","Better than the gulls."],
  ["Do you sing?","Once. 1956. A wedding. Never again."],
  ["What's Mike up to these days?","Something. Buys presents. Hums. (He ties a knot.) I'm old, not blind."]],
 "Don't tell Mike. He thinks I'm deaf."],
13:["Sea's cold early this year. I feel it through my boots.",[
  ["Is the snow coming back?","Middle of October. Earlier, maybe. My knee says so."],
  ["Did you get a free cola?","Poured it in the harbour. Fish didn't like it either."],
  ["Your knee forecasts the weather?","Better than the radio. Kaisa's a nice girl. Knows nothing about weather."]],
 "Wear wool. Not that London stuff."],
14:["Big day, they say. Everyone in one place.",[
  ["Are you going?","Anna says go. I go. Stand at the back. Nod."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A fair price for fish. Same as every day."],
  ["You don't seem excited.","Seventy-two. Seen a war, two floods, three kings. Excited's for the young."]],
 "Back row. That's where I'll be."],
15:["Church and palace fighting now. (He shrugs.) The sea doesn't care who's king.",[
  ["Are you religious?","My wife was. Lit a candle every time I went out. I always came back. Can't argue with that."],
  ["Who should be king?","Nobody. The sea's king. The rest of us rent."],
  ["You had a wife?",["Aili. Forty years. Gone six winters.",[
    ["I'm sorry.","So am I. (He ties a knot slowly.) She'd have liked you. She liked talkers."],
    ["Do you miss her?","Every tide."]]]]],
 "Light a candle if you pass a church. For her. Not me."],
16:["Anna's day. She practised on me eleven times. I know it better than she does.",[
  ["Will you go?","Front row. She looks at me when she forgets a word. I mouth it."],
  ["Who'll win?","Whoever talks less. That's never Erik."],
  ["What's the best line?","'Who owns your boat?' I own mine. Took thirty years. She says that's the point."]],
 "Wish her luck. I won't. She knows."],
17:["Ground shook. Boat rocked on the mooring with no wind. Never seen that.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?",["Grandfather said the mountain smoked once, when he was a boy. Ash on the snow. Fish went deep for a year.",[
    ["Did anyone die?","No. Lost a cow. Grandfather said the cow had it coming."],
    ["Should people be worried?","Should be ready. Worried's no use."]]]],
  ["How was the debate?","Anna forgot one word. I mouthed it. Nobody saw. That's all I'll say."],
  ["Were you scared?","Too old for scared. Held on to the rail."]],
 "Keep your boots on near the water."],
18:["Seven hundred knots. Fingers are slower. Mind isn't.",[
  ["Do you want a hand?","No. My father did it alone. So do I."],
  ["What's it like out there now?","Quiet. Too quiet. The birds went inland yesterday. All at once."],
  ["Will you finish?","Before the snow, or before me. Either way, finished."]],
 "Off you go. I've knots to tie."],
19:["Kate came with a clipboard. Wanted me to sign up Tom. I said Tom's Tom.",[
  ["What does that mean?","Tom likes everybody. Can't make a man like fewer people. Wouldn't want to."],
  ["Did you sign anyone?","Gunnar's thinking about it. Gunnar thinks for a long time. Like me."],
  ["Kate's very persuasive.","Kate stands very close. You sign to get some air."]],
 "Soup on Thursday. Good soup. Come."],
20:["Kate told the girl from the Gazette I married a mermaid. (He shakes his head.) Kate.",[
  ["Is it true?","(He looks at you for a long time.) No."],
  ["Are you angry?","No. Aili would've laughed till she cried. I laughed a bit. Don't tell Kate."],
  ["What was Nora asking about?","The Russian at the Hotel Aurora. Who he sees. Told her I see fish."]],
 "If anybody asks, I was a widower. Not a merman."],
21:["(Sam stands on the quay, looking north at the black cloud.) Grandfather was right.",[
  ["Are you safe?","I'm here. Boat's here. Ash on both. Safe enough."],
  ["What will happen to the fish?","They'll go deep. Come back in spring. They always do. Fish are patient."],
  ["Is it the end?","Seen the end four times. It's never the end. Just a bad week."]],
 "Get inside. Ash in the lungs is no good."],
22:["(Sam is picking dead fish off the tide line, one by one, into a bucket.) Somebody has to.",[
  ["Why one by one?","Each one was a fish. Deserves picking up. Not shovelling."],
  ["Kate cleared your step.","She did. Told her to go away. She didn't. Good girl. Don't tell her."],
  ["How many so far?","Three hundred and twelve. Counting keeps the head straight."]],
 "Go on. I'll finish."],
23:["Rig's still pumping. The mountain blew, and Dario's still pumping.",[
  ["Should it stop?","Should never have started. Said nothing when they built it. Saying it now."],
  ["What would you do?","(He ties a knot, very tight.) Old men don't do. Old men remember who did."],
  ["Is the oil still coming in?","Black line on every hull. Tom's scrubbing. Mike's swearing. I'm watching."]],
 "Write that. Old man, watching."],
24:["Aili's birthday. She'd be seventy. (There's a candle stub on the crate beside him.)",[
  ["I'm sorry.","Don't be. Forty good years. More than most get."],
  ["What was she like?","Talked for both of us. Laughed at my jokes. I made two, in forty years. She laughed at both."],
  ["Will you light the candle?","Later. In the window. Facing the sea. She liked to see the boats."]],
 "Go on. I'll sit a while."],
25:["Kate gave me two hundred cookbooks and a pen. Says put an R in 'herring'. (He holds one up.) Done forty.",[
  ["Why you?","Steady hand, Kate says. Also says I've nothing to do. That part's wrong."],
  ["Is the cookbook any good?","My recipe's in. 'Potatoes.' Boil them. That's the recipe."],
  ["Heard about Princess Daphne?","Erik's courting a princess. (He snorts.) Anna's courting fishermen. I know which I'd marry."]],
 "Herrring. Three R's on that one. Never mind."],
26:["Anna came to ask if I'll walk with the Party on the twenty-eighth. In Kingsgrad.",[
  ["Will you?","Seventy-two. Bad knee. (A pause.) Yes."],
  ["What's happening on the twenty-eighth?","Didn't ask. She'll tell me where to stand. I'll stand."],
  ["Why always yes to Anna?","Her mother. 1938. Told you. Some debts you pay till you die."]],
 "Kingsgrad. Twice in one life. Hm."],
27:["Party's off to the pictures. Soviet film. Anna's bought me a ticket.",[
  ["Do you go to the cinema?","Once. 1961. Cowboy film. The horse died. Didn't like it."],
  ["Will you like this one?","Tractors. Rivers. Nobody dies, probably. I'll sleep."],
  ["Why not STORMWING instead?","What's a Stormwing? (You explain.) Robots. No."]],
 "If I snore, nudge me."],
28:["(Sam has his best cap on, with a red ribbon tied round it.) The twenty-eighth. Anna said wear something red. This is red.",[
  ["You look smart.","Aili's ribbon. From her hair. First time out of the drawer in six years."],
  ["Where are you going?","Kingsgrad. With the Party. Slowly. Knee."],
  ["Are you nervous?","No. (A long pause.) Little bit."]],
 "Go on. I'll get there. Slowly."],
29:["Long day yesterday. Knee's done in. (He's back on his crate, with the net.)",[
  ["How do you feel?","Old. Proud. Tired. In that order, mostly."],
  ["Tomorrow's the last day.","Every day's somebody's last day. Tomorrow's the island's. We'll see."],
  ["How's the net?","Nearly done. Twelve knots to go. Might finish tomorrow. Fitting."]],
 "Come by tomorrow. See it finished."],
30:["(Sam ties a last knot, bites off the twine and holds the whole net up.) Done. Father's net. Fifty years torn, one month mended.",[
  ["It's beautiful.","It's a net. (A pause.) Yes. It is."],
  ["What happens to the island now?","Whatever happens, the fish come back in spring. Somebody catches them. Hope it's us."],
  ["Will you hang it up?","On the wall. By Aili's candle. Two things I couldn't let go of. Now I don't have to."]],
 "Thank you for coming by. All month. Good listener. Rare."],
};
// Sanna, cook at Polar Burger in Frostholm (food vendors). Threads: late nights for the oil workers and the jazz crowd, tiredness, her neighbour Birgit at Nordmart, her regulars, a secret recipe for the sauce, the volcano and the ash on the grill.
DAILY['Sanna']={
7:["(Sanna flips a patty without looking.) Late, aren't you? We're open till two, and I've been up since nine. The roads are open and the oil crews have come in hungry. What'll it be?",[
  ["What's good here?","The Polar Double. It's a burger, with an opinion. Everything else is a rumour."],
  ["Are you always this tired?","Only when I'm awake. The rest of the time I'm asleep standing up."],
  ["Who comes in?","Oil workers, jazz fans from the Midnight Note, and one very sad man who's been here every night since March."]],
 "Eat it while it's hot. It won't stay hot long."],
8:["(Sanna has the radio on low. A sermon is rolling out of it.) Sunday's the slow night. Everyone's feeling virtuous. They'll be back on Monday, full of sin and mustard.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","I work Sundays. If the Almighty wants a burger, he knows where I am."],
  ["Is the sauce a secret?","It's a family one. My grandmother's. I'd tell you, but I'd have to burn you."],
  ["Is it quiet?","Quiet's relative. A man's been asleep at the counter since ten. I keep him warm with soup."]],
 "Tip the cook. It's not mandatory. It's just nice."],
9:["(Sanna has a bottle of cola in one hand and a spatula in the other.) Everyone's asking me about this new cola. They think I've a view. I serve the stuff. I haven't had time to taste it.",[
  ["Does it go with a burger?","Everything goes with a burger. Even a bad day."],
  ["Which cola do you stock?","The one the supplier brings. Don't tell Birgit. She'd write a policy."],
  ["Do the oil lot care?","They want it cold and cheap. They're not connoisseurs. They're thirsty."]],
 "If someone asks for a flavour, say 'yes'. It's the shortest way to peace."],
10:["(Sanna has a black armband tied above the apron.) A funeral. A very long one, on the wireless. I've turned the grill off for five minutes. I didn't know him, but I've served his people.",[
  ["Did the king ever come in?","Not in person. His driver did, once. Ordered a cheeseburger, no pickle. I framed the receipt."],
  ["Is it a quiet night?","Quiet as a church. Which is odd, with the smell of onions."],
  ["What happens next?","I flip burgers. It's the only constant on this island. Kings come and go. The grill stays."]],
 "Be kind tonight. People need a hot dinner."],
11:["(Sanna rubs her eyes.) Pay disputes, alms, a lot of talk. I hear it all at the grill. Men with grease on their hands say very big things at one in the morning.",[
  ["What do they say?","That they're underpaid. That they're overworked. That they'll vote for whoever gives them a Saturday off."],
  ["Do you agree?","I'd vote for anyone who gave me a Tuesday."],
  ["Who are the loudest?","The oil crews. They've money and no sense of volume."]],
 "Don't repeat that. Or do. I'll deny it."],
12:["(Sanna lowers the flame.) A man in a good coat came in tonight, ordered a plain burger and asked about the records at the Midnight Note. The record. There's a very old one, apparently. Everyone's after it.",[
  ["Who was he?","I don't know. He tipped well and never smiled. That's the most suspicious thing I've seen all week."],
  ["Do you go to the Midnight Note?","When I've time. Which is never. But the music reaches my window. It's mostly saxophone."],
  ["Is the record valuable?","Apparently. I'd rather have a good grill."]],
 "If you meet him, tell him the burger was on the house. It wasn't, but it makes me sound generous."],
13:["(Sanna drops a tray of tiny cups on the counter.) Free samples. Kelp Cola's team has been through Frostholm in matching jackets. They gave me a crate, a poster and a speech.",[
  ["Did you hang the poster?","Behind the grill, where it can't be read. A fair compromise."],
  ["Is cola good for business?","Everything's good for business if people are thirsty."],
  ["Who else dropped by?","A man for the Syndicate, asking about 'the secret recipe'. I told him mine's in my head and nowhere else."]],
 "Try a sample. I'd say it's good. I'd be lying, but kindly."],
14:["(Sanna has the shutters half up.) Everyone's at the big day, and I'm the only fool who's working. I'll open at four and keep the grill warm for the ones who come back hungry.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'm sending my regards. By burger."],
  ["What do you expect?","Chaos, followed by a very big queue. Fed people are calm people."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm tired. It's a different feeling. But related."]],
 "If you're hungry after, I'm here. If you're not, you will be."],
15:["(Sanna slaps a patty down hard.) The Church and the Royals are quarrelling. Crown before Cross, Cross before Crown. I've a customer who's a monk. He ordered a burger, and said grace over it. Over a burger!",[
  ["Which monk?","One of the young ones, with a good appetite. He asked for extra onion. I think he's on the run."],
  ["Do the monks come often?","Never. This one came down in a hurry. He looked over his shoulder twice."],
  ["Do you take sides?","Only on pickles. Pro-pickle, always."]],
 "If you see a monk, tell him the grill's on."],
16:["(Sanna has the radio on a debate, the Party against the Democrats.) I like to listen while I fry. It's the only time politicians sound sensible. The sizzle drowns out the nonsense.",[
  ["Who's making the better case?","The one who's not interrupting. Which changes every ninety seconds."],
  ["Would you vote?","If they'd close at midnight. I've no time otherwise."],
  ["What would you ask them?","Why it takes five parties to pass the salt."]],
 "I'll keep the grill hot for the loser. Everyone needs a burger after a debate."],
17:["(The grill rattles. Sanna steadies the pan.) It's been happening all night. The ground. Every five minutes, a small shake. The ketchup bottles walk across the counter.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say so. I say it's the oil. They've been drilling too deep. It's the island's complaint."],
  ["Where's Dario?","Nobody's seen him. The oil men are in a panic. They've eaten twelve burgers in an hour. It's the first sign of a crisis: appetite."],
  ["Are you scared?","I'm too tired to be scared. The grill's hot, and the world can shake all it likes."]],
 "Hold the counter. It helps."],
18:["(Sanna is chewing a sandwich as she works.) The jazz crowd's in tonight, and they're all talking about the Midnight Note, a night 'in disguise'. I told them nobody ever needs a disguise in Frostholm. We're all in one already.",[
  ["Is something happening at the Midnight Note?","They say so. Someone's coming in disguise. A royal, a spy, a cola man. Everybody's a suspect."],
  ["Will you go?","If someone fries my burgers. But no one does."],
  ["What's the music like?","A sax, a bass, and a drum like a man knocking on a door. It's the only place on the island that listens."]],
 "I'll keep the grill hot. The jazz lot always come in at one."],
19:["(Sanna points the spatula at a poster.) 'Comrades Wanted.' Right in my window, someone put it. I'd like to be wanted, but not by a poster.",[
  ["Who put it there?","A very polite man with a stack of them. He bought a burger and left a slogan."],
  ["Would you join?","If they paid for the gas, and gave me Sundays. A comrade needs a day off."],
  ["Is the Party popular among the oil crews?","Not very. They say comrades don't tip."]],
 "Burger's ready. Don't take the poster. I've got a second use for it."],
20:["(Sanna looks at the clock.) A strange night. A man at the Hotel Aurora's been seen. I heard it from a bus driver, who heard it from a woman at the Palace. By the time it got to me, he was a Russian giant.",[
  ["Is he a giant?","He's a man in a hotel. Everything else is gossip, and I deal in grease, not gossip."],
  ["Does everyone know?","Everyone knows something. That's the trouble. Nobody knows the same something."],
  ["Is it a story?","Everything's a story if you tell it with enough onions."]],
 "I never heard a thing. I flip burgers. It's very noisy."],
21:["(Sanna holds the pan in one hand and a scarf over her mouth with the other.) The mountain's blown! Ash on the grill! On the buns! On me! I've never seen a burger so grey.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. It's just gritty. I've served worse, to men with fewer manners."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody, thank God. The wind took it north. We're lucky. I'll say it twice."],
  ["Are you open?","Of course I'm open. If the world ends, they'll want a burger first."]],
 "Eat quickly. It's seasoned with the island."],
22:["(Sanna scrapes ash from the grill.) It's in the batter, in the pickles, in my hair. I've washed it four times. I'm going to be grey till spring.",[
  ["Is it dangerous to cook?","Not if you cover it. The oil lot say it's worse at the rigs."],
  ["Are the customers complaining?","Not a one. They're too tired. They say 'extra grit' and move on."],
  ["Is Birgit all right?","She's mopping. She mops through disasters. It's her way."]],
 "A free coffee for anyone with a mask. It's a new policy."],
23:["(Sanna leans on the counter.) The oil men are busy. 'Deep Repairs,' they say. They come in at three, eat in silence, and go back. They don't even say thank you. They're frightened.",[
  ["What's broken?","Something at the rigs. They won't say. They look at their plates and ask for more mustard."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm worried they'll stop eating. That's the real danger. A hungry man's a talker. A full man's a liar."],
  ["Any Party men?","One. He fixed my fan, then asked me to vote. I voted for the fan."]],
 "If anyone asks, the fan was a gift."],
24:["(Sanna yawns so widely that her jaw clicks.) The clean-up crews are in. A very efficient lot, with good boots. They ordered twenty burgers and asked if I'd seen anything unusual.",[
  ["Did you?","Everything's unusual this week. I said so. They said, 'More unusual than that.'"],
  ["Who are they?","They wore grey jackets and no badges. I think they're the Syndicate's. They paid in clean cash."],
  ["Did you tell them anything?","I told them the pickles are fresh. It's the one thing I'm sure of."]],
 "If they come back, I've run out of onions."],
25:["(Sanna reads a flier over the grill.) A crown for everyone, a princess for democracy. It has the air of a pub quiz. I like it already.",[
  ["Would you vote for it?","Depends who's the princess. I'd vote for anyone with a spare hour."],
  ["Is it popular in Frostholm?","The oil men think it's a joke. The jazz crowd think it's art. The cook thinks it's lunch."],
  ["What do you want?","A day off. A new grill. And fewer men asking me about the sauce."]],
 "Take a flier. Wrap it round a chip."],
26:["(Sanna slaps a tray of cola on the counter.) A Prince in Every Bottle. The man left twelve crates. I've never seen a prince in a burger, but I've seen plenty of toads.",[
  ["Is the cola selling?","Like mad. The young ones love the prince. The old ones love the fizz."],
  ["Who's the prince?","Leopold. He looks like a man who's never washed up. I'd say that's an advantage."],
  ["Is Dario around?","He's bought a spa, they say. A spa! It's a funny place to hide a man who loves oil."]],
 "Take a bottle. If a prince falls out, I want a word."],
27:["(Sanna has a ticket in her pocket.) A film tonight, 'Revolution at the Pictures', and High Stakes at the card table. I'm too tired for both. I'll fry through them.",[
  ["Is the Syndicate hosting the cards?","So they say. A big game, with big money, in a back room. The sort that ends in a fight."],
  ["Would you go to the film?","If someone brought me a burger in the dark. I've never seen the other side of the counter."],
  ["Who's going?","Everyone. The street's empty. It's a ghost town with ketchup."]],
 "I'll be here. The grill's never closed."],
28:["(Sanna has pinned a flag of every colour behind the counter.) The big push. Everyone's in the street. A man gave me a leaflet, a second gave me a badge, and a third offered a job. I took the badge and the job.",[
  ["What's the job?","Cooking. Same as now, but with a banner. I said yes. It's the same hours, but with applause."],
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm always nervous. It's how I keep the burgers from burning."],
  ["What do you hope for?","Peace, and a pay rise. Or a nap. Whichever comes first."]],
 "Eat up. It might be your last decent meal for a while."],
29:["(Sanna wipes down the counter for the fourth time.) Tomorrow's the last day. I'll open at noon, and I'll flip burgers till they tell me to stop. It's a calm kind of fear, like waiting for the sauce to thicken.",[
  ["Are you ready?","I'm always ready. It's the one skill I've perfected."],
  ["What will you do after?","Sleep. For a week. Then, I imagine, a burger."],
  ["Any last words?","Eat well. Vote late. And tip the cook."]],
 "I'll save you a burger. It's the least I can do."],
30:["(Sanna is leaning on the counter, her eyes half-closed, a spatula in her hand like a sceptre.) The last day. Odd to think the grill's seen it all, and tomorrow it'll just be a Wednesday.",[
  ["How has the month been?","Long. Greasy. Grey. I wouldn't change a bun."],
  ["What will you remember?","The nights. The jazz lot, the oil men, the monk in the corner. It's been a good crowd."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come back. I'll fry you something. On the house, if I like you."]],
 "Take care. And eat something green. I mean it."],
};
// Sergeant Hanna, desk sergeant at Kingsgrad Police Station (police, no faction). Threads: Mario in the middle cell, being the whole police force, the paperwork Bertil sends her, her night shifts and bad coffee, a stolen bicycle case that never ends.
DAILY['Sergeant Hanna']={
2:["(Sergeant Hanna doesn't look up from a ledger.) Name, business, and make it quick. Oh. The reporter. Chief of police, desk sergeant and tea lady, all at your service. I'm the whole force.",[
  ["The whole force? How many officers?","One. Me. The old chief retired in the spring and nobody's replaced him. The island's quiet, usually."],
  ["Quiet, with a king just dead?","Quiet's relative. I've a cell full of bookie and a drawer full of complaints about bicycles."],
  ["Can I see the cells?","You can see the corridor. The middle cell's occupied, and he's not receiving."]],
 "Wipe your feet. The floor's the only thing I can keep clean."],
3:["(Hanna taps a form with a pen.) A stolen bicycle. Third this month. Green, with a bell. I've a report from an old woman who says she saw the thief. It was a goat.",[
  ["A goat stole a bicycle?","I'm following several lines of enquiry. The goat's at the top."],
  ["Is crime much of a problem here?","Not as such. A few fights, the odd smuggler. And then there's the Syndicate, who I don't discuss on a Tuesday."],
  ["Who is in the cell?","Mario. A bookie. He says he's innocent. They all do. He also asks for a fresh towel, which is more than the others did."]],
 "If you see a goat with a bicycle, please phone."],
4:["(Hanna is eating a sandwich over a pile of paperwork.) Bertil from the Bureau sent me a form this morning for requisitioning a pencil. A pencil! I've a drawer full of them.",[
  ["Is he always like that?","Bertil? He's a good man. He thinks the island runs on forms. He may be right, which is the terrifying part."],
  ["Does the Bureau trouble you often?","Daily. Every pencil, every lamp, every cell blanket, in triplicate."],
  ["What's the strangest form you've had?","One to request permission to fill out another form. I did it. I felt very small."]],
 "Sign here, and here, and here. Thank you."],
5:["(Hanna is staring at the cell corridor.) Mario's been singing again. Very badly. Opera. He says it calms his nerves. It certainly doesn't calm mine.",[
  ["Can't you ask him to stop?","I did. He sang louder. A man with nothing to do in a cell is a dangerous thing."],
  ["Does he have visitors?","A lawyer, and a nephew with a cake. I checked the cake. It was just cake."],
  ["Is he a threat?","To the peace and quiet, yes. To the island, no. He's a small fish in a very large pond."]],
 "If you hear a high C, that's Mario. Cover your ears."],
6:["(Hanna drinks from a chipped mug.) Coffee's awful, I know. But it's my own, and I won't be lectured about it. Nine hours in, and I've a feeling it's going to be a long night.",[
  ["Do you work nights?","Evenings, mostly. A desk sergeant's duty is to be at the desk. Most days that means all day."],
  ["Is there anything you enjoy?","Dominoes with the old men in the square. They cheat. I let them."],
  ["Is your family here?","My mother lives in the north. She worries about me and the cells. I tell her it's all paperwork, which isn't far off."]],
 "Mind the bucket by the door. The roof leaks."],
7:["(Hanna looks over her glasses.) The north-west road's open, and I've already had a call from Frostholm about a missing crate. Diversified Petroleum. Missing crates are always interesting.",[
  ["What was in the crate?","I wasn't told. That's usually the interesting bit."],
  ["Will you investigate?","If a complaint's made, I write it down. If it's made twice, I take a walk. Three times, I take my hat."],
  ["Is oil money causing trouble?","Oil money causes trouble the same way water causes damp. It gets everywhere."]],
 "Report it properly, and I'll look into it. Eventually."],
8:["(Hanna has her feet on the desk.) Sunday. Quiet as a graveyard. A man came in to report his hat stolen. It was on his head. We both had a laugh and I felt a bit better.",[
  ["Do you have Sundays off?","The island has Sundays. I have a desk."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Whenever I'm not on duty, which is never. I say a prayer at the window. It counts."],
  ["Any trouble today?","A dispute over a fence. I solved it with a tape measure and a stern look."]],
 "Take your hat. It's yours. Probably."],
9:["(Hanna leafs through a notice from the palace.) A funeral tomorrow, and I'm to keep the traffic moving. All four vehicles. I've had to draw a map.",[
  ["Is it a big job?","It's the biggest job I've had in years. One road, one palace, and the whole island wants to go."],
  ["Do you get any help?","Three constables from Helsinki, I was promised. I've received a note saying they're thinking about it."],
  ["Are you going to the funeral?","In uniform, at the gate. Eyes front, and a mind full of potholes."]],
 "If you're driving, take the left fork. And drive slowly."],
10:["(Hanna adjusts her cap.) I've seen the king's coffin pass. The whole street went quiet. A baby cried, and someone cried with it. I held my salute until my arm hurt.",[
  ["How did it go?","Smoothly, as far as the traffic's concerned. The rest was in the hands of the clergy."],
  ["Is the town still upset?","It's in a quiet mood. Drinking tea, saying little. Tomorrow it'll start arguing again."],
  ["Did you know the king?","He once stopped his car to ask for directions. He knew the way. He wanted to talk to a policewoman. I've never forgotten it."]],
 "A good man. We could do with more like him."],
11:["(Hanna rubs her temples.) After the funeral, the island's gone back to bickering. Four reports of rowdy behaviour, two of stolen flags, and a goat in the churchyard.",[
  ["The same goat?","I believe so. He's become a local character. I've named him Constable."],
  ["Do the flags matter?","To the people who own them, very much. To me, they're another form to fill in."],
  ["Is Mario any better?","He's learning Swedish from a phrasebook. He recites it through the bars. It's mostly menus."]],
 "If anyone asks, I was out on patrol. Politely."],
12:["(Hanna stamps a document.) A complaint from Eastbrook: a record shop owner, Ruby, says a stranger tried to buy her rarest record at gunpoint. Not gunpoint. Rudely.",[
  ["Is it serious?","Not really. A man with a fat wallet, a loud voice, and no manners. I've sent a constable. Well, I went myself."],
  ["Who's the man?","From Diversified Petroleum, I think. A name I've heard before. I'll keep it to myself for now."],
  ["Does it happen often?","Rich men think they can buy anything. In this case, I hope not."]],
 "File your complaint, and I'll file it for you. In the right drawer."],
13:["(Hanna sighs.) Tomorrow's the big day, and every rumour in Kingsgrad has found its way to my desk. I've logged forty-two. Forty-one are nonsense.",[
  ["What was the forty-second?","That somebody's going to do something dramatic. Which could mean anything. It's always somebody."],
  ["Are you prepared?","As much as I can be. I've asked for extra constables. I'm waiting for the post."],
  ["Will there be trouble?","There's always a bit. I'll be at the desk, with my whistle and a thermos."]],
 "Lock your bicycle. Tonight and tomorrow."],
14:["(Hanna checks her watch.) The big day, and so far the streets are calm. Calm, in my experience, is the first thing to go. I'll keep the whistle handy.",[
  ["Is everyone behaving?","Mostly. A few raised voices at the corner, a scuffle over a banner. The usual."],
  ["Do you feel safe?","I feel on duty. It's not the same, but it'll do."],
  ["Any advice for a reporter?","Stay out of the way. Take notes. And if someone asks you to hold a flag, say no."]],
 "If you need me, I'm at the desk. I'm always at the desk."],
15:["(Hanna rubs her eyes.) Yesterday's over, and I've a stack of reports to prove it. A lost hat, a broken lantern, and a man who claimed the fourteenth was a hoax.",[
  ["Was it?","No. But nobody agrees what it was. That's the way of big days."],
  ["Did you get any sleep?","Two hours, on the cell bench. Mario was kind enough to move over."],
  ["Is Mario still singing?","Quietly. It's a lullaby, I think. I've started to enjoy it."]],
 "I'll sleep when I'm retired. Which is never."],
16:["(Hanna pins a notice to the board.) A debate tonight, between the Party and the Democrats. I'm sending a constable to keep order. By constable, I mean me, and a flask.",[
  ["Will there be trouble?","Debate and trouble are the same word in this town. I've a feeling it's going to be loud."],
  ["Who do you favour?","As a policewoman, nobody. As a woman with a lot of unsolved bicycle thefts, the side that finds the goat."],
  ["Will you listen to the speeches?","From the back, with my hands in my pockets. I'll clap for whoever speaks the shortest."]],
 "Stay out of the front row. It's where the tomatoes land."],
17:["(Hanna looks at a cracked mug.) Did you feel that? The whole desk shook. A tremor. The cells rattled, and Mario shouted something in Italian.",[
  ["Was he frightened?","He said a prayer. Then asked for a blanket. I gave him two."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Might be. I've called the Bureau. They told me to fill in a form."],
  ["What do you do in an earthquake?","Stand under the doorframe, and think about my pension."]],
 "If it happens again, duck first, and ask questions later."],
18:["(Hanna scribbles on a pad.) More tremors overnight, and a smell of sulphur this morning. Two calls from frightened old ladies. One from a cat. Well, the cat's owner.",[
  ["What did you tell them?","That it's probably nothing. And to keep their cats indoors. And to have a torch ready."],
  ["Is it really nothing?","I don't know. I'm a policewoman, not a geologist. I'd rather be wrong and calm."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm busy. It comes to the same thing."]],
 "Take a torch. The nights are long this month."],
19:["(Hanna reads a telegram.) A masquerade somewhere in Eastbrook, at the Midnight Note. I've been told to keep an eye out for noisy guests. And a prince in disguise. That last I've decided not to have seen.",[
  ["A prince in disguise?","Someone's in a false moustache. It's hardly a crime. Nor is dancing. I'll leave it alone."],
  ["Is the Syndicate involved?","The Midnight Note belongs to Sid, and Sid belongs to somebody. That's all I'll say."],
  ["Would you go yourself?","I've no costume. I've a uniform, and a face for it."]],
 "I'll keep the peace. Behind a desk, as always."],
20:["(Hanna points to a report.) The Syndicate's been busy. A burglary at the palace, rumoured for tonight. I've warned the guards, and I've told Mario to stay put. He promised, with his hand on a cake.",[
  ["Is a burglary planned?","There's always talk. If it comes to anything, I'll hear about it first."],
  ["What would they steal?","Something small and valuable, and not mentioned in any report. That's the usual way."],
  ["Will you stop them?","I'll try. A one-woman police force has its limits."]],
 "If you hear a bang, go the other way."],
21:["(Hanna is covered in grey ash.) It's gone up. The volcano. The sky's black, the streets are grey, and Kingsgrad has stopped to look. I've had two hundred calls, and I've answered twelve.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not one. Thank God. The lava's up north, away from people."],
  ["How are you coping?","With a scarf over my face and a list. The list is long."],
  ["Is Mario all right?","He's hysterical, and he's asked if he can pray. I let him. The ash is coming in through the window, and he's kneeling in it."]],
 "Keep a cloth over your mouth. The ash is terrible on the lungs."],
22:["(Hanna sweeps the floor.) Ash everywhere. On the desk, on the forms, in the coffee. I've swept it three times and it keeps on falling.",[
  ["Has crime dropped?","Everyone's too busy sweeping. It's the quietest day I've had in years."],
  ["Any good news?","A woman brought back the green bicycle. She'd found it in her shed. She'd forgotten she'd bought it."],
  ["Is Kingsgrad coping?","Better than you'd think. Neighbours are helping neighbours. Even the Party and the cola men."]],
 "Please don't track ash on my floor. It's been a long day."],
23:["(Hanna taps her pen.) A few aftershocks, but nothing like the first. I've decided to treat them like a bad-tempered neighbour. Annoying, but you get used to it.",[
  ["Do you worry?","I worry about the cells. If the wall cracks, Mario could escape. Though he'd probably come back for the cake."],
  ["What's the mood in town?","Tired. People want to forget it. A sock of ash on every shoulder."],
  ["Is there any trouble?","A sabotage at one of the oil installations, I hear. I haven't been told much, which is how I like it."]],
 "Stay calm. It won't help, but it's cheaper than panic."],
24:["(Hanna lays out a map.) Church people going up to the peaks, I hear. A retreat. They've asked me to keep an eye on the road, and a Syndicate gang's been spotted around the foothills.",[
  ["Is there a connection?","I couldn't say. Clean-up crews sound innocent, but I've heard that name before."],
  ["Will you go up there?","If I'm asked. For now, I'm watching the road, and the cells, and the post."],
  ["Is it dangerous?","Not as dangerous as the paperwork. But close."]],
 "If you're going north, keep your doors locked."],
25:["(Hanna opens a drawer.) A letter from the palace, asking for a constable at a tea party. Tea! With a crown, a princess, and some Democrats. I'm to stand in the corner and look reassuring.",[
  ["Will you go?","Yes. In my second-best uniform. I'll take Mario's cake."],
  ["Is it a political event?","Everything is, these days. Even the biscuits."],
  ["Do you like the royals?","I like the ones who say thank you. Daphne does. So did the old king."]],
 "If there's cake left, I'll save you a slice."],
26:["(Hanna wipes her glasses.) A dozen delegations at my desk this morning, asking where to park. Cola men, oil men, Party men, all want the same space outside the palace.",[
  ["What did you tell them?","First come, first served. Then I put a cone on it."],
  ["Is it getting tense?","Like a drawn bow. Everyone's polite, and everyone's counting."],
  ["Will you keep order?","I'll try. I've a whistle, a stern face, and a very large book."]],
 "If you must park, park behind the bakery."],
27:["(Hanna reads her notes.) A film at the cinema tomorrow, I hear. The Party's laid on a free showing. I'm told to stand at the door and keep people in line. For a film.",[
  ["Is it popular?","It's free. Everything free is popular."],
  ["Do you like pictures?","I like a western, and a good villain. I hope this one has both."],
  ["Is there a catch?","There's always a catch. I'll have my whistle ready."]],
 "Queue properly. I'll be watching."],
28:["(Hanna looks at the clock.) Today every faction's out in force. The whole island's on the move. I've had three reports of flags on the palace roof, and a plan for a fourth.",[
  ["Whose flag was it?","I'd rather not say. It's coming down by lunchtime."],
  ["Are you coping?","I'm a desk sergeant on the biggest day of the year. I've a flask, a whistle and no doubts."],
  ["Is there anything I should avoid?","Large crowds, red banners, and any man who offers you a free bottle."]],
 "Stay where I can see you. I like to keep track."],
29:["(Hanna sits down for the first time.) Tomorrow's the end of it, and my feet say I can't wait. I've done my rounds, and filled my forms. Whatever happens, it'll be on the record.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","About the rest of the island, yes. About my own desk, no."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Same as always. Sit at the desk, answer the phone, and keep the peace."],
  ["Any hopes?","That everyone gets home safely. And that somebody finally finds my goat."]],
 "Go home, rest. It'll be a long day."],
30:["(Hanna puts down her pen.) The last day. The island's about to find out what it's going to be. I'm tired, and proud, and very ready for a cup of tea.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like a woman who's carried a very heavy tray a very long way."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The snow melting, the king going, the ash. And a reporter with too many questions."],
  ["Any last words?","Keep the peace, keep the receipts, and keep an eye out for a green bicycle."]],
 "Thank you for your patience. Close the door on your way out."],
};
// Sergeant Rask, quartermaster at the barracks armoury (The Army). Threads: his exact inventory of rifles, boots and blankets and the exact number missing, his terrified privates, his dry respect for the Colonel, the ash and the shaking ground, his quiet soft spot for the men.
DAILY['Sergeant Rask']={
6:["(Sergeant Rask is writing in a ledger and doesn't look up.) Four hundred and twelve blankets. Four hundred and nine on the shelves. Three have gone for a walk. State your business, reporter. Quickly.",[
  ["I'm just introducing myself, Sergeant.","Very civil. Rask. Quartermaster. I count things. If it's in the army, I've counted it, and so has the moth."],
  ["What's missing, besides the blankets?","Two pairs of boots, a bayonet, and my patience. Only one of those I expected to see again."],
  ["Do you ever relax?","On Sundays I count more slowly."]],
 "Don't touch anything. I'll know."],
7:["(Rask is polishing a rifle.) The north-west road's open now, and Frostholm's lorries are thundering past. Every one of them could carry a rifle, and I've counted mine twice this morning.",[
  ["Are you worried about theft?","I'm worried about arithmetic. If the number's wrong, someone's wrong. I intend to find out who."],
  ["How many rifles do you have?","On paper, a hundred and forty. On the rack, a hundred and thirty-eight. Two are, as they say, wandering."],
  ["Who do you suspect?","Everyone. It's my best quality."]],
 "Close the door when you leave. It sticks. Like the soldiers."],
8:["(Rask is listening to the radio sermon with his arms folded.) Father Thyme's lost for words. I understand. I once lost a whole barrel of buttons and couldn't speak for a week.",[
  ["Are you religious, Sergeant?","I'm methodical. It's the same thing, with less incense."],
  ["How are the men?","Aho writes too much. Niemi sings too much. Koski reads too much. I'm surrounded by excess."],
  ["Do you like them?",["(He pauses.) They're good lads. I'd never say so. If you print that, I'll deny it and count your fingers.",[
    ["Aho's frightened of you.","Good. He'll live longer. A frightened soldier checks his boots twice."],
    ["What would you say to him?","Write to your mother, and mean it. And fold your blanket in thirds, not quarters."]]]]],
 "Take care, reporter. Mind the step. I counted it."],
9:["(Rask is inspecting a crate of cola with a frown.) A van left twenty crates at the gate and refused a receipt. I don't like anything without a receipt. The Colonel says accept. I say account.",[
  ["Is the army drinking the new cola?","The men are. Niemi's using it in the sauce. I've found it in the pot, the pockets and my own tea."],
  ["Are you suspicious?","Of free things? Always. A free gift is a debt in disguise."],
  ["What would you do with it?","Count it. Then log it. Then, perhaps, taste it. In that order."]],
 "If anyone offers you a free crate, ask for a receipt."],
10:["(Rask is in full dress uniform, every medal in place, every button shining.) The funeral. The Colonel is with the guard of honour. I'm left to mind the armoury. I'd rather be there, but someone must keep the count.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","I served him thirty years. He signed my commission. He was a decent man, and he never misplaced a thing."],
  ["How are you?","Calm. Professional. A little cold around the eyes."],
  ["What will happen now?","The Colonel will tell us. And we'll do it. That's the whole of the army, reporter."]],
 "A moment's silence, if you please."],
11:["(Rask is stacking boxes of boots with a clatter.) The mill hands want a fair day's pay. A soldier doesn't strike. But a quartermaster knows when the books don't balance. Theirs don't, I'd say.",[
  ["Do you think they're right?","I think a day's pay should match a day's work. It's arithmetic."],
  ["Does the army pay fairly?","The army pays exactly what's written. Not a coin more. It's a point of honour."],
  ["Are you in the Union?","Certainly not. I'm in the ledger."]],
 "The boots are for the army, not the mill. Move along."],
12:["(Rask is staring at a shelf of empty hooks.) Someone's been at the armoury again. A cap, a belt and a very old whistle. Nothing important. Everything important. It's the principle.",[
  ["Who could have done it?","A thief, a prankster or a ghost. I suspect the ghost. It has the least alibi."],
  ["Is there a pattern?","There's always a pattern. I'll find it. I'll draw a chart."],
  ["Have you told the Colonel?","He said, Sergeant, it's a whistle. I said, Sir, it's a principle. He changed the subject."]],
 "Don't touch the hooks. They're evidence."],
13:["(Rask holds a tiny cola bottle up to the light, like a jeweller.) A sample, from the cola men. They gave Niemi forty. I've counted thirty-nine. One is missing, and I know who.",[
  ["Is Niemi in trouble?","He'll be asked, politely. Then less politely. Then he'll confess in verse."],
  ["Are you strict with him?","I'm accurate. It's not the same, though it feels alike from underneath."],
  ["What do you think of Kelp Cola?","A very well-run company. It counts its bottles. I respect that."]],
 "Take care. Everything has a number, and the number always comes calling."],
14:["(Rask is polishing the same buckle for the third time.) Tomorrow, the gathering. The whole island in one place. The Colonel's briefed us. We'll stand where we're told, and we'll count heads. I'll count twice.",[
  ["Are you expecting trouble?","I never expect. I prepare. There's a difference, and it's worth a stripe."],
  ["How are the men?","Aho's gone pale. Koski's restless. Niemi's baking. I'd say they're on form."],
  ["What would you say to the island?","Stand in line, and don't lose anything."]],
 "See that you're in order tomorrow. Collar, boots, bearing."],
15:["(Rask is glaring at a dust cloud on a rifle butt.) The royals and the Church are squabbling, crown against cross. The army serves neither. I'm sure the Colonel will explain it at dinner. At the palace.",[
  ["Does the Colonel dine there often?","Frequently. He says it's strategy. I say it's the cheese."],
  ["Whose side are you on?","The army's. Which is nobody's. We keep the peace, count the rifles and go to bed."],
  ["Is that hard?","Every day. But I've a ledger and a very sturdy chair."]],
 "If you meet the Prince, remind him to sign for his chair."],
16:["(Rask is sitting rigidly beside the radio.) The debate. I've no interest in it. But the privates are listening, so I must listen too, in case it's seditious. It's mostly shouting.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The one with the better microphone. Not a quality I'd want in a soldier."],
  ["Does the army care who wins?","The army cares that the winner pays on time."],
  ["What do you think of the Democrats' vote?","A fine thing, if properly counted. I'd offer to count it myself."]],
 "If anyone asks, I wasn't listening. I was counting the pauses."],
17:["(Rask holds a stack of mess tins steady, looking at the ceiling.) That was a tremor. Four seconds. Two sharp, two rolling. I've logged it. The ledger will want it noted.",[
  ["Do you log earthquakes?","I log anything that moves. Especially if it isn't supposed to."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Very likely. The Colonel's called a meeting. I've counted the shovels."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm quartermaster. I'm paid to worry about the things that run out first."]],
 "If it shakes again, don't sit on the rifles."],
18:["(Rask looks very stern.) Two young monks came to my armoury looking for a relic. I admit, I was suspicious. A relic is just an unlisted item with better branding.",[
  ["Did you help them?","I walked them through every shelf. I know where everything is. A relic isn't among it."],
  ["What was the relic?","They said a small gilded box. I said I'd check the manifest. They said it wasn't on the manifest. That was my point."],
  ["Do you believe in them?","I believe in inventory. A holy relic that isn't listed is a security risk."]],
 "If it turns up, it should be logged. Under R."],
19:["(Rask is cleaning his glasses with great care.) The Party are recruiting. Aho nearly took a leaflet. Koski nearly took two. I took the leaflets and filed them under Miscellaneous. A neat solution.",[
  ["Are the soldiers tempted?","Some are. The young always are. They want a cause, and a good pair of boots."],
  ["Is the Colonel worried?","He's stiff. That's how I know."],
  ["What's the Midnight Note?","A nightclub, apparently. I've never been. I don't dance. I count the steps."]],
 "If you see a recruiter, ask for his serial number."],
20:["(Rask is staring at a map pinned on the wall.) There's a stranger at the Hotel Aurora. Sergeant Hanna of the police is on his trail. If the army has a role in it, no one's told me.",[
  ["Have you heard anything?","Only what the stones say. Everyone's nervous. Everyone's counting."],
  ["Is the armoury secure?","I've doubled the locks and halved the keys. If anyone wants a rifle, they'll need a very good story."],
  ["Who's the stranger?","I haven't a name. Only a hat, a rumour, and an unusual rate of rumour."]],
 "Be careful. A man with a hat is a man with a plan."],
21:["(Rask is standing in the yard, grey from head to toe, giving orders very quietly.) The mountain's blown. The Colonel's ordered everyone out to help. The armoury's sealed. The shovels are issued. Everyone's accounted for.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","Present, correct, and covered in ash. Not a single casualty. I've counted three times."],
  ["Did you see it?","From the yard. A great dark pillar, taller than the church. I stood still. It seemed the dignified thing."],
  ["What now?","We clear the roads. We help the island. We tidy up. It's the only plan we have."]],
 "Cover your face. And sign for a shovel on your way out."],
22:["(Rask is sweeping the armoury floor himself.) Ash in the barrels, ash in the ledgers, ash in my moustache. It's a grim thing, a thin film of grey over an orderly life.",[
  ["How are the men?","Better than I expected. Aho's been writing home a lot. Koski's holding the gate. Niemi's making soup for half the island."],
  ["Is the armoury damaged?","A few cracks, nothing serious. I've logged them all."],
  ["What's the Colonel doing?","Telephoning. Dining. Coordinating. It's what a colonel does."]],
 "Wipe your feet. Twice."],
23:["(Rask notes down a tremor on a card.) Another aftershock. Three seconds, small. The aftershocks come as regular as pay day, and about as welcome. I've a chart of them now.",[
  ["Is there a pattern?","They're closer together and weaker. It's a good sign, probably. I'm not a geologist."],
  ["How are you coping?","I count. It's what I do. One, two, three, rumble. It steadies the nerves."],
  ["Do the men know you're nervous?","They think I'm made of stone. I'd prefer to keep it that way."]],
 "If you're nervous, count. It never hurts."],
24:["(Rask is arranging a row of blankets.) The Church is going up to the peaks. The Colonel has lent them twenty blankets and a stove. I've logged every one, and I've written a very polite reminder.",[
  ["Will they return the blankets?","Monks are honest. It's a delight to deal with them. They return everything, even prayers."],
  ["Does the army help the Church?","On request. We help anyone who asks, and who signs."],
  ["Is it hard to lend things?","Terribly. But it's duty. And I've written the dates in red."]],
 "A kindness is only a loan with better manners."],
25:["(Rask is holding a leaflet by the corner.) A crown for everyone, the royals say. A princess for democracy. I counted the crowns on the leaflet. There are seven. There should be eight.",[
  ["Whose side is the army on?","Order. Always order. Whoever keeps it is the right side."],
  ["Do you like Prince Leopold?","He's well turned out, if a bit loose with the buttons."],
  ["And the Democrats?","Methodical, in their way. I'd trust a man who counts votes."]],
 "Don't drop the leaflet. I'll have to file it."],
26:["(Rask checks his watch against the wall clock.) They say Dario has bought the spa at Hope Springs, and that the cola people are putting princes under bottle caps. Everyone's spending. I'm counting.",[
  ["Is the army buying anything?","Not a stitch. The Colonel says we'll make do. I say we've made do since 1962."],
  ["Do you mind the oil men?","They've a lot of money and a very poor sense of paperwork."],
  ["Would you take a job at the spa?","I'd run the towel cupboard. Every towel numbered."]],
 "Report any odd transactions. I'll log them."],
27:["(Rask is standing in the armoury door, arms behind his back.) Tomorrow looks serious. A revolution at the pictures, they say, and who knows what at the card tables. The Colonel wants the armoury locked down. Doubly.",[
  ["Are you expecting violence?","I never expect. I prepare. There are three separate keys, and I hold two."],
  ["Who holds the third?","The Colonel. And he's at the palace. So, in effect, no one."],
  ["Are the men ready?","They're drilled. They're nervous. They're ready."]],
 "I'm counting on you to keep your head. And your boots dry."],
28:["(Rask has a fresh shave, a clean ledger and a very straight back.) The big day. I've issued every item in the stores. Every man has a blanket, a boot and a rifle, as required. If something goes wrong, it won't be the paperwork.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm professionally apprehensive. It's a better word."],
  ["What will the army do?","Stand firm. Hold the line. Do nothing rash. It's the Colonel's order, and mine."],
  ["What do you hope for?",["That the day ends with everything where it should be. People, weapons, hopes. All present and correct.",[
    ["That's a lot to ask.","It's an inventory, not a miracle."],
    ["Anything for your men?","A hot meal. A dry bed. And a quiet word, which I'll deny later."]]]]],
 "Fall in, reporter. You're as ready as the rest of us."],
29:["(Rask checks his ledger one last time.) Tomorrow it ends. Primaries. Lines of succession. I've never understood politics, but I do understand an audit. The island's about to have a very big one.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Applause, outrage and paperwork."],
  ["Are the men ready?","As ready as I can make them. They've been drilled, fed, and counted."],
  ["How do you feel?","Exactly as I should. Steady, with a slight tremor. It may be the ground."]],
 "Get some sleep. That's an order, though I've no authority."],
30:["(Rask is standing in the yard at dawn, perfectly still.) The last day. Every shovel, every rifle, every blanket accounted for. Whatever happens, the books balance. That's a comfort.",[
  ["How are you?","Steady. Cold. Slightly proud. I'd rather not say which."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash. The shaking. And Aho's face the first day, when he saw my ledger."],
  ["Any message for London?","Say the army counted every man, every night. And that no one was lost."]],
 "Dismissed, reporter. And thank you."],
};
// Sid, owner and bartender of The Midnight Note, the jazz club in Frostholm (The Proprietors). Threads: Leo, who loves Rita and only
// says it through his saxophone; the back booths and who sits in them (he sees nothing, on principle); the piano that won't stay in
// tune and the tuner coming from Helsinki; his seven waistcoats; the night in 1959 he carried ice for the Arvo Lind Quartet.
DAILY['Sid']={
7:["(Sid, in a burgundy waistcoat, sets down a napkin and a glass without being asked.) Welcome to The Midnight Note. The road's open, the band's back, and you look like a man who needs a slow drink and a fast tune.",[
  ["What do you recommend?","Whisky, neat, and Leo on the saxophone in about an hour. Between them, they'll make you forget what you came to Frostholm for."],
  ["I'm a reporter. What's the story here?",["The story here is the music. Everything else that happens in my club is nobody's story. (He polishes a glass.) That's the house rule.",[
    ["What else happens?","People meet. People talk. In the back booths, mostly. I serve them drinks and look at the ceiling. It's a very interesting ceiling."],
    ["That sounds like a story.","It sounds like a good way to lose a liquor licence. Have a drink and listen to the band."]]]],
  ["Nice waistcoat.","Thank you. Tuesday's. I have seven. Burgundy's for Tuesdays, when people need cheering up."]],
 "Stay for the second set. It's the better one."],
8:["(Leo is on the stage, playing to an empty room, something low and aching.) He does that every afternoon. Rehearsing, he calls it. It's always the same song, and it's always for Rita.",[
  ["Does Rita know?","Rita knows everything about everyone, and says less than I do. Half the island's in love with her. Leo's just the only one who's set it to music."],
  ["Why doesn't he tell her?","Leo doesn't talk. He sleeps till noon, plays till three in the morning, and says about four words a day. The horn does the rest."],
  ["What's the song?","He hasn't named it. I call it 'Leo's Sunday'. It's Sunday. He played the same one last Sunday. It's getting better."]],
 "Sit as long as you like. It's free till the first set."],
9:["The piano's out of tune. Ever since the cold came, middle C sounds like a goose. Rita won't sing over it. I've telephoned Helsinki for a tuner.",[
  ["Is there no tuner on the island?","There was. Old Pekka, in Eastbrook. He died in the spring, and took his tuning fork with him. Now there's me with a spanner, and that's worse."],
  ["When does the tuner come?","Two weeks, if the boats run, and if he's sober, and if he can find Sarivalto on a map. It's a lot of ifs."],
  ["Can't Leo play over it?","Leo plays over anything. He played over a fistfight in 1979 and nobody noticed the fight. But a singer needs a piano."]],
 "Go on. Mind the goose."],
10:["(Sid is in a black waistcoat today.) Funeral day. I've closed the curtains and Leo's playing a dirge. The oil men are drinking to the king and the Syndicate men are drinking to whatever comes next.",[
  ["A black waistcoat. Not Wednesday's?","Wednesday's is grey. Black's for funerals and for the night the club opened. I've worn it four times."],
  ["Did the king ever come here?","Not as a king. Once, years ago, a gentleman in a scarf and dark glasses sat at the bar and asked for 'Autumn in New York'. Paid in a very old banknote. I've never said who I thought he was."],
  ["What's Rita singing today?","Nothing. She's in black, at a corner table, with a glass of something. She says some days you sing and some days you listen."]],
 "Raise a glass to him, if you like. Leo's taking requests."],
11:["(Arto and Raili are at a table near the stage, sharing one drink.) They've been nursing that beer for two hours. I'll top it up when nobody's looking. Don't tell them.",[
  ["Who are they?","Arto lost his job when the cannery closed. Raili lost hers at the Aurora. They come in to stay warm and listen. They're the best audience I've got."],
  ["Why help them?","Because a club with an empty room is a sad club. And because I was them, once. In 1958, in a worse coat."],
  ["Won't you go broke?","On a glass of beer? The oil men pay for it. They just don't know."]],
 "Go on. And if anyone asks, the beer refills itself."],
12:["Funny thing. Two different people asked me about 'Midnight at the Aurora' this week. The record. The Arvo Lind Quartet. Live, in 1959. I was there.",[
  ["You were there?",["Nineteen, and carrying ice for the ballroom bar at the Aurora. They played till dawn. I stood behind a pillar with a bucket and listened to every note.",[
    ["What was it like?","Like hearing your own heartbeat played back to you by four men who'd never met you. Arvo played the last song with his eyes shut. Then he was sick in my bucket."],
    ["Was anyone else listening?","Everyone. And a chambermaid with a coffee tray, at the back, who never moved. I've always wondered who she was."]]]],
  ["Who asked about it?","A man from the oil company, very keen, and a soft-spoken fellow who telephoned from somewhere with an echo. I said I didn't have a copy. That's true. I wish it weren't."],
  ["Is it a valuable record?","Two hundred pressed. Most of them broken, lost or played into dust. If there's one left in good shape, it's worth more than this bar."]],
 "Go on. If you ever find that record, bring it here. I'd like to hear it once more."],
13:["(Sid is reading the reservation book.) Somebody booked the far back booth for tomorrow night under 'K.C.' Kelp Cola, or someone who wants me to think so. In my club, initials only. That's the rule.",[
  ["Do you ever find out who?","Always. I just never say. You can tell a man by what he drinks. A cola man drinks gin and acts like it's water."],
  ["Who books the back booths most?","Oil men, cola men, Syndicate men, and a Russian who drinks vodka and tips like a tsar. Sometimes all four on the same night, in different booths, pretending not to see each other."],
  ["Isn't that dangerous?","It's jazz. Everything's dangerous after midnight. That's why it's good."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Come by after and drink to whatever happened."],
14:["(The club's empty, and Sid is cleaning glasses that are already clean.) The whole island's at the big day. I've sent Leo and Rita. Someone has to keep the club warm.",[
  ["Why didn't you go?","I've seen big days. They're like big nights: everyone remembers them differently afterwards. I'll hear all the versions in here later."],
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches by people who've never sung, cheers by people who've never listened. And a lot of thirsty people by sundown."],
  ["Is Leo going to talk to Rita?","Leo's going to stand next to Rita in a crowd and say nothing. It's progress. Last year he stood behind her."]],
 "Go on. Tell them Sid's open after."],
15:["(Sid is in a green waistcoat with a small hole in it.) A moth. In Sunday's waistcoat. Thirty years I've kept moths out of my waistcoats, and one got Sunday.",[
  ["Can it be mended?","There's a tailor in Kingsgrad, Grace, who could darn a cobweb. I'll take it in. Until then, I wear it with the hole showing. Like a scar."],
  ["What happened yesterday?","Everyone came in afterwards, everyone had a different story, and Leo played until four. Rita sang one song. She wouldn't say which, after. It was a good one."],
  ["Church or crown, Sid?","I serve both. Augustus came in once and ordered water. Leopold never comes in. Make of that what you like."]],
 "Go on. Watch out for moths."],
16:["(Rita is at the bar, smoking, and Sid is pouring her a coffee with something in it.) Rita's going to sing tonight with the goose of a piano. She's decided. She says if the island's falling apart, she might as well sing out of tune.",[
  ["Is the island falling apart?","The Communists and the Democrats had a debate. Everyone came here after to keep arguing. I've confiscated six beer mats with diagrams on."],
  ["Does Rita ever talk about her past?","Never. And I never ask. She came off a boat from Stockholm in 1977 with one suitcase and the best voice in the north. That's all the past anyone needs."],
  ["Where does she go between sets?","(Sid smiles.) Out. For forty minutes. Wherever she likes. She comes back happier. I don't ask."]],
 "Go on. Stay for Rita. Out of tune or not."],
17:["(The bottles are trembling on the shelves, and Sid is holding the top row with one hand.) The ground's been shaking all day. I've lost two bottles of gin and a decanter. The piano's gone from bad to tragic.",[
  ["Is the club safe?","The club's survived a fire, a flood and the Syndicate's Christmas party. A few shakes won't hurt it. They'll hurt my gin."],
  ["Dario's missing, I hear.","His men came in and searched the back booths. Under the tables. Behind the curtain. They found Arto asleep and an old earring. Not Dario."],
  ["Had he been in?","I don't say who's been in. (He pauses.) But nobody's sat in his favourite booth for two days. I've kept it empty. Just in case."]],
 "Go on. Hold on to your glass."],
18:["I had a telephone call from someone very polite, asking whether my staff are discreet, and whether a gentleman could enjoy the music without being bothered. I said this is The Midnight Note. Nobody's ever bothered.",[
  ["Who was it?","A voice that's used to being listened to. That's all I'll say. People who want to be invisible always sound like they're used to being noticed."],
  ["Will you tell the Gazette?","The Gazette? Nora would sell her own grandmother for a story out of my club. I'd sooner sell my waistcoats."],
  ["Is your staff discreet?","Leo says four words a day. Rita says even fewer about herself. And I see nothing. We're the most discreet staff in the north."]],
 "Go on. And forget I mentioned the telephone."],
19:["(Sid is in a midnight blue waistcoat, the best one, with brass buttons.) Busy tonight, I expect. Anyone could walk in. And in my club, anyone who walks in is just a man who likes jazz.",[
  ["The blue waistcoat. Special occasion?","Friday's. Fridays are always special. Don't read anything into a waistcoat."],
  ["Who's coming tonight?","Music lovers. Gentlemen in scarves. Ladies in furs. People who'd like to be nobody for an evening. I can't remember a single face, and I've got an excellent memory."],
  ["What's Leo playing?","His Sunday song, on a Friday. He's finally given it a name: 'Rita, Don't Look Up'. He hasn't told her. He's told the band."]],
 "Go on. Come back later, if you like jazz."],
20:["(Sid is polishing the bar very slowly.) Whatever happened in this club last night stays in this club. I'll say that once. It was a good night. Leo played his best.",[
  ["Did anything happen?","Music happened. A lot of it. People clapped. People went home. That's what happens in clubs."],
  ["Has Nora from the Gazette been in?","Nora's been in twice today asking questions about last night. I gave her a coffee and a long story about the piano. She left very well informed about pianos."],
  ["Did Leo play his song for Rita?","He did. 'Rita, Don't Look Up.' She looked up. (He smiles.) That's all I'll say about that, too."]],
 "Go on. My memory's terrible today."],
21:["(The club is dim, candles on every table, and grey ash on the windows.) The mountain went up. Power's out. I've lit the candles and opened the bar. People don't want to be alone tonight. Today. Whatever this is.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","Leo's here, Rita's here. Arto and Raili came in grey from head to foot. Everyone I know is accounted for. Leo's playing in the dark. Listen."],
  ["You're giving drinks away.","Today, everything's on the house. It's a thing my father taught me. You don't charge people for surviving."],
  ["How's the piano?","Fell off its blocks in the shake. Rita's singing with no piano at all. Just Leo and her and candles. It's the best set we've ever done."]],
 "Stay. There's a seat by Arto. Nobody's going anywhere."],
22:["(Sid is in a grey waistcoat, sweeping ash off the stage.) The day after. The club's grey, I'm grey, and the piano's on the floor with a broken leg. But we're open.",[
  ["Is the piano ruined?","It's broken, not dead. Like a lot of people today. A carpenter from the mill's coming to fix the leg. Then the tuner from Helsinki. If he still dares to come."],
  ["How's everyone?","Shaken. Leo hasn't stopped playing since yesterday. Rita's asleep in the back booth. Raili's helping me sweep. She's very good with a broom."],
  ["Business must be terrible.","Business is the best it's ever been. Everyone wants to be somewhere warm with other people. A volcano's very good for jazz."]],
 "Go on. Wipe your shoes, if you can."],
23:["Rita and Leo had breakfast together. At noon. At my bar. Leo said six words. I counted. That's a record.",[
  ["What did he say?","'Pass the salt.' 'Thank you.' 'Coffee?' 'Yes.' Then nothing for an hour. Then, 'You sing beautifully.' That's six, or nine, depending how you count."],
  ["And what did Rita say?","She said, 'I know.' And then she laughed, and took his hand for a second. Leo went pink to the ears. Then he went off to play."],
  ["You're a romantic, Sid.","I run a jazz club. You can't do that and not be. Don't spread it about. I've a reputation for seeing nothing."]],
 "Go on. Some days are good days. Even with ash."],
24:["(The piano is back on its legs, mended with fresh pine.) The mill sent a carpenter. Dagny charged me double and called it a volcano surcharge. But it's standing.",[
  ["Dagny from the lumber mill?","Tight as a drum, Dagny. She drinks one gin a month in here, and she makes it last two sets. But her timber's good."],
  ["How does it sound?","Like a goose with a broken leg. The tuner's still in Helsinki. Rita says she'll sing over it. Leo says nothing. He's happy."],
  ["What's the back booth gossip?","There isn't any. There's never any. (He looks at the far booth, which is empty.) But it's been busy all week. A lot of people deciding things in whispers."]],
 "Go on. Keep your voice down near the booths."],
25:["(Sid is reading a telegram.) The tuner. He's coming. He's on tomorrow's boat from Helsinki. Says he's always wanted to see a volcano, and he'll tune a piano while he's at it.",[
  ["At last!","At last. Thirty years I've had that piano, and it's never been so out of tune. Mind you, the island's never been so out of tune either."],
  ["What will Rita sing first?","She hasn't said. Leo wants her to sing his song. She wants to sing something that's never been sung on this island. I want to sell a lot of whisky."],
  ["Will the club stay open, whatever happens?","Whatever happens. Kings, commissars, oil men or monks: they all like a drink and a tune after dark."]],
 "Go on. Come by tomorrow and hear the piano sing."],
26:["(A thin, nervous man is bent over the piano, plinking one note at a time.) That's the tuner. Mr Aalto, from Helsinki. He's been at it since noon. He says middle C is 'emotionally damaged'.",[
  ["Can he fix it?","He says so. He says it'll take until midnight, and he wants paying in whisky and a boat home before the volcano 'does anything'. I've agreed to both."],
  ["What's the news in Frostholm?","Dario wants to buy the spa on the mountain, they say, and he's been in the back booth all week with men who know about money. Not that I've noticed."],
  ["Is Rita ready to sing?","She's been at the bar since six, waiting for the piano to be good enough for her. She's very patient. She's also on her third coffee with something in it."]],
 "Go on. Come back after midnight. The piano will be singing."],
27:["(Sid looks very happy.) The piano's tuned. Rita sang last night like she was sixteen. Leo cried. Mr Aalto cried. I cried. I've told everyone I had something in my eye.",[
  ["What did she sing?","A song she wrote herself, she said, years ago, in Stockholm. About a girl who got on a boat. It's the first time she's ever said anything about Stockholm."],
  ["How's Leo?","Asleep in the back booth, holding his saxophone like a teddy bear. I'm letting him sleep. He's earned it."],
  ["Will you get the record now?","Midnight at the Aurora? I've given up on it. I've got my own night now. That'll do me."]],
 "Go on. My eye's still got something in it."],
28:["(Sid is in the burgundy waistcoat, though it's not Tuesday.) The whole island's out on the streets. Rallies, marches, parties. And all of them will end up here tonight, wanting to forget it.",[
  ["Burgundy on a Saturday?","For cheering up. Everybody needs it today. Even me."],
  ["Who's booked the back booths?","Every one of them. Initials only. Some of the initials are the same people as last week, pretending to be other people. I'll serve them all the same."],
  ["What's your hope for the island?","That there's still a Midnight Note at the end of it, and still people who want to come. That's all a jazz club ever hopes for."]],
 "Go on. Come back tonight, if you can. It'll be a night."],
29:["(The club's quiet, and Sid is listening to Leo play something new.) Sunday. Leo's written a new song. He says it's about the island. It's slow, and then it gets very fast, and then it stops.",[
  ["Does it have a name?","'September.' He says he'll finish it when the month's done. He doesn't know how it ends yet. None of us do."],
  ["Is Rita staying on the island?","She says she's thinking. People who come off boats always think about going back on one. But she's bought new curtains for her flat. Women don't buy curtains for places they're leaving."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Open at six, as always. Whatever's decided, Frostholm will want a drink by sundown."]],
 "Go on. Get some sleep. Tomorrow's a long night."],
30:["(Sid is in the black waistcoat again, the one for funerals and opening nights.) The last day. Black, because I don't know yet whether it's an ending or a beginning. It works for both.",[
  ["What's the plan for tonight?","Leo's going to play his September song, all the way to the end. Rita's going to sing. And I'm going to stand behind the bar and see nothing."],
  ["Will you miss this month?","I'll remember it. Like 1959. Like a night with a band that never comes back. (He pours two small whiskies.) To September."],
  ["Thank you, Sid.","For what? I never saw you. I never heard a word you said. (He smiles.) That's how you'll know I liked you."]],
 "Go on. Come back tonight. The best set's the last one."],
};
// Signe, lighthouse keeper at Eastbrook Harbour (administrators, no faction). Threads: twenty years of the lamp, every boat she logs by day or night, the tide charts, lamp oil and the harbour customs shed, her solitude and her cat-less loneliness.
DAILY['Signe']={
4:["(Signe is polishing a brass lens cover.) Visitors are rare. The road's just opened, I hear. You'll be the reporter. Mind the stair, it's steeper than it looks. Two hundred and twelve steps, if you're counting.",[
  ["Do you count them?","Every night, going up. It's my evening prayer."],
  ["Twenty years in a lighthouse. Don't you get lonely?","I've the lamp, the sea and the gulls. The gulls are poor conversationalists, but loyal."],
  ["What do you see from up there?","Everything that moves on the water. Boats, weather, and the odd seal with opinions."]],
 "Take care on the way down. The stair has a view of its own."],
5:["(Signe sets a ledger on the table.) Every boat that passes, I log. Name, direction, hour. Twenty years of them. Nobody's ever read it, but it's all there.",[
  ["Could I read it?","You could. It's dull. Page after page of 'fishing boat, west, dusk.'"],
  ["Has anything interesting come in?","A great many things come in, and nothing's declared. But a keeper learns what a boat's carrying by how deep it sits."],
  ["Does anyone ask you about them?","Ole, the harbourmaster at Frostholm. He phones every week. He's afraid I'll miss a barrel."]],
 "Mind the oil on the floor. It's slippery."],
6:["(Signe trims a wick.) The lamp wants cleaning every week. The lens, the chimney, the little brass cogs. It's a clock with a flame in it, and I'm its servant.",[
  ["Does it ever go out?","Never in twenty years. I'd rather go out myself."],
  ["Who taught you the work?","The keeper before me, an old man called Ansgar. He said a lighthouse is a promise. I've kept it."],
  ["What's the hardest part?","The storms. When the wind howls, I sit by the lamp and sing. Badly, to keep the dark off."]],
 "Don't touch the lens. It's older than I am."],
7:["(Signe peers through a telescope.) A big tanker off the north coast, sitting low and riding quiet. Diversified Petroleum's, from the flag. It's not due at Frostholm till next week.",[
  ["Is that unusual?","Everything's unusual at the moment. The roads are open, and the sea's opening too."],
  ["Does it worry you?","A keeper worries about shoals and storms, not oil. But a quiet tanker's a worry of its own."],
  ["Can you see the harbour from here?","Eastbrook, yes. Frostholm's too far. I rely on Ole's phone calls and my own suspicion."]],
 "Mind the telescope. It's got a mind of its own."],
8:["(Signe breaks a biscuit in half.) Sunday, and the sea's like glass. The monastery bells carry across the water, thin and high. I light the lamp early, so the boats can hear it too.",[
  ["Do the boats hear a lamp?","Not the lamp. But they know it's there. It's the feeling that matters."],
  ["Are you religious?","I keep a light. That's prayer enough."],
  ["Do you get visitors on Sundays?","Fishermen, sometimes. They bring a fish, and I give them a flask of tea. It's an old trade."]],
 "Have a biscuit. They're stale, but so is the island."],
9:["(Signe winds a clock.) Tomorrow's the king's funeral. I've hung a black ribbon on the door. And I'll sound the foghorn at noon, the way they do for kings. Three long blasts.",[
  ["Did you know him?","He came here once, as a young man. He climbed all the steps and told me he'd never seen anything so lonely. I told him it was a compliment."],
  ["Will anyone hear the horn?","The whole harbour. It's the only way I can attend."],
  ["Is that allowed?","Ole telephoned to say it's against the rules. I said it's against the rules not to."]],
 "Cover your ears at noon. It's louder than it looks."],
10:["(Signe is in her good coat.) I've sounded the horn, three times. It rolled over the water and came back. A fisherman took his cap off, and a seal looked up. That was the funeral, for me.",[
  ["It sounds beautiful.","It's the loneliest sound I know. That's why it fits."],
  ["How is the harbour today?","Still. Everyone's listening to the radio, in their boats and on the quay."],
  ["Will you go to the chapel?","I can't leave the lamp. But the king would understand. He knew what it is to be kept."]],
 "A good day, as such days go. Mind the wind."],
11:["(Signe hangs her coat on a peg.) The harbour's back to work. Cargo, nets, and shouting. It's good to hear it. A harbour should be loud, and a lighthouse should be quiet.",[
  ["What's coming in today?","Timber, fish, and one boat I don't recognise. It flew no flag, and kept its lights low."],
  ["Did you log it?","Of course. 'Unknown vessel, south-east, mid-afternoon.' The ledger doesn't judge."],
  ["Do you tell anyone?","Ole. He telephones, and I tell. He logs it too, and says nothing. We're a very quiet network."]],
 "If you see an unknown boat, write it down."],
12:["(Signe adjusts a dial.) A letter from the Bureau. Bertil. He writes to ask whether my lamp oil receipts are in order. Twenty years of oil, and now they're curious.",[
  ["Are they in order?","I've kept every one. In a biscuit tin. Bertil seemed surprised."],
  ["Does the Bureau trouble you?","Once a year. It's never urgent, and never over."],
  ["What would you do without the lamp?","I'd be a very quiet woman by the sea. Not much different, really."]],
 "Don't spill the oil. It's the only thing I can't replace."],
13:["(Signe scans the horizon.) Tomorrow's the great day, and the sea's strangely calm. A big yacht's anchored off the point, white as a wedding cake. Nobody knows whose it is.",[
  ["Do you know who owns it?","I've a guess. I'll keep it to myself, until I'm sure."],
  ["Is it for the gathering?","Possibly. Everything's for the gathering, at the moment."],
  ["Does it worry you?","A boat that big, so quiet, makes a keeper think. But I'll watch."]],
 "Come back and tell me what you see tomorrow."],
14:["(Signe is up in the lamp room.) The big day, and the harbour's empty. Every boat's tied up. Everyone's ashore, listening. I've never seen the water so still.",[
  ["Is it eerie?","A little. Like a held breath. But I like the quiet. It lets me think."],
  ["What do you see?","Smoke, from the town. Flags, in the square. The tiny figures of people deciding things."],
  ["Do you feel left out?","Not at all. I've the best seat in the house."]],
 "If anything happens, I'll see it first."],
15:["(Signe taps a barometer.) The glass is falling, and the swell's from the north. There's weather on the way. And something else, but I can't put my finger on it.",[
  ["What do you mean?","The sea has moods, and this is a new one. Edgy. Like a cat before a storm."],
  ["Is it just weather?","It might be. Or it might be the island, breathing."],
  ["Will you be all right?","I've a lamp, a stove, and a cup of tea. What could go wrong?"]],
 "Batten down your hatches. Metaphorically."],
16:["(Signe puts on her glasses.) A debate tonight, in the town, so I'm told. The radio's on in the shed. I hear the Party's got the better speaker, but the Democrats have the better biscuits.",[
  ["Whom do you favour?","Whoever keeps the harbour open and the oil in the lamp."],
  ["Do you vote?","Every time. By post, in pencil. It's a keeper's privilege."],
  ["Will you listen?","With one ear. The other's on the sea."]],
 "If the debate's dull, count the waves."],
17:["(Signe holds the rail.) A tremor this morning, and the lens trembled. The whole tower hummed. Like a bell, very faintly. I've never felt it before.",[
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm in a tall stone tower on a rock. It's one of the safer places to be."],
  ["Did the gulls notice?","They left. Every one. I've never seen the roof so empty."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","Probably. It's an old mountain with a grievance. I'll watch the north."]],
 "If the lamp wobbles, hold on to it. And to me."],
18:["(Signe sniffs the wind.) Sulphur. Faintly, from the north. And the water's warmer than it should be, by a degree. The fish are restless. The fishermen are quiet.",[
  ["Is that a bad sign?","It's a sign. Whether it's bad depends on the mountain."],
  ["Do you write it down?","Everything. 'Tuesday, sulphur on the wind, water warm.' It might be useful later."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a lighthouse keeper. I'm paid to worry on behalf of everyone else."]],
 "Keep your eyes on the north. And your coat on."],
19:["(Signe peers at a distant light.) A party boat, off the harbour, with lanterns and music. A masquerade, I'm told. A great many masks, and one very tall man in a false moustache.",[
  ["Who was the tall man?","A prince, if I'm any judge of posture. He walked like a man who's never carried his own coat."],
  ["Did you log it?","'Pleasure vessel, music, lanterns.' The moustache didn't fit the form."],
  ["Is it a secret?","The sea keeps nothing secret. It just doesn't tell."]],
 "Wave to the boat, if you see it. They like attention."],
20:["(Signe holds a lamp steady.) A small boat at the quay, last night, long after dark. Two men and a crate. They looked at the lighthouse, and I looked back. They left in a hurry.",[
  ["Do you know who they were?","Not by name. By their boots, I'd say Kingsgrad. By their manners, no one you'd invite."],
  ["Did you tell the police?","Sergeant Hanna. She said she'd note it. She's one woman with a very big island."],
  ["Is it the Syndicate?","I couldn't say. But they know my light. And my light knows them."]],
 "I never saw a thing. And I'd hate to see it again."],
21:["(Signe has her coat over her head.) It's gone up. The whole north is red. The cloud's rising, like a great black tree. I've never seen the like.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. The lava's far from the towns. We were lucky."],
  ["What do you see?","A glow, and a column of smoke. And the sea, strangely calm. It's very beautiful, and I hate it."],
  ["Are you staying?","Of course. A lighthouse in a crisis is a lighthouse. It needs its keeper."]],
 "Cover your face. The ash will get in your eyes."],
22:["(Signe wipes ash from the lens.) A grey snow, all over the tower, all over the lamp. I've been wiping since dawn, and it settles as fast as I clean it.",[
  ["Can the boats see the light?","Dimly. I've lit a second lamp, and I'm ringing the bell. They'll find their way."],
  ["Is the harbour busy?","Boats coming in, loaded with families. They've left the north, and they've nowhere else to go."],
  ["Are you coping?","I've two lamps, a bell and a pot of tea. I've done more with less."]],
 "Don't sit on the rail. It's grey."],
23:["(Signe listens at the stair.) The tower hums, now and then. A deep note, from the rock below. It frightens the gulls, but I find it rather comforting.",[
  ["Is it an aftershock?","It's the island, getting used to itself. It'll quieten soon."],
  ["Any news on the water?","A bad smell off a tanker, and a lot of whispers. Something went wrong at an oil rig, I'm told."],
  ["Does it concern you?","Anything on the water concerns me. It's the job."]],
 "Keep one hand on the rail. It's a good habit."],
24:["(Signe signals with a lantern.) A string of small boats leaving for the north, with monks and crates. A retreat to the peaks, I've heard. They asked for a light to guide them, so I've given them one.",[
  ["Are the monks good sailors?","Dreadful. One was sick over the side before he'd left the quay. But they sing beautifully while they row."],
  ["Is it wise to go north now?","I'd not choose it. But faith has its own charts, and I don't argue with them."],
  ["Do you ever wish you could go too?","Somewhere? Yes. Away from the lamp? No. I'm bolted to this rock, and glad."]],
 "Fair winds to them. They'll need them."],
25:["(Signe is eating a cold pie.) A pleasure launch came in this morning with a young woman in a blue scarf and a very grave gentleman. The princess, I think, and a Democrat. They stood on the quay and looked at the lamp.",[
  ["Did they come up?","She did. Climbed every step, and asked how I stay sane. I said, I'm not sure I do."],
  ["What did she say?","That she'd like a life as plain as mine. I told her to be careful what she wished for."],
  ["Is she on the Democrats' side?","She's on her own side. It's a very small party, and a very good one."]],
 "She left a scarf pin on the rail. I'll keep it safe."],
26:["(Signe checks the ledger.) Seven boats today, and every one with a flag. Cola, oil, Party red. It's like a regatta, but nobody's smiling.",[
  ["Is anyone fishing?","Not a soul. The fish must be laughing."],
  ["What are they all doing?","Delivering things. Crates, mostly. I've given up guessing what's in them."],
  ["Will you log them all?","Every one. If anyone asks later, I'll have it in black and white."]],
 "Mind the wet rope on the quay. It's treacherous."],
27:["(Signe polishes a brass rail.) A night of heavy cloud ahead, and the lamp must be right. Bertil telephoned to say an inspector may call. In twenty years nobody's inspected me, and now they want to see the lamp.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I'm a keeper. I'm always ready. But I'll wear my good cardigan."],
  ["What would an inspector look for?","Dust, mostly. And a better excuse for the oil receipts."],
  ["Do you mind?","Not at all. It's good to be noticed, after twenty years."]],
 "Dust the stairs for me if you pass. Please."],
28:["(Signe stands at the window.) Every boat on the island seems to be moving today. Flags, banners, horns. I can hear a band playing, somewhere in Kingsgrad, over the water.",[
  ["It sounds festive.","It sounds loud. And a bit desperate, like a man whistling in the dark."],
  ["Will you join in?","I'll sound the horn, once, at noon. For the island, and for the king."],
  ["What do you think of the factions?","I think a lighthouse is the only thing on Sarivalto that doesn't take sides. It simply shows the way."]],
 "Keep your eyes on the horizon. It helps."],
29:["(Signe lights the lamp early.) Tomorrow's the last day, and the sky's the colour of a bruise. I've polished every lens, and filled every tank. Whatever comes, the light will be on.",[
  ["Are you hopeful?","I'm a lighthouse keeper. Hope is my stock in trade."],
  ["What would you like to see?","A calm sea, and a quiet shore. And the boats coming home."],
  ["Any fears?","Only that I'll run out of oil. The rest I can handle."]],
 "Go home safely. I'll keep the lamp burning."],
30:["(Signe has a flask and a blanket.) The last day, and the sea's flat as a plate. I've logged every boat, polished every lens, and said my prayers. Now it's the island's turn.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Steady. A lighthouse doesn't change, whoever's king. That's the comfort of it."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The horn for the king, the ash, and a reporter who climbed all two hundred and twelve steps."],
  ["Any last words?","Look for the light when it's dark. It'll be there."]],
 "Thank you for the visit. Mind the stair."],
};
// Sirpa, out of work in Saltwick since the fish plant cut its hours (The Unemployed). Threads: the gossip from her bench on the
// main street (Lucia, Kaisa, Hannu, the Party), waiting for the plant to bring back the hours, the scarf she's knitting for Ray at
// the bar (and pretending she isn't), and looking out for Tiina, who's still grieving her husband.
DAILY['Sirpa']={
1:["(Sirpa looks you up and down.) Well! You came in on Tom's boat this morning. Don't look like that: I see everything that comes up this street. Sirpa. And you're the reporter.",[
  ["News travels fast here.","News is all we've got, love. Fish and news. And the fish plant's gone short hours, so mostly news."],
  ["What's the gossip today?","Anna's had the Party lights on since five, Lucia came home at three in the morning with sand on her shoes, and Hannu's selling last week's paper again."],
  ["Do you ever do anything else?","I knit. I watch. I talk. Three things, and I'm good at all of them. More than the plant manager can say."]],
 "Off you go. I'll know where you went by teatime anyway."],
2:["(Sirpa leans in.) Road to Kingsgrad's open. Half the village went up on the first tram like it was Christmas. Not me. What's Kingsgrad got that I haven't seen?",[
  ["The palace, for one.","The palace has got a dead king and a lot of people pretending to be sad. We've got that here, and it's cheaper."],
  ["Who went up?","Mike, to buy boots. Olga, to sell fish. And Lucia, to do whatever Lucia does. You don't ask Lucia."],
  ["Why don't you go?","Tram fare's a day's dinner. And the bench would miss me."]],
 "If you're going up, bring back some gossip. Fair's fair."],
3:["(Sirpa is knitting something red, fast.) Don't look at it. It's nothing. It's a scarf. For nobody.",[
  ["Who's nobody?","Nobody who pours drinks. Nobody with a moustache. Leave it, will you."],
  ["Ray, from the bar?","(She goes pink.) Tiina told you. I'll kill her. He's cold, that's all. He stands in the door every night with no scarf. It's a public service."],
  ["Lovely colour.","It is, isn't it? Communist red. Anna gave me the wool. Don't tell her who it's for."]],
 "Not a word to Ray. Or to Tiina. Especially Tiina."],
4:["(Sirpa nods down the street.) The plant manager went past at eight. Didn't look at us. When he doesn't look at us it means no more hours this week.",[
  ["How long since the cut?","Since June. Kate and Bob kept their shifts. The rest of us were 'surplus'. I've never been surplus before. I quite liked being plus."],
  ["Who decides the hours?","The manager, the fish, and the people in Kingsgrad who buy the fish. Mostly the people in Kingsgrad."],
  ["You could work elsewhere.","Where? Olga doesn't need anyone, Helmi barely sells a hat. Saltwick's a small pond, love, and it's all fished out."]],
 "Next time the manager goes past, I'll wave. Give him something to think about."],
5:["(Sirpa lowers her voice.) Tiina had a bad night. Her Matti's birthday would've been this week. Go gently if you talk to her.",[
  ["What happened to Matti?","Boat went down last season, in fog off the point. Tiina's never believed it was the weather. Don't you ask her about it, mind."],
  ["You look after her.","Somebody has to. She looks after me too, in her way. Tells me when my knitting's lumpy."],
  ["Is she all right?","She's Tiina. She'll sit here, say nothing, and watch the street like she's waiting for the boat to come round the corner."]],
 "Be kind to her. That's all I'm asking."],
6:["(Sirpa is beaming.) Kaisa played my request last night! On Radio Sarivalto! 'For Sirpa on the bench, keep your chin up.' Half the block heard it.",[
  ["What did you request?","A slow one. Don't ask what. A woman's allowed a secret. (She glances at the bar.)"],
  ["Kaisa lives in your block?","Second floor. Comes home at all hours with a thermos and a headache. Lovely girl, terrible taste in men."],
  ["Famous now, then.","Saltwick famous. That means Olga knows, Hannu knows, and the cat knows. Same thing."]],
 "Keep your radio on. You never know who's sending a message."],
7:["(Sirpa sniffs.) Anna's been round knocking on doors. Wants everyone at a Party meeting. Free tea and a lecture about the means of production.",[
  ["Will you go?","For the tea. I'll sit at the back and knit through the lecture. Anna doesn't mind, as long as I clap."],
  ["Do you like the Communists?","I like Helen. She got my sister's back pay out of the plant. Anna I'm less sure of. She looks at you like she's counting you."],
  ["The means of production?","The means of production is a fish plant that won't give me any hours. I could tell her that for free."]],
 "If they're giving out biscuits as well, I'll save you one."],
8:["(Sirpa points her needle at you.) Did you hear about the oil rig? One of their boats came into West Saltwick this morning in a tearing hurry. Something's gone wrong out there.",[
  ["What have you heard?","Something burst. Oil everywhere, the boys rushing about. Nobody's saying more than that. The oil company never says anything."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. But the men off that boat didn't stop for a drink at Ray's, and oil men always stop for a drink at Ray's."],
  ["The oil company's not popular here.","They're from Frostholm, love. Glass towers and shiny shoes. They come down here to buy fish and look at us like we're scenery."]],
 "If the sea goes black, the fish go with it. Then where are we."],
9:["(Sirpa holds up her knitting.) Half a scarf. Ray asked what it was. I said it was a tea cosy for a very long teapot.",[
  ["Did he believe you?","He said 'right'. Ray says 'right' to everything. That man could hear the world was ending and say 'right' and polish a glass."],
  ["Why not just tell him?","At my age? He'd laugh. Or worse, he'd be kind about it. Kind's worse."],
  ["Long teapot, then.","The longest in Saltwick. He'll get it at Christmas, if I'm brave. Or I'll wear it myself."]],
 "Not a word, now. It's a teapot."],
10:["(Sirpa has a black ribbon pinned to her coat.) The king's being buried. Everyone who matters is at the palace. So nobody here, then.",[
  ["You're wearing a ribbon.","Tiina made it. She's good with her hands. The king was a decent old thing, for a king. He came here once, opened the plant. That's gone well."],
  ["Who do you want next?","Someone who knows where Saltwick is. That rules out most of them."],
  ["Want to hear about the funeral?","Oh, go on. Who wore what? Who cried? Did anybody fall over? (She leans in.)"]],
 "Tell me who was there. All of them."],
11:["(Sirpa chuckles.) Saw Father Thyme's people walking down to the beach this morning in their robes. Off to save Pat, I expect.",[
  ["Pat needs saving?","Pat needs a coat, a bath and a hot meal. He wants something else. The Church'll give him a hymn. Somebody'll give him what he wants."],
  ["Do you know Pat?","Everyone knows Pat. Tiina and I take him a flask sometimes. He tells us stories about the steamer and swears blind he cooked for the king."],
  ["What does Pat want?","(She laughs.) A drink and the green stuff, love, what else? Ask the farm. Tiina's cousin works out there."]],
 "If you see Pat, tell him the flask's coming Friday."],
12:["(Sirpa is watching someone go into the Party HQ.) That's the third man in a suit today. Kingsgrad suits, not Saltwick. Something's cooking at Anna's.",[
  ["What's Anna planning?","She doesn't tell the likes of me. But she bought twenty tins of paint at Helmi's last week. Red, I'd guess."],
  ["You watch closely.","I've got nothing else to do, love. Give me a job and I'll stop watching. Until then, I see everything."],
  ["Maybe they're just visiting.","Nobody 'just visits' Saltwick. Not in a suit. Not in September."]],
 "I'll let you know if they come out with anything heavy."],
13:["(Sirpa sniffs.) Word is the cola people are giving a free bottle to everyone on the island today. Free! I don't trust free.",[
  ["Take one, it's free.","That's how they get you. First it's free, then you can't do without it, then it's half your dole. I've seen it with cigarettes."],
  ["Would Tiina drink it?","She won't touch it. Says it tastes of the bottom of a harbour. She's not wrong, but it's a nice harbour."],
  ["Why give it away?","Because they want us drinking theirs and not whatever cola Anna keeps talking about. Ray says the Party wants one of its own. Ray says a lot, for a man who says 'right'."]],
 "If they come down this street with a crate, I'll take two. For Tiina."],
14:["(Sirpa has her best coat on.) The big day! Everybody's going. Tiina says it's a lot of fuss. I say a lot of fuss is what this island needs.",[
  ["What do you think will happen?","Everybody in one place, saying what they want. That's either a party or a fight. I've been to both in Saltwick."],
  ["You look smart.","Thank you, love. Helmi's finest, nineteen seventy-two. Ray said 'right'. I'm choosing to take that as a compliment."],
  ["Will Tiina come?","She'll come. She'll stand at the back with her arms folded and say it was rubbish. That's how Tiina enjoys things."]],
 "Come on, then. We'll be late for history."],
15:["(Sirpa is still talking about yesterday.) I haven't stopped thinking about it. All those people in one place. I didn't know the island had so many faces.",[
  ["What stayed with you?","The children. All of them standing on walls to see. They'll remember it when we're gone. I hope they remember something good."],
  ["Did you talk to anyone?","Everybody! A woman from Eastbrook told me her mill's as bad as our plant. Nice to know misery's island-wide."],
  ["Back to the bench now?","Back to the bench. History's very nice, but somebody's got to keep an eye on Hannu's prices."]],
 "Same time tomorrow, love. The street won't watch itself."],
16:["(Sirpa looks thoughtful.) The Party and the Democrats are debating today. Anna against Erik. Ray's put the radio on at the bar for it.",[
  ["Who do you want to win?","Whoever promises to open the plant. Neither of them will. They'll promise freedom and the people's will. Can't fry freedom."],
  ["Will you listen?","With half an ear. The other half's for the scarf. I've dropped three stitches already, thinking about politics."],
  ["How's the scarf?","Two feet longer than Ray. I may have overdone it. He can wrap it round twice and still trip on it."]],
 "If anyone says 'fish plant' on the radio, I'll cheer."],
17:["(Sirpa grips the bench.) Did you feel that? The whole bench went. Tiina's tea went in her lap. She swore like a fisherman.",[
  ["An earthquake?","A little one. Old Hannu says it's the mountain waking up. Hannu also says the moon landing was filmed in Frostholm."],
  ["Is everyone all right?","A few cups broke at Olga's. And Ray's bottles rattled, and he said 'right' very quietly. I've never seen him rattled before."],
  ["Should we worry?","Probably not. But I'm keeping my good coat by the door, just in case."]],
 "If the ground goes again, hold on to something. Not me."],
18:["(Sirpa nods north.) There's steam coming off the volcano. You can see it from the end of the pier. Kaisa says on the radio it's 'perfectly normal'.",[
  ["Is it normal?","My grandmother lived ninety years on this island and never once saw that mountain steam. So no, love."],
  ["What are people saying?","Half the street's packing a bag, half's laughing at them. I'm in the middle. I've packed a small bag and I'm laughing nervously."],
  ["What's in the bag?","My good coat, a tin of biscuits and Ray's scarf. Priorities."]],
 "Keep an eye on the north. And on the biscuits."],
19:["(Sirpa smirks.) Anna's out on the street signing people up for the Party. Free soup and a red badge. Half of Saltwick's thinking about it.",[
  ["Are you thinking about it?","I'm thinking about the soup. The badge I'd have to think harder about. Anna counts badges like other people count money."],
  ["Who's joined, then?","I couldn't possibly say. (She can, and is longing to.) Let's just say there's men in this street practising their clenched fists in the shop windows."],
  ["What would Tiina say?","Tiina would say 'what's in it for them?' She says it about everyone. She's usually right."]],
 "Soup's soup, love. Politics comes after lunch."],
20:["(Sirpa has her knitting in her lap but isn't knitting.) Ray asked me to sit at the bar last night, not the table. The bar. Do you think that means anything?",[
  ["It might mean he likes you.","Or it might mean the table wobbles. Men are very hard to read when they only say 'right'."],
  ["What did you talk about?","The weather, the mountain, the price of herring. Then he gave me the last of the good whisky and didn't charge. (She looks at her needles.)"],
  ["Give him the scarf.","It's not finished. (She looks at it.) It's been finished a week. I keep adding rows so I don't have to give it."]],
 "Don't laugh. I'm too old for this and I know it."],
21:["(Sirpa's hair is grey with ash and she's coughing.) The volcano's gone up! I saw it from the bench: a great black cloud, and fire under it. Are you all right?",[
  ["I'm fine. Are you?","I'm alive, Tiina's alive, Ray's alive. Everybody's alive, they're saying. The fire went north, thank God."],
  ["What's Saltwick like?","Grey. Every roof, every boat. Olga's sweeping her step and it's coming back faster than she sweeps."],
  ["Was it frightening?","I've never been so frightened. Tiina held my hand, and Tiina never holds anyone's hand. That's how frightened."]],
 "Get inside, love. Wet a cloth and breathe through it."],
22:["(Sirpa is sweeping ash off the bench with her sleeve.) Everyone's sweeping. The whole village. It's the most work Saltwick's seen since June.",[
  ["Any jobs going in the clean-up?","Mike's paying lads to shovel the harbour clear. I asked. He said 'not for ladies'. I said 'watch me'."],
  ["How's the plant?","Shut. Ash in the machines. Kate's beside herself. So now nobody's got hours, which is fair, I suppose, in a horrid way."],
  ["You're very cheerful.","When the sky falls in and you're still here, you're cheerful. It's that or crying, and I've done my crying."]],
 "Keep your mouth covered. That stuff gets everywhere."],
23:["(Sirpa is smiling to herself.) I gave Ray the scarf. Last night. After the mountain, I thought, what am I waiting for? Another volcano?",[
  ["What did he say?","He said 'right'. Then he put it on. Then he wore it all night, behind the bar, in the warm. (She laughs.)"],
  ["Good for you.","At my age. Honestly. Tiina says I'm a disgrace and she's never been happier for anyone."],
  ["Did he like the length?","He wrapped it round three times and still had enough to tuck in his belt. He said it was 'very thorough'."]],
 "Don't you dare put that in your paper."],
24:["(Sirpa jumps as the ground trembles.) Another aftershock. My nerves are shot. Every time a lorry goes past I think it's the mountain.",[
  ["How's Tiina coping?","Better than me. She's quieter, mind. She says it was like the night Matti's boat didn't come in: waiting to see who's missing."],
  ["Ray must be a comfort.","(She smiles.) He came round to check on the block. He said 'right' to everybody, and then sat with me an hour. Not saying anything."],
  ["Will the plant reopen?","When the ash is out of the machines. Kate says next week. She's said next week since June, but this time she means it."]],
 "Hold on to the railings on the stairs. They're shaking loose."],
25:["(Sirpa is gossiping at full speed.) You've missed everything! Lucia's had a black car outside the block twice this week, and Kaisa's been crying in the stairwell.",[
  ["Why is Kaisa crying?","A man, I expect. With Kaisa it's always a man or a record. This time I'd say a man. Nobody cries like that over a record."],
  ["Whose black car?","You don't ask whose car it is when Lucia's getting out of it. You look at your knitting very hard."],
  ["You're back to your old self.","I'm back on the bench. The mountain stopped me for a day. It'll take more than a volcano to keep me quiet."]],
 "I'll have more by tomorrow. I always do."],
26:["(Sirpa holds up a letter.) The plant's sent letters. Hours back from Monday, for some of us. I'm one of the some. I've read it nine times.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you, love. Three days a week, gutting herring. It's not glamorous. I could kiss the manager. I won't."],
  ["What about Tiina?","She's not one of the some. She says she's glad for me. She is, too. That's what makes it hard."],
  ["Will you miss the bench?","Two days a week I'll still be here. And the bench'll keep. Tiina'll keep it warm."]],
 "Monday I'm a working woman. Mind your manners."],
27:["(Sirpa looks worried.) Tiina's been funny since my letter. Quiet. Quieter than usual, which is very quiet.",[
  ["Is she jealous?","No. Tiina's not built that way. She's scared of sitting here alone. With Matti gone, I'm all the company she's got."],
  ["Can anyone help her?","Tom's talking about giving her all his winter nets to mend, for cash. If he means it. Fishermen say a lot of things over a pint."],
  ["You'll still see her.","Of course I will. Every evening at Ray's. But it's not the same, is it, when one of you has somewhere to be."]],
 "Be nice to her if you see her. She'll pretend she doesn't need it."],
28:["(Sirpa is wide-eyed.) Everybody's doing something today. Red flags, cola men, rallies, the palace in a state. Ray's shut the bar shutters at noon.",[
  ["Are you worried?","A bit. When everyone wants something on the same day, somebody doesn't get it. And they're never happy."],
  ["What's the gossip?","Anna's not been to bed. Helen's been up and down the street like a hen. Lucia's sitting on her step watching. Nobody's watching Lucia."],
  ["What do you want for the island?","Hours at the plant, a scarf on Ray, and nobody hurt. If any of the parties can promise that, they can have my vote."]],
 "Keep your head down today, love. Today of all days."],
29:["(Sirpa is knitting again: something blue.) Don't ask. It's for Tiina. A hat. She needs something new. Something that isn't black.",[
  ["That's kind.","She's worn black since last season. A bit of blue won't bring Matti back, but it might bring Tiina back a bit."],
  ["Tomorrow's the big day.","Everybody says so. All the parties, all the fuss, settled. I'll believe it when Hannu's paper says so, and even then I'll check."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","Sit on this bench with Tiina, like always. Whatever happens to the island, somebody's got to watch it happen."]],
 "Don't tell Tiina about the hat. It's a surprise."],
30:["(Sirpa and Tiina are sitting close together on the bench.) Last day of the month. The day it all gets decided, they say. We've got the best seat on the island.",[
  ["How do you feel?","Like the night before a wedding. Not mine. Somebody else's, where you don't know if the groom's going to turn up."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That whoever's in charge tomorrow walks down this street and looks at us. Just looks. That'd be a start."],
  ["It's been quite a month.","A king buried, a mountain blown, a scarf given. (She grins.) I'll be telling this one for years, love."]],
 "Whatever happens, there'll be tea at Ray's after. Come along."],
};
// Solveig, sub-editor at the Eastbrook Gazette (The Reporters). Threads: her suspicion of the foreign journalist (days 4 to 8; from
// day 9, after Cover Blown, a wry 'very bad memory' that fits talking her round or paying her off), her eleven years on Fleet Street
// and coming home for her mother's funeral, subbing Nora's and Henrik's copy, the Gazette crossword she sets, her neighbours in the
// Timber Lofts.
DAILY['Solveig']={
4:["(Solveig doesn't get up. She's marking a proof with a blue pencil.) Solveig. I sub the Gazette: I cut what Nora writes and fix what Henrik writes. You'll be the one from London. Which paper?",[
  ["A daily. You won't have read it.","I read all of them, for eleven years, on the night train home. Try me another day."],
  ["What does a sub-editor do?","Takes a reporter's best sentence out and puts a full stop where it was. Then writes the headline, and gets the blame for it."],
  ["You were in London?",["Eleven years on Fleet Street. The subs' desk, then the night editor's chair, then the train home.",[
    ["Why come back?","My mother was ill last winter. I came home for her funeral in the spring, and the Gazette needed a sub. That's the whole story. I've cut longer ones."],
    ["Do you miss it?","The pubs. Not the people. Well. Some of the people."]]]]],
 "Don't split your infinitives in my paper."],
5:["(Solveig is filling in a crossword grid of her own.) I set the Gazette crossword. Twelve across: 'Foreign correspondent lost in a fog of questions', seven letters. Don't strain yourself. I haven't worked it out either.",[
  ["Does anyone solve it?","Two people. Father Thyme, by post, every week, in red ink. And Bjarne, who cheats."],
  ["Is that clue about me?","Everything's about you, on an island this size. You'll get used to it."],
  ["Seven letters: 'mystery'.","Very good. Wrong, but very good. You think like a reporter. Almost."]],
 "Twelve across. Think on it."],
6:["(Solveig puts the telephone down.) The London exchange. Two hours to get a line, and then it dropped. I was trying to ring an old friend on a news desk. Professional curiosity.",[
  ["Curious about what?","Whether anyone there remembers you. Nobody remembered me either, so don't take it badly."],
  ["The lines are terrible here.","They're terrible everywhere north of Copenhagen. Henrik says it's the cold. I say it's the Post Office."],
  ["Nora had a strange call too.","So she says. A man asking about the foreign reporter. The whole island's very interested in you. So am I."]],
 "I'll try London again tomorrow."],
7:["(Solveig is cutting one of Nora's columns down with scissors and paste.) Nora's been to Frostholm to ask Dario where the money goes. Four hundred words of her and not one of his. I'm cutting it to ninety.",[
  ["Isn't that cruel?","It's kind. Ninety good words get read. Four hundred get used to wrap fish in Saltwick."],
  ["Will Henrik print it?","Henrik prints everything Nora writes. That's why he needs me."],
  ["What would you ask Dario?","Nothing. I'd watch who he looks at when he doesn't answer. That's the story. The answer never is."]],
 "Keep it short. Always."],
8:["(Solveig has the radio on low: a hymn from the monastery church.) Father Thyme's asked a writer to help with his sermon, they're saying. Which writer, I wonder. There's only Henrik, Nora, me and you.",[
  ["Not me. I can't write sermons.","No? You can't write news stories either, from what I've seen. You must do something."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas and funerals. This year's been heavy on funerals."],
  ["Who's singing?","Kaisa, on the radio after this. She's a better voice than the whole choir, and she knows it."]],
 "Mind the hymn on your way out."],
9:["(Solveig has two glasses on her desk: one of Kelp Cola, one of something brown and suspicious.) The Party's making its own cola, and Kelp's making a new one. Henrik wants a taste test on the front page. I drew the short straw.",[
  ["Which is better?","Neither. Both taste of a committee."],
  ["Is that really news?","On this island, a new fizzy drink is news. In London it'd be a paragraph on page nine, next to the weather."],
  ["Are we all right, you and me?","(She doesn't look up.) I've a very bad memory, journalist. It's served me well in this trade."]],
 "Drink the Kelp. It's safer."],
10:["(Solveig is ruling a black border round the front page, slowly, with a steel rule.) The king's funeral. I did the border for the old queen too, when I was seventeen. Henrik let me hold the ruler. It was the first thing I ever did for this paper.",[
  ["Did you know the king?","I shook his hand once, at a school prize-giving. He asked what I wanted to be. I said 'a sub-editor', and he looked very sorry for me."],
  ["Aren't you going to the chapel?","No. Somebody has to stay and turn everyone's copy into English. Henrik will be weeping into his notebook. Nora will be counting the hats."],
  ["Henrik gave you your first job?",["And my second, when I came back. Twenty years in that chair, and he still can't spell 'accommodate'.",[
    ["Were you friends?","We were rivals. That's friendlier, on a newspaper. He's the better writer and I'm the better sub, and we both know it."],
    ["Why did you leave?","Fleet Street rang. Nobody on this island says no to London when it rings. Except Henrik."]]]]],
 "Black ink today. All of it."],
11:["(Solveig hangs up her coat. There's sawdust on the sleeve.) I came down the stairs of the Timber Lofts with Knut and Ilse from the mill. They're talking about wages. Loudly.",[
  ["Will they get a rise?","I don't print wishes. Ask Dagny, not me."],
  ["Who else lives in your block?","Half of Eastbrook. Erik, who leaves leaflets on the stairs. Jonna, who plays records at midnight. Maya, who smells of coffee even on a Sunday."],
  ["Are you a union woman?","Eleven years in the union on Fleet Street, and I walked out with the rest of them twice. Here I'm the management. It's a terrible thing to find out about yourself."]],
 "Mind the stairs. The fourth one wobbles."],
12:["(Solveig is checking a column of figures with a pencil.) Ruby at the record shop is selling something rare, I hear, and Dario's people want it. Ruby won't tell me what. She's been my neighbour for months and she's never told me anything.",[
  ["What do you think it is?","A record, presumably. She runs a record shop. But nobody gets that excited about a record unless it's the one they were young to."],
  ["Do you like music?","Jazz, the old kind. I used to go to Ronnie Scott's on Thursdays, with a man who played the trumpet badly."],
  ["Are those the oil company's figures?","Diversified Petroleum's annual report. Very glossy. I'm adding it up. It doesn't."]],
 "Never trust a column that adds up first time."],
13:["(Solveig has a green bottle on her desk, unopened.) Someone's going round the island giving Kelp Cola away, they say. Phil's rung Henrik twice for a photographer. We haven't got one.",[
  ["Free is free.","Nothing's free. A free cola is an advert you drink."],
  ["Who takes the pictures, then?","Nora, with Henrik's camera from 1961. Half her pictures have her thumb in them. The readers have grown fond of it."],
  ["Will you drink it?","I'll keep it for a rainy day. On this island, that's tomorrow."]],
 "Tell Nora: thumbs out of the frame."],
14:["(Solveig is sharpening pencils, a whole row of them.) The big day. The whole island in one place, and nobody can tell me what for. Henrik's cleared the front page. I've sharpened eleven pencils.",[
  ["Are you going?","I'll stand at the back with a notebook, as I did at every rally in Trafalgar Square. The back's where the real story is."],
  ["Nervous?","Of crowds? No. Of a crowd that knows something I don't? Always."],
  ["What will the headline be?","I'll know when I see it. You don't write the headline first. Henrik does, and I take it out."]],
 "Stand at the back."],
15:["(Solveig is subbing two letters to the editor, one in each hand.) 'Crown before cross', says this one. 'Cross before crown', says that one. Same day, same postman. I'm printing them side by side and letting the readers fight.",[
  ["Which side are you on?","The side of the full stop. I've no religion and no king, and I'm told that makes me a Londoner."],
  ["Who wrote them?","One's signed 'A Loyal Subject', in a palace hand. The other's 'A Servant of God', in red ink. I have my suspicions about the red ink."],
  ["Isn't that stirring things up?","That's what a newspaper's for. The island's stirred already. We just hold the spoon."]],
 "Red ink. I'd know it anywhere."],
16:["(Solveig has a stopwatch on her desk.) The great debate: the Party and the Democrats, on one stage. Henrik wants it word for word. I'm timing who talks longest. My money's on Anna.",[
  ["Who'll win?","Whoever the Gazette says won. That's a frightening thing to say out loud, so I won't."],
  ["Do you vote?","When there's something to vote for. There hasn't been, here, in my lifetime. Perhaps that's what they're arguing about."],
  ["Erik lives in your block.","He rehearses on the landing. I've heard his closing speech four times. It's good. The second time was better."]],
 "My money's on Anna."],
17:["(Solveig is picking pieces of type off the floor.) The ground shook this morning and a whole tray of type went over. Thirty-six point, all over the lino.",[
  ["Was anyone hurt?","The press. It makes a noise now like Henrik getting out of a chair."],
  ["Is the mountain waking up?","Signe at the lighthouse says she's seen steam. Henrik says it's cloud. Henrik has never once been right about the weather."],
  ["Let me help.","(She hands you a fistful of letters.) They go in upside down and back to front. Don't ask me why. I've stopped asking."]],
 "Mind the letters underfoot."],
18:["(Solveig has a map of the north pinned up, with a pencil cross on the mountain.) Nora wants to go up and look at the steam. Henrik said no. I said nothing, and lent her my good boots.",[
  ["Is that wise?","No. But I was twenty-four once, and someone lent me boots, and I went to Belfast. You don't forget who lent you the boots."],
  ["Are you worried?","About the mountain? I'm from here. We grew up with it grumbling. About Nora? A little."],
  ["What's the Gazette saying?","'KEEP CALM', in large type. People only print that when they aren't."]],
 "Always lend the boots."],
19:["(Solveig peels a red poster off her window.) 'Comrades Wanted.' They're all over Eastbrook. There was one on the door of the Timber Lofts. Erik took it down. Then he put it back, in case it looked rude.",[
  ["Will people join?","Some. The Party's the only one on the island that brings sandwiches to its meetings."],
  ["Were you ever a Red?","On Fleet Street everybody was something. I was a sub. We vote with a blue pencil."],
  ["Any royal gossip?","Someone very grand is going to the Midnight Note in disguise, they say. Sid won't tell me who. Sid never tells anyone anything, which is why the club's still open."]],
 "Keep your posters off my window."],
20:["(Solveig holds a sheet of paper up to the light.) The Hotel Aurora has a guest who never comes down for breakfast, and the Democrats want to know who. Greta won't say. Nobody on this island says anything, and they all say it to me.",[
  ["Who do you think it is?","Somebody who pays in cash and tips like a foreigner. You'd know the type."],
  ["Heard anything about the palace?","Only that something's going to happen there that the palace doesn't know about. Paolo's taking bets. I've put ten on 'nothing'."],
  ["Have you a source at the hotel?",["Everyone has a source at a hotel. Mine changes the sheets.",[
    ["What does she say?","That the guest has more passports than suitcases. She counted. She's very thorough."],
    ["Isn't that underhand?","It's journalism. The difference is the headline."]]]]],
 "You didn't hear it from me."],
21:["(Solveig is at the window, very still, with ash on her shoulders.) It went up this morning. I watched from the roof of the Timber Lofts with Jonna and Ruby. Nobody said a word for ten minutes. On this island, that's a record.",[
  ["Is everyone safe?","So far. Henrik's ringing round. I'm writing it down. It's all we're good for today."],
  ["What's the headline?","Henrik's got 'VOLCANO ERUPTS. NO ONE KILLED.' I didn't take a word out. First time in twenty years."],
  ["Are you all right?","(She brushes the ash off.) I'm a sub. I'll be all right when it's in print, spelt correctly."]],
 "Shut the door. The ash gets in."],
22:["(Solveig is shaking ash out of a typewriter.) Ash in the keys, ash in the ink, ash in my tea. Henrik says it'll make the paper look distinguished. It makes it look grey.",[
  ["Can you still print?","This paper's printed through a strike, a flood and the old king's coronation. It'll print through ash."],
  ["Heard anything from the farms?","Hilda rang: Kelp Cola's people are poking about on her land, and she wants it in the paper. I said write me a letter, two hundred words, no swearing. She said she'd try."],
  ["Need a hand?","Blow. Not on me. On the keys."]],
 "Shake your coat out at the door."],
23:["(Solveig is threading a new ribbon into the typewriter.) Somebody's put a spanner in a machine somewhere, they say, and the oil men have divers going down at the rig. Two stories and no facts. On Fleet Street we called that a Tuesday.",[
  ["Which do you believe?","Neither, until somebody's name is on it. That's the rule: names, then facts, then the headline."],
  ["Do you miss London?",["Today? Yes. In London it rains, and nothing blows up.",[
    ["What do you miss most?","The presses in the basement, shaking the whole building at ten o'clock. Here the building shakes and it's the mountain."],
    ["Would you go back?","I've a flat with wobbly stairs and a view of a volcano. Why would I leave?"]]]],
  ["Can I borrow your typewriter?","No reporter ever touches a sub's typewriter. It's the oldest rule on Fleet Street. You'd know that."]],
 "Names, then facts."],
24:["(Solveig watches a crew with brooms go past the window.) The monks have gone up to the peaks, and a crew is sweeping Eastbrook with brooms nobody paid for. Gustavo's men, Paolo says. Very civic of them.",[
  ["Is that suspicious?","When a syndicate sweeps the street, ask what's under the dust."],
  ["Why have the monks gone?","To pray, they say. Father Thyme sent a note for the paper, in red ink, of course: 'Gone to be quiet. Do not print this.' I'm printing it."],
  ["Paolo's your neighbour?","Two floors down. He runs a book on everything. He offered me odds on the end of the world. I said no. Who'd collect?"]],
 "Watch what's under the dust."],
25:["(Solveig is cutting out a paper crown with the big scissors.) Leopold wants a crown for everyone on the island, they say. Nora's made me a paper one, to be ready. I'm using it as a ruler.",[
  ["Will you wear it?","I wore one at a wedding in Hampstead in 1974. Never again."],
  ["Is Daphne joining the Democrats?","That's the talk on every landing in the Timber Lofts. Erik's walking about like a man who's been promised a pony."],
  ["Who's winning, do you think?","On this island? Whoever everyone's talking about this week. This week it's paper crowns."]],
 "Long live the paper king."],
26:["(Solveig holds a Kelp Cola label up to the lamp.) A prince in every bottle: Kelp want Leopold's face on the label, they say. And Dario's buying the spa. If it gets any sillier, I'll start believing it.",[
  ["Would Leopold do an advert?","I saw an earl advertise biscuits once, in London. He ate one on camera and was never taken seriously again."],
  ["Why a spa?","Dario's tired. Rich men get tired and buy hot water. Poor men run a bath."],
  ["It sounds like one of your clues.","Fourteen down: 'Oil man in hot water', five letters. I set that last week. I'm a prophet."]],
 "Fourteen down. Five letters."],
27:["(Solveig is working through the cinema listings.) Otto says the Party's booked the pictures for a showing, and he won't say of what. And there's a big card game in a cellar in Kingsgrad, Paolo says. It's all happening in the dark this week.",[
  ["Will you go to the pictures?","I'll go to anything with a seat and the lights off. It's the only rest a sub ever gets."],
  ["Will Nora cover the card game?","Henrik's forbidden it. Which means she will."],
  ["You look tired.","Twenty-seven days of this island. In London a month was four weeks. Here it's a lifetime, with ash."]],
 "Sit at the back, near the door."],
28:["(Solveig has six proofs pinned up, one for each faction, and a coffee gone cold.) Everyone's big push, all on one day. Marches, rallies, a state visit, an office party. I'm subbing six front pages, and we only print one.",[
  ["Which one will you print?","The one that's true by four o'clock. The others go in the drawer."],
  ["Do you care who runs the island?",["More than I'd say in print.",[
    ["Then say it here.","I want an island where the Gazette can print what it likes, and nobody knocks on the door afterwards. That's all. It's more than most places have."],
    ["Which of them would give you that?","None of them, if you ask. All of them, if you watch them closely enough."]]]],
  ["There's coffee on your proof.","That's not coffee. That's house style."]],
 "Go on. I've six pages to cut."],
29:["(Solveig is cleaning her blue pencil with a penknife.) Tomorrow it's settled, one way or another. Henrik's left a hole in the front page the size of a country, and I'm to fill it with whatever's true.",[
  ["Are you ready?","I've been ready for eleven years. That's subbing: waiting for the big one, with your pencil sharp."],
  ["What happens to you after?","The Gazette goes on. Somebody has to tell people what happened, and spell the names right."],
  ["Thank you. For everything.","(She doesn't look up.) I don't know what you mean. I've a very bad memory, journalist."]],
 "Pencils sharp."],
30:["(Solveig stands by the press with her coat on, though she isn't going anywhere.) The last day. Whatever happens, by tomorrow it's yesterday's news, and someone in Saltwick will wrap a fish in it.",[
  ["Isn't that sad?","It's the best thing about newspapers. Nothing lasts. You get to try again in the morning."],
  ["Will you stay on the island?","I came home for a funeral and stayed for a newspaper. I think I'll stay for whatever comes next."],
  ["Will you write about me?","No. Nobody would believe it, and I've a reputation to think of. Go and get your story, journalist."]],
 "Spell the names right."],
};
// Sven, fuel dock attendant at North Frostholm Harbour, lives in the Skyline Apartments (Diversified Petroleum). Threads: every boat
// on the north coast and where it's been, told for the price of a coffee; his running feud with Ole, the harbourmaster, whose logbook
// knows less than Sven does; Sanna at Polar Burger, whom he's sweet on; the coffee itself, and who's paying today.
DAILY['Sven']={
7:["(Sven is leaning on a fuel pump, holding an empty mug meaningfully.) The harbour's open, the boats are back, and I'm parched. A man who fills every boat on the north coast hears a great deal, you know. For a coffee.",[
  ["I'll get you a coffee.","(He beams.) Black, two sugars. In return: Phil's yacht came round the point yesterday, slow, as if he was having a look at Dario's rig. Phil never looks at anything without a reason."],
  ["What sort of things do you hear?","Who's been where, who's in a hurry, who pays cash and who asks for a receipt. The ones who don't want a receipt are the interesting ones."],
  ["Isn't that the harbourmaster's job?","Ole? (He snorts.) Ole writes in a book. I talk to people. Ole knows what time a boat came in. I know why."]],
 "Same time tomorrow? Bring a mug. I'll bring news."],
8:["(Sven is watching the sea through binoculars.) The rig boat went out like a scalded cat. Then a second one, full of empty drums. You only send empty drums for one reason.",[
  ["What reason?","Something's spilt. Don't say I said so. Say 'sources at the harbour'. I'd like to be a source. It sounds very important."],
  ["What else have you seen?","A fishing boat out of Saltwick hanging about near the rig, nets up, not fishing. Communists, I'd bet. They like to watch Dario's mistakes."],
  ["Can I borrow the binoculars?","(He hands them over.) Mind the strap. They were my father's. He watched this harbour for thirty years and saw everything, and told everyone."]],
 "Sources at the harbour. Remember that. Spell it right."],
9:["(Sven is drinking a coffee somebody else bought him.) Sanna from Polar Burger brought this down. On her own. Without me asking. Do you know what that means?",[
  ["That she likes you?","That she likes me! Or that she had a spare coffee. But it was two sugars. She knows I take two sugars."],
  ["What's Sanna like?","She makes the best burger on the island and laughs like a seal. I've been going in every day for a year. I've put on a stone."],
  ["What's the news today?","Ole's put up the mooring fees again. The fishermen are furious. I've been selling them fuel and sympathy all day."]],
 "Two sugars. She remembered. I'm going to think about that all day."],
10:["(Sven is in a black armband, filling a launch with diesel.) They're burying the king today. Half the boats on the coast went down to Kingsgrad. And one boat came up the coast that I didn't recognise.",[
  ["What boat?","Dark hull, no name on the bow, heading south for Eastbrook. Ole hasn't got it in his book. I've got it in my head, which is better."],
  ["Who do you think it was?","Gustavo's people use Eastbrook when they don't want Ole looking at the cargo. Liv in customs down there is easier to get out of the way."],
  ["Are you sad about the king?","He came down to this dock once, in 1971, to open the new pumps. He bought his own fuel and paid cash. I liked him. He tipped."]],
 "No name on the bow. That's always a story."],
11:["(Sven is glaring at the port office across the quay.) Ole's been at it again. He's told me my coffee table's 'an obstruction on the quay'. It's a crate. It's been there six years.",[
  ["What will you do?","I've moved it two feet to the left. It's now an obstruction in a different place. He'll need a new form."],
  ["Why don't you and Ole get on?","He's a book man. I'm a people man. He thinks gossip's a sin. I think it's a public service. We've been neighbours twenty years."],
  ["Neighbours?","Same floor of the Skyline. His door's opposite mine. He says good morning to me every day like a man reading out a fine."]],
 "Two feet to the left. That's how the war is won."],
12:["(Sven is counting cash with a wet thumb.) Busy day. Every boat in the harbour wants filling, and everyone's in a hurry. Magnus came down with a briefcase and asked if any boats were going to Stockholm.",[
  ["Were there?","Not today. He looked disappointed. Magnus only looks disappointed when a sum doesn't add up."],
  ["Why would Magnus need Stockholm?","Banks, probably. Or a holiday. Magnus on holiday is like a fish on a bicycle. I don't believe it."],
  ["You notice everything.","It's my gift. And my curse. Mostly my gift."]],
 "If you see Magnus, tell him the next Stockholm boat's Tuesday."],
13:["(Sven has crates of Kelp Cola stacked by the pump.) Free samples from Kingsgrad! I went down on the supply boat and came back with four crates. I've given them out all up the coast. Ruth, Britta, the rig. Dario'd have a fit.",[
  ["Aren't you an oil man?","I'm a harbour man. The harbour runs on oil and gossip and anything that's free. I'm loyal to all three."],
  ["Is it any good?","It's the best thing that's ever been free. I gave one to Sanna. She said it tasted of seaweed. She drank it anyway."],
  ["Did Ole get one?","He said accepting gifts was against his principles. I left one on his windowsill. It's gone."]],
 "Take one. Everyone's had one. You'll fit in."],
14:["(Sven has his hair wetted down and a clean collar.) The big day. The whole island in one place. You know what that means for me? Gossip. Everyone in one place, talking. It's Christmas.",[
  ["What do you expect from it?","Everybody pretending they're not looking at everybody else. And me, looking at everybody."],
  ["Will you take Sanna?","I'm going to ask her to stand with me. If she says yes, it's the best day of my life. If she says no, I'll still have the gossip."],
  ["Will Ole be there?","With his logbook, writing down the time of everyone's arrival. I'll tell him who they came with. He hates that."]],
 "Wish me luck with Sanna. Not the gossip. The gossip I can manage."],
15:["(Sven is grinning from ear to ear.) She stood with me. Sanna. At the big day. The whole time. She held my coffee while I talked to the Cola men. That's practically engaged.",[
  ["Congratulations!","Thank you. I haven't asked her anything. But in my heart I've asked her four times."],
  ["What did you hear at the gathering?","Everything. Too much. The Church and the palace aren't speaking, the Syndicate's buying drinks for Republicans, and Phil wore a hat that cost more than my boat."],
  ["Prince or priest, Sven?","Whichever one comes down here for fuel. I'll make up my mind when I've seen if they tip."]],
 "Practically engaged. Write it down. Don't tell her."],
16:["(Sven is reading the Gazette with a coffee.) The Great Debate's on the radio, and nobody's buying fuel, because they're all listening. So I'm listening too. I've never been so bored or so interested.",[
  ["Who's winning?","Anna's sharper, but Erik's kinder. On this island, kinder usually loses, then wins later, when people feel bad."],
  ["Seen any interesting boats?","A Russian trawler, off the point, all day. Not fishing. Lots of aerials. I waved. Nobody waved back."],
  ["Bored and interested at once?","That's the radio. Or marriage, according to my mother. I'll let you know."]],
 "A trawler with aerials. Tell nobody. Tell everybody. I can't decide."],
17:["(Sven is jumpy, and keeps looking up the quay.) Dario's gone missing. Nobody's seen him. Magnus has been down here twice asking if his boat's gone out. It hasn't. I'd know.",[
  ["Where do you think he is?","Somewhere on land, then. And there's people on this island who'd like him to stay missing. I'm not saying who. I'm just saying the pizzeria's been busy."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a gossip. Gossips worry professionally. Something's not right today. The gulls are quiet."],
  ["Have you told Ole?","Ole's written 'Dario's boat: in harbour' in his book. That's his whole contribution. Very helpful."]],
 "If anyone asks, I've seen nothing. For once, it's true."],
18:["(Sven is gripping the pump as the quay judders.) There it goes again! The whole dock moves. The boats are bumping their fenders like they're nervous.",[
  ["The volcano?","Steam up over the mountain in the north, plain as day. For once, Ole and I agree on something. He wrote it in his book. I told everyone."],
  ["Is the harbour safe?","If the ground goes, the sea goes, and then nowhere's safe. But I've got fuel for every boat on the coast, so I'm the most popular man in Frostholm today."],
  ["You look nervous.","I'm a harbour man. We don't like the ground moving. That's what the sea's for."]],
 "If you hear a big bang, run for a boat. I'll fill it."],
19:["(Sven leans in with a conspiratorial look.) Have you heard? A royal's been sneaking into the Midnight Note in disguise. A false moustache! I told Dario. He paid me in milkshakes.",[
  ["Which royal?","I've narrowed it down to one of them. (He grins.) No, I don't know. But Sid at the bar says the moustache is very convincing, apart from the bit that keeps falling off."],
  ["Who told you?","Rita, the singer, told Britta, who told Ruth, who told me, at the depot gate. That's how news works on this island. It's very reliable, apart from the bits that aren't."],
  ["Milkshakes?","Strawberry. Dario's got a machine. I've never been paid in milkshakes before. I liked it."]],
 "A prince in a moustache. Best news this year."],
20:["(Sven is tapping his nose.) The Russian at the Hotel Aurora had a boat in last week. No cargo on paper. Very heavy crates. Ole stamped it through. I watched them unload, and they hummed.",[
  ["What's in the crates?","Something electrical. You don't need to be an engineer to know a humming crate isn't full of tinned fish."],
  ["Did you tell Ole?","He said the papers were in order. That's Ole all over. If the paperwork's right, a crate could have a bear in it and he'd wave it through."],
  ["Should someone look into him?","Somebody should. Not me. I'm a fuel man. I'll tell whoever does, for a coffee."]],
 "Humming crates. Write that down. Spell 'humming' right."],
21:["(Sven is soot-grey and shouting directions to boats.) The mountain's gone up! Lava in the north and ash all over the harbour. Everyone's trying to get boats out, just in case. I'm filling them as fast as I can!",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. And I'd have heard. Every boat that comes in tells me something. So far it's all 'scared' and nobody's 'hurt'."],
  ["Where are they going?","Round to Kingsgrad, south, away from the ash. Ole's logging every one. For once, I'm glad he's got the book."],
  ["Can I help?","Hold that hose. (He shoves it at you.) Point it at the boat, not at me. There. Now you're a harbour man."]],
 "Keep your mouth shut, the ash gets in your teeth. That's the only time I'll ever say that."],
22:["(Sven is sweeping ash off the quay with a broom.) Ash everywhere. On the pumps, on the boats, in my coffee. Sanna brought me a new one and it had ash on it before I'd had a sip.",[
  ["Is Sanna all right?","She's fine. Polar Burger's shut for sweeping. She came and sat on my crate for an hour. We didn't say much. It was nice."],
  ["Did all the boats come back?","Every one. Ole counted them out and counted them in. He shook my hand afterwards. Twenty years. First time."],
  ["What's the news?","Dario's got the depot trucks washing down the streets, for the Gazette. The Gazette's got a photographer up here. I've been in it twice. Waving."]],
 "Ole shook my hand. Write that down, nobody'll believe me."],
23:["(Sven is watching a launch race out to sea.) That's the welders, and the divers after them. Something's gone wrong at the rig, under the water. Third boat out this hour.",[
  ["What's broken?","Something below the waterline. A fishing boat out of Saltwick was hanging about near the rig a couple of nights back. I'm not saying that's connected. I'm just saying I noticed."],
  ["Will it sink?","Not if those divers are any good. They were very quiet at the pump. Divers are always quiet. It's all that time underwater."],
  ["You sound worried.","Half the men on that rig buy their coffee off me. Lars owes me four. I'd like him to live long enough to pay."]],
 "Three boats out. I'll count them back in."],
24:["(Sven is studying a new sign on the port office door.) Ole's put up a new rule: no loitering on the quay. I've been loitering on this quay for twenty years. It's my profession.",[
  ["Will you obey it?","I've stopped loitering. I'm now 'waiting for customers'. It's completely different, and there's no form for it."],
  ["Is Ole getting worse?","He's frightened. Since the eruption, he counts the boats three times a day. I'd feel sorry for him, if he'd stop putting up signs."],
  ["Any news of the rig?","Boats came back from it quiet. Nobody'd say anything. That's when you know it's big. Usually it's me that can't keep quiet."]],
 "Waiting for customers. Very much not loitering."],
25:["(Sven is wearing a new scarf, very proudly.) Sanna knitted it. For me. She says it's because I was cold during the ash. I think it's because she loves me.",[
  ["It's a lovely scarf.","It's lopsided and it's got a hole in it, and it's the best thing I've ever owned. Don't tell her about the hole. She knows."],
  ["Will you ask her out properly?","I'm going to. Next week. Or the week after. When the world's stopped ending."],
  ["Any gossip today?","The palace has started handing out crowns, they say. And the Democrats have a princess. Everybody's royal now except me."]],
 "A scarf is practically a ring, on this island. Ask anyone."],
26:["(Sven is watching a big car on the coast road.) That's Dario's car, heading for the mountain road. He's going to buy the spa. The whole harbour knows. I told them.",[
  ["Will he get it?","The owner's turned him down twice. Saga, who works up there, says the owner'd sooner sell to the volcano. But Dario doesn't hear 'no'. It's a medical condition."],
  ["How do you know where he's going?","He stopped for fuel, and asked me if the mountain road's clear of ash. Nobody goes up the mountain road for any other reason now."],
  ["Did he pay you in milkshakes?","Cash, this time. And he tipped. Dario tipping means he's nervous. I've never seen him nervous before."]],
 "If he comes back smiling, I'll know before the Gazette does."],
27:["(Sven is counting coins from a cigar box.) The harbour pool's running on the poker game at the pizzeria. Who's going to win. I've got a dollar on Gustavo. He usually wins at his own table.",[
  ["Who else is playing?","Everyone who matters, they say. Carlo, Phil's man Harald, and a few men who don't use their names. I wish I'd been asked. I'd have lost, but I'd have heard everything."],
  ["Isn't gambling against the law?","It's against the law in the same way Ole's coffee table rule is against the law. Everyone agrees, and nobody stops."],
  ["Going to Dario's party tomorrow?","Wouldn't miss it. Free milkshakes and every executive in the company drunk on strawberry. I'll come back with a week of news."]],
 "A dollar on Gustavo. The house always wins. It's in the rules."],
28:["(Sven is holding court by the milkshake machine.) Dario's party! And the whole island's out pushing today, every one of them, all at once. I don't know where to look. I'm looking everywhere.",[
  ["What have you heard?","Magnus thinks he's winning tomorrow. Lars still owes Nico. Ruth's dressed like a bandit. And Dario's been very nice to everyone, which frightens me more than anything."],
  ["Has Ole come?","Ole's on the quay with his book. Somebody's got to watch the harbour. He said it like a martyr. I brought him a milkshake. He drank it."],
  ["Have you bowled?","Twice. I got a strike and then fell over. Sanna wasn't here to see either. It doesn't count."]],
 "Strawberry. Have one. They're free, and that's the best flavour."],
29:["(Sven is reading out results from memory to a fisherman.) The Republicans are voting today. I've got a man at the party HQ. Well, I've got Arto, from the phone box, who's watching the door. He'll ring when there's news.",[
  ["Who do you think will win?","I've heard everything, so I know nothing. Magnus has the rig, Carlo has the casino, Harald has the cola. It's three islands fighting over one chair."],
  ["Arto's your man?","Arto's out of work, so he has time, and he's got the phone box. It's the best intelligence network on the island. It costs me two coffees."],
  ["What will you do if Magnus wins?","Ask him for a new pump. If Carlo wins, I'll ask him not to put anyone in the harbour. If Harald wins, I'll ask for free cola."]],
 "When the phone rings, I'll know before Ole. That's all I want from life."],
30:["(Sven is sitting on his crate with his coffee, unusually quiet.) The last day. Everyone says it's all decided today. I've got nothing to tell you. I don't know anything. First time in my life.",[
  ["How does it feel?","Terrible. Like being out of fuel. I keep opening my mouth and nothing comes out."],
  ["What do you hope for?","Boats coming and going. Sanna bringing coffee. Ole putting up signs. The island, staying the island. That's all."],
  ["Where's Ole?","Across the quay, watching the sea, same as me. (He waves. Ole, after a moment, waves back.) Don't tell anyone. We have a reputation."]],
 "Whatever happens, I'll be here tomorrow. Boats need fuel, and people need news."],
};
// Tiina, a net-maker out of work in Saltwick since her husband Matti's boat, the Lumikki, went down last season (The Unemployed).
// Threads: Matti and the night the Lumikki sank (she heard an engine with no lights in the fog, and doesn't believe it was the
// weather), her cousin Jussi, a farmhand at East Farm, mending nets again for Tom, and Sirpa and Ray, watched with a dry eye.
DAILY['Tiina']={
1:["(Tiina doesn't look up.) Sirpa's told you everything already, I expect. She'll have told you my name's Tiina. That's all you need to know today.",[
  ["Nice to meet you, Tiina.","Is it? You don't know me yet. Wait a week. (The corner of her mouth moves.)"],
  ["I'm a reporter, from London.","I know what you are. Everybody knows what you are. What I don't know is what you want."],
  ["You don't like strangers?","I don't dislike them. I just don't tell them things. That's a different thing."]],
 "Go on. Sirpa's got more to say than I have."],
2:["(Tiina is mending a sock with neat, quick stitches.) Still here? Most reporters would've gone to Kingsgrad by now. That's where the dead king is.",[
  ["Saltwick's interesting too.","Saltwick's tired. Broken cobbles, empty nets, a plant on short hours. If that's interesting, London must be very dull."],
  ["Lovely stitching.","Net-maker's hands. Twenty years making nets for the boats. Now I darn socks. The knot's the same, the money isn't."],
  ["Are you always this suspicious?","Since last season, yes. Before that I was cheerful. Sirpa will tell you. She tells everyone everything."]],
 "Mind the cobbles by Hannu's. They bite."],
3:["(Tiina is looking out towards the harbour.) The boats are going out late. Fog on the water this morning. They don't like fog here. Not any more.",[
  ["Why not any more?","Because of last season. You've heard. Sirpa will have told you. My husband's boat went down in a fog like this one."],
  ["I'm sorry.","(She nods once.) Everyone's sorry. Being sorry doesn't find a boat. But thank you."],
  ["Fog's dangerous everywhere.","Fog's only dangerous if someone else is out in it who shouldn't be. Matti knew those waters better than his own house."]],
 "The fog'll lift by noon. It always does, after."],
4:["(Tiina is quiet a while before she speaks.) The Lumikki. That was the boat. Matti named it for the snow. Painted it white every spring.",[
  ["Was it ever found?","A bit of the wheelhouse washed up by the point. White paint, with a black scrape down it. Like something hit it."],
  ["What do you think happened?",["The harbourmaster says fog and bad luck. (She pulls her coat tighter.) The harbourmaster wasn't out in it.",[
    ["Were you?","I was on the pier with a lamp, waiting. I heard Matti's engine. Then another engine, bigger, with no lights. Then only one."],
    ["Did you tell anyone?","I told the police in Kingsgrad. Sergeant Hanna wrote it down. That's all that happened: it got written down."]]]],
  ["Do you want to talk about it?","Not today. I've said more than I meant to. You've got that kind of face. I don't like it."]],
 "Don't put that in your paper. Not yet."],
5:["(Tiina watches Sirpa go into the shop.) Has she told you about the scarf? She thinks nobody knows. The whole street knows. Ray probably knows.",[
  ["Does Ray like her?","Ray's liked her for ten years. Ray polishes the same glass for an hour when she's in. Men are slow. Bartenders are slower."],
  ["Should someone tell them?","Not me. I'm enjoying it. It's the only love story in Saltwick, and I've got a seat at the front."],
  ["You're kinder than you let on.","Don't spread that about. I've got a reputation."]],
 "Not a word to Sirpa that I told you."],
6:["(Tiina is turning a little brass compass over in her hands.) Matti's. They gave me back his things in a box. This is the only one that still works.",[
  ["Does it still point north?","Always. Even after the sea had it. Matti would like that. He said a compass is the only honest thing on a boat."],
  ["Do you keep it with you?","Every day. It's silly. I don't go anywhere it'd be any use. But it knows where north is, and some days I don't."],
  ["He sounds like a good man.","He was stubborn and he snored and he never once forgot my birthday. That's a good man, on this island."]],
 "Go on. I'm all right. I'm always all right."],
7:["(Tiina is reading a letter, frowning.) From my cousin Jussi. He's a farmhand out at East Farm. Now the roads are open he's remembered he can write.",[
  ["What's the news from the farm?","Hilda's cows, Olaf's back, the potato crop. And lights in the old barn at night, which nobody's supposed to be using."],
  ["Lights in a barn?","Jussi says it smells like a skunk when the wind's right. He thinks someone's growing something out there. Jussi thinks a lot of things."],
  ["Are you close?","He's the only family I've got left this side of the water. He writes twice a year and borrows money once."]],
 "Don't repeat the bit about the barn. Jussi's got a big mouth on paper."],
8:["(Tiina is looking north-west, eyes narrowed.) Something's happened at the oil rig. The boats are all going that way and none of them are fishing.",[
  ["Do you know what happened?","No. And the oil company won't say. They never say anything. They didn't say anything last season, either."],
  ["Last season?","Their supply boats run past the point in all weathers. Big engines. (She stops.) Never mind. I'm tired."],
  ["You don't trust the oil men.","I don't trust anyone with a boat bigger than a house who doesn't fish. That's not the same as having a reason."]],
 "Watch the sea today. It's got something to say."],
9:["(Tiina has a ball of tarry net in her lap.) Tom brought me this off the beach. Oil in it. He says the sea's dirty all the way to the point.",[
  ["Can you clean it?","Turpentine and patience. I've got patience. Tom's paying for the turpentine. That's the first work I've had in a month."],
  ["Is it from the rig?","Where else? There's only one thing out there that bleeds black."],
  ["You're mending again.","One net. It's not a job. But my hands remembered it before I did."]],
 "Don't touch the net. You'll smell of it for a week."],
10:["(Tiina has a black armband on, neatly stitched.) I made a dozen of these for the street. For the king. Sirpa wanted a ribbon. Sirpa always wants a ribbon.",[
  ["Did you admire the king?","I didn't think about him much. But he came down here when the Lumikki was lost and sent a letter. Only person in Kingsgrad who did."],
  ["Not going to the funeral?","They don't want net-makers at funerals. They want people in good black. Mine's good black, but it's been worn a year."],
  ["The armbands are lovely.","They're straight, that's all. Matti always said I couldn't sew a crooked stitch if I tried."]],
 "Wear one if you like. There's spares."],
11:["(Tiina is watching the beach path.) The Church has gone down to Pat. A whole procession of them. He'll hate that.",[
  ["Is Pat a friend?","Matti used to give him fish. Pat came to the memorial. Stood at the back with his hat off. I don't forget things like that."],
  ["What does Pat need?","A dry bed and a hot meal. He'll ask for a bottle. If anyone gives it to him, that's on them, not Pat."],
  ["Where would he get a bottle?","Ray's, if he's feeling soft. Or he'll want the stuff from the farm. Jussi's barn. (She grimaces.) Everybody knows about that barn now. Except Olaf."]],
 "If you see him, tell him Tiina says keep warm."],
12:["(Tiina is sitting alone for once.) Sirpa's gone to the plant to beg for hours again. She goes every Thursday. She comes back every Thursday.",[
  ["Don't you go with her?","I'm not a fishmonger. I'm a net-maker. The plant doesn't need nets, it needs hands. Mine are the wrong shape."],
  ["What was Matti's last day like?","(She looks at you a long time.) Ordinary. Porridge, an argument about the roof, a kiss at the door. He said 'back by dark'. That was the only lie he ever told me."],
  ["Nice to have the bench to yourself?","It's too quiet. You don't know how much someone talks until they stop."]],
 "She'll be back by noon, cross. She always is."],
13:["(Tiina has an envelope in her lap, addressed in careful capitals.) I've written to the harbourmaster in Frostholm. Ole. I want the port log for the night the Lumikki went down.",[
  ["What do you hope to find?","Which boats were out. Which ones logged in and which didn't. If a big boat came in that night, someone wrote it down. Or someone didn't."],
  ["Why now?","Because the roads are open and the post goes to Frostholm again. And because you asked me about Matti, and I couldn't stop thinking."],
  ["Will Ole help?","Ole's a fair man, they say, and slow. Slow I can live with. I've waited a year."]],
 "Don't tell Sirpa. She'll want to read it before I post it."],
14:["(Tiina has her black coat brushed and buttoned to the neck.) Sirpa's dragged me out for the big day. I'll stand at the back. Somebody has to see the whole of it.",[
  ["What do you expect?","A lot of people saying 'the island' as if it was one thing. It isn't. It's Saltwick and Frostholm and the palace, and they don't speak."],
  ["You came anyway.","Matti would've come. He liked a crowd. He'd have bought me a hot sausage and said something rude about the speeches."],
  ["Will it change anything?","Maybe. Things change when everybody's in one place and can see each other. That's why they don't do it often."]],
 "Stay where I can see you. Crowds swallow people."],
15:["(Tiina looks almost pleased.) I saw Ole yesterday, at the big gathering. The harbourmaster. I went straight up and asked him about my letter.",[
  ["What did he say?","He said he'd got it and he'd look. He took his hat off when I said Matti's name. I liked that."],
  ["That was brave.","It wasn't brave. It was rude. Sirpa was mortified. But in a crowd nobody can walk away from you very fast."],
  ["Do you trust him?","More than I did. A man who takes his hat off for a drowned fisherman's not hiding much."]],
 "Now we wait. I'm good at waiting."],
16:["(Tiina is listening to a radio through Ray's open door.) The debate's on. Anna and Erik. Neither of them has said the word 'fishermen' yet. I'm counting.",[
  ["What would you want them to say?","That a boat that goes down on this island matters. That someone looks into it. That's all. It's not much."],
  ["Who's winning?","Anna's louder. Erik's nicer. Neither's mine. I'll know who to vote for when someone says 'Lumikki'."],
  ["Do you want a vote?","I want to be asked. I've never been asked anything by anyone in charge. A vote would be a start."]],
 "If anyone says 'fishermen', come and tell me."],
17:["(Tiina holds out Matti's compass.) The ground shook this morning, and the needle swung right round. First time it hasn't known where north was.",[
  ["Is it broken?","No. It settled again, after. But for a minute it didn't know, and neither did I. (She closes it.) I didn't like that."],
  ["The earth shook?","A little tremor. Sirpa spilt my tea on me. That's the real disaster. I've only got the one skirt."],
  ["A bad omen?","I don't believe in omens. Matti did. He wouldn't sail if a priest came on the quay. I'd laugh at him. (She doesn't.)"]],
 "Keep your feet under you. The ground's not to be trusted this week."],
18:["(Tiina has another letter from her cousin.) Jussi again. Twice in one month. Something's wrong. He says the cows won't milk and the mountain's steaming like Hilda's kitchen.",[
  ["Is the farm in danger?","It's a long way from the volcano. But cows know things. Jussi says they've been standing facing south all week."],
  ["Anything about the barn?","(She almost smiles.) He says 'everything's very normal in the barn'. Underlined twice. Jussi was always a terrible liar."],
  ["Will he come to Saltwick?","Not him. He'd rather be eaten by lava than leave Hilda's cooking."]],
 "If you're going north, look at the cows. Tell me if they're facing south."],
19:["(Tiina looks sour.) Anna came round asking me to join the Party. Free soup and a badge. 'For the widows of the sea,' she said. As if she knew Matti.",[
  ["What did you say?","I asked her what's in it for her. She said 'the people'. I said I'm the people and I'd like to know what's in it for me."],
  ["Is the soup good?","Very good. Helen makes it. Helen I'd join anything for. But it's Anna's name on the door."],
  ["Will Sirpa join?","Sirpa's thinking about the soup. Sirpa's always thinking about the soup. It's what I love about her."]],
 "Everybody wants something this month. Watch who's asking."],
20:["(Tiina is holding a letter very still.) Ole wrote back. The log for the night of the Lumikki. Matti's boat logged out. Nothing else logged out. Not one other boat.",[
  ["So Matti was alone?","On paper. But I heard another engine. Bigger. No lights. So whoever it was didn't log out, and didn't want to."],
  ["Who goes out without logging?","People who don't want anyone to know they've been. People with crates, not fish. (She folds the letter.) Ole underlined that part. He believes me."],
  ["What will you do?","Nothing yet. A woman who asks too many questions about boats with no lights ends up in the water herself. I'm not stupid."]],
 "Not a word of this. To anyone. Not even Sirpa."],
21:["(Tiina is grey with ash, and holding Sirpa's sleeve.) The mountain's gone up. We watched it from the bench. Sirpa screamed, and I held her hand. I don't hold hands.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","No. Nobody's hurt, they're saying. The fire went north. The ash came here. Of course it came here."],
  ["What did it look like?","Like the sky had a wound in it. Black, then red, then grey falling on everything. Matti would've stood and watched with his mouth open."],
  ["You're still holding her sleeve.","(She lets go, then takes it again.) Mind your own business."]],
 "Cover your mouth. Go inside. Don't argue."],
22:["(Tiina is shaking ash out of a net.) Tom brought more nets round. Grey with ash, every one. It gets into the knots. It's like mending sand.",[
  ["Is Tom paying you?","Cash, and a fish on Fridays. It's the nearest thing to a job I've had since spring. I'd mend ash for him all winter."],
  ["Are the boats going out?","Not today. Ash on the water, ash in the engines. The whole harbour's sitting on its hands. Like me, but louder."],
  ["Your hands look sore.","They've been sore before. They're net-maker's hands. They don't know how to be anything else."]],
 "Shake your coat out before you go indoors."],
23:["(Tiina is trying not to smile.) She gave him the scarf. Sirpa. Last night, at the bar. And he put it on. Ray. In front of everybody.",[
  ["You must be happy for her.","I am. Don't tell her I said so. She'll cry, and then I'll have to look at the sea till she stops."],
  ["Did you see it happen?","From my table. She went pink to the ears. He said 'right', and wrapped it round three times. The whole bar clapped."],
  ["Will you have someone again?","(A long pause.) Not this year. Maybe not any year. Matti was a lot of husband. You don't find two."]],
 "Let her have her day. She's earned it."],
24:["(Tiina flinches as the ground trembles.) Every shake, I think of the night on the pier. Waiting to see what's still standing when it stops.",[
  ["That must be hard.","Everyone's doing it now. The whole island's on the pier with a lamp. At least I've had practice."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. A chimney down in Kingsgrad, Olga's window cracked. Nobody missing. That's a good shake, in my book."],
  ["You're steadier than most.","When the worst thing's already happened, the rest of it's only noise."]],
 "Hold the rail on the harbour steps. They're loose."],
25:["(Tiina is thoughtful.) I went and talked to Pat. Pat sees everything at night. He's seen boats with no lights at the river mouth for years.",[
  ["Who are they?","He doesn't know, or won't say. Crates, handcarts, men who don't wave. Pat's not stupid either."],
  ["The same boat that hit Matti?","Maybe. A boat that comes in dark to land crates doesn't stop for a fishing boat in the fog. It doesn't stop for anything."],
  ["Will you tell the police?","And say what? 'A hobo saw lights'? Sergeant Hanna's got a drawer full of my letters already. I'll keep this one."]],
 "Don't go poking at night boats. I mean it."],
26:["(Tiina is quiet.) Sirpa's got hours back at the plant. A letter came. Three days a week. I'm glad for her. I am.",[
  ["Not you, though?","No. Fishmongers first, they said. I'm a net-maker. They don't need nets, they need gutting knives."],
  ["You'll still see her.","Evenings, at Ray's. But days, it'll be me and the bench and Hannu's prices. I'll have to start gossiping myself."],
  ["You don't seem jealous.","I'm not. I'm frightened of being quiet all day. There's a difference. Sirpa knows it. She's been very kind. It's unbearable."]],
 "Don't tell her I'm frightened. She'll stay home to keep me company."],
27:["(Tiina is turning Matti's compass in her fingers.) Tom's offered me all his winter nets. All of them. And Sam's, if I want them. Cash every Friday.",[
  ["That's wonderful.","It's work. Good work, that I'm good at. I keep wanting to say yes and then I see Matti's nets in the pile."],
  ["Will you take it?","I think so. A net doesn't know whose boat it's from. It just wants mending. Maybe I'm the same."],
  ["Sam's a Communist, isn't he?","Sam's a fisherman first and a Communist on Tuesdays. Nets don't vote."]],
 "I'll tell Tom tomorrow. Or the day after. Soon."],
28:["(Tiina has her arms folded tight.) Everybody's out today. Flags, rallies, speeches. Ray's got the shutters down. I'm staying right here.",[
  ["Not interested?","I'm interested. I'm just not getting in the way of it. Days like this, someone always ends up on the ground."],
  ["What's the street saying?","Anna's lot are wild with it. Lucia's on her step with her arms folded, same as me. That's the only thing we've got in common."],
  ["Who do you hope wins?","Whoever looks after the boats. I'll find out which one that is when I see what they do, not what they say."]],
 "Mind yourself out there today. Come back in one piece."],
29:["(Tiina looks at you, and nods.) I said yes to Tom. I start on his nets on Monday. In Matti's old shed. My shed, I suppose, now.",[
  ["I'm glad.","So am I. I didn't think I would be. It's a strange thing, being glad again. Like a coat you've grown out of."],
  ["And the Lumikki?","I'll keep Ole's letter. When the island's settled, whoever's in charge, I'll take it to Kingsgrad and make them read it. I've got time."],
  ["Tomorrow's the big day.","Everybody's big day. Mine was today. (She almost smiles.) Don't tell Sirpa I said anything that soppy."]],
 "Go and rest. Tomorrow the island makes up its mind."],
30:["(Tiina is wearing a blue knitted hat, and looks cross about it.) Don't say anything. Sirpa made it. She cried when she gave it me. I had to wear it.",[
  ["It suits you.","It's blue. I haven't worn a colour since last season. (She touches it.) It's warm, I'll give it that."],
  ["Last day of the month.","The day it all gets decided. Kings, parties, colas. And I'm sitting here in a blue hat. Matti would laugh himself sick."],
  ["What are you hoping for?","That the island remembers people like Matti. That's all. Whoever wins, I'll be here, and I'll be asking."]],
 "Whatever happens today, the nets still need mending on Monday."],
};
// Tom, fisherman in West Saltwick Harbour (Union). Threads: his boat Marit and her sulky engine, the lobster pots, his lending habit (Sam and Mike), the cold coming on, the volcano.
DAILY['Tom']={
1:["(Tom is coiling rope on the quay.) Snow's off the harbour road at last. You'll be the reporter. Tom. I fish, and I lend my boat to anyone who asks nicely.",[
  ["Nice to meet you. Good boat?","Marit. Forty years old, and she's got opinions. Mostly about starting."],
  ["Is the fishing any good?","Cod, when the ice lets me out. Today I'm mending nets, which is what fishermen do instead of fishing."],
  ["Lending boats to strangers?","Only if they ask nicely. You haven't asked yet, so I haven't had to decide."]],
 "Mind the nets. They bite."],
2:["(Tom nods at the grey water.) They say the Kingsgrad road is open. Folk keep going past me with suitcases, as if the palace has a sale on.",[
  ["Are you going to Kingsgrad?","What would I do there? Curtsey? The sea's my palace."],
  ["What does Saltwick think of the king's death?","Quiet about it. A king's like a tide table: you don't notice it till it stops."],
  ["Any good stories down here?","Sam caught a boot last week and swears it was a good one. That's about it."]],
 "Come back when you want a story with a fish in it."],
3:["(Tom is scraping a hull.) Cola boys are in Kingsgrad now, I hear. The bottling line's humming. Half of Saltwick's got a cousin on it.",[
  ["Do you drink the cola?","One a day, with lunch. Kelpy on the label looks like a cup with a grudge."],
  ["Is the Union behind it?","Helen keeps an eye. The Union wants the workers paid, and Kelp Cola wants them quick. They get on like cats and a bath."],
  ["Does the factory buy your fish?","No, but they buy Olga's, and Olga buys mine. It all comes round."]],
 "Mind the gulls, they steal."],
4:["(Tom is blowing on his hands.) Eastbrook's open too. Boat to Eastbrook Harbour's cheaper than the tram, if you don't mind a bit of spray.",[
  ["Could you take me?","If you ask nicely. (He grins.) You just did. Not today, though: Marit's engine is sulking."],
  ["What's wrong with the engine?","She coughs. Mike says it's the plugs, Sam says it's the fuel, and I say it's age."],
  ["Do you go there often?","For the Gazette's fish and chip ads. Henrik pays in newspapers. I've got a stack by the stove."]],
 "Keep your hat on. The wind's got teeth."],
5:["(Tom is hauling a crate.) Monastery road's open, they say. Father Thyme will be ringing bells again. You can hear them across the water if the wind's right.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Christmas and funerals. The monks don't mind, they get my cod at Easter."],
  ["Do the monks fish?","They've a pond up there. A man who fishes in a pond isn't a fisherman. Don't tell them."],
  ["What's the mood on the quay?","Folk are wondering who'll sit on the throne. Mostly they're wondering what's for dinner."]],
 "Mind the wet planks."],
6:["(Tom is eating a sandwich on an upturned bucket.) Somebody telephoned the harbour master this morning and asked after you. A stranger, asking nicely. I said you were seeing the island.",[
  ["Who was he?","No name. Polite voice. I told him I fish and don't ask."],
  ["That's a bit odd.","It's an island. Everything's odd by Thursday."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm hungry. Worry comes after lunch."]],
 "Eat something. It's cold out."],
7:["(Tom points north-west.) The Frostholm road's open as of today. The whole island's loose again. Folk are going about like cows let out in spring.",[
  ["Are you glad?","I like a quiet harbour. But the oil lot will be down with their clipboards, wait and see."],
  ["Does oil worry you?","They've a rig out past the point. A rig's a lump of metal in my fishing."],
  ["Seen the Diversified men?","One came to ask for a boat. I said I'm only lending to people who ask nicely. He didn't."]],
 "Mind the diesel, it's slippery."],
8:["(Tom has a flask of tea.) Sunday. Bells all morning from up the hill. Father Thyme's been going on about words, I hear. Can't find the right ones for the sermon.",[
  ["What would you say to him?","Say the sea's wide and the cod's cold. That's all the sermon I need."],
  ["Does the Church matter to the harbour?","They bless the boats in spring. Marit got blessed in '71. She still sulks."],
  ["Any gossip today?","Mike says Kaisa played a hymn on the radio and a rumba after. The monks aren't sure which they liked."]],
 "Warm hands, warm tea."],
9:["(Tom is holding a cola bottle up to the light.) Strange taste, this. They say Kelp's changing the formula, and the Party wants its own cola too. A fisherman can't keep up.",[
  ["Which one would you drink?","The cheap one. A bottle's a bottle."],
  ["Does anyone ask you about flavours?","Folk ask me about bait. I say the lobsters like fish heads, and cola's no good to them."],
  ["What would a Union cola taste of?","Salt, I'd think. And a bit of tar from the quayside."]],
 "I'll stick to tea."],
10:["(Tom's cap is in his hands.) They bury the king today. I've had the flag half down on Marit since dawn. He never went out on a boat, but he knew we were here.",[
  ["Did you ever see the king?","Once, in 1969, on a ceremonial cutter. He waved at a seal. I think it was a seal."],
  ["Are you going to the funeral?","Too far. I'll stand on the quay with the others and look at the water."],
  ["What now for the island?","Heaven knows. They all want something. I'd like a fair price for cod."]],
 "Quiet today. Respect."],
11:["(Tom is leaning on a post.) The mill hands are making a fuss about wages over in Eastbrook. Helen says it's about time. I say a day's pay is a day's pay, and a day's fish is a day's fish.",[
  ["Do you support them?","Always. I'm in the Union, aren't I? Though I sell fish, not planks."],
  ["Is Helen involved?","Helen's always involved. She'd organise the tide if she could."],
  ["Does your pay ever come late?","Sometimes the cod just doesn't turn up. It's a wage of sorts."]],
 "Fair's fair."],
12:["(Tom is whistling.) Folk say Ruby over in Eastbrook has a record someone from Frostholm wants badly. A boy told me. Fishermen get all the news, because we sit still.",[
  ["Do you know Ruby?","Everyone knows Ruby. She told me my taste in music was 'regrettable'."],
  ["What record is it?","Haven't a clue. If it's a shanty, it's mine, and I'd like it back."],
  ["Who'd want a record that badly?","Someone with more money than ears. Plenty of those up north."]],
 "Fair winds."],
13:["(Tom is eyeing a stack of cola crates on the quay.) Free samples, they say. Kelp's handing out bottles on every corner. I took two. A fisherman takes what's free.",[
  ["Is it good?","Fizzy. It fights back. Marit's engine does the same."],
  ["Why free?","So we'll buy it later. I'm not daft. I'm just thirsty."],
  ["Did the Union approve?","Helen said take the bottle but keep your vote. Sound advice, that."]],
 "Take care of those bottles."],
14:["(Tom is wearing a clean jumper, which is rare.) Tomorrow's the big day, the fourteenth. Everyone's on about it. I don't know what they expect, but they expect it hard.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Crowds. Noise. Someone making a speech with a lot of 'my fellow islanders' in it."],
  ["Will you go?","I'll go if the boat's tied up and the nets are done. Marit won't mind."],
  ["Are you nervous?","A bit. The island's like the sea before weather: everyone looking up."]],
 "Big day. Wear a hat."],
15:["(Tom is rubbing his chin.) Palace and monastery are arguing about who comes first, crown or cross. I'd say the tide comes first, but nobody asks.",[
  ["Which side are you on?","Mine. I'm a Union man. I'd like both of them to pay a decent price for fish."],
  ["Who'd make a good king?","Someone who knows what a rope is for."],
  ["Is the harbour divided?","Sam's for the prince. Mike's for the monks. I'm for lunch."]],
 "Don't argue on the slippery bit."],
16:["(Tom is polishing a hook.) Big debate today, the Party against the Democrats. Helen's practising her speech so loud I could hear her over the gulls.",[
  ["Who will win?","The one who talks longest. Or the one who brings sandwiches."],
  ["Do you follow politics?","Only on the radio. Kaisa reads the news in a voice so nice I'd believe anything."],
  ["Would you vote?","Happily. A vote's like a line in the water: you never know what you'll get."]],
 "Eat something first."],
17:["(Tom is looking at his mug of tea.) The tea trembled just now. I said to myself, that's the tram. But there's no tram in the harbour.",[
  ["The ground shook?","A bit. The old men say the mountain's grumbling."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm a fisherman. I know what the sea does. The land's new to me."],
  ["Did the boats feel it?","Marit shuddered. She'd do that anyway, though."]],
 "Keep your eyes on the north."],
18:["(Tom is squinting at the sky.) Steam over the north, did you see? Thin white line. And Mike's gone to the Cola Museum to see Kelpy, which tells you how bad things are.",[
  ["Why Kelpy?","Mike says the cup brings luck. A cup. I said it's a person in a suit."],
  ["Are you scared of the volcano?","It's a long way off. I've a boat, which is a good thing to have."],
  ["Heard about a lost relic?","The monks lost something. They're being quiet. Quiet monks are worried monks."]],
 "Keep the lamp lit."],
19:["(Tom is baiting a pot.) They say there's a new song at the Midnight Note, and a lot of comrades about. Songs and comrades. Not for me.",[
  ["Do you go to the Midnight Note?","Once. They played something so slow the fish rose to listen."],
  ["What are the comrades up to?","Recruiting, mostly. Helen would call it organising."],
  ["Would you join the Party?","I'm in the Union, which is plenty. Marit doesn't do politics."]],
 "Stay out of draughts."],
20:["(Tom is rinsing a bucket.) Something's up at the palace, folk say. And a stranger's staying at the hotel in Frostholm. Always strangers, these days.",[
  ["Who's at the hotel?","I don't know. I only know boats."],
  ["Do you think the palace is in trouble?","Palaces always are. It's the price of a big house."],
  ["Are you getting nervous?","The ground shook twice this morning. I'm getting nervous."]],
 "Watch your step."],
21:["(Tom is standing at the quay edge, ash on his cap.) You saw it. The mountain went up like a kettle. Glowing orange in the north. Nobody's hurt, thank God.",[
  ["Are you all right?","Fine. Marit's fine. The sky's a funny colour, that's all."],
  ["What now?","Wait. It's all we can do. Tea, mostly."],
  ["Will the boats go out?","Not till the ash settles. Fish don't like grit either."]],
 "Keep a cloth over your mouth."],
22:["(Tom sweeps ash off Marit's deck.) Grey on everything. Like someone's been at the island with a pepper pot. I've swept the deck twice and it's grey again.",[
  ["How's the harbour?","Quiet. Ash on the water, and the gulls look disgusted."],
  ["Heard anything from the farms?","Cattle upset, they say. Cows don't like ash. Nor do I."],
  ["Is anyone helping?","Everyone is, which is the one nice thing about a disaster."]],
 "Sweep little and often."],
23:["(Tom is wiping his hands on a rag.) The engine's coughing worse than before. Ash in the filter, I reckon. Mike's coming to look. He'll say it's the plugs.",[
  ["Is there anyone to repair it?","Mike knows engines a bit. Gunnar knows crates a lot. I know fish."],
  ["What about the oil lot's repairs?","They've been fixing something deep down at the rig. Out of sight, out of mind, as I'd like it."],
  ["Will you manage?","I always do. It's how I've lasted."]],
 "Stay dry."],
24:["(Tom nods at the north.) Some of the monks are heading up to the peaks, they say, to retreat. In ash. I'd stay home and light the stove.",[
  ["Why would they go up there?","To be nearer heaven, I suppose. It's nearer the volcano too."],
  ["Is anyone cleaning up?","Folk are shovelling in turns. Clean-up crews, they call them. Some more official than others."],
  ["Does the harbour need help?","Always. A broom's welcome. A kind word's better."]],
 "Wrap up warm."],
25:["(Tom is tying a knot slowly.) A princess for democracy, I heard, and a crown for everyone. Fine words. Fine words don't catch fish.",[
  ["What do you think of the princess?","I've never met her. If she can row, she's got my vote."],
  ["Is the harbour talking about it?","Everyone's talking about everything. It's all politics and ash."],
  ["Will you vote?","I will. Sam says I'll vote for whoever has the nicest hat."]],
 "Pick your hat well."],
26:["(Tom is gripping a mug.) Kelp's got a prince in every bottle now, apparently. And the oil man's buying a spa. I'd sooner buy a net.",[
  ["Do you like the new bottles?","The prince looks worried. I'd be worried with cola in my chest."],
  ["Why would the oil man buy a spa?","To soak. They have money; they've got to put it somewhere."],
  ["What would you buy?","A new engine. Marit's earned it."]],
 "Soak your feet, not your boots."],
27:["(Tom is sitting on the upturned hull.) The Party's showing something at the pictures, folk say. I like a film, but Helen's films mostly have a tractor in them.",[
  ["Are you going?","If there's a seat and no tractor."],
  ["Do you like films?","Last one I saw had a whale in it. I cried at the whale."],
  ["What's the Union think?","That it's about time we had one of our own, with a boat."]],
 "Take a flask."],
28:["(Tom stares at the horizon.) Everyone's doing their biggest push today, it feels like. Flags, banners, posters. I've got a net and a cup of tea, and that's my campaign.",[
  ["Are you worried?","A bit. When everyone shouts at once, nobody listens."],
  ["Who will win?","I haven't the foggiest. I'd like the one who leaves the harbour alone."],
  ["Will you be on the quay tomorrow?","Where else? That's where I live."]],
 "Don't shout. Save your voice."],
29:["(Tom is helping Sam with a rope.) Another day of it. Banners on the tram, speeches at the corners. Marit's grumbling, and Sam's grumbling about Marit.",[
  ["Tired of it?","A bit. I'd just like the thing decided, and the tea warm."],
  ["Any predictions?","I predict rain, then ash, then a nap."],
  ["Is there hope?","Always. It's what the harbour runs on, with diesel."]],
 "Early night, if you can."],
30:["(Tom's sitting very still.) Today's the day, then. Whatever's decided, the tide'll come in tomorrow all the same. That's a comfort.",[
  ["Nervous?","Like before a storm. But I've sailed through plenty."],
  ["What do you hope for?","Peace on the quay. A fair price for fish. A decent engine."],
  ["Thank you for talking.","Any time. Ask nicely, and I'll even lend you the boat."]],
 "Fair winds, friend."],
};
// Toni, attendant at the arcade in Frostholm, who lives in the Kingsgrad Apartments (The Proprietors). Threads: her high scores on
// every cabinet, and the stranger who signs RIT and keeps beating her at Invaders; the new cabinet on its way from Japan; her
// neighbours in the Kingsgrad Apartments (Saga, Mats, Bertil); fixing machines with a screwdriver and a kick.
DAILY['Toni']={
2:["(Toni is sitting on the floor of her flat with the back off a television, a screwdriver in her teeth.) Mm. (She takes it out.) Sorry. You're the reporter. I work the arcade in Frostholm, and the road's shut, so I'm fixing the telly.",[
  ["What's wrong with it?","Nothing, now. It was making a noise like a cat in a drain. I gave it a kick and tightened a thing. Most machines just want attention."],
  ["Tell me about the arcade.","Snake, Brick Breaker and Invaders, a dollar a game, under a pink neon sign. And I hold the high score on every one of them. Write that down."],
  ["Do you fix everything with a kick?","The kick's for the machines. The screwdriver's for everything else. People, I just ignore."]],
 "Close the door, the draught makes the picture roll."],
3:["(Toni has a notebook full of numbers.) Practising. Can't get to the arcade, so I'm playing Invaders in my head. Left, left, fire, wait, fire. I'm at level nine.",[
  ["In your head?","You learn the patterns. The invaders always come down the same way. Once you see it, you see it in your sleep. I do, anyway."],
  ["What's your high score?","On Invaders, forty-one thousand two hundred. On Snake, sixty thousand and something. On Brick Breaker I stopped counting. It's mine."],
  ["Isn't that a bit sad?","It's a bit sad. It's also the only thing on this island I'm the best at. Let me have it."]],
 "Go on. I'm on level ten."],
4:["Saga next door hums in the bath. Every evening, the same three songs from the radio. The walls in these flats are made of cardboard.",[
  ["Who's Saga?","Works at the spa up the volcano. Lovely, warm, always smells of pine. And sings like a kettle."],
  ["Which songs?","Something by Kaisa off the radio, something about a sailor, and one I think she made up. I know them by heart now. I hate them."],
  ["Bang on the wall.","I did once. She thought it was applause and did an encore."]],
 "Mind the stairs. The third one's loose. I keep meaning to fix it."],
5:["(Toni is untangling a big knot of wires.) Mats from upstairs asked me to fix his radio. He works in the Petro Tower and he's the most nervous man I've ever met. He apologised to the radio.",[
  ["What was wrong with it?","Mats had dropped it in the bath. He wouldn't say why he was listening to the radio in the bath. I didn't ask."],
  ["Do you fix things for everyone?","Everyone in the block. Bertil's lamp, Elin's kettle, Saga's hairdryer. They pay me in cake. I'm getting fat on repairs."],
  ["Do you like your neighbours?","I like them better when they've got something broken. Then they knock and I know what to say."]],
 "If you've got anything broken, bring it round."],
6:["They say the north road opens tomorrow. I've ironed my work shirt. (She holds up a pink shirt.) It says ARCADE on the back. I ironed the letters by mistake. They're melted.",[
  ["It still says ARCADE.","It says ARCA. The D and the E are on the iron. It's fine. People will know."],
  ["Excited to be back?","I've missed the noise. Bleeps, coins, kids yelling. It's like a choir that never stops."],
  ["Who goes to the arcade?","Kids skipping school, oil workers on shore leave, Arto and Raili from the street when it's cold, and Nico the bookie, who bets on everyone else's games."]],
 "See you in Frostholm. Bring change."],
7:["(Toni is behind the change counter at the arcade, under the pink neon, with a screwdriver in her belt.) Back! And look: someone's been at the Snake machine. Its screen's gone green on one side.",[
  ["Can you fix it?","(She thumps the side of the cabinet with her palm. The green goes.) Fixed. A month of cold does that. The machines sulk."],
  ["Show me your high scores.","(She points. Every cabinet shows TON at the top.) T, O, N. Toni didn't fit. It's still me."],
  ["Who's been here while you were away?","Nobody. The boss in Helsinki left it locked. But there's a dent in the Invaders cabinet I don't remember. Somebody's kicked it the wrong way."]],
 "Go on, have a game. Dollar a go."],
8:["The boss rang from Helsinki. There's a new machine coming on the next boat from Japan, via Helsinki. A driving game. Steering wheel and everything.",[
  ["What's it called?","Turbo Rally. You drive a little car round a track and the screen moves. Moves! The kids are going to lose their minds."],
  ["When does it come?","Two weeks, if the boat's on time. Three if it isn't. Four if it's Sarivalto. I've cleared a space by the door."],
  ["Will you hold that high score too?","Before anyone else has even touched it. I'll be here the day it comes, with a dollar and no mercy."]],
 "Go on. And don't kick the Snake machine. I mean it."],
9:["(Toni is staring at the Invaders cabinet like it's insulted her.) Look at that. Forty-one thousand nine hundred. RIT. Somebody's beaten my score. In the night. While I was asleep.",[
  ["Who's RIT?","I don't know! Three letters, that's all you get. R, I, T. Nobody I know has those letters."],
  ["Can't you beat it back?","I will. But that's not the point. The point is someone was in my arcade, on my machine, being better than me. I'm in shock."],
  ["Maybe it's a fluke.",["Seven hundred points over? On Invaders? That's not a fluke, that's a professional.",[
    ["Who's on at night?","The night girl, Hanna's niece. She says she didn't see who. She was reading. She's always reading."],
    ["Stay late and catch them.","I'm going to. Tonight. With a thermos."]]]]],
 "Go on. Keep your eyes peeled for anyone called Rit."],
10:["(Toni has a black band on her arm.) Funeral today. The boss in Helsinki said to put the lights down for the morning. I did, and stayed here anyway, and beat RIT back.",[
  ["What's the score?","Forty-two thousand three hundred. TON on top again. I played for three hours. My thumb's gone numb."],
  ["Did you watch the funeral?","I heard it on the radio while I played. The bells and the choir, and the Invaders bleeping. Strange, but nice."],
  ["Did you catch RIT last night?","Fell asleep on the change counter at two. RIT didn't come. Of course RIT didn't come."]],
 "Pay your respects, then come back for a game."],
11:["(Toni's head and shoulders are inside the Brick Breaker cabinet.) Mmph. Paddle's stuck. (She emerges.) The kids have been putting buttons in the coin slot again.",[
  ["Buttons? Like from coats?","The exact same size as a dollar coin. I've got a jar of them. Some of them are very nice. I'm sewing them on a cardigan."],
  ["Kids skipping school?","Half of them. The truant officer's been in twice. He played a game of Snake both times. He's not bad."],
  ["And RIT?","Back on top. Forty-two thousand eight hundred. I wasn't even gone a day. I'm starting to take it personally."]],
 "Mind the buttons on the floor. They roll."],
12:["Saga's started humming a new song. Something jazzy, low and slow. She says she heard it at a jazz club. I've never heard of Saga going to a jazz club.",[
  ["Which jazz club?","The Midnight Note, next door to the arcade. You can hear the saxophone through the wall at night. I always thought it sounded like a cow in love."],
  ["Maybe she's got a sweetheart.","Saga? She only talks about steam and towels. ...Although she's been wearing lipstick on her day off."],
  ["What's the song?","She doesn't know the words. She makes them up. They're about a boat. All her songs are about a boat."]],
 "Go on. Dollar a game, or a dollar to hear Saga's song. I'll do it."],
13:["(Toni is drinking a Kelp Cola.) Free samples! They had girls handing them out in the street. I took twelve. They make good coin weights when the machines are wobbly.",[
  ["What do they taste like?","Like the sea had a party. I like it. Sanna at Polar Burger says it rots your teeth. She sells it, though."],
  ["Any news on RIT?","I've started leaving a note on the cabinet. 'Who are you?' Somebody wrote under it: 'Better than you.' In very nice handwriting."],
  ["Nice handwriting?","Loopy. Like someone who signs autographs. Or a teacher. Or a doctor, but doctors can't write."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. The arcade's open, and I'm going anyway. I'll leave the night girl in charge."],
14:["(Toni is locking the arcade's door, in a coat.) Going to the big day. The whole island. I want to see what everyone looks like when they're not playing games.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches. Lots of standing. I'll bring a pocket game. Don't tell anyone."],
  ["Who'll be there?","Everybody. Maybe even RIT. I'll be looking at everybody's thumbs."],
  ["Is anyone minding the arcade?","The night girl, with a book. Somebody could steal a whole cabinet and she'd turn the page."]],
 "Come on, you'll miss it."],
15:["(Toni is waving a sheet of paper.) I've made a list. Everyone on the island whose name has an R, an I and a T in it. It's a short list, and none of them play Invaders.",[
  ["Who's on it?","Rita, at the jazz club. Ritva, at the bakery in Saltwick. Brother Mikael, if you spell it wrong. And Gerrit, the Dutch harbour pilot, who's eighty."],
  ["Rita from the jazz club?","Rita who sings, with the voice like smoke? I don't think she's ever been in. She'd never play Invaders. She's too... Rita."],
  ["It could be a nickname.","Thank you. Now my list is the whole island. Very helpful."]],
 "Go on. If you meet anyone with good thumbs, tell me."],
16:["The boss rang from Helsinki. The Turbo Rally's been loaded on a boat. It sails tomorrow. A week, maybe ten days, and it's here!",[
  ["Exciting!","I've cleared a space, I've bought a padlock, and I've made a sign: COMING SOON. I'll take it down the minute I've set the record."],
  ["How do you play?","You sit in a seat, hold the wheel, and press a pedal. It's like driving, but nobody dies and you don't need a licence."],
  ["Do you drive?","Never learnt. I'll learn on the Turbo Rally. Same thing, I reckon."]],
 "Go on. Busy debate day, they say. Arcade's quiet. Lovely."],
17:["(The cabinets are rocking gently, and Toni is holding one steady.) Ground's shaking. Third time today. Every time, all the machines go into 'tilt'. Look at them. They think someone's cheating.",[
  ["Are the machines all right?","They're fine. I'm not. Each time they tilt, the games reset. Two kids lost good scores, and they cried."],
  ["What are people saying?","That the volcano's waking up. The spa's on the mountain, so Saga's in a state. She says the water's hotter every day."],
  ["Dario's missing, I hear.","His men came in, looked in every cabinet, and went out. In every cabinet. Like he'd be hiding in the Snake machine."]],
 "Go on. Hold on to something."],
18:["RIT's back. Forty-four thousand. After the shake reset everything, RIT came in and took the top spot first. (She grinds her teeth.) The night girl says it was a woman in a fur coat.",[
  ["A fur coat?","Black fur, long, and she smoked while she played. She tipped the night girl a dollar. A dollar! That's a game."],
  ["Did she see her face?","She was reading. She saw the coat. That's the night girl for you: three hundred pages a night and no idea who's in the room."],
  ["Isn't Rita a woman in a fur coat?","(Toni stops.) Rita from the jazz club wears a black fur coat. Every night. (She stares at the wall that joins the jazz club.) No."]],
 "Go on. I'm thinking."],
19:["(Toni has her ear against the wall to the jazz club.) Rita sings at eleven, and then there's a break. Then she sings again at one. I've timed it. There's a gap of forty minutes.",[
  ["Forty minutes is a lot of Invaders.","Enough to set a score, smoke a cigarette, and get back on stage. The timing works. It's horrible."],
  ["Why would Rita play arcade games?","Half the island's in love with her and she's got a past she won't talk about. Maybe she wanted something no one knew about."],
  ["What will you do?","Wait. Watch. And practise. When I meet RIT, I want to have the high score."]],
 "Go on. Shh. I'm listening for footsteps."],
20:["(Toni is fanning herself with a comic.) It's hot, isn't it? The machines are running hot too. Invaders keeps crashing on level four. The whole mountain's turned the heating up.",[
  ["Is it the steam?","Saga came home last night with her hair frizzy and her face red. She says the spa's like a kettle. She's scared, but she won't say."],
  ["Will the machines break?","If they get much hotter, I'll be fixing them every hour. I've got the screwdriver in my belt and a spare kick ready."],
  ["Any sign of RIT?","Not last night. Too hot to play, maybe. Or she's got other things on her mind."]],
 "Go on. Stay cool. Don't touch the machines, they bite when they're hot."],
21:["(The arcade's dark, and the neon's out. Toni is sitting on the counter with a torch.) The power went when the mountain went. All the machines died at once. I've never heard it so quiet in here.",[
  ["Are you all right?","I'm fine. Scared. The ground shook so hard the Brick Breaker fell over. I picked it up by myself. I didn't know I could."],
  ["Is Saga safe?","She came down the mountain with the bathers, all in towels, I heard. She's at home. I'm going to knock on the wall when I get back and listen for humming."],
  ["What will you do now?","Wait till the power's back. Then I'll fix every one of them. Then I'll set new high scores. Somebody's got to make things normal again."]],
 "Go on. Get home safe. Don't go north."],
22:["(Toni is wiping ash off the Invaders screen.) Power's back. Every machine was full of ash. The Snake game's snake is grey. I think it'll stay grey.",[
  ["Did all the scores reset?","All of them. Every cabinet's at zero. The whole month of scores. Mine, RIT's, the kids'. It's like the year started again."],
  ["Is that sad?","It's sad and it's fair. Now nobody's on top. First one to play gets the record. I'm going to play every machine tonight."],
  ["Did Saga hum last night?","She did. Quietly. The sailor song. I didn't bang on the wall. I nearly cried, actually. Don't print that."]],
 "Go on. Shake the ash off your shoes, please."],
23:["The Turbo Rally's boat's been held up in Helsinki because of the volcano. Nobody wants to sail to an island that's on fire. The boss says another week.",[
  ["The island's not on fire.","Try telling Helsinki. They've heard 'volcano' and stopped listening. My cousin wrote asking if I'm alive."],
  ["What's the space by the door for now?","A sign that says COMING SOON and a chair for Arto. He sits there because it's warm. He's become part of the arcade."],
  ["Whose scores are on top now?","Mine, on everything, again. And no RIT. I've been checking every morning."]],
 "Go on. Dollar a game. I've got change for ash."],
24:["(Toni looks very pleased with herself, and a little shocked.) I met RIT. Last night. Ten past midnight, a black fur coat came in and put a dollar in Invaders. I said, 'Hello, Rita.'",[
  ["What did she say?","She said, 'Hello, Toni.' She knew my name all along. She'd been reading my scores for a year."],
  ["Why does she play?",["She said when she's playing, nobody's looking at her. They're looking at the invaders. (Toni shrugs.) I get that.",[
    ["Is she any good?","She's better than me at Invaders. I'm better at everything else. We played until three. The night girl finished her book."],
    ["Does Sid know?","She says Sid knows everything and says nothing. That's why she works for him."]]]],
  ["Do you hate her now?","I thought I would. She's lovely. She smokes too much and she's got a laugh like a car starting. We're having a rematch."]],
 "Don't tell anyone about Rita. That's her secret, and now it's mine."],
25:["(Toni is wiring a seat in the space by the door.) The boat's sailed! Turbo Rally's coming. I've built a seat for it out of an old car seat Nils sold me from the electronics store.",[
  ["From the electronics store?","Nils sells everything. Radios, televisions, and a car seat from a wrecked Volvo. He threw in a gear stick. I don't know what I'll do with a gear stick."],
  ["Will Rita come back?","She's coming tonight. We're playing Invaders, best of five. She says if I win, she'll sing a song just for me. I'll ask for one without a boat in it."],
  ["Who'll set the Turbo Rally record?","Me. First. Then Rita. Then the kids. In that order."]],
 "Go on. Don't sit in the seat. It's not ready."],
26:["(A huge crate is by the door, and Toni is opening it with a crowbar.) It's here! Turbo Rally! It came in at North Frostholm Harbour with ash on the box and a sticker from Yokohama.",[
  ["Does it work?","Give me an hour and a screwdriver. It's in pieces and the instructions are in Japanese. I'm doing it by looking."],
  ["Who wins the first game?","Me. I've got the first dollar ready. It's been in my pocket for a week."],
  ["How did best of five go?","Rita won three to two. I'm not talking about it. (She smiles.) She sang a song about a lighthouse for me. No boats."]],
 "Go on. Come back in an hour and see it run."],
27:["(The Turbo Rally is running, screen bright, and a queue of children goes out of the door.) It works! Look at them! I set the record on the first lap and now every kid in Frostholm wants a go.",[
  ["What's the record?","One minute twelve seconds. I crashed twice and still won. The kids can't touch it. They will, in about a week."],
  ["How's the queue?","Arto's managing it. He's very strict. He's found a purpose."],
  ["Isn't it a big day somewhere?","The pictures in Kingsgrad, and cards at the pizzeria, they say. Everyone's somewhere. All the kids are here."]],
 "Go on. Join the queue. There's a long one."],
28:["(The arcade is half-empty, and Toni is at the counter, chewing her thumbnail.) The whole island's out on the streets. Rallies, marches, parties. And here I am with a driving game and nine kids.",[
  ["Aren't you going?","Somebody's got to be here when people come in to forget it all. Anyway, there's no queue at the Turbo Rally. Look."],
  ["What do you want for the island?","For the lights to stay on, and boats to keep bringing machines. And somewhere for kids to go that isn't a march."],
  ["What does Rita say?","She says she's seen islands like this before, in places she won't name. She says don't worry until the music stops. It hasn't stopped."]],
 "Go on. Mind yourself out there."],
29:["(Toni is setting up the Invaders cabinet in a corner, with a little card on it that says RIT.) I've given Rita her own machine. Nobody else is allowed to play it after midnight. It's our deal.",[
  ["That's sweet of you.","Don't. I'll go red. She says she might not be on the island after tomorrow. Depends what happens, she says."],
  ["Will you stay, whatever happens?","Where would I go? The arcade's mine. My machines are here. And somebody has to be best at something."],
  ["What does your neighbour say?","Saga? She's been humming the same song all week. The made-up one, about a boat that comes home. I don't mind it any more."]],
 "Go on. I'll be here tomorrow, whatever's on the news."],
30:["(Toni is by the Turbo Rally, a screwdriver in her belt and her pink shirt on.) The last day. I've polished every screen. If the island's going to change, it's going to change with clean machines.",[
  ["What's the plan for today?","Open the doors, turn on the neon, and wait for everyone. Whatever happens, kids will come. They always come."],
  ["Will you play a game with me?","(She slots in two dollars.) Turbo Rally, two players. You're blue. I'm pink. Don't cry when I lap you."],
  ["Thank you, Toni.","For what? Being here. (She grins.) Now hold the wheel properly. You're steering like Mats."]],
 "Go on. Good luck out there. Bring change."],
};
// Tor, warehouseman at the Warehouse in Eastbrook Harbour (Communist Party). Threads: dialectics on his lunch break, arguing with Democrats, Liv in the next flat of the Lofts, lifting crates, his reading, the ash.
DAILY['Tor']={
4:["(Tor sets down a crate.) Eastbrook's open and so's my mouth. I'm Tor, warehouseman, Communist, and the only one of either in this building who's read the whole of Marx.",[
  ["Pleased to meet you. Heavy work?","It builds the arms. The mind builds itself, on lunch breaks."],
  ["What do you argue about?","Everything. Mostly with Democrats. They're polite about losing."],
  ["Do people listen?","Not at first. By dessert they're nodding."]],
 "Keep your arguments dry."],
5:["(Tor wipes his hands on his apron.) A crate of books turned up today, addressed to a monk. 'Theology', it said. I opened it. Four volumes of Hegel.",[
  ["What did you do?","Sent it on. A monk reading Hegel is good news for everybody."],
  ["Do you read Hegel?","I read him with a pencil and a headache."],
  ["Is it dialectics?","Thesis, antithesis, lunch. That's how it goes."]],
 "Mind the corners."],
6:["(Tor eats a sandwich with relish.) Someone in a hat asked me about you at the gate. I said I don't talk about customers. He said you weren't a customer. I said then I don't talk about you.",[
  ["Thank you. Who was he?","Hat, gloves, grin. Seemed to be someone's brother."],
  ["Syndicate?","Could be. Syndicate men always have a brother."],
  ["Should I worry?","A reporter should always be watched. It means you're doing it right."]],
 "Don't let him follow you."],
7:["(Tor stacks planks.) The north-west's open and the Frostholm crates arrive tomorrow. Oil, mostly. The Party doesn't love oil, but the Party loves wages.",[
  ["Do you hate Diversified?","I disapprove of them, from a polite distance."],
  ["Why?","They think everything's a price. A tree is a price. A man is a price."],
  ["Any theory on them?","A contradiction in boots. Thesis: they're rich. Antithesis: they're lonely."]],
 "Read more, lift less."],
8:["(Tor stretches.) Sunday and I'm working. The bells from the monastery come over the fjord. I once told Liv that church bells are the sound of the old order. She said they were the sound of lunch.",[
  ["Do you go to church?","Never. But I admire the architecture."],
  ["Is Liv religious?","Liv is a customs officer. She believes in stamps."],
  ["What about the Church's politics?","They want Prince Augustus. Pious young fellow. Won't last a week."]],
 "Don't preach."],
9:["(Tor holds up a cola bottle.) The Party wants a cola of its own. I've been asked for my opinion on flavour. I said, make it bitter. Like the truth.",[
  ["Is that wise?","No. But it's honest."],
  ["What does Helen say?","Helen says beetroot. I said beetroot's a vegetable, not a flavour."],
  ["Would you drink it?","I'd have to. It's the cause."]],
 "Bitter is better."],
10:["(Tor's cap is off.) The king's funeral. I'm a Communist, but a man's a man, and the old fellow's dead. I'll stop lifting for a minute at noon.",[
  ["Do you respect him?","I respect the dead. I disapprove of the throne."],
  ["Is the Party going to the funeral?","Anna's sent a representative. A tidy wreath, red ribbon, no slogan."],
  ["What do you think comes next?","Argument. Delicious, loud argument."]],
 "A minute's silence."],
11:["(Tor grins.) The mill hands are marching for fair pay. I've lent them a banner and a sandwich. It's all the revolution needs.",[
  ["Are you going?","After my shift. And I'll take Liv, if she can leave her stamps."],
  ["Is it a good fight?","It's the best. A day's pay for a day's work."],
  ["What do the Democrats say?","They agree. Which ruins it. I like a good argument with someone who disagrees."]],
 "Raise your voice."],
12:["(Tor stands, hands on his hips.) The oil company's moving money around, so they say. Moving things is my trade. If a crate went like that, I'd lose my job.",[
  ["How would you hide a crate?","In plain sight, under a heap of other crates. It's the best place."],
  ["Who's the thief?","Not thief. Accountant. Same thing, with a pen."],
  ["Is the Party interested?","Always. Liv hears and I lift."]],
 "Eyes on the books."],
13:["(Tor squints at a crate.) Free samples, and every Democrat in Eastbrook is clutching a bottle. I told them it's a bribe. They said it's refreshing. They're both right.",[
  ["Did you take one?","I took the empty. For the sake of the argument."],
  ["What's the Syndicate's recipe?","A rumour. Something about a secret formula in a safe."],
  ["Who steals recipes?","The same ones who steal anything. Hungry men with good coats."]],
 "Don't drink and argue."],
14:["(Tor puts down a pallet.) The big day, tomorrow. The fourteenth. Everyone's got a speech. I've got three. The first is for the Party, the second for my mother, the third for myself.",[
  ["What's your speech about?","Dialectics, mainly. And bread."],
  ["Will anyone listen?","Liv will. She has to. She lives next door."],
  ["What do you expect tomorrow?","Noise. The island's going to speak at once."]],
 "Save your voice."],
15:["(Tor chuckles.) Crown or cross. Two old rivals, and I'm in the middle with a pallet jack. If you want a thesis and antithesis, there's your pair.",[
  ["What's the synthesis?","The Party, of course."],
  ["Is that modest?","Not at all. But it's logical."],
  ["Who do you like better?","Neither. But I'd rather argue with a prince than a monk. A monk simply smiles."]],
 "Keep your balance."],
16:["(Tor is humming the Internationale.) The great debate, tonight. Helen against Erik. I've prepared questions. They're very short. 'Why not?' and 'Who pays?'",[
  ["Good questions.","They've sunk better men than Erik."],
  ["Is Erik clever?","He's decent. Decent beats clever in a crowd."],
  ["Will you shout?","I'll comment, loudly."]],
 "Don't heckle too hard."],
17:["(Tor looks down at his boots.) The floor shook, did you notice? I almost dropped a crate of ice-skates. Liv says it's a tremor. I said it's the old regime shaking.",[
  ["Is it the mountain?","Old Mike says so. I put it down to tectonic dialectics."],
  ["Are you scared?","A little. A man shouldn't argue with a volcano."],
  ["Heard about Dario?","The oil man? Vanished. Which is a first for oil."]],
 "Stand clear of the shelves."],
18:["(Tor points north.) Steam. White steam. People are pretending it isn't there. I said, whoever ignores the steam gets steamed. Nobody laughed.",[
  ["Will it erupt?","Possibly. History says everything does."],
  ["Heard about the lost relic?","The monks have mislaid something. Typical. Wasn't it theirs to keep?"],
  ["What about Kelpy the cup?","A capitalist mascot. But I bought a postcard."]],
 "Watch the sky."],
19:["(Tor pins a leaflet to the wall.) Comrades wanted. I've put this up on every beam in the warehouse. A man should have a cause. Preferably with a pension.",[
  ["Has anyone joined?","One apprentice. And a cat."],
  ["A cat?","It keeps coming to meetings. We think it's an informant."],
  ["Heard about the Midnight Note?","I don't go. Slow songs make me think of my ex."]],
 "Join up."],
20:["(Tor rubs the back of his neck.) Someone's planning something at the palace, they say, and a stranger's staying at the Hotel Aurora. I suspect it's all connected, but I haven't the paperwork.",[
  ["Do you know who?","Not a clue. But I'd sell a crate to find out."],
  ["What would the Party do?","Watch. The Party's good at watching."],
  ["Are you afraid?","Of a hotel? Never."]],
 "Watch and wait."],
21:["(Tor, covered in grey.) It went up. The whole sky. We all stood on the quay and nobody said anything. Even I had no thesis.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not that I've heard. Thank God, and I'm an atheist."],
  ["What did it look like?","Like the end of an argument."],
  ["What now?","Clean up. Sweep. Then, probably, argue."]],
 "Wear a scarf."],
22:["(Tor shovels ash into a barrow.) Grey on every crate, grey on the stairs. Liv's calling it the great restamp. I call it a damn nuisance.",[
  ["Is the warehouse safe?","The roof held. The windows didn't love it."],
  ["Are people helping?","The Party's out in force. And the Democrats, and the monks. Even Henrik with a broom."],
  ["How's your mood?","Fine. A bit gritty."]],
 "Don't breathe it."],
23:["(Tor tests a rusty winch.) A spanner in the works, they say, over at the docks. I've never liked sabotage. It's bad dialectics, not enough thinking.",[
  ["Who'd do it?","Someone cross at a winch."],
  ["What about the deep repairs?","The oil lot's fixing something below. We all will be."],
  ["Can you fix this winch?","With a hammer and a prayer."]],
 "Keep it oiled."],
24:["(Tor lifts a barrow.) Monks are off to the peaks, and strangers in neat overalls are sweeping everyone's steps. Anyone with a broom and a smile is a clean-up crew now.",[
  ["Do you trust them?","One of them kept looking at the safe. I told him to sweep the floor."],
  ["What's the retreat for?","Praying, thinking, avoiding the ash."],
  ["Would you retreat?","To where? I live over a warehouse."]],
 "Sweep gently."],
25:["(Tor lowers his voice.) A princess for democracy, a crown for everyone. The Gazette's printed it, and Henrik's very pleased with himself.",[
  ["Do you believe it?","I believe in crowns the way I believe in unicorns."],
  ["Is it good for the Party?","Anything that splits the other side is good."],
  ["Would you meet the princess?","If she reads Marx, yes. If she reads only fashion pages, no."]],
 "Don't print it all."],
26:["(Tor frowns.) Kelp's put a prince in every bottle, and the oil man's bought a spa. The island's for sale, one cap at a time.",[
  ["Is that bad?","It's capitalism. It's very consistent."],
  ["What would the Party buy?","A printing press. A better one."],
  ["Have you seen the prince bottle?","He looks like he's been bottled against his will."]],
 "Don't buy the bottle."],
27:["(Tor claps his hands.) Revolution at the pictures tonight. I've seen the poster. A man with a flag and a horse. It's my kind of film.",[
  ["Are you going?","In the front row, with a flask."],
  ["Is it realistic?","It's inspirational. Realism is for accountants."],
  ["What about the Syndicate's high stakes?","Gamblers. They'll lose their shirts, and I'll lift the crates."]],
 "Don't miss the horse."],
28:["(Tor is in his best shirt.) The Party's marching on Kingsgrad with a red flag. I'll hold the other end of it. Liv holds the stamp.",[
  ["Are you ready?","I've lifted worse. A flag's light."],
  ["Is everyone pushing today?","Everyone. It's like a tug of war with five ropes."],
  ["Do you expect a result?","I expect blisters."]],
 "March in step."],
29:["(Tor sits on a crate.) One more day. Every faction's made its final push. I've eaten more banners than bread this week.",[
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote. A fair count. And a pay rise."],
  ["Are you tired?","Like a horse after the pictures."],
  ["Will you still argue after?","Always. It's the last freedom."]],
 "Rest your voice."],
30:["(Tor stands quiet, for once.) Today we find out where the island goes. I've said everything I know. The rest is out of my hands. And on my hands, grease.",[
  ["Good luck, Tor.","And you. Don't print any lies."],
  ["Nervous?","A little. Like before a big argument."],
  ["Any last thesis?","The workers will outlast us all. Even the ash."]],
 "Solidarity, reporter."],
};
// Veikko, out of work in Eastbrook since a log kicked back off the big saw at the mill last winter and smashed his leg (The
// Unemployed). Threads: the mill's safety (the wired-up guard, the accident book, Knut and Ilse still on the floor), his leg, his
// cane and Doctor Lind's walks (which take him past the old barn at East Farm), the community centre and Erik's coffee, and Hely.
DAILY['Veikko']={
4:["(Veikko shifts his weight onto a cane.) The road's open and the first thing through is a reporter. Not the coal lorry. A reporter. Veikko. Sit down, if you like: the bench is the only thing in Eastbrook that's free.",[
  ["What happened to your leg?","The big saw at the mill. A log kicked back off the blade last January and broke it in three places. Doctor Lind put it back together. Mostly."],
  ["What's Eastbrook like?","Coffee, records, timber and Democrats. Everybody's a Democrat here, more or less. Even me, on a good day."],
  ["Mind if I sit?","Go on. Hely's usually there, but she's in the tavern already. She says it's to keep warm."]],
 "Mind the ice by the community centre steps. I learnt that one the hard way too."],
5:["(Veikko is rubbing his knee.) Cold's in the bone today. Doctor Lind says walk it every day, a mile at least. I've worked out a mile's exactly the road to East Farm and back.",[
  ["Does it help?","It hurts less after. It hurts more during. The doctor says that's the point. Doctors."],
  ["Nice walk?","Fir trees, cows, Hilda waving from the farmhouse. It's the only time I see the sky properly. On the bench you only see feet."],
  ["Does anyone walk with you?","Hely, once. She talked the whole way about running off with a circus. Then she stopped to throw stones at a crow."]],
 "If you're going east, the farm road's the pretty one."],
6:["(Veikko is reading the Gazette.) Henrik's put in a piece about the mill's 'record year'. Timber for Kelp Cola, for the palace, for half of Kingsgrad. The lads haven't had a rise in six years.",[
  ["Who's making the money?","Dagny, who owns it. She's got a flat in the Skyline Apartments in Frostholm and comes down twice a week in a fur hat to look at the logs."],
  ["Did the mill pay for your leg?","A ham at Christmas and a card. 'Get well soon, from all at the Eastbrook Lumber Company.' I've still got the card. The ham I ate."],
  ["Write to the Gazette.","I have. Four times. Henrik prints them on page nine, under the knitting patterns. It's something."]],
 "Read page nine next week. I'm on it again."],
7:["(Veikko lowers his voice.) Knut came by last night. The guard on the big saw's been wired up out of the way again. To keep the logs moving faster.",[
  ["Is that what hurt you?","Same saw, same trick. With the guard down, a log that catches comes straight back at you. Mine did. It'll do it again."],
  ["Who wired it?","Nobody, officially. Bjarne, the foreman, turns a blind eye. Dagny wants the numbers up. The numbers are always up."],
  ["Can't Knut refuse?","And lose his job? He's got rent to pay and a sawyer's wage. Refusing's a luxury for people like me, who've nothing left to lose."]],
 "Don't put Knut's name in your paper. He's got enough trouble."],
8:["(Veikko is at the community centre door, stacking chairs.) Erik's got me setting up for a meeting. I can't stand for long, but I can carry chairs one at a time. Slowly.",[
  ["You're a Democrat, then?","I'm a man with a bad leg who wants a vote on who runs the mill. If that's a Democrat, then yes."],
  ["What's Erik like?","Too much coffee and not enough sleep. Good man. Talks like a pamphlet when he's nervous, and he's always nervous."],
  ["Need a hand?","You can take that end. There. Nobody ever offers. They just step round you."]],
 "Thanks for the chairs. Don't tell Erik I let a reporter carry one."],
9:["(Veikko is sitting very still.) I've been reading the accident book. Ilse copied it out for me. Eleven injuries since spring. Four of them hands.",[
  ["That's appalling.","It's a lumber mill. They're dangerous. But they don't have to be that dangerous. Half of those are the guard, and the stop lever tied off with string."],
  ["How did Ilse get it?","It's nailed to a post on the mill floor. Anybody can read it. Nobody does, except the people in it."],
  ["What will you do with it?","Give it to Erik. Or the union. Somebody with a voice louder than page nine."]],
 "Eleven. Write that number down somewhere."],
10:["(Veikko has his cap in his hands.) They're burying the old king today. Linnea's got the wireless on at the tavern, and she's crying into the glasses.",[
  ["Did you admire him?","He came to open the new saw shed in 1971. Shook every hand on the floor, mine too. He had soft hands. I remember thinking: he's never had a splinter."],
  ["Linnea's a royalist?","Linnea thinks Leopold walks on water. She's got his picture behind the bar. We let her. Everyone's allowed one daft thing."],
  ["Will it change things?","The mill will run the same whoever's in the palace. Unless somebody changes the mill."]],
 "Take your hat off for a minute. For the soft hands."],
11:["(Veikko is watching the road to the mill.) Helen from the union's sent someone down to the mill today, I hear. To talk to Dagny. About pay. About the saw.",[
  ["Do you think it'll work?","Dagny's never listened to anybody. But she's never had anyone come who could put it in a newspaper either."],
  ["What would you say to Dagny?","Look at the saw. Not the ledger. The saw. Then look at my leg. Then tell me the numbers matter more."],
  ["Is Helen a Communist?","She is. And the lads are Democrats. And they'll all shake her hand if she gets them a rise. A union card's a union card."]],
 "Whatever happens up there, it's about time somebody went."],
12:["(Veikko lowers his voice.) The barn out at East Farm. Lights again last night, on my walk back. And that smell. You know the one.",[
  ["What's going on there?","Not my business. Olaf hasn't used that barn in years. Somebody has. Round the back, the boards are loose."],
  ["Will you tell Olaf?","Olaf's got a bad back and a short temper. I'm not walking up to a farmer to tell him someone's growing weed in his barn. Not with this leg."],
  ["You walk a lot at night?","Doctor's orders: a mile a day. Nobody said it had to be in daylight. The leg's quieter in the dark, for some reason."]],
 "You didn't hear it from me. I just walk."],
13:["(Veikko is wincing.) Leg's bad today. Hely brought me a cola. She says the cola people mean to give a bottle to everyone on the island. I said what's the catch. She said 'you, drinking it'.",[
  ["Sounds like Hely.","Sharp as a saw blade, that girl. Wasted on the bench. Wasted on the tavern. I keep telling her."],
  ["Is it good for the leg?","Nothing's good for the leg. But the bottle's cold, and that's something. I'm holding it against my knee."],
  ["What does the doctor say?","Doctor Lind says it'll never be right, and to stop expecting it to. He's honest. I'd rather he lied a bit."]],
 "If they hand out new legs next, I'll queue."],
14:["(Veikko has polished his boots.) The big day. Erik's got the whole community centre going. I'll stand at the back with my stick. They'll see me, anyway. People always see the stick.",[
  ["What are you hoping for?","That somebody from the mill stands up and says the word 'safety' out loud, in front of everyone. That's all."],
  ["Is Hely coming?","She's been up since five. She says something's going to happen today. She's said that every day for a year."],
  ["Will you be all right in the crowd?","Hely'll elbow a path for me. She's got very sharp elbows. It's her best quality, after her mouth."]],
 "Come on. Let's go and be counted."],
15:["(Veikko is quiet.) Yesterday. All those people. Mill hands from here, fishermen from Saltwick, men from Frostholm in good shoes. I never knew how many of us there were.",[
  ["What stayed with you?","A man from the Saltwick plant with no thumb. We looked at each other's hands and legs and didn't say a word. Didn't need to."],
  ["Did anyone mention safety?","Not up front. But round the edges, everyone was talking about it. That's where things start. Round the edges."],
  ["Back to the bench?","Back to the bench. My leg did more yesterday than all summer. It's telling me about it today."]],
 "Same bench, same leg. Different feeling, though."],
16:["(Veikko has been setting up chairs since dawn.) The debate. Erik and Anna, at the community centre. I've put out a hundred and forty chairs. One at a time.",[
  ["Who'll win?","Anna's got the better lines. Erik's got the better ideas. Depends if the crowd's listening or just clapping."],
  ["Is there going to be trouble?","There's a lot of Saltwick men coming up the road and a lot of mill men waiting. I've put the chairs where they can't be thrown easily."],
  ["Your leg must be aching.","My leg's screaming. But I'm in the front row, reserved. Erik insisted. First time I've had a reserved anything."]],
 "If chairs fly, duck. I've done my best."],
17:["(Veikko grips his cane as the ground shudders.) Did you feel that? Through the stick, right up my arm. Like the saw floor when the big blade starts.",[
  ["An earthquake?","A tremor, Erik says. He's put out a statement already. He's the only man on the island who'd write a statement about a tremor."],
  ["Is the mill all right?","Ilse says a log stack shifted. Nobody under it, thank God. With the stacks shifting, that mill's a bear trap."],
  ["Does it scare you?","The ground I can't do anything about. The saw, somebody could fix tomorrow. That's what makes me angry."]],
 "Watch the log stacks if you go near the mill. They're moving."],
18:["(Veikko points north with his stick.) See the steam over the mountains? Clear as day from the farm road. The cows at East Farm are all standing facing south.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","Has to be. Hilda says she's never seen it do that. Hilda's seventy."],
  ["Should people leave?","Nobody official's said anything. Erik wants a meeting about it. Erik wants a meeting about everything."],
  ["Will you keep walking out there?","Doctor's orders. And I want to see what the cows do. Cows know more than the radio."]],
 "If the cows start walking south, follow them."],
19:["(Veikko looks worried.) Hely's been at Paolo's again, betting. On the debate, on the mountain, on anything. She owes him more than she'll say.",[
  ["Who's Paolo?","Bookie, in Eastbrook. Nice suit, nice manners, friends you don't want to meet. He'll give you all the credit you like, till he doesn't."],
  ["How much does she owe?","She won't tell me. She laughs. Hely laughs when she's frightened. I've known her since she was twelve."],
  ["Can you help her?","With what? A ham at Christmas? (He shakes his head.) I can keep an eye on her. That's all I've got."]],
 "Don't encourage her if she asks you for a loan."],
20:["(Veikko is sitting on the bench with his leg up on a crate.) The ground's been twitching all morning. Even the mill shut early. First time in thirty years.",[
  ["Dagny shut the mill?","Bjarne did. Said the stacks weren't safe with the ground moving. Bjarne. Maybe the old fox has a conscience after all."],
  ["How's the leg?","Swollen. The doctor says rest. Erik says chairs. My leg says nothing, it just screams."],
  ["Anyone else worried?","Everybody. Ruby's taken her best records home. Maya's sleeping in the roastery with the beans. Hely's the only one who's excited."]],
 "If you hear a rumble, get out from under anything heavy."],
21:["(Veikko's hat is grey with ash.) The mountain's blown. A black tower over the hills, and fire under it. I watched it from the bench. Couldn't run if I wanted to.",[
  ["Are you hurt?","No. Nobody's hurt, they're saying. The lava's gone north, nowhere near anyone. We got the ash instead."],
  ["Where's Hely?","Ran up the hill to see it better. Of course she did. She came back grey from head to foot, grinning like a lunatic."],
  ["What can I do?","Help me up and in. The stick slides on this ash like ice. (He takes your arm.) Thank you."]],
 "Get indoors. Cover your mouth. That's all there is to do today."],
22:["(Veikko is sweeping ash off the bench with one hand.) Everybody's out with brooms. Erik's organised a rota. Of course he has. I've got the bench and the steps.",[
  ["The mill?","Ash in the motors, ash in the sawdust. Bjarne's got the lads cleaning. Knut says it'll be a week before a blade turns."],
  ["Is Eastbrook all right?","Grey but standing. Maya's giving out free coffee to sweepers. Ruby's playing records through the shop door. It's like a party with a cough."],
  ["You're a good sweeper.","I'm a slow sweeper. But I never miss a bit. Slow's got its uses."]],
 "Mind the ash on the steps. It's slippery as soap."],
23:["(Veikko has the copied accident book on his knee.) I've added a page. Two more since the mountain. A cut hand and a man hit by a stack that shifted.",[
  ["Thirteen, then.","Thirteen. I'm going to take it to Erik properly, before the end of the month. Before whoever's in charge is in charge."],
  ["Why Erik?","Because he'll read it. Anna would read it too, but she'd read it into a speech. Erik might actually do something boring with it, like a law."],
  ["Will Dagny listen?","Dagny listens to money and newspapers. Maybe somebody should give her both."]],
 "Thirteen. Remember that number."],
24:["(Veikko winces at an aftershock.) Every time the ground shakes, my leg remembers January. It's not the pain. It's the noise. That crack.",[
  ["Do you talk about it much?","No. Hely knows. Doctor Lind knows. Now you. That's the whole list."],
  ["What happened that day?","Minus twenty, the logs frozen hard. One caught on the blade and came back like a train. I heard my leg before I felt it."],
  ["Do the aftershocks frighten you?","Not the shaking. Waiting for the crack. Everybody on the island's waiting for a crack this week. I've just had more practice."]],
 "Sit down when it shakes. It's easier on everybody's knees."],
25:["(Veikko looks pleased with himself.) I took the accident book to Erik. Thirteen injuries, every one in Ilse's handwriting. He read it twice. Then he went very quiet.",[
  ["What did he say?","'This goes in front of whoever's running the island on the first of October.' Then he made more coffee. That's Erik being moved."],
  ["Will anything come of it?","Something. Maybe not soon. But it's in a folder now, with a name on the front. Mine. That's more than a ham."],
  ["You must be proud.","I walked there without the stick. Twenty yards. Then I needed it. But twenty yards, mind."]],
 "Twenty yards. Don't laugh. It's a lot, for me."],
26:["(Veikko is angry, which you haven't seen before.) Paolo came round to the Lofts looking for Hely. Polite as a vicar. Asking when she'd be 'settling up'.",[
  ["How much does she owe?","Enough that Paolo comes round himself. He doesn't come round for small money."],
  ["What did you do?","Told him she was out. Then I went to the pawnshop window in Kingsgrad and looked at my father's watch for a long time."],
  ["Will you pawn it?","(He doesn't answer for a while.) It's a watch. It tells the time. Hely's twenty-three. She's got more time than the watch."]],
 "Don't tell her. She'd never let me."],
27:["(Veikko's wrist is bare.) Paolo's been settled. Don't ask how. Hely doesn't know. If she finds out, it wasn't me.",[
  ["Your father's watch?","My father kept it forty years and never once was on time. He'd laugh at me pawning it for a girl's card debts. Then he'd have done the same."],
  ["Does Paolo know it was you?","Paolo knows everything. He shook my hand. Told me I was 'a gentleman'. Coming from Paolo, I'm not sure it's a compliment."],
  ["That was kind.","It was stupid. Kind and stupid are cousins. They turn up at the same funerals."]],
 "Not a word to Hely. I mean it."],
28:["(Veikko is buttoning his coat.) Erik's rally in Kingsgrad today. In the square. I'm going up on the tram. Me, the stick and thirteen injuries in a folder.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","For Erik. He's been up all night. He was practising his speech on the coffee urn."],
  ["What do you want from today?","For the mill hands to hear their own island say the word 'safety' out loud. That's all I've ever wanted from anybody."],
  ["Busy day everywhere.","Flags, rallies, oil parties, palace visitors. The whole island's out today. My leg'll be telling me about it for a week."]],
 "Mind yourself in the crowds. They push."],
29:["(Veikko is sitting with his leg up, exhausted.) Tomorrow's the end of it. The island decides. I've been sitting here trying to decide what I'll do, whichever way it goes.",[
  ["What will you do?","Walk. A mile a day, like the doctor said. The farm road and back. Whoever's in charge, the cows'll still be there."],
  ["Will the mill change?","It'll change when somebody makes it change. Tomorrow, the day after, next year. I'll still be on this bench with the book."],
  ["How's Hely?","Lighter, these last two days. She doesn't know why. Good. Let her think the world's just being kind for once."]],
 "Get some sleep. Nobody else will tonight."],
30:["(Veikko is standing by the bench, holding his stick, not leaning on it.) Last day of the month. I walked here from the Lofts without it. All the way. Carried it like an umbrella.",[
  ["That's wonderful!","It hurts like the devil. But I wanted to stand up for today. Whatever happens, I wanted to be standing."],
  ["How do you feel about today?","Like the morning I went back to the mill to look at the saw, after. Frightened, and curious, and too stubborn to go home."],
  ["What do you hope for?","A vote, a safe mill, and Hely somewhere she can't get bored. I'll settle for two out of three."]],
 "Whatever they decide today, the leg and I will still be walking tomorrow."],
};
// Viktor, groundskeeper at the Royal Palace (palace workers, no faction). Threads: his hedges and the snow, the front gate he watches from his shed, the starlings and one stubborn crow, his quiet feud with Marta over the herb bed.
DAILY['Viktor']={
2:["(Viktor leans on a snow shovel by the front gate.) The Kingsgrad road's open and you're the first stranger through it. You'll be the reporter. I saw you coming from the shed.",[
  ["Good eye. Do you watch the gate all day?","It's what the shed's for. Nobody asks a gardener anything, so I hear and see the lot."],
  ["As a reporter, who comes and goes here?","Deliverymen, the butler's cousins, the prince's friends. I keep no list. I just remember."],
  ["Bit of snow, this.","Last week it was over the hedges. Now it's only to my knees. I'm told that's progress."]],
 "Mind the gravel. It's under there somewhere."],
3:["(Viktor is knocking snow off a clipped yew.) Every hedge in this garden is older than the king. Some are older than the palace, and they've outlived plenty of princes.",[
  ["Do you talk to them?","Only to apologise when I take too much off. They sulk for a year."],
  ["What will happen to the gardens now the king's gone?","Gardens don't care who's king. They care about water and a sharp pair of shears."],
  ["Is it always this quiet?","Today it is. Cola men came by this morning, in a van, to look at the gate. Marta chased them off with a ladle."]],
 "The yews need me. Go and bother someone with a title."],
4:["(Viktor is spreading grit on the steps.) Edmund wants the steps clear by ten tomorrow. He says nobody can be solemn on ice. I say the king managed it for forty years.",[
  ["Is Edmund a hard man to work for?","Fair, and fussy. He once measured my hedge with a ruler. It was straight. He was very disappointed."],
  ["Is everyone solemn up at the palace?","Not Oskar. He whistles in the pantry when he thinks Edmund's out. Edmund's never out."],
  ["Any gossip from the gate?","A great many boots going in, and not many coming out. Kingsgrad's busy this week."]],
 "Grit's a poor man's carpet. Watch your step."],
5:["(Viktor holds up a frozen sprig of rosemary.) Marta's herb bed. She says it'll come back in spring. I say it was dead before the snow. We haven't spoken since lunchtime.",[
  ["Who's right?","She is, usually, which is the worst of it. I'll never tell her."],
  ["Do you grow anything for the royal table?","Potatoes, leeks, a few apples. Marta grumbles about them and cooks them beautifully."],
  ["Plants have more sense than people.","Plants don't argue about succession. They just go brown and wait."]],
 "If Marta asks, I never said it was dead."],
6:["(Viktor's shed door is open, a tin mug steaming on the bench.) The church people have been walking past the gate in pairs this week. Looking up at the palace. Counting windows, I'd say.",[
  ["Why would they count windows?","They want a prince of their own. Augustus, the pious one. Perhaps they're measuring him for a bigger house."],
  ["Which prince do you prefer?","I prefer whichever one waters the hydrangeas. Neither has so far."],
  ["Can I have a look at the shed?","It's mostly spades and an old kettle. The view of the gate is the best thing in it."]],
 "Keep your hands near the stove. The rest is draughts."],
7:["(Viktor looks up from a barrow of salt.) They say the north-west road's open now, over to Frostholm. Which means oil men will be wandering up our drive by Friday.",[
  ["You don't like oil men?","I don't like anyone who looks at a lawn and sees a car park."],
  ["Do the palace staff talk about politics?","Elsa knows everything, from the washing. Ingrid and Lotta know the rest. I know the weather."],
  ["Cheer up, spring will come.","It always does. It just takes its time on Sarivalto, like most things."]],
 "Salt on the steps, salt in the stew. That's my week."],
8:["(Viktor has brought a bucket of grit and is using it to prop the gate.) Sunday, so the bells from the monastery carry down the valley. You hear them clearest from the shed. I like that.",[
  ["Are you a churchgoer?","On feast days. My wife was devout. I light a candle and let her do the praying."],
  ["Did you know the old king?","I saw him every morning at the same window. He waved at the roses, never at me. I took it as a compliment to the roses."],
  ["Any news from the gate?","A cart of lilies came in for the funeral. Lilies, in this weather. Someone has paid a lot to bring them."]],
 "Sundays are quiet. Make the most of it."],
9:["(Viktor is stacking pine boughs against the wall.) Everyone's asking Edmund for the funeral running order. He says it's in hand. That means it's in a drawer somewhere.",[
  ["What's your part in the funeral?","The paths. Sanded, swept, and not a leaf on them. Twenty-five years on the grounds, and the day arrives, and it's about the paths."],
  ["Will the whole island come?","The ones who dress properly. The rest will stand at the gate. I'll be among them, in the shed, with the door ajar."],
  ["Do you think the new king will keep you on?","Whoever gets the crown still needs a hedge cut. We gardeners outlast dynasties."]],
 "Tomorrow, then. Dress warmly, whatever else."],
10:["(Viktor is in a black coat that's too big for him.) They're carrying him down the main path now, over my sand. I stayed out of the way. A gardener at a funeral is like a cat at a christening.",[
  ["How is the palace today?","Silent. Even Marta's ladle is quiet. Oskar's polished everything twice."],
  ["Who stands where at a royal funeral?","Leopold by the coffin, Augustus a pace behind and looking at the ceiling. The family argues in how it stands."],
  ["I'm sorry for your loss.","(He nods slowly.) Thank you. He was a kind old man, whatever else they say. He never trod on a flower bed."]],
 "Go in and pay your respects. The chapel's warm."],
11:["(Viktor is raking the sand off the paths.) It's all tracks and mud now. Four hundred boots on a drive built for one carriage. And a lily on the lawn, which I'm not touching until the family says.",[
  ["Is the family still in mourning?","In black, anyway. Leopold's gone quiet. Daphne has been seen in the greenhouse more than usual."],
  ["Did anyone say anything at the gate yesterday?","A man from the Party, giving out leaflets. Very polite. He asked if I'd read them. I said I didn't read anything that came with a smile."],
  ["What happens to the funeral flowers?","The church people take half. The rest go on the compost. Nothing wasted in a garden."]],
 "Don't step on the lily. It's the only one the king would've liked."],
12:["(Viktor is wrapping the rose bushes in sacking.) Frost tonight, they say, and a harder one next week. Roses are like royalty: they wrap them in warm coverings and complain they can't breathe.",[
  ["Is that why you're doing it?","Because they can't take the cold, whatever they say. Same as the rest of us."],
  ["What do you hear at the gate these days?","Voices in the corridors, bigger than they used to be. The prince sends for the butler three times a day now."],
  ["Do you like your job?","Better than any other, on a good day. Snow's a bad day, but even that's quiet."]],
 "Frost by evening. Cover anything you love."],
13:["(Viktor is sharpening a pair of shears on a whetstone.) Edmund says the island's due a great day tomorrow, and the palace must look its best. I'm being asked to polish the gravel. I'm not sure it can be done.",[
  ["What's happening tomorrow?","The fourteenth. The big day, they keep calling it. I don't know the half of it, and I've a theory I'd rather not."],
  ["Will you be watching from the shed?","With the door ajar, and a flask of coffee. That's my seat at every great event."],
  ["Nervous?","About the hedges, yes. The rest is for people with better coats."]],
 "Sleep well. Tomorrow nobody will."],
14:["(Viktor is standing very still at the gate.) A big day, and the quietest it's been all week. I've seen four carriages go in, none come out, and a boy on a bicycle with a message for no one.",[
  ["What does a gardener think of days like this?","That the hedges will outlast whoever wins. It's a comfort. Or a very long view."],
  ["Is everyone on edge?","Oskar dropped a tray. Marta swore at the stove. Edmund said nothing, which is the loudest of the lot."],
  ["I'll leave you to it.","Please do. I'm busy not looking worried."]],
 "Out the gate and to the left. The right's all carriages."],
15:["(Viktor is mending a rake.) Yesterday's over, and the island's still here, which I'm taking as good news. The palace is full of low voices, though. Elsa says the sheets are a disgrace, and she's not talking about the laundry.",[
  ["What did she mean?","Beds slept in by people who'd rather not be seen. She won't say more. I'm not asking."],
  ["Is the family feuding?","Over the chapel, mostly. The Church and the crown are always arguing about who owns the pews."],
  ["Do you get involved?","I get involved with moss and slugs. Nothing else is in my gift."]],
 "The slugs are winning. Mind the lettuces."],
16:["(Viktor tips his cap.) There's a debate on tonight, I hear. The Party against the Democrats, in the town. Marta wants to go. I told her a debate's only two people being rude to each other politely.",[
  ["Will you listen on the radio?","If the shed's wireless picks it up. It mostly picks up the shipping forecast and static."],
  ["Which side would you vote for?","The one that leaves the hedges alone. So far none of them have promised it."],
  ["Does the palace take sides?","Officially, no. Edmund keeps a straight face. Oskar bets on the loser, to be contrary."]],
 "If I hear any good insults, I'll tell you."],
17:["(Viktor is frowning at the lawn.) The ground trembled this morning. The cup on my bench danced about an inch. The birds went silent for a minute. I don't like it.",[
  ["Could it be the volcano?","It's been asleep as long as I've lived here. My grandfather said it grumbled once or twice. Nothing came of it."],
  ["Have the others felt it?","Elsa says her washing line jumped. Marta says she imagined it. Marta felt it too, I'd wager."],
  ["Perhaps a lorry went by.","On our drive? A lorry would flatten my roses. No, that was the island clearing its throat."]],
 "Keep your boots on tonight, I'd say."],
18:["(Viktor points to a bare patch of lawn.) Another tremor in the night. Smaller than yesterday. The starlings left the roof altogether, which is the thing that worries me most.",[
  ["Animals know first, I've heard.","They do. The crow stayed, mind. He's too stubborn to leave, and too fat to fly far."],
  ["Is the palace worried?","Edmund says nothing's amiss. His left eye twitches when he says it."],
  ["What do you hear at the gate today?","A priest and a monk in earnest talk, looking at the sky. A relic's gone missing, they say. They're asking everyone."]],
 "If the crow leaves, I'm leaving too."],
19:["(Viktor is trimming a hedge with great care.) Someone's planning a masquerade in town, tonight, from what I overhear. Disguises, and a prince slipping out by the back gate when he thinks I don't see.",[
  ["Which prince?","I couldn't say. I'm only a gardener. I merely noticed a man in a false moustache leaving in a car belonging to the palace."],
  ["You see everything, don't you?","I see the gate. What goes through it is another matter, and not mine to speak of."],
  ["Is the ground still shaking?","A little, at dawn. Dust off the shelves in the shed. I've started sleeping with my boots on."]],
 "If anyone asks, I was at the compost heap."],
20:["(Viktor is peering at the sky.) There's a smudge of grey in the north, past the monastery. Steam, or cloud. The wind's brought a smell, too: eggs gone off.",[
  ["Sulphur, from the volcano?","That's my guess. The old men used to say you could smell it before it spoke."],
  ["Is it safe to stay here?","Kingsgrad is a long way from the mountain. We'd get ash, not fire. I've checked the barrow and the sheets I'll need for the roses."],
  ["Is the palace getting ready?","Marta's counting candles. Edmund's counting exits. Between them, we'll be all right."]],
 "Cover your plants, if you have any."],
21:["(Viktor is covered in grey dust.) It went up an hour ago. You could see the cloud from the shed. The sky's the colour of wet slate, and it's raining ash on my hedges.",[
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Not here. Not that I've heard. The lava's off in the north, away from people, thank God."],
  ["How are the staff taking it?","Ingrid cried a bit. Lotta fetched everyone tea. Oskar hid in the pantry, and Edmund pretended not to notice."],
  ["Is your garden ruined?","Buried. It'll be a month of sweeping before I know what's left. But gardens are patient, and so am I."]],
 "Wear a scarf over your mouth. The ash gets in everywhere."],
22:["(Viktor is shovelling ash into a barrow.) A hundredweight a minute, and the wind just keeps bringing more. I've swept the same path four times. It's like trying to empty the sea.",[
  ["Can the ash be useful?","In a year or two. Volcanic soil grows marvellous leeks. It's the interim that's the trouble."],
  ["Is the palace carrying on regardless?","Of course. Edmund's holding a meeting about the meeting. Elsa's hanging sheets indoors, grey with ash and furious."],
  ["Any visitors at the gate?","Fewer than yesterday. The ash puts them off. It's the one advantage."]],
 "If you see a clean spot on the lawn, tell me. I'll frame it."],
23:["(Viktor is knocking ash off a statue.) The tremors are less now, but we get them still, like the island has a cough. The statues look like they've been dusted with flour.",[
  ["Do you mind the aftershocks?","I mind the crockery. Marta's lost three cups already. She's blaming the volcano, not Oskar."],
  ["Are the royals still arguing?","They've gone quiet. Disasters are good for that, for a day or two."],
  ["Is there anything you miss?","The sound of the starlings. They've not come back. The crow has, and he's insufferable."]],
 "Keep your voice down near the cracked wall."],
24:["(Viktor is wiping ash from a bench.) Church people went by the gate again, in a line, with bags and blankets. A retreat to the mountains, I think. Odd time to choose, with the sky as it is.",[
  ["Will you go with them?","Not I. Someone has to see to the garden, and the garden can't climb mountains."],
  ["Why would they leave?","To pray in higher air, I'd guess. Or to get away from the ash. Or from each other. Who knows with the clergy?"],
  ["Is it quiet at the palace?","Quiet like a held breath. Whatever's coming is coming soon, and we all know it."]],
 "The wind's gone round. Ash will be on your back all day."],
25:["(Viktor is sweeping the same path again.) The princes have been walking in the grounds, one after the other, never together. I've seen Leopold stop at the same bench three times in an hour.",[
  ["What's on his mind?","The crown, I'd think. A man doesn't stare at a hedge for a quarter of an hour for the hedge's sake."],
  ["Have you spoken to him?","He asked me what I thought of the lawn. I said it needed rain. He said he agreed, and left."],
  ["Does the palace expect a change?","Everyone does. Nobody knows what kind. We're all pretending to look at the weather."]],
 "Don't lean on that bench. It's wet and it's grey."],
26:["(Viktor is carrying a bundle of rakes.) Cars at the gate all morning. Some with flags, some with none. A man in a Stetson tried to hand me a card. I said I wasn't in the market for oil.",[
  ["Was he an oil man?","He had boots as expensive as my shed. I'd say Frostholm, with a lot of money and no sense."],
  ["Is everyone calling at the palace?","The factions, in turn. Like callers at a sickbed, with their own bottle of medicine."],
  ["Do you get tired of watching?","Never. It's the best show on the island, and I've got the best seat."]],
 "Tomorrow'll be busier. Bring a flask."],
27:["(Viktor is polishing the gate lock with an oily rag.) Edmund says no one's to be let in without an appointment from tomorrow. I said that's a lot of gate for one old man to guard.",[
  ["Will you manage?","I'll have Oskar. He'll let in anyone who flatters his waistcoat."],
  ["What's the hurry?","A state visit, I've heard. A foreign someone, with a car and a flag. The whole drive must be raked."],
  ["Does everyone know what's coming?","The staff do, roughly. The royals know exactly, and aren't telling. They never do."]],
 "The drive's raked. For all the good it'll do."],
28:["(Viktor stands very straight at the gate.) Today's the big push for every flag on the island, they say. I've counted five cars so far and a man on a bicycle with a banner. A banner! On a bicycle!",[
  ["Which side was the banner?","Red. And rather large. He wobbled a good deal."],
  ["Are you nervous?","About the hedges, yes. Everyone else will be fine. They have speeches."],
  ["What does a gardener hope for?","A quiet winter, and a spring with proper rain. The rest is for kings and clerks."]],
 "Cars are lining up. Go round the back, if you'd like a calm path."],
29:["(Viktor is leaning on his shovel.) Tomorrow it's all decided, and today everyone is polite to me. A footman gave me a cigar. Marta gave me a pie. I'm suspicious.",[
  ["Why suspicious?","People are only kind to the gardener when they want something, or when the end of the world's coming."],
  ["What will you do tomorrow?","The paths again, and the gate. Whoever's won, they'll still want a clear way in."],
  ["Are you hopeful?","As hopeful as a man can be who's seen forty winters and a volcano."]],
 "Eat the pie. It's good, whatever the reason."],
30:["(Viktor is standing at the gate with his cap in his hands.) Well. It's the last day, and the island's about to find out where it's going. I've swept the drive for it, at any rate.",[
  ["How are you feeling?","Like the evening before a first frost. Nothing to be done, but everything has been done."],
  ["What will you remember about September?","The snow leaving, the king going, the ash coming. And you, wandering about with your notebook."],
  ["Any last words for the paper?","Print that the hedges are in good order. Nobody ever prints that."]],
 "Thank you for asking me things. Nobody ever does."],
};
// Ville, newsstand vendor outside Kelp Cola HQ in Kingsgrad (no faction). Threads: watching the comings and goings by the palace and the cola offices, headlines, his neighbour Rosa and her hot dog cart, Phil and Dex, the tram, the ash.
DAILY['Ville']={
2:["(Ville lowers a newspaper and peers over it.) New face. You'll be the reporter. I saw you coming from the tram. Slightly lost, slightly cold, very observant. A professional.",[
  ["Good eye.","It's the job. I stand here all day and watch the world walk by. It's the best seat on the island."],
  ["What do you see?","The cola men in, the cola men out. A palace car at ten, another at two. Everyone's in a hurry, and nobody buys a paper."],
  ["Which paper's best?","The Gazette, for the crossword. The rest is opinion, and opinions are free."]],
 "Buy something. I've a family. Well, a cat."],
3:["(Ville nods towards the gate.) That's Phil, the cola chief, getting out of the car. Never carries a coat. Thinks it makes him look young. He's blue by noon.",[
  ["Does Phil buy a paper?","Every morning. He turns to the business page and then folds it up very fast. Something's never right."],
  ["Who else comes by?","Dex, with his hot dog cart. Rosa, with hers, in the next lane. And the tram, every twelve minutes."],
  ["Is the cola business doing well?","They sell more than they ever did. And they look more worried than they ever did. Work that one out."]],
 "Mind the tram. It doesn't stop for reporters."],
4:["Rosa and I share the pavement, and a great many opinions. (Ville gestures at a hot dog cart.) She says I read too much. I say she talks too much. We're both right.",[
  ["Is Rosa a friend?","The best. We live in the same block. She hears everything in the square and I see everything on the road. Between us, we've the whole story."],
  ["What does she say about the Party?","She says they're all talk and no mustard. She thinks the Democrats are too thin."],
  ["Do you share what you know?","Over a hot dog, at lunchtime. It's a very democratic arrangement."]],
 "Tell her I said hello. She'll say I owe her a coin."],
5:["(Ville folds a page, reading it with pursed lips.) The Gazette's gone for 'Seven Factions and a Funeral.' Nice rhythm. I'd have said eight, counting the cat. Now, here's a thing: a man in grey walked past four times, and never bought a paper.",[
  ["Who was he?","I don't know. He was looking at the HQ gate. Four times, in an hour. Either he's lost, or he's counting."],
  ["Did you tell anyone?",["I told Rosa. She said he was a spy. I said he was a surveyor. We bet a hot dog.",[
    ["Who won?","Neither. He turned up again with a clipboard and said he was from the tram company. We've both decided to be suspicious."],
    ["Do you think he was a spy?","Everyone's a spy until they buy a paper."]]]],
  ["Do you like watching people?","Better than watching television. It has plots. And no adverts."]],
 "If you see him, give me a signal. A cough will do."],
6:["(Ville raises a paper like a flag.) 'Eastbrook Open, Frostholm Waiting.' It's a very good headline. I sold forty of them before nine. The cola men bought ten, to see if they were in it.",[
  ["Were they?","Page four. A small photo. Phil looked startled, as if he'd been caught in the act of being photographed."],
  ["Do they read the paper?","They read themselves. It's the only part they like."],
  ["What does the island read?","Whatever tells them they're right. It's the oldest rule in the business."]],
 "Come again tomorrow. There'll be a new face on the front."],
7:["(Ville is stacking a bundle.) Every road's open, and the stand's heaving. An oil man asked for a map of the whole island. I sold him one. He paid for two. He said it was a 'consultancy fee'.",[
  ["Did he look lost?","He looked like a man who's trying to find a hole in the ground. Which, in his business, is entirely natural."],
  ["Do you sell many maps?","To reporters, tourists and spies. And the three look the same."],
  ["Is the cola HQ busy?","Like an anthill after rain. Men in and out, all day, with folders."]],
 "Take a map. A reporter's lost without one."],
8:["(Ville has the radio on, a sermon rolling.) Sunday. The tram's half-empty, the stand's slow, and Phil's in the office. He never takes a day off. A man like that has a secret, or a very big debt.",[
  ["What does Phil do all day?","Strategy, they call it. A great deal of walking up and down and looking at the floor."],
  ["Does anyone ever leave early?","Dex does. He comes out at four, smelling of caramel, with a bottle in each pocket."],
  ["Do you go to church?","Occasionally. For the singing. And to see who's there."]],
 "A quiet day's a good day. Don't tell the editor."],
9:["(Ville has a new bottle of cola on the stand, labelled in a different colour.) New Formula, it says. And at the other end of the street, a rival: The People's Cola. I've two bottles and no opinions.",[
  ["Is the formula different?","I couldn't tell. It's as brown, as sweet, and as loud. They say the secret's in the bubbles."],
  ["Who's making the new one?","The Party. At least, they say so. The label says 'by the workers, for the workers'."],
  ["What's Dex say?","He says the old one's fine. He says it with a mouth full of free samples."]],
 "Don't drink either. Water's cheaper and nobody's selling it."],
10:["(Ville has a black ribbon around the stand.) The day of the funeral. They've closed the road to the palace and half the staff are in black. The cola HQ is flying the flag low. They're very proper.",[
  ["Did you go?","I watched from the corner. The coffin's very plain. It's the loudest silence I've known."],
  ["What did the papers say?","'The King Is Dead.' I sold more copies than ever. It's an awful thing to say, but a funeral's good for circulation."],
  ["Is Phil in mourning?","He's in a black tie and a blue mood. He keeps looking at a sheet of paper."]],
 "I'll fold the paper tonight. A little early. Out of respect."],
11:["(Ville is arranging papers by hand.) The mill pay's the story, and the church's alms. And two men in suits who've been standing at the gate since seven, pretending to look at the tram timetable.",[
  ["Who are they?","One's a Democrat, the other's a Communist. They've been told to watch the other. Neither has said a word."],
  ["Is it a good story?","Very. 'The Watchers.' I'll hold it until one of them sits down."],
  ["Do you like the Democrats?","I like the tea. And the way Erik shakes everyone's hand and apologises."]],
 "If they get hungry, Rosa has hot dogs. She's an equal opportunity vendor."],
12:["(Ville whistles, sorting papers.) A man in a fur collar came by. Asked for a map and a very specific newspaper, a month old. Then he asked if I knew Ruby, who keeps records. I said everyone knows Ruby.",[
  ["Who was he?","From Frostholm. Oil. The collar's a giveaway. He had a very thick wallet and a very thin smile."],
  ["What did he want with the record?","I don't know. It's old, it's rare, and it's in Eastbrook. That's all I got, and I'd say that's a headline."],
  ["Did you tell Rosa?","Of course. She's already planned a trip."]],
 "If you hear anything, I'll pay in biscuits."],
13:["(Ville sits beside a stack of cola crates.) Free samples again. They've given me four crates, an apron and a hat. I'm to hand out cola at the kiosk and say 'enjoy!' I've said it nineteen times. I've never meant it.",[
  ["Do people like it?","Some. Children love it. Adults pretend to, and bring back the bottle."],
  ["Is it good for the stand?","A crowd's a crowd. Some of them even buy a paper."],
  ["How's Rosa?","She's selling hot dogs to people with cola. They're the best of friends. The cola, the hot dog, and my profit."]],
 "Take a bottle. And take the hat, if you like. It's ridiculous."],
14:["(Ville has put a sign on the stand: 'Gone to Watch'.) The whole street's empty. I'm taking the tram to the big day. I'll sit near the back and watch the watchers.",[
  ["What will you see?","Everything, if I'm lucky. Faces in a crowd tell you more than speeches."],
  ["Are you worried?","I'm curious. It's the only safe thing to be."],
  ["Will you write it up?","In my head. I've no paper. I sell them, I don't write them."]],
 "If you see a tall man in a grey coat, don't wave. Just remember."],
15:["(Ville taps the Gazette.) 'Crown Before Cross.' And on the next page, 'Cross Before Crown'. Same paper. Same day. I've sold both, to the same man.",[
  ["Who was he?","A priest, I think. He bought the first, and then the second, and sat on the bench to read them against each other."],
  ["Do you take a side?","I stand in the middle. It's a very good spot for sales."],
  ["Will there be a fight?","In the square, perhaps. Rosa's hot dogs have never been more popular."]],
 "Read both. It's the only way to get the truth."],
16:["(Ville has a radio, propped on the stand.) The Great Debate's on, and I can't hear a word. The tram's too loud. I think that's the best thing that's happened to politics all year.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The tram. It's got more horsepower."],
  ["Do you want to listen?","Between ringing the bell and the brakes. I get a word in four."],
  ["Would you rather watch?","I'd rather watch the audience. They never lie."]],
 "If you hear the end, tell me. I'll nod and say 'obviously'."],
17:["(The stand shudders, and Ville grabs the papers.) Did you feel that? The ground. It's been going all morning. The cola bottles are singing.",[
  ["Is it the volcano?","They say so. They also say it's the tram. I've decided it's the Party."],
  ["Where's Dario?","I'd like to know. The man's been off the island's radar since yesterday. The oil men in the street look like they've lost a dog."],
  ["Are you worried?","A little. But a newsstand's a very stable thing. It's mostly paper."]],
 "If it gets worse, stand under the arch. It's old and wide."],
18:["(Ville sniffs the air.) There's a smell. A sulphur kind of smell. The cola lot say it's the new formula. Rosa says it's the mustard. I say it's the mountain.",[
  ["Are you sure?","Not at all. But the paper says there's steam on the horizon. I sold three hundred copies with that headline."],
  ["Is Phil nervous?","He's buying torches. A man who sells cola buying torches. I'd write that up, if I wrote."],
  ["What's the mood?","Excited. And worried. It's a very good mix for sales."]],
 "Take a paper. It's the last on the pile with the good photo."],
19:["(Ville gestures at a poster.) 'Comrades Wanted.' It's all over the lamp-posts. A man gave me one, said the Party needs people who 'observe'. I said, I observe all day, and they haven't paid me yet.",[
  ["Are you going to apply?","I'm waiting for a better offer. Perhaps the Democrats."],
  ["Is Rosa tempted?","She said she'd join if they served hot dogs. They said they'd think about it."],
  ["Who's been watching the stand?","A man in a very new coat. He bought a paper, asked for a receipt, and wrote something down. Nobody asks for a receipt for a paper."]],
 "If you see him again, send him over. I've a receipt book."],
20:["(Ville lowers his voice.) The Hotel Aurora's been busy. Two black cars, three men in hats and a woman who didn't speak. They came out at dawn. I saw it from the tram stop.",[
  ["What are they doing there?","Meeting someone. Or watching someone. Or both. It's a hotel. That's all it's for."],
  ["Do you know who's staying?","Someone important. The staff won't say. But there are twenty new beds, I'm told."],
  ["Would you print it?","In a minute. If someone paid for the ink."]],
 "I never saw a thing. I was looking at the tram."],
21:["(Ville stands in a rain of grey flakes, holding a newspaper over his head.) The volcano! It's blown! Ash in the street, on the tram, on the cola! It looks like the whole island's been dusted with flour!",[
  ["Are you hurt?","Not a scratch. Coughing like a goat, but alive. The stand's a ruin, though."],
  ["Is anyone hurt?","Nobody. Not here, nor in the north. It's a miracle. A gritty one."],
  ["What will the paper say?","'Mountain Erupts. Island Survives.' It's a very good headline. It's also true."]],
 "Cover your mouth. The grit's worse than the smoke."],
22:["(Ville is sweeping, with the Gazette as a dustpan.) The tram's running late, the kiosk's grey, and I've sold out of cloths. Rosa's using her apron on the cart.",[
  ["How is Rosa?","Furious and cheerful. She says the mustard's gone grey. She won't sell it."],
  ["Is Kelp Cola closed?","Not a chance. They've a crew at the gate with brushes. Phil's in a mask, giving orders."],
  ["What are people reading?","The special edition. 'Ash'. One word. It's selling like cake."]],
 "If you've a broom, bring it. I'll pay in papers."],
23:["(Ville wipes a paper on his knee.) The Party's up to something, 'Spanner in the Works', I heard. And the oil company's 'Deep Repairs'. I've seen two vans from each. They didn't speak.",[
  ["What are they doing?","Fixing, or breaking. It's hard to tell, with the ash."],
  ["Is the cola HQ safe?","Safer than most. It's concrete and spite."],
  ["Is Phil involved?","Phil's involved in everything. He just doesn't know it yet."]],
 "Don't ask me what's going on. I only sell the paper."],
24:["(Ville leans on the stand, tired.) The clean-up's started. A great many men with hoses and no badges. They're thorough. They were at the palace gates twice.",[
  ["Who's paying them?","Somebody with a very big budget and a very small conscience."],
  ["Did they find anything?","They found a lot of ash. They took it very seriously."],
  ["What about the monks?","Heading for the peaks. A string of them, on mules, in the rain. It was like a procession in a very strange film."]],
 "Buy a paper. It's grey, but it's current."],
25:["(Ville holds up a poster.) A crown for everyone, a princess for democracy. It has a certain ring. And a certain absurdity. The best slogans always do.",[
  ["Do you support it?","I support the printer. He's had a fortnight of work. A cheerful man, now."],
  ["Who's the princess?","I don't know. I've a theory, but it involves a hat."],
  ["Is the street excited?","Cautiously. It's been a long week."]],
 "I'll sell you a poster. They're very cheap, and it's a very good frame."],
26:["(Ville peers at a bottle.) A prince, on a label. 'A Prince in Every Bottle.' I'd never have thought of it. Dex says it's a stroke of genius. Rosa says it's a stroke.",[
  ["Is it selling?","Everyone wants the prince. The cola's an afterthought."],
  ["Who's the prince?","Leopold, with a very good jaw. They've airbrushed the rest."],
  ["Does Phil like it?","He's smiling. It's the first time I've seen his teeth."]],
 "Buy one. Keep the label. It might be valuable."],
27:["(Ville has a ticket in his hat.) A film tonight. The Party's booked the cinema. 'Revolution at the Pictures'. I'm told it's three hours of men in caps.",[
  ["Will you go?","I'll go for the interval. I've heard they hand out cola."],
  ["Is Rosa going?","She's got a cart outside, for the crowd. She plans to make a fortune."],
  ["Is there a card game too?","High Stakes. Somewhere in Hope Springs. I've been told not to ask."]],
 "If you see a film star, wave. I've never seen one."],
28:["(Ville has strung a line of bunting along the stand.) The big push. Every faction's in the square, shouting. I've sold a paper to each and told each they're winning. It's a very nice day's work.",[
  ["Is that honest?","It's diplomatic. A newsagent's the only neutral country on the island."],
  ["Are you nervous?","Not for me. For the island, a little."],
  ["What do you expect tomorrow?","A last push, a last speech, and a very large crowd. Rosa's ordered a thousand rolls."]],
 "Take a paper. It'll be a collector's item."],
29:["(Ville stands very still, his papers in a neat stack.) Tomorrow's the end of it. I've been here all month, watching the same gate, and tomorrow it all changes. Or doesn't.",[
  ["Are you ready?","The stand's stocked. The tram's on time. Everything else, I leave to others."],
  ["Who do you think will win?","The one who looks most surprised. It's always the winner."],
  ["Any last advice?","Keep your eyes open and your coat on. And buy a paper."]],
 "I'll be here at six. Come and see it start."],
30:["(Ville is holding the final edition in both hands.) The last day. The front page is blank, it's waiting for the news. I've never seen a paper so full of nothing and so heavy.",[
  ["How has the month been?","The busiest of my life. I've sold more papers than in the last three years. And I've never been so tired."],
  ["What will you remember?","The ash on the tram. The cat. Rosa's mustard in the grey. It was beautiful, in its way."],
  ["Goodbye, then?","Come back. I'll keep you a paper. A good one. Not a grey one."]],
 "Take the last copy. It's yours, no charge."],
};
// Vince, who deals blackjack and works the cashier's desk at the casino in Frostholm, and lives in Kingsgrad (The Proprietors).
// Threads: snowed off work until the north road opens, then back on the floor; who owes the Syndicate (he knows, and only
// hints); the generous Russian timber man who loses on purpose to the right people; his Monte Carlo savings in a biscuit tin.
DAILY['Vince']={
2:["(Vince is at his kitchen table, dealing patience in a cardigan.) You're the reporter. Come in, shut the door. I work the casino up in Frostholm, and the road north's buried, so I'm dealing to myself.",[
  ["Who's winning?","The house. Even when I'm the house and the player, the house wins. It's a law, like gravity."],
  ["What's the casino like?",["Slot machines, roulette, and the blackjack tables, which are mine. Frostholm money, oil money, and a bit of money that's better not asked about.",[
    ["Whose money's that?","(He smiles and turns a card.) The kind that comes in through the front door and goes out through the back. That's all a dealer says."],
    ["Do people win?","Some do, on a Tuesday. Nobody does over a year. I've seen fortunes made and lost at one table in one night."]]]],
  ["Patience in a cardigan. Very glamorous.","I'm on my day off. My forty-third, it feels like. In Frostholm I wear a waistcoat and bow tie. Here I wear what my mother knitted."]],
 "Shut the door on the way out. The cards blow about."],
3:["Still snowed off. (He holds up a battered postcard: palm trees, a white building, a blue sea.) Monte Carlo. One day I'll deal there. Or play there. Or just stand in it.",[
  ["Have you been?","Never. A croupier from Monaco came through the casino in 1979 and left this on my table as a tip. He said I had good hands."],
  ["How far off is it?","About eleven thousand dollars, by my reckoning. (He taps a biscuit tin on the shelf.) I'm a little short."],
  ["Monte Carlo's overrated.","Says a man who's never been warm. Look at that sea. You could put your feet in it."]],
 "Go on. I'll be here, saving."],
4:["(Vince is shuffling with one hand, endlessly.) Somebody telephoned asking when the casino reopens. Third time. A gambler without a table's like a fish on a dock.",[
  ["Who rang?","Nobody you'd know. Somebody who's lost a lot and needs to win it back before someone notices. That's always who rings."],
  ["Show me a trick.","(He fans the deck, closes it, and turns the top card: the queen of hearts.) Not a trick. Practice. Fourteen years of it."],
  ["Is gambling a sickness?","For some. For others it's the only time in the week they feel lucky. I'm not a doctor. I just deal."]],
 "Mind the snow on the steps. It's deep this year."],
5:["Kingsgrad's waking up. I walked to the City Bar yesterday and Eddie poured me a measure that could have floated a boat. Never tells you anything, Eddie. I like that in a person.",[
  ["Do you tell people things?","I tell nobody anything. I know a great deal. Those two things are how you stay healthy in a casino."],
  ["What do you know?",["I know who owes money to whom. (He lowers his voice.) The Syndicate keeps a book. Half this island is in it.",[
    ["Is anyone I've met in it?","Probably. Someone at the palace likes roulette. Someone in a dog collar's been seen at the slots. I don't say names. I value my kneecaps."],
    ["Who keeps the book?","Nico, in Frostholm, and Paolo in Eastbrook. Mario did too, till the police got him. Nobody's quite sure who has his share now."]]]],
  ["Sounds like you're bored.","I'm going mad. I counted the tiles on the kitchen floor. Then I counted them again in case I'd made a mistake."]],
 "Go on. If you see a snowplough, tell it Vince says hurry."],
6:["Hope Springs road's open, they say, and the north road's tomorrow. (He's brushing a velvet waistcoat.) Getting the work clothes ready. It's like the first day at school.",[
  ["Who lives in Hope Springs?","Phil, Dario, Gustavo. The big villas. Two of them play at my tables, and one of them owns the man who owns the tables."],
  ["What do you look forward to most?","The noise. The wheel, the chips, the bells on the slots. A quiet house gets into your head."],
  ["How much is in the tin now?","Same as last week. Sixty-four dollars short of being sixty-four dollars more. I haven't earned a cent in a month."]],
 "If the road's open tomorrow, come and lose some money. I'll be gentle."],
7:["(Vince is at the cashier's desk in Frostholm, in waistcoat and bow tie, counting chips.) Back on the floor! The road's open, the wheel's spinning, and I've already seen two men lose their shirts. Welcome to Frostholm.",[
  ["Two already?","Gamblers who've been snowed in for a month. They come in with a month's wages and a month's patience used up. It's like feeding sharks."],
  ["What's it like on a busy night?","Oil men at the tables, Syndicate men by the door, and a Russian timber man buying drinks for everyone. That's Frostholm."],
  ["Deal me in.","Blackjack's a dollar a hand. Sit down. I'll deal straight. I always deal straight. That's the only rule I've got."]],
 "Good luck. You'll need it. Everybody does."],
8:["The Russian was in again. The timber man from the Hotel Aurora. He loses on purpose, you know. Only to certain people.",[
  ["How can you tell?","He sits down with a man he wants to know, plays badly all night, and pushes his chips across like he's paying for dinner. And he smiles. Real losers don't smile."],
  ["Who does he lose to?","A man from the docks last week. A Party man. And somebody from the oil company, the night before the rig went wrong. Don't write that down."],
  ["Is that a crime?","Losing at cards? It's the most legal thing anyone does in here. That's what's clever about it."]],
 "Go on. Keep your eyes open. That's free advice."],
9:["(Vince is running a deck through his fingers.) Quiet afternoon. A Kelp Cola man came in trying to get the casino to sell their new flavour at the bar. The manager said he'd have to taste it first. He's still tasting.",[
  ["What does he think?","He thinks it tastes like a bribe. That's high praise from the manager."],
  ["Do you drink cola?","I drink coffee, black, from six till six. It's the only thing that keeps a dealer's hands steady."],
  ["Has the Russian been back?","Last night. Bought the whole bar a vodka and lost forty dollars to one of the fishermen from Saltwick. The fisherman thought he was a genius. I didn't correct him."]],
 "Mind the slot machines on your way out. One of them bites."],
10:["(The casino is half-empty and Vince has a black band on his sleeve.) Funeral today. Half my regulars went in black and came back here in black, to forget it. There's a lot of black at the tables.",[
  ["Did the king gamble?","Once a year, at the charity night. He'd play one hand, lose, and give the casino a medal for it. We've got six medals for losing."],
  ["Who's here?","Oil men and Syndicate men, the ones who weren't asked. They're toasting him with the house's whisky. Very touching."],
  ["Are you all right?","I'm a dealer. I'm always all right. That's the face. Underneath I'm thinking the island's changing and I'm stood still at a table."]],
 "Go on. Wear something warm for the walk."],
11:["Nico came round to the desk with his little book and asked me about three names. I told him I'd seen none of them. I'd seen all of them.",[
  ["Why lie to him?","Because they're my regulars, and because once Nico knows something, Gustavo knows it. And I'd like my regulars to keep their fingers."],
  ["Who were the names?","(He smiles.) I've forgotten them already. A good dealer has a terrible memory, on purpose."],
  ["Isn't that dangerous for you?","Everything's dangerous for me. I count chips in a building full of oil money and Syndicate men. You get used to it, like the cold."]],
 "If anyone asks, you've never been here."],
12:["(Vince is making a careful tower of chips.) The Petro Tower crowd were in last night, and they were sober. Oil men, sober, in a casino. Something's going on with their money.",[
  ["How can you tell?","They counted their change twice and left before midnight. An oil man who leaves before midnight has an accountant on his mind."],
  ["Who was there?","Clerks, mostly, and one of the executives, Magnus, very friendly with everyone. Friendly's always the worst sign."],
  ["Maybe they just lost.","They didn't play. That's what worried me. People who don't play at a casino are here to watch someone."]],
 "Go on. If you're in Frostholm, keep your wallet buttoned."],
13:["(Vince is scratching a new figure on the lid of his biscuit tin.) A good night. A tourist from Helsinki tipped me twenty dollars after a winning hand. Monte Carlo's twenty dollars closer.",[
  ["How much now?","Four hundred and thirty-one dollars. And eleven thousand to go. At this rate I'll get there when I'm a hundred, and my hands will shake."],
  ["Who tips best?","Winners tip, losers don't. Oil men tip the least. Fishermen tip the most. Funny world."],
  ["Why Monte Carlo, Vince?","Because there's no snow, and nobody there knows who owes who on Sarivalto. I'd like to know nothing for once."]],
 "Big day tomorrow. Half the floor's going. I'm working."],
14:["(The casino's almost empty.) Everyone's gone to the big day. It's me, two slot machines and Arto from the street, asleep on the bench. Lovely.",[
  ["Don't you want to see it?","I'd like to. But somebody has to watch the money, and the money doesn't go to gatherings."],
  ["What does Frostholm expect?","The oil men think it's their day. The Syndicate don't care whose day it is, as long as people bet on it. Nico's taking odds."],
  ["What are Nico's odds?","On what happens today? I won't say. Gambling on the island's future's a sin, even for Nico. He does it anyway."]],
 "Go on, go and see it. Tell me who looked worried."],
15:["Sunday. Church people come in on a Sunday and pretend they're lost. (He nods at a man at the slots.) That one's been lost every week for a year.",[
  ["Who is he?","A man with a collection plate in his past and a slot machine in his future. That's all you'll get from me."],
  ["Do you go to church?","My mother did. She lit a candle for my soul every Sunday, because I worked in a 'den of iniquity'. It's carpeted. Dens aren't carpeted."],
  ["Church or crown, Vince?","Whichever one doesn't close the casino. The church would, I think. The crown liked its charity night."]],
 "Go on. Light a candle for me while you're up there."],
16:["The Communists and the Democrats had their debate today. Nico had a book on it. Nobody could agree who'd won, so nobody's been paid. There's nearly been a fight at the door.",[
  ["How do you bet on a debate?","Who talks longest, who loses their temper first, who gets the biggest cheer. Nico's very creative."],
  ["Did you bet?","I never bet. I've seen what it does. I deal, I watch, I go home to my tin."],
  ["Who lost their temper first?","Depends who you ask. Everybody in this room's lying, because everybody's got money on it."]],
 "Go on. And don't bet on anything Nico's running."],
17:["(The chandelier sways, and the roulette ball jumps out of the wheel.) There. Did you see that? The ground's shaking, and Dario's gone missing. Frostholm's in a state.",[
  ["Dario's missing?","Nobody's seen him since yesterday. His men have been in here twice, turning over tables. They think he might've come in for a hand. He never plays."],
  ["What about the shaking?","The wheel's never thrown a ball before. The manager says it's a fault. I say the island's got the shakes, like a gambler on a bad night."],
  ["Who'd want Dario gone?","(He looks at you very hard.) Here? Ask who wouldn't. And then don't ask anyone else."]],
 "Go on. Careful out there. It's not a good day for questions."],
18:["Nico's book's got a page about yesterday, and it's not about horses. Everyone's betting on where Dario got to. I've told the manager it's in poor taste. He opened a book on that too.",[
  ["Is there any news?","There's always news. There's never any truth. The oil company won't say a word, and the Syndicate's saying even less, very loudly."],
  ["Is Nico worried?","Nico's never worried. Nico's paid. Today he's very, very busy writing small numbers in a small book."],
  ["You look tired.","I worked a double. The shakes kept the slots going off by themselves, and every time they did, a gambler ran over thinking it was a win."]],
 "Go on. Sleep for me. I'll be here."],
19:["(Vince is opening a letter at the desk.) A tournament. High stakes. In the pizzeria back room, on the twenty-seventh. Gustavo's hosting, and I'm asked to deal.",[
  ["That's an honour.","It's an order wrapped in an honour. You say yes, or you find out what's inside the wrapping."],
  ["Who's playing?","Everyone who matters, they say. Oil, cola, the Syndicate, maybe a Russian. A dealer at that table hears enough to sink an island."],
  ["Will you deal straight?",["Always. (He holds up his hands.) These deal straight or they don't deal. That's why Gustavo asked me.",[
    ["Even for Gustavo?","Especially for Gustavo. A crooked dealer only gets caught once. A straight one gets asked back."],
    ["What does it pay?","Enough for the tin to look interested. And a promise of a very long life. That's the bonus."]]]]],
 "Go on. Keep it quiet. Gustavo likes his surprises."],
20:["The Democrats have been asking questions about the Russian at the Aurora, Greta says. He came in last night, lost two hundred dollars to an Eastbrook man, laughed, and left early.",[
  ["Lost to a Democrat?","An Eastbrook man, in a coat too thin for Frostholm. Your Russian lost like a man paying a fine. Then he looked at the door the whole time."],
  ["Who's Greta?","Hotel Aurora's receptionist. Knows every guest's name and room. Comes in Fridays and plays one hand. Always stands on fourteen. Never tells me why."],
  ["Is the Russian really a timber man?","He talks about timber like I talk about fishing: as a man who's seen a picture of it."]],
 "Go on. And if anyone asks you the time, look at your watch first."],
21:["(The casino is dark, lit by candles, and Vince is behind the desk with a torch.) The power's gone and the mountain's up. Everybody ran outside. I stayed to guard the money. Somebody had to.",[
  ["Is everyone all right?","As far as I know. The floor's empty, the wheel's stopped, and the slots are silent. I've never heard it so quiet."],
  ["You stayed for the money?","For the job. If a single chip's missing tomorrow, it's my fault. Volcanoes don't count with the manager."],
  ["What does it look like outside?","Red, in the north, through the ash. Like the whole sky's a roulette wheel landing on red."]],
 "Go on, find somewhere with a roof. And keep your head down."],
22:["The power's back and so are the gamblers. Nothing makes a man want to bet like nearly dying. (He waves at a full table.) Look at them go.",[
  ["How's the casino?","Ash in the carpet, ash on the felt, ash in the slot machines. One of them pays out in grey dust now. It's the most popular one."],
  ["How's your house in Kingsgrad?","The roof held and the tin's safe. I checked it first, before the windows. I know what that says about me."],
  ["Isn't it grim, betting after that?","It's human. People want to feel lucky. The mountain didn't kill them, so they think it's their night. Most of them are wrong."]],
 "Shake the ash off before you sit down. The felt's new."],
23:["(Vince is pinning a new postcard to the cashier's window: Monte Carlo again, the harbour.) A second one. A tourist sent it from Monaco, he'd been through here last year. Says the sea's still warm.",[
  ["Sounds like a sign.","Signs are what gamblers read. I'm a dealer. I read numbers. But I did look at it a long time."],
  ["How's the tin?","Five hundred and eighty dollars. It went up after the volcano. People tip when they're glad to be alive."],
  ["Why not just go?","Go on what? Five hundred dollars? I'd get to Helsinki and deal blackjack in a basement."]],
 "Go on. I'll be here, looking at postcards."],
24:["Nico was in, looking pale, for Nico. Something's going on with the Syndicate today. He's had three men in and out of the back office and none of them have played.",[
  ["What's going on?","I don't ask what's in the back office. That's the rule that keeps me dealing and not swimming."],
  ["You seem worried.","I'm careful. It's not the same. Worried's when you don't know what's happening. Careful's when you know not to find out."],
  ["Is the tournament still on?","Still on. Gustavo doesn't cancel things. He sent the cards round already: new decks, sealed. I'm to open them at the table, in front of everyone."]],
 "If you've got business in Frostholm today, finish it early."],
25:["(Vince is practising a riffle shuffle with two sealed decks.) Two days. I've been practising every hand. Gustavo likes it smooth. One slip and it's a story for the rest of my life.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","My hands aren't. The rest of me is. That's the trick: keep the nerves in the shoulders, never let them get to the fingers."],
  ["Who's in the tournament?","I hear the oil company, Kelp Cola and the Syndicate's own men. And a few people with something to prove. That's the most dangerous kind."],
  ["Teach me something.","(He lays out cards.) Watch the hands, not the faces. Faces lie for a living. Hands only lie when they're good."]],
 "Go on. Watch the hands."],
26:["Dario was here last night talking about the spa on the mountain. He wants to buy it, he says. He told the whole bar how he's going to put a casino in it.",[
  ["A casino at the spa?","With volcano views and a steam room for the losers to cry in. He's very excited. The manager's very quiet."],
  ["Will he get it?","Dario gets most things he wants. The question is what he pays for it, and who. That's always the question."],
  ["You'd lose your job.","If he builds it, maybe he'll want a dealer. Volcano Monte Carlo. Don't laugh. I nearly asked."]],
 "Go on. If you see Saga at the spa, tell her I'll take a towel job if it comes to it."],
27:["(Vince is in his best waistcoat, with a fresh shave.) The tournament's today. I'm off to the pizzeria in an hour. If I don't come back, the tin goes to my mother.",[
  ["You'll be fine.","I'll be fine if nobody cheats. If somebody cheats, I'll have to say so. Then I'll be interesting."],
  ["Will I see you there?","Everyone who matters is there, they say. If you're there, you matter, or you're being watched. Possibly both."],
  ["What's your advice for the table?","Fold more than you want to. And never, ever, look at Gustavo when you're bluffing."]],
 "Wish me luck. Dealers aren't supposed to need it."],
28:["(Vince looks like he hasn't slept, and is very happy about it.) The tournament's done. I dealt every hand straight. My fingers didn't shake once. I won't say who won.",[
  ["Not even a hint?","Not for a hundred dollars. Not for Monte Carlo. A dealer who talks deals once."],
  ["What did you hear at the table?","Enough to know this island's being decided at card tables as much as anywhere else. Then I forgot it all. On purpose."],
  ["The whole island's out today.","And the casino's packed with people who don't want to be out there. Frostholm hides from history in here. I don't blame it."]],
 "Go on. I've a tin to count."],
29:["Gustavo's man came by with an envelope. The tournament fee. (He holds up the biscuit tin.) It's heavy. For the first time, it's heavy.",[
  ["How heavy?","Not Monte Carlo heavy. But a boat ticket and a month somewhere warm, heavy. I keep lifting it."],
  ["Will you go?","After tomorrow. When I know what kind of island I'd be leaving. If there's an island to come back to, I'll come back."],
  ["What's tomorrow mean for the casino?","Depends who wins. Some of them would close it. Some would buy it. Some would gamble it away. I'll deal whatever happens."]],
 "Go on. Get some sleep. I won't."],
30:["(Vince is at the desk, with the Monte Carlo postcards in his breast pocket.) The last day. Whatever they decide out there, I'll know by tonight whether to stay or pack.",[
  ["What will you do if you go?","Find the sea, put my feet in it, and deal cards to strangers who don't know who owes who. Bliss."],
  ["What if you stay?","Then I'll be here, at this desk, watching the island lose its shirt and win it back. Somebody has to."],
  ["Deal me one last hand.","(He deals: an ace and a king.) Blackjack. On the house. Don't tell the manager."]],
 "Go on. Good luck. You'll need it. We all do, today."],
};
// Wilma, curator of the Kelp Cola Museum and clerk in its gift shop, Eastbrook (Kelp Cola). Threads: dating bottles by sight and smell,
// the Kelpy figurines she sells, Jaana whom she protects, her loyalty to the old company against Kurt's new ideas, her private grief.
DAILY['Wilma']={
4:["(A woman in a cardigan looks up from a bottle held to the light.) 1948. Hand-blown, the seam at the neck gives it away. You're the reporter. Mind the glass, please, and don't breathe on the labels.",[
  ["That's impressive. Can you date them all?","Any bottle on the island, on sight. People test me. They've never caught me out. Well, twice, and both times the bottle was a fake."],
  ["Is this museum really worth a visit?","It's the only honest record of the company. Phil's office has the story the company likes. This room has the story as it happened."],
  ["Do you sell a lot of souvenirs?","Kelpy figurines, mostly. Three hundred last month. I think people take them home to frighten their relatives."]],
 "Don't touch the bottling line. It's older than you and much more temperamental."],
5:["(Wilma is polishing a shelf with a soft cloth.) The roads are open, so I'm expecting visitors. I've put the good bottles at the front. It's like laying a table for people you're not sure will come.",[
  ["Who usually visits?","Schoolchildren, retired sailors, and the occasional spy from Diversified Petroleum, who takes notes on our fridge."],
  ["Do you like the job?","I love it. I don't love the till. Numbers and I came to an arrangement long ago: they leave me alone and I don't count them."],
  ["Any odd stories about the bottles?","The 1931 cap in the case by the door. A sailor sent it from Hamburg with a note that said only 'sorry'. I've never found out for what."]],
 "If you want a souvenir, buy two. One breaks, one doesn't."],
6:["(Wilma has a magnifying glass over a label.) Someone's been forging old Kelp Cola labels. Look at the printing: the blue's too bright. It's a good fake, but a fake.",[
  ["Who'd bother forging labels?","Collectors. There's a man in Helsinki who pays well. He'd pay a month's wages for something with 'Kelp' on it and a fault."],
  ["Does the company know?","Kurt does. He wants me to ignore it, because it's free advertising. I told him a forged label's a lie wearing the company's coat."],
  ["Can you teach me to spot a fake?","Smell it. Real ones smell of dust and sugar. Fakes smell of fresh ink. And look at the 'K'. The real 'K' has a tiny chip."]],
 "A museum is a place where you learn to doubt politely."],
7:["(Wilma is wiping the glass cabinet.) The north-west road's open now, and I've had two telephone calls from Frostholm, asking if we've got any pre-war Diversified Petroleum bottles. I told them we've got a lot of pre-war cola.",[
  ["Why would the oil company want old bottles?","To say they've been here as long as we have. Everything's about who's older, here. I've got dates, and they've got a fuel-tank."],
  ["What does the king's death mean for the museum?","He opened it. There's a plaque. I polish it every morning, and today I did it twice."],
  ["Is Jaana in today?","In the cup, yes. That girl's the best thing that ever happened to the place. She doesn't know it, and I'd never say it."]],
 "Careful with that door. It sticks in cold weather, and so do I."],
8:["(Wilma holds up a dark bottle to the light.) The old recipe's got a hint of anise. I can taste it, but I can't prove it. It's in no document in this building.",[
  ["Does anyone know the full recipe?","A very few. Phil says he does, but I've watched his face when he says it. He's got the look of a man reciting a poem he's only half learned."],
  ["Why is the formula such a secret?","Because it's the only thing the company has that nobody else can copy. A recipe's a ghost: you can't catch it, you can only keep it company."],
  ["Have you tried to find out?","For twenty years. I've got a notebook. It has fourteen guesses in it, and every one of them's been wrong."]],
 "If the notebook's ever right, I'll make you a cup of tea in celebration."],
9:["(Wilma has a letter spread on the counter.) Kurt's written to say the company's changing the formula. He's put it as though it were a weather report. It's a death, if you ask me.",[
  ["Is it a bad thing?","It's a change. The old one's what people remember from their childhood. You don't fix childhood."],
  ["What does Jaana say?","She says we should make a joke of it. 'Kelpy Version Two.' She's a clever girl, and she'll do the sums when I'm not looking."],
  ["Does the museum change too?","I'd have to rewrite half the labels. That's all right. Labels I can do. What I can't do's pretend a thing's the same when it isn't."]],
 "If anyone asks me, the old one's still the best. Don't quote me."],
10:["(Wilma has a black ribbon pinned to her cardigan, and has tied another round the plaque.) They're burying him this morning. I've kept the museum shut till noon. It's the first time since the flood.",[
  ["You liked the king?","I liked what he stood for: that nothing needs to change all at once. I don't know if that's true. But it was a comfort."],
  ["What was he like when he opened the museum?","Kind. Tired. He drank the cola and said, 'It tastes of the sea.' I've been writing that on a card for the cabinet ever since."],
  ["Will you go to the funeral?","No. I've got the museum to mind. And I'd probably start talking about the ceremony's silverware. I'd be thrown out."]],
 "A quiet day to you. It's the only kind it should be."],
11:["(Wilma peers out of the window at a crowd with leaflets.) The mill hands are out. I've sold fourteen Kelpy figurines to them since breakfast. Solidarity, apparently, comes with a souvenir.",[
  ["What do you make of the dispute?","Dagny's tight but she's not a fool. And the hands aren't wrong. It'll end with someone's pride bruised, which is how most things in Eastbrook end."],
  ["Do you know Dagny?","She comes in once a year to check the dates on the brass plaque for the lumber company's donation. She's meticulous about her own legacy, I'll say that."],
  ["Is Jaana joining in?","Of course not. She'd come in a cup. It would distract from the cause."]],
 "Come back when the shouting's over. Museums are best in the afterwards."],
12:["(Wilma is slowly writing a label in copperplate.) I'm always the last to know anything. A woman came by this morning to ask if the museum had any records. I said, 'Of what?' She said, 'Everything.'",[
  ["Was it Ruby?","Ruby asked about a particular 78, something about a cola jingle from the fifties. She thinks there's a version on a disc I've got in the vault."],
  ["Do you have a vault?","A cupboard. With a very serious lock. It's got three jingles, two radio advertisements and a gentleman's letter of complaint."],
  ["Is it exciting, working in a museum?","In a quiet way. Every object's a small mystery with a date on it. I like mysteries that don't expect you to solve them."]],
 "If you see Ruby, tell her I'll look. But she owes me a Coltrane."],
13:["(Wilma has a tray of tiny paper cups and an air of resentment.) Free samples. They're pushing them on every corner. Kurt thinks you can sell the past by giving it away. He's a young man.",[
  ["Is it a good idea?","It'll work. Which is not the same as being good. A cup of cola in your hand makes you like the company, for a while."],
  ["Do you hand them out too?","In the museum, yes. With a short lecture. People always say it's the lecture they remember. They mean the cola, but they're polite."],
  ["What would you do instead?","Give them a story. People will walk across the island for a story. A pint of fizz is fine, but a story's got legs."]],
 "I'll keep the cups tidy. It's a kind of faith."],
14:["(Wilma has put on her good shoes.) The big day. I'm closing early for it, which I've never done. A curator's job is to stay put and wait for history to arrive, but it seems to have moved.",[
  ["What are you expecting?","Something I won't like. And something I'll be glad of. It's usually both. The trouble is you never know which comes first."],
  ["Will the museum be involved?","It's got a stall, I'm told. Kurt's idea. Jaana's going in the cup, with a bell. I've told her to stay by the cart and away from the crowd."],
  ["How do you feel?","Old. And a bit like a bottle in a cabinet: I'd like to be looked at, but not handled."]],
 "Wish me a sensible day. It's all I ask of the world."],
15:["(Wilma is sweeping something small into a dustpan.) Whatever yesterday was, it's left glitter. I don't know where it came from. Some of it's in the till.",[
  ["How did the day go for you?","I didn't hear most of it. Jaana was knocked into a hedge, a man tried to buy the plaque, and I sold two hundred figurines. It was loud and it was profitable."],
  ["Are you worried about the company?","I'm always worried. It's the beginning of wisdom. I'm worried that it's growing and I'm not."],
  ["What's the mood on the street?","Tight. Like a coat that's a size too small. Everyone's smiling, and breathing in."]],
 "Mind the glitter. It gets everywhere. Even in the safe."],
16:["(Wilma has a poster rolled under her arm.) The debate's today. The Party and the Democrats. I'm told there'll be a thousand people in Eastbrook. I've ordered an extra box of cups.",[
  ["Who do you support?","Nobody. The company pays me, but I don't have to like all its friends. I've been to three elections and I've yet to see a speech that moved me."],
  ["Is your shop busy?","It'll be. Politics makes people thirsty. Both sides need a cola, and they'll both pay the same."],
  ["Is Jaana going?","She's already gone, in a smaller cup. A little one for travelling, she says. She looks like a tin of beans."]],
 "Don't let anyone talk you into anything. It's a dangerous day for opinions."],
17:["(Wilma has a hand flat on the counter.) Did you feel it? The whole cabinet hummed. A glass bottle sings when the ground moves. I've heard them do it twice in thirty years.",[
  ["What was the first time?","1953. The old mine collapsed in the north. This morning felt a little like it. The bottles knew before I did."],
  ["Is it the volcano?","It's something under the island. Lisa says the ground's always shaken. Lisa says a lot of things."],
  ["Have you moved anything?","The oldest ones, to the bottom shelf. If the floor drops, I'd rather lose the shelf than the bottle."]],
 "I'll tell you if I hear them sing again. It's the island's gossip."],
18:["(Wilma has a clipboard and a very small smile.) I'm giving someone a tour of the back rooms today. I've been told to show them 'everything'. I'm going to show them the cupboard of broken things.",[
  ["Who's the guest?","I'm told he's a visitor from head office. He's got a clipboard too. We stared at each other for a full minute before he spoke."],
  ["What's in the cupboard of broken things?","Everything the company's ever got wrong. A cola called 'Kelp Light', a mascot that was a seal, and the first ever cup."],
  ["A seal?","Seal Sam. He frightened children. He's in a sack. Jaana won't go near him."]],
 "A museum with only the successes would be a lie. Come and see the failures."],
19:["(Wilma has the glass cabinet door open, and is feeling the shelf with her palm.) The tremors again. This time the shelf moved a half a centimetre. I've marked it with a pencil line.",[
  ["Are you keeping a record?","Of course. If it goes, someone will want the date. I like to be the one who has it."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I'm methodical. It looks similar from outside. A curator can't be frightened. We put the object in the case and say: it was here."],
  ["What does Jaana say about it?","'Keep calm and wave.' She's a great philosopher in a very silly outfit."]],
 "If the pencil line moves again, I'll write it in the log. And tell you."],
20:["(Wilma smells the air with great suspicion.) Sulphur. I know the smell. It's the smell of the hot springs and of the old matches in my grandmother's kitchen.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","It's a sign. Rotten eggs mean the earth's clearing its throat. Dex says the steam's up above the north road. I've put the oldest bottles in the back."],
  ["Will you stay open?","As long as it's safe. If not, I'll lock up and take the cap collection home in a pram."],
  ["Do you believe the rumours?","I believe the smell. Rumours are voices. A smell's a fact."]],
 "Keep your boots by the door. And your wits in your pocket."],
21:["(Wilma has a mask of grey around her eyes, and a broom.) It happened. We're all alive, and everything's the colour of old pewter. I've stood in the doorway since dawn counting bottles.",[
  ["Is anything damaged?","Two. The glass is fine, but the 1936 beer-bottle one's lost its cork. It was a gift. I'm a bit tearful."],
  ["How are you?","Rather shaken, thank you. I've never seen the sky turn like that. It looked as if someone had spilled a cup of tea on the world."],
  ["Is it safe to go out?","For the moment. Keep a scarf over your mouth. Jaana's wearing her cup, which is very effective."]],
 "Take care of yourself. There's only one of you, unlike bottles."],
22:["(Wilma is on a step-ladder, wiping a shelf.) I've dusted the same shelf three times. Every time, a little more ash drifts in. It's like trying to bail out a boat with a teacup.",[
  ["What can you do?","Keep dusting. It's the only thing I'm good at. It's a kind of prayer, I suppose, for people who like cloths."],
  ["Is the company helping?","They sent a case of bottles to the north, and a man with a camera. The camera arrived first."],
  ["How's Jaana?","Frightened, and covering it with a dance. I've told her she can sit down when she needs to. She hasn't."]],
 "It'll pass. It always passes. And then you get to write the label."],
23:["(Wilma has a new label, drying on the counter.) 'Ash, 21 September.' I've added it to the cabinet. A jar of grey stuff next to the first cola. It seemed right.",[
  ["Is it for sale?","The ash? Of course. Ten marks. Jaana named it 'Volcano Dust'. I wouldn't have dared, but it's moving faster than the figurines."],
  ["Isn't it in poor taste?","Probably. But people want a piece of the thing that frightened them. It's the oldest trade there is."],
  ["Are you getting any rest?","I'm not sure what rest is. I tried to read, and fell asleep on the first line. I woke with a bottle on my lap."]],
 "Don't spend too much on souvenirs. The ash is free outside."],
24:["(Wilma is at the till, counting something slowly.) The aftershocks keep coming. They've begun to feel like a clock ticking in the walls. A grandfather clock, with bad news.",[
  ["Are you worried about Kurt's plans?","Yes. He wants to take the cola abroad. I'm not against change, but I fear we'll leave the best part of ourselves in a warehouse."],
  ["What's the best part?","The stubbornness. The odd old things. The fact that it was made on an island nobody could find. You can't ship that."],
  ["Will the museum survive?","Museums are the toughest things we've got. They survive wars, and then they survive being forgotten."]],
 "Come on a quiet afternoon. I'll show you where the best bottle's kept."],
25:["(Wilma's eyes are red, and she's blaming the ash.) A letter came this morning, from my sister in Turku. She's ill. I've not seen her in nine years. I was going to go. I kept saying next month.",[
  ["I'm sorry. Is it serious?","I think so. The letter doesn't say. It says 'come when you can', which is what people write when they mean 'come'."],
  ["Will you go?","Once the island's settled. There are boats again. But the museum, and Jaana, and the month. It's always something."],
  ["Does anyone else know?","You. I wouldn't normally. But reporters are good at keeping a story in their pockets, aren't they?"]],
 "Thank you for listening. It's been a long time since someone did."],
26:["(Wilma folds a sign neatly, with its words inward.) A Prince in Every Bottle. I wouldn't put it in the museum, but Kurt says it's the new campaign. I've put it in the cupboard with the seal.",[
  ["You don't like it?","I like princes, in the right place, which is a book. I've never been convinced you could bottle one. Or that one'd want it."],
  ["Is the company backing Leopold?","It's backing whoever's good for the cola. At present that's whoever has a nice smile and no opinions."],
  ["What do you think Phil wants?","A quiet life with a large bank balance. It's the most honest ambition anyone in this island has."]],
 "I'll do my job and I'll wait. A good curator always outlasts the exhibit."],
27:["(Wilma is wrapping a figurine in tissue.) I sold forty today. People are buying them like charms. 'Take Kelpy with you.' As if a small plastic cup could stop a lava flow.",[
  ["Is that what they think?","It's what they hope. Everyone needs a charm. Mine's a bottle cap. I keep it in my cardigan pocket."],
  ["Which cap?","1931, the one from Hamburg that says 'sorry'. I put it there because I'd like to be forgiven for something I've forgotten."],
  ["How's the company?","Busy. Fidgety. Harald's been through twice this week in a very good coat. He doesn't look at the bottles. He looks at the door."]],
 "Take a figurine. It's a smaller risk than a prayer."],
28:["(Wilma has locked the cabinets, one at a time, and is polishing the keys.) The company's biggest day, and I'm tidying. It's all I can think to do. Kurt rang. He's abroad, or about to be. He didn't say.",[
  ["Is the company going abroad?","Its cola is. I'm told the bottles are packed. I would like it understood that I was against the packing."],
  ["Will the museum close?","Not while I'm alive. And I'm in good health, thank you, apart from my knees and my sister."],
  ["What do you hope for?","That whatever happens, someone remembers to put the old bottles somewhere dry."]],
 "I'll stay. Someone has to keep the date."],
29:["(Wilma has put on her good cardigan.) Tomorrow it's all decided. I've been dusting since dawn, and I don't know what I'm dusting for. I only know that if I stop, I'll start thinking.",[
  ["What are you thinking about?","My sister. The company. Whether a museum's a place where the past lives or a place where it's been put to sleep."],
  ["Which do you think?","On a good day, the first. On a bad one, the second. Today I'd say it's a waiting room."],
  ["Is Jaana all right?","She's practising a speech in the cup. I've no idea what it is. It's sure to be better than mine."]],
 "Be careful. I'd like you to come back and tell us how it ended."],
30:["(Wilma's standing very still in the middle of the museum, with the key in her hand.) Whatever it is, it comes today. I've dusted every bottle. I've said good morning to every one. It seemed the right thing.",[
  ["Any last thoughts, Wilma?","Look after what's old, even when it's awkward. And look after the young, even when they're silly. Between them, they're the island."],
  ["Are you afraid?","A little. But I've seen the 1931 cap, and if it can survive a sailor, a war and Kurt, so can I."],
  ["What will you do next?","Buy a ticket to Turku. I decided last night. It isn't the island's business. It's mine."]],
 "Goodbye, reporter. Do come back and see us. We keep everything."],
};
// Yuri, a timber businessman (really KGB; he never says so) upstairs at Hotel Aurora, Frostholm (Communist Party). Threads: timber contracts, vodka and rounds, charm, his "letters home", the Party's vote, Anna and Helen, the volcano.
DAILY['Yuri']={
7:["(Yuri rises with a wide smile and a glass.) A reporter! Sit, sit. Yuri. Timber. I buy fir from anyone who'll sell it and vodka for anyone who'll drink it.",[
  ["Nice room. Is timber good business?","Splendid. Trees do not argue. Only the people who own them do."],
  ["What brings a timber man to Sarivalto?","Fir, pine, and a very good lake. The island is a gift, no?"],
  ["Is that a lot of radio equipment?","A hobby. I like to hear the world. In my line one must hear prices."]],
 "Drink, my friend. The first round is free."],
8:["(Yuri pours two small glasses.) Sunday, yes? Bells all morning. I like bells. In my country they were forbidden, here they are free. Everything is a matter of whose island it is.",[
  ["Are you religious?","I am respectful. It costs nothing, and it makes the monks sell me timber."],
  ["Which side would you like to win?","I am a guest. A guest likes whoever feeds him. Anna feeds me, mostly."],
  ["Heard any gossip?","Father Thyme cannot find words. Poor man. I would lend him some, but mine are all in Russian."]],
 "Another? No? Next time."],
9:["(Yuri toys with a bottle of cola.) Everyone makes cola now. The Party wants a cola, Kelp wants a cola. Cola is the new oil. I find that charming.",[
  ["Would you invest in cola?","I invest in trees. A tree is a safe bank."],
  ["Does the Party need a cola?","Helen is passionate. A people's drink, she says. I say a people's drink is vodka. Nobody listens."],
  ["Have you tried the new formula?","Too sweet. Like a politician's smile."]],
 "A toast to fizz."],
10:["(Yuri has put on a black tie.) The king's funeral. One respects the dead, even a king. I sent a wreath, with the card of my timber company. Very proper.",[
  ["Is a wreath from a timber company usual?","It is Sarivalto wood, so it is a local wreath. Practical sadness."],
  ["What did you think of the king?","I never met him. I met his accountants, which is like meeting a man's shadow."],
  ["What next for the island?","Much talk. Much vodka. Eventually, a vote."]],
 "Respect the dead and the weather."],
11:["(Yuri pats his coat.) The mill hands argue about wages. A lovely cause. Workers should be paid, that is the whole of my philosophy, and I am a capitalist, so you see my difficulty.",[
  ["Are you backing the mill hands?","Warmly, from a distance. The Party takes the credit, I take the timber."],
  ["Do you know Helen well?","She is a force of nature. I admire her, and I stay out of her weather."],
  ["Isn't it a contradiction?","Of course. The world is made of contradictions, like a good stew."]],
 "Contradictions are good for digestion."],
12:["(Yuri is writing a letter.) To my wife. Or someone. One writes letters home. They are very long, and full of weather and prices.",[
  ["Do you miss home?","The cold is the same. The food is worse. That is how I know I am abroad."],
  ["Do you write much?","Every night. My handwriting is bad, so the letters take hours."],
  ["Who reads them?","Many people, I imagine. Mail is a crowded road."]],
 "Weather is a safe subject."],
13:["(Yuri smiles.) Free samples on every corner. Such generosity from Kelp. One only gives free things when one wants a great deal.",[
  ["Is that a Communist view?","It is a human view. Nothing is free but air, and even that they are working on."],
  ["Did you take a sample?","I took three, for the glasses. The bottle is nice."],
  ["Who's winning the cola war?","The one who sells most. Or the one who steals the recipe. I hear there is talk of a recipe."]],
 "Careful with free things."],
14:["(Yuri adjusts his cuffs.) Tomorrow, the fourteenth. The whole island together. A big day. I shall be there, in the back, with my hat on, like a respectable man.",[
  ["What do you expect?","Speeches. Faces. A great deal of looking at each other to see who is looking."],
  ["Will the Party do well?","The Party does well when people are afraid of the future. The future is arriving tomorrow."],
  ["You sound confident.","I am a timber man. I know how things grow."]],
 "Wear your best coat."],
15:["(Yuri laughs softly.) Crown against cross. Delicious. Two old rivals fighting over an island that wants neither. In such a fight a third party wins.",[
  ["Which third party?","I did not say a party. I said a third."],
  ["Who's stronger, crown or cross?","The crown has soldiers, the cross has candles. Candles last longer."],
  ["Where does the Party stand?","On its own two feet, and a boot on someone else's, if invited."]],
 "Careful with boots."],
16:["(Yuri lifts a glass.) The great debate today. Helen and the Democrats. She will eat them. A vote will be the big topic, you know. Votes are very useful things.",[
  ["Useful how?","A vote is a door. Once the door is open, one can walk through it. Maybe to Moscow."],
  ["You want the island in the Soviet Union?","I did not say. I said a door."],
  ["Who will win the debate?","The best-prepared. That is Helen. She rehearsed for a week, in front of a mirror."]],
 "Doors, doors, doors."],
17:["(Yuri's glass trembles on the table.) You felt that? The earth, a little shake. In my home we have such things. I do not like them.",[
  ["Is it the mountain?","Everyone says. Old men, mainly. Old men know mountains."],
  ["Are you frightened?","I am cautious. Fear is a luxury."],
  ["Heard that Dario is missing?","Dario, the oil man? Interesting. A man of that size does not simply disappear. It is a mystery, and I like mysteries."]],
 "Hold your glass tight."],
18:["(Yuri looks out of his window.) Steam in the north. A white feather. Everybody pretends not to look at it. I look at it. I am bad at pretending.",[
  ["Will it erupt?","Someone with a thermometer would know. I have only a bottle."],
  ["What do you make of the relic story?","The Church lost something. It was always going to. Churches are good at collecting and bad at keeping."],
  ["Do you go to Kelp's museum?","I find the cup alarming. A cup with a face. It is a very capitalist idea."]],
 "Watch the sky."],
19:["(Yuri refills a glass.) Comrades wanted, they say, and everywhere the Party signs up fishermen and clerks. I give money to the cause. A small amount, with a large smile.",[
  ["Is Anna recruiting?","She recruits with soup. It is effective."],
  ["Will people join?","Some. People join what's warm."],
  ["Heard about the Midnight Note?","A club of songs. Leo plays saxophone. I enjoy his slow notes. They are good for thinking."]],
 "Sing something."],
20:["(Yuri has the radio on, low.) Another stranger in the hotel. Staying downstairs, asks about everyone. A man with a camera and a nervous smile. I like him. He is bad at it.",[
  ["Who is he?","A tourist, he says. Nobody tours Sarivalto in a month like this."],
  ["Are you suspicious?","I am hospitable. It is the same thing, looked at sideways."],
  ["What about the palace job?","I heard whispers. A crowd of whispers. The palace is a good place for them."]],
 "Don't trust cameras."],
21:["(Yuri's hands are steady, but his glass is empty.) That was no small shake. The mountain opens like a book. The sky is orange. I have seen worse, but not recently.",[
  ["Is it dangerous?","Lava, they say, to the north, in the empty places. People are safe, thank God. And I am not a man who says thank God easily."],
  ["What does Moscow think?","Moscow thinks what it thinks. I think about my window."],
  ["Will this change the vote?","A disaster changes everything. That is the one rule."]],
 "Close the window."],
22:["(Yuri brushes ash from the sill.) Grey everywhere. Like a winter that has run out of snow. Helen's people sweep the streets, and the Party gets the credit. Clever.",[
  ["Are you helping?","I donate vodka to the sweepers. It warms the hands."],
  ["How is business?","Timber is warm and dry. I do better in a crisis."],
  ["What about the farms?","A farm lost a roof. The Party sent blankets. Trust is bought in small things."]],
 "Cover your drink."],
23:["(Yuri is wiping ash from his radio.) A spanner in the works, I hear, at the dock. Someone wishes to slow the Party. Who? So many people.",[
  ["Do you suspect anyone?","It is better to suspect everyone gently."],
  ["Who's fixing the oil rig?","Diversified. Deep repairs. Deep is an interesting word."],
  ["Is the Party worried?","The Party is always a little worried. It keeps us thin."]],
 "Keep your tools close."],
24:["(Yuri raises an eyebrow.) The monks go up the mountain, to retreat. Brave men, or very devout, or avoiding something. Meanwhile the clean-up crews tidy up. I do like a tidy island.",[
  ["Who are the clean-up crews?","Some are Party, some are others. All of them know where the bodies are, in an expression."],
  ["Are you worried about the retreat?","The peaks are cold. Prayer's good for the knees."],
  ["Will you go up?","I go nowhere without a coat and a reason."]],
 "Cold air, clear mind."],
25:["(Yuri laughs.) A princess for democracy! A crown for everyone! Everyone's a monarchist today. If I stay another week, I will be one too.",[
  ["Is there really a princess?","There is always a princess. It is a law of islands."],
  ["How does the Party see it?","With patience. A princess is a pretty face on an old argument."],
  ["Do you like royals?","They are good for postage stamps."]],
 "Stamps are good business."],
26:["(Yuri taps a bottle.) Kelp puts a prince in every bottle. A prince in a bottle! It is cheaper than a prince on a throne. And Dario buys a spa. A very oily man in a hot bath.",[
  ["Why a spa?","Steam is where deals are done. Men talk when they are naked."],
  ["Do you like spas?","I have a sauna in my head. It is quiet."],
  ["Would you buy a prince?","A prince is a poor investment. They need so much feeding."]],
 "Stay warm."],
27:["(Yuri smiles over a cup.) The Party shows a revolution at the pictures tonight. It is a good film, with horses. Everybody cries at the end, or at the horses.",[
  ["Are you going?","In the second row. It is where one sees the faces of the audience."],
  ["Do you like propaganda?","It is simply persuasion in a good hat."],
  ["What about the Syndicate's high stakes?","They gamble. I invest. We are not the same, though we sit at the same table."]],
 "Pick a good seat."],
28:["(Yuri is in his best suit.) The day everyone pushes. The Party marches on Kingsgrad with a red flag. A very big day, and I am a small man in a window.",[
  ["Are you nervous?","I am never nervous. I am attentive."],
  ["What will the Party achieve?","A good march. After that, the vote. A vote is in the heart of the matter."],
  ["Will you march?","I do not march. I observe, and occasionally, I toast."]],
 "Toasts are my marching."],
29:["(Yuri lights a cigarette.) One day left, and every faction sends their biggest guns. The oil men, the cola men, the Syndicate. It is a very noisy island.",[
  ["Who will win?","The one who is not noisy. That is always the lesson."],
  ["What will you do after?","Write a long letter home."],
  ["Is the Party ready?","As ready as a Party can be. Anna has soup, Helen has speeches. It is enough."]],
 "Sleep, if you can."],
30:["(Yuri lifts his glass, slowly.) So. The last day. Whatever it brings, it has been a pleasure to know you, reporter. I hope your readers in London will understand us. I doubt it.",[
  ["Thank you, Yuri. Any last words?","A toast to the island, to the wind, and to the trees."],
  ["How do you feel?","Calm. It is the calm of a man who has done his work."],
  ["Will you stay?","Wherever I am sent, I go. Even to the end of a vodka bottle."]],
 "Za zdorovye. Go well."],
};
